window.LibraryIndex = {"schema_version": 1, "sermons": [{"author": "Louis Bourdaloue", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-lent", "calendar_label": "Lent", "centuries": [17, 18], "composition": "Approximately late seventeenth century\u20131704; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition", "language": "fr", "language_label": "French", "metadata": ["Louis Bourdaloue, Sermons du Pere Bourdaloue, de la Compagnie de Jesus, pour le Caresme, tome premier, nouvelle \u00e9dition (Paris: aux d\u00e9pens de Rigaud, Directeur de l'Imprimerie Royale, 1716), Sermon pour le Mercredi de la seconde Semaine. Sur l'Ambition, pp. 502\u2013546. Kelly Library copy.", "Internet Archive; John M. Kelly Library Rare Books, University of Toronto; sponsored by the University of Ottawa.", "Lent", "Wednesday of the second week of Lent", "Matthew 11", "Matthew 20 (printed references; adapted service sayings identified separately)", "Matthew \u2014 chapter numeral uncertain in print; following Ibidem./Ibid. retained", "Matthew 20:22\u201323 \u2014 added by editors; printed chapter remains uncertain", "Matthew 20:26 / Luke 22:26 \u2014 parallels added by editors for adapted service sayings", "Sirach 15:14 \u2014 added by editors; no printed marginal locator", "Hebrews 5 (damaged initial H supplied)", "Psalm 82 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 53", "Hebrews 1", "Matthew 10 (printed locator; quotation identified separately)", "Matthew 20:26 \u2014 reference added by editors for Non ita erit inter vos", "1 Peter 5", "Psalm 118 (historical numbering)", "1 Timothy 3"], "occasion": "Mercredi de la seconde Semaine [du Car\u00eame]", "occasion_detail": "Wednesday of the second week of Lent", "occasion_label": "Lent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Beginning with the sons of Zebedee seeking the highest places in Christ\u2019s kingdom, Bourdaloue examines worldly honors in relation to God, to one\u2019s neighbor and to oneself. The three parts treat honors as callings from God, obligations to serve others, and commitments to labor and suffering. He applies this argument to civil and ecclesiastical office, condemning advancement through intrigue or birth, domineering authority and neglect of duty. The conclusion prays for a right use of honors rather than the loss of eternal glory."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-h001", "label": "French original", "text": "SERMON POUR LE MERCREDI de la seconde Semaine. Sur l'Ambition."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON FOR WEDNESDAY of the Second Week. On Ambition.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-e001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Respondens autem Jesus, dixit : Nescitis quid petatis. Potestis bibere calicem quem ego bibiturus sum ? Dicunt ei : Possumus. Ait illis : Calicem quidem meum bibetis : sedere autem ad dexteram meam vel sinistram, non est meum dare vobis."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-e001", "label": "English", "text": "But Jesus answered and said: You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup that I am to drink? They say to him: We can. He says to them: You shall indeed drink my cup; but to sit at my right hand or my left is not mine to grant you.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-e002", "label": "French original", "text": "Jesus leur r\u00e9pondit, & leur dit : Vous ne s\u00e7avez ce que vous demandez. Pouvez-vous boire le calice que je boirai ? Ils lui dirent : Nous le pouvons. Alors il leur r\u00e9pliqua : Vous boirez le calice que je dois boire : mais d'\u00eatre assis \u00e0 ma droite ou \u00e0 ma gauche, ce n'est pas \u00e0 moi de vous l'accorder. En saint Matth. chap. 20."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-e002", "label": "English", "text": "Jesus answered them and said: You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup that I shall drink? They said to him: We can. Then he replied: You shall drink the cup that I must drink; but to sit at my right hand or my left is not mine to grant you. In Saint Matthew, chapter 20.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-h002", "label": "French original", "text": "Sire,"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-h002", "label": "English", "text": "Sire,", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p001", "label": "French original", "text": "Ce n'est pas sans une providence particuli\u00e8re, que Jesus-Christ qui venoit enseigner aux hommes l'humilit\u00e9, choisit des disciples dont les sentimens furent d'abord si oppos\u00e9s \u00e0 cette vertu ; & qui dans la bassesse de leur condition, avant que le Saint Esprit les e\u00fbt purifi\u00e9s, ne laissoient pas d'\u00eatre superbes, ambitieux, & jaloux des honneurs du monde. Il vouloit dans les d\u00e9sordres de leur ambition, nous d\u00e9couvrir les n\u00f4tres ; & dans les le\u00e7ons toutes divines qu'il leur faisoit sur un point si essentiel, nous donner des r\u00e9gles pour former nos m\u0153urs, & pour nous r\u00e9duire \u00e0 la pratique de cette sainte & bienheureuse humilit\u00e9, sans laquelle il n'y a point de pi\u00e9t\u00e9 solide ni m\u00eame de vrai Christianisme. C'est le sujet de notre Evangile : deux disciples se pr\u00e9sentent devant le Sauveur du monde, & le prient de leur accorder les deux premi\u00e8res places de son Royaume. Comme ils ne le connoissoient pas encore ce Royaume spirituel, & qu'ils ne l'envisageoient que comme un Royaume temporel, il est \u00e9vident que l'ambition seule, & le d\u00e9sir de s'\u00e9lever au-dessus des autres les porta \u00e0 lui faire cette demande. Mais vous s\u00e7avez, Chr\u00e9tiens, comment ils furent re\u00e7us : & de ce qui se passa dans une occasion si remarquable, nous pouvons ais\u00e9ment reconno\u00eetre en quoi consiste le d\u00e9sordre de l'ambition, quels en sont les divers caract\u00e8res, quels en sont les effets & les suites, & quels en doivent \u00eatre enfin les rem\u00e8des. Mati\u00e8re d'autant plus importante & plus n\u00e9cessaire, que l'ambition est sur-tout le vice de la Cour. Car quoiqu'il n'y ait point d'\u00e9tat \u00e0 couvert de cette passion, & que sa sph\u00e9re, pour ainsi parler, soit aussi \u00e9tendue que le monde ; on peut dire n\u00e9anmoins, & il est vrai, que c'est particuli\u00e8rement dans les Palais des Rois que se trouvent les ambitieux ; Ecce in domibus Regum sunt ; que c'est l\u00e0 qu'ils forment de plus grands projets, l\u00e0 qu'ils font jouer plus de ressorts, & l\u00e0 m\u00eame qu'il est beaucoup plus difficile de les d\u00e9tromper & de les gu\u00e9rir. Il y a des vices, dit saint Chrysostome, que l'on combat sans peine & qui se d\u00e9truisent d'eux-m\u00eames, parce que le monde, tout aveugle & tout corrompu qu'il est, a toutefois encore assez de lumi\u00e8re pour en avoir la honte & assez de raison pour les condamner. Mais \u00e0 la Cour, bien loin de se faire un crime de l'ambition, on s'en fait une vertu ; ou si elle y passe pour un vice, du reste on la regarde comme le vice des grandes ames, & l'on aime mieux les vices des grandes ames que les vertus des simples & des petits. J'ai donc aujourd'hui sp\u00e9cialement besoin des graces du ciel. Demandons-les par l'intercession de la plus humble des Vierges. Ave, Maria."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p001", "label": "English", "text": "It was through a particular providence that Jesus Christ, who came to teach men humility, chose disciples whose sentiments were at first so opposed to this virtue, and who, despite their lowly station, before the Holy Spirit had purified them, were nevertheless proud, ambitious and eager for worldly honours. In the disorders of their ambition he wished to reveal our own to us; and in the wholly divine lessons he gave them on so essential a point, to give us rules for shaping our conduct and bringing us to practise that holy and blessed humility without which there is no solid piety or even true Christianity. This is the subject of our Gospel: two disciples come before the Saviour of the world and ask him to grant them the two highest places in his Kingdom. Since they did not yet understand this spiritual Kingdom, and saw it only as a temporal kingdom, it is clear that ambition alone, and the desire to rise above others, prompted their request. But you know, Christians, how they were received; and from what happened on so remarkable an occasion we can readily recognise what constitutes the disorder of ambition, its different characteristics, its effects and consequences, and finally what its remedies must be. The subject is all the more important and necessary because ambition is above all the vice of the Court. For although no station is safe from this passion, and its sphere, so to speak, is as extensive as the world, one may nevertheless truly say that it is especially in the palaces of kings that the ambitious are found: Behold, they are in kings' houses. There they devise greater schemes, there they set more machinery in motion, and there it is far harder to undeceive and cure them. There are vices, Saint Chrysostom says, that one can combat without difficulty and that destroy themselves, because the world, blind and corrupt though it is, still has enough light to be ashamed of them and enough reason to condemn them. But at Court, far from regarding ambition as a crime, people make a virtue of it; or if it passes for a vice, they still consider it the vice of great souls, and prefer the vices of great souls to the virtues of the simple and lowly. Today, then, I have a special need of heaven's graces. Let us ask for them through the intercession of the humblest of Virgins. Hail, Mary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p002", "label": "French original", "text": "Il n'appartient qu'\u00e0 Dieu de nous donner les v\u00e9ritables id\u00e9es des choses ; & dans le sujet que je traite, renon\u00e7ant \u00e0 mes propres pens\u00e9es, je dois m'en tenir uniquement aux instructions de notre divin ma\u00eetre, puisqu'en trois paroles de l'Evangile il me fournit lui-m\u00eame le dessein le plus naturel, le plus juste & le plus complet. Comprenez-le bien, s'il vous pla\u00eet."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p002", "label": "English", "text": "God alone can give us true ideas of things; and in the subject I am treating, renouncing my own thoughts, I must adhere solely to the instructions of our divine master, since in three sayings from the Gospel he himself provides me with the most natural, accurate and complete plan. Please understand it well.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p003", "label": "French original", "text": "Ces deux freres, enfans de Z\u00e9b\u00e9d\u00e9e, demandent au Sauveur du monde les deux premi\u00e8res places de son royaume ; & le Sauveur du monde, au lieu de leur r\u00e9pondre pr\u00e9cis\u00e9ment, & de s'expliquer sur leur proposition, leur en fait trois autres bien diff\u00e9rentes. Car premi\u00e8rement il leur d\u00e9clare, que ce n'est point lui, mais son Pere qui doit nous \u00e9lever \u00e0 ces places & \u00e0 ces rangs d'honneur dont ils paroissent si jaloux : Sedere autem ad dexteram meam vel sinistram, non est meum dare vobis, sed quibus paratum est \u00e0 Patre meo. Secondement, il leur fait entendre qu'ils ne doivent point chercher, comme les nations infid\u00e8les, \u00e0 dominer ; mais que celui d'entr'eux qui veut \u00eatre grand, doit \u00e9tablir pour principe de se regarder comme le serviteur des autres, & croire que la pr\u00e9s\u00e9ance o\u00f9 il aspire, ne sera pour lui qu'un fonds de d\u00e9pendance & d'assujettissement : Non ita erit inter vos, sed qui voluerit inter vos major fieri, fiat sicut minor ; & qui pr\u00e6cessor est, sicut ministrator. Enfin il les interroge \u00e0 son tour, & il veut s\u00e7avoir d'eux s'ils pourront boire son calice, c'est-\u00e0-dire, le calice de ses souffrances : Potestis bibere calicem, quem ego bibiturus sum ? Trois choses, Chr\u00e9tiens, parfaitement propres \u00e0 d\u00e9truire trois erreurs, dont ces deux Ap\u00f4tres \u00e9toient pr\u00e9venus. Car ils supposoient sans remonter plus haut, que Jesus-Christ en qualit\u00e9 d'homme leur pouvoit donner ces places honorables qu'ils ambitionnoient ; & Jesus-Christ leur fait conno\u00eetre que nul ne peut l\u00e9gitimement les occupper, hors ceux \u00e0 qui elles ont \u00e9t\u00e9 pr\u00e9par\u00e9es & assign\u00e9es par son Pere c\u00e9leste. Leur pr\u00e9tention, en obtenant ces deux places, \u00e9toit de se distinguer des autres, & de prendre l'ascendant sur eux ; & Jesus-Christ les d\u00e9trompe en les avertissant que d'\u00eatre plac\u00e9 au-dessus des autres, n'est qu'une obligation plus \u00e9troite de travailler pour les autres & de les servir. Enfin ils se proposoient dans ce pr\u00e9tendu royaume de Jesus-Christ & dans cette pr\u00e9s\u00e9ance imaginaire, une vie douce & commode ; & Jesus-Christ leur apprend combien cette pr\u00e9s\u00e9ance leur doit co\u00fbter, & que pour l'avoir, il faut boire un calice d'amertume, & \u00eatre baptis\u00e9 d'un bapt\u00eame de sang."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p003", "label": "English", "text": "These two brothers, the sons of Zebedee, ask the Saviour of the world for the two highest places in his kingdom; and the Saviour of the world, instead of answering them directly and explaining his position on their proposal, puts three very different propositions before them. First he declares that it is not he but his Father who must raise us to those places and ranks of honour which they seem so eager to obtain: To sit at my right hand or my left is not mine to grant you, but belongs to those for whom it has been prepared by my Father. Second, he makes them understand that they must not seek to dominate like the unbelieving nations; rather, whoever among them wishes to be great must make it his principle to regard himself as the servant of the others, and believe that the precedence to which he aspires will be for him only a source of dependence and subjection: It shall not be so among you, but let him who wishes to become greater among you be as the lesser, and the leader as one who serves. Finally, he questions them in turn and wishes to know whether they can drink his cup, that is, the cup of his sufferings: Can you drink the cup that I am to drink? Three things, Christians, perfectly suited to destroy three errors that had taken hold of these two Apostles. For, without looking any higher, they supposed that Jesus Christ as man could give them those honourable places they coveted; and Jesus Christ makes them understand that no one can legitimately occupy them except those for whom his heavenly Father has prepared and assigned them. In obtaining these two places they intended to distinguish themselves from the others and gain ascendancy over them; and Jesus Christ undeceives them by warning them that to be placed above others is simply to be more strictly obliged to work for them and serve them. Finally, in this supposed kingdom of Jesus Christ and this imaginary precedence, they envisaged a pleasant, comfortable life; and Jesus Christ teaches them how much that precedence must cost them, and that to obtain it they must drink a cup of bitterness and be baptised with a baptism of blood.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p004", "label": "French original", "text": "Le\u00e7ons admirables, o\u00f9 il semble que le Fils de Dieu ait voulu ramasser tout ce que la morale Chr\u00e9tienne a de plus fort, pour corriger les d\u00e9sordres de notre ambition. Car prenez garde, mes chers Auditeurs : les honneurs du si\u00e9cle que notre ambition nous fait rechercher avec tant d'ardeur, peuvent \u00eatre consid\u00e9r\u00e9s en trois mani\u00e8res, ou selon trois rapports qui leur conviennent. Par rapport \u00e0 Dieu, qui en est le distributeur ; par rapport au prochain, au-dessus de qui ils nous \u00e9l\u00e8vent ; & par rapport \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames, qui les poss\u00e9dons ou qui nous les procurons. Sous le premier rapport les honneurs du si\u00e9cle sont dans l'ordre de la pr\u00e9destination \u00e9ternelle, autant de vocations de Dieu : & notre ambition les prophane, en les recherchant comme des avantages purement temporels : ce sera la premi\u00e8re partie. Sous le second rapport les honneurs du si\u00e9cle sont de vrais assujettissemens \u00e0 servir le prochain ; & notre ambition en abuse, en les recherchant pour exercer un vain empire & une fi\u00e9re domination : ce sera la seconde partie. Sous le troisi\u00e9me rapport les honneurs du si\u00e9cle sont des engagemens indispensables \u00e0 travailler & \u00e0 souffrir ; & notre ambition les corrompt, en les recherchant dans la vue d'y trouver une vie tranquille & agr\u00e9able : ce sera la conclusion de ce discours. Armons-nous donc aujourd'hui, contre une passion si dangereuse, des trois maximes du Sauveur du monde ; & quand l'ambition nous tente, & qu'elle nous sollicite de nous pousser \u00e0 certains rangs distingu\u00e9s dans le monde, disons-lui que ce n'est pas elle, mais Dieu qui nous y doit appeller, parce que ces rangs, quoique rangs du monde, sont en effet de la disposition & du ressort de Dieu ; Sed quibus paratum est \u00e0 Patre meo : premi\u00e8re v\u00e9rit\u00e9. Quand elle nous inspire un orgueil cach\u00e9 & qu'elle nous flatte d'une secr\u00e9te complaisance de voir les autres au-dessous de nous, opposons-lui ce grand oracle de la sagesse \u00e9vang\u00e9lique, que celui qui se trouve le premier, doit \u00eatre le serviteur & l'esclave ; Et qui pr\u00e6cessor est, sicut ministrator : seconde v\u00e9rit\u00e9. Quand elle nous attire par l'esp\u00e9rance des commodit\u00e9s de la vie, & des douceurs qui semblent accompagner les dignit\u00e9s & les emplois \u00e9clatans, confondons-la par le souvenir des devoirs laborieux, & m\u00eame des croix ins\u00e9parables de ces emplois & de ces dignit\u00e9s, & demandons-nous \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames, pourrai-je boire ce calice ? Potestis bibere calicem ? troisi\u00e9me & derni\u00e8re v\u00e9rit\u00e9. C'est tout le sujet de votre attention."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p004", "label": "English", "text": "Admirable lessons, in which the Son of God seems to have gathered everything most powerful in Christian morality to correct the disorders of our ambition. For observe, my dear hearers: the worldly honours which our ambition makes us seek so eagerly may be considered in three ways, or under three relations proper to them. In relation to God, who distributes them; in relation to our neighbour, above whom they raise us; and in relation to ourselves, who possess or procure them. Under the first relation, worldly honours are, in the order of eternal predestination, so many calls from God; and our ambition profanes them by seeking them as purely temporal advantages. This will be the first part. Under the second relation, worldly honours truly subject us to serving our neighbour; and our ambition abuses them by seeking them in order to exercise a vain sway and proud domination. This will be the second part. Under the third relation, worldly honours are indispensable commitments to labour and suffering; and our ambition corrupts them by seeking them in the hope of finding a peaceful and pleasant life. This will conclude the discourse. Let us therefore arm ourselves today against so dangerous a passion with the three maxims of the Saviour of the world. When ambition tempts us and urges us to push ourselves into distinguished ranks in the world, let us tell it that God, rather than ambition, must call us there, because those ranks, worldly though they are, are in fact subject to God's disposal and jurisdiction: But belongs to those for whom it has been prepared by my Father. This is the first truth. When it inspires hidden pride in us and flatters us with a secret satisfaction at seeing others beneath us, let us oppose to it this great declaration of Gospel wisdom: whoever holds the first place must be the servant and slave; And the leader as one who serves. This is the second truth. When it attracts us by the hope of life's comforts and of the pleasures that seem to accompany dignities and illustrious offices, let us confound it by recalling the laborious duties, and even the crosses, inseparable from these offices and dignities, and ask ourselves: Shall I be able to drink this cup? Can you drink the cup? This is the third and final truth. This is the whole subject to which I ask your attention.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-h003", "label": "French original", "text": "I. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-h003", "label": "English", "text": "PART I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p005", "label": "French original", "text": "Quelque libert\u00e9 que Dieu ait donn\u00e9e \u00e0 l'homme en le laissant, comme parle l'Ecriture, entre les mains de son conseil, c'est une maxime g\u00e9n\u00e9rale, fond\u00e9e sur tous les principes de la religion, qu'il n'y a point d'\u00e9tat dans la vie, o\u00f9 il soit permis \u00e0 l'homme Chr\u00e9tien d'entrer sans vocation de Dieu ; point de condition dont la premi\u00e8re & l'essentielle r\u00e9gle ne soit d'y \u00eatre appell\u00e9 de Dieu ; point de rang, ni d'emploi qui ne devienne dangereux, quand on s'y engage sans avoir consult\u00e9 Dieu. En cela, dit S. Chrysostome, consiste le droit de souverainet\u00e9 que Dieu s'est r\u00e9serv\u00e9 sur la cr\u00e9ature raisonnable & intelligente ; & moi je dis, en cela consiste le bienheureux engagement qu'a la cr\u00e9ature raisonnable & intelligente, \u00e0 n'user de sa libert\u00e9 & de ses droits, que d\u00e9pendamment de Dieu son Seigneur & son souverain, puisqu'il n'y a rien qui se trouve si \u00e9troitement li\u00e9 avec le salut que ce que nous appellons vocation."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p005", "label": "English", "text": "Whatever freedom God has given man by leaving him, as Scripture puts it, in the hands of his own counsel, it is a general maxim, founded on all the principles of religion, that there is no state in life which a Christian may enter without God's call; no condition whose first and essential rule is not that God should call him to it; no rank or office that does not become dangerous when he enters it without consulting God. In this, says Saint Chrysostom, consists the sovereign right that God has reserved over the rational and intelligent creature; and I say, in this consists the blessed obligation of the rational and intelligent creature to use his freedom and his rights only in dependence on God, his Lord and sovereign, since nothing is so closely bound up with salvation as what we call vocation.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p006", "label": "French original", "text": "En effet, mes chers Auditeurs, toute notre pr\u00e9destination roule presque sur ce point, je veux dire, sur le choix des \u00e9tats que nous embrassons. De-l\u00e0 d\u00e9pend presque uniquement le bonheur ou le malheur de notre \u00e9ternit\u00e9 ; & en voici la raison : parce que la pr\u00e9destination, disent les th\u00e9ologiens, n'est rien autre chose de la part de Dieu, qu'un certain encha\u00eenement de graces qui nous sont pr\u00e9par\u00e9es ; & de notre part qu'une suite d'actions sur quoi est appuy\u00e9 le jugement d\u00e9cisif que Dieu fait de nous. Or la plupart des graces que nous recevons, sont des graces d\u00e9termin\u00e9es \u00e0 notre \u00e9tat ; & presque tous les p\u00e9ch\u00e9s que nous commettons, viennent des tentations & des dangers o\u00f9 nous expose notre \u00e9tat. Combien de r\u00e9prouv\u00e9s dans l'enfer auroient v\u00e9cu sur la terre comme des Saints, s'ils avoient suivi la voix de Dieu en embrassant l'\u00e9tat o\u00f9 Dieu les appelloit : & combien de Saints dans le ciel auroient \u00e9t\u00e9 sur la terre des impies & des libertins, s'ils avoient choisi telle condition o\u00f9 Dieu ne les appelloit pas !"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p006", "label": "English", "text": "Indeed, my dear hearers, almost the whole of our predestination turns on this point, I mean on the choice of the states of life we embrace. On this almost entirely depends the happiness or misery of our eternity; and here is the reason: predestination, theologians say, is on God's part nothing other than a certain chain of graces prepared for us, and on our part a succession of actions upon which God's final judgment of us rests. Now most of the graces we receive are graces appointed for our state; and almost all the sins we commit arise from the temptations and dangers to which our state exposes us. How many of the damned in hell would have lived on earth as Saints had they followed God's voice by embracing the state to which he called them! And how many Saints in heaven would have been impious men and libertines on earth had they chosen a condition to which God did not call them!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p007", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est le raisonnement que tout Chr\u00e9tien doit faire en prenant les choses dans leur source primitive, qui est l'adorable providence. Or quoique ce principe soit universel, & qu'il convienne \u00e9galement \u00e0 tout ce qui peut \u00eatre dans la vie un sujet de d\u00e9lib\u00e9ration & d'\u00e9lection, il faut n\u00e9anmoins reconno\u00eetre qu'il doit \u00eatre sur-tout appliqu\u00e9 \u00e0 ce qui regarde les honneurs du si\u00e9cle & notre agrandissement dans le monde. Je veux dire, que pour parvenir s\u00fbrement & irr\u00e9prochablement aux honneurs du si\u00e9cle, il faut une vocation plus expresse, plus certaine, plus infaillible. Car c'est ainsi que l'Ap\u00f4tre l'a hautement d\u00e9clar\u00e9, en publiant cette loi si solemnelle que l'ambition des hommes a toujours affect\u00e9 de contredire, mais que la parole de Dieu lui opposera \u00e9ternellement, s\u00e7avoir, que nul ne doit s'attribuer l'honneur \u00e0 lui-m\u00eame, mais qu'il est uniquement pour celui \u00e0 qui Dieu le destine ; Nec quisquam sumit sibi honorem, sed qui vocatur \u00e0 Deo. R\u00e9gle \u00e9galement fond\u00e9e, & sur l'int\u00e9r\u00eat de Dieu, & sur l'int\u00e9r\u00eat de l'homme. Int\u00e9r\u00eat de Dieu, puisque c'est \u00e0 lui que l'honneur appartient & par cons\u00e9quent \u00e0 lui seul qu'il appartient aussi de le donner comme il lui pla\u00eet, quand il lui pla\u00eet, & \u00e0 qui il lui pla\u00eet. Car s'il est de son droit & de sa grandeur d'ordonner de tout dans le monde, n'est-il pas \u00e0 plus forte raison de cette m\u00eame grandeur & de ce m\u00eame droit, de r\u00e9gler \u00e0 son gr\u00e9 & selon ses v\u00fbes ce qu'il y a dans le monde de plus distingu\u00e9 ? Int\u00e9r\u00eat de l'homme, puisqu'on peut dire en g\u00e9n\u00e9ral qu'il n'y a rien de plus dangereux pour le salut de l'homme, que l'\u00e9l\u00e9vation : mais si toute \u00e9l\u00e9vation est dangereuse, combien l'est celle o\u00f9 l'on s'est port\u00e9 de soi-m\u00eame & selon les d\u00e9sirs de son c\u0153ur ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p007", "label": "English", "text": "This is how every Christian must reason, taking things back to their first source, which is adorable providence. Now although this principle is universal and applies equally to everything in life that may call for deliberation and choice, we must nevertheless acknowledge that it applies above all to worldly honours and our advancement in the world. I mean that to attain worldly honours safely and blamelessly, we need a more express, more certain, more unfailing call. For the Apostle declared this openly in proclaiming that solemn law which men's ambition has always made a point of contradicting, but which God's word will eternally set against it: no one must take honour upon himself; it belongs solely to him for whom God intends it. No one takes honour upon himself, but he who is called by God. This rule rests equally on God's interest and on man's. God's interest, because honour belongs to him, and consequently he alone may give it as he pleases, when he pleases and to whom he pleases. For if his right and greatness entitle him to order everything in the world, do not that same greatness and right entitle him all the more to dispose of the world's most distinguished positions as he pleases and according to his designs? Man's interest, because one may say in general that nothing is more dangerous to man's salvation than elevation; but if all elevation is dangerous, how much more dangerous is that to which a man has raised himself, following the desires of his heart?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p008", "label": "French original", "text": "Quoi qu'il en soit, Chr\u00e9tiens, voil\u00e0 la r\u00e9gle que nous devons suivre ; mais est-ce la r\u00e9gle que nous suivons ? Ah ! c'est ici que votre attention m'est n\u00e9cessaire, & je n'aurois qu'\u00e0 consulter l'exp\u00e9rience pour vous convaincre de ce que j'ai maintenant \u00e0 vous reprocher ou \u00e0 d\u00e9plorer avec vous. Les honneurs du monde sont dans les principes de la pr\u00e9destination \u00e9ternelle, autant de vocations de Dieu : mais le scandale du Christianisme est de les voir aujourd'hui trait\u00e9s comme les choses les plus prophanes. Car au m\u00e9pris de saint Paul & de sa r\u00e9gle, on y entre sans vocation ; on les obtient par brigue & par artifice ; de quelque nature qu'ils soient, on les regarde comme d\u00fbs \u00e0 sa naissance ; on les poursuit comme des r\u00e9compenses de ses services : on en fait des \u00e9tablissemens de famille & de maison ; on les mesure par le plus ou le moins d'int\u00e9r\u00eat, le plus ou le moins de profit qui en revient ; on en fait des commerces sordides & honteux. Et tout cela sans remords, sans inqui\u00e9tude, parce qu'on s'autorise d'une prescription imaginaire & d'un faux usage ; comme si le d\u00e9r\u00e9glement de notre conduite pouvoit jamais devenir un titre contre les droits de Dieu. Sur quoi g\u00e9missons-nous, si ce n'est pas sur de semblables abus ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p008", "label": "English", "text": "Be that as it may, Christians, this is the rule we ought to follow; but is it the rule we do follow? Ah, here I need your attention, and I should only have to consult experience to convince you of what I must now reproach you with, or lament with you. In the principles of eternal predestination, worldly honours are so many calls from God; but it is a scandal to Christianity to see them treated today as the most profane things. For in contempt of Saint Paul and his rule, people enter upon them without a vocation; obtain them through canvassing and artifice; regard them, whatever their nature, as owed to their birth; pursue them as rewards for their services; make them means of establishing their family and house; assess them by the greater or lesser advantage and profit they yield; and make them objects of sordid and shameful traffic. All this without remorse or concern, because they claim the authority of an imaginary right established by long possession and a false custom, as though the disorder of our conduct could ever become a title against God's rights. What should we lament, if not such abuses?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p009", "label": "French original", "text": "Venons au d\u00e9tail ; & quelque confusion qu'il nous en co\u00fbte, ne craignons point de d\u00e9couvrir nos plaies, dans la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 pressante & extr\u00eame o\u00f9 nous sommes de les gu\u00e9rir. On se pousse aux honneurs du si\u00e9cle sans vocation ; & je n'en suis pas surpris, puisque l'erreur va jusqu'\u00e0 supposer, qu'il ne faut point pour ces sortes d'\u00e9tats de vocation. Il faut une grace de vocation pour embrasser une vie humble dans le clo\u00eetre ; on en convient : mais pour s'\u00e9lever aux premiers rangs, mais pour \u00eatre assis sur les tribunaux, mais pour se charger des affaires publiques, mais pour exercer des emplois, o\u00f9 l'on a entre les mains les int\u00e9r\u00eats de toute une ville, de toute une province, de tout un Royaume ; mais pour occuper des places qui demanderoient, s'il \u00e9toit possible, la saintet\u00e9 des Anges, l'ambition d'un homme & sa cupidit\u00e9 suffit ; c'est \u00e0 lui-m\u00eame d'\u00eatre l'auteur de sa destin\u00e9e, & il n'a qu'\u00e0 s'en rapporter \u00e0 son propre t\u00e9moignage ou plut\u00f4t \u00e0 sa pr\u00e9somption. Le Fils de Dieu a beau dire dans notre Evangile, que ces places ne sont que pour ceux \u00e0 qui son Pere les a destin\u00e9es ; Sed quibus paratum est \u00e0 Patre meo : cette destination du Pere c\u00e9leste est un myst\u00e8re inconnu \u00e0 l'ambitieux. Envain saint Chrysostome lui remontre-t-il, que ces emplois ont des engagemens n\u00e9cessaires avec la conscience, & par cons\u00e9quent qu'ils doivent \u00eatre, si j'ose ainsi parler, du domaine de la grace : ce domaine de la grace qui l'incommode, & qui borneroit ses projets, lui paro\u00eet chim\u00e9rique. Envain S. Bernard lui fait-il entendre que plus ces honneurs sont relev\u00e9s & distingu\u00e9s, plus ils demandent une vocation qui les sanctifie : l'habitude qu'il s'est faite de n'y proc\u00e9der que par les v\u00fbes d'une prudence charnelle, le rend insensible \u00e0 tout. Pour les dignit\u00e9s m\u00eames de l'Eglise quel \u00e9gard a-t-on aujourd'hui \u00e0 la vocation divine ? y engager des enfans encore incapables d'\u00eatre appell\u00e9s, les y faire entrer avant qu'ils soient en \u00e9tat de les conno\u00eetre ; & quand cette connoissance leur est enfin venue, les forcer, au hasard de leur damnation, \u00e0 s'en tenir l\u00e0, est-ce agir dans la pens\u00e9e que ces dignit\u00e9s Eccl\u00e9siastiques sont d'un ordre spirituel, & qu'il n'appartient qu'\u00e0 Dieu m\u00eame d'en disposer ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p009", "label": "English", "text": "Let us turn to particulars; and however much shame it costs us, let us not fear to uncover our wounds, so urgent and extreme is our need to heal them. People push themselves into worldly honours without a vocation; and I am not surprised, since the error goes so far as to suppose that such states require no vocation. A grace of vocation is needed to embrace a humble life in the cloister: that is admitted. But to rise to the highest ranks, to sit in judgment, to take charge of public affairs, to exercise offices in which one holds the interests of a whole city, a whole province, a whole Kingdom in one's hands; to occupy positions which would require, were it possible, the holiness of Angels: for all this, a man's ambition and greed suffice. He himself must be the author of his destiny, and need only rely on his own testimony, or rather on his presumption. In vain does the Son of God say in our Gospel that these places belong only to those for whom his Father has intended them: But belongs to those for whom it has been prepared by my Father. This designation by the heavenly Father is a mystery unknown to the ambitious man. In vain does Saint Chrysostom point out to him that these offices necessarily bind the conscience, and consequently must belong, if I may put it so, to the domain of grace: this domain of grace, which inconveniences him and would limit his plans, seems imaginary to him. In vain does Saint Bernard tell him that the higher and more distinguished these honours, the more they require a vocation to sanctify them: his habit of proceeding in them only by the calculations of carnal prudence makes him deaf to everything. Even in the dignities of the Church, what regard is paid today to divine vocation? To commit children to them while they are still incapable of being called; to make them enter before they can understand them; and when that understanding finally comes, to force them to remain there at the risk of their damnation: is this to act in the belief that these ecclesiastical dignities belong to a spiritual order, and that God alone may dispose of them?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p010", "label": "French original", "text": "Ce n'est rien encore : car si le m\u00e9rite & la vertu suppl\u00e9oient en quelque mani\u00e8re au d\u00e9faut de la vocation & de la grace, quoiqu'il y e\u00fbt toujours, selon saint Gr\u00e9goire Pape, de l'ind\u00e9cence \u00e0 s'attirer par ces voies-l\u00e0 m\u00eames les honneurs du si\u00e9cle, encore pourroit-on dire qu'ils ne seroient pas absolument prophan\u00e9s. Mais quand, \u00e0 l'exclusion du m\u00e9rite, on voit, comme il n'arrive que trop, remuer tous les ressorts de l'intrigue, de la cabale, de l'intercession, de la faveur : quand le cr\u00e9dit & l'amiti\u00e9 s'en m\u00ealent, & qu'ils y ont la meilleure part : quand on y emploie la ruse & la fraude, qu'on y joint l'importunit\u00e9 ; & qu'\u00e0 l'exemple de la mere des deux disciples, on joue toute sorte de personnages, de suppliant, de n\u00e9gociant, d'offrant, d'adorateur & de client, Adorans & petens : quand on ne se cache pas m\u00eame d'user de tels moyens, mais qu'on s'en d\u00e9clare, qu'on s'explique ouvertement de ses pr\u00e9tentions, qu'on se fait une politique d'en venir \u00e0 bout, & qu'apr\u00e8s n'y avoir \u00e9pargn\u00e9 ni souplesse ni bassesse, on se glorifie encore du succ\u00e8s, comme d'un trait d'habilet\u00e9 : le dirai-je ? quand on s'introduit aux honneurs par la porte de l'infamie, & que pour s'en ouvrir le chemin, on corrompt celui-ci par promesses, celle-l\u00e0 par pr\u00e9sens, cet autre par menaces : enfin, quand pour y r\u00e9ussir plus s\u00fbrement, on s'appuye du vice m\u00eame & de l'iniquit\u00e9 dont on recherche la protection : quand tout cela, dis-je, \u00e0 force d'\u00eatre commun, passe m\u00eame pour innocent, pour l\u00e9gitime, pour honn\u00eate ; que peut-on conclurre, sinon que toutes les id\u00e9es de l'honneur, j'entends celles que Dieu nous avoit imprim\u00e9es, s'effacent tous les jours de nos esprits, puisque nous n'envisageons plus ces honneurs du monde comme des rangs marqu\u00e9s par la providence, mais comme des objets de nos passions, ou comme des dons de la fortune, expos\u00e9s aux entreprises des plus hardis."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p010", "label": "English", "text": "That is not all. For if merit and virtue in some way made up for the absence of vocation and grace, although according to Pope Saint Gregory it would still be unbecoming to draw worldly honours to oneself even by these means, one might at least say that they were not wholly profaned. But when, to the exclusion of merit, one sees, as happens all too often, every device of intrigue, faction, intercession and favour set in motion; when influence and friendship intervene and play the chief part; when cunning and fraud are employed, with importunity added; when, like the mother of the two disciples, people play every role, as suppliant, negotiator, giver, worshipper and dependent, Bowing in worship and asking; when they do not even conceal their use of such means, but proclaim it, openly state their claims, make it their policy to succeed, and after sparing neither pliancy nor baseness still glory in their success as a feat of skill; dare I say it, when they enter upon honours through the gate of infamy, and to open the way corrupt this man by promises, that woman by presents, another by threats; finally, when to succeed more surely they seek the support and protection of vice and iniquity themselves; when all this, I say, by becoming common, passes even for innocent, legitimate and honourable: what can we conclude except that all ideas of honour, I mean those God had impressed upon us, are daily being erased from our minds? For we no longer regard these worldly honours as ranks appointed by providence, but as objects of our passions, or as gifts of fortune, open to the ventures of the most daring.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p011", "label": "French original", "text": "Ecoutez-moi toujours, Chr\u00e9tiens, & ne perdez rien d'une morale si \u00e9tendue. On poursuit les honneurs m\u00eame les plus saints, comme d\u00fbs \u00e0 sa naissance, autre pr\u00e9varication ; & sans nul fondement que celui-l\u00e0, on se croit bien \u00e9tabli, & m\u00eame en droit de pr\u00e9tendre \u00e0 tout. C'est assez d'avoir de la qualit\u00e9, pour aspirer \u00e0 ce qu'il y a de plus \u00e9minent dans le sacerdoce. C'est assez d'\u00eatre n\u00e9 d'un pere opulent, pour se pousser aux plus grandes charges. C'est assez, selon le langage ordinaire, qu'un tel soit fils d'un tel, pour que le fils ait l'assurance de vouloir \u00eatre tout ce qu'a \u00e9t\u00e9 le pere. Avec cela, quelle que soit son indignit\u00e9 & son incapacit\u00e9 personnelle, il n'y aura rien qu'il n'entreprenne : il jugera, il condamnera, il gouvernera, il d\u00e9cidera du sort & de la vie des hommes, il sera, comme dit l'Evangile, sur le chandelier, lorsqu'il devroit \u00eatre cach\u00e9 sous le boisseau. Moyse, remarque Philon le Juif, se voyant sur le point de mourir, n'osa jamais nommer un de ses proches, pour lui succ\u00e9der dans l'honorable commission qu'il avoit re\u00e7ue de conduire le peuple : pourquoi ? parce qu'il ne crut pas, ajoute le m\u00eame auteur, qu'un choix de cette cons\u00e9quence lui appart\u00eent, ni qu'il lui f\u00fbt permis d'appeller les siens \u00e0 un minist\u00e8re, o\u00f9 lui-m\u00eame n'\u00e9toit parvenu, que par une vocation expresse de Dieu : Aut quia non putavit rem tantam ad suum pertinere judicium, aut quia ipse non potuerat nisi Deo vocante principatum suscipere. Ainsi raisonna ce saint L\u00e9gislateur. Mais l'ambitieux bien plus \u00e9clair\u00e9, ou bien moins scrupuleux que Moyse, se destine sans h\u00e9siter, pour successeur \u00e0 qui il lui pla\u00eet, & fait valoir aussi-bien que les enfans de Z\u00e9b\u00e9d\u00e9e, la proximit\u00e9 du sang, pour venir \u00e0 bout de tous les desseins que lui sugg\u00e8re son ambition. Il n'est pas jusqu'aux dignit\u00e9s les plus sacr\u00e9es, dont certains esprits du monde, esprits int\u00e9ress\u00e9s & avares, ne continuent \u00e0 dire aujourd'hui, mais avec bien plus de scandale, ce que disoient d\u00e9j\u00e0 du tems de David, les premiers du peuple d'Isra\u00ebl : allons, poss\u00e9dons le sanctuaire de Dieu comme notre h\u00e9ritage ; Omnes principes eorum, qui dixerunt : H\u00e6reditate possideamus sanctuarium Dei : c'est un b\u00e9n\u00e9fice qui depuis tant d'ann\u00e9es est dans notre maison, & qu'il y faut conserver. Mais moi je r\u00e9ponds avec le m\u00eame Proph\u00e8te : Deus meus, pone illos ut rotam, & sicut stipulam ante faciem venti. Faites-les, mon Dieu, tourner comme une roue, & dissipez-les, comme le vent dissipe la paille ; c'est-\u00e0-dire, humiliez-les, d\u00e9truisez-les, an\u00e9antissez-les ; & puisque dans ce qui concerne m\u00eame votre culte, ils ont si peu d'\u00e9gard \u00e0 vous, n'ayez que des mal\u00e9dictions pour eux. Et en effet rien de plus fatal, ni de plus sujet \u00e0 des suites malheureuses, que ces possessions h\u00e9r\u00e9ditaires du sanctuaire de Dieu."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p011", "label": "English", "text": "Continue to listen, Christians, and miss nothing of so far-reaching a moral teaching. People pursue even the holiest honours as owed to their birth: another breach of duty. With no other foundation than this, they think their position secure, and even believe themselves entitled to aspire to everything. Noble birth is enough to aspire to the highest positions in the priesthood. Being born to a wealthy father is enough to push oneself into the greatest offices. It is enough, as people commonly say, that so-and-so is the son of so-and-so, for the son to have the assurance to wish to be everything his father was. With this, whatever his personal unworthiness and incapacity, there is nothing he will not undertake: he will judge, condemn, govern and decide men's fate and lives; he will be, as the Gospel says, on the lampstand, when he should be hidden under the bushel. Moses, observes Philo the Jew, seeing himself about to die, never dared appoint one of his relatives to succeed him in the honourable commission he had received to lead the people. Why? Because, the same author adds, he did not believe a choice of such consequence belonged to him, or that he was permitted to call his own relations to a ministry he himself had attained only through an express call from God: Either because he did not think so great a matter belonged to his own judgment, or because he himself could not have undertaken the leadership unless God called him. Thus reasoned that holy Lawgiver. But the ambitious man, far more enlightened or far less scrupulous than Moses, unhesitatingly designates himself as successor to whomever he pleases, and, like the sons of Zebedee, makes use of kinship by blood to accomplish all the designs his ambition suggests. Even of the most sacred dignities certain worldly minds, self-interested and avaricious minds, continue to say today, but with far greater scandal, what the leaders of Israel's people were already saying in David's time: Come, let us possess God's sanctuary as our inheritance; All their princes, who said: Let us possess God's sanctuary by inheritance. It is a benefice that has been in our house for so many years, and we must keep it there. But I reply with the same Prophet: My God, make them like a wheel, and like stubble before the wind. My God, make them turn like a wheel, and scatter them as the wind scatters straw; that is, humble them, destroy them, bring them to nothing; and since they have so little regard for you even in what concerns your worship, have only curses for them. Indeed, nothing is more fatal or more liable to unhappy consequences than these hereditary possessions of God's sanctuary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p012", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais j'ai rendu, dites-vous, des services consid\u00e9rables, & cette place qui vient de vacquer & que je poursuis, est une r\u00e9compense qui me regarde naturellement. H\u00e9 bien, reprend S. Bernard, que concluez-vous de ces services tant vant\u00e9s par vous-m\u00eame ? Pour avoir rendu des services, qui n'ont commun\u00e9ment ni rapport, ni proportion avec la place que vous ambitionnez, en \u00eates-vous plus capable de la remplir ? cette place est-elle faite pour reconno\u00eetre des services, tels que ceux dont vous voulez vous pr\u00e9valoir ? Est-il juste, par exemple, que le sacerdoce, & ce qui lui est annex\u00e9, soit la r\u00e9compense d'un service temporel & mondain ? y auroit-il simonie plus visible & plus condamnable que celle-l\u00e0 ? Faut-il parce que vous avez servi, qu'un pouvoir de mal faire & de vous perdre, vous soit mis en main ? Ayez servi avec tout le z\u00e9le, avec toute la fid\u00e9lit\u00e9 qu'on pouvoit attendre de vous, cette fid\u00e9lit\u00e9 doit-elle \u00eatre r\u00e9compens\u00e9e dans votre personne, souffrez que je m'exprime ainsi, par la prostitution de l'autorit\u00e9 ? N'y a-t-il point pour ces pr\u00e9tendus services que vous mettez \u00e0 un si haut prix, d'autre justice \u00e0 vous rendre, que de vous faire monter \u00e0 un degr\u00e9, o\u00f9 Dieu ne vous veut pas ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p012", "label": "English", "text": "But I have rendered considerable services, you say, and this position, which has just become vacant and which I am pursuing, is a reward that naturally belongs to me. Well then, Saint Bernard replies, what do you conclude from these services you praise so highly yourself? Does rendering services which ordinarily have neither relation nor proportion to the position you covet make you more capable of filling it? Was this position created to acknowledge services such as those on which you wish to rely? Is it just, for example, that the priesthood and what is attached to it should reward a temporal and worldly service? Could any simony be more obvious or more reprehensible than that? Because you have served, must you be given the power to do wrong and ruin yourself? Suppose you have served with all the zeal and fidelity that could be expected of you: must that fidelity be rewarded in your person, allow me the expression, by the prostitution of authority? Is there no other way of doing you justice for these supposed services which you value so highly than by raising you to a rank where God does not want you?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p013", "label": "French original", "text": "Cependant, mes chers Auditeurs, tel est l'aveuglement de notre cupidit\u00e9 : contre toutes les vues de Dieu, des honneurs o\u00f9 l'on doit \u00eatre appell\u00e9 par la vocation du ciel, on se fait par une indigne prophanation, des \u00e9tablissemens pour la terre. Combien de peres, & m\u00eame de peres Chr\u00e9tiens, ou plut\u00f4t oubliant qu'ils sont Chr\u00e9tiens, tiennent le langage de cette mere de notre Evangile : Dic ut sedeant hi duo filii mei : placez mes deux enfans aupr\u00e8s de vous, & qu'ils ayent, l'un \u00e0 votre droite, l'autre \u00e0 votre gauche, les plus hauts minist\u00e8res de votre Royaume. S'il y en a quelques-uns assez retenus, pour ne s'en pas d\u00e9clarer si grossi\u00e9rement, o\u00f9 sont ceux qui dans le c\u0153ur ne se le disent pas \u00e0 eux-m\u00eames ? Car c'est l\u00e0 un des articles sur quoi je soutiens que la morale de Jesus-Christ, dont nous nous glorifions tant quelquefois, ne nous a point encore r\u00e9form\u00e9s. Tant de d\u00e9votion, tant de r\u00e9gularit\u00e9 qu'on le voudra sur tout autre point ; on y consent, on s'en pique. Mais on veut voir sa famille honorablement \u00e9tablie, je dis honorablement selon les maximes du monde. On veut voir ses enfans pourv\u00fbs, & pourv\u00fbs avantageusement selon les id\u00e9es du monde ; c'est-\u00e0-dire, les uns dans l'Eglise avec tout le faste du monde, les autres dans le monde avec tout le luxe du Paganisme ; les uns riches des d\u00e9pouilles des peuples, les autres du patrimoine de l'autel ; les uns sur le pinacle du temple, o\u00f9 souvent la t\u00eate leur tourne ; les autres dans les magistratures, o\u00f9 le poids de leurs obligations les accable : & parce que la corruption des m\u0153urs suit presque infailliblement de l\u00e0, les uns & les autres d\u00e9r\u00e9gl\u00e9s & scandaleux dans leur \u00e9tat ; Dic ut sedeant hi duo filii mei. Mal\u00e9diction, qui par un juste, mais terrible jugement de Dieu, semble \u00eatre de nos jours attach\u00e9e \u00e0 toutes les familles des grands. Vous diriez m\u00eame que cet abus ait d\u00e9sormais pass\u00e9 en loi, & que Dieu avec toute la sup\u00e9riorit\u00e9 de sa sagesse & de sa grace soit oblig\u00e9 de s'y assujettir. Il suffit que ce jeune homme soit le cadet de sa maison, pour ne pas douter qu'il ne soit d\u00e8s-l\u00e0 appell\u00e9 aux fonctions redoutables de pasteur des ames. Si les choses changeoient de face, sa vocation changeroit de m\u00eame. Tandis qu'il aura un a\u00een\u00e9, elle subsistera ; & cela, dit-on, parce que pour l'int\u00e9r\u00eat de la famille il faut que l'un des deux s'avance par-l\u00e0. Disons mieux & plus simplement ; & cela, parce que la fin qu'on se propose & que se proposent m\u00eame bien des peres d\u00e9vots, est de faire des familles puissantes, & non de faire des familles Chr\u00e9tiennes."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p013", "label": "English", "text": "Yet, my dear hearers, such is the blindness of our greed: contrary to all God's designs, by an unworthy profanation we turn honours to which heaven should call us into means of establishing ourselves on earth. How many fathers, even Christian fathers, or rather fathers forgetting that they are Christians, speak like the mother in our Gospel: Say that these two sons of mine may sit. Place my two children beside you, and let them hold the highest ministries of your Kingdom, one at your right hand and the other at your left. If some are restrained enough not to declare themselves so crudely, where are those who do not say it inwardly to themselves? For this is one of the points on which I maintain that the morality of Jesus Christ, in which we sometimes glory so much, has not yet reformed us. Let there be as much devotion and regularity as you wish on every other point: people consent to it and pride themselves on it. But they want to see their family honourably established, I mean honourably according to the world's maxims. They want their children provided for, and advantageously provided for according to worldly ideas: some in the Church with all the world's pomp, others in the world with all the luxury of Paganism; some enriched by the spoils of the people, others by the patrimony of the altar; some on the pinnacle of the temple, where their heads often grow dizzy, others in public office, where the weight of their obligations overwhelms them; and since moral corruption follows almost inevitably, both groups disorderly and scandalous in their state. Say that these two sons of mine may sit. A curse which, by a just but terrible judgment of God, seems in our day attached to all the families of the great. One would even say that this abuse has now become law, and that God, with all the superiority of his wisdom and grace, is obliged to submit to it. It is enough for this young man to be a younger son of his house for no one to doubt that he is thereby called to the awesome duties of a pastor of souls. If circumstances changed, his vocation would change with them. As long as he has an elder brother, it will remain; and this, people say, because the family's interests require one of the two to advance by that route. Let us say it better and more simply: because the aim people set themselves, even many devout fathers, is to make powerful families, rather than Christian families.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p014", "label": "French original", "text": "Je ne parle point d'un autre d\u00e9sordre, qui se trouve joint \u00e0 celui-ci, & qui faisoit autrefois g\u00e9mir Salvien, ce saint Pr\u00eatre de Marseille ; s\u00e7avoir, que dans ce d\u00e9partement de conditions, fait par des parens aveugles & pr\u00e9venus de l'esprit du monde, si de plusieurs enfans qui composent la m\u00eame famille, il y en a un plus m\u00e9prisable, c'est toujours celui \u00e0 qui les honneurs de l'Eglise sont r\u00e9serv\u00e9s. S'il est disgraci\u00e9, mal fait, ou s'il n'a pas l'inclination du pere & de la mere, d\u00e8s-l\u00e0 il en faut faire un b\u00e9n\u00e9ficier. O impi\u00e9t\u00e9, s'\u00e9crioit ce grand homme ! comme si de n'avoir pas les qualit\u00e9s n\u00e9cessaires pour tout le reste, c'\u00e9toit une vocation pour la maison de Dieu, & que les autels dussent \u00eatre pourv\u00fbs des rebuts du monde : At ver\u00f2 nunc nulli Deo magis voventur, qu\u00e0m quos parentum pietas minus respicit ; & qui indigni censentur h\u00e6reditate, digni judicantur consecratione. Pouvoit-il s'\u00e9noncer en des termes plus forts, & plus propres pour nous ? mais maintenant, dit-il, on ne donne point d'enfans plus volontiers \u00e0 Dieu, que ceux qui ont moins de part \u00e0 la bienveillance paternelle ; & quand on les juge indignes de soutenir l'honneur de leur naissance, on les estime capables d'\u00eatre les ministres de Jesus-Christ & les dispensateurs de ses myst\u00e8res."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p014", "label": "English", "text": "I shall not speak of another disorder connected with this one, which once made Salvian, that holy priest of Marseille, lament: namely that, in this distribution of states of life made by parents blinded and possessed by the spirit of the world, if among several children of the same family there is one held in greater contempt, it is always for him that the honours of the Church are reserved. If he is ill-favoured, deformed, or lacks his father's and mother's affection, that is reason enough to make him a benefice-holder. O impiety, that great man exclaimed! As though lacking the qualities needed for everything else were a vocation to God's house, and the altars should be supplied with the world's rejects: But now none are more readily vowed to God than those whom parental affection regards less; and those judged unworthy of an inheritance are judged worthy of consecration. Could he have expressed himself in stronger terms, or ones more applicable to us? But now, he says, no children are more readily given to God than those who have less share in paternal goodwill; and when they are judged unworthy of upholding the honour of their birth, they are deemed capable of being ministers of Jesus Christ and dispensers of his mysteries.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p015", "label": "French original", "text": "Faut-il s'\u00e9tonner apr\u00e8s cela, Chr\u00e9tiens, si Dieu, juste vengeur de sa providence & de ses droits, s'\u00e9l\u00e8ve contre nous ? De quel \u0153il peut-il voir une telle prophanation ? Seroit-il ce qu'il est, c'est-\u00e0-dire, seroit-il un Dieu sage, un Dieu saint, un Dieu parfait, s'il souffroit tranquillement de pareils abus ? Mais sur-tout, faut-il s'\u00e9tonner si toutes les conditions du monde sont si avilies, si elles se trouvent remplies de tant d'indignes sujets, si l'on voit tant d'Eccl\u00e9siastiques scandaleux, tant de juges corrompus, tant de grands sans conscience & m\u00eame sans religion ? Ne seroit-ce pas une esp\u00e9ce de miracle, si cela n'\u00e9toit pas ainsi ? Comment voulez-vous que des gens qui n'ont ni grace, ni vocation pour un \u00e9tat, y soient fid\u00e8les \u00e0 leurs devoirs & qu'ils ne s'y perdent pas ? que la m\u00eame cupidit\u00e9, la m\u00eame ambition qui les y a fait entrer, ne les porte pas \u00e0 mille autres d\u00e9sordres ? Ah ! Seigneur, je pr\u00eache une morale toute raisonnable, toute solide, toute Chr\u00e9tienne : mais o\u00f9 est-ce que je la pr\u00eache ? au milieu de la Cour, & devant des Auditeurs appliqu\u00e9s \u00e0 m'\u00e9couter, mais peu dispos\u00e9s \u00e0 me croire. Ce sont des mondains ; & qui, parmi ces mondains, comprendra ce langage, ou le voudra comprendre ? Domine, quis credidit auditui nostro ? Mais au moins, Seigneur, si le monde n'est pas touch\u00e9 de ces maximes, s'il ne les re\u00e7oit pas, elles lui auront \u00e9t\u00e9 annonc\u00e9es, il en aura \u00e9t\u00e9 instruit, il ne se pr\u00e9vaudra pas contre votre loi de son ignorance ; & les ministres, par leur silence, ne laisseront pas l'ambition prescrire contre votre Evangile. Car ce que je dis, je le redirai toujours, & toujours je rendrai contre le monde ce t\u00e9moignage \u00e0 la v\u00e9rit\u00e9, que les honneurs du si\u00e9cle doivent \u00eatre de votre part autant de vocations ; & que ce sont encore par rapport au prochain de vrais assujettissemens & des engagemens \u00e0 le servir, comme nous l'allons voir dans la seconde partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p015", "label": "English", "text": "After this, Christians, should we be surprised if God, the just vindicator of his providence and his rights, rises against us? How can he look upon such profanation? Would he be what he is, that is, a wise God, a holy God, a perfect God, if he calmly tolerated such abuses? Above all, should we be surprised that every worldly condition is so degraded, that so many unworthy persons fill them, that we see so many scandalous churchmen, so many corrupt judges, so many great men without conscience or even religion? Would it not be a kind of miracle if things were otherwise? How can you expect people who have neither grace nor vocation for a state to be faithful to their duties in it and avoid ruining themselves? How can you expect the same greed and ambition that brought them into it not to lead them into a thousand other disorders? Ah, Lord, I preach a morality entirely reasonable, solid and Christian; but where am I preaching it? In the midst of the Court, before hearers intent on listening to me but little disposed to believe me. They are worldly people; and who among these worldly people will understand this language, or wish to understand it? Lord, who has believed our report? At least, Lord, if the world is not moved by these maxims, if it does not accept them, they will have been proclaimed to it, it will have been instructed in them, and it will not plead ignorance against your law. Nor will your ministers, by their silence, allow ambition to establish a right by long possession against your Gospel. For what I say I shall always say again, and shall always bear this witness to truth against the world: worldly honours must be so many calls from you; and in relation to our neighbour they are also true subjections and commitments to serve him, as we shall see in the second part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-h004", "label": "French original", "text": "II. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-h004", "label": "English", "text": "PART II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p016", "label": "French original", "text": "Il n'y a que Dieu, Chr\u00e9tiens, qui soit grand absolument & par lui-m\u00eame. Tout ce qui est grand hors de Dieu & parmi les hommes, ne l'est qu'avec d\u00e9pendance & que par rapport au prochain, je veux dire, pour le bien & pour l'utilit\u00e9 du prochain : & il n'est rien dans le monde de plus odieux ni de plus injuste, qu'une fortune qui devient fi\u00e9re \u00e0 mesure qu'elle s'\u00e9l\u00e8ve & qui se pr\u00e9vaut de ce qu'elle est, puisque ce qu'elle est, bien loin de lui inspirer un esprit de hauteur & d'orgueil, doit \u00eatre pour elle-m\u00eame un fonds de modestie, de condescendance, de charit\u00e9 & d'humilit\u00e9. En effet, dit excellemment S. Ambroise, dominer pour dominer, c'est le privil\u00e9ge de l'\u00eatre de Dieu. Mais le propre de la cr\u00e9ature, est de dominer pour servir : & autant de fois qu'il arrive \u00e0 l'homme de s\u00e9parer ces deux choses, en s'attribuant ce qu'il n'a pas, il d\u00e9truit m\u00eame ce qu'il a : pourquoi ? parce que la domination de l'homme, prise dans les desseins de Dieu, n'\u00e9tant qu'un v\u00e9ritable minist\u00e8re, du moment qu'il en \u00f4te l'esprit de z\u00e9le & de charit\u00e9 pour le prochain, il en \u00f4te la partie la plus essentielle, & par cons\u00e9quent il l'an\u00e9antit."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p016", "label": "English", "text": "God alone, Christians, is great absolutely and of himself. Whatever is great outside God and among men is so only in dependence and in relation to one's neighbour, I mean for the good and benefit of one's neighbour. Nothing in the world is more hateful or unjust than a fortune that grows prouder as it rises and takes advantage of its position, since that very position, far from inspiring haughtiness and pride, should be a source of modesty, consideration, charity and humility. Indeed, as Saint Ambrose admirably says, to rule for the sake of ruling is the privilege of God's being. But the creature's proper role is to rule in order to serve; and whenever a man separates these two things, by claiming what he does not possess he destroys even what he does possess. Why? Because human dominion, understood according to God's designs, is truly a ministry; the moment one removes from it the spirit of zeal and charity for one's neighbour, one removes its most essential part and consequently brings it to nothing.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p017", "label": "French original", "text": "De s\u00e7avoir si ce point de morale a \u00e9t\u00e9 connu dans le Paganisme, ou si c'est une obligation nouvelle que l'Evangile nous ait impos\u00e9e, c'est ce que je n'entreprens point d'examiner. Cependant il semble que ce soit une diff\u00e9rence que l'Evangile de ce jour mette entre les Payens & nous. Car les grands parmi les Payens, dit le Fils de Dieu, traitent les petits avec empire ; au lieu que parmi vous, les petits doivent \u00eatre trait\u00e9s des grands avec amour, & m\u00eame, selon les r\u00e9gles de la foi, avec un sentiment de respect : Scitis quia principes gentium dominantur eorum. Ainsi parloit ce divin Ma\u00eetre : mais S. J\u00e9r\u00f4me remarque fort bien, que le Sauveur du monde en parlant ainsi, supposoit l'usage des nations infidelles comme un d\u00e9sordre, & non pas comme une l\u00e9gitime possession ; & qu'en nous apprenant \u00e0 b\u00e2tir sur un fondement tout contraire, c'est-\u00e0-dire, \u00e0 nous faire un engagement de charit\u00e9, de ce qui nous \u00e9l\u00e8ve au-dessus des autres, & particuli\u00e9rement de ce qui nous met en pouvoir de leur commander, il ne nous a point donn\u00e9 d'autre loi, que celle m\u00eame qui nous \u00e9toit d\u00e9j\u00e0 prescrite \u00e0 tous par la raison, mais que les t\u00e9n\u00e8bres du p\u00e9ch\u00e9 avoient obscurcie, & qui avoit besoin des lumi\u00e8res de sa sainte doctrine pour \u00eatre mise dans un plein jour."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p017", "label": "English", "text": "Whether this moral principle was known in Paganism or is a new obligation the Gospel has imposed upon us, I do not undertake to examine. Yet it seems to be a distinction that today's Gospel draws between the Pagans and ourselves. For the great among the Pagans, says the Son of God, treat the lowly imperiously; whereas among you the lowly must be treated by the great with love and even, according to the rules of faith, with a feeling of respect: You know that the princes of the nations lord it over them. Thus spoke this divine Master. But Saint Jerome very aptly observes that in speaking so the Saviour of the world assumed the practice of the unbelieving nations to be a disorder, rather than a legitimate right; and that in teaching us to build on an entirely contrary foundation, that is, to make what raises us above others, and particularly what gives us power to command them, an obligation of charity, he gave us no other law than the one reason had already prescribed to us all. The darkness of sin had obscured it, and it needed the light of his holy teaching to be brought into full daylight.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p018", "label": "French original", "text": "Non, mes chers Auditeurs, il n'est point n\u00e9cessaire de recourir \u00e0 l'Evangile pour \u00eatre convaincu de cette v\u00e9rit\u00e9. Le Prince des Philosophes n'avoit aucun principe de Christianisme, & il la comprenoit n\u00e9anmoins, quand il disoit que les Rois dans ce haut degr\u00e9 d'\u00e9l\u00e9vation qui nous les fait regarder comme les divinit\u00e9s de la terre, ne sont apr\u00e8s tout que des hommes faits pour les autres hommes, & que ce n'est pas pour eux-m\u00eames qu'ils sont Rois, mais pour les peuples. Or si cela est vrai de la Royaut\u00e9, nul de vous ne m'accusera de porter \u00e0 son \u00e9gard trop loin la chose, si j'avance qu'on ne peut rien \u00eatre dans le monde, ni s'\u00e9lever, quoique par des voies droites & l\u00e9gitimes, aux honneurs du monde, que dans la v\u00fbe de s'employer, de s'int\u00e9resser, de se consacrer & m\u00eame de se d\u00e9vouer au bien de ceux que la providence fait d\u00e9pendre de nous : qu'un homme, par exemple, rev\u00eatu d'une dignit\u00e9, n'est qu'un sujet destin\u00e9 de Dieu & choisi pour le service d'un certain nombre de personnes, \u00e0 qui il doit ses soins ; qu'un particulier qui prend une charge, d\u00e8s-l\u00e0 n'est plus \u00e0 soi, mais au public ; qu'un sup\u00e9rieur, qu'un ma\u00eetre n'a l'autorit\u00e9 en main, que parce qu'il doit \u00eatre utile \u00e0 toute une maison, & que sans autorit\u00e9 il ne le peut \u00eatre. Pr\u00e6es, disoit S. Bernard, \u00e9crivant \u00e0 un grand du monde, & lui mettant devant les yeux l'id\u00e9e qu'il devoit avoir de sa condition, Pr\u00e6es, non ut de subditis crescas, sed ut ipsi de te. Vous \u00eates en place de commander, & il est juste qu'on vous ob\u00e9isse ; mais souvenez-vous que cette ob\u00e9issance ne vous est due qu'\u00e0 titre on\u00e9reux, & que vous \u00eates pr\u00e9varicateur, si vous ne la faites servir toute enti\u00e8re au profit de ceux qui vous la doivent."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p018", "label": "English", "text": "No, my dear hearers, one need not turn to the Gospel to be convinced of this truth. The Prince of Philosophers had no principle of Christianity, yet he understood it when he said that kings, in that lofty elevation which makes us regard them as the gods of the earth, are after all only men made for other men, and are kings for their peoples rather than for themselves. Now if this is true of royalty, none of you will accuse me of carrying the matter too far in his own case if I assert that no one may hold any position in the world, or rise to worldly honours, even by upright and legitimate means, except with the intention of employing himself, taking an interest, consecrating himself and even sacrificing himself for the good of those whom providence makes dependent on him. A man invested with a dignity, for example, is simply a person appointed by God and chosen to serve a certain number of people, to whom he owes his care; a private individual who takes an office thereby belongs to the public rather than to himself; a superior or master holds authority only because he must benefit a whole household, and cannot do so without authority. You preside, Saint Bernard said, writing to a worldly dignitary and setting before his eyes the idea he ought to have of his condition: You preside so that your subjects may grow through you, rather than you through them. You are in a position to command, and it is just that people obey you; but remember that this obedience is owed to you only with a corresponding burden of duty, and you betray your trust unless you direct it entirely to the benefit of those who owe it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p019", "label": "French original", "text": "De-l\u00e0 je conclus, que s'il se trouve un Chr\u00e9tien, (or combien ne s'en trouve-t-il pas !) qui par le rang que lui donne, ou sa fortune, ou sa naissance, ayant sous soi des vassaux & des sujets, ne les consid\u00e9re que pour soi-m\u00eame, que pour ses int\u00e9r\u00eats propres, que pour s'en glorifier & s'en faire honneur ; & qui du reste les n\u00e9glige, sans se mettre en peine de pourvoir \u00e0 leurs avantages, & de leur procurer les biens solides qu'ils ont droit d'attendre de lui, d\u00e8s-lors sans autre crime, il m\u00e9rite d'\u00eatre r\u00e9prouv\u00e9 de Dieu : pourquoi ? parce qu'il renverse cet ordre de Dieu, qui n'a fait les grands que pour les petits, & les puissans, les forts que pour les foibles. Ainsi l'a d\u00e9cid\u00e9 saint Augustin, raisonnant sur les principes g\u00e9n\u00e9raux de la providence."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p019", "label": "English", "text": "From this I conclude that if there is a Christian (and how many there are!) who, through the rank given him by his fortune or birth, has vassals and subjects beneath him, yet considers them only for himself, his own interests, and the glory and honour he can derive from them, while otherwise neglecting them, taking no trouble to provide for their advantage or procure the lasting benefits they are entitled to expect from him, then, without any other crime, he deserves to be rejected by God. Why? Because he overturns God's order, which made the great for the lowly, and the powerful and strong for the weak. Thus Saint Augustine decided, reasoning from the general principles of providence.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p020", "label": "French original", "text": "Je s\u00e7ais que le Christianisme a bien encore ench\u00e9ri sur cela, & que l'exemple du Fils de l'homme, qui n'est pas venu pour \u00eatre servi, mais pour servir les autres, a rendu ce devoir beaucoup plus indispensable. Car ne seroit-il pas honteux, dit saint Chrysostome, que dans une religion o\u00f9 nous reconnoissons Jesus-Christ pour ma\u00eetre & pour ma\u00eetre souverain, il y e\u00fbt des hommes qui voulussent exercer un empire plus absolu que lui ? Pens\u00e9e touchante pour un Chr\u00e9tien. N'est-il pas juste que le Verbe de Dieu ayant pris la qualit\u00e9 de serviteur, que l'ayant annoblie, l'ayant comme divinis\u00e9e dans sa personne, elle soit honor\u00e9e parmi nous : & n'est-ce pas, ajoute S. Chrysostome, \u00e0 quoi Dieu sagement a pourv\u00fb, lorsqu'il lui a m\u00eame assujetti la qualit\u00e9 de ma\u00eetre, & que pour rendre hommage aux humiliations de son Fils, il nous ordonne \u00e0 quelque degr\u00e9 de sup\u00e9riorit\u00e9 que nous ayons \u00e9t\u00e9 \u00e9lev\u00e9s, de nous y regarder, & sur-tout de nous y comporter comme des serviteurs & des ministres : en sorte qu'on puisse nous appliquer cette parole de l'Ap\u00f4tre ; Omnes sunt quasi administratorii spiritus ? Tout cela est vrai, Chr\u00e9tiens : mais ma douleur est, que la foi nous donnant sur ce point des v\u00fbes si hautes & si parfaites, \u00e0 peine dans la pratique l'on s'en tienne aux simples v\u00fbes de la raison. Si je vous disois, que cet assujettissement & ce devoir va, selon l'esprit de l'Evangile, jusqu'\u00e0 r\u00e9pondre du prochain & de son salut, c'est-\u00e0-dire, que tout homme rev\u00eatu de l'autorit\u00e9, suivant la mesure de cette autorit\u00e9-m\u00eame, est garant de la conduite du prochain, est charg\u00e9 devant Dieu des d\u00e9sordres & des crimes du prochain, est responsable de la perte & de la damnation du prochain, & cela toujours sur le mod\u00e8le de Jesus-Christ qui n'a \u00e9t\u00e9 le ma\u00eetre des ma\u00eetres, que pour travailler \u00e0 la r\u00e9demption & \u00e0 la sanctification de plusieurs, Non ministrari, sed ministrare, & animam suam dare in redemptionem pro multis ; en vous parlant de la sorte je vous ferois trembler. Mais quoi qu'il en soit de cette importante obligation, qui seule demanderoit un discours entier, voil\u00e0, Grands du monde, reprend saint Bernard, voil\u00e0 le plan que vous devez suivre, & la forme de vie que vous trace votre religion. Forma evangelica h\u00e6c est, dominatio vobis interdicitur, indicitur ministratio. En qualit\u00e9 de Chr\u00e9tiens, plus vous \u00eates grands, plus vous devez \u00eatre charitables & bienfaisans : toute domination vous est interdite, & votre fonction est de servir. Voil\u00e0 l'abr\u00e9g\u00e9 de cette morale \u00e9vang\u00e9lique qui doit sanctifier votre \u00e9tat."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p020", "label": "English", "text": "I know that Christianity has gone much further still, and that the example of the Son of man, who came to serve others rather than to be served, has made this duty far more indispensable. For would it not be shameful, says Saint Chrysostom, if in a religion where we acknowledge Jesus Christ as master and sovereign master, there were men wishing to exercise a more absolute dominion than he? A moving thought for a Christian. Since the Word of God took the condition of a servant, ennobled it and, as it were, divinised it in his person, is it not just that it should be honoured among us? And is this not, Saint Chrysostom adds, what God wisely provided for when he even subjected the condition of master to it, and, to render homage to his Son's humiliations, commands us, whatever degree of superiority we have attained, to regard ourselves and above all conduct ourselves there as servants and ministers, so that these words of the Apostle may be applied to us: Are they all as ministering spirits? All this is true, Christians; but my grief is that, although faith gives us such lofty and perfect insights on this point, in practice people scarcely abide even by the simple insights of reason. If I told you that this subjection and duty extend, according to the spirit of the Gospel, to answering for one's neighbour and his salvation; that is, that every man invested with authority, in proportion to that very authority, is answerable for his neighbour's conduct, bears before God the burden of his neighbour's disorders and crimes, and is responsible for his neighbour's ruin and damnation, always following the model of Jesus Christ, who was master of masters only to labour for the redemption and sanctification of many, To serve rather than be served, and to give his life as a ransom for many: if I spoke to you in this way, I would make you tremble. But whatever may be said of this important obligation, which alone would require a whole discourse, here, you great ones of the world, Saint Bernard continues, is the plan you must follow and the form of life your religion sets before you. This is the Gospel's pattern: domination is forbidden you; service is enjoined upon you. As Christians, the greater you are, the more charitable and beneficent you must be: all domination is forbidden you, and your function is to serve. This is the summary of that Gospel morality which must sanctify your state.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p021", "label": "French original", "text": "De-l\u00e0 vient que S. Augustin, sans se laisser \u00e9blouir de sa pr\u00e9lature, trouvoit dans sa dignit\u00e9 m\u00eame sa confusion, & dans sa grandeur de quoi s'humilier & s'instruire : Quod enim Christiani sumus, propter nos est ; quod pr\u00e6positi, propter vos. Car c'est pour vous, mes Freres, disoit-il aux fid\u00e8les qu'il conduisoit, c'est pour vous que Dieu m'a fait Ev\u00eaque dans son Eglise, comme c'est pour moi-m\u00eame qu'il m'a fait Chr\u00e9tien ; & si je pensois \u00e0 me glorifier de mon sacerdoce, ce seroit assez pour attirer sur moi ses vengeances. Or par-l\u00e0, concluoit admirablement ce saint Docteur, Dieu a trouv\u00e9 le secret de temp\u00e9rer l'in\u00e9galit\u00e9 des conditions de la vie, d'\u00f4ter aux petits tout sujet de se plaindre dans leur abbaissement, & aux grands tout droit de s'enfler dans leur \u00e9l\u00e9vation. Je suis quelque chose dans le monde ; mais l'avantage que j'ai d'\u00eatre quelque chose dans le monde, n'est qu'un engagement \u00e0 n'y \u00eatre rien pour moi-m\u00eame, afin d'y \u00eatre tout pour les autres. Car s'il y a des services qu'ils me doivent, il y en a aussi que je leur dois. Si d'une mani\u00e8re ils me sont sujets, je leur suis sujet de l'autre, & je ne leur rends pas justice, si je ne m'employe pas encore plus pour eux qu'ils ne doivent s'employer pour moi."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p021", "label": "English", "text": "Hence Saint Augustine, without allowing himself to be dazzled by his prelacy, found in his very dignity a cause for shame, and in his greatness something to humble and instruct him: For that we are Christians is for ourselves; that we are set over you is for you. For it is for you, my brothers, he said to the faithful he guided, it is for you that God has made me a bishop in his Church, just as it is for myself that he made me a Christian; and were I to think of glorying in my priesthood, that alone would suffice to draw his vengeance upon me. In this way, that holy Doctor admirably concluded, God has found the means of moderating the inequality of conditions in life, removing from the lowly every reason to complain in their lowliness, and from the great every right to swell with pride in their elevation. I am something in the world; but the advantage of being something in the world is only an obligation to be nothing there for myself, so that I may be everything for others. For if there are services they owe me, there are also services I owe them. If in one way they are subject to me, in another I am subject to them, and I do not do them justice unless I labour for them even more than they must labour for me.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p022", "label": "French original", "text": "L'entendez-vous, mes chers Auditeurs, & puis-je esp\u00e9rer que dans la corruption du si\u00e9cle vous go\u00fbtiez une maxime si Chr\u00e9tienne & si sainte ? Il s'agit de s\u00e7avoir si vous la faites entrer dans la conduite de votre vie, & si vos sentimens sont conformes l\u00e0-dessus & aux exemples & aux instructions de votre Dieu. Car enfin Jesus-Christ l'a dit, que ce seroit la marque qui nous distingueroit des Payens ; & c'\u00e9toit \u00e0 vous-m\u00eames & de vous-m\u00eames qu'il parloit en d\u00e9fendant \u00e0 ses Ap\u00f4tres d'\u00eatre de ces hommes vains & superbes qui cherchent \u00e0 dominer, Non ita erit inter vos. Voyons donc si parmi ceux qui se poussent aux honneurs du monde, on ne trouve point de ces ames Payennes, qui abusent de leur condition, & qui joignant l'orgueil \u00e0 l'autorit\u00e9, la rendent \u00e9galement imp\u00e9rieuse & insupportable. Voyons si dans le Christianisme, malgr\u00e9 l'exemple d'un Dieu humili\u00e9 & an\u00e9anti, on ne trouve pas encore tous les jours de ces ma\u00eetres hautains & durs, qui ne s\u00e7avent que se faire ob\u00e9ir, que se faire servir, que se faire craindre, sans s\u00e7avoir ni compatir, ni soulager, ni condescendre, ni se faire aimer ; qui usant de toute la force & souvent m\u00eame de toute l'aigreur du commandement, n'y m\u00ealent jamais, selon le pr\u00e9cepte de l'Ap\u00f4tre, l'onction & la douceur de la charit\u00e9. L'esprit de domination que je combats, ne manquera pas de pr\u00e9textes pour se justifier : mais la parole que je pr\u00eache, aura encore plus d'efficace pour le confondre. Appliquez-vous."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p022", "label": "English", "text": "Do you understand, my dear hearers, and may I hope that amid the corruption of the age you will relish so Christian and holy a maxim? The question is whether you bring it into the conduct of your life, and whether your sentiments on this point conform both to your God's examples and to his instructions. For Jesus Christ said that this would be the mark distinguishing us from the Pagans; and he was speaking to you yourselves and about you yourselves when he forbade his Apostles to be among those vain and proud men who seek to dominate: It shall not be so among you. Let us therefore see whether among those who push themselves into worldly honours there are any of those Pagan souls who abuse their condition and, joining pride to authority, make it both imperious and unbearable. Let us see whether within Christianity, despite the example of a God humbled and emptied of himself, we still find every day those haughty and harsh masters who know only how to make themselves obeyed, served and feared, without knowing how to show compassion, bring relief, be considerate or make themselves loved; who exercise all the force and often even all the bitterness of command, without ever mingling with it the balm and gentleness of charity according to the Apostle's precept. The spirit of domination I combat will have no lack of pretexts to justify itself; but the word I preach will have still greater power to confound it. Attend closely.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p023", "label": "French original", "text": "On se flatte parce qu'on est \u00e9lev\u00e9, d'un pr\u00e9tendu z\u00e9le de faire sa charge, de soutenir ses droits, de garder son rang : on va plus loin, & quelquefois m\u00eame on se fait de ses fiert\u00e9s & de ses hauteurs un devoir ; tant l'amour-propre est ing\u00e9nieux \u00e0 nous d\u00e9guiser les vices les plus grossiers, sous l'apparence des plus pures vertus. Mais, r\u00e9pond saint Bernard, si c'est un z\u00e9le de faire sa charge & un vrai z\u00e9le, pourquoi ce z\u00e9le ne s'allume-t-il qu'en certaines rencontres, & lorsqu'il est question d'abaisser les autres & de prendre l'ascendant sur eux ? pourquoi dans tout le reste devient-il si paresseux & si lent ? pourquoi le voit-on languir & s'\u00e9teindre du moment que l'ambition est satisfaite ? Car quelque subtils que nous soyons \u00e0 nous tromper nous-m\u00eames, voici, Chr\u00e9tiens, le sujet de notre honte, & il faut que nous en convenions. Ne s'agit-il que d'une fonction p\u00e9nible, laborieuse, de pure charit\u00e9 & de nul \u00e9clat, ce z\u00e9le de faire sa charge & de maintenir son rang nous inqui\u00e9te peu : mais qu'il y ait une pr\u00e9s\u00e9ance \u00e0 disputer, une soumission \u00e0 exiger, une loi \u00e0 imposer, c'est l\u00e0 qu'il se r\u00e9veille & qu'il se r\u00e9veille tout entier. Il \u00e9toit assoupi, & sur toute autre chose il le seroit encore : mais il n'y a que ce point d'honneur qui le pique & qui le ranime. Or est-ce l\u00e0 seulement ce qui doit piquer & animer un z\u00e9le Chr\u00e9tien ? De plus, poursuit saint Bernard, est-ce faire sa charge que d'en rendre le joug f\u00e2cheux, pesant, & presque insoutenable \u00e0 ceux qui le doivent porter ? est-ce faire sa charge, que d'irriter les esprits, au lieu de les gagner, que de r\u00e9volter les c\u0153urs, au lieu de les soumettre, que d'accabler les uns de chagrin, de jetter les autres dans le d\u00e9sespoir, d'insulter \u00e0 ceux-ci, de rebuter & de d\u00e9soler ceux-l\u00e0, d'exciter mille murmures, & de renverser toute la subordination en voulant l'\u00e9tablir & la rendre trop exacte. Car voil\u00e0 \u00e0 quoi aboutit ce z\u00e9le, dont l'ambition se pare ; \u00e0 ne rien faire pour vouloir trop faire, & \u00e0 d\u00e9truire au lieu d'\u00e9difier. On s'ent\u00eate de certains droits qu'on veut soutenir ; & parce qu'on ne consulte point l'humilit\u00e9 Chr\u00e9tienne, il faut les soutenir, ces droits, soit r\u00e9els, soit pr\u00e9tendus, \u00e0 quelque prix que ce puisse \u00eatre. Il faut, quelque plaie qu'en re\u00e7oive la charit\u00e9, & quoi qu'il en doive co\u00fbter au prochain, les faire valoir dans toute leur \u00e9tendue, les poursuivre dans toute leur rigueur, n'en rien c\u00e9der, n'en rien rabbattre, n'entendre \u00e0 nul accommodement, \u00e0 nulle composition ; pourquoi ? parce qu'on est poss\u00e9d\u00e9 de cet esprit d'empire & de domination, qui souvent m\u00eame, par le plus d\u00e9plorable aveuglement, d'une pure jalousie d'autorit\u00e9 se fait une vertu & une justice."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p023", "label": "English", "text": "Because one holds an elevated position, one flatters oneself with a supposed zeal to fulfil one's office, uphold one's rights and preserve one's rank. One goes further, sometimes even making pride and haughtiness a duty, so ingenious is self-love at disguising the grossest vices as the purest virtues. But, Saint Bernard replies, if this is zeal to fulfil one's office, and true zeal, why does it flare up only on certain occasions, when it is a question of lowering others and gaining ascendancy over them? Why does it become so lazy and slow in everything else? Why do we see it languish and die away the moment ambition is satisfied? For however subtle we are at deceiving ourselves, here, Christians, is our shame, and we must admit it. If the task is merely painful, laborious, purely charitable and without display, this zeal to fulfil our office and maintain our rank concerns us little. But let there be precedence to dispute, submission to demand, a law to impose: then it awakens, and awakens in full. It was asleep, and on any other matter would still be so; only this point of honour stings and revives it. Is that alone what should sting and animate Christian zeal? Moreover, Saint Bernard continues, is it fulfilling one's office to make its yoke vexatious, heavy and almost unbearable to those who must carry it? Is it fulfilling one's office to irritate minds instead of winning them, to provoke hearts to revolt instead of bringing them to submission, to overwhelm some with grief, cast others into despair, insult these, rebuff and desolate those, provoke a thousand complaints, and overturn all subordination by seeking to establish it and make it too exacting? For that is where the zeal with which ambition adorns itself leads: to achieving nothing through wishing to do too much, and to destroying instead of building up. One becomes obsessed with certain rights one wishes to uphold; and because one does not consult Christian humility, those rights, real or supposed, must be upheld at any cost. Whatever wound charity may suffer, whatever it may cost one's neighbour, they must be asserted to their full extent, pursued with all their rigour, with nothing yielded, nothing relinquished, no accommodation or compromise entertained. Why? Because one is possessed by that spirit of sway and domination which, often through the most deplorable blindness, makes a virtue and a form of justice out of sheer jealousy for authority.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p024", "label": "French original", "text": "Jalousie d'autorit\u00e9, ah ! tentation funeste, \u00e0 quelles extr\u00e9mit\u00e9s & \u00e0 quels exc\u00e8s ne portes-tu pas tous les jours les hommes ? combien de scandales as-tu caus\u00e9s ? combien de ressentimens & de vengeances as-tu autoris\u00e9s ? de quels maux n'as-tu pas \u00e9t\u00e9 le principe, & quels biens n'as-tu pas mille fois arr\u00eat\u00e9s ? Si l'humilit\u00e9, telle que notre Evangile nous la propose, servoit \u00e0 cette passion de correctif & de rem\u00e8de, Dieu en tireroit sa gloire ; & ces droits qui nous touchent si sensiblement, n'en seroient que mieux maintenus : mais parce qu'on ne s\u00e7ait rien m\u00e9nager, & que pour venir \u00e0 bout de ses entreprises, on suit le g\u00e9nie altier & ind\u00e9pendant de l'ambition, il faut que pour un droit souvent tr\u00e8s-frivole, souvent douteux, souvent chim\u00e9rique, la paix soit troubl\u00e9e, l'union & la concorde ruin\u00e9e, l'innocence opprim\u00e9e, la patience outr\u00e9e ; que le d\u00e9pit & la haine s'emparent des c\u0153urs, & qu'un phant\u00f4me mette par-tout le d\u00e9sordre & la confusion."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p024", "label": "English", "text": "Jealousy for authority! Ah, fatal temptation, to what extremes and excesses do you not drive men every day? How many scandals have you caused? How many resentments and acts of revenge have you sanctioned? Of what evils have you not been the source, and what good have you not thwarted a thousand times? If humility, as our Gospel presents it, served to correct and cure this passion, God would derive his glory from it, and those rights which touch us so keenly would be all the better upheld. But because people know no restraint, and follow ambition's haughty and independent spirit to accomplish their undertakings, a right often utterly trivial, often doubtful, often imaginary must cause peace to be disturbed, union and concord ruined, innocence oppressed and patience driven beyond endurance. Resentment and hatred must take hold of hearts, and a phantom must spread disorder and confusion everywhere.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p025", "label": "French original", "text": "Ce qu'il y a de plus \u00e9trange, c'est que les plus imp\u00e9rieux ; ce sont commun\u00e9ment ceux \u00e0 qui cet empire qu'ils affectent, doit moins convenir. Des gens qui de leur fonds ne sont rien, des gens sortis de l'obscurit\u00e9 & du n\u00e9ant, mais devenus grands par machines & par ressorts, ce sont l\u00e0 ceux qui parlent avec plus d'ostentation, qui agissent avec plus d'autorit\u00e9, & qui pour relever leur fausse grandeur, se font une gloire d'abaisser m\u00eame & de dominer les vrais grands. Ce n'est pas assez : des gens d\u00e9vots par \u00e9tat & par profession, des gens plus oblig\u00e9s par-l\u00e0 m\u00eame \u00e0 d\u00e9pouiller, du moins \u00e0 m\u00e9priser toute sup\u00e9riorit\u00e9 humaine, ce sont quelquefois les plus jaloux de leurs pr\u00e9tentions, les plus obstin\u00e9s dans leurs sentimens, les plus absolus dans leurs ordres. Qui voudroit leur r\u00e9sister, qui voudroit les contredire & contester avec eux, \u00e0 quels retours ne s'exposeroit-il pas, & quels scandales n'en a-t-on pas v\u00fbs !"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p025", "label": "English", "text": "What is strangest is that the most imperious are usually those least suited to the dominion they affect. People who are nothing in themselves, people risen from obscurity and nothingness but made great through devices and manoeuvres, are the very ones who speak with greater ostentation, act with greater authority, and, to exalt their false greatness, glory even in lowering and dominating the truly great. That is not all: people devout by their state and profession, people thereby all the more obliged to cast off, or at least despise, all human superiority, are sometimes the most jealous of their claims, most obstinate in their opinions and most absolute in their commands. Anyone wishing to resist them, contradict them or dispute with them: to what retaliation would he not expose himself, and what scandals have we not seen from it!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p026", "label": "French original", "text": "Tel est, mes chers Auditeurs, le cours du monde ; & sur quoi nous ne pouvons assez g\u00e9mir, tel est le cours du monde le plus Chr\u00e9tien. Ce n'est pas seulement dans les Cours des Rois, ni dans le monde prophane, qu'on se laisse enfler de la sorte, & qu'on aime \u00e0 exercer son pouvoir & \u00e0 le faire sentir. Rien de plus commun, \u00f4 opprobre de notre si\u00e9cle, disons mieux, \u00f4 opprobre de tous les si\u00e9cles ! non, rien de plus commun dans l'Eglise m\u00eame, dans cette Eglise fond\u00e9e n\u00e9anmoins sur l'humilit\u00e9 de Jesus-Christ. Contre l'avis que nous donne l'Ap\u00f4tre de ne chercher point \u00e0 dominer dans le Clerg\u00e9, Neque ut dominantes in cler\u012ds ; on envisage les plus saintes dignit\u00e9s par les respects, par les hommages qu'elles attirent, & non point par le travail qui en doit \u00eatre ins\u00e9parable. On oublie qu'on est pere, qu'on est pasteur, & l'on se souvient seulement qu'on est ma\u00eetre. On r\u00e9duit les ames dans une esp\u00e9ce de servitude. Saint Paul veut que l'on traite les serviteurs, comme ses freres, & l'on traite ses freres comme des esclaves. On a une secrette complaisance \u00e0 tenir bas ceux-ci ; on se vante comme d'un succ\u00e8s d'avoir humili\u00e9 ceux-l\u00e0 ; on s'en glorifie, on en fait troph\u00e9e. On veut que tout plie, que tout se soumette d\u00e8s qu'on a prononc\u00e9 une parole, & souvent on refuse soi-m\u00eame de se soumettre \u00e0 des puissances sup\u00e9rieures dont on rel\u00e9ve, & de plier sous une juste domination. Qu'on e\u00fbt une semblable autorit\u00e9, on s\u00e7auroit bien la faire valoir : mais qu'on y soit sujet, on ne veut plus la reconno\u00eetre. Est-ce l\u00e0 l'esprit de Dieu ? sont-ce l\u00e0 les enseignemens que Jesus-Christ nous a donn\u00e9s ? est-ce ainsi que les Ap\u00f4tres ont converti le monde ? Ah ! Chr\u00e9tiens, tenons-nous toujours & en tout \u00e0 la belle maxime du Sauveur des hommes : Qui major est inter vos, fiat sicut minister. Plus votre rang vous distingue des autres, plus devez-vous vous en approcher ; plus devez-vous, pour user de cette expression, vous humaniser ; plus devez-vous avoir de douceur, de mod\u00e9ration, de charit\u00e9. Si j'insiste sur cette morale, & si je le fais avec la sainte libert\u00e9 de la chaire, vous ne pouvez la condamner. Quand je parle aux peuples, mon minist\u00e8re m'oblige \u00e0 leur apprendre le respect & l'ob\u00e9issance qu'ils vous doivent : mais puisque je vous parle dans cette Cour, puisque je parle \u00e0 des Grands, je dois leur dire ce qu'ils doivent aux peuples. Honneurs du si\u00e9cle, vocations de Dieu ; honneur du si\u00e9cle, assujettissemens \u00e0 servir le prochain ; enfin honneurs du si\u00e9cle, engagemens \u00e0 travailler & \u00e0 souffrir, c'est la troisi\u00e9me partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p026", "label": "English", "text": "Such, my dear hearers, is the world's course; and what we cannot lament enough is that such is the course of the most Christian world. It is not only at kings' Courts or in the profane world that people let themselves swell with pride in this way, and delight in exercising their power and making it felt. Nothing is more common, O disgrace of our age, or rather, O disgrace of every age! No, nothing is more common even within the Church, that Church nevertheless founded on Jesus Christ's humility. Against the Apostle's counsel not to seek domination among the clergy, Nor as lording it over the clergy, people view the holiest dignities in terms of the respect and homage they attract, rather than the labour that must be inseparable from them. They forget they are fathers and pastors, and remember only that they are masters. They reduce souls to a kind of servitude. Saint Paul wishes servants to be treated as brothers, and people treat their brothers as slaves. They take secret pleasure in keeping these low, boast as though of a success that they have humiliated those, glory in it and make a trophy of it. They want everything to bend and submit the moment they have spoken, and often refuse themselves to submit to the higher powers on which they depend, or to bow beneath a just dominion. If they held such authority, they would know how to assert it; but if they are subject to it, they no longer wish to acknowledge it. Is this God's spirit? Are these the teachings Jesus Christ gave us? Is this how the Apostles converted the world? Ah, Christians, let us always and in everything adhere to the beautiful maxim of the Saviour of mankind: Let him who is greater among you be as a servant. The more your rank distinguishes you from others, the closer you must draw to them; the more, to use the expression, you must make yourselves approachable; the more gentleness, moderation and charity you must have. If I insist on this moral teaching, and do so with the holy freedom of the pulpit, you cannot condemn it. When I speak to the people, my ministry obliges me to teach them the respect and obedience they owe you; but since I speak to you at this Court, since I address the great, I must tell them what they owe the people. Worldly honours, calls from God; worldly honour, subjections to serving one's neighbour; finally, worldly honours, commitments to labour and suffering: this is the third part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-h005", "label": "French original", "text": "III. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-h005", "label": "English", "text": "PART III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p027", "label": "French original", "text": "Le monde n'en conviendra jamais ; mais de quelque mani\u00e8re qu'en juge le monde, c'est une v\u00e9rit\u00e9 \u00e9ternelle qui subsistera toujours, que les \u00e9tablissemens & les rangs d'honneur, tout propres qu'ils paroissent \u00e0 flatter notre cupidit\u00e9, ne sont n\u00e9anmoins \u00e0 les bien prendre, que des engagemens \u00e0 souffrir. Aussi quand ces deux freres, enfans de Z\u00e9b\u00e9d\u00e9e, demand\u00e8rent au Fils de Dieu les premi\u00e8res places de son Royaume, & qu'ils crurent y devoir trouver une b\u00e9atitude & une f\u00e9licit\u00e9 anticip\u00e9e, le Sauveur s\u00e7ut bien les d\u00e9tromper, par cette r\u00e9ponse qu'il leur fit : Potestis bibere calicem quem ego bibiturus sum ? Pouvez-vous boire le calice de mes souffrances ? leur donnant \u00e0 entendre que l'un \u00e9toit ins\u00e9parable de l'autre, & que cette pr\u00e9s\u00e9ance dont ils se formoient une fausse id\u00e9e, ne seroit pour eux, s'ils l'obtenoient, qu'une mesure plus abondante de travaux, de tribulations, de croix : Calicem quidem meum bibetis. Apr\u00e8s cela, mes Freres, dit saint Augustin, devons-nous chercher dans le monde, & y pouvons-nous esp\u00e9rer des honneurs exempts de cette condition ; c'est-\u00e0-dire, des honneurs purs, & qui ne soient pas m\u00eal\u00e9s ou m\u00eame remplis d'afflictions & de peines ? S'il en est de tels, c'est pour le ciel qu'ils sont r\u00e9serv\u00e9s : ceux de la terre sont d'une autre esp\u00e9ce, & Dieu ne nous les propose que comme des calices d'amertume. Si nous les envisageons autrement, nous ne les connoissons pas ; & si nous en usons autrement, nous les corrompons."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p027", "label": "English", "text": "The world will never admit it; but whatever the world's judgment, this is an eternal truth that will always stand: established positions and ranks of honour, however suited they may seem to flatter our greed, are nevertheless, properly understood, only commitments to suffering. Thus when these two brothers, the sons of Zebedee, asked the Son of God for the highest places in his Kingdom, believing they would find there a foretaste of blessedness and happiness, the Saviour knew well how to undeceive them by his reply: Can you drink the cup that I am to drink? Can you drink the cup of my sufferings? He made them understand that the one was inseparable from the other, and that this precedence, of which they formed a false idea, would, if they obtained it, mean only a greater measure of labours, tribulations and crosses: You shall indeed drink my cup. After that, my brothers, says Saint Augustine, ought we to seek in the world, or can we hope to find there, honours exempt from this condition: pure honours, neither mixed with nor even filled with afflictions and pains? If such honours exist, they are reserved for heaven. Those of earth are of another kind, and God offers them to us only as cups of bitterness. If we regard them otherwise, we do not understand them; and if we use them otherwise, we corrupt them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p028", "label": "French original", "text": "Pour vous faire entendre ma pens\u00e9e, je ne vous parlerai point de ces accidens impr\u00e9v\u00fbs, de ces \u00e9v\u00e9nemens tragiques, dont nous sommes si souvent spectateurs. Je ne vous dirai rien de ces revers & de ces tristes r\u00e9volutions, que nous appellons d\u00e9cadences & malheurs du si\u00e9cle ; o\u00f9 ces m\u00eames honneurs qui furent pour nous d'abord le sujet d'une douce joie, tout \u00e0 coup \u00e9vanouis & perdus, nous tiennent lieu, par les regrets qu'ils nous laissent, de tourment & de supplice. Ne nous en prenons point \u00e0 la malignit\u00e9 de la fortune, qui jalouse, pour ainsi dire, de nous avoir \u00e9lev\u00e9s, & comme ennemie de son propre ouvrage, nous en attire bient\u00f4t elle-m\u00eame la haine & l'envie : en sorte que ces graces nous deviennent dans la suite une source in\u00e9puisable d'ennuis, de d\u00e9go\u00fbts, de troubles, de chagrins. Vous en \u00eates bien mieux instruits que nous ; & si j'en cherchois des t\u00e9moins, je n'en voudrois point d'autres que vous-m\u00eames. Arr\u00eatons-nous donc \u00e0 ce qu'il y a dans cette mati\u00e8re de plus essentiel. Supposons l'homme Chr\u00e9tien dans une prosp\u00e9rit\u00e9 constante & toujours \u00e9gale ; & voyons, si pour \u00eatre plus \u00e9lev\u00e9, il a droit de se promettre une vie plus douce & plus commode. Je soutiens moi, que par cette raison-l\u00e0 m\u00eame, il n'y a rien au contraire dans la vie de si amer \u00e0 quoi il ne doive s'attendre, ni rien de si dur qu'il ne doive \u00eatre pr\u00eat \u00e0 supporter. Pourquoi ? en voici les preuves : \u00e9coutez-les. C'est que l'\u00e9l\u00e9vation o\u00f9 il se trouve, l'oblige \u00e0 se faire de continuelles violences ; c'est qu'elle le r\u00e9duit \u00e0 la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 d'endurer souvent beaucoup des autres ; c'est qu'elle l'engage dans une vie pleine de soins affligeans, dont il ne lui est pas permis de se d\u00e9charger ; c'est qu'elle exige de lui qu'en mille occasions, il soit dispos\u00e9 \u00e0 s'immoler, \u00e0 se sacrifier comme une victime, tant\u00f4t de la v\u00e9rit\u00e9, & tant\u00f4t de la justice & de l'innocence. Or se faire de telles violences, souffrir de la sorte, agir de la sorte, se sacrifier, s'immoler de la sorte, est-ce go\u00fbter le repos, & y a-t-il l\u00e0 de quoi contenter les sens ? Reprenons."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p028", "label": "English", "text": "To make my thought clear, I shall not speak to you of those unforeseen accidents and tragic events of which we are so often spectators. I shall say nothing of those reversals and sad upheavals we call worldly downfalls and misfortunes, in which the very honours that at first gave us sweet joy, suddenly vanished and lost, become torment and punishment through the regrets they leave us. Let us not blame fortune's malice, which, jealous, so to speak, of having raised us, and as though hostile to its own work, soon itself brings hatred and envy upon us because of it, so that its favours later become an inexhaustible source of vexation, disgust, trouble and grief. You know this far better than we do; and if I sought witnesses, I would want none but yourselves. Let us therefore dwell on what is most essential in this matter. Suppose a Christian enjoys constant and unvarying prosperity, and let us see whether being more elevated entitles him to expect a pleasanter and more comfortable life. I maintain, on the contrary, that for that very reason there is nothing in life so bitter that he should not expect it, or so hard that he should not be ready to bear it. Why? Here are the proofs: listen to them. His elevation obliges him continually to do violence to himself; it makes it necessary for him often to endure much from others; it commits him to a life full of distressing cares of which he is not permitted to relieve himself; it requires him on a thousand occasions to be ready to immolate himself, to sacrifice himself as a victim, sometimes for truth, sometimes for justice and innocence. Now, to do such violence to oneself, to suffer and act in this way, to sacrifice and immolate oneself in this way: is this to enjoy rest, and is there anything here to gratify the senses? Let us take these points again.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p029", "label": "French original", "text": "Se faire violence \u00e0 soi-m\u00eame, premier engagement des honneurs du si\u00e9cle. Car comment un homme constitu\u00e9 en dignit\u00e9, s'il veut vivre selon les d\u00e9sirs de son c\u0153ur, & s'il n'a nul usage de la mortification \u00e9vang\u00e9lique, peut-il satisfaire aux obligations de son \u00e9tat ? Comment un Chr\u00e9tien, s'il a pour principe de s'\u00e9pargner en tout, & de ne se contraindre en rien, peut-il accomplir selon Dieu le minist\u00e8re d'une charge ; \u00eatre assidu aux fonctions ennuyeuses, se rendre ponctuel aux tems incommodes, se fixer aux lieux d\u00e9sagr\u00e9ables, o\u00f9 sa conscience l'attache aussi-bien que son rang ? Si c'est un homme de plaisir, comment soutiendra-t-il mille fatigues qu'attire tout emploi, sur-tout un emploi important ? Il faut donc qu'il apprenne \u00e0 se g\u00eaner ; & pour le bien apprendre, pour bien remplir la place qu'il occupe, il faut qu'il renonce \u00e0 la mollesse & aux d\u00e9lices, qu'il prenne sur son repos, qu'il ne m\u00e9nage pas m\u00eame sa sant\u00e9 ; & qu'\u00e0 l'exemple de saint Paul, ne tenant pas sa vie plus pr\u00e9cieuse que lui-m\u00eame, c'est-\u00e0-dire, que son devoir & son salut, il trouve presque sans y penser, dans l'usage des honneurs du si\u00e9cle, la pratique de cette abn\u00e9gation Chr\u00e9tienne, qui consiste \u00e0 porter sa croix, & \u00e0 mortifier son esprit & sa chair."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p029", "label": "English", "text": "To do violence to oneself: the first commitment imposed by worldly honours. For how can a man invested with dignity fulfil the obligations of his state if he wishes to live according to his heart's desires and has no practice of Gospel mortification? How can a Christian, if his principle is to spare himself in everything and constrain himself in nothing, fulfil the ministry of an office according to God: diligently perform tedious duties, be punctual at inconvenient times, remain in disagreeable places to which his conscience as well as his rank binds him? If he is a man of pleasure, how will he sustain the thousand fatigues brought by any office, especially an important one? He must therefore learn to constrain himself; and to learn it well, to fill his position well, he must renounce softness and delights, give up some of his rest, not even spare his health; and, following Saint Paul's example, not holding his life more precious than himself, that is, than his duty and salvation, he must find almost without thinking of it, in the exercise of worldly honours, the practice of that Christian self-denial which consists in carrying one's cross and mortifying one's spirit and flesh.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p030", "label": "French original", "text": "Souffrir souvent & beaucoup des autres, second engagement des honneurs du monde. En effet, plus vous \u00eates \u00e9lev\u00e9, & plus vous \u00eates environn\u00e9 & assi\u00e9g\u00e9 d'hommes qui ont leurs d\u00e9fauts, qui ont leurs humeurs, qui ont leurs caprices, qui ont leurs int\u00e9r\u00eats, qui ont leurs passions & leurs vices ; plus vous \u00eates expos\u00e9 aux traits de l'envie, \u00e0 la censure, \u00e0 la m\u00e9disance. Combien en co\u00fbta-t-il \u00e0 Moyse pour \u00eatre le conducteur du peuple de Dieu ? de quelle patience dut-il s'armer pour fournir toute la carri\u00e8re, & pour porter jusques au bout une qualit\u00e9 si on\u00e9reuse ? L'e\u00fbt-il dignement soutenue, si par une constance in\u00e9branlable & par une mod\u00e9ration, que ces esprits indociles mettoient tous les jours \u00e0 de nouvelles \u00e9preuves, il ne se f\u00fbt comme endurci \u00e0 la contradiction & aux injures ? Et pouvez-vous, mon cher Auditeur, dans votre condition, quelle qu'elle soit, \u00eatre fid\u00e8le \u00e0 vos devoirs, si vous ne s\u00e7avez vous vaincre, si vous ne s\u00e7avez vous taire dans les rencontres, si vous ne s\u00e7avez \u00e9touffer vos ressentimens, r\u00e9primer les saillies de votre c\u0153ur, recevoir mille d\u00e9boires & les d\u00e9vorer ? Car fussiez-vous encore plus grand, fussiez-vous au fa\u00eete de l'honneur, on vous enviera, & par cons\u00e9quent on vous contr\u00f4lera, on vous traversera, on vous offensera. Si vous vous emportez, vous souffrirez de votre emportement m\u00eame. Si vous vous surmontez, vous souffrirez de l'emportement des autres. Quoi qu'il en soit, vous n'\u00e9viterez jamais que ce qui vous \u00e9l\u00e9ve, ne soit au m\u00eame-tems ce qui vous p\u00e9se, & que les croix ne vous viennent de-l\u00e0 m\u00eame d'o\u00f9 vous tirez votre grandeur."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p030", "label": "English", "text": "To suffer often and much from others: the second commitment imposed by worldly honours. Indeed, the higher you rise, the more you are surrounded and besieged by men with their faults, tempers, whims, interests, passions and vices; the more you are exposed to the arrows of envy, criticism and slander. How much did it cost Moses to be the leader of God's people? With what patience did he have to arm himself to run the whole course and bear so burdensome a position to the end? Could he have sustained it worthily unless, through unshakable constancy and a moderation which those unruly spirits put to fresh tests every day, he had as it were hardened himself to opposition and insults? And can you, my dear hearer, in your condition, whatever it may be, remain faithful to your duties unless you know how to conquer yourself, keep silent when occasions arise, stifle your resentments, restrain your heart's outbursts, and receive and swallow a thousand bitter disappointments? For were you greater still, were you at the summit of honour, people would envy you, and consequently criticise, thwart and offend you. If you lose your temper, you will suffer from your own anger. If you master yourself, you will suffer from the anger of others. Either way, you will never prevent what raises you from being at the same time what weighs upon you, or crosses from coming to you from the very source of your greatness.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p031", "label": "French original", "text": "Mener une vie pleine de soins, & de soins affligeans, de soins inquiets, & dont on n'est pas en pouvoir de se d\u00e9faire, troisi\u00e9me engagement des honneurs du si\u00e9cle. Je vous le demande, mes Freres & sans parler des Monarques & des Souverains, qui ne sont pas eux-m\u00eames exempts de cette loi, dites-moi o\u00f9 est aujourd'hui le Seigneur, o\u00f9 est le Ma\u00eetre, o\u00f9 est le Juge, le Pr\u00e9lat, le Magistrat, qui pour l'\u00eatre en Chr\u00e9tien, ne puisse pas & ne doive pas s'appliquer ces paroles de David, Tribulatio & angustia invenerunt me ; les inqui\u00e9tudes & les embarras me sont venus trouver ? Je ne les cherchois pas, & je t\u00e2chois m\u00eame \u00e0 les \u00e9loigner de moi. Mais cette providence adorable de mon Dieu, qui dispose toutes choses pour mon salut, leur a donn\u00e9 entr\u00e9e dans mon ame, & je me vois charg\u00e9 de soins qui m'accablent : Tribulatio & angustia invenerunt me. Sentiment, dit S. Bernard, bien capable de rabbattre ces vaines enflures, & de mod\u00e9rer ces complaisances qu'inspirent d'abord certaines distinctions & certains rangs honorables dans le monde, puisqu'on n'est gu\u00e9res sensible \u00e0 l'honneur quand on y trouve plus de peine que d'\u00e9clat : Non est quod blandiatur celsitudo, ubi sollicitudo major."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p031", "label": "English", "text": "To lead a life full of cares, distressing cares, anxious cares, which one has no power to cast off: the third commitment imposed by worldly honours. I ask you, my brothers, without speaking of monarchs and sovereigns, who are themselves subject to this law: tell me where today is the lord, master, judge, prelate or magistrate who, to hold that position as a Christian, is not both able and obliged to apply David's words to himself: Trouble and anguish have found me. Anxieties and difficulties have come to find me. I did not seek them, and even tried to keep them away. But my God's adorable providence, which orders everything for my salvation, has admitted them to my soul, and I find myself burdened with cares that overwhelm me: Trouble and anguish have found me. A sentiment, says Saint Bernard, well able to bring down that vain swelling of pride and moderate the satisfaction that certain distinctions and honourable worldly ranks first inspire; for one is scarcely sensitive to honour when one finds more pain than splendour in it: Elevation has no reason to flatter where anxiety is greater.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p032", "label": "French original", "text": "Enfin, avoir toujours son ame entre ses mains, & toujours \u00eatre en disposition de s'immoler soi-m\u00eame, pour la justice, ou pour la v\u00e9rit\u00e9, quatri\u00e9me engagement des honneurs du monde. Car pourquoi Dieu vous a-t-il donn\u00e9 ce cr\u00e9dit, pourquoi vous a-t-il plac\u00e9 sur la t\u00eate des autres, si ce n'est pour lui faire, quand sa cause le demande, un plus grand sacrifice de vous-m\u00eame ? Vous vous autorisez quelquefois de la parole de l'Ap\u00f4tre, que celui qui d\u00e9sire la plus sainte de toutes les dignit\u00e9s, d\u00e9sire une \u0153uvre lo\u00fcable & honn\u00eate. Qui Episcopatum desiderat, bonum opus desiderat : mais saint J\u00e9r\u00f4me vous ferme la bouche, en vous r\u00e9pondant que la plus sainte de toutes les dignit\u00e9s, \u00e9toit dans le tems qu'en parloit saint Paul, la plus prochaine disposition au martyre, & \u00e0 la mort. J'ajoute \u00e0 la pens\u00e9e de saint J\u00e9r\u00f4me, ce que vous n'avez peut-\u00eatre jamais compris, & ce qu'il est bon que vous compreniez une fois, qu'il n'y a point sur la terre de sup\u00e9riorit\u00e9, point de dignit\u00e9, qui ne vous engage indispensablement \u00e0 vous faire, en certaines conjonctures, le martyr du bon droit & de l'\u00e9quit\u00e9, le martyr de l'innocence, le martyr de la religion, le martyr de la gloire de Dieu ; que vous devez alors abandonner tous vos int\u00e9r\u00eats ; & qu'autrement, tout Chr\u00e9tien que vous \u00eates de profession, vous n'\u00eates en effet qu'un mondain & un r\u00e9prouv\u00e9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p032", "label": "English", "text": "Finally, always to hold one's soul in one's hands, and always to be ready to sacrifice oneself for justice or truth: the fourth commitment imposed by worldly honours. For why has God given you this influence, why has he placed you above others, unless to make him a greater sacrifice of yourself when his cause demands it? You sometimes appeal to the Apostle's words, that whoever desires the holiest of all dignities desires a praiseworthy and honourable work: Whoever desires the episcopate desires a good work. But Saint Jerome silences you by replying that the holiest of all dignities was, at the time Saint Paul spoke of it, the most immediate preparation for martyrdom and death. To Saint Jerome's thought I add what you may never have understood, and what it is good for you to understand once and for all: there is no superiority or dignity on earth that does not bind you indispensably, in certain circumstances, to become a martyr for rightful claims and equity, a martyr for innocence, a martyr for religion, a martyr for God's glory. Then you must abandon all your own interests; otherwise, however Christian your profession, you are in fact only a worldly man and one of the reprobate.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p033", "label": "French original", "text": "Cela est difficile, je le veux : mais n'est-il pas juste, dit saint Ambroise, qu'apr\u00e8s avoir re\u00e7u beaucoup de Dieu, vous soyez tenu \u00e0 beaucoup pour Dieu ? N'est-ce pas ainsi que Dieu par sa sagesse a ordonn\u00e9 les choses, attachant l'honneur aux charges & aux emplois pour en adoucir la peine, & joignant la peine aux emplois & aux charges pour en bannir la pr\u00e9somption & la corruption ? Car voil\u00e0 l'id\u00e9e qu'en ont eu tous les vrais fid\u00e8les, qui dans les hauts rangs o\u00f9 Dieu les a fait monter, ne se sont jamais regard\u00e9s, que comme des hosties vivantes pour essuyer tout, pour porter tout, pour se d\u00e9vo\u00fcer \u00e0 tout, pour seconder les desseins de la providence sur eux & pour les remplir."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p033", "label": "English", "text": "This is difficult, I grant it; but is it not just, says Saint Ambrose, that after receiving much from God you should be bound to do much for God? Is this not how God in his wisdom has ordered things, attaching honour to offices and employments to soften their hardship, and joining hardship to employments and offices to banish presumption and corruption from them? For this is the idea held by all the truly faithful, who in the high ranks to which God raised them never regarded themselves except as living sacrifices, to endure everything, bear everything and devote themselves to everything, to further providence's designs for them and fulfil them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p034", "label": "French original", "text": "Or l\u00e0-dessus qu'avez-vous \u00e0 r\u00e9pondre, hommes du si\u00e9cle ? par o\u00f9 justifiez-vous cette vie oisive & sans action, dans des places qui demandent une vigilance sans rel\u00e2che & toute votre attention ? Paisibles possesseurs & vains idol\u00e2tres d'un honneur dont l'\u00e9clat repa\u00eet votre vanit\u00e9, mais dont les obligations \u00e9tonnent votre amour-propre, venez vous contempler dans le tableau que je vous pr\u00e9sente : venez reconno\u00eetre l'\u00e9norme opposition qui se rencontre entre votre conduite & vos devoirs : venez apprendre ce que vous devez \u00eatre, & vous confondre de ce que vous n'\u00eates pas. Je s\u00e7ais que vous trouverez assez de vaines excuses, je s\u00e7ais que vous imaginerez assez de pr\u00e9textes pour vous persuader que dans l'exercice de votre minist\u00e8re on doit \u00eatre aussi content de vous, que vous l'\u00eates de vous-m\u00eames. Mais examinons de bonne foi la chose, & raisonnons. Car \u00eatre sans cesse occup\u00e9 de ses divertissemens & de son plaisir, & presque jamais de ses fonctions & de son emploi ; fuir un travail que vous devez au public, & que le public attend de vous ; avoir horreur d'une assiduit\u00e9 n\u00e9cessaire, que vous traitez de captivit\u00e9 & d'esclavage ; se d\u00e9charger sur autrui des soins qui vous regardent personnellement, & dont vous \u00eates par vous-m\u00eames responsables ; ne pouvoir se tenir l\u00e0 o\u00f9 il faut \u00eatre, & se trouver par tout o\u00f9 il faudroit n'\u00eatre pas ; rejetter toute affaire qui incommode, qui fatigue, quoique Dieu ne vous ait fait ce que vous \u00eates, que pour en \u00eatre fatigu\u00e9s & incommod\u00e9s ; n'\u00e9couter que la prudence humaine, & ne vouloir jamais se commettre en rien, jamais s'exposer \u00e0 rien, dans des occasions o\u00f9 l'on craint de se perdre, mais o\u00f9 Dieu veut que vous vous perdiez selon le monde, & que vous vous exposiez ; en un mot, ne prendre de votre condition que le doux & l'agr\u00e9able, & en laisser le p\u00e9nible & le rigoureux ; secret que le monde enseigne, & que vous avez si bien appris : ce n'est pas assez : regarder d'un \u0153il indiff\u00e9rent ce qui devroit vous donner de saintes inqui\u00e9tudes, ce qui devroit exciter tout votre z\u00e9le, des abus qu'il faudroit corriger, des violences qu'il faudroit r\u00e9primer, des injustices qu'il faudroit r\u00e9parer, des scandales qu'il faudroit faire cesser : au contraire, \u00e9clater avec impatience, avec chaleur, avec emportement sur les moindres sujets, & dans une place n\u00e9anmoins o\u00f9 l'on doit toujours se poss\u00e9der soi-m\u00eame, o\u00f9 l'on doit toujours \u00eatre ma\u00eetre de soi-m\u00eame toujours se mod\u00e9rer, se retenir, sans jamais \u00e9couter la sensibilit\u00e9 & sans jamais la faire paro\u00eetre : que dis-je ? abuser de son pouvoir pour satisfaire ses animosit\u00e9s particuli\u00e8res & ses ressentimens, pour autoriser ses vengeances, pour se rendre redoutable dans une ville, pour faire souffrir tout un pays, & ne rien souffrir soi-m\u00eame : tout cela & tout ce que je passe (car je serois infini, si je voulois \u00e9puiser cette morale & toucher mille autres articles non moins importans) tout cela encore une fois vous convient-il ? Est-ce l\u00e0 ce que demande votre \u00e9tat ? Est-ce pour cela que la providence a \u00e9tabli dans le monde cette diversit\u00e9 de conditions, qu'elle a plac\u00e9 les uns sur le buffet comme des vases d'honneur, & qu'elle a laiss\u00e9 les autres dans la poussi\u00e9re ? Dieu en vous distinguant & en vous \u00e9levant, a-t-il pr\u00e9tendu vous entretenir dans l'oisivet\u00e9, vous faire vivre dans le repos, fournir \u00e0 toutes vos commodit\u00e9s, vous abandonner \u00e0 vous-m\u00eames, & \u00e0 tous les d\u00e9sirs, \u00e0 tous les ressentimens de votre c\u0153ur ? n'a-t-il fait le monde que pour vous ? ou n'est-ce pas pour le gouvernement & le bon ordre du monde qu'il vous a choisis ? or pour maintenir cet ordre, n'y a-t-il ni r\u00e9flexions \u00e0 faire, ni mesures \u00e0 prendre, ni pr\u00e9cautions \u00e0 garder, ni hazards \u00e0 courir, ni obstacles \u00e0 vaincre, ni \u00e9tude, ni m\u00e9nagemens n\u00e9cessaires ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p034", "label": "English", "text": "Now what have you to answer to this, men of the world? How do you justify this idle and inactive life in positions that demand unceasing vigilance and all your attention? Untroubled possessors and vain idolaters of an honour whose splendour feeds your vanity but whose obligations frighten your self-love, come and contemplate yourselves in the picture I set before you. Come and recognise the enormous opposition between your conduct and your duties. Come and learn what you ought to be, and be ashamed of what you are not. I know you will find enough empty excuses; I know you will imagine enough pretexts to persuade yourselves that in the exercise of your ministry others should be as satisfied with you as you are with yourselves. But let us examine the matter honestly and reason it through. To be constantly occupied with one's diversions and pleasure, and almost never with one's functions and office; to flee work you owe the public and which the public expects of you; to abhor a necessary diligence which you call captivity and slavery; to unload on others cares that concern you personally and for which you yourselves are responsible; to be unable to remain where you must be, and to be found everywhere you ought not to be; to reject every affair that inconveniences or tires you, although God made you what you are precisely to be tired and inconvenienced by it; to listen only to human prudence, never willing to commit or expose oneself in any way on occasions when one fears ruin, but when God wishes you to risk worldly ruin and expose yourselves; in a word, to take only what is sweet and pleasant in your condition and leave what is painful and rigorous, a secret the world teaches and which you have learnt so well; and more still, to look indifferently upon what should give you holy anxieties and stir all your zeal: abuses that should be corrected, violence that should be restrained, injustices that should be repaired, scandals that should be stopped; on the contrary, to flare up impatiently, heatedly and angrily over the slightest matters, even in a position where one must always possess oneself, always be master of oneself, always moderate and restrain oneself, never yielding to touchiness or showing it; what am I saying? To abuse one's power to gratify private animosities and resentments, sanction one's revenge, make oneself feared in a city, cause a whole country to suffer and suffer nothing oneself: all this, and everything I pass over (for I would never finish if I exhausted this moral teaching and touched on a thousand other points no less important), all this, once again, is it fitting for you? Is this what your state demands? Is this why providence established this diversity of conditions in the world, placing some upon the sideboard like vessels of honour and leaving others in the dust? In distinguishing and elevating you, did God intend to maintain you in idleness, make you live at rest, provide all your comforts, abandon you to yourselves and to all your heart's desires and resentments? Did he make the world only for you? Or did he choose you for the world's government and good order? And to maintain that order, are there no reflections to make, measures to take, precautions to observe, risks to run, obstacles to overcome, no study or careful handling required?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p035", "label": "French original", "text": "Ah ! mon cher Auditeur, saint Bernard le disoit dans un sentiment d'humilit\u00e9, mais ne pouvez-vous pas le dire avec v\u00e9rit\u00e9 : je suis la chim\u00e8re de mon si\u00e9cle, Chim\u00e6ra s\u00e6culi ? Car je suis tout, & je ne suis rien, ou plut\u00f4t, je veux parvenir \u00e0 tout, & ne m'acquitter de rien. Je suis dans la magistrature, & je n'ai du magistrat que l'autorit\u00e9 & la robbe : c'est l'\u00eatre, & ne l'\u00eatre pas. Je suis dans les affaires, & je n'ai de l'homme d'affaires que l'opulence & le faste : c'est l'\u00eatre, & ne l'\u00eatre pas. Je suis dans l'Eglise, & je n'ai d'Eccl\u00e9siastique que le caract\u00e8re & l'habit : c'est l'\u00eatre, & ne l'\u00eatre pas : Chim\u00e6ra s\u00e6culi. Le beau spectacle, poursuivoit le m\u00eame Pere, au sujet de certains ministres de Jesus-Christ, le beau spectacle de les voir engag\u00e9s dans l'Eglise, pourquoi ? pour en recueillir les revenus ; pour se montrer sous la mitre & sous la pourpre ; jamais pour servir \u00e0 l'Autel, jamais pour assister \u00e0 l'office divin, jamais pour subvenir aux besoins des pauvres, jamais pour vacquer \u00e0 l'instruction des peuples, jamais pour s'employer \u00e0 l'\u00e9dification des ames que la Providence leur a confi\u00e9es. Que sont-ils ? on ne peut bien le dire, puisqu'ils ne sont, \u00e0 proprement parler, ni du monde, ni de l'Eglise, ni de la robbe, ni de l'\u00e9p\u00e9e : Chim\u00e6ra s\u00e6culi."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p035", "label": "English", "text": "Ah, my dear hearer, Saint Bernard said it in a spirit of humility, but can you not say it truthfully: I am the chimera of my age, The chimera of the age? For I am everything and I am nothing; or rather, I wish to attain everything and fulfil nothing. I am in the magistracy, and have of a magistrate only the authority and robe: that is to be one and not to be one. I am in affairs, and have of a man of affairs only wealth and pomp: that is to be one and not to be one. I am in the Church, and have of a churchman only the sacramental character and dress: that is to be one and not to be one. The chimera of the age. A fine spectacle, the same Father continued, speaking of certain ministers of Jesus Christ; a fine spectacle to see them committed to the Church, for what? To gather its revenues; to display themselves under the mitre and in purple; never to serve at the Altar, never to attend the divine office, never to meet the needs of the poor, never to devote themselves to instructing the people, never to labour for the edification of the souls Providence has entrusted to them. What are they? One cannot rightly say, since properly speaking they belong neither to the world nor to the Church, neither to the robe nor to the sword. The chimera of the age.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p036", "label": "French original", "text": "Ouvrons, mes Freres, ouvrons aujourd'hui les yeux : & pour nous apprendre, \u00f4 mon Dieu, \u00e0 bien user des honneurs du si\u00e9cle, apprenez-nous seulement \u00e0 \u00eatre raisonnables : car il ne faut qu'\u00eatre raisonnable, pour en comprendre les obligations. D\u00e9trompez-nous, Seigneur, des fausses id\u00e9es que nous avons des choses, & dissipez par les lumi\u00e8res de votre Evangile les erreurs o\u00f9 nous sommes tomb\u00e9s par la corruption du monde. Ne permettez pas qu'une lueur passag\u00e9re nous \u00e9blouisse, & que des honneurs mortels & p\u00e9rissables nous fassent perdre cette gloire immortelle o\u00f9 vous nous appellez, & o\u00f9 nous conduise, &c."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-p036", "label": "English", "text": "Let us open our eyes, my brothers, let us open them today; and to teach us, O my God, to make good use of worldly honours, teach us simply to be reasonable, for one need only be reasonable to understand their obligations. Undeceive us, Lord, about our false ideas of things, and dispel by the light of your Gospel the errors into which the world's corruption has led us. Do not allow a passing gleam to dazzle us, or mortal and perishable honours to make us lose that immortal glory to which you call us, and to which may lead us, etc.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n001", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 11."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n001", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 11.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n002", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. [unclear chapter numeral]."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n002", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew [unclear chapter numeral].", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n003", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibidem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n003", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n004", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n004", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n005", "label": "French original", "text": "[H]ebr. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n005", "label": "English", "text": "Hebrews 5. [Initial H supplied; damaged in print.]", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n006", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 20."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n006", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 20.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n007", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 20."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n007", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 20.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n008", "label": "French original", "text": "Philo."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n008", "label": "English", "text": "Philo.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n009", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal. 82."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n009", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 82.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n010", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibidem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n010", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n011", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. 20."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n011", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 20.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n012", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. 20."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n012", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 20.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n013", "label": "French original", "text": "Salvian."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n013", "label": "English", "text": "Salvian.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n014", "label": "French original", "text": "Isai. 53."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n014", "label": "English", "text": "Isaiah 53.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n015", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 20."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n015", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 20.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n016", "label": "French original", "text": "Bernard."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n016", "label": "English", "text": "Bernard.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n017", "label": "French original", "text": "Hebr. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n017", "label": "English", "text": "Hebrews 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n018", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 20."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n018", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 20.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n019", "label": "French original", "text": "Bernard."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n019", "label": "English", "text": "Bernard.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n020", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n020", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n021", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 10."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n021", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 10.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n022", "label": "French original", "text": "I. Petr. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n022", "label": "English", "text": "1 Peter 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n023", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 20."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n023", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 20.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n024", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 20."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n024", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 20.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n025", "label": "French original", "text": "Ps. 118."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n025", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 118.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n026", "label": "French original", "text": "Bernard."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n026", "label": "English", "text": "Bernard.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n027", "label": "French original", "text": "I. Tim. 3."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n027", "label": "English", "text": "1 Timothy 3.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n028", "label": "French original", "text": "Bernard."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-n028", "label": "English", "text": "Bernard.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-ed001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "A few damaged letters have been supplied in the French words for \u201clessons\u201d and \u201cecclesiastical,\u201d and in nearby words about God\u2019s authority over church offices. These readings follow the surviving print and the context. The damaged H in the Hebrews reference is shown in brackets. No line or passage has been supplied."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-ed002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The printed chapter number after Matthew is blotted and cannot be read with certainty. The sayings here draw on Matthew 20:22\u201323, 26 and Luke 22:26. These identifications have been added by the editors; the uncertain printed reference is preserved."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-ed003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The book prints Matthew 10 beside \u201cIt shall not be so among you.\u201d The saying is in Matthew 20:26. The printed reference is preserved."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-ed004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The book ends with an abbreviated prayer (\u201cetc.\u201d) and leaves the final verb without an expressed subject. The English preserves that incomplete construction; no missing words have been supplied."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-scripture-05", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Matthew 20:26 / Luke 22:26 \u2014 parallels added by editors for adapted service sayings."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-ambition-scripture-06", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Sirach 15:14 \u2014 added by editors; no printed marginal locator."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "On Ambition", "topics": ["ambition", "vocation", "humility", "charity", "suffering", "responsibility"], "url": "sermons/bourdaloue-careme-ambition.html"}, {"author": "Louis Bourdaloue", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-lent", "calendar_label": "Lent", "centuries": [17, 18], "composition": "Approximately late seventeenth century\u20131704; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone", "language": "fr", "language_label": "French", "metadata": ["Louis Bourdaloue, Sermons du P\u00e8re Bourdaloue, de la Compagnie de J\u00e9sus, pour le Caresme, tome premier, nouvelle \u00e9dition (Paris: aux d\u00e9pens de Rigaud, 1716), Sur l\u2019Aum\u00f4ne, sermon pour le I. vendredi du Caresme, pp. 145\u2013194.", "Internet Archive; John M. Kelly Library Rare Books, University of Toronto.", "Lent", "First Friday of Lent", "Matthew 25", "Proverbs 3", "1 John 3", "2 Corinthians 8", "Song of Songs 2", "Philippians 1", "2 Corinthians 6", "Proverbs 25", "Isaiah 61", "Hebrews 13", "Matthew 5", "Matthew 6", "Ecclesiasticus 29", "Galatians 6", "Psalm 36 (source numbering)", "James 2"], "occasion": "Premier vendredi du Car\u00eame", "occasion_detail": "First Friday of Lent", "occasion_label": "Lent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This sermon is headed for the first Friday of Lent in Bourdaloue's 1716 Lenten volume. Its salutation addresses a royal prince whom a marginal note identifies as the king's only brother; no precise delivery date is established here. Three parts examine the obligation of almsgiving, the surplus goods assigned to it, and the order of charity. The conclusion distinguishes giving from restitution and urges assistance during life."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-h001", "label": "French original", "text": "SERMON POUR LE I. VENDREDI DU CARESME."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON FOR THE FIRST FRIDAY OF LENT", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-h002", "label": "French original", "text": "Sur l'Aum\u00f4ne."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On Almsgiving.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-e001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "C\u00f9m ergo facis eleemosynam, noli tub\u00e2 canere ante te, sicut hypocritae faciunt in Synagogis & in vicis, ut honorificentur ab hominibus."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-e001", "label": "English", "text": "When therefore you give alms, do not sound a trumpet before you, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and in the streets, to be honoured by men.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-e002", "label": "French original", "text": "Quand donc vous faites l'aum\u00f4ne, ne faites pas sonner de la trompette devant vous, comme font les hypocrites dans les Synagogues & dans les places publiques, pour \u00eatre honor\u00e9s des hommes. En saint Matth. chap. 6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-e002", "label": "English", "text": "When therefore you give alms, do not sound a trumpet before you, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and public squares, to be honoured by men. In Saint Matthew, chapter 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-h003", "label": "French original", "text": "MONSEIGNEUR,"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-h003", "label": "English", "text": "MY LORD,", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p001", "label": "French original", "text": "Si l'Evangile condamne ces ames vaines qui corrompent les plus saintes \u0153uvres par une intention criminelle, & qui cherchent dans leurs aum\u00f4nes \u00e0 contenter leur orgueil & \u00e0 se distinguer; c'est encore avec bien plus de raison & plus de rigueur, qu'il doit condamner ces ames dures, qui laissent impitoyablement souffrir tant de pauvres, & qui les voient presque r\u00e9duits aux derni\u00e8res extr\u00e9mit\u00e9s, sans se mettre en peine de les assister dans leurs mis\u00e9res & de pourvoir \u00e0 leurs besoins. Car ce d\u00e9sordre n'est-il pas plus condamnable que l'autre? Et que serviroit, Chr\u00e9tiens, de vous apprendre quelles v\u00fbes vous devez vous proposer en faisant l'aum\u00f4ne, lorsque vous n'\u00eates pas m\u00eame instruits, ou que vous paroissez au-moins dans la pratique si peu persuad\u00e9s du devoir indispensable qui vous engage \u00e0 la faire?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p001", "label": "English", "text": "If the Gospel condemns those vain souls who corrupt the holiest works by a sinful intention, and who seek in their almsgiving to gratify their pride and distinguish themselves, with far greater reason and severity must it condemn those hard souls who pitilessly let so many poor people suffer, and who see them reduced almost to the last extremity without troubling to assist them in their misery or provide for their needs. For is this disorder not more blameworthy than the other? And what use would it be, Christians, to teach you the intentions you ought to have in giving alms, when you are not even instructed in, or at least show in practice so little conviction about, the indispensable duty that binds you to give them?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p002", "label": "French original", "text": "Quand la loi de Dieu ne nous l'ordonneroit pas, faudroit-il une autre loi que les sentimens naturels? Et voil\u00e0, Monseigneur, les heureuses dispositions que votre Altesse Royale a re\u00e7ues en naissant, & qu'elle a si bien cultiv\u00e9es. Si les Princes sont les images de Dieu, & si la mis\u00e9ricorde est un des premiers caract\u00e8res de la divinit\u00e9; je puis dire que nous voyons dans votre Altesse Royale les plus beaux traits de cet excellent mod\u00e9le. Car nous y voyons, Monseigneur, un Prince bienfaisant, dont l'inclination pr\u00e9dominante est d'obliger & de faire des graces: un Prince lib\u00e9ral & magnifique, qui prend plaisir \u00e0 dispenser ses dons, & qui met sa grandeur \u00e0 les r\u00e9pandre, non moins sur les petits que sur les grands m\u00eames: un Prince pr\u00e9venant & affable, qui par des mani\u00e9res toujours engageantes, par un accueil toujours ouvert & un visage o\u00f9 la douceur est peinte, inspire \u00e0 ceux qui l'approchent autant de confiance, que la pompe de sa Cour, l'\u00e9clat de sa naissance, la dignit\u00e9 de sa personne leur impriment de respect & de v\u00e9n\u00e9ration: un Prince charitable & compatissant, toujours pr\u00eat \u00e0 \u00e9couter les humbles supplications des afflig\u00e9s, & toujours dispos\u00e9 \u00e0 prendre en main leur cause & \u00e0 d\u00e9fendre leurs int\u00e9r\u00eats. Ce ne sont point l\u00e0, Monseigneur, de ces \u00e9loges \u00e9tudi\u00e9s que la flatterie donne aux Princes, & qui quelquefois expriment plut\u00f4t ce qu'ils doivent \u00eatre que ce qu'ils sont: je ne dis rien que n'ait dit cent fois avant moi, que ne dise encore tous les jours comme moi & aussi hautement que moi, tout ce peuple qui m'\u00e9coute & dont vous poss\u00e9dez les c\u0153urs. Juste & glorieuse possession, o\u00f9 vous a maintenu jusqu'\u00e0 pr\u00e9sent, & o\u00f9 vous maintiendra cette grandeur d'ame qui paro\u00eet en tout, cette g\u00e9n\u00e9rosit\u00e9 de sentimens, cette bont\u00e9 de naturel, tant d'autres qualit\u00e9s que nous admirons; & s'il m'est permis de le dire, Monseigneur, pour m'acquitter de mon minist\u00e8re & pour votre \u00e9dification, qui ne doivent pas seulement servir \u00e0 faire de votre Altesse Royale, un Prince selon le c\u0153ur des hommes, mais un Prince vraiment Chr\u00e9tien & selon le c\u0153ur de Dieu. J'aurai donc l'avantage, Monseigneur, en parlant de l'aum\u00f4ne & du soin des pauvres, d'entrer dans vos v\u00fbes & de seconder votre z\u00e9le. Les Peres semblent avoir \u00e9puis\u00e9 sur ce sujet leur \u00e9loquence: saint Jean Chrysostome ne faisoit presque pas un discours au peuple, qu'il ne recommand\u00e2t la charit\u00e9 & la mis\u00e9ricorde Chr\u00e9tienne, & c'est ce qui le fit appeller le Pr\u00e9dicateur de l'aum\u00f4ne. Avant que de proposer mon dessein, implorons le secours du Ciel, & adressons-nous pour l'obtenir \u00e0 la mere de mis\u00e9ricorde, en lui disant: Ave, Maria."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p002", "label": "English", "text": "Even if God's law did not command it, would any law beyond natural feeling be needed? And such, my Lord, are the fortunate dispositions which Your Royal Highness received at birth and has cultivated so well. If princes are images of God, and mercy is one of the foremost characteristics of divinity, I may say that in Your Royal Highness we see the finest features of that excellent model. For in you, my Lord, we see a beneficent prince whose prevailing inclination is to oblige and bestow favours; a liberal and magnificent prince who delights in dispensing his gifts and makes it his greatness to distribute them no less among the lowly than among the great themselves; a considerate and affable prince who, by always engaging manners, an always open welcome, and a face on which gentleness is depicted, inspires in those who approach him as much confidence as the splendour of his court, the lustre of his birth, and the dignity of his person impress respect and veneration upon them; a charitable and compassionate prince, always ready to hear the humble petitions of the afflicted, and always disposed to take up their cause and defend their interests. These, my Lord, are not those studied praises which flattery offers princes, and which sometimes express what they ought to be rather than what they are. I say nothing that all these people who hear me, and whose hearts you possess, have not said a hundred times before me and do not still say every day as I do and as loudly as I do. It is a just and glorious possession in which you have been maintained until now, and will be maintained, by that greatness of soul which appears in everything, that generosity of feeling, that goodness of disposition, and so many other qualities which we admire; and, if I may say so, my Lord, to discharge my ministry and for your edification, qualities which ought to serve not merely to make Your Royal Highness a prince after men's hearts, but a truly Christian prince after God's heart. Thus, my Lord, in speaking of almsgiving and care for the poor, I shall have the advantage of entering into your intentions and supporting your zeal. The Fathers seem to have exhausted their eloquence on this subject: Saint John Chrysostom scarcely addressed the people without recommending Christian charity and mercy, and this caused him to be called the preacher of almsgiving. Before proposing my design, let us implore heaven's help, and to obtain it let us address the mother of mercy, saying to her: Hail, Mary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p003", "label": "French original", "text": "Rien n'est plus ordinaire dans le Christianisme que d'entendre parler de l'excellence & des avantages de l'aum\u00f4ne: mais on n'est gu\u00e9res accoutum\u00e9, ou du moins on ne se pla\u00eet gu\u00e9res \u00e0 entendre parler du pr\u00e9cepte & de la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 de l'aum\u00f4ne. Ceux qui ne la font pas, n'en ont commun\u00e9ment nul scrupule, & ne s'en accusent jamais au tribunal de la p\u00e9nitence: & ceux qui la font, dit S. Jean Chrysostome, la regardent volontiers comme une \u0153uvre de sur\u00e9rogation, & non point comme une obligation \u00e9troite & rigoureuse. Ils la font, mais au m\u00eame tems, ils ont une secrette complaisance de faire au-del\u00e0 de leurs devoirs; ils se flattent de cette pens\u00e9e, & ils aiment \u00e0 s'y entretenir, soit pour se conserver la libert\u00e9 de ne pas donner, soit pour s'attribuer tout le m\u00e9rite de ce qu'ils donnent. C'est n\u00e9anmoins une v\u00e9rit\u00e9 incontestable, que la loi de Dieu nous oblige \u00e0 soulager les pauvres par nos aum\u00f4nes; & cette loi, Chr\u00e9tiens, est si s\u00e9v\u00e9re, qu'il n'y va pas moins que de notre salut \u00e9ternel. Dieu ne veut point vous \u00f4ter le m\u00e9rite de votre charit\u00e9, quand vous faites l'aum\u00f4ne; mais il n'est pas juste aussi que vous lui \u00f4tiez, ou que vous pr\u00e9tendiez lui \u00f4ter le pouvoir qu'il a, & qu'il aura toujours, de vous la commander: comme il ne vous refuse point l'un, vous ne pouvez lui contester l'autre; & pour vous inspirer l\u00e0-dessus toute la soumission n\u00e9cessaire, il faut vous bien convaincre de trois choses. En premier lieu, que l'aum\u00f4ne n'est point un simple conseil, mais un pr\u00e9cepte. En second lieu, que ce n'est point un commandement vague & ind\u00e9fini, mais d\u00e9termin\u00e9 \u00e0 une certaine mati\u00e9re. En troisi\u00e9me lieu, que ce pr\u00e9cepte doit \u00eatre observ\u00e9 avec ordre & selon les r\u00e9gles de la charit\u00e9. Or voil\u00e0 les trois points qui vont partager ce discours. Je dis donc qu'il y a un pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne, & mon dessein est de vous faire voir sur quoi il est fond\u00e9; ce sera la premi\u00e9re partie. Je dis qu'il y a une mati\u00e9re affect\u00e9e & destin\u00e9e de Dieu pour l'aum\u00f4ne, & je pr\u00e9tens aujourd'hui vous la d\u00e9terminer; ce sera la seconde partie. Enfin, je dis qu'il y a un ordre \u00e0 garder dans l'aum\u00f4ne; & je veux vous le faire conno\u00eetre; ce sera la conclusion. Trois points de morale que je vais d\u00e9velopper selon les principes les plus communs de la Th\u00e9ologie. Car ne pensez pas que j'affecte ici une s\u00e9v\u00e9rit\u00e9 particuli\u00e9re & outr\u00e9e. Quand il s'agit d'obligation de conscience, sur-tout de p\u00e9ch\u00e9 mortel, nous ne devons dire que ce qu'il y a de vrai & d'incontestablement vrai. Pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne, mati\u00e9re de l'aum\u00f4ne, ordre de l'aum\u00f4ne, c'est tout le sujet de votre attention."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p003", "label": "English", "text": "Nothing is more usual in Christianity than to hear of the excellence and advantages of almsgiving; but people are little accustomed, or at least little pleased, to hear of the precept and necessity of almsgiving. Those who do not give commonly have no scruple about it, and never accuse themselves of it at the tribunal of penance; and those who do give, says Saint John Chrysostom, readily regard it as a work beyond obligation rather than as a strict and binding duty. They give, yet at the same time secretly congratulate themselves on doing more than their duties require. They flatter themselves with this thought and like to dwell on it, either to retain the freedom not to give, or to claim all the merit of what they give. Nevertheless it is an incontestable truth that God's law obliges us to relieve the poor by our alms; and this law, Christians, is so strict that nothing less than our eternal salvation is at stake. God does not wish to take away the merit of your charity when you give alms; but neither is it just for you to take away, or claim to take away, his power, which he has and always will have, to command it of you. As he does not refuse you the one, you cannot dispute the other with him. To inspire all the necessary submission in you on this matter, you must be thoroughly convinced of three things. First, almsgiving is not a mere counsel, but a precept. Second, it is not a vague and indefinite commandment, but one concerning a definite subject matter. Third, this precept must be observed in due order and according to the rules of charity. These are the three points that will divide this discourse. I say, then, that there is a precept of almsgiving, and my purpose is to show you its foundation; this will be the first part. I say that God has assigned and destined certain goods for almsgiving, and I intend today to determine them for you; this will be the second part. Finally I say that an order must be kept in almsgiving, and I wish to make it known to you; this will be the conclusion. I shall develop these three points of morality according to the most generally accepted principles of theology. Do not think that I am affecting some peculiar and excessive severity here. When an obligation of conscience is at issue, especially mortal sin, we must say only what is true and incontestably true. The precept of almsgiving, its subject matter, and its proper order: these are the whole subject of your attention.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-h004", "label": "French original", "text": "I. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-h004", "label": "English", "text": "FIRST PART", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p004", "label": "French original", "text": "Il y a un pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne, & ce pr\u00e9cepte sur quoi est-il fond\u00e9? ce pr\u00e9cepte en quelles conjonctures, en quelles n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s des pauvres oblige-t-il? Ce sont les points importans que j'ai d'abord \u00e0 \u00e9claircir, & qui demandent, Chr\u00e9tiens, toute votre r\u00e9flexion. Qu'il y ait un pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne, c'est une v\u00e9rit\u00e9 constante. Le Sauveur du monde nous l'a express\u00e9ment d\u00e9clar\u00e9 en son Evangile; & ce commandement est si rigoureux, qu'il suffira de ne l'avoir pas accompli, pour \u00eatre reprouv\u00e9 de Dieu, & pour entendre ce formidable arr\u00eat: Discedite \u00e0 me, maledicti; retirez-vous de moi, maudits. Mais o\u00f9 iront-ils, & \u00e0 quoi sont-ils r\u00e9serv\u00e9s? au feu \u00e9ternel: In ignem aeternum. Pourquoi? en voici la raison: c'est, dira le Seigneur, que j'ai eu faim, & que vous ne m'avez pas donn\u00e9 \u00e0 manger; Esurivi enim, & non dedistis mihi manducare: c'est que j'ai \u00e9t\u00e9 malade & en prison, & que vous ne m'avez pas visit\u00e9; Infirmus & in carcere, & non visitastis me: c'est que dans la personne des pauvres, que je regardois comme mes freres, comme mes membres vivans, j'ai souffert des besoins extr\u00eames, & que vous n'avez pas pens\u00e9 \u00e0 me secourir; Nudus, & non cooperuistis me. Chose \u00e9trange, reprend saint Chrysostome! l'Evangile ne marque point d'autre chef d'accusation que celui-l\u00e0: comme si toute la rigueur du jugement de Dieu devoit consister dans la discussion de ce seul article; & que Jesus-Christ en qualit\u00e9 de souverain juge ne d\u00fbt venir \u00e0 la fin des si\u00e9cles, que pour condamner la duret\u00e9 & l'insensibilit\u00e9 des riches envers les pauvres. Or ce Dieu si juste & si \u00e9quitable, ajoute le m\u00eame Pere, ne reprouvera jamais les hommes, pour avoir omis de simples conseils, mais pour avoir viol\u00e9 ses pr\u00e9ceptes. Il faut donc, conclut-il, que l'aum\u00f4ne soit un pr\u00e9cepte: cette preuve est convaincante, & r\u00e9sout en peu de paroles toute la question."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p004", "label": "English", "text": "There is a precept of almsgiving: on what is it founded? In what circumstances, in what necessities of the poor, does this precept bind? These are the important points which I must first clarify, and which, Christians, demand all your reflection. That there is a precept of almsgiving is an established truth. The Saviour of the world has expressly declared it in his Gospel; and this commandment is so strict that simply failing to fulfil it will suffice to be rejected by God and hear that fearful sentence: Discedite a me, maledicti; depart from me, accursed ones. But where will they go, and what awaits them? Eternal fire: In ignem aeternum. Why? Here is the reason: because, the Lord will say, I was hungry and you did not give me food; Esurivi enim, et non dedistis mihi manducare. Because I was sick and in prison and you did not visit me; Infirmus et in carcere, et non visitastis me. Because in the persons of the poor, whom I regarded as my brothers, as my living members, I suffered extreme needs, and you did not think of helping me; Nudus, et non cooperuistis me. An extraordinary thing, Saint Chrysostom remarks! The Gospel names no other charge than this: as though all the severity of God's judgment were to consist in examining this single article, and Jesus Christ as sovereign judge were to come at the end of the ages only to condemn the hardness and insensibility of the rich towards the poor. Now this God, so just and equitable, adds the same Father, will never reject men for omitting mere counsels, but for violating his precepts. Therefore, he concludes, almsgiving must be a precept. This proof is convincing and resolves the whole question in a few words.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p005", "label": "French original", "text": "Allons plus avant, Chr\u00e9tiens, & voyons sur quoi ce pr\u00e9cepte est fond\u00e9. Car de-l\u00e0 comme d'une source f\u00e9conde, je tirerai non-seulement de grandes lumi\u00e9res pour vous instruire, mais de puissans motifs pour vous exciter \u00e0 la pratique d'un devoir si essentiel, & d'une loi dont la transgression doit avoir pour vous des cons\u00e9quences si affreuses. Sur quoi, dis-je, est fond\u00e9 le pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne? ceci est remarquable. Sur deux titres, r\u00e9pond le Docteur Ang\u00e9lique S. Thomas: s\u00e7avoir, la souverainet\u00e9 de Dieu d'une part, & de l'autre l'indigence du prochain. Deux principes d'o\u00f9 r\u00e9sulte pour les riches du si\u00e9cle une obligation si \u00e9troite, que l'aum\u00f4ne n'est pas seulement \u00e0 leur \u00e9gard un pr\u00e9cepte, mais un pr\u00e9cepte de droit naturel, mais un pr\u00e9cepte de droit divin; & par cons\u00e9quent un pr\u00e9cepte, dont nulle puissance sur la terre ne les peut dispenser. Appliquez-vous, & ne perdez rien de cette morale."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p005", "label": "English", "text": "Let us go further, Christians, and see the foundation of this precept. From it, as from a fruitful spring, I shall draw not only great light for your instruction, but powerful motives to stir you to practise so essential a duty and a law whose transgression must have such dreadful consequences for you. On what, I say, is the precept of almsgiving founded? This deserves attention. On two grounds, replies the Angelic Doctor, Saint Thomas: God's sovereignty on the one hand, and our neighbour's need on the other. From these two principles arises an obligation so strict for the world's wealthy that almsgiving is not merely a precept for them, but a precept of natural law, a precept of divine law; and consequently a precept from which no power on earth can dispense them. Attend closely and lose nothing of this moral teaching.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p006", "label": "French original", "text": "En effet, mes chers Auditeurs, Dieu est le souverain ma\u00eetre de vos biens, il en est le seigneur, il en est m\u00eame absolument le vrai propri\u00e9taire; & par comparaison de vous \u00e0 lui, vous n'en \u00eates, \u00e0 le bien prendre, que les \u0153conomes & les dispensateurs. C'est ce que la raison & la foi nous d\u00e9montrent \u00e9videmment. Or puisque vos biens sont \u00e0 Dieu par droit de souverainet\u00e9, vous lui en devez le tribut, l'hommage, la reconnoissance; & puisqu'il en a la propri\u00e9t\u00e9 m\u00eame, & qu'elle lui appartient, il en doit avoir les fruits. Que fait Dieu, Chr\u00e9tiens? il affecte ce tribut & ces fruits \u00e0 la subsistance des pauvres; c'est-\u00e0-dire, qu'au lieu d'exiger ce tribut par lui-m\u00eame, & pour lui-m\u00eame, ce qui ne convient pas \u00e0 sa grandeur, il l'exige par les mains des pauvres; ou plut\u00f4t il substitue les pauvres, pour l'exiger en son nom. Tellement que l'aum\u00f4ne, qui par rapport au pauvre est un devoir de charit\u00e9 & de mis\u00e9ricorde, est par rapport \u00e0 Dieu un devoir de justice, un devoir de d\u00e9pendance & de suj\u00e9tion: & c'est ce que le S. Esprit nous a fait entendre par cette belle parole: Honora Dominum de tua substantia. Prenez garde, s'il vous pla\u00eet: il veut que l'homme fasse honneur \u00e0 Dieu de ses biens, qu'il a re\u00e7us de la main de Dieu; & l'homme, dit S. Leon Pape, s'acquitte de ce devoir, en payant \u00e0 Dieu, & comme vassal, & comme sujet, les droits dont il lui est redevable. Droits honorifiques, puisqu'en effet ils honorent Dieu; mais au m\u00eame-tems droits utiles & profitables aux pauvres, \u00e0 qui Dieu par sa providence les a r\u00e9sign\u00e9s. Car Dieu, je le r\u00e9p\u00e9te, a \u00e9tabli les pauvres dans le monde pour recueillir ses droits en sa place; & l'aum\u00f4ne est le seul moyen par o\u00f9 les riches puissent rendre \u00e0 Dieu ce qu'ils lui doivent. C'est pourquoi S. Pierre Chrysologue parlant des pauvres, leur donne une qualit\u00e9 bien glorieuse & une commission bien honorable, lorsqu'il les appelle les receveurs du domaine de Dieu, & qu'il nous fait consid\u00e9rer la main du pauvre comme le tr\u00e9sor de Dieu sur la terre: Gazophylacium Dei, manus pauperis."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p006", "label": "English", "text": "Indeed, my dear hearers, God is the sovereign master of your goods, their lord, and even absolutely their true owner; compared with him, you are, properly speaking, merely their stewards and dispensers. Reason and faith plainly demonstrate this to us. Since your goods belong to God by sovereign right, you owe him their tribute, homage, and acknowledgment; and since he has ownership itself, and it belongs to him, he must receive their fruits. What does God do, Christians? He assigns this tribute and these fruits to the support of the poor; that is, instead of demanding this tribute himself and for himself, which does not befit his greatness, he demands it through the hands of the poor; or rather he appoints the poor to demand it in his name. Thus almsgiving, which in relation to the poor is a duty of charity and mercy, is in relation to God a duty of justice, dependence, and subjection. The Holy Spirit has made us understand this by these beautiful words: Honora Dominum de tua substantia, honour the Lord with your substance. Notice, please: he wishes man to honour God with the goods he has received from God's hand; and man, says Saint Leo the Pope, fulfils this duty by paying God, as a vassal and a subject, the dues he owes him. These are honorary dues, since they indeed honour God, but at the same time useful and profitable dues for the poor, to whom God in his providence has assigned them. For God, I repeat, has established the poor in the world to collect his dues in his place; and almsgiving is the only means by which the rich can render to God what they owe him. Hence Saint Peter Chrysologus, speaking of the poor, gives them a most glorious title and a most honourable commission when he calls them the collectors of God's domain, and has us regard the poor man's hand as God's treasury on earth: Gazophylacium Dei, manus pauperis.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p007", "label": "French original", "text": "Que fait donc le riche quand il oublie le pauvre, & qu'il lui refuse l'aum\u00f4ne? vous ne vous \u00eates peut-\u00eatre jamais form\u00e9 l'id\u00e9e de ce p\u00e9ch\u00e9, telle que je la con\u00e7ois, & telle que l'Ecriture m\u00eame nous la donne. Je dis qu'un riche qui refuse au pauvre l'aum\u00f4ne, est un sujet rebelle qui refuse le tribut \u00e0 son souverain; que c'est un vassal orgueilleux, qui par un esprit d'ind\u00e9pendance ne veut pas reconno\u00eetre son Seigneur. Excellente id\u00e9e, qui nous fait comprendre d'une part la sup\u00e9riorit\u00e9 infinie de l'\u00eatre de Dieu, & de l'autre la nature de l'aum\u00f4ne. Car de l\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs, je tire deux cons\u00e9quences, qui ne peuvent \u00eatre ni assez attentivement m\u00e9dit\u00e9es, ni assez fortement pr\u00each\u00e9es dans le Christianisme. La premi\u00e9re, qu'il est essentiel \u00e0 l'aum\u00f4ne d'\u00eatre faite dans un sentiment d'humilit\u00e9; & que bien loin que ce soit une \u0153uvre propre \u00e0 nous inspirer l'orgueil & \u00e0 nous enfler, elle nous tient au contraire dans la soumission, en nous r\u00e9duisant \u00e0 la connoissance de nous-m\u00eames. Pourquoi? parce que l'aum\u00f4ne est essentiellement un aveu, que l'homme fait \u00e0 Dieu de sa d\u00e9pendance. Or il n'est pas naturel, qu'un sujet tire vanit\u00e9 de sa condition de sujet, ni du t\u00e9moignage m\u00eame qu'il rend de sa fid\u00e9lit\u00e9 & de son ob\u00e9issance."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p007", "label": "English", "text": "What then does the rich man do when he forgets the poor and refuses him alms? Perhaps you have never formed an idea of this sin as I conceive it, and as Scripture itself presents it. I say that a rich man who refuses alms to the poor is a rebellious subject refusing tribute to his sovereign; that he is a proud vassal who, in a spirit of independence, refuses to acknowledge his Lord. This is an excellent conception, enabling us to understand both the infinite superiority of God's being and the nature of almsgiving. From it, my dear hearers, I draw two consequences which cannot be meditated on too attentively or preached too strongly in Christianity. First, it is essential that almsgiving be done in a spirit of humility; far from being a work apt to inspire pride and inflate us, it instead keeps us in submission by bringing us to knowledge of ourselves. Why? Because almsgiving is essentially man's acknowledgment to God of his dependence. Now it is not natural for a subject to take pride in being a subject, or even in the testimony he gives of his fidelity and obedience.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p008", "label": "French original", "text": "Et c'est le secret que comprit parfaitement Abraham, lorsqu'il re\u00e7ut trois Anges dans sa maison sous la figure & sous l'habit de trois pauvres. L'Ecriture dit, que pour se disposer \u00e0 leur rendre ce devoir d'hospitalit\u00e9, il s'humilia; & S. Augustin ajo\u00fbte, que prostern\u00e9 en leur pr\u00e9sence, les voyant trois, il n'en adora qu'un; Tres vidit, & unum adoravit. Que signifient ces paroles? en adora-t-il un des trois qu'il voyoit? ou s'\u00e9levant au-dessus des trois, en adora-t-il un quatri\u00e9me qu'il ne voyoit pas? Quelques-uns ont cr\u00fb que Dieu deslors par une grace particuli\u00e9re lui r\u00e9v\u00e9la l'auguste myst\u00e9re de l'ineffable Trinit\u00e9; & que l'adoration d'un seul \u00e0 la v\u00fbe de trois, fut comme la confession de foi qu'en fit ce saint Patriarche, reconnoissant en trois personnes l'unit\u00e9 d'un Dieu: c'est la pens\u00e9e de saint Augustin, aussi solide qu'ing\u00e9nieuse. Mais il me semble que saint J\u00e9r\u00f4me a pris la chose dans un sens plus naturel; & j'aime mieux dire avec lui, qu'Abraham voyant trois pauvres, se prosterna devant Dieu, parce qu'il alloit payer \u00e0 Dieu dans la personne de ces trois pauvres le tribut de ses biens: comme s'il e\u00fbt ainsi voulu marquer le principe de l'aum\u00f4ne qu'il alloit faire, & nous montrer par son exemple avec quel esprit nous la devons faire nous-m\u00eames. Car tel est, mes Freres, dit S. Chrysostome, la premi\u00e9re v\u00fbe que nous devons avoir dans nos aum\u00f4nes, puisque l'aum\u00f4ne est une esp\u00e9ce de culte que nous rendons \u00e0 Dieu. Tel est le premier sentiment que la foi doit former dans nos c\u0153urs, & dont elle nous doit remplir: un sentiment de v\u00e9n\u00e9ration pour Dieu. Que vais-je faire par cette aum\u00f4ne? Je vais reconno\u00eetre l'empire de Dieu sur moi. Je vais protester \u00e0 Dieu qu'il est mon Dieu, & que je suis sa cr\u00e9ature. Oui, Seigneur, & c'est pour cela que je me mets en devoir d'assister le pauvre, d\u00e9laiss\u00e9 & abandonn\u00e9. En le soulageant dans sa mis\u00e9re, je ne vous donnerai rien; & que pourrois-je vous donner, \u00f4 mon Dieu? vous \u00eates trop riche, & je suis trop foible: mais je pr\u00e9tens par l\u00e0-m\u00eame avouer ma foiblesse; je pr\u00e9tens confesser par-l\u00e0 que tout ce que j'ai est \u00e0 vous, & que je n'ai rien qui ne rel\u00e8ve de vous. Ainsi, dis-je, y doit proc\u00e9der un Chr\u00e9tien, qui veut satisfaire au pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne en chr\u00e9tien."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p008", "label": "English", "text": "This is the secret Abraham understood perfectly when he received three angels into his house in the appearance and clothing of three poor men. Scripture says that, to prepare himself to render them this duty of hospitality, he humbled himself; and Saint Augustine adds that, prostrate in their presence, though he saw three, he adored only one: Tres vidit, et unum adoravit. What do these words mean? Did he adore one of the three he saw? Or, rising above the three, did he adore a fourth whom he did not see? Some have believed that God then revealed to him, by a special grace, the august mystery of the ineffable Trinity; and that the adoration of one at the sight of three was, as it were, that holy patriarch's profession of faith, acknowledging in three persons the unity of one God. Such is Saint Augustine's thought, as solid as it is ingenious. But it seems to me that Saint Jerome understood the matter in a more natural sense; and I prefer to say with him that Abraham, seeing three poor men, prostrated himself before God because he was about to pay God, in their persons, the tribute of his goods: as though he wished thus to indicate the principle of the alms he was about to give, and show us by his example the spirit in which we ourselves must give them. For this, my brothers, says Saint Chrysostom, is the first intention we must have in our almsgiving, since almsgiving is a kind of worship we render to God. This is the first sentiment faith must form in our hearts and fill them with: reverence for God. What shall I do by these alms? I shall acknowledge God's dominion over me. I shall declare to God that he is my God and I am his creature. Yes, Lord, and that is why I undertake to assist the poor man, forsaken and abandoned. In relieving his misery I shall give you nothing; and what could I give you, my God? You are too rich, and I too weak. But by this very act I intend to acknowledge my weakness; I intend to confess that all I have is yours and that I have nothing that does not depend upon you. Thus, I say, must a Christian proceed who wishes to fulfil the precept of almsgiving as a Christian.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p009", "label": "French original", "text": "De l\u00e0 suit une autre cons\u00e9quence: que l'aum\u00f4ne, pour \u00eatre faite dans la rigueur du pr\u00e9cepte, doit \u00eatre proportionn\u00e9e aux biens & \u00e0 leur quantit\u00e9. Car Dieu, mes chers Auditeurs, qui r\u00e9gle tout par sa sagesse, & qui a tout fait avec nombre, poids & mesure, exige de vous ce tribut selon toute l'\u00e9tendue de votre pouvoir. Les Princes de la terre n'en usent pas toujours de la sorte; & souvent par des raisons de politique, que la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 m\u00eame autorise, ils se trouvent oblig\u00e9s \u00e0 tirer les plus grands secours de leurs moindres sujets, pendant qu'ils m\u00e9nagent les plus opulens & les plus ais\u00e9s. Mais notre Dieu qui ne voit point de n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 sup\u00e9rieure \u00e0 sa loi, & devant qui toutes les conditions du monde ne sont rien, sans se rel\u00e2cher de ses droits, & sans \u00e9gard \u00e0 vos personnes, fait une imposition r\u00e9elle sur vos biens. Etes-vous dans l'abondance? il attend de vous un tribut abondant; & c'est vous flatter, ou pour mieux dire, c'est vous tromper vous-m\u00eames, si vous vous en tenez quittes pour de l\u00e9g\u00e9res aum\u00f4nes, quand vous pouvez les grossir, & que vous avez de quoi fournir \u00e0 de plus amples largesses. Abus, disoit saint Ambroise; ce n'est point aum\u00f4ne, que de donner peu, lorsqu'on a beaucoup re\u00e7u: Non est eleemosyna \u00e8 multis pauca largiri. Sur quoi ce saint Docteur ajo\u00fbtoit: Non ergo quid fastidio expuas, sed quid religionis affectu & studio conferas pensandum est. Prenez donc garde, concluoit-il, en parlant \u00e0 un riche Chr\u00e9tien, que l'aum\u00f4ne n'est point une \u0153uvre de sur\u00e9rogation, mais une dette dont Dieu vous a charg\u00e9; & qu'il ne s'agit pas seulement pour vous, de donner aux pauvres le rebut de votre maison, & je ne s\u00e7ais quels restes de votre luxe jett\u00e9s au hazard ou arrach\u00e9s par importunit\u00e9, comme peut-\u00eatre vous vous \u00eates content\u00e9 jusques \u00e0 pr\u00e9sent de le faire; parce que traiter ainsi votre Dieu, & le partager si mal, c'est le m\u00e9priser: Non ergo quid fastidio expuas. Mais voulez-vous lui rendre ce qui lui est d\u00fb? rentrez en vous-m\u00eame, examinez vos facult\u00e9s & vos forces; pesez, mais dans la balance du sanctuaire, comment vous faites l'aum\u00f4ne: si vous la faites avec cet esprit d'\u00e9quit\u00e9, avec cette exacte proportion que la loi demande; si vous la faites suffisamment, si vous la faites lib\u00e9ralement, si vous la faites pleinement. Car ce que vous devez craindre, poursuivoit saint Ambroise, c'est qu'au lieu d'\u00eatre r\u00e9compens\u00e9 pour avoir donn\u00e9, vous ne soyez puni pour avoir donn\u00e9 trop peu: Metuendum est enim ne plus plectaris ob retenta, quam compenseris ob data."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p009", "label": "English", "text": "Another consequence follows: almsgiving, to fulfil the strict requirement of the precept, must be proportionate to one's goods and their amount. For God, my dear hearers, who orders everything by his wisdom and has made all things in number, weight, and measure, demands this tribute from you according to the full extent of your ability. Earthly princes do not always act thus; often for political reasons sanctioned by necessity itself, they find themselves obliged to draw the greatest assistance from their humblest subjects while sparing the most opulent and prosperous. But our God, who sees no necessity above his law, and before whom all worldly ranks are nothing, neither relinquishes his rights nor shows regard for your persons, but imposes a real levy on your goods. Are you in abundance? He expects abundant tribute from you. You flatter yourselves\u2014or rather deceive yourselves\u2014if you consider yourselves discharged by slight alms when you can increase them and have the means for greater generosity. An abuse, said Saint Ambrose: to give little when one has received much is not almsgiving; Non est eleemosyna e multis pauca largiri. To this the holy Doctor added: Non ergo quid fastidio expuas, sed quid religionis affectu et studio conferas pensandum est: one must consider not what one casts away in disgust, but what one contributes out of religious affection and zeal. Take heed, then, he concluded, addressing a wealthy Christian, that almsgiving is not a work beyond obligation, but a debt God has laid upon you. Your task is not merely to give the poor your household's refuse and some scraps of your luxury, tossed out at random or wrung from you by importunity, as perhaps you have hitherto been content to do; for to treat your God thus and allot him so poor a share is to despise him: Non ergo quid fastidio expuas. But do you wish to render him his due? Enter into yourself, examine your means and resources; weigh, in the balance of the sanctuary, how you give alms: whether with that spirit of equity and that exact proportion the law demands, whether sufficiently, liberally, and fully. For what you must fear, Saint Ambrose continued, is that instead of being rewarded for giving, you may be punished for giving too little: Metuendum est enim ne plus plectaris ob retenta, quam compenseris ob data: you must fear being punished more for what you retained than recompensed for what you gave.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p010", "label": "French original", "text": "Or quel est, mes chers Auditeurs, le grand d\u00e9sordre qui r\u00e9gne aujourd'hui dans le monde, je dis m\u00eame dans le monde Chr\u00e9tien? Permettez-moi de vous le repr\u00e9senter, & portez-en devant Dieu la confusion. Quel est, dis-je, l'injuste proc\u00e9d\u00e9 des riches mondains? le voici: ils mesurent tout hors l'aum\u00f4ne, sur le pied de leurs revenus & de leurs biens. Je m'explique. Ils veulent \u00eatre servis \u00e0 proportion de leurs biens, ils veulent \u00eatre v\u00eatus \u00e0 proportion de leurs biens, ils veulent \u00eatre log\u00e9s, meubl\u00e9s \u00e0 proportion de leurs biens; & non-seulement \u00e0 proportion, mais souvent bien au-del\u00e0 de cette proportion: car \u00e0 quels exc\u00e8s ne va-t-on pas? Il n'y a que l'aum\u00f4ne, o\u00f9 l'on ne se pique de nulle proportion, quoiqu'il n'y ait que l'aum\u00f4ne o\u00f9 la proportion soit un devoir indispensable. Car en v\u00e9rit\u00e9, mes Freres, les riches du si\u00e9cle r\u00e9glent-ils leurs aum\u00f4nes par leurs biens; & quelle proportion voyons-nous entre ce qu'il leur en co\u00fbte pour le soulagement des pauvres, & ce que l'esprit du monde leur fait sacrifier \u00e0 tant d'autres d\u00e9penses? c'est-\u00e0-dire, les riches du si\u00e9cle sont-ils magnifiques dans leurs aum\u00f4nes, autant par proportion qu'ils sont superbes dans leurs habits, autant qu'ils sont splendides dans leurs tables, autant qu'ils sont prodigues dans leur jeu? J'en appelle \u00e0 eux-m\u00eames. Est-ce de leur part que viennent les grandes contributions pour l'entretien des pauvres? est-ce par eux que les h\u00f4pitaux subsistent? par eux que tant de malades sont consol\u00e9s? par eux que tant de prisonniers sont secourus? Qu'une famille soit ruin\u00e9e, qu'une province soit dans la d\u00e9solation, qu'un \u00e9tablissement de pi\u00e9t\u00e9 soit pr\u00eat \u00e0 tomber; est-ce sur eux que l'on doit faire fond pour y pourvoir? N'est-ce pas au contraire dans les conditions, dans les fortunes m\u00e9diocres, que Dieu par sa mis\u00e9ricorde fait trouver les plus abondantes ressources? combien dans cette ville capitale de personnes vertueuses, \u00e0 qui leur \u00e9tat ne fournit rien ou presque rien au-del\u00e0 du n\u00e9cessaire, s\u00e7avent n\u00e9anmoins m\u00e9nager sur ce n\u00e9cessaire de quoi subvenir aux besoins des pauvres? le dirai-je? combien de pauvres sont plus charitables, plus lib\u00e9raux pour les pauvres, que ces puissans, que ces opulens, qui tiennent dans le monde les premi\u00e9res places, & que Dieu a combl\u00e9s de ses b\u00e9n\u00e9dictions temporelles? Cependant, c'est une loi, & une loi g\u00e9n\u00e9rale & absolue, que l'aum\u00f4ne & les biens doivent \u00eatre proportionn\u00e9s: & quand Dieu viendra pour vous juger, il est de la foi qu'il prendra pour r\u00e9gle de son jugement cette proportion. Vos biens compar\u00e9s \u00e0 vos aum\u00f4nes, ou vos aum\u00f4nes compar\u00e9es \u00e0 vos biens, c'est ce qui doit faire \u00e0 son tribunal, ou votre justification, ou votre condamnation. Pourquoi? parce qu'\u00e9tant le souverain Seigneur, plus il vous a fait part de ses dons, plus il a droit d'en exiger le l\u00e9gitime hommage, & que la raison m\u00eame naturelle le veut ainsi. Souverainet\u00e9 de Dieu, premier fondement du pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne. Quel est le second?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p010", "label": "English", "text": "Now what, my dear hearers, is the great disorder reigning today in the world, even in the Christian world? Allow me to put it before you, and bear its shame before God. What, I say, is the unjust conduct of the worldly rich? It is this: they measure everything except almsgiving by their income and possessions. Let me explain. They want service proportionate to their wealth, clothing proportionate to their wealth, housing and furniture proportionate to their wealth; and not merely proportionate, but often far beyond that proportion: for what excesses do people not reach? Only in almsgiving do they show no concern for proportion, although only in almsgiving is proportion an indispensable duty. For truly, my brothers, do the world's rich regulate their alms by their goods? What proportion do we see between what they spend on relieving the poor and what the spirit of the world makes them sacrifice to so many other expenses? That is, are the world's rich as magnificent proportionately in their alms as they are splendid in their clothes, lavish at their tables, and prodigal in their gambling? I appeal to them themselves. Do the great contributions for maintaining the poor come from them? Are hospitals supported by them? Are so many sick people comforted through them, so many prisoners assisted? When a family is ruined, a province desolated, or a pious institution about to collapse, should we rely on them to provide? Is it not rather in moderate stations and fortunes that God in his mercy makes the most abundant resources available? How many virtuous people in this capital, whose circumstances provide nothing or almost nothing beyond necessities, nevertheless know how to save from those necessities enough to meet the needs of the poor! Shall I say it? How many poor people are more charitable and generous towards the poor than those powerful and opulent people who hold the foremost places in the world and whom God has loaded with temporal blessings! Yet it is a law, a general and absolute law, that alms and goods must be proportionate; and when God comes to judge you, faith teaches that he will take this proportion as the rule of his judgment. Your goods compared with your alms, or your alms compared with your goods, must determine at his tribunal either your justification or your condemnation. Why? Because, being sovereign Lord, the more he has shared his gifts with you, the more right he has to demand their lawful homage, and natural reason itself requires this. God's sovereignty is the first foundation of the precept of almsgiving. What is the second?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p011", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est l'indigence & la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 du prochain, \u00e0 quoi Dieu vous oblige de pourvoir, & par titre de justice, & par titre de charit\u00e9: suivez-moi. Titre de justice, parce que c'est pour cela m\u00eame, & uniquement pour cela, que sa providence vous a faits ce que vous \u00eates, & qu'elle vous a \u00e9lev\u00e9s \u00e0 ce degr\u00e9 de prosp\u00e9rit\u00e9 qui vous distingue. Car il faut vous d\u00e9tromper, Chr\u00e9tiens, d'une erreur aussi commune dans la pratique, qu'elle est insoutenable dans la sp\u00e9culation; & ne vous pas persuader, si vous \u00eates riches, que vous le soyez pour vous-m\u00eames. Ce ne sont point l\u00e0 les v\u00fbes de Dieu, ce n'est point l\u00e0 sa conduite. Vous \u00eates riches, mais pour qui? pour les pauvres; & s'il n'y avoit des pauvres dans le monde, j'ose dire que Dieu l'arbitre & le supr\u00eame mod\u00e9rateur de toutes les conditions du monde, ne vous auroit jamais donn\u00e9 ces biens que vous poss\u00e9dez. Qu'a-t-il donc pr\u00e9tendu, & que pr\u00e9tend-il encore? que vous soyez les substituts, les ministres, les coop\u00e9rateurs de sa providence \u00e0 l'\u00e9gard des pauvres. Voil\u00e0 ce qu'il s'est propos\u00e9, & \u00e0 quoi il vous a destin\u00e9s. Emploi plus glorieux pour vous, emploi mille fois plus estimable que vos richesses m\u00eames. Car qu'est-ce pour des hommes, que d'\u00eatre les coop\u00e9rateurs de leur Dieu? Or comprenez ma pens\u00e9e: si Dieu imm\u00e9diatement & par lui-m\u00eame avoit pris soin de pourvoir aux besoins des pauvres, il y auroit pourvu abondamment & en Dieu. Vous donc les coop\u00e9rateurs de Dieu, vous les ministres, les substituts de Dieu, comment y devez-vous subvenir? comme Dieu. Tel est le soin dont il s'est d\u00e9charg\u00e9 sur vous; telle est la commission qu'il vous a donn\u00e9e. Il a voulu faire d\u00e9pendre les pauvres de votre charit\u00e9, afin que cette d\u00e9pendance f\u00fbt le lien qui form\u00e2t entre eux & vous une mutuelle soci\u00e9t\u00e9. Mais du reste, ce que je conclus, c'est que l'aum\u00f4ne n'est point seulement une charit\u00e9 pure, une charit\u00e9 gratuite, puisque vous ne donnez au pauvre que ce que vous avez re\u00e7u pour le pauvre & avec une obligation \u00e9troite de l'employer au profit du pauvre. Ce que je conclus, c'est que manquant \u00e0 faire l'aum\u00f4ne, ou la faisant au-dessous de votre condition, vous outragez, vous deshonorez, je dis plus, vous d\u00e9truisez en quelque sorte, vous an\u00e9antissez la providence de Dieu. Pourquoi? parce qu'autant qu'il est en vous, vous la rendez imparfaite & d\u00e9fectueuse, parce que vous autorisez contre elle les plaintes & les murmures des pauvres; parce que vous leur donnez un sp\u00e9cieux pr\u00e9texte de l'accuser, de la blasph\u00e9mer, de la renoncer."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p011", "label": "English", "text": "It is our neighbour's indigence and necessity, which God obliges you to provide for on grounds both of justice and of charity: follow me. On grounds of justice, because it is for this very purpose, and solely for it, that his providence has made you what you are and raised you to the prosperity that distinguishes you. For you must be disabused, Christians, of an error as common in practice as it is untenable in theory: do not persuade yourselves, if you are rich, that you are rich for yourselves. These are not God's intentions or his conduct. You are rich, but for whom? For the poor. If there were no poor people in the world, I dare say that God, the arbiter and supreme governor of every worldly condition, would never have given you the goods you possess. What then was his intention, and what is it still? That you should be the deputies, ministers, and fellow-workers of his providence towards the poor. This is what he proposed and destined you for: an office more glorious for you, a thousand times more estimable than your riches themselves. For what a thing it is for men to be their God's fellow-workers! Understand my thought: if God had directly and personally undertaken to provide for the poor's needs, he would have provided abundantly and as God. You, then, God's fellow-workers, ministers, and deputies, how ought you to supply them? As God does. Such is the care he has entrusted to you, the commission he has given you. He wished to make the poor dependent on your charity, so that this dependence might be the bond forming a mutual society between them and you. But what I conclude is that almsgiving is not merely pure charity, gratuitous charity, since you give the poor only what you received for the poor, with a strict obligation to employ it for their benefit. What I conclude is that by failing to give alms, or giving below your station, you outrage and dishonour\u2014I say more, in a manner destroy and annihilate\u2014God's providence. Why? Because, so far as lies in you, you make it imperfect and defective, because you sanction the complaints and murmurs of the poor against it, and give them a plausible pretext to accuse, blaspheme, and renounce it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p012", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais pensez-vous que Dieu jaloux de sa gloire, & touch\u00e9 des reproches injurieux que lui attirent vos sordides \u00e9pargnes \u00e0 l'\u00e9gard des pauvres, ne les fasse pas retomber sur vous-m\u00eames, souvent par des vengeances d'autant plus terribles qu'elles sont moins connues? Je ne parle point de ces mal\u00e9dictions temporelles, qu'il r\u00e9pand quelquefois sur ces riches si insensibles & si resserr\u00e9s. Je ne parle point de ces renversemens de fortune, de ces coups impr\u00e9vus qui partent de la main du Dieu vengeur des pauvres. S'il ne s'attaque pas toujours \u00e0 vos biens, vous en devez plus craindre pour vos personnes, vous en devez plus craindre pour votre ame. Vous oubliez ses pauvres, d'autres ne les oublieront pas. Dieu vous avoit \u00e9lev\u00e9s pour leur soulagement, d'autres seront substitu\u00e9s pour en \u00eatre les tuteurs: mais en prenant sur la terre votre place aupr\u00e8s des pauvres, ils auront dans le ciel la place qui vous \u00e9toit r\u00e9serv\u00e9e aupr\u00e8s de Dieu."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p012", "label": "English", "text": "But do you think that God, jealous of his glory and moved by the insulting reproaches your sordid economies towards the poor bring upon him, will not make them fall back upon you, often by punishments all the more terrible for being less known? I am not speaking of those temporal curses he sometimes pours upon the rich who are so insensible and close-fisted. I am not speaking of reversals of fortune or unforeseen blows from the hand of God, the avenger of the poor. If he does not always strike your goods, you have more to fear for your persons, more to fear for your soul. You forget his poor; others will not forget them. God raised you up for their relief; others will be appointed as their guardians. But in taking your place with the poor on earth, they will have in heaven the place reserved for you with God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p013", "label": "French original", "text": "Titre de charit\u00e9: ah! mes chers Auditeurs, qui sont ces infortun\u00e9s dont je plaide aujourd'hui la cause; & qui que vous puissiez \u00eatre selon le monde, ne sont-ce pas vos freres? n'est-ce pas dans le langage du Saint-Esprit, votre propre chair; c'est-\u00e0-dire, ces pauvres ne sont-ce pas des hommes de m\u00eame nature que vous? ne sont-ce pas les enfans de Dieu comme vous, appell\u00e9s \u00e0 la m\u00eame adoption que vous, \u00e0 la m\u00eame grace que vous, \u00e0 la m\u00eame gloire que vous? ne sont-ce pas les h\u00e9ritiers de Dieu, les coh\u00e9ritiers de Jesus-Christ aussi-bien que vous? Or quel moyen, reprend le disciple bien-aim\u00e9 saint Jean, que leur \u00e9tant uni d'un n\u0153ud si intime & par tant d'endroits, vous les puissiez voir dans la souffrance, & ne leur pas ouvrir les entrailles de votre mis\u00e9ricorde? ou que vous puissiez les abandonner dans leur disette, & avoir l'amour & la charit\u00e9 de Dieu en vous? Mais si vous n'avez pas alors l'amour de Dieu, vous \u00eates donc ennemis de Dieu; si vous \u00eates ennemis de Dieu, vous avez donc viol\u00e9 un pr\u00e9cepte de Dieu; ce pr\u00e9cepte ne peut \u00eatre que l'incontestable & l'indispensable commandement de l'aum\u00f4ne: Qui habuerit substantiam hujus mundi, & viderit fratrem suum necessitatem habere, & clauserit viscera sua ab eo, quomodo charitas Dei manet in eo?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p013", "label": "English", "text": "On grounds of charity: ah, my dear hearers, who are these unfortunate people whose cause I plead today? Whatever you may be in the world's eyes, are they not your brothers? Are they not, in the Holy Spirit's language, your own flesh? That is, are not these poor people men of the same nature as you? Are they not God's children like you, called to the same adoption, the same grace, the same glory as you? Are they not God's heirs and Christ's fellow-heirs just as you are? How then, asks the beloved disciple Saint John, can you, united to them by so intimate a bond and in so many respects, see them suffering without opening to them the depths of your mercy? How can you abandon them in their want and have God's love and charity within you? But if you do not then have God's love, you are his enemies; if you are his enemies, you have violated a precept of God. This can only be the incontestable and indispensable commandment of almsgiving: Qui habuerit substantiam hujus mundi, et viderit fratrem suum necessitatem habere, et clauserit viscera sua ab eo, quomodo charitas Dei manet in eo? Whoever has this world's goods and sees his brother in need, yet shuts his heart against him, how does God's charity abide in him?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p014", "label": "French original", "text": "Et ne pensez pas que ce devoir ne regarde que certaines n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s des pauvres plus pressantes & plus rares. Quand je dis que la justice, que la charit\u00e9 nous oblige \u00e0 aider nos freres dans leurs besoins, qu'est-ce que j'entens: besoins communs, tels qu'ils se pr\u00e9sentent tous les jours \u00e0 nos yeux, ou tels que nous ne les connoissons pas; mais dont sans doute nous serions \u00e9mus, tout communs qu'ils sont, si nous \u00e9tions plus attentifs \u00e0 les d\u00e9couvrir & \u00e0 les conno\u00eetre. Car c'est une autre illusion non moins grossi\u00e9re, & qui renverse toutes les loix de l'humanit\u00e9, de croire que le pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne n'est rigoureux qu'\u00e0 l'\u00e9gard des n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s extr\u00eames des pauvres. Outre ces extr\u00eames n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s, il y a des n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s gri\u00e9ves & plus fr\u00e9quentes; & si Dieu dans ces gri\u00e9ves n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s nous permettoit de laisser les pauvres sans secours, comment le Sauveur du monde, en condamnant un jour tant de reprouv\u00e9s, prendroit-il pour le sujet capital & universel de leur r\u00e9probation, l'oubli volontaire des pauvres? Y a-t-il donc tant de riches assez impitoyables, pour voir p\u00e9rir un pauvre \u00e0 leurs yeux, pour le voir presque r\u00e9duit aux abois & pr\u00eat \u00e0 rendre l'ame, sans prendre soin de lui conserver la vie & de le tirer d'une telle extr\u00e9mit\u00e9? Y a-t-il d'ailleurs tant de pauvres dans un \u00e9tat si mis\u00e9rable & si d\u00e9pourv\u00fb? Par cons\u00e9quent, concluent les Th\u00e9ologiens, pour expliquer l'Evangile, il ne faut pas seulement l'entendre de ces n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s extraordinaires, mais des autres qui nous frappent plus commun\u00e9ment la v\u00fbe, & \u00e0 quoi Dieu nous ordonne sous peine d'une damnation \u00e9ternelle d'apporter le rem\u00e9de qui d\u00e9pend de nous & que nous avons dans les mains. En sorte que, suivant la pens\u00e9e d'un des plus s\u00e7avans hommes du si\u00e9cle pass\u00e9, un Chr\u00e9tien qui formeroit, ou qui forme en effet cette r\u00e9solution, de ne faire l'aum\u00f4ne que dans les derni\u00e8res n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s des pauvres, d\u00e8s-l\u00e0 commet un p\u00e9ch\u00e9 grief, & perd la grace de Dieu, parce qu'il est dans une disposition criminelle & dans une volont\u00e9 directement oppos\u00e9e \u00e0 la loi de Dieu."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p014", "label": "English", "text": "Do not think this duty concerns only certain more urgent and rarer necessities of the poor. When I say that justice and charity oblige us to assist our brothers in their needs, what do I mean? Ordinary needs, such as present themselves to our eyes every day, or such as we do not know, but which would undoubtedly move us, ordinary though they are, if we paid more attention to discovering and understanding them. For it is another equally gross illusion, overturning all the laws of humanity, to believe that the precept of almsgiving binds strictly only in the poor's extreme necessities. Besides these extreme necessities there are grave and more frequent ones; and if God allowed us to leave the poor unaided in these grave necessities, how could the Saviour of the world, when one day condemning so many of the reprobate, take their deliberate neglect of the poor as the principal and universal ground of their rejection? Are there so many rich people pitiless enough to see a poor man perish before their eyes, almost at his last gasp and ready to give up his soul, without taking care to preserve his life and rescue him from such extremity? Besides, are there so many poor people in so miserable and destitute a state? Consequently, theologians conclude, in explaining the Gospel we must understand it not only of these extraordinary necessities but also of the others which more commonly strike our eyes, and which God commands us, under pain of eternal damnation, to remedy as far as depends on us and lies in our hands. Thus, according to the thought of one of the most learned men of the past century, a Christian who should form, or actually forms, the resolution to give alms only in the last necessities of the poor thereby commits a grave sin and loses God's grace, because he has a sinful disposition and a will directly opposed to God's law.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p015", "label": "French original", "text": "Tristes v\u00e9rit\u00e9s pour vous, Riches du monde, & qui ne confirment que trop ce terrible anath\u00eame que le Fils de Dieu a prononc\u00e9 contre vous; Vae vobis divitibus, malheur \u00e0 vous qui vivez dans l'opulence: pourquoi? parce que votre opulence m\u00eame a presque toujours l'un de ces deux effets, ou d'allumer dans votre c\u0153ur la cupidit\u00e9 & l'envie d'avoir, au lieu de l'\u00e9teindre; ou de vous rendre plus sensuels & plus amateurs de vous-m\u00eames. Deux principes de votre indiff\u00e9rence pour les pauvres. Car poss\u00e9d\u00e9s d'une avare convoitise, vous voulez profiter de tout & ne vous d\u00e9saisir de rien. Toujours biens sur biens, toujours acqu\u00eats sur acqu\u00eats; toujours les mains ouvertes pour recevoir, & jamais pour donner: que dis-je? & souvent m\u00eame fall\u00fbt-il d\u00e9pouiller le pauvre & lui arracher le peu qui lui reste, bien loin de contribuer \u00e0 sa subsistance; fall\u00fbt-il l'opprimer, bien loin de le relever, tout n'est-il pas mis en usage pour contenter la faim insatiable qui vous d\u00e9vore? les droits les plus saints ne sont-ils pas foul\u00e9s aux pi\u00e9s? ne se porte-t-on pas jusqu'\u00e0 la violence la plus injuste & la plus criante, jusqu'\u00e0 la cruaut\u00e9, jusqu'\u00e0 la barbarie? Ou bien idol\u00e2tres de vos sens & tout occup\u00e9s de vous-m\u00eames, vous n'avez d'attention que sur vous-m\u00eames, de sentiment que pour vous-m\u00eames. Que le pauvre p\u00e2tisse dans la disette, que le malade languisse sur la paille, que la veuve charg\u00e9e d'enfans & perc\u00e9e de leurs cris, ressente toutes leurs douleurs & ne puisse r\u00e9pondre \u00e0 leurs g\u00e9missemens que par ses larmes; comme ce sont des maux \u00e9trangers & qui n'approchent point de vous, pourv\u00fb que votre sensualit\u00e9 soit satisfaite, pourv\u00fb que votre corps ait toutes ses commodit\u00e9s & toutes ses aises, vous \u00eates contens & vous ne pensez gu\u00e9res si les autres le doivent \u00eatre. Mais Dieu y pense; & viendra le tems o\u00f9 il s\u00e7aura vous y faire penser malgr\u00e9 vous, quand pour la justification de sa providence, il vous demandera raison du pauvre; quand il vous traitera comme vous avez trait\u00e9 le pauvre; quand il vous jugera sans mis\u00e9ricorde, comme vous avez rejett\u00e9 le pauvre sans compassion. Voil\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs, sur quoi il faudroit s'examiner, s'accuser soi-m\u00eame. Voil\u00e0 de tous les points de conscience, l'un des plus essentiels, & sur quoi les ministres du Seigneur devroient \u00eatre plus vigilans & plus s\u00e9v\u00e9res, puisqu'il y va de l'honneur de Dieu & de l'int\u00e9r\u00eat du prochain. Cependant convaincus du pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne, vous voulez s\u00e7avoir quelle en doit \u00eatre la mati\u00e9re, & c'est ce que je vais vous apprendre dans la seconde partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p015", "label": "English", "text": "Sad truths for you, rich people of the world, and truths that only too well confirm the terrible anathema pronounced against you by the Son of God: Vae vobis divitibus, woe to you who live in opulence! Why? Because your very opulence almost always has one of two effects: either to kindle greed and the desire to possess in your heart instead of extinguishing it, or to make you more sensual and more in love with yourselves. These are two sources of your indifference to the poor. Possessed by miserly covetousness, you wish to profit from everything and part with nothing. Always goods upon goods, acquisitions upon acquisitions; always hands open to receive, never to give. What am I saying? Often, even if the poor must be stripped and the little they have left torn from them, far from contributing to their subsistence; even if they must be oppressed, far from being raised up\u2014is not everything employed to satisfy the insatiable hunger devouring you? Are not the holiest rights trampled underfoot? Do people not proceed to the most unjust and outrageous violence, to cruelty, to barbarity? Or else, idolaters of your senses and wholly occupied with yourselves, you give attention only to yourselves and feel only for yourselves. Let the poor suffer want, the sick languish on straw, the widow burdened with children and pierced by their cries feel all their sufferings and answer their groans only with tears: since these are other people's evils and do not come near you, provided your sensuality is satisfied and your body has every convenience and comfort, you are content and scarcely consider whether others ought to be. But God considers it; and the time will come when he will know how to make you consider it against your will, when, to vindicate his providence, he demands an account of the poor from you; when he treats you as you treated the poor; when he judges you without mercy, as you rejected the poor without compassion. This, my dear hearers, is what you ought to examine and accuse yourselves about. Of all matters of conscience this is one of the most essential, and one concerning which the Lord's ministers ought to be more vigilant and severe, since God's honour and our neighbour's interest are at stake. Meanwhile, convinced of the precept of almsgiving, you wish to know what its subject matter must be; this I shall teach you in the second part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-h005", "label": "French original", "text": "II. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-h005", "label": "English", "text": "SECOND PART", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p016", "label": "French original", "text": "Etablir le pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne, & n'en pas d\u00e9terminer la mati\u00e9re, c'est dans le sentiment du docte Chancelier Gerson, troubler les ames foibles & scrupuleuses, & autoriser sans le pr\u00e9tendre les ames insensibles & dures. C'est, dis-je, troubler les ames foibles & scrupuleuses, en les jettant dans l'embarras d'une d\u00e9cision dont elles sont par elles-m\u00eames incapables; & c'est autoriser les ames insensibles & dures, en leur laissant de vains pr\u00e9textes pour \u00e9luder la loi de Dieu, & l'obligation qu'elle leur impose. C'est, ajo\u00fbtoit ce grand personnage, assigner aux pauvres une dette sur le riche, mais une dette sans fonds, une dette litigieuse, une dette dont le pauvre se verra immanquablement frustr\u00e9, & dont le riche croira toujours \u00eatre en droit de se d\u00e9fendre. Or il est important & n\u00e9cessaire d'obvier \u00e0 de tels inconv\u00e9niens; & voici ce que la Th\u00e9ologie me fournit de r\u00e9gles & de principes, pour en arr\u00eater les dangereuses cons\u00e9quences. Elle m'apprend que dans les n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s communes des pauvres, c'est le superflu des riches qui doit faire la mati\u00e9re de l'aum\u00f4ne. Voil\u00e0 d'abord ce qu'elle suppose; & en le supposant, elle se fonde sur les maximes les plus constantes de la raison & de la foi. Car elle s'attache \u00e0 la parole expresse de saint Paul, qui veut que dans le Christianisme l'abondance des uns soit le suppl\u00e9ment de l'indigence des autres; Vestra autem abundantia inopiam illorum suppleat. Or ce que l'Ap\u00f4tre appelle abondance, n'est rien autre chose que le superflu m\u00eame dont je parle. Elle s'en tient au consentement unanime des Peres, qui s'expliquant sur ce superflu, l'ont toujours regard\u00e9 comme un bien qui appartient aux pauvres, comme un bien dont les riches sont seulement les d\u00e9positaires & les distributeurs, comme un bien qu'ils ne peuvent retenir dans les n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s publiques sans commettre la plus criminelle injustice, & selon l'expression de S. Ambroise, sans se rendre coupables de vol. Car c'est ainsi que s'en d\u00e9clare ce saint Docteur, dont la morale d'ailleurs est des plus exactes & d'un caract\u00e9re moins outr\u00e9: Non enim majus crimen est habenti tollere, qu\u00e0m c\u00f9m abundas indigenti denegare. Oui, disoit ce Pere, vous devez \u00eatre persuad\u00e9 que ce n'est pas un moindre crime, de refuser au pauvre votre superflu, que de lui enlever son bien m\u00eame. Elle s'appuye sur le raisonnement de saint Thomas, tir\u00e9 de la nature m\u00eame des choses, & de l'ordre primitif o\u00f9 Dieu les avoit cr\u00e9\u00e9es. Car dans la premi\u00e9re intention de Dieu, dit le Docteur Ang\u00e9lique, c'est-\u00e0-dire, avant que le p\u00e9ch\u00e9 e\u00fbt d\u00e9pouill\u00e9 l'homme de cette justice originelle qui tenoit dans une r\u00e9gle si parfaite ses affections & ses d\u00e9sirs, tous les biens de la terre \u00e9toient communs; & si Dieu dans la suite des tems en a ordonn\u00e9 le partage, ce n'est que pour corriger le d\u00e9sordre du p\u00e9ch\u00e9 & pour r\u00e9primer la cupidit\u00e9 de l'homme. Or ce partage, reprend saint Thomas, ne seroit pas l'ouvrage de Dieu, si le superflu des uns ne devoit \u00eatre communiqu\u00e9 aux autres."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p016", "label": "English", "text": "To establish the precept of almsgiving without determining its subject matter is, in the judgment of the learned Chancellor Gerson, to trouble weak and scrupulous souls and unintentionally encourage insensible and hard ones. It troubles weak and scrupulous souls by casting them into the perplexity of a decision they cannot make by themselves; and it encourages insensible and hard souls by leaving them empty pretexts to evade God's law and the obligation it imposes. It is, that great man added, to assign the poor a claim against the rich, but a claim without funds, a disputed claim, one of which the poor will inevitably be deprived and against which the rich will always think themselves entitled to defend themselves. It is important and necessary to prevent such difficulties; here are the rules and principles theology supplies me to arrest their dangerous consequences. It teaches me that, in the ordinary necessities of the poor, the surplus of the rich must be the subject matter of almsgiving. This is its first premise, founded on the most established maxims of reason and faith. It adheres to Saint Paul's express word that, in Christianity, some people's abundance should supply others' indigence: Vestra autem abundantia inopiam illorum suppleat, let your abundance supply their want. What the Apostle calls abundance is nothing other than the very surplus of which I speak. Theology holds to the unanimous consent of the Fathers, who, explaining this surplus, have always regarded it as goods belonging to the poor, of which the rich are merely custodians and distributors; goods they cannot retain in public necessities without committing the most sinful injustice and, in Saint Ambrose's expression, becoming guilty of theft. For this is the declaration of that holy Doctor, whose moral teaching is otherwise most exact and of a less extreme character: Non enim majus crimen est habenti tollere, quam cum abundas indigenti denegare: taking from one who possesses is no greater crime than refusing the needy when you have abundance. Yes, said this Father, you must be convinced that refusing the poor your surplus is no lesser crime than taking away their own goods. Theology relies on Saint Thomas's reasoning from the very nature of things and the original order in which God created them. For in God's first intention, says the Angelic Doctor\u2014that is, before sin stripped man of that original justice which kept his affections and desires under such perfect rule\u2014all earthly goods were common; and if God later ordained their division, it was only to correct sin's disorder and restrain human greed. Now this division, Saint Thomas continues, would not be God's work if some people's surplus were not to be shared with others.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p017", "label": "French original", "text": "Et en effet, Chr\u00e9tiens, \u00e0 le bien prendre; Dieu n'a rien fait de superflu dans le monde; & ce que nous appellons superflu, n'est point en soi ni absolument superflu: ou si vous voulez, ce qu'il est pour le riche, il ne l'est pas pour le pauvre. Pour le riche, c'est superflu; pour le pauvre, c'est n\u00e9cessaire. Myst\u00e9re de providence, & d'une providence infiniment sage: myst\u00e9re que le grand Ap\u00f4tre d\u00e9veloppoit aux Corinthiens, en leur faisant remarquer comment Dieu par-l\u00e0 avoit voulu r\u00e9tablir cette bienheureuse \u00e9galit\u00e9 de l'\u00e9tat d'innocence; Vestra autem abundantia illorum inopiam suppleat, ut fiat aequalitas, sicut scriptum est: qui multum, non abundavit; & qui modicum, non minoravit. Que votre abondance, ce sont toujours les paroles du Ma\u00eetre des Nations, que votre abondance suppl\u00e9e \u00e0 la disette de vos freres, afin que tout soit \u00e9gal, conform\u00e9ment \u00e0 ce qui est \u00e9crit de la manne, qui se partageoit de telle sorte parmi le peuple, que l'un n'en avoit ni plus ni moins que l'autre, soit qu'il en e\u00fbt beaucoup ou peu recueilli. Saint Thomas porte encore la chose plus loin; & il soutient qu'il est m\u00eame de l'avantage du riche, que Dieu l'ait ainsi ordonn\u00e9: pourquoi? parce que si le riche avoit du superflu, dont il ne f\u00fbt ni comptable, ni redevable aux pauvres, ce superflu non-seulement ne seroit plus un don de Dieu, mais une mal\u00e9diction, puisque ce seroit un des plus grands obstacles du salut. Car il est vrai que rien n'est, ni ne doit \u00eatre plus dangereux pour le salut, que la superfluit\u00e9 du bien, sur-tout d'un bien abandonn\u00e9 \u00e0 la discr\u00e9tion & au gr\u00e9 de l'amour-propre, avec un pouvoir sans r\u00e9serve d'en disposer. Il a donc \u00e9t\u00e9 de la mis\u00e9ricorde & de la providence de Dieu sur les riches, de leur \u00f4ter un pouvoir dont infailliblement ils abuseroient, & de ne leur donner le superflu que pour en faire part aux pauvres. Tels sont les principes des Th\u00e9ologiens. Mais quoiqu'il en soit, Chr\u00e9tiens, de toutes ces r\u00e9flexions, on convient, & c'est un sentiment universel, que le superflu est la mati\u00e9re de l'aum\u00f4ne, & que vous \u00eates indispensablement oblig\u00e9s de l'employer selon que les n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s des pauvres le demandent. Or ces n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s, poursuivent les Docteurs, ne manqueront jamais dans le monde; & il y en aura toujours assez pour \u00e9puiser tout ce superflu, quand les riches touch\u00e9s de leur devoir y satisferont avec une enti\u00e9re fid\u00e9lit\u00e9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p017", "label": "English", "text": "Indeed, Christians, properly understood, God has made nothing superfluous in the world; what we call surplus is not in itself or absolutely superfluous. Or, if you wish, what it is for the rich it is not for the poor. For the rich it is surplus; for the poor it is necessary. This is a mystery of providence, an infinitely wise providence: a mystery the great Apostle unfolded to the Corinthians, showing how God wished thereby to restore that blessed equality of the state of innocence: Vestra autem abundantia illorum inopiam suppleat, ut fiat aequalitas, sicut scriptum est: qui multum, non abundavit; et qui modicum, non minoravit. Let your abundance\u2014these are still the Teacher of the Nations' words\u2014supply your brothers' want, so that all may be equal, in accordance with what is written of the manna, which was distributed among the people so that one had neither more nor less than another, whether he had gathered much or little. Saint Thomas carries the matter further still, maintaining that God's ordering it thus is even to the rich man's advantage. Why? Because if the rich man had surplus for which he were neither accountable nor indebted to the poor, this surplus would cease to be God's gift and become a curse, since it would be one of the greatest obstacles to salvation. For nothing is, or ought to be, more dangerous to salvation than superabundant wealth, especially wealth left to the discretion and pleasure of self-love with unrestricted power to dispose of it. It was therefore God's mercy and providence towards the rich to remove a power they would infallibly abuse, and give them surplus only to share it with the poor. Such are the theologians' principles. But whatever may be said of all these reflections, Christians, it is agreed, and universally held, that surplus is the subject matter of almsgiving, and that you are indispensably obliged to employ it as the needs of the poor demand. These needs, the Doctors continue, will never be lacking in the world; there will always be enough to exhaust all this surplus when the rich, moved by their duty, fulfil it with entire fidelity.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p018", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais qu'est-ce que ce superflu? Voil\u00e0 l'importante & l'essentielle question, qu'il s'agit maintenant de bien r\u00e9soudre. Si je consulte la th\u00e9ologie, que me r\u00e9pond-elle? que sous ce terme de superflu, elle comprend tout ce qui n'est point n\u00e9cessaire \u00e0 l'entretien honn\u00eate de la condition & de l'\u00e9tat, & c'est l\u00e0 qu'elle s'en tient. Mais c'est de-l\u00e0 m\u00eame que l'ambition, que le luxe, que la cupidit\u00e9, que la volupt\u00e9 empruntent des armes pour combattre le pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne. Car de cette d\u00e9finition du superflu, naissent les pr\u00e9textes, non-seulement pour secouer le joug & pour s'affranchir de la loi, mais pour la d\u00e9truire & pour l'an\u00e9antir; & si nous ne les renversons, ces faux pr\u00e9ceptes, c'est ne rien faire. Ecoutez donc ce qu'opposent les avares & les ambitieux du si\u00e9cle. Ils n'ont point, disent-ils, de superflu; & tout ce qu'ils ont, leur est n\u00e9cessaire pour subsister dans leur \u00e9tat, & selon leur \u00e9tat: mais voici ma r\u00e9ponse; & je dis qu'il faut examiner sur cela deux choses. En premier lieu, quel est cet \u00e9tat; & en second lieu, ce qui est n\u00e9cessaire dans cet \u00e9tat. Quel est cet \u00e9tat? est-ce un \u00e9tat Chr\u00e9tien, ou est-ce un \u00e9tat payen? est-ce un \u00e9tat r\u00e9el, ou est-ce un \u00e9tat imaginaire? est-ce un \u00e9tat born\u00e9, ou est-ce un \u00e9tat sans limites? est-ce un \u00e9tat dont Dieu soit l'auteur, ou est-ce un \u00e9tat que se soit fait une passion aveugle? car voil\u00e0 le n\u0153ud de toute la difficult\u00e9. Si c'est un \u00e9tat qui n'ait point de bornes, un \u00e9tat qui ne soit fond\u00e9 que sur les vastes id\u00e9es de votre orgueil, un \u00e9tat dont le Paganisme m\u00eame auroit condamn\u00e9 les abus, & dont le faste immod\u00e9r\u00e9 soit le scandale & la honte du Christianisme, ah! mon cher Auditeur, je con\u00e7ois alors comment il peut \u00eatre vrai que vous n'ayez point de superflu; comment il est possible que le n\u00e9cessaire m\u00eame vous manque. Car pour maintenir ces sortes d'\u00e9tats, \u00e0 peines des revenus immenses suffiroient-ils? & bien loin d'en avoir trop, on n'en a jamais assez. C'est, dis-je, ce que je comprends: mais ce que je ne comprends pas, c'est qu'\u00e9tant Chr\u00e9tien comme vous l'\u00eates, vous apportiez une telle excuse pour vous dispenser de l'aum\u00f4ne. En effet, si ces sortes d'\u00e9tats pr\u00e9tendus \u00e9toient autoris\u00e9s, & s'il \u00e9toit permis de les maintenir, que deviendroit donc le pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne? ou plut\u00f4t, que deviendroient les pauvres en faveur de qui Dieu l'a port\u00e9? o\u00f9 trouveroit-on pour leur entretien du superflu dans le monde, & faudroit-il que Dieu sans cesse f\u00eet des miracles pour y pourvoir?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p018", "label": "English", "text": "But what is this surplus? That is the important and essential question we must now resolve correctly. If I consult theology, what does it answer? That under the term surplus it includes everything not necessary for the decent maintenance of one's condition and station, and there it stops. Yet from this very point ambition, luxury, greed, and sensual pleasure borrow weapons against the precept of almsgiving. From this definition of surplus arise pretexts not only to shake off the yoke and free oneself from the law, but to destroy and annihilate it; and unless we overthrow these false precepts, we accomplish nothing. Hear, then, the objections of the world's misers and ambitious people. They have no surplus, they say; everything they possess is necessary to subsist in and according to their station. Here is my answer: two things must be examined. First, what is this station? Second, what is necessary in it? What is this station? Is it Christian or pagan? Real or imaginary? Bounded or limitless? A station of which God is the author, or one fashioned by blind passion? There lies the knot of the entire difficulty. If it is limitless, founded only on the vast notions of your pride, a station whose abuses even paganism would have condemned, and whose immoderate pomp is Christianity's scandal and shame\u2014ah, my dear hearer, then I understand how it may be true that you have no surplus, how even necessities may be lacking. For immense revenues would scarcely suffice to maintain such stations; far from having too much, one never has enough. This I understand; what I do not understand is that, being a Christian, you should offer such an excuse to dispense yourself from almsgiving. Indeed, if these supposed stations were sanctioned and maintaining them permitted, what would become of the precept of almsgiving? Or rather, what would become of the poor for whose benefit God enacted it? Where in the world would surplus be found for their support, and would God have to work continual miracles to provide it?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p019", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais n'entrons point, je le veux, Chr\u00e9tiens, dans la discussion de vos \u00e9tats. Supposons-les tels que vous les imaginez, tels que votre pr\u00e9somption vous les fait envisager: voyons seulement ce qu'il y a dans ces \u00e9tats, ou de n\u00e9cessaire pour vous, ou de superflu. Or j'appelle au moins superflu, ce qui vous est, je ne dis pas pr\u00e9cis\u00e9ment inutile, mais m\u00eame \u00e9videmment pr\u00e9judiciable. Car pour ne rien exag\u00e9rer, je ne prends de ces \u00e9tats que ce qui sert \u00e0 en fomenter les d\u00e9r\u00e9glemens, les exc\u00e8s, les crimes; & cela me suffit pour y trouver du superflu. J'appelle superflu ce que vous donnez tous les jours \u00e0 vos d\u00e9bauches, \u00e0 vos plaisirs honteux: renoncez \u00e0 cette idole dont vous \u00eates adorateur, & vous aurez du superflu. J'appelle superflu, Femme mondaine, ce que vous d\u00e9pensez, disons mieux, ce que vous prodiguez en mille ajustemens frivoles, qui entretiennent votre luxe, & qui seront peut-\u00eatre un jour le sujet de votre r\u00e9probation: retranchez une partie de ces vanit\u00e9s, & vous aurez du superflu. J'appelle superflu ce que vous ne craignez pas de risquer \u00e0 un jeu qui ne vous divertit plus, mais qui vous attache, mais qui vous passionne, mais qui vous d\u00e9r\u00e9gle, mais sur-tout qui vous ruine & qui vous damne: sacrifiez ce jeu, & vous aurez du superflu. Quoi donc? vous avez de quoi fournir \u00e0 vos passions, & \u00e0 vos passions les plus d\u00e9r\u00e9gl\u00e9es tout ce qu'elles demandent, & vous pr\u00e9tendez ne point avoir de superflu? vous avez du superflu pour tout ce qu'il vous pla\u00eet, & vous n'en avez point pour les pauvres? Voil\u00e0 ce que le devoir de mon minist\u00e8re m'oblige \u00e0 vous repr\u00e9senter, & ce que je vous conjure de vouloir bien vous repr\u00e9senter \u00e0 vous-m\u00eames."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p019", "label": "English", "text": "But let us, if you wish, Christians, refrain from examining your stations. Let us suppose them as you imagine them, as your presumption makes you view them; let us see only what in them is necessary for you or surplus. Now I call at least surplus what is, I do not merely say useless to you, but plainly harmful. To exaggerate nothing, I take from these stations only what fosters their disorders, excesses, and crimes; that is enough for me to find surplus. I call surplus what you give daily to debauchery and shameful pleasures: renounce the idol you worship, and you will have surplus. I call surplus, worldly woman, what you spend\u2014or rather squander\u2014on a thousand frivolous adornments that sustain your luxury and may one day cause your damnation: cut off some of these vanities, and you will have surplus. I call surplus what you fearlessly risk in gambling which no longer diverts you, but binds you, inflames your passions, disorders you, and above all ruins and damns you: sacrifice this gambling, and you will have surplus. What then? You have the means to supply your passions, even the most disordered, with all they demand, yet claim to have no surplus? You have surplus for whatever pleases you, and none for the poor? This is what the duty of my ministry obliges me to put before you, and what I implore you to put before yourselves.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p020", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais ne puis-je pas me servir de ce superflu, pour m'aggrandir & pour accro\u00eetre ma fortune? Ah! Chr\u00e9tiens, voici l'\u00e9cueil & la pierre de scandale pour tous les riches du si\u00e9cle: ce d\u00e9sir de s'aggrandir, de s'\u00e9lever, de parvenir \u00e0 tout, sans jamais borner ses v\u00fbes, & sans jamais dire, c'est assez. Mais enfin ce d\u00e9sir est-il criminel? car il faut parler exactement & dans la rigueur de l'\u00e9cole. H\u00e9 bien, j'y consens, parlons dans la rigueur de l'\u00e9cole; elle me sera avantageuse, & je ne crains point qu'elle affoiblisse la v\u00e9rit\u00e9 que je vous pr\u00eache. Je ne dis rien de ceux qui rev\u00eatus des b\u00e9n\u00e9fices & des dignit\u00e9s de l'Eglise, voudroient employer le superflu des revenus eccl\u00e9siastiques \u00e0 se faire une fortune & \u00e0 se distinguer dans le monde; ils s\u00e7avent mieux que moi quels anath\u00eames l'Eglise a fulmin\u00e9s contre ce d\u00e9sordre; ils s\u00e7avent que le rel\u00e2chement de la morale n'a point encore \u00e9t\u00e9 jusqu'\u00e0 favoriser l\u00e0-dessus en aucune sorte leur ambition & leur convoitise; ils s\u00e7avent avec quelle s\u00e9v\u00e9rit\u00e9 les Th\u00e9ologiens les moins \u00e9troits & les plus indulgens ont raisonn\u00e9 sur l'emploi de ce superflu, qui m\u00eame ind\u00e9pendamment des pauvres, n'appartient point aux riches b\u00e9n\u00e9ficiers; & ils n'ignorent pas que tout usage prophane qu'ils en font, est, de l'aveu de tous les Docteurs & incontestablement, un sacril\u00e9ge. Que si vous me demandiez \u00e0 quoi leur sert donc cette multiplicit\u00e9 de b\u00e9n\u00e9fices, qu'ils recherchent avec tant d'ardeur, & qu'ils poursuivent avec tant d'empressement, puisqu'elle ne fait qu'augmenter le poids de leurs obligations, sans leur pouvoir \u00eatre de nul avantage par rapport \u00e0 ces fins humaines d'accroissement & d'\u00e9l\u00e9vation; c'est sur quoi je n'aurois garde ici de m'\u00e9tendre, & j'aimerois mieux m'en rapporter \u00e0 leurs consciences, que de faire une censure de leur conduite, dont vous seriez peu \u00e9difi\u00e9s, & dont peut-\u00eatre ils seroient encore moins touch\u00e9s. Ainsi revenons au point & \u00e0 la question g\u00e9n\u00e9rale."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p020", "label": "English", "text": "But may I not use this surplus to advance myself and increase my fortune? Ah, Christians, here is the reef and stumbling-block for all the world's rich: the desire to advance, rise, and attain everything, never setting bounds to one's aims or saying, It is enough. But after all, is this desire sinful? For we must speak exactly and with the precision of the theological schools. Very well, I agree: let us speak with that precision; it will favour my case, and I do not fear that it will weaken the truth I preach. I say nothing of those invested with the Church's benefices and dignities who would use the surplus of ecclesiastical revenues to build a fortune and distinguish themselves in the world. They know better than I what anathemas the Church has hurled against this disorder; they know that relaxation in moral teaching has not yet gone so far as to favour their ambition and covetousness in any way here; they know with what severity even the least restrictive and most indulgent theologians have reasoned about the use of this surplus, which, even apart from the poor, does not belong to wealthy benefice-holders. They are not ignorant that every profane use they make of it is, by all the Doctors' admission and incontestably, a sacrilege. If you asked me, then, what use they have for this multiplicity of benefices, sought so ardently and pursued so eagerly, since it only increases the weight of their obligations without benefiting those human aims of advancement and elevation, I should take care not to dwell on it here. I would rather leave it to their consciences than censure their conduct, by which you would be little edified and they perhaps still less moved. Let us therefore return to the point and the general question.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p021", "label": "French original", "text": "Est-ce un d\u00e9sir injuste & criminel que de vouloir aggrandir son \u00e9tat? Non, Chr\u00e9tiens, il ne l'est pas toujours; ou si vous voulez, il ne l'est pas en soi. Mais prenez bien garde aux conditions requises, afin qu'il ne le soit pas; & voyez si de tous les d\u00e9sirs que l'on peut former, il y en a un plus dangereux & commun\u00e9ment plus pernicieux. Je veux qu'il vous soit permis d'aggrandir votre \u00e9tat, mais comment? selon les loix de votre religion. Par exemple, qu'il vous soit permis d'acheter cette charge, si vous avez le m\u00e9rite n\u00e9cessaire pour l'exercer, si vous \u00eates capable d'y glorifier Dieu, si c'est pour l'utilit\u00e9 publique: car pourquoi vous \u00e9leverez-vous aux d\u00e9pens du public & de Dieu m\u00eame? Or combien de riches n\u00e9anmoins voyons-nous tous les jours ainsi s'\u00e9lever? Il \u00e9toit de l'int\u00e9r\u00eat de Dieu, que cet homme qui n'a ni conscience, ni probit\u00e9, n'e\u00fbt jamais le pouvoir & l'autorit\u00e9 entre les mains; & toutefois parce qu'il \u00e9toit riche, il a s\u00e7\u00fb monter aux premiers rangs & parvenir \u00e0 tout. L'ignorance & l'incapacit\u00e9 de celui-ci devoit l'exclurre de toutes affaires & de toute administration; mais parce qu'il \u00e9toit opulent, sa pr\u00e9somption l'a port\u00e9 \u00e0 vouloir \u00eatre assis sur les tribunaux de la justice pour d\u00e9cider & pour juger. Cependant, si l'un & l'autre ne se f\u00fbt point mis en t\u00eate d'aggrandir son \u00e9tat, ils auroient eu l'un & l'autre du superflu; & c'est de ce superflu qu'ils auroient accompli le pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne. Mais cette morale nous conduit trop loin."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p021", "label": "English", "text": "Is it an unjust and sinful desire to wish to advance one's station? No, Christians, not always; or, if you wish, not in itself. But attend carefully to the conditions required for it not to be so, and see whether any desire one can form is more dangerous and commonly more pernicious. I grant that you may advance your station, but how? According to your religion's laws. For example, you may purchase that office if you have the necessary merit to exercise it, if you can glorify God in it, if it serves the public good. For why should you rise at the expense of the public and God himself? Yet how many rich people do we see rising thus every day? God's interest required that this man, without conscience or integrity, should never hold power and authority; nevertheless, because he was rich, he knew how to climb to the highest ranks and attain everything. That one's ignorance and incapacity should have excluded him from all affairs and administration; but because he was opulent, his presumption led him to wish to sit in courts of justice to decide and judge. Yet had neither conceived the idea of advancing his station, each would have had surplus, and from it would have fulfilled the precept of almsgiving. But this moral discussion carries us too far.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p022", "label": "French original", "text": "Je veux, Chr\u00e9tiens, qu'il vous soit permis d'aggrandir votre \u00e9tat, pourv\u00fb que vous vous conteniez dans les termes d'une modestie raisonnable & sage, & que ce d\u00e9sir n'aille pas jusqu'\u00e0 l'infini: pourquoi? non-seulement parce qu'il n'est rien de plus oppos\u00e9 \u00e0 l'esprit du Christianisme que de vouloir toujours s'\u00e9lever, & que cela seul, dit S. Bernard, est un crime devant Dieu; mais parce qu'il s'ensuivroit de-l\u00e0, que le commandement de l'aum\u00f4ne ne seroit plus qu'un commandement chim\u00e9rique & en sp\u00e9culation. Car il est \u00e9vident que les riches ayant droit alors, comme ils l'auroient, d'\u00e9pargner tout, de m\u00e9nager tout, de retenir tout, il n'y auroit plus de superflu dans le monde; & qu'ainsi le pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne ne seroit plus que l'ombre d'une ancienne loi, qui obligeoit nos Peres tandis que la simplicit\u00e9 du si\u00e9cle bornoit leurs v\u00fbes & les fixoit \u00e0 un \u00e9tat; mais qui dans la suite auroit perdu toute sa force, depuis que la science du monde nous a inspir\u00e9 de plus hautes id\u00e9es, & appris \u00e0 b\u00e2tir de grandes fortunes. Or dites-moi, mes chers Auditeurs, si cette cons\u00e9quence est soutenable?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p022", "label": "English", "text": "I grant, Christians, that you may advance your station, provided you remain within the bounds of reasonable and wise modesty and this desire does not extend to infinity. Why? Not only because nothing is more opposed to Christianity's spirit than always wishing to rise, and because that alone, says Saint Bernard, is a sin before God; but because it would follow that the commandment of almsgiving became merely imaginary and theoretical. For clearly, if the rich then had\u2014as they would have\u2014the right to save everything, economize everything, retain everything, there would be no surplus left in the world. The precept of almsgiving would thus be only the shadow of an ancient law which bound our fathers while the age's simplicity limited their aims and fixed them in a station, but which afterwards lost all its force, once worldly knowledge inspired higher ideas in us and taught us to build great fortunes. Tell me, my dear hearers, whether this consequence is defensible.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p023", "label": "French original", "text": "Je veux qu'il vous soit permis d'aggrandir votre \u00e9tat, pourv\u00fb qu'en m\u00eame-tems vos aum\u00f4nes grossissent \u00e0 proportion, & que vous posiez pour principe qu'elles sont une partie & une partie essentielle de votre \u00e9tat. Mais ce que je veux sur-tout, retenez bien cette maxime, c'est qu'il ne vous soit point permis d'aggrandir votre \u00e9tat, qu'apr\u00e8s que vous aurez pourv\u00fb aux n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s des pauvres, & qu'autant que les n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s des pauvres pourront s'accorder avec cette nouvelle grandeur. Est-il rien de plus juste? Quoi, mon Frere, vous travaillerez par de continuelles & de longues \u00e9pargnes \u00e0 vous \u00e9tablir & \u00e0 vous pousser dans le monde, pendant que les pauvres souffriront? Au lieu de les soulager, vous n'aurez point d'autre soin que d'amasser & d'acqu\u00e9rir; & vous insulterez, pour ainsi parler, \u00e0 leur mis\u00e9re, en leur faisant voir dans votre \u00e9l\u00e9vation l'\u00e9clat & la pompe qui vous environne? Non, mon Dieu, direz vous si vous \u00eates Chr\u00e9tien, il n'en ira pas de m\u00eame. Je s\u00e7ais trop \u00e0 quoi m'engage la charit\u00e9 que je dois \u00e0 mon prochain. Il n'est pas n\u00e9cessaire que je sois plus riche, ni plus grand; mais il est n\u00e9cessaire que vos pauvres subsistent. Mon premier devoir sera donc de les secourir; & tandis que je les verrai dans l'indigence, je ne regarderai le superflu de mes biens que comme un d\u00e9p\u00f4t que vous m'avez confi\u00e9 pour eux. Voil\u00e0 comment vous parlerez: & si la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 des pauvres devenoit extr\u00eame, non-seulement vous y employeriez le superflu, mais le n\u00e9cessaire m\u00eame de votre \u00e9tat: pourquoi? parce que vous devez aimer votre prochain pr\u00e9f\u00e9rablement \u00e0 votre \u00e9tat; & s'il faut rabbattre quelque chose de votre \u00e9tat, pour conserver votre frere, c'est \u00e0 quoi vous devez consentir & vous soumettre, afin que votre frere ne p\u00e9risse pas. Ainsi l'enseigne toute l'\u00e9cole."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p023", "label": "English", "text": "I grant that you may advance your station, provided your alms increase proportionately at the same time, and you establish as a principle that they are a part, an essential part, of your station. But above all\u2014remember this maxim well\u2014I require that you not be permitted to advance your station until you have provided for the necessities of the poor, and only insofar as those necessities can be reconciled with this new greatness. Could anything be more just? What, my brother, will you labour through continual, lengthy economies to establish and push yourself forward in the world while the poor suffer? Instead of relieving them, will you care only to amass and acquire, and, so to speak, insult their misery by displaying the splendour and pomp surrounding your elevation? No, my God, you will say if you are a Christian, it shall not be so. I know too well what the charity I owe my neighbour binds me to. It is not necessary for me to be richer or greater, but it is necessary that your poor subsist. My first duty shall therefore be to assist them; while I see them in indigence, I shall regard my surplus goods only as a deposit you entrusted to me for them. This is how you will speak; and if the poor's necessity became extreme, you would employ not merely your surplus but even what is necessary to your station. Why? Because you must love your neighbour in preference to your station; and if something of your station must be reduced to preserve your brother, you must consent and submit to this so that your brother does not perish. All the theological schools teach thus.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p024", "label": "French original", "text": "Et quand je dis n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 extr\u00eame du prochain, je n'entens pas seulement n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 extr\u00eame par rapport \u00e0 la vie: j'entens n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 extr\u00eame par rapport aux biens, \u00e0 l'honneur, \u00e0 la libert\u00e9. Je m'explique. Vous s\u00e7avez que ce malheureux doit languir des ann\u00e9es enti\u00e9res dans une prison, si l'on ne contribue \u00e0 sa d\u00e9livrance; vous s\u00e7avez que cette jeune personne va se perdre, si l'on ne s'empresse de l'aider: c'est du n\u00e9cessaire m\u00eame de votre \u00e9tat que leur doit venir ce secours; par quelle raison? parce que ce sont l\u00e0 des n\u00e9cessit\u00e9s extr\u00eames. Telle est ma pens\u00e9e; & ce que je pense n'est point ce qui s'appelle morale s\u00e9v\u00e9re, puisque c'est la morale m\u00eame de ceux qu'on a le plus soup\u00e7onn\u00e9s & accus\u00e9s de rel\u00e2chement."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p024", "label": "English", "text": "When I say our neighbour's extreme necessity, I do not mean extreme necessity only in relation to life; I mean extreme necessity in relation to goods, honour, and liberty. Let me explain. You know that this unfortunate man must languish for whole years in prison unless someone contributes to his release; you know that this young person will be lost unless someone hastens to help: this assistance must come even from what is necessary to your station. Why? Because these are extreme necessities. Such is my thought; and it is not what is called severe moral teaching, since it is the very teaching of those most suspected and accused of laxity.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p025", "label": "French original", "text": "Ah! Chr\u00e9tiens, qu'il y a de v\u00e9rit\u00e9s, dont on n'est pas encore persuad\u00e9 dans le Christianisme! Je vois bien, reprend S. Augustin dans ses commentaires sur le pseaume trente-huiti\u00e9me; (& j'avoue, mes Freres, que voici le seul pr\u00e9texte qui seroit capable de m'arr\u00eater, & que j'aurois peine \u00e0 combattre, si ce saint Docteur ne l'avoit lui-m\u00eame d\u00e9truit) je vois ce que vous m'allez opposer. Vous dites que vous avez une famille & des enfans \u00e0 pourvoir: d'o\u00f9 vous concluez, que vous pouvez donc garder votre superflu; Video quid dicturus es; filiis servio. Mais je vous r\u00e9ponds, ajo\u00fbte ce Pere, que sous une apparence de pi\u00e9t\u00e9, cette parole n'est qu'une vaine excuse de votre iniquit\u00e9: Sed haec vox pietatis, excusatio est iniquitatis. Non, Chr\u00e9tiens, ce pr\u00e9texte, tout sp\u00e9cieux qu'il est, ne vous justifiera jamais devant Dieu. Soit que vous ayez des enfans \u00e0 \u00e9tablir ou non, du moment que vous avez du superflu, vous le devez aux pauvres, selon les r\u00e9gles de la charit\u00e9: car ces r\u00e9gles sont faites pour vous, & elles n'ont rien d'incompatible avec vos autres devoirs. Vous devez pourvoir vos enfans; mais vous ne devez pas oublier les membres de Jesus-Christ. Si Dieu vous avoit charg\u00e9 d'une plus nombreuse famille, vous s\u00e7auriez bien partager vos soins paternels entre tous les sujets dont elle seroit compos\u00e9e. Or regardez ce pauvre comme un enfant de surcro\u00eet dans votre maison. Excellente pratique, d'adopter les pauvres qui repr\u00e9sentent Jesus-Christ, & de les mettre au nombre de vos enfans!"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p025", "label": "English", "text": "Ah, Christians, how many truths there are of which people are still unconvinced within Christianity! I see well, Saint Augustine remarks in his commentary on the thirty-eighth psalm\u2014and I admit, my brothers, that this is the one pretext capable of stopping me, which I should find hard to combat had that holy Doctor not himself destroyed it\u2014I see what you are about to object. You say that you have a family and children to provide for, and conclude that you may therefore keep your surplus: Video quid dicturus es; filiis servio: I see what you will say; I serve my children. But I answer, this Father adds, that under an appearance of piety these words are only an empty excuse for your iniquity: Sed haec vox pietatis, excusatio est iniquitatis: this voice of piety is an excuse for iniquity. No, Christians, plausible as it is, this pretext will never justify you before God. Whether or not you have children to establish, as soon as you have surplus you owe it to the poor according to charity's rules. These rules are made for you and are not incompatible with your other duties. You must provide for your children, but must not forget Christ's members. If God had charged you with a larger family, you would know well how to divide your paternal care among all its members. Regard this poor man, then, as an additional child in your household. What an excellent practice, to adopt the poor who represent Jesus Christ and number them among your children!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p026", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais enfin, ajo\u00fbtez-vous, les tems sont mauvais, chacun souffre; & n'est-il pas alors de la prudence, de penser \u00e0 l'avenir, & de garder son revenu? C'est ce que la prudence vous dicte, mais une prudence r\u00e9prouv\u00e9e, une prudence charnelle & ennemie de Dieu. Tout le monde souffre & est incommod\u00e9, j'en conviens: mais apr\u00e8s tout si j'en jugeois par les apparences, peut-\u00eatre aurois-je peine \u00e0 en convenir: car jamais le faste, jamais le luxe fut-il plus grand qu'il l'est aujourd'hui? & qui s\u00e7ait si ce n'est point pour cela que Dieu nous ch\u00e2tie, Dieu, dis-je, qui selon l'Ecriture a en horreur le pauvre superbe? Mais encore une fois, je le veux, les tems sont mauvais, & que concluez-vous de-l\u00e0? si tout le monde souffre, les pauvres ne souffrent-ils point? & si les souffrances des pauvres se trouvent jusques chez les riches, \u00e0 quoi doivent \u00eatre r\u00e9duits les pauvres m\u00eames? Or \u00e0 qui est-ce d'assister ceux qui souffrent plus, si ce n'est pas \u00e0 ceux qui souffrent moins? Est-ce donc bien raisonner de dire que vous avez droit de retenir votre superflu, parce que les tems sont mauvais, puisque c'est justement pour cela m\u00eame que vous ne le pouvez retenir sans crime, & que vous \u00eates dans une obligation particuli\u00e9re de le donner?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p026", "label": "English", "text": "But after all, you add, times are bad, everyone suffers; is it not prudent then to think of the future and keep one's income? That is what prudence dictates to you, but a condemned prudence, a carnal prudence hostile to God. Everyone suffers and is inconvenienced, I grant it; yet if I judged by appearances, perhaps I should find it hard to agree. For was pomp or luxury ever greater than today? And who knows whether this is not precisely why God chastises us\u2014God, I say, who according to Scripture abhors the proud poor man? But once again I grant that times are bad: what do you conclude? If everyone suffers, do the poor not suffer? If the poor's sufferings reach even into rich households, to what must the poor themselves be reduced? Who should assist those who suffer more, if not those who suffer less? Is it sound reasoning, then, to say you have a right to retain your surplus because times are bad, when it is precisely for this reason that you cannot retain it without sin and have a particular obligation to give it?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p027", "label": "French original", "text": "Cette morale vous \u00e9tonne, & vous paro\u00eet n'aller \u00e0 rien moins qu'\u00e0 la damnation de tous les riches. Il me suffit de vous r\u00e9pondre avec le Chancelier Gerson, que ce n'est point cette morale qui damne les riches, mais que ce sont les riches qui se damnent, pour ne vouloir pas suivre cette morale. Aussi le Fils de Dieu n'attribue point la r\u00e9probation du mauvais riche de l'Evangile \u00e0 une autre cause. De conclurre, que tous les riches sont damn\u00e9s, c'est mal penser de son prochain; c'est vouloir entrer dans les conseils de Dieu, & juger des autres avec t\u00e9m\u00e9rit\u00e9 & avec malignit\u00e9. Faisons notre devoir, mes Freres, dit S. Augustin; & il ne nous arrivera jamais de tirer de pareilles cons\u00e9quences. Quand nous serons charitables & mis\u00e9ricordieux, nous trouverons qu'il y en a d'autres qui le sont aussi-bien que nous, & qui le sont plus que nous. Quoi qu'il en soit, mon cher Auditeur, n'abusez point du superflu de vos biens; & puisque Dieu vous le demande pour servir \u00e0 votre salut, ne le faites pas servir \u00e0 votre perte \u00e9ternelle. Souvenez-vous qu'il le faudra laisser un jour, ce superflu; & qu'apr\u00e8s vous \u00eatre rendu odieux dans le monde en le r\u00e9servant, apr\u00e8s vous \u00eatre attir\u00e9 la haine de Dieu, vous le quitterez \u00e0 la mort: au lieu qu'en le consacrant \u00e0 la charit\u00e9, vous le m\u00e9nagez pour le ciel. Souvenez-vous que rien m\u00eame n'engagera plus Dieu \u00e0 verser sur vous ses b\u00e9n\u00e9dictions temporelles, qu'un saint usage de vos biens en faveur des pauvres. La parole de Jesus-Christ y est expresse: Donnez, & vous recevrez. Achevons. Pr\u00e9cepte de l'aum\u00f4ne, mati\u00e9re de l'aum\u00f4ne, c'est de quoi je vous ai parl\u00e9. En voici l'ordre, & c'est le sujet de la derni\u00e9re partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p027", "label": "English", "text": "This moral teaching astonishes you and seems to tend to nothing less than the damnation of all the rich. It suffices to answer with Chancellor Gerson that it is not this teaching that damns the rich, but the rich who damn themselves by refusing to follow it. The Son of God likewise assigns no other cause for the damnation of the bad rich man in the Gospel. To conclude that all the rich are damned is to think ill of one's neighbour, to wish to enter God's counsels, and to judge others rashly and maliciously. Let us do our duty, my brothers, says Saint Augustine, and we shall never draw such conclusions. When we are charitable and merciful, we shall find others who are so as well as we are, and more than we are. Whatever the case, my dear hearer, do not misuse your surplus goods; since God asks them of you to serve your salvation, do not make them serve your eternal ruin. Remember that one day you must leave this surplus, and that, after making yourself odious in the world by reserving it and drawing God's hatred upon yourself, you will abandon it at death; whereas by consecrating it to charity you store it up for heaven. Remember too that nothing will more induce God to pour his temporal blessings upon you than a holy use of your goods for the poor. Christ's word is express: Give, and you will receive. Let us conclude. I have spoken of the precept and subject matter of almsgiving. Here is its proper order, the subject of the last part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-h006", "label": "French original", "text": "III. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-h006", "label": "English", "text": "THIRD PART", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p028", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est l'ordre qui donne la perfection aux choses; & quand le Saint-Esprit dans l'Ecriture veut nous faire entendre que Dieu a tout fait en Dieu, il se contente de nous dire qu'il a tout fait avec ordre & avec mesure. La charit\u00e9 m\u00eame, dit S. Thomas, cette Reine des vertus, cesseroit d'\u00eatre vertu, si l'ordre y manquoit. Aussi l'Epouse des Cantiques comptoit parmi les graces les plus singuli\u00e9res qu'elle e\u00fbt re\u00e7ues de son Epoux, celle d'avoir ordonn\u00e9 la charit\u00e9 dans son c\u0153ur: Ordinavit in me charitatem. Mais quoi? demande S. Augustin: la charit\u00e9 a-t-elle besoin d'\u00eatre ordonn\u00e9e; & n'est-ce pas elle qui met l'ordre par-tout, ou n'est-elle pas elle-m\u00eame l'ordre & la r\u00e9gle de tout? Oui, mes Freres, r\u00e9pond ce saint Docteur; la charit\u00e9, la vraie charit\u00e9 est ordonn\u00e9e dans elle-m\u00eame, & ne doit point chercher l'ordre hors d'elle-m\u00eame: mais il y a une fausse charit\u00e9, & un de ses caract\u00e8res est d'\u00eatre d\u00e9r\u00e9gl\u00e9e & sans ordre. De-l\u00e0 vient, continue ce Pere, que l'Epouse, figure de l'ame chr\u00e9tienne, se tient redevable \u00e0 Dieu de deux grandes graces; l'une de lui avoir donn\u00e9 la charit\u00e9, & l'autre d'avoir \u00e9tabli dans elle l'ordre de la charit\u00e9: Ordinavit in me charitatem. C'est l'explication que fait S. Augustin de ces paroles. Or ce qu'il dit de la charit\u00e9 en g\u00e9n\u00e9ral, se doit dire en particulier de l'aum\u00f4ne, puisque l'aum\u00f4ne est essentiellement une partie de la charit\u00e9. Il faut donc de l'ordre dans l'aum\u00f4ne: & cet ordre, selon les Th\u00e9ologiens, doit \u00eatre observ\u00e9, premi\u00e9rement par rapport aux pauvres \u00e0 qui l'aum\u00f4ne est d\u00fbe; secondement, par rapport aux riches \u00e0 qui l'aum\u00f4ne est command\u00e9e: voil\u00e0 une instruction dont il ne nous s'il vous pla\u00eet, rien perdre."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p028", "label": "English", "text": "Order gives things their perfection; and when the Holy Spirit in Scripture wishes us to understand that God has done everything as God, he is content to tell us that he has done everything with order and measure. Even charity, Saint Thomas says, that queen of virtues, would cease to be a virtue if order were lacking in it. Thus the Bride of the Canticles counted among the most singular graces received from her Bridegroom that of having charity ordered in her heart: Ordinavit in me charitatem, he has ordered charity within me. But what, Saint Augustine asks, does charity need to be ordered? Does it not put order everywhere, or is it not itself the order and rule of everything? Yes, my brothers, answers this holy Doctor, charity, true charity, is ordered within itself and need not seek order outside itself. But there is a false charity, and one of its characteristics is to be disordered and without order. Hence, this Father continues, the Bride, a figure of the Christian soul, considers herself indebted to God for two great graces: one, that he gave her charity; the other, that he established charity's order within her: Ordinavit in me charitatem. Such is Saint Augustine's explanation of these words. What he says of charity generally must be said of almsgiving particularly, since almsgiving is essentially a part of charity. There must therefore be order in almsgiving; according to the theologians, this must be observed first in relation to the poor to whom alms are owed, and second in relation to the rich to whom almsgiving is commanded. Please lose nothing of this instruction.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p029", "label": "French original", "text": "Je dis que par rapport aux pauvres \u00e0 qui l'aum\u00f4ne est d\u00fbe, il y a un ordre \u00e0 garder; & cet ordre quel est-il? c'est que l'aum\u00f4ne, du moins dans la pr\u00e9paration du c\u0153ur, ou pour parler plus intelligiblement, c'est que la volont\u00e9 de faire l'aum\u00f4ne, doit \u00eatre g\u00e9n\u00e9rale & universelle: c'est-\u00e0-dire, qu'elle doit s'\u00e9tendre \u00e0 tous les pauvres de Jesus-Christ, sans en exclurre un seul; car d\u00e8s que vous en excepterez un seul, vous n'aurez plus le v\u00e9ritable esprit de la charit\u00e9. Il faut, dit S. Chrysostome, que cette vertu ramasse dans notre c\u0153ur tout ce qu'il y a au monde de n\u00e9cessiteux & de mis\u00e9rables, comme ils sont tous ramass\u00e9s dans le c\u0153ur de Dieu. C'est l\u00e0, pour m'exprimer de la sorte, c'est dans les entrailles de la charit\u00e9 de Dieu, que S. Paul trouvoit tous les hommes r\u00e9unis, & que tous les hommes nous doivent paro\u00eetre \u00e9galement dignes de nos soins: Cupio vos omnes in visceribus Christi Jesu: En sorte que s'il se pouvoit faire que votre charit\u00e9 e\u00fbt une aussi grande \u00e9tendue que les mis\u00e9res du prochain; vous voudriez soulager par votre charit\u00e9 toutes les mis\u00e9res du monde, afin de pouvoir dire en parlant aux pauvres, ce que disoit le m\u00eame Ap\u00f4tre aux Corinthiens: Cor nostrum dilatatum est; non angustiamini in nobis: non, mes Freres, qui que vous soyez, mon c\u0153ur n'est point resserr\u00e9 pour vous; mais vous y avez tous place. Car voil\u00e0 le caract\u00e9re de la charit\u00e9 & de la mis\u00e9ricorde Chr\u00e9tienne."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p029", "label": "English", "text": "I say that in relation to the poor to whom alms are owed, an order must be observed. What is it? Almsgiving, at least in the heart's preparation\u2014or, to speak more intelligibly, the will to give alms\u2014must be general and universal. It must extend to all Christ's poor without excluding a single one; for as soon as you except even one, you will no longer have charity's true spirit. This virtue, Saint Chrysostom says, must gather into our hearts all the needy and miserable in the world, as they are all gathered into God's heart. There, to speak thus, in the depths of God's charity, Saint Paul found all men united; and all men must seem equally worthy of our care: Cupio vos omnes in visceribus Christi Jesu: I long for you all in the heart of Christ Jesus. Thus, if your charity could be as extensive as your neighbour's miseries, you would wish to relieve through it all the world's miseries, so as to be able to say to the poor what the same Apostle said to the Corinthians: Cor nostrum dilatatum est; non angustiamini in nobis: our heart is enlarged; you are not confined in us. No, my brothers, whoever you are, my heart is not closed to you; there is room for all of you. This is the character of Christian charity and mercy.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p030", "label": "French original", "text": "Que dis-je, de la mis\u00e9ricorde Chr\u00e9tienne? Dieu m\u00eame dans l'ancien Testament ne prescrivoit-il pas aux Juifs cette loi; & en leur ordonnant l'aum\u00f4ne, ne leur marquoit-il pas en particulier la personne de leur ennemi; Si esurierit inimicus tuus, ciba illum; si sitit, potum da illi: voulant par-l\u00e0 leur faire entendre que l'aum\u00f4ne ne devoit point \u00eatre born\u00e9e, mais qu'\u00e9tant, selon l'expression de S. Pierre Chrysologue, l'\u00e9mule de la mis\u00e9ricorde de Dieu, elle doit se r\u00e9pandre aussi-bien sur les ennemis que sur les amis, comme Dieu fait lever son soleil aussi-bien sur les m\u00e9chans que sur les justes; Si esurierit inimicus tuus, ciba illum. Or si Dieu le vouloit de la sorte dans une loi, o\u00f9 il \u00e9toit, ce semble, permis de ha\u00efr son ennemi, ou du moins quelque ennemi, ainsi que l'expliquent les Peres; jugez, Chr\u00e9tiens, ce qu'il exige de nous, pour qui l'amour des ennemis est un devoir propre & un commandement particulier."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p030", "label": "English", "text": "Why do I say Christian mercy? Did not God himself prescribe this law to the Jews in the Old Testament? In commanding almsgiving, did he not point out particularly the person of their enemy: Si esurierit inimicus tuus, ciba illum; si sitit, potum da illi: if your enemy is hungry, feed him; if he thirsts, give him drink? He thereby wished them to understand that almsgiving must not be limited, but, being, in Saint Peter Chrysologus's expression, an imitator of God's mercy, must be poured upon enemies as well as friends, as God makes his sun rise upon the wicked as well as the just: Si esurierit inimicus tuus, ciba illum. If God wished it thus under a law in which it seemed permissible to hate one's enemy, or at least some enemy, as the Fathers explain, judge, Christians, what he requires of us, for whom love of enemies is a proper duty and a particular commandment.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p031", "label": "French original", "text": "Et de-l\u00e0 m\u00eame concluons quel est l'aveuglement & l'erreur de certaines personnes, qui jusques dans leurs aum\u00f4nes se laissent gouverner par leurs passions & leurs affections naturelles: qui donnent \u00e0 ceux-ci, parce que ceux-ci leur plaisent; & qui ne donnent jamais \u00e0 ceux-l\u00e0, parce que ceux-l\u00e0 n'ont pas le bonheur de leur agr\u00e9er: qui se font une gloire & un point d'honneur de pourvoir aux besoins des uns, & qui n'ont que de la duret\u00e9 ou de l'indiff\u00e9rence pour les autres: c'est-\u00e0-dire, qui contentent leur amour-propre en faisant l'aum\u00f4ne, & qui suivent le mouvement d'une antipathie secrette en ne la faisant pas. Car c'est ce qui arrive aux spirituels m\u00eames, sans qu'ils y fassent r\u00e9flexion. Or est-ce l\u00e0 l'esprit de l'Evangile? Accoutumons-nous, mes chers Auditeurs, \u00e0 faire les actions Chr\u00e9tiennes chr\u00e9tiennement, & n'en corrompons point la saintet\u00e9 par le m\u00e9lange de l'iniquit\u00e9. Faire ainsi l'aum\u00f4ne, ce n'est point pratiquer, mais prophaner une vertu. Si je fais l'aum\u00f4ne dans l'ordre de Dieu, je dois \u00eatre pr\u00eat \u00e0 la faire sans distinction & sans exception; \u00e0 la faire par tout o\u00f9 je verrai le besoin, & selon la mesure du besoin que Dieu me fera conno\u00eetre. Tellement qu'\u00e0 prendre la chose en g\u00e9n\u00e9ral, si je vois mon ennemi m\u00eame dans une n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 plus pressante, je dois le secourir par pr\u00e9f\u00e9rence \u00e0 tout autre. Voil\u00e0 ce que m'apprend le Christianisme que je professe; & sans cela je n'ai qu'une charit\u00e9 apparente. Car je ne m\u00e9rite rien dans les aum\u00f4nes que je fais; & je me rends doublement coupable dans celles que je ne fais pas: pourquoi? parce que dans les aum\u00f4nes que je fais, je ne suis que mon inclination; & dans celles que je ne fais pas, je satisfais mon ressentiment, & je manque \u00e0 une de mes plus \u00e9troites obligations."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p031", "label": "English", "text": "From this let us conclude how blind and mistaken are certain people who even in their alms let themselves be governed by their passions and natural affections: who give to these because they like them, and never give to those because those are not fortunate enough to please them; who make it a glory and point of honour to supply some people's needs, and have only hardness or indifference for others. That is, they gratify self-love in giving alms, and follow a secret antipathy in not giving. This happens even to spiritual people without their reflecting upon it. Is that the Gospel's spirit? Let us accustom ourselves, my dear hearers, to perform Christian actions in a Christian way, and not corrupt their holiness by mixing iniquity with it. Giving alms thus is not practising but profaning a virtue. If I give alms in God's order, I must be ready to give without distinction or exception, wherever I see need and according to the measure of need God makes known to me. Thus, generally speaking, if I see even my enemy in more urgent necessity, I must assist him in preference to anyone else. This is what the Christianity I profess teaches me; without it I have only apparent charity. For I merit nothing in the alms I give, and make myself doubly guilty in those I do not give. Why? Because in those I give I merely follow my inclination, and in those I do not give I gratify resentment and fail in one of my strictest obligations.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p032", "label": "French original", "text": "Ce n'est pas qu'il ne soit permis, & qu'il ne soit m\u00eame \u00e0 propos d'avoir l\u00e0-dessus certains \u00e9gards; & je conviens avec tous les ma\u00eetres de la morale, que les proches & les domestiques doivent commun\u00e9ment l'emporter sur les \u00e9trangers; ceux qui se trouvent dans une impuissance absolue de s'aider, sur ceux \u00e0 qui il reste encore dans leur travail quelque ressource; ceux qui s'emploient \u00e0 procurer la gloire de Dieu & \u00e0 sanctifier le prochain, sur ceux qui ne sont occup\u00e9s que d'eux-m\u00eames & de leur propre salut. Ce fut le puissant motif qui porta saint Louis \u00e0 r\u00e9pandre si lib\u00e9ralement ses graces sur ces deux Ap\u00f4tres de son si\u00e9cle, saint Dominique & saint Fran\u00e7ois d'Assise. Il n'\u00e9pargna rien pour les soutenir, pour les seconder, parce qu'il les regarda comme les d\u00e9fenseurs de l'Eglise, comme les propagateurs de la foi, comme les dispensateurs de la parole de Dieu. Ce n'est plus gu\u00e9res peut-\u00eatre la d\u00e9votion de notre tems; mais la d\u00e9votion de saint Louis \u00e9toit sans doute aussi solide que la n\u00f4tre."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p032", "label": "English", "text": "This does not mean that certain considerations are forbidden or even inappropriate; I agree with all masters of moral teaching that relatives and household members should commonly take precedence over strangers; those absolutely unable to help themselves over those who still have some resource in their work; those occupied in procuring God's glory and sanctifying their neighbour over those occupied only with themselves and their own salvation. This was the powerful motive that moved Saint Louis to pour his favours so liberally upon those two apostles of his age, Saint Dominic and Saint Francis of Assisi. He spared nothing to support and assist them, regarding them as the Church's defenders, propagators of the faith, and dispensers of God's word. Perhaps this is scarcely the devotion of our time any longer; but Saint Louis's devotion was undoubtedly as sound as ours.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p033", "label": "French original", "text": "L'ordre de l'aum\u00f4ne ainsi r\u00e9gl\u00e9 par rapport au pauvre \u00e0 qui l'aum\u00f4ne est d\u00fbe, il reste \u00e0 le r\u00e9gler par rapport au riche \u00e0 qui l'aum\u00f4ne est command\u00e9e; & c'est ce que je r\u00e9duis \u00e0 cinq articles, par o\u00f9 je finis en peu de paroles, pour ne pas fatiguer votre patience."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p033", "label": "English", "text": "With the order of almsgiving thus regulated in relation to the poor to whom alms are owed, it remains to regulate it in relation to the rich to whom almsgiving is commanded. I reduce this to five articles, with which I shall conclude briefly so as not to weary your patience.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p034", "label": "French original", "text": "Premi\u00e9re r\u00e9gle: que l'aum\u00f4ne soit faite d'un bien propre, & non point du bien d'autrui, comme il arrive tous les jours; non point d'un bien injustement acquis, & que la conscience me reproche. Car notre Dieu, Chr\u00e9tiens, a l'injustice en horreur, & la d\u00e9teste jusques dans le sacrifice & l'holocauste, comme parle l'Ecriture: Odio habens rapinam in holocausto. Faire des aum\u00f4nes du bien d'autrui, dit S. Chrysostome, c'est faire Dieu le complice de nos larcins, & vouloir qu'il participe \u00e0 notre p\u00e9ch\u00e9. Puisque l'aum\u00f4ne, selon S. Paul, est comme une hostie qui nous rend Dieu favorable, Talibus enim hostiis promeretur Deus; offrons-lui cette hostie toute pure, & ne confondons jamais une aum\u00f4ne & une restitution: car ce sont deux choses essentiellement distingu\u00e9es, que la restitution & l'aum\u00f4ne; & jamais l'aum\u00f4ne ne peut \u00eatre le suppl\u00e9ment de la restitution, si ce n'est que la restitution nous soit impossible."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p034", "label": "English", "text": "First rule: alms must be given from one's own goods, not another's, as happens daily; not from unjustly acquired goods for which conscience reproaches me. For our God, Christians, abhors injustice and detests it even in sacrifice and burnt offering, as Scripture says: Odio habens rapinam in holocausto, hating robbery in a burnt offering. To give alms from another's goods, says Saint Chrysostom, is to make God an accomplice in our thefts and wish him to share our sin. Since almsgiving, according to Saint Paul, is like a sacrifice that renders God favourable to us\u2014Talibus enim hostiis promeretur Deus, by such sacrifices God is pleased\u2014let us offer him this sacrifice entirely pure, and never confuse almsgiving and restitution. Restitution and almsgiving are essentially distinct; almsgiving can never substitute for restitution unless restitution is impossible for us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p035", "label": "French original", "text": "Seconde r\u00e9gle: que les actions de justice envers les pauvres, passent toujours devant les \u0153uvres de pure charit\u00e9; ou si je puis ainsi parler, que l'aum\u00f4ne de justice pr\u00e9c\u00e9de toujours l'aum\u00f4ne de charit\u00e9. Car il y a, mes Freres, une aum\u00f4ne de justice; & j'appelle aum\u00f4ne de justice, payer aux pauvres ce qui leur appartient, payer de pauvres domestiques, payer de pauvres artisans, payer de pauvres marchands, ou m\u00eame de riches marchands, mais qui de riches qu'ils \u00e9toient, tombent dans la pauvret\u00e9, parce qu'on les laisse trop long-tems attendre. Or la loi de Dieu veut que cette esp\u00e9ce d'aum\u00f4ne ait le premier rang, & c'est par-l\u00e0 qu'il faut commencer. Mais avouons-le, Chr\u00e9tiens, c'est une morale que bien des riches du monde ne veulent pas entendre aujourd'hui. Vous le s\u00e7avez. On traite ce marchand, cet artisan, qui fait quelque instance, de f\u00e2cheux & d'importun. On le fait languir des ann\u00e9es enti\u00e9res; & apr\u00e8s bien des remises, qui l'ont peut-\u00eatre \u00e0 demi ruin\u00e9, on lui donne \u00e0 regret ce qui lui est le plus l\u00e9gitimement acquis, comme si c'\u00e9toit une grace qu'on lui accord\u00e2t, & non une dette dont on s'acquitt\u00e2t. Combien m\u00eame en usent de la sorte par une politique d'int\u00e9r\u00eat, que je n'examine point ici: voulant paro\u00eetre incommod\u00e9s dans leurs affaires, & cacher leur \u00e9tat aux yeux des hommes, mais sans le pouvoir cacher aux yeux de Dieu. Quoi qu'il en soit, ce n'est pas sans raison que je touche ce point, & sans que je m'explique davantage, tel qui m'\u00e9coute, comprend assez ce que je dis, ou ce que je veux dire."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p035", "label": "English", "text": "Second rule: acts of justice towards the poor must always precede works of pure charity; or, if I may put it thus, almsgiving of justice must always precede almsgiving of charity. For there is, my brothers, an almsgiving of justice; by it I mean paying the poor what belongs to them: paying poor servants, poor craftsmen, poor merchants, or even rich merchants who fall from wealth into poverty because they are kept waiting too long. God's law requires this kind of almsgiving to hold first place, and this is where we must begin. But let us admit, Christians, that many rich people today do not wish to hear this moral teaching. You know it. The merchant or craftsman who presses his claim is called troublesome and importunate. He is left languishing for whole years; after many postponements, which may have half ruined him, he is grudgingly given what he has most lawfully earned, as though a favour were being granted rather than a debt discharged. How many even act thus from a self-interested policy I do not examine here: wishing to appear to be in financial difficulty and conceal their condition from men's eyes, though they cannot conceal it from God's. Whatever the case, I touch this point for a reason, and without further explanation someone listening understands well enough what I say, or wish to say.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p036", "label": "French original", "text": "Troisi\u00e9me r\u00e9gle: que les aum\u00f4nes ne soient point jett\u00e9es au hasard, mais donn\u00e9es avec mesure, avec r\u00e9flexion. Autrement, ce sont des aum\u00f4nes souvent mal plac\u00e9es. L'un re\u00e7oit, parce que le hasard vous l'a pr\u00e9sent\u00e9; & l'autre ne re\u00e7oit rien, parce que vous n'avez pas pris soin de le chercher & de le conno\u00eetre. Mais celui-l\u00e0 peut-\u00eatre que vous soulagez, pouvoit encore se passer d'un tel secours; & celui-ci que vous ne soulagez pas, manque de tout, & se voit r\u00e9duit aux derni\u00e8res extr\u00e9mit\u00e9s."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p036", "label": "English", "text": "Third rule: alms must not be cast out at random, but given with measure and reflection. Otherwise they are often misplaced. One receives because chance brought him before you; another receives nothing because you did not trouble to seek him out and know him. Yet perhaps the one you relieve could still have done without such assistance, while the one you do not relieve lacks everything and is reduced to the last extremity.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p037", "label": "French original", "text": "Quatri\u00e9me r\u00e9gle: que les aum\u00f4nes soient publiques, quand il est constant & public que vous poss\u00e9dez de grands biens, & que vous \u00eates dans l'abondance; pourquoi? pour satisfaire \u00e0 l'\u00e9dification, pour donner l'exemple, pour accomplir la parole de Jesus-Christ, Luceat lux vestra coram hominibus, ut videant opera vestra bona. Car n'est-ce pas un scandale, de voir des riches vivre dans l'opulence, & de ne s\u00e7avoir, ni s'ils font l'aum\u00f4ne, ni o\u00f9 ils la font. Ce n'est point pour eux que le Sauveur du monde a dit: Nesciat sinistra tua quid faciat dextera bona; que votre main gauche ne s\u00e7ache pas ce que fait votre main droite. Ce seroit une fausse humilit\u00e9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p037", "label": "English", "text": "Fourth rule: alms must be public when it is an established and public fact that you possess great goods and live in abundance. Why? To fulfil the duty of edification, give an example, and accomplish Christ's word: Luceat lux vestra coram hominibus, ut videant opera vestra bona, let your light shine before men, that they may see your good works. Is it not a scandal to see rich people living in opulence without knowing whether or where they give alms? It was not for them that the Saviour of the world said: Nesciat sinistra tua quid faciat dextera bona; let your left hand not know what your right hand does. That would be false humility.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p038", "label": "French original", "text": "Cinqui\u00e9me & derni\u00e9re r\u00e9gle: c'est de faire l'aum\u00f4ne dans le tems o\u00f9 elle vous peut \u00eatre utile pour le salut, sans attendre \u00e0 la mort, ou m\u00eame apr\u00e8s la mort. Et voil\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs, le point important que je ne puis assez vous recommander. Car de quel m\u00e9rite peuvent \u00eatre devant Dieu des aum\u00f4nes faites seulement \u00e0 la mort; & quel fruit en pouvez-vous retirer alors, qui soit comparable \u00e0 ce qu'elles auroient valu pendant la vie? Est-ce bien t\u00e9moigner \u00e0 Dieu votre amour, que de lui faire part de vos biens, quand vous n'\u00eates plus en \u00e9tat de les poss\u00e9der, quand la mort vous les arrache par violence, quand ils ne sont plus proprement \u00e0 vous? On dit: Cet homme a beaucoup donn\u00e9 en mourant; & moi je dis: Il n'a rien donn\u00e9, mais il a laiss\u00e9; & il n'a laiss\u00e9 que ce qu'il ne pouvoit retenir, & que parce qu'il ne le pouvoit retenir. Il l'a gard\u00e9 jusqu'au dernier moment; & s'il e\u00fbt p\u00fb l'emporter avec lui, ni Dieu, ni les pauvres n'auroient e\u00fb rien \u00e0 y pr\u00e9tendre. Aussi, que lui servent de telles aum\u00f4nes, & quel profit en doit-il esp\u00e9rer? Car il est de la foi, Chr\u00e9tiens, que toutes vos aum\u00f4nes apr\u00e8s la mort n'ont plus de vertu pour vous sauver. Elles peuvent bien soulager votre ame dans le purgatoire: mais quant au salut, ce sont apr\u00e8s la vie des \u0153uvres st\u00e9riles; pourquoi? parce que l'affaire du salut est d\u00e9j\u00e0 d\u00e9cid\u00e9e, & que l'arr\u00eat est sans appel. Cependant, riches du si\u00e9cle, la grande vertu de l'aum\u00f4ne \u00e0 votre \u00e9gard, c'est de contribuer \u00e0 votre salut. Si ce riche dans la vie e\u00fbt fait une partie des aum\u00f4nes qu'il a ordonn\u00e9es \u00e0 la mort, ses aum\u00f4nes l'auroient sauv\u00e9: elles lui auroient attir\u00e9 des graces de conversion; elles auroient pri\u00e9 pour lui, selon le langage de l'Ecriture. Car ce ne sont pas tant les pauvres qui prient pour nous, que l'aum\u00f4ne m\u00eame: Conclude eleemosynam in sinu pauperis, & ipsa exorabit pro te. Que le pauvre prie, ou qu'il ne prie pas, l'aum\u00f4ne prie toujours ind\u00e9pendamment du pauvre: mais en vain apr\u00e8s la mort, prieroit-elle pour votre conversion, puisque ce n'est plus le tems de se convertir; en vain reclameroit-elle pour vous la mis\u00e9ricorde divine, puisque ce n'est plus le tems de la mis\u00e9ricorde."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p038", "label": "English", "text": "Fifth and last rule: give alms while they can be useful to you for salvation, without waiting until death or even after death. This, my dear hearers, is the important point I cannot recommend enough. What merit before God can alms given only at death have, and what fruit can you then draw from them comparable to their value during life? Is it truly showing God your love to share your goods with him when you can no longer possess them, when death violently tears them from you, when they are no longer properly yours? People say: This man gave much when dying; I say: he gave nothing, but left it; he left only what he could not retain, and only because he could not retain it. He kept it to the last moment; if he could have carried it with him, neither God nor the poor would have had any claim to it. What use, then, are such alms to him, and what profit should he hope for? For faith teaches, Christians, that all your alms after death no longer have power to save you. They may indeed relieve your soul in purgatory; but as regards salvation they are sterile works after life. Why? Because the matter of salvation is already decided and the sentence admits no appeal. Yet, worldly rich, the great power of almsgiving in your regard is to contribute to your salvation. Had this rich man during life given some of the alms he ordered at death, his alms would have saved him: they would have obtained graces of conversion and prayed for him, in Scripture's language. For it is not so much the poor who pray for us as almsgiving itself: Conclude eleemosynam in sinu pauperis, et ipsa exorabit pro te: enclose your alms in the poor man's bosom, and they themselves will plead for you. Whether or not the poor man prays, almsgiving always prays independently of him. But after death it would pray in vain for your conversion, since it is no longer the time to be converted; in vain would it call down divine mercy for you, since it is no longer the time of mercy.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p039", "label": "French original", "text": "La cons\u00e9quence qui suit de-l\u00e0, c'est la grande le\u00e7on que nous fait S. Paul: Dum tempus habemus, operemur bonum. Si nous aimons Dieu, & si nous nous aimons nous-m\u00eames, faisons de bonnes \u0153uvres tandis que nous en avons le tems. Je ne pr\u00e9tens pas vous d\u00e9tourner d'en faire \u00e0 la mort; \u00e0 Dieu ne plaise! c'\u00e9toit un usage trop saint & trop chr\u00e9tien que celui des fid\u00e8les autrefois, de vouloir que Jesus-Christ f\u00fbt leur h\u00e9ritier & qu'il e\u00fbt part \u00e0 leurs derni\u00e8res volont\u00e9s. Mais du reste, souvenons-nous que les bonnes \u0153uvres de la vie sont de tout un autre poids. Ah! Chr\u00e9tiens, voici le tems o\u00f9 Dieu se dispose \u00e0 verser plus abondamment ses graces, & o\u00f9 il vous appelle plus fortement \u00e0 la p\u00e9nitence. Or un des moyens les plus efficaces pour le toucher en votre faveur, c'est de lui envoyer, selon la figure de l'Evangile, des m\u00e9diateurs qui lui parlent pour vous, & qui s'engagent \u00e0 consommer l'affaire de votre conversion, & celle de votre salut & de votre sanctification. On s'\u00e9tonne quelquefois de voir des p\u00e9cheurs changer tout \u00e0 coup; des libertins & des impies renoncer \u00e0 leurs habitudes, & s'attacher \u00e0 Dieu; des aveugles & des endurcis, se reconno\u00eetre, & devenir sensibles aux v\u00e9rit\u00e9s \u00e9ternelles; des imp\u00e9nitens de plusieurs ann\u00e9es, par une esp\u00e9ce de prodige, apr\u00e8s une vie d\u00e9r\u00e9gl\u00e9e & dissolue, mourir de la mort des Saints: mais moi je n'en suis pas surpris, si ces p\u00e9cheurs, si ces impies & ces libertins, si ces aveugles & ces endurcis, si ces imp\u00e9nitens ont \u00e9t\u00e9 charitables envers les pauvres. C'est l'accomplissement des oracles de l'Ecriture; c'est un effet des paroles de Jesus-Christ; c'est la b\u00e9n\u00e9diction de l'aum\u00f4ne. Il faut pour cela que Dieu fasse des miracles: mais les miracles, pour r\u00e9compenser l'aum\u00f4ne, ne lui co\u00fbtent point. Il faut que Dieu se rel\u00e2che de ses droits, & qu'il arr\u00eate tous les foudres de sa justice: mais, si j'ose m'exprimer de la sorte, l'aum\u00f4ne fait violence \u00e0 la justice divine; & pour les int\u00e9r\u00eats du pauvre, & du riche qui l'assiste, Dieu n'a point de droits si l\u00e9gitimes & si chers qu'il ne soit pr\u00eat \u00e0 c\u00e9der. David disoit qu'il n'avoit point v\u00fb de juste abandonn\u00e9, Non vidi justum derelictum; & je puis dire, que je n'ai point v\u00fb de riche lib\u00e9ral & tendre pour les pauvres, en qui je n'aye remarqu\u00e9 certains effets de la grace, qui m'ont rempli de consolation. Mais au contraire, il n'est que trop commun, de voir ces riches avares, ces riches insensibles aux mis\u00e9res du prochain, vivre sans foi & sans loi; vieillir & blanchir dans leurs d\u00e9sordres, & mourir enfin dans leur imp\u00e9nitence: pourquoi? parce que suivant l'arr\u00eat du Saint-Esprit, il n'y a point de mis\u00e9ricorde pour celui qui n'exerce point la mis\u00e9ricorde: Judicium sine misericordia, ei qui non facit misericordiam. Pr\u00e9venons, mes chers Auditeurs, un jugement si terrible. R\u00e9veillons dans nos c\u0153urs tous les sentimens de la charit\u00e9 Chr\u00e9tienne; & par de saintes aum\u00f4nes, faisons-nous des amis qui nous re\u00e7oivent dans l'\u00e9ternit\u00e9 bienheureuse, que je vous souhaite, &c."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-p039", "label": "English", "text": "The consequence is the great lesson Saint Paul gives us: Dum tempus habemus, operemur bonum, while we have time, let us do good. If we love God and ourselves, let us do good works while we have time. I do not intend to dissuade you from doing them at death\u2014God forbid! The faithful's former practice of wishing Jesus Christ to be their heir and share in their last wills was too holy and Christian for that. But let us remember that good works done during life have an altogether different weight. Ah, Christians, this is the time when God prepares to pour out his graces more abundantly and calls you more strongly to penance. One of the most effective means of moving him in your favour is to send him, according to the Gospel's figure, mediators who speak for you and undertake to bring to completion your conversion, salvation, and sanctification. Sometimes people are astonished to see sinners change suddenly; libertines and the impious renounce their habits and attach themselves to God; the blind and hardened come to their senses and become responsive to eternal truths; those impenitent for many years, after a disordered and dissolute life, die the death of the saints by a kind of wonder. I am not surprised, however, if these sinners, impious people and libertines, blind and hardened, and impenitent people have been charitable towards the poor. This fulfils Scripture's oracles; it is an effect of Christ's words, the blessing of almsgiving. For this God must work miracles; but miracles to reward almsgiving cost him nothing. God must relinquish his rights and arrest all the thunderbolts of his justice; but, if I dare speak thus, almsgiving does violence to divine justice. For the interests of the poor man and the rich man assisting him, God has no rights so legitimate and dear that he is not ready to yield them. David said he had never seen a just man forsaken, Non vidi justum derelictum; I can say that I have never seen a rich man generous and tender towards the poor without observing certain effects of grace in him that filled me with consolation. On the contrary, it is only too common to see those miserly rich, insensible to their neighbour's misery, live without faith or law, grow old and grey in their disorders, and finally die impenitent. Why? Because, according to the Holy Spirit's sentence, there is no mercy for one who exercises no mercy: Judicium sine misericordia, ei qui non facit misericordiam, judgment without mercy for one who does not show mercy. Let us forestall, my dear hearers, so terrible a judgment. Let us awaken in our hearts all the sentiments of Christian charity, and by holy alms make friends who will receive us into blessed eternity, which I wish you, etc.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n001", "label": "French original", "text": "Monsieur frere unique du Roi."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n001", "label": "English", "text": "Monsieur, the King's only brother.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n002", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 25."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n002", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 25.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n003", "label": "French original", "text": "Prov. 3."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n003", "label": "English", "text": "Proverbs 3.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n004", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n004", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n005", "label": "French original", "text": "Ambros."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n005", "label": "English", "text": "Ambrose.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n006", "label": "French original", "text": "Ambros."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n006", "label": "English", "text": "Ambrose.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n007", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Joan. 3."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n007", "label": "English", "text": "1 John 3.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n008", "label": "French original", "text": "II. Cor. 8."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n008", "label": "English", "text": "2 Corinthians 8.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n009", "label": "French original", "text": "Ambros."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n009", "label": "English", "text": "Ambrose.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n010", "label": "French original", "text": "II. Cor. 8."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n010", "label": "English", "text": "2 Corinthians 8.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n011", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n011", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n012", "label": "French original", "text": "Idem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n012", "label": "English", "text": "The same author.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n013", "label": "French original", "text": "Cant. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n013", "label": "English", "text": "Song of Songs 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n014", "label": "French original", "text": "Phil. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n014", "label": "English", "text": "Philippians 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n015", "label": "French original", "text": "II. Cor. 6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n015", "label": "English", "text": "2 Corinthians 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n016", "label": "French original", "text": "Prov. 25."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n016", "label": "English", "text": "Proverbs 25.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n017", "label": "French original", "text": "Isai. 61."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n017", "label": "English", "text": "Isaiah 61.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n018", "label": "French original", "text": "Hebr. 13."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n018", "label": "English", "text": "Hebrews 13.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n019", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n019", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n020", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n020", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n021", "label": "French original", "text": "Eccles. 29."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n021", "label": "English", "text": "Ecclesiasticus 29.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n022", "label": "French original", "text": "Galat. 6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n022", "label": "English", "text": "Galatians 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n023", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal. 36."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n023", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 36 (source numbering).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n024", "label": "French original", "text": "Jacob. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n024", "label": "English", "text": "James 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n025", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The book prints \u201cfalse precepts\u201d here, although the preceding sentence speaks of pretexts for avoiding almsgiving. The French and English preserve the printed word."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n026", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Words appear to be missing from this sentence in the book. The French preserves the surviving wording; the English gives its apparent sense: \u201cPlease lose nothing of this instruction.\u201d"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-aumone-n027", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The book prints the unusual Latin dextera bona. The English follows the French explanation immediately after it: \u201cyour right hand.\u201d"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "On Almsgiving", "topics": ["almsgiving", "charity", "justice", "mercy", "repentance", "wealth", "Lent"], "url": "sermons/bourdaloue-careme-aumone.html"}, {"author": "Louis Bourdaloue", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-lent", "calendar_label": "Lent", "centuries": [17, 18], "composition": "Approximately late seventeenth century\u20131704; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "bourdaloue-careme-communion", "language": "fr", "language_label": "French", "metadata": ["Louis Bourdaloue, Sermons du Pere Bourdaloue, de la Compagnie de Jesus, pour le Caresme, tome premier, nouvelle \u00e9dition (Paris: aux d\u00e9pens de Rigaud, Directeur de l'Imprimerie Royale,1716), Sermon pour le premier Jeudi du Caresme. Sur la Communion, pp.97\u2013144. Kelly Library copy.", "Internet Archive; John M. Kelly Library Rare Books, University of Toronto; sponsored by the University of Ottawa.", "Lent", "First Thursday of Lent", "Matthew 8", "Luke 5", "John 6 (printed chapter locator and repeated quotations)", "1 Corinthians 11", "Matthew 26", "Luke 14", "I Regum 2 (printed historical book numbering)", "Matthew 23", "Rev. A. Carroll, S.J.", "Louis Bourdaloue, Sermons, and Moral Discourses, on the Important Duties of Christianity, translated by the Rev. A. Carroll, S.J., second edition, vol. II (Dublin: James Duffy, 1843), sermon XXIII, \u201cOn Communion\u201d, pp. 137\u2013162."], "occasion": "Premier Jeudi du Car\u00eame", "occasion_detail": "First Thursday of Lent", "occasion_label": "Lent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Starting from the centurion\u2019s confession of unworthiness in Matthew 8, Bourdaloue distinguishes three uses of the same excuse for staying away from Communion. In sincere sinners it is a reason requiring clarification: avoiding unworthy reception must lead to conversion and worthy reception, rather than permanent abstention. In self-deceived worldly people it is a pretext unsupported by sorrow, desire or a change of life. In hypocrites it becomes a scandal that discredits good Communions along with bad ones and can undermine other Christian duties. The sermon explicitly distinguishes fervent frequent Communion from careless or fruitless receptions."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-h001", "label": "French original", "text": "SERMON POUR LE PREMIER JEUDI DU CARESME. Sur la Communion."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON FOR THE FIRST THURSDAY OF LENT. On Communion.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-e001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Ait illi Jesus : Ego veniam, & curabo eum. Et respondens Centurio, ait : Domine, non sum dignus, ut intres sub tectum meum."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-e001", "label": "English", "text": "Jesus said to him: I shall come and heal him. And the centurion answered, saying: Lord, I am not worthy that you should enter under my roof.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-e002", "label": "French original", "text": "Jesus-Christ dit au Centenier : J'irai moi-m\u00eame, & je le gu\u00e9rirai. Mais le Centenier lui r\u00e9pondit : Seigneur, je ne suis pas digne que vous entriez dans ma maison. En saint Matth. chap. 8."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-e002", "label": "English", "text": "Jesus Christ said to the centurion: I shall go myself and heal him. But the centurion answered him: Lord, I am not worthy that you should enter my house. In Saint Matthew, chapter 8.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p001", "label": "French original", "text": "Voil\u00e0, Chr\u00e9tiens, entre Jesus-Christ & le Centenier une esp\u00e9ce de combat mais dans ce combat qu'admirerons-nous davantage, ou la charit\u00e9 d'un Dieu, ou l'humilit\u00e9 d'un payen ? Je puis dire qu'il n'y eut jamais de contestation plus sainte, ni plus propre tout ensemble, & \u00e0 nous instruire, & \u00e0 nous \u00e9difier. Le Sauveur du monde par un mouvement de sa charit\u00e9 bienfaisante, veut aller en personne dans la maison du Centenier ; & le Centenier ne croit pas pouvoir accepter cet honneur. Le Fils unique de Dieu, dont la mis\u00e9ricorde n'a point de bornes, lui dit qu'il ira, & que par sa pr\u00e9sence il gu\u00e9rira son serviteur paralytique ; Ego veniam, & curabo eum : mais le Centenier confus d'une si insigne faveur, proteste hautement qu'il ne la m\u00e9rite pas, & s'en reconno\u00eet indigne ; Domine, non sum dignus. Prenez garde, s'il vous pla\u00eet. C'est un gentil, \u00e0 qui Jesus-Christ en qualit\u00e9 de Messie, n'a point \u00e9t\u00e9 encore annonc\u00e9 ni r\u00e9v\u00e9l\u00e9 comme aux Juifs ; & cependant tout gentil qu'il est, il se sent d\u00e9j\u00e0 pr\u00e9venu pour ce Messie qui lui parle, d'une id\u00e9e si haute & d'un respect si profond, qu'il ne peut m\u00eame consentir \u00e0 recevoir sa visite. Humilit\u00e9, s'\u00e9crie S. Augustin, qui proc\u00e9da d'une foi vive & ardente, & qui par un effet si sensible de la grace du R\u00e9dempteur, forma d\u00e8s-lors dans ce gentil, non-seulement un v\u00e9ritable Isra\u00eblite, mais un parfait Chr\u00e9tien. Humilit\u00e9 que Jesus-Christ agr\u00e9a, que Jesus-Christ admira, dont Jesus-Christ fit l'\u00e9loge ; mais \u00e0 laquelle il est pourtant vrai qu'il ne d\u00e9f\u00e9ra pas, puisque ce fut au contraire pour cela m\u00eame qu'il persista \u00e0 vouloir entrer chez le Centenier."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p001", "label": "English", "text": "Here, Christians, is a kind of combat between Jesus Christ and the centurion; but in this combat what shall we admire more, the charity of a God or the humility of a pagan? I can say that there never was a holier contest, nor one more fitted at once to instruct and edify us. The Savior of the world, moved by his beneficent charity, wishes to go in person to the centurion's house; and the centurion does not believe he can accept this honor. The only Son of God, whose mercy knows no bounds, tells him that he will go and by his presence heal his paralytic servant: I shall come and heal him. But the centurion, overwhelmed by so signal a favor, declares aloud that he does not deserve it and acknowledges himself unworthy of it: Lord, I am not worthy. Consider, please. This is a gentile, to whom Jesus Christ as Messiah has not yet been announced or revealed as to the Jews; and yet, gentile though he is, he already finds himself filled with so high an idea of this Messiah who speaks to him, and so profound a respect, that he cannot even consent to receive his visit. Humility, Saint Augustine exclaims, which proceeded from a lively and ardent faith, and which, by so perceptible an effect of the Redeemer's grace, formed in this gentile from that moment not only a true Israelite but a perfect Christian. Humility which Jesus Christ accepted, which Jesus Christ admired, which Jesus Christ praised; but to which it is nevertheless true that he did not yield, since it was on the contrary for that very reason that he persisted in wishing to enter the centurion's house.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p002", "label": "French original", "text": "Arr\u00eatons-nous l\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs ; & pour profiter selon le dessein de Dieu d'un si grand exemple, appliquons-nous tout le myst\u00e8re de cet Evangile. Car, comme dit S. Chrysostome, ce qui se passa entre Jesus-Christ & le Centenier, se renouvelle encore aujourd'hui entre Jesus-Christ & nous. Je m'explique. Ce m\u00eame Sauveur instituant la divine Eucharistie, nous a laiss\u00e9 un Sacrement par o\u00f9 il pr\u00e9tend se communiquer \u00e0 nous, & habiter, tout Dieu qu'il est, corporellement en nous. Un Sacrement, par o\u00f9 il vient en personne nous visiter, & gu\u00e9rir nos infirmit\u00e9s spirituelles & nos foiblesses. Quand donc nous nous pr\u00e9parons \u00e0 le recevoir dans ce myst\u00e8re adorable, il nous dit encore avec autant de v\u00e9rit\u00e9 qu'il le dit alors : Ego veniam, & curabo : j'irai ; & en quelque \u00e9tat de langueur que vous soyez, si de bonne foi vous voulez \u00eatre gu\u00e9ris, je vous gu\u00e9rirai. Et nous par un sinc\u00e9re aveu de notre foiblesse & de notre n\u00e9ant, nous lui r\u00e9pondons comme le Centenier : Non, Seigneur, je ne suis pas digne que vous veniez \u00e0 moi & dans moi. Car ce sont les paroles v\u00e9n\u00e9rables que l'Eglise nous met dans la bouche, lorsque ce Dieu de gloire cach\u00e9 sous les sacr\u00e9s symboles, est sur le point d'entrer dans nous : Domine, non sum dignus. Paroles efficaces, qui, selon l'ing\u00e9nieuse remarque de S. Augustin, ont la vertu d'op\u00e9rer dans l'ame Chr\u00e9tienne un miracle tout oppos\u00e9 \u00e0 ce qu'elles signifient ; puisqu'en m\u00eame-tems que nous les prof\u00e9rons, elles font cesser l'indignit\u00e9 que nous nous attribuons, & nous donnent \u00e0 l'\u00e9gard de Jesus-Christ & de son Sacrement un fonds de m\u00e9rite, que sans elles nous n'aurions pas. Paroles, qui par un secret merveilleux de la grace, nous conduisent au terme m\u00eame dont elles semblent nous \u00e9loigner ; puisque dans la doctrine de tous les P\u00e8res, la premi\u00e8re & l'essentielle disposition pour approcher dignement du corps de Jesus-Christ, est de nous en croire & de nous en confesser indignes. Paroles enfin, qui marquent au Fils de Dieu notre humilit\u00e9, sans mettre un obstacle \u00e0 sa charit\u00e9 ; & qui loin de le d\u00e9tourner de nous, lui servent d'attrait pour venir \u00e0 nous."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p002", "label": "English", "text": "Let us stop there, my dear listeners; and, to profit according to God's design from so great an example, let us apply the whole mystery of this Gospel to ourselves. For, as Saint Chrysostom says, what took place between Jesus Christ and the centurion is renewed even today between Jesus Christ and us. Let me explain. This same Savior, in instituting the divine Eucharist, left us a Sacrament by which he intends to communicate himself to us and, God though he is, dwell bodily in us. A Sacrament by which he comes in person to visit us and heal our spiritual infirmities and weaknesses. When, then, we prepare to receive him in this adorable mystery, he still says to us with as much truth as he said it then: I shall come and heal; I shall go, and whatever state of languor you may be in, if you sincerely wish to be healed, I shall heal you. And we, by a sincere acknowledgment of our weakness and our nothingness, answer him like the centurion: No, Lord, I am not worthy that you should come to me and into me. For these are the venerable words the Church puts into our mouths when this God of glory, hidden under the sacred signs, is on the point of entering into us: Lord, I am not worthy. Effective words which, according to Saint Augustine's ingenious observation, have the power to work in the Christian soul a miracle quite opposite to what they signify; for at the very time we utter them they put an end to the unworthiness we attribute to ourselves, and give us in relation to Jesus Christ and his Sacrament a foundation of merit we would not have without them. Words which, by a wonderful secret of grace, lead us to the very goal from which they seem to draw us away; since, in the teaching of all the Fathers, the first and essential disposition for worthily approaching the body of Jesus Christ is to believe and confess ourselves unworthy of it. Words, finally, which show the Son of God our humility without setting an obstacle to his charity, and which, far from turning him away from us, attract him to come to us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p003", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais qu'arrive-t-il, Chr\u00e9tiens ? suivez ma pens\u00e9e. Nous nous appliquons ces paroles, souvent au-del\u00e0 des intentions m\u00eames de Jesus Christ ; & pour en user trop selon nos v\u00fbes, nous nous mettons en danger d'aller directement contre les v\u00fbes de ce Dieu Sauveur. Comment cela ? le voici. Jesus-Christ nous recherche dans ce Sacrement, & nous nous en retirons. Il veut par un exc\u00e8s de son amour nous honorer de ses saintes visites, & nous nous y opposons. Il nous demande l'entr\u00e9e dans notre c\u0153ur ; & sous des pr\u00e9textes non-seulement sp\u00e9cieux, mais religieux, nous la lui refusons. Car pour nous disculper de ce refus, nous nous retranchons sur notre indignit\u00e9 ; & nous disons, mais par un esprit peut-\u00eatre bien diff\u00e9rent de celui du Centenier : Seigneur, je ne suis pas digne ; Domine, non sum dignus. Comme cette excuse est la plus apparente & la plus commune, j'ai cru devoir m'y attacher, non pas absolument pour la combattre, non pas aussi pour l'autoriser ; mais pour l'\u00e9xaminer dans ce discours, & pour avoir lieu de vous instruire des plus solides & des plus importantes v\u00e9rit\u00e9s qui regardent la pratique & l'usage de la communion. Quel besoin pour cela n'aurai-je pas des lumi\u00e8res du ciel ? Demandons-les par l'intercession de la m\u00e8re de Dieu. Ave, Maria."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p003", "label": "English", "text": "But what happens, Christians? Follow my thought. We apply these words to ourselves, often beyond the very intentions of Jesus Christ; and by using them too much according to our own views, we put ourselves in danger of going directly against the views of this saving God. How so? Here it is. Jesus Christ seeks us in this Sacrament, and we withdraw from it. He wishes, in the excess of his love, to honor us with his holy visits, and we oppose them. He asks entrance into our heart; and under pretexts not only plausible but religious, we refuse it to him. For, to excuse ourselves for this refusal, we take refuge in our unworthiness and say, but perhaps in a spirit very different from the centurion's: Lord, I am not worthy; Lord, I am not worthy. Since this excuse is the most plausible and the most common, I thought I ought to dwell on it, not simply to combat it, nor yet to authorize it, but to examine it in this discourse, and to have occasion to instruct you in the most solid and important truths concerning the practice and use of Communion. How much shall I need heaven's light for this! Let us ask for it through the intercession of the Mother of God. Hail, Mary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p004", "label": "French original", "text": "S'Eloigner de la communion dans la v\u00fbe de son indignit\u00e9, c'est une excuse, Chr\u00e9tiens, qui selon la qualit\u00e9 & les dispositions de ceux qui s'en servent, peut avoir des caract\u00e8res bien diff\u00e9rens ; & mon dessein, dont voici d'abord l'id\u00e9e, est de vous repr\u00e9senter aujourd'hui la diff\u00e9rence de ces caract\u00e8res, pour vous faire juger de la nature de cette excuse, & des bonnes ou des mauvaises cons\u00e9quences qu'on en peut tirer. Car il y a dans le Christianisme deux sortes de personnes, qui se fondent sur ce principe, & qui peuvent dire avec le Centenier : Seigneur, je ne suis pas digne que vous entriez chez moi. Les justes qui vivent dans la pratique de la loi de Dieu, & les p\u00e9cheurs qui sont engag\u00e9s dans les d\u00e9sordres d'une vie criminelle. Pour les justes, on ne peut gu\u00e9res douter que ce ne soit un sentiment d'humilit\u00e9, qui les fait parler de la sorte : mais de s\u00e7avoir jusqu'\u00e0 quel point cette humilit\u00e9 doit \u00eatre port\u00e9e, & s'il est raisonnable qu'elle aille jusqu'\u00e0 les \u00e9loigner en effet de J. C. & de son Sacrement ; de s\u00e7avoir si la privation de la divine Eucharistie peut \u00eatre cens\u00e9e pour une ame juste un exercice ordinaire de p\u00e9nitence, & si cette esp\u00e9ce de p\u00e9nitence est conforme aux intentions du Fils de Dieu ; si elle s'accorde avec la fin & l'institution de ce myst\u00e8re, si elle r\u00e9pond \u00e0 l'usage de la primitive Eglise, si elle est re\u00e7\u00fbe ou approuv\u00e9e par l'Eglise des derniers si\u00e9cles, si les P\u00e8res l'autorisent & si elle peut \u00eatre utile ; en un mot, de s\u00e7avoir si J. C. en tant qu'il est contenu dans le Sacrement de son corps, se tient honor\u00e9 que les justes, au lieu d'aller \u00e0 lui, se retirent de lui ; & si c'est lui rendre un vrai respect, en tant qu'il est le pain de vie, que de se contenter seulement de le r\u00e9v\u00e9rer & de l'adorer, sans le manger : ce sont des questions, mes chers Auditeurs, o\u00f9 bien des raisons particuli\u00e8res & g\u00e9n\u00e9rales m'emp\u00eachent d'entrer, & que je vous laisse \u00e0 examiner vous-m\u00eames. Outre qu'il seroit assez difficile de vous rien dire de nouveau sur cette mati\u00e8re, peut-\u00eatre le fruit en seroit-il moindre que je ne le dois pr\u00e9tendre d'un discours uniquement consacr\u00e9 \u00e0 l'\u00e9dification de vos ames."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p004", "label": "English", "text": "To keep away from Communion in view of one's unworthiness is an excuse, Christians, which, according to the character and dispositions of those who use it, can have very different features. My purpose, of which here is the outline at once, is to set before you today the difference between these features, so that you may judge the nature of this excuse and the good or bad consequences that can be drawn from it. For in Christianity there are two sorts of persons who rely on this principle and can say with the centurion: Lord, I am not worthy that you should enter my house. The just, who live in the practice of God's law, and sinners, who are engaged in the disorders of a criminal life. As for the just, there can scarcely be any doubt that it is a feeling of humility that makes them speak in this way. But to know how far this humility ought to be carried, and whether it is reasonable that it should go so far as actually to keep them away from Jesus Christ and his Sacrament; to know whether deprivation of the divine Eucharist can be considered an ordinary exercise of penance for a just soul, and whether this kind of penance conforms to the intentions of the Son of God; whether it accords with the purpose and institution of this mystery, corresponds to the practice of the primitive Church, is received or approved by the Church of later centuries, is authorized by the Fathers, and can be useful; in a word, to know whether Jesus Christ, insofar as he is contained in the Sacrament of his body, considers himself honored when the just, instead of going to him, withdraw from him, and whether it is to show him true respect, insofar as he is the bread of life, to be content merely to revere and adore him without eating him\u2014these are questions, my dear listeners, into which many particular and general reasons prevent me from entering, and which I leave you to examine yourselves. Besides the fact that it would be rather difficult to tell you anything new on this matter, perhaps its fruit would be less than I ought to seek from a discourse devoted solely to the edification of your souls.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p005", "label": "French original", "text": "Parlons donc pr\u00e9cis\u00e9ment des p\u00e9cheurs, qui bien plus que saint Pierre ont droit de dire \u00e0 Jesus-Christ : Retirez-vous de moi, parce que je suis un p\u00e9cheur ; Exi \u00e0 me, quia homo peccator sum. Je les divise comme en trois esp\u00e9ces. J'appelle les premiers, p\u00e9cheurs sinc\u00e9res ; les seconds, p\u00e9cheurs aveugles ; & les derniers, p\u00e9cheurs hypocrites & dissimul\u00e9s. P\u00e9cheurs sinc\u00e9res, qui traitent avec Dieu de bonne foi, & qui ne sont pas tromp\u00e9s. P\u00e9cheurs aveugles, qui ne se connoissent pas, & qui se trompent eux-m\u00eames. Enfin, p\u00e9cheurs hypocrites & dissimul\u00e9s, qui couvrent leur libertinage d'un voile de pi\u00e9t\u00e9, & affectent de tromper les autres. Les premiers ont de la religion, & agissent par esprit de religion. Les seconds, quoiqu'ils ayent de la religion, se flattent, & sont dans l'erreur de croire qu'ils agissent par religion. Et les derniers, quoiqu'ils veulent paro\u00eetre agir par religion, n'ont dans le fond nulle religion. Or ces trois sortes de p\u00e9cheurs peuvent tenir le langage de ce Centenier de notre Evangile, Domine, non sum dignus ; & s'excuser de communier sur ce qu'ils s'en jugent indignes. Mais, quoiqu'ils le disent \u00e9galement, ils n'en doivent pas \u00eatre \u00e9galement crus. Car pour continuer \u00e0 vous d\u00e9velopper mon dessein, dans les premiers, c'est-\u00e0-dire, dans les p\u00e9cheurs sinc\u00e9res, cette excuse est une raison ; dans les seconds, c'est-\u00e0-dire, dans les p\u00e9cheurs aveugles, cette excuse est un pr\u00e9texte ; & dans les derniers, c'est-\u00e0-dire, dans les p\u00e9cheurs hypocrites & libertins, cette excuse est un abus & m\u00eame un scandale : voil\u00e0 ce que j'ai \u00e0 vous montrer. Mais ce n'est pas assez : car \u00e0 cela j'ajo\u00fbte trois choses, qui vous feront conno\u00eetre ces trois caract\u00e8res de p\u00e9cheurs, & qui doivent \u00eatre pour vous d'une grande instruction. Dire, je ne communie pas parce que j'en suis indigne ; c'est une raison dans un p\u00e9cheur sinc\u00e9re ; mais moi je dis que cette raison a besoin d'\u00eatre \u00e9claircie. C'est un pr\u00e9texte dans un p\u00e9cheur aveugle qui se flatte, & il est important de lui \u00f4ter ce pr\u00e9texte. C'est un abus & un scandale dans un p\u00e9cheur hypocrite, & il est de mon devoir de combattre ce scandale & cet abus : voil\u00e0 tout le sujet de votre attention."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p005", "label": "English", "text": "Let us therefore speak specifically of sinners, who have far more right than Saint Peter to say to Jesus Christ: Withdraw from me, because I am a sinner; Depart from me, for I am a sinful man. I divide them, as it were, into three kinds. I call the first sincere sinners; the second blind sinners; and the last hypocritical and dissembling sinners. Sincere sinners, who deal with God in good faith and are not deceived. Blind sinners, who do not know themselves and deceive themselves. Finally, hypocritical and dissembling sinners, who cover their libertinism with a veil of piety and seek to deceive others. The first have religion and act from a spirit of religion. The second, though they have religion, flatter themselves and mistakenly believe they act from religion. And the last, though they wish to appear to act from religion, have at bottom no religion. Now these three kinds of sinners can use the language of the centurion in our Gospel, Lord, I am not worthy, and excuse themselves from receiving Communion because they judge themselves unworthy of it. But although they say it alike, they should not be believed alike. For, to continue unfolding my purpose to you, in the first\u2014that is, sincere sinners\u2014this excuse is a reason; in the second\u2014that is, blind sinners\u2014this excuse is a pretext; and in the last\u2014that is, hypocritical and libertine sinners\u2014this excuse is an abuse and even a scandal: this is what I have to show you. But that is not enough: for to this I add three things which will make these three characters of sinners known to you and ought to be a great instruction for you. To say, I do not receive Communion because I am unworthy of it, is a reason in a sincere sinner; but I say that this reason needs to be clarified. It is a pretext in a blind sinner who flatters himself, and it is important to take this pretext away from him. It is an abuse and a scandal in a hypocritical sinner, and it is my duty to combat this scandal and this abuse: there is the whole subject of your attention.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-h002", "label": "French original", "text": "I. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-h002", "label": "English", "text": "PART ONE.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p006", "label": "French original", "text": "Pour bien expliquer ma premi\u00e8re pens\u00e9e, je parle, Chr\u00e9tiens, d'un p\u00e9cheur, qui ne laisse pas au milieu de ses d\u00e9sordres de conserver le fonds de sa religion ; qui traite au moins de bonne foi & sinc\u00e9rement avec Dieu ; qui reconno\u00eet le malheureux \u00e9tat de sa conscience, qui confesse son p\u00e9ch\u00e9, qui en g\u00e9mit & qui le d\u00e9plore, mais qui ne se sent pas n\u00e9anmoins encore parfaitement dispos\u00e9 \u00e0 le quitter. S'\u00e9loigner alors de la communion, parce que l'on s'en trouve indigne, j'avoue que c'est une raison & une raison tr\u00e8s-bien fond\u00e9e, puisqu'il est \u00e9vident & de la foi m\u00eame, que le p\u00e9cheur, tandis que son p\u00e9ch\u00e9 subsiste, ne peut approcher de ce Sacrement, sans se rendre coupable d'un sacril\u00e9ge. Mais je dis, mes chers Auditeurs, que cette raison a besoin d'\u00eatre \u00e9claircie ; & cet \u00e9claircissement consiste \u00e0 vous faire voir que le p\u00e9cheur n'en doit pas demeurer l\u00e0, c'est-\u00e0-dire, qu'il ne doit pas tellement s'\u00e9loigner de la communion pour son indignit\u00e9, qu'il croye en s'abstenant de participer au divin myst\u00e8re, avoir satisfait pleinement \u00e0 son devoir ; mais qu'il doit \u00eatre persuad\u00e9 d'un autre principe non moins essentiel ni moins incontestable, je veux dire, de l'obligation o\u00f9 il est de sortir au plut\u00f4t & incessamment de l'\u00e9tat de son indignit\u00e9 pour pouvoir \u00eatre admis \u00e0 la table du Seigneur. Ensorte que la communion m\u00eame lui soit un motif, mais un motif pressant, qui le r\u00e9duise \u00e0 la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 de se convertir ; & que dans la v\u00fbe de l'adorable Sacrement dont son p\u00e9ch\u00e9 le tient \u00e9loign\u00e9, il fasse les derniers efforts pour m\u00e9riter par une v\u00e9ritable & prompte p\u00e9nitence de s'en approcher. Voil\u00e0, s'il conno\u00eet bien ses devoirs, la disposition o\u00f9 il doit \u00eatre, & sans laquelle je pr\u00e9tens qu'il n'y a rien de solide dans sa conduite."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p006", "label": "English", "text": "To explain my first thought properly, I am speaking, Christians, of a sinner who, amid his disorders, nevertheless preserves the foundation of his religion; who at least deals with God sincerely and in good faith; who recognizes the unhappy state of his conscience, confesses his sin, groans over it and deplores it, but nevertheless does not yet feel perfectly disposed to leave it. To keep away from Communion then, because one finds oneself unworthy of it, I admit is a reason, and a very well-founded reason, since it is evident and a matter of faith itself that the sinner, while his sin persists, cannot approach this Sacrament without making himself guilty of sacrilege. But I say, my dear listeners, that this reason needs to be clarified; and this clarification consists in showing you that the sinner must not stop there. That is, he must not keep away from Communion because of his unworthiness in such a way that he believes, by abstaining from participation in the divine mystery, that he has fully satisfied his duty. He must be persuaded of another principle no less essential and incontestable: I mean the obligation he has to leave the state of his unworthiness as soon as possible and without delay, so as to be admitted to the Lord's table. Communion itself must thus be a motive for him, but a pressing motive, which brings him to the necessity of conversion; and, in view of the adorable Sacrament from which his sin keeps him away, he must make the utmost efforts to deserve, through true and prompt penance, to approach it. There, if he knows his duties well, is the disposition in which he must be, and without which I maintain that there is nothing sound in his conduct.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p007", "label": "French original", "text": "Car la grande maxime, Chr\u00e9tiens, sur laquelle doit rouler toute la conduite d'un p\u00e9cheur, en ce qui regarde l'usage de la communion, est de ne s\u00e9parer jamais ces deux v\u00e9rit\u00e9s, qui sont deux r\u00e9gles inviolables dans le Christianisme ; l'une, que Jesus-Christ nous commande de manger sa chair ; & l'autre, qu'il nous d\u00e9fend de la manger indignement : l'une que la chair de cet homme-Dieu doit \u00eatre la nourriture de nos ames ; & l'autre, que cette nourriture, quoique par elle-m\u00eame salutaire, devient un poison pour quiconque en use dans l'\u00e9tat du p\u00e9ch\u00e9 : l'une, que comme il est impossible d'entretenir la vie naturelle sans le secours des alimens, aussi est-il impossible d'entretenir, sans la sainte Eucharistie, la vie de la grace ; & l'autre, que comme les alimens dans un corps malade, bien loin de le fortifier & de le nourrir, l'affoiblissent & se tournent en corruption, jusqu'\u00e0 d\u00e9truire le principe de la vie, ainsi la divine Eucharistie cause-t-elle la mort \u00e0 tout homme, qui sans avoir purifi\u00e9 son c\u0153ur, est assez t\u00e9m\u00e9raire pour la recevoir. Si le p\u00e9cheur s'attache \u00e0 l'une de ces v\u00e9rit\u00e9s sans y joindre l'autre, il s'\u00e9gare & il se perd ; mais s'il les embrasse toutes deux, il commence \u00e0 entrer dans la voie de Dieu. Car \u00e9coutez comment il raisonne. Jesus-Christ me d\u00e9fend de manger sa chair, & me s\u00e9pare de lui, tandis que le p\u00e9ch\u00e9 r\u00e9gne en moi ; il ne faut donc pas que je la mange dans l'\u00e9tat pr\u00e9sent o\u00f9 je suis. Mais il m'avertit d'ailleurs, que si je ne la mange pas, je n'ai pas en moi, ni ne puis avoir cette vie surnaturelle qui fait la sanctification & le bonheur des justes ; il faut donc, quoi qu'il m'en co\u00fbte, que je sorte de l'\u00e9tat o\u00f9 je suis, pour me rendre capable de la manger. Je ne puis me dispenser d'ob\u00e9ir \u00e0 l'un & \u00e0 l'autre de ces deux commandemens ; au premier, pour l'int\u00e9r\u00eat de Jesus-Christ ; au second, pour mon int\u00e9r\u00eat propre. Si je communie indignement, je prophane le corps du Seigneur ; voil\u00e0 l'int\u00e9r\u00eat de Jesus-Christ, \u00e0 quoi je dois pourvoir. Si je ne communie pas, je suis homicide de mon ame, en la privant de ce qui seul peut la nourrir & la faire vivre ; voil\u00e0 mon int\u00e9r\u00eat propre, que je dois sauver. Si je mange ce pain des Anges, moi p\u00e9cheur & demeurant p\u00e9cheur, je le mange \u00e0 ma condamnation. Mais d'ailleurs si je ne le mange pas, il est s\u00fbr que je p\u00e9rirai. Il ne me reste donc qu'un parti \u00e0 prendre, & qu'il faut que je prenne n\u00e9cessairement, s\u00e7avoir, de changer de vie, de renoncer \u00e0 mon p\u00e9ch\u00e9, de rentrer en grace avec Dieu, & de me mettre en \u00e9tat de manger ce pain vivant, afin qu'il puisse \u00eatre pour moi un pain vivifiant. Car je satisferai par-l\u00e0 \u00e0 ce qui regarde l'honneur de Jesus-Christ, & je satisferai par-l\u00e0 m\u00eame \u00e0 ce qui regarde mon avantage particulier. Ainsi j'accomplirai tout ce que Dieu exige de moi, qui est que je mange, & que je vive de ce pain en le mangeant utilement. Voil\u00e0, dis-je, comment il raisonnera : & ce raisonnement encore une fois sera la cause d\u00e9terminante & infaillible de sa conversion : au lieu que s'il s'arr\u00eate uniquement \u00e0 son indignit\u00e9, il en demeurera toujours au terme d'une vie criminelle sans rien r\u00e9soudre pour son salut & sans faire aucune d\u00e9marche pour retourner promptement \u00e0 Dieu."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p007", "label": "English", "text": "For the great maxim, Christians, on which a sinner's whole conduct concerning the use of Communion ought to turn is never to separate these two truths, which are two inviolable rules in Christianity: the one, that Jesus Christ commands us to eat his flesh; the other, that he forbids us to eat it unworthily. The one, that the flesh of this God-man must be the nourishment of our souls; the other, that this nourishment, though salutary in itself, becomes poison for whoever uses it in the state of sin. The one, that just as it is impossible to maintain natural life without the help of food, so it is impossible to maintain the life of grace without the holy Eucharist; the other, that just as food in a sick body, far from strengthening and nourishing it, weakens it and turns to corruption until it destroys the principle of life, so the divine Eucharist causes death to every man who, without having purified his heart, is rash enough to receive it. If the sinner attaches himself to one of these truths without joining the other to it, he goes astray and loses himself; but if he embraces both, he begins to enter God's way. For hear how he reasons. Jesus Christ forbids me to eat his flesh, and separates me from himself, while sin reigns in me; I must therefore not eat it in my present state. But he also warns me that if I do not eat it, I do not have, and cannot have, within me that supernatural life which makes the sanctification and happiness of the just. Whatever it costs me, I must therefore leave my present state to make myself capable of eating it. I cannot exempt myself from obeying both of these two commandments: the first, for the interest of Jesus Christ; the second, for my own interest. If I receive Communion unworthily, I profane the Lord's body: there is the interest of Jesus Christ, for which I must provide. If I do not receive Communion, I murder my soul by depriving it of what alone can nourish it and make it live: there is my own interest, which I must save. If I eat this bread of angels, a sinner and remaining a sinner, I eat it to my condemnation. But on the other hand, if I do not eat it, it is certain that I shall perish. There remains, then, only one course for me to take, and one I must necessarily take: to change my life, renounce my sin, return to grace with God, and put myself in a condition to eat this living bread, so that it may be a life-giving bread for me. For by this I shall satisfy what concerns the honor of Jesus Christ, and by this very means I shall satisfy what concerns my own advantage. Thus I shall accomplish all that God requires of me: that I eat, and live by this bread by eating it fruitfully. This, I say, is how he will reason; and this reasoning, once again, will be the determining and unfailing cause of his conversion. Whereas, if he stops solely at his unworthiness, he will always remain confined to a sinful life, resolving nothing for his salvation and taking no step to return promptly to God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p008", "label": "French original", "text": "Or ce principe, Chr\u00e9tiens, que le p\u00e9cheur lui-m\u00eame doit s'appliquer, est encore celui dont les ministres de Jesus-Christ doivent se servir en travaillant \u00e0 son instruction. De ces deux pr\u00e9ceptes que je viens de vous expliquer, ils ne doivent jamais lui repr\u00e9senter l'un sans le faire au m\u00eame tems souvenir de l'autre : pourquoi ? parce que l'un sans l'autre, ne lui peut \u00eatre qu'inutile, ou m\u00eame pr\u00e9judiciable. Car si vous remontrez sans cesse \u00e0 un p\u00e9cheur l'affreux danger d'une communion indigne, sans jamais lui parler de la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 indispensable d'une bonne communion, vous le portez \u00e0 ne communier jamais, contre le commandement du Fils de Dieu : Nisi manducaveritis carnem Filii hominis, non habebitis vitam in vobis. Au contraire, si vous lui parlez seulement de la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 de communier, sans jamais lui faire craindre le danger d'une communion indigne, vous lui donnez lieu de faire bien des communions imparfaites & m\u00eame sacril\u00e9ges, contre le commandement de S. Paul : Probet autem seipsum homo. Et voil\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs, permettez-moi de faire ici une r\u00e9flexion, dont je suis certain que vous conviendrez avec moi, voil\u00e0 quelle a \u00e9t\u00e9 la source de tous les maux qu'a produits la diversit\u00e9 des opinions qu'on a v\u00fbe de tout tems dans l'Eglise, & qui si souvent a partag\u00e9 les esprits touchant l'usage du Sacrement de nos Autels. Les uns bornant leur z\u00e9le \u00e0 intimider les p\u00e9cheurs, pour les \u00e9loigner des saints myst\u00e8res ; & les autres \u00e0 leur donner de la confiance, pour les en approcher : ceux-ci leur r\u00e9p\u00e9tant mille fois ces paroles terribles, Qui manducat indign\u00e8, judicium sibi manducat & bibit ; & ceux-l\u00e0 les invitant toujours par ces paroles consolantes, Qui manducat hunc panem, vivet in aeternum : les premiers r\u00e9duisant toute leur conduite \u00e0 donner horreur des communions indignes ; & les seconds semblant la rapporter toute \u00e0 exciter dans les c\u0153urs le d\u00e9sir d'une sainte communion, ni les uns ni les autres ne s'unissoient parfaitement pour l'ex\u00e9cution des desseins de Jesus-Christ. S'ils \u00e9toient convenus ensemble, on auroit fait de leurs divers sentimens un temp\u00e9rament admirable, dont l'Eglise auroit profit\u00e9, & qui \u00e9toit le grand moyen de sanctifier les p\u00e9cheurs. Mais parce qu'ils ne s'entendoient pas, & que chacun d'eux peut-\u00eatre abondoit en son sens, ni les p\u00e9cheurs ni l'Eglise n'en tiroient l'avantage que Dieu pr\u00e9tendoit. Car ceux qui n'avoient dans la bouche que les anath\u00eames de la parole de Dieu contre les abus de la communion, sans jamais rien dire qui p\u00fbt servir d'attrait \u00e0 ce Sacrement, alloient peu \u00e0 peu \u00e0 en abolir l'usage, & \u00e0 faire disparo\u00eetre de la table de l'Epoux tous les convi\u00e9s : mais ceux aussi qui ne pensoient qu'\u00e0 donner une haute id\u00e9e des fruits de la communion, & qui se proposoient d'attirer \u00e0 la table du Sauveur un grand nombre de convi\u00e9s, se mettoient au hasard, comme les serviteurs de la parabole, d'y attirer indiff\u00e9remment les bons & les mauvais. Ce qu'ils disoient de part & d'autre pouvoit \u00eatre vrai, & cependant ils ne disoient ni de part ni d'autre ce qui devoit produire l'entier effet du Sacrement de Jesus-Christ, parce que chacun n'en disoit qu'une partie. Que falloit-il donc ? c'est la judicieuse remarque du saint Ev\u00eaque de Gen\u00e9ve. Il falloit dire tout, & joindre aux menaces de ceux-ci les invitations de ceux-l\u00e0. Dire aux p\u00e9cheurs : Craignez d'approcher de cette sainte table, & craignez de n'en approcher pas. Craignez d'en approcher, si vous n'avez pas la robe de n\u00f4ces, qui est la grace ; & craignez de n'en approcher pas, parce qu'il n'y a que les ennemis de Dieu qui en soient exclus. La viande qui vous est pr\u00e9sent\u00e9e est mortelle pour vous, si vous n'en faites pas un juste discernement par l'esprit de la foi : mais comprenez aussi que c'est une viande salutaire, sans laquelle le Fils de Dieu ne demeurera point en vous, ni vous en lui. Ainsi, tremblez en recevant cette viande ; car trembler respectueusement, c'est m\u00eame une des dispositions n\u00e9cessaires pour la recevoir : mais tremblez encore davantage si vous ne la recevez pas, parce que vous ne voulez pas y apporter la pr\u00e9paration n\u00e9cessaire. Voil\u00e0 comment il falloit parler."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p008", "label": "English", "text": "Now this principle, Christians, which the sinner himself must apply to himself, is also the one the ministers of Jesus Christ must use in working to instruct him. Of these two precepts I have just explained to you, they must never present one without at the same time reminding him of the other. Why? Because one without the other can only be useless to him, or even harmful. For if you continually show a sinner the dreadful danger of an unworthy Communion, without ever speaking to him of the indispensable necessity of a good Communion, you lead him never to receive Communion, against the commandment of the Son of God: Unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man, you will not have life in you. On the contrary, if you speak to him only of the necessity of receiving Communion, without ever making him fear the danger of an unworthy Communion, you give him occasion to make many imperfect and even sacrilegious Communions, against Saint Paul's commandment: But let a man examine himself. And here, my dear listeners\u2014allow me to make a reflection here with which I am certain you will agree\u2014is the source of all the evils produced by the diversity of opinions which has been seen at all times in the Church and has so often divided minds concerning the use of the Sacrament of our altars. Some limited their zeal to intimidating sinners to keep them away from the holy mysteries; others to giving them confidence to bring them near. The former repeated to them a thousand times these terrible words, Whoever eats unworthily eats and drinks judgment to himself; the latter continually invited them with these consoling words, Whoever eats this bread will live forever. The first reduced all their conduct to inspiring horror of unworthy Communions; the second seemed to direct it entirely to stirring up in hearts the desire for a holy Communion. Neither group united perfectly for the execution of Jesus Christ's designs. Had they agreed together, their diverse views would have formed an admirable balance from which the Church would have profited, and which was the great means of sanctifying sinners. But because they did not understand one another, and each perhaps abounded in his own opinion, neither sinners nor the Church drew from it the benefit God intended. For those who had on their lips only the anathemas of God's word against abuses of Communion, without ever saying anything that might attract people to this Sacrament, gradually tended to abolish its use and make all the guests disappear from the Bridegroom's table. But those too who thought only of giving a high idea of the fruits of Communion, and proposed to draw a great number of guests to the Savior's table, ran the risk, like the servants in the parable, of drawing the good and the bad there indiscriminately. What each side said might be true; yet neither side said what ought to produce the entire effect of Jesus Christ's Sacrament, because each said only a part of it. What, then, was needed? This is the judicious observation of the holy Bishop of Geneva. Everything had to be said, joining the invitations of one group to the threats of the other. Tell sinners: Fear to approach this holy table, and fear not to approach it. Fear to approach it if you do not have the wedding garment, which is grace; and fear not to approach it, because only God's enemies are excluded from it. The food presented to you is deadly for you if you do not rightly discern it by the spirit of faith; but understand also that it is salutary food, without which the Son of God will not dwell in you, nor you in him. Thus tremble in receiving this food; for to tremble respectfully is itself one of the dispositions necessary to receive it. But tremble still more if you do not receive it because you do not want to bring the necessary preparation to it. This is how one ought to have spoken.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p009", "label": "French original", "text": "Et c'est, Chr\u00e9tiens, le langage qu'ont tenu tous les Peres de l'Eglise, quand ils se sont expliqu\u00e9s sur cette mati\u00e8re. Comme ces grands hommes \u00e9toient conduits par l'esprit de Dieu, ils n'ont eu garde de s\u00e9parer ces deux choses, qu'ils s\u00e7avoient bien n'avoir jamais \u00e9t\u00e9 s\u00e9par\u00e9es dans l'intention du Sauveur du monde. Eprouvons-nous, dit S. Chrysostome, & jugeons-nous, de peur qu'en participant au corps de Jesus Christ, nous n'attirions sur nos t\u00eates des charbons de feu, c'est-\u00e0-dire, l'indignation de Dieu & ses vengeances. Car ainsi ce Pere s'exprimoit-il ; & ces paroles \u00e9toient capables d'inspirer aux fid\u00e8les qui l'\u00e9coutoient, de la frayeur. Mais en m\u00eame-tems il y ajo\u00fbtoit le correctif : Or je ne vous dis point ceci afin que vous n'y participiez pas ; \u00e0 Dieu ne plaise ! mais pour vous engager \u00e0 y participer avec les dispositions & selon les r\u00e9gles que la loi de Dieu vous prescrit : Hoc autem non dico ut non accedatis, sed ut temer\u00e8 non accedatis. Car de m\u00eame, poursuivoit-il que d'y participer indiscr\u00e9tement, c'est s'exposer \u00e0 se perdre ; aussi n'y point participer, c'est la ruine & la mort de l'homme Chr\u00e9tien : Nam sicut temer\u00e8 accedere periculum est, ita omnino non accedere fames est & mors. J'en vois parmi vous, disoit S. Augustin, qui se retirent de la communion, parce qu'ils se sentent coupables ; Adverto nonnullos ex vobis communionem declinare, idque ex conscientia gravium delictorum. Et moi, reprenoit-il, d\u00e9cision importante de ce saint Docteur, je leur d\u00e9clare que s'ils s'en tiennent pr\u00e9cis\u00e9ment l\u00e0, ils ne font qu'augmenter le poids & le nombre de leurs p\u00e9ch\u00e9s, en commettant encore un nouveau p\u00e9ch\u00e9, & se privant du plus n\u00e9cessaire & du plus souverain rem\u00e9de : Hoc est enim reatum congregare, & remedium declinare. Je vous conjure donc, mes Freres, concluoit-il, que si quelqu'un de vous se juge indigne de la communion, il travaille \u00e0 s'en rendre digne, parce que quiconque n'est pas digne de ce Sacrement, n'est pas digne de Dieu : Quapropter hortor vos, Fratres, ut si quis ex vobis indignum se communione Ecclesiastica putat, dignum se faciat. Voil\u00e0 comment parloient les Peres. Or ce qu'ils disoient g\u00e9n\u00e9ralement & absolument, est encore plus vrai par rapport \u00e0 ce saint tems o\u00f9 le pr\u00e9cepte de Jesus-Christ, d\u00e9termin\u00e9 par celui de l'Eglise, impose aux fid\u00e8les une obligation expresse & particuli\u00e8re de communier. Telle est la solemnit\u00e9 de P\u00e2que, \u00e0 laquelle nous devons nous pr\u00e9parer chaque jour de ce Car\u00eame, & qui ne peut \u00eatre c\u00e9l\u00e9br\u00e9e dans le Christianisme que par la manducation de l'Agneau, qui est Jesus-Christ. Car se contenter alors de menacer un p\u00e9cheur de la col\u00e8re de Dieu, s'il est assez t\u00e9m\u00e9raire pour communier dans l'\u00e9tat de son p\u00e9ch\u00e9, & ne le pas menacer de la col\u00e8re du m\u00eame Dieu, s'il ne quitte son p\u00e9ch\u00e9 & s'il ne communie pour satisfaire \u00e0 ce commandement, Nisi manducaveritis ; c'est ne l'instruire qu'\u00e0 demi, & lui donner lieu de fomenter par-l\u00e0 son imp\u00e9nitence. Il faut lui signifier l'ordre du ma\u00eetre, j'entends du grand ma\u00eetre, en lui disant ce que le Sauveur par deux de ses disciples envoya dire \u00e0 cet homme dont il avoit choisi la maison pour y faire la p\u00e2que : Magister dicit, apud te facio pascha. C'est chez vous, mon Frere, ainsi doit-on parler \u00e0 un p\u00e9cheur, c'est chez vous ou plut\u00f4t dans vous que le myst\u00e8re de la p\u00e2que doit \u00eatre accompli ; puisque le tems approche o\u00f9 Jesus-Christ qui est la v\u00e9ritable p\u00e2que des Chr\u00e9tiens, veut & doit \u00eatre re\u00e7\u00fb de vous dans l'adorable Eucharistie Vous n'y \u00eates pas dispos\u00e9 ; mais c'est pour cela-m\u00eame qu'on vous l'annonce de bonne heure, afin que vous vous y disposiez, & que vous vous y disposiez s\u00e9rieusement, promptement, efficacement. Car il n'y a point ici de milieu pour vous. Demeurant dans votre p\u00e9ch\u00e9, & ne vous disposant pas, vous ne pouvez \u00e9viter d'\u00eatre, ou un prophanateur, ou un d\u00e9serteur du Sacrement de Jesus Christ : un prophanateur, si vous mangez cette p\u00e2que sans vous y \u00eatre pr\u00e9par\u00e9 par une conversion sinc\u00e9re ; un d\u00e9serteur, si faute de pr\u00e9paration & de conversion vous vous trouvez hors d'\u00e9tat de la manger. De pr\u00e9tendre qu'on a eu tort de vous r\u00e9duire \u00e0 cette extr\u00e9mit\u00e9, c'est vouloir controller la conduite, & de l'Eglise qui est votre mere, & de Jesus-Christ qui est votre Dieu. De dire que cette extr\u00e9mit\u00e9 peut vous porter \u00e0 des abus, c'est vouloir vous justifier par votre propre d\u00e9sordre, qui consiste \u00e0 abuser de tout, m\u00eame des choses les plus saintes. Quoi qu'il en soit, voici la peine dont l'Eglise, en vertu du pouvoir qu'elle a de lier & de d\u00e9lier, est en droit selon les Canons de punir votre d\u00e9sob\u00e9issance ; s\u00e7avoir, de vous retrancher de sa communion comme un membre scandaleux, quand par l'endurcissement de votre c\u0153ur, ou par un attachement opini\u00e2tre \u00e0 l'objet de votre passion, vous venez \u00e0 vous s\u00e9parer vous-m\u00eame de la communion du corps de Jesus-Christ. Elle n'a point pr\u00e9tendu par-l\u00e0 vous dresser un pi\u00e9ge, ni vous exposer au p\u00e9ril d'ajo\u00fbter p\u00e9ch\u00e9 sur p\u00e9ch\u00e9 ; mais comme une mere z\u00e9l\u00e9e, elle a pr\u00e9tendu vous faire un devoir n\u00e9cessaire, un devoir indispensable de ce qu'il y a dans le Christianisme que vous professez, de plus salutaire pour vous & de plus sacr\u00e9. Pour cela il faut rompre vos liens, & sortir des engagemens criminels o\u00f9 vous \u00eates ; mais c'est justement \u00e0 quoi tend le pr\u00e9cepte de la communion. Pour cela il faut arracher l'\u0153il qui vous scandalise, c'est-\u00e0-dire, renoncer \u00e0 ce commerce qui est le scandale de votre vie ; mais c'est en quoi vous devez admirer le pr\u00e9cepte de la communion, qui vous force, pour ainsi dire, \u00e0 ce qui doit faire selon Dieu tout votre bonheur."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p009", "label": "English", "text": "And this, Christians, is the language all the Fathers of the Church used when they explained themselves on this matter. Since these great men were led by the spirit of God, they took care not to separate these two things, which they well knew had never been separated in the intention of the Savior of the world. Let us examine ourselves, says Saint Chrysostom, and judge ourselves, lest in participating in the body of Jesus Christ we draw coals of fire upon our heads\u2014that is, God's indignation and his vengeance. For this was how that Father expressed himself; and these words could inspire fear in the faithful who heard him. But at the same time he added the qualification: I do not say this so that you should not participate; God forbid! But to engage you to participate with the dispositions and according to the rules God's law prescribes for you: I do not say this so that you should not approach, but so that you should not approach rashly. For just as, he continued, to participate indiscreetly is to expose oneself to ruin, so not to participate at all is the ruin and death of the Christian man: For just as to approach rashly is a danger, so not to approach at all is hunger and death. I see some among you, Saint Augustine said, who withdraw from Communion because they feel guilty: I observe that some of you turn away from Communion, and that from consciousness of grave offenses. And I, he continued\u2014an important decision of this holy Doctor\u2014declare to them that if they stop precisely there, they only increase the weight and number of their sins, committing yet another sin and depriving themselves of the most necessary and most sovereign remedy: For this is to accumulate guilt and turn away from the remedy. I therefore beseech you, my brothers, he concluded, that if any of you judges himself unworthy of Communion, he work to make himself worthy of it, because whoever is not worthy of this Sacrament is not worthy of God: Therefore I exhort you, brothers, that if any of you thinks himself unworthy of ecclesiastical Communion, he make himself worthy. This is how the Fathers spoke. Now what they said generally and absolutely is still truer in relation to this holy season when the precept of Jesus Christ, specified by that of the Church, imposes on the faithful an express and particular obligation to receive Communion. Such is the solemnity of Easter, for which we must prepare each day of this Lent, and which can be celebrated in Christianity only by eating the Lamb, who is Jesus Christ. For to be content then to threaten a sinner with God's anger if he is rash enough to receive Communion in the state of his sin, and not to threaten him with the same God's anger if he does not leave his sin and receive Communion to satisfy this commandment, Unless you eat, is to instruct him only halfway and give him occasion thereby to foster his impenitence. The master's order must be made known to him\u2014I mean the great master's\u2014by saying to him what the Savior sent two of his disciples to say to the man whose house he had chosen for keeping the Passover: The master says, At your house I keep the Passover. It is at your house, my brother\u2014this is how one ought to speak to a sinner\u2014it is at your house, or rather within you, that the mystery of Easter must be accomplished, since the time is approaching when Jesus Christ, who is the true Passover of Christians, wishes and ought to be received by you in the adorable Eucharist. You are not disposed for it; but that is precisely why it is announced to you in good time, so that you may dispose yourself, and dispose yourself seriously, promptly, effectively. For there is no middle course for you here. Remaining in your sin, and not disposing yourself, you cannot avoid being either a profaner or a deserter of the Sacrament of Jesus Christ: a profaner if you eat this Passover without having prepared yourself for it by sincere conversion; a deserter if, for lack of preparation and conversion, you find yourself unable to eat it. To maintain that it was wrong to reduce you to this extremity is to wish to pass judgment on the conduct both of the Church, who is your mother, and of Jesus Christ, who is your God. To say that this extremity may lead you to abuses is to wish to justify yourself by your own disorder, which consists in abusing everything, even the holiest things. However that may be, here is the penalty with which the Church, by virtue of her power to bind and loose, is entitled according to the canons to punish your disobedience: to cut you off from her communion as a scandalous member, when by the hardening of your heart, or an obstinate attachment to the object of your passion, you come to separate yourself from communion with the body of Jesus Christ. She did not intend by this to lay a trap for you or expose you to the danger of adding sin upon sin; but, like a zealous mother, she intended to make a necessary and indispensable duty for you of what is most salutary for you and most sacred in the Christianity you profess. For this you must break your bonds and leave the criminal commitments in which you are involved; but this is precisely the aim of the precept of Communion. For this you must pluck out the eye that causes you to stumble\u2014that is, renounce the relationship that is the scandal of your life; but in this you should admire the precept of Communion, which forces you, so to speak, to what ought, according to God, to make all your happiness.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p010", "label": "French original", "text": "Et en effet, quel a \u00e9t\u00e9 le dessein de l'Eglise, quand elle a \u00e9tabli ces loix rigoureuses contre les p\u00e9cheurs endurcis, qui d\u00e9sob\u00e9issent \u00e0 ses ordres, & qui n\u00e9gligent de c\u00e9l\u00e9brer la p\u00e2que ? Elle a voulu les obliger, les n\u00e9cessiter, & puisque le Saint-Esprit m\u00eame s'en explique ainsi, le contraindre en quelque mani\u00e8re \u00e0 se purifier par la p\u00e9nitence pour m\u00e9riter d'\u00eatre admis \u00e0 la table de Jesus-Christ : Compelle intrare. Voil\u00e0 l'utile contrainte dont elle usoit autrefois, & la sainte violence qu'elle faisoit \u00e0 ces sortes de p\u00e9cheurs. Car tout p\u00e9cheurs qu'ils \u00e9toient, ne cessant pas d'\u00eatre Chr\u00e9tiens & ses enfans, elle se promettoit de leur religion & de leur loi, qu'ils ne seroient jamais assez endurcis pour se pr\u00e9senter \u00e0 cette table sans s'\u00eatre auparavant bien \u00e9prouv\u00e9s. Aussi, touch\u00e9s eux-m\u00eames, quoique p\u00e9cheurs, d'un respect religieux & d'une profonde v\u00e9n\u00e9ration pour ce Sacrement, ils faisoient dans la v\u00fbe de le recevoir, ce que jamais sans cela ils n'auroient fait ; je veux dire, qu'on voyoit en eux des changemens & des r\u00e9formes, \u00e0 quoi tout autre motif ne les auroit jamais engag\u00e9s. Cette obligation de manger la chair d'un Dieu, & d'ailleurs cette horreur de la manger indignement, voil\u00e0 ce qui les convertissoit, voil\u00e0 ce qui leur faisoit prendre toutes les mesures n\u00e9cessaires pour rentrer en grace avec Dieu, voil\u00e0 ce qui arrachoit de leurs c\u0153urs les passions les plus dominantes. Vous me direz encore une fois, que de-l\u00e0 venoient aussi les sacril\u00e9ges : & moi je r\u00e9pons qu'il n'y a rien en effet de si sacr\u00e9, que l'homme ne puisse prophaner ; mais qu'il est toujours vrai, que le danger de cette prophanation n'a point emp\u00each\u00e9 le Sauveur du monde, d'obliger tous les fid\u00e8les \u00e0 manger sa chair sous peine d'une \u00e9ternelle mort ; & que l'Eglise son Epouse n'auroit pas agi conform\u00e9ment \u00e0 ses intentions, si dans le m\u00eame tems qu'elle publie aux fid\u00e8les l'anath\u00eame de S. Paul contre les communions indignes, elle ne les r\u00e9duisoit par ses censures \u00e0 l'heureuse n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 d'en faire de saintes & de profitables."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p010", "label": "English", "text": "And indeed, what was the Church's purpose when she established those rigorous laws against hardened sinners who disobey her orders and neglect their Easter duty? She wished to oblige them, to put them under necessity, and, since the Holy Spirit himself speaks in this way, to compel them in some manner to purify themselves by penance so as to deserve admission to the table of Jesus Christ: Compel them to enter. This is the useful constraint she formerly employed, and the holy violence she did to these kinds of sinners. For, sinners though they were, since they did not cease to be Christians and her children, she expected of their religion and law that they would never be hardened enough to present themselves at this table without first having examined themselves well. Thus, though sinners, themselves touched by religious respect and profound veneration for this Sacrament, they did, with a view to receiving it, what they would never have done without it: I mean that changes and reforms were seen in them to which no other motive would ever have led them. This obligation to eat the flesh of a God, and on the other hand this horror of eating it unworthily: this is what converted them, what made them take all the necessary measures to return to grace with God, what tore from their hearts the most dominant passions. You will tell me once again that sacrileges also came from this; and I answer that there is indeed nothing so sacred that man cannot profane it. But it remains true that the danger of this profanation did not prevent the Savior of the world from obliging all the faithful to eat his flesh under pain of eternal death; and that the Church, his Spouse, would not have acted in conformity with his intentions if, at the same time as she publishes to the faithful Saint Paul's anathema against unworthy Communions, she did not bring them by her censures to the happy necessity of making holy and fruitful ones.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p011", "label": "French original", "text": "Cependant, pour ne pas joindre ces deux v\u00e9rit\u00e9s, voici, mes chers Auditeurs, les deux \u00e9cueils, o\u00f9 conduit aujourd'hui l'esprit du si\u00e9cle. Pourv\u00fb qu'on persuade \u00e0 un p\u00e9cheur, & qu'on obtienne de lui qu'il fasse au dehors son devoir de Chr\u00e9tien, & qu'il approche des autels, on croit avoir beaucoup gagn\u00e9. Avec cela, & cela seul, on loue sa religion, on ne doute point de sa conversion, on se promet tout de sa pers\u00e9v\u00e9rance : c'est le premier \u00e9cueil. Mais d'ailleurs aussi, pourv\u00fb qu'on fasse entendre \u00e0 un p\u00e9cheur qu'il n'y a point de communion pour lui, tandis qu'il est dans l'habitude de son p\u00e9ch\u00e9, on croit avoir tout fait ; & si ce p\u00e9cheur confessant son indignit\u00e9, se tient \u00e9loign\u00e9 des autels, on en est content, comme s'il avoit accompli toute la justice : avec cela, qu'il pers\u00e9v\u00e9re dans son libertinage, on le tol\u00e8re, on le souffre. Vous diriez que l'\u00e9loignement de la communion mette tout le reste \u00e0 couvert, & qu'il lui soit permis alors de vivre avec impunit\u00e9, & selon tous les d\u00e9sirs de son c\u0153ur. Du premier de ces deux abus que s'ensuit-il ? que parmi ceux qui communient, il y en a tant de foibles, tant d'assoupis & de languissans, & pour user du terme de S. Paul, tant qui dorment du sommeil de la mort : Ide\u00f2 inter vos multi infirmi & imbecilles, & dormiunt multi. Et qu'arrive-t-il du second ? que parmi ceux qui ne communient pas, il y en a tant de scandaleux, qui sont aujourd'hui comme en possession de ne donner plus \u00e0 l'Eglise nulle marque de Christianisme, puisque la plus essentielle marque qui nous distingue en qualit\u00e9 de Chr\u00e9tiens, est, selon l'Ap\u00f4tre, la participation du corps de Jesus-Christ. De-l\u00e0 vient que par un exc\u00e8s de rel\u00e2chement, & m\u00eame par une malheureuse prescription, on ne s'\u00e9tonne presque plus de voir des mondains & des mondaines, qui de notori\u00e9t\u00e9 publique semblent depuis plusieurs ann\u00e9es s'\u00eatre eux-m\u00eames librement & volontairement excommuni\u00e9s : & qu'au m\u00e9pris de la religion, ces canons & ces loix si saintes, qui punissoient un tel d\u00e9sordre, ne sont, ou paroissent n'\u00eatre plus de nul usage. D\u00e9cadence qui plonge dans l'amertume les vrais Pasteurs, & qui les jette dans le trouble, lorsqu'ils sont t\u00e9moins de la perte de tant d'ames. Et tout cela, je le r\u00e9p\u00e8te, parce qu'on n'instruit pas assez les p\u00e9cheurs de leurs devoirs, parce qu'on ne leur en fait pas conno\u00eetre toute l'\u00e9tendue, parce qu'on leur fait seulement \u00e9viter un scandale par un autre scandale ; le scandale de la mauvaise communion par le scandale de l'imp\u00e9nitence & de l'irreligion ; ou le scandale de l'irreligion & de l'imp\u00e9nitence par le scandale de la mauvaise communion : au lieu de leur faire bien entendre, qu'il ne suffit pas de retrancher l'un ou l'autre scandale, mais qu'il faut tout \u00e0 la fois se pr\u00e9server de l'un & de l'autre."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p011", "label": "English", "text": "Yet, through failing to join these two truths, here, my dear listeners, are the two reefs toward which the spirit of the age leads today. Provided one persuades a sinner, and obtains from him that he outwardly perform his Christian duty and approach the altars, one believes much has been gained. With this, and this alone, his religion is praised, his conversion is not doubted, everything is expected of his perseverance: this is the first reef. But on the other hand, provided one makes a sinner understand that there is no Communion for him while he remains in the habit of his sin, one believes everything has been done. And if this sinner, confessing his unworthiness, stays away from the altars, one is satisfied, as if he had fulfilled all justice. With this, let him persevere in his libertinism: he is tolerated and endured. You would say that keeping away from Communion shelters all the rest, and that he is then allowed to live with impunity and according to all the desires of his heart. What follows from the first of these two abuses? That among those who receive Communion there are so many weak, so many drowsy and languishing people, and, to use Saint Paul's expression, so many who sleep the sleep of death: Therefore among you many are infirm and weak, and many sleep. And what comes of the second? That among those who do not receive Communion there are so many scandalous people who today seem to have acquired the right to give the Church no further mark of Christianity, since the most essential mark distinguishing us as Christians is, according to the Apostle, participation in the body of Jesus Christ. Hence, through an excess of laxity, and even through an unhappy prescriptive custom, people are scarcely surprised any longer to see worldly men and women who, to public knowledge, seem for several years to have freely and voluntarily excommunicated themselves; and, in contempt of religion, those canons and laws so holy which punished such a disorder are, or appear to be, no longer of any use. A decline which plunges true pastors into bitterness and casts them into trouble when they witness the loss of so many souls. And all this, I repeat, because sinners are not sufficiently instructed in their duties, because their full extent is not made known to them, because they are only made to avoid one scandal by another scandal: the scandal of bad Communion by the scandal of impenitence and irreligion, or the scandal of irreligion and impenitence by the scandal of bad Communion. Instead, they ought to be made to understand clearly that it is not enough to remove one or the other scandal, but that both must be guarded against together.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p012", "label": "French original", "text": "Car c'est pour les p\u00e9cheurs, \u00f4 mon Dieu, comme pour les justes, que votre Sacrement est institu\u00e9. Je ne dis pas pour les p\u00e9cheurs imp\u00e9nitens, mais pour les p\u00e9cheurs convertis, pour les p\u00e9cheurs chang\u00e9s & sanctifi\u00e9s. Tandis que vous \u00e9tiez sur la terre, adorable Sauveur, vous n'avez pas d\u00e9daign\u00e9 de manger \u00e0 la table des p\u00e9cheurs ; maintenant par une conduite bien diff\u00e9rente, mais toujours par le m\u00eame esprit, vous admettez les p\u00e9cheurs p\u00e9nitens \u00e0 votre table : & comme autrefois vous mangiez \u00e0 la table de ces p\u00e9cheurs que votre grace convertissoit, bien plus volontiers qu'\u00e0 la table des Pharisiens orgueilleux & superbes ; aussi puis-je dire pour la consolation de mes Auditeurs & pour la mienne, qu'il n'y a point de Chr\u00e9tiens plus favorablement re\u00e7\u00fbs de vous, que les p\u00e9cheurs qui se convertissent, & qui renoncent \u00e0 leur p\u00e9ch\u00e9, pour se rapprocher de vous. Mais cela, comme j'ai dit, suppose, que ce sont des p\u00e9cheurs sinc\u00e9res & qui agissent de bonne foi ; car si ce sont des mondains qui s'aveuglent & qui se flattent, le respect pr\u00e9tendu qu'ils all\u00e9guent pour s'\u00e9loigner du Sacrement de Jesus-Christ, n'est plus une raison \u00e0 \u00e9claircir, mais un pr\u00e9texte que je dois lever dans la seconde partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p012", "label": "English", "text": "For it is for sinners, O my God, as for the just, that your Sacrament is instituted. I do not say for impenitent sinners, but for converted sinners, for changed and sanctified sinners. While you were on earth, adorable Savior, you did not disdain to eat at sinners' tables; now, by a very different manner of acting, but always in the same spirit, you admit penitent sinners to your table. And as formerly you ate at the table of those sinners whom your grace converted far more willingly than at the table of proud and haughty Pharisees, so I can say, for my listeners' consolation and my own, that no Christians are received more favorably by you than sinners who are converted and renounce their sin to draw near to you again. But this, as I have said, supposes that they are sincere sinners acting in good faith. For if they are worldly people who blind and flatter themselves, the supposed respect they allege for keeping away from the Sacrament of Jesus Christ is no longer a reason to clarify, but a pretext I must remove in the second part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-h003", "label": "French original", "text": "II. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-h003", "label": "English", "text": "PART TWO.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p013", "label": "French original", "text": "Il n'est rien de plus subtil que l'esprit du monde pour nous conduire \u00e0 ses fins, ni rien de plus artificieux pour donner aux choses la couleur & la forme qu'il lui pla\u00eet, quand il s'agit de nous \u00e9blouir & de nous tromper dans le discernement que nous avons \u00e0 faire des voies de Dieu. Car il n'y a point alors de motif sp\u00e9cieux qu'il ne nous propose ; & souvent nous nous y laissons surprendre, jusques \u00e0 nous persuader & \u00e0 croire, qu'en nous \u00e9loignant m\u00eame de Dieu, nous honorons Dieu."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p013", "label": "English", "text": "There is nothing subtler than the spirit of the world in leading us to its ends, nor anything more artful in giving things whatever color and form it pleases, when it is a question of dazzling and deceiving us in the discernment we must make of God's ways. For there is then no plausible motive it does not propose to us; and often we let ourselves be taken in by it, even to the point of persuading ourselves and believing that, in withdrawing even from God, we honor God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p014", "label": "French original", "text": "Or voil\u00e0 le caract\u00e8re de ces autres p\u00e9cheurs, dont j'ai pr\u00e9sentement \u00e0 vous parler ; je veux dire, de ces mondains, qui se flattant d'avoir de la religion, & d'agir par esprit de religion, se trompent eux-m\u00eames ; & qui s'\u00e9cartant du chemin droit & simple de la v\u00e9rit\u00e9, se font une erreur grossi\u00e9re de leur pr\u00e9tendue humilit\u00e9. Je m'explique. Ils disent, & m\u00eame ils le pensent, que c'est par respect qu'ils se retirent de la communion, parce qu'ils conviennent devant Dieu qu'ils en sont indignes. Et moi je soutiens, que ce respect dans eux est un vain respect. Je pr\u00e9tens, & je vais leur d\u00e9montrer, que ce respect dans l'usage qu'ils en font, & \u00e0 l'examiner dans ses circonstances, est un faux respect. Enfin j'ajo\u00fbte, que c'est un respect qui n'a nulle conformit\u00e9 avec celui qu'ont fait paro\u00eetre dans tous les tems les vrais Chr\u00e9tiens, quand ils se sont s\u00e9par\u00e9s du Sacrement de Jesus-Christ selon les r\u00e9gles & l'esprit de l'Eglise. Trois importantes r\u00e9flexions par o\u00f9 j'entreprens, non pas de les confondre, mais de confondre dans leurs personnes l'esprit du monde qui les aveugle, & qui pour les attirer dans le pr\u00e9cipice & pour les perdre, fait luire \u00e0 leurs yeux un faux jour de d\u00e9votion jusques dans leur ind\u00e9votion m\u00eame."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p014", "label": "English", "text": "Now this is the character of those other sinners of whom I now have to speak to you: I mean those worldly people who, flattering themselves that they have religion and act from a spirit of religion, deceive themselves; and who, straying from the straight and simple path of truth, make a gross error of their supposed humility. Let me explain. They say, and even think, that it is out of respect that they withdraw from Communion, because they acknowledge before God that they are unworthy of it. And I maintain that this respect in them is an empty respect. I assert, and shall demonstrate to them, that this respect, in the use they make of it and when examined in its circumstances, is a false respect. Finally, I add that it is a respect having no conformity with that which true Christians have shown at all times when they have been separated from the Sacrament of Jesus Christ according to the Church's rules and spirit. Three important reflections by which I undertake, not to put them to shame, but to put to shame in their persons the spirit of the world which blinds them and, to draw them over the precipice and destroy them, makes a false light of devotion shine before their eyes even in their very lack of devotion.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p015", "label": "French original", "text": "Je dis que c'est un vain respect ; en voici la preuve. Car qu'est-ce que j'appelle vain respect ? celui qui n'op\u00e9re rien, qui n'est suivi de rien, qui n'aboutit \u00e0 rien, qui n'engage \u00e0 rien, qui ne fait rien faire pour se rendre moins indigne de Jesus-Christ & de son sacrement ; celui qui laisse toujours le p\u00e9cheur dans ses m\u00eames imperfections, qui ne le rend ni plus fervent, ni plus r\u00e9gulier, ni plus saint ; en un mot, celui dont l'unique marque est de ne pas communier. N'est-ce pas l\u00e0 \u00e9videmment un respect inutile & sans fruit ? Or tel est le respect de ces p\u00e9cheurs \u00e0 qui j'adresse cette seconde instruction ; & s'ils s\u00e7avent se faire justice, ils seront les premiers \u00e0 le reconno\u00eetre. Et en effet, si le respect qu'ils ont, ou qu'ils croient avoir pour Jesus Christ, \u00e9toit le vrai motif qui les \u00e9loign\u00e2t de la communion, ce motif \u00e0 force d'agir & de faire impression sur eux, les engageroit \u00e0 quelque chose de plus ; & pour peu qu'il e\u00fbt d'efficace, au moins paro\u00eetroit-il dans leur conduite qu'ils en sont touch\u00e9s. Or c'est ce qui ne paro\u00eet en aucune sorte. Car \u00e0 quoi ce motif, s'ils en \u00e9toient r\u00e9ellement touch\u00e9s, \u00e0 quoi dans la pratique ce sentiment de respect les porteroit-il ? \u00e0 se d\u00e9tacher du monde, puisque c'est de leur propre aveu l'amour du monde qui les rend indignes de la table du Fils de Dieu. P\u00e9n\u00e9tr\u00e9s qu'ils seroient de leur indignit\u00e9, & reconnoissant que leur indignit\u00e9 vient de la passion malheureuse qu'ils ont pour le monde, pour les fausses joies du monde, pour les divertissemens peu Chr\u00e9tiens & dangereux du monde, pour les intrigues du monde, pour la vanit\u00e9 & le luxe du monde, que feroient-ils ? ils se priveroient de ces divertissemens, ils s'interdiroient ces plaisirs, ils retrancheroient ce luxe, ils renonceroient \u00e0 cette vanit\u00e9, ils quitteroient ces intrigues ; & par ce sacrifice parfait qu'ils en feroient \u00e0 Jesus-Christ, d'indignes qu'ils sont de manger sa chair ils commenceroient \u00e0 s'en rendre dignes. Ce sont l\u00e0 les solides t\u00e9moignages qu'ils lui donneroient, & qu'ils devroient lui donner de leur respect. Ils ne font rien de tout cela ; & \u00e0 juger d'eux par leurs \u0153uvres, on ne peut pas croire qu'ils y ayent encore la moindre disposition. Eux-m\u00eames, si j'en attestois leurs consciences, ils avoueroient qu'ils en sont tr\u00e8s-\u00e9loign\u00e9s. Il n'est donc pas vrai que ce respect les touche autant qu'ils le pr\u00e9tendent : ce n'est donc pas ce respect qui les emp\u00eache d'approcher des divins myst\u00e8res. Mais quoi ? je l'ai dit, & je le redis : un attachement opini\u00e2tre au monde, & \u00e0 tout ce qui s'appelle monde. Ils sont du monde ; & ce monde que Dieu r\u00e9prouve, ne go\u00fbte point Jesus-Christ. Ils aiment le monde plus que Jesus-Christ, & voil\u00e0 pourquoi ils quittent Jesus-Christ pour le monde. Cette apparence de respect n'est qu'un voile dont ils se couvrent, & dont leur amour-propre se fait honneur. Mais au fond, c'est le monde qui les poss\u00e9de, & qui leur inspire pour la communion cette froideur, cette indiff\u00e9rence, disons mieux, ce d\u00e9go\u00fbt."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p015", "label": "English", "text": "I say that it is an empty respect; here is the proof. For what do I call empty respect? That which produces nothing, is followed by nothing, leads to nothing, commits to nothing, makes a person do nothing to render himself less unworthy of Jesus Christ and his Sacrament; that which always leaves the sinner in his same imperfections, makes him neither more fervent, more orderly, nor holier; in a word, that whose only sign is not to receive Communion. Is this not plainly a useless and fruitless respect? Now such is the respect of those sinners to whom I address this second instruction; and if they know how to judge themselves fairly, they will be the first to acknowledge it. Indeed, if the respect they have, or believe they have, for Jesus Christ were the true motive keeping them away from Communion, this motive, by acting and making an impression on them, would engage them to do something more; and if it had even a little efficacy, at least their conduct would show that they were touched by it. Now this does not appear in any way. For to what would this motive lead them, if they were truly touched by it? To what would this feeling of respect lead them in practice? To detach themselves from the world, since by their own admission it is love of the world that makes them unworthy of the Son of God's table. Penetrated by their unworthiness, and recognizing that it comes from their unhappy passion for the world, for the world's false joys, for its unchristian and dangerous amusements, its intrigues, its vanity and luxury, what would they do? They would deprive themselves of these amusements, forbid themselves these pleasures, cut off this luxury, renounce this vanity, leave these intrigues; and by the perfect sacrifice they would make of them to Jesus Christ, from being unworthy to eat his flesh they would begin to make themselves worthy of it. These are the solid testimonies of their respect they would give him and ought to give him. They do none of this; and, judging them by their works, one cannot believe they yet have the slightest disposition for it. They themselves, if I called their consciences to witness, would confess that they are very far from it. It is therefore not true that this respect touches them as much as they claim; it is therefore not this respect that prevents them from approaching the divine mysteries. But what is it? I have said it, and I say it again: an obstinate attachment to the world and everything called the world. They are of the world; and this world, which God condemns, has no taste for Jesus Christ. They love the world more than Jesus Christ, and this is why they leave Jesus Christ for the world. This appearance of respect is only a veil with which they cover themselves and in which their self-love takes pride. But at bottom it is the world that possesses them and inspires in them this coldness, this indifference\u2014let us say better, this distaste\u2014for Communion.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p016", "label": "French original", "text": "Et c'est ce que le Sauveur lui-m\u00eame a voulu nous faire comprendre dans la parabole des convi\u00e9s qui n\u00e9glig\u00e9rent de venir au festin, parce que d'autres soins leur occupoient l'esprit & le c\u0153ur. Avec cette diff\u00e9rence bien remarquable, reprend saint Augustin, qu'au moins les convi\u00e9s de la parabole confess\u00e9rent de bonne foi les vraies raisons qui les arr\u00eat\u00e9rent : au lieu que ces mondains dont il est ici question, affectent de ne pas conno\u00eetre, & se cachent \u00e0 eux-m\u00eames la cause de leur d\u00e9sordre ; se pr\u00e9valant toujours de ce vain pr\u00e9texte, qu'indignes qu'ils sont de communier, le meilleur pour eux est de s'en abstenir ; se consolant int\u00e9rieurement, comme s'ils honoroient par-l\u00e0 Jesus-Christ, & que Jesus-Christ d\u00fbt un jour les r\u00e9compenser de ce qu'ils abandonnent ses autels, pour jouir plus en repos & avec plus de libert\u00e9 des plaisirs du si\u00e9cle. Car voil\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs, jusqu'o\u00f9 va leur aveuglement. Et pour les convaincre, ajo\u00fbtoit saint Chrysostome, (ceci paro\u00eet sans r\u00e9plique) pour les convaincre, que par rapport \u00e0 eux ce pr\u00e9tendu respect n'est qu'un pr\u00e9texte, & non pas une raison, c'est que pour communier plus rarement, ils n'en communient pas plus dignement, c'est-\u00e0-dire, que lorsqu'ils communient, ils ne s'y disposent pas mieux, qu'ils ne s'\u00e9prouvent pas avec plus de soin, qu'ils ne s'en s\u00e9parent pas plus du monde, & si j'ose ainsi m'expliquer, que pour recevoir chez eux Jesus Christ, ils ne s'en mettent pas plus en frais, se persuadant par la plus fausse de toutes les maximes, que communier peu sans y rien ajo\u00fbter de plus, doit leur tenir lieu de m\u00e9rite & de tout m\u00e9rite ; & par une visible erreur, dont ils ne s'apper\u00e7oivent pas, mesurant tout le respect qu'ils rendent au divin myst\u00e8re, non par plus d'attention sur eux-m\u00eames, non par plus de fid\u00e9lit\u00e9 \u00e0 leurs devoirs, non par plus d'exactitude ni plus de r\u00e9gularit\u00e9, mais par l'intervalle & l'espace de tems qu'ils mettent entre une communion & l'autre : Non munditiam animi, sed intervalla temporis longioris meritum putantes. Marque infaillible, dit ce Pere, que ce n'est ni humilit\u00e9 ni respect, mais une illusion toute pure de l'esprit du monde qui les s\u00e9duit."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p016", "label": "English", "text": "And this is what the Savior himself wished to make us understand in the parable of the guests who neglected to come to the feast because other concerns occupied their minds and hearts. With this very remarkable difference, Saint Augustine continues, that at least the guests in the parable confessed in good faith the true reasons that held them back; whereas the worldly people in question here pretend not to know, and hide from themselves, the cause of their disorder. They continually avail themselves of this empty pretext, that, unworthy as they are to receive Communion, the best thing for them is to abstain from it; consoling themselves inwardly, as though they honored Jesus Christ by this and Jesus Christ ought one day to reward them for abandoning his altars to enjoy the pleasures of the age more peacefully and freely. For this, my dear listeners, is how far their blindness goes. And to convince them, Saint Chrysostom added\u2014this seems unanswerable\u2014to convince them that, in their case, this supposed respect is only a pretext and not a reason, there is this: though they receive Communion more rarely, they do not receive it more worthily. That is, when they receive Communion, they do not dispose themselves better, examine themselves with more care, or separate themselves more from the world; and, if I dare express myself so, they do not put themselves to more expense to receive Jesus Christ in their house. They persuade themselves, by the falsest of all maxims, that receiving Communion little, without adding anything more to it, should count for them as merit and all merit; and by a visible error of which they are unaware, they measure all the respect they show the divine mystery, not by greater attention to themselves, greater fidelity to their duties, greater exactness or regularity, but by the interval and space of time they put between one Communion and another: Deeming not purity of soul, but intervals of longer time, to be merit. An unfailing sign, this Father says, that it is neither humility nor respect, but a pure illusion of the spirit of the world deceiving them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p017", "label": "French original", "text": "Or je dis, Chr\u00e9tiens, qu'il est d'une importance extr\u00eame de leur \u00f4ter ce pr\u00e9texte : & comment ? prenez garde, s'il vous pla\u00eet ; non pas en leur facilitant la communion, ni en les y portant, tandis qu'ils sont encore dans les engagemens d'une vie mondaine : je s\u00e7ais trop ce que la dignit\u00e9 de ce Sacrement exige d'une ame fid\u00e9le ; & malheur \u00e0 moi si dans la plus grande action du Christianisme, & dans les dispositions qu'il y faut apporter, je venois jamais \u00e0 ouvrir la porte aux moindres rel\u00e2chemens. Mais j'appelle \u00f4ter \u00e0 une ame mondaine ce pr\u00e9texte, l'obliger \u00e0 parler juste, & \u00e0 ne plus dire ; Je m'\u00e9loigne du corps de Jesus-Christ, parce que je le respecte ; mais, Je m'en \u00e9loigne, parce que je suis une ame libertine, qui ne veux pas m'assujettir aux saintes loix que ma religion me prescrit pour en approcher. Je m'en \u00e9loigne, parce que je suis une ame dissip\u00e9e, qui n'ai en t\u00eate que le monde & que mon plaisir. Je m'en \u00e9loigne, parce que je suis une ame l\u00e2che, qui n'ai pas le courage de rien faire ni de rien entreprendre pour mon salut. Je m'en \u00e9loigne, parce que j'ai un empressement pour les affaires temporelles, qui me dess\u00e9che le c\u0153ur, & qui m'endurcit \u00e0 l'\u00e9gard de Dieu. Je m'en \u00e9loigne, parce que je ne puis me r\u00e9soudre \u00e0 me mortifier ni \u00e0 me faire la moindre violence. Je m'en \u00e9loigne, parce que je veux vivre sans r\u00e9gle, & selon le caprice de mon humeur. Obliger, dis-je, les mondains \u00e0 convenir de tout cela, & leur remontrer ensuite le d\u00e9sordre de leur conduite & l'injure qu'ils font \u00e0 Jesus Christ, de n\u00e9gliger ainsi son adorable Sacrement : leur bien faire entendre, que non-seulement il ne s'en tient pas honor\u00e9 ; mais que c'est l'outrager, que c'est l'irriter, que c'est s'attirer de sa part cette terrible mal\u00e9diction, par o\u00f9 il conclut la parabole de l'Evangile : Dico autem vobis, quod nemo virorum illorum qui vocati sunt, gustabit coenam meam : ma table \u00e9toit pr\u00eate & dress\u00e9e pour eux, & ils ont cherch\u00e9 des pr\u00e9textes pour s'en \u00e9loigner ; mais je s\u00e7aurai bien les en punir ; car je vous d\u00e9clare, que pas un d'eux ne sera re\u00e7\u00fb au sacr\u00e9 banquet que je leur avois pr\u00e9par\u00e9 : voil\u00e0 de quoi les d\u00e9tromper de la dangereuse illusion qui les aveugle. Combien de fois, mes chers Auditeurs, cette pr\u00e9diction du Sauveur du monde, quoiqu'elle ne soit, si vous voulez, que comminatoire, s'est-elle accomplie \u00e0 la lettre ? & combien de Chr\u00e9tiens pour avoir abandonn\u00e9 pendant la vie l'usage de la communion, par un secret jugement de Dieu, en ont-ils \u00e9t\u00e9 priv\u00e9s \u00e0 la mort ? Mais allons plus avant."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p017", "label": "English", "text": "Now I say, Christians, that it is of the utmost importance to take this pretext away from them. And how? Consider, please: not by making Communion easy for them, nor by urging them toward it while they are still involved in the commitments of a worldly life. I know too well what the dignity of this Sacrament requires of a faithful soul; and woe to me if, in Christianity's greatest action and in the dispositions that must be brought to it, I were ever to open the door to the slightest laxity. But what I call taking this pretext away from a worldly soul is obliging it to speak accurately, and no longer to say, I keep away from the body of Jesus Christ because I respect him, but, I keep away because I am a libertine soul, unwilling to submit to the holy laws my religion prescribes for approaching him. I keep away because I am a scattered soul, with nothing in my head but the world and my pleasure. I keep away because I am a cowardly soul, lacking the courage to do or undertake anything for my salvation. I keep away because I have an eagerness for temporal affairs that dries up my heart and hardens it toward God. I keep away because I cannot resolve to mortify myself or do myself the least violence. I keep away because I wish to live without rule and according to the caprice of my humor. To oblige worldly people, I say, to admit all this, and then show them the disorder of their conduct and the injury they do Jesus Christ by thus neglecting his adorable Sacrament; to make them understand clearly that he not only does not consider himself honored by it, but that it outrages him, provokes him, and draws from him that terrible curse with which he concludes the Gospel parable: But I tell you that none of those men who were invited will taste my supper. My table was ready and set for them, and they sought pretexts to keep away; but I shall know how to punish them for it, for I declare to you that not one of them will be received at the sacred banquet I had prepared for them. This is enough to undeceive them of the dangerous illusion blinding them. How many times, my dear listeners, has this prediction of the Savior of the world, though it be, if you wish, only a threat, been fulfilled to the letter? And how many Christians, for having abandoned the use of Communion during life, have been deprived of it at death by a secret judgment of God? But let us go further.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p018", "label": "French original", "text": "Non-seulement vain respect, mais faux respect : pourquoi ? parce qu'il n'est pas accompagn\u00e9 des deux conditions essentielles qu'il doit avoir. L'une est la douleur, & une douleur vive d'\u00eatre s\u00e9par\u00e9 du corps de Jesus-Christ ; l'autre est le d\u00e9sir, & un d\u00e9sir sinc\u00e9re d'en approcher : deux conditions ins\u00e9parables du vrai respect ; mais que le mondain, s'il veut bien rentrer en lui-m\u00eame, ne trouvera pas dans son c\u0153ur. Douleur vive d'\u00eatre s\u00e9par\u00e9 du corps de Jesus-Christ : car si j'honore Jesus-Christ autant que je dois l'honorer, si j'ai pour Jesus Christ ce respectueux attachement dont je me flatte, je dois regarder comme mon souverain bien dans cette vie de lui \u00eatre uni ; je dis uni sur-tout par le Sacrement qu'il a lui-m\u00eame institu\u00e9, pour entretenir entre lui & moi une sainte & ineffable union : d'o\u00f9 il s'ensuit que je dois par la m\u00eame r\u00e9gle, regarder comme mon souverain mal, d'\u00eatre s\u00e9par\u00e9 de ce Sacrement, dont la participation est le gage de ma b\u00e9atitude, ou pl\u00fbt\u00f4t, est ma b\u00e9atitude anticip\u00e9e. Et c'est ce que saint Chrysostome comprenoit si bien, quand il disoit en parlant de la communion : Unus sit vobis dolor hac esc\u00e2 privari ; que votre grande douleur, mes Freres, ou pour mieux dire, que votre unique douleur soit d'\u00eatre priv\u00e9s de cette viande c\u00e9leste, qui est la chair de Jesus-Christ. Votre unique douleur, Unus dolor : car quels sont en comparaison de celui-ci, tous les autres sujets qui vous affligent ? S'il est donc vrai que je respecte le Sacrement de Jesus-Christ, autant qu'il est respectable, & autant que je veux paro\u00eetre le respecter, rien ne doit \u00eatre plus douloureux & plus affligeant pour moi, que de me voir priv\u00e9 de cette divine nourriture ; & j'y dois \u00eatre plus sensible qu'\u00e0 toutes les pertes du monde, qu'\u00e0 toutes les afflictions du monde. Cette pens\u00e9e, je suis s\u00e9par\u00e9 de mon Dieu, si j'ai de la foi, doit me d\u00e9soler, doit me consterner, doit me jetter dans un abbatement pareil \u00e0 celui d'Esa\u00fc, quand il se vit exclus de la b\u00e9n\u00e9diction de son pere ; & par-l\u00e0, j'entre comme Chr\u00e9tien dans le sentiment de saint Chrysostome : Unus sit vobis dolor hac esc\u00e2 privari."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p018", "label": "English", "text": "Not only empty respect, but false respect. Why? Because it is not accompanied by the two essential conditions it ought to have. One is sorrow, a lively sorrow at being separated from the body of Jesus Christ; the other is desire, a sincere desire to approach it. Two conditions inseparable from true respect, but which the worldly man, if he is willing to enter into himself, will not find in his heart. Lively sorrow at being separated from the body of Jesus Christ: for if I honor Jesus Christ as much as I ought to honor him, if I have for Jesus Christ that respectful attachment on which I flatter myself, I ought to regard being united with him as my supreme good in this life. I mean united above all by the Sacrament he himself instituted to maintain between himself and me a holy and ineffable union. Hence it follows that by the same rule I ought to regard being separated from this Sacrament as my supreme evil, since participation in it is the pledge of my beatitude, or rather my beatitude anticipated. And this is what Saint Chrysostom understood so well when he said, speaking of Communion: Let your one sorrow be to be deprived of this food. Let your great sorrow, my brothers\u2014or, better said, your only sorrow\u2014be to be deprived of this heavenly food, which is the flesh of Jesus Christ. Your only sorrow\u2014One sorrow\u2014for what, compared with this, are all the other things that afflict you? If, then, it is true that I respect the Sacrament of Jesus Christ as much as it deserves respect and as much as I wish to appear to respect it, nothing ought to be more painful and afflicting for me than to see myself deprived of this divine nourishment. I ought to be more sensitive to this than to all the losses in the world, all the afflictions in the world. This thought, I am separated from my God, if I have faith, ought to desolate me, overwhelm me, cast me into dejection like Esau's when he saw himself excluded from his father's blessing; and by this, as a Christian, I enter into Saint Chrysostom's feeling: Let your one sorrow be to be deprived of this food.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p019", "label": "French original", "text": "Douleur encore plus vive, si j'ai \u00e0 me reprocher que c'est moi-m\u00eame qui m'en s\u00e9pare ; moi-m\u00eame qui m'en s\u00e9pare par mon infid\u00e9lit\u00e9, moi-m\u00eame qui m'en s\u00e9pare par mon attachement opini\u00e2tre \u00e0 l'objet d'une honteuse passion dont je me suis rendu esclave, moi-m\u00eame qui m'en s\u00e9pare pour ne vouloir pas faire \u00e0 Jesus-Christ le sacrifice qu'il attend de moi Mais quel surcro\u00eet de peine, si je comprens tout le malheur d'une si triste s\u00e9paration ! Quand l'Eglise, exer\u00e7ant sur les premiers Chr\u00e9tiens la s\u00e9v\u00e9rit\u00e9 de sa discipline, les retranchoit pour un tems de la communion, que faisoient-ils, & quels \u00e9toient leurs sentimens ? Les Peres nous apprennent qu'ils en tomboient dans la plus profonde tristesse, qu'ils g\u00e9missoient, qu'ils soupiroient, qu'ils versoient des torrens de larmes, qu'ils regardoient cet \u00e9tat comme une r\u00e9probation passag\u00e9re. Ainsi, quoique s\u00e9par\u00e9s de Jesus-Christ, marquoient-ils n\u00e9anmoins leur respect, & un respect solide \u00e0 Jesus-Christ. Mais ces mondains dont je parle, ont-ils jamais senti les impressions de cette douleur Chr\u00e9tienne & religieuse ? J'en appelle au t\u00e9moignage de leur c\u0153ur, & je les en atteste eux-m\u00eames. Eloign\u00e9s de la communion, avec quelle tranquillit\u00e9 ne soutiennent-ils pas cet \u00e9loignement ? Avec quelle indolence ne se voient-ils pas s\u00e9par\u00e9s du Dieu de leur salut ? Avec quelle insensibilit\u00e9 ne s'y accoutument-ils pas, non-seulement jusqu'\u00e0 n'en \u00eatre plus afflig\u00e9s, mais jusqu'\u00e0 s'en trouver soulag\u00e9s ? La communion dans le cours de leur vie mondaine, est un fardeau pesant, & ils s'en d\u00e9chargent : la communion trouble ou interrompt leurs vains plaisirs ; pour les go\u00fbter sans interruption & sans trouble, ils l'abandonnent : il faudroit pour communier, garder des mesures & se contraindre ; il leur est plus commode de s'en abstenir, & de ne communier plus. Avec de telles dispositions me persuaderont-ils qu'ils ont pour Jesus-Christ & son Sacrement un vrai respect ; & s'ils le pr\u00e9tendoient encore, n'ai-je pas droit de ne les en pas croire ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p019", "label": "English", "text": "Sorrow still more lively if I have to reproach myself that it is I myself who separate myself from him; I myself who separate myself by my infidelity, I myself who separate myself by my obstinate attachment to the object of a shameful passion of which I have made myself the slave, I myself who separate myself because I do not wish to make to Jesus Christ the sacrifice he awaits from me. But what an increase of pain, if I understand all the misfortune of so sad a separation! When the Church, exercising the severity of her discipline over the first Christians, cut them off from Communion for a time, what did they do, and what were their feelings? The Fathers teach us that they fell into the deepest sadness, groaned, sighed, poured out torrents of tears, and regarded this state as a passing reprobation. Thus, though separated from Jesus Christ, they nevertheless showed their respect, and a solid respect, for Jesus Christ. But have these worldly people of whom I speak ever felt the impressions of this Christian and religious sorrow? I appeal to the testimony of their heart and call them themselves to witness it. Away from Communion, with what tranquility do they bear this distance! With what indifference do they see themselves separated from the God of their salvation! With what insensibility do they grow accustomed to it, not only to the point of no longer being afflicted by it, but even of finding relief in it! Communion in the course of their worldly life is a heavy burden, and they unload it; Communion troubles or interrupts their vain pleasures: to enjoy them without interruption or disturbance, they abandon it. To receive Communion they would have to observe limits and restrain themselves; it is more convenient for them to abstain and receive Communion no longer. With such dispositions, will they persuade me that they have true respect for Jesus Christ and his Sacrament? And if they still claimed it, would I not have the right not to believe them?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p020", "label": "French original", "text": "Faux respect, parce qu'il n'est accompagn\u00e9 d'aucun d\u00e9sir de la communion. Autre preuve contre eux. Car observez bien, Chr\u00e9tiens, ce que j'ajo\u00fbte : le respect que je dois avoir pour Jesus-Christ, peut bien m'engager quelquefois \u00e0 me retirer pour un tems de la communion ; mais il ne doit jamais, s'il est v\u00e9ritable, \u00e9teindre en moi, ni m\u00eame diminuer le d\u00e9sir de la communion. Au contraire, plus je me trouve indigne de communier, plus je dois dans un sens d\u00e9sirer avec ardeur de communier ; pourquoi ? parce qu'il est \u00e9vident que ce d\u00e9sir est au moins une ressource contre mon indignit\u00e9. Et en effet, c'est par ce d\u00e9sir que je reviens \u00e0 Jesus-Christ, & en vertu de ce d\u00e9sir que je t\u00e2che \u00e0 me rapprocher de lui. C'est par ce d\u00e9sir que j'en cherche tous les moyens, que j'en surmonte tous les obstacles, que je suis fid\u00e9le \u00e0 en ex\u00e9cuter toutes les r\u00e9solutions. Tandis que ce d\u00e9sir est en moi, le principe de la vie y est encore, & il n'y a rien dont je ne sois capable : au lieu que ce d\u00e9sir cessant, je suis comme mort, n'ayant plus aucun sentiment qui me ram\u00e8ne \u00e0 Jesus-Christ, ni qui me presse de retourner \u00e0 lui : d'o\u00f9 il s'ensuit, que non-seulement toute mon indignit\u00e9 subsiste, mais que l'extinction de ce d\u00e9sir est comme la consommation de mon indignit\u00e9. Indignit\u00e9 consomm\u00e9e, dont saint Ambroise ne craignoit point d'exag\u00e9rer les suites affreuses, quand il soutenoit que la perte de ce d\u00e9sir n'\u00e9toit pas moins qu'un pr\u00e9sage de la r\u00e9probation future. Ah ! Seigneur, disoit-il, c'est de ce pain adorable de l'Eucharistie qu'il est \u00e9crit, que tous ceux qui s'\u00e9loignent de vous p\u00e9riront, c'est-\u00e0-dire, que tous ceux qui perdent le d\u00e9sir de s'unir \u00e0 vous, seront rejett\u00e9s de vous : Domine, de hoc pane scriptum est : Omnes qui elongant se \u00e0 te, peribunt."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p020", "label": "English", "text": "False respect, because it is accompanied by no desire for Communion. Another proof against them. For observe carefully, Christians, what I add: the respect I ought to have for Jesus Christ may indeed sometimes lead me to withdraw from Communion for a time; but, if it is true, it must never extinguish in me, or even diminish, the desire for Communion. On the contrary, the more unworthy I find myself to receive Communion, the more, in a sense, I ought ardently to desire to receive it. Why? Because it is evident that this desire is at least a resource against my unworthiness. Indeed, it is by this desire that I return to Jesus Christ, and by virtue of this desire that I try to draw near to him again. It is by this desire that I seek all the means, overcome all the obstacles, and am faithful in carrying out all the resolutions. While this desire is in me, the principle of life is still there, and there is nothing of which I am not capable. Whereas, when this desire ceases, I am as if dead, no longer having any feeling that leads me back to Jesus Christ or urges me to return to him. Hence it follows that not only does all my unworthiness persist, but the extinction of this desire is, as it were, the consummation of my unworthiness. Consummated unworthiness, whose dreadful consequences Saint Ambrose did not fear to set out in strong terms when he maintained that the loss of this desire was nothing less than a presage of future reprobation. Ah, Lord, he said, it is of this adorable bread of the Eucharist that it is written that all who go far from you will perish\u2014that is, all who lose the desire to unite themselves to you will be rejected by you: Lord, of this bread it is written: All who go far from you will perish.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p021", "label": "French original", "text": "Ainsi le comprenoient parfaitement les premiers fid\u00e8les. J'en reviens \u00e0 leur exemple, & je ne puis trop vous le proposer. Car c'est pour cela que priv\u00e9s de l'usage des saints myst\u00e8res & de la communion, ils t\u00e9moignoient un empressement si vif & si ardent d'y \u00eatre r\u00e9tablis. C'est pour cela qu'ils le demandoient avec tant d'instance, & que prostern\u00e9s aux pieds des Pr\u00eatres, ils les conjuroient par les entrailles de la mis\u00e9ricorde de Jesus-Christ, de leur abr\u00e9ger ces jours malheureux o\u00f9 ils vivoient s\u00e9par\u00e9s de leur Sauveur. C'est pour cela qu'ils employoient m\u00eame l'intercession des Martyrs ; & en cela, dit saint Cyprien, paroissoit leur respect & leur vrai respect. Que fait le mondain ? Content de leur ressembler dans cette triste s\u00e9paration, il est peu en peine de les imiter sur le reste, & confondant avec la communion le d\u00e9sir de la communion, il renonce \u00e9galement \u00e0 l'un & \u00e0 l'autre, & n'a plus pour le Sacrement de Jesus Christ qu'une indiff\u00e9rence de c\u0153ur dont il devroit \u00eatre effray\u00e9. Car voil\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs, ce que les Peres de l'Eglise d\u00e9ploroient si am\u00e9rement ; voil\u00e0 ce qu'ils regardoient comme une des plaies & comme un des plus grands malheurs de leur si\u00e9cle ; voil\u00e0 ce que saint Chrysostome reprochoit au peuple d'Antioche avec tant de force. Quelle honte, leur disoit-il, mes Freres, de voir votre froideur, quand on vous parle de recevoir le Saint des Saints ? S'agit-il d'un spectacle dans votre ville ? vous y courez en foule ; & rien ne vous peut attirer, quand il est question de venir prendre part au sacrifice de nos Autels. Toutes vos places publiques, tous vos amphith\u00e9\u00e2tres sont remplis ; & la table de Jesus-Christ est vuide. Envain y sommes-nous assidus, pour vous distribuer les dons c\u00e9lestes ; aucun de vous ne s'y pr\u00e9sente. Jesus-Christ en personne vous y attend, & il y est d\u00e9laiss\u00e9. Tant\u00f4t ce Pere leur repr\u00e9sentoit avec quel z\u00e9le ils s'assembloient pour \u00e9couter ses pr\u00e9dications, tandis qu'ils en marquoient si peu pour recevoir de ses mains le gage pr\u00e9cieux de leur salut. Tant\u00f4t il se plaignoit de leur duret\u00e9 \u00e0 l'\u00e9gard de ce Sacrement d'amour. Tant\u00f4t il leur remettoit devant les yeux les funestes cons\u00e9quences de ce respect mal entendu, dont ils vouloient se pr\u00e9valoir, & de l'abus qu'ils en faisoient. Imaginez-vous mes chers Auditeurs, que c'est encore ici saint Chrysostome qui vous parle, puisqu'en effet c'est lui-m\u00eame ; ou b\u00e9nissez le ciel, de ce que Dieu d\u00e8s lors inspiroit \u00e0 ce grand homme, ce qui doit aujourd'hui confondre vos pitoyables, mais pernicieuses erreurs."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p021", "label": "English", "text": "Thus the first faithful understood it perfectly. I return to their example and cannot propose it to you too often. For this is why, deprived of the use of the holy mysteries and of Communion, they showed so lively and ardent an eagerness to be restored to it. This is why they asked for it so insistently and, prostrate at the priests' feet, besought them by the depths of Jesus Christ's mercy to shorten those unhappy days when they lived separated from their Savior. This is why they even employed the intercession of the martyrs; and in this, Saint Cyprian says, their respect and their true respect appeared. What does the worldly man do? Content to resemble them in this sad separation, he troubles little to imitate them in the rest; and, confusing Communion with the desire for Communion, he renounces both alike and has for the Sacrament of Jesus Christ only an indifference of heart that ought to frighten him. For this, my dear listeners, is what the Fathers of the Church deplored so bitterly; this is what they regarded as one of the wounds and one of the greatest misfortunes of their age; this is what Saint Chrysostom so forcefully reproached the people of Antioch with. What a shame, he said to them, my brothers, to see your coldness when you are spoken to of receiving the Holy of Holies! Is there a spectacle in your city? You run to it in crowds; and nothing can attract you when it is a question of coming to take part in the sacrifice of our altars. All your public squares, all your amphitheaters are full; and the table of Jesus Christ is empty. In vain are we assiduous there to distribute heavenly gifts to you; none of you presents himself. Jesus Christ in person awaits you there, and he is forsaken. Sometimes this Father showed them with what zeal they assembled to hear his preaching, while showing so little zeal to receive from his hands the precious pledge of their salvation. Sometimes he complained of their hardness toward this Sacrament of love. Sometimes he set before their eyes the fatal consequences of this misunderstood respect, of which they wished to avail themselves, and of the abuse they made of it. Imagine, my dear listeners, that it is Saint Chrysostom who speaks to you here again, since it is indeed he himself; or bless heaven that God even then inspired in this great man what must today put your pitiful but pernicious errors to shame.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p022", "label": "French original", "text": "Enfin, j'ai dit, & je viens d\u00e9j\u00e0 de vous le faire voir en partie, que le respect dont s'autorisent les mondains pour s'\u00e9loigner de la communion, n'a nulle conformit\u00e9 avec celui des premiers si\u00e9cles de l'Eglise ; la preuve en est sensible. Car dans ces si\u00e9cles florissans du Christianisme, tandis qu'un p\u00e9cheur demeuroit s\u00e9par\u00e9 du corps de Jesus-Christ, il \u00e9toit dans les exercices d'une p\u00e9nitence laborieuse, \u00e0 laquelle il se condamnoit, & dont il subissoit avec courage toutes les rigueurs ; & cette p\u00e9nitence, selon les loix de l'Eglise, n'\u00e9toit point une simple c\u00e9r\u00e9monie, puisqu'elle consistoit en de tr\u00e8s-p\u00e9nibles aust\u00e9rit\u00e9s. L'abstinence & le je\u00fbne, le sac & la cendre, le cilice & les mac\u00e9rations du corps en \u00e9toient, comme nous s\u00e7avons, les accompagnemens ins\u00e9parables ; & cela pour montrer combien le p\u00e9cheur honoroit Jesus-Christ, puisqu'il vouloit bien se soumettre \u00e0 de si rigoureuses pratiques, & qu'aux d\u00e9pens de lui-m\u00eame, il vouloit bien faire \u00e0 Jesus-Christ une telle r\u00e9paration. Or avouons-le \u00e0 notre honte : de pareilles \u00e9preuves ne sont, ni du go\u00fbt, ni de la d\u00e9votion des mondains. De quelque respect qu'ils se piquent pour Jesus-Christ, ils ne veulent pas qu'il leur en co\u00fbte tant. Aveugl\u00e9s par l'esprit du monde, par cet esprit de mollesse, ils pr\u00e9tendent en \u00eatre quittes \u00e0 meilleur compte. Toute leur p\u00e9nitence se termine \u00e0 ne communier plus ; & ce genre de p\u00e9nitence ne les incommode point. Bien loin de les incommoder, il flatte leurs inclinations, & il leur donne lieu de vivre dans une plus grande libert\u00e9, disons mieux, dans un plus grand libertinage. Car voil\u00e0 o\u00f9 le pr\u00e9texte de ce faux respect porte les choses ; & pl\u00fbt au ciel que ce que je combats ici, f\u00fbt une chim\u00e8re, & non une v\u00e9rit\u00e9. J'ach\u00e8ve, & il me reste \u00e0 vous montrer que ce pr\u00e9tendu respect est un scandale dans le p\u00e9cheur hypocrite. C'est la troisi\u00e9me partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p022", "label": "English", "text": "Finally, I said, and have just shown you in part, that the respect worldly people invoke to authorize their withdrawal from Communion has no conformity with that of the Church's first centuries; the proof is plain. For in those flourishing centuries of Christianity, while a sinner remained separated from the body of Jesus Christ, he was engaged in exercises of laborious penance to which he condemned himself and whose rigors he courageously endured. And this penance, according to the Church's laws, was no mere ceremony, since it consisted in very painful austerities. Abstinence and fasting, sackcloth and ashes, the hair shirt and mortifications of the body were, as we know, its inseparable companions; and this showed how much the sinner honored Jesus Christ, since he was willing to submit to such rigorous practices and, at his own expense, to make such reparation to Jesus Christ. Now let us confess it to our shame: such trials suit neither the taste nor the devotion of worldly people. Whatever respect for Jesus Christ they pride themselves on, they do not wish it to cost them so much. Blinded by the spirit of the world, by this spirit of softness, they claim to discharge their obligation more cheaply. All their penance ends in receiving Communion no longer; and this kind of penance does not inconvenience them. Far from inconveniencing them, it flatters their inclinations and gives them occasion to live in greater freedom\u2014let us say better, in greater libertinism. For this is where the pretext of this false respect carries matters; and would to heaven that what I combat here were a fantasy and not a truth! I finish, and it remains for me to show you that this supposed respect is a scandal in the hypocritical sinner. This is the third part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-h004", "label": "French original", "text": "III. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-h004", "label": "English", "text": "PART THREE.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p023", "label": "French original", "text": "C'Est une maxime commun\u00e9ment re\u00e7\u00fbe, que ce qui est bon en soi, ne l'est pas toujours par rapport au principe d'o\u00f9 il part ; & une des r\u00e9gles de la prudence humaine, est de tenir les choses m\u00eames les plus salutaires pour suspectes, quand nous d\u00e9couvrons qu'elles viennent d'une source infect\u00e9e & empoisonn\u00e9e. Or nous pouvons & nous devons m\u00eame appliquer cette r\u00e9gle \u00e0 ce qui concerne la religion & les pratiques de pi\u00e9t\u00e9. Je ne s\u00e7ais, Chr\u00e9tiens, si vous avez jamais fait une r\u00e9flexion, qui m'a paru bien solide, & dont je suis s\u00fbr que vous comprendrez encore mieux que moi la v\u00e9rit\u00e9, s\u00e7avoir, que lorsqu'il s'est \u00e9lev\u00e9 dans le Christianisme des contestations sur le rel\u00e2chement ou la s\u00e9v\u00e9rit\u00e9 de la discipline, certains libertins du monde n'ont presque jamais manqu\u00e9 \u00e0 se d\u00e9clarer pour le parti s\u00e9v\u00e9re ; non pas afin de l'embrasser dans la pratique & de le suivre, dispositions dont ils \u00e9toient bien \u00e9loign\u00e9s ; mais, ou par une conduite bizarre, pour avoir le plaisir d'en parler, ou par un int\u00e9r\u00eat secret, pour s'en servir comme d'un voile propre \u00e0 couvrir d'autres desseins. Ainsi tant de fois a-t-on v\u00fb des hommes engag\u00e9s d'ailleurs dans des d\u00e9sordres honteux, des hommes \u00e9galement corrompus & dans l'esprit & dans le c\u0153ur, vains, sensuels, amateurs d'eux m\u00eames, \u00eatre les premiers & les plus z\u00e9l\u00e9s en apparence \u00e0 s'expliquer en faveur de la r\u00e9forme & \u00e0 la maintenir. Ainsi a-t-on v\u00fb des femmes trop connues pour ce qu'elles avoient \u00e9t\u00e9, & peut-\u00eatre pour ce qu'elles \u00e9toient encore ; des femmes \u00e0 qui le pass\u00e9 devoit au moins fermer la bouche, devenir les plus \u00e9loquentes sur la d\u00e9pravation des m\u0153urs, ne trouver rien d'assez exact ni d'assez rigide dans la police de l'Eglise, & en appeller sans cesse aux anciens Canons, tels qu'ils s'observoient dans leur premi\u00e8re institution. Mais ce z\u00e9le de la puret\u00e9 des m\u0153urs & de la perfection du Christianisme n'est-il pas louable dans un Chr\u00e9tien ? Oui, r\u00e9pond saint Bernard : mais autant qu'il est louable dans un Chr\u00e9tien, autant, pour ne rien dire de plus, est-il \u00e9quivoque & douteux dans un libertin ; & je dois selon le pr\u00e9cepte de Jesus-Christ m'en d\u00e9fier comme de la plus dangereuse hypocrisie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p023", "label": "English", "text": "It is a commonly received maxim that what is good in itself is not always so in relation to the principle from which it proceeds; and one of the rules of human prudence is to hold even the most salutary things suspect when we discover that they come from an infected and poisoned source. Now we can, and indeed must, apply this rule to what concerns religion and practices of piety. I do not know, Christians, whether you have ever made a reflection which has seemed very sound to me, and whose truth I am sure you will understand still better than I: namely, that when disputes have arisen in Christianity over laxity or severity of discipline, certain worldly libertines have almost never failed to declare for the severe side. Not so as to embrace and follow it in practice, dispositions from which they were very far; but either, by a strange course of conduct, to have the pleasure of speaking of it, or, from a secret interest, to use it as a veil fitted to cover other designs. Thus so often have men been seen, otherwise involved in shameful disorders, men equally corrupt in mind and heart, vain, sensual, lovers of themselves, to be the first and apparently most zealous in speaking in favor of reform and supporting it. Thus women have been seen, too well known for what they had been and perhaps for what they still were\u2014women whose past should at least have closed their mouths\u2014becoming the most eloquent on the depravity of morals, finding nothing exact or rigid enough in the Church's governance, and continually appealing to the ancient canons as observed at their first institution. But is not this zeal for purity of morals and the perfection of Christianity praiseworthy in a Christian? Yes, Saint Bernard answers; but as much as it is praiseworthy in a Christian, so much, to say no more, is it ambiguous and doubtful in a libertine; and according to Jesus Christ's precept I must distrust it as the most dangerous hypocrisy.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p024", "label": "French original", "text": "Or ce que remarquoit en g\u00e9n\u00e9ral saint Bernard, touchant la puret\u00e9 & la r\u00e9gularit\u00e9 des m\u0153urs, c'est encore plus particuli\u00e8rement & plus sensiblement ce qui s'est v\u00e9rifi\u00e9 & ce qui se v\u00e9rifie tous les jours \u00e0 l'\u00e9gard de la communion. Car qu'est-il arriv\u00e9 ? vous le s\u00e7avez : on a parl\u00e9 & avec raison des abus qui se commettoient, ou qui pouvoient se commettre dans la fr\u00e9quentation du Sacrement de nos Autels, de l'extr\u00eame facilit\u00e9 avec laquelle il \u00e9toit \u00e0 craindre qu'on n'y adm\u00eet les p\u00e9cheurs, de la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 d'en s\u00e9parer pour un tems certaines ames imparfaites qui n'en profitoient pas, de la discr\u00e9tion & de la prudence que les Pasteurs y devoient apporter. Tout cela \u00e9toit bon, saint, \u00e9difiant ; & je ne doute point (appliquez-vous, s'il vous pla\u00eet, \u00e0 ce que je dis) je ne doute point que les vrais fid\u00e8les, touch\u00e9s de l'int\u00e9r\u00eat de Dieu & de celui de son Eglise, n'ayent eu des intentions tr\u00e8s-pures, en t\u00e9moignant l\u00e0-dessus leur z\u00e9le. Mais ce qui m'\u00e9tonne, c'est que des gens d'un caract\u00e8re tout oppos\u00e9, j'entens les libertins du si\u00e9cle, ayent pr\u00e9tendu \u00eatre de la partie ; & que s'ing\u00e9rant dans une cause o\u00f9 ils n'avoient rien de commun, ils se soient quelquefois montr\u00e9s les plus vifs & les plus ardens \u00e0 faire valoir le respect d\u00fb au Sacrement de Jesus-Christ & \u00e0 son corps adorable. Ce qui m'\u00e9tonne, c'est que des hommes qui parmi les intelligens passoient pour avoir peu de religion, des hommes engag\u00e9s dans les derniers d\u00e9r\u00e9glemens, ayent affect\u00e9 de parler avec plus de chaleur contre les communions fr\u00e9quentes, se soient plus hautement scandalis\u00e9s sur ce point des moindres rel\u00e2chemens, ou r\u00e9els, ou imaginaires, & soient entr\u00e9s dans cette question comme dans leur affaire propre. Voil\u00e0 ce qui m'a toujours surpris."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p024", "label": "English", "text": "Now what Saint Bernard observed generally concerning purity and regularity of morals is still more particularly and perceptibly what has proved, and daily proves, true concerning Communion. For what has happened? You know it: people have spoken, and rightly, of the abuses committed or liable to be committed in frequenting the Sacrament of our altars; of the extreme ease with which it was to be feared sinners might be admitted; of the necessity of separating from it for a time certain imperfect souls who did not profit from it; of the discretion and prudence pastors ought to bring to it. All this was good, holy, edifying; and I do not doubt\u2014attend, please, to what I say\u2014I do not doubt that the true faithful, moved by the interest of God and his Church, had very pure intentions in showing their zeal on this subject. But what astonishes me is that people of an entirely opposite character\u2014I mean the libertines of the age\u2014claimed to be of the party; and, intruding into a cause with which they had nothing in common, sometimes showed themselves the liveliest and most ardent in upholding the respect due to the Sacrament of Jesus Christ and his adorable body. What astonishes me is that men who among the discerning passed for having little religion, men involved in the worst disorders, affected to speak with greater warmth against frequent Communions, took louder scandal on this point at the slightest laxities, whether real or imaginary, and entered into this question as into their own affair. This is what has always surprised me.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p025", "label": "French original", "text": "Car enfin d'o\u00f9 leur peut venir ce z\u00e9le ? Impies comme je les suppose, ils n'ont pour tous les autres devoirs du Christianisme qu'un secret m\u00e9pris, & ils tiennent sur celui-ci le langage des parfaits & des spirituels. Il faut donc qu'ils y envisagent quelque int\u00e9r\u00eat, & vous \u00eates trop \u00e9clair\u00e9s pour ne pas comprendre d'abord en quoi cet int\u00e9r\u00eat consiste, puisqu'il est facile \u00e0 conno\u00eetre, & qu'au moins il est certain qu'en parlant de la sorte ils se mettent en possession d'\u00eatre libertins, non-seulement avec s\u00fbret\u00e9, mais si j'ose le dire, avec honneur. Car encore une fois, ce sont de ces hommes que saint Paul d\u00e9peignoit \u00e0 Timoth\u00e9e, des hommes corrompus dans le principe & dont la foi est comme \u00e9teinte ; des hommes \u00e0 qui tout exercice de religion est on\u00e9reux, & qui veulent s'en d\u00e9charger. Cependant, parce qu'ils n'ignorent pas que la communion a toujours \u00e9t\u00e9 regard\u00e9e comme une marque sp\u00e9ciale de Christianisme, & que d'y renoncer ouvertement, ce seroit une esp\u00e9ce d'apostasie qu'ils auroient peine \u00e0 soutenir ; pour ne se pas commettre jusques-l\u00e0, & n\u00e9anmoins pour secouer le joug qui les incommode, ils se font un voile de religion de leur propre irreligion (je ne s\u00e7ais si je m'explique bien) & ils se portent pour approbateurs de cette maxime qui va \u00e0 nous \u00e9loigner de Jesus Christ par un sentiment de crainte & de respect, afin qu'on ne puisse plus les distinguer d'avec les Chr\u00e9tiens m\u00eame les plus exacts, puisqu'ils parlent comme eux & qu'ils paroissent aussi z\u00e9l\u00e9s qu'eux."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p025", "label": "English", "text": "For, after all, whence can this zeal come to them? Impious as I suppose them, they have only a secret contempt for all the other duties of Christianity, and on this one they use the language of the perfect and the spiritual. They must therefore see some interest in it; and you are too enlightened not to understand at once what this interest consists in, since it is easy to know, and at least it is certain that, by speaking in this way, they put themselves in a position to be libertines not only safely but, if I dare say it, honorably. For, once again, they are of those men Saint Paul depicted to Timothy: men corrupt in principle, whose faith is as if extinguished; men to whom every exercise of religion is burdensome and who wish to free themselves from it. Yet, because they are not ignorant that Communion has always been regarded as a special mark of Christianity, and that openly renouncing it would be a kind of apostasy they would have difficulty sustaining, in order not to commit themselves so far, and nevertheless to shake off the yoke that inconveniences them, they make a veil of religion out of their own irreligion\u2014I do not know whether I explain myself well\u2014and put themselves forward as approvers of this maxim which tends to keep us away from Jesus Christ through a feeling of fear and respect, so that they can no longer be distinguished from even the most exact Christians, since they speak like them and appear as zealous as they.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p026", "label": "French original", "text": "Or je pr\u00e9tens que ce langage dans la bouche du libertin, est un scandale pour les foibles : pourquoi ? encore un moment d'attention : parce qu'il aboutit \u00e0 deux choses \u00e9galement pernicieuses, s\u00e7avoir, \u00e0 d\u00e9crier indiff\u00e9remment les bonnes & les mauvaises communions, c'est la premi\u00e8re ; & \u00e0 d\u00e9tourner les ames, non-seulement de la communion, mais universellement de tout ce qu'il y a de saint dans la religion, c'est la seconde. Je dis \u00e0 d\u00e9crier indiff\u00e9remment les bonnes & les mauvaises communions : car comme raisonnoit fort bien saint Jean Chrysostome, s'il est toujours dangereux en bl\u00e2mant la fausse pi\u00e9t\u00e9, de d\u00e9cr\u00e9diter la vraie ; beaucoup plus l'est-il, quand celui qui se m\u00eale d'en juger, est un esprit prophane qui se soucie peu de confondre l'une avec l'autre ; ou pl\u00fbt\u00f4t, qui n'attaque l'une, que parce qu'il est secrettement ennemi de l'autre, & qui bien loin d'user de la pr\u00e9caution n\u00e9cessaire pour s\u00e9parer le vrai d'avec le faux, semble n'avoir point d'autre but que de d\u00e9truire le vrai par le faux. Or ce que disoit ce Pere de la d\u00e9votion, j'ai droit de le dire, & la m\u00eame exp\u00e9rience le confirme touchant la communion. S'il faut toujours craindre, en condamnant les mauvaises communions, de condamner les bonnes ; beaucoup plus, quand celui qui s'en fait le censeur, est un esprit perverti, qui n'a ni pour les bonnes ni pour les mauvaises nul \u00e9gard v\u00e9ritable, & qui ne compte pour rien de pr\u00e9judicier \u00e0 celles-ci en d\u00e9clamant contre celles-l\u00e0."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p026", "label": "English", "text": "Now I maintain that this language in the libertine's mouth is a scandal for the weak. Why? Another moment's attention: because it leads to two equally pernicious things: to disparage good and bad Communions indiscriminately\u2014this is the first; and to turn souls away not only from Communion, but generally from everything holy in religion\u2014this is the second. I say, to disparage good and bad Communions indiscriminately: for, as Saint John Chrysostom very rightly reasoned, if in blaming false piety it is always dangerous to discredit true piety, it is much more so when the one who presumes to judge it is a profane spirit who cares little about confusing the one with the other; or rather, who attacks the one only because he is secretly the enemy of the other, and who, far from taking the necessary precaution to separate the true from the false, seems to have no other aim than to destroy the true by means of the false. Now what this Father said of devotion I have a right to say, and the same experience confirms it, concerning Communion. If, in condemning bad Communions, we must always fear condemning good ones, much more so when the person who makes himself their censor is a perverted spirit who has no true regard for either the good or the bad, and thinks nothing of harming the former by declaiming against the latter.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p027", "label": "French original", "text": "Et en effet, \u00e0 quoi se termine le z\u00e9le malin que je combats, que je combats, dis-je, dans les impies du si\u00e9cle qui s'en pr\u00e9valent, & qui par-l\u00e0 troublent les ames justes & innocentes : \u00e0 quoi se r\u00e9duit-il ? A faire dans l'Eglise de Dieu ce que faisoient autrefois les enfans du grand Pr\u00eatre H\u00e9li, qui d\u00e9tournoient les hommes du sacrifice : crime que d\u00e9testoit le Seigneur, & pour lequel il les r\u00e9prouva ; Peccatum grande nimis, quia retrahebant homines \u00e0 sacrificio Domini ; ou bien, si vous voulez, \u00e0 renouveller ce que firent dans la suite les Pharisiens, \u00e0 qui pour cela le Sauveur du monde disoit avec indignation : Malheur \u00e0 vous qui fermez aux autres le Royaume de Dieu ; car vous n'y entrez pas vous-m\u00eame, & vous arr\u00eatez encore ceux qui voudroient y entrer ; Vos enim non intratis, nec introeuntes sinitis intrare. Figure sensible de ce qui s'accomplit tous les jours dans la personne de ces mondains, qui par un endurcissement de c\u0153ur s'\u00e9tant eux-m\u00eames s\u00e9par\u00e9s du divin myst\u00e8re, o\u00f9 selon la pens\u00e9e de saint Cyrille, le Royaume de Dieu nous est ouvert, voudroient, s'il leur \u00e9toit possible, en exclurre tous les autres. Voil\u00e0 \u00e0 quoi ils travaillent, & m\u00eame \u00e0 quoi ils parviennent, en controllant les gens de bien sur leurs communions, en censurant leur vie, en critiquant leur conduite, en relevant leurs moindres d\u00e9fauts, en ne leur pardonnant rien, & en leur faisant un crime de tout. Saint Augustin avec toutes ses lumi\u00e8res n'osoit pas d\u00e9sapprouver l'usage de communier tous les jours ; un mondain t\u00e9m\u00e9raire & aveugle dans les choses de Dieu, le condamne hardiment & sans h\u00e9siter. Le dernier Concile souhaitoit de voir la fr\u00e9quente communion r\u00e9tablie dans l'Eglise ; & le mondain voudroit au contraire l'exterminer & l'an\u00e9antir. Ne pensez pas, mes chers Auditeurs, que par-l\u00e0 je pr\u00e9tende justifier toutes les communions fr\u00e9quentes ; il y en a de fr\u00e9quentes que je d\u00e9plore, mais dont je laisse \u00e0 Dieu le jugement : c'est-\u00e0-dire, il y en a de fr\u00e9quentes, mais inutiles ; de fr\u00e9quentes, mais l\u00e2ches ; de fr\u00e9quentes, mais tr\u00e8s-peu \u00e9difiantes, mais qui pourroient m\u00eame pl\u00fbt\u00f4t scandaliser qu'\u00e9difier. Peut-\u00eatre en parlerai-je dans un autre discours, & vous verrez bien que mon intention ne fut jamais de les autoriser. Du reste, j'ai dit que j'en laissois \u00e0 Dieu le jugement, parce qu'autant que je craindrois de rien avancer qui favoris\u00e2t de telles communions, autant me croirois-je pr\u00e9varicateur, de donner la moindre atteinte aux communions fr\u00e9quentes, mais ferventes. Les autres d\u00e9shonorent Jesus-Christ, mais celles-ci le glorifient ; & comme je dirois anath\u00eame \u00e0 quiconque approuveroit les communions vaines & imparfaites, aussi le dirai-je toujours au libertinage, quand il s'\u00e9l\u00e8vera contre celles qui sanctifient les ames, & dont le Fils de Dieu tire sa gloire. Qui pourroit dire combien le d\u00e9mon par ce seul artifice a retir\u00e9 de justes des Autels ? combien d'Epouses de Jesus-Christ il a troubl\u00e9es dans leurs saintes communications avec l'Epoux c\u00e9leste ? combien de communions dont les Anges se seroient r\u00e9jouis dans le ciel, il a comme interdites sur la terre ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p027", "label": "English", "text": "And indeed, where does the malicious zeal I combat end\u2014the zeal I combat, I say, in the impious people of the age who avail themselves of it and thereby trouble just and innocent souls? To what does it amount? To doing in God's Church what the sons of the high priest Eli formerly did, turning men away from sacrifice: a crime the Lord detested, and for which he rejected them. An exceedingly great sin, because they drew men back from the Lord's sacrifice. Or, if you wish, to renewing what the Pharisees later did, to whom the Savior of the world therefore said with indignation: Woe to you who close the Kingdom of God to others; for you do not enter yourselves, and you also stop those who would wish to enter. For you do not enter, nor do you allow those entering to enter. A striking figure of what is daily accomplished in those worldly people who, having by hardness of heart separated themselves from the divine mystery in which, according to Saint Cyril's thought, the Kingdom of God is opened to us, would exclude everyone else from it if they could. This is what they work at, and even what they achieve, by passing judgment on good people's Communions, censuring their lives, criticizing their conduct, pointing out their slightest faults, pardoning them nothing, and making a crime of everything. Saint Augustine with all his enlightenment did not dare disapprove the practice of receiving Communion every day; a worldly man, rash and blind in the things of God, boldly condemns it without hesitation. The last Council wished to see frequent Communion reestablished in the Church; and the worldly man, on the contrary, would wish to exterminate and annihilate it. Do not think, my dear listeners, that by this I intend to justify all frequent Communions. There are frequent ones I deplore, but whose judgment I leave to God: that is, there are frequent but useless ones; frequent but slack ones; frequent but very little edifying ones, which might even scandalize rather than edify. Perhaps I shall speak of them in another discourse, and you will see clearly that my intention was never to authorize them. Moreover, I said that I left their judgment to God because, as much as I should fear to advance anything favoring such Communions, so much should I believe myself unfaithful to my duty if I gave the slightest injury to frequent but fervent Communions. The others dishonor Jesus Christ, but these glorify him. And as I would pronounce anathema against whoever approved vain and imperfect Communions, so shall I always pronounce it against libertinism when it rises against those which sanctify souls and from which the Son of God draws his glory. Who could say how many just people the demon has withdrawn from the altars by this one artifice? How many spouses of Jesus Christ he has troubled in their holy communications with the heavenly Bridegroom? How many Communions, at which the angels would have rejoiced in heaven, he has, as it were, forbidden on earth?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p028", "label": "French original", "text": "Je dis plus : de l'\u00e9loignement de la communion le scandale passe, si l'on n'a soin de s'en pr\u00e9server, jusqu'\u00e0 l'abandon & au retranchement de tout ce qui se pratique de plus saint dans le Christianisme, & c'est la seconde remarque de saint Chrysostome. Car suppos\u00e9 ce principe d'une humilit\u00e9 feinte & mal con\u00e7\u00fbe, quelle cons\u00e9quence n'en peut-on pas tirer, & \u00e0 quel exercice de la religion une ame fid\u00e9le n'est-elle pas tent\u00e9e de renoncer ? Vous n'\u00eates pas dignes de vous pr\u00e9senter \u00e0 la table de Jesus-Christ, ce sont les paroles de saint Chrysostome : & \u00eates-vous digne d'entrer dans le Temple de Dieu ? & \u00eates-vous digne de prier & d'invoquer Dieu ? & \u00eates-vous digne d'entendre la parole de Dieu ? & \u00eates-vous digne d'\u00eatre admis \u00e0 la p\u00e9nitence, & au tribunal de la mis\u00e9ricorde de Dieu ? & \u00eates-vous digne de chanter avec l'Eglise les louanges de Dieu ? & \u00eates-vous digne d'assister au sacrifice qui est offert \u00e0 Dieu ? Il faudra donc par la m\u00eame raison abandonner tout cela, & que la v\u00fbe de votre indignit\u00e9, si j'ose m'exprimer de la sorte, vous tienne dans une esp\u00e9ce d'excommunication, o\u00f9 vous n'ayez plus de part \u00e0 tout ce qui s'appelle culte & devoir Chr\u00e9tien : Sum, inquit, indignus communione altaris ; ergo & ill\u00e2 quoque communione quae in precibus est ; ergo & ill\u00e2 quae in verbo Dei est. Ainsi concluoit ce saint Docteur : & sans parler des bonnes ames, dont la simplicit\u00e9 peut \u00eatre s\u00e9duite par cette illusion, voil\u00e0 l'avantage que les libertins en voudroient remporter. Ils se feroient un plaisir d'\u00e9tendre \u00e0 toutes les obligations chr\u00e9tiennes ces paroles du Centenier expliqu\u00e9es & corrompues selon leur sens ; Domine, non sum dignus. Et comme ils s'en servent pour paro\u00eetre, tout libertins qu'ils sont, humbles & religieux, en ne communiant pas ; aussi passant plus loin, se s\u00e7auroient-ils bon gr\u00e9, d'avoir trouv\u00e9 moyen de ne paro\u00eetre jamais dans nos Temples par respect, de ne plus prier par respect, de s'affranchir, par respect, de tous leurs devoirs. Or c'est l\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs, le scandale qu'il falloit combattre. Pardonnez-moi, si j'en parle avec quelque v\u00e9h\u00e9mence : c'est pour l'int\u00e9r\u00eat de Jesus-Christ & de sa religion. Que les Pr\u00e9lats de l'Eglise fassent des loix & des ordonnances, pour corriger les abus de la communion, c'est ce qui les regarde & ce que je respecterai toujours. Que les Pr\u00eatres & les Pasteurs des ames travaillent \u00e0 y apporter rem\u00e9de, c'est leur minist\u00e8re, & c'est pour cela que Dieu les a \u00e9tablis. Que les particuliers m\u00eame y contribuent selon la mesure de la grace que Dieu leur a donn\u00e9e, en commen\u00e7ant par eux m\u00eames, avant que d'\u00e9tendre leur z\u00e9le sur les autres, c'est ce qui m'\u00e9difiera. Mais que des mondains, que des prophanes, aveugles dans les choses de Dieu ; que des hommes peut-\u00eatre sans foi, entreprennent de d\u00e9cider ce qu'il y a de plus important dans la religion, de le r\u00e9gler, d'y m\u00ealer leurs erreurs, leurs int\u00e9r\u00eats, leur impi\u00e9t\u00e9 ; c'est ce que je condamnerai toujours, & sur quoi je m'\u00e9l\u00e8verai hautement contre eux. Appliquons nous, mes Freres (c'est \u00e0 vous \u00e0 qui je parle, Pr\u00eatres du Dieu vivant & Ministres de ses Autels, s\u00e9culiers ou r\u00e9guliers) appliquons-nous \u00e0 pr\u00e9parer au Seigneur un peuple parfait. Unis par le lien de la charit\u00e9, travaillons \u00e0 convertir les p\u00e9cheurs, \u00e0 perfectionner les justes, \u00e0 purifier les ames fid\u00e9les, pour les rendre dignes du Sacrement de Jesus Christ. Voil\u00e0 \u00e0 quoi nous devons nous employer ; voil\u00e0 le but que nous devons nous proposer. Car je vous le dis, mes Freres, jamais l'Eglise de Dieu ne sera sanctifi\u00e9e, ni jamais le Christianisme ne sera bien r\u00e9form\u00e9, que par le bon usage de la communion. Raisonnons tant qu'il nous plaira ; il en faudra toujours revenir \u00e0 ces adorables paroles du Sauveur : si vous ne mangez la chair du Fils de l'homme, vous n'aurez point la vie en vous : Nisi manducaveritis carnem Filii hominis, non habebitis vitam in vobis : au contraire, si quelqu'un mange de ce pain, il vivra \u00e9ternellement ; Qui manducat hunc panem, vivet in aeternum ; il vivra en ce monde par la grace, & dans l'autre par la gloire, o\u00f9 nous conduise, &c."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-p028", "label": "English", "text": "I say more: from keeping away from Communion, the scandal passes, unless care is taken to guard against it, to abandoning and cutting off everything holiest in Christian practice; and this is Saint Chrysostom's second observation. For, once this principle of a feigned and ill-conceived humility is granted, what consequence cannot be drawn from it, and what exercise of religion is a faithful soul not tempted to renounce? You are not worthy to present yourselves at Jesus Christ's table\u2014these are Saint Chrysostom's words\u2014and are you worthy to enter God's temple? Are you worthy to pray and invoke God? Are you worthy to hear God's word? Are you worthy to be admitted to penance and to the tribunal of God's mercy? Are you worthy to sing God's praises with the Church? Are you worthy to attend the sacrifice offered to God? By the same reason, then, you will have to abandon all this, and the sight of your unworthiness, if I dare express myself so, must keep you in a kind of excommunication in which you no longer have any share in all that is called Christian worship and duty: I am, he says, unworthy of communion at the altar; therefore also of that communion which is in prayers; therefore also of that which is in God's word. Thus this holy Doctor concluded. And, without speaking of good souls whose simplicity may be deceived by this illusion, here is the advantage libertines would wish to gain from it. They would delight in extending to all Christian obligations those words of the centurion, explained and corrupted according to their meaning: Lord, I am not worthy. And as they use them to appear, libertines though they are, humble and religious by not receiving Communion, so, going further, they would congratulate themselves on having found the means never to appear in our temples out of respect, no longer to pray out of respect, and to free themselves out of respect from all their duties. Now this, my dear listeners, is the scandal that had to be combated. Pardon me if I speak of it with some vehemence: it is for the interest of Jesus Christ and his religion. Let the Church's prelates make laws and ordinances to correct abuses of Communion: this is their concern, and I shall always respect it. Let priests and pastors of souls work to remedy them: it is their ministry, and it is for this that God has established them. Let even private persons contribute according to the measure of grace God has given them, beginning with themselves before extending their zeal to others: this will edify me. But that worldly people, profane people blind in the things of God, men perhaps without faith, should undertake to decide what is most important in religion, regulate it, mingle their errors, their interests, their impiety with it: this is what I shall always condemn and against which I shall raise my voice against them. Let us apply ourselves, my brothers\u2014it is to you I speak, priests of the living God and ministers of his altars, secular or regular\u2014let us apply ourselves to preparing a perfect people for the Lord. United by the bond of charity, let us work to convert sinners, perfect the just, purify faithful souls, to make them worthy of the Sacrament of Jesus Christ. This is what we must employ ourselves in; this is the goal we must set ourselves. For I tell you, my brothers, God's Church will never be sanctified, nor Christianity ever well reformed, except by the good use of Communion. Let us reason as much as we please: we shall always have to return to these adorable words of the Savior: If you do not eat the flesh of the Son of Man, you will not have life in you; Unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man, you will not have life in you. On the contrary, if anyone eats of this bread, he will live forever; Whoever eats this bread will live forever. He will live in this world by grace, and in the other by glory, to which may he lead us, &c.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n001", "label": "French original", "text": "Luc. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n001", "label": "English", "text": "Luke 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n002", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan. 6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n002", "label": "English", "text": "John 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n003", "label": "French original", "text": "I. Cor. 11."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n003", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians 11.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n004", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibidem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n004", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n005", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan. 6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n005", "label": "English", "text": "John 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n006", "label": "French original", "text": "Chrisost."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n006", "label": "English", "text": "Chrysostom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n007", "label": "French original", "text": "Idem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n007", "label": "English", "text": "The same author.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n008", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n008", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n009", "label": "French original", "text": "Idem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n009", "label": "English", "text": "The same author.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n010", "label": "French original", "text": "Idem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n010", "label": "English", "text": "The same author.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n011", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. 26."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n011", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 26.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n012", "label": "French original", "text": "Luc. 14."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n012", "label": "English", "text": "Luke 14.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n013", "label": "French original", "text": "I. Cor. 11."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n013", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians 11.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n014", "label": "French original", "text": "Chrysost."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n014", "label": "English", "text": "Chrysostom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n015", "label": "French original", "text": "Luc. 14."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n015", "label": "English", "text": "Luke 14.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n016", "label": "French original", "text": "Chrysost. homil. 60. ad pop. Antioch."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n016", "label": "English", "text": "Chrysostom, homily 60 to the people of Antioch.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n017", "label": "French original", "text": "Ambros."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n017", "label": "English", "text": "Ambrose.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n018", "label": "French original", "text": "I. Reg. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n018", "label": "English", "text": "1 Kings (historical Regum numbering) 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n019", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 23."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n019", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 23.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n020", "label": "French original", "text": "Chrysost."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-n020", "label": "English", "text": "Chrysostom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-ed001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Several sentence breaks have no punctuation in the printed French. The French preserves these gaps; punctuation has been added in English to make the sentences clear."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-ed002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The book abbreviates Chrysostom\u2019s name as Chrisost. in one margin. It also switches from plural to singular in its account of compelling sinners to prepare for Communion. These printed forms are preserved; the English follows the surrounding plural sense."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-ed003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The book cites I. Reg. 2 using historical Latin book numbering. It also refers to \u201cthe last Council\u201d without naming it. The references and attributions follow the edition; they have not been independently authenticated."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s001", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "SERMON XXIII.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s002", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "ON COMMUNION.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s003", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "For the Third Sunday after Epiphany.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s004", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "\u201cJesus saith unto him: I will come and heal him.\u201d And the centurion answered and said: Lord, I am not worthy that thou shouldest come under my roof.\u201d MATT. viii.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s005", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "In this passage, Christians, we see a kind of contest between Christ and the centurion; but in this contest, which seems the most to claim our admiration\u2014the charity of a God, or the humility of a pagan? I may safely say, that there never was strife more holy, or more proper for instruction and edification. Our Saviour, by an impulse of beneficent charity is for going in person into the house of the centurion, and the centurion accounts himself unworthy of that honour. The only begotten Son of God, whose mercy is unlimited, assures him that he will come, and by his presence will cure his paralytic servant: \u201cI will come and heal him;\u201d but the centurion, confused at so signal a favour, protests openly that he is unworthy of it, and acknowledges his unworthiness: \u201cLord, I am not worthy.\u201d Be pleased to take notice. This is a Gentile, to whom our blessed Saviour, in quality of Messiah, hath not been as yet announced or revealed, as he hath been to the Jews; and, notwithstanding that, Gentile as he is, he is already prepossessed with so high an opinion of, and so profound a respect for the Messiah who accosts him, that he cannot consent to receive his visit. A humility (says St. Augustin) which arose from a lively and ardent faith; and which, by an operation of our Redeemer\u2019s grace, transformed, on this spot, this Gentile not only into a veritable Israelite, but a perfect Christian. A humility with which Jesus Christ was well pleased, which he very much set by, and which he honoured with commendations; but to which, notwithstanding, it may truly be said that he paid no regard, whereas on the contrary, upon that very account, he insisted upon going into the centurion\u2019s house.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s006", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Here let us stop: and to profit, pursuant to the views of God, by so great an example, let us bring this gospel narrative home to our own case. For, that which passed (as St. Chrysostom remarks) between Christ and the centurion, is every day repeated between Christ and us. My meaning is, that this same Saviour, by instituting the eucharist, hath left us a sacrament by which he wills to impart himself to us; and, God as he is, to dwell corporally between our breasts; a sacrament, by which he visits us in person, with a view to heal our spiritual infirmities. As often, therefore, as we prepare to receive him in this adorable mystery, he still says to us with as much truth as he said heretofore: \u201cI will come and heal;\u201d how low soever you may be reduced, if, by a sincere acknowledgment of our weakness, we answer like the centurion: no, my Lord, I am not worthy that you should come to me and enter into my breast. For, these are the venerable words which the church puts into our mouths, when this God of glory, hidden under the form of the elementary species, is ready to take up his abode in our breasts: \u201cLord, I am not worthy.\u201d Efficacious words, which (according to the words of St. Augustin) have the virtue to operate in a Christian soul a miracle quite contrary to that which they signify: for when we pronounce them, they cancel the unworthiness we attribute to ourselves, and give us, with regard to Christ and his sacrament, a fund of merit which we should not be able to acquire without them. Words, which by a wonderful operation of grace, direct our steps to the term from which they seemingly withhold us, as according to the doctrine of all the fathers, the chief, and indeed essential disposition for worthily receiving the body of Christ is, to believe and confess ourselves unworthy of it. Words, in fine, which point out our humility to the Son of God, without the least obstructing his charity; and which, far from making him keep away from us, serves as an attractive to make him come to us.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s007", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "But, beloved hearers, it so falls out, that often in applying these words to ourselves, we strain the sense of them beyond the intention of our blessed Saviour; and, in order to square them to our own fancy, we run the hazard of directly counteracting the views of God. In this sacrament, Christ Jesus seeks us, and we withdraw from him. Through excess of love, he wishes to honour us with his holy visits and we will not receive them. He asks to take up his abode in our hearts, and we under specious, nay, religious pretexts refuse him admittance: for, to exculpate ourselves upon this refusal, we plead our unworthiness; and we say, problably [sic] in a different spirit from that of the centurion, \u201cLord, I am not worthy.\u201d As this is the most obvious and common excuse, it is incumbent on me to consider it particularly, not absolutely with a view to oppose and decry it, nor yet to prove its rectitude, but to examine its nature, which will give me an opportunity of delivering the most solid and important doctrines respecting the practice and use of communion.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s008", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "To abstain from communion under colour of unworthiness, is an excuse, Christians, according to the different dispositions and qualities of those who allege it, susceptible of very different interpretations; and my design, of which this is a succinct notion, is to lay these different interpretations before you, that you may enter into the nature of this excuse, and of the good or bad consequences deducible from it. For there are among Christians two sorts of people who set out on this principle, and may say with the centurion, Lord, I am not worthy that thou shouldest enter into my breast; the righteous, who live in the observance and practice of the law of God, and sinners, who are overwhelmed in the vices and follies of a criminal life. In regard to the righteous it can hardly be doubted, that they are induced by the feelings of an humble heart to speak in that manner. But to know to what length this humility should be carried, and whether it be reasonable that it should make them keep away from Jesus Christ and his sacrament: to know whether the privation of the blessed eucharist, for a righteous soul, may be deemed an ordinary exercise of penance; whether penance of this kind be conformable to the intentions of the Son of God; whether it accords with the end and institution of this mystery; whether it corresponds with the standing usage of the earliest times of the Christian religion; whether it be received and approved by the church in later ages; whether it be countenanced by the ancient fathers, and whether it may be profitable; in a word, whether Jesus Christ, as contained in the divine sacrament of his body, esteems himself honoured, when the righteous, instead of approaching him, withdraw from him, and whether it be rendering him true respect, as he is the Bread of Life, to be contented only with reverencing and adoring him, and not corporally receiving him, are questions, beloved hearers, of which, for sundry and weighty reasons, general and particular, I shall wave the discussion, leaving them to your own particular examination. Besides, as it would be no easy matter to say anything new upon this topic, perhaps it might not do so much good, as may, with good reason, be hoped for from a discourse solely consecrated to the edification of your souls.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s009", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "I shall, therefore, speak only of those sinners, who may tell Jesus Christ with much more reason than did St. Peter: \u201cDepart from me, because I am a sinful man.\u201d Luke v. These I divide into three classes. The first are sincere sinners; the second, blind sinners; and the last, hypocritical sinners. Sincere sinners behave with candour in the presence of God, and are not deceived. Blind sinners are totally ignorant of their own condition, and impose upon themselves. In fine, hypocritical and dissembling sinners cover their libertinism with a veil of piety, and make it their business to deceive others. The first have religion, and are actuated by the spirit of it. The second, although they have some religion, have a wrong notion that they are actuated by the spirit of it. The last, although they would seem to be actuated by the spirit of religion, have in reality no religion at all. Now these three kinds of sinners may hold the language we find the centurion in the gospel held: \u201cLord, I am not worthy,\u201d and decline communicating on account of unworthiness. But, although they all of them assert it equally, they are not all of them equally to be credited. For, to lay still more open the plan of my discourse: in the first, that is, in sincere sinners, this excuse is a reason; in the second, that is, in blind sinners, this excuse is a pretext; and in the last, that is, in hypocritical and libertine sinners, this excuse is an abuse, and even a scandal. And this is now what I undertake to demonstrate. But I proceed farther: for, three things I add to this, which will put the characters of these three sinners in a clear point of view, and must needs convey material instruction.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s010", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "First: unworthiness is a reason for the sincere sinner to abstain from communion; and I hold that this reason ought farther to be explained.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s011", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Secondly: unworthiness is a pretext for the blind sinner to abstain from communion; and to remove this pretext is of the utmost consequence.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s012", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Thirdly: unworthiness is an abuse and scandal in the hypocritical sinner, and consequently a reason to abstain from communion; and my duty obliges me to impugn and bear down this abuse and scandal.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s013", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "PART I. That my first position may appear in as clear a light as possible, I must premise, Christians, that I speak of a sinner, who preserves in the midst of his evil courses a fund of religion; who behaves toward God at least with openness and unfeigned sincerity; who acknowledges the unhappy state of his conscience; confesses his sin, bewails it with sighs, but as yet is not quite disposed to relinquish it. To abstain in that case from the sacred table, because he is unworthy, is a well-founded reason, I own, for the sinner, as the doctrine of faith makes it clear and evident, that so long as his sin is not obliterated, he cannot approach the divine sacrament, without being involved in the guilt of sacrilege. But, beloved hearers, I say that this reason should farther be elucidated, and this elucidation consists in showing you that he must not stop there: I mean, that he must not so abstain from communion, on account of unworthiness, as to think he hath fully performed his duty, by declining to partake of this sacred mystery; but that he must lay down another principle, equally essential, and equally incontestable: I mean, he must know the obligation he is under of quitting forthwith his state of unworthiness in order to be admitted to the holy repast. So that communion itself becomes a pressing motive, which induces him to the necessity of working his conversion, and obliges him to use his utmost endeavours, by a speedy repentance, to procure admission to the adorable sacrament, from which his sinful state withholds him. This is the disposition, if he knows his duty, in which it is his bounden duty to be; and, without which, I maintain that there is nothing solid in his conduct.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s014", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "For, beloved Christians, the great maxim on which is hinged the whole conduct of a sinner, in regard to communion is, never to separate these two points\u2014two inviolable rules in our holy religion: one, that Jesus Christ lays his commands on us to eat his flesh; the other, that he puts us under a strict prohibition of eating it unworthily: one, that the flesh of this God-man is the food of our souls; the other, that this food, though wholesome in itself, is poison for those who presume to take it in the state of sin: one, that as it is not possible to support the natural life without the help of aliments, so it is not possible to support the supernatural life of grace, without the holy eucharist; the other, that as aliments in a diseased body, far from supplying it with strength and nourishment, weaken it, and destroy the source of life; so the eucharist is death to those who receive it without purifying their heart. If the sinner adheres to one of these points, without joining the other, he assuredly goes astray, and is lost for ever; but if he unites them, he enters directly into the way of God. Hear how he reasons: Christ Jesus forbids me to eat his flesh, and keeps me at a distance, so long as my heart is swayed by sin: it is, therefore, my duty to beware of eating it in my present state. But he tells me that if I eat it not, I neither have, nor can have, the supernatural life in which consist the sanctification and happiness of the righteous man; I am therefore obliged, cost what it will, to quit my present sinful state, in order to render myself capable of eating it. I cannot withdraw from the obedience due to both the one and the other of these commandments; to the former for Christ\u2019s sake, to the latter for my own. If I receive communion unworthily, I profane the body of the Lord; and thus I must take care of the interests of Christ. If I do not receive communion at all, I murder my soul, by depriving her of that which alone could nourish her and keep her in union and co-operation with grace; and thus I must secure my own interests. If, remaining a sinner, I participate of the eucharist, I participate of it to my own condemnation; but if I do not participate of it, I shall certainly perish. There remains, therefore, but one alternative; to change my life, to renounce my sin, to return into the grace and favor of God, to fit myself for eating this bread of life, in order that it may become for me a life-giving bread. For by that means, I shall comply with my duty both in what concerns the honour of Jesus, and my own advantage. Thus shall I fulfil what God requires, which is, that I eat, and that I live by eating this bread with profit. Thus, I say, the sinner will reason; and this way of reasoning will be the decisive and infallible cause of his conversion. Whereas, if he only minds his unworthiness, he will spend his days in a circle of crimes, not taking so much as one resolution relative to salvation, or one step in the view of returning with speed to God.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s015", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Now this principle, beloved Christians, which every sinner should lay down for himself, is that which the ministers of the gospel should adopt in delivering instruction upon this topic. Of the two precepts, which I have just been explaining, they should never inculcate one, without remonstrating, at the same time, the necessity of the other. The reason is this: one without the other is at best unserviceable, perhaps prejudicial. For if you perpetually represent to a sinner the horrible danger of an unworthy communion, without ever mentioning the indispensable necessity of communicating worthily, the consequence is, that he will never communicate; which is directly contrary to the express command of our Saviour, who says: \u201cUnless ye eat the flesh of the Son of man, ye shall have no life in you.\u201d John vi. But if, on the other hand, you mention only the necessity of communicating, without ever making him apprehend the danger of an unworthy communion, you put him in the way of making many imperfect, nay, sacrilegious communions, in direct opposition to St. Paul\u2019s words: \u201cLet a man prove himself.\u201d 1 Cor. xi. And this, beloved hearers, (give me leave in this place to make a natural reflection, in which I am sure you will join issue with me,) this hath been the cause of all the evils which the diversity of opinions hath produced in all ages of the Christian era, as it gave too often contrariant biases to the minds of men concerning the use of the divine sacrament. The zeal of some was confined to such methods of intimidation as might keep the sinner from the sacred mysteries; and they repeat to him over and over again these terrible words: \u201cHe that eateth unworthily, eateth damnation to himself,\u201d (1 Cor. xi.) which tended only to inspire him with a horror of an unworthy communion. Others exerted their whole powers in exhorting him to receive the divine food; and they invited him incessantly by that comfortable promise of our Saviour: \u201cHe that eateth this bread shall live for ever;\u201d (John vi.;) by which they seemed only to aim at raising in him an efficacious desire of holy communion. They never were perfectly agreed in their manner of carrying Christ\u2019s design into execution. Had they gone hand in hand, and joined in sentiment, very great advantage would have accrued to the church from so admirable a coalition, inasmuch as it would have been a certain means of sanctifying sinners. But as they disagreed, and as each side was wedded to their own opinion, neither sinners nor the church could receive the advantage which God intended. For they who ever and anon rung in the ears of their hearers the anathematical curse pronounced by St. Paul against the abuse of communion, without mentioning what might serve as a motive for receiving that sacrament, by little and little abolished the use of it, and made the guests withdraw from the bridegroom\u2019s table. But they, likewise, who endeavoured only to give a high idea of the fruits of communion, and the tendency of whose speeches was solely to draw a great number of guests to the table of our Saviour, ran a mighty hazard (like the servants in the parable) of convening indifferently the good and the bad. What was said on both sides might have been very true; and yet on neither side did they say enough to produce the effect of the sacrament of Christ Jesus, because they said on neither side but half of that which ought to have been said. Upon this the holy bishop of Geneva judiciously remarks, that the whole should have been said; and that the invitation of these should have been brought in conjunction with the menaces of those. The sinner should be told: be afraid of approaching this holy table, and be afraid of not approaching it. Be afraid of approaching it, if you are not robed in the nuptial garment\u2014which is divine grace; and be afraid of not approaching it, because none but the enemies of God are excluded. The food which is offered you is mortal for you, if you discern it not justly by the spirit of faith; but know, moreover, that it is a wholesome food, without which Christ Jesus will not abide in you, nor will you in him. Wherefore, receive this food with trembling; for to receive it with trembling is one of the necessary dispositions in receiving it. But tremble still more if you do not receive it, because you will not be prevailed upon to receive it with the necessary preparation. Thus they should have spoken.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s016", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "And indeed, Christians, all the fathers of the church, when they opened their minds upon the same subject held the same language. As those great men were certainly actuated by the Spirit of God, they were far from separating those two things, which they knew full well our Saviour intended should never be separated. St. Chrysostom bids us examine and judge ourselves, lest in participating of Christ\u2019s body, we draw down upon our heads coals of living fire\u2014that is, the indignation and vengeance of God. Thus he expressed himself; and the energy of his words was capable to inspire the faithful who heard him with dread and horror. But this he immediately softens by telling them: now this I say not with intent to deter you from partaking of it\u2014God forbid! but that you may partake of it with proper dispositions, and according to the sacred rules prescribed by the law of God. For, (continues he,) as by partaking of it, without due consideration, we rashly expose ourselves to be lost for ever; so the not partaking of it is the ruin and death of the Christian man. I observe (says St. Augustin) that some among you decline communicating from a consciousness of great and unrepented sins; but I solemnly declare to them\u2014(an important decision this of that father)\u2014that if they stop there, and proceed not to action, they increase the weight and number of their sins, by sinning anew, and depriving themselves of the most necessary and most sovereign remedy. Therefore, my brethren, (concludes he,) I conjure you, if any of you judge yourselves unworthy of communion, to render yourselves worthy with all expedition; because whoever is unworthy of this divine sacrament is unworthy of God.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s017", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Thus spoke the fathers. Now what they said in general and absolute terms, is still more weighty, when considered relatively to that holy time, in which the faithful are obliged by the express and particular command of Christ himself, determined by the church, to receive communion; it is the Easter solemnity, which cannot be celebrated but by the manducation of the Lamb, Christ Jesus. For, on that occasion, to threaten the sinner with the wrath of God if he presume to communicate in a sinful state, and not to threaten him with the same wrath if he do not relinquish his sinful state, in order to satisfy this commandment, \u201cUnless you eat,\u201d would be to instruct him but by halves, and to afford him an opportunity of fomenting his impenitence. He must be made to know the order of the Master\u2014I mean the great Master, by telling him what Christ, by two of his disciples, sent to tell the man whose house he made choice of wherein to celebrate the Feast of the Passover: \u201cThe master saith, I will keep the Passover at thy house.\u201d Matt. xxvi. He must be told: brother, it is within yourself that the mystery of the Passover must be fulfilled, as the time approaches in which Jesus Christ, who is the true Passover, desires to be received by you in the adorable eucharist. True it is, you are not in readiness; but for that very reason it is, that we declare it to you, to the intent you may prepare yourself seriously, speedily, and efficaciously. For there is, in this case, no mean, no temperature between the two extremes. Remaining in sin, you cannot avoid either profaning or deserting the sacrament of Christ; profaning it, if you presume to eat this Passover, without preparing for it by a sincere conversion; deserting it, if for want of preparation and conversion, you are not in a condition requisite to eat it. To think it not strictly agreeable to propriety that you should be reduced to this dilemma, were to arraign the conduct of the church, which is your mother, and of Christ, who is your God; and to say that this dilemma may lead you into excesses, were to endeavour to clear yourself by your very excesses, which consist in the abuse of the most holy things. Be that as it may, this is the penalty by which the church hath, in virtue of her power of binding and unbinding, a canonical right to punish your disobedience; namely, to cut you off from her communion as a scandalous member, when through hardness of heart, or an inveterate attachment to the object of your passion, you withdraw from the communion of the body Christ Jesus. By this, however, she meant not to entangle you in difficulties and perplexities, or expose you to the danger of adding sin to sin; but, like a zealous mother, would lay on you an obligation of making use of what is most salutary and sacred in the Christian religion. To this end, you must break your bands, and quit for ever your criminal engagements. But let it not be forgotten, that this is the object to which the precept of communion tends. You must pull out the eye that gives you scandal; that is, you must break off those intercourses which are the scandal of your life. But herein, you should admire the precept of communion, which forces you, as it were, to do that which is pleasing in the sight of God.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s018", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "But what were the views of the church in enacting these rigorous laws against obdurate sinners, disobedient to her mandates, and neglectful of solemnizing the Paschal Feast? She meant to lay them under a strict obligation, and compel them by necessity. And, whereas, the Holy Ghost declares the legality of such proceeding in express terms, \u201ccompel them to come in;\u201d (Luke xiv.;) she chooses to make use of compulsive methods, that, purified by penance, they may deserve admittance to the table of our Lord. These were the holy methods she used in former times, and this was the violence she exerted against that kind of sinners. For, whatever she deemed of their sinful practices, being Christians and her children, their religion and faith gave her room to hope, that they would never appear at the sacred table, without previous preparation. Accordingly, being affected themselves, though sinners, with a religious respect and profound veneration for the holy sacrament, they did, to receive it, that which otherwise they would never have done; I mean, they were totally changed and reformed, to which they would never have been induced by any other motive whatever. This obligation, by which they were compelled to eat the flesh of a God, and the horror they conceived of unworthily eating it, was what converted them\u2014what tore from their hearts the most predominant passions, and what made them take all the necessary measures, in order to return into favour with God. You object that this method occasions sacrileges; and I answer, that there is nothing, how sacred soever, which may not be profaned by the wickedness of man; and yet that our Saviour commands, under pain of eternal reprobation, every one of the faithful, without exception, to eat his flesh; and that the church, his spouse, would not have acted conformable to his intention, if, at the same time that she publishes to the world St. Paul\u2019s anathema against unworthy communion, she did not lay them under the happy necessity of receiving communion holily and profitably.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s019", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "And here, beloved hearers, are two rocks against which we are usually dashed in these times by the spirit of the world. Provided a sinner be induced to perform, to outward appearance, the duty of a Christian, and to approach the sanctuary, we think much is done. Upon no other grounds we extol his religion\u2014we make no doubt that he is fully converted, and are pleased with the thoughts of his perseverance: this is the first rock. But, on the other side, provided he be made (so long as he remains in his sinful habit) to beware of communicating, we think all is done; and if this sinner, confessing his unworthiness, keeps away from the altar, we are satisfied as though he had fulfilled all justice. And thus he is suffered to range in an endless round of follies, and to waste his thoughtless days in vice. It would seem as if his not receiving communion was a cover for the rest, and he then was allowed to live with impunity, and after the corrupt desires of his heart. From the former of these two abuses, it follows that there are, among those who receive communion, many weak, sluggish Christians; or to use the great apostle\u2019s expression, there are many who sleep the sleep of death: \u201cFor this cause, there are many weak and sickly among you, and many sleep.\u201d 1 Cor. xi. But of the latter, what is the consequence? That among those who withdraw from communion, there are innumerable multitudes who lead scandalous lives, and who claim to themselves the infamous privilege of giving to the church no mark of their Christianity, as the mark by which we are essentially distinguished, in quality of Christians, is (according to St. Paul) the participation of the body of Jesus Christ. From this decay of Christian observances\u2014nay, from this prescription to live at large, we are scarce any longer surprised to see that worldlings are notoriously known to be involved, as it were, in spontaneous excommunication; and that those holy canons, those sacred laws by which such disorders were directed to be punished, are no more\u2014or seem to be no more in force. A decay, which plunges the true pastors of God\u2019s church into agonies of sorrow, being witnesses to the loss of so many souls. And all this, because such sinners are not sufficiently instructed in their duty\u2014because they are not made to know the whole extent of it; because they are taught only to avoid one scandal by another; the scandal of a bad communion by the scandal of impenitence, or the scandal of impenitence by the scandal of a bad communion; instead of being made to understand\u2014that it is not sufficient barely to retrench either the one or the other, but that they must avoid both the one and the other.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s020", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "For, O my God, your holy sacrament was instituted equally for sinners and the righteous. Not, indeed, for impenitent, but converted sinners\u2014changed, reformed, sanctified sinners. While you tarried upon earth, O adorable Saviour, you disdained not to eat at the table of sinners; and now, from a very different motive, but through the same spirit, you admit penitent sinners to your table. And as, heretofore, you ate at the table of sinners, converted by your holy grace much more willingly than at the table of the proud and haughty Pharisees; so I may say for the comfort of my audience and myself, that there are no Christians more favourably received by you than converted sinners\u2014sinners who renounce their evil practices, in order to come to you. But this, as I said, is to be understood only of those sinners who act with openness and a candid mind. For if they be worldlings, who shut their eyes against their own failings and impose upon themselves, the supposed respect which they allege for withdrawing from the sacrament of Christ is no longer a reason that requires explanation, but a pretext\u2014which I am to remove in the second part.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s021", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "PART II. There is nothing more subtile than the spirit of the world in leading us to its end, nor more artful in giving to things whatever colour and form it pleases when the point is to dazzle and to warp the judgment in the ways of God. For then it proposes every specious motive; and not unfrequently we let ourselves be ensnared insomuch, that we are fully persuaded and believe that we honour God by keeping away from him. Now this is the case with those other sinners of whom I am at present about to speak: they flatter themselves that they want not religion, nay, that they are influenced by the spirit of religion, yet are much mistaken; they deviate widely from the straight and simple path of truth, and their humility is, in fact, a gross error. My meaning is this: they say, nay think that it is out of respect they keep from communion, because they acknowledge in the presence of God that they are unworthy of it. But I shall show them, first, that it is a vain respect: secondly, that considering it in the manner they apply it, and in all its circumstances, it is a false respect: finally, that it hath no conformity with that respect which true Christians have always shown whenever they separated (according to the rules and spirit of the church) from the sacrament of Christ. Three important reflections these, by which I undertake, not to put them to confusion but to confound, in their persons, the spirit of the world\u2014that spirit which blinds them, which, to bring them to the precipice and work their destruction, makes a glimmer of devotion, a false glimmer, play before their eyes even in their indevotion.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s022", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "1st. It is a vain respect, and thus I prove the assertion. For, what is understood by a vain respect? What operates nothing; what hath no consequence; what answers no purpose; what tends to no end; what doth nothing to render the Christian more worthy of Christ Jesus and his sacrament; what leaves the sinner in the same imperfections; what makes him neither more regular nor more fervent, nor more holy; in a word, what the only solid proof of is\u2014to abstain from communion. Now such is the respect of that kind of sinners to whom I address this second instruction; and if pride will permit them to own their weakness, they will join issue with me.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s023", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "And, indeed, if the respect which they have, or believe they have for the Son of God were the real motive that withheld them from communion, it would, by dint of instigation, and by frequent impressions induce them to undertake something more; and if it were ever so little effectual, they would give some sign, by their outward demeanour, that they were affected with it. Now this they do not in any shape. For, to what would this motive\u2014to what would this sentiment of respect induce them? To withdraw from the world, as (by their own confession) it is the love of the world that renders them unworthy of the sacred banquet. Affected with their unworthiness, and owning that it arose from the untoward passion they entertained for the world\u2014for the false, the fleeting joys of the world; for the unchristian and dangerous diversions of the world; for the intrigues, the vanity, the luxury of the world\u2014what would they do? They would wean their affections from those diversions, those pleasures\u2014that luxury, that vanity\u2014those intrigues; and, by offering this perfect sacrifice to Christ, from unworthy sinners would be Christians worthy to partake of his flesh. These are the testimonies of respect which they would give him, and which it is incumbent on them to give him. Nothing however, of all this is done; and to judge by their actions, they seem to have no disposition that way. They themselves, if I were to appeal to their own consciences, would frankly confess that they are far from it.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s024", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "It is, therefore, a very mistaken notion, that this respect affects them as much as they would make us believe. It is not, therefore, this respect that keeps them away from the divine mysteries. What then? I have said it, and I say it again\u2014an obstinate attachment to the world, and all that is called the world. They belong to the world, they are part of the world; and that world which God reprobates finds no relish for the things of God. They love the world more than Christ\u2014and therefore, they relinquish Christ for the world. This outward respect is a veil in which their self-love glories; but it is, at bottom, the world that possesses their very souls, and inspires into them a coolness, an indifference, or to speak more properly, a disgust of communion.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s025", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "And this is what our Saviour himself would give us to understand, in the parable, by the guests who neglected to come to the feast because other cares had wholly engrossed their thoughts and affections. There is, however, (St. Augustin observes,) this remarkable difference; that the guests in the parable sincerely confessed the reasons which induced them to keep away; whereas the worldlings, of whom I speak, affect to know not, nay, to hide from themselves the cause of their disorder. They always allege this weak pretext\u2014that as they are unworthy to receive communion they cannot do better than to refrain from it; and accordingly they harbour a secret complacency, as though they honoured Christ Jesus thereby, and Christ Jesus would, one day, load them with rewards for abandoning his altars in order to enjoy the pleasures of the world with more ease and greater liberty. For their infatuation, beloved hearers, rises to that height; and to convince them (says St. Chrysostom)\u2014this seems unanswerable\u2014to convince them that this supposed respect, so far as it regards them, is a pretext, and no reason, though they communicate but rarely they do not communicate the more worthily. That is, when they do communicate, they do not prepare themselves for it the more; they search not their hearts with more care; they do not retire the more from the world; and, if the expression be not too bold, they are at no more charge for Christ\u2019s reception. By the most fallacious of all maxims they flatter themselves, that to communicate seldom, without anything else, will stand them in lieu of all merit; and by an evident error, which they do not discern, they measure the whole respect which they render to this divine mystery, not by greater attention to themselves, not by greater application to their duty, not by greater exactness and regularity, but by the interval\u2014the space which elapses between one communion and another. An infallible sign (subjoins St. Chrysostom) that they are not influenced either by humility or respect, but are seduced by the delusions and spirit of the world.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s026", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Now I say, Christians, that to remove this pretext is of the utmost importance; not by facilitating communion to them, nor by prompting them to it, while they walk in the paths of worldly folly. I am well aware how much the dignity of this sacrament requires of the faithful; and wo is me, if, in the greatest and most holy of all Christian actions, and in the disposition requisite to the duly performing of it, I should ever countenance the slightest abatement. But what I understand by the removing of this pretext is, that they ought to be made to speak to the purpose, and not to say: I refrain from receiving the body of Christ, because I hold it in profound respect; but, I refrain from receiving it because I lead a debauched life, and will not submit to the sacred laws which religion prescribes in order to receive it. I refrain from receiving it because I lead a dissipated life, and my whole thoughts are taken up with nothing but the world and my pleasures. I refrain from receiving it because I lead a pusillanimous life, and have not the courage to do anything or undertake anything for the salvation of my soul. I refrain from receiving it because I attend to my temporal affairs with excessive eagerness, which drains my heart of spiritual unction and hardens it in regard to the things of God. I refrain from receiving it, because I am unwilling to take up a resolution of mortifying my senses and offering my inclinations the least violence. I refrain from receiving it, because I would live in mental emancipation, independent of submission, rule and order, and after the capriciousness of my own humour. To oblige the worldling to agree to all this, and to show him forthwith the irregularity of his conduct, and the flagrant injury done to our Saviour by thus neglecting his adorable sacrament\u2014to make him understand that he doth not an honour but an outrage to God; to make him apprehend that he draws upon himself that terrible curse with which our Saviour concludes the parable: \u201cFor, I say unto you, that none of those men who were bidden shall taste my supper;\u201d (Luke xiv.;) to convince him of all this were the surest means to dispel the dangerous infatuating cloud that darkens his understanding and holds his mind in perpetual delusion. How often, Christians, hath this prediction of the Saviour of men, though minatory only, been fulfilled to the letter! And how many Christians, for having abandoned communion in the course of life, have been deprived of it, by a secret judgment of the heavenly ruler, at the hour of death! But to proceed:", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s027", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "2ndly. Not only it is a vain, but a false respect. The reason is, that it is not accompanied with the two conditions essential to it: one, a piercing sorrow for being separated from the body of Christ; the other, a sincere desire of approaching it: conditions inseparable from true respect, but of which the worldling, if he enters but a little into himself, will find that his heart is wholly destitute. A piercing sorrow for being separated from the body of Christ; for if I honour him as much as I ought to do, and have that respectful attachment to him, of which I flatter myself with the possession, I ought to look upon it as my supreme good to be united with him in the present life. I say united, especially by a sacrament which he hath himself instituted to keep up a holy and ineffable union between him and me. Whence it follows, that by the same rule, I ought to look upon it as my sovereign evil to be separated from this adorable sacrament, of which the participation is a pledge, or rather an anticipation of my beatitude.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s028", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "And this St. Chrysostom understood perfectly, when, speaking of communion, he said to his audience: \u201cLet your only grief be, that you are deprived of this food,\u201d this heavenly food, the flesh of Christ Jesus. Your only grief: for in comparison with this, what are all the other causes of affliction? If, then, I really respect this sacrament in the degree it is respectable, and I would be thought to respect it, nothing should be more painful or afflicting to me than to see myself deprived of this divine nourishment. And, indeed, it ought to create in my heart greater grief than all the losses and crosses in the world. This consideration, \u201cI am separated from my God,\u201d if I believe the revealed truths of religion, ought to fill my soul with greater bitterness, and pierce it with sharper stings of sorrow, than the son of Isaac felt when excluded from parental benediction; and thereby I enter, in a Christian manner, into the spirit and meaning of St. Chrysostom\u2019s words: \u201cLet your only grief be, that you are deprived of this food.\u201d", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s029", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "But still more piercing ought my grief to be if I have room to attribute the separation to myself\u2014a separation arising from my own infidelity, from my obstinate attachment to a shameful object that holds me captive, from my disinclination to make to Christ the sacrifice he expects: but what an increase of heart-anguish, if I rightly discern the whole complication of evils caused by so woful a separation! When the church, exerting the severity of her discipline on the primitive Christians, deprived them for a time of the sacred banquet, what did they do, and what were their feelings? The fathers informs us, that their spirits sunk, that they fell into melancholy, that they sighed, that they moaned, that they shed floods of tears, that they looked upon their state as a temporary reprobation. Thus, though deprived of communication with him, they retained a real respect for Christ Jesus. But have those worldlings, of whom I speak, ever felt this Christian and religious sorrow? I appeal for testimony to their own hearts, and call themselves to witness. Forbidden to communicate, with what tranquillity do they bear the forbiddance? With what indolent ease do they see themselves separated from the God of their salvation? With what insensibility do they bring themselves not only to feel no regret at it, but to find relief by it? Communion in the course of a worldly life is a heavy burden, and they rid themselves of it. It checks, or interrupts their vain pleasures, and they abandon it, to enjoy them without check or interruption. In order to communicate, certain measures must be kept and restraints put up with; but they find it more convenient to keep away from it, and communicate no more. With these dispositions shall they persuade me that they entertain a true respect for Christ Jesus and his sacrament? And although they should persist in their asseverations, should I not be at liberty to reject their words as clashing with truth and right reason?", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s030", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Again, it is a false respect, because it carries not with it a desire of communion. This, I presume, will appear in the sequel. For, beloved Christians, the respect for Christ Jesus, which I am bound to have, may induce me for a time to withdraw from communion, but will never extinguish, no nor diminish, if it be real and true, my desire of communion. So far from it, the more I find myself unworthy to communicate, with the greater ardour I ought in one sense to desire to communicate: for, this desire is evidently a means of repairing my unworthiness. And, in effect, it is through this desire that I return to Christ Jesus, and it is in virtue of it that I endeavour to come nearer and nearer to him. It is through this desire, that, to the same intent, I seek every means, surmount every obstacle, and am faithful in executing every resolution. So long as I am actuated by this desire, the seeds of life are still within me and I am equal to any the most holy undertaking. Whereas when this desire ceases, I am as it were, dead without any sentiment to bring me back to Christ Jesus, or any instigation to make me return to him. Whence it follows, that not only all my unworthiness subsists, but that the extinguishing of this desire is, in some degree, the consummation of my unworthiness. A consummate unworthiness, the consequences of which St. Ambrose was not afraid of swelling to exaggeration, when he held that the loss of this desire might be deemed a presage of future reprobation. Ah! my Lord, (cries out this father,) it is of this adorable eucharistic bread the sacred writings speak when they tells us, that \u201cthey who estrange themselves from thee shall perish:\u201d that is, all those who lose the desire of being united with you, shall be cast off by you.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s031", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Now all this coincides with the notions of the first faithful. I shall dwell a little upon their example, an example that cannot be too often proposed. Upon that account it was, that when they were deprived of the holy mysteries, and communion was withheld them, they expressed so lively, so ardent a desire of being readmitted. Upon that account it was that they asked it so pressingly, and conjured the priests, prostrate at their feet, to shorten the melancholy days they were ordered to live separate from their Saviour. Upon that account it was, that they besought the martyrs to lend them their intercession; and in this (says St. Cyprian) appeared their respect, their true respect. But how doth the man of the world demean himself? Content to resemble them in this separation, he takes no pains to follow their example in other respects; and, confounding communion with the desire of communion, he renounces equally both the one and the other, and retains no more for the sacrament of Christ, than an indifference of heart which should fill him with horror.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s032", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "This, beloved hearers, is what the fathers deplored so bitterly; this is what they looked upon as one of the wounds, and greatest misfortunes of the times they lived in; this is what St. Chrysostom objected with such energy to the people of Antioch. What a shame, (says he,) my brethren, to see your coolness, when you are spoke to about receiving the Holy of Holies! If a pompous spectacle be exhibited in town, it attracts your attention and you run to it in crowds, but nothing can induce you to take part in the august sacrifice of our altars. All your public places, all your amphitheatres are filled, but the table of Christ Jesus is abandoned. In vain are we assiduous in distributing to you the celestial gifts: no one among you is willing to receive them. Christ Jesus waits for you in person, and he is forsaken. Sometimes this father represented to them with what zeal they flocked to hear his discourses, while they discovered so little, to receive from his hands the pledge of their salvation. Sometimes he complained of their obduracy concerning this sacrament of love. Sometimes he showed them the dangerous consequences of the ill-judged respect on which they plumed themselves. And here, beloved hearers, imagine to yourselves that it is St. Chrysostom that addresses you, as in fact it is; or bless kind heaven, which inspired that great man, in those days, with that which should now confound your pitiable, but pernicious errors.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s033", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "3rdly. I have told you, and have already made appear in great part, that the respect which the worldling pleads in excuse for not receiving the body of the Lord, hath no conformity with that of the primitive ages of the church. Of this the proof is very obvious: for in those flourishing ages of Christianity, the sinner, while separate from the body of Christ, was taken up with the exercise of a laborious penance, of which he underwent the whole rigour with surprising courage. And this penance, according to the laws and discipline of the church, was not a mere ceremony, whereas it consisted of painful austerities. Abstinence and fasts, sack-cloth and ashes, hair-shirts and chains, and severe flagellations, were, it is well known, its inseparable concomitants. And hence it appears how greatly the sinner honoured Christ Jesus, since he desired to undergo such rigorous practices, and make him, at the expense of his ease and comfort, so signal a reparation.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s034", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "And now be it owned, to our eternal shame, that proofs of this nature suit not the taste or devotion of worldlings. In whatever respect for Christ they may pride themselves, they will not put themselves to that trouble. Infatuated with the world, and with its false joys and false delicacy, they expect to come off at an easier rate. The sum total of their penance is, that they communicate no more; a kind of penance that gives them no trouble. So far from it, it sooths their inclination, and occasions their living in greater liberty, or, more properly, in greater libertinism. For, to this pass it is that things are brought by their pretext of false respect; and I would to God that the mischievous error which I am now endeavouring to defeat, were a chimerical phantom, and not a real truth! But I shall dismiss this topic, and proceed to show you, that this supposed respect for the sacrament is, in fact, a scandal in the hypocritical sinner, the subject of the third and last part.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s035", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Part III. It is a pretty generally received maxim, that what is good in itself, is not always so with respect to the principle from which it derives; and one of the rules of prudence is, to look on things even the most salutary with a suspicious eye, so soon as we find that they flow from an infected and poisoned source. Now we may, nay, we ought to apply this rule to what regards religion and the practice of piety. I know not, Christians, whether you have ever made a reflection that to me seems solid, and the truth of which I am sure you will comprehend more easily than I do: to wit, that whenever disputes arose among Christians concerning relaxation or severity of discipline, certain libertines were not only ready, but eager to declare for and side with the severe party: not that they intended to embrace such opinion in practice and follow it, a disposition from which they were very remote; but to enjoy the unaccountable pleasure of talking of it, or to make it serve as a convenient veil for covering their designs. Accordingly, there have been, time after time, men otherwise immersed in scandalous vices, men equally wrong-headed and corrupt-hearted, vain, voluptuous, self-loving men who were the first and most zealous in standing forth in behalf and support of reformation. Women there have been, but too well known for what they had been, and perhaps for what they were at that present; women, whose mouths the past should have shut, who declaimed eloquently against depravity of manners, who thought nothing sufficiently exact or rigid in the government of the church, and who appealed incessantly to the ancient canons as they were in force at their first institution. But is not this zeal for the purity of morals and perfection of Christianity to be commended in a Christian? Yes: (replies St. Bernard:) but the more commendable it is in a Christian, the more, to say the least of it, equivocal and doubtful it is in a libertine; and I ought, according to the precept of our Saviour, to beware of it as I should of the most dangerous and base hypocrisy.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s036", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Now what St. Bernard remarked in general, concerning the purity and regularity of morals, is still more particularly and sensibly verified with respect to communion. You know what came to pass: much was said, and with great reason, of abuses committed in the frequent use of the eucharistic sacrament, of the extreme facility with which it was to be feared that sinners were admitted to it, of the necessity of keeping from it for a limited time\u2014of lukewarm souls who were not amended by it\u2014of the discretion and prudence which the pastors of the church were bound to use in these respects. All this was good, holy, edifying; and I make no doubt\u2014(be pleased to mind it)\u2014I make no doubt, that true believers, affected with the interests of God and of his church had upright intentions in this extraordinary display of their zeal. But to me it is amazing that persons of a quite different character, I mean your libertines and loose livers, should expect to be supposed of the same party; and, that making a common cause with those with whom they had nothing in common, they should be much the most strenuous and ardent in asserting the respect due to the sacrament of Christ and his adorable body. It is amazing that persons, thought by the most knowing to have but little religion, persons addicted to the greatest vices, should take upon them to talk with so much warmth against frequent communion, should so openly take offence at the least abatement, whether real or imaginary in this behalf, and should take part in the debate, as though it were a thing that nearly concerned them. All this is amazing.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s037", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "For, whence can all this zeal arise? Impious as I suppose them, they harbour in their minds a secret contempt for every other Christian duty; and of this they talk in the spiritual language of the perfect and the godly. Therefore they must have some interested view, and you are too clear-sighted not immediatetly [sic] to discern in what this interested view consists; for, it is easily discoverable; and it is at least certain, that by so speaking they take to themselves the privilege of being libertines, not only with security, but, if I may venture to say it, with honour. For, these are they whom St. Paul describes in his epistle to Timothy; men of tainted principles, whose faith is extinct in a great measure, and to whom every religious exercise and practice is a heavy burden they would gladly shake off. As they know, however, that communion was always regarded as a special mark of Christianity, and that to renounce it openly would be a kind of apostacy, the disgrace of which would be painful to them: for fear of being exposed to that degree, and yet to get rid of a troublesome yoke, they screen with religion their want of religion, (I know not whether I explain my meaning,) and they pass for approvers of that maxim, which, through a sentiment of fear and respect, withholds us from Christ, to the end they may not be distinguished from even the most exact Christians, as they speak like them, and seem equally zealous.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s038", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Now I hold that this language in the mouth of the libertine is a scandal to the weak. You ask me why? Be attentive, if you please. Because it hath a tendency to two things equally pernicious; namely, to decry, indifferently, good and bad communions; to keep away souls not only from communion but universally from everything holy in religion.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s039", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "1st. To decry indifferently good and bad communions. For, (as St. Chrysostom rightly argues,) if at all times there is danger of bringing true piety into disesteem by running down the false, much more so is it, if the self-made judge is a profane wretch, little solicitous whether he confound the one with the other; a wretch, who, more probably impeaches the one, for no other reason than that he is a secret enemy to the other; a wretch, who, far from taking precautions to sift out and separate the truth from falsehood, seems rather affected with a pruriency to destroy the truth by falsehood. Now what this father expressed concerning devotional duties, I may safely affirm (for experience confirms it) with regard to communion. If, by condemning bad communions, we must always be afraid of condemning good ones; much more so, when the officious inspector\u2019s perverted reason makes him disregard both good and bad, and have no scruple of prejudicing those by decrying these.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s040", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "And, in reality, where tends the malignant zeal of impious, freethinking livers, who avail themselves of it to the great detriment of innumerable righteous and innocent souls? It tends to do that in the church of God which the children of the high priest, Heli, did in the temple of Jerusalem: they kept away the people from the holy sacrifice. A crime which the Almighty held in execration, and for which he cast them off: \u201cThe sin was very great, because they kept men from the sacrifice of the Lord;\u201d (1 Kings ii.;) or, if you please, it tends to do that which the Pharisees did, when the Saviour of the world for that reason told with indignation: \u201cWo be to you, who shut out others from the kingdom of heaven; for ye neither go in yourselves, nor suffer them that are entering to go in.\u201d Matt. xxiii. This typifies what is every day done by a certain kind of men, who, having withdrawn, through obduracy of heart, from the divine mysteries, by which (according to the doctrine of St. Cyril) the kingdom of God is laid open to us, would, were it possible, exclude all others. To this are all their endeavours directed. And truly they succeed but too well, by carping and sneering at worthy men on account of communion; by censuring their lives; by blaming their conduct; by animadverting on every the slightest of their failings; by forgiving nothing, but charging them with everything. St. Augustin, notwithstanding his vast comprehension, did not disapprove of daily communion; but the rash worldling, blind and unskilled in the things of God, shall condemn it boldly and without hesitation. It was the sincere and hearty wish of the last \u0153cumenical council, to see frequent communion restored in the church; but the rash worldling would have it on the contrary be abolished and annihilated.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s041", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Think not, however, beloved hearers, that I mean by this to assert the rectitude of every kind of frequent communion. Some there are which I cannot but deplore, but the judging of which I leave to God: that is, there are frequent communions, but unprofitable, unbecoming, disedifying, and calculated to give scandal rather than edification. I said I left it to God to pass judgment on them; for as I should have the greatest horror of advancing aught that might seem to countenance such communions; so should I condemn myself for prevarication, should I blame communion, however frequent, if performed with fervour. Others dishonour, these glorify Christ; and as I should ever account those accursed who should ever approve of imperfect communion, the same shall I think of him who shall oppose what sanctifies souls, and redounds to the honour and glory of Christ. Who can pretend to say what numbers of righteous souls the evil spirit, by this sole artifice, hath kept from our altars? How he hath disturbed the brides of Christ in their holy communications with the heavenly bridegroom? How many communions, at which the angels would have rejoiced in heaven, hath he, as I may say, interdicted upon earth?", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s042", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "2ndly. Further: from neglect of communion the scandal passes, if care be not taken to fence against it, to the abandoning or retrenching of all the most holy practices in religion; and it is St. Chrysostom\u2019s second observation: For, admitting this counterfeit and mistaken humility as a rule of action, what dangerous consequences may not be drawn from it, and to what religious duties and observances is not a soul, though devoted to God, tempted to renounce? You are unworthy to present yourself at the table of Christ, (it is St. Chrysostom that speaks,) and are you worthy to set foot in the temple of God? Are you worthy to pray to and invoke God? Are you worthy to hear the word of God? Are you worthy to be admitted to penance, and to the tribunal of the mercy of God? Are you worthy, with his church, to sing the praises of God? Are you worthy to be present when the divine sacrifice is offered up to God? For the same reason, your duty obliges you to relinquish all this, and the sight of your unworthiness, if I may so speak, should keep you in a kind of excommunication, which would cut you off from whatever is called Christian worship and duty.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s043", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Thus concluded this holy doctor; and to say nothing of virtuous souls, whose simplicity may be imposed upon by this delusion, this is the advantage which libertines would draw from it. They would be delighted with extending to all Christian obligations these words of the centurion, explained and wrested to their own sense: \u201cLord, I am not worthy.\u201d And as they employ them, libertines as they are, to seem humble and religious in not communicating; so going a step farther, they would gladly find out a handle to forsake our churches through respect, to discontinue the use of prayer through respect\u2014to rid themselves of every duty through respect. Now this is the scandal that ought to have been opposed. Pardon me, if I speak of it with some vehemence: it is to promote the interests of Christ and religion. Let the prelates of the church promulgate laws, and give pastoral charges, in order to reform the abuse of communion, they do their duty, and I shall always respect them. Let the priests, entrusted with the cure of souls, labour all they can to find proper remedies for the growing evil, it is essential to their function, and for that it was that God appointed them. Let even the laity, according to the measure of the grace which God hath imparted to them, contribute thereunto, by beginning with themselves, before they extend their zeal to others, and I shall be edified at it. But that profane worldlings, bewildered and sightless in heavenly matters, perhaps destitute of divine faith, should take upon them to decide, to regulate, to disgrace with their errors and impiety, a case the most important in our holy religion, is what I shall always condemn and oppose with all my might.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-s044", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Let us use our endeavours, beloved brethren\u2014(it is to you, ye priests of the living God, and ministers of his altars, it is to you I address myself)\u2014let us use our endeavours to prepare for the Lord a godly people. United by the sacred bond of charity, let us labour indefatigably to convert sinners, to perfectionate the righteous, to purify the faithful, with a view of making them worthy to receive the sacrament of Christ. This work it is that must take up our time; to this end it is, that our labours must tend. For I am bold enough to affirm, beloved brethren, that the church of God will never be sanctioned, nor Christianity reformed but by good use of holy communion. Reason and refine as much as you please, you must always recur to these adorable words of our Saviour: \u201cUnless ye eat the flesh of the Son of man, ye have no life in you.\u201d On the contrary, \u201che that eateth this bread, shall live for ever,\u201d shall live here in grace, and hereafter in glory.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n001", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "Carroll's English follows the French paragraph by paragraph: no complete French paragraph is missing, and he divides the text into more paragraphs and supplies some Scripture references the French gives only in Latin. It is not a close translation, however. Carroll omits clauses and sentences, adds a few phrases of his own, softens or changes several doctrinal statements and prints 'sanctioned' where the French has 'sanctified'; the notes on individual passages identify these differences. Carroll's underlying French edition has not been identified here; the comparisons describe differences from the 1716 French edition used in this library without establishing the cause of each difference.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n002", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French heading gives the occasion as 'Sermon pour le premier Jeudi du Caresme' (the first Thursday of Lent); Carroll's heading gives 'For the Third Sunday after Epiphany', which has the same Gospel of the centurion (Matt. 8). The French historical occasion and the separate calendar browse mapping remain unchanged.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n003", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French has Christ promise 'I will come', adding 'whatever your state of languor, if you sincerely wish to be healed, I will heal you'; the communicants then reply with the centurion. Carroll omits that conditional promise and restructures the sentence so that the condition attaches to the communicants' reply.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n004", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French speaks of the God of glory 'hidden under the sacred symbols'; Carroll renders this 'hidden under the form of the elementary species.'", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n005", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French closes the introduction by asking for light 'through the intercession of the Mother of God' and saying 'Ave, Maria.' Carroll ends with the announcement of the subject and omits both.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n006", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "Carroll adds the reference 'Luke v.' to the quotation 'Exi a me, quia homo peccator sum', which the French gives without reference.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n007", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "Where the French calls this excuse in the hypocritical sinner 'an abuse and even a scandal' that the preacher must oppose, Carroll adds that it is 'consequently a reason to abstain from communion.' That clause is absent from the French and sits uneasily with its argument, which treats the excuse as an abuse to be opposed.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n008", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French requires the sinner to 'merit by a true and prompt penance' approaching the sacrament. Carroll has him strive 'by a speedy repentance, to procure admission', omitting 'true' penance and the language of meriting the approach.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n009", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French says the Eucharist alone can 'nourish' the soul 'and make it live.' Carroll renders this 'nourish her and keep her in union and co-operation with grace', a formulation not in the French.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n010", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French describes Easter as the solemnity 'for which we must prepare every day of this Lent.' Carroll omits that clause.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n011", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French says Christ 'wishes and must be received by you' in the Eucharist. Carroll says only that he 'desires to be received by you', omitting the stated necessity.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n012", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French twice sets the scandal of a bad communion against 'the scandal of impenitence and irreligion.' Carroll has 'impenitence' alone on both sides of the contrast, omitting 'irreligion.'", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n013", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French says Christ now admits penitent sinners to his table 'by a very different conduct, but always by the same spirit.' Carroll renders this 'from a very different motive, but through the same spirit', attributing a different motive rather than a different manner of acting.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n014", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French says the world that God reprobates 'has no taste for Jesus Christ.' Carroll generalizes this to 'finds no relish for the things of God.'", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n015", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "After the Gospel quotation 'Dico autem vobis...' the French adds Christ's explanation: his table was prepared for those invited, they sought pretexts to stay away, he will punish them, and none of them will be received at the banquet prepared for them. Carroll omits this explanation and supplies the reference 'Luke xiv.'", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n016", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French describes the worldlings as 'away from communion', a distance they keep and bear calmly. Carroll renders this 'Forbidden to communicate' and 'the forbiddance', turning a voluntary distance into an imposed prohibition.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n017", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French reports St. Ambrose as holding that losing the desire for communion 'was no less than a presage' of future reprobation. Carroll makes this less categorical: the loss 'might be deemed' such a presage.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n018", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French has the penitents implore the priests 'by the bowels of the mercy of Jesus Christ' to shorten their separation. Carroll keeps their entreaty but omits that appeal.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n019", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "Where the French lists the penitential austerities as 'sackcloth and ashes, the hair shirt and the mortifications of the body', Carroll renders them 'sack-cloth and ashes, hair-shirts and chains, and severe flagellations.'", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n020", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French says the Church of God will never be 'sanctified' except through the good use of communion. Carroll's printed text reads 'sanctioned' (checked against the 1843 page image), which changes sanctification into approval.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-communion-carroll-n021", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French ends with the customary closing formula 'o\u00f9 nous conduise, &c.'; Carroll stops at 'shall live here in grace, and hereafter in glory.'", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "On Communion", "topics": ["Eucharist", "Communion", "penance", "conversion", "humility", "hypocrisy", "Church", "salvation"], "url": "sermons/bourdaloue-careme-communion.html"}, {"author": "Louis Bourdaloue", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-lent", "calendar_label": "Lent", "centuries": [17, 18], "composition": "Approximately late seventeenth century\u20131704; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination", "language": "fr", "language_label": "French", "metadata": ["Louis Bourdaloue, Sermons du Pere Bourdaloue, de la Compagnie de Jesus, pour le Caresme, tome premier, nouvelle \u00e9dition (Paris: aux d\u00e9pens de Rigaud, Directeur de l'Imprimerie Royale, 1716), Sermon pour le Vendredi de la premi\u00e8re Semaine. Sur la Pr\u00e9destination, pp. 378\u2013425. Kelly Library copy.", "Internet Archive; John M. Kelly Library Rare Books, University of Toronto; sponsored by the University of Ottawa.", "Lent", "Friday of the first week of Lent", "John 5", "Psalm 17 (historical numbering)", "Lamentations 5", "Isaiah 45", "1 Peter 5", "Matthew 26", "Psalm 26 (historical numbering)", "2 Peter \u2014 chapter numeral uncertain in print", "2 Peter 1:10 \u2014 added by editors; printed chapter remains uncertain", "1 Thessalonians \u2014 chapter numeral uncertain at image edge", "1 Thessalonians 5:21 \u2014 added by editors; printed chapter remains uncertain", "1 Corinthians 1", "Psalm 155 (printed locator; apparent reference error)", "Psalm 55:5 (56:5 in modern numbering) \u2014 reference added by editors for the adapted quotation", "Ephesians 4", "2 Timothy 4 (printed locator; quotation identified separately)", "2 Timothy 1:12 \u2014 added by editors", "Wisdom 1", "Jeremiah 5 (printed locator; quotation identified separately)", "Jeremiah 31:3 \u2014 added by editors", "Romans 11", "Psalm 30 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 1:24, 26 \u2014 added by editors; no printed marginal locator", "1 Corinthians 9 (additional printed locator beside the 1 Timothy quotation)", "1 Timothy 2", "Matthew 23", "Psalm 5", "Psalm 58 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 50 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 61 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "Vendredi de la premi\u00e8re Semaine [du Car\u00eame]", "occasion_detail": "Friday of the first week of Lent", "occasion_label": "Lent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Taking Christ\u2019s question to the sick man at the pool in John 5 as its starting point, Bourdaloue\u2019s sermon on predestination opposes two abuses: presumption that neglects the work of salvation, and despair that abandons hope of it. The first part joins the necessity of grace to free human cooperation, vigilance and prayer; the second grounds hope in God\u2019s goodness while holding hearers responsible for their response. The conclusion asks for confidence and fear to be governed together."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-h001", "label": "French original", "text": "SERMON POUR LE VENDREDI de la premi\u00e8re Semaine. Sur la Pr\u00e9destination."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON FOR FRIDAY of the First Week. On Predestination.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-e001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Erat autem quidam homo ibi, triginta & octo annos habens in infirmitate sua. Hunc c\u00f9m vidisset Jesus jacentem, & cognovisset quia jam multum tempus haberet, dicit ei : Vis sanus fieri ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-e001", "label": "English", "text": "Now there was a man there who had been infirm for thirty-eight years. When Jesus saw him lying there and knew that he had been so for a long time, he said to him: Do you wish to be made well?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-e002", "label": "French original", "text": "Or il y avoit l\u00e0 un homme malade depuis trente-huit ans. Jesus l'ayant v\u00fb couch\u00e9 par terre, & s\u00e7achant depuis combien de tems il \u00e9toit dans cet \u00e9tat, lui dit : Voulez-vous \u00eatre gu\u00e9ri ? En saint Jean, chap. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-e002", "label": "English", "text": "Now there was a man there who had been ill for thirty-eight years. Jesus, seeing him lying on the ground and knowing how long he had been in this condition, said to him: Do you wish to be healed? In Saint John, chapter 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-h002", "label": "French original", "text": "Sire,"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-h002", "label": "English", "text": "Sire,", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p001", "label": "French original", "text": "A en juger par les apparences, fut-il jamais une demande moins n\u00e9cessaire, que celle du Fils de Dieu \u00e0 ce paralytique de notre Evangile ? C'\u00e9toit un malade de trente-huit ans, expos\u00e9 comme les autres sur le bord de la piscine miraculeuse. Il attendoit avec impatience qu'on l'y jett\u00e2t au moment que l'eau seroit remu\u00e9e par l'Ange du Seigneur ; il cherchoit un homme charitable pour lui rendre ce bon office ; il \u00e9toit afflig\u00e9, & il se plaignoit m\u00eame de n'en avoir encore p\u00fb trouver ; enfin il ne d\u00e9siroit rien plus ardemment que sa gu\u00e9rison, & il n'avoit point d'autre pens\u00e9e ni d'autre soin qui l'occup\u00e2t : pourquoi donc lui demander s'il veut \u00eatre gu\u00e9ri, Vis sanus fieri ? Mais ce n'est pas sans raison, r\u00e9pond saint Augustin. Ce paralytique \u00e9toit la figure des p\u00e9cheurs ; & lui-m\u00eame, comme p\u00e9cheur, il ne pouvoit \u00eatre gu\u00e9ri, sans \u00eatre converti, selon la pratique du Sauveur des hommes de ne gu\u00e9rir jamais les corps qu'au m\u00eame-tems il ne sanctifi\u00e2t les ames. Or quelque dispos\u00e9 que f\u00fbt ce malade \u00e0 sa gu\u00e9rison, peut-\u00eatre ne l'\u00e9toit-il pas \u00e9galement \u00e0 sa conversion ; & c'est pour cela que Jesus-Christ qui s\u00e7avoit que l'un d\u00e9pendoit de l'autre, & qui ne vouloit pas lui accorder l'un s'il ne consentoit \u00e0 l'autre, lui demande avant toutes choses : Vis sanus fieri ? voulez-vous \u00eatre gu\u00e9ri ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p001", "label": "English", "text": "Judging by appearances, was any question ever less necessary than the one the Son of God put to this paralytic in our Gospel? He had been ill for thirty-eight years, laid out like the others beside the miraculous pool. He waited impatiently for someone to throw him into it the moment its water was stirred by the Angel of the Lord; he sought a charitable man to do him this kindness; he was distressed, and even complained that he had not yet been able to find one. In short, he desired nothing more ardently than his healing, and no other thought or concern occupied him. Why, then, ask him whether he wishes to be healed: Do you wish to be made well? Yet there is a reason, Saint Augustine replies. This paralytic represented sinners; and, being himself a sinner, he could not be healed without being converted, in accordance with the Saviour of mankind's practice of never healing bodies without at the same time sanctifying souls. Now, however disposed this sick man was towards his healing, perhaps he was not equally disposed towards his conversion. That is why Jesus Christ, who knew that the one depended on the other and would not grant him the one unless he consented to the other, asks him before anything else: Do you wish to be made well? Do you wish to be healed?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p002", "label": "French original", "text": "Tel est, Chr\u00e9tiens, notre \u00e9tat en qualit\u00e9 de p\u00e9cheurs : il y a peut-\u00eatre long-tems que nous languissons, & que nous sommes sans action & sans mouvement dans la voie de Dieu, ou plut\u00f4t hors de la voie de Dieu. Peut-\u00eatre Dieu voit-il parmi nous des paralytiques de plusieurs ann\u00e9es, c'est-\u00e0-dire, des hommes endurcis dans leurs habitudes criminelles : & plaise au ciel qu'entre ceux \u00e0 qui je parle, il n'y en ait pas dont on puisse dire : Erat autem quidam triginta & octo annos habens in infirmitate sua : ce p\u00e9cheur est depuis trente-huit ans dans son d\u00e9sordre. Nous avions besoin d'un homme pour nous affranchir de la servitude du p\u00e9ch\u00e9. Cet homme est venu, & c'est Jesus-Christ. Il nous a jett\u00e9s dans la piscine, je veux dire, dans les eaux salutaires du bapt\u00eame, o\u00f9 nous avons \u00e9t\u00e9 r\u00e9g\u00e9n\u00e9r\u00e9s. Au lieu de nous maintenir dans la possession de cette grace, nous en sommes d\u00e9ch\u00fbs : & il est encore pr\u00eat de nous faire entrer dans une seconde piscine, qui est celle des larmes & de la p\u00e9nitence. Mais auparavant il nous demande \u00e0 tous en g\u00e9n\u00e9ral & \u00e0 chacun en particulier : Vis sanus fieri ? est-ce de bonne foi que vous voulez \u00eatre gu\u00e9ri ? C'est \u00e0 quoi il faut que nous r\u00e9pondions, & ce qui me donne lieu de vous entretenir d'une mati\u00e8re importante, puisqu'il s'agit des desseins de Dieu sur nous par rapport au salut, & de la mani\u00e8re dont nous y devons coop\u00e9rer. C'est en cela m\u00eame aussi que consiste le grand myst\u00e8re de la pr\u00e9destination. Myst\u00e8re profond & adorable : myst\u00e8re sur lequel on a form\u00e9 & l'on forme encore dans le Christianisme tant de questions : myst\u00e8re dont je veux vous parler aujourd'hui, pour vous apprendre l'usage que vous en devez faire, les \u00e9garemens, les \u00e9cueils qu'il y faut \u00e9viter. Saluons d'abord Marie, & disons-lui, Ave, Maria."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p002", "label": "English", "text": "Such, Christians, is our condition as sinners. Perhaps we have long been languishing, inactive and motionless in God's way, or rather outside God's way. Perhaps God sees among us people paralysed for many years, that is, men hardened in their sinful habits. Heaven grant that among those I address there are none of whom one could say: There was a man who had been infirm for thirty-eight years; this sinner has persisted in his disorder for thirty-eight years. We needed a man to free us from slavery to sin. That man has come, and he is Jesus Christ. He has cast us into the pool, I mean the saving waters of baptism, where we were born anew. Instead of retaining possession of this grace, we have fallen from it; and he is still ready to bring us into a second pool, that of tears and penance. But first he asks all of us together and each of us individually: Do you wish to be made well? Do you sincerely wish to be healed? This is what we must answer, and it gives me occasion to speak to you about an important matter, for it concerns God's designs for our salvation and the way in which we must cooperate with them. This is precisely what constitutes the great mystery of predestination: a profound and adorable mystery; a mystery about which so many questions have been raised and are still raised within Christianity; a mystery about which I wish to speak to you today, to teach you the use you should make of it and the errors and pitfalls to avoid in it. Let us first salute Mary and say to her: Hail, Mary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p003", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est le malheur de l'homme d'abuser de tout, & de corrompre soit par la malice de son c\u0153ur, soit par les erreurs de son esprit, jusques aux dons de Dieu, jusques aux attributs de Dieu, jusques aux myst\u00e8res de Dieu. V\u00e9rit\u00e9 que saint Augustin a voulu nous faire entendre, lorsque se servant d'une expression bien hardie, il a dit que Dieu qui est la saintet\u00e9, la puret\u00e9 par excellence, n'est pour les impies & pour les p\u00e9cheurs, ni saint, ni pur ; puisque les p\u00e9cheurs & les impies se font tous les jours de Dieu m\u00eame comme un sujet de prophanation : Immundis, ne Deus quidem ipse mundus est. Or ce que saint Augustin disoit de Dieu, est encore plus vrai de la pr\u00e9destination de Dieu. Car cette pr\u00e9destination est un myst\u00e8re de grace ; & par l'abus qu'en font les hommes, elle leur devient une mati\u00e8re de scandale. Ils s'en servent comme d'un pr\u00e9texte, les uns pour vivre dans une vaine confiance qui leur fait n\u00e9gliger le salut, & les autres pour s'entretenir dans des d\u00e9fiances criminelles qui ruinent en eux l'esp\u00e9rance du salut. Ceux-ci s'en pr\u00e9valent pour pr\u00e9sumer trop de Dieu, & ceux-l\u00e0 en sont troubl\u00e9s jusqu'\u00e0 d\u00e9sesp\u00e9rer des bont\u00e9s de Dieu : les premiers par un exc\u00e8s de t\u00e9m\u00e9rit\u00e9, & comptant sur la pr\u00e9destination de Dieu, concluent que leur salut est en assurance, sans qu'ils se mettent en peine d'y travailler ; & les seconds par une pusillanimit\u00e9 de c\u0153ur & dans un sentiment tout contraire, se persuadent qu'il n'y a plus de salut pour eux, & que ce seroit envain qu'ils y travailleroient. Deux grands d\u00e9sordres auxquels nous sommes expos\u00e9s \u00e0 l'\u00e9gard de la pr\u00e9destination : deux \u00e9cueils dont nous avons \u00e0 nous pr\u00e9server, la pr\u00e9somption & le d\u00e9sespoir. Ce sont aussi, Chr\u00e9tiens, ces deux d\u00e9sordres que j'entreprends de combattre dans ce discours, en vous faisant voir que la pr\u00e9destination de Dieu ne favorise ni l'un ni l'autre ; & que nous sommes inexcusables, lorsqu'en cons\u00e9quence de ce myst\u00e8re, nous nous abandonnons, ou \u00e0 la pr\u00e9somption qui nous fait oublier le soin du salut ; ce sera le premier point ; ou au d\u00e9sespoir qui nous fait renoncer au salut, ce sera le second. Il ne me faudroit point d'autre r\u00e9gle, ni d'autre preuve que la parole de Jesus-Christ : Vis sanus fieri ? Voulez-vous \u00eatre gu\u00e9ri ? Car puisque sur le salut on nous demande aussi-bien qu'au paralytique de l'Evangile, si nous le voulons, il faut donc en effet le vouloir & y travailler, & voil\u00e0 le rem\u00e8de \u00e0 notre pr\u00e9somption : & puisqu'on nous fait au m\u00eame-tems conno\u00eetre qu'il ne s'agit que de le vouloir, nous ne devons donc pas nous troubler ni d\u00e9sesp\u00e9rer, & voil\u00e0 le rem\u00e8de \u00e0 notre d\u00e9fiance. Deux v\u00e9rit\u00e9s fondamentales de notre Religion, sur lesquelles je vais vous d\u00e9couvrir mes pens\u00e9es, & qui peuvent beaucoup servir \u00e0 la r\u00e9formation de vos m\u0153urs."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p003", "label": "English", "text": "It is man's misfortune to abuse everything and, through the malice of his heart or the errors of his mind, to corrupt even God's gifts, even God's attributes, even God's mysteries. Saint Augustine wished to make us understand this truth when, using a very bold expression, he said that God, who is holiness and purity itself, is neither holy nor pure for the impious and for sinners, since sinners and the impious daily make even God an occasion of profanation: To the unclean, not even God himself is clean. Now what Saint Augustine said of God is still more true of God's predestination. For this predestination is a mystery of grace; yet men's abuse of it makes it a cause of scandal to them. Some use it as a pretext for living in a vain confidence that makes them neglect salvation; others, for maintaining sinful misgivings that destroy their hope of salvation. The former use it to presume too much upon God, while the latter are troubled by it to the point of despairing of God's goodness. The first, through excessive rashness and reliance on God's predestination, conclude that their salvation is secure without their troubling to work at it; the second, through faintheartedness and a quite contrary conviction, persuade themselves that there is no longer any salvation for them and that working for it would be in vain. These are two great disorders to which we are exposed concerning predestination, two pitfalls against which we must guard: presumption and despair. These, Christians, are also the two disorders I undertake to combat in this discourse, by showing you that God's predestination favours neither, and that we are inexcusable when, as a consequence of this mystery, we abandon ourselves either to the presumption that makes us forget the care of salvation, which will be my first point, or to the despair that makes us renounce salvation, which will be my second. I should need no other rule or proof than Jesus Christ's words: Do you wish to be made well? Do you wish to be healed? For, since we are asked whether we desire salvation, just as the paralytic in the Gospel was asked, we must indeed desire it and work for it: there is the remedy for our presumption. And since we are told at the same time that it is only a matter of willing it, we must neither be troubled nor despair: there is the remedy for our distrust. These are two fundamental truths of our Religion, on which I shall lay my thoughts before you and which can do much to reform your conduct.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-h003", "label": "French original", "text": "I. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-h003", "label": "English", "text": "PART I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p004", "label": "French original", "text": "Se confier en Dieu, & mettre en lui toute son esp\u00e9rance ; le regarder comme l'auteur, ou selon le langage de l'Ecriture, comme le Dieu de son salut, Deus salutis meae ; faire fond sur les m\u00e9rites de Jesus-Christ, & compter sur le bienfait de la R\u00e9demption ; dire, Je puis tout en celui qui me fortifie, & tout ce que je ferai jamais devant Dieu, c'est par la grace que je le ferai : je l'avoue, Chr\u00e9tiens, ce sont des sentimens de pi\u00e9t\u00e9, que la Religion nous inspire, que nous devons avoir dans le c\u0153ur, & qui s'accordent parfaitement avec toutes les r\u00e9gles de la foi. Mais en demeurer absolument l\u00e0, & se reposer du soin de son salut sur cette providence g\u00e9n\u00e9rale, qui en conduit les ressorts, & qui en ordonne les moyens ; dire, J'attends l'heure & le moment, qu'il plaira \u00e0 Dieu de me toucher, & cependant vivre en paix & sans inqui\u00e9tude dans son p\u00e9ch\u00e9 ; regarder sa conversion comme une affaire, que Dieu ait prise enti\u00e8rement sur lui, & dont il ne nous rendra pas responsables ; c'est une pr\u00e9somption, mes chers Auditeurs, aussi mal fond\u00e9e dans son principe, qu'elle est pernicieuse dans ses effets. Prenez bien garde \u00e0 ces deux choses : pr\u00e9somption dont le principe est ruineux, & pr\u00e9somption dont les effets sont tr\u00e8s-pernicieux. Je vais vous en convaincre si vous voulez me suivre avec attention."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p004", "label": "English", "text": "To trust in God and place all one's hope in him; to regard him as the author, or in the language of Scripture, the God of one's salvation, God of my salvation; to rely on Jesus Christ's merits and count on the benefit of Redemption; to say, I can do all things in him who strengthens me, and whatever I shall ever do before God, I shall do through grace: I acknowledge, Christians, that these are sentiments of piety which Religion inspires in us, which we ought to have in our hearts, and which agree perfectly with every rule of faith. But to stop there entirely, and leave the care of one's salvation to that general providence which directs its workings and orders its means; to say, I await the hour and moment when it will please God to touch me, while living peacefully and unconcernedly in one's sin; to regard one's conversion as a matter that God has taken wholly upon himself and for which he will not hold us responsible: this, my dear hearers, is a presumption as ill-founded in its principle as it is pernicious in its effects. Mark these two things well: a presumption whose principle is unsound, and a presumption whose effects are most pernicious. I shall convince you of this if you will follow me attentively.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p005", "label": "French original", "text": "Je dis que cette pr\u00e9somption est mal fond\u00e9e dans son principe : en voici la raison, qui est \u00e9vidente. Parce que de quelque mani\u00e8re que Dieu nous ait pr\u00e9destin\u00e9s, il est de la foi qu'il ne nous sauvera jamais sans notre coop\u00e9ration. Or s'il est vrai que je dois, pour \u00eatre sauv\u00e9, y coop\u00e9rer avec Dieu, il ne m'est donc plus permis de m'assurer tellement de Dieu, que j'abandonne le soin de mon salut, & que je m'en d\u00e9charge enti\u00e8rement sur lui. J'ai droit d'esp\u00e9rer en Dieu : mais au m\u00eame-tems j'ai une obligation indispensable de travailler avec Dieu, d'agir avec Dieu ; & si je s\u00e9pare cette confiance de ce travail, de cette action, je me perds & je renverse l'ordre de Dieu. En effet, quel est l'ordre de Dieu, dans la disposition du salut des hommes ? Le voici exprim\u00e9 dans ces deux paroles de S. Augustin, que vous avez cent fois entendues, Qui fecit te sine te, non salvabit te sine te. Ce Dieu plein de sagesse & tout-puissant qui vous a cr\u00e9\u00e9 sans vous, n'a pas voulu vous sauver sans vous ; & \u00e0 prendre m\u00eame le salut dans cette \u00e9tendue que lui donne la th\u00e9ologie, c'est-\u00e0-dire, en tant qu'il pr\u00e9suppose, ou qu'il renferme notre conversion, il n'est pas en quelque sorte au pouvoir de Dieu de nous sauver sans nous : pourquoi ? parce que, dit S. Thomas, c'est dans nous-m\u00eames, je veux dire, dans notre volont\u00e9 pr\u00e9par\u00e9e, \u00e9lev\u00e9e & fortifi\u00e9e par la grace, que tout le myst\u00e8re de notre conversion doit consister."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p005", "label": "English", "text": "I say that this presumption is ill-founded in its principle. Here is the evident reason: however God has predestined us, it is an article of faith that he will never save us without our cooperation. Now, if it is true that to be saved I must cooperate with God in it, I am no longer permitted to count upon God so completely that I abandon the care of my salvation and cast the whole burden of it upon him. I have a right to hope in God; but at the same time I have an indispensable obligation to work with God, to act with God. If I separate this confidence from this work and action, I ruin myself and overturn God's order. Indeed, what is God's order in arranging mankind's salvation? Here it is, expressed in these words of Saint Augustine that you have heard a hundred times: He who made you without you will not save you without you. This all-wise and almighty God, who created you without you, has not willed to save you without you. Indeed, taking salvation in the full sense theology gives it, that is, insofar as it presupposes or includes our conversion, it is in a certain sense beyond God's power to save us without us. Why? Because, says Saint Thomas, the whole mystery of our conversion must consist within ourselves, I mean within our will prepared, raised up and strengthened by grace.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p006", "label": "French original", "text": "Il n'en est pas ainsi de tous les autres ouvrages de Dieu ; & en particulier, il n'en \u00e9toit pas de m\u00eame du miracle rapport\u00e9 dans notre Evangile. Quand le Fils de Dieu demanda \u00e0 ce paralytique, s'il vouloit \u00eatre gu\u00e9ri, Vis ? ce n'\u00e9toit pas, remarque S. Ambroise, qu'il e\u00fbt besoin, pour le gu\u00e9rir, de son consentement : car il le pouvoit gu\u00e9rir d'une pleine autorit\u00e9, sans que ce malade le voul\u00fbt, & m\u00eame quoiqu'il ne le voul\u00fbt pas. Mais quand Dieu entreprend de nous convertir, & qu'il nous demande int\u00e9rieurement si nous le voulons, c'est par une esp\u00e9ce d'engagement, auquel, tout Dieu qu'il est, sa sagesse & sa providence se trouvent comme assujetties. Car quoi que Dieu fasse de son c\u00f4t\u00e9, il est infaillible que nous ne serons jamais convertis, si nous ne le voulons \u00eatre ; & il y auroit m\u00eame de la contradiction que nous le fussions, & que nous ne le voulussions pas, puisque selon la maxime de tous les Peres, \u00eatre converti n'est rien autre chose que le vouloir, & le vouloir efficacement."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p006", "label": "English", "text": "It is not so with all God's other works, and in particular it was not so with the miracle related in our Gospel. When the Son of God asked this paralytic whether he wished to be healed, Do you wish it? it was not, Saint Ambrose observes, that he needed his consent to heal him; for he could heal him by his sovereign authority without the sick man's willing it, and even against his will. But when God undertakes to convert us, and asks us inwardly whether we desire it, this involves a kind of commitment to which, God though he is, his wisdom and providence are, as it were, bound. For whatever God does on his side, it is certain that we shall never be converted unless we will to be. There would even be a contradiction in our being converted without willing it, since, according to the maxim of all the Fathers, to be converted is nothing other than to will it, and to will it effectively.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p007", "label": "French original", "text": "Je s\u00e7ais que la grace est le grand principe, & la premi\u00e8re cause qui op\u00e8re en nous cette volont\u00e9. Mais je s\u00e7ais aussi qu'elle ne l'op\u00e8re pas toute seule ; & quelque victorieuse, quelque puissante que je la con\u00e7oive, c'est toujours sans pr\u00e9judice de ce que la foi m'enseigne, que cet acte de la volont\u00e9 qui fait notre conversion est un acte libre : or du moment qu'il doit \u00eatre libre, nous ne pouvons plus nous en reposer sur un autre ; mais c'est \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames \u00e0 l'exiger de nous-m\u00eames, \u00e0 nous en demander compte \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames, pour en pouvoir un jour rendre compte \u00e0 Dieu."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p007", "label": "English", "text": "I know that grace is the great principle and first cause that brings about this willing within us. But I also know that it does not bring it about all alone; and however victorious and powerful I conceive it to be, this is always without prejudice to what faith teaches me: that the act of will constituting our conversion is a free act. Now, since it must be free, we can no longer leave it to another; we ourselves must require it of ourselves and call ourselves to account for it, so that one day we may render an account of it to God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p008", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est pour cela que le m\u00eame esprit qui nous fait dire \u00e0 Dieu dans l'Ecriture, Converte nos, Domine, Seigneur, convertissez-nous, met aussi dans la bouche de Dieu ces autres paroles, Convertimini ad me ; convertissez-vous \u00e0 moi. Or reprend S. Augustin, comment accorder ces deux textes ensemble ? Si c'est Dieu qui nous convertit, pourquoi nous ordonne-t-il de nous convertir ? & si c'est nous-m\u00eames qui nous convertissons, pourquoi demandons-nous \u00e0 Dieu qu'il nous convertisse ? Ah ! mes Freres, r\u00e9pond ce saint Docteur, voil\u00e0 justement le secret de cette pr\u00e9destination adorable, sur quoi sont fond\u00e9s tous les devoirs de la vie chr\u00e9tienne. C'est qu'autant qu'il seroit injurieux \u00e0 Dieu que nous eussions jamais sans lui la pens\u00e9e de nous convertir, autant nous est-il inutile de nous flatter que Dieu seul nous convertira : c'est que pour nous sauver, selon les loix \u00e9tablies par la divine providence, deux conversions sont n\u00e9cessaires, la conversion de Dieu & la n\u00f4tre ; la conversion de Dieu \u00e0 nous, & notre conversion \u00e0 Dieu. Il faut que Dieu se convertisse \u00e0 nous, en nous pr\u00e9venant par sa grace ; & il faut que nous nous convertissions \u00e0 Dieu, en suivant avec fid\u00e9lit\u00e9 le mouvement de sa grace. Voil\u00e0 toute la th\u00e9ologie d'un Chr\u00e9tien. Il est vrai que Dieu s'est charg\u00e9 de la premi\u00e8re de ces deux conversions, & qu'elle est uniquement de son ressort. Mais il n'est pas moins vrai qu'il a pr\u00e9tendu que nous fussions charg\u00e9s de l'autre, comme d'une condition dont nous devons personnellement lui r\u00e9pondre. Je dois donc, si je raisonne bien, jetter tellement, comme parle l'Ap\u00f4tre, dans le sein de Dieu toutes mes inqui\u00e9tudes, Omnem sollicitudinem vestram projicientes in eum, que je m'en r\u00e9serve n\u00e9anmoins une partie ; ou plut\u00f4t, je dois tellement les jetter toutes en Dieu, qu'elles demeurent encore toutes en moi. Pourquoi cela ? parce que mon salut d\u00e9pendant tout \u00e0 la fois, & de Dieu, & de moi, comme je suis oblig\u00e9 en tant qu'il d\u00e9pend de Dieu, de l'abandonner \u00e0 sa sagesse & \u00e0 sa mis\u00e9ricorde ; aussi en tant qu'il d\u00e9pend de moi, suis-je oblig\u00e9 de m'y appliquer avec tout le z\u00e9le & toute la ferveur dont je suis capable. Je dois selon le pr\u00e9cepte de Jesus-Christ, m'attacher inviolablement \u00e0 ces deux termes, & en faire comme les deux points fixes, sur lesquels roulent toute ma pr\u00e9destination & toute ma conduite : Vigilate & orate, veillez & priez. Je dois prier, parce que je ne puis rien sans la grace ; & je dois veiller, parce que la grace toute puissante qu'elle est, ne fait rien sans moi. Si je veille sans prier, c'est orgueil ; si je prie sans veiller, c'est illusion. La vigilance d\u00e9tach\u00e9e de la pri\u00e8re, me fait oublier ma d\u00e9pendance ; & la pri\u00e8re d\u00e9tach\u00e9e de la vigilance, me fait oublier le soin que je dois avoir de moi-m\u00eame. L'une & l'autre, jointes ensemble, font ce juste temp\u00e9rament, dans lequel consiste de notre part la pr\u00e9destination divine ; & par-l\u00e0 je sauve tout, & ne risque rien."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p008", "label": "English", "text": "That is why the same spirit which makes us say to God in Scripture, Turn us, Lord, Lord, convert us, also puts these other words into God's mouth: Turn to me; turn yourselves to me. Now, Saint Augustine asks, how can these two texts be reconciled? If it is God who converts us, why does he command us to convert ourselves? And if we convert ourselves, why do we ask God to convert us? Ah, my brethren, this holy Doctor replies, that is precisely the secret of this adorable predestination, on which all the duties of the Christian life are founded. Just as it would offend God for us ever to conceive the thought of conversion without him, so it is useless for us to flatter ourselves that God alone will convert us. To save us, according to the laws established by divine providence, two turnings are necessary, God's and ours: God's turning to us and our turning to God. God must turn to us by going before us with his grace; and we must turn to God by faithfully following the movement of his grace. There is a Christian's entire theology. It is true that God has taken charge of the first of these two turnings and that it belongs to him alone. But it is no less true that he has intended us to be charged with the other, as a condition for which we must answer to him personally. If I reason correctly, then, I must cast all my anxieties into God's bosom, as the Apostle says, Casting all your care upon him, in such a way that I nevertheless reserve a share of them for myself; or rather, I must cast them all upon God in such a way that they all still remain in me. Why? Because my salvation depends at once upon God and upon me: insofar as it depends on God, I am obliged to entrust it to his wisdom and mercy; insofar as it depends on me, I am equally obliged to apply myself to it with all the zeal and fervour of which I am capable. According to Jesus Christ's precept, I must hold inviolably to these two terms and make them, as it were, the two fixed points on which my entire predestination and conduct turn: Watch and pray; keep watch and pray. I must pray because I can do nothing without grace; and I must watch because grace, all-powerful though it is, does nothing without me. If I watch without praying, it is pride; if I pray without watching, it is delusion. Vigilance severed from prayer makes me forget my dependence; prayer severed from vigilance makes me forget the care I must take of myself. Joined together, they constitute that proper balance in which divine predestination consists on our part; thereby I preserve everything and risk nothing.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p009", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais si je suis pr\u00e9destin\u00e9, direz-vous, je n'ai rien \u00e0 craindre ; & si je ne le suis pas, tous mes soins & toutes mes craintes ne me peuvent sauver. Ecoutez-moi, Chr\u00e9tiens : voil\u00e0 le faux raisonnement dont le libertinage a de tout tems pr\u00e9tendu se pr\u00e9valoir. Si je suis pr\u00e9destin\u00e9, je n'ai rien \u00e0 craindre, quelle cons\u00e9quence ! & moi je r\u00e9ponds que vous devez conclure tout au contraire, & dire, si je suis pr\u00e9destin\u00e9, je dois travailler \u00e0 mon salut avec crainte & avec tremblement ; si je suis pr\u00e9destin\u00e9, cela m'engage \u00e0 \u00eatre attentif & \u00e0 veiller continuellement sur moi-m\u00eame. On diroit d'abord que cette proposition a quelque chose de paradoxe. Nullement, Chr\u00e9tiens : elle est fond\u00e9e sur les principes, non-seulement les plus solides, mais les plus naturels & les plus simples de la raison. Car si je suis pr\u00e9destin\u00e9, il est \u00e9vident que je ne le suis, & que je ne le puis \u00eatre, que d\u00e9pendamment des moyens aux quels Dieu a voulu attacher ma pr\u00e9destination ; ou pour parler plus juste, que d\u00e9pendamment des moyens qui sont renferm\u00e9s dans ma pr\u00e9destination. Or la foi m'apprend qu'un des moyens les plus essentiels est le soin de mon salut, est la crainte des jugemens de Dieu, est une d\u00e9fiance salutaire de ma propre fragilit\u00e9, est une vigilance exacte qui me serve de frein & qui m'emp\u00eache de me livrer \u00e0 mes passions, & de tomber dans le rel\u00e2chement. S'il y a une pr\u00e9destination pour nous, il est certain qu'elle comprend & qu'elle embrasse tout cela. Que fais-je donc quand je viens \u00e0 me n\u00e9gliger sous ce vain pr\u00e9texte de pr\u00e9destination dont j'abuse ? admirez, Chr\u00e9tiens, la foiblesse de l'esprit de l'homme dans ses \u00e9garemens : ce que je fais ? je d\u00e9truis moi-m\u00eame le fondement sur lequel je b\u00e2tis, c'est-\u00e0-dire, je d\u00e9truis ma pr\u00e9destination, au m\u00eame-tems que je la suppose : & pourquoi ? parce que j'en s\u00e9pare ce qui en est ins\u00e9parable, ce qui s'y trouve essentiellement li\u00e9, & sans quoi elle ne peut subsister dans le dessein de Dieu. Ainsi en voulant faire le Th\u00e9ologien, je raisonne en homme sans principes & sans connoissances."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p009", "label": "English", "text": "But if I am predestined, you will say, I have nothing to fear; and if I am not, all my efforts and fears cannot save me. Listen to me, Christians: this is the false reasoning of which irreligion has always sought to take advantage. If I am predestined, I have nothing to fear: what a conclusion! My answer is that you should conclude exactly the opposite and say: if I am predestined, I must work out my salvation with fear and trembling; if I am predestined, that obliges me to be attentive and keep continual watch over myself. At first this proposition might seem somewhat paradoxical. Not at all, Christians: it is founded on the principles of reason that are not only the soundest but also the most natural and simplest. For if I am predestined, it is evident that I am so, and can be so, only in dependence upon the means to which God has willed to attach my predestination; or, more accurately, only in dependence upon the means included in my predestination. Now faith teaches me that among the most essential means are care for my salvation, fear of God's judgements, a salutary distrust of my own frailty, and exact vigilance to restrain me and prevent me from yielding to my passions and falling into laxity. If there is predestination for us, it certainly includes and embraces all this. What, then, am I doing when I become negligent under this vain pretext of predestination which I abuse? Marvel, Christians, at the weakness of man's mind in its errors. What am I doing? I am destroying the very foundation on which I build; that is, I am destroying my predestination at the very moment I assume it. And why? Because I separate from it what is inseparable, what is essentially bound up with it and without which it cannot subsist in God's design. Thus, in trying to play the theologian, I reason like a man without principles or knowledge.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p010", "label": "French original", "text": "En effet, mes Freres, disoit saint Prosper, Dieu ne nous a pas pr\u00e9destin\u00e9s selon nos id\u00e9es, ni de telle sorte que notre pr\u00e9destination puisse jamais fomenter nos d\u00e9reglemens : il nous a pr\u00e9destin\u00e9s comme des cr\u00e9atures raisonnables, libres, capables de m\u00e9riter, & qui doivent gagner le ciel par titre de conqu\u00eate ou de r\u00e9compense. C'est ce que nous enseignent toutes les Ecritures. Il est donc vrai que le bon usage de notre raison, que la soumission de notre volont\u00e9, que nos m\u00e9rites acquis, j'entens acquis par la grace & avec le secours de Dieu, que nos bonnes \u0153uvres, que nos vertus, que nos actions, que notre attachement au bien, que notre application \u00e0 fuir le mal, que tout cela doit n\u00e9cessairement entrer dans notre pr\u00e9destination \u00e9ternelle, si nous sommes du nombre des pr\u00e9destin\u00e9s & des \u00e9lus. Et l'on peut dire que c'est en cela m\u00eame que paro\u00eet la sagesse de notre Dieu, de nous avoir pr\u00e9destin\u00e9s par sa grace d'une mani\u00e8re si conforme & si proportionn\u00e9e \u00e0 notre nature. D'o\u00f9 il s'ensuit que cette confiance pr\u00e9somptueuse qui nous fait abandonner \u00e0 Dieu notre salut, sans pr\u00e9tendre y donner nous-m\u00eames nos soins, est dans la conduite de la vie, une contradiction manifeste, o\u00f9 l'homme en quittant les voies droites que Dieu lui a marqu\u00e9es, s'\u00e9gare, se confond ; & pour me servir de l'expression du Proph\u00e8te Royal, se d\u00e9ment dans son iniquit\u00e9, Et mentita est iniquitas sibi. En faudroit-il davantage pour nous pr\u00e9server d'une erreur si grossi\u00e9re & si sensible ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p010", "label": "English", "text": "Indeed, my brethren, Saint Prosper used to say, God has not predestined us according to our notions, nor in such a way that our predestination could ever foster our disorders. He has predestined us as rational and free creatures, capable of merit, who must gain heaven by way of conquest or reward. All the Scriptures teach us this. It is therefore true that the good use of our reason, the submission of our will, our acquired merits, acquired, I mean, through grace and with God's help, our good works, our virtues, our actions, our attachment to good, our diligence in fleeing evil: all this must necessarily enter into our eternal predestination, if we belong to the number of the predestined and elect. One may say that our God's wisdom is shown precisely in having predestined us by his grace in a manner so consonant with and proportioned to our nature. It follows that the presumptuous confidence which makes us leave our salvation to God without intending to give it our own care is a manifest contradiction in the conduct of life. In abandoning the straight paths God has marked out for him, man goes astray and confounds himself; and, to use the Royal Prophet's expression, gives himself the lie in his iniquity: And iniquity has lied to itself. Would anything more be needed to preserve us from so gross and obvious an error?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p011", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais si cette erreur est mal fond\u00e9e dans son principe, elle n'est pas moins funeste dans ses effets, & c'est ici que je vous demande toute votre r\u00e9flexion. Car \u00e0 quoi va cette pernicieuse maxime, de se reposer du soin de son salut sur ce que Dieu en a d\u00e9termin\u00e9 ? \u00e0 deux choses \u00e9galement dangereuses & in\u00e9vitables, s\u00e7avoir, \u00e0 \u00e9teindre absolument dans l'homme le z\u00e9le des bonnes \u0153uvres, & \u00e0 nourrir son libertinage. Je dis que cette pr\u00e9somption \u00e9teint dans l'homme le z\u00e9le des bonnes \u0153uvres ; c'est sa premi\u00e8re propri\u00e9t\u00e9 : preuve infaillible qu'elle ne vient pas de Dieu. Car enfin, en quelque sens que nous prenions la chose, & de quelque mani\u00e8re que nous envisagions la pr\u00e9destination dans Dieu, il en faut toujours revenir \u00e0 cette r\u00e9gle, dont il ne nous est pas permis de nous d\u00e9partir, s\u00e7avoir, que si l'id\u00e9e que nous nous formons de cette pr\u00e9destination, va \u00e0 diminuer dans nous la ferveur Chr\u00e9tienne & \u00e0 nous faire n\u00e9gliger nos devoirs, quelque sp\u00e9cieuse qu'elle nous paroisse, c'est une id\u00e9e fausse. Nous sembl\u00e2t-elle appuy\u00e9e sur le t\u00e9moignage de tous les Peres de l'Eglise, nous nous trompons, & nous l'entendons mal : pourquoi ? parce que nous ne l'entendons pas comme l'Ap\u00f4tre, qui en \u00e9toit mieux instruit que nous, & qui rapportoit tout ce qu'il en s\u00e7avoit \u00e0 cette excellente conclusion : Quapropter, Fratres, magis satagite, ut per bona vestra opera, certam vestram vocationem & electionem faciatis : C'est pourquoi, mes Freres, efforcez-vous d'autant plus \u00e0 assurer votre vocation & votre \u00e9lection, par votre pers\u00e9v\u00e9rance dans les bonnes \u0153uvres. Comme s'il e\u00fbt dit : au lieu de philosopher, de contester, de subtiliser sur le choix que Dieu a fait de vous (recherche qui sera toujours inutile & m\u00eame pernicieuse pour vous) appliquez-vous pl\u00fbt\u00f4t, Magis satagite ; \u00e0 quoi ? \u00e0 vous rendre ce choix favorable par tout le bien que vous pouvez faire, & que vous ne faites pas, tandis que vous perdez le tems \u00e0 raisonner & \u00e0 disputer : Quapropter magis satagite, ut per bona opera, certam vestram vocationem & electionem faciatis."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p011", "label": "English", "text": "But if this error is ill-founded in its principle, it is no less disastrous in its effects, and here I ask for your fullest reflection. For what does this pernicious maxim lead to, of leaving the care of one's salvation to what God has determined about it? To two equally dangerous and inevitable consequences: utterly extinguishing man's zeal for good works, and nourishing his irreligious licence. I say that this presumption extinguishes man's zeal for good works; this is its first characteristic, an infallible proof that it does not come from God. For however we take the matter, and in whatever way we consider predestination in God, we must always return to this rule, from which we may not depart: if the idea we form of this predestination tends to lessen Christian fervour within us and make us neglect our duties, then, however plausible it seems, it is a false idea. Even if it seems supported by the testimony of all the Fathers of the Church, we are mistaken and understand it wrongly. Why? Because we do not understand it as the Apostle did, who knew it better than we and brought everything he knew about it to this excellent conclusion: Therefore, brethren, strive all the more to make your calling and election sure through your good works. Therefore, my brethren, strive all the more to secure your calling and election by persevering in good works. As though he had said: instead of philosophising, disputing and drawing subtle distinctions about God's choice of you, an inquiry that will always be useless and even pernicious for you, apply yourselves instead, Strive all the more. To what? To making this choice favourable to you through all the good you can do and fail to do while you waste time reasoning and disputing: Therefore strive all the more to make your calling and election sure through good works.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p012", "label": "French original", "text": "Et voil\u00e0, disent les Th\u00e9ologiens, la marque essentielle, pour discerner dans ces mati\u00e8res importantes, mais pour discerner s\u00fbrement, ce qu'il y a de solide, & ce qui ne l'est pas. Je m'explique. Telle doctrine touchant la pr\u00e9destination de Dieu est-elle saine & orthodoxe, ne l'est-elle pas ? c'est de quoi vous doutez : & soit pour l'int\u00e9r\u00eat de votre salut, soit pour ob\u00e9ir au commandement de saint Paul, vous voulez en faire l'\u00e9preuve, Omnia autem probate : Et moi je dis, Chr\u00e9tiens, que voici par o\u00f9 il en faut juger. Est-ce une doctrine qui me dispose \u00e0 travailler pour Dieu, qui m'y engage, qui m'y excite, qui m'en fasse na\u00eetre le d\u00e9sir, qui me soutienne & qui m'anime dans les r\u00e9solutions que j'en ai form\u00e9es ? d\u00e8s-l\u00e0 je dois moins m'en d\u00e9fier. Mais ne fait-elle rien de tout cela ? je dois la tenir pour suspecte ; & quelque couleur de v\u00e9rit\u00e9 qu'elle ait d'ailleurs, je dois m'en \u00e9loigner comme d'un \u00e9cueil. Car ce fut ainsi que l'Eglise dans le dernier concile jugea des opinions de Luther & de Calvin : elle les censura, elle les r\u00e9prouva, pourquoi ? parce que sous pr\u00e9texte d'exalter le myst\u00e8re imp\u00e9n\u00e9trable de la pr\u00e9destination divine, elles inspiroient un m\u00e9pris secret des \u0153uvres du salut."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p012", "label": "English", "text": "And that, the theologians say, is the essential mark for distinguishing, and distinguishing surely, in these important matters what is sound from what is not. Let me explain. Is a particular doctrine about God's predestination sound and orthodox, or is it not? You are uncertain; and, whether for the sake of your salvation or to obey Saint Paul's command, you wish to test it: But test all things. I say, Christians, that this is how it must be judged. Is it a doctrine that disposes me to work for God, engages and impels me to do so, awakens the desire for it, sustains and animates me in the resolutions I have formed? On that account I should distrust it less. But does it do none of these things? I must hold it suspect; and whatever appearance of truth it otherwise has, I must keep away from it as from a dangerous reef. For this was how the Church in the last council judged the opinions of Luther and Calvin. She censured them, she condemned them. Why? Because, under pretext of exalting the impenetrable mystery of divine predestination, they inspired a secret contempt for works conducive to salvation.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p013", "label": "French original", "text": "Aussi, Chr\u00e9tiens, l'un ou l'autre de ces fameux h\u00e9r\u00e9siarques, n'auroit-il pas eu bonne grace en s'attachant aux principes de sa secte, de pousser un point de morale sur les devoirs de la pi\u00e9t\u00e9 Chr\u00e9tienne ? Apr\u00e8s avoir fait entendre \u00e0 ses Auditeurs, que la pr\u00e9destination de Dieu impose \u00e0 l'homme une absolue n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 d'agir ; que toutes nos actions, bonnes & mauvaises, roulent sur ce d\u00e9cret que Dieu a form\u00e9 de toute \u00e9ternit\u00e9 ; que soumis \u00e0 ce d\u00e9cret nous n'avons plus le pouvoir de nous d\u00e9terminer au bien, ni de nous d\u00e9tourner du mal ; que nous avons perdu notre libre arbitre, & que par cons\u00e9quent les pr\u00e9ceptes de la loi, \u00e0 ceux qui ne les observent pas, sont impossibles : l'un ou l'autre, dis-je, apr\u00e8s avoir \u00e9tabli ces fondemens, n'auroit-il pas \u00e9t\u00e9 bien re\u00e7u \u00e0 faire le pr\u00e9dicateur, & \u00e0 nous dire en nous pr\u00eachant la p\u00e9nitence : Faites un effort, mes Freres ; rompez vos liens, affranchissez-vous de l'esclavage o\u00f9 vous \u00eates, sortez de l'occasion, renoncez \u00e0 votre p\u00e9ch\u00e9. Mais comment l'entendez-vous, auroit p\u00fb lui r\u00e9pliquer un p\u00e9cheur ? Si mon p\u00e9ch\u00e9 est arr\u00eat\u00e9 dans cet ordre immuable des d\u00e9crets de Dieu, le moyen que j'y renonce ; & le moyen au contraire que je n'y renonce pas, si mon salut est r\u00e9solu ? Si je ne suis pas pr\u00e9destin\u00e9, comment puis-je me convertir ; & si je le suis, comment puis-je ne me convertir pas ? pourquoi donc me presser de la sorte, puisque selon vous je suis n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 \u00e0 l'un ou \u00e0 l'autre ? Vous dites que c'est Dieu seul qui me d\u00e9termine \u00e0 faire le bien : pourquoi donc employer votre z\u00e9le \u00e0 m'y d\u00e9terminer & \u00e0 m'y r\u00e9soudre ? par une telle r\u00e9ponse, l'homme le plus endurci n'auroit-il pas justifi\u00e9 son imp\u00e9nitence contre les maximes les plus s\u00e9v\u00e8res de cette pr\u00e9tendue r\u00e9forme ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p013", "label": "English", "text": "Indeed, Christians, would not either of these famous heresiarchs have cut a fine figure, while adhering to his sect's principles, in pressing a moral instruction on the duties of Christian piety? After explaining to his hearers that God's predestination imposes an absolute necessity of action upon man; that all our actions, good and bad, turn upon the decree God has formed from all eternity; that, subject to this decree, we no longer have the power to determine ourselves to good or turn ourselves from evil; that we have lost our free will, and consequently the law's precepts are impossible for those who do not observe them: after laying these foundations, I say, would either have been well received in playing the preacher and telling us, in preaching penance: Make an effort, my brethren; break your bonds, free yourselves from your slavery, leave the occasion of sin, renounce your sin? But what do you mean? a sinner might have replied. If my sin is fixed in this immutable order of God's decrees, how can I renounce it? And how, on the contrary, could I fail to renounce it if my salvation is resolved upon? If I am not predestined, how can I convert myself? And if I am, how can I fail to convert myself? Why press me so, since according to you I am necessitated to the one or the other? You say that God alone determines me to do good: why, then, expend your zeal in determining and resolving me to do it? By such a reply, would not the most hardened man have justified his impenitence against the severest maxims of this supposed reformation?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p014", "label": "French original", "text": "De-l\u00e0 vient, que ceux qui la pr\u00eachoient, (c'est la r\u00e9flexion d'un s\u00e7avant Cardinal, l'ornement de notre si\u00e9cle, & le d\u00e9fenseur de l'Eglise (de-l\u00e0 vient que les pr\u00e9dicateurs de cette r\u00e9forme, ou pl\u00fbt\u00f4t les ministres de cette h\u00e9r\u00e9sie, ne s'attachoient presque jamais \u00e0 l'exhortation, quand ils \u00e9toient oblig\u00e9s d'instruire les peuples. Ils parloient sans cesse \u00e0 leurs Auditeurs de cette profondeur & de cet abysme des jugemens de Dieu, ils leur en inspiroient de l'horreur ; ils leur faisoient admirer cette adorable in\u00e9galit\u00e9, qui fait des uns des vases de col\u00e8re & de perdition, & des autres des vases de mis\u00e9ricorde : mais \u00e0 peine s'engageoient-ils, ou \u00e0 les presser sur les obligations de leur \u00e9tat, ou \u00e0 les confondre sur le d\u00e9sordre de leurs m\u0153urs. S'ils le faisoient quelquefois, c'\u00e9toit foiblement, & avec une secrette r\u00e9pugnance ; comme s'ils eussent bien senti, qu'ils se contredisoient eux-m\u00eames ; & qu'ils eussent reconnu, que ces grands & ces \u00e9nergiques mouvemens d'indignation, de reproches, de menaces, d'invectives contre les p\u00e9cheurs, qui sont si propres \u00e0 la parole de Dieu, & o\u00f9 les Proph\u00e8tes ont fait paro\u00eetre toute la force & toute la grace de l'Esprit saint qui les animoit : que tout cela, dis-je, ne leur convenoit pas. Pourquoi ? parce que tout cela supposoit une libert\u00e9, qu'ils avoient entrepris d'abolir, & dont ils ne retenoient que le nom. Jusques-l\u00e0 que pour parler cons\u00e9quemment, & pour soutenir leur erreur par une autre erreur, ils en vinrent enfin \u00e0 publier que les bonnes \u0153uvres n'avoient nulle part au salut ; & que toute l'affaire de la justification se r\u00e9duisoit \u00e0 un seul point, je veux dire, \u00e0 une simple imputation des m\u00e9rites de Jesus-Christ, sans qu'il en d\u00fbt co\u00fbter autre chose, pour \u00eatre sauv\u00e9, que de croire, & de s'assurer soi-m\u00eame par l'esprit int\u00e9rieur de la foi qu'on \u00e9toit en effet justifi\u00e9 & pr\u00e9destin\u00e9. Secret admirable, pour applanir le chemin du ciel, & pour y faire marcher \u00e0 l'aise, non-seulement les ames l\u00e2ches, mais m\u00eame les plus charg\u00e9es de crimes. Or je vous demande, si cela seul ne suffisoit pas pour les convaincre de fausset\u00e9 ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p014", "label": "English", "text": "That is why those who preached it, as a learned Cardinal, the ornament of our age and defender of the Church, has observed, that is why the preachers of this reformation, or rather the ministers of this heresy, almost never devoted themselves to exhortation when obliged to instruct the people. They spoke incessantly to their hearers of this depth and abyss of God's judgements, inspiring awe of it in them. They made them admire that adorable inequality which makes some vessels of wrath and perdition and others vessels of mercy. Yet they scarcely undertook either to press them about the obligations of their state or to put them to shame over their disordered conduct. If they sometimes did so, it was feebly and with a secret reluctance, as though they felt that they were contradicting themselves and recognised that those great and forceful movements of indignation, reproach, threat and invective against sinners, which are so proper to God's word and in which the Prophets displayed all the strength and grace of the Holy Spirit who animated them, that all this, I say, did not befit them. Why? Because all this presupposed a freedom they had undertaken to abolish and of which they retained only the name. To such a point that, to speak consistently and support their error by another error, they finally came to proclaim that good works had no share in salvation and that the whole matter of justification came down to one point: a mere imputation of Jesus Christ's merits, without salvation costing anything more than believing and assuring oneself, through the inward spirit of faith, that one was indeed justified and predestined. An admirable secret for smoothing the road to heaven and making it easy to walk, not only for lax souls but even for those most laden with crimes! Now I ask you whether this alone did not suffice to convict them of falsehood.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p015", "label": "French original", "text": "Vous me direz que cette doctrine, en rapportant tout \u00e0 la pr\u00e9destination de Dieu, & ne laissant rien \u00e0 la libert\u00e9 de l'homme, est bien plus capable d'humilier l'homme & de r\u00e9primer son orgueil : & moi, Chr\u00e9tiens, je ne con\u00e7ois pas comment on peut se laisser s\u00e9duire par une difficult\u00e9 aussi vaine que celle-l\u00e0. Car en quoi consiste la vraie humiliation de l'homme ? n'est-ce pas, dit saint Bernard, en ce que l'homme ait quelque chose \u00e0 se reprocher ; en ce qu'il soit oblig\u00e9 \u00e0 se repentir, \u00e0 s'accuser, \u00e0 se condamner soi-m\u00eame ; en ce qu'il envisage toujours son p\u00e9ch\u00e9, comme un sujet de honte, comme une malice punissable, comme une infid\u00e9lit\u00e9 criminelle ; en ce qu'il ne puisse pas se d\u00e9fendre de porter contre lui-m\u00eame ce t\u00e9moignage, qu'en p\u00e9chant il est all\u00e9 contre les desseins de Dieu, & qu'il a manqu\u00e9 \u00e0 sa grace ? Voil\u00e0, selon toutes les Ecritures, ce qui peut, & ce qui doit humilier le p\u00e9cheur. Or comment entrera-t-il dans aucun de ces sentimens, s'il est imbu de l'erreur que je combats ? & s'il est pr\u00e9venu de cette pens\u00e9e, qu'il n'a p\u00fb \u00e9viter le mal, comment se le reprochera-t-il ? s'il est dans cette opinion, que son p\u00e9ch\u00e9 n'a \u00e9t\u00e9 qu'une suite fatale & n\u00e9cessaire d'une destin\u00e9e dont il n'\u00e9toit pas le ma\u00eetre, comment s'en accusera-t-il ? que ne pourra-t-il point all\u00e9guer \u00e0 Dieu, pour se justifier du bl\u00e2me de l'avoir commis ? Il n'en va pas de m\u00eame dans la cr\u00e9ance commune, & dans les principes de la doctrine Catholique. Car nous disons \u00e0 Dieu : Seigneur, il est vrai, j'ai \u00e9t\u00e9 r\u00e9belle \u00e0 vos ordres ; vous m'avez appell\u00e9, & j'ai refus\u00e9 de vous ob\u00e9ir ; je suis un ingrat & un perfide ; & ce qui fait ma confusion, c'est que je ne le suis, que parce que je l'ai voulu, & qu'\u00e9tant aid\u00e9, comme je l'\u00e9tois, de votre secours, je pouvois ne le pas vouloir. En parlant de la sorte, nous nous humilions : mais quiconque s'\u00e9carte de cette voie simple de la foi, tient un langage tout diff\u00e9rent. Au lieu de s'accuser, il accuse Dieu, il fait Dieu auteur de ses d\u00e9sordres, il s'en prend \u00e0 Dieu de ce qu'il est vitieux & emport\u00e9 : ainsi bien loin qu'on lui inspire l'humilit\u00e9, en lui \u00f4tant l'exercice de sa libert\u00e9, c'est au contraire par-l\u00e0 qu'on lui apprend, \u00e0 s'\u00e9lever contre Dieu-m\u00eame."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p015", "label": "English", "text": "You will tell me that this doctrine, by referring everything to God's predestination and leaving nothing to man's freedom, is far better suited to humble man and repress his pride. For my part, Christians, I cannot understand how anyone can be led astray by so empty an objection. For in what does man's true humbling consist? Is it not, says Saint Bernard, in man's having something with which to reproach himself; in his being obliged to repent, accuse himself and condemn himself; in his always viewing his sin as a cause of shame, a punishable wickedness, a sinful unfaithfulness; in his being unable to avoid bearing witness against himself that, in sinning, he has opposed God's designs and failed his grace? That, according to all the Scriptures, is what can and must humble the sinner. But how will he enter into any of these sentiments if he is imbued with the error I combat? If he is possessed by the thought that he could not avoid evil, how will he reproach himself for it? If he holds that his sin was merely the fated and necessary consequence of a destiny over which he had no control, how will he accuse himself of it? What argument could he not put before God to clear himself of blame for committing it? It is otherwise in the common belief and principles of Catholic doctrine. For we say to God: Lord, it is true, I have rebelled against your commands; you called me, and I refused to obey you; I am ungrateful and faithless. What puts me to shame is that I am so only because I willed it, and that, assisted as I was by your help, I could have chosen not to will it. In speaking thus we humble ourselves. But whoever departs from this simple way of faith speaks quite differently. Instead of accusing himself, he accuses God, makes God the author of his disorders, and blames God for his being vicious and unrestrained. Thus, far from inspiring humility in him by depriving him of the exercise of his freedom, one thereby teaches him, on the contrary, to rise up against God himself.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p016", "label": "French original", "text": "De plus, il ne suffit pas pour \u00eatre saine, qu'une doctrine serve \u00e0 nous humilier ; il faut qu'elle nous rende tout \u00e0 la fois humbles & fervens ; & si l'humilit\u00e9 qu'elle produit en nous, n'est suivie de cette ferveur, c'est une humilit\u00e9 trompeuse qui nous s\u00e9duit & qui nous perd. Or il n'y que la cr\u00e9ance Catholique qui puisse bien concilier ces deux choses, la ferveur & l'humilit\u00e9, parce que c'est la seule, o\u00f9 l'on trouve cette alliance parfaite de la pr\u00e9destination & de la libert\u00e9. Car le P\u00e9lagianisme, attribuant des forces \u00e0 l'homme pour agir ind\u00e9pendamment de Dieu, sembloit rendre l'homme fervent : mais il lui donnoit de quoi s'enorgueillir. Le Calvinisme d'ailleurs pour \u00e9lever la pr\u00e9destination de Dieu, an\u00e9antissant le libre arbitre de l'homme, humilioit l'homme en apparence : mais il lui \u00f4toit en effet toute la pratique des bonnes \u0153uvres. Que fait l'Eglise ? elle tient le milieu entre ces deux extr\u00e9mit\u00e9s ; & conduite par l'esprit de v\u00e9rit\u00e9 qui la gouverne, elle nous enseigne une voie qui nous maintient dans l'humilit\u00e9 Chr\u00e9tienne sans pr\u00e9judice de la ferveur, & qui excite en nous la ferveur sans int\u00e9resser l'humilit\u00e9 Chr\u00e9tienne ; & cette voie c'est la doctrine que je vous pr\u00eache, s\u00e7avoir, que pour l'accomplissement de la pr\u00e9destination de Dieu, nous devons coop\u00e9rer & travailler avec Dieu."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p016", "label": "English", "text": "Moreover, to be sound it is not enough for a doctrine to serve to humble us; it must make us both humble and fervent. If the humility it produces in us is not followed by this fervour, it is a deceptive humility that misleads and ruins us. Now only Catholic belief can properly reconcile these two things, fervour and humility, because it alone contains this perfect union of predestination and freedom. For Pelagianism, by attributing to man powers to act independently of God, seemed to make man fervent, but gave him grounds for pride. Calvinism, on the other hand, by annihilating man's free will in order to exalt God's predestination, humbled man in appearance, but in reality deprived him of the whole practice of good works. What does the Church do? She holds the middle between these two extremes. Led by the spirit of truth that governs her, she teaches us a way that maintains us in Christian humility without prejudice to fervour, and awakens fervour within us without impairing Christian humility. This way is the doctrine I preach to you: that, for the fulfilment of God's predestination, we must cooperate and work with God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p017", "label": "French original", "text": "Sans cela, non-seulement nous nous rel\u00e2chons dans les devoirs du Christianisme ; mais nous tombons par une suite n\u00e9cessaire, dans les derniers d\u00e9sordres. Car sur ce principe, que quand Dieu le voudra & l'aura pr\u00e9v\u00fb, on ne manquera pas de se convertir, & que jusques-l\u00e0 il seroit inutile d'y penser, on s'abandonne \u00e0 tout, on se laisse emporter \u00e0 la violence de ses d\u00e9sirs, on contente ses app\u00e9tits les plus sensuels, on ne se mod\u00e8re en rien. Et de l\u00e0 vient que les libertins du si\u00e9cle, par une politique & un int\u00e9r\u00eat qu'il est ais\u00e9 de comprendre, ont toujours appuy\u00e9 & paru go\u00fbter ces opinions dures de la pr\u00e9destination : pourquoi ? parce que dans la duret\u00e9 m\u00eame de ces opinions, ils trouvoient de quoi se consoler, en se justifiant \u00e0 eux-m\u00eames le d\u00e9reglement de leur conduite & de leurs plus scandaleux d\u00e9bordemens. Car ils \u00e9toient heureux, que ce myst\u00e8re de la pr\u00e9destination divine leur f\u00fbt propos\u00e9 d'une mani\u00e8re qui les rend\u00eet plus dignes de compassion, que de r\u00e9pr\u00e9hension ; qui leur \u00e9pargn\u00e2t la honte de leurs crimes ; qui leur fourn\u00eet des expressions pour s'en accuser sans peine, en disant, c'est Dieu qui m'a manqu\u00e9 ; qui les autoris\u00e2t, pour ainsi parler, \u00e0 \u00eatre violens, m\u00e9disans, lascifs, impudiques, sans qu'on e\u00fbt droit de leur en faire d'autre reproche, sinon qu'ils s'\u00e9toient rendus coupables de tout cela dans la personne du premier homme, en commettant avec lui, ou pl\u00fbt\u00f4t par lui, ce premier p\u00e9ch\u00e9 qui nous a tous perdus. Ce qu'ils n'avoient nulle peine \u00e0 reconno\u00eetre, & ce qu'ils confessoient volontiers, parce que ce reproche leur \u00e9toit commun avec le reste des hommes. Au lieu que la doctrine de l'Eglise leur \u00e9toit une source de remords, parce qu'elle leur opposoit toujours ce mauvais usage de leur libert\u00e9, sur quoi ils ne pouvoient se d\u00e9fendre. Celle-ci les rappelloit \u00e0 l'ordre, les reprenoit, les convainquoit, les condamnoit, & par-l\u00e0 m\u00eame les importunoit : mais l'autre n'exigeant d'eux rien autre chose, que de d\u00e9plorer leur mis\u00e9re, de s'humilier sous la puissante main de Dieu, s'accommodoit parfaitement \u00e0 leur go\u00fbt. Car ils vouloient bien s'humilier devant Dieu, pourv\u00fb qu'ils en fussent quittes pour cela, & qu'on ne leur demand\u00e2t rien davantage."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p017", "label": "English", "text": "Without this, not only do we grow lax in the duties of Christianity; we necessarily fall into the worst disorders. For, on the principle that one will unfailingly be converted when God wills and has foreseen it, and that until then it would be useless to think about it, one abandons oneself to everything, lets oneself be swept along by violent desires, satisfies one's most sensual appetites, and exercises no restraint. That is why the worldly libertines, through a policy and self-interest easy to understand, have always supported and seemed to relish these harsh opinions about predestination. Why? Because in the very harshness of these opinions they found consolation, by justifying to themselves the disorder of their conduct and their most scandalous excesses. For they were pleased to have this mystery of divine predestination presented to them in a manner that made them more deserving of compassion than rebuke; that spared them the shame of their crimes; that supplied expressions in which to accuse themselves of them painlessly, by saying, It is God who has failed me; that authorised them, so to speak, to be violent, slanderous, lascivious and immodest, without anyone having the right to reproach them for anything except having become guilty of all this in the person of the first man, by committing with him, or rather through him, that first sin which ruined us all. They had no difficulty acknowledging this and confessed it readily, because they shared this reproach with the rest of mankind. The Church's doctrine, by contrast, was a source of remorse to them, because it continually confronted them with their misuse of freedom, against which they could offer no defence. The latter recalled them to order, rebuked, convicted and condemned them, and thus vexed them. But the other doctrine, requiring nothing of them except to deplore their misery and humble themselves beneath God's mighty hand, suited their taste perfectly. For they were quite willing to humble themselves before God, provided that this discharged their obligation and nothing more was demanded of them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p018", "label": "French original", "text": "De-l\u00e0 vient encore, que dans les tems o\u00f9 la corruption des m\u0153urs a \u00e9t\u00e9 plus g\u00e9n\u00e9rale, ces mati\u00e8res de la pr\u00e9destination & du libre arbitre, sont devenues plus communes, & si j'ose dire, plus \u00e0 la mode. Chacun a pr\u00e9tendu en discourir, jusqu'\u00e0 ceux m\u00eames & jusqu'\u00e0 celles qui devoient moins en parler. Elles ont affect\u00e9 cette vaine science que S. Paul leur d\u00e9fendoit si express\u00e9ment ; elles se sont rendues \u00e9loquentes sur la foiblesse de l'homme, & sur sa d\u00e9pendance infinie de Dieu ; elles se sont fait une d\u00e9votion d'en raisonner, & elles ont enfin r\u00e9duit toute leur pi\u00e9t\u00e9 \u00e0 cette sp\u00e9culation & \u00e0 ce langage d'humilit\u00e9. Or j'avoue, Chr\u00e9tiens, que bien loin d'\u00eatre touch\u00e9 de ce langage, j'ai toujours eu de la peine \u00e0 ne m'en pas d\u00e9fier : car on ne s\u00e7ait que trop jusqu'o\u00f9 peut aller l'abus de cette pr\u00e9tendue foiblesse, & les cons\u00e9quences qu'en tire le libertinage. Qu'une ame vertueuse & attach\u00e9e \u00e0 ses devoirs, g\u00e9misse de la foiblesse extr\u00eame o\u00f9 nous sommes tomb\u00e9s par le p\u00e9ch\u00e9, j'en suis \u00e9difi\u00e9 ; pourquoi ? parce que sa vie m'est un t\u00e9moignage qu'elle prend la chose dans le bon sens & dans le v\u00e9ritable esprit de la foi. Mais qu'une ame mondaine s'en explique sans cesse, & en revienne toujours \u00e0 ce myst\u00e8re de la pr\u00e9destination de Dieu & de l'impuissance de la cr\u00e9ature, c'est un scandale pour moi. Car sans entreprendre de juger ce qu'elle conclut de-l\u00e0, je ne puis m'emp\u00eacher de voir ce qu'elle en peut conclurre. Or \u00e0 quoi n'iroit pas cette conclusion ? Encore une fois, l'ame simple & bien intentionn\u00e9e ne fait point tant la Th\u00e9ologienne & la s\u00e7avante. Elle s\u00e7ait ce que Dieu lui commande, & elle met en lui sa confiance. Voil\u00e0 \u00e0 quoi elle s'en tient. Mais suppos\u00e9 ce commandement & cette confiance, elle s\u00e7ait que c'est \u00e0 elle du reste \u00e0 se conduire, \u00e0 r\u00e9pondre de ses actions, & \u00e0 se garantir par-l\u00e0 non-seulement de la censure des hommes, mais du jugement de Dieu. Ainsi sans philosopher, elle trouve le point de la vraie philosophie Chr\u00e9tienne, qui est de se tenir dans le devoir, & de bien vivre."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p018", "label": "English", "text": "That is also why, in times when the corruption of morals has been more general, these subjects of predestination and free will have become more common and, if I may say so, more fashionable. Everyone has presumed to discuss them, even the very men and women who should have spoken of them least. These women have affected that vain learning which Saint Paul so expressly forbade them; they have become eloquent on man's weakness and his infinite dependence upon God; they have made a devotion of reasoning about it, and finally reduced all their piety to this speculation and this language of humility. Now I confess, Christians, that, far from being moved by this language, I have always found it difficult not to distrust it; for we know only too well how far the abuse of this supposed weakness can go and what consequences irreligious licence draws from it. If a virtuous soul devoted to its duties laments the extreme weakness into which sin has brought us, I am edified. Why? Because that person's life testifies that the matter is understood rightly and in the true spirit of faith. But if a worldly soul speaks of it incessantly and continually returns to this mystery of God's predestination and the creature's powerlessness, it is a scandal to me. For, without undertaking to judge what such a soul concludes from it, I cannot help seeing what it could conclude. And how far might that conclusion go? Once again, a simple and well-intentioned soul does not make so much of playing the theologian and scholar. It knows what God commands and places its confidence in him. It holds to that. But, given this command and this confidence, it knows that for the rest it must conduct itself, answer for its actions, and thereby guard itself not only against men's censure but against God's judgement. Thus, without philosophising, it finds the essence of true Christian philosophy: to remain in one's duty and live well.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p019", "label": "French original", "text": "Et certes, o\u00f9 en serions-nous si cette r\u00e9gle venoit \u00e0 \u00eatre abolie ? S'il falloit que le gouvernement du monde roul\u00e2t sur ce principe, que les hommes cons\u00e9quemment \u00e0 la pr\u00e9destination de Dieu, ne sont plus ma\u00eetres de leur volont\u00e9 ; o\u00f9 en seroit, je ne dis pas le Christianisme & la religion, mais m\u00eame la police qui maintient tous les \u00e9tats ? Quelle probit\u00e9 y auroit-il dans le commerce, quelle fid\u00e9lit\u00e9 dans les mariages, quelle soumission dans les inf\u00e9rieurs, quelle mod\u00e9ration dans les sup\u00e9rieurs ? L'un diroit, la col\u00e8re m'emporte, & je ne puis me retenir : l'autre, la domination me r\u00e9volte, & je ne suis pas n\u00e9 pour ob\u00e9ir : celui-ci, je ne me sens pas encore assez efficacement inspir\u00e9 de payer mes dettes : celle-l\u00e0, j'attens que Dieu me touche, pour garder la foi conjugale. Et de-l\u00e0 quel renversement dans l'univers, quelle d\u00e9pravation de m\u0153urs ? Vous le voyez, Chr\u00e9tiens, & plaise au Ciel que cette maladie dont notre si\u00e9cle n'est que trop infect\u00e9, n'ach\u00e8ve point enfin de le corrompre, & qu'elle n'en fasse pas le si\u00e9cle de l'iniquit\u00e9 consomm\u00e9e ! Au moins est-il vrai, que les payens m\u00eames en ont pr\u00e9v\u00fb les affreuses cons\u00e9quences. Car c'est pour cela, dit saint Augustin, que Ciceron n'ayant pas assez de lumi\u00e8re, pour accommoder la libert\u00e9 de l'homme avec la pr\u00e9science de Dieu, & se croyant oblig\u00e9 de nier l'une ou l'autre, aima mieux douter de la pr\u00e9science de Dieu, que de la libert\u00e9 de l'homme : pourquoi ? parce qu'en conservant la libert\u00e9 de l'homme, il sauvoit le fondement des m\u0153urs, des vertus, des devoirs. Mais pour nous, ajo\u00fbte S. Augustin, nous embrassons l'une & l'autre ensemble : la pr\u00e9science, pour croire ce que nous devons croire de Dieu ; & la libert\u00e9, pour faire ce que Dieu demande de nous. Nos autem utramque complectimur ; illam, ut ben\u00e8 credamus ; istam, ut ben\u00e8 vivamus. Or ce qu'il disoit de la pr\u00e9science, je le dis, & encore avec plus de sujet, de la pr\u00e9destination."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p019", "label": "English", "text": "Indeed, where should we be if this rule were abolished? If the government of the world had to turn on the principle that, as a consequence of God's predestination, men are no longer masters of their wills, what would become, I do not say of Christianity and religion, but even of the civil order that sustains every state? What honesty would there be in commerce, what fidelity in marriage, what submission in inferiors, what moderation in superiors? One would say, Anger carries me away and I cannot restrain myself; another, Authority revolts me and I was not born to obey; this man, I do not yet feel effectively inspired enough to pay my debts; that woman, I am waiting for God to touch me before keeping my marriage vows. What upheaval throughout the world, what corruption of morals would follow! You see it, Christians; heaven grant that this sickness, with which our age is only too infected, does not finally complete its corruption and make it the age of consummate iniquity! At least it is true that even the pagans foresaw its dreadful consequences. For that is why, Saint Augustine says, Cicero, lacking sufficient light to reconcile man's freedom with God's foreknowledge, and believing himself obliged to deny one or the other, preferred to doubt God's foreknowledge rather than man's freedom. Why? Because, in preserving man's freedom, he preserved the foundation of morals, virtues and duties. But we, Saint Augustine adds, embrace both together: foreknowledge, to believe what we must believe about God; and freedom, to do what God asks of us. We, however, embrace both: the former, that we may believe rightly; the latter, that we may live rightly. Now what he said of foreknowledge I say, with still more reason, of predestination.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p020", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais peut-\u00eatre, me direz-vous, que le libre arbitre & cette coop\u00e9ration de l'homme nous donne lieu de nous glorifier. H\u00e9 bien, mes Freres, reprend saint Augustin, si nous sommes justes & enfans de Dieu, ne devons nous pas, aussi-bien que saint Paul, avoir de quoi nous glorifier en lui & par lui ? Qui gloriatur, in Domino glorietur ? N'est-ce pas ainsi que les Saints se sont glorifi\u00e9s, & en particulier David, quand il s'\u00e9crioit, In Deo laudabo sermones meos ; je me glorifierai en Dieu, de mes \u0153uvres : de mes \u0153uvres, parce que je les ai faites pour Dieu ; & en Dieu, parce que c'est de lui que j'ai re\u00e7u le pouvoir de les faire : Et in Deo, & meos ; in Deo, quia ab ipso ; meos, quia accepi ? N'est-ce pas pour cela, dit le m\u00eame Pere, que nos bonnes \u0153uvres, qui sont des bienfaits & des graces de la part de Dieu, sont aussi des m\u00e9rites de notre part ; & que quand Dieu nous r\u00e9compense, il couronne en nous ses propres dons, Coronat in nobis dona sua ? Non, non, mes Freres, conclut ce saint Docteur, il ne nous est point d\u00e9fendu de nous glorifier dans notre Dieu, puisqu'il est vrai au contraire que si nous n'avons de quoi nous glorifier dans le Seigneur, il nous r\u00e9prouve. Malheur \u00e0 nous, disoit saint Bernard, si nous paroissons devant Dieu pr\u00e9somptueux & superbes ; mais aussi malheur \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames, si nous paroissons devant lui sans m\u00e9rites & sans \u0153uvres. Heureuse l'Epouse de Jesus-Christ, c'est-\u00e0-dire, l'Eglise, parce qu'elle a des m\u00e9rites solides sans pr\u00e9somption & une sainte pr\u00e9somption sans de vains m\u00e9rites. Felix Ecclesia, cui nec merita sine praesumptione, nec praesumptio sine meritis deest. Elle a de quoi pr\u00e9sumer, mais non pas de ses m\u00e9rites propres : elle a des m\u00e9rites acquis par la grace, mais non pas pour pr\u00e9sumer d'elle-m\u00eame : Habet unde praesumat, sed non merita ; habet merita, sed non ad praesumendum. D'o\u00f9 il s'ensuit par un secret divin, que sa pr\u00e9somption m\u00eame la sanctifie, parce qu'elle est uniquement fond\u00e9e sur Jesus-Christ ; & que ses m\u00e9rites la glorifient devant Dieu, parce qu'ils proc\u00e8dent d'une libert\u00e9 parfaitement soumise \u00e0 Dieu."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p020", "label": "English", "text": "But perhaps you will tell me that free will and this human cooperation give us grounds for glorying. Well then, my brethren, Saint Augustine replies, if we are just and children of God, should we not, like Saint Paul, have grounds for glorying in him and through him? Let him who glories glory in the Lord. Is this not how the Saints gloried, and David in particular, when he exclaimed, In God I will praise my words; I shall glory in God for my works: for my works, because I have done them for God; and in God, because it is from him that I received the power to do them. Both in God and mine: in God, because from him; mine, because I have received them. Is it not for this reason, says the same Father, that our good works, which are benefits and graces on God's part, are also merits on ours, and that when God rewards us, he crowns his own gifts within us: He crowns his gifts in us? No, no, my brethren, concludes this holy Doctor, we are not forbidden to glory in our God, since on the contrary it is true that, if we have nothing in which to glory in the Lord, he rejects us. Woe to us, Saint Bernard used to say, if we appear before God presumptuous and proud; but woe likewise to us if we appear before him without merits and without works. Happy is the Spouse of Jesus Christ, that is, the Church, because she has solid merits without presumption and a holy presumption without vain merits. Happy the Church, which lacks neither merits without presumption nor presumption without merits. She has grounds for confidence, but not on the strength of her own merits; she has merits acquired through grace, but not so as to presume upon herself: She has grounds for presuming, but not merits; she has merits, but not for presuming. Hence, by a divine secret, her very presumption sanctifies her, because it is founded solely on Jesus Christ; and her merits give her glory before God, because they proceed from a freedom perfectly subject to God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p021", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est ainsi, mes chers Auditeurs, que tout homme Chr\u00e9tien doit raisonner. Confiance en Dieu, mais en m\u00eame-tems vigilance sur soi-m\u00eame, & attention \u00e0 son salut pour correspondre aux desseins de Dieu : sans cela l'on tombe dans une pr\u00e9somption criminelle. Et s\u00e7avez-vous, Chr\u00e9tiens, par o\u00f9 Dieu nous confondra sur cette pr\u00e9somption ? par nous-m\u00eames, par nos propres sentimens, & aussi bien que le serviteur de l'Evangile par notre propre confession : Ex ore tuo. Car dans les autres affaires, tout persuad\u00e9s que nous sommes de la providence & de la pr\u00e9destination de Dieu, nous ne n\u00e9gligeons rien de notre part, & nous ne prenons m\u00eame que trop de moyens & trop de mesures. S'agit-il d'une entreprise o\u00f9 notre fortune, o\u00f9 notre honneur est int\u00e9ress\u00e9 ? quoique nous s\u00e7achions que Dieu a pr\u00e9v\u00fb ce qui en doit r\u00e9ussir, & que le succ\u00e8s en est d\u00e9j\u00e0 marqu\u00e9 dans l'ordre de sa pr\u00e9destination, nous ne laissons pas d'y apporter tous nos soins, d'y employer tout notre cr\u00e9dit, d'en pr\u00e9venir toutes les suites, d'en \u00e9loigner tous les obstacles ; & nous nous faisons m\u00eame de notre z\u00e9le l\u00e0-dessus & de notre activit\u00e9 une sagesse & une vertu. Dieu s\u00e7ait, disons-nous, ce qui en arrivera : mais il veut n\u00e9anmoins que je m'aide ; car il n'est pas oblig\u00e9 \u00e0 faire des miracles pour moi ; & sa pr\u00e9destination m\u00eame m'engage \u00e0 me servir des moyens qu'il me pr\u00e9sente, pour parvenir \u00e0 la fin que je me propose. C'est ainsi que nous raisonnons, & en cela nous raisonnons bien. Il n'y a que l'affaire du salut, o\u00f9 nous prenons d'autres id\u00e9es, o\u00f9 nous voulons que Dieu fasse tout, o\u00f9 nous nous reposons de tout sur sa providence, tandis que nous demeurons tranquilles & sans action."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p021", "label": "English", "text": "This, my dear hearers, is how every Christian ought to reason. Confidence in God, but at the same time vigilance over oneself and attention to one's salvation, in order to correspond to God's designs: without this one falls into sinful presumption. And do you know, Christians, how God will confound us for this presumption? Through ourselves, our own sentiments, and, like the servant in the Gospel, through our own confession: Out of your own mouth. For in other affairs, however convinced we are of God's providence and predestination, we neglect nothing on our part, and indeed take only too many means and precautions. Is it an undertaking affecting our fortune or honour? Although we know that God has foreseen its outcome and that its success is already marked out in the order of his predestination, we nevertheless give it all our care, employ all our influence, anticipate every consequence and remove every obstacle. We even account our zeal and activity in it a wisdom and a virtue. God knows, we say, what will happen; yet he wishes me to help myself, for he is not obliged to work miracles for me, and his very predestination obliges me to use the means he presents to reach the end I propose. That is how we reason, and in this we reason rightly. Only in the matter of salvation do we adopt other ideas, wish God to do everything, and leave everything to his providence while we remain tranquil and inactive.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p022", "label": "French original", "text": "Or voil\u00e0, Chr\u00e9tiens, ce qui achevera notre condamnation au jugement de Dieu, cette opposition de nous-m\u00eames \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames, cette contradiction de nos sentimens, cet empressement, cette ardeur \u00e0 l'\u00e9gard des choses temporelles, & cette l\u00e2chet\u00e9, cette n\u00e9gligence \u00e0 l'\u00e9gard du salut ; voil\u00e0 ce qui nous fermera la bouche & \u00e0 quoi nous ne r\u00e9pondrons jamais. Que faudroit-il faire ? Ah ! mes chers Auditeurs, la grande maxime, & que ne puis-je vous l'imprimer profond\u00e9ment dans le c\u0153ur ! comprenez-la bien. Nous nous appliquons aux affaires du monde, comme s'il n'y avoit, ni providence, ni pr\u00e9destination divine, & que tout d\u00e9pend\u00eet de nous ; & nous traitons l'affaire du salut comme si nous n'en \u00e9tions pas charg\u00e9s & que tout d\u00e9pend\u00eet de Dieu. Rectifions l'un par l'autre ; servons-nous de l'exc\u00e8s de l'un pour suppl\u00e9er au d\u00e9faut de l'autre : c'est-\u00e0-dire, travaillons aux affaires du monde avec un peu plus de cet abandon \u00e0 la providence que nous portons trop loin dans l'affaire du salut : & travaillons \u00e0 l'affaire du salut, avec plus de cet empressement & de cette inqui\u00e9tude que nous avons trop dans les affaires du monde. Vacquons aux affaires du monde avec plus de confiance en Dieu, avec plus de soumission aux ordres de Dieu, reconnoissant que sans lui tous nos soins sont inutiles : & vacquons \u00e0 l'affaire du salut avec plus de r\u00e9flexion sur nous-m\u00eames, avec plus de d\u00e9fiance de nous-m\u00eames, avec plus de z\u00e9le pour nous-m\u00eames, reconnoissant que sans nous, Dieu ne veut pas accomplir l'\u0153uvre de notre sanctification. Joindre ces deux choses ensemble, & les allier dans la conduite de la vie, voil\u00e0 de quoi nous rendre de parfaits Chr\u00e9tiens."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p022", "label": "English", "text": "Now, Christians, this is what will complete our condemnation at God's judgement: our opposition to ourselves, the contradiction in our sentiments, our eagerness and ardour concerning temporal things and our laxity and negligence concerning salvation. This will close our mouths, and we shall never have an answer to it. What should we do? Ah, my dear hearers, what a great maxim, and would that I could imprint it deeply in your hearts! Understand it well. We apply ourselves to worldly affairs as though there were neither providence nor divine predestination and everything depended upon us; and we treat the matter of salvation as though we had no charge of it and everything depended upon God. Let us correct the one by the other, using the excess of the one to supply the deficiency of the other. That is, let us work at worldly affairs with a little more of that abandonment to providence which we carry too far in the matter of salvation; and let us work at salvation with more of the eagerness and anxiety we have in excess in worldly affairs. Let us attend to worldly affairs with more confidence in God and submission to his commands, acknowledging that without him all our efforts are useless; and let us attend to salvation with more reflection upon ourselves, more distrust of ourselves and more zeal for ourselves, acknowledging that without us God does not will to accomplish the work of our sanctification. Joining these two things together and uniting them in the conduct of life will make us perfect Christians.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p023", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais sur-tout revenons-en toujours \u00e0 cette demande du Sauveur, & \u00e0 cette volont\u00e9 dont nous devons \u00eatre nous-m\u00eames garans : Vis sanus fieri ? H\u00e9 bien, ne veux-je donc pas gu\u00e9rir de cette maladie inv\u00e9t\u00e9r\u00e9e qui cause la mort \u00e0 mon ame, de cette passion d\u00e9r\u00e9gl\u00e9e, de cet attachement criminel, de cette foiblesse honteuse ? ne m'en releverai-je jamais ? ne veux-je pas enfin y mettre ordre ? car \u00e0 force de nous le demander & d'en concevoir la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9, nous le voudrons ; & \u00e0 force de le vouloir, cette volont\u00e9 \u00e9tant le commencement de notre gu\u00e9rison, ou pl\u00fbt\u00f4t de notre conversion m\u00eame, nous y parviendrons. C'est ainsi qu'on \u00e9vite la pr\u00e9somption, & vous allez voir comment on doit encore \u00e9viter la d\u00e9fiance & le d\u00e9sespoir : c'est la seconde partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p023", "label": "English", "text": "But above all let us always return to the Saviour's question and to that willing for which we ourselves must answer: Do you wish to be made well? Well then, do I not wish to recover from this inveterate sickness that brings death to my soul, from this disordered passion, this sinful attachment, this shameful weakness? Shall I never rise out of it? Do I not at last wish to put it right? For by repeatedly asking ourselves and grasping its necessity, we shall will it; and by persisting in willing it, this willing being the beginning of our healing, or rather of our conversion itself, we shall attain it. This is how presumption is avoided; you will now see how we must also avoid distrust and despair. This is the second part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-h004", "label": "French original", "text": "II. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-h004", "label": "English", "text": "PART II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p024", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est une maxime fond\u00e9e sur toutes les r\u00e9gles de la prudence, qu'en mati\u00e8re de d\u00e9lib\u00e9ration, il faut toujours commencer par ce qu'il y a de s\u00fbr & d'\u00e9vident, pour se d\u00e9terminer ensuite sur les points douteux & obscurs ; & un des \u00e9garemens de l'homme dans la recherche de la v\u00e9rit\u00e9, est de s'attacher, comme il arrive quelquefois, \u00e0 ce qu'il y a d'obscur & de douteux, pour s'en faire un sujet de peine, sur les points m\u00eames les plus sensibles & les plus certains. Or cet \u00e9garement dont les cons\u00e9quences d'ailleurs sont si pernicieuses, est celui m\u00eame o\u00f9 nous tombons sur le sujet de la pr\u00e9destination. Je m'explique : dans le myst\u00e8re de la pr\u00e9destination consid\u00e9r\u00e9 par rapport \u00e0 nous, il y a quelque chose d'incertain & quelque chose d'assur\u00e9, quelque chose d'\u00e9vident & quelque chose de cach\u00e9 : ce qu'il y a d'\u00e9vident & d'assur\u00e9, c'est que Dieu de quelque mani\u00e8re qu'il pr\u00e9destine les hommes, est un Dieu de mis\u00e9ricorde & de bont\u00e9 ; & que si jamais il nous r\u00e9prouve, ce ne sera que parce que nous n'aurons pas voulu coop\u00e9rer \u00e0 notre salut, & que nous aurons abus\u00e9 des moyens & des secours qu'il nous avoit fournis. Principe indubitable dans la religion, & que nous comprenons sans peine : mais ce qu'il y a d'incertain & de cach\u00e9, c'est la mani\u00e8re dont Dieu a pr\u00e9destin\u00e9 les hommes ; pourquoi il traite les uns plus favorablement que les autres ; pourquoi il choisit ceux-ci pr\u00e9f\u00e9rablement \u00e0 ceux-l\u00e0 ; pourquoi il ne donne pas toujours tous les secours qu'il pourroit absolument donner : car ce sont l\u00e0 ces questions profondes dont parloit le Pape C\u00e9lestin premier, sur lesquelles l'Ecriture ne s'est point expliqu\u00e9e suffisamment \u00e0 nous, & que Dieu veut que nous regardions comme des secrets qui lui sont r\u00e9serv\u00e9s. De-l\u00e0 vient que l'Eglise elle-m\u00eame n'a point port\u00e9 jusques-l\u00e0 ses d\u00e9cisions, & qu'elle a mieux aim\u00e9 nous laisser dans l'obscurit\u00e9 & dans le doute, que de p\u00e9n\u00e9trer dans les conseils de Dieu ; & voil\u00e0 encore une fois ce que nous ne comprenons pas. Or prenez garde, Chr\u00e9tiens, ce qui nous trouble dans ce myst\u00e8re de la pr\u00e9destination ; c'est ce que nous n'y comprenons pas & dont nous doutons : mais au contraire, ce que nous y comprenons, & de quoi nous ne doutons pas, a une vertu admirable pour nous consoler, pour nous fortifier, pour dissiper tous les nuages qui s'\u00e9l\u00e8vent dans nos esprits, & pour nous rass\u00fbrer."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p024", "label": "English", "text": "It is a maxim founded upon every rule of prudence that in deliberating we must always begin with what is sure and evident, so as then to decide upon doubtful and obscure points. One of man's errors in seeking truth is to attach himself, as sometimes happens, to what is obscure and doubtful, and make it a cause of distress even concerning the clearest and most certain points. Now this error, whose consequences are so pernicious, is precisely the one into which we fall concerning predestination. Let me explain. In the mystery of predestination considered in relation to us, there is something uncertain and something assured, something evident and something hidden. What is evident and assured is that God, however he predestines men, is a God of mercy and goodness; and that, if he ever rejects us, it will only be because we have been unwilling to cooperate in our salvation and have abused the means and helps he supplied us. This is an indubitable principle in religion, which we understand without difficulty. But what is uncertain and hidden is the manner in which God has predestined men: why he treats some more favourably than others; why he chooses these rather than those; why he does not always give every help he could possibly give. For these are those profound questions of which Pope Celestine the First spoke, on which Scripture has not explained itself sufficiently to us and which God wills us to regard as secrets reserved to himself. That is why the Church herself has not extended her decisions so far and has preferred to leave us in obscurity and doubt rather than penetrate God's counsels. This, once again, is what we do not understand. Now take heed, Christians: what troubles us in this mystery of predestination is what we do not understand and are uncertain about. On the contrary, what we do understand and do not doubt has an admirable power to console and strengthen us, dispel every cloud that arises in our minds, and reassure us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p025", "label": "French original", "text": "Si donc on agissoit conform\u00e9ment aux desseins de Dieu, on corrigeroit l'un par l'autre : & des v\u00e9rit\u00e9s consolantes que Dieu nous a express\u00e9ment r\u00e9v\u00e9l\u00e9es pour animer notre esp\u00e9rance & pour la soutenir, on se feroit des armes pour combattre ses pens\u00e9es & ses d\u00e9fiances qui ne sont tout au plus fond\u00e9es que sur des incertitudes. Mais que faisons-nous ? tout le contraire : de ces incertitudes mal con\u00e7\u00fbes, nous nous faisons des sujets de tentation au pr\u00e9judice des assurances que Dieu nous a positivement donn\u00e9es ; je ne s\u00e7ais si vous m'entendez bien : & parce qu'il y a dans le myst\u00e8re de la pr\u00e9destination certains points qui sont au-dessus de nos connoissances, qui nous \u00e9tonnent & qui nous effrayent, nous nous en pr\u00e9occuppons jusqu'\u00e0 douter si Dieu en effet nous a sinc\u00e9rement aim\u00e9s, jusqu'\u00e0 croire qu'il n'a pas eu la volont\u00e9 de nous sauver, jusqu'\u00e0 nous abandonner \u00e0 un d\u00e9sespoir qui presque toujours est suivi des derniers d\u00e9sordres : Desperantes semetipsos tradiderunt impudicitiae, in operationem immunditiae omnis. Y a-t-il un \u00e9garement plus dangereux & plus funeste ? Revenons-en, Chr\u00e9tiens, aux deux grands principes que l'Evangile nous met aujourd'hui devant les yeux pour nous pr\u00e9server d'un tel malheur, la bont\u00e9 de Dieu d'une part, & notre libert\u00e9 de l'autre : la bont\u00e9 de Dieu, dans l'offre que le Sauveur du monde fait au paralytique de le gu\u00e9rir ; notre libert\u00e9, dans la condition qu'il y ajo\u00fbte, en lui demandant s'il le veut : Vis sanus fieri ? la bont\u00e9 de Dieu, qui nous r\u00e9pond de Dieu ; & notre libert\u00e9, qui nous fait imputer \u00e0 nous m\u00eames notre perte : toutes deux, qui doivent nous relever de ce d\u00e9couragement o\u00f9 notre l\u00e2chet\u00e9 nous plonge, pour nous entretenir dans l'imp\u00e9nitence."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p025", "label": "English", "text": "If, then, we acted in conformity with God's designs, we would correct the one by the other. From the consoling truths God has expressly revealed to enliven and sustain our hope, we would fashion weapons to combat our thoughts and misgivings, which at most rest only on uncertainties. But what do we do? The very opposite. From these ill-conceived uncertainties we make occasions of temptation, to the detriment of the assurances God has positively given us. I do not know whether you understand me clearly. Because certain points in the mystery of predestination exceed our knowledge, astonish and frighten us, we become preoccupied with them to the point of doubting whether God has truly loved us sincerely, of believing that he has not willed to save us, and of abandoning ourselves to a despair almost always followed by the worst disorders: Despairing of themselves, they gave themselves over to shamelessness, to the working of every kind of uncleanness. Is there an error more dangerous and disastrous? Let us return, Christians, to the two great principles which the Gospel places before our eyes today to preserve us from such a misfortune: God's goodness on the one hand, and our freedom on the other. God's goodness appears in the Saviour of the world's offer to heal the paralytic; our freedom, in the condition he adds by asking whether he wills it: Do you wish to be made well? God's goodness vouches for God; our freedom makes us impute our ruin to ourselves. Both should raise us from the discouragement into which our cowardice plunges us in order to keep us in impenitence.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p026", "label": "French original", "text": "Car voici comment je raisonne, & comment il me semble que tout homme Chr\u00e9tien doit raisonner. Je ne connois pas les voies secrettes que Dieu a tenues, ni les mesures qu'il a prises dans la disposition de mon salut, & il ne m'appartient pas de les examiner : mais je s\u00e7ais par-dessus toutes choses que Dieu est bon, & que ce myst\u00e8re de pr\u00e9destination qui me paro\u00eet d'abord si terrible, est souverainement le myst\u00e8re de sa mis\u00e9ricorde. Je s\u00e7ais, & c'est ce qui doit faire ma plus solide consolation, qu'en cons\u00e9quence de ce myst\u00e8re, mon salut est entre les mains de Dieu : voil\u00e0 ce que je s\u00e7ais, & dont je ne me d\u00e9partirai jamais. C'\u00e9toit le sentiment de l'Ap\u00f4tre : Scio cui credidi ; je s\u00e7ais, disoit-il, quel est celui \u00e0 qui j'ai confi\u00e9 mon d\u00e9p\u00f4t ; & cette connoissance sur laquelle je me fonde, me rend in\u00e9branlable dans ma confiance. Que Dieu soit bon, en puis-je douter, \u00e0 moins que je ne doute de son \u00eatre m\u00eame, & comme parle S. Augustin, que je ne lui dispute jusqu'\u00e0 son essence ? Si donc en me parlant de Dieu, on m'en fait une image qui me le repr\u00e9sente comme un Dieu cruel, comme un Dieu qui ne m'a cr\u00e9\u00e9 que pour me perdre, comme un Dieu qui attache mon salut \u00e0 des choses que je ne puis faire, & qu'il ne veut pas me donner le pouvoir de faire, d\u00e9termin\u00e9 toutefois \u00e0 me punir si je ne les fais pas ; en un mot, comme un Dieu qui dispose tellement de ses cr\u00e9atures, qu'il n'y a point de pere, pour peu \u00e9quitable & pour peu sensible qu'il soit, qui n'e\u00fbt honte d'en user de m\u00eame \u00e0 l'\u00e9gard de ses enfans ; (car c'est l'id\u00e9e qu'en donnoit Calvin, & la pr\u00e9destination dans les maximes de sa secte renfermoit tout cela) si, dis-je, on me figure un Dieu de la sorte, je ne dois point m'allarmer, beaucoup moins d\u00e9sesp\u00e9rer. Car j'ai de quoi m'inscrire en faux contre cette id\u00e9e chim\u00e9rique & injurieuse \u00e0 Dieu ; j'ai de quoi la d\u00e9truire ; en disant : Non, ce n'est point l\u00e0 le Dieu qui m'a fait ce que je suis. S'il \u00e9toit tel, je ne pourrois plus l'aimer ; & si je ne pouvois plus l'aimer, il ne seroit plus mon Dieu, ni je ne serois plus sa cr\u00e9ature. Ce n'est point l\u00e0 le Dieu que l'Ecriture m'apprend \u00e0 r\u00e9clamer comme le Dieu de mon salut, Deus salutis meae ; Etant de ce caract\u00e8re, il seroit pl\u00fbt\u00f4t le Dieu de ma damnation. Il est vrai que c'est un Dieu terrible dans ses conseils ; mais il n'est pas moins vrai que ses conseils sont les conseils d'un Dieu souverainement aimable, & que sa mis\u00e9ricorde au moins dans cette vie l'emporte toujours sur sa justice. Or dans cette id\u00e9e, non-seulement sa justice surpasseroit sa mis\u00e9ricorde, mais elle l'an\u00e9antiroit ; & Dieu, si j'ose parler ainsi, d\u00e9pouill\u00e9 du plus divin de ses attributs, ne seroit plus \u00e0 mon \u00e9gard qu'une partie de lui-m\u00eame. Je le craindrois, mais de la crainte des d\u00e9mons. Je croirois en lui, mais d'une esp\u00e9ce de foi, qui ne produiroit que l'aversion & la haine. Or en quelque sens que je prenne les choses, la premi\u00e8re r\u00e9gle que me donne le Saint-Esprit, c'est d'avoir toujours des sentimens avantageux de la bont\u00e9 de mon Dieu, Sentite de Domino in bonitate : & si l'id\u00e9e que je me forme de la pr\u00e9destination ne s'accorde pas avec ces sentimens, je dois conclurre que c'est une id\u00e9e fausse, & qu'il ne m'est plus permis de m'y arr\u00eater."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p026", "label": "English", "text": "For this is how I reason, and how it seems to me every Christian ought to reason. I do not know the secret ways God has followed or the measures he has taken in arranging my salvation, nor is it for me to examine them. But above all I know that God is good, and that this mystery of predestination, which at first seems so terrible to me, is supremely the mystery of his mercy. I know, and this should be my surest consolation, that as a consequence of this mystery my salvation is in God's hands. That is what I know and shall never abandon. This was the Apostle's conviction: I know whom I have believed; I know, he said, who it is to whom I have entrusted my deposit, and this knowledge on which I rely makes me unshakable in my confidence. Can I doubt that God is good unless I doubt his very being and, as Saint Augustine says, dispute even his essence? If, then, someone speaking to me of God fashions an image of him as a cruel God, a God who created me only to destroy me, a God who makes my salvation depend on things I cannot do and for which he refuses to give me the power to act, yet who is determined to punish me if I do not do them; in a word, as a God who disposes of his creatures in a manner that any father, however little justice or feeling he possessed, would be ashamed to use towards his children, for this was Calvin's conception, and predestination according to his sect's maxims included all this: if, I say, someone depicts such a God to me, I must not be alarmed, much less despair. For I have grounds to challenge as false this fanciful idea so offensive to God; I have grounds to destroy it, by saying: No, that is not the God who made me what I am. If he were such, I could no longer love him; and if I could no longer love him, he would no longer be my God, nor I his creature. That is not the God whom Scripture teaches me to invoke as the God of my salvation, God of my salvation. Were that his character, he would rather be the God of my damnation. It is true that he is a God terrible in his counsels; but it is no less true that his counsels are those of a supremely lovable God, and that his mercy, at least in this life, always prevails over his justice. Now, according to that conception, not only would his justice exceed his mercy, it would annihilate it; and God, if I dare speak so, stripped of the most divine of his attributes, would be only a part of himself in relation to me. I would fear him, but with the fear of demons. I would believe in him, but with a kind of faith producing only aversion and hatred. Now, however I take things, the first rule the Holy Spirit gives me is always to think favourably of my God's goodness: Think of the Lord in goodness. If the idea I form of predestination does not agree with these sentiments, I must conclude that it is false and that I may no longer dwell upon it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p027", "label": "French original", "text": "Je dis plus, & je pr\u00e9tens que ce myst\u00e8re de la pr\u00e9destination de Dieu, bien loin d'avoir de quoi nous troubler, doit positivement nous consoler ; pour en \u00eatre persuad\u00e9, il me suffit de me souvenir que c'est le myst\u00e8re de cette charit\u00e9 \u00e9ternelle dont Dieu nous a aim\u00e9s : In charitate perpetua dilexi te. Je puis donc bien l'admirer cet incompr\u00e9hensible myst\u00e8re : je puis m'\u00e9crier avec l'Ap\u00f4tre : O altitudo ! \u00f4 profondeur ! \u00f4 abysme ! mais le terme qui suit, me fait bien conno\u00eetre que cette profondeur & cet abysme n'a rien qui doive me d\u00e9courager, puisque l'Ap\u00f4tre me dit que c'est un abysme de tr\u00e9sors & de richesses : O altitudo divitiarum ! Or un abysme de richesses peut me causer de la surprise, mais non pas me jetter dans l'abattement & dans la d\u00e9fiance."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p027", "label": "English", "text": "I go further: I maintain that this mystery of God's predestination, far from containing anything to trouble us, should positively console us. To be convinced of this, I need only remember that it is the mystery of that eternal charity with which God has loved us: With an everlasting love I have loved you. I may indeed admire this incomprehensible mystery; I may exclaim with the Apostle: O depth! O depth, O abyss! But the word that follows makes me understand clearly that this depth and abyss contain nothing that should discourage me, since the Apostle tells me that it is an abyss of treasures and riches: O depth of riches! Now an abyss of riches may astonish me, but cannot cast me into dejection and distrust.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p028", "label": "French original", "text": "C'\u00e9toit aussi sur ce fondement que saint Pierre apprenoit aux fid\u00e8les \u00e0 \u00e9tablir la paix de leurs ames : Omnem sollicitudinem vestram projicientes in eum, quoniam ipsi est cura de vobis. D\u00e9chargez-vous, leur disoit-il, mes Freres, de toutes ces inqui\u00e9tudes & ces anxi\u00e9t\u00e9s qui pourroient vous accabler : & sur qui vous en d\u00e9chargerez-vous ? sur votre Dieu qui vous aime en pere, & qui veut toujours prendre soin de vous. J'avoue que notre salut est entre ses mains, & qu'il d\u00e9pend m\u00eame bien plus de lui que de nous. Mais n'est-ce pas ce qui doit faire le comble de notre joie, de pouvoir dire \u00e0 Dieu comme David, In manibus tuis sortes meae : c'est entre vos mains, Seigneur, qu'est ma destin\u00e9e, je ne dis pas seulement ma fortune temporelle, mais mon \u00e9ternit\u00e9. Quand il seroit en mon pouvoir de mettre mon sort ailleurs, o\u00f9 pourrois-je le placer plus s\u00fbrement, qu'entre les mains de ce Dieu \u00e9galement puissant, bon & fid\u00e9le ? S'il \u00e9toit entre les miennes, o\u00f9 en serois-je : & aussi l\u00e9ger, aussi fragile que je le suis, sur quoi compterois-je, & o\u00f9 seroit ma confiance & mon appui ? Quelle pens\u00e9e plus douce pour un Chr\u00e9tien, que de consid\u00e9rer Dieu comme le gardien & le d\u00e9positaire de son salut ? & pour le p\u00e9cheur le plus inv\u00e9t\u00e9r\u00e9 dans ses d\u00e9sordres, quel fonds d'esp\u00e9rance que cette r\u00e9flexion qu'il peut faire, mon salut est encore dans les mains de Dieu ? Dieu pourroit-il le punir plus s\u00e9v\u00e8rement, que de lui abandonner la conduite de cette grande affaire, en l'abandonnant \u00e0 lui-m\u00eame ? & quand Dieu veut en effet exercer toute la rigueur de sa justice sur une ame libertine, n'est-ce pas ainsi qu'il en use ? N'\u00e9prouvons-nous pas, quand nous sortons de l'\u00e9tat du p\u00e9ch\u00e9, que le premier mouvement de notre conversion est d'aller trouver en Dieu ce salut, que nous avions perdu dans le commerce du monde ? & si les impies veulent nous rendre t\u00e9moignage de ce qui se passe dans eux, ne seront-ils pas oblig\u00e9s de reconno\u00eetre & de confesser que le dernier pas qui les conduit \u00e0 l'endurcissement est cette damnable conclusion qu'ils tirent, que d\u00e9sormais il n'y a plus pour eux en Dieu de salut, & qu'il leur seroit inutile de l'y vouloir chercher ? Il est donc de notre int\u00e9r\u00eat que le salut d\u00e9pende de Dieu, & que ce soit lui qui en dispose le premier par cette pr\u00e9paration de graces que saint Augustin appelle pr\u00e9destination."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p028", "label": "English", "text": "It was on this foundation also that Saint Peter taught the faithful to establish their souls' peace: Casting all your care upon him, because he cares for you. Unburden yourselves, he told them, my brethren, of all the anxieties and cares that might overwhelm you. And upon whom will you cast them? Upon your God, who loves you as a father and always wishes to care for you. I acknowledge that our salvation is in his hands, and indeed depends far more upon him than upon us. But should it not be the height of our joy to be able to say to God with David, My lot is in your hands: my destiny is in your hands, Lord, not merely my temporal fortune, but my eternity? Even if I had the power to place my lot elsewhere, where could I put it more safely than in the hands of this God, equally powerful, good and faithful? If it were in my own hands, where should I be? Fickle and frail as I am, what could I count on, and where would my confidence and support lie? What thought is sweeter for a Christian than to consider God the guardian and trustee of his salvation? And for the sinner most inveterate in his disorders, what a fund of hope in this reflection he can make: my salvation is still in God's hands! Could God punish him more severely than by leaving the direction of this great affair to him, abandoning him to himself? And when God indeed wishes to exercise the full rigour of his justice upon a licentious soul, is that not how he acts? Do we not experience, when we leave the state of sin, that the first movement of our conversion is to go and find in God that salvation which we lost in intercourse with the world? If the impious will testify to what takes place within them, must they not acknowledge and confess that the final step leading them to hardness of heart is the damnable conclusion they draw, that henceforth there is no salvation for them in God and that it would be useless to seek it there? It is therefore in our interest that salvation depend upon God and that he be the first to dispose it through that preparation of graces which Saint Augustine calls predestination.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p029", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais enfin, dites-vous, les Saints ont trembl\u00e9, en consid\u00e9rant ce myst\u00e8re, & si ce myst\u00e8re a fait trembler les Saints, pourquoi ne pourra-t-il pas d\u00e9sesp\u00e9rer les p\u00e9cheurs ? Encore un mot pour votre \u00e9dification : j'ach\u00e8ve par la plus invincible de toutes les preuves. J'en conviens ; les Saints ont trembl\u00e9 dans la v\u00fbe de ce myst\u00e8re : mais bien loin que ce qui leur a caus\u00e9 tant de frayeur, puisse autoriser notre d\u00e9sespoir, je soutiens que c'est ce qui le condamne ; & la raison en est sensible. Car ils n'ont trembl\u00e9, que parce qu'ils s\u00e7avoient que ce myst\u00e8re, outre la d\u00e9pendance infinie qu'il a de Dieu, avoit encore un encha\u00eenement n\u00e9cessaire avec leur libert\u00e9 & qu'ils ont envisag\u00e9 leur libert\u00e9 comme la source de tous les d\u00e9reglemens. Or cela m\u00eame, c'est ce qui rend notre d\u00e9sespoir inexcusable par rapport \u00e0 notre salut : pourquoi ? parce que du moment que notre libert\u00e9 y entre, il s'ensuit toujours, que si nous nous perdons, ce n'est que parce que nous le voulons. Notre libertinage voudroit n'en pas convenir : & un de ses artifices, est de nous faire croire par exemple, qu'il est impossible de se sauver dans le monde, au moins dans certaines conditions du monde, pour avoir droit de se porter \u00e0 tout, & pour se maintenir dans la possession de tout entreprendre & de tout faire. Mais Dieu, Chr\u00e9tiens, renverse bien ce pr\u00e9texte par la menace foudroyante qu'il fait aux impies dans l'Ecriture : Vocavi, & renuistis : ego quoque in interitu vestro ridebo. Car il ne dit pas, Je vous ai appell\u00e9, & vous n'avez p\u00fb me suivre : paroles qui, tout Dieu qu'il est, le rendroient responsable de notre perte, & nous donneroient en quelque sorte gain de cause contre lui. Mais je vous ai appell\u00e9, & vous n'avez pas voulu venir \u00e0 moi : c'est-\u00e0-dire, vous ne l'avez pas voulu efficacement, vous ne l'avez pas voulu absolument, vous ne l'avez pas voulu constamment ; vous ne l'avez pas voulu de la mani\u00e8re dont vous avez coutume de vouloir les choses, quand vous les vouliez de bonne foi. Or suppos\u00e9 qu'il ait tenu \u00e0 nous de le vouloir, quel sujet avions-nous donc, ou avons-nous encore de d\u00e9sesp\u00e9rer ? Si pour devenir grands & riches nous n'avions qu'\u00e0 le vouloir, qui d\u00e9sesp\u00e9reroit de l'\u00eatre ? Voyez, mon Frere, dit saint Augustin, si vous pouvez vous plaindre dans un point o\u00f9 l'on n'exige rien de vous, sinon que vous le vouliez ? Vide si labor est, ubi velle satis est ? Le d\u00e9sespoir des damn\u00e9s est de penser : je le pouvois, & je ne l'ai pas voulu. Que dis-je ? leur d\u00e9sespoir ne vient pas seulement de-l\u00e0 ; il vient de penser : je le pouvois alors, mais je ne l'ai pas voulu ; & maintenant que je le voudrois, je ne le puis plus. Or notre condition dans cette vie n'est jamais telle. Car nous ne pouvons jamais dire, je le veux & ne le puis pas : mais nous devons toujours dire avec certitude, je le puis encore par la grace de mon Dieu, & il ne s'agit pour moi que de le vouloir."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p029", "label": "English", "text": "But after all, you say, the Saints trembled in considering this mystery; and if this mystery made the Saints tremble, why could it not drive sinners to despair? One more word for your edification: I conclude with the most invincible of all proofs. I grant it: the Saints trembled at the sight of this mystery. But far from authorising our despair, what caused them so much fear is precisely what condemns it; and the reason is plain. They trembled only because they knew that this mystery, besides its infinite dependence upon God, also had a necessary connection with their freedom, and because they regarded their freedom as the source of every disorder. Now this very thing makes our despair about salvation inexcusable. Why? Because, once our freedom enters into it, it always follows that, if we ruin ourselves, it is only because we will it. Our irreligious licence would rather not admit this. One of its artifices is to make us believe, for example, that it is impossible to be saved in the world, at least in certain worldly conditions, in order to claim a right to indulge in everything and retain possession of the liberty to attempt and do everything. But God, Christians, thoroughly overturns this pretext by the thunderous threat he makes to the impious in Scripture: I called and you refused; I too shall laugh at your destruction. For he does not say, I called you and you could not follow me: words which, God though he is, would make him responsible for our ruin and in a certain sense give us a case against him. Rather: I called you and you would not come to me. That is, you did not will it effectively, absolutely or constantly; you did not will it in your customary way of willing things when you wanted them sincerely. Now, supposing it lay with us to will it, what ground did we have, or still have, for despair? If to become great and rich we had only to will it, who would despair of becoming so? See, my brother, Saint Augustine says, whether you can complain in a matter where nothing is required of you except that you will it: See whether there is toil where willing is enough. The despair of the damned is to think: I could have done it and would not. What am I saying? Their despair does not arise from this alone; it comes from thinking: I could have done it then, but would not; and now that I would, I no longer can. Our condition in this life is never such. For we can never say, I will it and cannot do it; we must always say with certainty, I can still do it through my God's grace, and all that is required of me is to will it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p030", "label": "French original", "text": "Voil\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs, par o\u00f9 Dieu confondra un jour nos d\u00e9sespoirs, ou pl\u00fbt\u00f4t, ces honteux rel\u00e2chemens dont le d\u00e9sespoir que je combats, est le principe. Envain nous retrancherons-nous sur les difficult\u00e9s du salut : vous le pouviez, nous r\u00e9pondra Dieu, mais vous ne l'avez pas voulu. Et bien loin que ce pr\u00e9texte d'une impossibilit\u00e9 pr\u00e9tendue de se sauver dans le monde, nous rende moins coupables devant lui, ce sera, dit S. Chrysostome, le premier chef de notre condamnation. Car le premier de tous nos devoirs \u00e9toit de s\u00e7avoir, de croire, d'\u00eatre bien persuad\u00e9, que nous pouvions nous sauver dans le monde, & dans la condition du monde o\u00f9 Dieu nous avoit engag\u00e9s. De nous \u00eatre donc figur\u00e9 que nous ne le pouvions pas, & d'avoir par-l\u00e0 ruin\u00e9 toute l'esp\u00e9rance Chr\u00e9tienne, de nous \u00eatre par-l\u00e0 r\u00e9duits nous-m\u00eames \u00e0 un abandon criminel, c'est par o\u00f9 Dieu commencera notre jugement."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p030", "label": "English", "text": "This, my dear hearers, is how God will one day confound our despair, or rather those shameful lapses of which the despair I combat is the source. In vain shall we take refuge behind salvation's difficulties. You could have done it, God will answer us, but you would not. Far from making us less guilty before him, this pretext of a supposed impossibility of being saved in the world will, Saint Chrysostom says, be the first count in our condemnation. For the first of all our duties was to know, believe and be thoroughly convinced that we could be saved in the world and in the worldly condition in which God had placed us. Having imagined, then, that we could not, and thereby destroyed all Christian hope and reduced ourselves to a sinful abandonment of effort: this is where God will begin our judgement.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p031", "label": "French original", "text": "Nous voulons le salut : car o\u00f9 fut jamais l'insens\u00e9 qui ne le voul\u00fbt pas ? mais nous le voulons d'une volont\u00e9 g\u00e9n\u00e9rale & ind\u00e9termin\u00e9e : on s'en tient \u00e0 des d\u00e9sirs vagues, sans descendre jamais aux moyens. Nous le voulons d'une volont\u00e9 foible & l\u00e2che : le moindre obstacle nous arr\u00eate, & les plus l\u00e9g\u00e8res difficult\u00e9s nous rebutent. Nous le voulons d'une volont\u00e9 inefficace & sans action : d\u00e8s qu'il faut mettre la main \u00e0 l'\u0153uvre & travailler, nous assujettir \u00e0 certains devoirs indispensables, \u00e0 certaines pratiques, \u00e0 certaines r\u00e9gles, le courage nous manque, & nous nous rendons. Nous le voulons d'une volont\u00e9 \u00e9troite & born\u00e9e : nous sommes pr\u00eats \u00e0 prendre telle & telle voie, \u00e0 faire telle & telle chose, mais rien au de-l\u00e0."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p031", "label": "English", "text": "We desire salvation: for where was there ever a madman who did not desire it? But we will it with a general and indeterminate will, content with vague desires without ever coming down to the means. We will it with a weak and slack will: the slightest obstacle stops us, and the lightest difficulties discourage us. We will it with an ineffective and inactive will: as soon as we must put our hands to the work and labour, submit to certain indispensable duties, practices and rules, our courage fails and we surrender. We will it with a narrow and limited will: we are ready to take this or that path, do this or that thing, but nothing beyond.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p032", "label": "French original", "text": "Est-ce ainsi, nous dira Dieu, que vous vouliez tout le reste ? Est-ce ainsi que vous vouliez la gu\u00e9rison d'une maladie mortelle ? Est-ce ainsi que vous vouliez le gain d'un proc\u00e8s ? Combien de ces volont\u00e9s st\u00e9riles & sans effet, Dieu ne r\u00e9prouvera-t-il pas en les rejettant comme de fausses volont\u00e9s ? Pilate vouloit sauver Jesus-Christ : en sera-t-il cr\u00fb pour dire, Je le voulois ? H\u00e9rodes vouloit \u00e9pargner Jean-Baptiste : osera-t-il dire qu'il le voul\u00fbt comme il falloit le vouloir ? Ce jeune homme de l'Evangile vouloit \u00eatre parfait : mais le vouloit-il, quand il s'en retourna triste & afflig\u00e9 apr\u00e8s l'avis que lui donna le Sauveur du monde ? Non, non, Chr\u00e9tiens, ne nous flattons pas, en disant que nous voulons nous sauver : c'est imposer \u00e0 Dieu & nous d\u00e9mentir nous-m\u00eames, puisqu'au m\u00eame-tems nous nous rendons malgr\u00e9 nous mille t\u00e9moignages secrets, que le salut est de toutes les choses du monde celle que nous voulons moins & que nous nous effor\u00e7ons moins de vouloir."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p032", "label": "English", "text": "Is this, God will say to us, how you desired everything else? Is this how you desired recovery from a mortal illness? Is this how you desired to win a lawsuit? How many of these sterile and ineffectual wills will God condemn, rejecting them as false wills! Pilate wished to save Jesus Christ: will he be believed merely for saying, I wished it? Herod wished to spare John the Baptist: will he dare say that he wished it as he ought to have wished it? The young man in the Gospel wished to be perfect: but did he wish it when he went away sad and afflicted after the advice the Saviour of the world gave him? No, no, Christians, let us not flatter ourselves by saying we wish to be saved. It is to deceive God and give ourselves the lie, since at the same time, despite ourselves, we bear a thousand secret witnesses within us that, of all things in the world, salvation is the one we desire least and make least effort to desire.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p033", "label": "French original", "text": "Et c'est ici qu'il faut encore vous d\u00e9couvrir une autre erreur, que vous n'avez peut-\u00eatre jamais remarqu\u00e9e, mais dont vous conviendrez sans peine, pour peu que vous vous appliquiez \u00e0 la comprendre. Car que faisons-nous ? Excellente r\u00e9flexion de saint Chrysostome, & qui vaut une pr\u00e9dication toute enti\u00e8re ! que faisons-nous ? le voici. Dieu nous d\u00e9clare en mille endroits de l'Ecriture & dans les termes les plus expr\u00e8s qu'il nous veut sauver ; Qui vult omnes homines salvos fieri : & en mille endroits de l'Ecriture il nous reproche dans les m\u00eames termes que nous ne le voulons pas ; Quoties volui congregare filios tuos, & noluisti ? Mais nous, par une obstination bisarre, nous t\u00e2chons \u00e0 nous persuader que nous le voulons, & nous pr\u00e9tendons que c'est Dieu qui ne le veut pas. Au lieu de douter de nous-m\u00eames, & de nous tenir s\u00fbrs de lui, nous nous d\u00e9fions de lui, & nous nous r\u00e9pondons de nous. Nous cherchons des subtilit\u00e9s pour nous prouver qu'il ne le veut pas, lorsqu'il le veut ; & nous sommes ing\u00e9nieux \u00e0 nous faire accroire que nous le voulons, lorsqu'il est constant que nous ne le voulons pas. Mais \u00e0 quoi se terminent l'un & l'autre ? \u00e0 une n\u00e9gligence totale & absolue de tout ce qui regarde le salut. Cependant il sera toujours vrai, quoi que nous fassions, que notre perte vient de nous ; de nous, dis-je, librement & volontairement ; que c'est nous qui avons p\u00e9ch\u00e9, nous qui nous sommes \u00e9gar\u00e9s, nous qui nous sommes pr\u00e9cipit\u00e9s dans l'abysme."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p033", "label": "English", "text": "And here I must disclose another error which you may never have noticed, but which you will readily acknowledge if you apply yourselves at all to understanding it. For what do we do? An excellent reflection of Saint Chrysostom's, worth an entire sermon! What do we do? Here it is. God declares to us in a thousand places of Scripture, in the most explicit terms, that he wills to save us: Who wills all men to be saved. And in a thousand places of Scripture he reproaches us, in the same terms, for not willing it: How often I wished to gather your children, and you would not! Yet through a strange obstinacy we try to persuade ourselves that we will it, and maintain that it is God who does not. Instead of doubting ourselves and being sure of him, we distrust him and vouch for ourselves. We seek subtleties to prove to ourselves that he does not will it when he does; and we are ingenious in making ourselves believe that we will it when it is certain that we do not. But what do both end in? In a total and absolute neglect of everything concerning salvation. Yet whatever we do, it will always remain true that our ruin comes from ourselves: from ourselves, I say, freely and voluntarily; that it is we who have sinned, we who have gone astray, we who have cast ourselves into the abyss.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p034", "label": "French original", "text": "Ah, mes chers Auditeurs, n'entrons point tant dans ces questions imp\u00e9n\u00e9trables de la grace & dans ce t\u00e9n\u00e9breux myst\u00e8re de la pr\u00e9destination : mais tenons-nous-en \u00e0 ce qu'il a pl\u00fb \u00e0 Dieu de nous r\u00e9v\u00e9ler. C'est un myst\u00e8re qui a servi de fonds aux h\u00e9r\u00e9sies ; faisons-en pour nous un myst\u00e8re de foi : c'est un myst\u00e8re o\u00f9 l'on a donn\u00e9 ais\u00e9ment dans l'erreur ; attachons-nous aux d\u00e9cisions de l'Eglise : c'est un myst\u00e8re dont les libertins se sont pr\u00e9valus pour demeurer dans leurs d\u00e9reglemens ; servons-nous-en pour nous exciter \u00e0 la pratique des bonnes \u0153uvres. Portons m\u00eame encore, s'il le faut, la chose plus loin, & \u00e0 une extr\u00e9mit\u00e9 toute oppos\u00e9e ; & disons comme ce solitaire attaqu\u00e9 d'une violente tentation de d\u00e9sespoir : H\u00e9 bien, si je suis r\u00e9prouv\u00e9, au moins je glorifierai Dieu dans cette vie. Mais pourquoi le penserois-je de la sorte, puisque Dieu me commande d'esp\u00e9rer en lui, puisqu'il m'a oblig\u00e9 de l'invoquer comme mon Sauveur, puisqu'il m'invite \u00e0 la p\u00e9nitence, puisqu'il me punit si je ne la fais pas, & que par-l\u00e0 il m'apprend que je puis la faire si je le veux, & me sauver. Voil\u00e0 ce que je ne puis ignorer, ce que je reconnois, & ce qu'il me suffit de conno\u00eetre, pour me soutenir, pour m'animer, pour m'encourager."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p034", "label": "English", "text": "Ah, my dear hearers, let us not enter so deeply into these impenetrable questions of grace and this dark mystery of predestination; let us hold to what God has been pleased to reveal to us. It is a mystery that has furnished a foundation for heresies: let us make it a mystery of faith for ourselves. It is a mystery in which people have easily fallen into error: let us adhere to the Church's decisions. It is a mystery of which libertines have taken advantage to remain in their disorders: let us use it to stir ourselves to the practice of good works. Let us even, if necessary, carry the matter further, to an entirely opposite extreme, and say with that solitary assailed by a violent temptation to despair: Well then, if I am reprobate, at least I shall glorify God in this life. But why should I think thus, since God commands me to hope in him, obliges me to invoke him as my Saviour, invites me to penance, and punishes me if I do not perform it, thereby teaching me that I can perform it if I will, and be saved? This is what I cannot be ignorant of, what I acknowledge, and what it is enough for me to know in order to sustain, animate and encourage me.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p035", "label": "French original", "text": "Il n'y a donc point d'\u00e9tat dans la vie o\u00f9 l'on doive d\u00e9sesp\u00e9rer de son salut : car la vie pr\u00e9sente est la voie du salut ; & tandis que je suis dans la voie, je puis toujours arriver au terme, parce que j'ai toujours tous les moyens n\u00e9cessaires pour y parvenir, que je puis toujours les prendre, & que je n'ai qu'\u00e0 le vouloir & le bien vouloir. Autrement, pourquoi Dieu me demanderoit-il si je veux \u00eatre gu\u00e9ri, Vis sanus fieri ? David devient tout \u00e0 la fois coupable & d'un meurtre & d'un adult\u00e8re : cependant tout coupable qu'il est, il ne perd pas pour cela toute esp\u00e9rance. Que dis-je, au lieu qu'avant son p\u00e9ch\u00e9 il appelloit Dieu seulement son souverain & son Roi ; Rex meus, & Deus meus : apr\u00e8s son p\u00e9ch\u00e9, comme remarque saint Augustin, il lui parle d'une mani\u00e8re plus tendre : mon Dieu & ma mis\u00e9ricorde, Deus meus, misericordia mea. Sur quoi ce Pere s'\u00e9crie : O nom de consolation & de confiance ! \u00f4 nom qui ne me permet pas de me d\u00e9fier jamais de mon Dieu ! O nomen sub quo nemini fas est desperare !"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p035", "label": "English", "text": "There is therefore no condition in life in which one should despair of salvation. For the present life is the way of salvation; and while I am on the way I can always reach the goal, because I always have all the means necessary to attain it, can always take them, and need only will it and will it sincerely. Otherwise, why would God ask me whether I wish to be healed: Do you wish to be made well? David becomes guilty at once of murder and adultery; yet guilty though he is, he does not thereby lose all hope. What am I saying? Whereas before his sin he called God only his sovereign and his King, My King and my God, after his sin, as Saint Augustine observes, he speaks to him more tenderly: my God and my mercy, My God, my mercy. At this the Father exclaims: O name of consolation and confidence! O name that never permits me to distrust my God! O name under which no one may despair!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p036", "label": "French original", "text": "Ce qui fit le malheur de Judas & ce qui le damna, ce ne fut pas pr\u00e9cis\u00e9ment sa trahison, mais son d\u00e9sespoir. Il pouvoit \u00eatre un apostat, un sacril\u00e9ge, un tra\u00eetre, & devenir ensuite un pr\u00e9destin\u00e9 ; comme saint Pierre, de d\u00e9serteur & de blasph\u00e9mateur, devint le Prince des Ap\u00f4tres, & le chef de l'Eglise. Ce qui mit entre ces deux p\u00e9cheurs une diff\u00e9rence si essentielle, ce ne fut pas le p\u00e9ch\u00e9, mais la vraie p\u00e9nitence de l'un & la fausse p\u00e9nitence de l'autre, mais la confiance de l'un & la d\u00e9fiance de l'autre. Si Judas e\u00fbt esp\u00e9r\u00e9 comme saint Pierre, ce seroit actuellement un saint comme lui ; & si saint Pierre e\u00fbt d\u00e9sesp\u00e9r\u00e9 comme Judas, ce seroit actuellement comme lui un r\u00e9prouv\u00e9. L'un crut qu'il y avoit encore pour lui un fonds de mis\u00e9ricorde ; & voil\u00e0 le commencement de sa pr\u00e9destination : mais l'autre crut qu'il n'y avoit plus de pardon pour lui ; & voil\u00e0 sa condamnation. Grande le\u00e7on pour vous-m\u00eames, Chr\u00e9tiens : \u00e9coutez-la. Bien loin qu'il vous soit permis de d\u00e9sesp\u00e9rer des bont\u00e9s de Dieu, ce d\u00e9sespoir est un nouveau crime que vous ajo\u00fbtez aux autres. Car dans quelque abysme que vous vous soyez plong\u00e9s, il y a toujours un pr\u00e9cepte qui vous oblige \u00e0 vous confier en Dieu. Plus m\u00eame vous \u00eates p\u00e9cheurs, plus devez-vous redoubler votre confiance, & dire avec David : Ah ! Seigneur, usez envers moi de mis\u00e9ricorde & de votre grande mis\u00e9ricorde : Secundum magnam misericordiam tuam. Ce qui a perdu Judas, c'est ce qui perd encore tous les jours certains p\u00e9cheurs du si\u00e9cle. Je dis certains p\u00e9cheurs, & non pas tous les p\u00e9cheurs. Car les p\u00e9cheurs ordinaires se perdent par un exc\u00e8s d'esp\u00e9rance : mais les insignes p\u00e9cheurs, les libertins & les impies se perdent par un d\u00e9faut d'esp\u00e9rance. Et tel est l'artifice du d\u00e9mon : il \u00f4te aux uns la vraie confiance, & aux autres la vraie crainte ; & \u00e0 la place de cette vraie crainte, de cette vraie confiance, il donne \u00e0 ceux-l\u00e0 une fausse confiance & \u00e0 ceux-ci une fausse crainte."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p036", "label": "English", "text": "What caused Judas's misfortune and damned him was not precisely his betrayal but his despair. He could have been an apostate, a sacrilegious man, a traitor, and afterwards become one of the predestined, just as Saint Peter, from being a deserter and blasphemer, became the Prince of the Apostles and head of the Church. What made so essential a difference between these two sinners was not the sin, but the true penance of the one and the false penance of the other, the confidence of the one and the distrust of the other. Had Judas hoped as Saint Peter did, he would now be a saint like him; had Saint Peter despaired like Judas, he would now be reprobate like him. The one believed there was still a fund of mercy for him: there is the beginning of his predestination. The other believed there was no longer pardon for him: there is his condemnation. A great lesson for you yourselves, Christians: listen to it. Far from your being permitted to despair of God's goodness, this despair is a new crime you add to the others. For into whatever abyss you have plunged yourselves, there is always a precept obliging you to trust in God. Indeed, the greater sinners you are, the more you must redouble your confidence and say with David: Ah, Lord, show me mercy, your great mercy: According to your great mercy. What ruined Judas is what still ruins certain worldly sinners every day. I say certain sinners, not all sinners. For ordinary sinners are ruined by an excess of hope; notorious sinners, libertines and the impious are ruined by a lack of hope. Such is the devil's artifice: he takes true confidence from some and true fear from others; and in place of this true fear and true confidence, he gives the latter a false confidence and the former a false fear.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p037", "label": "French original", "text": "Apprenez-moi donc, \u00f4 mon Dieu, \u00e0 bien m\u00e9nager ces deux sentimens, la confiance & la crainte : la confiance sans la crainte m'emportera au-dessus de moi, & me rendra pr\u00e9somptueux ; & la crainte sans la confiance m'\u00e9loignera de vous, & me rendra pusillanime. Apprenez-moi comment je dois craindre en esp\u00e9rant, & esp\u00e9rer en craignant : craindre votre justice, mais en m\u00eame-tems esp\u00e9rer en votre mis\u00e9ricorde ; esp\u00e9rer en votre mis\u00e9ricorde, mais en m\u00eame-tems, craindre votre justice. Le Seigneur n'a parl\u00e9 qu'une fois, disoit le Proph\u00e8te Royal, il n'a prononc\u00e9 qu'une parole, & j'en ai entendu deux ; s\u00e7avoir, qu'il est tout-puissant & plein de mis\u00e9ricorde : Semel locutus est Deus, duo haec audivi, quia potestas tibi est & misericordia. Que veut dire cela, demande saint Augustin ? Il est vrai, r\u00e9pond ce Pere, que Dieu n'a jamais produit qu'une parole au dedans de lui-m\u00eame, qui est son verbe : mais ce verbe, cette parole sortie de Dieu nous a fait entendre deux voix, celle de la mis\u00e9ricorde & celle de la justice : Misericordiam, qu\u00e2 plena est terra ; & justitiam qu\u00e2 reddet unicuique secundum opera sua. La voix de la justice nous menace, & la voix de la mis\u00e9ricorde nous rass\u00fbre. L'une & l'autre par cet admirable temp\u00e9rament de confiance & de crainte, nous conduisent dans le chemin de l'\u00e9ternit\u00e9 bienheureuse que je vous souhaite, &c."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-p037", "label": "English", "text": "Teach me, then, O my God, to govern rightly these two sentiments, confidence and fear. Confidence without fear will carry me above myself and make me presumptuous; fear without confidence will draw me away from you and make me fainthearted. Teach me how I must fear while hoping and hope while fearing: fear your justice, but at the same time hope in your mercy; hope in your mercy, but at the same time fear your justice. The Lord has spoken only once, the Royal Prophet said; he has uttered but one word, and I have heard two: namely, that he is almighty and full of mercy. God has spoken once; these two things I have heard: that power and mercy are yours. What does this mean? Saint Augustine asks. It is true, this Father replies, that God has never produced within himself more than one word, which is his Word; but this Word, this utterance proceeding from God, has made us hear two voices, that of mercy and that of justice: Mercy, with which the earth is full; and justice, by which he will render to each according to his works. The voice of justice threatens us, and the voice of mercy reassures us. Through this admirable balance of confidence and fear, both lead us on the path to the blessed eternity which I wish for you, etc.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n001", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n001", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n002", "label": "French original", "text": "Ps. 17."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n002", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 17.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n003", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n003", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n004", "label": "French original", "text": "Thren. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n004", "label": "English", "text": "Lamentations 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n005", "label": "French original", "text": "Isa\u00ef. 45."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n005", "label": "English", "text": "Isaiah 45.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n006", "label": "French original", "text": "I. Petr. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n006", "label": "English", "text": "1 Peter 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n007", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 26."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n007", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 26.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n008", "label": "French original", "text": "Ps. 26."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n008", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 26.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n009", "label": "French original", "text": "II. Petr. [uncertain chapter numeral]."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n009", "label": "English", "text": "2 Peter, [uncertain chapter numeral].", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n010", "label": "French original", "text": "I. Thess. [uncertain chapter numeral at image edge]."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n010", "label": "English", "text": "1 Thessalonians, [uncertain chapter numeral at image edge].", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n011", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n011", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n012", "label": "French original", "text": "I. Cor. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n012", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n013", "label": "French original", "text": "Ps. 155."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n013", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 155.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n014", "label": "French original", "text": "Idem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n014", "label": "English", "text": "The same author.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n015", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n015", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n016", "label": "French original", "text": "Bernard."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n016", "label": "English", "text": "Bernard.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n017", "label": "French original", "text": "Idem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n017", "label": "English", "text": "The same author.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n018", "label": "French original", "text": "Ephes. 4."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n018", "label": "English", "text": "Ephesians 4.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n019", "label": "French original", "text": "II. Tim. 4."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n019", "label": "English", "text": "2 Timothy 4.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n020", "label": "French original", "text": "Sap. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n020", "label": "English", "text": "Wisdom 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n021", "label": "French original", "text": "Ierem. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n021", "label": "English", "text": "Jeremiah 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n022", "label": "French original", "text": "Rom. 11."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n022", "label": "English", "text": "Romans 11.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n023", "label": "French original", "text": "I. Petr. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n023", "label": "English", "text": "1 Peter 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n024", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal. 30."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n024", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 30.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n025", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n025", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n026", "label": "French original", "text": "I. Cor. 9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n026", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians 9.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n027", "label": "French original", "text": "I. Tim. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n027", "label": "English", "text": "1 Timothy 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n028", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt. 23."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n028", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 23.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n029", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n029", "label": "English", "text": "John 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n030", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n030", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n031", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal. 58."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n031", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 58.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n032", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n032", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n033", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal. 50."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n033", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 50.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n034", "label": "French original", "text": "Ps. 61."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n034", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 61.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n035", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-n035", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-ed001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Two chapter numbers in the margin are damaged. The number after 2 Peter is unclear; the number after 1 Thessalonians is cut off at the scan\u2019s edge. Both are marked uncertain rather than supplied from the quotations."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-ed002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The book leaves two parentheses unclosed in the aside about a cardinal (p. 394), and appears to omit a short word in the sentence on Catholic belief (p. 398). The French preserves these forms; the English uses ordinary punctuation and grammar."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-ed003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The French explanation and the Latin quotations about merit and confidence do not agree completely. Both are preserved. The French rejects confidence in one\u2019s own merits, rather than denying that a Christian has merits. The Latin is translated as printed; its attribution to Bernard has not been independently authenticated."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-scripture-14", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Psalm 55:5 (56:5 in modern numbering) \u2014 reference added by editors for the adapted quotation."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-scripture-17", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "2 Timothy 1:12 \u2014 added by editors."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-scripture-20", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Jeremiah 31:3 \u2014 added by editors."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-predestination-scripture-23", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Proverbs 1:24, 26 \u2014 added by editors; no printed marginal locator."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "On Predestination: Presumption and Despair", "topics": ["predestination", "grace", "free will", "presumption", "despair", "hope"], "url": "sermons/bourdaloue-careme-predestination.html"}, {"author": "Louis Bourdaloue", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-lent", "calendar_label": "Lent", "centuries": [17, 18], "composition": "Approximately late seventeenth century\u20131704; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "bourdaloue-careme-priere", "language": "fr", "language_label": "French", "metadata": ["Louis Bourdaloue, Sermons du Pere Bourdaloue, de la Compagnie de Jesus, pour le Caresme, tome premier, nouvelle \u00e9dition (Paris: aux d\u00e9pens de Rigaud, Directeur de l'Imprimerie Royale, 1716), Sermon pour le Jeudi de la premi\u00e8re Semaine. Sur la Pri\u00e8re, pp.334\u2013377. Kelly Library copy.", "Internet Archive; John M. Kelly Library Rare Books, University of Toronto; sponsored by the University of Ottawa.", "Lent", "Thursday of the first week of Lent", "Matthew 15", "Psalm 148 (historical numbering; printed locator)", "John 15", "Joshua 10", "James 4", "Isaiah 43", "Matthew 6", "2 Maccabees \u2014 chapter numeral worn, best read 9", "John \u2014 chapter numeral damaged, tentative 16", "John 16", "Genesis 27", "Hebrews 5", "John 17", "Psalm 26 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 76 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 6 (printed locator; apparent reference anomaly)", "Sirach 25", "Matthew 10 (printed locator; apparent reference anomaly)", "James 1", "Sirach 2", "Isaiah 28", "Psalm 108 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 5 (historical numbering)", "Luke 11", "Rev. A. Carroll, S.J.", "Louis Bourdaloue, Sermons, and Moral Discourses, on the Important Duties of Christianity, translated by the Rev. A. Carroll, S.J., second edition, vol. II (Dublin: James Duffy, 1843), sermon XXIV, \u201cOn Prayer\u201d, pp. 162\u2013183."], "occasion": "Jeudi de la premi\u00e8re Semaine [du Car\u00eame]", "occasion_detail": "Thursday of the first week of Lent", "occasion_label": "Lent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Taking the Canaanite woman\u2019s petition in Matthew 15 as its example, Bourdaloue asks why prayers so often appear fruitless. The first part examines what is requested: desires opposed to salvation, temporal advantages placed before spiritual goods, and imagined graces that would dispense with conversion and action. The second treats the manner of prayer: humility, confidence, perseverance, attention of mind and affection of heart. Its conclusion calls for the gift of prayer itself."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-h001", "label": "French original", "text": "SERMON POUR LE JEUDI de la premi\u00e8re Semaine. Sur la Pri\u00e8re."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON FOR THURSDAY of the first Week. On Prayer.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-e001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Ecce mulier Cananaea \u00e0 finibus illis egressa, clamavit, dicens : Miserere me\u00ee, Domine, fili David ; filia mea mal\u00e8 \u00e0 daemonio vexatur."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-e001", "label": "English", "text": "Behold, a Canaanite woman coming out from those regions cried out, saying : Have mercy on me, Lord, son of David; my daughter is grievously tormented by a demon.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-e002", "label": "French original", "text": "Alors une femme Chanan\u00e9enne venue de ces quartiers-l\u00e0, s'\u00e9cria, en lui disant : Seigneur, Fils de David, ayez piti\u00e9 de moi ; ma fille est cruellement tourment\u00e9e par le d\u00e9mon. En saint Matth. chap. 15."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-e002", "label": "English", "text": "Then a Canaanite woman who had come from those parts cried out to him, saying : Lord, Son of David, have pity on me; my daughter is cruelly tormented by the demon. In Saint Matthew, chap. 15.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p001", "label": "French original", "text": "Si jamais la force de la pri\u00e8re parut sensiblement & d'une mani\u00e8re \u00e9clatante, n'est-ce pas, Chr\u00e9tiens, dans l'exemple que nous propose l'Evangile de ce jour, o\u00f9 nous voyons, pour parler avec saint Ambroise, un Dieu m\u00eame surpris & dans l'admiration ; un Dieu qui confond les puissances de l'enfer, qui fait des miracles, & qui d\u00e9ploie toute sa vertu en faveur d'une \u00e9trang\u00e9re laquelle a recours \u00e0 lui, & qui toute idol\u00e2tre qu'elle est, nous sert de mod\u00e8le & nous apprend \u00e0 prier ? Je dis un Dieu surpris & dans l'admiration : O mulier, magna est fides tua ! \u00f4 femme, votre foi est grande ! c'est ainsi que J\u00e9sus-Christ lui-m\u00eame s'en explique ; & ne semble-t-il pas que la foi de cette Chanan\u00e9enne, & que la ferveur de sa pri\u00e8re ait quelque chose pour lui de surprenant & de nouveau ? Je dis un Dieu qui confond les puissances de l'enfer, & qui fait des miracles : que lui demande cette femme ? qu'il gu\u00e9risse sa fille cruellement tourment\u00e9e du d\u00e9mon ; & le Fils de Dieu d'une m\u00eame parole, non-seulement d\u00e9livre la fille, mais sanctifie encore la m\u00e8re ; Fiat tibi sicut vis ; qu'il vous soit fait comme vous le souhaitez."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p001", "label": "English", "text": "If the power of prayer ever appeared visibly and in a striking manner, Christians, was it not in the example set before us by today's Gospel? There we see, to speak with Saint Ambrose, God himself surprised and filled with admiration; God confounding the powers of hell, working miracles, and displaying all his power in favor of a foreign woman who turns to him. Although she is an idolater, she serves as our model and teaches us to pray. I say, God surprised and filled with admiration : O woman, great is your faith ! O woman, great is your faith! This is how Jesus Christ himself expresses it; and does not the faith of this Canaanite woman, and the fervor of her prayer, seem to hold something surprising and new for him? I say, God confounding the powers of hell and working miracles : what does this woman ask of him? That he heal her daughter, cruelly tormented by the demon; and with one and the same word the Son of God not only delivers the daughter, but also sanctifies the mother : Let it be done for you as you will; let it be done for you as you wish.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p002", "label": "French original", "text": "Il n'est donc rien de plus efficace aupr\u00e8s de Dieu que la pri\u00e8re : & d'o\u00f9 vient toutefois, mes chers Auditeurs, que Dieu tous les jours se montre si peu favorable \u00e0 nos v\u0153ux ; que nous prions, & qu'il ne nous \u00e9coute pas ; que nous demandons, & que nous n'obtenons pas ? C'est ce que je veux examiner aujourd'hui, & ce qui va faire le fonds de ce discours. Sujet d'une extr\u00eame cons\u00e9quence, & qui m\u00e9rite une r\u00e9flexion toute particuli\u00e8re. Car il s'agit, Chr\u00e9tiens, de vous enseigner la plus excellente de toutes les sciences, il s'agit de vous apprendre \u00e0 bien user du moyen de salut le plus puissant ; il s'agit de vous faire conno\u00eetre le secret inestimable & l'art tout divin de toucher le c\u0153ur de Dieu, & de faire descendre sur nous les plus pr\u00e9tieux tr\u00e9sors de sa gr\u00e2ce. Pour recevoir ce don de la pri\u00e8re, employons la pri\u00e8re elle-m\u00eame, & implorons le secours du ciel par l'intercession de Marie. Ave, Maria."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p002", "label": "English", "text": "There is therefore nothing more effective with God than prayer. Yet how does it happen, my dear hearers, that day after day God shows so little favor to our petitions; that we pray and he does not hear us; that we ask and do not obtain? This is what I wish to examine today, and what will form the substance of this discourse. It is a subject of the greatest consequence, deserving particularly careful reflection. For the task, Christians, is to teach you the most excellent of all sciences, to show you how to make good use of the most powerful means of salvation, and to make known to you the priceless secret and wholly divine art of touching God's heart and drawing down upon us the most precious treasures of his grace. To receive this gift of prayer, let us employ prayer itself, and implore heaven's help through Mary's intercession. Hail, Mary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p003", "label": "French original", "text": "Rien n'est plus solidement \u00e9tabli dans la religion & la th\u00e9ologie Chr\u00e9tienne, que l'infaillibilit\u00e9 de la pri\u00e8re. Elle a une telle force, dit saint Jean Chrysostome, qu'elle rend, \u00e0 ce qu'il semble, la parole de l'homme aussi puissante & m\u00eame plus puissante que la parole de Dieu. Aussi puissante : car comme Dieu d'une parole a fait toutes choses, Dixit & facta sunt; l'homme n'a qu'\u00e0 parler & \u00e0 demander, tout lui est accord\u00e9 : Quodcumque volueritis petetis, & fiet vobis. Plus puissante m\u00eame en quelque sorte, puisque si Dieu se fait ob\u00e9ir, ce n'est que des \u00eatres cr\u00e9\u00e9s ; au lieu que par la vertu de la pri\u00e8re, tout Dieu qu'il est, il ob\u00e9it, selon l'expression de l'Ecriture, \u00e0 la voix de l'homme : Obediente Domino voci hominis. Nous entendons tous les jours des Chr\u00e9tiens qui se plaignent de l'inutilit\u00e9 de leurs pri\u00e8res & du peu de fruit qu'ils en retirent : je ne m'en \u00e9tonne pas. Car en quel sens disons-nous que la pri\u00e8re est infaillible ? nous supposons pour cela une pri\u00e8re sainte, une pri\u00e8re faite avec toutes les conditions qui la doivent accompagner, & que Dieu attend de nous, lorsque de sa part il s'engage \u00e0 nous accorder tout ce que nous demanderons. Or voil\u00e0 souvent ce qui manque \u00e0 nos pri\u00e8res : Ce sont des pri\u00e8res d\u00e9fectueuses, & quant au sujet, & quant \u00e0 la forme : quant au sujet, qui en fait la mati\u00e8re ; & quant \u00e0 la forme, qui en fait la qualit\u00e9 : L'Ap\u00f4tre saint Jacques le disoit aux fid\u00e8les de son temps, & je vous le dis \u00e0 vous-m\u00eames : vous demandez, mes Freres, & vous ne recevez pas, parce que vous ne demandez pas bien : Petitis & non accipitis, e\u00f2 qu\u00f2d mal\u00e8 petatis. En effet, nous ne demandons pas \u00e0 Dieu ce que Dieu veut que nous lui demandions ; d\u00e9faut par rapport au sujet de la pri\u00e8re. Nous ne lui demandons pas de la mani\u00e8re qu'il veut que nous lui demandions ; d\u00e9faut par rapport \u00e0 la forme ou \u00e0 la qualit\u00e9 de la pri\u00e8re. Mais prions comme la Chanan\u00e9enne. Rien de plus juste que la pri\u00e8re qu'elle fait \u00e0 J\u00e9sus-Christ : elle lui demande qu'il d\u00e9livre sa fille du d\u00e9mon dont elle est poss\u00e9d\u00e9e. Rien de plus engageant : elle pratique dans sa pri\u00e8re toutes les vertus qui peuvent gagner & int\u00e9resser le Sauveur du monde. Prions, dis-je, comme cette femme : sans cela, pri\u00e8res infructueuses ; pourquoi ? ou parce que nous ne demandons pas ce qu'il faut, ce sera la premi\u00e8re partie ; ou parce que nous ne demandons pas comme il faut, ce sera la seconde. Deux le\u00e7ons que j'ai \u00e0 mettre dans tout leur jour. Rendez-vous-y attentifs, Chr\u00e9tiens, & t\u00e2chez \u00e0 en profiter."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p003", "label": "English", "text": "Nothing is more firmly established in Christian religion and theology than the infallibility of prayer. Its power is such, says Saint John Chrysostom, that it seems to make the word of man as powerful as, and even more powerful than, the word of God. As powerful : for just as God made all things by a word, He spoke, and they were made, man has only to speak and ask, and everything is granted him : You will ask whatever you wish, and it will be done for you. In a certain respect even more powerful : for when God makes himself obeyed, it is only by created beings; whereas through the power of prayer, God himself, God though he is, obeys the voice of man, as Scripture puts it : With the Lord obeying the voice of man. Every day we hear Christians complain that their prayers are useless and bear little fruit; I am not surprised. For in what sense do we say that prayer is infallible? We presuppose a holy prayer, one made with all the conditions that must accompany it and that God expects of us when, for his part, he undertakes to grant everything we ask. Yet this is often what our prayers lack. They are defective both in their object and in their form : in their object, which constitutes their matter; and in their form, which constitutes their quality. The Apostle Saint James told the faithful of his time, and I say the same to you : you ask, my brothers, and do not receive, because you do not ask rightly : You ask and do not receive, because you ask wrongly. Indeed, we do not ask God for what God wants us to ask him : a defect concerning the object of prayer. We do not ask him in the manner in which he wants us to ask : a defect concerning its form or quality. But let us pray like the Canaanite woman. Nothing could be more just than her prayer to Jesus Christ : she asks him to deliver her daughter from the demon who possesses her. Nothing could be more persuasive : in her prayer she practices all the virtues that can win over the Savior of the world and enlist his concern. Let us pray, I say, like this woman. Otherwise our prayers are fruitless. Why? Either because we do not ask for what we ought, which will be the first part; or because we do not ask as we ought, which will be the second. These are two lessons I must set in their fullest light. Give them your attention, Christians, and try to profit from them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-h002", "label": "French original", "text": "I. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-h002", "label": "English", "text": "PART ONE.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p004", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est sur-tout de la nature des choses qu'on demande \u00e0 Dieu, que d\u00e9pend l'essence de la pri\u00e8re, & par cons\u00e9quent son m\u00e9rite, son efficace, sa vertu. C'est donc aussi par-l\u00e0, dit S. Chrysostome, que nous devons commencer \u00e0 nous faire justice sur le peu de valeur & le peu d'effet qu'ont presque toutes nos pri\u00e8res devant Dieu ; & c'est l'admirable instruction que nous fournit d'abord l'Evangile de la femme Chanan\u00e9enne. Car prenez garde, s'il vous pla\u00eet, & qu'il me soit permis de m'expliquer de la sorte, au lieu que cette femme prostern\u00e9e aux pi\u00e9s de Jesus-Christ lui demande que sa fille soit d\u00e9livr\u00e9e du d\u00e9mon qui la poss\u00e9de ; nous, par un esprit tout oppos\u00e9, nous demandons tous les jours \u00e0 Dieu ce qui entretient dans nos ames le r\u00e8gne du d\u00e9mon, & m\u00eame de plusieurs d\u00e9mons dont nous voulons \u00eatre poss\u00e9d\u00e9s. En faut-il davantage pour vous faire comprendre, pourquoi le Sauveur du monde \u00e9coute cette \u00e9trang\u00e9re & lui accorde un miracle de sa toute-puissance ; & pourquoi Dieu au contraire se rend sourd \u00e0 nos v\u0153ux, & rejette commun\u00e9ment nos pri\u00e8res ? Appliquez-vous, Chr\u00e9tiens, aux grandes v\u00e9rit\u00e9s que ce sujet renferme, & que je vais d\u00e9velopper comme les secrets les plus importans de votre pr\u00e9destination."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p004", "label": "English", "text": "It is above all on the nature of the things we ask of God that the essence of prayer depends, and consequently its merit, its efficacy, its power. It is therefore here too, says Saint Chrysostom, that we must begin to judge ourselves fairly concerning the little worth and the little effect almost all our prayers have before God; and this is the admirable lesson the Gospel of the Canaanite woman gives us at the outset. For consider, please, and allow me to express myself in this way: whereas this woman, prostrate at the feet of Jesus Christ, asks him to deliver her daughter from the demon possessing her, we, in an entirely opposite spirit, ask God every day for what maintains the reign of the demon in our souls, and even of several demons by whom we wish to be possessed. Is anything more needed to make you understand why the Savior of the world hears this foreign woman and grants her a miracle of his almighty power, and why God, on the contrary, turns a deaf ear to our wishes and commonly rejects our prayers? Attend, Christians, to the great truths this subject contains, which I shall unfold as the most important secrets of your predestination.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p004b", "label": "French original", "text": "Je dis que nous demandons tous les jours \u00e0 Dieu ce qui entretient dans nos ames le r\u00e8gne du d\u00e9mon, comment cela ? c'est que dans nos pri\u00e8res nous demandons, ou des choses pr\u00e9judiciables au salut ; ou des biens purement temporels & inutiles au salut ; ou m\u00eame des graces surnaturelles, mais qui de la mani\u00e8re que nous les concevons & que nous les voulons, bien loin de nous sanctifier, servent plut\u00f4t \u00e0 nous s\u00e9duire, & \u00e0 nous retirer de la voie du salut. Donnons \u00e0 ceci tout l'\u00e9claircissement n\u00e9cessaire."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p004b", "label": "English", "text": "I say that we ask God every day for what maintains the reign of the demon in our souls. How so? Because in our prayers we ask either for things harmful to salvation, or for purely temporal goods useless to salvation, or even for supernatural graces which, in the way we conceive and desire them, far from sanctifying us, serve rather to lead us astray and draw us away from the path of salvation. Let us give this all the explanation it needs.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p005", "label": "French original", "text": "Nous demandons des choses pr\u00e9judiciables au salut : premier obstacle que nous opposons aux mis\u00e9ricordes divines, & qui en arr\u00eate le cours. Car ne pensons pas, mes chers Auditeurs, que pour \u00eatre Chr\u00e9tiens de profession, nous en soyons moins sujets dans la pratique aux d\u00e9sordres du Paganisme. Or un des d\u00e9sordres des Payens, si nous en croyons les Payens m\u00eames, c'\u00e9toit de recourir \u00e0 leurs Dieux & de leur demander, quoi ? ce qu'ils n'auroient pas eu le front de demander \u00e0 un homme de bien, ce qu'ils n'auroient pu demander ouvertement dans les temples & au pi\u00e9 des Autels, sans en rougir : la mort d'un parent dont ils attendoient la d\u00e9pouille, la mort d'un concurrent dont le cr\u00e9dit ou le m\u00e9rite leur faisoit ombrage, le patrimoine d'un pupille qu'ils cherchoient \u00e0 enlever, & sur lequel ils jettoient des regards de concupiscence. Tel \u00e9toit le sujet de leurs pri\u00e8res : & pour leur donner plus de poids, ils les accompagnoient de toutes les c\u00e9r\u00e9monies d'un culte superstitieux, ils y joignoient les offrandes & les sacrifices, ils se purifioient. Cela nous semble \u00e9norme & insens\u00e9 : mais, Chr\u00e9tiens, en les condamnant, n'est-ce pas nous-m\u00eames que nous condamnons ? A comparer leurs pri\u00e8res & les n\u00f4tres, sommes-nous moins coupables ? que dis-je ? ne sommes-nous pas encore plus coupables qu'ils ne l'\u00e9toient ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p005", "label": "English", "text": "We ask for things harmful to salvation: the first obstacle we set against the divine mercies, which stops their course. For let us not imagine, my dear listeners, that because we are Christians by profession we are any less subject in practice to the disorders of paganism. Now one of the disorders of the pagans, if we believe the pagans themselves, was to turn to their gods and ask them\u2014for what? For what they would not have had the impudence to ask of an upright man, what they could not have asked openly in the temples and at the foot of the altars without blushing: the death of a relative whose estate they awaited, the death of a rival whose influence or merit gave them offense, the property of a ward which they sought to seize and on which they cast covetous eyes. Such was the subject of their prayers; and, to give them more weight, they accompanied them with all the ceremonies of a superstitious cult, joined offerings and sacrifices to them, and purified themselves. This seems monstrous and senseless to us; but, Christians, in condemning them, is it not ourselves whom we condemn? Comparing their prayers with ours, are we less guilty? What am I saying? Are we not still more guilty than they were?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p006", "label": "French original", "text": "Car enfin c'\u00e9toient des Payens, & ces Payens n'adoroient pas seulement de vaines & de fausses divinit\u00e9s, mais selon leur cr\u00e9ance m\u00eame des divinit\u00e9s vicieuses & dissolues. Or \u00e0 de telles divinit\u00e9s que pouvoient-ils demander plus naturellement, que ce qui favorisoit leurs vices & la corruption de leurs m\u0153urs ? n'\u00e9toit-ce pas une suite presque n\u00e9cessaire de leur infid\u00e9lit\u00e9 ? Mais nous, mes Freres, nous servons un Dieu non moins pur, ni moins saint, que puissant & grand ; un Dieu aussi essentiellement ennemi de toute injustice & de tout p\u00e9ch\u00e9, qu'il est essentiellement Dieu : & toutefois ce Dieu si pur, ce Dieu si saint, ce Dieu si \u00e9quitable & si droit, que lui demandons-nous ? l'accomplissement de nos d\u00e9sirs les plus sensuels & le succ\u00e8s de nos entreprises les plus criminelles. Ce n'est plus seulement un d\u00e9sordre ; c'est, j'ose le dire, une impi\u00e9t\u00e9, c'est un sacril\u00e9ge."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p006", "label": "English", "text": "For, after all, they were pagans; and these pagans worshiped not only empty and false divinities, but, according to their own belief, vicious and dissolute divinities. What, then, could they more naturally ask of such divinities than what favored their vices and the corruption of their morals? Was this not an almost necessary consequence of their unbelief? But we, my brothers, serve a God no less pure and holy than powerful and great; a God as essentially the enemy of all injustice and all sin as he is essentially God. And yet what do we ask of this God so pure, this God so holy, this God so equitable and upright? The fulfillment of our most sensual desires and the success of our most criminal undertakings. This is no longer merely a disorder; it is, I dare say, impiety; it is sacrilege.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p007", "label": "French original", "text": "Il est vrai, & j'en conviens, que dans le Christianisme nous s\u00e7avons mieux colorer nos pri\u00e8res & les exprimer en des termes moins odieux : car on a trouv\u00e9 le secret de d\u00e9guiser tout. Mais si nous nous trompons nous-m\u00eames, nous ne trompons pas Dieu qui nous entend, & qui s\u00e7ait bien discerner la malignit\u00e9 de nos intentions de la simplicit\u00e9 de nos expressions. Envain donc un homme du si\u00e9cle demande-t-il \u00e0 Dieu de quoi subsister dans sa condition, & de quoi maintenir son \u00e9tat : comme son \u00e9tat, ou plut\u00f4t, comme l'id\u00e9e qu'il se forme de son \u00e9tat, ne roule que sur les principes, ou d'une ambition d\u00e9mesur\u00e9e, ou d'une avarice insatiable, Dieu dont la p\u00e9n\u00e9tration est infinie, conno\u00eet ses desseins, & prend plaisir \u00e0 les faire \u00e9chouer. Envain un pere demande-t-il \u00e0 Dieu l'\u00e9tablissement de ses enfans : comme il n'a sur ses enfans que des vues toutes prophanes, que des vues mondaines, & qui ne sont ni r\u00e9gl\u00e9es selon la conscience, ni soumises \u00e0 la vocation divine, Dieu sans s'arr\u00eater aux apparences d'une humble pri\u00e8re, en d\u00e9couvre la fin ; & par un juste jugement, bien loin d'\u00e9lever cette famille, la ruine de fond en comble, & la laisse malheureusement tomber. Envain une femme demande-t-elle \u00e0 Dieu la sant\u00e9 du corps : comme sa sant\u00e9 dans l'usage qu'elle en veut faire, ne doit servir qu'\u00e0 son oisivet\u00e9, \u00e0 sa mollesse, & peut-\u00eatre \u00e0 son libertinage & \u00e0 son d\u00e9r\u00e9glement, Dieu qui le voit, au lieu de retirer son bras, lui porte encore de plus rudes coups, & lui fait perdre dans une langueur habituelle tout ce qui peut entretenir ses complaisances & flatter sa vanit\u00e9. Envain un plaideur de mauvaise foi demande-t-il \u00e0 Dieu le gain d'un proc\u00e8s, o\u00f9 toute sa fortune est engag\u00e9e : comme ce proc\u00e8s n'est au fond qu'une injustice couverte, mais soutenue par la chicane, Dieu qui ne peut l'ignorer, prend contre lui la cause de la veuve & de l'orphelin, & le fait honteusement d\u00e9choir de toutes ses pr\u00e9tentions. Cependant on n'oublie rien pour int\u00e9resser le ciel & pour le toucher ; on y employe jusqu'au sacrifice, & aux pri\u00e8res de l'Eglise : mais parce que cette affaire qu'on poursuit avec tant de chaleur, n'est qu'une cabale, qu'une intrigue qui ne peut r\u00e9ussir qu'aux d\u00e9pens du prochain, Dieu tuteur de l'innocent & du pauvre, rejette alors jusques au plus adorable sacrifice, jusques aux plus saintes pri\u00e8res de son Eglise. Ce d\u00e9tail me conduiroit trop loin, si j'entreprenois de lui donner toute son \u00e9tendue : mais si vous voulez, mes chers Auditeurs, aller plus avant & vous l'appliquer \u00e0 vous-m\u00eames, vous aurez bient\u00f4t reconnu, que cent fois votre c\u0153ur vous a s\u00e9duits de la sorte, & fait abuser de la pri\u00e8re, pour porter devant Dieu m\u00eame les int\u00e9r\u00eats de vos passions."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p007", "label": "English", "text": "It is true, and I admit it, that in Christianity we know better how to put a favorable color on our prayers and express them in less hateful terms: for we have found the secret of disguising everything. But though we deceive ourselves, we do not deceive God, who hears us and knows well how to distinguish the malice of our intentions from the simplicity of our expressions. In vain, then, does a man of the world ask God for the means to subsist in his station and maintain his rank: since his rank, or rather the idea he forms of his rank, rests only on the principles of either boundless ambition or insatiable avarice, God, whose discernment is infinite, knows his designs and takes pleasure in making them fail. In vain does a father ask God to establish his children: since he has only profane and worldly aims for them, neither governed by conscience nor subject to the divine calling, God, without stopping at the appearance of a humble prayer, discovers its purpose; and by a just judgment, far from raising up this family, ruins it from top to bottom and lets it fall into misfortune. In vain does a woman ask God for bodily health: since her health, in the use she wishes to make of it, is to serve only her idleness, her softness, and perhaps her libertinism and disorder, God, who sees this, instead of withdrawing his arm, strikes her still harder blows and makes her lose in habitual languor everything that might nourish her self-satisfaction and flatter her vanity. In vain does a dishonest litigant ask God to win a lawsuit in which his entire fortune is involved: since this lawsuit is at bottom only a concealed injustice sustained by legal trickery, God, who cannot be ignorant of it, takes the widow's and the orphan's case against him and makes him shamefully lose all his claims. Meanwhile nothing is left undone to interest heaven and move it; even the sacrifice and the prayers of the Church are employed. But because this affair, pursued with such ardor, is only a cabal, an intrigue that can succeed only at a neighbor's expense, God, guardian of the innocent and the poor, then rejects even the most adorable sacrifice and the holiest prayers of his Church. These details would take me too far if I undertook to give them their full extent; but if you wish, my dear listeners, to go further and apply them to yourselves, you will soon recognize that your heart has deceived you in this way a hundred times and made you abuse prayer to bring even before God the interests of your passions.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p008", "label": "French original", "text": "Revenons, & pour donner \u00e0 ce point important toute la force qu'il doit avoir, souffrez que je me pr\u00e9vale encore de la morale des Payens. J'ai dit qu'elle suffisoit pour nous convaincre ; mais j'en ai dit trop peu, & j'ajoute qu'elle est m\u00eame ici, dans un sens, plus propre \u00e0 nous confondre que la morale des Peres. Qu'il me soit donc permis de faire parler dans cette chaire un auteur profane, & de vous adresser, ou pour votre instruction ou pour votre confusion, les m\u00eames reproches qu'il faisoit \u00e0 son si\u00e9cle en des termes si \u00e9nergiques & si forts. Car r\u00e9pondez-moi, disoit-il, en d\u00e9plorant les abus de l'ancienne Rome, & s'\u00e9levant contre les faux d\u00e9vots du Paganisme qui fatiguoient les Dieux de leurs injustes pri\u00e8res ; dites-moi ce que vous pensez de Jupiter, & quelle estime vous en faites ? si vous avez pour le plus grand des Dieux le m\u00eame respect que pour le plus sage de vos Magistrats ? Cette question vous surprend, poursuivoit-il : mais ce n'est pas sans raison que je la fais : car l'iriez-vous trouver ce Magistrat dont vous respectez la vertu, pour lui faire dans son Palais l'infame pri\u00e8re que vous venez faire \u00e0 Jupiter dans le plus auguste de ses temples ? vous supposez donc Jupiter moins int\u00e9gre & plus ais\u00e9 \u00e0 corrompre, quand vous le croyez dispos\u00e9 \u00e0 vous \u00e9couter, & pr\u00eat m\u00eame \u00e0 vous exaucer : Ainsi s'expliquoit un Payen. Ainsi par de sanglantes ironies, reprochoit-il \u00e0 des Payens les scandales de leur religion, & peut-\u00eatre les corrigeoit-il. Or c'est bien ici, Chr\u00e9tiens, que l'infid\u00e9lit\u00e9 nous fait des le\u00e7ons, & qu'elle nous condamne. Appliquons ceci \u00e0 nos m\u0153urs."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p008", "label": "English", "text": "Let us return; and, to give this important point all the force it ought to have, allow me to draw once more on the morality of the pagans. I said that it was enough to convict us; but I said too little, and I add that here, in a sense, it is even more fitted to put us to shame than the morality of the Fathers. Let me therefore make a profane author speak in this pulpit and address to you, either for your instruction or for your shame, the same reproaches he addressed to his own age in terms so energetic and strong. Answer me, he said, deploring the abuses of ancient Rome and rising against the false devotees of paganism who wearied the gods with their unjust prayers: tell me what you think of Jupiter and what esteem you have for him? Whether you have the same respect for the greatest of the gods as for the wisest of your magistrates? This question surprises you, he continued; but I do not ask it without reason: would you go and find that magistrate whose virtue you respect, to make in his palace the infamous prayer you come to make to Jupiter in the most august of his temples? You therefore suppose Jupiter less upright and easier to corrupt when you think him disposed to hear you and even ready to grant your request. Thus a pagan spoke. Thus, with biting irony, he reproached pagans with the scandals of their religion, and perhaps corrected them. Here indeed, Christians, unbelief teaches us lessons and condemns us. Let us apply this to our conduct.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p009", "label": "French original", "text": "En effet, comment regardons-nous notre Dieu, je dis ce Dieu de saintet\u00e9 ? est-il donc le fauteur de nos vices ? est-il le complice de nos crimes ? & le veut-il, le peut-il \u00eatre ? Toutefois c'est sur ce principe que nous agissons & que nous traitons avec lui. Car quand je prie, ne perdez pas cette remarque de saint Chrysostome ; quand je prie, mon intention est que Dieu par un effet de sa mis\u00e9ricorde & par une condescendance toute paternelle, se conforme \u00e0 moi : que sa volont\u00e9, qui est efficace & toute-puissante, se joigne \u00e0 la mienne qui n'est que foiblesse, & qu'il accomplisse enfin ce que je veux, mais ce que sans lui je veux inutilement. Si donc aveugl\u00e9 par l'esprit du monde, bien loin de prier en Chr\u00e9tien, je prie dans la v\u00fbe de satisfaire mon ambition, mon orgueil, mon ressentiment, ma vengeance, que fais-je ? je demande \u00e0 Dieu qu'il s'accorde l\u00e0 dessus avec moi : c'est-\u00e0-dire, qu'il soit vain comme moi, passionn\u00e9 comme moi, violent comme moi ; & que pour moi qui suis sa cr\u00e9ature, il veuille ce qu'il ne peut vouloir sans cesser d'\u00eatre mon Dieu. Or le prier de la sorte, est-ce le prier en Dieu, & n'est-ce pas plut\u00f4t le deshonorer ? n'est-ce pas autant qu'il d\u00e9pend de moi, le faire servir \u00e0 mes iniquit\u00e9s ? comme il s'en plaint lui-m\u00eame par son Proph\u00eate : Verumtamen servire me fecisti peccatis tuis, & laborem mihi praebuisti in iniquitatibus tuis. Observez cette expression, Et laborem mihi praebuisti : comme s'il disoit au p\u00e9cheur, votre pri\u00e8re m'a \u00e9t\u00e9 un sujet de peine ; car j'aurois voulu d'une part me rendre propice \u00e0 vos v\u0153ux, & de l'autre je n'y pouvois r\u00e9pondre favorablement ; mon c\u0153ur \u00e9toit donc dans une esp\u00e9ce de violence, & comme partag\u00e9 entre ma saintet\u00e9 & ma bont\u00e9 ; ma bont\u00e9, qui s'int\u00e9ressoit pour vous, & ma saintet\u00e9 qui s'opposoit \u00e0 vous ; ma bont\u00e9, qui me portoit \u00e0 vous \u00e9couter, & ma saintet\u00e9 qui m'obligeoit \u00e0 vous rejetter : Et laborem mihi praebuisti in iniquitatibus tuis. Et certes, Chr\u00e9tiens, si Dieu oubliant ce qu'il est, avoit alors \u00e9gard \u00e0 nos pri\u00e8res, ne seroit-ce pas un scandale pour nous, & ne commencerions-nous pas nous-m\u00eames \u00e0 douter de sa providence ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p009", "label": "English", "text": "Indeed, how do we regard our God\u2014I mean this God of holiness? Is he, then, the promoter of our vices? Is he the accomplice of our crimes? And does he wish, can he be so? Yet this is the principle on which we act and deal with him. For when I pray\u2014do not lose this observation of Saint Chrysostom\u2014when I pray, my intention is that God, by an effect of his mercy and an entirely fatherly condescension, should conform himself to me: that his will, which is effective and almighty, should join mine, which is nothing but weakness, and that he should finally accomplish what I will, but what without him I will in vain. If, then, blinded by the spirit of the world, far from praying as a Christian, I pray with a view to satisfying my ambition, my pride, my resentment, my vengeance, what am I doing? I am asking God to agree with me in this: that is, to be vain like me, passionate like me, violent like me; and, for me who am his creature, to will what he cannot will without ceasing to be my God. Now to pray to him in this way, is it to pray to him as God? Is it not rather to dishonor him? Is it not, as far as lies in me, to make him serve my iniquities, as he himself complains through his Prophet: Yet you have made me serve your sins, and have burdened me with your iniquities. Notice this expression, And you have burdened me: as if he said to the sinner, Your prayer has been a cause of pain to me; for on the one hand I would have wished to favor your petitions, and on the other I could not answer them favorably. My heart was therefore under a kind of constraint, and as if divided between my holiness and my goodness; my goodness, which was concerned for you, and my holiness, which opposed you; my goodness, which inclined me to hear you, and my holiness, which obliged me to reject you: And you have burdened me with your iniquities. And certainly, Christians, if God, forgetting what he is, then had regard to our prayers, would this not be a scandal to us, and would we not ourselves begin to doubt his providence?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p010", "label": "French original", "text": "Je s\u00e7ais, & saint Jean nous l'apprend, que nous avons aupr\u00e8s du Pere un puissant Avocat, qui est le Fils, & que c'est par les m\u00e9rites de ce Fils adorable que nous prions. Mais ce que d'abord & en g\u00e9n\u00e9ral j'ai dit de Dieu, pour l'appliquer en particulier \u00e0 l'homme-Dieu, voulons-nous en faire le patron de cette aveugle concupiscence qui nous domine ? & si ce n'est pas l\u00e0 le sentiment que nous en avons, pourquoi comptons-nous sur ses m\u00e9rites, dans des pri\u00e8res que la seule concupiscence nous a inspir\u00e9es ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p010", "label": "English", "text": "I know, and Saint John teaches us, that we have a powerful Advocate with the Father, who is the Son, and that it is through the merits of this adorable Son that we pray. But, to apply particularly to the God-man what I first said of God in general, do we want to make him the patron of the blind concupiscence that rules us? And if this is not how we think of him, why do we count on his merits in prayers inspired in us by concupiscence alone?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p011", "label": "French original", "text": "Non, mes Freres, non ; ce n'est point pour un tel usage que Dieu dans la personne de Jesus-Christ nous a donn\u00e9 un m\u00e9diateur. Il est l'avocat des p\u00e9cheurs ; mais il ne le fut jamais & il ne le peut \u00eatre des p\u00e9ch\u00e9s : & vouloir me servir ainsi de son cr\u00e9dit, ce n'est rien moins dans la doctrine de S. Augustin, que de vouloir l'an\u00e9antir lui-m\u00eame. Comment cela ? parce qu'au lieu que la foi nous le repr\u00e9sente comme l'auteur des graces & des vertus, c'est en faire malgr\u00e9 lui le m\u00e9diateur de notre vanit\u00e9, le m\u00e9diateur de notre avarice, le m\u00e9diateur de notre concupiscence & de notre sensualit\u00e9. Car si vous en jugez autrement, reprend saint Augustin, auriez-vous l'ass\u00fbrance d'interposer le nom du R\u00e9dempteur, pour demander ce qui d\u00e9truit l'ouvrage de la R\u00e9demption ; & rempli de vos projets ambitieux, oseriez-vous prendre pour intercesseur aupr\u00e8s de Dieu, celui m\u00eame qui s'est r\u00e9duit dans la plus profonde humiliation pour vous enseigner l'humilit\u00e9 ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p011", "label": "English", "text": "No, my brothers, no: it is not for such a use that God has given us a mediator in the person of Jesus Christ. He is the advocate of sinners; but he never was, and cannot be, the advocate of sins. And to wish to use his influence in this way is nothing less, in Saint Augustine's teaching, than to wish to annihilate him himself. How so? Because, whereas faith represents him to us as the author of graces and virtues, this is to make him, against his will, the mediator of our vanity, the mediator of our avarice, the mediator of our concupiscence and our sensuality. For if you judge otherwise, Saint Augustine continues, would you have the audacity to invoke the Redeemer's name to ask for what destroys the work of Redemption? And, full of your ambitious projects, would you dare take as your intercessor before God the very one who reduced himself to the deepest humiliation to teach you humility?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p012", "label": "French original", "text": "Heureux encore, que Dieu pour votre salut, devienne inflexible \u00e0 votre pri\u00e8re. C'est dans cette rigueur apparente que vous devez reconno\u00eetre sa mis\u00e9ricorde ; & o\u00f9 en seriez-vous si c'\u00e9toit un Dieu plus indulgent & selon votre gr\u00e9 ? Ce qui a perdu les Pomp\u00e9es & les C\u00e9sars, ajo\u00fbtoit ce fameux Satirique, dont je n'ai pas fait difficult\u00e9 d'emprunter ici les pens\u00e9es, & qui semble n'avoir parl\u00e9 que pour nous m\u00eames : ce qui a renvers\u00e9 & ce qui renverse tous les jours des familles enti\u00e8res, ne sont-ce pas des souhaits trop vastes & sans bornes, des souhaits criminels, accomplis par des divinit\u00e9s d'autant plus mortellement & plus malignement ennemies, qu'elles \u00e9toient plus condescendantes & plus faciles ? Magnaque numinibus vota exaudita malignis. Et moi je dis pour consacrer ces paroles : quelle a \u00e9t\u00e9 la source de la r\u00e9probation de tant de Chr\u00e9tiens : n'est-ce pas d'avoir obtenu du ciel ce que le ciel ne leur accordoit, & ce qu'il ne pouvoit leur accorder que dans l'exc\u00e8s de sa col\u00e8re ? Et d'o\u00f9 vient encore la perte de tant de mondains qui se damnent au milieu de l'opulence & dans la mollesse ; si ce n'est pas de ces pr\u00e9tendues faveurs de Dieu, qui les exauce selon les d\u00e9sirs insens\u00e9s de leurs c\u0153urs, plut\u00f4t que selon les desseins de son aimable providence ? Vous demandez \u00e0 Dieu ce qui flatte votre passion ; & si Dieu vous le donne, lui qui pr\u00e9voit ce qui vous pervertira, ce qui vous corrompra, ce qui vous entra\u00eenera dans l'abysme, peut-il exercer sur vous un jugement plus rigoureux & une vengeance plus terrible ? N'en demeurons pas l\u00e0."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p012", "label": "English", "text": "It is fortunate, even, that God should become inflexible to your prayer for your salvation. It is in this apparent severity that you must recognize his mercy; and where would you be if he were a more indulgent God, one according to your liking? What ruined the Pompeys and the Caesars, added that famous satirist whose thoughts I have not hesitated to borrow here, and who seems to have spoken only for us\u2014what overthrew and daily overthrows entire families: is it not wishes too vast and boundless, criminal wishes fulfilled by divinities all the more fatally and maliciously hostile for being more accommodating and yielding? And great wishes granted by malignant divinities. And I say, to give these words a sacred application: what has been the source of the reprobation of so many Christians? Is it not to have obtained from heaven what heaven granted them, and could grant them, only in the excess of its anger? And whence comes the loss of so many worldly people who damn themselves amid opulence and softness, if not from these supposed favors of God, who grants their requests according to the senseless desires of their hearts rather than according to the designs of his loving providence? You ask God for what flatters your passion; and if God gives it to you, he who foresees what will pervert you, what will corrupt you, what will drag you into the abyss, can he exercise upon you a more rigorous judgment or a more terrible vengeance? Let us not stop there.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p013", "label": "French original", "text": "Si l'on ne demande pas toujours \u00e0 Dieu des choses pr\u00e9judiciables & dans des v\u00fbes directement contraires au salut, au moins lui demande-t-on des biens purement temporels & inutiles au salut. Je ne veux pas dire que les biens temporels ne soient pas des dons de Dieu, ni qu'ils soient absolument contraires au salut. Mais quand le sont-ils, & pourquoi Dieu les refuse-t-il alors ? quand nous ne les demandons, ni selon l'ordre qu'il a \u00e9tabli, ni par rapport \u00e0 la fin qu'il a marqu\u00e9e."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p013", "label": "English", "text": "If people do not always ask God for harmful things and with aims directly contrary to salvation, at least they ask him for purely temporal goods useless to salvation. I do not mean that temporal goods are not gifts of God, nor that they are absolutely contrary to salvation. But when are they so, and why does God then refuse them? When we ask for them neither according to the order he has established nor in relation to the end he has marked out.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p014", "label": "French original", "text": "Car premi\u00e8rement, on ne lui demande que les graces temporelles, qui toutes se terminent aux besoins de cette vie; & \u00e0 peine pense-t-on aux spirituelles, \u00e0 quoi le salut est attach\u00e9 : les avantages de la fortune, la prosp\u00e9rit\u00e9, le repos ; voil\u00e0 ce que nous d\u00e9sirons & ce que nous recherchons, & ce que d\u00e9sirent, ce que recherchent aussi-bien que nous les infid\u00e8les : Haec enim omnia gentes inquirunt. Ce sont des biens, je l'avoue : mais ce sont des biens p\u00e9rissables, des biens d'un ordre inf\u00e9rieur \u00e0 l'homme, & sur-tout \u00e0 l'homme Chr\u00e9tien, des biens dangereux & sujets \u00e0 se convertir en de vrais maux. Pour les biens solides & incorruptibles, c'est-\u00e0-dire, la puret\u00e9 des m\u0153urs, la bonne conscience, l'humilit\u00e9, la foi, l'amour du prochain, tout ce qui sert \u00e0 sanctifier l'ame & qui en fait la perfection, disons-le & confondons-nous en le disant, c'est \u00e0 quoi nous sommes peu sensibles, & ce qui rarement nous attire au pi\u00e9 des autels. Qui de vous a jamais eu recours \u00e0 Dieu, pour devenir plus mod\u00e9r\u00e9 dans ses passions, & plus r\u00e9gl\u00e9 dans sa conduite ? On visite les tombeaux des Martyrs, mais pourquoi ? pour \u00eatre gu\u00e9ri d'une maladie, & non point pour \u00eatre d\u00e9livr\u00e9 d'une tentation. On invoque les Saints, mais pourquoi ? pour \u00eatre plus heureux & plus opulent, & non point pour \u00eatre plus humble & plus ennemi des plaisirs. Ah ! mes Fr\u00e8res, s'\u00e9crioit Salvien, si nous sommes afflig\u00e9s de calamit\u00e9s publiques, si nous sommes menac\u00e9s d'une famine ou d'une contagion, s'il r\u00e9gne une mortalit\u00e9 parmi nous, nous courons en foule au temple du Dieu vivant ; tout retentit de nos g\u00e9missemens & de nos pri\u00e8res : mais s'agit-il d'un libertinage qui d\u00e9shonore le Christianisme & qui d\u00e9sole l'Eglise ? on nous voit tranquilles & sans inqui\u00e9tude ; & au lieu d'engager le ciel \u00e0 faire cesser de scandaleuses impi\u00e9t\u00e9s, nous vivons en paix & dans la plus affreuse indolence. Ainsi nous prions comme ce malheureux Antiochus, dont la pri\u00e8re int\u00e9ress\u00e9e ne put trouver grace devant Dieu; Orabat scelestus Dominum, \u00e0 quo non erat misericordiam consecuturus ; il prioit, Orabat, & l'on ne peut douter qu'il ne pri\u00e2t avec toute l'ardeur possible : mais il prioit en mondain, Orabat scelestus ; car il ne demandoit \u00e0 Dieu ni l'esprit de p\u00e9nitence, ni le don de pi\u00e9t\u00e9, ni le respect des choses saintes qu'il avoit prophan\u00e9es, mais une sant\u00e9 qu'il pr\u00e9f\u00e9roit \u00e0 tout le reste & dont il \u00e9toit idol\u00e2tre ; Orabat scelestus Dominum : & c'est pour cela que le sein de la mis\u00e9ricorde lui \u00e9toit ferm\u00e9, A quo non erat misericordiam consecuturus. Voil\u00e0 comment nous prions; mais envain, puisque le Fils de Dieu n'a jamais pr\u00e9tendu se faire garant de telles pri\u00e8res : pourquoi ? consultons l'Evangile ; il va nous l'apprendre."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p014", "label": "English", "text": "For, first, people ask him only for temporal graces, all of which end with the needs of this life; and they scarcely think of the spiritual graces on which salvation depends. The advantages of fortune, prosperity, repose: these are what we desire and seek, and what unbelievers desire and seek as well as we do: For the nations seek all these things. They are goods, I admit; but they are perishable goods, goods of an order inferior to man, and above all to the Christian man, dangerous goods liable to turn into real evils. As for solid and incorruptible goods\u2014that is, purity of conduct, a good conscience, humility, faith, love of one's neighbor, everything that serves to sanctify the soul and makes its perfection\u2014let us say it, and be ashamed as we say it: these are things to which we are little sensitive, and which rarely draw us to the foot of the altars. Which of you has ever had recourse to God to become more moderate in his passions and more orderly in his conduct? People visit the tombs of the martyrs; but why? To be cured of an illness, and not to be delivered from a temptation. They invoke the saints; but why? To be happier and wealthier, and not to be humbler and more hostile to pleasures. Ah, my brothers, Salvian exclaimed, if we are afflicted by public calamities, threatened with famine or contagion, if mortality prevails among us, we run in crowds to the temple of the living God; everything resounds with our groans and prayers. But is it a question of libertinism that dishonors Christianity and desolates the Church? We are seen calm and unconcerned; and instead of moving heaven to put an end to scandalous impieties, we live in peace and the most dreadful indifference. Thus we pray like that wretched Antiochus whose self-interested prayer could find no favor before God: The wicked man prayed to the Lord, from whom he was not to obtain mercy. He prayed\u2014He prayed\u2014and one cannot doubt that he prayed with all possible ardor; but he prayed as a worldly man\u2014The wicked man prayed\u2014for he asked God neither for the spirit of penance, nor the gift of piety, nor respect for the holy things he had profaned, but for health, which he preferred to everything else and idolized: The wicked man prayed to the Lord. And this is why the bosom of mercy was closed to him: From whom he was not to obtain mercy. This is how we pray; but in vain, since the Son of God never intended to make himself the guarantor of such prayers. Why? Let us consult the Gospel; it will teach us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p015", "label": "French original", "text": "Le Fils de Dieu dit \u00e0 ses disciples : Si vous demandez quelque chose \u00e0 mon Pere, & que ce soit en mon nom que vous le demandiez, il vous l'accordera : Si quid petieritis Patrem in nomine meo, dabit vobis. Mais remarquez, c'est la r\u00e9flexion de S. Augustin, remarquez bien cette parole, Si quid ; par o\u00f9 Jesus-Christ nous fait entendre que ce que nous demandons en son nom doit \u00eatre quelque chose, & quelque chose digne de lui, parce qu'autrement il ne lui conviendroit pas de s'employer pour nous. Or tous les biens de la terre s\u00e9par\u00e9s du salut \u00e9ternel, ne sont rien devant Dieu. Les demander donc pr\u00e9cis\u00e9ment \u00e0 Dieu, c'est ne rien demander; & quoique la promesse du Sauveur du monde soit g\u00e9n\u00e9rale ou semble l'\u00eatre, ils n'y sont point par eux-m\u00eames compris. Pour vous en convaincre, \u00e9coutez ce qu'il ajoute \u00e0 ses Ap\u00f4tres : Usque mod\u00f2 non petistis quidquam in nomine meo ; Jusques \u00e0 pr\u00e9sent vous n'avez rien demand\u00e9 en mon nom. Mais comment est-ce, reprend S. Augustin, que le Fils de Dieu leur pouvoit tenir ce langage, puisqu'il est \u00e9vident que les Ap\u00f4tres lui avoient d\u00e9ja demand\u00e9 plusieurs graces ? Saint Pierre, de demeurer sur le Thabor ; les enfans de Z\u00e9b\u00e9d\u00e9e, d'\u00eatre \u00e9lev\u00e9s aux deux premi\u00e8res places de son Royaume. Ah ! r\u00e9pond ce saint Docteur, il est vrai qu'ils lui avoient demand\u00e9 ces sortes de graces; mais parce que ces graces n'\u00e9toient que des avantages humains, & que dans l'id\u00e9e du Sauveur tous les avantages humains ne m\u00e9ritoient nulle estime, il croyoit avoir droit de compter pour rien tout ce qu'ils lui avoient demand\u00e9, Usque modo non petistis quidquam. En effet, demeurer avec lui sur le Thabor, ce n'\u00e9toit qu'une douceur sensible, que saint Pierre e\u00fbt voulu go\u00fbter : occuper les premi\u00e8res places de son Royaume, ce n'\u00e9toit dans l'intention des deux Disciples qu'un vain honneur dont se repaissoit leur ambition, parce qu'ils ne le concevoient pas tel qu'il est : mais le z\u00e8le des ames, mais la constance dans les pers\u00e9cutions, mais le renoncement \u00e0 eux-m\u00eames, c'\u00e9toient les graces essentielles dont ils avoient besoin, & qui devoient les soutenir, les animer, les perfectionner dans leur minist\u00e8re Apostolique, & c'est ce qu'ils n'avoient jamais demand\u00e9 \u00e0 leur ma\u00eetre : Usque mod\u00f2 non petistis quidquam. Or \u00e0 combien de Chr\u00e9tiens ne pourrois-je pas faire aujourd'hui la m\u00eame plainte ; & \u00e0 combien m\u00eame de ceux qui m'\u00e9coutent, n'aurois-je pas lieu de dire par la m\u00eame raison : Mondain, vous n'avez rien demand\u00e9 jusques \u00e0 pr\u00e9sent \u00e0 votre Dieu, parce que vous ne lui avez encore jamais demand\u00e9 le d\u00e9tachement & le m\u00e9pris du monde : p\u00e9cheur, vous ne lui avez rien demand\u00e9, parce que dans l'\u00e9tat de votre p\u00e9ch\u00e9 vous ne lui avez encore jamais demand\u00e9 votre conversion, jamais un c\u0153ur contrit & humili\u00e9, jamais la grace de vous surmonter vous-m\u00eame & de renoncer \u00e0 vos habitudes : c'\u00e9toient l\u00e0 n\u00e9anmoins les graces, mais les graces par excellence que vous deviez d\u00e9sirer & rechercher."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p015", "label": "English", "text": "The Son of God says to his disciples: If you ask my Father for anything, and ask it in my name, he will grant it to you: If you ask the Father for anything in my name, he will give it to you. But notice\u2014this is Saint Augustine's reflection\u2014notice carefully this word, Anything, by which Jesus Christ makes us understand that what we ask in his name must be something, and something worthy of him, because otherwise it would not be fitting for him to act on our behalf. Now all the goods of the earth, separated from eternal salvation, are nothing before God. To ask God for these alone is therefore to ask for nothing; and although the promise of the Savior of the world is general, or seems to be, they are not in themselves included in it. To convince yourselves, hear what he adds to his Apostles: Until now you have asked for nothing in my name; until now you have asked for nothing in my name. But how, Saint Augustine continues, could the Son of God speak to them in this way, since it is clear that the Apostles had already asked him for several graces? Saint Peter, to remain on Tabor; the sons of Zebedee, to be raised to the two first places in his Kingdom. Ah, answers this holy Doctor, it is true that they had asked him for these kinds of graces; but because these graces were only human advantages, and in the Savior's judgment all human advantages deserved no esteem, he believed he had a right to count as nothing everything they had asked him for: Until now you have asked for nothing. Indeed, to remain with him on Tabor was only a sweetness perceptible to the senses that Saint Peter would have wished to taste; to occupy the first places in his Kingdom was, in the intention of the two disciples, only a vain honor on which their ambition fed, because they did not conceive of it as it is. But zeal for souls, constancy in persecutions, self-renunciation: these were the essential graces they needed, which were to sustain, animate, and perfect them in their apostolic ministry, and these they had never asked of their master: Until now you have asked for nothing. Now of how many Christians could I not make the same complaint today? And to how many even of those listening to me would I not have reason to say on the same grounds: Worldly man, you have asked nothing of your God until now, because you have never yet asked him for detachment from and contempt for the world; sinner, you have asked him for nothing, because in your sinful state you have never yet asked him for your conversion, never for a contrite and humbled heart, never for the grace to overcome yourself and renounce your habits. These nevertheless were the graces, the graces above all, which you ought to have desired and sought.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p016", "label": "French original", "text": "De plus, quand le Sauveur du monde nous assure dans l'Evangile, que tout ce que nous demanderons en son nom, nous sera donn\u00e9, il entend que nous le demanderons selon la r\u00e9gle qu'il nous a lui-m\u00eame prescrite. Car comme remarque Tertullien, c'est lui-m\u00eame qui r\u00e9glant la pri\u00e8re & l'animant de son esprit, lui a communiqu\u00e9 le pouvoir sp\u00e9cial & le privil\u00e9ge qu'elle a de monter au plus haut des cieux, & de toucher le c\u0153ur de Dieu en lui exposant les mis\u00e9res des hommes : Ab ipso enim ordinata, & de ipsius spiritu animata jam tunc oratio, suo quasi privilegio ascendit in caelum, commendans patri quae filius docuit. Or quelle est cette r\u00e9gle divine selon laquelle le Fils de Dieu nous a ordonn\u00e9 de prier ? La voici : cherchez, nous dit-il, avant toutes choses le Royaume de Dieu & sa justice, & rien ne vous manquera. Demandez au Pere c\u00e9leste la sanctification de son nom, l'av\u00e9nement de son r\u00e9gne, l'accomplissement de sa volont\u00e9, sans lui demander d'abord ce pain mat\u00e9riel qui doit vous servir d'aliment, & alors je vous seconderai. Mais si vous renversez cet ordre ; si par un attachement au monde, indigne de votre profession, vous demandez le pain mat\u00e9riel avant le Royaume de Dieu, ne vous appuyez plus sur mes m\u00e9rites tout infinis qu'ils sont, puisque votre pri\u00e8re toute fervente qu'elle peut \u00eatre, n'est plus selon le plan que j'ai trac\u00e9. Quaerite prim\u00f9m regnum Dei & justitiam ejus."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p016", "label": "English", "text": "Moreover, when the Savior of the world assures us in the Gospel that whatever we ask in his name will be given us, he means that we shall ask according to the rule he himself has prescribed for us. For, as Tertullian observes, it is he himself who, by regulating prayer and animating it with his spirit, has communicated to it the special power and privilege it has of ascending to the highest heavens and touching God's heart by laying human miseries before him: For prayer, ordered by him and animated by his spirit, already then ascends into heaven by its own privilege, as it were, commending to the Father what the Son has taught. Now what is this divine rule according to which the Son of God has ordered us to pray? Here it is: seek, he tells us, before all things the Kingdom of God and his justice, and nothing will be lacking to you. Ask the heavenly Father for the sanctification of his name, the coming of his reign, the accomplishment of his will, without first asking him for the material bread that is to serve as your food, and then I shall support you. But if you reverse this order; if, through an attachment to the world unworthy of your profession, you ask for material bread before the Kingdom of God, no longer rely on my merits, infinite though they are, since your prayer, fervent though it may be, no longer follows the plan I have traced. Seek first the Kingdom of God and his justice.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p017", "label": "French original", "text": "Ce n'est donc pas, Chr\u00e9tiens, qu'on ne puisse absolument demander \u00e0 Dieu les biens temporels ; l'Eglise les demande elle-m\u00eame pour nous : mais demandons-les comme l'Eglise ; demandons-les apr\u00e8s avoir demand\u00e9 d'abord & sur toutes choses les biens spirituels; demandons la b\u00e9n\u00e9diction de Jacob & non point celle d'Esa\u00fc. Belle figure, que l'exemple de ces deux freres ! Ecoutez l'application que j'en fais \u00e0 mon sujet, & prenez garde : ils eurent tous deux dans leur partage la ros\u00e9e du ciel ; & tous deux ils eurent pareillement la graisse de la terre. En quoi furent-ils diff\u00e9rens, & quelle marque l'Ecriture donne-t-elle de l'\u00e9lection de Jacob & de la r\u00e9probation d'Esa\u00fc ? Ah ! Chr\u00e9tiens, c'est que dans la b\u00e9n\u00e9diction de Jacob, la ros\u00e9e du ciel fut exprim\u00e9e avant la graisse de la terre ; De rore caeli & de pinguedine terrae sit benedictio tua : au lieu que dans la b\u00e9n\u00e9diction d'Esa\u00fc, il est parl\u00e9 de la graisse de la terre avant la ros\u00e9e du ciel : Det tibi de pinguedine terrae & de rore caeli. Voil\u00e0 ce qui se passe encore parmi nous, & ce qui discerne les pri\u00e8res Chr\u00e9tiennes de celles qui ne le sont pas. Un juste & un homme du monde prient dans le m\u00eame temple & au m\u00eame autel ; mais l'un prie en juste, & l'autre en mondain. Comment cela ? Est-ce que l'un ne demande \u00e0 Dieu que les biens de la grace, & l'autre que les biens de la terre ? Non : car il se peut faire que le juste avec les biens de la grace demande encore quelquefois les biens de la fortune, comme le mondain ; & que le mondain avec les biens de la fortune demande aussi les biens de la grace, comme le juste. Mais le mondain conduit par l'esprit du monde, place les biens de la fortune devant les biens de la grace, De pinguedine terrae & de rore caeli : & le juste conduit par l'esprit de Dieu, donne la pr\u00e9f\u00e9rence aux biens de la grace sur les biens de la fortune, De rore caeli & de pinguedine terrae. Il dit \u00e0 Dieu : Seigneur, sanctifiez-moi ; rendez-moi chaste, charitable, mis\u00e9ricordieux, patient, De rore caeli ; & puis donnez-moi des biens de la terre, ce qui peut m'\u00eatre utile pour mon salut, & de pinguedine terrae. Mais l'homme du monde dit : Seigneur, faites-moi riche, grand, puissant, De pinguedine terrae ; & ne me refusez pas aussi les graces n\u00e9cessaires pour bien vivre dans le monde, & de rore caeli. Pri\u00e8re de r\u00e9prouv\u00e9. Quand nous prions de la sorte, faut-il s'\u00e9tonner si Dieu ne nous \u00e9coute pas ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p017", "label": "English", "text": "It is not, then, Christians, that temporal goods cannot be asked of God at all; the Church herself asks them for us. But let us ask for them as the Church does; let us ask for them after first and above all asking for spiritual goods; let us ask for Jacob's blessing, and not Esau's. What a beautiful figure the example of these two brothers is! Listen to the application I make of it to my subject, and pay attention: both had the dew of heaven as their portion, and both likewise had the fatness of the earth. In what did they differ, and what mark does Scripture give of Jacob's election and Esau's reprobation? Ah, Christians, it is that in Jacob's blessing the dew of heaven was expressed before the fatness of the earth: May your blessing be of the dew of heaven and of the fatness of the earth; whereas in Esau's blessing the fatness of the earth is spoken of before the dew of heaven: May he give you of the fatness of the earth and of the dew of heaven. This is what still takes place among us, and what distinguishes Christian prayers from those that are not. A just man and a man of the world pray in the same temple and at the same altar; but one prays as a just man, and the other as a worldly man. How so? Is it that one asks God only for the goods of grace, and the other only for the goods of the earth? No: for it may happen that the just man, together with the goods of grace, sometimes also asks for the goods of fortune, like the worldly man, and that the worldly man, together with the goods of fortune, also asks for the goods of grace, like the just man. But the worldly man, led by the spirit of the world, places the goods of fortune before the goods of grace: Of the fatness of the earth and of the dew of heaven; and the just man, led by the spirit of God, gives the goods of grace preference over the goods of fortune: Of the dew of heaven and of the fatness of the earth. He says to God: Lord, sanctify me; make me chaste, charitable, merciful, patient\u2014Of the dew of heaven\u2014and then give me earthly goods, what may be useful to me for my salvation\u2014And of the fatness of the earth. But the man of the world says: Lord, make me rich, great, powerful\u2014Of the fatness of the earth\u2014and do not also refuse me the graces necessary to live well in the world\u2014And of the dew of heaven. A reprobate's prayer. When we pray in this way, should we be surprised if God does not hear us?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p018", "label": "French original", "text": "Allons \u00e0 la source, & pour conno\u00eetre plus \u00e0 fond sur quoi l'importante v\u00e9rit\u00e9 que je vous pr\u00eache est \u00e9tablie, comprenez ce principe de S. Cyprien, que nos pri\u00e8res n'ont de vertu qu'autant qu'elles sont unies aux pri\u00e8res de Jesus-Christ. Car il n'y a que Jesus-Christ, de qui l'on puisse dire avec S. Paul, qu'il a \u00e9t\u00e9 exauc\u00e9 pour le respect d\u00fb \u00e0 sa personne : Exauditus est pro sua reverentia. Quand Dieu nous exauce, ce n'est point en v\u00fbe, ni de ce que nous sommes, ni de ce que nous m\u00e9ritons, puisque par nous-m\u00eames nous ne sommes rien, & que par nous-m\u00eames nous ne m\u00e9ritons rien ; mais il nous exauce en v\u00fbe de son Fils, & parce que son Fils a pri\u00e9 pour nous avant que nous fussions en \u00e9tat de prier nous-m\u00eames. Cela suppos\u00e9, comment Dieu pourroit-il agr\u00e9er des pri\u00e8res, o\u00f9 par pr\u00e9f\u00e9rence au salut nous lui demandons des biens temporels, puisqu'elles n'ont alors nulle conformit\u00e9, nulle liaison avec les pri\u00e8res de cet homme-Dieu qui s'est fait notre m\u00e9diateur ? qu'a-t-il demand\u00e9 pour nous ? vous le s\u00e7avez : que nous soyons unis par le lien de la charit\u00e9, Rogo, pater, ut sint unum ; que sans ostentation, sans d\u00e9guisement, nous soyons saints en esprit & en v\u00e9rit\u00e9 ; Pater, sanctifica eos in veritate ; que vivant au milieu du monde selon notre vocation & notre \u00e9tat, nous soyons assez attentifs sur nous-m\u00eames, & assez heureux pour nous pr\u00e9server de son iniquit\u00e9, Non rogo ut tollas eos de mundo, sed ut serves eos a malo. Mais que faisons-nous ? nous demandons \u00e0 Dieu des richesses, des honneurs, une vaine r\u00e9putation, une vie commode ; & sans les demander apr\u00e8s le salut & par rapport au salut, nous ne les demandons ces richesses, que pour \u00eatre dans l'abondance ; ces honneurs, que pour \u00eatre dans l'\u00e9clat ; cette r\u00e9putation, que pour \u00eatre connus & distingu\u00e9s; cette vie commode, que pour en jouir : c'est-\u00e0-dire, que nous demandons ce que Jesus-Christ n'a jamais demand\u00e9 pour nous. Et pourquoi ne l'a-t-il jamais demand\u00e9 ? appliquez-vous \u00e0 ceci : parce qu'il n'a p\u00fb prier, ajo\u00fbte saint Cyprien, que conform\u00e9ment \u00e0 la fin pour laquelle il \u00e9toit envoy\u00e9. Or il \u00e9toit envoy\u00e9 en qualit\u00e9 de Sauveur ; & la mission qu'il avoit re\u00e7\u00fbe, ne regardoit que le salut de l'homme. C'est donc uniquement pour le salut de l'homme qu'il a d\u00fb travailler, qu'il a d\u00fb souffrir, qu'il a d\u00fb m\u00e9riter ; & par une cons\u00e9quence n\u00e9cessaire, c'est uniquement pour le salut de l'homme & pour tout ce qui se rapporte au salut de l'homme, qu'il a d\u00fb prier."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p018", "label": "English", "text": "Let us go to the source; and, to know more deeply what establishes the important truth I preach to you, understand this principle of Saint Cyprian: our prayers have power only insofar as they are united with the prayers of Jesus Christ. For it is of Jesus Christ alone that one can say with Saint Paul that he was heard on account of the reverence due to his person: He was heard for the reverence due to him. When God hears us, it is not in consideration of what we are or what we merit, since of ourselves we are nothing and of ourselves we merit nothing; but he hears us in consideration of his Son, and because his Son prayed for us before we were in a condition to pray ourselves. This granted, how could God accept prayers in which we ask him for temporal goods in preference to salvation, since they then have no conformity, no connection with the prayers of this God-man who has made himself our mediator? What did he ask for us? You know it: that we should be united by the bond of charity\u2014I ask, Father, that they may be one; that without ostentation or disguise we should be holy in spirit and truth\u2014Father, sanctify them in truth; that, living amid the world according to our calling and station, we should be sufficiently attentive to ourselves and fortunate enough to preserve ourselves from its iniquity\u2014I do not ask that you take them out of the world, but that you keep them from evil. But what do we do? We ask God for riches, honors, a vain reputation, a comfortable life; and, without asking for them after salvation and in relation to salvation, we ask for these riches only to be in abundance; these honors only to shine; this reputation only to be known and distinguished; this comfortable life only to enjoy it. That is, we ask for what Jesus Christ never asked for us. And why did he never ask it? Attend to this: because, Saint Cyprian adds, he could pray only in conformity with the end for which he was sent. Now he was sent as Savior; and the mission he had received concerned only man's salvation. It was therefore solely for man's salvation that he had to work, suffer, and merit; and, by a necessary consequence, it was solely for man's salvation and everything related to man's salvation that he had to pray.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p019", "label": "French original", "text": "De-l\u00e0, Chr\u00e9tiens, vous demandez, mais vous n'obtenez rien ; parce que vous ne demandez pas avec Jesus-Christ, & que vous pourriez dire, si vos pri\u00e8res ind\u00e9pendamment de cette union, \u00e9toient efficaces, que vous avez re\u00e7\u00fb des biens sans en \u00eatre redevables \u00e0 ce Dieu Sauveur : ce qui dans les maximes de la religion que nous professons, est un blasph\u00eame. Et voil\u00e0 sur quoi s'appuye saint Augustin, quand il prouve si solidement, que l'esp\u00e9rance Chr\u00e9tienne n'a point pour objet les biens de cette vie. Non, disoit ce saint Docteur, ne vous y trompez pas, & que personne de vous ne se promette une f\u00e9licit\u00e9 temporelle parce qu'il a l'honneur d'appartenir \u00e0 Jesus-Christ : Nemo sibi promittat felicitatem hujus mundi quia Christianus est. Ce n'est point pour cela que Jesus-Christ nous a choisis, ni \u00e0 cette condition qu'il nous a appell\u00e9s. Il peut sans manquer \u00e0 sa parole, nous laisser dans la pauvret\u00e9, dans l'abbaissement, dans la souffrance. Il s'est engag\u00e9 \u00e0 pr\u00e9senter lui-m\u00eame vos pri\u00e8res devant le Tr\u00f4ne de Dieu ; mais il a suppos\u00e9 que vous prieriez en Chr\u00e9tiens, & pour le ciel, o\u00f9 il a plac\u00e9 votre h\u00e9ritage. Excellente oraison dont se servoit encore le m\u00eame Pere contre les railleries des Payens. Vous nous reprochez, leur r\u00e9pondoit-il, que malgr\u00e9 nos pri\u00e8res nous vivons dans la disette & dans l'abandon de toutes choses. Mais pour nous justifier pleinement de ce reproche aussi-bien que notre Dieu, il suffit de vous dire que quand nous le prions, ce n'est point pr\u00e9cis\u00e9ment pour les biens de la terre, mais pour les biens de l'\u00e9ternit\u00e9. Si donc nous sommes pauvres en ce monde, non-seulement cet \u00e9tat pauvre o\u00f9 nous vivons, n'est point une preuve de l'inutilit\u00e9 de nos pri\u00e8res, mais c'est une assurance que le fruit nous en est r\u00e9serv\u00e9 ailleurs & dans une vie immortelle."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p019", "label": "English", "text": "Hence, Christians, you ask but obtain nothing, because you do not ask with Jesus Christ; and if your prayers were effective independently of this union, you could say that you had received goods without owing them to this saving God\u2014which, by the principles of the religion we profess, is blasphemy. And this is what Saint Augustine relies on when he proves so solidly that Christian hope does not have the goods of this life as its object. No, said this holy Doctor, do not deceive yourselves, and let none of you promise himself temporal happiness because he has the honor of belonging to Jesus Christ: Let no one promise himself the happiness of this world because he is a Christian. It is not for this that Jesus Christ chose us, nor on this condition that he called us. Without failing in his word, he can leave us in poverty, abasement, suffering. He has pledged himself to present your prayers before God's throne; but he has supposed that you would pray as Christians, and for heaven, where he has placed your inheritance. An excellent prayer which the same Father also used against the pagans' mockery. You reproach us, he answered them, that despite our prayers we live in want and deprived of everything. But to justify both ourselves and our God fully against this reproach, it is enough to tell you that when we pray to him, it is not precisely for earthly goods, but for the goods of eternity. If, then, we are poor in this world, not only is the poor state in which we live no proof of the uselessness of our prayers, but it is an assurance that their fruit is reserved for us elsewhere, in an immortal life.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p020", "label": "French original", "text": "Telle \u00e9toit la reponse de saint Augustin, qu'il concluoit par la pens\u00e9e la plus touchante. Car c'est en cela, poursuivoit-il, que nous devons admirer la lib\u00e9ralit\u00e9 de notre Dieu. Il ne borne pas ses faveurs \u00e0 des biens temporels, parce que ce sont des biens au-dessous de nous, parce que ce sont des biens incapables de nous satisfaire, parce que ce sont des biens trop peu proportionn\u00e9s, & \u00e0 la noblesse de notre \u00eatre, & \u00e0 la valeur de nos pri\u00e8res. Il ne veut pas nous traiter comme des enfans, que l'on amuse par des bagatelles. Il ne veut pas nous traiter comme les idol\u00e2tres, dont il r\u00e9compense dans cette vie les vertus morales par un bonheur apparent. Mais il veut \u00eatre lui-m\u00eame tout notre bonheur, lui-m\u00eame toute notre r\u00e9compense. Ah ! mes Freres, ne prenons donc pas le change dans le choix des biens que nous demandons. Tenons nous-en \u00e0 la parole de notre Dieu, qui nous a promis de se donner \u00e0 nous ; & pour l'engager \u00e0 s'y tenir lui-m\u00eame, ne lui demandons que lui-m\u00eame. Il y en a plusieurs qui esp\u00e9rent en Dieu, mais qui sans nul \u00e9gard \u00e0 Dieu esp\u00e9rent toute autre chose que Dieu ; Multi de Deo sperant, sed non Deum. Gardons-nous de faire une s\u00e9paration si d\u00e9savantageuse pour nous ; & comme nous n'esp\u00e9rons rien que de Dieu, n'esp\u00e9rons rien aussi que Dieu ou que par rapport \u00e0 Dieu : A Deo alia petunt praeter Deum ; tu ipsum Deum pete."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p020", "label": "English", "text": "Such was Saint Augustine's answer, which he concluded with the most touching thought. For it is in this, he continued, that we must admire our God's liberality. He does not limit his favors to temporal goods, because they are goods beneath us, goods incapable of satisfying us, goods too little proportioned both to the nobility of our being and to the value of our prayers. He does not wish to treat us like children who are amused with trifles. He does not wish to treat us like the idolaters whose moral virtues he rewards in this life with an apparent happiness. But he wishes himself to be all our happiness, himself all our reward. Ah, my brothers, let us not mistake one thing for another in choosing the goods we ask for. Let us hold to the word of our God, who has promised to give himself to us; and, to engage him to hold to it himself, let us ask him only for himself. There are many who hope in God, but who, without any regard for God, hope for anything but God: Many hope from God, but not for God. Let us beware of making a separation so disadvantageous to us; and as we hope for nothing except from God, let us also hope for nothing except God, or in relation to God: They ask of God other things besides God; you, ask for God himself.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p021", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais ce ne sont point en effet des graces temporelles que je demande \u00e0 Dieu. Ce sont des graces surnaturelles, des graces de salut; & cependant je ne les ai pas. Non, mon cher Auditeur, vous ne les avez pas, parce que sur cela m\u00eame vous faites un troisi\u00e9me abus de la pri\u00e8re, dont vous ne vous appercevez pas peut-\u00eatre, & que je vais vous d\u00e9couvrir."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p021", "label": "English", "text": "But in fact it is not temporal graces I ask of God. They are supernatural graces, graces of salvation; and yet I do not have them. No, my dear listener, you do not have them, because on this very point you make a third abuse of prayer, of which perhaps you are unaware, and which I shall reveal to you.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p022", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est qu'au lieu d'envisager la pri\u00e8re comme l'instrument que Dieu nous a mis en main pour faire descendre sur nous les v\u00e9ritables graces du salut, c'est-\u00e0-dire, les graces r\u00e9elles & possibles, les graces solides & n\u00e9cessaires, les graces r\u00e9gl\u00e9es & mesur\u00e9es selon l'ordre des d\u00e9crets divins ; nous nous en servons pour demander des graces chim\u00e9riques, des graces superflues, des graces selon notre go\u00fbt & selon nos fausses id\u00e9es. Je m'explique. Nous prions, & nous prions, \u00e0 ce qu'il nous semble, dans un vrai d\u00e9sir de parvenir au salut : mais par une confiance aveugle, nous faisons fond sur la pri\u00e8re comme si la pri\u00e8re suffisoit sans les \u0153uvres ; comme si tout le salut rouloit sur la pri\u00e8re ; comme si Jesus-Christ en nous disant, Priez, ne nous avoit pas dit au m\u00eame tems, Veillez & agissez ; comme s'il y avoit des graces qui pussent & qui dussent nous sauver sans nous. Nous prions & nous demandons la grace d'une bonne mort, persuad\u00e9s que c'est assez de la demander sans se mettre en peine de la m\u00e9riter, & sans s'y pr\u00e9parer par une bonne vie. Nous prions & nous demandons des graces de p\u00e9nitence, des graces de sanctification : mais des graces pour l'avenir, & non pour le pr\u00e9sent; mais des graces qui l\u00e8vent toutes les difficult\u00e9s, & non qui nous laissent des efforts \u00e0 faire & des obstacles \u00e0 vaincre ; mais des graces miraculeuses qui nous entra\u00eenent comme saint Paul, & non des graces qui nous disposent peu \u00e0 peu & avec lesquelles nous soyons oblig\u00e9s de marcher ; mais des graces qui nous suivent par-tout, qui nous soient assur\u00e9es par-tout, qui nous permettent de nous exposer par-tout, & non des graces que nous ayons soin de m\u00e9nager : c'est-\u00e0-dire, que nous demandons des graces qui changent tout l'ordre de la providence, & qui renversent toute l'\u0153conomie de notre salut."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p022", "label": "English", "text": "It is that, instead of regarding prayer as the instrument God has put into our hands to draw down upon us the true graces of salvation\u2014that is, real and possible graces, solid and necessary graces, graces regulated and measured according to the order of the divine decrees\u2014we use it to ask for imaginary graces, superfluous graces, graces according to our taste and our false ideas. Let me explain. We pray, and we pray, as it seems to us, with a true desire to attain salvation; but through a blind confidence we rely on prayer as if prayer were sufficient without works; as if all salvation rested on prayer; as if Jesus Christ, in saying to us, Pray, had not said at the same time, Watch and act; as if there were graces that could and should save us without us. We pray and ask for the grace of a good death, persuaded that it is enough to ask for it without troubling to merit it, and without preparing for it by a good life. We pray and ask for graces of penance, graces of sanctification\u2014but graces for the future and not for the present; graces that remove all difficulties and not ones that leave us efforts to make and obstacles to overcome; miraculous graces that carry us away like Saint Paul, and not graces that dispose us little by little and with which we are obliged to walk; graces that follow us everywhere, that are assured to us everywhere, that allow us to expose ourselves everywhere, and not graces we take care to preserve. That is, we ask for graces that change the whole order of providence and overturn the whole economy of our salvation.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p023", "label": "French original", "text": "Concluons, Chr\u00e9tiens, cette premi\u00e8re partie, par la pri\u00e8re du Proph\u00e9te. Unam petii \u00e0 Domino : je ne demande plus proprement au Seigneur qu'une seule chose : Hanc requiram; c'est ce que je dois uniquement rechercher. Et quoi ? Ut inhabitem in domo Domini : de demeurer dans sa sainte maison, & de le poss\u00e9der \u00e9ternellement dans sa gloire : car je le reconnois, \u00f4 mon Dieu, ajo\u00fbte S. Augustin, & je vois bien maintenant pourquoi vous avez si souvent rejett\u00e9 les pri\u00e8res de votre serviteur. C'est que pour r\u00e9pondre aux desseins de votre mis\u00e9ricorde, je devois vous demander des choses qui ne me fussent pas communes avec les payens & les impies : Ea quippe a te desiderare debui, quae mihi cum impiis non essent communia. Vous vouliez que mes pri\u00e8res me distinguassent des ennemis de votre nom ; cependant je trouve qu'entre leurs pri\u00e8res & les miennes il n'y a presque point eu jusqu'\u00e0 pr\u00e9sent de diff\u00e9rence, sinon qu'ayant demand\u00e9 comme eux des faveurs temporelles, ils les ont commun\u00e9ment obtenues, & que vous me les avez ordinairement refus\u00e9es, ou parce qu'elles \u00e9toient par elles-m\u00eames contraires \u00e0 mon salut, ou parce que je ne les demandois pas pour mon salut. Mais en cela, Seigneur, je confesse encore que vous m'avez fait grace, parce que ces faveurs temporelles que je vous demandois, auroient achev\u00e9 de me pervertir, au lieu que les fl\u00e9aux de votre justice ont servi \u00e0 me corriger. En devenant heureux dans le monde, je vous aurois plus ais\u00e9ment oubli\u00e9. J'aurois imit\u00e9 l'exemple des autres, si mes v\u0153ux eussent \u00e9t\u00e9 suivis de la m\u00eame prosp\u00e9rit\u00e9. Ainsi, mon Dieu, bien loin de me plaindre de vos refus, je vous en b\u00e9nis, & je compte pour un bienfait de ne m'avoir pas exauc\u00e9 selon mes d\u00e9sirs, mais selon l'ordre de votre sagesse & pour mon salut : Et gaudeo quod non exaudieris ad voluntatem, ut exaudires ad salutem. Mais maintenant, mon Dieu, vous \u00e9couterez mes demandes, parce que je ne veux plus vous demander que les biens \u00e9ternels ; parce que si je vous en demande d'autres, je ne veux plus vous les demander, que par subordination & par rapport aux biens \u00e9ternels ; parce qu'entre les graces du salut que je vous demanderai, je ne veux plus vous demander que celles qui me doivent \u00eatre utiles, que celles qui peuvent plus s\u00fbrement, plus directement me conduire aux biens \u00e9ternels. Ainsi, Chr\u00e9tiens, la parole de Jesus-Christ s'accomplira-t-elle \u00e0 notre \u00e9gard : nous demanderons & nous recevrons. Au lieu que nous ne recevons pas, ou parce que nous ne demandons pas ce qu'il faut, \u00e7'a \u00e9t\u00e9 la premi\u00e8re partie ; ou parce que nous ne demandons pas comme il faut, c'est la seconde."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p023", "label": "English", "text": "Let us conclude this first part, Christians, with the Prophet's prayer. One thing I have asked of the Lord: properly speaking, I now ask the Lord for only one thing. This I shall seek: it is what alone I must seek. And what? That I may dwell in the house of the Lord: to remain in his holy house, and possess him eternally in his glory. For I acknowledge it, O my God, Saint Augustine adds, and I now see clearly why you have so often rejected the prayers of your servant. It is that, to respond to the designs of your mercy, I ought to have asked you for things I did not have in common with pagans and the impious: For I ought to have desired from you things I would not share with the impious. You wanted my prayers to distinguish me from the enemies of your name; yet I find that until now there has been almost no difference between their prayers and mine, except that, having asked like them for temporal favors, they have commonly obtained them, and you have ordinarily refused them to me, either because they were in themselves contrary to my salvation or because I did not ask for them for my salvation. But in this, Lord, I confess again that you have shown me grace, because those temporal favors I asked you for would have completed my corruption, whereas the scourges of your justice have served to correct me. Becoming happy in the world, I would more easily have forgotten you. I would have imitated the example of others if my wishes had been followed by the same prosperity. Thus, my God, far from complaining of your refusals, I bless you for them, and count it a benefit that you have not heard me according to my desires, but according to the order of your wisdom and for my salvation: And I rejoice that you did not hear according to my will, so that you might hear for salvation. But now, my God, you will hear my requests, because I no longer wish to ask you for anything but eternal goods; because if I ask you for others, I no longer wish to ask them except in subordination and relation to eternal goods; because among the graces of salvation I shall ask you for, I no longer wish to ask you for any but those that must be useful to me, those that can lead me more surely and more directly to eternal goods. Thus, Christians, the word of Jesus Christ will be fulfilled in our regard: we shall ask and receive. Whereas we do not receive either because we do not ask for what we ought\u2014this has been the first part\u2014or because we do not ask as we ought: this is the second.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-h003", "label": "French original", "text": "II. Partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-h003", "label": "English", "text": "PART TWO.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p024", "label": "French original", "text": "Si Dieu veut \u00e9couter nos pri\u00e8res, c'est \u00e0 certaines conditions n\u00e9cessaires & essentielles : mais de quelque mani\u00e8re, Chr\u00e9tiens, que Dieu en use avec nous, & qu'il ait pl\u00fb \u00e0 sa providence de disposer les choses, ce seroit une erreur, & une grossi\u00e9re erreur, de se persuader que les conditions de la pri\u00e8re fussent un obstacle \u00e0 l'accomplissement de nos v\u0153ux, & un pr\u00e9texte dont Dieu se serv\u00eet pour avoir droit de nous refuser ses dons. Ah ! mes Freres, disoit S. Augustin, \u00e0 Dieu ne plaise que nous entrions jamais dans ce sentiment, puisqu'il n'est rien de plus oppos\u00e9 \u00e0 la conduite de notre Dieu. Lui qui, selon l'Ecriture, ne peut arr\u00eater le cours de ses mis\u00e9ricordes, lors m\u00eame que nous irritons sa col\u00e8re ; Numquid continebit in ira sua misericordias suas : lui qui n'attend pas qu'on le prie, mais qui dans la pens\u00e9e du Proph\u00e8te Royal se pla\u00eet \u00e0 exaucer les simples d\u00e9sirs ; Desiderium pauperum exaudivit Dominus : lui dont l'oreille est si d\u00e9licate, qu'il entend jusqu'\u00e0 la pr\u00e9paration des c\u0153urs ; Praeparationem cordis eorum audivit auris tua : il n'a garde, si j'ose parler ainsi, d'\u00eatre de si difficile composition quand on l'invoque de bonne foi ; & bien loin qu'il se pr\u00e9vale de sa grandeur, dans le commerce qu'il nous permet d'avoir avec lui par la pri\u00e8re ; on pourroit plut\u00f4t douter, s'il ne s'y rel\u00e2che point trop de ce qui lui est d\u00fb, & s'il ne supporte point avec trop de condescendance nos foiblesses & nos imperfections. J'avoue que la pri\u00e8re pour \u00eatre efficace, doit \u00eatre rev\u00eatue de certaines qualit\u00e9s : mais en cela je soutiens, qu'on ne peut accuser Dieu, ni de restreindre ses promesses, ni d'ench\u00e9rir ses gr\u00e2ces. Pourquoi ? parce qu'\u00e0 bien examiner ces qualit\u00e9s, il n'y en a aucune, qui ne soit ais\u00e9e dans la pratique, aucune dont la raison ne nous justifie la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9, aucune que les hommes m\u00eames n'exigent par proportion les uns des autres ; & ce que je vous ai d\u00e9j\u00e0 fait remarquer, aucune dont cette femme de notre Evangile ne nous ait donn\u00e9 l'exemple, & dont elle ne soit pour nous le plus sensible mod\u00e8le."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p024", "label": "English", "text": "If God is willing to hear our prayers, it is on certain necessary and essential conditions. But however God deals with us, Christians, and however his providence has been pleased to arrange things, it would be an error, and a gross error, to imagine that the conditions of prayer were an obstacle to the fulfillment of our wishes, and a pretext God used to give himself a right to refuse us his gifts. Ah, my brothers, said Saint Augustine, God forbid that we should ever entertain this thought, since nothing is more opposed to the way our God acts. He who, according to Scripture, cannot stop the course of his mercies even when we provoke his anger\u2014Will he withhold his mercies in his anger?\u2014he who does not wait for us to pray to him, but who, in the thought of the royal Prophet, delights in granting mere desires\u2014The Lord has heard the desire of the poor\u2014he whose ear is so sensitive that it hears even the preparation of hearts\u2014Your ear has heard the preparation of their heart\u2014takes care, if I may speak so, not to be so difficult to deal with when he is invoked in good faith. Far from making much of his greatness in the dealings he allows us to have with him through prayer, one might rather wonder whether he does not relax too much of what is due to him, and bear our weaknesses and imperfections with too much condescension. I admit that prayer, to be effective, must possess certain qualities; but in this I maintain that God cannot be accused either of restricting his promises or of making his graces more costly. Why? Because, on examining these qualities closely, there is none that is not easy in practice, none whose necessity reason does not justify to us, none that people themselves do not proportionately require of one another; and, as I have already pointed out to you, none of which this woman in our Gospel has not given us the example, and of which she is not the most striking model for us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p025", "label": "French original", "text": "Car enfin, demande saint Chrysostome dans l'excellente hom\u00e9lie qu'il a compos\u00e9e sur ce sujet, quelles conditions exige notre Dieu pour l'infaillibilit\u00e9 de la pri\u00e8re ? l'humilit\u00e9, la confiance, la pers\u00e9v\u00e9rance, l'attention de l'esprit, l'affection du c\u0153ur. Or y a t-il rien l\u00e0, je ne dis pas d'impratiquable & d'impossible, mais de p\u00e9nible & d'on\u00e9reux ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p025", "label": "English", "text": "For, asks Saint Chrysostom in the excellent homily he composed on this subject, what conditions does our God require for the unfailing efficacy of prayer? Humility, confidence, perseverance, attention of mind, affection of heart. Now is there anything in this\u2014I do not say impracticable and impossible, but painful and burdensome?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p026", "label": "French original", "text": "Prier dans la disposition d'un esprit humble, quoi de plus raisonnable & m\u00eame de plus naturel ? Peut-on avoir une juste id\u00e9e de la pri\u00e8re, & oublier en priant cette r\u00e9gle fondamentale ? Prie-t-on autrement les Princes & les Monarques de la terre ? Se fait on une peine de leur rendre des hommages & des respects, lorsqu'on a des requ\u00eates \u00e0 leur pr\u00e9senter ? & si par ces respects & par ces hommages on vient \u00e0 bout de ses pr\u00e9tentions, se plaint-on qu'il en ait trop co\u00fbt\u00e9 ? Dit-on qu'ils fassent acheter trop cher leurs gr\u00e2ces, quand ils les refusent \u00e0 un t\u00e9m\u00e9raire, qui les demande avec hauteur ? & pourquoi le diroit-on de Dieu, devant qui il est d'ailleurs bien plus raisonnable, & par cons\u00e9quent bien plus facile de s'humilier que devant les hommes ? La Chanan\u00e9enne dont parle saint Matthieu, fit-elle difficult\u00e9 de se prosterner en la pr\u00e9sence de Jesus-Christ & de l'adorer ? Fut-ce un grand effort pour elle de confesser \u00e0 ses pi\u00e9s son indignit\u00e9 ? & compta-t-elle pour beaucoup d'essuyer les rebuts auxquels elle se vit d'abord expos\u00e9e ? Non non, lui dit le Sauveur du monde, il ne faut pas donner le pain des enfans aux chiens ; Non est bonum sumere panem filiorum, & mittere canibus. Est-il une comparaison plus humiliante ? mais toute humiliante qu'elle p\u00fbt \u00eatre, cette Chanan\u00e9enne en parut-elle touch\u00e9e & contrist\u00e9e ? que dis-je ? ne reconnut elle pas elle-m\u00eame la v\u00e9rit\u00e9 de ces paroles en se les appliquant ? Il est vrai, Seigneur ; Etiam, Domine. Ce fut ainsi qu'elle pria : mais comment prions-nous ? Elle \u00e9toit payenne, & cette payenne s'humilie ; nous sommes Chr\u00e9tiens, & nous apportons \u00e0 la pri\u00e8re un esprit d'orgueil, dont nous ne pouvons nous d\u00e9faire, lors m\u00eame que nous sommes forc\u00e9s \u00e0 reconno\u00eetre nos mis\u00e9res & nos besoins : & parce que cet esprit nous domine, nous prions avec pr\u00e9somption, comme si Dieu devoit avoir des \u00e9gards pour nous, comme s'il devoit nous distinguer, comme s'il devoit nous tenir compte de nos pri\u00e8res. Sans parler de ce faste ext\u00e9rieur qui souvent accompagne nos sacrifices, & qui bien loin d'engager Dieu \u00e0 nous \u00e9couter, l'engage \u00e0 nous punir : sans parler de ce luxe que nous portons jusques dans le sanctuaire, de cet air de grandeur & de suffisance que nous y retenons, de ces postures vaines & n\u00e9glig\u00e9es, que nous y affectons ; \u00e9tats bien contraires \u00e0 l'action d'un suppliant, & qui selon l'Ecriture rendent nos pri\u00e8res abominables devant Dieu, puisque Dieu ne hait rien davantage qu'un pauvre orgueilleux, Pauperem superbum : sans en venir \u00e0 ce d\u00e9tail, nous demandons \u00e0 Dieu des gr\u00e2ces, mais comment ? non point comme des gr\u00e2ces, mais comme des dettes, pr\u00eats \u00e0 nous \u00e9lever & \u00e0 nous enfler s'il nous les accorde, pr\u00eats \u00e0 murmurer & \u00e0 nous plaindre s'il ne nous les accorde pas. Nous les demandons pour oublier, apr\u00e8s les avoir re\u00e7ues, que nous les tenons de lui, pour les poss\u00e9der & en user sans les rapporter \u00e0 lui. Or devons-nous \u00eatre surpris alors, que Dieu nous ferme son sein ? voulons-nous qu'il nous exauce aux d\u00e9pens de sa propre gloire ? & ne seroit-ce pas prodiguer ses biens, que de les r\u00e9pandre indiff\u00e9remment & sur les superbes & sur les humbles ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p026", "label": "English", "text": "To pray in the disposition of a humble spirit: what could be more reasonable, or even more natural? Can one have a true idea of prayer and forget this fundamental rule while praying? Do we petition the princes and monarchs of the earth otherwise? Do we find it difficult to pay them homage and respect when we have requests to present to them? And if by this respect and homage we succeed in our claims, do we complain that it has cost us too much? Do we say that they make us buy their favors too dearly, when they refuse them to a rash person who asks for them haughtily? And why should we say this of God, before whom it is in any case much more reasonable, and consequently much easier, to humble oneself than before men? Did the Canaanite woman of whom Saint Matthew speaks hesitate to prostrate herself in the presence of Jesus Christ and adore him? Was it a great effort for her to confess her unworthiness at his feet? And did she think it much to endure the rebuffs to which she at first found herself exposed? No, no, the Savior of the world said to her, the children's bread must not be given to the dogs: It is not good to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs. Is there a more humiliating comparison? Yet, however humiliating it might be, did this Canaanite woman appear wounded and distressed by it? What am I saying? Did she not herself acknowledge the truth of these words by applying them to herself? It is true, Lord: Yes, Lord. Thus she prayed; but how do we pray? She was a pagan, and this pagan woman humbles herself; we are Christians, and we bring to prayer a spirit of pride from which we cannot free ourselves even when we are forced to recognize our miseries and our needs. And because this spirit rules us, we pray presumptuously, as if God owed us consideration, as if he ought to single us out, as if he ought to give us credit for our prayers. Without speaking of the outward pomp that often accompanies our sacrifices, and which, far from moving God to hear us, moves him to punish us; without speaking of the luxury we carry even into the sanctuary, of the air of grandeur and self-sufficiency we retain there, of those vain and careless postures we affect, so contrary to the action of a suppliant, and which, according to Scripture, make our prayers abominable before God, since God hates nothing more than a proud poor man\u2014a proud poor man\u2014without entering into these details, we ask God for graces; but how? Not as graces, but as debts, ready to exalt and puff ourselves up if he grants them to us, ready to murmur and complain if he does not. We ask for them in order, once we have received them, to forget that we hold them from him, to possess and use them without referring them to him. Should we then be surprised that God closes his bosom to us? Do we want him to grant our requests at the expense of his own glory? And would it not be to squander his gifts to pour them out indiscriminately on the proud and the humble alike?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p027", "label": "French original", "text": "Prier dans le sentiment d'une vive confiance, quoi de plus juste ? C'est notre Souverain & notre Dieu, qui par un effet de sa mis\u00e9ricorde, non-seulement veut \u00eatre pri\u00e9 de la sorte, mais se tient m\u00eame honor\u00e9 de cette confiance ; qui dans mille endroits de l'Ecriture lui attribue plut\u00f4t qu'\u00e0 sa mis\u00e9ricorde (ne vous offensez pas de ma proposition, elle est saine & orthodoxe) qui, dis-je, en mille endroits de l'Ecriture, attribue \u00e0 cette confiance, plut\u00f4t qu'\u00e0 sa mis\u00e9ricorde m\u00eame, la vertu miraculeuse de la pri\u00e8re ; ne disant pas \u00e0 ceux qui ont recours \u00e0 lui, & qui le reclament, C'est ma bont\u00e9 & ma puissance ; mais, C'est votre foi & votre confiance qui vous a sauv\u00e9s, Fides tua te salvum facit. Pouvoit-il nous proposer un parti plus avantageux ? Toute infid\u00e9le qu'\u00e9toit la Chanan\u00e9enne, n'est-ce pas celui qu'elle embrassa d'abord ? Cette ouverture de c\u0153ur qu'elle marqua \u00e0 Jesus-Christ, en lui portant elle-m\u00eame la parole, Seigneur, ayez piti\u00e9 de moi, Miserere me\u00ee, Domine : ce motif tendre & affectueux par o\u00f9 elle l'int\u00e9ressa, en l'appellant Fils de David, Fili David : ces cris qu'elle redoubla \u00e0 mesure que les Ap\u00f4tres la reprenoient & lui ordonnoient de se taire, Dimitte eam, quia clamat post nos : cette assurance qu'elle eut de renoncer volontiers au pain de la table, pourv\u00fb qu'on lui donn\u00e2t seulement les miettes qui en tomboient ; c'est-\u00e0-dire, selon l'explication de saint J\u00e9r\u00f4me, de se contenter des moindres efforts de la puissance du Sauveur, convaincue que ce seroit assez pour op\u00e9rer le miracle qu'elle demandoit, Nam & catelli edunt de micis quae cadunt de mensa dominorum suorum : tout cela n'\u00e9toit-il pas d'une ame bien s\u00fbre du Dieu qu'elle invoquoit ? Qu'e\u00fbt-elle fait, si d\u00e9j\u00e0 Chr\u00e9tienne, elle e\u00fbt connu Jesus-Christ aussi parfaitement que nous ? si comme nous, au lieu de le conno\u00eetre pour Fils de David, elle l'e\u00fbt connu pour Fils du Dieu vivant ? Et n'est-il pas n\u00e9anmoins vrai qu'avec toutes les id\u00e9es que notre religion nous donne de cet homme-Dieu, nous ne le prions presque jamais de cette mani\u00e8re simple, mais h\u00e9ro\u00efque, qui nous est marqu\u00e9e par l'Ap\u00f4tre, je veux dire avec foi & sans aucun doute ? Postulet autem in fide, nihil haesitans."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p027", "label": "English", "text": "To pray with the feeling of lively confidence: what could be more just? It is our Sovereign and our God who, by an effect of his mercy, not only wishes to be prayed to in this way, but even considers himself honored by this confidence; who in a thousand places in Scripture attributes to it, rather than to his mercy\u2014do not take offense at my proposition; it is sound and orthodox\u2014who, I say, in a thousand places in Scripture attributes to this confidence, rather than even to mercy, the miraculous power of prayer. He does not say to those who have recourse to him and call upon him, It is my goodness and my power; but, It is your faith and your confidence that have saved you: Your faith saves you. Could he offer us a more advantageous bargain? Unbeliever though the Canaanite woman was, was this not what she embraced at once? That openness of heart she showed to Jesus Christ, addressing him herself with these words, Lord, have pity on me\u2014Have mercy on me, Lord; that tender and affectionate appeal by which she moved him, calling him Son of David\u2014Son of David; those cries she redoubled as the Apostles rebuked her and told her to be silent\u2014Send her away, for she cries out after us; that assurance she had of willingly foregoing the bread from the table, provided she were given only the crumbs that fell from it\u2014that is, according to Saint Jerome's explanation, of being content with the smallest efforts of the Savior's power, convinced that this would be enough to work the miracle she asked of him\u2014For even the little dogs eat of the crumbs that fall from their masters' table: did not all this come from a soul quite sure of the God she invoked? What would she have done if, already a Christian, she had known Jesus Christ as perfectly as we do? If, like us, instead of knowing him as Son of David, she had known him as Son of the living God? Yet is it not true that, with all the ideas our religion gives us of this God-man, we hardly ever pray to him in that simple but heroic manner marked out for us by the Apostle: I mean with faith and without any doubt? But let him ask in faith, hesitating in nothing.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p028", "label": "French original", "text": "Quoi que Jesus-Christ ait p\u00fb faire pour nous y aider, & quoique pour vaincre notre incr\u00e9dulit\u00e9 & notre d\u00e9fiance, il se soit engag\u00e9 \u00e0 nous par le serment le plus solemnel, & qu'il en ait jur\u00e9 par lui-m\u00eame, lui, comme dit saint Paul, qui n'avoit point de plus grand que lui-m\u00eame par qui il p\u00fbt jurer ; notre d\u00e9fiance & notre incr\u00e9dulit\u00e9 l'emportent. Nous croyons un homme sur sa parole, & nous ne croyons pas un Dieu : nous prions, mais en m\u00eame-tems nous nous troublons, nous nous entretenons dans de vaines inqui\u00e9tudes, nous nous abandonnons \u00e0 de secrets desespoirs : nous avons recours \u00e0 Dieu, mais toujours dans l'extr\u00e9mit\u00e9 & quand tout le reste nous manque : nous comptons moins sur Dieu que sur nous-m\u00eames, & nous faisons plus de fond sur notre prudence que sur nos pri\u00e8res. Aveuglement que d\u00e9ploroit S. Ambroise, & qui justifie bien la conduite de Dieu, quand il raccourcit son bras \u00e0 notre \u00e9gard, & qu'il ne daigne pas l'\u00e9tendre pour nous secourir."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p028", "label": "English", "text": "Whatever Jesus Christ has been able to do to help us, and although, to overcome our unbelief and our mistrust, he has bound himself to us by the most solemn oath, swearing it by himself\u2014he who, as Saint Paul says, had no one greater than himself by whom he could swear\u2014our mistrust and our unbelief prevail. We believe a man on his word, and we do not believe a God. We pray, but at the same time we trouble ourselves, we maintain ourselves in vain anxieties, we abandon ourselves to secret despair. We have recourse to God, but always at the last extremity and when everything else fails us. We count less on God than on ourselves, and put more trust in our prudence than in our prayers. Saint Ambrose deplored this blindness, which fully justifies God's conduct when he shortens his arm toward us and does not deign to stretch it out to help us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p029", "label": "French original", "text": "Prier avec pers\u00e9v\u00e9rance, quoi de plus convenable ? Dieu, ma\u00eetre de ses dons & \u00e0 qui seul il appartient d'en disposer, ne peut-il pas les mettre \u00e0 tel prix qu'il lui pla\u00eet ; & ses gr\u00e2ces ne sont-elles pas en effet assez pr\u00e9cieuses pour les demander souvent & long-tems ? Quand Jesus-Christ par son silence \u00e9prouva cette m\u00e8re de l'Evangile, & qu'il ne lui r\u00e9pondit pas m\u00eame une parole ; Et non respondit ei verbum : quand il sembla vouloir l'\u00e9loigner par un refus s\u00e9v\u00e9re & mortifiant, & que devant elle il d\u00e9clara aux Ap\u00f4tres qu'il n'\u00e9toit point envoy\u00e9 pour elle ; Non sum missus, nisi ad oves quae perierunt dom\u00fbs Isra\u00ebl ; cessa-t-elle pour cela de prier, de solliciter, de presser ? Non, Chr\u00e9tiens ; la r\u00e9sistance de Jesus-Christ augmenta sa pers\u00e9v\u00e9rance, & sa pers\u00e9v\u00e9rance triompha de la r\u00e9sistance de Jesus-Christ. Elle comprit d'abord le myst\u00e8re, & les inclinations de ce Dieu Sauveur ; & dans l'engagement o\u00f9 elle se trouva, d'entrer, pour ainsi dire, en lice avec lui, opposant \u00e0 une duret\u00e9 apparente les empressemens v\u00e9ritables d'une sainte opini\u00e2tret\u00e9, elle for\u00e7a en quelque sorte les loix de la providence ; elle m\u00e9rita, quoiqu'\u00e9trang\u00e9re, d'\u00eatre trait\u00e9e en Isra\u00e9lite ; elle obtint le double miracle, & de la d\u00e9livrance de sa fille & de sa propre conversion. O charit\u00e9 de mon Dieu, s'\u00e9crie un Pere, que vous \u00eates adorable dans vos dissimulations, & dans les stratag\u00eames dont vous usez, pour combattre en apparence contre ceux m\u00eames pour qui vous combattez en effet ! O dissimulatrix clementia, quae duritiem te simulas, quant\u00e2 pietate pugnas adversus eos pro quibus pugnas ! Ne d\u00e9sesp\u00e9rez donc point, ajo\u00fbtoit-il, \u00f4 ame Chr\u00e9tienne, vous qui avez commenc\u00e9 dans la pri\u00e8re \u00e0 lutter avec votre Dieu : car il aime que vous lui fassiez violence ; il se pla\u00eet \u00e0 \u00eatre d\u00e9sarm\u00e9 pour vous : Noli igitur desperare, \u00f4 anima, quae cum Deo luctari coepisti : amat utique vim abs te pati, desiderat \u00e0 te superari. Et ne craignons pas, mes Freres, conclut-il, que ce Dieu de mis\u00e9ricorde puisse \u00eatre fort & invincible contre nous, lui qui par le plus \u00e9tonnant prodige a voulu jusques \u00e0 la mort \u00eatre foible pour nous : Et absit, Fratres, ut fortis sit adversum nos, qui pro nobis usque ad mortem infirmatus est. Ainsi le concevoient les Saints : mais nous, vous le s\u00e7avez, pr\u00e9venus d'une erreur toute contraire & emport\u00e9s par un esprit volage & l\u00e9ger, nous c\u00e9dons \u00e0 Dieu malgr\u00e9 lui-m\u00eame ; nous lui c\u00e9dons lorsqu'il voudroit lui m\u00eame nous c\u00e9der ; nous nous ennuyons de lui dire, que nous sommes pauvres & que nous attendons son secours, & il veut \u00eatre importun\u00e9. Cette assiduit\u00e9 nous fatigue, nous g\u00eane, nous cause des d\u00e9go\u00fbts & des impatiences. Nous voudrions en \u00eatre quittes pour nous \u00eatre une fois pr\u00e9sent\u00e9s \u00e0 la porte ; & nous oublions la grande maxime du Sage, qui nous avertit de supporter les lenteurs de Dieu : Sustine sustentationes Dei. Nous ne pouvons nous accommoder de cette parole d'Isa\u00efe, Expecta, attendez ; Reexpecta, attendez encore. Le moindre d\u00e9lai nous rebutte ; & souvent sur le point m\u00eame de voir nos v\u0153ux remplis, nous en perdons tout le m\u00e9rite & tout le profit. A qui nous en devons-nous prendre ? est-ce \u00e0 Dieu ? ou n'est-ce pas \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p029", "label": "English", "text": "To pray with perseverance: what could be more fitting? God, master of his gifts and the only one to whom their disposal belongs, may he not set whatever price on them he pleases? And are his graces not indeed precious enough to ask for them often and for a long time? When Jesus Christ tested this mother in the Gospel by his silence, and did not answer her even a word\u2014And he answered her not a word; when he seemed to wish to drive her away by a severe and humiliating refusal, and declared before her to the Apostles that he was not sent for her\u2014I was sent only to the sheep that have perished of the house of Israel\u2014did she therefore stop praying, pleading, pressing him? No, Christians: the resistance of Jesus Christ increased her perseverance, and perseverance triumphed over the resistance of Jesus Christ. She at once understood the mystery and the inclinations of this saving God; and in the engagement she found herself in, of entering, so to speak, into a contest with him, opposing the genuine urgency of holy persistence to an apparent harshness, she in some manner forced the laws of providence. She deserved, though a foreigner, to be treated as an Israelite; she obtained the double miracle, both the deliverance of her daughter and her own conversion. O charity of my God, a Father cries out, how adorable you are in your concealments, and in the stratagems you employ to fight in appearance against the very ones for whom you fight in reality! O mercy that conceals itself, that feigns harshness, with what compassion you fight against those for whom you fight! Do not despair, then, he added, Christian soul, you who have begun to wrestle with your God in prayer: for he loves you to do violence to him; he delights to be disarmed for you. Do not despair, then, O soul that has begun to wrestle with God: he loves to suffer force from you; he desires to be overcome by you. And let us not fear, my brothers, he concludes, that this God of mercy can be strong and invincible against us, he who by the most astonishing wonder was willing to be weak for us even unto death. And far be it, brothers, that he should be strong against us, who for us was made weak even unto death. Thus the saints understood him. But we, as you know, prejudiced by an entirely contrary error and carried away by a fickle and light spirit, yield to God despite himself; we yield to him when he himself would wish to yield to us. We grow tired of telling him that we are poor and await his help, and he wishes to be importuned. This constancy wearies and constrains us, causes us distaste and impatience. We would like to be released from it for having once presented ourselves at the door, and we forget the great maxim of the wise man, who warns us to bear with God's delays: Endure God's delays. We cannot accommodate ourselves to this word of Isaiah: Wait, wait; wait again, wait again. The slightest delay repels us; and often, on the very point of seeing our wishes fulfilled, we lose all their merit and all their benefit. Whom should we blame for this? Is it God, or is it not ourselves?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p030", "label": "French original", "text": "Enfin, prier avec attention, avec affection, je dis avec attention de l'esprit, avec affection du c\u0153ur, quoi de plus n\u00e9cessaire & de plus essentiel \u00e0 la pri\u00e8re ? Je finis par ce point le plus important de tous. Attention de l'esprit, affection du c\u0153ur, c'est ce que j'appelle apr\u00e8s saint Thomas l'ame de la pri\u00e8re, & sans quoi elle ne peut-pas plus subsister qu'un corps sans l'esprit qui le vivifie & qui l'anime. Car qu'est-ce que la pri\u00e8re ? ne consultons point ici la Th\u00e9ologie, mais le seul bon sens & l'id\u00e9e commune que nous avons de ce saint exercice : qu'est-ce encore une fois que la pri\u00e8re ? un entretien avec Dieu, o\u00f9 l'ame admise, pour m'exprimer de la sorte, & introduite dans le sanctuaire, expose \u00e0 Dieu ses besoins, lui repr\u00e9sente ses foiblesses, lui d\u00e9couvre ses tentations, lui demande grace pour ses infid\u00e9lit\u00e9s. Or tout cela ne suppose-t-il pas un recueillement & un sentiment int\u00e9rieur ? Si donc il arrive qu'au moment que je traite avec Dieu, mon esprit s'\u00e9gare, jusques \u00e0 perdre absolument & volontairement cette attention int\u00e9rieure & cette d\u00e9votion : quoi que je fasse du reste, ce n'est plus une pri\u00e8re. Quand je chanterois les louanges du Seigneur, quand j'employerois les nuits enti\u00e8res au pied des autels ; quand mon corps selon l'expression & l'exemple de David, demeureroit comme attach\u00e9 & coll\u00e9 \u00e0 la terre, d\u00e8s que je cesse de m'appliquer, je cesse de prier. Et de-l\u00e0, Chr\u00e9tiens, le Docteur Ang\u00e9lique tiroit trois grandes cons\u00e9quences, auxquelles je n'ajouterai rien, mais que je vous prie de bien m\u00e9diter pour votre \u00e9dification. Cons\u00e9quences terribles, & qui vous feront pleinement conno\u00eetre pourquoi nos pri\u00e8res ont si peu d'efficace aupr\u00e8s de Dieu."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p030", "label": "English", "text": "Finally, to pray with attention and affection\u2014I mean with attention of mind and affection of heart: what could be more necessary and more essential to prayer? I end with this point, the most important of all. Attention of mind, affection of heart: this is what, following Saint Thomas, I call the soul of prayer, without which it can no more subsist than a body without the spirit that gives it life and animates it. For what is prayer? Let us consult here not theology, but only good sense and the common idea we have of this holy exercise. What, once again, is prayer? A conversation with God in which the soul, admitted, so to speak, and introduced into the sanctuary, lays its needs before God, represents its weaknesses to him, reveals its temptations to him, and asks his pardon for its infidelities. Does not all this presuppose recollection and an inward feeling? If, then, at the moment I deal with God, my mind wanders so far as absolutely and voluntarily to lose this inward attention and this devotion, whatever else I do, it is no longer prayer. Though I should sing the Lord's praises, though I should spend entire nights at the foot of the altars, though my body, according to David's expression and example, should remain as if attached and glued to the earth, as soon as I cease to apply myself, I cease to pray. And from this, Christians, the Angelic Doctor drew three great consequences, to which I shall add nothing, but which I beg you to meditate on carefully for your edification. Terrible consequences, which will make you understand fully why our prayers have so little efficacy with God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p031", "label": "French original", "text": "Premi\u00e8re cons\u00e9quence. Puisqu'il est vrai que l'attention est de l'essence de la pri\u00e8re, on peut dire avec sujet, mais encore avec plus de douleur, que l'exercice de la pri\u00e8re est comme an\u00e9anti dans le Christianisme : pourquoi ? parce que si l'on y prie encore quelquefois, c'est sans r\u00e9flexion. A quoi se r\u00e9duit toute notre pi\u00e9t\u00e9 ? \u00e0 quelques pri\u00e8res que nous r\u00e9citons, mais du reste avec un esprit dissip\u00e9 & presque toujours distrait. Nous remuons les l\u00e8vres, non pas comme cette m\u00e8re de Samu\u00ebl dont le grand Pr\u00eatre H\u00e9li jugea t\u00e9m\u00e9rairement ; mais comme les Juifs, \u00e0 qui Dieu reprochoit que leur c\u0153ur \u00e9toit bien loin de lui tandis qu'ils le glorifioient de bouche. Ainsi nos pri\u00e8res ne sont plus commun\u00e9ment qu'hypocrisie ; & Jesus-Christ pourroit bien nous redire ce qu'il disoit aux Pharisiens : Hypocritae, ben\u00e8 prophetavit de vobis Isa\u00efas : populus hic labiis me honorat, cor autem eorum long\u00e8 est \u00e0 me. Ce n'est pas seulement le peuple qui tombe dans ce d\u00e9sordre, & qui par une fatale grossi\u00e9ret\u00e9 prie tous les jours sans prier, c'est-\u00e0-dire, sans penser \u00e0 qui il parle, ni \u00e0 ce qu'il demande. Ce n'est pas seulement le sexe d\u00e9vot, qui plus addonn\u00e9 \u00e0 la pri\u00e8re fait son capital de dire beaucoup, mais sans fixer sa l\u00e9g\u00e8ret\u00e9 naturelle & en s'appliquant tr\u00e8s-peu. Ce sont m\u00eame les hommes les plus \u00e9clair\u00e9s & les mieux instruits ; ce sont les personnes m\u00eames consacr\u00e9es \u00e0 Dieu, les ministres m\u00eames de Dieu, qui par le plus d\u00e9plorable renversement, \u00e0 force de prier, ne prient point du tout ; & au lieu de perfectionner une si sainte pratique par l'habitude, la corrompent & la d\u00e9truisent."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p031", "label": "English", "text": "First consequence. Since it is true that attention belongs to the essence of prayer, we can say with reason, but with still greater sorrow, that the exercise of prayer is as if annihilated in Christianity. Why? Because, though people still pray sometimes, it is without reflection. To what is all our piety reduced? To a few prayers we recite, but otherwise with a scattered and almost always distracted mind. We move our lips, not like that mother of Samuel whom the high priest Eli judged rashly, but like the Jews whom God reproached because their heart was far from him while they glorified him with their mouth. Thus our prayers are most commonly nothing but hypocrisy; and Jesus Christ could well say to us what he said to the Pharisees: Hypocrites, Isaiah prophesied well of you: This people honors me with their lips, but their heart is far from me. It is not only the ordinary people who fall into this disorder, and who, through a fatal lack of understanding, pray every day without praying\u2014that is, without thinking to whom they speak or what they ask. It is not only the devout sex, which, being more given to prayer, makes much of saying a great deal, but without restraining its natural flightiness and with very little application. It is even the most enlightened and best-instructed men; it is even persons consecrated to God, the very ministers of God, who, by the most deplorable reversal, through praying so much do not pray at all, and instead of perfecting so holy a practice through habit, corrupt and destroy it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p032", "label": "French original", "text": "Seconde cons\u00e9quence. Puisque la pri\u00e8re renferme essentiellement l'attention, il s'ensuit que dans les pri\u00e8res qui nous sont command\u00e9es, l'attention est elle-m\u00eame de pr\u00e9cepte : en sorte qu'il ne suffit point alors de prononcer, mais qu'une distraction notable & volontaire doit \u00eatre consid\u00e9r\u00e9e comme une offense gri\u00e9ve & mortelle. Or je dis sur-tout ceci, mes Freres, & pour vous & pour moi, parce que c'est en cela que consiste un des premiers engagemens de votre profession & de la mienne ; & que la pri\u00e8re vocale est comme le sacr\u00e9 tribut que l'Eglise chaque jour exige de nous. Car il seroit bien \u00e9trange, que cette action si sainte d'elle-m\u00eame, & qui doit nous-m\u00eames nous sanctifier, ne serv\u00eet qu'\u00e0 nous condamner ; & que ce qui doit \u00eatre pour nous la source des gr\u00e2ces, dev\u00eent une des sources de notre r\u00e9probation. Souvenons-nous qu'en nous obligeant \u00e0 l'office divin, nous nous sommes oblig\u00e9s \u00e0 un acte de religion ; qu'un acte de religion n'est point une pratique purement ext\u00e9rieure ; & que comme l'Eglise en nous commandant la confession, nous commande la contrition du c\u0153ur, aussi nous commande-t-elle l'attention de l'esprit en nous commandant la pri\u00e8re. Soit que cette obligation naisse imm\u00e9diatement & directement du pr\u00e9cepte de l'Eglise m\u00eame, comme l'estiment de tr\u00e8s-habiles Th\u00e9ologiens ; soit qu'elle vienne du pr\u00e9cepte naturel qui accompagne celui de l'Eglise, en vertu duquel Dieu nous ordonne de faire saintement & dignement ce qui nous est prescrit, comme veulent quelques autres : quoi qu'il en soit, cette diff\u00e9rence de sentimens n'est qu'une subtilit\u00e9 de l'\u00e9cole ; & dans l'une & l'autre opinion, l'on p\u00e8che toujours \u00e9galement. Ah, mes Freres, n'attirons pas sur nous cette mal\u00e9diction dont le Proph\u00e8te dans l'exc\u00e8s de son z\u00e9le mena\u00e7oit le p\u00e9cheur, quand il disoit : que sa pri\u00e8re devienne un p\u00e9ch\u00e9 pour lui ; Oratio ejus fiat in peccatum. Or \u00e0 combien de ministres, ou de combien de ministres n'est-il pas \u00e0 craindre qu'on n'en puisse dire autant ? Si saint Augustin s'accusoit sur cela de n\u00e9gligence, nous avons bien encore plus lieu de nous en accuser nous-m\u00eames."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p032", "label": "English", "text": "Second consequence. Since prayer essentially includes attention, it follows that, in the prayers commanded of us, attention itself is commanded: so that it is not then enough to pronounce the words, but a considerable and voluntary distraction must be regarded as a grave and mortal offense. Now I say this above all, my brothers, both for you and for myself, because in this consists one of the first obligations of your profession and mine, and because vocal prayer is like the sacred tribute the Church demands of us each day. For it would be very strange if this action, so holy in itself, which ought to sanctify us, served only to condemn us; and if what ought to be the source of graces for us became one of the sources of our reprobation. Let us remember that, in binding ourselves to the divine office, we have bound ourselves to an act of religion; that an act of religion is not a purely outward practice; and that, just as the Church, in commanding confession, commands contrition of heart, so, in commanding prayer, she commands attention of mind. Whether this obligation arises immediately and directly from the Church's own precept, as very able theologians hold, or whether it comes from the natural precept accompanying that of the Church, by virtue of which God orders us to do holily and worthily what is prescribed to us, as certain others maintain\u2014however this may be, this difference of opinion is only a subtlety of the schools, and in either opinion one sins equally. Ah, my brothers, let us not draw upon ourselves that curse with which the Prophet, in the excess of his zeal, threatened the sinner when he said: Let his prayer become sin\u2014Let his prayer become sin. Of how many ministers, or of how many ministers, is it to be feared that the same could be said? If Saint Augustine accused himself of negligence in this, we have still more reason to accuse ourselves.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p033", "label": "French original", "text": "Troisi\u00e8me & derni\u00e8re cons\u00e9quence. Ce n'est donc pas sans raison que Dieu rejette nos pri\u00e8res, puisque ce ne sont rien moins que des pri\u00e8res ; & que bien loin de l'honorer, nous l'offensons, & l'irritons contre nous. Car quelle injustice, mon cher Auditeur ? Vous voulez que Dieu s'applique \u00e0 vous, quand il vous pla\u00eet de le prier ; & vous ne voulez pas en le priant vous appliquer vous-m\u00eame \u00e0 Dieu. Vous dites \u00e0 Dieu comme le Proph\u00e8te : Seigneur, pr\u00eatez l'oreille \u00e0 mes paroles : Verba mea auribus percipe : Seigneur, \u00e9coutez mes cris ; Intellige clamorem meum : Seigneur, soyez attentif \u00e0 mes v\u0153ux, Intende voci orationis meae : mais au m\u00eame-tems vous portez votre esprit ailleurs. Vous demandez que Dieu vous parle, & vous ne lui parlez pas : vous demandez que Dieu vous \u00e9coute, & vous ne l'\u00e9coutez pas ; vous ne vous \u00e9coutez pas vous-m\u00eame, vous ne vous comprenez pas."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p033", "label": "English", "text": "Third and final consequence. It is therefore not without reason that God rejects our prayers, since they are anything but prayers, and since, far from honoring him, we offend him and provoke him against us. For what injustice, my dear listener! You want God to attend to you when it pleases you to pray to him; and while praying you do not want to attend to God yourself. You say to God with the Prophet: Lord, lend your ear to my words\u2014Give ear to my words; Lord, hear my cries\u2014Understand my cry; Lord, be attentive to my petitions\u2014Attend to the voice of my prayer; but at the same time you lend your mind elsewhere. You ask God to speak to you, and you do not speak to him; you ask God to listen to you, and you do not listen to him; you do not listen to yourself; you do not understand yourself.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p034", "label": "French original", "text": "R\u00e9formons-nous, Chr\u00e9tiens, sur ce seul article, & nous r\u00e9formerons toute notre vie : car on s\u00e7ait bien vivre, dit saint Augustin, quand on s\u00e7ait bien prier : Rect\u00e8 novit vivere, qui novit orare. Pourquoi sommes nous sujets \u00e0 tant de d\u00e9sordres ? c'est parce que nous ne prions point, ou que nous prions mal ; & par un retour trop ordinaire, pourquoi ne prions-nous point, ou pourquoi prions-nous mal ? c'est parce que nous ne voulons pas sortir de nos d\u00e9sordres & que nous craignons de gu\u00e9rir. Demandons \u00e0 Dieu des choses dignes de lui & dignes de nous. Demandons-les d'une mani\u00e8re digne de lui & digne de nous. En deux mots, demandons-lui ses graces & demandons-les bien ; nous les obtiendrons : mais entre les autres gr\u00e2ces, demandons-lui sur-tout le don de la pri\u00e8re. Disons-lui comme les Ap\u00f4tres : Domine, doce nos orare ; Ah ! Seigneur, notre foiblesse est telle, que nous ne pouvons pas m\u00eame sans vous, vous bien exposer nos besoins, ni bien implorer votre secours. C'est \u00e0 vous \u00e0 nous faire sentir efficacement nos mis\u00e9res ; c'est \u00e0 vous \u00e0 nous attirer au pied de votre autel pour vous les repr\u00e9senter ; c'est \u00e0 vous \u00e0 nous inspirer ce que nous devons vous dire pour vous toucher. Donnez-nous donc vous-m\u00eame, \u00f4 mon Dieu, cette science si n\u00e9cessaire ; & par une grace o\u00f9 sont en quelque sorte renferm\u00e9es, comme dans leur source, toutes les autres graces, apprenez-nous \u00e0 nous servir de la pri\u00e8re pour faire descendre sur nous des gr\u00e2ces de conversion, des gr\u00e2ces de sanctification, des gr\u00e2ces de salut qui nous conduisent \u00e0 la gloire, &c."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-p034", "label": "English", "text": "Let us reform ourselves, Christians, on this one point, and we shall reform our whole life: for one knows how to live well, says Saint Augustine, when one knows how to pray well\u2014He knows how to live rightly who knows how to pray rightly. Why are we subject to so many disorders? Because we do not pray, or because we pray badly; and, by an all too common reversal, why do we not pray, or why do we pray badly? Because we do not want to leave our disorders and we fear being cured. Let us ask God for things worthy of him and worthy of us. Let us ask for them in a manner worthy of him and worthy of us. In two words, let us ask him for his graces, and ask for them well: we shall obtain them. But among other graces, let us ask him above all for the gift of prayer. Let us say to him with the Apostles: Lord, teach us to pray. Ah, Lord, our weakness is such that, without you, we cannot even set our needs properly before you or rightly implore your help. It is for you to make us feel our miseries effectively; it is for you to draw us to the foot of your altar to lay them before you; it is for you to inspire in us what we must say to move you. Then give us this so necessary knowledge yourself, O my God; and by a grace in which all other graces are in some manner enclosed, as in their source, teach us to make use of prayer to draw down upon us graces of conversion, graces of sanctification, graces of salvation that may lead us to glory, &c.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n001", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt.15."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n001", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 15.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n002", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibidem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n002", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n003", "label": "French original", "text": "Ps.148."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n003", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 148.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n004", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan.15."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n004", "label": "English", "text": "John 15.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n005", "label": "French original", "text": "Josue.10."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n005", "label": "English", "text": "Joshua 10.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n006", "label": "French original", "text": "Jacob.4."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n006", "label": "English", "text": "James 4.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n007", "label": "French original", "text": "Isai.43."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n007", "label": "English", "text": "Isaiah 43.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n008", "label": "French original", "text": "Juven."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n008", "label": "English", "text": "Juvenal.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n009", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt.6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n009", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n010", "label": "French original", "text": "II.Mach.[9?]."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n010", "label": "English", "text": "2 Maccabees [9?].", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n011", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan.[1?]6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n011", "label": "English", "text": "John [1?]6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n012", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan.16."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n012", "label": "English", "text": "John 16.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n013", "label": "French original", "text": "Tertull."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n013", "label": "English", "text": "Tertullian.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n014", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt.6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n014", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n015", "label": "French original", "text": "Genes.27."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n015", "label": "English", "text": "Genesis 27.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n016", "label": "French original", "text": "Hebr.5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n016", "label": "English", "text": "Hebrews 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n017", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan.17."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n017", "label": "English", "text": "John 17.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n018", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibidem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n018", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n019", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibidem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n019", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n020", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n020", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n021", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n021", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n022", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibidem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n022", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n023", "label": "French original", "text": "Ps.26."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n023", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 26.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n024", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n024", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n025", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n025", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n026", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n026", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n027", "label": "French original", "text": "Ps.76."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n027", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 76.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n028", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal.6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n028", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n029", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibidem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n029", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n030", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt.15."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n030", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 15.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n031", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth.15."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n031", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 15.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n032", "label": "French original", "text": "Eccli.25."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n032", "label": "English", "text": "Sirach 25.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n033", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt.10."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n033", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 10.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n034", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt.15."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n034", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 15.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n035", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibidem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n035", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n036", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibidem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n036", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n037", "label": "French original", "text": "Jacob.1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n037", "label": "English", "text": "James 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n038", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt.15."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n038", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 15.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n039", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt.15."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n039", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 15.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n040", "label": "French original", "text": "Eccli.2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n040", "label": "English", "text": "Sirach 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n041", "label": "French original", "text": "Isai.28."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n041", "label": "English", "text": "Isaiah 28.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n042", "label": "French original", "text": "Matt.15."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n042", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew 15.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n043", "label": "French original", "text": "Ps.108."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n043", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 108.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n044", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal.5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n044", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n045", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibidem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n045", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n046", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n046", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n047", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n047", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n048", "label": "French original", "text": "Luc.11."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-n048", "label": "English", "text": "Luke 11.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-ed001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Two chapter numbers in the margin are damaged. The number after 2 Maccabees is best read as 9; the first digit after John is uncertain, probably giving chapter 16. Brackets and question marks indicate these uncertainties; they are additions by the editors."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-ed002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Bible references follow the book, including historical Psalm numbering. The references printed as Psalm 6 and Matthew 10 appear inconsistent with the quotations; they have been retained rather than silently corrected."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-ed003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Latin quotation about mercy feigning harshness is grammatically irregular in the book. The English follows the explanation in the surrounding French. The Latin has not been silently corrected, and its attribution has not been independently authenticated."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-ed004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The French edition prints Excellente oraison (excellent prayer), retained in this library\u2019s AI version. Carroll\u2019s historical version reads \u2018this excellent way of reasoning.\u2019 The French has not been silently changed to raison (reasoning); neither wording independently verifies the attribution to Augustine."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-reading-repetition", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The repeated reference to ministers is in the printed sermon. The French shifts from saying this \u201cto\u201d ministers to saying it \u201cof\u201d them."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s001", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "SERMON XXIV.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s002", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "ON PRAYER.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s003", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "\u201cBehold a woman of Canaan, who came out of those coasts, saying: Have mercy on me, O Lord, thou Son of David! my daughter is grievously troubled by a devil.\u201d MATT. xv.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s004", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "If ever the force and efficacy of prayer were discovered in a sensible and striking manner, was it not, Christians, in the example which this passage of the gospel exhibits? It discovers (says St. Ambrose) even a God surprised and struck with admiration. He confounds the powers of hell and darkness; works miracles and exerts his powers in behalf of a foreigner who hath recourse to him; and who, though an idolater, ought to serve us as a pattern, and teach us how to pray. Surprised he was, and struck with admiration: \u201cO woman, great is thy faith!\u201d Matt. xv. Thus, Christ explains himself. And do not her faith and the fervour of her prayer seem to give him surprise and unusual emotion? He confounds the powers of hell and darkness, and works miracles. For, what petition doth this woman make? That he would cure her daughter, who was cruelly worried by the evil spirit; and the Son of God, by one word speaking, not only restored the possessed daughter to her right mind, but sanctified the mother: \u201cBe it unto thee as thou wilt.\u201d Matt. xv.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s005", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Nothing, therefore, is more efficacious with God than prayer. And yet, beloved hearers, how comes it that we find him, by daily experience, not inclined to hear our petitions? That although we pray, he hears us not? And that although we ask, we obtain nothing? This is what I now am about to examine, and this is to be the ground of the present discourse. It is a great subject, and of extreme consequence, and therefore deserving of particular reflection; for I mean to inculcate the most excellent of all sciences\u2014that of using the most powerful means of salvation in a proper manner. I mean to let you know the inestimable method and divine art of obtaining admittance to the throne of God, and prevailing with him to send down upon us the most precious treasures of his holy grace. But in order to receive this gift of prayer, let us have recourse to prayer, and let us implore the divine aid in the usual form.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s006", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "There is nothing more solidly established in religion, and in Christian theology, than the infallibility of prayer. Such is the force of it, (says St. Chrysostom,) that it renders, it should seem, the word of man equally powerful in a manner with the word of God. Equally powerful: for as God, with a word created all things: \u201cHe said, and they were made;\u201d (Ps. cxlviii.;) so man need but ask, and all is granted him: \u201cYe shall ask what ye will, and it shall be done unto you.\u201d John xv. Every day we hear Christians complain of the little advantage that accrues from prayer. Nor is it surprising. For how do we say that prayer is infallible? We suppose, to that purpose, a holy prayer: we suppose it performed with all the conditions that ought to attend it, and that God requires from us when he promises to grant whatever we shall ask. Now this is a common defect in our prayers. They are defective in regard to the matter and the form: in regard to the matter, which is the thing we pray for; and in regard to the form, which is the manner of praying. This, St. James told the faithful of his time: \u201cYe ask, and receive not, because ye ask amiss.\u201d James iv. We ask not, in fact, for that for which God would have us ask; and this is a defect as to the matter of prayer. Neither do we ask in the manner in which he would have us ask; and this is a defect as to the form of prayer. But let us put up our prayers like the woman of Canaan. Nothing more proper than her prayer to Christ. She asks him to cast out the devil from her daughter. Nothing more engaging. In her prayer she puts all the virtue in practice which can gain upon our Saviour and move his heart. I say, let us pray like this woman, else our prayers are fruitless. Either because we ask not for the things for which we ought to ask: or, because we ask not for them in the manner in which we ought to ask for them.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s007", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "These are two lessons on which my duty obliges me to throw what light I can. Be attentive, Christians, and use your best endeavours to profit by them.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s008", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "PART I. It is on the nature of the things which we ask of God, that the very essence, and of course the merit, the efficacy, and the virtue of prayer depends; and St. Chrysostom tells us, that it is by this rule we must call ourselves to an account for the small value which God sets on all the prayers we offer, and the little effect they have in his sight. And this is the admirable instruction which the gospel affords us by the example of the woman of Canaan. For observe, if you please, (and may I be allowed to explain myself in this manner,) that whereas this woman, prostrate at the feet of our blessed Saviour, beseeches him to deliver her daughter from the power of the devil that possesses her; we, on the contrary, influenced by a spirit directly opposite, beg of God every day what preserves in our souls the dominion of the devil\u2014nay of many devils, of which we are unwilling to be dispossessed. What more is necessaay [sic] to make you comprehend why the Saviour of the world gives ear to this foreigner, and works in her favour a miracle of his omnipotence; and why, on the contrary, God is deaf to our supplications and rejects our prayers for the greatest part? Apply your minds, beloved Christians, to the sublime doctrines which this subject will give me a favourable opportunity of displaying, as it includes the most important points concerning your predestination.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s009", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "I say we beg every day of God what preserves in our souls the dominion of Satan\u2014because we beg of him things in our prayers, either prejudicial to salvation, or purely temporal and unprofitable for salvation; or even supernatural and divine graces, but which, seeing the manner in which we conceive them, and in which we desire them, far from sanctifying us, serve rather to seduce us and withhold and divert us from the way of salvation. These three positions I shall endeavour to explain with all the clearness and precision I am able.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s010", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "1st. In our prayers to God we ask for things prejudical [sic] to salvation: the first obstacle which stops the course of the divine mercies. For we must not imagine, beloved hearers, that we are not, though Christians, addicted in practice to the irregularities of paganism. Now, if we may credit the pagans themselves, one of the irregularities of the pagans was to have recourse to their gods, and to ask of them that which they would not have asked of an honest man; which they would not have asked openly in the temple, or at the foot of the altar without a blush\u2014as the death of a relation whose estate they longed for, or of a competitor at whose credit and merit they repined and envied\u2014the ward\u2019s inheritance which they meant to embezzle, and which they beheld with avaricious eyes. Such was the subject of their prayers. And, to give them more weight, they added the ceremonies of a superstitious worship, offerings, sacrifices, and corporal lustrations.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s011", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "To us, all this appears enormous and senseless. But, beloved Christians, in condemning them do we not pronounce sentence against ourselves? If we compare their prayers with our own, are we less culpable? What do I say? Are we not more culpable to a great degree? For, in short, they were pagans, and these pagans adored not only vain and false, but even vicious and dissolute deities, according to their own belief. Now what was more natural, than to ask of such deities that which favoured their vices, and the corruption of their morals? Was it not almost a necessary consequence of their infidelity? But, Christian brethren, the God whom we serve is as pure and holy as he is powerful and great; a God as essentially an enemy to injustice and to all sin, as he is God. And yet of this God, so pure, so holy, so equitable and just, what do we ask? The accomplishment of our wishes and sensual desires, and the success of our base and criminal undertakings. This is miscalled an irregular proceeding: it is, I am bold enough to say it, an impiety, a sacrilege.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s012", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "True it is, and I freely grant, that now a-days Christians have learned the method of giving their prayers a more specious appearance, and a better varnish, and of expressing them in terms not quite so invidious; for, modern dissimulation and artfulness are carried to the highest perfection. But, although we may impose upon our own weakness, we cannot deceive God Almighty who hears us, and sees us, and distinguishes between the malignity of our intentions and the simplicity of our expressions. To no purpose, therefore, doth the man of the world beg of God a subsistence suitable to his profession, and wherewithal to support him according to his rank: for as his rank, or rather the idea which he forms of his rank, is founded in the principles of an unbounded ambition, or rather an insatiable avarice, God\u2019s infinite penetration discovers his designs, counteracts his schemes, and defeats his hopes. To no purpose doth the father of a family beg of God a settlement for his children in the world: for, as his views and schemes in their regard are profane and worldly, regulated neither by conscience nor by divine vocation, God Almighty overlooks the external appearance of an humble prayer, and knows its tendency; and, by a just judgment, far from raising this family to dignities, permits its ruin to all intents and purposes, letting it moulder away, or sink into obscurity. To no purpose doth the woman of the world beg of God health of body. For, whereas her health, considering the use to which she turns it, is productive only of sloth and idleness, of vicious delicacy, perhaps of libertinism and a disorderly life, God, who beholds it, instead of keeping back the weight of his arm, strikes the more severely, and deprives her in that habitual listlessness of what might feed her self-complacency, and flatter her vanity. To no purpose doth a man unjustly engaged in a law-suit, in which his whole fortune and dependence are at stake, beg of God to determine the cause in his favour: for as this law-suit is nothing, at bottom, but a covert injustice, maintained by chicanery, God Almighty, who knows it, in opposition to him espouses the cause of the widow and the orphan, and compels him to give up his pretensions with shame. Every means possible, however, are employed to move the will of God and procure his assistance. The very prayers, the very sacrifice of the church is interposed. But because this business, which is carried on so warmly, is nothing but the secret machinations of a junto, a wicked intrigue, which can succeed only to the detriment of their neighbour, God Almighty, the protector of the innocent and the poor, on this occasion rejects even the most holy prayers of the church, even the most adorable sacrament of the altar. These particulars, were I to set them forth in their full extent, would lead me too far. But were you, beloved hearers, to apply them to yourselves, you would soon perceive that hundreds of times your hearts have beguiled you in the same manner, and induced you to pervert the end of prayer, in order to obtain of God himself the gratification of your passions.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s013", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "But to return to that on which I was discoursing, and to depict this matter in the strongest colours of which it is susceptible, give me leave, Christians, to dwell a little longer on the morals of the pagans. I said they were enough to work conviction; but I said too little, and I add that in one sense, even in this place, they are more proper to put us to the blush than the doctrine of the fathers. Permit me, therefore, to make a profane author speak in this pulpit, and to impeach your conduct\u2014either for your instruction, or for your confusion\u2014as he did the manners of his own times in such spirited and energetic terms. Tell me, (says he,) deploring the abuses in ancient Rome, and upbraiding the false devotees of paganism, who importuned the gods with their unrighteous prayers; tell me your thoughts concerning Jupiter, and the place he holds in your estimation. Have you the same respect for the greatest of your gods as for the wisest of your magistrates. This question (continues he) surprises you. But I do not propose it without reason. For would you wait on this magistrate, whose virtue you revere, to offer him in his palace the infamous supplication which you make to Jupiter in the most awful of his temples? You, therefore, suppose Jupiter more liable to be corrupted, when you think him disposed to hearken to your prayers, and even ready to grant them a favourable hearing. Thus spoke a pagan upon this subject. In this sarcastic strain he reproached his fellow-subjects with the scandal of their religion, and perhaps was conducive to their reformation. Thus infidelity gives us wholesome lessons, and condemns the impropriety of our proceedings. Let us apply this to our own morals.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s014", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "And what must our notions be of our God, the God of holiness? Is he the promoter of our vices? The accomplice of our crimes. Is it possible he could, or would be such? From this source, however, flow all our actions, and our behaviour towards him. For when I pray\u2014take with you in your minds this remark of St. Chrysostom\u2014when I pray I expect, that God in his mercy and paternal condescension will conform to my wishes; that his divine will, efficacious and all-powerful, shall coincide with mine which is nothing but weakness; and that he will accomplish my desires, which without his aid would be to no purpose. Put then the case, that blinded by the spirit and maxims of the world, far from praying in a Christian manner, I pray with a view to satisfy my ambition, my pride, my resentment, my revengeful heart\u2014what do I do? I ask God to agree with me upon these heads; that is, I wish he would be vain like myself, passionate like myself, violent like myself; and that for me, who am his creature, he would vouchsafe to do that which he cannot do without ceasing to be God. Now to address our prayers to him in this manner, would not be to pray to him as the God of heaven, but to dishonour in a high degree the most adorable of his attributes; it would be to make him, as far as on me depends, subservient to my iniquities, as he complains himself by the mouth of his prophet: \u201cThou hast made me to serve with thy sins, thou hast put me to pain with thy iniquities.\u201d Isai. xliii. The expression is remarkable: \u201cThou hast put me to pain.\u201d As who should say: your prayer hath given me uneasiness; for, on one hand, I would gladly have been propitious to it; and, on the other, I could not give it a favourable hearing; my heart was according in a kind of fluctuation, and, as it were, divided between my holiness and my goodness: my goodness which took part with you, and my holiness which was against you; my goodness which inclined me to compassionate your distress, and my holiness which obliged me to reject your supplication: \u201cThou hast put me to pain with thy iniquities.\u201d And truly, Christians, if God in that case had made account of our prayers, would it not be a stumbling-block? Would it not make us call his providence in question?", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s015", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "I know that we have (as St. John informs us) the Son, a powerful advocate with the Father, and that we pray through the merits of this adorable Son. But to apply in particular to God made man what I said of God in a general way\u2014would we make him the patron of our blind desires, by which we are swayed? And if this be not the sentiment by which we are impelled, why should we lay stress and dependence on his merits, in the prayers which our desires alone inspire?", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s016", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Happy it is for you, that your gracious God, to obstruct your perdition, is inflexible to your prayers. In this seeming rigour you should acknowledge his mercy. What would become of you, were he more indulgent and more conformable to our inclinations? What hath been the ruin of, and what ruins daily, such numbers of families, but too vast and unlimited, nay, criminal wishes? What hath occasioned the eternal reprobation of so many Christians? Was it not the having obtained what God could not grant but in the excess of his wrath? And whence comes the loss of so many worldlings, who are damned amidst opulence, ease, and effeminacy, but from those supposed favours of God, who hears them and indulges the senseless desires of their heart, contrary to the designs of his amiable providence? You ask that of God which sooths your passion; and if he lends a favourable ear to your request, he who foresees that which will pervert you, that which will corrupt you, that which will plunge you into the mighty gulf, what more rigorous punishment can be inflicted? What more dreadful vengeance can be exerted? But to proceed:", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s017", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "2ndly. If you do not ask always for things prejudicial, and with views directly contrary to salvation, you supplicate, at least, for things merely temporal and no way profitable to that end. I would not, however, be supposed to insinuate that the good things of this life are not the gifts of heaven, or that they are absolutely contrary to salvation. But when are they such\u2014and on what account doth God refuse them? When we ask for them neither according to the order which himself hath established, nor relatively to the end which he hath pointed out to us.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s018", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "In the first place, regardless of spiritual favours, we ask only for temporal ones, as the advantages of fortune, prosperity, tranquillity, all which terminate in the necessaries of life. These we desire, and these we seek; and these are desired and sought by infidels, as well as by us: \u201cFor, all these things do the heathens seek.\u201d Matt. vi. These are good things, I own: but they are perishable things\u2014things of an order inferior to man, especially to a Christian man; dangerous things and liable to be perverted, though really good, into real evils. In regard to solid and incorruptible things, as purity of manners, a good conscience, humility, faith, the love of our neighbour, whatever may serve to sanctify the soul, and raise it to perfection, to our shame be it said, we supinely neglect and seldom revolve at the foot of the altar. Which of you hath ever had recourse to God with a view of becoming more moderate in his passions and more regular in his conduct? You visit the tombs of the martyrs, but why? To be cured of a distemper, not to be delivered from a temptation. You invoke the saints, but why? To be more happy and more opulent, not more humble and more an enemy to pleasures. Ah! cries Salvian, if we groan under the pressure of public calamities; if the land be threatened with famine or pestilence; if the people be swept away by a raging mortality, we run precipitately and in crowds to the temple of the living God, and our sighs, lamentations, and prayers are heard in every corner. But though unbridled libertinism dishonours Christianity, and brings grief to the church, we enjoy tranquillity and a peaceful mind. Instead of praying that heaven would put a stop to scandalous impieties, we suffer our days to pass in quietness and a dreadful indolence.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s019", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Thus we pray like the wretched Antiochus, whose selfish prayer was unable to find grace in the presence of God: \u201cThis wicked man prayed unto the Lord, of whom he was not to find mercy.\u201d Mach. ix. He prayed, neither can it be denied that he prayed with all possible ardour; he prayed, but he prayed in a worldly way; he prayed, but asked not for the spirit of penitence, or the gift of piety, or respect for the sacred things he hath profaned, but for health which he preferred to all the rest and of which he was fond to a great degree: \u201cThis wicked man prayed unto the Lord,\u201d but the Lord shut the bowels of his mercy against him. We, too, pray in the same manner; but God never promised to warrant such prayers. Examine the gospel, and you will there find the reason of it.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s020", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Christ says to his disciples: \u201cIf you shall ask the Father any thing in my name, he will give it you.\u201d John xvi. But observe (says St. Augustin) these words, any thing, by which our Saviour lets us understand that what we ask in his name should be something, and something worthy of him, because it were improper, in the other supposition, that he should exert and manifest his power in our behalf. Now all the good things of the world together, considered disjunctively from eternal bliss, are as nothing in the presence of Almighty God. To ask, therefore, of God in this manner, is to ask for nothing; and although the promise of Christ is general, or should seem to be so, they are not by themselves comprised therein. In order to be convinced of it, hear what he adds, speaking to the apostles: \u201cHitherto ye have asked nothing in my name.\u201d John xvi. And, indeed, to remain in his presence on Thabor was a delightful sensation of which St. Peter desired the continuance. To possess in his kingdom the chief places, was, according to the meaning of those two disciples, a vain honour which their ambition grasped at, because they beheld it through a false medium. But the zeal of souls, constancy in persecution, and self-denial, were the essential favours of which they stood in need, as no other favours could uphold and animate them, and raise them to perfection in their apostolic functions. For these, however, it never entered into their heads to petition their Master: \u201cHitherto ye have asked nothing.\u201d", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s021", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Now, of how many Christians might I not at this day make the same complaint? And of those who hear me, to how many might I not have just cause to say for the same reason: O man of the world, thou hast hitherto petitioned thy God for nothing, forasmuch as thou hast not, in thy sinful state, petitioned him for thy conversion, for an humble mind, for a contrite heart, for the grace to renounce thy evil habits, and to stifle and conquer thy vicious inclinations? This, however, was the signal grace, the grace of graces which thou oughtest to have asked for and sought with all diligence.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s022", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Further: when the Saviour of the world assures us, in the gospel, that whatever we shall ask in his name shall be granted, he supposes we shall ask according to the rule which he hath himself laid down. For, as Tertullian well observes, it is he himself who regulates prayer, and quickens it with his Spirit, communicating to it the special power and privilege of penetrating into the highest heaven, and moving to compassion the heart of God, by laying before his all-seeing eyes the weaknesses and miseries of mankind. Now what is that rule, that divine rule by which Christ Jesus commands us to pray? Seek first, says he, the kingdom of God and his righteousness, and you shall want for nothing. Ask of my Father the sanctification of his name, the coming of his kingdom, the fulfilling of his will, not the material bread which serves for aliment, and then will I assist you. But if you run counter to this regulation, and, by an attachment to the world unbecoming your profession, you seek earthly bread before the kingdom of God, put your affiance no longer in my merits, however infinite, as your prayer how fervent soever it may be is not calculated according to the plan which I have sketched: \u201cSeek first the kingdom of God and his righteousness.\u201d Matt. vi. Not but we may ask for temporal blessings: the church herself is wont to do so. But let us ask for them like the church; let us ask for them after having first, and above all things, asked for spiritual favours; let us ask for the blessing conferred on Jacob and not on Esau. What a noble allegory doth the example of these two brothers exhibit! Mind the application I make of it to my subject. They had each to his share the dew af [sic] heaven and the fat of the earth. Where lies the difference; and by what special sign doth the scripture make appear the election of Jacob and reprobation of Esau? Ah! Christians, by this\u2014that in the blessing conferred on Jacob, the dew of heaven is expressed antecedently to the fat of the earth: \u201cGod give thee of the dew of heaven and fatness of the earth;\u201d whereas in the other, the fat of the earth is mentioned previously to the dew of heaven: \u201cIn the fatness of the earth and in the dew of heaven from above shall be thy blessing.\u201d Gen. xxvii.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s023", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "This is what every day happens among us, and what discriminates prayers truly Christian from those which are not. A righteous man and a worldly-minded man put up their prayers in the same church, and before the same altar; but one prays righteously, and the other like a worldling. How? Doth one beg only the influence of grace, and the other nothing but earthly advantages? No: for it may so happen that the righteous man, with the influence of grace shall supplicate sometimes for an increase of fortune, as well as the worldly man; and that the worldly man shall ask with an increase of fortune the influence of grace, as well as the righteous man. But the worldly man, instigated by the spirit of the world, places an increase of fortune before the influence of grace, \u201cthe fatness of earth and the dew of heaven;\u201d but the righteous man, prompted by the Spirit of God, prefers the influence of grace to an increase of fortune, \u201cthe dew of heaven and the fatness of the earth.\u201d He says to God: Lord, sanctify me; make me chaste, charitable, merciful, patient; give me the dew of heaven, and grant me forthwith the good things of this life, so far as they may conduce to my eternal welfare; give me the fatness of the earth. But the worldly man says: \u201cLord, make me rich, great, powerful; give me the fatness of the earth; neither refuse me the grace which is necessary to the leading of a virtuous life\u2014give me the dew of heaven. An accursed prayer, which when we offer up, it is not to be wondered at, that God should reject it and hold it in execration.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s024", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "3rdly. But let us proceed to the fountain-head; and, in order to discover on what rests the important doctrine I am expounding, take this sound principle of St. Cyprian with you: that your prayers have no virtue, but inasmuch as they are united with the intercession of Christ. For, it is only of Christ that we can say with St. Paul: \u201cHe was heard for his own reverence.\u201d Heb. v. When the Almighty hears us, it is not in consideration either of what we are or of what we merit, as we are nothing of ourselves and can merit nothing; but in consideration of his only begotten Son, who prayed for us before we could pray for ourselves. Taking this for granted, how can those prayers, by which we petition for the things of this world in preference to heaven be acceptable to God, whereas, in that case they have no conformity or connexion with the prayers of the God-man who became our Mediator? What did he ask for us? You know it well. That we might be united by the bond of charity, \u201cthat we might be one;\u201d (John xvii.;) that, without ostentation or dissimulation, we might be sanctified in truth; that, living in the midst and hurry of the world, according to our vocation and situation in life, we might have over ourselves a watchful eye, and be fortunate enough to keep clear of its iniquity: \u201cI pray not,\u201d says our Redeemer, \u201cthat thou shouldest take them out of the world, but that thou shouldest keep them from evil.\u201d John xvii. But what is our practice? We ask Almighty God for riches, honours, a vain reputation, an easy life; and without asking for them after salvation, or relatively to salvation, we ask for those riches, to wallow in plenty; for these honours, to make a figure in the world; for this reputation, to be known and distinguished; for this easy life, to enjoy the sweets of it; that is, we ask for those things for which our Saviour never meant to ask. And why did he not ask for them? Mind this reason. Because (says St. Cyprian) he could not but pray conformably to the end for which he was sent. Now he was sent as a Saviour, and his mission regarded the salvation only of mankind. It was, therefore, for the salvation only of mankind that he could labour, that he could suffer, that he could merit, and, by a necessary consequence, it was for the salvation only of mankind, and for whatever bore relation thereto, that he could pray.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s025", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Wherefore, Christians, you ask but obtain not, because you ask not with Christ our Lord, and because, were your prayers, independently of this, capable to produce the intended consequence, you might say that favours were conferred on you without your being indebted to your Redeemer for them, which were blasphemy, according to the maxims of our religion. And on this basis it is that St. Augustin rests his argument, when he proves so solidly, that the things of this life are by no means the object of Christian hope. Let no one deceive himself, (says this holy doctor,) and vainly promise himself temporal felicity, because he appertains to Christ our Lord. Christ did not choose us for that end, nor call us to faith on that condition. Without failing in his word, he may leave us in poverty, debasement, and sufferings. True it is, he engaged his word to present our prayers before the throne of God; but he supposed we should pray, like Christians, for heaven where he placed our inheritance. And this excellent way of reasoning the same father made use of afterwards in opposition to the raillery of the pagans. You object to us, (says he,) that notwithstanding our prayers, we live in indigence and want of all things. But to wipe off thoroughly this reproach, and to justify our God, suffice it to say, that when we put up our prayers to heaven, we pray not precisely for the things appertaining to this life, but to eternity. If therefore, we are poor and needy in this world, the distressful situation in which we live, not only is no proof that our prayers are unprofitable, but gives us an assurance that the fruit of them is reserved for an immortal life.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s026", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Such was the reply of the great St. Augustin, which he concludes with a reflection extremely affecting. In this (says he) we should admire the liberality of our God. He limits not his favours to the things of this world, because they are beneath us; because they are insufficient to satisfy our desires; because they are disproportionate to the nobleness of our being, and to the worth of our prayers. He would not put us in the category of children, who are pleased with trifles, and things of no moment; nor in that of idolaters, whose moral virtues he rewards in this life with apparent happiness. He will himself be our happiness\u2014our reward. Ah! my brethren, let us therefore beware of mistaking in the choice of the things we ask. Let us place our confidence in the word of our God, who hath faithfully promised that he himself will be our reward; and as he hath promised, let us only petition that he would give himself to us. Many there are, St. Augustin tells us, who hope in God, but who hope for everything except God. Let us dread a separation so disadvantageous to us; and, as we hope for nothing but from the hand of God, let us hope for nothing but God, or things relating to God.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s027", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "4thly. But I ask not for temporal favours of God; my prayers are offered up for supernatural grace\u2014the grace of salvation; and yet I have it not. No, beloved hearers, you have it not I know; because even in that respect you are guilty of a third abuse in prayer, which perhaps you perceive not, but which I now shall make appear and lay before you.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s028", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "It is this; that instead of regarding prayer as the instrument which God hath put into our hands in order to procure the true grace of salvation, that is real and possible grace\u2014grace regulated and proportionally adjusted to the divine appointment; we employ it for the imploring of chimerical grace, superfluous grace\u2014grace adapted to our own taste and mistaken notions. My [m]eaning is this: we pray, and as we think with a true desire of obtaining salvation; but by a blind confidence, we rely on prayer, as though it were sufficient without good works; as though all depended on it; as though Christ Jesus in commanding us to pray, did not also command us to watch and do good; as though there were graces which might and should save us without our own co-operation. We pray, and ask the grace of a good death, persuaded that no more than to pray is required; and thus we remain without care or solicitude, or the trouble to merit it or to prepare for it by a good life. We pray, and ask the grace of penance and sanctification; but the grace we ask is for the future only; grace which should remove all difficulties and dangers, and leave no laborious endeavour to be made or obstacle to be surmounted; miraculous grace which should transport us like St. Paul, and not dispose us by little and little, and oblige us to proceed; grace which should accompany us wherever we went, which should be ready at hand on all occasions; which should allow us to expose ourselves to every kind of hazard, and not grace which it should import us to attend to watchfully, and make the best advantage of. That is, in short, we ask for grace that would change the whole order of divine providence, and annul the dispensation by which our eternal lot is ascertained.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s029", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "And now, Christians, let us close this first part with the prophet\u2019s prayer: \u201cOne thing have I desired of the Lord, that will I seek after; that I may dwell in the house of the Lord.\u201d Ps. xxvi. For (adds St. Augustin) I now perceive and thoroughly comprehend the reasons that induced you so often to reject the prayers of your servant. In order to answer the designs of your mercy I should have put up my prayers for things not common to me, and to pagans, and to impious men. You wanted that my prayers should distinguish me from the enemies of your holy name. Nevertheless I find that between their prayers and mine the difference was, that they for the most part obtained their request, and you generally refused me the things I petitioned for, either because they were contrary in themselves to salvation, or I asked not for them with a view to salvation. But in this, O Lord, I confess you favoured me; because those favours would have drawn me to perversion, whereas the paternal scourge of your justice hath been my amendment. By being happy in the world, I should have forgotten you the more easily; and if my wishes had been crowned with prosperity, I should have trodden in the footsteps of bad men. Accordingly, my God, far from complaining that you gave me the denial, I bless your holy name and reckon it as a favour that you heard me not according to the desires of my heart, but according to the order which your wisdom had established, and my salvation required. But henceforward, my God, you will hear my supplications, because they will only be offered up for things appertaining to eternity; because among the graces of salvation which I shall ask, I shall ask only for those which may be profitable to me\u2014which may operate the most securely, and which may lead me the most directly to everlasting bliss. Thus, beloved Christians, the words of Jesus Christ will be fulfilled in our regard: we shall ask and shall receive. Whereas we receive not, either because we ask not for the things for which we ought to ask, as you have seen in the first part; or because we ask not for them in the manner in which we ought to ask for them, as I shall show you in the second.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s030", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Part II. If God hears my prayers, it is on certain necessary and essential conditions. But, beloved hearers, in what manner soever he is pleased to deal by us, and in his providential wisdom to dispose and order things, it would be a gross error to suppose that the conditions required to the due performance of prayer were an obstacle to our wishes, and a motive which God used to refuse us his gifts. Ah! (cries St. Augustin) heaven forbid we should ever entertain or admit such notions, as nothing is more contrary to the goodness of God! He who does not stop (according to the scriptures) the course of his mercies, at the very time that we provoke him to anger: \u201cWill he in his wrath keep in his mercies?\u201d (Ps. lxxvi.) he who (according to the royal prophet) waits not for our prayers but beholds them, hearkening to mere desires: \u201cOur Lord hath heard the desire of the poor;\u201d (Ps. x.;) he who is so tender of us as to be affected with the very preparation of the heart: \u201cThe preparation of the heart thy ear hath heard;\u201d (Ps. x.;) will he (if I may venture so to speak) be uncomplying when we invoke him with affection and sincerity? So far is he from availing himself of his greatness in the intercourse which he permits us to have with him by prayer, that we ought to confide that he will too indulgently and with too great condescension pass by our weaknesses, frailties, and imperfections. It cannot be denied that prayer, to be efficacious, should have certain qualities; but herein I maintain we cannot say that God withdraws his promises or withholds his graces. The reason is, that of these qualities there is not one which will not, on examination, be found easy in practice; not one of which the necessity is not justified by right reason; not one which is not required in a proportional degree between man and man; and (as I have already observed to you) not one of which the woman of Canaan hath not given us an example, and is not represented as a striking model. For, in short, (says St. Chrysostom, in the excellent homily which he composed on this subject,) what conditions doth God require for the infallibility of prayer? Humility, confidence, perseverance, attention of mind, and affection of heart. Now is there anything in all this, I do not say impracticable or impossible, but painful or burdensome?", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s031", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "1st. Is anything more reasonable or even more natural, than to pray with the disposition of an humble mind? Is it possible to have a just idea of prayer, and forget this fundamental rule in our prayers? Do we otherwise offer our petitions to the princes and monarchs of the earth? Do we think it a hardship when we ask for favours, to pay homage and respect? And, if by this homage and this respect we compass our designs, do we murmur or complain as though our submission was ill bestowed? Do we say that they prize their favours too high, when they refuse them to the rash audacious man who demands them haughtily? And why should we make that objection to God, as it is much more reasonable, and consequently more easy to humble ourselves in his presence than in the presence of a man? Did the woman of Canaan make a difficulty to fall prostrate at the feet of Christ Jesus, and to pay him adoration? Was it a mighty effort to confess her unworthiness in that posture? And did she deem it a hardship to bear the repulse to which she was exposed? No, no, (says the Saviour of the world,) \"it is not good to take the children's bread and cast it to dogs.\" Matt. xv. Could any comparison be more humiliating? But however humiliating it may be or seem to be, doth it seem to affect or afflict this woman? What do I say? Doth she not acknowledge the truth of those words by making the application of them to herself: \"Yea, Lord?\" Matt. xv.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s032", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Such was this woman's way of praying: but which is ours? She was a pagan, and the pagan humbles herself: we are Christians, and bring with us to prayer a spirit of pride which we cherish in our breasts, at the same time that we are going to acknowledge our miseries and wants. And because this spirit hath dominion over us, we pray with presumption, as if God was obliged to consider us particularly, to treat us with distinction, and make account of our prayers. To say nothing of the ostentation which too frequently accompanies our offerings, and which instead of prevailing on God to hear us, induces him to punish us: to say nothing of the luxury we bear about us and display even in the house of God\u2014of the grandeur and self-sufficiency we retain there; of the vain attitudes and artful negligence we affect there, directly contrary to the manner of a suppliant; all which (according to the sacred text) makes our prayers abominable in the sight of God, as he hates nothing more than \"the poor man proud.\" Waving a detail of these matters, we ask favours of God\u2014but we ask them as due; which if he graciously deign to grant we are puffed up and elated, but which if he refuse we murmur and complain. We ask them, and having received them, forget who it was that granted them. We employ them and use them without referring them to him. Can we then be surprised that he should have no bowels of compassion for us? Would it not be to dispense his favours and graces with a lavish hand\u2014to bestow them indifferently on the proud and the humble?", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s033", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "2ndly. Is anything more just than to pray with sentiments of a lively confidence? It is our sovereign Lord, our gracious God, who in his infinite mercy will be prayed to in that manner, and declares himself glorified by such confidence; who in a thousand places of the inspired writings attributes rather to it than to the divine mercy, (take not offence at this assertion, it is sound and orthodox,) when I say rather than to his very mercy the miraculous virtue and efficacy of prayer; not saying to those who have recourse to him and call upon him: it is my goodness and my power\u2014but it is thy faith and thy confidence which hath been thy salvation: \"Thy faith hath made thee whole.\" How could he have proposed a more advantageous condition? Did not the Canaanite, though an infidel, directly accept it? That openness of heart which she discovered to Christ by speaking to him in person: \"Have mercy on me, O Lord;\" (Matt. xv.;) that tender and affectionate motive which she used, with a view to move him, by calling him \"Son of David;\" those cries which she redoubled in proportion as the apostles reprimanded her importunity and commanded her to be silent: \"Send her away, for she crieth after us;\" (Matt. xv.;) that confidence with which she disclaimed all pretensions to the bread on the table, provided they furnished her with the crumbs that fell from it: that is, (according to St. Jerom's explanation,) to be resigned to, and content with the slightest efforts of our Saviour's power, convinced that it would be enough for the miraculous deed which she beseeched him to perform: \"For the whelps partake of the crumbs which fall from their master's table.\" Matt. xv. Did not all this proceed from a soul that sincerely trusted in the God she invoked? What would she have done if, already a Christian, she had known Christ Jesus as perfectly as we do? If, instead of knowing him for the Son of David, she had known him for the Son of the living God? And yet is it not true that, notwithstanding all the ideas with which our religion furnisheth us of this God-man, scarce ever do we pray to him in that simple but heroical manner which the apostle hath marked out; I mean with faith, and without any doubt: \"Let him ask in faith, nothing wavering.\" James i.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s034", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Although Christ Jesus is so powerful to assist us, and although to overcome our incredulity and diffidence he engaged himself to us by the most solemn oath, swearing by himself; he who could not (as St. Paul testifies) have sworn by anything greater than himself; yet our diffidence and incredulity are more prevalent and carry it. We believe a man on his bare word, and we believe not the promise of God. We pray, but are inwardly at the same time disturbed; harbour vain disquietudes and carking agonies, and give ourselves up to a secret despondency. We have recourse to God, but always in extremity and when every other means of relief hath failed. We depend not so much on God as on ourselves, and place greater reliance on our prudence than our prayers. A strange infatuation, which St. Ambrose deplored, and which thoroughly manifests God's displeasure, when he withdraws his arm, and will not stretch it forth to our aid and relief.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s035", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "3rdly. Is anything more expedient than to pray with perseverance? Is not God master of his own gifts? Who but himself hath power to dispose of them? Cannot he put them at what prices he pleases? And are not his graces sufficiently precious to be frequently asked and sought with perseverance? When Christ by his silence made trial of the woman I have mentioned from the gospel, \"who answered not a word;\" (Matt. xv.;) when he seemed by a severe and mortifying refusal inclined to neglect her and banish her from his presence; and when in her hearing he told the apostles that he was not sent for her: \"I was not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Israel;\" (Matt. xv.;) did she cease, on that account to pray, to solicit, to press with earnestness? No, Christians; the resistance of Christ increased her perseverance, and her perseverance outdid the resistance of Christ. The mystery was soon unfolded to her. She quickly comprehended the designs and inclinations of this God and Saviour; and seeing the necessity (as I may say) she was under of urging him, and even urging him by opposing real earnestness to seeming harshness, she broke through, in some measure, the laws of Providence; she deserved, though a foreigner, to be treated like an Israelite; she obtained a double miracle\u2014the deliverance of her daughter from the evil spirit, and her own conversion to the true faith. O the charity of my bountiful God, (cries one of the fathers,) how adorable in your dissimulations and in the stratagems which you use to combat, in appearance, the very persons for whom in fact you combat. Wherefore, despair not, (adds he,) O Christian, who have begun to struggle with your God in prayer; for that kind of violence he loves to suffer, and it gives him pleasure to be disarmed by your hand. Neither must we imagine, (concludes he) my brethren, that this God of mercy can be invincible against us, who became, by a most astonishing prodigy, \"weak, even unto death,\" upon our account.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s036", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Such were the sentiments of the ancient fathers upon this head. But we, you well know, being influenced by an error directly opposite, hurried into the devious ways by frivolousness and volatility of mind, yield to God against his will. We yield to him, when he wishes, himself, to yield to us. We grow weary of telling him that we are poor, distressed, and in need of his assistance; and yet he is much pleased with our importunity. Assiduity of this kind is tiresome to us, provokes our patience, and causes disgust. We would have it be enough to present ourselves once. Delay appals us; and often on the point of obtaining our wishes, we lose all the merit and advantage of them. And tell me, at whose door ought the fault to be laid\u2014at God's, or at our own?", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s037", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "4thly. In fine, is anything more necessary and essential in our prayers to God, than attention of mind and affection of heart? I shall conclude with this point, which of all is much the most important. Attention of mind and affection of heart are what I call, with St. Thomas, the soul of prayer, without which it cannot any more subsist than a body without a soul, to quicken it and give it motion. For, what is prayer? Let us not here examine theological definitions, but the dictates of reason and good sense, and the common notions which mankind have of this holy exercise. What, then, is prayer? A conversation with God, in which the soul admitted and, if I may so express myself, introduced into the sanctuary, lays her wants before the Deity, represents her weaknesses, discovers her temptations, begs pardon for her unfaithfulness. And doth not all this suppose mental recollection, and inward sentiment? If, therefore, it so happen, that while I treat with God, my mind so wanders as absolutely and voluntarily to lose this internal attention and devotion, whatever else I may be said to do\u2014I cannot be said to pray. Although I should sing laud and glory to the Lord; although I should spend whole days and nights at the foot of the altar; although, after the expression and example of David, I should be as it were fixed and glued to the ground\u2014if I cease to be attentive, I cease to pray. And from hence, Christians, the angelical doctor made three deductions, to which I shall not make any addition, but which I earnestly beseech you to ponder for your edification. They will fully set forth the reasons why our prayers are disregarded and inefficacious in the presence of God.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s038", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "The first deduction. As it cannot be denied that attention is essentially requisite to prayer, we may say with great reason, but with still greater sorrow, that the exercise of it is, as it were, annihilated in the Christian world; because, if sometimes we seem to pray, it is without reflection. For, to what is all our piety reduced? To some prayers which we say with a dissipated and almost always absent mind. We move the lips, not like the godly mother of Samuel, on whom Heli, the high priest, passed so rash a judgment, but like the Jewish people, to whom God objected that their hearts were far from him while they praised him with their mouths. Thus our prayers are mostly hypocritical, and our blessed Saviour might well say to us what he said to the pharisees: \"Ye hypocrites, well did Isaias prophecy of you, saying; this people honoureth me with their lips, but their heart is far from me.\" Matt. xv. This disorder is not peculiar to the lower sort of people, whose intellectual coarseness is unhappily such, that day by day they say their prayers without praying, that is, without thinking either of what they say or of what they ask; nor to the devout sex, who, though addicted to prayer, make it their business to say a great deal without fixing the natural levity of their minds, or being at much pains to apply attentively. Men the most enlightened and the best instructed, men consecrated to God, the very ministers of God, by a deplorable perversion, from a habit of praying, pray not at all, and, instead of perfecting, corrupt and destroy so holy a practice.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s039", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "The second deduction. As prayer essentially includes attention, it follows that attention is, itself, of precept in those prayers which are strictly enjoined us. So that to pronounce the words is not then sufficient, but that a remarkable and voluntary evagation of mind should be looked upon as a grievous and deadly offence. Now this, my brethren, I say both on your account and my own, for that one of the principal engagements of your profession and mine consists therein, and for that vocal prayer is a kind of attribute which the church requires of us every day. And, indeed, it were strange that this action, in itself so holy\u2014an action that naturally leads to holiness\u2014should serve to condemn us, and that the origin of grace should be the source of reprobation. Nor let it be forgotten, that when we submitted to the obligation of reciting the Divine Office, we bound ourselves to perform an act of religion; that an act of religion is not a practice merely external; and that as the church, by enjoining confession enjoins contrition, so she enjoins attention by enjoining prayer. Whether this obligation arises from the precept of the church immediately, as some very able divines maintain, or from the precept naturally conjoined with that of the church, in virtue of which God commands us to perform holily and worthily the thing prescribed, as others imagine: however it be, the difference is only a scholastic question; and in either opinion we equally violate the laws of religion. Ah! my brethren, let us not draw down that curse upon us with which the prophet, in the excess of his zeal, threatened the sinner, when he said: \"Let his prayer become sin.\" And is it not probable that this is applicable to numbers of those who attend at the altar? With far greater reason ought we to accuse ourselves than the great St. Augustin, who condemned himself for negligence in this behalf.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s040", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "The third and last deduction. God, therefore, with justice, rejects our prayers, as they resemble nothing so little as prayer\u2014and as, far from honouring him, they are offensive to him and irritate him against us. For, what an injustice, beloved hearers! You expect that God will take heed of your prayers, when you chose to pray, and are heedless yourself of the words you utter. You say with the prophet: \u201cO Lord, give ear unto my words, hearken to the voice of my prayer, (Ps. v.,) and suffer your mind at the same time to rove through various and useless objects. You desire him to speak to you, and you speak not to him; you desire him to hearken to you, and you hearken not to him: you hearken not to yourself, you wilfully distract yourself.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-s041", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843", "text": "Let us only reform, beloved Christians, in this one point, and a thorough reformation of life will ensue; for, according to St. Augustin, \u201che knows how to live well who knows how to pray well.\u201d Why are we subject to so many lapses? Either because we pray not at all, or not as we should; and this, because we are unwilling to relinquish our inordinate ways, and fear to be led into the right path. Let us ask things of God worthy both of him and of ourselves, and in a manner worthy both of him and of ourselves. In a word, let us ask him as we ought to do, for graces and favours and we shall obtain them; but, above all, let us ask for the gift of prayer. Let us say like the apostle, Lord teach us to pray; our weakness is such that we cannot, without you, lay our wants before you, or implore your assistance in a proper manner. It is only yourself that can make us efficaciously feel our wants, that can bring us to the foot of your altar with a view to represent to you; that can inspire us with words and sentiments proper to move your heart. Give us, therefore, my God, yourself, that knowledge so necessary for us; and by a special grace in which are included, as in their origin, all the other graces, teach us to use prayer in order to procure the graces of conversion, of sanctification, of salvation, which may lead us to the mansions of eternal repose.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n001", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "This is an abridged historical counterpart of the French sermon, not an unabridged translation. Carroll's underlying French edition has not been identified here; the comparisons describe differences from the 1716 French edition used in this library without establishing the cause of each difference. His heading supplies no occasion. The French Thursday heading and the separate calendar browse mapping remain unchanged. Duplicate Latin/French quotations often become a single English quotation; the French edition's marginal references remain its own apparatus, available with the AI version or in Original only.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n002", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French explicitly concludes the introduction by asking heavenly aid through Mary's intercession and saying 'Ave, Maria.' Carroll replaces that invocation with 'in the usual form.'", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n003", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French adds a qualified claim that prayer makes man's word 'even more powerful' in a certain respect: God commands creatures, while through prayer God himself listens to man's voice. It supports this with the Joshua 10 quotation 'the Lord obeying the voice of a man.' Carroll omits that claim and explanation, retaining only the comparison of equal power.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n004", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French concludes this example with 'even the most adorable sacrifice, even the holiest prayers of his Church.' Carroll reverses the order and substitutes 'sacrament of the altar' for 'sacrifice.' Both editions' surrounding discussion concerns attempts to enlist the Church's prayer and sacrifice for unjust purposes; Carroll earlier retains 'the very sacrifice of the church.'", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n005", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "Carroll condenses the French's classical example, omitting the names Pompey and Caesar and the quotation about malign divinities. The warning that criminal wishes can destroy families remains.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n006", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French separately rebukes the worldly person for never asking detachment from and contempt for the world, then rebukes the sinner for never asking conversion and a contrite heart. Carroll combines the addresses and retains the conversion appeal, omitting the distinct request for detachment. The French identifies 'those two disciples' as the sons of Zebedee.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n007", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French says not to ask for material bread first. Its point is the order of petitions, not a prohibition of asking bodily necessities. Carroll drops 'first' from this clause, although the following sentences preserve that distinction and explicitly permit requests for temporal blessings.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n008", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French reads 'Excellente oraison' ('excellent prayer'); Carroll calls it 'this excellent way of reasoning.' This library retains the French reading without silently changing it to 'raison' ('reasoning'). The historical version's wording is a separate witness, not proof that the French should be emended.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n009", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "Printed page 174 has an ink-obscured initial letter in 'meaning'; 'eaning' is visible. The bracketed [m] is supplied from the immediate context, not claimed as a fully legible letter. Carroll's printed '4thly.' is followed by 'a third abuse'; both are retained.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n010", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French conclusion explicitly permits asking for other goods insofar as they are subordinate to eternal goods. Carroll compresses the conclusion and does not retain that clause here; his earlier discussion also permits properly ordered temporal requests.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n011", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "Carroll combines the Part II heading and two French paragraphs, retaining the listed conditions of prayer but condensing their introduction. Citations belong to their respective editions: the French margin prints Psal.6. here; Carroll prints Ps. x. Neither locator is silently harmonized.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n012", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French additionally asks whether God should hear the proud person's prayer at the cost of his own glory. Carroll compresses this part of the humility argument.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n013", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "The French opening refers to what Christ has done to help us; Carroll instead emphasizes Christ's power to assist. The French ends by saying this justifies God's conduct toward us, whereas Carroll writes that it 'manifests God's displeasure'. These are differences in historical wording, not changes made for this library.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n014", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "Carroll prints 'the woman I have mentioned from the gospel,' followed by 'who answered not a word'; this wording can ambiguously attach the silence to the woman. The French explicitly attributes the silence to Jesus Christ; the woman continues petitioning, as Carroll's surrounding paragraph also describes. His next paragraph condenses the French discussion of perseverance and omits its Sirach and Isaiah exhortations to wait.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n015", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "Where Carroll says they should consider 'what they say' while praying, the French asks them to consider to whom they are speaking. Both passages insist on attentive prayer and criticize deliberate distraction.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n016", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "Carroll prints 'vocal prayer is a kind of attribute'; the French calls vocal prayer a sacred tribute that the Church requires daily. 'Attribute' is retained as historical wording, not a correction to 'tribute.' The passage's qualifications concerning serious fault, voluntary and notable distraction, and the Divine Office remain in Carroll.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n017", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "Carroll shortens the French's sequence of Psalm invocations and prints an opening quotation before 'O Lord' without a closing mark, followed by '(Ps. v.,)'. The French says 'you do not understand yourself'; Carroll writes 'you wilfully distract yourself.' These historical readings and punctuation are retained.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-careme-priere-carroll-n018", "label": "A. Carroll \u00b7 1843 editorial note", "text": "Carroll prints 'Let us say like the apostle' (singular), 'with a view to represent to you' without an expressed object, and 'Give us, therefore, my God, yourself, that knowledge'. The French has the Apostles (plural), asks God to bring us to his altar to represent our miseries, and asks him himself to give this necessary knowledge. Carroll's awkward syntax is retained; his ending 'eternal repose' renders the French conclusion 'glory, &c.' without reproducing its abbreviation.", "translation_id": "carroll-1843"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "On Prayer", "topics": ["prayer", "salvation", "grace", "humility", "faith", "confidence", "perseverance", "providence", "conversion"], "url": "sermons/bourdaloue-careme-priere.html"}, {"author": "Louis Bourdaloue", "calendar": "dayKey:misereris", "calendar_label": "Ash Wednesday", "centuries": [17, 18], "composition": "Approximately late seventeenth century\u20131704; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "bourdaloue-cendres-ceremonie", "language": "fr", "language_label": "French", "metadata": ["Louis Bourdaloue, Sermons du P\u00e8re Bourdaloue, de la Compagnie de J\u00e9sus, pour le Caresme, tome premier, nouvelle \u00e9dition (Paris: aux d\u00e9pens de Rigaud, 1716), Autre sermon pour le mercredi des Cendres: Sur la C\u00e9r\u00e9monie des Cendres, pp. 52\u201396.", "Internet Archive; John M. Kelly Library Rare Books, University of Toronto.", "Ash Wednesday", "Ash Wednesday", "Exodus 9", "Philippians 4", "Ecclesiasticus 10", "Psalm 48 (the edition's numbering)", "Job 13", "Ezekiel 28", "Micah 6", "Psalm 43 (the edition's numbering)", "1 Peter 5", "Joel 2", "Ecclesiasticus 19", "Romans 12", "Galatians 5", "2 Corinthians 4", "1 Corinthians 9", "Genesis 6", "Job 28", "Jeremiah 6"], "occasion": "Mercredi des Cendres", "occasion_detail": "Ash Wednesday", "occasion_label": "Ash Wednesday", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "In this Ash Wednesday sermon, Bourdaloue presents ashes as a reminder of mortality and a summons to penitence. Its two parts call for the humbling of pride and the mortification of the body. He addresses converts newly received into the Catholic Church."}, {"anchor": "b-h001", "label": "French original", "text": "AUTRE SERMON POUR LE MERCREDI DES CENDRES."}, {"anchor": "b-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ANOTHER SERMON FOR ASH WEDNESDAY", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-h002", "label": "French original", "text": "Sur la C\u00e9r\u00e9monie des Cendres."}, {"anchor": "b-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the Ceremony of Ashes", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-q001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Pulvis es, & in pulverem revert\u00e9ris."}, {"anchor": "b-q001", "label": "English", "text": "You are dust, and to dust you will return.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-q002", "label": "French original", "text": "Vous \u00eates poussi\u00e9re, & vous retournerez en poussi\u00e9re. Dans la Genese, chap. 3."}, {"anchor": "b-q002", "label": "English", "text": "You are dust, and you will return to dust. In Genesis, chapter 3.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p001", "label": "French original", "text": "CE sont les m\u00e9morables paroles, que Dieu dit au premier homme dans le moment de sa d\u00e9sob\u00e9issance: & ce sont celles que l'Eglise adresse en particulier \u00e0 chacun de nous par la bouche de ses ministres dans la c\u00e9r\u00e9monie de ce jour. Paroles de mal\u00e9diction, dans le sens que Dieu les pronon\u00e7a; mais paroles de gr\u00e2ce & de salut, dans la fin que l'Eglise se propose, en nous les faisant entendre. Paroles terribles & foudroyantes pour l'homme p\u00e9cheur, puisqu'elles lui signifi\u00e9rent l'arr\u00eat de sa condamnation; mais paroles douces & consolantes pour le p\u00e9cheur p\u00e9nitent, puisqu'elles lui enseignent la voie de sa conversion & de sa justification. Ainsi, remarque saint Chrysostome, Dieu en a-t-il souvent us\u00e9, & s'est-il servi du m\u00eame moyen, tant\u00f4t pour imprimer aux hommes la terreur de ses jugemens, & tant\u00f4t pour leur faire \u00e9prouver l'efficace de ses mis\u00e9ricordes."}, {"anchor": "b-p001", "label": "English", "text": "THESE are the memorable words God spoke to the first man at the moment of his disobedience; they are also the words the Church addresses to each of us personally, through the mouths of her ministers, in today's ceremony. They were words of malediction in the sense in which God pronounced them, but words of grace and salvation in the purpose the Church has in making us hear them. They were terrible, thunderous words for sinful man, announcing the sentence of his condemnation; but they are gentle, consoling words for the repentant sinner, teaching him the way to conversion and justification. As Saint John Chrysostom observes, God has often acted in this way, using the same means at one time to impress upon men the terror of his judgments, and at another to make them experience the power of his mercies.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p002", "label": "French original", "text": "Je ne s\u00e7ais, Chr\u00e9tiens, si vous avez jamais fait r\u00e9flexion \u00e0 ce que nous lisons dans le livre de l'Exode. Ecoutez-le: l'application vous en paro\u00eetra naturelle, & elle convient parfaitement \u00e0 mon sujet. Quand Dieu voulut punir l'Egypte, il commanda \u00e0 Mo\u00efse de prendre dans sa main une poign\u00e9e de cendres; & en pr\u00e9sence de Pharaon, de la r\u00e9pandre sur tout le peuple: Tollite manus plenas cineris, & spargat illum Moyses coram Pharaone. L'Ecriture ajoute que cette cendre ainsi dispers\u00e9e, fut comme la mati\u00e8re, dont Dieu forma ces fl\u00e9aux qui afflig\u00e8rent toute l'Egypte, & qui y caus\u00e9rent une d\u00e9solation si g\u00e9n\u00e9rale: Sitque pulvis super omnem terram AEgypti. A en juger par l'apparence, Dieu fait aujourd'hui le m\u00eame commandement aux ministres de son Eglise. Il veut que les Pr\u00eatres de la loi de gr\u00e2ce, comme dispensateurs de ses myst\u00e9res, prennent la cendre de dessus l'autel, & qu'ils la r\u00e9pandent solemnellement sur tout le peuple Chr\u00e9tien: Tollite manus plenas cineris. Mais dans l'intention de Dieu, l'effet de cette c\u00e9r\u00e9monie est par rapport au Christianisme, bien diff\u00e9rent de ce qu'elle op\u00e9ra dans l'ancienne loi. Car au lieu que Mo\u00efse & Aaron ne r\u00e9pandirent la cendre sur les Egyptiens, que pour leur faire sentir le poids de la col\u00e8re de Dieu; que pour marquer \u00e0 Pharaon, qu'il \u00e9toit r\u00e9prouv\u00e9 de Dieu; que pour dompter l'impi\u00e9t\u00e9 & l'endurcissement de ce Monarque livr\u00e9 d\u00e8s-lors \u00e0 la vengeance de Dieu: par une conduite toute oppos\u00e9e, les Pr\u00eatres de la loi nouvelle ne r\u00e9pandent aujourd'hui la cendre sur nos t\u00eates, que pour nous attirer les gr\u00e2ces & les faveurs du m\u00eame Dieu, que pour nous mettre en \u00e9tat & nous rendre capables d'en \u00e9prouver la bont\u00e9, que pour exciter dans nos coeurs les sentimens d'une v\u00e9ritable p\u00e9nitence. C'est ce que j'entreprends de vous faire voir, & par o\u00f9 je commence \u00e0 m'acquitter aupr\u00e8s de vous du minist\u00e9re dont Dieu m'a charg\u00e9 & que j'ai \u00e0 remplir pendant tout ce saint tems du Car\u00eame."}, {"anchor": "b-p002", "label": "English", "text": "Christians, I do not know whether you have ever reflected on what we read in Exodus. Listen: you will find the application natural and perfectly suited to my subject. When God wished to punish Egypt, he commanded Moses to take a handful of ashes and scatter it over all the people in Pharaoh's presence: \u2018Take handfuls of ashes, and let Moses scatter them before Pharaoh.\u2019 Scripture adds that the ashes thus dispersed became, as it were, the material from which God formed the plagues that afflicted all Egypt and caused such general devastation: \u2018Let it become dust over the whole land of Egypt.\u2019 Judging by appearances, God gives the same command today to the ministers of his Church. He wishes the priests of the law of grace, as stewards of his mysteries, to take ashes from the altar and solemnly scatter them over all the Christian people: \u2018Take handfuls of ashes.\u2019 But in God's intention the effect of this ceremony in Christianity is very different from what it accomplished under the old law. Moses and Aaron scattered ashes over the Egyptians only to make them feel the weight of God's anger, to show Pharaoh that God had rejected him, and to subdue the impiety and hardness of this monarch already handed over to God's vengeance. By an entirely opposite course, the priests of the new law scatter ashes on our heads today only to draw down upon us the graces and favours of that same God, to prepare us and make us capable of experiencing his goodness, and to awaken in our hearts the sentiments of true repentance. This is what I undertake to show you, and how I begin to fulfil among you the ministry God has entrusted to me throughout this holy season of Lent.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p003", "label": "French original", "text": "Vous, mes Fr\u00e9res, qui par la mis\u00e9ricorde du Seigneur, avez enfin renonc\u00e9 au schisme pour vous r\u00e9unir \u00e0 l'Eglise. Vous, pour qui je suis particuli\u00e9rement envoy\u00e9, que je regarde ici comme le premier objet de mon z\u00e9le, & plaise au ciel que je puisse vous appeller un jour ma couronne & ma joie, Gaudium meum & corona mea! Vous, dis-je, nouvelle conqu\u00eate de la gr\u00e2ce de Jesus-Christ, apprenez \u00e0 respecter une de ces c\u00e9r\u00e9monies religieuses dont use l'Eglise Catholique, dans le sein de laquelle vous \u00eates rentr\u00e9s. Il y en a de plus essentielles: mais sans parler des autres, ou pour juger des autres par celle-ci comment l'h\u00e9r\u00e9sie l'a-t-elle p\u00fb rejetter, puisque l'Auteur m\u00eame de cette fatale division o\u00f9 vous f\u00fbtes malheureusement engag\u00e9s, reconno\u00eet que les c\u00e9r\u00e9monies peuvent aider la pi\u00e9t\u00e9 des fid\u00e9les; qu'il est non-seulement bon, mais n\u00e9cessaire d'en conserver quelques-unes; que pour n'\u00eatre plus dans la loi de Mo\u00efse, il ne s'ensuit pas qu'il les faille toutes abolir; qu'il est juste que par des signes ext\u00e9rieurs l'on montre les sentimens de religion qu'on a dans le coeur, & que d'\u00f4ter tout ce qui s'appelle c\u00e9r\u00e9monie, c'est mettre parmi le troupeau une confusion monstrueuse: Or entre les c\u00e9r\u00e9monies, quelle autre a d\u00fb moins blesser l'Eglise Protestante que la c\u00e9r\u00e9monie des cendres? Qu'a-t-elle de superstitieux? qu'a-t-elle qui ne soit autoris\u00e9 par l'Ecriture? quel souvenir nous est plus utile que celui de notre foiblesse, de notre n\u00e9ant; & n'est-ce pas l\u00e0 ce qu'elle nous remet devant les yeux? Cependant cette c\u00e9r\u00e9monie dont la simplicit\u00e9 & la saintet\u00e9 devoient \u00e9difier, a \u00e9t\u00e9 un scandale pour ces ministres que vous avez suivis. Ils l'ont r\u00e9prouv\u00e9e, & ils vous l'ont fait r\u00e9prouver comme eux, parce qu'ils ne la connoissoient point assez, ou parce qu'ils ne vous la faisoient point assez conno\u00eetre. Mais oublions le pass\u00e9; & b\u00e9nissons Dieu du pr\u00e9sent. B\u00e9nissons-le m\u00eame par avance de l'avenir, qui nous promet l'entier accomplissement de ce grand ouvrage que le Seigneur a commenc\u00e9. Nous nous unirons tous; & tous de concert nous conspirerons \u00e0 le soutenir, \u00e0 le perfectionner, \u00e0 le consommer. Qu'il me soit permis d'en faire ici le voeu solemnel & public; ce ne sera pas en vain. Oui, mon Dieu, votre oeuvre s'ach\u00e8vera, votre nom sera glorifi\u00e9, votre loi observ\u00e9e, votre Eglise reconnue: vous verserez sur mes Auditeurs vos gr\u00e2ces les plus abondantes; vous les verserez sur moi, & elles donneront de l'efficace \u00e0 mes paroles. C'est pour cela m\u00eame encore que je m'adresse \u00e0 Marie, & que je lui dis, Ave, Maria."}, {"anchor": "b-p003", "label": "English", "text": "You, my brethren, who by the Lord's mercy have at last renounced schism to be reunited with the Church; you for whom I have been especially sent, whom I regard here as the first object of my zeal\u2014and may heaven grant that one day I may call you my crown and my joy, \u2018my joy and my crown\u2019! You, I say, newly won by the grace of Jesus Christ, learn to respect one of those religious ceremonies used by the Catholic Church, into whose bosom you have returned. There are more essential ceremonies; but without discussing the others\u2014or, to judge the others by this one\u2014how could heresy reject it? For the very author of that fatal division in which you were unhappily involved acknowledges that ceremonies can assist the piety of the faithful; that it is not only good but necessary to retain some of them; that no longer being under the law of Moses does not mean we must abolish them all; that it is right to show by outward signs the religious sentiments of the heart; and that removing everything called ceremony introduces monstrous confusion into the flock. Now among these ceremonies, which should have offended the Protestant Church less than the ceremony of ashes? What is superstitious about it? What does it contain that is not authorized by Scripture? What reminder is more useful than that of our weakness and our nothingness? And is that not what it sets before our eyes? Yet this ceremony, whose simplicity and holiness ought to have edified, became a stumbling block to those ministers you followed. They condemned it and made you condemn it with them, because they did not understand it sufficiently, or did not make it sufficiently understood by you. But let us forget the past and bless God for the present. Let us even bless him in advance for the future, which promises the full accomplishment of the great work the Lord has begun. We shall all unite and work together to sustain, perfect, and complete it. Allow me to make that solemn and public vow here; it will not be in vain. Yes, my God, your work will be completed, your name glorified, your law observed, your Church acknowledged. You will pour out your most abundant graces upon my hearers; you will pour them upon me, and they will give power to my words. For this same purpose I turn also to Mary and say to her: Hail Mary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p004", "label": "French original", "text": "IL ne suffit pas pour la foi de croire de coeur, si l'on ne confesse de bouche: c'est ce que saint Paul nous d\u00e9clare en termes expr\u00e8s; & \u00e0 quoi j'ajoute, suivant la doctrine du m\u00eame Ap\u00f4tre, qu'il ne suffit pas pour la p\u00e9nitence d'avoir un coeur contrit & humili\u00e9, si le p\u00e9cheur au m\u00eame tems n'offre \u00e0 Dieu en forme d'hostie, une chair mortifi\u00e9e & crucifi\u00e9e avec ses d\u00e9sirs corrompus. Tel est, dit S. Gr\u00e9goire Pape, le devoir de l'homme, qui se trouvant compos\u00e9 d'une ame & d'un corps, d'une ame spirituelle & toute c\u00e9leste, d'un corps terrestre & tout mat\u00e9riel, doit selon l'un & l'autre honorer Dieu, s'il veut rendre \u00e0 Dieu ce culte raisonnable, en quoi consiste l'int\u00e9grit\u00e9 de la religion."}, {"anchor": "b-p004", "label": "English", "text": "FOR faith it is not enough to believe in the heart without confessing with the mouth: Saint Paul tells us this in express terms. Following that same Apostle's teaching, I add that for repentance it is not enough to have a contrite and humbled heart unless the sinner at the same time offers God, as a sacrificial victim, a flesh mortified and crucified with its corrupt desires. Such, says Pope Saint Gregory, is man's duty: composed of soul and body, a spiritual and wholly heavenly soul and an earthly and wholly material body, he must honour God in both if he wishes to offer God that reasonable worship in which the wholeness of religion consists.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p005", "label": "French original", "text": "Excellent principe que je suppose d'abord & d'o\u00f9 je conclus que la p\u00e9nitence Chr\u00e9tienne prise dans toute son \u00e9tendue, est donc un double sacrifice que Dieu exige de nous. Sacrifice de l'esprit, & sacrifice du corps: sacrifice de l'esprit, par l'humilit\u00e9 de la componction; & sacrifice du corps, par l'aust\u00e9rit\u00e9 m\u00eame ext\u00e9rieure de la satisfaction: sacrifice de l'esprit, sans lequel, comme nous l'enseigne le ma\u00eetre des Gentils, le sacrifice du corps ne sert \u00e0 rien ou presque \u00e0 rien, ni ne peut jamais appaiser Dieu; & sacrifice du corps, sans quoi le sacrifice de l'esprit n'est souvent qu'une illusion & un phant\u00f4me devant Dieu. En sorte que l'union de ces deux sacrifices est absolument n\u00e9cessaire pour rendre parfait l'holocauste dont je parle, & d'o\u00f9 d\u00e9pend l'enti\u00e9re r\u00e9conciliation de l'homme p\u00e9cheur avec Dieu."}, {"anchor": "b-p005", "label": "English", "text": "This excellent principle is my starting point. From it I conclude that Christian penance, taken in its full extent, is a double sacrifice God requires of us: a sacrifice of the spirit and a sacrifice of the body. The spirit is sacrificed through humble compunction, and the body through the austerity, even outward austerity, of satisfaction. Without the sacrifice of the spirit, as the teacher of the Gentiles instructs us, bodily sacrifice is of no use or almost none, and can never appease God; without the sacrifice of the body, the sacrifice of the spirit is often only an illusion and a phantom before God. The union of these two sacrifices is therefore absolutely necessary to perfect the whole burnt offering of which I speak, upon which sinful man's complete reconciliation with God depends.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p006", "label": "French original", "text": "Je m'attache \u00e0 cette pens\u00e9e qui me conduit naturellement \u00e0 mon sujet: & parce que ces deux sacrifices que la p\u00e9nitence doit faire \u00e0 Dieu, trouvent en nous deux grands obstacles, dont le premier est l'esprit d'orgueil, & le second l'esprit de mollesse; l'esprit d'orgueil, incompatible avec l'humilit\u00e9 de la p\u00e9nitence; l'esprit de mollesse essentiellement oppos\u00e9 \u00e0 l'aust\u00e9rit\u00e9 de la p\u00e9nitence: je veux pour ne vous rien dire aujourd'hui qui ne soit utile & pratique, vous apprendre \u00e0 les surmonter par le souvenir de la mort que nous retrace l'Eglise dans la c\u00e9r\u00e9monie des cendres. C'est tout le dessein de ce discours, que je r\u00e9duits \u00e0 deux propositions. Il faut par une p\u00e9nitence solidement humble, an\u00e9antir devant Dieu l'orgueil de nos esprits; & c'est \u00e0 quoi nous oblige la vue de ces cendres, qui sont pour nous les marques & comme les symboles de la mort: ce sera le premier point. Il faut par une p\u00e9nitence g\u00e9n\u00e9reusement aust\u00e9re, sacrifier \u00e0 Dieu la mollesse & la d\u00e9licatesse de nos corps; & c'est \u00e0 quoi nous engage l'imposition de ces cendres, qui nous annoncent, ou plut\u00f4t qui nous font d\u00e9j\u00e0 sentir l'in\u00e9vitable n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 de la mort: ce sera le second point. Humiliation de l'esprit sous le joug de la p\u00e9nitence, mortification de la chair dans l'exercice de la p\u00e9nitence: deux fruits du saint usage que nous devons faire de ces cendres consacr\u00e9es par la b\u00e9n\u00e9diction des pr\u00eatres, & de la pens\u00e9e de la mort que nous rappelle une c\u00e9r\u00e9monie si touchante. Donnez-moi votre attention."}, {"anchor": "b-p006", "label": "English", "text": "I hold to this thought, which leads naturally to my subject. These two sacrifices that penance must offer to God encounter two great obstacles in us: first, the spirit of pride; second, the spirit of self-indulgence. Pride is incompatible with the humility of penance, and self-indulgence essentially opposed to its austerity. Wishing to say nothing today that is not useful and practical, I shall teach you to overcome them through the remembrance of death that the Church sets before us in the ceremony of ashes. That is the whole purpose of this discourse, which I reduce to two propositions. Through truly humble penance we must bring the pride of our spirits to nothing before God; the sight of these ashes, marks and symbols of death for us, obliges us to do so: this will be the first point. Through generously austere penance we must sacrifice to God our bodies' self-indulgence and delicacy; the imposition of these ashes, announcing\u2014or rather already making us feel\u2014the unavoidable necessity of death, calls us to do so: this will be the second point. Humbling the spirit beneath the yoke of penance, mortifying the flesh in the practice of penance: these are two fruits of the holy use we ought to make of ashes consecrated by the priests' blessing, and of the thought of death recalled by so moving a ceremony. Give me your attention.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-h003", "label": "French original", "text": "I. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "b-h003", "label": "English", "text": "PART ONE", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p007", "label": "French original", "text": "Comme il est de la foi que l'orgueil fut le premier p\u00e9ch\u00e9 de l'homme, & qu'il est encore la source & le principe de tout p\u00e9ch\u00e9, Initium omnis peccati superbia; il ne faut pas s'\u00e9tonner que le m\u00eame orgueil soit un obstacle essentiel \u00e0 la p\u00e9nitence, \u00e9tablie de Dieu pour \u00eatre le rem\u00e8de du p\u00e9ch\u00e9. Je m'explique. Si l'homme pers\u00e9v\u00e9rant dans le bienheureux \u00e9tat o\u00f9 Dieu l'avoit cr\u00e9\u00e9, \u00e9toit demeur\u00e9 dans les termes de cette humilit\u00e9, qui lui \u00e9toit comme naturelle, puisque l'humilit\u00e9 n'est rien autre chose que la parfaite connoissance de soi-m\u00eame; quelque avantage ou de la nature ou de la gr\u00e2ce qu'il e\u00fbt re\u00e7u, il n'auroit jamais couru risque d'en abuser au pr\u00e9judice de ce qu'il devoit \u00e0 Dieu: & si dans l'instant que nous violons la loi de Dieu, nous faisions un retour sur nous-m\u00eames; il nous suffiroit de nous conno\u00eetre nous-m\u00eames, pour rentrer dans l'ordre, & pour nous mettre comme p\u00e9cheurs en disposition de satisfaire \u00e0 Dieu. Mais cet esprit de p\u00e9nitence & de justice qui nous porte \u00e0 r\u00e9parer les offenses de Dieu, se trouve combattu dans nous par un autre esprit, qui est l'esprit d'orgueil; & de m\u00eame qu'en p\u00e9chant nous nous revoltons contre ce souverain L\u00e9gislateur, nous avons apr\u00e8s le p\u00e9ch\u00e9 une opposition secrette \u00e0 lui en faire la juste r\u00e9paration qui lui est due."}, {"anchor": "b-p007", "label": "English", "text": "Since it is an article of faith that pride was man's first sin, and remains the source and principle of every sin\u2014\u2018Pride is the beginning of every sin\u2019\u2014we should not be surprised that this same pride is an essential obstacle to penance, which God established as the remedy for sin. Let me explain. Had man persevered in the blessed state in which God created him and remained within the bounds of that humility which was, as it were, natural to him\u2014for humility is simply perfect self-knowledge\u2014whatever advantages of nature or grace he had received, he would never have risked abusing them to the detriment of what he owed God. And if, at the very moment we violate God's law, we reflected upon ourselves, self-knowledge alone would suffice to restore us to order and dispose us, as sinners, to make satisfaction to God. But this spirit of penance and justice, which moves us to repair our offences against God, is opposed within us by another spirit, the spirit of pride. Just as we rebel against this sovereign Lawgiver by sinning, so after sin we secretly resist making him the just reparation that is his due.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p008", "label": "French original", "text": "Quel rem\u00e8de, Chr\u00e9tien? celui m\u00eame que l'Eglise nous propose dans la c\u00e9r\u00e9monie de ce jour, en nous obligeant \u00e0 nous souvenir de ce que nous sommes, afin de corriger notre vanit\u00e9 par notre vanit\u00e9, comme parle S. Augustin. Car il faut faire de tems en tems remonter l'homme jusqu'\u00e0 son origine, dit ce grand Docteur; & par la consid\u00e9ration de sa foiblesse, de sa mis\u00e9re, de son n\u00e9ant, le forcer malgr\u00e9 lui de renoncer aux pr\u00e9somptueuses & vaines id\u00e9es qu'il a de lui-m\u00eame, & qui l'emp\u00eachant de s'humilier, l'emp\u00eachent de se convertir. Or c'est ce que fait la pens\u00e9e de la mort. Quand un homme sans qualit\u00e9 & sans naissance, mais \u00e9lev\u00e9 n\u00e9anmoins \u00e0 une haute fortune & combl\u00e9 de biens & d'honneurs, vient \u00e0 s'enorgueillir & \u00e0 s'oublier, le moyen de r\u00e9primer son orgueil est de lui remettre devant les yeux l'obscurit\u00e9 & la bassesse de son extraction. Ne vous enflez point, lui dit-on; on s\u00e7ait qui vous \u00eates, & d'o\u00f9 vous \u00eates venu. Cela seul est capable de le confondre, & de lui inspirer des sentimens de modestie. Mais si de plus, par une vue anticip\u00e9e de l'avenir, on lui marquoit ce qui lui doit bient\u00f4t arriver; si l'on pouvoit lui dire, & lui dire avec assurance: Prenez garde, quelque grand que vous soyez, vous \u00eates sur le point de votre ruine; une disgrace dont vous \u00eates menac\u00e9, & que vous n'\u00e9viterez pas, va vous r\u00e9duire \u00e0 n'\u00eatre plus que ce que vous \u00e9tiez dans votre premi\u00e8re condition: si, dis-je, on pouvoit lui parler ainsi, en sorte qu'on lui f\u00eet conno\u00eetre \u00e0 lui-m\u00eame la v\u00e9rit\u00e9 de ce qu'on lui annonce, cette vue sans doute feroit encore sur lui une bien plus forte impression. P\u00e9n\u00e9tr\u00e9 de cette pens\u00e9e, il n'y a plus pour moi de ressource & je vais p\u00e9rir, il seroit doux & humain; il ne feroit plus voir dans sa conduite, ni arrogance, ni fiert\u00e9; cette enflure de coeur que lui causoit la prosp\u00e9rit\u00e9 & l'\u00e9l\u00e9vation, s'abbaisseroit tout \u00e0 coup: pourquoi? parce qu'il n'envisageroit plus sa fortune, si je puis user de cette expression, que comme la hauteur du pr\u00e9cipice o\u00f9 il va tomber; & qu'au lieu de s'\u00e9blouir de ce qu'il est, il g\u00e9miroit sur ce qu'il va devenir."}, {"anchor": "b-p008", "label": "English", "text": "What is the remedy, Christian? The very one the Church proposes in today's ceremony, obliging us to remember what we are, so as to correct our vanity by our vanity, as Saint Augustine puts it. From time to time, this great Doctor says, man must be taken back to his origin; by considering his weakness, misery, and nothingness, he must be forced, despite himself, to renounce those presumptuous and vain ideas of himself which prevent him from humbling himself and therefore from being converted. This is what the thought of death accomplishes. When a man of no rank or birth, nevertheless raised to great fortune and loaded with wealth and honours, grows proud and forgets himself, the way to curb his pride is to set before him the obscurity and lowliness of his origins. \u2018Do not puff yourself up,\u2019 he is told; \u2018we know who you are and where you came from.\u2019 That alone can put him to shame and inspire modesty. But suppose that, looking ahead, one also showed him what must soon happen, and could say with certainty: \u2018Take care: however great you are, you stand on the brink of ruin. A disgrace threatens you that you cannot escape; it will reduce you to what you were in your original condition.\u2019 If, I say, one could speak to him like this and make him recognize the truth of the warning, this prospect would surely make a much stronger impression. Penetrated by the thought, \u2018There is no remedy left for me; I am about to perish,\u2019 he would become gentle and humane; his conduct would no longer show arrogance or haughtiness. The swelling of heart caused by prosperity and advancement would suddenly subside. Why? Because he would see his fortune, if I may put it so, only as the height of the precipice into which he must fall; instead of being dazzled by what he is, he would lament what he is about to become.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p009", "label": "French original", "text": "Or c'est justement, mes chers Auditeurs, de cette double vue, & de ce que nous avons \u00e9t\u00e9, & de ce que nous serons, que l'Eglise se sert aujourd'hui pour nous tenir devant Dieu dans l'humilit\u00e9 & dans la soumission. L'homme, dit l'Ecriture, \u00e9toit dans l'honneur & dans la gloire, o\u00f9 Dieu l'avoit \u00e9lev\u00e9 par la cr\u00e9ation; mais au milieu de sa gloire l'homme s'\u00e9toit m\u00e9connu: Homo cum in honore esset, non intellexit. Cet oubli de lui-m\u00eame, par une suite n\u00e9cessaire, l'avoit port\u00e9 jusqu'\u00e0 l'oubli & m\u00eame jusqu'au m\u00e9pris de Dieu. Que fait l'Eglise? Pour r\u00e9tablir en nous ce respect de Dieu, & cette crainte que nous perdons par le p\u00e9ch\u00e9 & qui doit \u00eatre le fondement de la p\u00e9nitence, elle nous engage, ou plut\u00f4t elle nous oblige \u00e0 concevoir du m\u00e9pris pour nous-m\u00eames, en nous adressant ces paroles: Memento, homo, quia pulvis es, & in pulverem revert\u00e9ris. Comme si elle nous disoit: pourquoi, homme mortel, vous attribuer sans raison une grandeur chim\u00e9rique & imaginaire? Souvenez-vous de ce que vous \u00e9tiez il y a quelques ann\u00e9es, quand Dieu par sa toute-puissance vous tira de la boue & du n\u00e9ant. Souvenez-vous de ce que vous serez dans quelques ann\u00e9es, quand ce petit nombre de jours qui vous reste encore, sera expir\u00e9. Voil\u00e0 les deux termes, o\u00f9 il faut malgr\u00e9 vous que tout votre orgueil se borne. Raisonnez tant qu'il vous plaira sur ces deux principes; vous n'en tirerez jamais de cons\u00e9quence, non-seulement qui ne vous humilie, mais qui ne vous rappelle \u00e0 votre devoir, lorsque vous serez assez aveugl\u00e9 & assez insens\u00e9 pour vous en \u00e9carter. Telle est encore un fois, Chr\u00e9tiens, la salutaire & importante le\u00e7on, que fait l'Eglise, comme une m\u00e8re sage, \u00e0 tous ses enfans."}, {"anchor": "b-p009", "label": "English", "text": "My dear hearers, it is precisely this double view\u2014what we have been and what we shall be\u2014that the Church uses today to keep us humble and submissive before God. Man, says Scripture, was in honour and glory, where God had placed him through creation; but in the midst of his glory man failed to know himself: \u2018Man, when he was in honour, did not understand.\u2019 This forgetfulness of himself necessarily led him to forget and even despise God. What does the Church do? To restore in us that reverence for God and that fear which sin takes away, and which must be the foundation of penance, she calls us\u2014or rather obliges us\u2014to regard ourselves with contempt, addressing these words to us: \u2018Remember, man, that you are dust, and to dust you will return.\u2019 It is as if she said: \u2018Mortal man, why attribute to yourself without reason a fanciful, imaginary greatness? Remember what you were a few years ago, when God in his almighty power drew you from clay and nothingness. Remember what you will be in a few years, when the few days remaining to you have expired. These are the two limits within which, despite yourself, all your pride must be confined.\u2019 Reason as much as you like from these two principles: you will never draw a conclusion that does not both humble you and recall you to your duty whenever you are blind and foolish enough to depart from it. Such, once again, Christians, is the saving and important lesson the Church gives all her children like a wise mother.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p010", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais examinons plus en d\u00e9tail la mani\u00e8re dont elle y proc\u00e8de, & toutes les circonstances de cette c\u00e9r\u00e9monie des cendres qu'elle observe en ce saint jour. Car il n'y en a pas une qui ne nous instruise, & qui n'aille directement \u00e0 ces deux fins, de rabbattre notre orgueil, & de nous disposer \u00e0 la p\u00e9nitence. En effet, c'est pour rabbattre notre orgueil qu'elle nous pr\u00e9sente des cendres, & qu'elle nous les fait mettre sur la t\u00eate. Pourquoi des cendres? parce que rien, dit saint Ambroise, ne doit mieux nous faire comprendre ce que c'est que la mort, & l'humiliation extr\u00eame o\u00f9 nous r\u00e9duit la mort, que la poussi\u00e9re & la cendre. Oui, ces cendres que nous recevons prostern\u00e9s aux pieds des Ministres du Seigneur, ces cendres dont la b\u00e9n\u00e9diction, selon la pens\u00e9e de saint Gr\u00e9goire de Nysse, est aujourd'hui comme le myst\u00e8re, ou si vous voulez, comme le sacrement de notre mortalit\u00e9 & par cons\u00e9quent de notre humilit\u00e9, si nous les consid\u00e9rons bien, ont quelque chose de plus touchant que tous les raisonnemens du monde, pour nous humilier en qualit\u00e9 d'hommes, & pour nous faire prendre en qualit\u00e9 de p\u00e9cheurs les sentimens d'une parfaite conversion & d'un retour sinc\u00e9re \u00e0 Dieu. Car elles nous apprennent ce que nous voudrions peut-\u00eatre ne pas s\u00e7avoir, & ce que nous t\u00e2chons tous les jours \u00e0 oublier. Mais malheur \u00e0 nous, si jamais nous tombons, ou dans une ignorance si d\u00e9plorable, ou dans un oubli si funeste."}, {"anchor": "b-p010", "label": "English", "text": "But let us examine more closely how she proceeds, and every circumstance of this ceremony of ashes observed on this holy day. Not one fails to instruct us or to serve directly these two ends: curbing our pride and disposing us to penance. It is to curb our pride that she presents ashes and has them placed on our heads. Why ashes? Because, says Saint Ambrose, nothing can better teach us what death is, and the extreme humiliation to which it reduces us, than dust and ashes. Yes, these ashes which we receive prostrate at the feet of the Lord's ministers, whose blessing today is, in Saint Gregory of Nyssa's thought, like the mystery\u2014or, if you will, the sacrament\u2014of our mortality and consequently of our humility, have, when rightly considered, something more moving than all the arguments in the world. They humble us as human beings and inspire in us as sinners the sentiments of complete conversion and a sincere return to God. They teach us what perhaps we would rather not know, and what we try every day to forget. But woe to us if we ever fall into such deplorable ignorance or such fatal forgetfulness.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p011", "label": "French original", "text": "Elles nous apprennent que toutes ces grandeurs dont le monde se glorifie, & dont l'orgueil des hommes se repa\u00eet; que cette naissance dont on se pique, que ce cr\u00e9dit dont on se flatte, que cette autorit\u00e9 dont on est si fier, que ces succ\u00e8s dont on se vante, que ces biens dont on s'applaudit, que ces dignit\u00e9s & ces charges dont on se pr\u00e9vaut, que cette beaut\u00e9, cette valeur, cette r\u00e9putation dont on est idol\u00e2tre; que tout cela malgr\u00e9 nos pr\u00e9ventions & nos erreurs n'est que vanit\u00e9 & que mensonge. Car que je m'approche du tombeau d'un Grand de la terre, & que j'en examine l'\u00e9pitaphe; je n'y vois qu'\u00e9loges, que titres sp\u00e9cieux, que qualit\u00e9s avantageuses, qu'emplois honorables: tout ce qu'il a jamais \u00e9t\u00e9 & tout ce qu'il a jamais fait, y est \u00e9tal\u00e9 en termes pompeux & magnifiques. Voil\u00e0 ce qui paro\u00eet au dehors. Mais qu'on me fasse l'ouverture de ce tombeau, & qu'il me soit permis de voir ce qu'il renferme; je n'y trouve qu'un cadavre hideux, qu'un tas d'ossemens infects & dess\u00e9ch\u00e9s, qu'un peu de cendres, qui semblent encore se ranimer pour me dire \u00e0 moi-m\u00eame: Memento, homo, quia pulvis es, & in pulverem revert\u00e9ris."}, {"anchor": "b-p011", "label": "English", "text": "They teach us that all the greatness in which the world glories and upon which human pride feeds\u2014the birth we boast of, the influence we flatter ourselves with, the authority we are so proud of, the successes we proclaim, the wealth we congratulate ourselves on, the dignities and offices we take advantage of, the beauty, courage, and reputation we idolize\u2014all of it, despite our prejudices and errors, is only vanity and falsehood. Let me approach the tomb of some great man of the earth and examine its epitaph: I see nothing but praise, impressive titles, advantageous qualities, honourable appointments. Everything he ever was and ever did is displayed in pompous, magnificent terms. That is what appears outside. But let the tomb be opened and allow me to see what it contains: I find only a hideous corpse, a heap of foul and dried bones, a little ash that seems to come alive again to say to me personally: \u2018Remember, man, that you are dust, and to dust you will return.\u2019", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p012", "label": "French original", "text": "Elles nous apprennent que nous sommes donc bien injustes, quand \u00e0 quelque prix que ce soit, & souvent contre l'ordre de la providence, nous pr\u00e9tendons nous distinguer, & que nous voulons faire dans le monde certaines figures qui ne servent qu'\u00e0 flatter notre vanit\u00e9: que ces rangs que nous disputons avec tant de chaleur, ces droits que nous nous attribuons, ces points d'honneur dont nous nous ent\u00eatons, ces singularit\u00e9s que nous affectons, ces airs de domination que nous nous donnons, ces soumissions que nous exigeons, ces hauteurs avec lesquelles nous en usons, ces m\u00e9nagemens & ces \u00e9gards que nous demandons, sont autant d'usurpations que fait notre orgueil, en nous persuadant aussi-bien qu'au Pharisien de l'Evangile, que nous ne sommes pas comme le reste des hommes: erreur, dont la cendre o\u00f9 nous r\u00e9duit la mort, nous d\u00e9trompe bien, par l'\u00e9galit\u00e9 o\u00f9 elle met toutes les conditions, disons mieux, par leur enti\u00e9re destruction. Car voyez, dit \u00e9loquemment saint Augustin au livre de la nature & de la grace; voyez si dans les d\u00e9bris des tombeaux vous distinguerez le pauvre d'avec le riche, le roturier d'avec le noble, le foible d'avec le fort. Voyez si les cendres des souverains & des monarques y sont diff\u00e9rentes de celles des sujets & des esclaves. Ah! l'esclave & le Roi ne sont l\u00e0 qu'une m\u00eame chose; & ce fut la belle r\u00e9ponse que fit un philosophe \u00e0 un fameux conqu\u00e9rant, lorsqu'interrog\u00e9, pourquoi il paroissoit si attentif \u00e0 contempler des ossemens de morts entass\u00e9s les uns sur les autres, Je t\u00e2che, lui dit-il, Seigneur, \u00e0 discerner dans ce m\u00e9lange le Roi votre Pere; je l'y cherche, mais en vain, parce que ces cendres confondues avec celles du peuple, n'y retiennent plus nulle marque de distinction par o\u00f9 je puisse le reconno\u00eetre. Paroles dont le plus fier des hommes, quoique payen, ne laissa pas de s'\u00e9difier, & qui reviennent \u00e0 ce qu'on nous dit aujourd'hui: Memento, homo, quia pulvis es, & in pulverem revert\u00e9ris."}, {"anchor": "b-p012", "label": "English", "text": "They teach us how unjust we are when, at any cost and often against the order of providence, we claim distinction and wish to cut figures in the world that only flatter our vanity. The ranks we dispute so heatedly, the rights we claim, the points of honour we stubbornly uphold, the peculiarities we affect, the airs of mastery we assume, the submission we demand, the haughtiness with which we behave, the consideration and deference we require\u2014all are usurpations of our pride, persuading us, like the Pharisee in the Gospel, that we are not like the rest of men. The ashes to which death reduces us thoroughly undeceive us of this error by making all conditions equal\u2014or rather, by destroying them entirely. For look, says Saint Augustine eloquently in his book on nature and grace: see whether among the remains in tombs you can distinguish the poor from the rich, the commoner from the nobleman, the weak from the strong. See whether the ashes of sovereigns and monarchs differ from those of subjects and slaves. Ah, slave and king are one and the same there! Such was the fine answer a philosopher gave a famous conqueror when asked why he seemed so intent on contemplating dead men's bones piled upon one another: \u2018My lord, I am trying to distinguish your father the King in this mixture. I search for him, but in vain: his ashes, mingled with the people's, retain no mark of distinction by which I can recognize him.\u2019 Words from which the proudest of men, though a pagan, still drew edification, and which amount to what we are told today: \u2018Remember, man, that you are dust, and to dust you will return.\u2019", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p013", "label": "French original", "text": "Elles nous apprennent que malgr\u00e9 les vastes desseins que forme l'ambitieux de s'\u00e9tablir, de s'aggrandir, de s'\u00e9lever, de cro\u00eetre toujours, sans dire, C'est assez; la mort par une triste destin\u00e9e le bornera bient\u00f4t \u00e0 six pieds de terre: c'est trop; \u00e0 une poign\u00e9e de cendres. Car voil\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs, pour m'exprimer ainsi, jusqu'o\u00f9 Dieu nous pousse \u00e0 son tour. Voil\u00e0 \u00e0 quoi aboutissent tous nos projets, toutes nos entreprises, toutes nos pr\u00e9tentions, toutes nos intrigues, en un mot toutes nos fortunes & toutes nos grandeurs, lorsque nos corps par la derni\u00e9re r\u00e9solution qui s'en fait dans le tombeau, se raccourcissent, s'abbr\u00e9gent presque jusques \u00e0 s'an\u00e9antir: Ecce vix totam Hercules implevit urnam. Quel changement, disoit un Sage, quoique mondain, en voyant l'urne s\u00e9pulchrale o\u00f9 \u00e9toient les cendres d'Hercule! Cet Hercule, ce h\u00e9ros \u00e0 qui la terre ne suffisoit pas, est ici ramass\u00e9 tout entier! \u00e0 peine a-t-il de quoi remplir cette urne! R\u00e9flexion que l'Eglise nous fait faire aujourd'hui bien plus saintement & bien plus efficacement, quand elle nous dit: Memento, homo, quia pulvis es, & in pulverem revert\u00e9ris."}, {"anchor": "b-p013", "label": "English", "text": "They teach us that, despite the ambitious man's vast designs to establish himself, to enlarge himself, to rise and grow endlessly without saying \u2018Enough,\u2019 death will soon, by a sad destiny, confine him to six feet of earth\u2014too much: to a handful of ashes. That, my dear hearers, is how far God, in his turn, presses us, if I may put it so. That is where all our plans, undertakings, claims, intrigues\u2014in a word, all our fortunes and greatness\u2014end, when our bodies, through their final dissolution in the tomb, shrink and dwindle almost to nothing: \u2018Look, Hercules scarcely filled the whole urn.\u2019 What a change, said a wise man, though a worldly one, on seeing the funeral urn containing Hercules' ashes! This Hercules, this hero for whom the earth was not enough, is gathered here in his entirety; he can scarcely fill this urn! The Church makes us reflect on this today far more holily and effectively when she tells us: \u2018Remember, man, that you are dust, and to dust you will return.\u2019", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p014", "label": "French original", "text": "Elles nous apprennent que non-seulement la mort d\u00e9truira ce phant\u00f4me de grandeur & de fortune apr\u00e8s lequel nous courons, mais que notre m\u00e9moire m\u00eame p\u00e9rira; qu'on ne parlera plus de nous, qu'on ne pensera plus \u00e0 nous, qu'on se consolera de notre perte; que quelques-uns s'en r\u00e9jouiront, que nos proches seront les premiers \u00e0 nous oublier; que ces amis sur qui nous comptions, se lasseront bient\u00f4t de nous pleurer; que l'indiff\u00e9rence des uns, que l'ingratitude des autres effacera dans peu de jours le souvenir des bons offices que nous leur avons rendus, & que tout ce que nous aurons fait dans une autre vue que celle de Dieu, sera semblable \u00e0 la poussi\u00e9re que le vent emporte: car ainsi le concevoit Job, Memoria vestra comparabitur cineri. Ainsi Dieu le marquoit-il lui-m\u00eame, quand il disoit par la bouche d'Ez\u00e9chiel \u00e0 ce Roi impie: Dabo te in cinerem, je te r\u00e9duirai en poudre, & ces \u00e9clatantes actions dont tu te promettois dans la m\u00e9moire des hommes une esp\u00e9ce d'immortalit\u00e9, s'\u00e9vanouiront & se dissiperont comme la cendre."}, {"anchor": "b-p014", "label": "English", "text": "They teach us that death will not only destroy the phantom of greatness and fortune we pursue, but that even our memory will perish. People will no longer speak or think of us; they will be consoled for our loss; some will rejoice in it. Our relatives will be the first to forget us; the friends on whom we counted will soon tire of weeping for us. The indifference of some and the ingratitude of others will erase within a few days the memory of the services we rendered them; everything we did with any end other than God in view will be like dust carried off by the wind. Job understood it so: \u2018Your remembrance will be compared to ashes.\u2019 God himself indicated it when he said through Ezekiel to that impious king: \u2018I shall make you ashes\u2019\u2014I shall reduce you to dust; and those brilliant deeds from which you promised yourself a kind of immortality in human memory will vanish and disperse like ashes.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p015", "label": "French original", "text": "En effet, Chr\u00e9tiens, c'est le v\u00e9ritable symbole de cette fausse gloire dont nous sommes si jaloux, puisqu'il est certain qu'elle a toutes les propri\u00e9t\u00e9s de la cendre; qu'elle est vile comme la cendre, l\u00e9g\u00e9re comme la cendre, st\u00e9rile & inutile comme la cendre; & que quand nous en aurions autant que notre vanit\u00e9 en peut demander, ce qui ne sera jamais, on auroit toujours droit de nous dire: Memento, homo, quia pulvis es, & in pulverem revert\u00e9ris."}, {"anchor": "b-p015", "label": "English", "text": "Indeed, Christians, ashes are the true symbol of that false glory of which we are so jealous. It certainly has every property of ashes: it is base like ashes, light like ashes, barren and useless like ashes. Even if we had as much of it as our vanity could demand\u2014which will never happen\u2014there would always be reason to tell us: \u2018Remember, man, that you are dust, and to dust you will return.\u2019", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p016", "label": "French original", "text": "Enfin, elles nous apprennent que quelque enracin\u00e9 que soit notre orgueil, il ne tient qu'\u00e0 nous de trouver dans nous notre humiliation, Humiliatio tua in medio tui; puisque cette partie de nous-m\u00eames, dont nous sommes si occup\u00e9s & si idol\u00e2tres, ce corps n'est au fond que le plus abjet de tous les \u00eatres, qu'un sujet de corruption, & selon l'expression de Tertullien, qu'un peu de boue figur\u00e9e en homme, Limus titulo hominis incisus. Or il est juste que la poussi\u00e9re & la boue s'enfle de ce qu'elle est; & que par la malice du p\u00e9ch\u00e9, elle s'\u00e9l\u00e8ve contre celui qui l'animant de son esprit, l'a \u00e9lev\u00e9e par sa mis\u00e9ricorde au-dessus de ce qu'elle \u00e9toit? Quid superbit terra & cinis? La mort que nous avons sans cesse devant les yeux, devoit \u00eatre sur tout cela pour nous une continuelle le\u00e7on: mais parce qu'il arrive, comme l'a fort bien remarqu\u00e9 saint Chrysostome, que tous les hommes voyent la mort, mais que peu ont le don de la comprendre, Mortem omnes vident, pauci intelligunt; l'Eglise joint \u00e0 cette vue de la mort, l'usage des cendres qu'elle nous pr\u00e9sente, & qui sanctifi\u00e9es par les pri\u00e9res de ses ministres ont une gr\u00e2ce sp\u00e9ciale pour faire entrer dans nos coeurs ces importantes v\u00e9rit\u00e9s: Memento, homo, quia pulvis es, & in pulverem revert\u00e9ris."}, {"anchor": "b-p016", "label": "English", "text": "Finally, they teach us that however deeply rooted our pride may be, it lies with us to find our humiliation within ourselves: \u2018Your humiliation is in your midst.\u2019 For this part of ourselves that so occupies us and that we so idolize, this body, is at bottom only the most abject of beings, a subject of corruption and, in Tertullian's expression, a little clay shaped into man: \u2018Clay impressed with the title of man.\u2019 Now is it right that dust and clay should puff itself up over what it is, and through the malice of sin rise against him who, breathing his spirit into it, mercifully raised it above what it was? \u2018Why is earth and ashes proud?\u2019 Death, continually before our eyes, should be a constant lesson on all this. But because, as Saint John Chrysostom well observed, all men see death and few have the gift of understanding it\u2014\u2018All see death; few understand\u2019\u2014the Church adds to this sight of death the use of the ashes she presents to us. Sanctified by the prayers of her ministers, these have a special grace to bring these important truths into our hearts: \u2018Remember, man, that you are dust, and to dust you will return.\u2019", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p017", "label": "French original", "text": "Cependant vous me demandez pourquoi l'on nous met ces cendres sur la t\u00eate & sur le front: autre myst\u00e8re qu'il est ais\u00e9 d'\u00e9claircir, & qui doit encore \u00e9difier votre pi\u00e9t\u00e9. On nous met ces cendres sur la t\u00eate, qui est le si\u00e9ge de la raison, pour nous faire entendre que l'objet le plus ordinaire de nos r\u00e9flexions & de nos consid\u00e9rations pendant la vie doit \u00eatre la mort & les suites de la mort. Or c'est ce que l'on nous d\u00e9clare quand on nous dit, Memento: souvenez-vous-en, & ne l'oubliez jamais; parce qu'en effet il nous serviroit peu d'\u00eatre une fois convaincus que nous sommes mortels, si par une forte pens\u00e9e & par un fr\u00e9quent souvenir, la conviction que nous en avons, n'\u00e9toit pour nous une source de sagesse, & ne produisoit en nous cette disposition d'humilit\u00e9, qui est d\u00e9j\u00e0 le commencement de la p\u00e9nitence."}, {"anchor": "b-p017", "label": "English", "text": "You ask, however, why these ashes are placed on our head and forehead: another mystery easy to explain, and one that should further edify your piety. Ashes are placed on the head, the seat of reason, to teach us that the most habitual object of our reflections and considerations during life should be death and its consequences. This is what we are told in the word \u2018Remember\u2019: remember it and never forget it. For it would do us little good to be convinced once that we are mortal if, through earnest thought and frequent remembrance, that conviction were not a source of wisdom and did not produce in us the disposition of humility which is already the beginning of penance.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p018", "label": "French original", "text": "Aussi est-ce le souvenir de la mort, qui de tout tems a le plus retenu les hommes dans l'ordre, & les a mis malgr\u00e9 les soul\u00e9vemens de leur orgueil, comme dans la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 d'\u00eatre humbles. De l\u00e0 vient, dit saint J\u00e9r\u00f4me (& ce ne sera point l\u00e0 une digression, ou cette digression n'aura rien d'ennuyeux & de fatiguant pour vous) de-l\u00e0 vient que parmi toutes les nations, non-seulement Chr\u00e9tiennes, mais payennes, le souvenir de la mort & m\u00eame l'usage de la cendre a \u00e9t\u00e9 une des principales circonstances des pompes les plus solemnelles & des c\u00e9r\u00e9monies les plus augustes: que les Grecs, au rapport du Cardinal Pierre Damien, apr\u00e8s avoir couronn\u00e9 leurs Empereurs, leur offroient un vase plein d'ossemens & de cendres, pour les avertir que la supr\u00eame dignit\u00e9, dont ils venoient d'\u00eatre rev\u00eatus, ne les exemptoit pas de la mort: que les Romains dans leurs triomphes faisoient marcher un H\u00e9raut apr\u00e8s le vainqueur, pour lui crier au milieu des applaudissemens publics, qu'il \u00e9toit homme & sujet \u00e0 la mort: que le grand Pr\u00eatre dans l'ancienne loi se purifioit avec la cendre, quand il devoit entrer dans le Sanctuaire; & que maintenant encore dans la cons\u00e9cration des Papes, on fait passer devant les yeux du nouveau Pontife quelques \u00e9toupes que le feu consume, pour lui faire entendre que la gloire du monde passe de m\u00eame, & que la thiare ne l'emp\u00eache point d'\u00eatre tributaire de la mort: comme si les hommes avoient eux-m\u00eames reconnu, qu'\u00e0 mesure que le monde ou que la providence les exalte, ils ont besoin d'un contrepoids qui les rabbaisse, & que le plus puissant & le meilleur est le souvenir de la mort. De-l\u00e0 vient que les peuples les plus barbares, par un secret instinct de religion, se sont fait un devoir de conserver les cendres de leurs anc\u00eatres. Ces cendres leur faisoient voir \u00e0 quoi leur sort devoit enfin se terminer; & ce souvenir les rendoit naturellement humbles, dans le m\u00eame sens que notre ame, selon le langage de Tertullien, est naturellement Chr\u00e9tienne. Ces cendres, s'ils se sentoient ou passionn\u00e9s ou pr\u00e9occup\u00e9s, leur suffisoient pour se dire \u00e0 eux-m\u00eames: Memento, homo: souviens-toi, homme, & humilie-toi; souviens-toi, & mod\u00e9re-toi; souviens-toi, & d\u00e9trompe-toi. De-l\u00e0 vient que Mo\u00efse sortant de l'Egypte, au lieu d'emporter les riches d\u00e9pouilles des Egyptiens, comme les autres H\u00e9breux dont il \u00e9toit le conducteur, se contenta d'emporter les cendres du Patriarche Joseph; ne croyant pas pouvoir mieux dompter ni mieux soumettre \u00e0 l'empire de Dieu ces esprits fiers & indociles, qu'en leur montrant les cendres de ce grand homme, dont ils se glorifioient d'\u00eatre descendus. De-l\u00e0 vient que les Isra\u00e9lites ayant abandonn\u00e9 Dieu dans le d\u00e9sert, & l'ayant irrit\u00e9 par une scandaleuse r\u00e9bellion, lorsqu'en l'absence de Mo\u00efse ils ador\u00e8rent un veau d'or, ce sage L\u00e9gislateur anim\u00e9 de z\u00e9le, prit le veau d'or, le br\u00fbla, le pulv\u00e9risa, & les obligea d'en boire la cendre, pour confondre leur idol\u00e2trie, en leur faisant voir la vanit\u00e9 de leur Idole. De-l\u00e0 vient enfin que quelques Princes Chr\u00e9tiens, par une pratique toute sainte, quoiqu'elle n'ait pas \u00e9t\u00e9 du go\u00fbt du monde, pour se former de la mort une id\u00e9e plus vive, non contens de la m\u00e9diter, ont voulu se la rendre sensible & palpable; & que les uns pendant leur vie m\u00eame ont fait placer dans leur palais la bi\u00e8re destin\u00e9e \u00e0 leur s\u00e9pulture; les autres ont gard\u00e9 parmi leurs meubles les plus pr\u00e9cieux le cr\u00e2ne d'un mort, qui sembloit leur redire sans cesse: Memento, homo, quia pulvis es, & in pulverem revert\u00e9ris. Excellente d\u00e9votion pour les grands du monde, qui dans l'\u00e9clat de leur condition, \u00e9blouis eux-m\u00eames de la pompe qui les environne, ne peuvent presque devenir humbles que par la pens\u00e9e & le souvenir de la mort."}, {"anchor": "b-p018", "label": "English", "text": "Indeed, throughout all ages the remembrance of death has done most to keep men in order and, despite the uprisings of their pride, to place them under something like a necessity of humility. Hence, says Saint Jerome\u2014and this will be no digression, or at least none that will bore or weary you\u2014among all nations, Christian and pagan alike, the remembrance of death and even the use of ashes have been principal features of the most solemn displays and august ceremonies. The Greeks, Cardinal Peter Damian reports, after crowning their emperors, offered them a vessel filled with bones and ashes to warn them that the supreme dignity just bestowed did not exempt them from death. The Romans in their triumphs made a herald walk behind the victor, crying out amid the public applause that he was a man and subject to death. The high priest under the old law purified himself with ashes before entering the sanctuary. Even now, at the consecration of popes, burning tow is passed before the new pontiff's eyes, to teach him that worldly glory passes likewise and that the tiara does not prevent his paying tribute to death. It is as if men themselves had recognized that, as the world or providence raises them, they need a counterweight to bring them low, and that the strongest and best is the remembrance of death. Hence the most barbarous peoples, through a secret religious instinct, made it a duty to preserve their ancestors' ashes. These ashes showed them what their own destiny must finally be, and this remembrance made them naturally humble, in the same sense in which our soul, in Tertullian's words, is naturally Christian. If they felt swayed by passion or prejudice, the ashes sufficed to make them say to themselves: \u2018Remember, man\u2019: remember and humble yourself; remember and moderate yourself; remember and undeceive yourself. Hence Moses, leaving Egypt, instead of carrying off rich Egyptian spoils like the other Hebrews he led, was content to carry the patriarch Joseph's ashes. He thought he could best subdue those proud, unruly spirits and submit them to God's rule by showing them the ashes of the great man from whom they gloried in descending. Hence, when the Israelites abandoned God in the desert and provoked him by scandalous rebellion, worshipping a golden calf in Moses' absence, that wise Lawgiver, fired with zeal, took the calf, burned it, ground it to powder, and compelled them to drink its ashes, confounding their idolatry by showing them their idol's emptiness. Hence, finally, some Christian princes, by a wholly holy practice though not one to the world's taste, were not content to meditate on death but wished to make it sensible and palpable, to form a livelier idea of it. Some had the coffin intended for their burial placed in their palace during their lifetime; others kept among their most precious furnishings a dead man's skull, which seemed constantly to repeat: \u2018Remember, man, that you are dust, and to dust you will return.\u2019 An excellent devotion for the world's great, who, amid the brilliance of their station, themselves dazzled by the splendour surrounding them, can scarcely become humble except through the thought and remembrance of death.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p019", "label": "French original", "text": "Or soit pour les grands, soit pour les petits, quand une fois l'humilit\u00e9 a pris possession d'un coeur, il est ais\u00e9 d'y faire entrer la componction & la p\u00e9nitence. Pourquoi? non-seulement parce que le grand obstacle de la p\u00e9nitence est lev\u00e9; j'entends ce fonds de pr\u00e9somption & d'orgueil avec lequel nous naissons: mais parce qu'\u00e0 bien examiner les choses, l'humilit\u00e9 est en effet la partie la plus essentielle de la conversion du p\u00e9cheur. Car du moment que je suis dispos\u00e9 \u00e0 m'humilier, d\u00e8s-l\u00e0 je le suis \u00e0 m'accuser, \u00e0 me condamner, \u00e0 me punir moi-m\u00eame; d\u00e8s-l\u00e0 je suis dans la voie de chercher Dieu, d'implorer la mis\u00e9ricorde de Dieu, de satisfaire \u00e0 la justice de Dieu, de me remettre sous l'ob\u00e9issance de la loi de Dieu: dispositions les plus n\u00e9cessaires \u00e0 la p\u00e9nitence Chr\u00e9tienne. Et voil\u00e0 pourquoi l'Eglise, apr\u00e8s nous avoir fait consid\u00e9rer deux sortes de cendres, celle de notre origine, Memento, quia pulvis es, & celle de notre corruption future, & in pulverem revert\u00e9ris: la premi\u00e9re, qui nous apprend que nous ne sommes que n\u00e9ant; & la seconde, qui nous dit que nous sommes encore quelque chose de moins, ou plut\u00f4t quelque chose de plus mauvais, puisque nous ne sommes que p\u00e9ch\u00e9: apr\u00e8s, dis-je, nous avoir mis devant les yeux cette double cendre, nous en impose une troisi\u00e9me, qui se rapporte parfaitement \u00e0 l'une & \u00e0 l'autre, s\u00e7avoir, la cendre de la p\u00e9nitence. Car que fait le p\u00e9cheur, quand il re\u00e7oit aujourd'hui par les mains du Pr\u00eatre la cendre qui lui est pr\u00e9sent\u00e9e? apprenez, mes chers Auditeurs, \u00e0 vous acquitter en Chr\u00e9tiens de ce devoir Chr\u00e9tien: que fait le p\u00e9cheur converti, quand il re\u00e7oit cette cendre consacr\u00e9e \u00e0 la p\u00e9nitence? C'est comme s'il disoit \u00e0 Dieu: Oui, je veux, Seigneur, accomplir d\u00e8s \u00e0 pr\u00e9sent en esprit, ce que vous acheverez bient\u00f4t d'accomplir r\u00e9ellement & en effet. Vous avez r\u00e9solu pour la punition de mon p\u00e9ch\u00e9, de me r\u00e9duire un jour en cendres; & j'en viens faire d\u00e8s aujourd'hui moi-m\u00eame l'essai. Je pr\u00e9viens l'arr\u00eat de votre justice, & je l'ex\u00e9cute d\u00e9j\u00e0. Ces cendres dans l'ordre de vos divins d\u00e9crets, doivent \u00eatre une partie de la satisfaction & de la vengeance que vous voulez tirer de moi: commencez, sans attendre davantage, \u00e0 vous satisfaire, Seigneur, & \u00e0 vous venger; car me voil\u00e0 couvert de cendres. Il est vrai que ce ne sont pas encore les cendres de la mort; mais au moins sont-ce les cendres de la p\u00e9nitence, qui est une esp\u00e9ce de mort, bien plus propre \u00e0 vous fl\u00e9chir & \u00e0 vous appaiser, que la mort m\u00eame. Appaisez-vous donc, \u00f4 mon Dieu, en voyant ces cendres, qui ne sont que les signes ext\u00e9rieurs de l'humiliation & de la contrition de mon ame; & faites que la p\u00e9nitence me rende aupr\u00e8s de vous ce bon office, de pr\u00e9venir dans moi l'effet de la mort; c'est-\u00e0-dire, de me soumettre volontairement & librement \u00e0 votre justice adorable, avant que la mort m'y soumette par cette in\u00e9vitable n\u00e9cessit\u00e9, dont le souvenir, quoiqu'amer, m'est si salutaire: Memento, homo, quia pulvis es, & in pulverem revert\u00e9ris."}, {"anchor": "b-p019", "label": "English", "text": "Now, for great and small alike, once humility has taken possession of a heart, compunction and penance can easily enter it. Why? Not only because penance's great obstacle has been removed\u2014I mean the fund of presumption and pride with which we are born\u2014but because, on close examination, humility is indeed the most essential part of the sinner's conversion. The moment I am disposed to humble myself, I am disposed to accuse, condemn, and punish myself; I am on the way to seeking God, imploring his mercy, satisfying his justice, and returning to obedience to his law: the dispositions most necessary for Christian penance. That is why, after making us consider two kinds of ashes, those of our origin\u2014\u2018Remember that you are dust\u2019\u2014and those of our future corruption\u2014\u2018and to dust you will return\u2019\u2014the first teaching that we are nothing, the second that we are something still less, or rather something worse, since we are only sin; after, I say, setting these two ashes before our eyes, the Church imposes a third, perfectly corresponding to both: the ashes of penance. For what does the sinner do when today he receives from the priest's hands the ashes offered to him? My dear hearers, learn to fulfil this Christian duty as Christians. What does the converted sinner do on receiving these ashes consecrated to penance? It is as though he said to God: \u2018Yes, Lord, I wish to accomplish in spirit now what you will soon complete in reality. To punish my sin you have resolved one day to reduce me to ashes; today I come to make the first trial myself. I anticipate the sentence of your justice and already execute it. In the order of your divine decrees these ashes must form part of the satisfaction and vengeance you wish to exact from me. Begin, Lord, without waiting longer, to receive satisfaction and to avenge yourself, for here I am covered in ashes. It is true they are not yet the ashes of death; but at least they are the ashes of penance, a kind of death far more fitted to move and appease you than death itself. Be appeased, then, my God, at the sight of these ashes, which are only the outward signs of my soul's humiliation and contrition. Let penance do me this good service before you: to anticipate in me death's effect, that is, to submit me willingly and freely to your adorable justice before death submits me to it by that unavoidable necessity whose remembrance, though bitter, is so saving for me: Remember, man, that you are dust, and to dust you will return.\u2019", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p020", "label": "French original", "text": "Voil\u00e0, Chr\u00e9tiens, les sentimens qu'une ame vraiement touch\u00e9e con\u00e7oit en ce jour au pied des Autels; & il faut toujours reconno\u00eetre que ce souvenir de la mort, est un admirable moyen pour pr\u00e9parer \u00e0 la p\u00e9nitence les p\u00e9cheurs les plus orgueilleux. En effet, nous voyons que ce moyen en certaines occasions, m\u00e9nag\u00e9 avec prudence & avec vigueur, a op\u00e9r\u00e9 des changemens qui parurent comme des miracles de la gr\u00e2ce. Et ne fut-ce pas ainsi que saint Ambroise dompta, si j'ose me servir de ce terme, la fiert\u00e9 de Th\u00e9odose; & qu'apr\u00e8s la sanglante journ\u00e9e de Thessalonique, il le rangea \u00e0 l'ordre de la p\u00e9nitence & de la rigoureuse discipline qui s'observoit alors dans l'Eglise? Peut-\u00eatre, lui dit-il, \u00f4 Empereur (car c'est la remontrance qu'il lui fit, rapport\u00e9e par Th\u00e9odoret; je n'y ajouterai rien, & je n'en fais qu'une traduction simple & fidelle:) peut-\u00eatre, \u00f4 Empereur, cette souveraine puissance que vous exercez dans le monde, est-elle comme un nuage \u00e9pais qui obscurcit votre raison, & qui vous emp\u00eache de voir l'\u00e9normit\u00e9 de votre p\u00e9ch\u00e9. Mais pour dissiper ce nuage, consid\u00e9rez le commencement & la fin de toute votre grandeur; c'est-\u00e0-dire, consid\u00e9rez cette cendre dont vous avez \u00e9t\u00e9 form\u00e9, & o\u00f9 vous \u00eates pr\u00eat \u00e0 retourner, & alors je me promets tout de votre religion. Avouez qu'assis sur le tr\u00f4ne, vous ne laissez pas d'\u00eatre homme, un homme rempli de mis\u00e9res & sujet \u00e0 la mort. Avouez que ces hommes qui vous r\u00e9v\u00e9rent & qui tremblent devant vous, sont de m\u00eame nature que vous; & puisque vous \u00eates mortel & p\u00e9cheur comme eux, pensez comme eux \u00e0 vous humilier devant ce Dieu de Majest\u00e9, aupr\u00e8s de qui vous ne devez point esp\u00e9rer grace, si vous ne vous h\u00e2tez de d\u00e9tourner son courroux par votre p\u00e9nitence & par vos larmes. Ces paroles \u00e9murent Th\u00e9odose. Il se prosterna aux pieds de saint Ambroise; il pleura son crime, il le d\u00e9testa; & tout Empereur qu'il \u00e9toit, il en fit la p\u00e9nitence la plus exemplaire & la plus \u00e9difiante: pourquoi? parce qu'on lui fit conno\u00eetre ce qu'il \u00e9toit & ce qu'il devoit \u00eatre un jour: Memento, homo, quia pulvis es, & in pulverem revert\u00e9ris. Or si l'on en usoit ainsi avec tous les grands du si\u00e9cle qui vivent dans le d\u00e9r\u00e9glement des moeurs, & qu'on leur r\u00e9p\u00e9t\u00e2t souvent qu'ils doivent mourir; que l'arr\u00eat qui les y condamne est sans appel; que pendant qu'ils abusent des biens de la vie, & qu'ils se laissent emporter au torrent de leurs passions, la mort s'avance \u00e0 grands pas; qu'elle n'aura nul \u00e9gard \u00e0 tout ce faste qui les accompagne, mais que la derni\u00e9re de toutes les humiliations, qui consiste \u00e0 devenir poussi\u00e9re & cendre, est le sort infaillible qui les attend; & qu'au m\u00eame-tems que la mort leur fera subir toute la rigueur de sa loi, elle les conduira devant ce Juge redoutable, qui doit rendre \u00e0 chacun selon ses oeuvres: si ceux qui les approchent leur tenoient souvent ce langage, quelque endurcis dans le p\u00e9ch\u00e9 que nous nous les figurions, ils penseroient \u00e0 se convertir. Ce qui les entretient dans l'imp\u00e9nitence, c'est un profond oubli de cette grande & incontestable v\u00e9rit\u00e9. C'est qu'au lieu de leur parler de leur mis\u00e9re & de leur foiblesse, on ne leur parle que de leur grandeur & de leur pouvoir. C'est qu'au lieu de les faire souvenir de la mort, on les flatte sans cesse d'une pr\u00e9tendue immortalit\u00e9 de gloire. C'est qu'au lieu de leur dire qu'ils sont hommes, on voudroit presque leur faire accroire qu'ils sont des Dieux."}, {"anchor": "b-p020", "label": "English", "text": "Christians, these are the sentiments a soul truly moved conceives today at the foot of the altars. We must always acknowledge that remembering death is an admirable means of preparing even the proudest sinners for penance. Indeed, we see that on certain occasions this means, handled prudently and vigorously, has effected changes that seemed miracles of grace. Was it not thus that Saint Ambrose subdued, if I dare use that term, Theodosius' pride, and after the bloody day at Thessalonica brought him under the order of penance and the rigorous discipline then observed in the Church? \u2018Perhaps, O Emperor,\u2019 he said\u2014for this is his remonstrance as Theodoret reports it; I shall add nothing and give only a simple, faithful translation\u2014\u2018perhaps, O Emperor, the sovereign power you exercise in the world is like a thick cloud darkening your reason and preventing you from seeing the enormity of your sin. To dispel this cloud, consider the beginning and end of all your greatness: consider the ashes from which you were formed and to which you are ready to return; then I shall expect everything from your piety. Admit that, seated on the throne, you remain a man, full of miseries and subject to death. Admit that those men who revere you and tremble before you share your nature; since you are mortal and sinful like them, think like them of humbling yourself before the God of Majesty, from whom you must not hope for grace unless you hasten to turn aside his anger by penance and tears.\u2019 These words moved Theodosius. He prostrated himself at Saint Ambrose's feet, wept for his crime, detested it, and, emperor though he was, performed the most exemplary and edifying penance. Why? Because he was made to know what he was and what he must one day become: \u2018Remember, man, that you are dust, and to dust you will return.\u2019 If all the worldly great who live in moral disorder were treated thus, frequently reminded that they must die; that the sentence condemning them admits no appeal; that while they misuse life's goods and are swept away by their passions, death approaches rapidly; that it will take no account of the splendour surrounding them, and that the last humiliation, becoming dust and ashes, is the unfailing destiny awaiting them; and that, as death subjects them to the full rigour of its law, it will lead them before that dreadful Judge who must render to each according to his works\u2014if those near them often spoke this way, however hardened in sin we imagine them, they would think of conversion. What keeps them impenitent is deep forgetfulness of this great and incontestable truth. Instead of their misery and weakness, they hear only of their greatness and power. Instead of being reminded of death, they are constantly flattered with a supposed immortality of glory. Instead of being told they are men, they are almost made to believe they are gods.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p021", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais il ne s'agit pas seulement ici de la conversion des grands; il s'agit, mes chers Auditeurs, de la v\u00f4tre & de la mienne, qui n'est peut-\u00eatre ni moins difficile, ni moins \u00e9loign\u00e9e. Car pour \u00eatre peu de chose dans le monde, on n'est pas exempt de la corruption de l'orgueil; & l'orgueil dans une condition m\u00e9diocre, est encore, selon l'Ecriture, plus r\u00e9prouv\u00e9 de Dieu. Cependant, Chr\u00e9tiens, tel est souvent notre caract\u00e8re; & voil\u00e0 le d\u00e9sordre affreux qui doit \u00eatre aujourd'hui le sujet de notre confusion. Malgr\u00e9 l'an\u00e9antissement o\u00f9 nous r\u00e9duit la mort, malgr\u00e9 l'aveu solemnel que nous en faisons dans la c\u00e9r\u00e9monie des cendres, nous ne laissons pas d'\u00eatre pleins d'estime pour nous-m\u00eames; & par une funeste cons\u00e9quence, d'\u00eatre ent\u00eat\u00e9s, d'\u00eatre infatu\u00e9s, d'\u00eatre enyvr\u00e9s de l'amour de nous-m\u00eames. Malgr\u00e9 le soin que prend l'Eglise de nous retracer & de nous imprimer vivement ces v\u00e9rit\u00e9s mortifiantes & tout ensemble vivifiantes, mortifiantes selon l'homme, vivifiantes selon Dieu, nous n'en sommes ni plus morts \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames, ni plus d\u00e9tach\u00e9s de nous-m\u00eames. Dieu, dit le Proph\u00e9te Royal, nous humilie dans ce s\u00e9jour d'affliction en nous couvrant de l'ombre de la mort, Humiliasti nos in loco afflictionis, & cooperuit nos umbra mortis; mais renversant les desseins de Dieu, plus nous paroissons humili\u00e9s, moins nous sommes humbles; plus l'ombre de la mort nous couvre, moins le souvenir de la mort nous convertit. Combien de Chr\u00e9tiens hypocrites: car pourquoi craindrois-je de les qualifier de la sorte, lorsque je vois une si monstrueuse opposition entre ce qu'ils professent au-dehors, & ce qu'ils cachent dans l'ame? combien de Chr\u00e9tiens, & peut-\u00eatre de ceux qui m'\u00e9coutent, ont re\u00e7u la cendre de la p\u00e9nitence avec des coeurs pleins d'ambition, avec des coeurs vains, avec des coeurs durs & incirconcis, avec des coeurs rebelles au Saint-Esprit? Or cela m\u00eame, n'est-ce pas une hypocrisie grossi\u00e9re? Combien de femmes mondaines & criminelles ont paru devant les Autels pour y recevoir cette cendre; mais y ont paru avec toutes les marques de leur vanit\u00e9, avec tout l'\u00e9talage de leur luxe, & ce qui en est comme ins\u00e9parable, avec toute l'enflure de leur orgueil? Or en de telles dispositions ont-elles eu l'esprit de la p\u00e9nitence; & n'ayant eu que l'ext\u00e9rieur de la p\u00e9nitence, sans en avoir l'esprit, ne sont-elles pas du nombre des hypocrites, que condamne aujourd'hui le Fils de Dieu dans l'Evangile? Ce sont n\u00e9anmoins, me direz-vous, des femmes r\u00e9gl\u00e9es; & du reste, hors la vanit\u00e9 qui les poss\u00e9de, irr\u00e9prochables dans leur conduite: mais, Chr\u00e9tiens, jugerons-nous toujours des choses selon les fausses id\u00e9es du monde, & jamais selon les pures maximes de la loi de Dieu? Appellez-vous femmes r\u00e9gl\u00e9es, celles qui n'ont pour principe de toutes leurs actions que l'amour d'elles-m\u00eames? appellez-vous femmes irr\u00e9prochables, celles qui voudroient n'\u00eatre au monde que pour y \u00eatre ador\u00e9es & idol\u00e2tr\u00e9es? appellez-vous simple vanit\u00e9, celle qui exclut & qui bannit d'une ame deux vertus les plus n\u00e9cessaires au salut, s\u00e7avoir l'humilit\u00e9 & la p\u00e9nitence?"}, {"anchor": "b-p021", "label": "English", "text": "But this concerns not only the conversion of the great; my dear hearers, it concerns yours and mine, perhaps no less difficult or distant. Being of little account in the world does not exempt anyone from the corruption of pride; and pride in a humble station is, according to Scripture, still more condemned by God. Yet, Christians, this is often our character; here is the dreadful disorder that ought today to put us to shame. Despite the nothingness to which death reduces us, despite our solemn acknowledgment of it in the ceremony of ashes, we remain full of self-esteem and, by a fatal consequence, obstinate, infatuated, intoxicated with self-love. Despite the Church's care to set before us and impress vividly upon us these truths, at once mortifying and life-giving\u2014mortifying according to man, life-giving according to God\u2014we are neither more dead to ourselves nor more detached from ourselves. God, says the royal Prophet, humbles us in this place of affliction by covering us with the shadow of death: \u2018You have humbled us in the place of affliction, and the shadow of death has covered us.\u2019 But, overturning God's designs, the more humbled we appear, the less humble we are; the more death's shadow covers us, the less its remembrance converts us. How many hypocritical Christians! Why should I hesitate to call them so when I see such a monstrous opposition between what they profess outwardly and what they hide in their souls? How many Christians, perhaps among those listening to me, have received the ashes of penance with hearts full of ambition, vain hearts, hard and uncircumcised hearts, hearts rebellious against the Holy Spirit? Is this not gross hypocrisy? How many worldly, sinful women have appeared before the altars to receive these ashes, yet appeared with every mark of vanity, the full display of luxury and, almost inseparable from it, the full swelling of pride? In such dispositions did they possess the spirit of penance? Having only its outward appearance without its spirit, are they not among the hypocrites the Son of God condemns in today's Gospel? \u2018Nevertheless,\u2019 you will tell me, \u2018they are well-behaved women, otherwise irreproachable in conduct apart from the vanity that possesses them.\u2019 But, Christians, shall we always judge by the world's false ideas and never by the pure maxims of God's law? Do you call women well-behaved whose sole principle in all their actions is self-love? Irreproachable, those who would wish to exist in the world only to be adored and idolized? Do you call it mere vanity when it excludes and banishes from a soul two virtues most necessary for salvation, humility and penance?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p022", "label": "French original", "text": "Terre, terre, disoit le Proph\u00e9te, \u00e9coutez la voix du Seigneur; Terra, terra, audi vocem Domini; c'est-\u00e0-dire, p\u00e9cheurs, qui form\u00e9s de la terre, devez bient\u00f4t retourner dans le sein de la terre; vous cependant qui oubliez ce que vous \u00eates, & qui vivez tranquilles dans l'\u00e9tat de votre p\u00e9ch\u00e9, \u00e9coutez Dieu qui vous parle par ma bouche, & ne m\u00e9prisez pas sa voix. Pour faire de dignes fruits de p\u00e9nitence, humiliez-vous sous sa toute-puissante main, Humiliamini sub potenti manu Dei, & que cette humiliation ne soit pas seulement ext\u00e9rieure & superficielle, mais qu'elle p\u00e9n\u00e9tre jusques dans l'int\u00e9rieur de vos ames. D\u00e9chirez vos coeurs, & non point vos v\u00eatemens; Scindite corda vestra, & non vestimenta vestra: & ne ressemblez pas \u00e0 celui que le Saint-Esprit r\u00e9prouve dans ces paroles; Est qui nequiter se humiliat, & interiora ejus plena sunt dolo. Tel s'humilie en apparence dont le coeur est rempli de mensonge & d'artifice. Tel prend la cendre de la p\u00e9nitence, qui sous cette cendre & sous un visage de p\u00e9nitent, entretient un orgueil de d\u00e9mon. Tel dit, Je suis poudre & je serai poudre, qui voudroit, s'il \u00e9toit possible, s'\u00e9lever comme Lucifer au-dessus des cieux. Pr\u00e9servons-nous de cette mal\u00e9diction par l'humilit\u00e9 & la sinc\u00e9rit\u00e9 de notre conversion. C'est ce que la voix du Seigneur vous fait entendre. Ecoutez-la, & respectez-la: Terra, terra, audi vocem Domini. Mais elle vous dit encore, qu'outre le sacrifice de vos esprits par l'humilit\u00e9, la p\u00e9nitence demande le sacrifice de vos corps par la mortification; & j'ajoute que rien ne doit plus vous faciliter ce second sacrifice, que le souvenir de la mort & la vue des cendres: c'est la seconde partie."}, {"anchor": "b-p022", "label": "English", "text": "\u2018Earth, earth,\u2019 said the Prophet, \u2018hear the voice of the Lord\u2019: \u2018Earth, earth, hear the Lord's voice.\u2019 That is, sinners formed from the earth who must soon return into its bosom, you who nevertheless forget what you are and live peacefully in your sinful state, listen to God speaking through my mouth, and do not despise his voice. To bear worthy fruits of penance, humble yourselves under his almighty hand: \u2018Humble yourselves under God's mighty hand.\u2019 Let this humiliation be neither merely outward nor superficial, but penetrate the depths of your souls. Rend your hearts, not your garments: \u2018Rend your hearts and not your garments.\u2019 Do not resemble the man the Holy Spirit condemns in these words: \u2018There is one who wickedly humbles himself, and his inward parts are full of deceit.\u2019 One appears humble whose heart is full of falsehood and artifice. One takes the ashes of penance while beneath them and a penitent's face he nourishes a demon's pride. One says, \u2018I am dust and shall be dust,\u2019 who would, if he could, raise himself like Lucifer above the heavens. Let us preserve ourselves from this curse through humble and sincere conversion. This is what the Lord's voice tells you. Hear and reverence it: \u2018Earth, earth, hear the Lord's voice.\u2019 But it tells you also that, besides the sacrifice of your spirits through humility, penance demands the sacrifice of your bodies through mortification. I add that nothing should make this second sacrifice easier than the remembrance of death and the sight of ashes: this is the second part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-h004", "label": "French original", "text": "II. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "b-h004", "label": "English", "text": "PART TWO", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p023", "label": "French original", "text": "C'Est une illusion, dont l'esprit du monde, cet esprit de mollesse, a voulu de tout tems se pr\u00e9valoir, de croire que la p\u00e9nitence soit une vertu purement int\u00e9rieure, & qu'elle n'exerce son empire que sur les puissances spirituelles de notre ame; qu'elle se contente de changer le coeur, qu'elle n'en veuille qu'\u00e0 nos vices & \u00e0 nos passions, & qu'elle puisse \u00eatre solidement pratiqu\u00e9e, sans que la chair s'en ressente, ni qu'il en co\u00fbte rien \u00e0 cet homme ext\u00e9rieur & terrestre qui fait une partie de nous-m\u00eames. Si cela \u00e9toit, dit saint Chrysostome, il faudroit retrancher de l'Ecriture des livres entiers, o\u00f9 l'esprit de Dieu a confondu sur ce point la prudence charnelle, par des t\u00e9moignages aussi contraires \u00e0 notre amour-propre, que la v\u00e9rit\u00e9 est oppos\u00e9e \u00e0 l'erreur. Il faudroit dire que saint Paul ne l'entendoit pas, & qu'il concevoit mal la p\u00e9nitence Chr\u00e9tienne quand il enseignoit, qu'elle doit faire de nos corps des hosties vivantes, Exhibeatis corpora vestra hostiam viventem: quand il vouloit que cette vertu m\u00eame all\u00e2t jusqu'au crucifiement de la chair; Qui sunt Christi, carnem suam crucifixerunt cum vitiis & concupiscentiis: quand il recommandoit aux fid\u00e9les, ou plut\u00f4t, quand il leur faisoit une loi de porter sensiblement & r\u00e9ellement dans leurs corps la mortification de Jesus-Christ; Semper mortificationem Jesu in corpore vestro circumferentes: enfin quand pour leur donner l'exemple, il matoit lui-m\u00eame son corps & le r\u00e9duisoit en servitude; craignant, ajo\u00fbtoit-il, qu'apr\u00e8s avoir pr\u00each\u00e9 aux autres la p\u00e9nitence & ne la pratiquant pas, il ne dev\u00eent un r\u00e9prouv\u00e9: Castigo corpus meum, & in servitutem redigo; ne forte cum aliis praedicavero, ipse reprobus efficiar."}, {"anchor": "b-p023", "label": "English", "text": "IT is an illusion that the spirit of the world, that spirit of self-indulgence, has always wished to exploit: to believe that penance is a purely inward virtue exercising dominion only over the spiritual powers of our soul; that it is content to change the heart and attack only our vices and passions; that it can be truly practised without the flesh feeling it or any cost to that outward, earthly man who forms part of ourselves. If this were so, says Saint John Chrysostom, we should have to cut whole books out of Scripture, where God's Spirit has confounded carnal prudence on this point by testimonies as contrary to our self-love as truth is opposed to error. We should have to say that Saint Paul did not understand it and misconceived Christian penance when he taught that it must make our bodies living sacrifices: \u2018Offer your bodies as a living sacrifice\u2019; when he wished this very virtue to extend to crucifying the flesh: \u2018Those who belong to Christ have crucified their flesh with its vices and desires\u2019; when he recommended to the faithful\u2014or rather laid it upon them as a law\u2014to bear sensibly and really in their bodies the mortification of Jesus Christ: \u2018Always carrying the mortification of Jesus in your body\u2019; finally, when, to give them an example, he himself subdued his body and reduced it to servitude, fearing, he added, that after preaching penance to others without practising it he might become a reprobate: \u2018I chastise my body and bring it into servitude, lest, after preaching to others, I myself should be rejected.\u2019", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p024", "label": "French original", "text": "Je s\u00e7ais que l'h\u00e9r\u00e9sie avec sa pr\u00e9tendue r\u00e9forme, n'a p\u00fb s'accommoder de ces pratiques ext\u00e9rieures: & qu'apr\u00e8s avoir an\u00e9anti la p\u00e9nitence dans ses parties les plus essentielles, en lui \u00f4tant & la confession & la contrition m\u00eame du p\u00e9ch\u00e9, au moins ne les admettant pas comme n\u00e9cessaires; elle a encore trouv\u00e9 moyen de l'adoucir, en rejettant comme inutiles les oeuvres satisfactoires; en abolissant le pr\u00e9cepte du je\u00fbne, & en traitant de foiblesses & de folies toutes les aust\u00e9rit\u00e9s des Saints. Mais il suffit que ce soient les ennemis de l'Eglise qui en aient jug\u00e9 de la sorte, pour ne pas suivre l'attrait pernicieux d'une doctrine aussi capable que celle-l\u00e0 de s\u00e9duire les ames & de les corrompre. Non, Chr\u00e9tiens, de quelque mani\u00e9re que nous prenions la chose, il n'y a point de v\u00e9ritable p\u00e9nitence, sans la mortification du corps; & tandis que nos corps apr\u00e8s le p\u00e9ch\u00e9 demeurent impunis, tandis qu'ils ne subiront pas les ch\u00e2timens qu'un saint z\u00e9le de venger Dieu nous oblige \u00e0 leur imposer, jamais nos coeurs ne seront bien convertis, ni jamais Dieu ne se tiendra pleinement satisfait. Depuis que le Sauveur du monde a fait p\u00e9nitence pour nous aux d\u00e9pens de sa chair adorable, il est impossible, dit S. Augustin, que nous la fassions autrement nous-m\u00eames. Il faut que nous accomplissions dans notre chair, ce qui manque, par un admirable secret de la sagesse de Dieu, aux satisfactions & aux souffrances de notre divin M\u00e9diateur. Puisque c'est dans notre chair que le p\u00e9ch\u00e9 r\u00e9gne, comme parle saint Paul, c'est dans notre chair que doit r\u00e9gner la p\u00e9nitence, car elle doit r\u00e9gner par-tout o\u00f9 r\u00e9gne le p\u00e9ch\u00e9. Nos corps par une malheureuse contagion, & par l'intime liaison qu'ils ont avec nos ames, deviennent les complices du p\u00e9ch\u00e9, servent d'instrument au p\u00e9ch\u00e9, sont souvent l'origine & la source du p\u00e9ch\u00e9, jusques-l\u00e0 que le m\u00eame Ap\u00f4tre ne craint point de les appeller des corps de p\u00e9ch\u00e9, Corpus peccati: comme si le p\u00e9ch\u00e9 \u00e9toit en effet incorpor\u00e9 dans nous, & que nos corps fussent par eux-m\u00eames des substances de p\u00e9ch\u00e9. Expression dont abusoient autrefois les Manich\u00e9ens; mais qui dans le sens orthodoxe ne signifie rien davantage que des corps sujets au p\u00e9ch\u00e9, des corps par o\u00f9 subsiste le p\u00e9ch\u00e9, des corps o\u00f9 habite le p\u00e9ch\u00e9: nos corps, dis-je, ont part au p\u00e9ch\u00e9; il est donc juste qu'ils participent \u00e0 l'expiation & \u00e0 la r\u00e9paration du p\u00e9ch\u00e9, qui se doit faire par la p\u00e9nitence. Quoique la vertu & le m\u00e9rite de la p\u00e9nitence soit dans la volont\u00e9, l'exercice & l'usage de la p\u00e9nitence doit consister en partie dans la mortification du corps; & quiconque raisonne autrement, est dans l'erreur & s'\u00e9gare. Voil\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs, la disposition o\u00f9 nous devons entrer aujourd'hui, si nous voulons profiter de la grace que Dieu nous offre pendant ce saint tems d'abstinence & de je\u00fbne."}, {"anchor": "b-p024", "label": "English", "text": "I know that heresy with its so-called reform could not accommodate these outward practices. After destroying penance in its most essential parts by removing confession and even contrition for sin, or at least not admitting their necessity, it found further means of softening it: rejecting works of satisfaction as useless, abolishing the precept of fasting, and treating all the saints' austerities as weaknesses and follies. But the fact that it was the Church's enemies who judged this way is enough to keep us from following the pernicious attraction of a doctrine so capable of seducing and corrupting souls. No, Christians: however we take the matter, there is no true penance without bodily mortification. As long as our bodies remain unpunished after sin and do not undergo the chastisements that a holy zeal to vindicate God obliges us to impose, our hearts will never be thoroughly converted, nor will God consider himself fully satisfied. Since the Saviour of the world did penance for us at the cost of his adorable flesh, it is impossible, says Saint Augustine, for us to do it otherwise ourselves. In our flesh we must complete what, by an admirable secret of God's wisdom, is lacking in the satisfactions and sufferings of our divine Mediator. Since sin reigns in our flesh, as Saint Paul says, penance must reign there too, for it must reign wherever sin reigns. Through an unhappy contagion and their intimate bond with our souls, our bodies become accomplices and instruments of sin, often its origin and source, so that the same Apostle does not hesitate to call them bodies of sin: \u2018The body of sin\u2019\u2014as if sin were actually embodied in us and our bodies were substances of sin in themselves. The Manichaeans once abused this expression; but in its orthodox sense it means only bodies subject to sin, bodies through which sin subsists, bodies in which sin dwells. Our bodies, I say, share in sin; it is therefore just that they share in its expiation and reparation through penance. Although penance's virtue and merit reside in the will, its exercise and practice must consist partly in bodily mortification; whoever reasons otherwise is mistaken and goes astray. My dear hearers, this is the disposition we must enter today if we wish to profit from the grace God offers during this holy time of abstinence and fasting.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p025", "label": "French original", "text": "Or \u00e0 cette loi de p\u00e9nitence ainsi \u00e9tablie, s'oppose une autre loi que nous portons dans nous-m\u00eames, & qui est l'amour d\u00e9r\u00e9gl\u00e9 de nos corps. Amour, concevez-en bien le progr\u00e8s pour en \u00e9viter le d\u00e9sordre & la corruption, amour de tout ce qui nous paro\u00eet n\u00e9cessaire, ou plut\u00f4t, de tout ce qu'une aveugle cupidit\u00e9 nous repr\u00e9sente comme n\u00e9cessaire pour l'entretien de nos corps; amour de toutes les commodit\u00e9s que nous recherchons avec tant de soin, & qui flattent nos corps; amour des d\u00e9lices de la vie, qui par leur superfluit\u00e9 & leur exc\u00e8s affoiblissent souvent, ou m\u00eame d\u00e9truisent nos corps; amour des plaisirs d\u00e9fendus & des volupt\u00e9s illicites, qui souillent nos corps. Car ce sont l\u00e0, confessons-le devant Dieu, Chr\u00e9tiens, & apprenons au moins \u00e0 nous conno\u00eetre par ce qu'il y a dans nous de plus grossier, ce sont l\u00e0 les d\u00e9marches d'une ame qui se d\u00e9r\u00e9gle, en se rendant esclave de son corps. Elle ne va pas d'abord au crime; mais sous ombre d'entretenir ce corps & de pourvoir \u00e0 ses besoins, du n\u00e9cessaire elle passe au commode, du commode au superflu, & du superflu au criminel: au lieu, dit saint Gr\u00e9goire Pape, que la p\u00e9nitence qui a pour but d'assujettir & de mortifier le corps, par une conduite toute contraire nous fait d'abord renoncer au criminel que nous avouons nous-m\u00eames criminel; ensuite \u00e0 mesure que nous avan\u00e7ons dans ses voies, nous retranche le superflu, que nous pr\u00e9tendions innocent; de-l\u00e0 nous prive m\u00eame du commode, dont nous avions cr\u00fb ne nous pouvoir passer; enfin nous \u00f4te, non pas le n\u00e9cessaire, mais l'attachement & l'attention trop grande au n\u00e9cessaire. Excellente id\u00e9e de la p\u00e9nitence, & de ses divers d\u00e9gr\u00e9s. S'il y en a o\u00f9 notre foiblesse n'ose encore esp\u00e9rer d'atteindre, du moins ne les ignorons pas, & d\u00e9sirons d'y parvenir. Elle nous fait renoncer au criminel, c'est-\u00e0-dire, aux plaisirs impurs que la loi de Dieu nous d\u00e9fend, parce qu'il n'y a point de p\u00e9ch\u00e9 plus oppos\u00e9 \u00e0 la saintet\u00e9 de Dieu, ni plus incompatible avec son esprit, que l'impuret\u00e9: Non permanebit Spiritus meus in homine, quia caro est. Elle nous retranche le superflu, c'est-\u00e0-dire, les d\u00e9lices de la vie, parce qu'il n'y a rien de plus difficile \u00e0 accorder ensemble qu'une vie molle & l'innocence des moeurs, & que cette innocence, dit Job, ne se trouve point parmi ceux qui ne pensent qu'\u00e0 satisfaire leurs sens: Non invenitur in terra suaviter viventium. Elle nous prive du commode, c'est-\u00e0-dire, des aises de la vie, qui, quoique absolument permises, ne laissent pas de fomenter la r\u00e9bellion de la chair. Et elle nous \u00f4te m\u00eame une trop grande attention au n\u00e9cessaire, parce que c'est un point de morale inconnu aux Saints, de pr\u00e9tendre ne souffrir rien, ne se refuser rien, ne manquer de rien, & faire n\u00e9anmoins p\u00e9nitence. Mais ce que les Saints ne comprenoient pas, est devenu un des secrets de la d\u00e9votion du si\u00e9cle. Car on peut dire que jamais si\u00e9cle n'a parl\u00e9 avec plus d'ostentation que le n\u00f4tre de la p\u00e9nitence s\u00e9v\u00e9re, ni n'a port\u00e9 plus loin dans la pratique le raffinement sur tout ce qui s'appelle vie douce. Ne s'aveugle-t-on pas m\u00eame quelquefois jusqu'\u00e0 se faire un devoir de m\u00e9nager son corps? Ne va-t-on pas jusqu'\u00e0 se persuader qu'on est n\u00e9cessaire au monde, & que c'est une raison sup\u00e9rieure pour se dispenser des loix les plus communes de la mortification Chr\u00e9tienne? Cependant l'Ap\u00f4tre l'a dit, & il est vrai: La p\u00e9nitence pour \u00eatre parfaite, doit s'\u00e9tendre jusqu'\u00e0 la haine de soi-m\u00eame; & l'on ne peut bien r\u00e9parer le p\u00e9ch\u00e9, qu'en crucifiant cette chair de p\u00e9ch\u00e9, qui est l'ennemie de Dieu: Qui Christi sunt, carnem suam crucifixerunt."}, {"anchor": "b-p025", "label": "English", "text": "But another law that we carry within us opposes this established law of penance: the disordered love of our bodies. Understand its progress well, to avoid its disorder and corruption: love of everything that seems necessary\u2014or rather everything blind desire represents as necessary\u2014for maintaining our bodies; love of all the comforts we seek so carefully, which gratify our bodies; love of life's delights, whose superfluity and excess often weaken or even destroy our bodies; love of forbidden pleasures and illicit sensuality, which defile our bodies. Let us confess it before God, Christians, and at least learn to know ourselves through what is grossest in us: these are the steps of a soul becoming disordered by enslaving itself to its body. It does not go straight to wrongdoing; under the pretext of maintaining the body and providing for its needs, it passes from necessity to convenience, from convenience to excess, from excess to sin. Penance, on the contrary, says Pope Saint Gregory, aims to subject and mortify the body. By an entirely opposite course it first makes us renounce the sinful, which we ourselves admit is sinful; then, as we advance in its ways, it removes the superfluous, which we claimed was innocent; next it deprives us even of conveniences we thought indispensable; finally it takes away not necessities, but attachment and excessive attention to them. An excellent understanding of penance and its various degrees! If our weakness does not yet dare hope to attain some of them, at least let us know them and desire to reach them. Penance makes us renounce the sinful, that is, impure pleasures forbidden by God's law, because no sin is more opposed to God's holiness or more incompatible with his spirit than impurity: \u2018My Spirit will not remain in man, because he is flesh.\u2019 It removes excess, that is, life's delights, because nothing is harder to reconcile than an indulgent life and innocence of conduct; this innocence, says Job, is not found among those who think only of satisfying their senses: \u2018It is not found in the land of those who live pleasantly.\u2019 It deprives us of convenience, that is, life's comforts, which, although entirely permitted, nevertheless foster the flesh's rebellion. It even removes excessive attention to necessities, for the saints knew no moral teaching that allowed one to suffer nothing, deny oneself nothing, lack nothing, and nevertheless do penance. Yet what the saints did not understand has become one of the secrets of worldly devotion. Never, we may say, has an age spoken more ostentatiously of severe penance than ours, or carried refinement further in the practice of everything called comfortable living. Do we not sometimes blind ourselves enough to make a duty of sparing our bodies? Do we not even persuade ourselves that we are necessary to the world, and that this is a superior reason to dispense ourselves from the commonest laws of Christian mortification? Yet the Apostle has said it, and it is true: to be perfect, penance must extend to hatred of self; sin cannot be properly repaired except by crucifying this flesh of sin, God's enemy: \u2018Those who belong to Christ have crucified their flesh.\u2019", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p026", "label": "French original", "text": "Or le moyen d'arriver l\u00e0? souvenons-nous de la mort, & consid\u00e9rons les cendres qu'on r\u00e9pand aujourd'hui sur nos t\u00eates; c'est assez: Memento. Occupons-nous de la pens\u00e9e qu'il faut mourir, & rendons-nous-la famili\u00e9re, Memento. Entrons par de s\u00e9rieuses & de solides r\u00e9flexions dans le myst\u00e8re de ces cendres, Memento: & jamais l'esprit de mollesse ne l'emportera sur l'esprit de mortification."}, {"anchor": "b-p026", "label": "English", "text": "How can we reach that point? Let us remember death and consider the ashes scattered on our heads today; that is enough: \u2018Remember.\u2019 Let us occupy ourselves with the thought that we must die and make it familiar: \u2018Remember.\u2019 Through serious, substantial reflection let us enter the mystery of these ashes: \u2018Remember.\u2019 Then the spirit of self-indulgence will never prevail over the spirit of mortification.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p027", "label": "French original", "text": "Oui, Chr\u00e9tiens, le souvenir de la mort vous d\u00e9tachera peu \u00e0 peu & presque malgr\u00e9 vous-m\u00eames de l'amour de votre corps; comment cela? en vous faisant conno\u00eetre l\u00e0-dessus votre aveuglement & votre injustice. Votre aveuglement; car dites-moi s'il en fut jamais un plus d\u00e9plorable, que d'idol\u00e2trer un corps qui n'est que poussi\u00e9re & que corruption; un corps destin\u00e9 \u00e0 servir de p\u00e2ture aux vers, & qui bient\u00f4t sera dans le tombeau l'horreur de toute la nature. Or voil\u00e0 le terme de tous les plaisirs des sens; c'est l\u00e0 que se r\u00e9duisent toutes ces graces ext\u00e9rieures de beaut\u00e9, de sant\u00e9, de teint, d'embonpoint, qui vous font n\u00e9gliger les plus pr\u00e9tieuses graces du salut. C'est l\u00e0 qu'elles vont aboutir: \u00e0 un corps qui commence d\u00e9j\u00e0 \u00e0 se d\u00e9truire, & qui apr\u00e8s un certain nombre de jours ne sera plus qu'un affreux cadavre dont on ne pourra pas m\u00eame supporter la vue. Ah, mes chers Auditeurs, quelle indignit\u00e9, qu'une ame Chr\u00e9tienne capable de poss\u00e9der Dieu, s'attache \u00e0 un sujet si m\u00e9prisable! Vous sur-tout, Mesdames, \u00e0 qui je parle, & qui avez de la pi\u00e9t\u00e9, ne devez-vous pas g\u00e9mir pour ces personnes de votre sexe, qui semblent n'\u00eatre sur la terre, & n'avoir une ame que pour servir leur corps? Combien en voit-on dans le Christianisme uniquement appliqu\u00e9es \u00e0 le parer, \u00e0 l'embellir, \u00e0 le nourrir. Combien en feroient, s'il leur \u00e9toit possible, l'idole du monde, & en font sans y penser une victime de l'enfer? Puisque ce corps est quelque chose de si vil & de si abjet, n'est-on pas bien plus sens\u00e9 de le m\u00e9priser, de le dompter, de l'assujettir, & de lui faire porter le joug de la p\u00e9nitence? Pour peu que nous consultions & la raison & la foi, ne doit-on pas rougir, de se rendre si attentif \u00e0 \u00e9tudier ses go\u00fbts, de s'asservir \u00e0 ses app\u00e9tits, & de lui donner honteusement tout ce qu'il demande & souvent plus qu'il ne demande?"}, {"anchor": "b-p027", "label": "English", "text": "Yes, Christians, the remembrance of death will gradually, almost despite yourselves, detach you from love of your body. How? By making you recognize your blindness and injustice in this matter. Your blindness: tell me, was there ever a more deplorable blindness than to idolize a body that is only dust and corruption, destined as food for worms, soon to become in the tomb the horror of all nature? That is the end of every sensual pleasure; that is what becomes of all those outward graces of beauty, health, complexion, and fullness of figure that make you neglect the more precious graces of salvation. They end in a body already beginning to decay, which after a certain number of days will be only a dreadful corpse whose very sight is unbearable. My dear hearers, how unworthy that a Christian soul, capable of possessing God, should attach itself to so contemptible an object! Especially you ladies whom I address and who possess piety: ought you not lament those of your sex who seem to exist on earth and possess a soul only to serve their bodies? How many in Christianity are concerned solely to adorn, beautify, and nourish it! How many would make it the world's idol if they could, and without thinking make it a victim of hell! Since this body is so base and abject, is it not far wiser to despise, subdue, and subject it, and make it bear the yoke of penance? If we consult reason and faith even a little, should we not blush to study its tastes so attentively, enslave ourselves to its appetites, and shamefully give it everything it asks, often more than it asks?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p028", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais d'ailleurs quelle injustice dans cet amour immod\u00e9r\u00e9 de notre corps, si nous envisageons la mort? Prenez garde \u00e0 ces trois pens\u00e9es. Quelle injustice envers Dieu, ce Dieu \u00e9ternel, d'aimer plus que lui un corps sujet \u00e0 la pourriture, & de l'aimer, comme dit S. Paul, jusqu'\u00e0 s'en faire une divinit\u00e9? Quelle injustice envers notre ame, cette ame immortelle, de lui pr\u00e9f\u00e9rer un corps qui doit mourir; & toute immortelle qu'elle est, d'abandonner sa f\u00e9licit\u00e9 & sa gloire aux sales d\u00e9sirs d'une chair corruptible? Quelle injustice envers ce corps m\u00eame, de l'exposer pour des volupt\u00e9s passag\u00e9res \u00e0 des souffrances qui ne finiront jamais, & de lui faire acheter un moment de plaisir par une \u00e9ternit\u00e9 de supplices? Ah! mes Fr\u00e9res, s'\u00e9crie saint Chrysostome, faisant une supposition qui vous surprendra, mais qui n'a rien dans le fond que de Chr\u00e9tien & de solide; si le corps d'un r\u00e9prouv\u00e9, maintenant enseveli dans le sein de la terre, mais pour \u00eatre un jour enseveli dans l'enfer, pouvoit au jugement de Dieu s'\u00e9lever contre son ame & l'accuser, quel reproche n'auroit-il pas \u00e0 lui faire, sur la cruelle indulgence dont elle a us\u00e9 \u00e0 son \u00e9gard? Et si cette ame qui s'est perdue, parce qu'elle a trop aim\u00e9 son corps, pouvoit au moment que je parle revenir du lieu de son tourment, pour voir ce corps dans le tombeau, quels reproches ne se feroit-elle pas \u00e0 elle-m\u00eame du criminel attachement qu'elle a eu pour lui? Disons mieux, que ne se reprocheroient-ils pas l'un \u00e0 l'autre, si Dieu venoit \u00e0 les confronter? Permettez-moi de pousser cette figure, qui toute irr\u00e9guli\u00e9re & toute outr\u00e9e qu'elle peut paro\u00eetre, vous fera plus vivement sentir la v\u00e9rit\u00e9 que je vous pr\u00eache. Ame infid\u00e9le, diroit l'un, deviez-vous me trahir de la sorte? falloit-il pour me rendre un moment heureux, me pr\u00e9cipiter avec vous dans l'abysme d'une \u00e9ternelle damnation? falloit-il avoir pour moi une si funeste condescendance? falloit-il d\u00e9f\u00e9rer l\u00e2chement \u00e0 mes inclinations? ne les deviez-vous pas r\u00e9primer? ne deviez-vous pas prendre l'ascendant sur moi? que ne m'avez-vous condamn\u00e9 aux salutaires rigueurs de la p\u00e9nitence? pourquoi ne m'avez-vous pas forc\u00e9 \u00e0 vivre selon les r\u00e9gles que Dieu vous obligeoit \u00e0 me prescrire? n'\u00e9toit-ce pas pour cela qu'il m'avoit soumis \u00e0 vous? Mais, corps rebelle & sensuel, r\u00e9pondroit l'ame, \u00e0 qui dois-je imputer ma perte qu'\u00e0 toi-m\u00eame? je ne te connoissois pas; je me laissois s\u00e9duire \u00e0 tes charmes, parce que je ne pensois, ni \u00e0 ce que tu avois \u00e9t\u00e9, ni \u00e0 ce que tu devois \u00eatre. Si j'avois toujours eu en vue l'affreux \u00e9tat o\u00f9 la mort devoit te r\u00e9duire, je n'aurois eu pour toi que du m\u00e9pris; & dans la soci\u00e9t\u00e9 qui nous unissoit, je ne t'aurois regard\u00e9 que comme le compagnon de mes mis\u00e9res, ou plut\u00f4t comme le complice de mes crimes, oblig\u00e9 par l\u00e0 m\u00eame \u00e0 en partager avec moi les ch\u00e2timens & les peines."}, {"anchor": "b-p028", "label": "English", "text": "Moreover, what injustice there is in this immoderate love of our body, if we consider death! Attend to these three thoughts. What injustice towards God, the eternal God, to love more than him a body subject to decay, and to love it, as Saint Paul says, to the point of making a god of it! What injustice towards our soul, that immortal soul, to prefer to it a body that must die and, immortal though it is, abandon its happiness and glory to the foul desires of corruptible flesh! What injustice towards the body itself, to expose it for passing pleasures to sufferings that will never end, making it purchase a moment's pleasure with an eternity of torments! \u2018Ah, my brethren,\u2019 cries Saint John Chrysostom, making a supposition that will surprise you but is at bottom wholly Christian and sound, \u2018if the body of a reprobate, now buried in the earth but one day to be buried in hell, could rise at God's judgment against its soul and accuse it, what reproaches would it not make for the cruel indulgence the soul showed it? And if that soul, lost through loving its body too much, could at this moment return from its place of torment to see the body in the tomb, how would it not reproach itself for its sinful attachment?\u2019 Better still, what would they not reproach one another with if God confronted them? Allow me to develop this figure: however irregular and exaggerated it may seem, it will make you feel more vividly the truth I preach. \u2018Faithless soul,\u2019 one would say, \u2018should you have betrayed me so? To make me happy for a moment, must you plunge me with you into the abyss of eternal damnation? Must you show me such fatal indulgence, yield so weakly to my inclinations? Should you not have restrained them and gained mastery over me? Why did you not condemn me to penance's saving rigours? Why did you not force me to live by the rules God obliged you to prescribe? Was that not why he subjected me to you?\u2019 \u2018But rebellious, sensual body,\u2019 the soul would answer, \u2018to whom should I impute my ruin but you? I did not know you; I let your charms seduce me because I thought neither of what you had been nor of what you must become. Had I always kept before me the dreadful state to which death must reduce you, I should have felt only contempt for you. In the partnership uniting us, I should have regarded you only as the companion of my miseries, or rather as the accomplice of my crimes, obliged for that very reason to share their chastisements and penalties with me.\u2019", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p029", "label": "French original", "text": "En effet, Chr\u00e9tiens, c'est de tout tems ce qui a produit dans les ames bien converties, non-seulement ce m\u00e9pris h\u00e9ro\u00efque, mais cette sainte haine de leur corps: c'est ce qui a tant de fois op\u00e9r\u00e9 dans le Christianisme des miracles de conversion. Il n'en fallut pas davantage \u00e0 un Fran\u00e7ois de Borgia, pour le d\u00e9terminer \u00e0 quitter le monde. La vue du cadavre d'une Reine & d'une Imp\u00e9ratrice, qu'il eut ordre de faire solemnellement inhumer, & qu'il ne reconnut presque plus, lorsqu'il fallut attester que c'\u00e9toit elle-m\u00eame, tant elle lui parut hideuse & d\u00e9figur\u00e9e, ce spectacle acheva de le persuader. Il ne put voir cette beaut\u00e9 que la mort par un changement si soudain & si prodigieux avoit d\u00e9truite, sans former la r\u00e9solution de mourir lui-m\u00eame \u00e0 toutes les vanit\u00e9s du si\u00e9cle. L'image de la mort en frappant ses yeux, fit na\u00eetre dans son coeur tous les sentimens de la p\u00e9nitence. Car pourquoi, se dit-il \u00e0 lui-m\u00eame & se sont dit comme lui les Saints, pourquoi traiter mollement un corps condamn\u00e9 \u00e0 la mort? Quand on a prononc\u00e9 l'arr\u00eat \u00e0 un criminel, on ne se met plus en peine de le bien nourrir: s'il faut encore le soutenir pendant quelques heures, on se contente de lui donner le n\u00e9cessaire; & l'on ne pense \u00e0 lui conserver la vie, que pour lui faire mieux sentir les douleurs de la mort. Or telle est la conduite de nos corps. Ce sont des criminels que la justice divine a condamn\u00e9s. L'arr\u00eat en est port\u00e9, & l'on ne diff\u00e9re l'ex\u00e9cution que de quelques jours. Mais ce sera bient\u00f4t. Il ne s'agit donc plus de leur procurer des douceurs & de les flatter; il s'agit de les maintenir dans l'ordre de cette justice rigoureuse \u00e0 laquelle Dieu les a livr\u00e9s; il s'agit de leur faire d\u00e9j\u00e0 go\u00fbter la mort par la pratique de la p\u00e9nitence, afin de les pr\u00e9server de cette seconde & derni\u00e9re mort, bien plus terrible que la premi\u00e9re, puisque c'est une mort \u00e9ternelle. Ainsi raisonne un p\u00e9cheur p\u00e9nitent: Memento, homo, quia pulvis es; & in pulverem revert\u00e9ris."}, {"anchor": "b-p029", "label": "English", "text": "Indeed, Christians, this has always produced in thoroughly converted souls not only heroic contempt but holy hatred of their bodies; it has so often worked miracles of conversion in Christianity. Nothing more was needed to determine a Francis Borgia to leave the world. The sight of the corpse of a queen and empress, whom he was ordered to have solemnly buried and could scarcely recognize when required to attest her identity, so hideous and disfigured did she appear\u2014this spectacle completed his persuasion. He could not see that beauty, destroyed by death in so sudden and astonishing a change, without resolving himself to die to all the world's vanities. Death's image, striking his eyes, awakened every sentiment of penance in his heart. For why, he said to himself, as the saints have said, treat indulgently a body condemned to death? Once sentence has been pronounced upon a criminal, no one troubles to feed him well. If he must be sustained for a few hours more, necessities suffice; life is preserved only to make him feel death's pains more keenly. Such, then, is the treatment of our bodies. They are criminals condemned by divine justice; sentence is passed, and execution delayed only a few days. It will be soon. We should no longer provide them indulgences and gratifications, but maintain them under the rigorous justice to which God has delivered them, already making them taste death through penance, to preserve them from that second and final death, far more terrible than the first, since it is eternal death. Thus reasons a repentant sinner: \u2018Remember, man, that you are dust, and to dust you will return.\u2019", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p030", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais cette haine de son corps est encore bien plus vive, quand il vient \u00e0 p\u00e9n\u00e9trer dans le myst\u00e8re des cendres que l'Eglise lui pr\u00e9sente: quand remontant plus haut, & jusques aux sources m\u00eames de sa religion, il cherche l'origine d'une si sainte pratique; & qu'il pense que ces cendres qui dans l'une & dans l'autre loi ont toujours \u00e9t\u00e9 le symbole de la p\u00e9nitence, n'\u00e9toient pas un symbole vuide, ni une pure c\u00e9r\u00e9monie; quand il se repr\u00e9sente les aust\u00e9rit\u00e9s & les mac\u00e9rations dont elles devoient \u00eatre accompagn\u00e9es suivant les r\u00e9gles de l'ancienne discipline: quand instruit par les Proph\u00e9tes, il apprend que le cilice & le je\u00fbne dans l'observance commune des fid\u00e9les, \u00e9toient ins\u00e9parables de la cendre, Accingere cilicio, & conspergere cinere, filia populi mei: quand il remarque dans les Conciles, avec quelle s\u00e9v\u00e9rit\u00e9 l'on condamnoit \u00e0 des oeuvres p\u00e9nibles & laborieuses ces sortes de p\u00e9nitens que Tertullien appelloit, Conciliati, & concinerati, couverts de cendres, quoique d\u00e9j\u00e0 reconcili\u00e9s. Car enfin, doit dire aujourd'hui dans l'amertume de son ame un homme touch\u00e9 de la vue de ses d\u00e9sordres & de l'esprit de componction, ces p\u00e9nitens de la primitive Eglise n'\u00e9toient pas plus charg\u00e9s de crimes, ni plus coupables que je le suis; & ces cendres qu'on leur imposoit, ne devoient pas \u00eatre pour eux un engagement plus \u00e9troit \u00e0 la p\u00e9nitence, qu'elles le doivent \u00eatre pour moi. Il seroit donc bien \u00e9trange que j'en fisse un usage tout diff\u00e9rent; & que cette c\u00e9r\u00e9monie ayant \u00e9t\u00e9 \u00e0 leur \u00e9gard un exercice de mortification, & de la plus r\u00e9elle, de la plus dure mortification, elle n'en f\u00fbt pour moi que l'apparence & que l'ombre. Il seroit bien indigne, apr\u00e8s avoir re\u00e7u ces cendres, de penser encore aux divertissemens & aux joies prophanes du monde, & comme parloit un solitaire, de chercher jusques dans la cendre de la p\u00e9nitence les d\u00e9lices de la vie."}, {"anchor": "b-p030", "label": "English", "text": "But this hatred of his body becomes still more lively when he penetrates the mystery of the ashes the Church presents to him; when, going back higher, to the very sources of his religion, he seeks the origin of so holy a practice and considers that these ashes, always the symbol of penance under both laws, were not an empty symbol or a mere ceremony; when he pictures the austerities and bodily disciplines that, under the rules of ancient discipline, must accompany them; when, instructed by the prophets, he learns that sackcloth and fasting in the common observance of the faithful were inseparable from ashes: \u2018Gird yourself with sackcloth and sprinkle yourself with ashes, daughter of my people\u2019; when he observes in the councils how severely those penitents Tertullian called \u2018reconciled and covered in ashes\u2019 were sentenced to painful, laborious works, covered in ashes though already reconciled. For finally, a man moved by the sight of his disorders and by the spirit of compunction ought today to say in his soul's bitterness: \u2018Those penitents of the early Church were no more burdened with crimes or guiltier than I am; the ashes imposed upon them should not have bound them more closely to penance than mine should bind me. How strange, then, if I used them quite differently; if this ceremony, for them an exercise in mortification\u2014the most real and harshest mortification\u2014were for me only its appearance and shadow! How unworthy, after receiving these ashes, still to think of worldly entertainments and profane joys, and, as a hermit said, seek life's delights even in the ashes of penance!\u2019", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p031", "label": "French original", "text": "Car quoique nous ne soyons plus \u00e0 ces premiers si\u00e9cles, o\u00f9 les p\u00e9cheurs achetoient si cher la grace de leur absolution & de leur r\u00e9conciliation, nous n'en devons pas moins satisfaire \u00e0 Dieu. L'Eglise a p\u00fb adoucir les peines qu'elle avoit ordonn\u00e9es pour chaque esp\u00e9ce de p\u00e9ch\u00e9: mais elle n'a rien rel\u00e2ch\u00e9 des peines prescrites par le droit divin; & Dieu lui-m\u00eame nous assure qu'il ne s'en rel\u00e2chera jamais qu'en faveur de la p\u00e9nitence. Il faut donc que ce soit la p\u00e9nitence qui m'acquitte aupr\u00e8s de lui. Et comme il s'agit de son int\u00e9r\u00eat, qui maintenant ou apr\u00e8s la mort, doit \u00eatre pleinement r\u00e9par\u00e9, il faut que je prenne le bon parti, & que par la p\u00e9nitence de cette vie je m'\u00e9pargne la p\u00e9nitence de l'autre. Il faut qu'en m'imposant des peines volontaires, qu'en me privant de certains plaisirs, m\u00eame permis, qu'en me faisant quelques violences, qu'en me r\u00e9duisant \u00e0 une vie plus exacte & plus r\u00e9gl\u00e9e, & qu'unissant enfin ma p\u00e9nitence \u00e0 la p\u00e9nitence de Jesus-Christ, je pr\u00e9vienne les affreux ch\u00e2timens que Dieu r\u00e9serve \u00e0 ceux qui refusent de se punir eux-m\u00eames. Ah! mon Dieu, que votre mis\u00e9ricorde est adorable, de nous en quitter \u00e0 ce prix, de vouloir bien accepter l'un en \u00e9change de l'autre, & de nous remettre ainsi pour une p\u00e9nitence temporelle une p\u00e9nitence \u00e9ternelle!"}, {"anchor": "b-p031", "label": "English", "text": "For although we no longer live in those early centuries when sinners paid so dearly for the grace of absolution and reconciliation, we owe no less satisfaction to God. The Church could soften the penalties she had prescribed for each kind of sin, but she has relaxed none of those prescribed by divine law. God himself assures us he will never relax them except in favour of penance. Penance, then, must discharge my debt to him. Since his rights are at stake, and must receive full reparation either now or after death, I must choose wisely and spare myself penance in the next life by penance in this one. By imposing voluntary penalties on myself, depriving myself of certain pleasures, even lawful ones, doing some violence to myself, bringing myself to a more exact and orderly life, and finally uniting my penance to that of Jesus Christ, I must forestall the dreadful chastisements God reserves for those who refuse to punish themselves. Ah, my God, how adorable is your mercy, to release us at this price, to accept the one in exchange for the other, and thus remit eternal punishment in exchange for temporal penance!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-p032", "label": "French original", "text": "Prenons, mes chers Auditeurs, des sentimens si raisonnables: ce sont ceux que nous doit inspirer la c\u00e9r\u00e9monie des cendres. Si nous entrons dans ce Car\u00eame bien p\u00e9n\u00e9tr\u00e9s de ces v\u00e9rit\u00e9s, le je\u00fbne ne sera plus un joug trop p\u00e9sant pour nous, comme il l'est pour les Chr\u00e9tiens l\u00e2ches; beaucoup moins un sujet de scandale & de p\u00e9ch\u00e9, comme il l'est pour les libertins. Nous l'entreprendrons avec joie, nous le continuerons avec ferveur, & nous l'acheverons avec constance. Heureux de nous trouver engag\u00e9s par un pr\u00e9cepte \u00e0 ce qui nous est d'ailleurs si utile & si n\u00e9cessaire, nous ne ferons point tant les d\u00e9licats; mais pour peu que nous soyons dispos\u00e9s \u00e0 nous faire justice, nous avouerons, que si le je\u00fbne nous paro\u00eet impossible, cette impossibilit\u00e9 pr\u00e9tendue n'est qu'un pur d\u00e9faut de notre volont\u00e9. Nous ne raisonnerons point tant sur notre sant\u00e9, ni sur notre temp\u00e9rament; mais nous nous souviendrons que nous sommes enfans de l'Eglise & p\u00e9cheurs devant Dieu: enfans de l'Eglise, & par cons\u00e9quent que nous devons lui ob\u00e9ir; p\u00e9cheurs devant Dieu, & par cons\u00e9quent que nous devons l'appaiser. Car c'est l\u00e0 de quoi nous rendrons compte \u00e0 Dieu, dit S. Bernard, ou de quoi nous devons nous rendre compte \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames, ayant plus d'\u00e9gard \u00e0 notre \u00e9tat & \u00e0 notre profession, qu'\u00e0 nos forces & \u00e0 notre complexion: Non de complexione judicandum, sed de professione. Nous ne nous pr\u00e9vaudrons point pour rompre le je\u00fbne, d'une indisposition l\u00e9g\u00e9re, puisque suivant cette r\u00e9gle, la loi du je\u00fbne deviendroit une loi chim\u00e9rique, & qu'il n'y auroit plus personne dans le Christianisme qui n'en f\u00fbt exempt. Nous ne craindrons pas m\u00eame en l'observant de nous incommoder, puisqu'il est vrai que si le je\u00fbne ne nous incommodoit en rien, il ne seroit plus ce qu'il doit \u00eatre. Nous ne demanderons plus de fausses dispenses; persuad\u00e9s qu'on ne trompe point Dieu, & que toutes les dispenses des hommes ne sont rien, si elles ne sont re\u00e7ues & autoris\u00e9es de Dieu. Bien loin de nous plaindre que l'Eglise en \u00e9tablissant le je\u00fbne du Car\u00eame, ou comme il est plus vraisemblable, en nous le proposant & nous l'expliquant, ait trop exig\u00e9 de nous; nous serons surpris qu'elle nous ait tant m\u00e9nag\u00e9s, & nous aurons honte que ce soit notre l\u00e2chet\u00e9 qui l'ait en quelque sorte r\u00e9duite \u00e0 nous traiter avec tant d'indulgence. Ce n'est pas assez; & apr\u00e8s avoir rempli ce que l'Eglise nous ordonne dans le commandement du je\u00fbne, nous ne croirons pas avoir pour cela satisfait au pr\u00e9cepte naturel de la p\u00e9nitence. Nous ferons \u00e9tat que ce qu'elle a r\u00e9gl\u00e9, ne nous exempte pas de ce qu'elle a du reste abandonn\u00e9 \u00e0 notre prudence & \u00e0 notre z\u00e9le. Et c'est ainsi que la pens\u00e9e de la mort & la vue des cendres servira \u00e0 humilier notre orgueil, \u00e0 mortifier notre d\u00e9licatesse; & que l'humilit\u00e9 nous conduira \u00e0 la vraie gloire, & la p\u00e9nitence au souverain bonheur que je vous souhaite, &c."}, {"anchor": "b-p032", "label": "English", "text": "My dear hearers, let us adopt sentiments so reasonable: these are what the ceremony of ashes ought to inspire. If we enter this Lent deeply penetrated by these truths, fasting will no longer be too heavy a yoke, as it is for fainthearted Christians, still less an occasion of scandal and sin, as it is for the libertines. We shall undertake it joyfully, continue it fervently, and complete it steadfastly. Happy to be bound by a precept to what is in any case so useful and necessary, we shall not be so fastidious. If we are at all disposed to deal justly with ourselves, we shall admit that when fasting seems impossible, this supposed impossibility is simply a failure of our will. We shall not reason so much about our health or temperament, but remember that we are children of the Church and sinners before God: children of the Church, and therefore obliged to obey her; sinners before God, and therefore obliged to appease him. For this, says Saint Bernard, is what we shall account for to God, or what we must account for to ourselves, attending more to our state and profession than to our strength and constitution: \u2018Judgment must be made by profession, not constitution.\u2019 We shall not use a slight indisposition as a reason to break the fast, since on that principle fasting's law would become imaginary and no one in Christianity would remain bound by it. We shall not even fear discomfort in observing it, for if fasting caused no discomfort, it would no longer be what it ought. We shall ask for no more false dispensations, convinced that God is not deceived and that human dispensations amount to nothing unless received and authorized by God. Far from complaining that the Church, in establishing the Lenten fast\u2014or, more probably, in proposing and explaining it\u2014has demanded too much, we shall be surprised that she has spared us so much, and ashamed that our cowardice has in some measure forced her to treat us so indulgently. This is not enough: after fulfilling the Church's commandment of fasting, we shall not think we have thereby satisfied the natural precept of penance. We shall recognize that what she has regulated does not exempt us from what she has otherwise left to our prudence and zeal. Thus the thought of death and the sight of ashes will humble our pride and mortify our delicacy; humility will lead us to true glory, and penance to the supreme happiness I wish you, etc.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n001", "label": "French original", "text": "Exod. 9."}, {"anchor": "b-n001", "label": "English", "text": "Exodus 9.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n002", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibidem."}, {"anchor": "b-n002", "label": "English", "text": "In the same place.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n003", "label": "French original", "text": "Le Pere Bourdaloue fut envoy\u00e9 par le Roi \u00e0 Montpellier en faveur des nouveaux convertis, pour y pr\u00eacher le Car\u00eame."}, {"anchor": "b-n003", "label": "English", "text": "Father Bourdaloue was sent by the King to Montpellier on behalf of the new converts, to preach Lent there.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n004", "label": "French original", "text": "Phil. 4."}, {"anchor": "b-n004", "label": "English", "text": "Philippians 4.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n005", "label": "French original", "text": "Eccles. 10."}, {"anchor": "b-n005", "label": "English", "text": "Ecclesiasticus 10.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n006", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal. 48."}, {"anchor": "b-n006", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 48 (the edition's numbering).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n007", "label": "French original", "text": "Job. 13."}, {"anchor": "b-n007", "label": "English", "text": "Job 13.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n008", "label": "French original", "text": "Ezech. 28."}, {"anchor": "b-n008", "label": "English", "text": "Ezekiel 28.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n009", "label": "French original", "text": "Mich. 6."}, {"anchor": "b-n009", "label": "English", "text": "Micah 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n010", "label": "French original", "text": "Tertull."}, {"anchor": "b-n010", "label": "English", "text": "Tertullian.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n011", "label": "French original", "text": "Eccles. 10."}, {"anchor": "b-n011", "label": "English", "text": "Ecclesiasticus 10.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n012", "label": "French original", "text": "Chrysost."}, {"anchor": "b-n012", "label": "English", "text": "Chrysostom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n013", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal. 43."}, {"anchor": "b-n013", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 43 (the edition's numbering).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n014", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Petr. 5."}, {"anchor": "b-n014", "label": "English", "text": "1 Peter 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n015", "label": "French original", "text": "Joel. 2."}, {"anchor": "b-n015", "label": "English", "text": "Joel 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n016", "label": "French original", "text": "Eccli. 19."}, {"anchor": "b-n016", "label": "English", "text": "Ecclesiasticus 19.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n017", "label": "French original", "text": "Rom. 12."}, {"anchor": "b-n017", "label": "English", "text": "Romans 12.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n018", "label": "French original", "text": "Galat. 5."}, {"anchor": "b-n018", "label": "English", "text": "Galatians 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n019", "label": "French original", "text": "2. Cor. 4."}, {"anchor": "b-n019", "label": "English", "text": "2 Corinthians 4.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n020", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. 9."}, {"anchor": "b-n020", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians 9.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n021", "label": "French original", "text": "Genes. 6."}, {"anchor": "b-n021", "label": "English", "text": "Genesis 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n022", "label": "French original", "text": "Job. 28."}, {"anchor": "b-n022", "label": "English", "text": "Job 28.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n023", "label": "French original", "text": "Galat. 5."}, {"anchor": "b-n023", "label": "English", "text": "Galatians 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n024", "label": "French original", "text": "Jerem. 6."}, {"anchor": "b-n024", "label": "English", "text": "Jeremiah 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n025", "label": "French original", "text": "Tertull."}, {"anchor": "b-n025", "label": "English", "text": "Tertullian.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "b-n026", "label": "French original", "text": "Bernard."}, {"anchor": "b-n026", "label": "English", "text": "Bernard.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "On the Ceremony of Ashes", "topics": ["Ash Wednesday", "repentance", "humility", "mortification", "death", "conversion"], "url": "sermons/bourdaloue-cendres-ceremonie.html"}, {"author": "Louis Bourdaloue", "calendar": "dayKey:viri-galilaei", "calendar_label": "The Ascension of the Lord", "centuries": [17, 18], "composition": "Approximately late seventeenth century\u20131704; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension", "language": "fr", "language_label": "French", "metadata": ["Louis Bourdaloue, Sermons du P\u00e8re Bourdalou\u00eb, de la Compagnie de Jesus, sur les myst\u00e8res, tome premier, quatri\u00e8me \u00e9dition, edited by Fran\u00e7ois de Paule Bretonneau (Paris: chez Rigaud, 1726), Sermon sur l\u2019Ascension de Jesus-Christ, pp. 385\u2013427. Kelly Library copy.", "Internet Archive; John M. Kelly Library Rare Books, University of Toronto; sponsored by the University of Ottawa.", "Ascension", "Ascension of Jesus Christ", "Acts 1", "Philippians 3", "1 John 3", "Ecclesiasticus 3", "1 Corinthians 12 (printed locator; apparent reference error)", "2 Corinthians 12:4 \u2014 added by editors", "John 14", "Luke 22", "Matthew 25", "Psalm 149", "Ecclesiasticus 16", "Colossians 1", "1 Corinthians 1", "Timothy 4 (epistle numeral unclear in source)", "2 Timothy 4:8 \u2014 added by editors", "2 Corinthians 4 (printed locator; apparent reference error)", "1 Corinthians 4:4 \u2014 added by editors", "Matthew 5", "Psalm 4", "Matthew 6", "Luke 24", "Psalm 142 (historical numbering)", "Romans 5", "Luke 6", "Acts 14", "Romans 8"], "occasion": "Ascension de Jesus-Christ", "occasion_detail": "Ascension of Jesus Christ", "occasion_label": "Ascension", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Bourdaloue\u2019s Ascension sermon presents heavenly glory as a reward to be merited through Christ and especially through Christian suffering. Its first part contrasts God\u2019s just reward with worldly honours, while insisting that human merits depend on Christ\u2019s merits and that grace precedes merit. Its second part distinguishes suffering for justice, charity, truth, peace and obedience from suffering driven by disordered passions. The conclusion directs hearers to the ascended Christ as mediator and high priest."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-h001", "label": "French original", "text": "SERMON SUR L'ASCENSION DE JESUS-CHRIST."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON ON THE ASCENSION OF JESUS CHRIST.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-e001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Et c\u00f9m hoc dixisset, videntibus illis elevatus est."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-e001", "label": "English", "text": "And when he had said this, he was lifted up before their eyes.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-e002", "label": "French original", "text": "Apr\u00e8s qu'il eut parl\u00e9 de la sorte, il fut \u00e9lev\u00e9 \u00e0 leur v\u00fbe vers le ciel. Aux Actes des Ap\u00f4tres, chap. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-e002", "label": "English", "text": "After he had spoken in this way, he was lifted up towards heaven before their eyes. In the Acts of the Apostles, chapter 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p001", "label": "French original", "text": "Pourquoi le Sauveur du monde d\u00e9couvre-t-il aujourd'hui sa gloire \u00e0 ses Ap\u00f4tres, & pourquoi veut-il qu'ils soient t\u00e9moins de son triomphe, apr\u00e8s avoir \u00e9t\u00e9 t\u00e9moins de ses humiliations & de ses souffrances ? Cette question, Chr\u00e9tiens, n'est pas difficile \u00e0 r\u00e9soudre ; & vous jugez ais\u00e9ment que le Fils de Dieu voulut par-l\u00e0 les affermir dans la foi, qu'il voulut les pr\u00e9munir contre les dangereuses tentations auxquelles ils devoient \u00eatre expos\u00e9s, qu'il voulut les pr\u00e9parer aux pers\u00e9cutions & aux croix, & les rendre capables de souffrir eux-m\u00eames comme lui, non-seulement avec patience, mais avec joie. C'est pour cela qu'il se fait voir \u00e0 eux dans tout l'\u00e9clat de sa Majest\u00e9 : c'est pour cela qu'en leur donnant une sensible & si haute id\u00e9e de ce s\u00e9jour bienheureux o\u00f9 il va marquer leurs places, il les remplit d'une douceur int\u00e9rieure & toute c\u00e9leste, qui les retient sur la montagne, lors m\u00eame qu'une nu\u00e9e leur a fait perdre de v\u00fbe leur divin Ma\u00eetre. En sorte qu'il faut que deux Anges descendent expr\u00e8s pour les retirer de cette profonde extase o\u00f9 ils demeuroient plong\u00e9s, & pour les renvoyer \u00e0 leurs travaux Apostoliques. Ecce duo viri astiterunt juxta illos in vestibus albis, qui & dixerunt : Viri Galilaei, quid statis aspicientes in c\u0153lum ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p001", "label": "English", "text": "Why does the Saviour of the world reveal his glory to his Apostles today, and why does he wish them to witness his triumph after witnessing his humiliations and sufferings? This question, Christians, is not difficult to resolve. You readily understand that the Son of God wished thereby to strengthen them in faith, to guard them against the dangerous temptations to which they would be exposed, to prepare them for persecutions and crosses, and to make them capable of suffering as he did, not only patiently but joyfully. That is why he shows himself to them in all the splendour of his Majesty. That is why, by giving them so vivid and exalted an idea of that blessed dwelling where he is going to mark out their places, he fills them with an inward and entirely heavenly sweetness that holds them on the mountain even after a cloud has hidden their divine Master from their sight. Two Angels must therefore descend expressly to draw them out of the profound ecstasy in which they remain immersed, and to send them back to their apostolic labours. Behold, two men stood beside them in white garments, and said: Men of Galilee, why do you stand looking into heaven?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p002", "label": "French original", "text": "Appliquons-nous ceci, mes chers Auditeurs : car en qualit\u00e9 de chr\u00e9tiens ce myst\u00e8re nous regarde, & il doit op\u00e9rer en nous les m\u00eames dispositions que dans les Ap\u00f4tres. En effet, il y a parmi nous des ti\u00e9des & des l\u00e2ches dans la voie de Dieu, & il est important de les animer. Il y en a qui g\u00e9missent sous le poids des adversit\u00e9s & des mis\u00e8res humaines, & il s'agit de les consoler. Peut-\u00eatre y en a-t-il, qui jouissant d'une tranquille prosp\u00e9rit\u00e9, sont sur le point de tomber dans des \u00e9tats d'autant plus affligeans & plus douloureux, qu'ils les pr\u00e9voyent moins, & je dois les y disposer. Or en voici l'excellent moyen. Nous attendons un Sauveur, qui, comme disoit le grand Ap\u00f4tre, transformera notre corps, & le rendra, tout vil & tout abjet qu'il est, conforme \u00e0 son corps glorieux : Salvatorem expectamus, qui reformabit corpus humilitatis nostrae configuratum corpori claritatis suae. Non-seulement nous l'attendons, mais \u00e9clair\u00e9s des vives lumi\u00e8res qui r\u00e9jaillissent de son humanit\u00e9 sainte, nous le voyons & nous l'admirons. Voil\u00e0 l'objet de nos esp\u00e9rances, voil\u00e0 le sujet de notre consolation, voil\u00e0 ce qui doit allumer notre ferveur & soutenir notre courage : la v\u00fbe de ce Sauveur couronn\u00e9 de gloire ; l'attente de cette gloire dont il nous assure la possession. Car nous sommes d\u00e9j\u00e0, selon l'expression de saint Jean, les enfans de Dieu : Nunc sumus Filii Dei ; & nous s\u00e7avons que quand Jesus-Christ viendra \u00e0 la fin des si\u00e9cles, & qu'il se montrera dans la m\u00eame gloire o\u00f9 il paro\u00eet en ce jour, nous serons semblables \u00e0 lui : Scimus quoniam c\u00f9m apparuerit, similes ei erimus. C'est-l\u00e0, dis-je, ce qui nous doit rendre fervens & patiens ; fervens dans l'accomplissement de nos devoirs, patiens dans les afflictions & dans les maux qui nous arrivent par l'ordre de la providence. Mon dessein est donc de vous parler de la gloire du ciel, & de vous la proposer comme le motif le plus touchant, le motif le plus propre \u00e0 faire impression sur vos c\u0153urs, & \u00e0 vous faire tout entreprendre & tout supporter dans la vie. J'ai besoin de la grace du Saint-Esprit, & je la demande par l'intercession de Marie, Ave, Maria."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p002", "label": "English", "text": "Let us apply this to ourselves, my dear hearers: as Christians, this mystery concerns us, and it should produce in us the same dispositions as in the Apostles. Indeed, among us there are people who are lukewarm and cowardly in God's way, and they need to be stirred to action. Others groan beneath the weight of adversity and human misery, and they need consolation. Perhaps some, enjoying undisturbed prosperity, are about to fall into conditions all the more distressing and painful because they foresee them so little; I must prepare them for these. Here is an excellent means of doing so. We await a Saviour who, as the great Apostle said, will transform our body, however lowly and abject it may be, and make it like his glorious body: We await a Saviour, who will refashion the body of our lowliness to conform to the body of his glory. We do more than await him: enlightened by the vivid rays that shine forth from his holy humanity, we see and admire him. Here is the object of our hopes, the source of our consolation, what should kindle our fervour and sustain our courage: the sight of this Saviour crowned with glory, and the expectation of that glory whose possession he assures us. For we are already, in Saint John's words, the children of God: Now we are children of God. And we know that when Jesus Christ comes at the end of the ages and manifests himself in the same glory in which he appears today, we shall be like him: We know that when he appears, we shall be like him. This, I say, should make us fervent and patient: fervent in fulfilling our duties, patient in the afflictions and evils that befall us by the ordering of providence. My purpose, then, is to speak to you of the glory of heaven and to set it before you as the most moving motive, the motive best suited to impress your hearts and make you undertake and endure everything in life. I need the grace of the Holy Spirit, and I ask for it through Mary's intercession. Hail Mary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p003", "label": "French original", "text": "Isa\u00efe l'avoit dit, & saint Paul dans les m\u00eames termes nous l'a d\u00e9clar\u00e9 ; que l'\u0153il n'a point vu, que l'oreille n'a point entendu, & que le c\u0153ur de l'homme n'a jamais con\u00e7u ce que Dieu dans les tr\u00e9sors de sa mis\u00e9ricorde a pr\u00e9par\u00e9 pour ceux qui l'aiment & qui le servent. Apr\u00e8s deux t\u00e9moignages si authentiques, il n'y a point de pr\u00e9dicateur de l'Evangile qui puisse sans t\u00e9m\u00e9rit\u00e9 entreprendre de donner \u00e0 ses auditeurs une id\u00e9e juste de la gloire du ciel. Mais aussi, dit saint Chrysostome, le pr\u00e9dicateur a-t-il en cela m\u00eame un grand avantage, puisque l'impuissance o\u00f9 il est r\u00e9duit, est justement l'id\u00e9e la plus haute, la plus vraie, la plus exacte que nous puissions avoir sur la terre, & qu'il puisse donner de cette gloire. Ne faisons donc point aujourd'hui d'efforts inutiles pour comprendre une gloire, dont la plus essentielle propri\u00e9t\u00e9, est d'\u00eatre incompr\u00e9hensible. Il nous doit suffire de la conno\u00eetre comme nous connoissons Dieu, c'est-\u00e0-dire, de s\u00e7avoir ce qu'elle est, par ce qu'elle n'est pas. Or nous le s\u00e7avons, & j'oserois m\u00eame ajouter que nous le sentons, lorsqu'il nous arrive, en contemplant l'univers, & le bel ordre des cr\u00e9atures qui le composent, de faire cette r\u00e9flexion aussi touchante que solide : Tout ce que je vois n'approche pas de ce que j'esp\u00e8re ; & tout ce que j'admire en cette vie, n'est qu'une ombre obscure & confuse de ce que Dieu me destine en l'autre. Car voil\u00e0, Chr\u00e9tiens, la plus excellente notion que nous ayons \u00e0 nous en former. En effet, c'est ainsi que saint Augustin voyant la Cour des Empereurs de Rome si pompeuse & si magnifique, se figuroit par proportion la magnificence & la beaut\u00e9 de la Cour c\u00e9leste. C'est ainsi qu'au milieu des c\u00e9r\u00e9monies les plus augustes, il s'\u00e9crioit : Si haec tam pulchra sunt, qualis ipse ? & si haec tanta, quantus ipse ? Si tout ceci est si brillant, si grand, si surprenant, que sera-ce de vous, \u00f4 mon Dieu ! Et c'est ainsi que nous en jugerions nous-m\u00eames, si nous ne nous laissions pas \u00e9blouir au vain \u00e9clat du monde, & que nous s\u00e7ussions, comme ce grand Saint, nous \u00e9lever des grandeurs visibles & mortelles, aux grandeurs invisibles & \u00e9ternelles. Mais encore une fois tenons-nous-en \u00e0 la r\u00e8gle du saint-Esprit, qui nous d\u00e9fend de chercher ce qui est au-dessus de nous, & qui nous ordonne d'\u00eatre attentifs \u00e0 ce que Dieu demande de nous : Altiora te ne quaesieris ; sed quae praecepit tibi Deus, illa cogita semper ; c'est-\u00e0-dire, sans avoir une vaine curiosit\u00e9 d'apprendre en quoi consiste la gloire des bienheureux, instruisons-nous avec humilit\u00e9 de ce que nous devons faire pour y parvenir. Le voici, mes chers Auditeurs, & il n'y a personne qui ne doive se l'appliquer. Le Sauveur du monde nous fait conno\u00eetre par son exemple, que cette gloire est une r\u00e9compense ; & il nous fait au m\u00eame tems entendre que cette r\u00e9compense est sur-tout le fruit & le prix des souffrances. Arr\u00eatons-nous \u00e0 ces deux pens\u00e9es, & faisons-en le partage de ce discours. Cette gloire o\u00f9 nous appelle apr\u00e8s lui Jesus-Christ, est une r\u00e9compense ; il faut donc la m\u00e9riter ; ce sera la premi\u00e8re partie. Cette r\u00e9compense est sur-tout le fruit & le prix des souffrances : c'est donc en particulier par le bon usage des souffrances, qu'il la faut m\u00e9riter ; ce sera la seconde partie. Ainsi le Fils de Dieu l'a-t-il m\u00e9rit\u00e9 lui-m\u00eame. Et voil\u00e0 en deux mots ce qu'il nous a r\u00e9v\u00e9l\u00e9 de notre gloire future, & ce qu'il nous est n\u00e9cessaire de ne pas ignorer. Tout le reste sont choses ineffables, myst\u00e8res cach\u00e9s, secrets qu'il n'est pas permis m\u00eame \u00e0 saint Paul de nous d\u00e9couvrir, & qu'il est beaucoup moins en mon pouvoir de vous expliquer : Arcana verba, quae non licet homini loqui. Mais pour votre \u00e9dification & pour satisfaire \u00e0 ce que vous attendez de moi, je dois vous dire, & je vous le dis avec tout le z\u00e9le que Dieu m'inspire, que si vous voulez arriver \u00e0 la m\u00eame gloire que Jesus-Christ, vous devez la m\u00e9riter comme Jesus-Christ, premiere proposition : & que si vous voulez la m\u00e9riter comme Jesus-Christ, vous devez souffrir comme Jesus-Christ, seconde proposition. Je vous demande pour l'une & pour l'autre une attention favorable. Elles sont simples ; mais dans leur simplicit\u00e9, elles renferment les plus importantes instructions."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p003", "label": "English", "text": "Isaiah had said it, and Saint Paul declared it to us in the same terms: eye has not seen, ear has not heard, and the human heart has never conceived what God, in the treasures of his mercy, has prepared for those who love and serve him. After two testimonies so authoritative, no preacher of the Gospel can undertake without presumption to give his hearers an adequate idea of heaven's glory. Yet, says Saint Chrysostom, the preacher has a great advantage in this very thing: the powerlessness to which he is reduced is precisely the highest, truest and most exact idea of that glory that we can have on earth and that he can convey. Let us therefore make no useless effort today to comprehend a glory whose most essential property is to be incomprehensible. It should suffice to know it as we know God, that is, to know what it is through what it is not. We do know this, and I would even venture to add that we feel it, when, contemplating the universe and the beautiful order of its creatures, we make this reflection, as moving as it is sound: All that I see falls short of what I hope for; and all that I admire in this life is only a dark and indistinct shadow of what God intends for me in the next. For this, Christians, is the finest conception we can form of it. Thus Saint Augustine, seeing the court of the Roman Emperors so splendid and magnificent, pictured by analogy the magnificence and beauty of the heavenly court. Thus, amid the most august ceremonies, he exclaimed: If these things are so beautiful, what is he himself like? And if these things are so great, how great is he himself? If all this is so brilliant, so great, so astonishing, what must you be, O my God! We ourselves would judge in this way if we did not let the world's empty splendour dazzle us, and if, like that great Saint, we knew how to rise from visible and mortal greatness to invisible and eternal greatness. But once again, let us hold to the Holy Spirit's rule, which forbids us to seek what is above us and commands us to attend to what God asks of us: Do not seek things higher than yourself; but always ponder what God has commanded you. That is, without a vain curiosity to learn what the glory of the blessed consists in, let us humbly learn what we must do to attain it. Here it is, my dear hearers, and everyone must apply it to himself. The Saviour of the world teaches us by his example that this glory is a reward; and at the same time he makes us understand that this reward is above all the fruit and recompense of sufferings. Let us dwell on these two thoughts and make them the divisions of this discourse. This glory to which Jesus Christ calls us after him is a reward: we must therefore merit it; that will be the first part. This reward is above all the fruit and recompense of sufferings: it must therefore be merited especially through the good use of sufferings; that will be the second part. Thus the Son of God himself merited it. There, in a few words, is what he has revealed about our future glory and what we need to know. Everything else is ineffable: hidden mysteries, secrets that even Saint Paul is not permitted to disclose, and that I am far less able to explain to you: Secret words which it is not lawful for a human being to utter. But for your edification and to fulfil what you expect of me, I must tell you, and I tell you with all the zeal God inspires in me, that if you wish to attain the same glory as Jesus Christ, you must merit it as Jesus Christ did: the first proposition. And if you wish to merit it as Jesus Christ did, you must suffer as Jesus Christ did: the second proposition. I ask your favourable attention for both. They are simple, but in their simplicity they contain the most important instructions.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-h002", "label": "French original", "text": "I. Partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-h002", "label": "English", "text": "Part I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p004", "label": "French original", "text": "Je m'en vais, disoit le Sauveur du monde \u00e0 ses Disciples, sur le point qu'il \u00e9toit de retourner \u00e0 son Pere : je vais prendre possession de la gloire qui m'est r\u00e9serv\u00e9e dans le ciel, & vous pr\u00e9parer au m\u00eame tems \u00e0 chacun votre place dans ce s\u00e9jour bienheureux : Vado parare vobis locum. Parole pleine de consolation ; mais parole pr\u00e9c\u00e9d\u00e9e d'une autre qui devoit \u00eatre pour eux, & qui est pour nous un grand fonds d'instruction. Car le m\u00eame Sauveur leur avoit dit auparavant : Ce Royaume o\u00f9 je veux vous appeller apr\u00e8s moi, je vous le promets, mais aux m\u00eames conditions que mon Pere me l'a promis, & vous ne l'aurez point autrement que moi. Dispono vobis, sicut disposuit mihi Pater meus regnum. Or le Fils de David n'y est entr\u00e9 que par la voie du m\u00e9rite. D'o\u00f9 il s'ensuit, mes chers Auditeurs, par la plus juste de toutes les cons\u00e9quences, que pour parvenir nous-m\u00eames \u00e0 cette gloire c\u00e9leste, il faut que nous l'ayons m\u00e9rit\u00e9e. Mettons dans tout son jour cette v\u00e9rit\u00e9, que je vous propose aujourd'hui comme le motif le plus capable d'exciter votre z\u00e9le, & d'allumer toute votre ferveur."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p004", "label": "English", "text": "I am going away, the Saviour of the world told his Disciples as he was about to return to his Father: I am going to take possession of the glory reserved for me in heaven, and at the same time to prepare a place for each of you in that blessed dwelling: I go to prepare a place for you. Words full of consolation, but preceded by others that were to be a rich source of instruction for them, as they are for us. For the same Saviour had told them before: I promise you this Kingdom to which I wish to call you after me, but on the same conditions on which my Father promised it to me; you will not have it in any other way than I do. I appoint a kingdom for you, as my Father appointed it for me. Now the Son of David entered it only by the way of merit. Hence it follows, my dear hearers, by the soundest of all conclusions, that to attain this heavenly glory ourselves, we must have merited it. Let us set this truth in its full light: I propose it to you today as the motive most capable of arousing your zeal and kindling all your fervour.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p005", "label": "French original", "text": "Oui, Chr\u00e9tiens, la gloire que nous attendons, est une r\u00e9compense que Dieu nous destine ; & pour peu que vous ayez de p\u00e9n\u00e9tration, vous y devez d\u00e9couvrir d'abord deux diff\u00e9rences bien remarquables, qui la rel\u00e8vent infiniment au-dessus de ces r\u00e9compenses fragiles & passag\u00e8res que le monde promet \u00e0 ceux qui le servent. Car, selon la belle r\u00e9flexion de saint Jean Chrysostome, telle est l'injuste distribution qui se fait des r\u00e9compenses du monde. On les a souvent sans les m\u00e9riter, & on les m\u00e9rite encore plus souvent sans les avoir. On les a sans les m\u00e9riter, & c'est ce qui devroit humilier la pl\u00fbpart des heureux du si\u00e9cle ; & on les m\u00e9rite encore plus souvent sans les avoir, c'est ce qui rebute & ce qui d\u00e9sesp\u00e9re les malheureux. Mais il n'en est pas ainsi de cette r\u00e9compense immortelle o\u00f9 nous aspirons. Comme on ne l'obtient jamais, qu'en la m\u00e9ritant, aussi est-on s\u00fbr de ne la m\u00e9riter jamais sans l'obtenir. Deux points \u00e0 quoi je m'attache, & que je voudrois imprimer profond\u00e9ment dans vos esprits."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p005", "label": "English", "text": "Yes, Christians, the glory we await is a reward God intends for us. With even a little insight, you should immediately discover two remarkable differences that raise it infinitely above the frail and passing rewards the world promises those who serve it. For, as Saint John Chrysostom so beautifully observes, this is how unjustly the world's rewards are distributed: people often possess them without deserving them, and still more often deserve them without possessing them. They possess them without deserving them: this should humble most of the world's fortunate people. Still more often they deserve them without possessing them: this disheartens the unfortunate and drives them to despair. It is otherwise with the immortal reward to which we aspire. Just as we never obtain it without meriting it, so we may be certain that we never merit it without obtaining it. These are the two points on which I dwell, and which I would imprint deeply upon your minds.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p006", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est une r\u00e9compense que ce Royaume \u00e9ternel, o\u00f9 Jesus-Christ, comme notre chef, entre glorieux & triomphant : mais cette r\u00e9compense, prenez garde \u00e0 ces trois pens\u00e9es, on ne l'a point qu'on ne la m\u00e9rite ; on ne l'a que parce qu'on la m\u00e9rite ; on ne l'a qu'autant qu'on la m\u00e9rite. On ne l'a point, dis-je, qu'on ne la m\u00e9rite. Dieu, comme ma\u00eetre de ses biens, pouvoit nous la donner gratuitement, sans qu'il nous en co\u00fbt\u00e2t rien : mais il ne l'a pas voulu ; & suivant l'ordre qu'il a \u00e9tabli, il faut de deux choses l'une, ou que nous m\u00e9ritions cette r\u00e9compense, ou que nous y renoncions. De quelque mani\u00e8re que Dieu nous ait pr\u00e9destin\u00e9s, en v\u00fbe ou ind\u00e9pendamment de nos bonnes \u0153uvres ; (question qui partage l'\u00e9cole, & qui n'appartient point \u00e0 mon sujet ;) il est certain, & c'est un principe de Religion, que nous n'aurons jamais part \u00e0 son h\u00e9ritage, si nous nous trouvons \u00e0 la mort d\u00e9pourvus de ces m\u00e9rites, qui sont, selon l'Evangile, les titres l\u00e9gitimes pour y pr\u00e9tendre. Venez, nous dira Jesus-Christ dans le jugement dernier, si nous sommes assez heureux pour \u00eatre du nombre de ses brebis, & plac\u00e9s \u00e0 sa droite ; venez, les bien-aim\u00e9s de mon Pere, poss\u00e9dez le Royaume qu'il vous pr\u00e9paroit, & qui vous est d\u00e9sormais acquis : Venite, possidete paratum vobis regnum. Mais en vertu de quoi nous le donnera-t-il ce Royaume ? Ecoutez ce qu'il ajoutera : Parce que vos bonnes \u0153uvres me parlent pour vous, parce que vous avez fait des choses dont je n'ai point perdu le souvenir, & qu'il est maintenant de ma justice & de ma fid\u00e9lit\u00e9 de les reconno\u00eetre ; parce que dans la personne des pauvres, qui \u00e9toient mes membres vivans, vous m'avez secouru, nourri, log\u00e9, visit\u00e9 ; enfin r\u00e9gnez avec moi, parce que vous avez \u00e9t\u00e9 charitables pour moi : Possidete paratum vobis regnum ; esurivi enim, & dedistis mihi manducare. Raisonnons tant qu'il nous plaira, voil\u00e0 dans le sens de Jesus-Christ m\u00eame, tout le d\u00e9nouement du myst\u00e8re imp\u00e9n\u00e9trable de la pr\u00e9destination. C'est en cela, remarque le Docteur Ang\u00e9lique saint Thomas, que cette r\u00e9compense du ciel est une v\u00e9ritable gloire, & m\u00eame la gloire par excellence, parce qu'elle est le fruit du m\u00e9rite, & du m\u00e9rite le plus parfait qui puisse convenir \u00e0 l'homme. En effet, ce qui se donne \u00e0 la faveur, peut bien \u00eatre une grace, peut bien \u00eatre une distinction, peut bien \u00eatre un privil\u00e9ge ; mais \u00e0 parler exactement, ce ne peut \u00eatre une gloire. Or ce n'est point l\u00e0 ce que Dieu r\u00e9serve \u00e0 ses Elus ; mais dans le langage du Saint-Esprit ce qu'il leur r\u00e9serve est une r\u00e9compense, & par-l\u00e0 m\u00eame une gloire : Gloria haec est omnibus sanctis ejus. Parce qu'ils ont \u00e9t\u00e9 saints sur la terre, ils sont bienheureux dans le ciel & combl\u00e9s de gloire ; mais leur bonheur, & comme r\u00e9compense & comme gloire, suppose qu'ils s'en sont rendus dignes : voil\u00e0 ce que nous enseigne la foi."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p006", "label": "English", "text": "That eternal Kingdom into which Jesus Christ enters as our head, glorious and triumphant, is a reward. But attend to these three thoughts about this reward: we do not possess it unless we merit it; we possess it only because we merit it; we possess it only in proportion to our merit. We do not possess it, I say, unless we merit it. God, as master of his own goods, could have given it to us freely, without its costing us anything. But he did not choose to do so; and according to the order he has established, one of two things is necessary: either we merit this reward, or we renounce it. However God may have predestined us, in view of our good works or independently of them\u2014a question that divides the schools and does not belong to my subject\u2014it is certain, and a principle of Religion, that we shall never share his inheritance if at death we are found without those merits which, according to the Gospel, are the legitimate titles to claim it. Come, Jesus Christ will say to us at the last judgment, if we are fortunate enough to be numbered among his sheep and placed at his right hand; come, beloved of my Father, possess the Kingdom he was preparing for you, and which is now yours: Come, possess the kingdom prepared for you. But on what grounds will he give us this Kingdom? Hear what he will add: Because your good works speak to me on your behalf; because you have done things I have not forgotten, and which my justice and faithfulness now require me to acknowledge; because in the persons of the poor, who were my living members, you helped, fed, housed and visited me. Reign with me, then, because you have shown charity to me: Possess the kingdom prepared for you; for I was hungry, and you gave me food. Let us reason as much as we please: there, in Jesus Christ's own meaning, is the entire resolution of the impenetrable mystery of predestination. It is in this, observes Saint Thomas, the Angelic Doctor, that heaven's reward is true glory, indeed glory in the highest sense, because it is the fruit of merit, and of the most perfect merit that can belong to a human being. What is bestowed through favour may indeed be a favour, a distinction or a privilege; but strictly speaking it cannot be glory. Now that is not what God reserves for his Elect. In the Holy Spirit's language, what he reserves for them is a reward, and thereby glory: This glory belongs to all his saints. Because they were holy on earth, they are blessed in heaven and filled with glory; but their happiness, both as reward and as glory, presupposes that they have made themselves worthy of it. This is what faith teaches us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p007", "label": "French original", "text": "Calvin a combattu cette v\u00e9rit\u00e9, & c'est un des points o\u00f9 je confesse que son h\u00e9r\u00e9sie m'a toujours paru plus insoutenable. Il a pr\u00e9tendu que nos plus saintes actions par rapport \u00e0 Dieu ne pouvoient jamais \u00eatre m\u00e9ritoires : cependant Dieu m\u00eame nous assure qu'elles le sont, & nous dit en termes expr\u00e8s qu'\u00e0 la fin des si\u00e9cles sa providence \u00e9clatera, lorsqu'il viendra pour rendre \u00e0 chacun selon le m\u00e9rite de ses \u0153uvres ; Unicuique secund\u00f9m meritum operum suorum : pouvoit-il s'expliquer plus clairement ? Mais ne suffit-il pas, disoit Calvin, que Jesus-Christ nous ait acquis la gloire que nous esp\u00e9rons, & qu'il l'ait m\u00e9rit\u00e9e pour nous ? Non, r\u00e9pondent les Th\u00e9ologiens, apr\u00e8s S. Augustin, cela ne suffit pas. Il faut qu'apr\u00e8s lui, que par lui, & qu'avec lui, nous la m\u00e9ritions encore pour nous-m\u00eames, comme il ne suffit pas que Jesus-Christ ait fait sur la croix p\u00e9nitence pour nous, si nous ne la faisons pour nous-m\u00eames. Il faut que notre p\u00e9nitence, jointe \u00e0 la p\u00e9nitence de cet Homme-Dieu, nous r\u00e9concilie avec Dieu ; & de m\u00eame il faut que nos m\u00e9rites joints \u00e0 ses m\u00e9rites, nous ouvrent le Royaume de Dieu, & c'est \u00e0 quoi le grand Ap\u00f4tre travailloit si saintement, & ce qui lui faisoit dire : Adimpleo ea quae desunt passionum Christi ; j'accomplis en moi, ce qui manqueroit sans cela \u00e0 ma r\u00e9demption, & \u00e0 ce que Jesus-Christ a souffert pour moi. Mais n'est-ce pas faire tort aux m\u00e9rites du R\u00e9dempteur, que d'accorder une r\u00e9compense aussi divine que celle-l\u00e0 \u00e0 d'autres m\u00e9rites que les siens ? Nullement, dit saint Augustin, & la raison qu'il en apporte est convaincante ; parce que les m\u00e9rites que nous devons acqu\u00e9rir, & ajouter \u00e0 ceux du R\u00e9dempteur, sont tellement des m\u00e9rites diff\u00e9rens des siens, qu'ils sont n\u00e9anmoins d\u00e9pendans des siens, fond\u00e9s sur les siens, tirant toute leur efficace & toute leur valeur des siens, & par cons\u00e9quent incapables de pr\u00e9judicier aux siens. Que fait Dieu quand il nous r\u00e9compense ? je l'avoue, il couronne nos m\u00e9rites ; mais parce que nos m\u00e9rites sont ses dons, en couronnant nos m\u00e9rites, il couronne dans nos personnes ses propres dons ; Coronat in nobis dona sua ; c'est la belle expression de saint Augustin. Mais avouer que l'homme peut m\u00e9riter le Royaume du ciel, n'est-ce pas lui donner le sujet de se glorifier ? Oui, continue ce saint Docteur ; & malheur \u00e0 nous, si faute d'un tel m\u00e9rite, nous n'\u00e9tions pas en \u00e9tat de nous glorifier dans le sens que Calvin veut nous le d\u00e9fendre. Car le Royaume c\u00e9leste n'est que pour ceux qui ont droit de se glorifier dans le Seigneur ; & un des caract\u00e8res de l'homme juste, les plus distinctement marqu\u00e9s par l'Ap\u00f4tre, est qu'il puisse, sans pr\u00e9somption, mais avec une sainte confiance, prendre part \u00e0 cette gloire dont le Seigneur est le principe & la fin : Qui gloriatur, in Domino glorietur. Or le foible de l'h\u00e9r\u00e9sie & de la pr\u00e9tendue r\u00e9forme de Calvin, est qu'elle d\u00e9pouille le juste de tout m\u00e9rite, j'entends de tout m\u00e9rite propre, & qu'elle lui \u00f4te ainsi tout moyen de se glorifier m\u00eame en Dieu. Condition n\u00e9anmoins essentielle pour \u00eatre r\u00e9compens\u00e9 de Dieu. Avan\u00e7ons."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p007", "label": "English", "text": "Calvin opposed this truth, and I confess that this is one of the points on which his heresy has always seemed to me most untenable. He claimed that our holiest actions could never be meritorious in relation to God. Yet God himself assures us that they are, and expressly tells us that at the end of the ages his providence will shine forth when he comes to render to each according to the merit of his works: To each according to the merit of his works. Could he have spoken more clearly? But is it not enough, Calvin said, that Jesus Christ has won for us the glory we hope for, and has merited it for us? No, the theologians reply, following Saint Augustine: that is not enough. After him, through him and with him, we must also merit it for ourselves, just as it is not enough that Jesus Christ did penance for us on the cross if we do not do penance for ourselves. Our penance, joined to the penance of this God-man, must reconcile us with God; likewise our merits, joined to his merits, must open God's Kingdom to us. This is what the great Apostle laboured at so holily, and what made him say: I fill up what is lacking in the sufferings of Christ; I accomplish in myself what would otherwise be lacking in my redemption and in what Jesus Christ suffered for me. But does it not wrong the Redeemer's merits to grant so divine a reward to merits other than his? Not at all, says Saint Augustine, and his reason is convincing: the merits we must acquire and add to the Redeemer's are indeed distinct from his, yet they depend upon his, are founded upon his, draw all their efficacy and value from his, and are therefore incapable of detracting from his. What does God do when he rewards us? I acknowledge that he crowns our merits; but because our merits are his gifts, in crowning our merits he crowns his own gifts in our persons: He crowns his gifts in us. Such is Saint Augustine's beautiful expression. But to acknowledge that a human being can merit the Kingdom of heaven\u2014is that not to give him grounds for glorying? Yes, continues this holy Doctor; and woe to us if, for lack of such merit, we were unable to glory in the sense in which Calvin wishes to forbid it. For the heavenly Kingdom is only for those who have the right to glory in the Lord. One of the marks of the just person most distinctly indicated by the Apostle is that, without presumption but with holy confidence, he can share in that glory whose beginning and end is the Lord: Let him who glories glory in the Lord. Now the weakness of Calvin's heresy and supposed reformation is that it strips the just person of all merit\u2014I mean all merit of his own\u2014and thus deprives him of every means of glorying even in God: nevertheless an essential condition for being rewarded by God. Let us proceed.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p008", "label": "French original", "text": "Non seulement on n'a point la r\u00e9compense du ciel qu'on ne la m\u00e9rite, mais ce que je vous prie de bien comprendre, on ne l'a que parce qu'on la m\u00e9rite : en sorte qu'elle est le partage du m\u00e9rite seul, \u00e0 l'exclusion de tout autre titre. De-l\u00e0 vient que saint Paul, pour la d\u00e9finir, l'appelle couronne de justice : In reliquo reposita est mihi corona justitiae. Parce qu'en effet, disent les Peres, cette r\u00e9compense ne doit \u00eatre donn\u00e9e aux hommes, que selon les r\u00e8gles d'une justice rigoureuse, d'une justice incorruptible, d'une justice que rien ne touchera, que rien ne fl\u00e9chira, que rien ne pr\u00e9occupera ; d'une justice qui n'aura d'\u00e9gards pour personne ; qui ne distinguera ni qualit\u00e9s, ni rangs, ni naissance, mais qui discernera parfaitement le m\u00e9rite. Les r\u00e9compenses du si\u00e9cle sont tous les jours en butte \u00e0 la cupidit\u00e9 & \u00e0 l'ambition : quoiqu'elles ne soient d\u00fbes qu'au m\u00e9rite, toute autre chose que le m\u00e9rite contribue \u00e0 les avoir. On les emporte par le cr\u00e9dit, on se les attire par la brigue, on les arrache par l'importunit\u00e9 : les plus hardis & les plus avides sont ceux qui y parviennent ; le hasard en d\u00e9cide, la bonne fortune de l'un, & souvent l'iniquit\u00e9 de l'autre. On se pr\u00e9vaut, pour les demander, du m\u00e9rite d'autrui ; le fils veut \u00eatre r\u00e9compens\u00e9 des services du pere ; l'ami croit \u00eatre en droit de profiter du travail & du pouvoir de son ami. Ceux m\u00eames que Dieu a fait les dispensateurs de ces r\u00e9compenses temporelles, quelque pr\u00e9caution qu'ils y apportent, & quelque envie qu'ils ayent de les distribuer avec \u00e9quit\u00e9, peuvent \u00e0 peine se r\u00e9pondre d'eux-m\u00eames, & compter qu'ils ne se laisseront pas pr\u00e9venir & surprendre. Comme ils sont hommes, dit saint Augustin, ils r\u00e9compensent en hommes, c'est-\u00e0-dire, bien plus souvent selon la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 de leurs affaires, que selon le degr\u00e9 de leur estime : bien plus souvent par inclination que par raison. Et en effet, ceux qui entrent dans leurs plaisirs, ont commun\u00e9ment bien plus de part \u00e0 leurs graces & \u00e0 leurs bienfaits, que ceux qui s'immolent pour leur service. Ceux qui les flattent & qui les trompent, sont commun\u00e9ment bien mieux r\u00e9compens\u00e9s que ceux qui leur sont fideles. Ainsi vont les choses humaines : & quelque z\u00e9le que nous ayons pour la r\u00e9formation de l'univers, nous ne devons pas esp\u00e9rer qu'elles prennent un autre cours."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p008", "label": "English", "text": "Not only do we not possess heaven's reward unless we merit it; what I ask you to understand clearly is that we possess it only because we merit it. Thus it belongs to merit alone, to the exclusion of every other title. That is why Saint Paul, defining it, calls it a crown of justice: For the rest, a crown of justice is laid up for me. For, the Fathers say, this reward is to be given to human beings only according to the rules of strict justice: incorruptible justice, which nothing will move, bend or prejudice; justice that shows favour to no one, that distinguishes neither dignities nor ranks nor birth, but discerns merit perfectly. The world's rewards are daily exposed to greed and ambition. Although they are owed only to merit, everything other than merit helps people obtain them. They are carried off through influence, attracted through intrigue, wrested away through importunity. The boldest and greediest obtain them; chance decides, one person's good fortune and often another's injustice. People claim them on the strength of someone else's merit: a son wants to be rewarded for his father's services; a friend thinks himself entitled to benefit from his friend's labour and power. Even those whom God has made dispensers of these temporal rewards, however cautious they are and however much they wish to distribute them fairly, can scarcely answer for themselves or count on avoiding bias and deception. Since they are human, says Saint Augustine, they reward as humans do: much more often according to the necessities of their affairs than to the degree of their esteem, much more often from inclination than from reason. Indeed, those who share their pleasures usually receive far more of their favours and benefits than those who sacrifice themselves in their service. Those who flatter and deceive them are generally much better rewarded than those who are faithful to them. Such is the course of human affairs; however zealous we may be to reform the world, we must not expect them to take another course.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p009", "label": "French original", "text": "Rien de tout cela dans la r\u00e9compense o\u00f9 Dieu nous appelle. Il p\u00e9sera ses Elus dans la balance, mais dans la balance du Sanctuaire ; & leur m\u00e9rite seul mis \u00e0 l'\u00e9preuve de son jugement, fera la d\u00e9cision de leur sort. Quiconque n'en aura pas l'exacte mesure, f\u00fbt-il un des Dieux de la terre, sera rejett\u00e9. Comme le fils ne portera point l'iniquit\u00e9 du pere, aussi le m\u00e9rite du pere ne suppl\u00e9era point \u00e0 l'indignit\u00e9 du fils. Tout devant Dieu sera personnel, & la r\u00e8gle du Saint-Esprit subsistera : Unicuique secund\u00f9m meritum operum suorum ; \u00e0 chacun selon ses \u0153uvres. Il ne dit pas : \u00e0 chacun selon ses lumi\u00e8res, \u00e0 chacun selon ses maximes, \u00e0 chacun selon ses talens. Il ne dit pas m\u00eame : \u00e0 chacun selon ses d\u00e9sirs, selon ses projets & ses intentions : mais \u00e0 chacun selon ses actions, \u00e0 chacun selon ce qu'il aura fait, & non point selon ce qu'il aura cru ou ce qu'il aura voulu faire : Unicuique secund\u00f9m meritum operum suorum. En un mot le tems de la grace & de la mis\u00e9ricorde sera expir\u00e9, & celui de la justice succ\u00e9dera : & par la m\u00eame raison, que la grace dans les Elus aura pr\u00e9c\u00e9d\u00e9 tout m\u00e9rite ; la gloire dont Dieu les comblera, aura pour fondement le m\u00e9rite acquis par la grace. Rien de plus vain dans l'opinion des mondains m\u00eames, que la gloire du monde : pourquoi ? parce qu'elle n'est ni une preuve certaine, ni une cons\u00e9quence s\u00fbre du m\u00e9rite ; parce qu'elle est presque toujours l'effet du caprice & de la pr\u00e9vention des hommes ; parce qu'il n'y a rien o\u00f9 la corruption du jugement des hommes, o\u00f9 leur peu d'\u00e9quit\u00e9, d'int\u00e9grit\u00e9, de sinc\u00e9rit\u00e9, paroisse plus \u00e9videmment, qu'en ce qui regarde cette gloire profane. Mais par une r\u00e8gle toute contraire, concevez de-l\u00e0 ce que c'est que la gloire des Pr\u00e9destin\u00e9s dans le ciel, puisque c'est un Dieu qui en est l'arbitre ; un Dieu souverainement \u00e9clair\u00e9, souverainement juste, & qui ne peut estimer que ce qui est essentiellement estimable ; un Dieu aussi d\u00e9termin\u00e9 \u00e0 ne glorifier que le m\u00e9rite, qu'il l'est \u00e0 r\u00e9prouver & \u00e0 punir le p\u00e9ch\u00e9 ; un Dieu dans l'un & dans l'autre \u00e9galement infaillible, inflexible, irr\u00e9pr\u00e9hensible ; tel est, mes Freres, concluoit saint Paul, tel est le ma\u00eetre dont il m'importe d'\u00eatre lou\u00e9 & d'\u00eatre favorablement jug\u00e9, parce que c'est celui dont l'approbation & la louange doit faire \u00e9ternellement la solide gloire : Qui autem judicat me, Dominus est."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p009", "label": "English", "text": "None of this applies to the reward to which God calls us. He will weigh his Elect in the balance, but in the balance of the Sanctuary; their merit alone, tested by his judgment, will decide their lot. Whoever lacks the exact measure will be rejected, even if he were one of the gods of the earth. Just as the son will not bear the father's iniquity, so the father's merit will not make up for the son's unworthiness. Before God everything will be personal, and the Holy Spirit's rule will stand: To each according to the merit of his works; to each according to his works. He does not say: to each according to his understanding, his principles or his talents. He does not even say: to each according to his desires, plans and intentions, but to each according to his actions, according to what he has done, rather than what he thought he had done or wished to do: To each according to the merit of his works. In a word, the time of grace and mercy will have expired, and the time of justice will follow. Just as grace in the Elect will have preceded all merit, so the glory with which God fills them will rest upon merit acquired through grace. Even in the opinion of worldly people, nothing is emptier than worldly glory. Why? Because it is neither a certain proof nor a sure consequence of merit; because it is almost always the effect of human caprice and prejudice; because nowhere are the corruption of human judgment and its lack of equity, integrity and sincerity more evident than in this profane glory. By an entirely opposite rule, then, understand what the glory of the Predestined in heaven is, since God is its arbiter: God, supremely enlightened and supremely just, who can esteem only what is essentially estimable; God, as resolved to glorify merit alone as to condemn and punish sin; God, equally infallible, inflexible and irreproachable in both. Such, my brothers, Saint Paul concluded, is the master whose praise and favourable judgment matter to me, because his approval and praise must constitute my solid glory eternally: But he who judges me is the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p010", "label": "French original", "text": "Enfin cette r\u00e9compense des bienheureux, on ne l'a qu'autant qu'on la m\u00e9rite ; & si l'un est plus r\u00e9compens\u00e9 que l'autre, s'il est dans un degr\u00e9 de gloire plus \u00e9minent que l'autre, ce n'est que parce qu'il a plus de m\u00e9rite que l'autre. Dans le monde, on voit tous les jours des m\u00e9rites m\u00e9diocres l'emporter sur des m\u00e9rites \u00e9clatans ; & vous le permettez, Seigneur, pour nous apprendre que ce n'est point ici que se doit faire le vrai discernement de nos personnes ; vous le permettez pour nous d\u00e9tacher malgr\u00e9 nous de la terre, & pour nous faire porter plus haut nos v\u00fbes : mais dans le Royaume de Dieu chacun est plac\u00e9 selon l'ordre o\u00f9 il doit \u00eatre ; & une des plus singuli\u00e9res beaut\u00e9s que l'Ecriture y fait remarquer, est cette admirable proportion entre la qualit\u00e9 du m\u00e9rite & le rang qu'il occupe. Il y a, disoit le Fils de Dieu, dans la Maison de mon Pere, diff\u00e9rentes demeures ; mais ces demeures, observe saint Bernard, ne sont diff\u00e9rentes, que parce qu'il s'y trouve des m\u00e9rites diff\u00e9rens. Le plus ou le moins de m\u00e9rite y fait donc le plus ou le moins d'\u00e9l\u00e9vation ; cinq talens de m\u00e9rite y produisent cinq talens de gloire, & deux n'y en produisent que deux. Tellement que la diversit\u00e9 du m\u00e9rite y distingue, y partage, y ordonne tout. Or si cela est, Chr\u00e9tiens, permettez-moi de m'arr\u00eater ici, & de faire avec vous une r\u00e9flexion dont il est difficile que vous ne soyez pas touch\u00e9s : \u00e0 quelle \u00e9tonnante r\u00e9volution ne doivent pas s'attendre la pl\u00fbpart des hommes, quand ce myst\u00e8re s'accomplira, & que Dieu dans son jugement dernier viendra faire ce partage ? Quelle d\u00e9solation, par exemple, pour tant de Grands, lorsqu'apr\u00e8s avoir tenu dans le monde des rangs honorables, que leur donnoient leurs dignit\u00e9s, leurs emplois, leurs charges, il leur en faudra prendre d'autres, que le m\u00e9rite seul r\u00e9glera, & o\u00f9 l'arr\u00eat de Dieu les r\u00e9duira ? Si Dieu, au moment que je parle, leur faisoit voir l'affreuse diff\u00e9rence de ce qu'ils sont aujourd'hui & de ce qu'ils seront alors, dans quelle consternation cette v\u00fbe ne les jetteroit-elle pas : & quand \u00e0 la mort il faudra quitter en effet ces rangs de naissance & de fortune, pour passer \u00e0 d'autres rangs, qu'une exacte & rigoureuse justice leur assignera, quelle douleur pour eux de se trouver dans un si prodigieux abaissement, dans un \u00e9loignement infini de Dieu, parce qu'ils n'auront presque rien fait pour Dieu ? Je s\u00e7ai que cette r\u00e9flexion est affligeante ; mais en est-elle moins salutaire ? & ne serois-je pas pr\u00e9varicateur, si dans une occasion aussi naturelle que celle-ci, je ne les faisois souvenir, qu'outre les grandeurs de la terre, il y en a d'autres dans le ciel o\u00f9 ils doivent aspirer ; qu'il y a d'autres honneurs dont ils doivent \u00eatre jaloux, d'autres places qu'ils doivent remplir, d'autres \u00e9tablissemens pour lesquels Dieu les a cr\u00e9\u00e9s ? Aurois-je pour leur salut le z\u00e9le que mon minist\u00e8re doit m'inspirer, si je ne les avertissois pas que la figure de ce monde passe, & qu'apr\u00e8s qu'elle sera pass\u00e9e, le m\u00e9rite d'une vie chr\u00e9tienne est le seul titre de distinction qui nous restera ?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p010", "label": "English", "text": "Finally, we possess this reward of the blessed only in proportion to our merit. If one person is rewarded more than another, if he enjoys a higher degree of glory, it is only because he has more merit. In the world we see mediocre merits prevail over outstanding ones every day. You permit this, Lord, to teach us that the true assessment of our persons is not to be made here; you permit it to detach us from the earth despite ourselves and make us direct our gaze higher. But in God's Kingdom everyone is placed in his proper order. One of its most remarkable beauties pointed out by Scripture is the admirable proportion between the quality of merit and the rank it occupies. In my Father's House, the Son of God said, there are different dwellings. But these dwellings, Saint Bernard observes, differ only because the merits found there differ. Greater or lesser merit therefore brings greater or lesser elevation; five talents of merit produce five talents of glory, and two produce only two. Thus diversity of merit distinguishes, distributes and orders everything there. If this is so, Christians, allow me to pause and reflect with you on something that can hardly fail to move you: what an astonishing reversal most people must expect when this mystery is fulfilled and God comes at the last judgment to make this distribution! What desolation, for example, for so many of the great, when, after holding honourable ranks in the world through their dignities, posts and offices, they must take other ranks determined by merit alone, to which God's sentence will reduce them! If God were to show them, at this very moment, the dreadful difference between what they are today and what they will be then, what consternation that sight would cause! When at death they must actually leave these ranks of birth and fortune for others assigned by exact and rigorous justice, what anguish to find themselves brought so exceedingly low, infinitely distant from God, because they will have done almost nothing for God! I know this reflection is distressing; but is it therefore less salutary? Would I not betray my duty if, on so natural an occasion, I did not remind them that besides earthly greatness there is another in heaven to which they should aspire; that there are other honours they should covet, other places they should fill, other positions for which God created them? Would I have the zeal for their salvation that my ministry should inspire if I did not warn them that the fashion of this world passes away, and that when it has passed, the merit of a Christian life is the only title of distinction that will remain to us?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p011", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais revenons. Je ne me suis pas content\u00e9 de vous dire qu'on a souvent les r\u00e9compenses du monde sans les m\u00e9riter : j'ai ajout\u00e9 que souvent aussi on les m\u00e9rite sans les avoir ; au lieu que nous sommes assur\u00e9s, en m\u00e9ritant la r\u00e9compense \u00e9ternelle, de l'obtenir. En effet comptez, si vous le pouvez, mes chers Auditeurs, combien de gens vous avez v\u00fbs dans le monde mener une vie obscure, & ne parvenir \u00e0 rien, avec un m\u00e9rite & des services qui devoient les \u00e9lever \u00e0 tout. Des patrons leur ont manqu\u00e9, des concurrens les ont \u00e9cart\u00e9s ; l'envie, l'intrigue, la cabale, mille mauvais offices les ont d\u00e9truits ; un ma\u00eetre aveugle & sans discernement, un ma\u00eetre insensible & indiff\u00e9rent, un ma\u00eetre tromp\u00e9 & pr\u00e9venu les a laiss\u00e9s dans la foule, les a oubli\u00e9s, m\u00e9pris\u00e9s, rebut\u00e9s. Que ne nous apprend pas l\u00e0-dessus l'usage & la science du monde ? Mais avec Dieu je suis \u00e0 couvert de toutes ces injustices. Quoi que je fasse, si c'est pour lui que je le fais, il m'en tiendra compte. Qu'est-ce qu'un verre d'eau ? cependant ce verre d'eau donn\u00e9 en son nom, ne sera pas sans r\u00e9compense. Qu'est-ce qu'une pens\u00e9e ? cependant cette bonne pens\u00e9e re\u00e7ue & suivie, aura son salaire. Qu'est-ce qu'un d\u00e9sir ? cependant ce bon d\u00e9sir con\u00e7u & form\u00e9 dans le c\u0153ur, produira, selon l'expression de l'Ap\u00f4tre, son rayon de gloire. Qu'est-ce qu'une parole ? cependant cette parole dite avec douceur, avec humanit\u00e9, avec charit\u00e9, sera \u00e9crite dans le Livre de vie. Or si Dieu doit r\u00e9compenser de la sorte jusqu'aux moindres m\u00e9rites, que sera-ce des autres ? C'est ainsi qu'il nous l'a promis ; & comme il est tout-puissant, c'est ainsi qu'il peut l'accomplir ; & comme il est fid\u00e9le, c'est ainsi qu'il veut l'accomplir : par cons\u00e9quent, c'est ainsi qu'il l'accomplira. Ensorte, conclut saint Augustin, que sa toute-puissance, qui est la toute-puissance d'un Dieu, n'aura point dans l'\u00e9ternit\u00e9 d'autre occupation, que de glorifier ses Elus, & tous leurs m\u00e9rites. Voil\u00e0 \u00e0 quoi il s'emploiera, en quoi il mettra une partie de ses complaisances, de quoi il ne se lassera jamais."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p011", "label": "English", "text": "But let us return. I did not merely tell you that people often possess the world's rewards without meriting them; I added that they also often merit them without possessing them, whereas we are assured of obtaining the eternal reward when we merit it. Count, if you can, my dear hearers, how many people you have seen in the world lead obscure lives and attain nothing, despite merits and services that should have raised them to everything. They lacked patrons; rivals pushed them aside; envy, intrigue, faction and a thousand harmful interventions ruined them. A blind and undiscerning master, an unfeeling and indifferent master, a deceived and prejudiced master left them in the crowd, forgot, despised and rebuffed them. What does experience and knowledge of the world not teach us about this? But with God I am safe from all these injustices. Whatever I do, if I do it for him, he will take account of it. What is a glass of water? Yet that glass of water given in his name will not go unrewarded. What is a thought? Yet that good thought, welcomed and followed, will have its recompense. What is a desire? Yet that good desire conceived and formed in the heart will produce, in the Apostle's expression, its ray of glory. What is a word? Yet that word spoken with gentleness, humanity and charity will be written in the Book of life. If God is to reward even the smallest merits in this way, what will he do for the others? This is what he has promised us; since he is almighty, he can fulfil it; since he is faithful, he wishes to fulfil it; consequently he will fulfil it. Thus, Saint Augustine concludes, his omnipotence, which is the omnipotence of God, will have no other occupation in eternity than to glorify his Elect and all their merits. That is what he will engage in, what will form part of his delight, what he will never tire of doing.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p012", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais cela pos\u00e9, Chr\u00e9tiens, & quoique nous fassions profession de le croire, vivons-nous & agissons-nous comme en \u00e9tant persuad\u00e9s ? Je parle \u00e0 des Auditeurs, qui, chacun dans leur condition, se piquent d'avoir leur m\u00e9rite, & je veux bien convenir de tout le m\u00e9rite dont vous vous piquez. Mais ce m\u00e9rite, que je n'ai garde de vous disputer, est-ce un m\u00e9rite pour le ciel ? est-ce un m\u00e9rite \u00e0 qui Jesus-Christ ait jamais rien promis ? est-ce un m\u00e9rite que vous osiez vous-m\u00eames pr\u00e9senter \u00e0 Dieu pour lui demander son Royaume ? Si les Saints, qui r\u00e9gnent avec ce Dieu & ce Roi de gloire, n'avoient point eu d'autre m\u00e9rite, recueilleroient-ils maintenant les fruits dont ils ont jett\u00e9 sur la terre les pr\u00e9cieuses semences ? Entrons dans le d\u00e9tail. Une vie aussi inutile & aussi vuide de bonnes \u0153uvres que celle d'un homme du monde, d'une femme du monde, r\u00e9gulier d'ailleurs & d'une conduite, selon le monde, irr\u00e9prochable ; est-ce la vie d'un Chr\u00e9tien, gag\u00e9, selon la parabole de l'Evangile, pour m\u00e9riter une r\u00e9compense immortelle ? Voyons ces merc\u00e9naires, qui, press\u00e9s par le besoin, donnent leur peine pour un salaire temporel : ces hommes \u00e0 qui le Fils de Dieu nous compare si souvent, & \u00e0 qui il veut, en quelque \u00e9tat que nous puissions \u00eatre, que nous nous conformions. Les imitons-nous ? sommes-nous attach\u00e9s comme eux \u00e0 un travail constant & assidu ? renon\u00e7ons-nous comme eux \u00e0 la mollesse & \u00e0 la douceur du repos ? avons-nous, comme eux, des jours pleins par une pratique entiere de nos devoirs ? Si malgr\u00e9 cette inutilit\u00e9 de vie l'on gagnoit le ciel, le ciel seroit-il ce Royaume de conqu\u00eate, qu'il faut emporter par violence & acheter si ch\u00e8rement ? Doit-il suffire \u00e0 des Chr\u00e9tiens, pour \u00eatre r\u00e9compens\u00e9s de Dieu, de se trouver exempts de crime ; & la maxime sur laquelle on s'appuie jusqu'\u00e0 s'en faire une conscience, que tout le m\u00e9rite du salut se r\u00e9duit \u00e0 ne point faire de mal, n'est-ce pas une erreur dont il faut aujourd'hui vous d\u00e9tromper ? M\u00e9rite-t-on des r\u00e9compenses en ne faisant rien ? le monde en juge-t-il de la sorte ? r\u00e9compense-t-il l'oisivet\u00e9, quoique d'ailleurs innocente ? n'exige-t-il pas des services r\u00e9els ? & pourquoi croirons-nous que Dieu nous en tiendra quittes \u00e0 moins de frais ? Vivant dans cette erreur grossiere, que je s\u00e7ai \u00eatre le d\u00e9sordre le plus ordinaire de ceux qui m'\u00e9coutent, puis-je, mes chers Auditeurs, vous dire \u00e0 la v\u00fbe de Jesus-Christ montant au ciel : Gaudete & exultate ; R\u00e9jouissez-vous, tressaillez de joie : pourquoi ? Quoniam merces vestra copiosa est in c\u0153lis ; parce que vous aurez la m\u00eame r\u00e9compense que ce Dieu glorifi\u00e9, une r\u00e9compense abondante. Ne dois-je pas vous dire plut\u00f4t : Pleurez & g\u00e9missez : pourquoi ? parce que travaillant si peu, il faut que votre r\u00e9compense soit bien petite ; pleurez, pourquoi ? parce qu'il est m\u00eame plus vraisemblable, que cette r\u00e9compense des Elus n'est point pour vous ; pleurez, pourquoi ? parce que ces m\u00e9rites dont vous voulez vous pr\u00e9valoir, & \u00e0 qui le monde donne de vains \u00e9loges, sont des m\u00e9rites p\u00e9rissables, dont vous avez d\u00e9j\u00e0 re\u00e7u la r\u00e9compense, & dont vous ne la recevrez jamais. Voil\u00e0 dans cette sainte solemnit\u00e9 & malgr\u00e9 la joie de l'Eglise, ce qui doit faire le sujet de votre douleur."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p012", "label": "English", "text": "But granting this, Christians, and although we profess to believe it, do we live and act as people convinced of it? I am speaking to hearers who, each in their own station, pride themselves on having merit, and I willingly acknowledge all the merit on which you pride yourselves. But this merit, which I have no intention of disputing, is it merit for heaven? Is it merit to which Jesus Christ has ever promised anything? Is it merit you yourselves would dare present to God to ask for his Kingdom? If the Saints who reign with this God and King of glory had possessed no other merit, would they now be gathering the fruits whose precious seeds they sowed on earth? Let us consider the details. A life as useless and empty of good works as that of a man or woman of the world, otherwise orderly and, by worldly standards, irreproachable in conduct\u2014is that the life of a Christian hired, according to the Gospel parable, to merit an immortal reward? Look at those hired workers who, pressed by need, give their labour for temporal wages: those men to whom the Son of God so often compares us and whom he wishes us to resemble, whatever our state in life. Do we imitate them? Are we devoted, as they are, to constant and assiduous labour? Do we renounce, as they do, self-indulgence and the sweetness of rest? Are our days full, as theirs are, through the complete fulfilment of our duties? If one won heaven despite such a useless life, would heaven be that Kingdom to be conquered, which must be taken by force and purchased so dearly? Should it suffice for Christians, to be rewarded by God, to be free from crime? Is not the maxim on which people rely to the point of making it a rule of conscience\u2014that all the merit of salvation consists in doing no evil\u2014an error from which you must be freed today? Does one merit rewards by doing nothing? Does the world judge in that way? Does it reward idleness, however innocent otherwise? Does it not require real services? Why should we believe that God will let us off at less cost? While you live in this gross error, which I know to be the most common disorder among those listening to me, can I, my dear hearers, say to you at the sight of Jesus Christ ascending to heaven: Rejoice and exult; rejoice, leap for joy? Why? Because your reward is abundant in heaven; because you will have the same reward as this glorified God, an abundant reward. Should I not say instead: Weep and groan? Why? Because, working so little, your reward must be very small. Weep\u2014why? Because it is even more likely that this reward of the Elect is not for you. Weep\u2014why? Because those merits you wish to claim, which the world praises with empty eulogies, are perishable merits, for which you have already received the reward and for which you will never receive it. Amid this holy solemnity, and despite the Church's joy, this should be the cause of your sorrow.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p013", "label": "French original", "text": "Enfans des hommes, concluoit le Proph\u00e9te Royal, jusques \u00e0 quand aimerez-vous la vanit\u00e9, & chercherez-vous le mensonge ? Usquequ\u00f2 diligitis vanitatem, & quaeritis mendacium ? Il leur en demandoit la raison, usquequ\u00f2 ? & il n'en attendoit pas la r\u00e9ponse, remarque saint Augustin, parce qu'il s\u00e7avoit bien qu'ils n'en avoient point \u00e0 lui faire. Souffrez que je vous fasse le m\u00eame reproche. Enfans des hommes, jusques \u00e0 quand vous fatiguerez-vous \u00e0 chercher des r\u00e9compenses corruptibles, dont la poursuite vous cause tant d'inqui\u00e9tudes, dont le retardement vous remplit de tant de chagrins, dont vous n'\u00eates jamais contens, & qui ne servent qu'\u00e0 vous jetter dans un plus profond oubli de Dieu ? Aussi ardens que vous l'\u00eates pour ces biens de fortune qui emportent toutes vos r\u00e9flexions & tous vos soins, & dont la mort vous d\u00e9pouillera bient\u00f4t, jusques \u00e0 quand n\u00e9gligerez-vous ces vrais biens & cette couronne que votre m\u00e9diateur & votre chef vous propose comme l'objet le plus digne de vos v\u0153ux ? Ecoutez-le parler lui-m\u00eame ; car c'est lui-m\u00eame qui du haut de sa gloire, s'adresse \u00e0 nous en ce jour, & nous dit : Usquequ\u00f2 diligitis vanitatem, & quaeritis mendacium ? Hommes terrestres & sensuels, jusques \u00e0 quand m\u00e9priserez-vous mes promesses pour celles du monde ? Puisque vous \u00eates si int\u00e9ress\u00e9s & si avides, que ne vous attachez-vous du moins au ma\u00eetre qui vous offre davantage ? Le monde a-t-il des r\u00e9compenses aussi solides & m\u00eame aussi pr\u00e9sentes que les miennes ? Le monde, quand vous vous \u00eates livr\u00e9s \u00e0 lui, vous a-t-il jamais rendus heureux, & trouve-t-on le centuple en le servant ? Voil\u00e0, Chr\u00e9tiens Auditeurs, \u00e0 quoi il faut que vous r\u00e9pondiez, mais \u00e0 quoi vous ne pouvez bien r\u00e9pondre que par la r\u00e9formation de vos m\u0153urs & par un parfait changement de vie. Que ce soit donc l\u00e0 d\u00e9sormais l'exercice de votre foi ; Thesaurisate vobis thesauros in c\u0153lo ; amassez des thr\u00e9sors pour le ciel. Au lieu de ces vertus mondaines dont vous vous parez, & qui ne sont devant Dieu de nul m\u00e9rite ; au lieu de cette prudence de la chair, de cette politique, de cette force pa\u00efenne, entrez dans la pratique de ces vertus chr\u00e9tiennes, qui seront pour vous des sources f\u00e9condes de b\u00e9atitude & de gloire. Appliquez-vous non-seulement \u00e0 vous assurer, mais \u00e0 augmenter votre r\u00e9compense par vos bonnes \u0153uvres. C'est \u00e0 quoi jusques \u00e0 pr\u00e9sent vous n'avez point pens\u00e9 ; mais il est encore tems d'y pourvoir : car vous pouvez encore r\u00e9parer par votre ferveur toutes vos pertes. Vous pouvez encore racheter ces jours malheureux, o\u00f9 vous n'avez rien fait, ni pour Dieu ni pour votre ame. Vous pouvez m\u00eame, \u00e0 l'exemple des ouvriers de l'Evangile, commen\u00e7ant tard & \u00e0 la derniere heure du jour, \u00eatre aussi bien r\u00e9compens\u00e9s, que ceux qui sont venus d\u00e8s le matin, & qui ont travaill\u00e9 toute la journ\u00e9e. Or si vous le pouvez, \u00eates-vous excusables de ne le pas faire ? R\u00e9compense du ciel, r\u00e9compense qu'il faut m\u00e9riter, comme Jesus-Christ ; c'est ce que vous avez v\u00fb : mais pour la m\u00e9riter comme Jesus-Christ, j'ajoute qu'il faut souffrir comme Jesus-Christ ; vous l'allez voir dans la seconde partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p013", "label": "English", "text": "Children of men, the Royal Prophet concluded, how long will you love vanity and seek falsehood? How long do you love vanity and seek a lie? He asked them for their reason\u2014how long?\u2014but did not expect an answer, Saint Augustine observes, because he knew very well they had none to give him. Allow me to make the same reproach to you. Children of men, how long will you weary yourselves seeking corruptible rewards, whose pursuit causes you so much anxiety, whose delay fills you with so much grief, with which you are never content, and which serve only to plunge you into deeper forgetfulness of God? Ardent as you are for those goods of fortune that absorb all your thoughts and cares, and of which death will soon strip you, how long will you neglect those true goods and that crown which your mediator and head sets before you as the object most worthy of your desires? Hear him speak himself: it is he who, from the height of his glory, addresses us today and says: How long do you love vanity and seek a lie? Earthly and sensual people, how long will you despise my promises for those of the world? Since you are so self-interested and eager for gain, why not at least attach yourselves to the master who offers you more? Does the world have rewards as solid, and even as immediate, as mine? When you gave yourselves to the world, did it ever make you happy? Does one find a hundredfold return in serving it? These, Christian hearers, are the questions you must answer; but you can answer them well only by reforming your conduct and making a complete change of life. Let this henceforth be the exercise of your faith: Lay up treasures for yourselves in heaven; gather treasures for heaven. Instead of those worldly virtues with which you adorn yourselves, and which have no merit before God; instead of that prudence of the flesh, that worldly calculation, that pagan fortitude, begin to practise those Christian virtues that will be fruitful sources of blessedness and glory for you. Apply yourselves not only to securing your reward, but to increasing it through good works. Until now you have not thought of this; but there is still time to provide for it. Through your fervour you can still repair all your losses. You can still redeem those unhappy days when you did nothing either for God or for your soul. Even beginning late, at the last hour of the day, like the Gospel's labourers, you can be rewarded as well as those who came in the morning and worked all day. If you can do this, are you excusable for failing to do it? Heaven's reward, a reward that must be merited as Jesus Christ merited it: this is what you have seen. But to merit it as Jesus Christ did, I add that you must suffer as Jesus Christ did. You will see this in the second part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-h003", "label": "French original", "text": "II. Partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-h003", "label": "English", "text": "Part II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p014", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est un ordre \u00e9tabli de Dieu, & le monde m\u00eame, tout perverti qu'il est dans ses maximes, est oblig\u00e9 de s'y soumettre & de le reconno\u00eetre : on n'arrive point \u00e0 la gloire par le plaisir ; mais il faut renoncer au plaisir, quand on se propose d'acqu\u00e9rir la v\u00e9ritable gloire. Car le plaisir ne conduit \u00e0 rien, je dis \u00e0 rien de solide, ni \u00e0 rien de grand. Jamais ce qui s'appelle vie de plaisir, n'a produit une vertu, n'a inspir\u00e9 de sentimens nobles, n'a \u00e9lev\u00e9 l'homme au-dessus de lui-m\u00eame. Soit donc par la nature des choses, soit par un effet de la corruption du p\u00e9ch\u00e9, le plaisir & la gloire dans cette vie sont incompatibles, & quiconque pr\u00e9sume qu'il pourra les accorder, se flatte & se trompe, s\u00e9duit par les fausses id\u00e9es qu'il a de l'un ou de l'autre. En un mot, o\u00f9 r\u00e8gne l'amour du plaisir, il faut que le d\u00e9sir de la gloire cesse : & o\u00f9 le d\u00e9sir de la gloire est sinc\u00e8re, il faut que le plaisir soit sacrifi\u00e9. C'est ainsi que le concevoient les sages m\u00eames du Paganisme, & ils le concevoient bien. Or si cela est vrai de la gloire en g\u00e9n\u00e9ral, & m\u00eame en particulier de cette gloire profane que l'ambition des hommes recherche, quel jugement devons-nous faire de la gloire du ciel : de cette gloire pour laquelle nous avons tous \u00e9t\u00e9 cr\u00e9\u00e9s, mais surquoi nous avons perdu nos droits, en perdant la grace de l'innocence, & o\u00f9 il n'y a plus de retour pour nous que par les \u0153uvres de la p\u00e9nitence ; de cette gloire o\u00f9 nous ne pouvons pr\u00e9tendre que par la croix de Jesus-Christ, & qu'il ne nous est pas m\u00eame permis d'esp\u00e9rer, si nous ne sommes, comme dit saint Paul, ent\u00e9s sur Jesus-Christ, & sur Jesus-Christ souffrant & mourant. Si complantati facti sumus similitudini mortis ejus, simul & resurrectionis erimus ? Non, mes chers Auditeurs ; je le r\u00e9p\u00e8te, jamais les plaisirs de la vie ne nous feront parvenir \u00e0 cette gloire. Il faut y aller par la voie des souffrances : premiere v\u00e9rit\u00e9, qui confondra \u00e9ternellement la mollesse & la d\u00e9licatesse des mondains. Mais d'ailleurs toutes sortes de souffrances ne conduisent pas \u00e0 cette gloire : autre v\u00e9rit\u00e9, qui doit d\u00e9tromper une infinit\u00e9 de chr\u00e9tiens que nous voyons souffrir dans le monde, mais qui ne souffrent pas en chr\u00e9tiens. Deux importantes le\u00e7ons que j'ai encore \u00e0 d\u00e9velopper ; & que je regarde comme les deux points les plus essentiels de la morale \u00e9vang\u00e9lique."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p014", "label": "English", "text": "This is an order established by God, and even the world, however perverted its principles, must submit to it and acknowledge it: one does not attain glory through pleasure; rather, one must renounce pleasure when seeking true glory. For pleasure leads to nothing\u2014I mean nothing solid or great. What is called a life of pleasure has never produced a virtue, inspired noble sentiments or raised a person above himself. Whether by the nature of things or as an effect of sin's corruption, pleasure and glory are incompatible in this life. Whoever presumes to reconcile them flatters and deceives himself, misled by false ideas of one or the other. In a word, where love of pleasure reigns, desire for glory must cease; where desire for glory is sincere, pleasure must be sacrificed. Even the pagan sages understood it in this way, and understood it rightly. If this is true of glory in general, and in particular of the profane glory sought by human ambition, what judgment should we make of heaven's glory? That glory for which we were all created, but to which we lost our rights when we lost the grace of innocence, and to which we can return only through works of penance; that glory which we can claim only through the cross of Jesus Christ, and which we may not even hope for unless, as Saint Paul says, we are grafted upon Jesus Christ, upon Jesus Christ suffering and dying. If we have been planted together in the likeness of his death, we shall also be in that of his resurrection? No, my dear hearers, I repeat: life's pleasures will never bring us to that glory. We must go there by the way of sufferings: a first truth that will eternally confound the self-indulgence and softness of worldly people. Yet not every kind of suffering leads to this glory: another truth that must disabuse countless Christians whom we see suffering in the world, but who do not suffer as Christians. These are two important lessons I still have to develop, and which I regard as the two most essential points of Gospel morality.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p015", "label": "French original", "text": "Il faut, comme Jesus-Christ, aller \u00e0 la gloire c\u00e9leste par la croix & par les souffrances. Heureux, si par-l\u00e0 nous en trouvons le chemin ; mais malheur \u00e0 nous, si nous nous figurons qu'on y arrive par une voie plus douce & plus commode, & qu'il y ait pour cela des conditions & des \u00e9tats privil\u00e9gi\u00e9s. Que n'ai-je le z\u00e9le de saint Paul, pour graver profond\u00e9ment dans vos c\u0153urs ce grand principe ! C'est ce que le Sauveur du monde fit entendre aux enfans de Z\u00e9b\u00e9d\u00e9e, qui passoient n\u00e9anmoins pour ses disciples favoris, quand il r\u00e9prima par la duret\u00e9 apparente de sa r\u00e9ponse la vanit\u00e9 de leur pr\u00e9tention. Vous me demandez, leur dit-il, d'\u00eatre assis & honorablement plac\u00e9s dans mon Royaume, & moi je vous demande si vous pouvez boire le calice que je boirai moi-m\u00eame avant vous ? Comme s'il leur e\u00fbt dit : Favoris & disciples tant qu'il vous plaira, si vous ne participez \u00e0 ce calice dont je vous parle, \u00e0 ce calice d'amertume & de douleur, il n'y a pour vous dans mon Royaume ni place ni rang ; & quiconque refuse d'accepter cette condition, & n'a pas le courage de passer par cette \u00e9preuve, doit renoncer \u00e0 ma gloire, & compter qu'il en est exclus. C'est ce que le m\u00eame Sauveur nous a fait voir dans sa propre personne, & ce qu'il d\u00e9clara \u00e0 ses Ap\u00f4tres sur le point qu'il \u00e9toit de retourner \u00e0 ce Royaume c\u00e9leste, qu'il avoit quitt\u00e9 pour descendre sur la terre : Nonne haec oportuit pati Christum, & ita intrare in gloriam suam ? Vous vous \u00e9tonnez de ce que le Christ a souffert, & votre foi en est troubl\u00e9e ; mais ignorez-vous les divines Ecritures, & n'avoit-il pas \u00e9t\u00e9 dit : qu'il souffriroit de la sorte, & qu'il entreroit ainsi dans la gloire ? Or s'il le falloit pour le Christ, Oportuit ; ne le faut-il pas pour nous, & qui peut se plaindre d'une loi que le Fils m\u00eame de Dieu a voulu & a d\u00fb subir ? Aussi les Saints, au moins ceux de la loi de grace, non-seulement se sont consol\u00e9s, mais se sont r\u00e9jouis, mais se sont glorifi\u00e9s dans les afflictions de cette vie, parce qu'ils les ont toujours regard\u00e9es comme la route s\u00fbre & infaillible, qu'ils devoient suivre pour parvenir au terme de leur bonheur. Au lieu que David, par un mouvement de confiance, mais d'une confiance encore juda\u00efque, c'est-\u00e0-dire, d'une confiance qui se proposoit encore quelque chose de terrestre & de charnel, & qui ne s'\u00e9levoit pas aussi parfaitement que la n\u00f4tre aux biens spirituels & c\u00e9lestes ; au lieu, dis-je, que David, pour chercher du soulagement dans ses maux, faisoit \u00e0 Dieu cette pri\u00e8re, & lui disoit : Educes de tribulatione animam meam ; D\u00e9livrez-moi, Seigneur, des tribulations qui m'accablent : les Saints de la loi nouvelle, par un esprit tout oppos\u00e9, mais bien plus \u00e9pur\u00e9 & plus \u00e9clair\u00e9, ont cru devoir dire : Non, Seigneur, ne nous en d\u00e9livrez pas. Ce sont des tribulations, il est vrai, mais des tribulations salutaires, dont malgr\u00e9 les r\u00e9voltes de la nature, nous nous glorifions, & nous nous f\u00e9licitons : Gloriamur in tribulationibus nostris. Ce sont des maux qui nous abattent ; mais qui par un effet tout divin, au m\u00eame tems qu'ils nous abattent aux yeux des hommes, nous d\u00e9tachent de nous-m\u00eames, & nous \u00e9l\u00e9vent \u00e0 vous. Des maux qui sont les gages pr\u00e9cieux de ce v\u00e9ritable, de cet unique, de ce souverain bien que nous attendons. Et \u00e0 quoi nous r\u00e9duiriez-vous, Seigneur, reprenoit, au nom de tous les autres, saint Gr\u00e9goire Pape p\u00e9n\u00e9tr\u00e9 de cette v\u00e9rit\u00e9, \u00e0 quoi mon Dieu, nous r\u00e9duiriez-vous, si par une mis\u00e9ricorde qui nous perdroit, vous veniez \u00e0 nous d\u00e9livrer de ces maux que nous endurons, puisque vous nous assurez dans toutes vos Ecritures que la souffrance doit faire tout le m\u00e9rite de notre esp\u00e9rance ; & par cons\u00e9quent, qu'elle en doit \u00eatre le plus ferme & le plus solide appui ? O\u00f9 en serions-nous, si n'\u00e9tant plus dans le monde, ni afflig\u00e9s, ni humili\u00e9s, ni mortifi\u00e9s, ni pers\u00e9cut\u00e9s, nous n'avions plus ce qui est, selon le t\u00e9moignage de votre Ap\u00f4tre, le caract\u00e8re le plus visible & le plus certain de vos pr\u00e9destin\u00e9s ? Ne nous \u00e9coutez donc point, Seigneur, si jamais nous souhaitions d'avoir sur la terre un sort plus tranquille ; & rejettez notre pri\u00e8re, si nous \u00e9tions assez insens\u00e9s pour vous demander une telle grace. Donnez-nous des secours puissans pour nous soutenir, & un fonds de patience pour souffrir avec soumission ; mais ne nous punissez pas, Seigneur, jusqu'\u00e0 nous traiter dans la vie plus favorablement que vous ne l'avez \u00e9t\u00e9, & jusqu'\u00e0 \u00e9loigner de nous ce qui doit nous donner une sainte ressemblance avec vous. Ainsi, dis-je, ont parl\u00e9 les Saints ; & ce langage, qui selon la prudence de la chair paro\u00eet folie, \u00e9toit dans eux la plus \u00e9minente sagesse."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p015", "label": "English", "text": "Like Jesus Christ, we must go to heavenly glory through the cross and through sufferings. Happy are we if by this means we find the way! But woe to us if we imagine that we can reach it by an easier, more comfortable road, and that certain conditions and states enjoy privileges in this respect. Would that I had Saint Paul's zeal to engrave this great principle deeply in your hearts! This is what the Saviour of the world made the sons of Zebedee understand\u2014although they were regarded as his favourite disciples\u2014when the apparent harshness of his answer checked the vanity of their ambition. You ask me, he told them, to be seated in honourable places in my Kingdom; I ask you whether you can drink the chalice that I myself shall drink before you. It was as though he had said: Call yourselves favourites and disciples as much as you please; if you do not share this chalice of which I speak, this chalice of bitterness and sorrow, there is neither place nor rank for you in my Kingdom. Whoever refuses this condition and lacks the courage to undergo this trial must renounce my glory and consider himself excluded from it. This the same Saviour showed us in his own person, and declared to his Apostles as he was about to return to that heavenly Kingdom he had left to descend to earth: Was it not necessary for Christ to suffer these things and thus enter into his glory? You are astonished that Christ suffered, and your faith is troubled by it. But do you not know the divine Scriptures? Had it not been said that he would suffer in this way and thus enter into glory? If it was necessary for Christ\u2014it was necessary\u2014is it not necessary for us? Who can complain of a law to which the very Son of God wished and was obliged to submit? Thus the Saints, at least those under the law of grace, have not merely found consolation, but rejoiced and gloried in this life's afflictions, because they always regarded them as the sure and unfailing road they must follow to attain their final happiness. David, moved by trust, but a trust still Judaic\u2014that is, a trust still aiming at something earthly and carnal, which did not rise as perfectly as ours to spiritual and heavenly goods\u2014David, I say, seeking relief in his troubles, prayed to God: You will bring my soul out of tribulation; Deliver me, Lord, from the tribulations that overwhelm me. The Saints of the new law, with an entirely opposite spirit, but one much more purified and enlightened, believed they should say: No, Lord, do not deliver us from them. They are tribulations, indeed, but salutary ones, in which, despite nature's rebellion, we glory and rejoice: We glory in our tribulations. They are evils that bring us low, but which, by an entirely divine effect, while bringing us low in human eyes, detach us from ourselves and raise us to you. They are evils that are precious pledges of that true, that unique, that supreme good which we await. And to what would you reduce us, Lord? Pope Saint Gregory, penetrated by this truth, went on in the name of all the others. To what, my God, would you reduce us if, by a mercy that would ruin us, you delivered us from these evils we endure? For throughout your Scriptures you assure us that suffering must constitute all the merit of our hope, and consequently must be its firmest and most solid support. Where would we be if, no longer afflicted, humiliated, mortified or persecuted in the world, we no longer possessed what your Apostle testifies is the most visible and certain mark of your predestined? Do not hear us, then, Lord, if ever we wish for a more peaceful lot on earth; reject our prayer if we are foolish enough to ask you for such a grace. Give us powerful help to sustain us and a store of patience to suffer submissively; but do not punish us, Lord, by treating us in life more favourably than you were treated, or by removing from us what should give us a holy resemblance to you. Thus, I say, the Saints have spoken; and this language, which seems madness according to the prudence of the flesh, was in them the highest wisdom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p016", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est cela m\u00eame qui a donn\u00e9 \u00e0 ces grands hommes & \u00e0 ces fid\u00e9les serviteurs de Dieu, sur le sujet des prosp\u00e9rit\u00e9s temporelles, des sentimens si contradictoirement oppos\u00e9s \u00e0 la cupidit\u00e9 & \u00e0 l'amour-propre. C'est ce qui les a fait trembler, quand ils se sont v\u00fbs dans des \u00e9tats, dont le monde leur applaudissoit, & o\u00f9 consiste en effet la f\u00e9licit\u00e9 des enfans du si\u00e9cle ; mais dont ils craignoient les suites funestes, par rapport \u00e0 cette f\u00e9licit\u00e9 qu'esp\u00e9rent les enfans de Dieu. C'est ce qui les a convaincus aussi-bien que saint Augustin, qu'une prosp\u00e9rit\u00e9 complette, s'il y en avoit une dans le tems pr\u00e9sent, seroit une r\u00e9probation commenc\u00e9e ; & qu'un homme sur la terre parfaitement heureux, s'il raisonnoit bien, devroit ou se croire perdu ou se condamner pour toute sa vie \u00e0 pleurer & \u00e0 g\u00e9mir : pourquoi ? parce qu'il n'y auroit point de moment, o\u00f9 il ne d\u00fbt \u00eatre touch\u00e9 & allarm\u00e9 de cette pens\u00e9e : Je ne suis pas dans la voie de Dieu ; ce n'est point par-l\u00e0 que Dieu a conduit ses \u00e9lus. Combl\u00e9 de biens, comme je le suis, & souffrant aussi peu que je souffre, s'il y a une \u00e9ternit\u00e9 bienheureuse, je n'ai nul lieu de croire qu'elle soit pour moi, & j'ai d'affreuses pr\u00e9somptions qu'elle n'est pas pour moi. Pens\u00e9e d\u00e9solante pour un Chr\u00e9tien ! C'est dans cette v\u00fbe que Jesus-Christ a prononc\u00e9 ces fameux anath\u00eames, \u00e0 quoi le monde ne souscrira jamais, mais qui subsisteront malgr\u00e9 le monde ; & qui malgr\u00e9 le monde auront leur effet : anath\u00eame contre les riches voluptueux : Vae vobis divitibus ; anath\u00eame contre ceux \u00e0 qui rien ne manque, & qui vivent selon les d\u00e9sirs de leur c\u0153ur : Vae vobis qui ridetis, qui saturati estis ; c'est-\u00e0-dire, anath\u00eame contre ceux que le monde a toujours \u00e9t\u00e9 en possession de b\u00e9atifier & de canoniser. Et c'est par la m\u00eame raison que ce divin Ma\u00eetre a \u00e9rig\u00e9 en b\u00e9atitude ce que le monde d\u00e9teste, & ce qu'il a le plus en horreur : bienheureux les pauvres : Beati pauperes ; bienheureux ceux qui pleurent : Beati qui lugent ; bienheureux ceux qui souffrent pers\u00e9cution : Beati qui persecutionem patiuntur. Enfin, c'est ce que les Ap\u00f4tres Paul & Barnab\u00e9 pr\u00eachoient avec tant de z\u00e9le quand ils alloient, dit saint Luc, visiter les Eglises Chr\u00e9tiennes, fortifiant le courage des Disciples, les exhortant \u00e0 pers\u00e9v\u00e9rer dans la foi, & leur remontrant que c'\u00e9toit par les afflictions & les peines, qu'ils devoient entrer dans le Royaume de Dieu : Confirmantes animas discipulorum, & exhortantes ut permanerent in fide, & quoniam per multas tribulationes oportet nos intrare in regnum Dei."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p016", "label": "English", "text": "This very thing gave those great men and faithful servants of God sentiments about temporal prosperity so directly opposed to greed and self-love. It made them tremble when they found themselves in conditions the world applauded, which indeed constitute the happiness of the children of this age, but whose fatal consequences they feared in relation to the happiness hoped for by God's children. It convinced them, as it did Saint Augustine, that complete prosperity, if there were such a thing in the present life, would be reprobation already begun; and that a perfectly happy man on earth, if he reasoned rightly, should either believe himself lost or condemn himself to weep and groan all his life. Why? Because there would be no moment when he should not be moved and alarmed by this thought: I am not in God's way; this is not how God has led his Elect. Laden with goods as I am, suffering as little as I do, if there is a blessed eternity, I have no grounds to believe it is for me, and I have dreadful grounds to presume it is not for me. A desolating thought for a Christian! With this in view Jesus Christ pronounced those famous anathemas to which the world will never assent, but which will stand despite the world and take effect despite it: anathema against the rich who indulge in pleasures: Woe to you who are rich; anathema against those who lack nothing and live according to their hearts' desires: Woe to you who laugh, who are filled. That is, anathema against those whom the world has always claimed the right to beatify and canonise. For the same reason this divine Master declared blessed what the world detests and most abhors: blessed are the poor; blessed are the poor; blessed are those who weep; blessed are those who mourn; blessed are those who suffer persecution; blessed are those who endure persecution. Finally, this is what the Apostles Paul and Barnabas preached with such zeal when, Saint Luke says, they went to visit the Christian Churches, strengthening the courage of the Disciples, exhorting them to persevere in faith, and showing them that through afflictions and hardships they must enter God's Kingdom: Strengthening the souls of the disciples, and exhorting them to remain in the faith, and that through many tribulations we must enter the kingdom of God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p017", "label": "French original", "text": "Telle est la loi, que Dieu dans le conseil de sa providence a port\u00e9e, & qu'il ne changera pas pour nous. Cependant au m\u00e9pris de cette loi, on veut \u00eatre heureux dans le monde ; & quoique les souffrances soient la marque la plus certaine des \u00e9lus de Dieu, par une infid\u00e9lit\u00e9 dont on ne fait nul scrupule, & qu'on ne se reproche jamais, on consent \u00e0 n'avoir point cette marque sp\u00e9ciale de pr\u00e9destination pour le ciel, pourvu que l'on soit, si je puis ainsi parler, des pr\u00e9destin\u00e9s de la terre. A quelque prix que ce puisse \u00eatre, on veut, autant qu'il est possible, \u00e9carter tout ce qui fait de la peine & qui incommode ; & sans balancer, on renonce au moins dans la pratique & par les \u0153uvres aux b\u00e9atitudes de Jesus-Christ, pour jouir des b\u00e9atitudes du si\u00e9cle. Que cet adorable Sauveur, que les Saints apr\u00e8s lui soient arriv\u00e9s \u00e0 la gloire par la croix & par les tribulations, on pr\u00e9tend y arriver par la joie & par le plaisir. Car au m\u00eame tems qu'on ne veut rien souffrir, on veut n\u00e9anmoins d'ailleurs, par un secret inconnu \u00e0 Jesus-Christ m\u00eame, & par une contradiction que les Saints n'ont jamais accord\u00e9e, se sauver dans le monde ; c'est-\u00e0-dire, qu'on veut se sauver dans le monde, tandis qu'on n'y respire que le plaisir, qu'on y rapporte tout au plaisir, qu'on y cherche avec soin & en tout le plaisir, qu'on ne pense qu'\u00e0 y mener une vie de plaisir, & qu'on n'y conno\u00eet point d'autre bien que le plaisir. Mais que fait Dieu, Chr\u00e9tiens ? Remarquez ces deux traits de sa mis\u00e9ricorde, & reconnoissez le d\u00e9sordre de votre conduite. Afin que les plaisirs du monde ne vous corrompent pas, & que ce ne soient pas des obstacles \u00e0 votre bonheur \u00e9ternel ; Dieu qui veut en quelque sorte malgr\u00e9 vous-m\u00eames vous sauver, m\u00eale ces plaisirs d'amertumes, vous y fait trouver des d\u00e9go\u00fbts, vous les rend fades & insipides. C'est ce que vous \u00e9prouvez \u00e0 toute heure ; & vous qui contre tous les desseins de Dieu voulez vous perdre, malgr\u00e9 toutes les amertumes qui s'y rencontrent, vous \u00eates avides de ces plaisirs, vous les d\u00e9sirez ardemment ; vous vous y attachez opini\u00e2tr\u00e9ment ; & tout insipides qu'ils sont, vous les pr\u00e9f\u00e9rez aux d\u00e9lices pures de cette gloire, dont la seule esp\u00e9rance seroit d\u00e9j\u00e0 pour vous une f\u00e9licit\u00e9 anticip\u00e9e. Semblable \u00e0 l'infortun\u00e9 Esa\u00fc, qui pour contenter seulement une fois la faim qui le pressoit, vendit son droit d'a\u00eenesse, & fut par-l\u00e0 frustr\u00e9 de la b\u00e9n\u00e9diction de son pere, vous sacrifiez \u00e0 de vaines douceurs & \u00e0 quelques momens d'une volupt\u00e9 passag\u00e8re, le saint h\u00e9ritage qui vous \u00e9toit acquis."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p017", "label": "English", "text": "Such is the law God has enacted in the counsel of his providence, and he will not change it for us. Yet in contempt of this law we wish to be happy in the world. Although sufferings are the surest mark of God's Elect, through an unfaithfulness about which we feel no scruple and never reproach ourselves, we consent to lack this special mark of predestination for heaven, provided we belong, if I may speak so, to the predestined of the earth. At whatever cost, we wish as far as possible to remove everything painful or inconvenient. Without hesitation we renounce, at least in practice and in our deeds, the beatitudes of Jesus Christ in order to enjoy the beatitudes of this age. Although this adorable Saviour and the Saints after him attained glory through the cross and tribulations, we claim to attain it through joy and pleasure. For while wishing to suffer nothing, we nevertheless wish, by a secret unknown even to Jesus Christ and through a contradiction the Saints never reconciled, to save ourselves in the world. That is, we wish to save ourselves in the world while breathing only pleasure there, referring everything to pleasure, carefully seeking pleasure in everything, thinking only of leading a life of pleasure, and knowing no other good than pleasure. But what does God do, Christians? Observe these two expressions of his mercy, and recognise the disorder of your conduct. So that the world's pleasures may not corrupt you or obstruct your eternal happiness, God, who in some sense wishes to save you despite yourselves, mixes bitterness into these pleasures, makes you find disgust in them, and renders them flat and insipid. You experience this at every hour. Yet you, who against all God's designs wish to ruin yourselves, remain greedy for these pleasures despite all their bitterness; you desire them ardently and cling to them obstinately. Insipid as they are, you prefer them to the pure delights of that glory whose very hope would already be happiness anticipated for you. Like unfortunate Esau, who sold his birthright to satisfy just once the hunger pressing upon him and thus forfeited his father's blessing, you sacrifice the holy inheritance won for you to empty sweetness and a few moments of passing sensual pleasure.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p018", "label": "French original", "text": "Ce n'est pas assez : Dieu vous envoie des souffrances, & par une bont\u00e9 paternelle, il les attache \u00e0 votre condition, \u00e0 vos emplois, aux engagemens que vous avez dans le monde. Car quelques mesures que l'on prenne, on ne peut \u00eatre en commerce avec le monde, sans y trouver sans cesse des sujets de mortification & de chagrin. Si vous connoissiez le don de Dieu, vous ne penseriez qu'\u00e0 le b\u00e9nir d'en avoir ainsi ordonn\u00e9, & vous n'auriez que des actions de graces \u00e0 lui rendre, de vous avoir pourv\u00fb d'un si puissant pr\u00e9servatif contre les dangers & les \u00e9cueils de votre \u00e9tat. Quelque avantageuse, selon le monde, que p\u00fbt \u00eatre votre destin\u00e9e, vous ne vous croiriez pas abandonn\u00e9s du ciel ni r\u00e9prouv\u00e9s, puisque vous auriez encore part au calice du salut. Mais quel usage faites-vous d'un si pr\u00e9cieux talent ? A ce d\u00e9sir insatiable des plaisirs du monde que je viens de vous reprocher, vous joignez l'abus des souffrances par o\u00f9 Dieu vouloit vous sanctifier : & comme vous vous pervertissez par les plaisirs m\u00eames que vous ne go\u00fbtez pas, & qui ne vous satisfont pas ; ainsi vous pervertissez-vous par les croix m\u00eames que vous portez, mais dont vous ne profitez pas. Car toutes sortes de souffrances ne conduisent pas \u00e0 la gloire que Dieu nous d\u00e9couvre dans le myst\u00e8re de ce jour. Si cela \u00e9toit, l'enfer ne seroit plus enfer. Il faut que ce soient des souffrances pour la justice, parce qu'il n'y en a point d'autres que Dieu puisse couronner, ni qui puissent avoir de la proportion avec cette vie bienheureuse, o\u00f9 Jesus-Christ apr\u00e8s lui nous appelle. Souffrir parce qu'on a le c\u0153ur d\u00e9chir\u00e9 de mille passions : souffrir parce qu'on est d\u00e9vor\u00e9 par une ambition que rien ne peut satisfaire ; souffrir parce qu'on est poss\u00e9d\u00e9 d'une envie secrette ; souffrir parce qu'on a dans l'ame la haine & le fiel, c'est souffrir plus que n'ont souffert les p\u00e9nitens les plus aust\u00e8res, & plus que ne souffrent ces malheureux, condamn\u00e9s par la justice & la rigueur des loix, \u00e0 tra\u00eener leurs cha\u00eenes dans un esclavage dur & honteux. Mais c'est souffrir comme les d\u00e9mons pour l'iniquit\u00e9 ; & il r\u00e9pugne \u00e0 la saintet\u00e9 de Dieu, de tenir compte aux hommes de ce qu'ils souffrent pour de si indignes sujets. Si donc l'on pr\u00e9tend au Royaume de Dieu, il faut souffrir pour la cause de Dieu ; il faut souffrir pour la charit\u00e9, souffrir pour la v\u00e9rit\u00e9, souffrir pour la paix, souffrir pour l'ob\u00e9issance : car tout cela est renferm\u00e9 dans cette justice chr\u00e9tienne, dont parloit le Fils de Dieu, quand il disoit : Beati qui persecutionem patiuntur propter justitiam, quoniam ipsorum est regnum c\u0153lorum. Souffrir plut\u00f4t que de se venger & de repousser une injure en rendant le mal pour le mal, c'est ce que j'appelle souffrir pour la charit\u00e9 ; souffrir plut\u00f4t que de trahir sa conscience, plut\u00f4t que de manquer \u00e0 son devoir, plut\u00f4t que d'approuver le vice, c'est ce que j'appelle souffrir pour la v\u00e9rit\u00e9 ; souffrir plut\u00f4t que de causer du trouble, en voulant se d\u00e9fendre & se justifier, c'est ce que j'appelle souffrir pour la paix ; souffrir plut\u00f4t que de murmurer & de se plaindre, plut\u00f4t que de r\u00e9sister aux puissances l\u00e9gitimes, c'est ce que j'appelle souffrir pour l'ob\u00e9issance. Voil\u00e0 les souffrances que Dieu accepte, & qu'il r\u00e9compense dans son Royaume. Mais ma douleur est, Chr\u00e9tiens, que les v\u00f4tres ne sont pas commun\u00e9ment de ce caract\u00e8re ; ma douleur est, qu'au lieu que les Saints disoient en s'adressant \u00e0 Dieu : Propter te mortificamur tot\u00e2 die ; C'est pour vous, Seigneur, que nous sommes pers\u00e9cut\u00e9s & que nous voulons l'\u00eatre ; c'est pour vous que nous nous pers\u00e9cutons en quelque maniere nous-m\u00eames, & que nous nous mortifions : peut-\u00eatre ceux qui m'\u00e9coutent & \u00e0 qui je parle, pourroient dire au monde : C'est pour toi que je me mortifie, monde, dont je me suis fait l'esclave ; c'est pour toi que je me captive, c'est pour toi que je me fais violence, c'est pour toi que je souffre tout ce que les serviteurs de Dieu ont souffert pour Dieu. Or qu'arrive-t-il de-l\u00e0 ? ce qui me paro\u00eet & qui doit vous paro\u00eetre, mes chers Auditeurs, le comble de tous les malheurs de l'homme : vous allez \u00e0 la perdition & \u00e0 la mort, par o\u00f9 les justes & les vrais Chr\u00e9tiens vont au salut & \u00e0 la vie. Car les souffrances m\u00e8nent \u00e0 l'un & \u00e0 l'autre : & ce n'est point, je le r\u00e9p\u00e8te, pr\u00e9cis\u00e9ment par les souffrances que Dieu fait le discernement des Elus & des R\u00e9prouv\u00e9s ; c'est par la qualit\u00e9, c'est par le motif, c'est par le principe & la fin des souffrances. Cependant, j'en reviens toujours \u00e0 la proposition g\u00e9n\u00e9rale, que pour entrer dans cette patrie, dont les portes aujourd'hui vous sont ouvertes, & pour m\u00e9riter d'y \u00eatre re\u00e7u, il faut souffrir."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p018", "label": "English", "text": "That is not all. God sends you sufferings and, in paternal goodness, attaches them to your condition, your occupations and your commitments in the world. Whatever precautions one takes, one cannot deal with the world without constantly finding occasions for mortification and grief. If you knew God's gift, you would think only of blessing him for ordering things this way, and you would have only thanksgiving to offer for his having provided you with so powerful a safeguard against the dangers and pitfalls of your state. However advantageous your destiny might be in worldly eyes, you would not believe yourselves abandoned by heaven or reprobate, since you would still share in the chalice of salvation. But what use do you make of so precious a talent? To that insatiable desire for worldly pleasures with which I have just reproached you, you add the misuse of sufferings through which God wished to sanctify you. Just as you corrupt yourselves through the very pleasures you do not enjoy and which do not satisfy you, so you corrupt yourselves through the very crosses you bear but from which you draw no profit. For not every kind of suffering leads to the glory God reveals in today's mystery. If that were so, hell would no longer be hell. They must be sufferings for justice, because no others can be crowned by God or bear a proportion to that blessed life to which Jesus Christ calls us after him. To suffer because the heart is torn by a thousand passions; because one is devoured by ambition that nothing can satisfy; because one is possessed by secret envy; because the soul is full of hatred and gall\u2014this is to suffer more than the most austere penitents have suffered, and more than those wretches condemned by the justice and severity of the laws to drag their chains in harsh and shameful slavery. But it is to suffer like the demons for iniquity; and it is repugnant to God's holiness to give people credit for what they suffer for such unworthy causes. If, then, we aspire to God's Kingdom, we must suffer for God's cause: for charity, for truth, for peace and for obedience. All this is contained in that Christian justice of which the Son of God spoke when he said: Blessed are those who endure persecution for justice, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven. To suffer rather than avenge oneself and repel an injury by returning evil for evil: this is what I call suffering for charity. To suffer rather than betray one's conscience, fail in one's duty or approve vice: this is suffering for truth. To suffer rather than cause disturbance by seeking to defend and justify oneself: this is suffering for peace. To suffer rather than murmur and complain or resist legitimate authorities: this is suffering for obedience. These are the sufferings God accepts and rewards in his Kingdom. But my sorrow, Christians, is that yours are not usually of this character. My sorrow is that whereas the Saints addressed God saying: For your sake we are put to death all day long; It is for you, Lord, that we are persecuted and wish to be; for you that we in some manner persecute and mortify ourselves\u2014perhaps those who listen to me could say to the world: It is for you that I mortify myself, world, whose slave I have become; for you that I bind myself, for you that I do violence to myself, for you that I suffer everything God's servants suffered for God. What follows? What seems to me, and should seem to you, my dear hearers, the height of all human misfortune: you go to perdition and death by the very means through which the just and true Christians go to salvation and life. For sufferings lead to both. It is not, I repeat, precisely by sufferings themselves that God distinguishes the Elect from the Reprobate, but by the quality, motive, principle and end of those sufferings. Yet I always return to the general proposition: to enter that homeland whose gates are opened to you today, and to merit admission there, one must suffer.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p019", "label": "French original", "text": "Cette parole, Chr\u00e9tiens, vous paro\u00eet dure ; mais j'ose dire qu'elle ne doit point l'\u00eatre pour vous, & en voici la raison \u00e0 laquelle je vous d\u00e9fie de r\u00e9pliquer. Car que ne souffrez-vous pas tous les jours, & que n'\u00eates-vous pas d\u00e9termin\u00e9s \u00e0 souffrir pour le monde ? Que ne souffrez-vous pas pour vous \u00e9tablir, & pour vous pousser dans monde ? Ce d\u00e9sir d'acqu\u00e9rir de la gloire, que ne vous fait-il pas entreprendre ? cette ambition de vous \u00e9lever, que ne vous fait-elle pas prendre sur vous ? S'il s'agit de votre fortune, \u00e9pargnez-vous votre sant\u00e9, m\u00e9nagez-vous votre repos, vous plaignez-vous qu'il vous en co\u00fbte de l'assujettissement & du travail ? Avec quelle patience ne supportez-vous pas tout ce qui se pr\u00e9sente de plus f\u00e2cheux & de plus p\u00e9nible ? Avec quelle ardeur & quel courage ne passez-vous pas par-dessus toutes les difficult\u00e9s ? Pour peu que vous ayez de bonheur & que les choses vous succ\u00e9dent, que ne trouvez-vous pas ais\u00e9 ? Faut-il vous exciter & vous animer ? Avez-vous besoin pour cela de remontrances ? ne vous les faites-vous pas \u00e0 vous-m\u00eames, & ne vous en dites-vous pas plus que je ne vous en dirai jamais ? Or souffrez pour Dieu ce que vous souffrez pour le monde, je ne vous en demande pas davantage. Vous en faut-il un motif pressant, touchant, convaincant ? Ne l'avez-vous pas dans cette gloire qui vous est propos\u00e9e comme le terme de votre esp\u00e9rance ? Y a t-il un autre bien plus pr\u00e9cieux pour vous, que cette gloire o\u00f9 vous n'aurez plus rien \u00e0 d\u00e9sirer, plus rien \u00e0 demander, plus rien \u00e0 rechercher, parce qu'elle comblera toute la capacit\u00e9 de votre c\u0153ur : que cette gloire durable & \u00e9ternellement assur\u00e9e, que jamais rien ne vous enl\u00e8vera, que jamais rien ne troublera, que jamais rien ne bornera : que cette gloire, apr\u00e8s laquelle les Saints ont tant soupir\u00e9, vers laquelle ils \u00e9levoient sans cesse leur esprit, ils tournoient sans cesse leurs regards ; & dont la seule v\u00fbe, quoiqu'obscure & encore imparfaite, dont le seul avant-go\u00fbt sur la terre les ravissoit, les transportoit, & pour m'exprimer ainsi, les enivroit : que cette gloire o\u00f9 le Fils de Dieu souhaitoit si ardemment de retourner, dont il parloit si souvent \u00e0 ses disciples, sur-tout depuis qu'il fut ressuscit\u00e9, & qu'il se vit sur le point d'aller recevoir la couronne que son Pere lui avoit pr\u00e9par\u00e9e ? C'est-l\u00e0 qu'il nous pr\u00e9c\u00e9de, Chr\u00e9tiens : nous sommes ses membres, & il est notre chef ; partout o\u00f9 le chef entre, il faut que les membres le suivent & qu'ils y soient plac\u00e9s avec lui. C'est-l\u00e0 qu'il tra\u00eene avec soi comme en triomphe, & qu'il introduit tant d'ames justes, tant de Patriarches, de Proph\u00e9tes, de pr\u00e9destin\u00e9s de l'ancienne Loi, qui depuis si long-tems attendoient ce Lib\u00e9rateur. Joignons-nous d'esprit & de c\u0153ur \u00e0 cette troupe glorieuse ; & disposons-nous \u00e0 la grossir un jour nous-m\u00eames, & \u00e0 partager avec eux la m\u00eame gloire. Mais du reste n'oublions jamais, (car c'est-l\u00e0 toujours qu'il s'en faut tenir, & ce qu'il faut poser pour un principe n\u00e9cessaire & incontestable,) que c'est une r\u00e9compense ; qu'elle l'a \u00e9t\u00e9 pour eux, & qu'elle le doit \u00eatre pour nous ; qu'ils l'ont acquise par la saintet\u00e9 de leurs \u0153uvres, par la ferveur de leur pi\u00e9t\u00e9, sur-tout par leur patience inalt\u00e9rable & leur constance \u00e0 souffrir, & que c'est ainsi que nous la devons m\u00e9riter. D\u00e9fions-nous de notre foiblesse ; mais ne craignons point toutefois que les forces nous manquent, puisque Jesus-Christ est \u00e0 la droite de son Pere comme notre M\u00e9diateur, comme notre Pontife, pour faire descendre sur nous ses graces les plus puissantes. Allons \u00e0 son Thr\u00f4ne, \u00e0 ce Thr\u00f4ne de gloire & de mis\u00e9ricorde tout ensemble, lui pr\u00e9senter nos hommages, lui offrir nos pri\u00e8res, lui exposer nos besoins, l'adorer & l'invoquer, jusqu'\u00e0 ce que nous puissions dans l'\u00e9ternit\u00e9 le voir & le poss\u00e9der. C'est ce que je vous souhaite, &c."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-p019", "label": "English", "text": "This saying seems harsh to you, Christians; but I venture to say it should not be harsh for you, and here is a reason I challenge you to answer. What do you not suffer every day, and what are you not resolved to suffer for the world? What do you not suffer to establish yourselves and advance in the world? What does this desire to acquire glory not make you undertake? What does this ambition to rise not make you endure? When your fortune is at stake, do you spare your health, protect your rest, complain that it costs you subjection and labour? With what patience do you not bear the most disagreeable and painful things that arise! With what ardour and courage do you not overcome every difficulty! If you have even a little good fortune and things go well, what do you not find easy? Must you be urged and encouraged? Do you need admonitions for this? Do you not give them to yourselves, saying more to yourselves than I shall ever say to you? Then suffer for God what you suffer for the world: I ask no more. Do you need an urgent, moving, convincing motive? Do you not have it in that glory set before you as the end of your hope? Is there any good more precious to you than that glory in which you will have nothing more to desire, ask or seek, because it will fill your heart's entire capacity? Than that enduring and eternally assured glory, which nothing will ever take from you, disturb or limit? Than that glory for which the Saints sighed so much, towards which they constantly raised their minds and turned their gaze, whose mere sight, though dim and still imperfect, whose mere foretaste on earth enraptured and transported them, and, so to speak, intoxicated them? Than that glory to which the Son of God longed so ardently to return, of which he spoke so often to his disciples, especially after he rose again and saw himself about to receive the crown his Father had prepared for him? There he precedes us, Christians: we are his members and he is our head; wherever the head enters, the members must follow and be placed with him. There he draws with him, as in triumph, and brings in so many just souls, so many Patriarchs, Prophets and predestined of the old Law, who had so long awaited this Deliverer. Let us join that glorious company in mind and heart; let us prepare to swell its number ourselves one day and share the same glory with them. But let us never forget\u2014for this is what we must always hold to, and establish as a necessary and incontestable principle\u2014that it is a reward; that it was so for them and must be so for us; that they acquired it through the holiness of their works, the fervour of their piety, above all their unchanging patience and constancy in suffering, and that this is how we must merit it. Let us distrust our weakness, but not fear that strength will fail us, since Jesus Christ is at his Father's right hand as our Mediator and High Priest, to bring down upon us his most powerful graces. Let us go to his Throne, that Throne at once of glory and mercy, to present our homage, offer our prayers, set out our needs, adore and invoke him, until in eternity we may see and possess him. This is what I wish for you, etc.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n001", "label": "French original", "text": "Act. c. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n001", "label": "English", "text": "Acts, chapter 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n002", "label": "French original", "text": "Philipp. c. 3."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n002", "label": "English", "text": "Philippians, chapter 3.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n003", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Joan. c. 3."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n003", "label": "English", "text": "1 John, chapter 3.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n004", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n004", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n005", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n005", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n006", "label": "French original", "text": "Eccli. c. 3."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n006", "label": "English", "text": "Ecclesiasticus, chapter 3.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n007", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 12."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n007", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, chapter 12.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n008", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan. c. 14."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n008", "label": "English", "text": "John, chapter 14.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n009", "label": "French original", "text": "Luc. c. 22."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n009", "label": "English", "text": "Luke, chapter 22.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n010", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 25."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n010", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, chapter 25.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n011", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal. 149."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n011", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 149.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n012", "label": "French original", "text": "Eccli. c. 16."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n012", "label": "English", "text": "Ecclesiasticus, chapter 16.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n013", "label": "French original", "text": "Coloss. c. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n013", "label": "English", "text": "Colossians, chapter 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n014", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n014", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n015", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n015", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, chapter 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n016", "label": "French original", "text": "[unclear epistle numeral]. Tim. c. 4."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n016", "label": "English", "text": "[unclear epistle numeral]. Timothy, chapter 4.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n017", "label": "French original", "text": "Eccli. c. 16."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n017", "label": "English", "text": "Ecclesiasticus, chapter 16.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n018", "label": "French original", "text": "2. Cor. c. 4."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n018", "label": "English", "text": "2 Corinthians, chapter 4.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n019", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n019", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, chapter 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n020", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n020", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n021", "label": "French original", "text": "Psalm. 4."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n021", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 4.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n022", "label": "French original", "text": "Psalm. 4."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n022", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 4.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n023", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n023", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, chapter 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n024", "label": "French original", "text": "Luc. c. 24."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n024", "label": "English", "text": "Luke, chapter 24.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n025", "label": "French original", "text": "Psalm. 142."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n025", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 142.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n026", "label": "French original", "text": "Rom. c. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n026", "label": "English", "text": "Romans, chapter 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n027", "label": "French original", "text": "Luc. c. 6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n027", "label": "English", "text": "Luke, chapter 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n028", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n028", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n029", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n029", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, chapter 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n030", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n030", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, chapter 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n031", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n031", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n032", "label": "French original", "text": "Act. c. 14."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n032", "label": "English", "text": "Acts, chapter 14.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n033", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n033", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, chapter 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n034", "label": "French original", "text": "Rom. c. 8."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-n034", "label": "English", "text": "Romans, chapter 8.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-ed001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The printed margin gives 1 Corinthians 12, but the quotation is from 2 Corinthians 12:4. The printed reference is preserved."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-ed002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The epistle number before \u201cTimothy 4\u201d is damaged in this copy. The quotation is from 2 Timothy 4:8; that identification does not supply the illegible numeral."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-ed003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The printed margin gives 2 Corinthians 4, but the quotation is from 1 Corinthians 4:4. The printed reference is preserved."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension-reading-punctuation", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints a question mark after this quotation; it is retained here."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "On the Ascension: Heavenly Glory and Christian Suffering", "topics": ["Ascension", "heaven", "merit", "grace", "suffering"], "url": "sermons/bourdaloue-mysteres-ascension.html"}, {"author": "Louis Bourdaloue", "calendar": "dayKey:ecce-advenit", "calendar_label": "Epiphany", "centuries": [17, 18], "composition": "Approximately late seventeenth century\u20131704; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie", "language": "fr", "language_label": "French", "metadata": ["Louis Bourdaloue, Sermons du P\u00e8re Bourdalou\u00eb, de la Compagnie de Jesus, sur les myst\u00e8res, tome premier, quatri\u00e8me \u00e9dition, edited by Fran\u00e7ois de Paule Bretonneau (Paris: chez Rigaud, 1726), Sermon sur l\u2019\u00c9piphanie, pp. 91\u2013140.", "Internet Archive; John Adams Library copy, Boston Public Library.", "Epiphany", "Epiphany", "Matthew 2", "Luke 2", "John 9", "Matthew 3", "1 Corinthians 2", "1 Corinthians 1", "John 12", "Matthew 19", "Psalm 25 (source numbering)", "Wisdom 1", "Psalm 24 (source numbering)", "Psalm 9 (source numbering)", "Psalm 68 (source numbering)", "Psalm 104 (source numbering)", "Deuteronomy 32", "1 John 5", "Luke 19", "Apocalypse 19", "Matthew 8", "Acts 13", "1 John 2", "Job 9", "Proverbs 21"], "occasion": "\u00c9piphanie", "occasion_detail": "Epiphany", "occasion_label": "Epiphany", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Two parts contrast the Magi's true wisdom in seeking God with Herod's false wisdom in seeking himself. Prompt response, perseverance, worship and detachment are set against ambition and hypocrisy. The preacher applies the examples to his Christian hearers."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-h001", "label": "French original", "text": "SERMON SUR L'EPIPHANIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON ON THE EPIPHANY", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-e001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "C\u00f9m natus esset Jesus in Bethleem Jud\u00e6, in diebus Herodis Regis, ecce Magi ab Oriente venerunt Hierosolimam, dicentes: Ubi est qui natus est Rex Jud\u00e6orum? Vidimus enim stellam ejus in Oriente, & venimus adorare eum. Audiens autem Herodes Rex, turbatus est, & omnis Hierosolyma cum illo."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-e001", "label": "English", "text": "When Jesus had been born in Bethlehem of Juda, in the days of King Herod, behold, Magi came from the East to Jerusalem, saying: Where is he who has been born King of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the East, and have come to adore him. Hearing this, King Herod was troubled, and all Jerusalem with him.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-e002", "label": "French original", "text": "Jesus \u00e9tant n\u00e9 dans Bethl\u00e9em de Juda, au tems que regnoit Herodes, des Mages vinrent d'Orient \u00e0 J\u00e9rusalem, & ils demandoient, O\u00f9 est le Roi des Juifs, qui est nouvellement n\u00e9? car nous avons v\u00fb son \u00e9toile en Orient; & nous sommes venus l'adorer. Le Roi Herodes ayant appris cela, en fut troubl\u00e9, & toute la ville de J\u00e9rusalem avec lui. En saint Matthieu, chap. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-e002", "label": "English", "text": "Jesus having been born in Bethlehem of Juda, in the time when Herod reigned, Magi came from the East to Jerusalem, and asked: Where is the King of the Jews who has just been born? For we have seen his star in the East, and we have come to adore him. King Herod, having learned this, was troubled, and the whole city of Jerusalem with him. In Saint Matthew, chapter 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p001", "label": "French original", "text": "Voil\u00e0, chr\u00e9tienne Compagnie, l'accomplissement de la parole de Simeon, lorsque tenant entre ses bras l'enfant Jesus, il disoit \u00e0 Marie sa m\u00e8re: Cet enfant que vous voyez, sera la ruine & la r\u00e9surrection de plusieurs; Ecce positus est hic in ruinam & in resurrectionem multorum. Des Mages partis de l'Orient pour venir adorer ce divin Sauveur, ce sont ceux pour la r\u00e9surrection desquels il commence \u00e0 paro\u00eetre au monde. Et l'impie H\u00e9rodes troubl\u00e9 de sa venue & du seul bruit de sa naissance, nous marque ceux au contraire pour qui il doit \u00eatre une occasion de ruine. Voil\u00e0 l'effet de ce que le m\u00eame Fils de Dieu, apr\u00e8s le c\u00e9l\u00e8bre miracle de la gu\u00e9rison de l'aveugle-n\u00e9, dit \u00e0 ses disciples: In judicium veni in hunc mundum, ut qui non vident, videant; & qui vident, c\u00e6ci fiant. Je suis venu dans le monde, pour y exercer un jugement, en cons\u00e9quence duquel les aveugles voient, & ceux qui voient deviennent aveugles. C'est en ce jour que ce jugement s'accomplit \u00e0 la lettre. Les Mages au milieu des t\u00e9n\u00e8bres de la gentilit\u00e9, sont \u00e9clair\u00e9s des plus vives lumi\u00e8res de la grace. H\u00e9rodes & les Juifs avec lui, dans le centre de la vraie religion, sont frapp\u00e9s d'un aveuglement terrible. La cr\u00e8che de Jesus-Christ est le tribunal, o\u00f9 en qualit\u00e9 de souverain juge il prononce ces deux Arr\u00eats; & o\u00f9 par avance il peut dire: In judicium veni in hunc mundum, ut qui non vident, videant; & qui vident, c\u00e6ci fiant. Figurez-vous donc, Chr\u00e9tiens, ce Sauveur naissant, sous l'id\u00e9e que Jean-Baptiste son pr\u00e9curseur en concevoit, ayant d\u00e8s-aujourd'hui le van \u00e0 la main. Cujus ventilabrum in manu sua; c'est-\u00e0-dire, faisant d\u00e8s-aujourd'hui le discernement des hommes; pr\u00e9destinant les uns, r\u00e9prouvant les autres; appellant & \u00e9clairant ceux-ci, abandonnant & aveuglant ceux-l\u00e0; attirant des \u00e9trangers & des infid\u00e8les, rejettant les enfans & les h\u00e9ritiers du Royaume. Myst\u00e8re \u00e9tonnant, o\u00f9 nous devons avec respect adorer les conseils de Dieu. Myst\u00e8re imp\u00e9n\u00e9trable, qui ne nous est pas permis de sonder, & o\u00f9 je dois n\u00e9anmoins trouver de quoi vous instruire. Or pour cela, mes chers Auditeurs, je m'arr\u00eate aux deux premi\u00e8res vues qui se pr\u00e9sentent d'abord, & qui semblent partager notre Evangile. Nous y voyons d'une part les Mages, qui viennent chercher Jesus-Christ; & de l'autre, H\u00e9rodes qui conspire contre Jesus-Christ. C'est \u00e0 quoi je m'attache, & d'o\u00f9 je veux tirer deux grandes instructions qui vont faire la mati\u00e8re de ce discours, apr\u00e8s que, &c. Ave, Maria."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p001", "label": "English", "text": "Here, Christian congregation, is the fulfilment of Simeon's saying when, holding the child Jesus in his arms, he said to Mary his mother: This child whom you see will be the fall and resurrection of many; Behold, this one is set for the fall and resurrection of many. The Magi who set out from the East to adore this divine Saviour are those for whose resurrection he begins to appear in the world. And impious Herod, troubled at his coming and at the mere report of his birth, shows us, on the contrary, those for whom he is to be an occasion of ruin. Here is the effect of what the same Son of God said to his disciples after the celebrated miracle of healing the man born blind: For judgment I have come into this world, that those who do not see may see, and those who see may become blind. I have come into the world to exercise a judgment in consequence of which the blind see and those who see become blind. On this day that judgment is fulfilled to the letter. The Magi, amid the darkness of paganism, are enlightened by the brightest lights of grace. Herod and the Jews with him, at the centre of the true religion, are struck by a terrible blindness. Jesus Christ's manger is the tribunal where, as sovereign judge, he pronounces these two decrees, and where he can say in anticipation: For judgment I have come into this world, that those who do not see may see, and those who see may become blind. Picture, then, Christians, this newborn Saviour as John the Baptist, his precursor, conceived him, already today holding the winnowing fan in his hand: Whose winnowing fan is in his hand. That is, already today separating men, predestining some and reprobating others, calling and enlightening these, abandoning and blinding those, drawing strangers and unbelievers, rejecting the children and heirs of the kingdom. An astounding mystery, in which we must respectfully adore God's counsels; an impenetrable mystery which we are not permitted to fathom, and in which I must nevertheless find instruction for you. For this purpose, my dear hearers, I dwell on the first two aspects that present themselves and seem to divide our Gospel. On one side we see the Magi coming to seek Jesus Christ; on the other, Herod conspiring against Jesus Christ. These are what I take up, and from them I wish to draw two great lessons which will form the matter of this discourse, after, etc. Hail Mary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p002", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est des Juifs en particulier, que saint Paul a voulu parler, quand il a dit que nul des Princes de ce monde n'avoit connu la sagesse cach\u00e9e dans le myst\u00e8re d'un Dieu fait homme: Sapientiam in mysterio, qu\u00e6 abscondita est, quam nemo principum hujus s\u00e6culi cognovit. Et la raison qu'il en apporte, le fait bien voir, puisqu'il ajoute, que s'ils avoient connu cette sagesse, ils n'auroient jamais crucifi\u00e9 le Seigneur de la gloire; Si enim cognovissent, numquam Dominum glori\u00e6 crucifixissent. Par-l\u00e0, dis-je, il est \u00e9vident que les seuls Juifs \u00e9toient ceux que l'Ap\u00f4tre avoit en v\u00fbe, & dont il d\u00e9ploroit le sort: car il ne pouvoit d'ailleurs ignorer, qu'entre les gentils il y avoit eu des sages du monde, des hommes distingu\u00e9s selon le monde, des Mages qui sous la conduite de l'\u00e9toile, ou plut\u00f4t sous la conduite de Dieu m\u00eame, ayant cherch\u00e9 Jesus-Christ, & l'ayant ador\u00e9, \u00e9toient parvenus \u00e0 la connoissance de cette sagesse divine. Mais saint Paul dans la suite du m\u00eame passage, nous fait remarquer que les Juifs, qui n'avoient pas connu, & qui avoient eu le malheur de ne vouloir pas conno\u00eetre cette sagesse de Dieu, cach\u00e9e dans le myst\u00e8re de l'homme-Dieu, s'\u00e9toient piqu\u00e9s de conno\u00eetre & de suivre une sagesse toute oppos\u00e9e, s\u00e7avoir, la sagesse du si\u00e8cle. Sagesse r\u00e9prouv\u00e9e, & dont Dieu, disoit-il, avoit pris plaisir \u00e0 confondre la vanit\u00e9, jusqu'\u00e0 la convaincre de folie: Nonne Deus stultam fecit sapientiam hujus s\u00e6culi? Or il est encore certain, qu'entre ces Princes du monde, qui d\u00e8s le tems de saint Paul s'\u00e9toient ainsi aveugl\u00e9s dans le Juda\u00efsme, H\u00e9rodes par toute sorte de raisons a d\u00fb tenir le premier rang. Voil\u00e0 donc, mes chers Auditeurs, les deux id\u00e9es que je me propose, & o\u00f9 je trouve que doit se rapporter toute la morale du grand myst\u00e8re que nous c\u00e9l\u00e9brons; l'id\u00e9e de la vraie sagesse, & l'id\u00e9e de la fausse sagesse: l'id\u00e9e de la vraie sagesse, qui consiste \u00e0 chercher Dieu, & l'id\u00e9e de la fausse sagesse qui consiste \u00e0 se chercher soi-m\u00eame: l'id\u00e9e de la vraie sagesse, dont nous avons le mod\u00e8le dans l'exemple des Mages; & l'id\u00e9e de la fausse sagesse, que je d\u00e9couvre dans l'exemple d'H\u00e9rodes: comprenez, s'il vous pla\u00eet, ces deux pens\u00e9es. Qu'\u00e9toit-ce que les Mages dont nous honorons la m\u00e9moire? C'\u00e9toient les sages de la gentilit\u00e9, & tous les P\u00e8res conviennent qu'ils ont \u00e9t\u00e9 les pr\u00e9mices de notre vocation \u00e0 la foi. Il \u00e9toit donc naturel que Dieu nous donn\u00e2t dans eux un parfait mod\u00e8le de la sagesse chr\u00e9tienne, & c'est ce qu'il a pr\u00e9tendu, comme je vais vous le montrer dans la premi\u00e8re partie. Au contraire, qu'\u00e9toit-ce qu'H\u00e9rodes dans le Juda\u00efsme? un sage politique, un sage mondain, le plus infid\u00e8le de tous les hommes envers Dieu. Il \u00e9toit donc plus propre que tout autre \u00e0 nous faire comprendre le d\u00e9sordre de la fausse prudence, & c'est ce que vous verrez avec \u00e9tonnement & avec frayeur dans la seconde partie. Ainsi la solide sagesse des \u00e9lus & des vrais chr\u00e9tiens dans la conduite des Mages en cherchant le fils de Dieu, & l'aveugle sagesse des r\u00e9prouv\u00e9s & des impies dans la conduite d'H\u00e9rodes en pers\u00e9cutant le fils de Dieu: l'une qui nous fait conno\u00eetre les saintes voies par o\u00f9 nous devons marcher pour arriver au terme du salut; l'autre qui nous fait voir sensiblement les voies d'iniquit\u00e9 dont nous devons nous pr\u00e9server, & qui ne peuvent aboutir qu'\u00e0 la perdition; c'est tout mon dessein."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p002", "label": "English", "text": "It was of the Jews in particular that Saint Paul meant to speak when he said that none of the princes of this world had known the wisdom hidden in the mystery of God made man: Wisdom in a mystery, which is hidden, which none of the princes of this age has known. The reason he gives makes this plain, since he adds that, had they known this wisdom, they would never have crucified the Lord of glory: For if they had known, they would never have crucified the Lord of glory. From this, I say, it is evident that the Jews alone were those whom the Apostle had in view and whose fate he deplored; for, moreover, he could not have been unaware that among the gentiles there had been wise men of the world, men distinguished in the world's eyes, Magi who, under the star's guidance, or rather under God's own guidance, had sought and adored Jesus Christ and attained knowledge of this divine wisdom. But later in the same passage Saint Paul makes us observe that the Jews who had not known, and had had the misfortune of refusing to know, this wisdom of God hidden in the mystery of the God-man had prided themselves on knowing and following a wholly contrary wisdom, namely, the wisdom of the age. A reprobate wisdom whose vanity God, he said, had been pleased to confound, even to convicting it of folly: Has not God made foolish the wisdom of this age? It is also certain that among those princes of the world who, from Saint Paul's time, had thus blinded themselves in Judaism, Herod must for every reason have held first place. Here, then, my dear hearers, are the two ideas I propose, to which I find that all the moral teaching of the great mystery we celebrate must be referred: true wisdom and false wisdom; true wisdom, which consists in seeking God, and false wisdom, which consists in seeking oneself; true wisdom, whose model we have in the Magi's example, and false wisdom, which I discover in Herod's example. Grasp, if you please, these two thoughts. What were the Magi whose memory we honour? They were the wise men of paganism, and all the Fathers agree that they were the first fruits of our calling to faith. It was therefore natural that God should give us in them a perfect model of Christian wisdom; this was his intention, as I shall show you in the first part. What, on the contrary, was Herod within Judaism? A worldly statesman, a man wise in worldly affairs, the most unfaithful of all men towards God. He was therefore better suited than any other to make us understand the disorder of false prudence, as you will see with astonishment and fear in the second part. Thus, the sound wisdom of the elect and of true Christians in the Magi's conduct as they seek the Son of God, and the blind wisdom of the reprobate and the impious in Herod's conduct as he persecutes the Son of God: the one acquaints us with the holy paths we must walk to reach salvation; the other makes us plainly see the paths of iniquity from which we must guard ourselves and which can end only in perdition. This is my whole design.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-h002", "label": "French original", "text": "I. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-h002", "label": "English", "text": "FIRST PART", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p003", "label": "French original", "text": "Non, chr\u00e9tiens, jamais la providence n'a donn\u00e9 au monde un mod\u00e8le plus achev\u00e9 de cette v\u00e9ritable sagesse, qui consiste \u00e0 chercher & \u00e0 trouver Dieu, que celui qu'elle nous propose dans la personne des Mages. Examinons tous les caract\u00e8res de leur foi, dans son commencement, dans son progr\u00e8s, & dans sa perfection. Dans son commencement, c'est-\u00e0-dire, dans la promptitude avec laquelle ils se d\u00e9terminent \u00e0 suivre la vocation divine qui leur est marqu\u00e9e par l'\u00e9toile; & dans le courage qu'ils font paro\u00eetre en abandonnant tout, pour ob\u00e9ir \u00e0 l'ordre de Dieu. Dans son progr\u00e8s, c'est-\u00e0-dire, dans la constance qu'ils t\u00e9moignent, lorsque l'\u00e9toile vient \u00e0 s'\u00e9clipser, s'informant avec soin du lieu o\u00f9 est n\u00e9 l'enfant qu'ils cherchent, le reconnoissant pour Roi des Juifs jusqu'au milieu de J\u00e9rusalem, & m\u00eame au milieu de la Cour d'H\u00e9rodes, & d\u00e9clarant avec une sainte libert\u00e9, qu'ils sont venus pour lui rendre leurs hommages. Dans sa perfection, je veux dire, dans l'admirable discernement qu'ils font de Jesus-Christ, ne se scandalisant point de l'\u00e9tat pauvre & humble o\u00f9 ils le trouvent; au contraire, concluant de-l\u00e0 m\u00eame qu'il est leur Sauveur, l'adorant en esprit & en v\u00e9rit\u00e9; & par les myst\u00e9rieux pr\u00e9sens qu'ils lui offrent, lui donnant autant de preuves de leur parfait d\u00e9vouement & de leur religion. Cherchez-vous Dieu de bonne foi, mes chers Auditeurs, & voulez-vous s\u00e7avoir comment on le trouve? en voil\u00e0 toute la science & tout le secret. Ne disons plus apr\u00e8s cela, que les voies de Dieu sont des voies obscures & inconnues: elles nous sont ici r\u00e9v\u00e9l\u00e9es trop clairement & trop distinctement, pour avoir droit de tenir d\u00e9sormais un tel langage. Ne nous plaignons plus des difficult\u00e9s qui s'y rencontrent, & des \u00e9garemens qui sont si ordinaires: apr\u00e8s l'exemple de ces Mages, qui n'y ont march\u00e9 avant nous que pour nous y servir de guides, nos plaintes seroient \u00e9galement vaines & injustes. Suppos\u00e9 l'excellent mod\u00e8le que Dieu nous met devant les yeux, nos erreurs, en mati\u00e8re de salut, ne peuvent plus \u00eatre excusables; & si malgr\u00e9 tant de lumi\u00e8res, nous sommes assez malheureux pour ne pas trouver Dieu & pour nous perdre, c'est \u00e0 notre infid\u00e9lit\u00e9, c'est \u00e0 notre l\u00e2chet\u00e9, c'est \u00e0 notre inconstance, c'est \u00e0 nos respects humains, c'est \u00e0 notre orgueil, c'est \u00e0 notre avarice & \u00e0 un attachement opini\u00e2tre aux biens de ce monde, c'est \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames enfin, que nous devons imputer notre malheur. Attention, Chr\u00e9tiens, ceci me fournit pour vous des le\u00e7ons bien importantes."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p003", "label": "English", "text": "No, Christians, providence has never given the world a more finished model of that true wisdom which consists in seeking and finding God than the one it presents to us in the Magi. Let us examine every mark of their faith in its beginning, progress and perfection. In its beginning: the readiness with which they resolve to follow the divine calling indicated by the star, and the courage they show in abandoning everything to obey God's command. In its progress: the constancy they show when the star disappears, carefully inquiring where the child they seek has been born, acknowledging him as King of the Jews in the very midst of Jerusalem and even at Herod's court, and declaring with holy freedom that they have come to pay him homage. In its perfection: I mean the admirable discernment with which they recognize Jesus Christ, taking no scandal at the poor and humble state in which they find him, but instead concluding from that very state that he is their Saviour, adoring him in spirit and truth, and giving him, through the mysterious gifts they offer, so many proofs of their perfect devotion and religious worship. Do you seek God in good faith, my dear hearers, and wish to know how he is found? There is the whole knowledge and secret. Let us no longer say after this that God's paths are dark and unknown; here they are revealed too clearly and distinctly for us to have any right to speak so henceforth. Let us no longer complain of the difficulties encountered in them and the wanderings that are so common. After the example of these Magi, who walked them before us only to serve as our guides, our complaints would be equally vain and unjust. Given the excellent model God sets before our eyes, our errors in matters of salvation can no longer be excusable. If, despite so much light, we are unfortunate enough not to find God and to lose ourselves, we must blame our misfortune on our unfaithfulness, our cowardice, our inconstancy, our fear of human opinion, our pride, our avarice and stubborn attachment to this world's goods\u2014in short, on ourselves. Pay attention, Christians; this gives me lessons of great importance for you.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p004", "label": "French original", "text": "Promptitude \u00e0 suivre la vocation du ciel: ce fut le premier effet de la foi des Mages, & le premier trait de cette haute sagesse, qui, par un changement divin, d'infid\u00e8les qu'ils \u00e9toient, les mit en \u00e9tat de trouver le Dieu Sauveur. D\u00e8s qu'ils virent son \u00e9toile, ils partirent pour venir \u00e0 lui: Vidimus stellam ejus, & venimus. Ils ne balancerent point, ils ne d\u00e9lib\u00e9r\u00e8rent point, ils ne s'arr\u00eat\u00e8rent point, ni \u00e0 former de vains projets, ni \u00e0 prendre de longues mesures. Attentifs \u00e0 l'\u00e9toile qui les \u00e9clairoit, & uniquement appliqu\u00e9s \u00e0 chercher celui qu'elle leur annon\u00e7oit, ils h\u00e2terent leur marche: pourquoi? parce qu'ils \u00e9toient d\u00e9j\u00e0 remplis de cet esprit & de cette sagesse surnaturelle, qui conduit les \u00e9lus de Dieu. Or, comme remarque saint Chrysost\u00f4me, chercher Dieu de la mani\u00e8re efficace & solide, dont le cherche une ame fid\u00e8le, ce n'est plus raisonner, ni d\u00e9lib\u00e9rer, c'est ex\u00e9cuter & agir: d'o\u00f9 il s'ensuit, dit ce saint Docteur, que quand on d\u00e9lib\u00e8re, quand on consulte & qu'on raisonne, quelque intention qu'on ait de trouver Dieu, le cherchant toujours, ou pour mieux dire, se flattant toujours de le chercher, on ne le trouve jamais. Voil\u00e0 sur quoi fut fond\u00e9e la promptitude des Mages. Ils virent l'\u00e9toile; & anim\u00e9s d'une foi vive, press\u00e9s d'un d\u00e9sir ardent d'arriver au terme o\u00f9 l'\u00e9toile les appelloit, ils n'\u00e9cout\u00e8rent rien de tout ce qui pouvoit les retenir: Vidimus, & venimus: nous avons v\u00fb, & nous sommes venus. Paroles, ajoute saint Chrysost\u00f4me, qui expriment admirablement la force & l'op\u00e9ration de la grace, puisqu'il est vrai que dans l'affaire du salut, tout d\u00e9pend de certaines v\u00fbes, \u00e0 quoi la grace est attach\u00e9e, ou plut\u00f4t en quoi consiste la grace m\u00eame. Ambulate dum lucem habetis: marchez, disoit le Fils de Dieu, pendant que vous avez la lumi\u00e8re. Or c'est ce que font \u00e0 la lettre ces sages pr\u00e9destin\u00e9s de la gentilit\u00e9. Ils marchent, parce qu'une lumi\u00e8re secrette p\u00e9n\u00e9tre int\u00e9rieurement, & touche leurs c\u0153urs, tandis qu'un nouvel astre brille ext\u00e9rieurement \u00e0 leurs yeux. Ils marchent parce que cette double lumi\u00e8re leur fait conno\u00eetre la naissance d'un Dieu & d'un Sauveur: d'un Dieu, qui ne se contentant plus d'\u00eatre connu dans la Jud\u00e9e, veut recevoir les hommages de toutes les Nations: d'un Sauveur qui les a choisis, & qui veut commencer par eux \u00e0 montrer qu'il n'est pas seulement venu pour Isra\u00ebl, mais pour tous les peuples de la terre. Ils marchent, & l'extr\u00eame diligence dont ils usent, est autant une preuve de leur sagesse, que de l'activit\u00e9 de leur z\u00e9le; ils s'empressent de chercher leur salut, en cherchant celui qui en est l'auteur, & qui en doit \u00eatre bient\u00f4t le consommateur; Vidimus & venimus."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p004", "label": "English", "text": "Readiness to follow heaven's calling: this was the first effect of the Magi's faith and the first feature of that lofty wisdom which, through a divine change, enabled them, unbelievers as they had been, to find God the Saviour. As soon as they saw his star, they set out to come to him: We have seen his star and have come. They did not hesitate or deliberate; they did not stop either to form vain projects or to make lengthy preparations. Attentive to the star that enlightened them, intent only on seeking him whom it announced, they hastened their steps. Why? Because they were already filled with that spirit and supernatural wisdom which guides God's elect. Now, as Saint Chrysostom observes, to seek God in the effective and sound manner of a faithful soul is no longer to reason or deliberate, but to carry out and act. Hence, says this holy Doctor, when one deliberates, consults and reasons, whatever one's intention of finding God, always seeking him\u2014or rather, always flattering oneself that one seeks him\u2014one never finds him. On this was founded the Magi's readiness. They saw the star; animated by living faith, urged by an ardent desire to reach the goal to which it called them, they listened to nothing that could hold them back: We have seen and have come. Words, adds Saint Chrysostom, which admirably express the power and working of grace, since in the business of salvation everything depends on certain insights to which grace is attached, or rather in which grace itself consists. Walk while you have the light, said the Son of God. This is precisely what those predestined wise men of paganism do. They walk because a secret light penetrates within and touches their hearts, while a new star shines outwardly before their eyes. They walk because this double light makes known to them the birth of God and of a Saviour: of God, who, no longer content to be known in Judaea, wishes to receive the homage of all nations; of a Saviour who has chosen them and wishes to begin through them to show that he has come not only for Israel but for all peoples of the earth. They walk, and their extreme diligence is as much proof of their wisdom as of their active zeal; they hasten to seek their salvation by seeking him who is its author and will soon be its consummator: We have seen and have come.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p005", "label": "French original", "text": "Ainsi agissent les Mages: mais nous comparant avec eux, mes chers Auditeurs, quel est ici le premier & le grand d\u00e9sordre que nous avons \u00e0 nous reprocher? Ne sont-ce pas les retardemens \u00e9ternels, les retardemens affect\u00e9s, les retardemens t\u00e9m\u00e9raires & insens\u00e9s, que nous apportons tous les jours \u00e0 l'ex\u00e9cution des ordres de Dieu, & \u00e0 ce que la grace nous inspire? Peut-\u00eatre y a-t-il des ann\u00e9es enti\u00e8res que Dieu nous appelle, & que nous lui r\u00e9sistons. Elev\u00e9s dans le Christianisme, nous avons pour marcher plus de lumi\u00e8res que les Mages: notre foi est plus \u00e9tablie, plus form\u00e9e, plus d\u00e9velopp\u00e9e; nous connoissons beaucoup plus distinctement qu'eux les volont\u00e9s & les desseins de Dieu sur nous. Pour une \u00e9toile qu'ils voyoient, mille raisons nous convainquent, mille exemples nous confondent, toutes les Ecritures nous parlent; tant de Docteurs nous instruisent, tant de Pr\u00e9dicateurs nous pressent, nous sollicitent, nous exhortent; mais en vain, parce que nous diff\u00e9rons toujours. Ne dirons-nous jamais comme les Mages: Vidimus, & venimus; nous avons v\u00fb, & nous sommes venus? Oui, j'ai v\u00fb, ou je vois aujourd'hui ce que Dieu demande de moi, & c'est pour cela, que d\u00e8s aujourd'hui je m'engage, & je commence \u00e0 l'accomplir: car, que s\u00e7ai-je si je le pourrai demain? que s\u00e7ai-je si je serai demain aussi touch\u00e9 de la v\u00fbe que Dieu m'en donne? que s\u00e7ai-je si ce rayon de grace fera dans mon ame la m\u00eame impression? que s\u00e7ai-je si la lumi\u00e8re de ma foi, apr\u00e8s tant de d\u00e9lais qui l'affoiblissent peu \u00e0 peu, ne viendra point tout-\u00e0-fait \u00e0 s'\u00e9teindre? que s\u00e7ai-je, si mettant par-l\u00e0 le comble \u00e0 mes iniquit\u00e9s, je ne tomberai point dans cet aveuglement fatal, dont Dieu punit les c\u0153urs rebelles; & si l'habitude que je me fais de temporiser, & de ne jamais rien conclure, ne sera point enfin la source de ma r\u00e9probation? Ah! suivons cette lumi\u00e8re favorable, qui luit encore pour nous. Marchons, de peur que les t\u00e9n\u00e8bres ne nous surprennent, & ne remettons point \u00e0 un autre tems ce qui doit avoir la pr\u00e9f\u00e9rence dans tous les tems, ou plut\u00f4t ce qui doit \u00eatre l'affaire de tous les tems. Dieu m'\u00e9claire maintenant, & je ne puis s\u00e7avoir s'il m'\u00e9clairera demain, ni s'il y aura m\u00eame un lendemain pour moi. Mais quand je le s\u00e7aurois, devrois-je & voudrois-je me pr\u00e9valoir contre lui de sa patience, & abuser de sa mis\u00e9ricorde pour l'offenser toujours avec plus d'obstination? Promptitude \u00e0 suivre la voix de Dieu d\u00e8s que Dieu nous la fait entendre, c'est la premi\u00e8re le\u00e7on que nous fait l'exemple des Mages; &, courage \u00e0 surmonter pour cela toutes les difficult\u00e9s qui se pr\u00e9sentent, c'est la seconde."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p005", "label": "English", "text": "Thus the Magi act. But comparing ourselves with them, my dear hearers, what is the first and great disorder with which we must reproach ourselves here? Is it not the endless delays, deliberate delays, rash and senseless delays, which we introduce every day into carrying out God's commands and what grace inspires in us? Perhaps for whole years God has been calling us and we have resisted him. Brought up in Christianity, we have more light for our journey than the Magi. Our faith is more established, more formed, more developed; we know far more distinctly than they God's will and designs for us. Against the one star they saw, a thousand reasons convince us, a thousand examples put us to shame, all the Scriptures speak to us; so many Doctors instruct us, so many preachers press, entreat and exhort us\u2014but in vain, because we always defer. Shall we never say with the Magi: We have seen and have come? Yes, I have seen, or see today, what God asks of me, and therefore from today I commit myself and begin to accomplish it. For what do I know of whether I shall be able tomorrow? What do I know of whether tomorrow I shall be as moved by the insight God gives me? Whether this ray of grace will make the same impression on my soul? Whether the light of my faith, after so many delays that weaken it little by little, will not finally go out altogether? Whether, thus filling up the measure of my iniquities, I shall fall into that fatal blindness with which God punishes rebellious hearts; and whether the habit I acquire of temporizing and never resolving anything will not at last become the source of my reprobation? Ah, let us follow this favourable light which still shines for us. Let us walk, lest darkness overtake us, and not postpone to another time what must take precedence at all times, or rather be the business of all times. God enlightens me now, and I cannot know whether he will enlighten me tomorrow, or even whether there will be a tomorrow for me. But even if I knew, should I, would I, turn his patience against him and abuse his mercy to offend him ever more obstinately? Readiness to follow God's voice as soon as he makes us hear it is the first lesson the Magi's example gives us; courage to overcome all the difficulties that arise in doing so is the second.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p006", "label": "French original", "text": "Car pour suivre l'\u00e9toile, & pour r\u00e9pondre \u00e0 la vocation du Ciel, les Mages aussi-bien qu'Abraham, furent oblig\u00e9s d'abandonner leur pays, leurs maisons, leurs familles; & selon la commune tradition, leurs Royaumes & leurs Etats. Ils d\u00fbrent faire d\u00e8s-lors ce que saint Pierre & les Ap\u00f4tres firent dans la suite des ann\u00e9es; c'est-\u00e0-dire, ils d\u00fbrent quitter tout pour Jesus-Christ, & ils eurent droit les premiers de dire comme saint Pierre, & m\u00eame dans un sens, avec plus de m\u00e9rite que saint Pierre: Ecce nos reliquimus omnia, & secuti sumus te. Or leur courage \u00e0 prendre une telle r\u00e9solution, leur d\u00e9tachement h\u00e9ro\u00efque, en s'\u00e9loignant de ce qu'ils avoient de plus cher, en essuyant les fatigues d'un long voyage, & en sacrifiant de la sorte leur repos, c'est ce que je puis consid\u00e9rer comme une seconde d\u00e9marche de leur foi naissante, & comme une nouvelle preuve de cette \u00e9minente sagesse, qui leur fit trouver Jesus-Christ. Car il est ais\u00e9, dit saint Chrysost\u00f4me, de suivre le mouvement de la grace, quand il n'en co\u00fbte rien \u00e0 la nature, & d'ob\u00e9ir \u00e0 l'inspiration de Dieu, quand il ne s'y rencontre nul obstacle de la part du monde. Le m\u00e9rite de la foi & de la sagesse chr\u00e9tienne, est de renoncer m\u00eame, quand il le faut, \u00e0 ce qu'on aime plus tendrement, de quitter ses habitudes, de rompre ses liens, de se priver des commodit\u00e9s & des douceurs de la vie, & de se faire certaines violences, sans lesquelles on ne parvient point au Royaume de Dieu. C'est alors, poursuit saint Chrysost\u00f4me, que la prudence de la chair est encore bien plus subtile & plu artificieuse, pour nous d\u00e9tourner de la voie o\u00f9 Dieu veut nous conduire. C'est alors que prenant le parti de notre amour propre, elle t\u00e2che \u00e0 nous persuader, qu'il y a de l'indiscr\u00e9tion dans un renoncement si g\u00e9n\u00e9ral & si absolu. C'est alors que tirant avantage de notre foiblesse, elle nous repr\u00e9sente ce parfait d\u00e9tachement, comme une entreprise au-dessus de nos forces; & que nous sommes incapables de soutenir. En un mot, c'est alors qu'\u00e9touffant les saints d\u00e9sirs, que Dieu par les vives lumi\u00e8res de sa grace avoit excit\u00e9s dans nos c\u0153urs, elle nous rend l\u00e2ches, froids, languissans dans une affaire qui demande toute notre ardeur & tout notre z\u00e9le. S'il s'agissoit d'un int\u00e9r\u00eat du monde cette pr\u00e9tendue impossibilit\u00e9, que la prudence humaine nous oppose, ne nous feroit pas balancer un moment. Pour une fortune temporelle, & pour satisfaire notre ambition, nous serions pr\u00eats \u00e0 tout, nous oserions tout, nous nous exposerions \u00e0 tout: mais parce qu'il s'agit de l'\u0153uvre de Dieu & de notre conversion, tout nous effraie, & tout nous devient impraticable. Or c'est cette l\u00e2chet\u00e9 que la foi doit combattre en nous si nous voulons imiter l'exemple des Mages; & par-l\u00e0 m\u00eame encore une fois, nous devons juger si la voie o\u00f9 nous marchons est la voie de Dieu. Car l'illusion la plus grossi\u00e8re, est de nous flatter d'avoir trouv\u00e9 cette voie de Dieu, tandis qu'il ne nous en co\u00fbte nul effort. Il y a, pour y entrer & pour y demeurer, des sacrifices \u00e0 faire; & nulle voie n'est s\u00fbre pour nous, qu'autant que nous les faisons \u00e0 Dieu ces sacrifices, ou que nous y sommes efficacement & sinc\u00e9rement dispos\u00e9s. Revenons \u00e0 notre mod\u00e8le, & voyons le progr\u00e8s de la foi des Mages."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p006", "label": "English", "text": "For to follow the star and answer heaven's calling, the Magi, like Abraham, were obliged to abandon their country, houses and families, and, according to the common tradition, their kingdoms and dominions. They had to do then what Saint Peter and the Apostles did in later years: leave everything for Jesus Christ. They were the first entitled to say with Saint Peter, and in one sense with even greater merit: Behold, we have left all things and followed you. Their courage in taking such a resolution, their heroic detachment in leaving what was dearest to them, enduring a long journey's weariness and thus sacrificing their rest, is what I may consider a second step of their newborn faith and a new proof of the eminent wisdom that made them find Jesus Christ. For it is easy, says Saint Chrysostom, to follow grace's movement when nature pays no cost, and to obey God's inspiration when no obstacle arises from the world. The merit of faith and Christian wisdom is to renounce, when necessary, even what one loves most tenderly, abandon one's habits, break one's bonds, deprive oneself of life's comforts and pleasures, and do oneself certain violences without which one does not reach God's kingdom. Then, Saint Chrysostom continues, carnal prudence becomes still more subtle and artful in turning us from the path along which God wishes to guide us. Then, taking our self-love's side, it tries to persuade us that such general and absolute renunciation is indiscreet. Then, exploiting our weakness, it presents this perfect detachment as an undertaking beyond our strength, which we are incapable of sustaining. In short, then it stifles the holy desires which God, through the bright lights of his grace, had awakened in our hearts, and makes us cowardly, cold and languid in a matter demanding all our ardour and zeal. If a worldly interest were at stake, this supposed impossibility which human prudence sets against us would not make us hesitate for a moment. For temporal fortune and to satisfy our ambition, we would be ready for everything, dare everything and expose ourselves to everything. But because God's work and our conversion are at stake, everything frightens us and becomes impracticable. This cowardice is what faith must combat in us if we wish to imitate the Magi; and by this, once again, we must judge whether the way we walk is God's way. For the grossest illusion is to flatter ourselves that we have found God's way while it costs us no effort. To enter and remain in it, sacrifices must be made; no way is safe for us except insofar as we make these sacrifices to God, or are effectively and sincerely disposed to do so. Let us return to our model and see the progress of the Magi's faith.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p007", "label": "French original", "text": "Ils arrivent \u00e0 J\u00e9rusalem; & l'\u00e9toile qui jusques-l\u00e0 leur avoit servi de guide, par une conduite de Dieu toute particuli\u00e8re, vient tout \u00e0 coup \u00e0 disparo\u00eetre. Que ne pouvoient-ils pas penser? que ne devoient-ils pas craindre? Leur foi n'en dut-elle pas \u00eatre \u00e9branl\u00e9e, troubl\u00e9e, d\u00e9concert\u00e9e? Mais non, Chr\u00e9tiens; la tentation la plus dangereuse, l'\u00e9preuve la plus subite & la moins attendue, le pr\u00e9texte le plus sp\u00e9cieux qu'elle leur fournit pour penser \u00e0 leur retour, rien ne les fait changer de r\u00e9solution. A quelque prix que ce soit, ils veulent trouver le Dieu qu'ils cherchent. Ils ont v\u00fb son \u00e9toile, & ils ont senti l'onction de sa grace, c'est assez. Si cette \u00e9toile ne paro\u00eet plus, c'est un secret de la Providence qu'ils adorent, mais dont ils n'ont garde de se faire un sujet de scandale; c'est une occasion que Dieu leur donne de lui marquer leur fid\u00e9lit\u00e9, & ils comprennent qu'il faut en de pareilles conjonctures se soutenir par la constance. Sans donc se troubler, sans se rebuter, ils esp\u00e9reront, aussi-bien qu'Abraham, contre l'esp\u00e9rance m\u00eame; ils continueront leur marche, s\u00fbrs du Dieu qui les a appell\u00e9s, & comptant qu'au d\u00e9faut de l'\u00e9toile, il leur tracera lui-m\u00eame le chemin."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p007", "label": "English", "text": "They arrive in Jerusalem; and the star which until then had guided them suddenly disappears through God's very particular providence. What might they not think? What must they not fear? Should not their faith have been shaken, troubled, disconcerted? No, Christians: the most dangerous temptation, the most sudden and unexpected trial, the most plausible pretext it gives them for thinking of returning\u2014nothing changes their resolution. At whatever price, they wish to find the God they seek. They have seen his star and felt the anointing of his grace; that is enough. If the star no longer appears, this is a secret of providence which they adore, but which they are far from making an occasion of scandal; it is an opportunity God gives them to show their fidelity, and they understand that in such circumstances they must sustain themselves by constancy. Without becoming troubled or discouraged, they will therefore hope, like Abraham, against hope itself; they will continue their journey, sure of the God who called them, counting on him to mark out their path himself when the star is lacking.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p008", "label": "French original", "text": "Or c'est en cela que paro\u00eet le don de sagesse, d'intelligence, de conseil, dont ils sont remplis; voil\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs, comment notre Dieu tous les jours en use avec nous. Apr\u00e8s nous avoir attir\u00e9s \u00e0 son service, & nous y avoir engag\u00e9s, il retire pour un tems certaines graces sensibles, dont il nous avoit d'abord pr\u00e9venus. Nous ne sentons plus ces touches secrettes qui nous rendoient son joug aimable, & qui nous faisoient courir comme David, avec une sainte all\u00e9gresse dans la voie de ses commandemens. Ainsi d\u00e9laiss\u00e9s au milieu de notre course, & pour ainsi dire, abandonn\u00e9s \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames, nous tombons dans des \u00e9tats d'obscurit\u00e9, de t\u00e9n\u00e8bres, de s\u00e9cheresse, de d\u00e9go\u00fbt; & alors non-seulement Dieu nous \u00e9prouve, mais il veut que nous-m\u00eames nous nous \u00e9prouvions. Car si ces graces sensibles nous \u00e9toient toujours pr\u00e9sentes, si nous ne perdions jamais de v\u00fbe cette \u00e9toile lumineuse qui fut le premier attrait de notre conversion, quoique nous fissions pour Dieu, nous ne pourrions ni r\u00e9pondre de nous \u00e0 Dieu, ni dans le sens que je l'entends, nous assurer de nous-m\u00eames; c'est-\u00e0-dire, notre ferveur dans cet \u00e9tat nous devroit \u00eatre suspecte; la sensibilit\u00e9 & l'abondance des consolations divines nous donneroit, ou devroit nous donner une d\u00e9fiance raisonnable de notre vertu: au moins est-il vrai que notre foi n'auroit pas cette fermet\u00e9 qu'elle doit avoir, pour \u00eatre une foi parfaite & digne de Dieu. Il faut donc qu'elle soit \u00e9prouv\u00e9e; & par o\u00f9? par ces d\u00e9la\u00efssemens, & ces privations si ordinaires aux ames les plus justes: & si nous ne sommes pas encore assez forts pour dire \u00e0 Dieu ce que lui disoit le Proph\u00e8te Royal: Proba me, Domine; \u00e9prouvez-moi, Seigneur, il faut qu'\u00e0 l'exemple des Mages nous soyons assez saintement dispos\u00e9s, pour pers\u00e9v\u00e9rer dans les \u00e9preuves o\u00f9 il lui pla\u00eet de nous mettre. Il faut que le souvenir des lumi\u00e8res dont nous avons \u00e9t\u00e9 touch\u00e9s, nous tienne lieu de ces lumi\u00e8res m\u00eames, quand Dieu vient \u00e0 nous les \u00f4ter, & qu'il nous suffise de pouvoir dire: Vidimus stellam ejus; je ne vois plus ce qui m'excitoit autrefois, & ce qui m'attachoit \u00e0 Dieu, mais je l'ai v\u00fb, mais j'en ai connu la v\u00e9rit\u00e9 & la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9, mais j'en ai \u00e9t\u00e9 persuad\u00e9. Or tout ce que j'ai v\u00fb subsiste encore; & puisqu'il subsiste encore, qu'il subsistera toujours, & qu'il aura toujours la m\u00eame force, pourquoi ne fera-t-il pas toujours sur moi la m\u00eame impression, & ne me servira-t-il pas toujours de motif pour m'animer, & de r\u00e8gle pour me conduire? Raisonner de la sorte, & ind\u00e9pendamment des go\u00fbts & des consolations int\u00e9rieures, tenir toujours la m\u00eame route, & agir de la m\u00eame fa\u00e7on; c'est-l\u00e0, Chr\u00e9tiens, que je reconnois la sagesse de l'Evangile, & ce que nous ne pouvons assez admirer dans les Mages."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p008", "label": "English", "text": "Here appears the gift of wisdom, understanding and counsel with which they are filled. This, my dear hearers, is how our God deals with us every day. After drawing us to his service and engaging us in it, he withdraws for a time certain sensible graces with which he had first favoured us. We no longer feel those secret touches that made his yoke lovable and made us run, like David, with holy gladness along the way of his commandments. Thus forsaken in mid-course and, so to speak, abandoned to ourselves, we fall into states of obscurity, darkness, dryness and distaste. Then God not only tests us; he wishes us to test ourselves. For if these sensible graces were always present, if we never lost sight of that luminous star which first drew us to conversion, whatever we did for God, we could neither answer to God for ourselves nor, in the sense I mean, be sure of ourselves. Our fervour in this state ought to be suspect to us; the felt abundance of divine consolations would, or ought to, give us a reasonable distrust of our virtue. At least it is true that our faith would lack the firmness it must have to be perfect and worthy of God. It must therefore be tested; and how? By those abandonments and deprivations so common to the most just souls. If we are not yet strong enough to say to God what the Royal Prophet said, Try me, Lord, we must, following the Magi's example, be sufficiently disposed in holiness to persevere in the trials in which he is pleased to place us. The memory of the lights that touched us must stand for those lights themselves when God takes them away; it must suffice to be able to say: We have seen his star. I no longer see what once stirred me and attached me to God, but I have seen it; I have known its truth and necessity; I have been convinced of it. Everything I have seen still subsists; since it still subsists, will always subsist and will always have the same force, why should it not always make the same impression on me and always serve as a motive to animate me and a rule to guide me? To reason thus, and, independently of inward relish and consolation, always keep the same course and act in the same way: there, Christians, I recognize Gospel wisdom, and what we cannot sufficiently admire in the Magi.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p009", "label": "French original", "text": "Cependant que font-ils pour suppl\u00e9er \u00e0 l'\u00e9toile qu'ils ne voient plus? ils se servent des moyens naturels que leur fournit la providence. Ils s\u00e7avent que le Dieu qu'ils cherchent, se pla\u00eet en effet \u00e0 \u00eatre cherch\u00e9, & que c'est \u00e0 ceux qui le cherchent qu'il se d\u00e9couvre plus volontiers. C'est pour cela qu'ils s'informent exactement du lieu de sa naissance: c'est pour cela qu'ils ont recours aux Pr\u00eatres & aux Docteurs de la loi: comme \u00e0 ceux qu'ils supposent plus intelligens, & plus capables par leur caract\u00e8re de les instruire: c'est pour cela qu'ils parlent, qu'ils consultent, qu'ils ne se donnent aucun repos. Autre preuve de leur sagesse, dont il faut que nous profitions. Car en quelque \u00e9tat d'aveuglement & d'obscurit\u00e9 que je tombe, en quelque ignorance des voies de Dieu que je puisse \u00eatre, en quelque d\u00e9sordre m\u00eame que f\u00fbt ma foi, si je cherche Dieu dans la simplicit\u00e9 du c\u0153ur, il est s\u00fbr que je le trouverai: c'est lui-m\u00eame qui me l'a dit, & sa parole y est expresse: In simplicitate cordis qu\u00e6rite illum, quoniam invenitur ab iis qui non tentant illum: c'est-\u00e0-dire, si je le cherche sinc\u00e9rement & avec une intention pure & droite, si je le cherche avec humilit\u00e9, si je le cherche avec confiance: si je le cherche avec pers\u00e9v\u00e9rance, il est s\u00fbr que je ne serai point confondu, Qui sustinent te non confundentur; & qu'il ne me manquera pas, Non dereliquisti qu\u00e6rentes te. Il est s\u00fbr que mon ame, en le cherchant, vivra de la vie des justes; Qu\u00e6rite Deum, & vivet anima vestra. Il est s\u00fbr qu'\u00e0 mesure que je le chercherai, je m'affermirai dans la pratique du bien & dans l'horreur du vice; Qu\u00e6rite Dominum, & confirmamini. Oracles de l'Ecriture, dont il ne m'est pas permis de douter. Or est-il rien de plus propre \u00e0 m'encourager dans le soin de chercher Dieu & d'\u00e9tudier les voies de mon salut? Vous me direz que vous n'avez point assez pour cela de p\u00e9n\u00e9tration, & que vos lumi\u00e8res sont trop foibles. Je le veux, mon cher Auditeur, mais vous avez, aussi-bien que les Mages, un moyen facile, pour \u00e9claircir tous vos doutes, & pour vous tirer de l'incertitude o\u00f9 vous pouvez \u00eatre. Il y a dans l'Eglise de Dieu des Docteurs & des Pr\u00eatres, comme il y en avoit alors. Il y a des hommes \u00e9tablis pour vous conduire, & qu'il ne tient qu'\u00e0 vous d'\u00e9couter. Interrogez-les comme vos P\u00e8res, & ils vous diront ce que vous avez \u00e0 faire: Interroga Patrem tuum, & annuntiabit tibi; majores tuos, & dicent tibi. Allez \u00e0 eux comme aux Ministres du Seigneur; leurs l\u00e8vres d\u00e9positaires de la science, vous enseigneront la science des sciences, qui est celle de trouver Dieu. Pouvez-vous l'ignorer avec cela, & avec cela pouvez-vous m\u00eame vous y tromper, sans vous rendre absolument inexcusables?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p009", "label": "English", "text": "Meanwhile, what do they do to make up for the star they no longer see? They use the natural means providence supplies. They know that the God they seek does indeed delight in being sought and reveals himself more willingly to those who seek him. Thus they inquire exactly into the place of his birth; thus they turn to the priests and Doctors of the law as those whom they suppose more understanding and, by their office, better able to instruct them; thus they speak, consult and give themselves no rest. Another proof of their wisdom from which we must profit. For into whatever state of blindness and obscurity I fall, in whatever ignorance of God's ways I may be, even however disordered my faith might be, if I seek God in simplicity of heart, I shall surely find him. He himself has told me so, and his word is explicit: Seek him in simplicity of heart, for he is found by those who do not put him to the test. That is, if I seek him sincerely with pure and upright intention, with humility, confidence and perseverance, I shall surely not be put to shame: Those who wait for you shall not be confounded; and he will not fail me: You have not forsaken those who seek you. My soul, seeking him, will surely live the life of the just: Seek God, and your soul shall live. As I seek him, I shall surely grow firm in practising good and abhorring vice: Seek the Lord and be strengthened. Oracles of Scripture which I may not doubt. What could better encourage me in taking care to seek God and study the ways of my salvation? You will tell me that you lack sufficient penetration for this and that your understanding is too weak. Granted, my dear hearer; but, like the Magi, you have an easy means of clearing all your doubts and escaping whatever uncertainty you may be in. In God's Church there are Doctors and priests, as there were then. There are men appointed to guide you; it depends only on you to listen. Ask them as your fathers and they will tell you what to do: Ask your father and he will tell you; your elders, and they will say it to you. Go to them as the Lord's ministers; their lips, entrusted with knowledge, will teach you the knowledge of all knowledge: how to find God. With this, can you remain ignorant, or even go wrong, without making yourselves utterly inexcusable?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p010", "label": "French original", "text": "Les Mages nous apprennent quelque chose encore de plus: & quoi? \u00e0 chercher Dieu avec un g\u00e9n\u00e9reux m\u00e9pris de tous les respects humains, & avec une libert\u00e9 digne de la saintet\u00e9 du christianisme que nous professons. En fut-il jamais un tel exemple? Au milieu de J\u00e9rusalem & en la pr\u00e9sence d'H\u00e9rodes, ils demandent o\u00f9 est n\u00e9 le nouveau Roi des Juifs. Sans nul m\u00e9nagement de politique, ils d\u00e9clarent qu'ils sont venus pour l'adorer. Uniquement occup\u00e9s de cette pens\u00e9e, il ne comptent pour rien toutes les consid\u00e9rations du monde qui pourroient refroidir leur z\u00e9le. Qu'H\u00e9rodes s'en offense & qu'il se trouble; que la synagogue s'en scandalise & qu'elle en murmure; qu'on pense & qu'on dise d'eux tout ce que l'on voudra; ni la censure des Juifs, ni la malignit\u00e9 d'H\u00e9rodes, ni la crainte de lui d\u00e9plaire, ni le danger qui les menace, rien ne les emp\u00eachera de rendre \u00e0 ce Sauveur & \u00e0 ce Dieu naissant le culte qui lui est d\u00fb. Est-ce ainsi, mon cher Auditeur, que vous l'honorez? est-ce ainsi que vous pratiquez les devoirs de votre religion? est-ce ainsi que vous \u00eates, quand il le faut \u00eatre, libre & sinc\u00e8re adorateur de Jesus-Christ? combien de fois un respect humain a-t-il retenu votre foi, dans l'esclavage? Combien de fois, jusques dans les sacr\u00e9s myst\u00e8res, lorsqu'il s'agissoit d'adorer le m\u00eame Dieu qu'adorent les Mages, avez-vous \u00e9t\u00e9 un l\u00e2che pr\u00e9varicateur? Combien de fois \u00e0 la face des autels, la crainte de passer pour un homme r\u00e9gulier & pieux, vous a-t-elle fait oublier que vous \u00e9tiez chr\u00e9tien, & par une foiblesse scandaleuse vous a-t-elle fait paro\u00eetre impie? combien de fois une honte criminelle vous a-t-elle ferm\u00e9 la bouche dans des occasions o\u00f9 il falloit s'expliquer hautement & parler? o\u00f9 \u00e9toit alors cette libert\u00e9 chr\u00e9tienne dont vous deviez vous faire, & devant les hommes & devant Dieu, non-seulement une obligation, mais une gloire? o\u00f9 \u00e9toit cet esprit de religion qui devoit vous \u00e9lever au-dessus du monde? sont-ce-l\u00e0 ces saintes victoires que la foi doit remporter? Et h\u00e6c est victoria qu\u00e6 vincit mundum, fides nostra. Ce point de morale occuperoit un discours entier: je le laisse; & pour vous faire voir la sagesse des Mages dans tout son jour, je passe \u00e0 ce que j'appelle la perfection de leur foi."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p010", "label": "English", "text": "The Magi teach us something more. What? To seek God with a generous contempt for all fear of human opinion, and with a freedom worthy of the holiness of the Christianity we profess. Was there ever such an example? In the midst of Jerusalem and in Herod's presence, they ask where the new King of the Jews has been born. Without any political accommodation, they declare that they have come to adore him. Intent only on this thought, they count as nothing every worldly consideration that might cool their zeal. Let Herod be offended and troubled, let the synagogue take scandal and murmur, let people think and say whatever they wish about them: neither Jewish censure, Herod's malice, fear of displeasing him nor the danger threatening them will prevent their giving this newborn Saviour and God the worship due to him. Is this, my dear hearer, how you honour him? How you practise your religion's duties? How you are, when necessary, a free and sincere adorer of Jesus Christ? How often has fear of human opinion held your faith in slavery? How often, even in the sacred mysteries when you should adore the same God whom the Magi adore, have you been a cowardly betrayer of duty? How often before the altars has fear of passing for a man of upright and pious conduct made you forget that you were Christian, and through scandalous weakness made you appear impious? How often has criminal shame shut your mouth when you ought to speak out openly? Where then was that Christian freedom which, before men and God, you ought to consider not only an obligation but a glory? Where was the spirit of religion which ought to raise you above the world? Are these the holy victories faith must win? And this is the victory that overcomes the world, our faith. This moral point would occupy an entire discourse; I leave it, and to show you the Magi's wisdom in its full light, pass to what I call the perfection of their faith.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p011", "label": "French original", "text": "Perfection de leur foi. Entrons avec eux dans l'\u00e9table de Bethl\u00e9em: car ils y arrivent enfin apr\u00e8s tant de peines & tant de p\u00e9rils. Or quel spectacle pour des Rois, qu'un enfant couch\u00e9 sur la paille & dans une cr\u00e8che? mais sous des dehors si vils & si m\u00e9prisables, le discernement qu'ils font de ce Sauveur n'est-il pas l'effet de la plus \u00e9minente sagesse? Ils le reconnoissent dans la pauvret\u00e9 & dans la mis\u00e8re, dans l'enfance & dans l'infirmit\u00e9, dans l'humiliation & dans le plus profond abaissement. Bien loin que cet \u00e9tat o\u00f9 ils le trouvent, alt\u00e8re leur foi, ils en sont touch\u00e9s, ils en sont \u00e9difi\u00e9s; & p\u00e9n\u00e9trant le myst\u00e8re, ils d\u00e9couvrent sous ces voiles obscurs le Messie promis au monde. S'ils n'eussent eu qu'une foi foible & chancelante, l'\u00e9table, la cr\u00e8che, les langes de cet enfant les eussent rebut\u00e9s; leur raison se seroit r\u00e9volt\u00e9e, leur sagesse alors toute mondaine leur e\u00fbt inspir\u00e9 du m\u00e9pris pour un Sauveur r\u00e9duit lui-m\u00eame en de telles extr\u00e9mit\u00e9s. Ils auroient dit ce que dirent ensuite les Juifs: Nolumus hunc regnare super nos; nous ne voulons point d'un Ma\u00eetre sans bien, sans forces, sans pouvoir, sans nom, d\u00e9nu\u00e9 de tout: qu'il paroisse sur le thr\u00f4ne, qu'on nous le fasse voir rev\u00eatu de gloire & de majest\u00e9, & nous nous soumettrons: voil\u00e0 comment ils auroient parl\u00e9, & ce qu'ils auroient pens\u00e9. Mais parce qu'ils sont anim\u00e9s d'une foi vive, d'une foi parfaite, d'une foi divine, ils en jugent tout autrement. Ils concluent que Jesus-Christ est Roi par lui-m\u00eame, c'est-\u00e0-dire, que pour se faire rechercher & ob\u00e9ir en cette qualit\u00e9, il n'a nul besoin de toutes les marques ext\u00e9rieures & de tous les ornemens de la pompe humaine. Si les autres Rois en \u00e9toient d\u00e9pouill\u00e9s, auroient-ils autour d'eux ces troupes de cliens, & ces Cours nombreuses qui remplissent leurs Palais? Ce n'est pas sur cet \u00e9clat & sur cette grandeur apparente qu'est fond\u00e9e leur Royaut\u00e9; elle vient de Dieu qui leur a fait part de sa puissance: mais apr\u00e8s tout si leur Royaut\u00e9 s'attire tant de respects, & si le monde lui rend tant d'honneurs, c'est parce qu'elle est accompagn\u00e9e d'une splendeur & d'une magnificence qui frappe les yeux; au lieu que sans cela ce Roi nouvellement n\u00e9 se fait respecter & honorer par les Rois m\u00eames. Ils concluent qu'il est Roi des esprits & des c\u0153urs, puisqu'il les a si miraculeusement \u00e9clair\u00e9s, inspir\u00e9s, touch\u00e9s. Les plus grands Rois de la terre n'ont pas ce pouvoir: ils regnent sur nous, dit saint J\u00e9r\u00f4me; mais Jesus-Christ r\u00e8gne dans nous, & il n'appartient qu'\u00e0 lui de s'insinuer, comme il veut, dans les ames, & de leur donner telle impression qu'il lui pla\u00eet. Ils concluent qu'il est Roi universel, Roi du ciel o\u00f9 il vient de faire \u00e9clater un nouvel astre, & Roi de la terre o\u00f9 il fait sentir sa souverainet\u00e9 & sa pr\u00e9sence aux nations m\u00eame les plus recul\u00e9es; Roi des Juifs & des gentils, de tous les \u00e9tats & de toutes les conditions, puisque de toutes les conditions & de tous les \u00e9tats il a \u00e9galement appell\u00e9 \u00e0 lui & les grands & les petits. C'est, dis-je, ce qu'une sagesse toute c\u00e9leste leur d\u00e9couvre; & c'est avec la m\u00eame sagesse & la m\u00eame foi, qu'une ame qui par un retour sinc\u00e8re & par une pleine cons\u00e9cration, s'attache d\u00e9sormais \u00e0 ce Sauveur qu'elle a retrouv\u00e9, lui dit comme ces bienheureux Mages (car je ne puis douter que ce ne f\u00fbt leur sentiment) Rex Regum, & Dominus dominantium: vous \u00eates le Roi des Rois, & le ma\u00eetre des ma\u00eetres; vous serez le mien en particulier. Trop long-tems le monde a exerc\u00e9 sur moi sa tyrannie; trop long-tems il m'a tenu dans une dure servitude, & soumis \u00e0 ses loix, ou plut\u00f4t \u00e0 ses bisarreries & \u00e0 ses caprices: il faut enfin secouer un joug si pesant & si honteux. Vous r\u00e9gnerez dans mon c\u0153ur & sur mon c\u0153ur: vous y r\u00e9gnerez seul, & seul vous en r\u00e9glerez tous les d\u00e9sirs, toutes les v\u00fbes, tous les desseins. Ainsi, le pensent les Mages; & ainsi mes chers Auditeurs, devez-vous le dire vous-m\u00eames, & encore plus le penser."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p011", "label": "English", "text": "The perfection of their faith. Let us enter Bethlehem's stable with them, for at last they arrive after so many hardships and perils. What a spectacle for kings: a child lying on straw in a manger! Yet beneath such lowly and contemptible appearances, is not their recognition of this Saviour the effect of the most eminent wisdom? They recognize him in poverty and misery, infancy and weakness, humiliation and deepest abasement. Far from impairing their faith, this state in which they find him touches and edifies them; penetrating the mystery, they discover beneath these dark veils the Messiah promised to the world. Had their faith been weak and wavering, the stable, manger and child's swaddling clothes would have repelled them; their reason would have revolted, their then wholly worldly wisdom would have inspired contempt for a Saviour himself reduced to such extremities. They would have said what the Jews later said: We do not wish this man to reign over us. We want no master without wealth, strength, power or name, destitute of everything. Let him appear on the throne; show him to us clothed in glory and majesty, and we will submit. Thus they would have spoken and thought. But because they are animated by living, perfect, divine faith, they judge quite otherwise. They conclude that Jesus Christ is King in himself: to be sought and obeyed in that capacity, he has no need of all the outward marks and adornments of human pomp. Were other kings stripped of these, would they have around them those crowds of dependants and numerous courts filling their palaces? Their kingship is not founded on this splendour and apparent greatness; it comes from God, who has shared his power with them. Yet, after all, if their kingship attracts such respect and the world pays it such honours, it is because it is accompanied by splendour and magnificence that strike the eyes; whereas without these this newborn King wins respect and honour from kings themselves. They conclude that he is King of minds and hearts, since he has so miraculously enlightened, inspired and touched them. Earth's greatest kings lack this power: they reign over us, says Saint Jerome; but Jesus Christ reigns within us, and to him alone belongs the power to enter souls as he wishes and impress them as he pleases. They conclude that he is universal King: King of heaven, where he has just made a new star shine, and King of earth, where he makes even the remotest nations feel his sovereignty and presence; King of Jews and gentiles, of every estate and condition, since from every estate and condition he has equally called great and small to himself. This, I say, is what wholly heavenly wisdom reveals to them. With the same wisdom and faith, a soul which through sincere return and full consecration henceforth attaches itself to the Saviour it has found again says to him, like these blessed Magi\u2014for I cannot doubt that this was their sentiment\u2014King of kings and Lord of lords: You are the King of kings and master of masters; you shall be mine in particular. Too long has the world exercised its tyranny over me, held me in harsh servitude and subjected me to its laws, or rather its whims and caprices. At last this heavy and shameful yoke must be shaken off. You will reign within and over my heart; you alone will reign there and govern all its desires, views and designs. Thus the Magi think; and thus, my dear hearers, you must yourselves speak, and still more think.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p012", "label": "French original", "text": "Perfection de leur foi: non contens d'honorer Jesus-Christ, comme le souverain Monarque du monde, ils l'adorent comme leur Dieu. Non contens de lui rendre un culte ext\u00e9rieur en se prosternant devant lui, Et procidentes; ils lui rendent un culte int\u00e9rieur, & l'adorent en esprit & en v\u00e9rit\u00e9, adoraverunt eum. Car ce fut un culte religieux; & pour \u00eatre un culte religieux, il devoit partir du c\u0153ur. Combien de faux adorateurs dans le Christianisme? c'est le vrai Dieu qu'ils adorent mais sans l'adorer comme le vrai Dieu le doit \u00eatre: pourquoi? parce qu'ils ne l'adorent que par coutume, parce qu'ils ne l'adorent que par c\u00e9r\u00e9monie, parce qu'ils ne l'adorent que par je ne s\u00e7ai quelles biens\u00e9ances, \u00e0 quoi ils ne veulent pas manquer, tandis que leur c\u0153ur porte ailleurs toutes ses pens\u00e9es & tous ses v\u0153ux; c'est-\u00e0-dire qu'ils sont chr\u00e9tiens en apparence, mais sans l'\u00eatre en effet, comme les Mages commencerent \u00e0 le devenir."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p012", "label": "English", "text": "The perfection of their faith: not content to honour Jesus Christ as the world's sovereign Monarch, they adore him as their God. Not content to give him outward worship by prostrating themselves before him\u2014And falling down\u2014they give him inward worship and adore him in spirit and truth: They adored him. For theirs was religious worship, and to be religious worship it had to come from the heart. How many false adorers there are in Christianity! It is the true God they adore, but without adoring him as the true God ought to be adored. Why? Because they adore him only from custom, ceremony or I know not what proprieties which they do not wish to neglect, while their heart carries all its thoughts and wishes elsewhere. That is, they are Christian in appearance without being so in fact, as the Magi began to become.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p013", "label": "French original", "text": "Perfection de leur foi: que pr\u00e9sentent-ils \u00e0 Jesus-Christ; & suivant l'explication des P\u00e8res & des Interpr\u00e8tes, que de myst\u00e8res sont renferm\u00e9s dans les trois offrandes qu'ils lui font? Toute l'id\u00e9e de Jesus-Christ m\u00eame y est exprim\u00e9e d'une mani\u00e8re sensible; sa divinit\u00e9, son humanit\u00e9, sa souverainet\u00e9: sa divinit\u00e9 par l'encens qui n'est d\u00fb qu'\u00e0 Dieu, son humanit\u00e9, par la myrrhe qui servoit \u00e0 embaumer & \u00e0 conserver les corps; enfin sa souverainet\u00e9, par l'or qui est le tribut ordinaire que nous payons aux Princes & aux Monarques: Et apertis thesauris suis, obtulerunt ei munera, aurum, thus & myrrham. Voil\u00e0 les grandes v\u00fbes que leur donna une sagesse sup\u00e9rieure \u00e0 toute la sagesse du si\u00e8cle: & ce fut d\u00e8s-lors que le Sauveur des hommes put bien dire qu'il n'avoit point trouv\u00e9 tant de foi, m\u00eame dans Isra\u00ebl; Non inveni tantam fidem in Isra\u00ebl. En effet, demande saint Augustin, que devons-nous plus admirer, ou la foi des Mages, ou l'aveuglement & l'infid\u00e9lit\u00e9 des Juifs? Les Juifs avoient au milieu d'eux le Messie, & ils ne le connoissoient pas: les Mages en \u00e9toient \u00e9loign\u00e9s; & malgr\u00e9 la plus longue distance des lieux, ils viennent le chercher dans la Jud\u00e9e, & ont le bonheur de l'y trouver. Les Juifs le renoncerent, quoique n\u00e9 dans leur pays: & les Mages, quoiqu'\u00e9trangers, l'adorerent. Les Juifs dans la suite des ann\u00e9es le crucifi\u00e8rent, lors m\u00eame qu'il op\u00e9roit les plus grands miracles; & les Mages, tout enfant qu'il \u00e9toit encore, se d\u00e9vou\u00e8rent \u00e0 lui, lors m\u00eame qu'il n'\u00e9toit pas en \u00e9tat de prononcer une parole. Ceux-ci le virent sur la paille, r\u00e9duit \u00e0 la plus vile condition des hommes, & cependant ils s'humili\u00e8rent devant lui comme devant un Dieu: ceux-l\u00e0 t\u00e9moins des hautes merveilles dont il \u00e9toit l'auteur, le virent agir en Dieu; & toutefois ils ne lui rendirent pas m\u00eame les devoirs de justice & de charit\u00e9, qu'on ne peut sans crime refuser \u00e0 un homme. Marque \u00e9vidente, reprend saint Augustin, mais effet terrible de leur endurcissement."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p013", "label": "English", "text": "The perfection of their faith: what do they present to Jesus Christ? And, according to the explanation of the Fathers and interpreters, how many mysteries are enclosed in the three offerings they make! The whole idea of Jesus Christ himself is expressed there in a sensible manner: his divinity, humanity and sovereignty. His divinity by incense, which is due to God alone; his humanity by myrrh, which served to embalm and preserve bodies; finally his sovereignty by gold, the ordinary tribute we pay princes and monarchs: And opening their treasures, they offered him gifts, gold, incense and myrrh. Such were the great insights given them by a wisdom superior to all the age's wisdom. From that moment the Saviour of men could well say that he had not found so much faith even in Israel: I have not found so great faith in Israel. Indeed, asks Saint Augustine, which ought we more to wonder at: the Magi's faith, or the Jews' blindness and unbelief? The Jews had the Messiah among them and did not know him; the Magi were far from him, yet despite the greatest distance between places, they came to seek him in Judaea and had the happiness of finding him there. The Jews rejected him although born in their country; the Magi, though strangers, adored him. In later years the Jews crucified him even while he worked the greatest miracles; the Magi devoted themselves to him while he was still a child, unable even to speak a word. These saw him on straw, reduced to the lowliest human condition, yet humbled themselves before him as before God; those witnessed the lofty wonders he wrought, saw him acting as God, yet did not give him even the duties of justice and charity which cannot without crime be refused to a man. An evident mark, Saint Augustine resumes, but a terrible effect of their hardening.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p014", "label": "French original", "text": "Ah! mes Fr\u00e8res, n'est-ce point une image de ce qui nous arrive \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames, ou de ce qui doit bient\u00f4t nous arriver? Jusques dans le sein de l'Eglise & dans le centre du christianisme, avons-nous la m\u00eame foi que les Mages? ou, si nous croyons comme eux, agissons-nous comme eux, & cherchons-nous Dieu comme eux? ils furent ces saints Mages, selon la pens\u00e9e & l'expression des P\u00e8res, les pr\u00e9mices de notre vocation \u00e0 la foi: c'est par eux que Jesus-Christ voulut commencer \u00e0 nous transmettre ce pr\u00e9cieux tr\u00e9sor de la foi, dont il les fit d\u00e9positaires: c'est par eux qu'il commen\u00e7a \u00e0 substituer les gentils en la place des Juifs, ou plut\u00f4t qu'il voulut associer les gentils & les Juifs dans la m\u00eame cr\u00e9ance. Mais au lieu d'imiter ces gentils fid\u00e8les, nous imitons les Juifs incr\u00e9dules. Nous sommes le peuple de Dieu, & \u00e0 peine connoissons-nous Dieu; ou si nous le connoissons, nous n'y pensons pas; ou si quelquefois nous y pensons, ce n'est que pour rendre notre malice plus obstin\u00e9e, en nous \u00e9loignant de lui, & ne retournant presque jamais \u00e0 lui. Il est vrai que nous avons re\u00e7u la foi, que les Juifs ne voulurent pas recevoir. Mais ce riche h\u00e9ritage, comment l'avons-nous conserv\u00e9, comment l'avons-nous cultiv\u00e9, quels fruits en retirons-nous, & comment le faisons-nous profiter? Car qu'est-ce maintenant que la foi des chr\u00e9tiens: cette foi si pure, si ferme, si g\u00e9n\u00e9reuse, si agissante dans les Mages; mais dans nous si languissante, mais dans nous si paresseuse & si lente, mais dans nous si st\u00e9rile & d\u00e9pouill\u00e9e de toutes les \u0153uvres qui la doivent accompagner, & qui la vivifient devant Dieu? Or ne craignons-nous point que Dieu ne prononce enfin contre nous le m\u00eame arr\u00eat qu'il pronon\u00e7a contre les Juifs par la bouche de son Ap\u00f4tre: Vobis oportebat prim\u00f9m loqui verbum Dei; sed quoniam repellitis illud, & indignos vos judicatis \u00e6tern\u00e6 vit\u00e6, ecce convertimur ad gentes: sic enim pr\u00e6cepit nobis Dominus. Mes fr\u00e8res, leur disoit saint Paul, c'\u00e9toit \u00e0 vous qu'il falloit d'abord annoncer la parole de Dieu, puisque Dieu vous avoit sp\u00e9cialement choisis: mais vous la rejettez cette divine parole, vous la m\u00e9prisez, & vous ne voulez pas l'entendre. C'est une parole de vie, mais vous renoncez \u00e0 cette vie \u00e9ternelle o\u00f9 elle devoit vous conduire. Le Seigneur donc nous ordonne de porter aux nations le saint Evangile que vous refusez d'embrasser, Ecce convertimur ad gentes: sic enim pr\u00e6cepit nobis Dominus. N'avons-nous pas, dis-je, sujet de craindre que Dieu ne nous traite de la sorte; qu'apr\u00e8s nous avoir distingu\u00e9s entre les Nations, qu'apr\u00e8s avoir fait luire sur nous sa lumi\u00e8re, & nous avoir donn\u00e9 la foi par pr\u00e9f\u00e9rence \u00e0 tant de peuples qu'il a laiss\u00e9s dans les t\u00e9n\u00e8bres, il ne nous enl\u00e8ve le talent qu'il nous a confi\u00e9, & qu'il ne le transporte loin de nous, dans des terres \u00e9trang\u00e8res? N'est-ce point d\u00e9j\u00e0 m\u00eame ce qui commence \u00e0 s'accomplir? Nous entendons parler des merveilles qu'op\u00e8re la pr\u00e9dication de l'Evangile au-del\u00e0 des mers; nous voyons partir d'aupr\u00e8s de nous des ministres de Jesus-Christ, pour aller cultiver une chr\u00e9tient\u00e9 naissante au milieu de l'Idol\u00e2trie; le nom du Seigneur est port\u00e9 aux extr\u00e9mit\u00e9s du monde. Que votre mis\u00e9ricorde, \u00f4 mon Dieu, en soit \u00e9ternellement b\u00e9nie; & malheur \u00e0 nous, si nous avions sur cela d'autres sentimens. Mais, Chr\u00e9tiens, selon la parole expresse du Sauveur des hommes, tandis que les peuples de l'Orient entrent dans le Royaume de Dieu, les enfans m\u00eames du Royaume n'en seront-ils point bannis? La ruine des Juifs fit l'abondance & l'\u00e9l\u00e9vation des gentils; & la richesse de tant de nations sur qui Dieu r\u00e9pand ses tr\u00e9sors, ne fera-t-elle point notre pauvret\u00e9 & notre mis\u00e8re? Si la foi passe en de vastes contr\u00e9es o\u00f9 elle \u00e9toit inconnue, n'est-ce point qu'elle nous quitte; apr\u00e8s que nous l'avons si long-tems outrag\u00e9e, si long-tems d\u00e9shonor\u00e9e, si long-tems retenue captive dans l'injustice & dans l'iniquit\u00e9? Pr\u00e9venons, mes chers Auditeurs, cet affreux ch\u00e2timent. Ranimons notre foi, & suivons-l\u00e0: c'est notre guide, c'est notre \u00e9toile: ne la perdons jamais de v\u00fbe. Allons \u00e0 Dieu, & n'y allons pas les mains vuides. L'encens que nous lui devons pr\u00e9senter, c'est selon l'explication de saint Gr\u00e9goire, la ferveur de nos pri\u00e8res; la myrrhe que nous lui devons offrir, c'est suivant la pens\u00e9e du m\u00eame P\u00e8re, la mortification de nos corps & l'aust\u00e9rit\u00e9 de la p\u00e9nitence; l'or qu'il attend de nous, ce sont nos bonnes \u0153uvres. Avec cela nous le trouverons aussi bien que les Mages: & j'ai dit que c'\u00e9toit le souverain point de la solide sagesse des \u00e9lus. Voyons maintenant dans l'exemple d'H\u00e9rodes, qu'elle est l'aveugle sagesse des impies & des r\u00e9prouv\u00e9s: c'est la seconde Partie"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p014", "label": "English", "text": "Ah, my brothers, is this not an image of what happens to us ourselves, or of what must soon happen? Even within the Church and at Christianity's centre, have we the same faith as the Magi? Or, if we believe like them, do we act and seek God like them? Those holy Magi were, in the Fathers' thought and expression, the first fruits of our calling to faith. Through them Jesus Christ wished to begin transmitting to us that precious treasure of faith with which he entrusted them; through them he began to put the gentiles in the Jews' place, or rather wished to associate gentiles and Jews in the same belief. But instead of imitating those faithful gentiles, we imitate the unbelieving Jews. We are God's people and scarcely know God; or if we know him, we do not think of him; or if sometimes we think of him, it is only to make our malice more obstinate by withdrawing from him and almost never returning. It is true we have received the faith the Jews refused to receive. But how have we preserved and cultivated this rich inheritance? What fruits do we draw from it, and how do we make it prosper? What now is Christians' faith: so pure, firm, generous and active in the Magi, but in us so languid, so lazy and slow, so sterile and stripped of all the works which ought to accompany it and give it life before God? Do we not fear that God may finally pronounce against us the same decree he pronounced against the Jews through his Apostle: It was necessary to speak God's word first to you; but since you reject it and judge yourselves unworthy of eternal life, behold, we turn to the gentiles; for so the Lord has commanded us. My brothers, Saint Paul said to them, it was to you that God's word had first to be announced, since God had specially chosen you. But you reject this divine word, despise it and refuse to hear it. It is a word of life, but you renounce the eternal life to which it should lead you. The Lord therefore commands us to carry to the nations the holy Gospel you refuse to embrace: Behold, we turn to the gentiles; for so the Lord has commanded us. Have we not, I say, reason to fear that God may treat us thus: that after distinguishing us among the nations, making his light shine upon us and giving us faith in preference to so many peoples left in darkness, he may take away the talent he entrusted to us and transport it far from us to foreign lands? Is this not already beginning to happen? We hear of wonders wrought by Gospel preaching beyond the seas; we see ministers of Jesus Christ leave us to cultivate a newborn Christian community amid idolatry; the Lord's name is carried to the world's ends. May your mercy, O my God, be eternally blessed for this, and woe to us if we felt otherwise! But Christians, according to the Saviour's express word, while the peoples of the East enter God's kingdom, will not the kingdom's own children be banished? The Jews' ruin brought the gentiles abundance and exaltation; will the wealth of so many nations upon which God pours his treasures not become our poverty and misery? If faith passes to vast countries where it was unknown, is it not leaving us after we have so long insulted and dishonoured it, so long held it captive in injustice and iniquity? Let us forestall this dreadful punishment, my dear hearers. Let us revive our faith and follow it; it is our guide, our star; never lose sight of it. Let us go to God, and not empty-handed. The incense we must present, according to Saint Gregory's explanation, is the fervour of our prayers; the myrrh we must offer, according to the same Father, is bodily mortification and the austerity of penance; the gold he awaits is our good works. With these we shall find him as the Magi did; and I have said that this is the highest point of the elect's sound wisdom. Let us now see in Herod's example the blind wisdom of the impious and reprobate: this is the second part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-h003", "label": "French original", "text": "II. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-h003", "label": "English", "text": "SECOND PART", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p015", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est un oracle de l'Ap\u00f4tre, & par cons\u00e9quent un oracle de la V\u00e9rit\u00e9 \u00e9ternelle, que la sagesse de ce monde est ennemie de Dieu. Mais comme elle est ennemie de Dieu, cette sagesse mondaine, aussi Dieu en est-il ennemi; & c'est lui-m\u00eame qui s'en d\u00e9clare par un de ses Proph\u00e8tes: Perdam sapientiam sapientium; je confondrai la prudence des prudens du si\u00e8cle. Voil\u00e0, dit saint Chrysost\u00f4me, les deux caract\u00e8res de cette fausse sagesse qui r\u00e8gne parmi les impies, & qui est le principe de leur conduite. Elle s'\u00e9l\u00e8ve contre Dieu, & Dieu la confond; elle fait la guerre \u00e0 Dieu, & Dieu la r\u00e9prouve; elle voudroit an\u00e9antir Dieu, & Dieu la d\u00e9truit & l'an\u00e9antit. Caract\u00e8res dont l'opposition m\u00eame fait la liaison, puisque l'un, comme vous verrez, est ins\u00e9parable de l'autre. Elle est ennemie de Dieu, voil\u00e0 son d\u00e9sordre; & Dieu par un juste retour est son plus mortel ennemi, voil\u00e0 son malheur. Or je soutiens que jamais ces deux caract\u00e8res de la sagesse du monde n'ont paru plus visiblement que dans la personne d'H\u00e9rodes. Car quelle a \u00e9t\u00e9 la destin\u00e9e de ce Prince, & \u00e0 quoi sa d\u00e9testable politique fut-elle occup\u00e9e? Vous le s\u00e7avez, Chr\u00e9tiens, \u00e0 former des desseins contre Jesus-Christ; \u00e0 lui susciter une cruelle pers\u00e9cution, \u00e0 vouloir l'\u00e9touffer d\u00e8s son berceau, & par la plus abominable hypocrisie \u00e0 le chercher en apparence pour l'adorer, mais en effet pour le faire p\u00e9rir. C'est ce que j'appelle le crime de la sagesse du si\u00e8cle. Et que fit de sa part Jesus-Christ naissant, ou plut\u00f4t que ne fit-il pas, pour montrer que cette pr\u00e9tendue sagesse \u00e9toit une sagesse maudite & r\u00e9prouv\u00e9e? Vous l'avez v\u00fb dans l'Evangile, il la troubla, il la rendit odieuse, il apprit \u00e0 tout l'univers combien elle est vaine & impuissante contre le Seigneur; enfin, il la fit servir malgr\u00e9 elle au dessein de Dieu qu'elle vouloit renverser. Quatre effets sensibles de la justice divine, qui par une singuli\u00e8re disposition de la providence, eurent dans H\u00e9rodes leur entier accomplissement, & c'est en quoi consiste le ch\u00e2timent de la politique du monde. Appliquez-vous, mes chers Auditeurs, \u00e0 l'excellente morale que je pr\u00e9tends tirer de-l\u00e0, & que j'aurai soin d'abr\u00e9ger, pour ne passer pas les bornes du tems qui m'est prescrit."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p015", "label": "English", "text": "It is an oracle of the Apostle, and therefore of eternal Truth, that this world's wisdom is God's enemy. But as this worldly wisdom is God's enemy, so God is its enemy; he himself declares this through one of his Prophets: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise; I will confound the prudence of the age's prudent men. Here, says Saint Chrysostom, are the two characteristics of the false wisdom reigning among the impious and governing their conduct. It rises against God, and God confounds it; it wages war against God, and God reprobates it; it would annihilate God, and God destroys and annihilates it. Their very opposition links these characteristics, since one, as you will see, is inseparable from the other. It is God's enemy: there is its disorder; God in just return is its deadliest enemy: there is its misfortune. I maintain that these two characteristics of worldly wisdom have never appeared more visibly than in Herod. For what was this prince's destiny, and with what was his detestable policy occupied? You know, Christians: plotting against Jesus Christ, raising a cruel persecution against him, wishing to stifle him in his cradle, and with the most abominable hypocrisy seeking him ostensibly to adore him, but actually to put him to death. This is what I call the crime of the age's wisdom. What, in turn, did the newborn Jesus Christ do\u2014or rather, what did he not do\u2014to show that this supposed wisdom was accursed and reprobate? You have seen in the Gospel: he troubled it, made it hateful, taught the whole universe how vain and powerless it is against the Lord; finally, he made it serve against its will the very design of God it wished to overturn. Four perceptible effects of divine justice which, through a singular disposition of providence, were wholly accomplished in Herod: in these consists the punishment of worldly policy. Attend, my dear hearers, to the excellent moral teaching I intend to draw from this, which I shall take care to abbreviate so as not to exceed my allotted time.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p016", "label": "French original", "text": "H\u00e9rodes, quoiqu'\u00e9tranger & usurpateur, vouloit r\u00e9gner dans la Jud\u00e9e, & sa passion dominante fut une damnable ambition \u00e0 laquelle il sacrifia tout. C'est ce qui le pervertit, ce qui l'aveugla, ce qui l'endurcit, ce qui le pr\u00e9cipita dans le plus profond abysme de l'iniquit\u00e9. Il s\u00e7ut que les Juifs attendoient un nouveau Roi, & par une grossi\u00e8re erreur, il crut que ce nouveau Roi venoit le d\u00e9poss\u00e9der. Il n'en fallut pas davantage pour piquer sa jalousie: sa jalousie inquiette & tyrannique le porta aux derniers exc\u00e8s de la violence & de la fureur, & lui inspira contre le Saint des saints une haine irr\u00e9conciliable. On lui dit que ce Roi qu'il craint, doit \u00eatre de la maison de David; pour s'assurer donc ou pour se d\u00e9livrer de lui, il forme l'affreuse r\u00e9solution d'exterminer toute la race de David. En vain lui remontre-t-on que celui qu'il veut perdre est le Messie promis par les Proph\u00e8tes, que c'est lui qui doit sauver & racheter Isra\u00ebl; il renonce \u00e0 la r\u00e9demption d'Isra\u00ebl plut\u00f4t que de renoncer \u00e0 son int\u00e9r\u00eat, & il aime mieux qu'il n'y ait point de Sauveur pour lui, que d'avoir un concurrent. Bien loin de se pr\u00e9parer \u00e0 recevoir ce Messie, & \u00e0 profiter de sa venue, il jure sa ruine: l'arriv\u00e9e des Mages \u00e0 J\u00e9rusalem lui fait comprendre qu'il est n\u00e9; il emploie la fourberie, l'imposture pour le d\u00e9couvrir; il feint de vouloir l'adorer, pour l'immoler plus s\u00fbrement \u00e0 sa fortune; & pour en \u00eatre le meurtrier, il contrefait l'homme de bien. Lorsqu'il se voit tromp\u00e9 par les Mages & frustr\u00e9 de son esp\u00e9rance, il l\u00e8ve le masque, il se livre \u00e0 la col\u00e8re & \u00e0 la rage, & dans son emportement il oublie toute l'humanit\u00e9. Les Princes qu'il a assembl\u00e9s, lui ont r\u00e9pondu que ce Roi des Juifs devoit na\u00eetre dans la contr\u00e9e de Bethl\u00e9em: pour ne le pas manquer, il ordonne que dans Bethl\u00e9em & aux environs on \u00e9gorge tous les enfans \u00e2g\u00e9s de deux ans & au dessous: pourvu qu'il s'affermisse la couronne sur la t\u00eate, il ne compte pour rien de remplir de sang & de carnage tout un pays. Telle fut la source de son d\u00e9sordre: son ambition le rendit jaloux, son ambition le rendit cruel, son ambition le rendit impie, son ambition le rendit fourbe & hypocrite, son ambition en fit un tyran, son ambition en fit non-seulement le plus m\u00e9chant de tous les hommes, mais le pers\u00e9cuteur d'un Dieu: il est vrai, & c'est ce qui doit nous faire trembler, quand nous voyons dans son exemple, ce que peut & jusqu'o\u00f9 va une passion, d\u00e8s qu'elle a pris une fois l'empire sur un c\u0153ur."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p016", "label": "English", "text": "Herod, though a foreigner and usurper, wished to reign in Judaea; his ruling passion was a damnable ambition to which he sacrificed everything. This perverted, blinded and hardened him, and hurled him into iniquity's deepest abyss. He learned that the Jews awaited a new King, and through a gross error believed that this new King was coming to dispossess him. Nothing more was needed to arouse his jealousy. His restless, tyrannical jealousy carried him to the utmost excesses of violence and fury and inspired an irreconcilable hatred against the Holy of holies. He is told that this King he fears must be of David's house; to secure himself or be rid of him, he forms the dreadful resolution to exterminate David's whole race. In vain is he reminded that the one he wishes to destroy is the Messiah promised by the Prophets, who must save and redeem Israel. He renounces Israel's redemption rather than renounce his own interest; he prefers having no Saviour to having a rival. Far from preparing to receive this Messiah and profit by his coming, he swears his destruction. The Magi's arrival in Jerusalem makes him understand that he has been born; he employs deceit and imposture to discover him, feigns a wish to adore him so as to sacrifice him more surely to his fortune, and plays the good man to become his murderer. When he sees himself deceived by the Magi and disappointed in his hope, he throws off the mask, yields to anger and rage, and in his fury forgets all humanity. The princes he assembled told him that this King of the Jews must be born in Bethlehem's region; to avoid missing him, he orders every child aged two and under to be slaughtered in Bethlehem and its surroundings. Provided he secures the crown upon his head, he thinks nothing of filling a whole country with blood and carnage. Such was the source of his disorder: his ambition made him jealous, his ambition made him cruel, his ambition made him impious, his ambition made him deceitful and hypocritical; his ambition made him a tyrant, his ambition made him not only the wickedest of men but the persecutor of God. This is true, and it must make us tremble when his example shows what a passion can do and how far it goes once it has gained dominion over a heart.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p017", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais il est encore vrai, que l'ambition d'H\u00e9rodes n'eut des suites si affreuses, que parce qu'elle fut conduite par les r\u00e8gles d'une politique humaine. Car si H\u00e9rodes dans sa malice e\u00fbt \u00e9t\u00e9 un insens\u00e9, un emport\u00e9, un homme volage & inconsid\u00e9r\u00e9, il e\u00fbt \u00e9t\u00e9 dans sa malice m\u00eame moins oppos\u00e9 \u00e0 Jesus-Christ, & moins ennemi de Dieu. Sa politique fut comme la consommation de son impi\u00e9t\u00e9, & c'est ce qui mit le comble \u00e0 tous ses vices. C'\u00e9toit un sage mondain: & par-l\u00e0, souffrez que je m'exprime ainsi, ce fut un parfait sc\u00e9l\u00e9rat. Or ce que vous concevez en lui de plus monstrueux, & ce qui vous fait plus d'horreur, est n\u00e9anmoins par proportion, ce qui se passe tous les jours parmi vous, & ce que vous avez m\u00eame cent fois d\u00e9test\u00e9 dans des sujets plus communs, mais aussi r\u00e9els. Car ne croyez pas, mes chers Auditeurs, qu'H\u00e9rodes soit un exemple singulier, ni que son p\u00e9ch\u00e9 ait cess\u00e9 dans sa personne. On voit encore dans le monde des H\u00e9rodes, & des pers\u00e9cuteurs de Jesus-Christ: peut-\u00eatre y sont-ils plus obscurs & plus cach\u00e9s aux yeux des hommes, mais peut-\u00eatre n'y sont-ils pas moins corrompus, ni moins criminels devant Dieu; & ma douleur est d'\u00eatre oblig\u00e9 de reconno\u00eetre que la m\u00eame impi\u00e9t\u00e9 se renouvelle sans cesse jusqu'au milieu du christianisme; que dans le sein de l'Eglise se trouve encore des hommes anim\u00e9s du m\u00eame esprit, & pleins des m\u00eames sentimens que ce Roi infid\u00e8le, dont au reste je puis dire, que jamais il n'e\u00fbt pers\u00e9cut\u00e9 le Fils de Dieu, s'il l'e\u00fbt connu comme nous le connoissons. Ce qui m'afflige, c'est de penser que je n'exag\u00e8re point, quand je parle de la sorte; & qu'H\u00e9rodes, dans l'opinion des P\u00e8res, ayant \u00e9t\u00e9 le premier Ante-Christ, il s'en est depuis form\u00e9 d'autres, dont le nombre cro\u00eet chaque jour: Et nunc Antechristi multi facti sunt. Car combien dans le monde de faux chr\u00e9tiens, si j'ose le dire, aussi Ante-Christs qu'H\u00e9rodes & d'esprit & de c\u0153ur? Expliquons-nous: combien dans le monde de faux chr\u00e9tiens, aussi contraires \u00e0 Jesus-Christ, aussi oppos\u00e9s \u00e0 ses maximes, aussi ennemis de son humilit\u00e9, aussi remplis d'orgueil & de fiert\u00e9, aussi ambitieux & aussi idol\u00e2tres de leur fortune, aussi jaloux de leur rang, aussi pr\u00eats \u00e0 tout sacrifier pour leur grandeur imaginaire? Combien de mondains du caract\u00e8re d'H\u00e9rodes, qui n'ont point d'autre Dieu que leur int\u00e9r\u00eat; qui ne connoissent ni foi ni loi, & ne distinguent ni sacr\u00e9 ni profane, quand il s'agit de maintenir cet int\u00e9r\u00eat; & qui cet int\u00e9r\u00eat fait oublier les plus inviolables devoirs non-seulement de la conscience, mais de la probit\u00e9 & de l'honneur; en qui ce d\u00e9mon de l'int\u00e9r\u00eat \u00e9touffe non-seulement la charit\u00e9, mais la pi\u00e9t\u00e9 & la compassion naturelle; que l'attachement \u00e0 cet int\u00e9r\u00eat rend durs, violens, intraitables; qui aveugl\u00e9s par cet int\u00e9r\u00eat, renoncent sans peine \u00e0 leur salut, non pas pour un Royaume comme H\u00e9rodes, mais pour de vaines pr\u00e9tentions? Combien d'hypocrites qui se couvrent aussi-bien qu'H\u00e9rodes, du voile de la religion pour arriver \u00e0 leurs fins criminelles; qui sous les apparences d'une trompeuse pi\u00e9t\u00e9, cachent toute la corruption d'une vie impure & d'un libertinage raffin\u00e9? Mais ce que je d\u00e9plore encore bien plus, combien d'esprits pr\u00e9occup\u00e9s & ent\u00eat\u00e9s des erreurs du si\u00e8cle, qui \u00e0 la honte du christianisme qu'ils professent, se font de tout cela une politique; je veux dire, qui par un renversement de principes, se font de leur ambition m\u00eame une vertu, une grandeur d'ame, une sup\u00e9riorit\u00e9 de g\u00e9nie; de leur injustice un talent, un art, un secret de r\u00e9ussir dans les affaires; de leur duplicit\u00e9 une prudence, une science du monde, une habilet\u00e9; qui en suivant le mouvement de leurs plus ardentes passions, se croient souverainement sages, affectent de passer pour tels, se glorifient & s'applaudissent de l'\u00eatre; qui se mocquent de tout ce que l'Ecriture appelle simplicit\u00e9 du juste; qui ne regardent qu'avec m\u00e9pris la soumission & la patience des gens de bien; qui traitent de foiblesse la conduite d'une ame fid\u00e8le, mod\u00e9r\u00e9e dans ses d\u00e9sirs, occup\u00e9e \u00e0 r\u00e9gler son c\u0153ur, tranquille dans sa condition, & sinc\u00e8re dans sa religion? Car voil\u00e0, mon Dieu, les d\u00e9sordres de cette prudence charnelle qui r\u00e8gne dans le monde. Elle n'a pas \u00e9pargn\u00e9 le Messie que vous y avez envoy\u00e9. D\u00e8s qu'il a paru, elle s'est \u00e9lev\u00e9e contre lui, elle lui a d\u00e9clar\u00e9 une guerre ouverte; & depuis tant de si\u00e8cles elle n'a point cess\u00e9 de lui susciter des pers\u00e9cuteurs plus dangereux qu'H\u00e9rodes m\u00eame. Peut-\u00eatre en voyez-vous dans cet auditoire. Ah! Seigneur, que ne puis-je les toucher aujourd'hui, & leur imprimer une sainte horreur de l'\u00e9tat o\u00f9 les a r\u00e9duits la fausse sagesse \u00e0 laquelle ils se sont abandonn\u00e9s & qui les a perdus!"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p017", "label": "English", "text": "But it is also true that Herod's ambition had such dreadful consequences only because it was guided by the rules of human policy. Had Herod in his malice been senseless, hot-headed, fickle and inconsiderate, even in his malice he would have been less opposed to Jesus Christ and less God's enemy. His policy was as it were the consummation of his impiety, crowning all his vices. He was a worldly wise man; and thereby, allow me the expression, a consummate villain. Yet what you conceive as most monstrous in him and what most horrifies you is, proportionately, what happens every day among you, what you have yourselves detested a hundred times in more ordinary but equally real instances. Do not believe, my dear hearers, that Herod is a unique example or that his sin ended with him. There are still Herods and persecutors of Jesus Christ in the world, perhaps more obscure and hidden from human eyes, but perhaps no less corrupt and criminal before God. My sorrow is to have to recognize that the same impiety is continually renewed within Christianity itself; that within the Church there are still men animated by the same spirit and filled with the same sentiments as that unbelieving king, of whom I can moreover say that he would never have persecuted the Son of God had he known him as we know him. What grieves me is to think that I do not exaggerate in speaking thus, and that, Herod having been in the Fathers' opinion the first Antichrist, others have since arisen whose number grows daily: And now many Antichrists have arisen. For how many false Christians in the world are, if I dare say it, as much Antichrists as Herod in mind and heart? Let us explain: how many false Christians are as contrary to Jesus Christ, as opposed to his maxims, as hostile to his humility, as full of pride and arrogance, as ambitious and idolatrous of their fortune, as jealous of their rank, as ready to sacrifice everything for their imaginary greatness? How many worldly men of Herod's character have no God but their interest, acknowledge neither faith nor law, distinguish neither sacred nor profane when maintaining that interest, and are made by it to forget the most inviolable duties not only of conscience but of probity and honour? In them this demon of self-interest stifles not only charity but piety and natural compassion; attachment to it makes them harsh, violent and intractable; blinded by it, they readily renounce salvation, not for a kingdom like Herod but for vain claims. How many hypocrites, like Herod, cover themselves with religion's veil to reach criminal ends and under deceptive piety's appearance hide all the corruption of an impure life and refined libertinism? But what I deplore still more: how many minds, prejudiced and obstinate in the age's errors, to the shame of the Christianity they profess, make all this a policy! I mean those who, reversing principles, make their very ambition a virtue, greatness of soul and superiority of genius; their injustice a talent, an art, a secret of success in affairs; their duplicity prudence, worldly knowledge and skill. Following their most ardent passions, they think themselves supremely wise, affect to pass for such, glory in it and congratulate themselves. They mock everything Scripture calls the just man's simplicity, view good people's submission and patience only with contempt, and call weak the conduct of a faithful soul, moderate in desire, occupied with governing its heart, content in its condition and sincere in religion. Such, my God, are the disorders of the carnal prudence reigning in the world. It did not spare the Messiah whom you sent there. As soon as he appeared it rose against him, declared open war against him, and for so many centuries has never ceased raising persecutors more dangerous than Herod himself. Perhaps you see some in this audience. Ah, Lord, would that I could touch them today and impress on them a holy horror of the state to which they have been reduced by the false wisdom to which they surrendered and which has ruined them!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p018", "label": "French original", "text": "Cependant si la sagesse du monde est ennemie de Dieu, j'ajoute que Dieu n'en est pas moins ennemi; & c'est ici, Chr\u00e9tiens, que je vous demande une attention toute nouvelle. Car que fait Jesus-Christ naissant, pour confondre la malheureuse politique d'H\u00e9rodes? En premier lieu, il la trouble; Audiens autem Herodes Rex, turbatus est. Ce Dieu de paix qui venoit pour pacifier le monde, commence par y r\u00e9pandre l'\u00e9pouvante & la terreur: & comment? voici la merveille: par son seul nom, par le seul bruit de sa venue, par le seul doute s'il est n\u00e9. Chose \u00e9trange, dit saint Chrysost\u00f4me, Jesus-Christ ne paro\u00eet point encore, il n'a point encore fait de miracles, il n'est pas encore sorti de l'\u00e9table de Bethl\u00e9em; c'est un enfant couch\u00e9 dans une cr\u00e8che, qui pleure & qui souffre: & cependant H\u00e9rodes est d\u00e9j\u00e0 d\u00e9concert\u00e9: le voil\u00e0 d\u00e9j\u00e0 combattu de mille soup\u00e7ons & de mille frayeurs: Audiens autem Herodes Rex, turbatus est. Quoiqu'il en soit de ce Prince, & quel que puisse \u00eatre le sujet de ses craintes, rien, mes Fr\u00e8res, ajoute le m\u00eame saint Docteur, rien n'est plus capable de troubler la paix d'un mondain, que l'id\u00e9e d'un Dieu pauvre & humble; sur-tout quand avec un esprit & un c\u0153ur poss\u00e9d\u00e9s du monde, il ne laisse pas d'avoir encore un reste de foi, & d'\u00eatre toujours, quoique tr\u00e8s-imparfaitement, chr\u00e9tien. Car c'est alors que l'id\u00e9e d'un tel Sauveur a quelque chose de bien consolant pour lui & de bien effrayant. Ce reste de foi avec les sentimens & les maximes d'un c\u0153ur mondain, ce reste de foi avec une ambition payenne, ce reste de foi avec le d\u00e9sordre d'une passion d\u00e9r\u00e9gl\u00e9e; voil\u00e0 ce qui fait le trouble int\u00e9rieur d'une ame partag\u00e9e entre le monde & sa religion. Si l'on ne croyoit point du tout ce myst\u00e8re de l'humilit\u00e9 d'un Dieu, peut-\u00eatre seroit-on moins \u00e0 plaindre: si on le croyoit bien, & que l'on conform\u00e2t sa vie \u00e0 sa cr\u00e9ance, on jouiroit d'un parfait repos. Mais le croire, quoique foiblement, & d'ailleurs penser, parler, agir, comme si l'on ne le croyoit pas, c'est ce que le mondain pr\u00e9tendu sage n'a jamais accord\u00e9, ni n'accordera jamais avec le calme."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p018", "label": "English", "text": "Yet if worldly wisdom is God's enemy, I add that God is no less its enemy; and here, Christians, I ask your renewed attention. What does the newborn Jesus Christ do to confound Herod's unhappy policy? First, he troubles it: Hearing this, King Herod was troubled. This God of peace, coming to pacify the world, begins by spreading dread and terror in it. How? Here is the wonder: by his name alone, the mere report of his coming, the mere uncertainty whether he has been born. A strange thing, says Saint Chrysostom: Jesus Christ has not yet appeared, has worked no miracles, has not yet left Bethlehem's stable; he is a child lying in a manger, weeping and suffering; yet Herod is already disconcerted, assailed by a thousand suspicions and fears: Hearing this, King Herod was troubled. Whatever may be true of this prince and the cause of his fears, nothing, my brothers, adds the same holy Doctor, can more disturb a worldly man's peace than the thought of God poor and humble\u2014especially when, with mind and heart possessed by the world, he nonetheless retains some faith and remains Christian, though very imperfectly. Then the thought of such a Saviour has something very consoling and very frightening for him. This remnant of faith together with a worldly heart's sentiments and maxims, this remnant of faith with pagan ambition, this remnant of faith with the disorder of an unruly passion: this creates the inward turmoil of a soul divided between the world and its religion. If one did not believe at all in this mystery of God's humility, perhaps one would be less pitiable; if one truly believed and conformed one's life to that belief, one would enjoy perfect repose. But to believe it, however weakly, and otherwise think, speak and act as though one did not believe it, is what the supposedly wise worldly man has never reconciled, and will never reconcile, with calm.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p019", "label": "French original", "text": "Et en effet, quoiqu'on fasse alors pour s'aveugler ou pour se dissiper, pour s'\u00e9tourdir ou pour s'endurcir, on sent malgr\u00e9 soi un fond de trouble qui subsiste, & dont on ne peut se d\u00e9faire. Car au moins est-il vrai que le mondain avec ce reste de foi, ne peut rentrer dans lui-m\u00eame sans \u00eatre allarm\u00e9 de ces r\u00e9flexions affligeantes: Si le Dieu qui vient pour me sauver, est tel qu'on m'assure, je suis un impie; si les maximes de ce Dieu sont aussi solides qu'on me le dit, je suis non-seulement un insens\u00e9, mais un r\u00e9prouv\u00e9; si je dois \u00eatre jug\u00e9 selon son Evangile, il n'y a point de salut pour moi. Or ces r\u00e9flexions, dont je d\u00e9fie le plus fier mondain de se pouvoir d\u00e9fendre, doivent l'agiter, pour peu qu'il ait de sens, des plus mortelles inqui\u00e9tudes. Avec cela, quoiqu'il s'efforce d'\u00e9touffer les remords de cette foi qui l'importune, il reconno\u00eet bien par lui-m\u00eame qu'il n'en peut venir \u00e0 bout: ou s'il en vient \u00e0 bout, sa condition pour cela n'en est pas meilleure. Du trouble que lui causoit sa foi, il tombe dans un autre trouble encore plus d\u00e9plorable, qui est celui de son incr\u00e9dulit\u00e9. Le seul doute, si Jesus-Christ \u00e9toit n\u00e9, fit trembler H\u00e9rodes; le seul doute d'un mondain, si ces maximes qu'on lui pr\u00eache, ne sont pas les vrais principes qu'il doit suivre; le seul doute, s'il ne se trompe pas; le seul doute sur les risques qu'il court & dont son libertinage ne le peut garantir, tout cela le doit jetter dans une affreuse confusion de pens\u00e9es, & former en lui comme un enfer. Ah! disoit le saint homme Job, ce sont deux choses incompatibles que d'\u00eatre tranquille & r\u00e9belle \u00e0 Dieu: Quis resistit ei, & pacem habuit? H\u00e9rodes ni put parvenir; qui le pourra?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p019", "label": "English", "text": "Indeed, whatever one then does to blind or distract oneself, to numb or harden oneself, one feels against one's will an enduring ground of disturbance from which one cannot free oneself. At least it is true that a worldly man with this remnant of faith cannot enter within himself without being alarmed by these distressing reflections: If the God coming to save me is such as I am assured, I am impious; if this God's maxims are as sound as I am told, I am not only senseless but reprobate; if I must be judged according to his Gospel, there is no salvation for me. These reflections, against which I challenge the proudest worldly man to defend himself, must agitate him with the deadliest anxieties if he has any sense. Moreover, however he strives to stifle the remorse of this faith that troubles him, he knows within himself that he cannot succeed; or if he does, his condition is no better for it. From the disturbance caused by his faith, he falls into another still more deplorable disturbance, that of his unbelief. The mere uncertainty whether Jesus Christ had been born made Herod tremble. A worldly man's mere doubt whether the maxims preached to him may be the true principles he should follow, whether he is deceiving himself, and about the risks he runs against which his libertinism cannot protect him\u2014all this must throw him into a dreadful confusion of thoughts and form within him something like hell. Ah, said holy Job, tranquillity and rebellion against God are incompatible: Who resists him and has had peace? Herod could not attain it; who will?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p020", "label": "French original", "text": "Je n'en ai point encore dit assez: outre que le fils de Dieu d\u00e8s sa naissance trouble la politique & la fausse sagesse du monde, il la rend odieuse. H\u00e9rodes comme pers\u00e9cuteur de Jesus-Christ, est devenu l'horreur du genre humain. Il a tout sacrifi\u00e9 \u00e0 son ambition, mais sa m\u00e9moire est en abomination. Il n'a rien \u00e9pargn\u00e9 pour satisfaire la passion qu'il avoit de r\u00e9gner, mais c'est pour cela que son r\u00e8gne, au rapport m\u00eame des historiens profanes, a \u00e9t\u00e9 un r\u00e8gne monstrueux. Il a cr\u00fb pour sa s\u00fbret\u00e9 devoir r\u00e9pandre du sang, mais ce sang r\u00e9pandu criera \u00e9ternellement contre lui, & Dieu jusques \u00e0 la fin des si\u00e8cles vengera ce sang innocent par le caract\u00e8re d'ignominie qui se trouve attach\u00e9 au seul nom d'H\u00e9rodes, & qui ne s'effacera jamais. In\u00e9vitable destin\u00e9e du sage mondain, qui malgr\u00e9 lui se rend odieux, en se cherchant lui-m\u00eame. Qu'y a-t-il en effet de plus odieux dans le monde qu'un homme int\u00e9ress\u00e9, qu'un homme ambitieux & jaloux; c'est-\u00e0-dire, un homme ennemi par profession de tous les autres hommes, je dis de tous ceux qui peuvent lui donner quelque ombrage, & s'opposer \u00e0 ses pr\u00e9tentions; un homme qui n'aime sinc\u00e9rement personne, & que personne ne peut sinc\u00e9rement aimer; un homme qui n'a de v\u00fbes que pour lui-m\u00eame, & qui rapporte tout \u00e0 lui-m\u00eame; un homme qui ne peut voir dans autrui la prosp\u00e9rit\u00e9 sans l'envier, ni le m\u00e9rite sans le combattre; toujours pr\u00eat dans la concurrence \u00e0 trahir l'un, \u00e0 supplanter l'autre, \u00e0 d\u00e9crier celui-ci, \u00e0 perdre celui-l\u00e0, pour peu qu'il esp\u00e8re en profiter? Qu'y a-t-il, encore une fois, non-seulement de plus ha\u00efssable dans l'id\u00e9e du monde, mais m\u00eame de plus ha\u00ef? Or par-l\u00e0, dit saint Chrysost\u00f4me, le monde, tout corrompu qu'il est, se fait lui-m\u00eame justice: car voil\u00e0, par un secret jugement de Dieu, ce que le mondain veut \u00eatre, & en m\u00eame tems ce qu'il ne peut souffrir; ce qu'il entretient dans lui-m\u00eame, & ce qu'il d\u00e9teste dans les autres; comme si Dieu, ajoute ce P\u00e8re, se plaisoit \u00e0 r\u00e9prouver la sagesse du monde par elle-m\u00eame; au lieu que le monde, quoique d'ailleurs plein d'injustice, ne peut s'emp\u00eacher n\u00e9anmoins d'aimer dans les autres l'humilit\u00e9, d'honorer dans les autres le d\u00e9sint\u00e9ressement, de respecter dans les autres la droiture, la bonne foi, toutes les vertus, & de rendre hommage par-l\u00e0 m\u00eame \u00e0 la sagesse chr\u00e9tienne."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p020", "label": "English", "text": "I have still not said enough. Besides disturbing worldly policy and false wisdom from his birth, the Son of God makes it hateful. Herod, as Jesus Christ's persecutor, has become humanity's horror. He sacrificed everything to ambition, but his memory is held in abomination. He spared nothing to satisfy his passion for reigning, but for this reason his reign was monstrous, even according to secular historians. He believed his safety required bloodshed, but that shed blood will cry eternally against him, and until the end of the ages God will avenge that innocent blood through the mark of ignominy attached to Herod's very name, which will never be erased. Such is the inevitable destiny of the worldly wise man, who against his will makes himself hateful by seeking himself. What indeed is more hateful in the world than a self-interested, ambitious and jealous man? That is, a man professionally hostile to all other men\u2014I mean all who can arouse his suspicion and oppose his claims; one who sincerely loves nobody and whom nobody can sincerely love; who has views only for himself and refers everything to himself; who cannot see another's prosperity without envying it or merit without combating it; always ready in rivalry to betray one, supplant another, discredit this man or ruin that one if he hopes for the slightest profit. What, once again, is not only more hateful in the world's judgment, but more hated? Thus, says Saint Chrysostom, the world, corrupt as it is, does justice upon itself. For by God's secret judgment, this is what the worldly man wishes to be and at the same time cannot endure; what he nourishes within himself and detests in others\u2014as though God, adds this Father, delighted in reprobating worldly wisdom through itself. Whereas the world, otherwise full of injustice, nonetheless cannot help loving humility in others, honouring disinterestedness, respecting uprightness, good faith and every virtue, and thereby paying homage to Christian wisdom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p021", "label": "French original", "text": "Jesus-Christ fait plus: il apprend \u00e0 tout l'univers combien la sagesse du monde est vaine & inutile. H\u00e9rodes a beau chercher le Roi des Juifs, il ne le trouvera pas: il a beau user d'artifice en dissimulant avec les Mages, pour les engager \u00e0 lui en venir dire des nouvelles; les Mages prendront une autre route, & ne retourneront plus \u00e0 J\u00e9rusalem. Il a beau faire un massacre de tous les enfans qui sont aux environs de Bethl\u00e9em; celui qu'il cherche n'y sera pas envelopp\u00e9. Il en \u00e9gorgea mille pour un seul, & ce seul dont il veut s'assurer, est celui qui lui \u00e9chappera: pourquoi? parce qu'il est \u00e9crit qu'il n'y a point de conseil, ni de prudence contre le Seigneur; Non est prudentia, non est consilium contra Dominum. Ainsi, Chr\u00e9tiens, sans parler d'H\u00e9rodes, jamais le mondain avec sa pr\u00e9tendue sagesse ne parvient, ni ne parviendra \u00e0 la fin qu'il se propose: car il se propose d'\u00eatre heureux, & jamais il ne le sera. Il sera riche si vous le voulez, combl\u00e9 d'honneur si vous le voulez, mais suivant les principes & les r\u00e8gles de la fausse prudence, il n'arrivera jamais au bonheur o\u00f9 il aspire. Or d\u00e8s-l\u00e0 sa sagesse n'est plus sagesse, puisqu'elle ne peut le conduire \u00e0 son but. V\u00e9rit\u00e9 aussi ancienne que Dieu m\u00eame, mais encore plus incontestable depuis que le Fils de Dieu a \u00e9tabli la b\u00e9atitude des hommes, dans des choses o\u00f9 \u00e9videmment la sagesse du monde n'est d'aucun usage. Car suppos\u00e9, comme l'Evangile nous l'enseigne, que la b\u00e9atitude d'un Chr\u00e9tien consiste \u00e0 \u00eatre pauvre de c\u0153ur, \u00e0 souffrir pers\u00e9cution pour la justice, \u00e0 pardonner les injures, en quoi la prudence du si\u00e8cle nous peut-elle \u00eatre d\u00e9sormais utile? Quelle prudence du si\u00e8cle, dit S. Chrysost\u00f4me, faut-il pour tout cela? Usant de cette prudence, quel avantage en tirez-vous, & \u00e0 quoi vous menera-t-elle? Si vous vous servez de cette prudence de la chair, pour satisfaire vos d\u00e9sirs, vous renoncez \u00e0 la b\u00e9atitude du christianisme. Si vous pr\u00e9tendez \u00e0 la b\u00e9atitude du christianisme, cette prudence de la chair n'y peut en rien contribuer. Par cons\u00e9quent elle n'est plus prudence, ou plut\u00f4t de prudence qu'elle sembloit \u00eatre, elle devient folie, puisque bien loin de vous d\u00e9couvrir la v\u00e9ritable f\u00e9licit\u00e9 & de vous aider \u00e0 la trouver, elle y devient un obstacle: ce qui faisoit dire \u00e0 l'Ap\u00f4tre, Nonne stultam fecit Deus sapientiam hujus s\u00e6culi?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p021", "label": "English", "text": "Jesus Christ does more: he teaches the whole universe how vain and useless worldly wisdom is. Herod may seek the King of the Jews, but he will not find him. He may use artifice and dissimulate with the Magi to persuade them to bring him news; the Magi will take another road and will not return to Jerusalem. He may massacre all the children around Bethlehem; the one he seeks will not be caught in it. He slaughtered a thousand for one, and the very one he wishes to secure will escape him. Why? Because it is written that there is neither counsel nor prudence against the Lord: There is no prudence, no counsel against the Lord. Thus, Christians, apart from Herod, the worldly man with his supposed wisdom never reaches, and never will reach, his intended end: for he intends to be happy and never will be. He will be rich, if you wish, loaded with honour, if you wish; but following false prudence's principles and rules, he will never attain the happiness he seeks. Hence his wisdom is no longer wisdom, since it cannot lead him to his goal. A truth as ancient as God himself, but still more incontestable since the Son of God established human blessedness in things where worldly wisdom plainly is of no use. For given, as the Gospel teaches, that a Christian's blessedness consists in being poor in heart, suffering persecution for justice and forgiving injuries, how can the age's prudence henceforth be useful? What worldly prudence, says Saint Chrysostom, is needed for all this? What advantage do you gain by using it, and where will it lead? If you employ this carnal prudence to satisfy your desires, you renounce Christianity's blessedness. If you aspire to Christianity's blessedness, carnal prudence can contribute nothing to it. Consequently it is no longer prudence, or rather, from the prudence it seemed to be, it becomes folly: far from revealing true felicity and helping you find it, it becomes an obstacle. Thus the Apostle said: Has not God made foolish the wisdom of this age?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p022", "label": "French original", "text": "Enfin, le Sauveur venant au monde, fait servir malgr\u00e9 elle aux desseins de Dieu la politique m\u00eame du monde. Car prenez garde, il falloit que la naissance de Jesus-Christ f\u00fbt publi\u00e9e & connue, & c'est la violence & la tyrannie d'H\u00e9rodes qui la rend publique. Il vouloit \u00e9teindre le nom de ce nouveau Roi d'Isra\u00ebl, & c'est lui qui le fait conno\u00eetre. Il vouloit qu'il n'en f\u00fbt point parl\u00e9; & la voie qu'il prend pour cela, est justement le moyen d'en faire parler par toute la terre & dans tous les si\u00e8cles. Quel bruit en effet & quel tumulte! Que de mouvemens diff\u00e9rens, & que d'effroi lorsque tant de victimes innocentes sont impitoyablement arrach\u00e9es du sein de leurs m\u00e8res, & immol\u00e9es devant leurs yeux. Quels cris confus, & quels g\u00e9missemens se firent entendre de toutes parts! Vox in Roma audita est, ploratus & ululatus multus. Etoit-il possible qu'une action si \u00e9clatante demeur\u00e2t cach\u00e9e? Etoit-il possible que de la Jud\u00e9e elle ne pass\u00e2t pas bien-t\u00f4t dans les pays voisins, & de-l\u00e0 chez les nations les plus \u00e9loign\u00e9es? Etoit-il possible qu'on n'en voul\u00fbt pas s\u00e7avoir le sujet, & qu'on ne pr\u00eet pas soin de s'en faire instruire? Et par une cons\u00e9quence n\u00e9cessaire, n'\u00e9toit-ce pas l\u00e0 de quoi rendre Jesus-Christ c\u00e9l\u00e8bre & de quoi faire admirer sa puissance, lorsqu'on apprendroit que des Mages & des Rois \u00e9toient venus l'adorer; qu'H\u00e9rodes en avoit con\u00e7u de la jalousie; que dans l'exc\u00e8s de sa fureur, il avoit fait les derniers efforts pour perdre cet enfant; & que malgr\u00e9 tous ses efforts, cet enfant sans armes & sans d\u00e9fense avoit s\u00e7u n\u00e9anmoins se d\u00e9rober \u00e0 ses coups? Sagesse adorable de mon Dieu, c'est ainsi que vous vous jouez de la sagesse des hommes, quand elle se tourne contre vous, & que vous employez \u00e0 ex\u00e9cuter vos immuables d\u00e9crets, cela m\u00eame qui devoit, selon nos v\u00fbes foibles, les arr\u00eater. C'est ainsi que s'accomplit cette menace que vous nous aviez fait entendre par la bouche de votre Ap\u00f4tre: Perdam sapientiam sapientium, & prudentiam prudentium reprobabo. Je d\u00e9truirai la sagesse des sages du si\u00e8cle, & je la r\u00e9prouverai. Combien de preuves en a-t-on eu dans les \u00e2ges pr\u00e9c\u00e9dens, & combien en avons-nous encore dans le n\u00f4tre? Combien de fois l'impie, selon le langage de l'Ecriture, a-t-il v\u00fb retomber sur lui son impi\u00e9t\u00e9 m\u00eame; & combien de fois s'est-il trouv\u00e9, par une secrette disposition de la Providence, engag\u00e9 & pris dans le pi\u00e8ge, o\u00f9 il vouloit attirer les autres? Aman vouloit perdre Mardoch\u00e9e & tous les Juifs avec lui; mais courtisan ambitieux, ce sera vous-m\u00eame qui servirez \u00e0 l'\u00e9tablissement de cette Nation que vous vouliez exterminer; vous-m\u00eame qui servirez \u00e0 relever la gloire de cet homme juste que vous vouliez opprimer; vous-m\u00eame qui p\u00e9rirez, & qui p\u00e9rirez par le m\u00eame supplice que vous lui avez pr\u00e9par\u00e9. L'orgueilleux veut s'aggrandir, & c'est par-l\u00e0 souvent qu'il est d\u00e9pouill\u00e9. Le voluptueux veut satisfaire sa passion, & sa passion devient son bourreau, & lui fait souffrir les plus cruelles peines. Effets sensibles de la supr\u00eame sagesse de notre Dieu. Mais que n'ai-je le tems de vous d\u00e9velopper tant d'autres myst\u00e8res qui nous sont cach\u00e9s: myst\u00e8res profonds, & sur-tout myst\u00e8res d'autant plus terribles, qu'ils regardent, non plus la ruine temporelle, mais l'\u00e9ternelle damnation du sage mondain."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p022", "label": "English", "text": "Finally, the Saviour, coming into the world, makes worldly policy itself serve God's designs against its will. Consider: Jesus Christ's birth had to be published and known; Herod's violence and tyranny make it public. He wished to extinguish this new King of Israel's name; he himself makes it known. He wished it not to be spoken of; the course he takes is precisely the means of making it spoken of throughout the earth and in every age. What noise and tumult! What varied commotion and terror when so many innocent victims are pitilessly torn from their mothers' breasts and sacrificed before their eyes! What confused cries and groans were heard on every side! A voice was heard in Roma, weeping and much lamentation. Could so conspicuous an action remain hidden? Could it fail to pass quickly from Judaea into neighbouring countries and thence to the remotest nations? Could people fail to want to know its cause and take care to learn it? By necessary consequence, was this not enough to make Jesus Christ renowned and his power admired, when people learned that Magi and kings had come to adore him; that Herod had become jealous; that in his furious excess he had made the utmost efforts to destroy this child; and that despite all his efforts, this unarmed, defenceless child had nonetheless known how to escape his blows? Adorable wisdom of my God, thus you make sport of human wisdom when it turns against you, using to execute your immutable decrees the very thing that, according to our weak views, ought to have stopped them. Thus is fulfilled the threat you made us hear through your Apostle: I will destroy the wisdom of the wise and reprobate the prudence of the prudent. I will destroy and reprobate the wisdom of the age's wise men. How many proofs have previous ages had, and how many have we still in our own! How often has the impious man, in Scripture's language, seen his very impiety recoil upon him; how often, through providence's secret disposition, has he found himself entangled and caught in the snare to which he wished to draw others? Aman wished to destroy Mardoch\u00e9e and all the Jews with him. But ambitious courtier, you yourself will serve to establish the nation you wished to exterminate, raise the glory of the just man you wished to oppress; you yourself will perish, by the very punishment you prepared for him. The proud man wishes to enlarge himself, and often by this very means is stripped. The voluptuous man wishes to satisfy his passion, and it becomes his executioner, making him suffer the cruellest pains. Perceptible effects of our God's supreme wisdom! But would that I had time to unfold so many other mysteries hidden from us: deep mysteries, and above all mysteries the more terrible because they concern no longer temporal ruin but the worldly wise man's eternal damnation.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p023", "label": "French original", "text": "Renon\u00e7ons, mes chers Auditeurs, mais renon\u00e7ons pour jamais & de bonne foi, \u00e0 cette sagesse r\u00e9prouv\u00e9e, qui se cherche elle-m\u00eame, & qui ne cherche qu'elle-m\u00eame: en nous cherchant nous-m\u00eames, nous nous perdrons. Je me trompe: en nous cherchant nous-m\u00eames, nous nous trouverons; mais le plus grand de tous les malheurs pour nous, est de nous trouver nous-m\u00eames, puisqu'en nous trouvant nous-m\u00eames, nous ne pouvons trouver que ce que nous sommes; c'est-\u00e0-dire, que confusion, que d\u00e9sordre, que mis\u00e8re, que p\u00e9ch\u00e9. Cherchons Dieu; & sans penser \u00e0 nous, nous nous trouverons saintement, s\u00fbrement, heureusement en Dieu. Cherchons Dieu, & d\u00e8s cette vie nous trouverons notre souverain bien, qui ne peut \u00eatre hors de Dieu. Et parce que Dieu ne peut plus \u00eatre d\u00e9sormais trouv\u00e9 qu'en Jesus-Christ; \u00e0 l'exemple des Mages, pour trouver Dieu, cherchons Jesus-Christ. Et parce que Jesus-Christ ne peut \u00eatre trouv\u00e9 lui-m\u00eame que dans les \u00e9tats o\u00f9 il a voulu se r\u00e9duire pour nous servir de mod\u00e8le, ne le cherchons point ailleurs; c'est-\u00e0-dire, parce que Jesus-Christ ne peut-\u00eatre trouv\u00e9 que par la voie d'une humilit\u00e9 sinc\u00e8re, d'une ob\u00e9issance fid\u00e8le, d'un v\u00e9ritable renoncement au monde, ne le cherchons point par d'autres voies que celles-l\u00e0. Aimons-les ces saintes voies, qui nous conduisent \u00e0 lui: & puisqu'il n'y a plus d'autre sagesse que la sienne, attachons-nous \u00e0 cette divine sagesse: \u00e9tudions-la dans les maximes de ce Sauveur, dans la puret\u00e9 de sa doctrine & de sa loi, dans la saintet\u00e9 de ses myst\u00e8res, dans la perfection de ses exemples. Pr\u00e9f\u00e9rons cette sagesse chr\u00e9tienne \u00e0 toute la sagesse du monde; ou plut\u00f4t, faisons profession de ne conno\u00eetre point d'autre sagesse, pour pouvoir dire avec saint Paul: Non judicavi me scire aliquid inter vos, nisi Jesum Christum, & hunc crucifixum. C'est cette sagesse qui nous \u00e9clairera, cette sagesse qui nous sanctifiera, cette sagesse qui fera de nous des hommes parfaits sur la terre, & des bienheureux dans le ciel, o\u00f9 nous conduise, &c."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-p023", "label": "English", "text": "Let us renounce, my dear hearers\u2014renounce for ever and in good faith\u2014that reprobate wisdom which seeks itself and seeks only itself. Seeking ourselves, we shall lose ourselves. I am mistaken: seeking ourselves, we shall find ourselves; but the greatest of all our misfortunes is to find ourselves, since in finding ourselves we can find only what we are: confusion, disorder, misery and sin. Let us seek God; without thinking of ourselves, we shall find ourselves holily, safely and happily in God. Let us seek God, and even in this life we shall find our sovereign good, which cannot be outside God. Since henceforth God can be found only in Jesus Christ, let us, following the Magi, seek Jesus Christ to find God. Since Jesus Christ himself can be found only in the states to which he wished to descend to serve as our model, let us not seek him elsewhere. That is, since Jesus Christ can be found only by the way of sincere humility, faithful obedience and true renunciation of the world, let us seek him by no other ways. Let us love these holy ways that lead us to him; since there is no other wisdom than his, let us attach ourselves to this divine wisdom. Let us study it in this Saviour's maxims, the purity of his doctrine and law, the holiness of his mysteries and the perfection of his examples. Let us prefer Christian wisdom to all worldly wisdom; or rather, profess to know no other wisdom, so as to say with Saint Paul: I judged myself to know nothing among you except Jesus Christ, and him crucified. This wisdom will enlighten us, this wisdom will sanctify us, this wisdom will make us perfect men on earth and blessed in heaven; may we be led there, etc.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n001", "label": "French original", "text": "Luc. c. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n001", "label": "English", "text": "Luke, ch. 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n002", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan. c. 9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n002", "label": "English", "text": "John, ch. 9.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n003", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 3."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n003", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, ch. 3.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n004", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n004", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, ch. 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n005", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n005", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid. (1 Corinthians, ch. 2.)", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n006", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n006", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, ch. 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n007", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n007", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, ch. 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n008", "label": "French original", "text": "Joann. c. 12."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n008", "label": "English", "text": "John, ch. 12.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n009", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 19."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n009", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, ch. 19.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-a001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The French prints plu rather than plus. The translation follows the intended comparative sense, \u201cmore\u201d."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n010", "label": "French original", "text": "Psalm. 25."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n010", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 25 (source numbering).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n011", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n011", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, ch. 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n012", "label": "French original", "text": "Sap. c. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n012", "label": "English", "text": "Wisdom, ch. 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n013", "label": "French original", "text": "Ps. 24."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n013", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 24 (source numbering).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n014", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal. 9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n014", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 9 (source numbering).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n015", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal. 68."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n015", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 68 (source numbering).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n016", "label": "French original", "text": "Psal. 104."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n016", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 104 (source numbering).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n017", "label": "French original", "text": "Deut. c. 32."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n017", "label": "English", "text": "Deuteronomy, ch. 32.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n018", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Joan. c. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n018", "label": "English", "text": "1 John, ch. 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-a002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The French has a singular pronoun with a plural verb here. The translation follows the plural subject, the Magi."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n019", "label": "French original", "text": "Luc. c. 19."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n019", "label": "English", "text": "Luke, ch. 19.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n020", "label": "French original", "text": "Apoc. c. 19."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n020", "label": "English", "text": "Apocalypse, ch. 19.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n021", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n021", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, ch. 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n022", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n022", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid. (Matthew, ch. 2.)", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n023", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n023", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid. (Matthew, ch. 2.)", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n024", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 8."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n024", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, ch. 8.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n025", "label": "French original", "text": "Act. c. 13."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n025", "label": "English", "text": "Acts, ch. 13.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n026", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n026", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, ch. 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n027", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Joan. c. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n027", "label": "English", "text": "1 John, ch. 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n028", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n028", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, ch. 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n029", "label": "French original", "text": "Job. c. 9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n029", "label": "English", "text": "Job, ch. 9.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-a003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The French prints ni where n\u2019y is expected. The translation follows the sentence\u2019s sense: Herod could not attain peace."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n030", "label": "French original", "text": "Prov. c. 21."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n030", "label": "English", "text": "Proverbs, ch. 21.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n031", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n031", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, ch. 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n032", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n032", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, ch. 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n033", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n033", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, ch. 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-a004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints Roma where Matthew 2:18 normally reads Rama. The translation preserves Roma; it does not identify the place as Rome."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n034", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie-n034", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, ch. 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "On the Epiphany: True and False Wisdom", "topics": ["Epiphany", "faith", "wisdom", "conversion", "humility", "self-love"], "url": "sermons/bourdaloue-mysteres-epiphanie.html"}, {"author": "Louis Bourdaloue", "calendar": "dayKey:cibavit", "calendar_label": "Corpus Christi", "centuries": [17, 18], "composition": "Approximately late seventeenth century\u20131704; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement", "language": "fr", "language_label": "French", "metadata": ["Louis Bourdaloue, Sermons du P\u00e8re Bourdalou\u00eb, de la Compagnie de Jesus, sur les myst\u00e8res, tome premier, quatri\u00e8me \u00e9dition, edited by Fran\u00e7ois de Paule Bretonneau (Paris: chez Rigaud, 1726), Sermon sur le tr\u00e8s-Saint Sacrement, pp. 512\u2013547. John Adams Library/Boston Public Library copy.", "Internet Archive; John Adams Library at the Boston Public Library.", "Corpus Christi", "Feast of the Most Holy Sacrament (Corpus Christi)", "John 6", "John 1", "Psalm 57 (printed locator; apparent reference error)", "Psalm 98:5 (historical Vulgate) / 99:5 (modern) \u2014 added by editors", "Matthew 9", "Acts 10", "Song of Songs 3", "1 Corinthians 12", "Ephesians 5", "Deuteronomy 4", "Ezekiel 48", "Daniel 2", "Proverbs 8", "Isaiah 2 (printed locator; apparent reference error)", "Isaiah 1:2 \u2014 added by editors", "1 Corinthians 9 (printed locator; apparent reference error)", "1 Corinthians 6:15, 19 \u2014 added by editors", "1 Corinthians 12:27 \u2014 added by editors", "1 Corinthians 1 (printed locator; apparent reference error)", "1 Corinthians 11:27, 29 \u2014 added by editors"], "occasion": "Tr\u00e8s-saint Sacrement", "occasion_detail": "Feast of the Most Holy Sacrament (Corpus Christi)", "occasion_label": "Corpus Christi", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Bourdaloue\u2019s sermon on the Blessed Sacrament presents the Eucharist as the glory of both Christ\u2019s natural body and his mystical body, the Church. Its two parts move from the honour given to Christ\u2019s flesh to the Church\u2019s possession of his sacramental presence, concluding with reverence for the Church and holiness of the faithful\u2019s bodies. The source explicitly names the feast of the body of Christ and discusses its procession and octave."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-h001", "label": "French original", "text": "SERMON SUR LE TR\u00c8S-SAINT SACREMENT."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON ON THE MOST HOLY SACRAMENT.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-e001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Caro mea ver\u00e8 est cibus."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-e001", "label": "English", "text": "My flesh is truly food.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-e002", "label": "French original", "text": "Ma chair est vraiment une viande. En saint Jean, chap. 6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-e002", "label": "English", "text": "My flesh is truly food. In Saint John, chapter 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p001", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est ainsi que le Sauveur du monde faisoit en deux mots l'\u00e9loge de son corps adorable, & c'est, Chr\u00e9tiens, de cette chair toute sainte & toute divine que j'ai moi-m\u00eame \u00e0 vous entretenir. Ce n'est point de la personne de Jesus-Christ; ce n'est ni de sa divinit\u00e9 ni de son ame, mais de sa chair: Caro mea. Et pour en venir d'abord au point que j'ai entrepris de traiter, remarquez, s'il vous pla\u00eet, avec moi, que dans les paroles de mon texte, le Fils de Dieu voulant recommander son corps aux Juifs, ne leur dit pas que c'est le temple du Saint-Esprit, que c'est le sanctuaire de Dieu, que c'est le chef-d'\u0153uvre des mains & de la toute-puissance du Seigneur, mais que c'est une nourriture & une viande: Caro mea ver\u00e8 est cibus. Cependant, cet \u00e9tat de viande & d'aliment n'est-il pas le plus imparfait? Il est vrai, mes chers Auditeurs, si nous l'entendons de cette viande commune qui sert \u00e0 r\u00e9parer les forces & \u00e0 soutenir la vie naturelle de nos corps: mais une viande sacramentelle, une viande qui toute mat\u00e9rielle qu'elle est, a la vertu de nous conf\u00e9rer la grace, de nous donner une vie surnaturelle & toute spirituelle, de nous purifier, de nous sanctifier, c'est ce qui nous la doit rendre infiniment pr\u00e9cieuse, & ce qui en fait l'excellence. Vierge sainte, c'est dans vos chastes entrailles que ce sacr\u00e9 corps fut con\u00e7u; votre chair innocente & pure a \u00e9t\u00e9 la chair de Jesus-Christ: & la chair de Jesus-Christ a \u00e9t\u00e9 la v\u00f4tre, c'est par l'op\u00e9ration de l'esprit c\u00e9leste que cet ineffable myst\u00e8re s'est accompli, & c'est aupr\u00e8s de ce divin Epoux que j'implore votre assistance, en vous disant: Ave, Maria."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p001", "label": "English", "text": "This is how the Saviour of the world praised his adorable body in a few words; and it is of this wholly holy and divine flesh, Christians, that I myself must speak to you. It is not of the person of Jesus Christ, nor of his divinity or his soul, but of his flesh: My flesh. To come at once to the point I have undertaken to treat, please observe with me that, in the words of my text, the Son of God, wishing to commend his body to the Jews, does not tell them that it is the temple of the Holy Spirit, the sanctuary of God, the masterpiece of the Lord's hands and almighty power, but that it is nourishment and food: My flesh is truly food. Yet is not this condition of being food and nourishment the most imperfect? That is true, my dear hearers, if we understand it of the ordinary food that restores strength and sustains the natural life of our bodies. But sacramental food, food which, material though it is, has the power to confer grace on us, to give us a supernatural and wholly spiritual life, to purify and sanctify us: this is what must make it infinitely precious to us, and what constitutes its excellence. Holy Virgin, it was in your chaste womb that this sacred body was conceived; your innocent and pure flesh was the flesh of Jesus Christ, and the flesh of Jesus Christ was yours. It was through the action of the heavenly Spirit that this ineffable mystery was accomplished, and I ask for your assistance before this divine Spouse, saying to you: Hail, Mary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-h002", "label": "French original", "text": "DIVISION."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-h002", "label": "English", "text": "DIVISION.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p002", "label": "French original", "text": "Le dessein que je me propose dans ce discours, vous surprendra peut-\u00eatre, Chr\u00e9tiens; mais j'ose dire, que si vous voulez vous appliquer \u00e0 le bien comprendre, il vous paro\u00eetra tr\u00e8s-convenable au myst\u00e8re de ce jour, & qu'il remplira parfaitement l'id\u00e9e que vous avez de cette f\u00eate. Je veux vous montrer que c'est aujourd'hui par excellence la f\u00eate du corps de Jesus-Christ, Festum corporis Christi. Car c'est le titre qu'elle porte, & sous lequel elle a \u00e9t\u00e9 institu\u00e9e; & mon dessein est de vous justifier ce titre en vous faisant voir, que le corps de Jesus-Christ ne pouvoit plus \u00eatre honor\u00e9 qu'il l'est par le myst\u00e8re de la divine Eucharistie: c'est-l\u00e0 ma proposition g\u00e9n\u00e9rale. Il faut seulement la r\u00e9duire \u00e0 quelques points particuliers, & la partager. Or pour cela je consid\u00e9re le corps de Jesus-Christ en deux mani\u00e8res; ou plut\u00f4t, je trouve que Jesus-Christ a tout \u00e0 la fois, & un corps naturel, & un corps mystique. Son corps naturel, c'est sa propre chair, cette chair dont il s'est rev\u00eatu pour nous; & son corps mystique c'est l'Eglise, qu'il s'est unie & incorpor\u00e9e selon la doctrine de S. Paul. Je dis donc que c'est aujourd'hui la grande f\u00eate de l'un & de l'autre: pourquoi? parce que c'est aujourd'hui tout ensemble le triomphe de la chair de Jesus-Christ, & le triomphe de l'Eglise de Jesus-Christ. Le Sauveur du monde ne pouvoit faire plus d'honneur \u00e0 sa chair, que de l'\u00e9tablir, comme il a fait, en sacrement & en sacrement le plus auguste de notre religion qui est l'Eucharistie. Et j'ajoute que ce m\u00eame Sauveur du monde ne pouvoit faire plus d'honneur \u00e0 son Eglise, qu'en lui laissant sa chair \u00e9tablie de la sorte, & comme \u00e9rig\u00e9e en sacrement. Ainsi l'Eglise & la chair de Jesus-Christ sont-elles honor\u00e9es r\u00e9ciproquement l'une par l'autre. Car la gloire du corps de Jesus-Christ, c'est d'avoir \u00e9t\u00e9 donn\u00e9 \u00e0 l'Eglise dans le saint Sacrement de l'Autel: vous le verrez dans la premi\u00e8re partie. Et la gloire de l'Eglise, c'est d'avoir re\u00e7u & de poss\u00e9der le corps de Jesus-Christ dans ce Sacrement: ce sera la seconde partie. Quoique ce soit l\u00e0 un \u00e9loge plut\u00f4t qu'une instruction, nous pourrons n\u00e9anmoins en tirer de solides cons\u00e9quences pour l'\u00e9dification de nos ames. Commen\u00e7ons."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p002", "label": "English", "text": "The purpose I propose in this discourse may surprise you, Christians; but I venture to say that, if you will apply yourselves to understanding it well, it will appear entirely suited to the mystery of this day and will perfectly fulfil your conception of this feast. I wish to show you that today is, above all, the feast of the body of Jesus Christ: The feast of the body of Christ. For this is its title, and the title under which it was instituted; and my purpose is to justify it by showing you that the body of Jesus Christ could not be more honoured than it is by the mystery of the divine Eucharist. This is my general proposition. We need only reduce it to a few particular points and divide it. For this purpose I consider the body of Jesus Christ in two ways; or rather, I find that Jesus Christ has at once both a natural body and a mystical body. His natural body is his own flesh, the flesh in which he clothed himself for us; and his mystical body is the Church, which he united and incorporated with himself according to the teaching of Saint Paul. I say, then, that today is the great feast of both. Why? Because today is at once the triumph of the flesh of Jesus Christ and the triumph of the Church of Jesus Christ. The Saviour of the world could do no greater honour to his flesh than to establish it, as he did, as a sacrament, and as the most august sacrament of our religion, the Eucharist. I add that this same Saviour of the world could do no greater honour to his Church than to leave her his flesh thus established and, as it were, constituted as a sacrament. Thus the Church and the flesh of Jesus Christ are honoured reciprocally by one another. For the glory of the body of Jesus Christ is to have been given to the Church in the holy Sacrament of the Altar: you will see this in the first part. And the glory of the Church is to have received and to possess the body of Jesus Christ in this Sacrament: this will be the second part. Although this is a eulogy rather than an instruction, we shall nevertheless be able to draw sound conclusions from it for the edification of our souls. Let us begin.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-h003", "label": "French original", "text": "I. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-h003", "label": "English", "text": "PART I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p003", "label": "French original", "text": "Il \u00e9toit juste que la chair de Jesus-Christ f\u00fbt honor\u00e9e, & que Jesus-Christ travaill\u00e2t lui-m\u00eame \u00e0 lui faire rendre les hommages qui lui sont d\u00fbs. Deux grandes raisons l'y obligeoient. Premi\u00e8rement, l'honneur qu'il avoit fait \u00e0 cette chair de contracter une si \u00e9troite alliance avec elle, & de l'unir \u00e0 sa personne divine dans l'incarnation; & secondement, les humiliations extr\u00eames \u00e0 quoi il l'avoit r\u00e9duite dans sa passion. Avez-vous jamais pris garde, Chr\u00e9tiens, \u00e0 une belle parole de saint Jean, pour exprimer le grand myst\u00e8re de l'incarnation du Verbe? Il ne dit pas que le Verbe s'est fait homme, il ne dit pas qu'il s'est alli\u00e9 \u00e0 une nature intelligente & spirituelle comme les Anges, il ne dit pas qu'il a pris une ame telle que la n\u00f4tre, mais il dit simplement que le Verbe s'est fait chair: Et verbum caro factum est. H\u00e9 quoi, reprend saint Augustin, la chair de l'homme, est ce qu'il y a dans l'homme de plus imparfait, c'est en quoi l'homme est semblable aux b\u00eates; pourquoi donc rapporter \u00e0 la chair seule cet \u00e9tonnant myst\u00e8re de l'union qui s'est faite entre l'homme & Dieu? Ah! r\u00e9pond ce saint Docteur, c'est pour nous apprendre ce que Dieu a fait pour nous, ce qu'il a voulu \u00eatre pour nous, jusqu'\u00e0 quel point il s'est an\u00e9anti pour nous, puisqu'\u00e9tant Dieu, il a bien daign\u00e9 se faire chair. Il est vrai, Chr\u00e9tiens; mais c'est par-l\u00e0 m\u00eame aussi que le Saint-Esprit nous a fait comprendre, ce qu'il \u00e9toit important que nous s\u00e7ussions, quelle est la dignit\u00e9 de la chair de Jesus-Christ, puisqu'en cons\u00e9quence de ces divines paroles: Et Verbum caro factum est; on peut dire, selon tous les principes de la Th\u00e9ologie & de la foi, que la chair de Jesus-Christ a \u00e9t\u00e9 la chair d'un Dieu, qu'elle a subsist\u00e9 de la substance d'un Dieu, qu'elle a fait partie d'un tout, qui \u00e9toit Dieu; & que comme le Verbe en s'incarnant est devenu chair: Et Verbum caro factum est; ainsi la chair de l'homme, par l'incarnation, est devenue la chair d'un Dieu. De-l\u00e0 concluons qu'il n'y a donc point de gloire, point de culte, qu'on ne doive \u00e0 la chair de Jesus-Christ; & que Jesus-Christ m\u00eame, apr\u00e8s une si noble alliance, n'en pouvoit trop faire pour honorer sa chair."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p003", "label": "English", "text": "It was just that the flesh of Jesus Christ should be honoured, and that Jesus Christ himself should work to have the homage due to it rendered. Two great reasons obliged him to do so. First, the honour he had done this flesh by contracting so close an alliance with it and uniting it to his divine person in the Incarnation; and second, the extreme humiliations to which he had reduced it in his Passion. Have you ever noticed, Christians, a beautiful expression of Saint John for the great mystery of the Incarnation of the Word? He does not say that the Word became man, that he allied himself with an intelligent and spiritual nature like the angels, or that he took a soul like ours; he simply says that the Word became flesh: And the Word was made flesh. What, asks Saint Augustine, is man's flesh not the most imperfect thing in man, that in which man resembles the beasts? Why, then, refer solely to the flesh this astonishing mystery of the union established between man and God? Ah, replies this holy Doctor, it is to teach us what God has done for us, what he has willed to be for us, how far he has emptied himself for us, since, being God, he has indeed deigned to become flesh. That is true, Christians; but by this very means the Holy Spirit has also made us understand what it was important for us to know: the dignity of the flesh of Jesus Christ. For, as a consequence of these divine words, And the Word was made flesh, we may say, according to all the principles of theology and faith, that the flesh of Jesus Christ was the flesh of one who is God, that it subsisted from the substance of one who is God, that it formed part of a whole which was God; and that, just as the Word became flesh in becoming incarnate, And the Word was made flesh, so man's flesh, through the Incarnation, became the flesh of one who is God. Let us conclude that there is no glory, no worship, that is not owed to the flesh of Jesus Christ; and that Jesus Christ himself, after so noble an alliance, could not do too much to honour his flesh.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p004", "label": "French original", "text": "D'autant plus qu'il la r\u00e9duisit dans sa passion aux derni\u00e8res humiliations. Car c'est cette chair v\u00e9n\u00e9rable qui fut combl\u00e9e pour nous d'ignominies & d'opprobres, c'est elle qui fut d\u00e9chir\u00e9e de fouets, c'est elle qui fut profan\u00e9e par les mains des bourreaux; & pour tout dire en un mot, c'est elle, si j'ose user ici de cette mani\u00e8re de parler, qui fit tous les frais de notre r\u00e9demption. Ce ne fut point l'ame de Jesus-Christ, qui servit de victime pour notre salut; ce fut son corps, ce fut sa chair virginale. Ce fut elle qu'il immola sur l'autel de la croix; elle \u00e9toit sainte, & il en fit un anath\u00eame & un sujet de mal\u00e9diction; elle \u00e9toit digne de tous les respects des hommes; & il permit qu'elle f\u00fbt expos\u00e9e \u00e0 toutes leurs insultes. Il falloit donc qu'il la r\u00e9compens\u00e2t & qu'il l'honor\u00e2t autant qu'elle avoit \u00e9t\u00e9 humili\u00e9e, ou plut\u00f4t autant qu'il l'avoit lui-m\u00eame humili\u00e9e. Or c'est justement ce que Jesus-Christ a fait dans la divine Eucharistie; voil\u00e0 la fin qu'il s'est propos\u00e9e dans l'institution de ce myst\u00e8re; & voil\u00e0 aussi pourquoi nous c\u00e9l\u00e9brons aujourd'hui la F\u00eate de son Corps."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p004", "label": "English", "text": "All the more so because, in his Passion, he reduced it to the deepest humiliations. For it was this venerable flesh that was overwhelmed for us with ignominy and reproach; it was torn by scourges and profaned by the executioners' hands; and, to say everything in a word, it was this flesh, if I may use such an expression here, that bore the entire cost of our redemption. It was not the soul of Jesus Christ that served as the victim for our salvation; it was his body, his virginal flesh. This was what he immolated on the altar of the Cross. It was holy, and he made it an anathema and an object of malediction; it was worthy of all men's reverence, and he allowed it to be exposed to all their insults. He therefore had to recompense and honour it as much as it had been humiliated, or rather as much as he himself had humiliated it. This is precisely what Jesus Christ did in the divine Eucharist; this is the end he proposed in instituting this mystery, and this is also why today we celebrate the feast of his Body.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p005", "label": "French original", "text": "En effet, Chr\u00e9tiens, l'Eucharistie seule fait plus d'honneur \u00e0 la chair de Jesus-Christ que tous les autres myst\u00e8res glorieux de cet Homme-Dieu; & quand il sortit du tombeau, la gloire qu'il communiqua \u00e0 son corps ne fut point comparable \u00e0 celle qu'il lui avoit donn\u00e9e & qu'il lui donne encore tous les jours dans son saint Sacrement. Cette proposition vous paro\u00eet nouvelle; mais \u00e9coutez-moi, en voici la d\u00e9monstration. J'avoue, mes Freres, que Jesus-Christ sortant du tombeau, donna \u00e0 sa chair d'admirables qualit\u00e9s, impassibilit\u00e9, subtilit\u00e9, agilit\u00e9, lumi\u00e8re & splendeur: mais apr\u00e8s tout, ces qualit\u00e9s n'ont rien qui surpasse l'ordre de la cr\u00e9ature; au lieu qu'ici, c'est-\u00e0-dire, dans l'adorable Eucharistie, la chair du Sauveur est \u00e9lev\u00e9e \u00e0 un ordre tout divin, elle y prend un \u00eatre, elle y acquiert des propri\u00e9t\u00e9s, elle y fait ce que Dieu seul peut faire. Et quoi? il faudroit un discours entier pour vous l'expliquer. Je m'arr\u00eate \u00e0 ce qu'il y a de plus essentiel, & \u00e0 ce qui doit le plus vous toucher. Je ne vous dis point que cette chair bienheureuse poss\u00e9de une esp\u00e9ce d'immensit\u00e9 dans l'auguste Sacrement de l'autel, puisqu'il est certain qu'elle n'y est born\u00e9e par aucun espace, & qu'en vertu de ce myst\u00e8re, elle peut \u00eatre tout \u00e0 la fois dans tous les lieux du monde; qualit\u00e9 propre de Dieu. Je ne vous dis point qu'elle y devient toute spirituelle, mais bien autrement que dans sa r\u00e9surrection, puisque la chair de Jesus-Christ est dans l'hostie \u00e0 la mani\u00e8re des esprits, toute en tout, & toute en chaque partie; autre qualit\u00e9 miraculeuse. Je laisse ce qu'a remarqu\u00e9 l'Abb\u00e9 Ruppert, qu'elle est comme \u00e9ternelle & incorruptible dans ce Sacrement, parce qu'elle y sera jusqu'\u00e0 la consommation des si\u00e9cles: ou plut\u00f4t, Chr\u00e9tiens, qu'elle y meurt tous les jours, mais d'une mort mille fois plus merveilleuse que l'immortalit\u00e9 m\u00eame dont elle jouit dans le ciel, puisque c'est pour y rena\u00eetre continuellement par les paroles de la cons\u00e9cration. Tout cela autant d'effets de la toute-puissance divine pour honorer le corps du Sauveur."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p005", "label": "English", "text": "Indeed, Christians, the Eucharist alone does more honour to the flesh of Jesus Christ than all the other glorious mysteries of this God-man; and when he came forth from the tomb, the glory he communicated to his body was not comparable to that which he had given it and still gives it every day in his holy Sacrament. This proposition seems new to you; but listen to me, and here is its demonstration. I acknowledge, my brethren, that Jesus Christ, coming forth from the tomb, gave his flesh admirable qualities: impassibility, subtlety, agility, light and splendour. Yet these qualities contain nothing that surpasses the order of the creature; whereas here, that is, in the adorable Eucharist, the Saviour's flesh is raised to an entirely divine order: it takes on a mode of being, acquires properties, and does what God alone can do. And what is that? It would take a whole discourse to explain it to you. I confine myself to what is most essential and should most deeply move you. I do not tell you that this blessed flesh possesses a kind of immensity in the august Sacrament of the Altar, since it is certain that it is bounded there by no space and, by virtue of this mystery, can be at once in every place in the world: a quality proper to God. I do not tell you that it becomes wholly spiritual there, but in a very different way from its resurrection, since the flesh of Jesus Christ is in the host in the manner of spirits, whole in the whole and whole in each part: another miraculous quality. I leave aside Abbot Rupert's observation that it is as though eternal and incorruptible in this Sacrament, because it will be there until the consummation of the ages; or rather, Christians, that it dies there every day, but by a death a thousand times more wonderful than the very immortality it enjoys in heaven, since it is so as to be continually reborn there through the words of consecration. All these are so many effects of divine almighty power to honour the Saviour's body.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p006", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais le grand miracle, & celui qui comprend tous les autres, & celui que Jesus-Christ nous a marqu\u00e9 plus express\u00e9ment dans l'Evangile, & celui \u00e0 quoi les hommes font moins de r\u00e9flexion, & celui qui devroit \u00eatre plus m\u00e9dit\u00e9, & celui que je trouve incontestablement le plus glorieux \u00e0 la chair du Fils de Dieu, je l'ai dit & il faut le d\u00e9velopper davantage, c'est que la chair de Jesus-Christ dans l'Eucharistie est l'aliment de nos ames. Quoiqu'elle ne soit qu'une substance terrestre & mat\u00e9rielle, elle a la vertu de vivifier nos esprits. Au lieu que naturellement c'est l'esprit qui doit vivifier la chair, ici c'est la chair qui, par un prodige bien surprenant, vivifie l'esprit, & qui le soutient, & qui l'anime, & qui lui sert de nourriture pour le conserver. Car prenez garde, je vous prie: c'est la r\u00e9flexion de saint Ambroise, quand le Fils de Dieu parloit aux Juifs de ce Sacrement, il ne leur disoit pas: Ego sum cibus; je suis la viande: mais il leur disoit: Caro mea ver\u00e8 est cibus: ma chair est la viande dont il faut que vous soyez spirituellement nourris. Ce n'est point l'ame, ce n'est point la divinit\u00e9 de Jesus-Christ qui fait notre aliment spirituel dans l'Eucharistie, c'est sa chair: Caro mea. Si la divinit\u00e9 & l'ame s'y trouvent, c'est comme parle l'Ecole, par concomitance: ce qui nous nourrit, & ce qui nous est directement donn\u00e9 en qualit\u00e9 de nourriture, c'est la chair de cet Homme-Dieu, dont notre ame est substant\u00e9e & fortifi\u00e9e, & pour me servir du mot de Tertullien, engraiss\u00e9e. Or quel honneur pour une chair, que ce soit elle qui nous rende tout spirituels, elle qui nous communique la grace, & qui nous fasse vivre de la vie de Dieu m\u00eame. Oui, Chr\u00e9tiens, je le r\u00e9p\u00e8te, ce miracle seul \u00e9l\u00e8ve la chair du Sauveur du monde \u00e0 un ordre surnaturel & divin. Car il n'y a que la chair d'un Dieu qui puisse op\u00e9rer de telles merveilles; & Dieu prenant une chair, ne pouvoit plus l'honorer qu'en lui donnant la force & la vertu de les produire. Or tout cela convient \u00e0 la chair de Jesus-Christ dans l'Eucharistie; & c'est ce que l'Eglise exprime en un mot, lorsqu'elle nous la pr\u00e9sente par les mains des Pr\u00eatres: Corpus Domini nostri Jesu Christi custodiat animam tuam in vitam \u00e6ternam. Re\u00e7ois, Chr\u00e9tien, nous dit-elle, re\u00e7ois le corps de ton Seigneur & de ton Dieu: & pourquoi? afin qu'il conserve ton ame pour la vie \u00e9ternelle. Voyez-vous, mes chers Auditeurs, l'inestimable pr\u00e9rogative du corps de Jesus-Christ? Dans l'ordre de la nature, c'est \u00e0 l'ame de conserver le corps; mais dans l'ordre de la grace, c'est le corps de Jesus-Christ qui conserve notre ame; & cet ordre, qui est un ordre de grace pour nous, est pour le corps de Jesus-Christ un ordre de gloire, mais de la gloire la plus \u00e9minente & la plus sublime."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p006", "label": "English", "text": "But the great miracle, the one that contains all the others, the one Jesus Christ has indicated most expressly to us in the Gospel, the one on which men reflect least and which should be meditated on more, the one I find incontestably most glorious for the flesh of the Son of God\u2014I have said it, and must develop it further\u2014is that the flesh of Jesus Christ in the Eucharist is the food of our souls. Although it is only an earthly and material substance, it has the power to give life to our spirits. Whereas by nature it is the spirit that must give life to the flesh, here it is the flesh which, by a most surprising prodigy, gives life to the spirit, sustains and animates it, and serves as nourishment to preserve it. Please pay attention: this is Saint Ambrose's reflection. When the Son of God spoke to the Jews of this Sacrament, he did not say to them, I am food; but he said, My flesh is truly food: my flesh is the food by which you must be spiritually nourished. It is not the soul or the divinity of Jesus Christ that constitutes our spiritual food in the Eucharist; it is his flesh: My flesh. If the divinity and the soul are present there, it is, as the School says, by concomitance. What nourishes us and is given to us directly as nourishment is the flesh of this God-man, by which our soul is sustained and strengthened and, to use Tertullian's word, fattened. What an honour for flesh that it should make us wholly spiritual, communicate grace to us, and make us live by the very life of God! Yes, Christians, I repeat it: this miracle alone raises the flesh of the Saviour of the world to a supernatural and divine order. For only the flesh of one who is God can work such wonders; and God, in taking flesh, could not honour it more than by giving it the strength and power to produce them. All this belongs to the flesh of Jesus Christ in the Eucharist; and this is what the Church expresses in a word when she presents it to us through the hands of priests: May the body of our Lord Jesus Christ preserve your soul unto eternal life. Receive, Christian, she tells us, receive the body of your Lord and your God. And why? That it may preserve your soul for eternal life. Do you see, my dear hearers, the inestimable prerogative of the body of Jesus Christ? In the order of nature, it is the soul's task to preserve the body; but in the order of grace it is the body of Jesus Christ that preserves our soul. And this order, which is an order of grace for us, is for the body of Jesus Christ an order of glory, but of the most eminent and sublime glory.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p007", "label": "French original", "text": "Apr\u00e8s cela faut-il s'\u00e9tonner, que Dieu, par une conduite pleine de sagesse & par une disposition de sa providence, nous ait propos\u00e9 ce Corps \u00e0 adorer dans nos Temples? A qui rendrons-nous plus justement le culte de l'adoration, qu'\u00e0 une chair qui est le principe de notre vie & de notre immortalit\u00e9? Et o\u00f9 l'adorerons-nous avec plus de raison, que dans son Sacrement, puisque c'est-l\u00e0 que Dieu l'a rendue toute-puissante, pour nous animer de la vie de la grace & nous vivifier selon l'esprit. Oui, mes Freres, dit saint Ambroise, nous adorons encore aujourd'hui la chair de notre R\u00e9dempteur, & nous l'adorons dans les myst\u00e8res qu'il a institu\u00e9s lui-m\u00eame, & qui se c\u00e9l\u00e8brent tous les jours sur nos Autels. Voil\u00e0, Chr\u00e9tiens, des paroles bien pressantes contre nos h\u00e9r\u00e9tiques, & qui de tout tems les ont jett\u00e9s dans un \u00e9trange embarras. Cette chair de Jesus-Christ, continue saint Ambroise, a \u00e9t\u00e9 form\u00e9e de la terre aussi-bien que la n\u00f4tre, & la terre est appell\u00e9e dans l'Ecriture l'escabeau des pieds de Dieu; mais cet escabeau consid\u00e9r\u00e9 dans la personne du Sauveur & dans le Sacrement de sa chair, est plus v\u00e9n\u00e9rable que tous les thr\u00f4nes des Rois, & c'est pour cela que nous l'adorons. Je ne s\u00e7avois pas, ajo\u00fbte saint Augustin, ce que Dieu vouloit dire par son Proph\u00e8te, quand il nous ordonne d'adorer l'escabeau de ses pieds, qui est la terre: Adorate scabellum pedum ejus: & je ne comprenois pas comment cela se pouvoit faire sans impi\u00e9t\u00e9: mais j'en ai trouv\u00e9 le secret & le myst\u00e8re dans le Sacrement de Jesus-Christ. Car c'est ce que nous faisons tous les jours, lorsque nous mangeons sa chair, & qu'avant que de la manger nous l'adorons, non-seulement sans superstition, mais avec tout le m\u00e9rite de la foi, parce que cette chair \u00e9tant un aliment de salut, quoiqu'elle soit de terre & l'escabeau m\u00eame des pieds de Dieu, il faut l'adorer: & bien loin que nous p\u00e9chions en l'adorant, ce seroit un crime de ne pas l'adorer. Et quia illam carnem manducandam nobis ad salutem dedit, nemo autem illam manducat nisi pri\u00f9s adoraverit; sic inventum est, quemadmodum adoretur tale scabellum pedum Domini, ut non sol\u00f9m non peccemus adorando, sed peccemus non adorando."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p007", "label": "English", "text": "After this, should we be surprised that God, through a course full of wisdom and a disposition of his providence, has set this Body before us to be adored in our churches? To whom could we more justly render the worship of adoration than to flesh which is the principle of our life and immortality? And where could we adore it with greater reason than in its Sacrament, since it is there that God has made it almighty, to animate us with the life of grace and give us life according to the spirit? Yes, my brethren, says Saint Ambrose, we still adore today the flesh of our Redeemer, and we adore it in the mysteries which he himself instituted and which are celebrated daily on our altars. Christians, these are powerful words against our heretics, which have always placed them in a strange difficulty. This flesh of Jesus Christ, Saint Ambrose continues, was formed from the earth just like ours, and the earth is called in Scripture the footstool of God's feet; but this footstool, considered in the person of the Saviour and in the Sacrament of his flesh, is more venerable than all the thrones of kings, and this is why we adore it. I did not know, Saint Augustine adds, what God meant through his Prophet when he commands us to adore the footstool of his feet, which is the earth: Adore the footstool of his feet. I did not understand how this could be done without impiety; but I have found its secret and mystery in the Sacrament of Jesus Christ. For this is what we do every day when we eat his flesh and, before eating it, adore it, not only without superstition, but with all the merit of faith. Because this flesh is food of salvation, although it is of earth and the very footstool of God's feet, it must be adored; and far from sinning by adoring it, we would commit a crime by not adoring it. And because he gave us that flesh to eat for salvation, and no one eats that flesh unless he has first adored it, a way has thus been found for such a footstool of the Lord's feet to be adored, so that not only do we not sin by adoring, but we sin by not adoring.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p008", "label": "French original", "text": "C'est pour cela, Chr\u00e9tiens, que l'Eglise a institu\u00e9 cette f\u00eate que nous solemnisons sous le titre & \u00e0 l'honneur du corps de Jesus-Christ. Elle a voulu se conformer aux sentimens & \u00e0 l'exemple de Jesus-Christ m\u00eame. Jesus-Christ a pr\u00e9tendu honorer sa chair dans l'Eucharistie; & l'Eglise honore l'Eucharistie pour honorer cette m\u00eame chair. Vous me demandez sur quoi est fond\u00e9e cette c\u00e9r\u00e9monie de porter en pompe le corps du Fils de Dieu? sur les raisons les plus solides & les plus touchantes. Ecoutez-les. On le porte, remarque un s\u00e7avant Th\u00e9ologien: premi\u00e8rement, en m\u00e9moire de ce qu'il se porta lui-m\u00eame, quand il distribua \u00e0 ses Ap\u00f4tres sa chair & son sang. Car alors, dit saint Augustin, il est \u00e9vident qu'il portoit son propre corps; & que ce que l'Ecriture disoit de David dans un sens figur\u00e9, s\u00e7avoir, qu'il se portoit lui-m\u00eame dans ses mains, s'accomplit \u00e0 la lettre dans la personne du Sauveur: ce sont les termes expr\u00e8s de saint Augustin. Mais que fit cet Homme-Dieu, quand il se porta ainsi lui-m\u00eame? il se fit comme un triomphe \u00e0 soi-m\u00eame; car il ne pouvoit \u00eatre plus honorablement port\u00e9 que par soi-m\u00eame & dans ses propres mains. Or c'est le myst\u00e8re que l'Eglise nous repr\u00e9sente aujourd'hui, faisant porter ce corps v\u00e9n\u00e9rable dans les mains des Pr\u00eatres, qui sont comme les propres mains du Fils de Dieu. Mais pourquoi le porter hors du Temple, pourquoi dans les rues & dans les places publiques? c'est, r\u00e9pond l'auteur que j'ai cit\u00e9, en action de grace, de ce qu'il alloit lui-m\u00eame autrefois parcourant les Villes & les Bourgades, faisant le tour de la Jud\u00e9e & de la Galil\u00e9e, & gu\u00e9rissant les malades par-tout o\u00f9 il passoit: Circuibat omnes civitates & castella. Voil\u00e0 pourquoi l'Eglise le fait encore porter par toute la chr\u00e9tient\u00e9; esp\u00e9rant du reste qu'il op\u00e9rera parmi nous les m\u00eames merveilles qu'il op\u00e9roit parmi les Juifs. Car ne doutez pas, mes chers Auditeurs, que ce Sauveur passant aujourd'hui devant vos maisons, ne les ait sanctifi\u00e9es par sa pr\u00e9sence; ne doutez pas qu'il n'ait r\u00e9pandu dans toutes les places publiques des b\u00e9n\u00e9dictions particulieres, & qu'on n'ait pu dire de lui: Pertransiit benefaciendo; il a pass\u00e9, & il a laiss\u00e9 sur tout son passage des effets de sa lib\u00e9ralit\u00e9. C'est ce que Dieu semble avoir voulu nous marquer dans une des plus belles figures de l'ancien Testament. L'Ecriture dit, que parce que Joseph avoit pourv\u00fb de pain toute l'Egypte dans le tems de la st\u00e9rilit\u00e9 & de la famine, le Roi Pharaon le fit monter sur un char, & le fit conduire par toutes les Provinces de son Royaume, avec ordre \u00e0 chacun de l'adorer, & de se prosterner devant lui. Ainsi, parce que le Fils de Dieu nous a donn\u00e9 ce pain c\u00e9leste qui est son Corps, l'Eglise le fait paro\u00eetre comme sur un thr\u00f4ne & sous le dais; & dans cet \u00e9tat elle le conduit dans tous les lieux du monde chr\u00e9tien, ordonnant \u00e0 tous les fid\u00e9les de fl\u00e9chir le genoux devant lui, & de lui pr\u00e9senter leurs respects & leurs adorations. Il y a plus: elle le porte, ajo\u00fbte le bienheureux Ev\u00eaque de Gen\u00e8ve, pour lui faire une r\u00e9paration authentique de tous les opprobres qu'il souffrit dans les rues de J\u00e9rusalem, lorsqu'il fut tra\u00een\u00e9 de Consistoire en Consistoire, & de Tribunal en Tribunal. L'Eglise veut lui faire satisfaction de cette injure; & dans cette vue, elle le porte publiquement & le fait suivre de tout le peuple, avec des acclamations & des chants d'all\u00e9gresse. Enfin, pourquoi le porte-t-elle? voici, Chr\u00e9tiens, la raison capitale. Elle le porte, dit le grand Cardinal du Perron, pour lui faire honneur, mais un honneur solemnel, de toutes les victoires qu'il a remport\u00e9es sur l'h\u00e9r\u00e9sie & sur l'infid\u00e9lit\u00e9, dans le Sacrement de son corps. Ne perdez pas, s'il vous pla\u00eet, cette remarque: nos h\u00e9r\u00e9tiques nous reprochent que ces processions sont des nouveaut\u00e9s, qui n'ont jamais \u00e9t\u00e9 en usage dans les premiers si\u00e9cles de l'Eglise; & nous leur r\u00e9pondons, qu'il faut bien que ce soient des nouveaut\u00e9s, puisqu'elles ne se font qu'en signe de leurs nouvelles erreurs d\u00e9truites & confondues par la v\u00e9rit\u00e9 de l'Eucharistie. On ne portoit point de la sorte autrefois le corps du Fils de Dieu, parce qu'il n'y avoit point encore eu d'erreurs dont il e\u00fbt triomph\u00e9: mais depuis qu'il s'est \u00e9lev\u00e9 des h\u00e9r\u00e9siarques pour le combattre, depuis qu'il y a eu des hommes conjur\u00e9s contre sa pr\u00e9sence r\u00e9elle dans le Sacrement, & que par la force de sa parole il les a foudroy\u00e9s & terrass\u00e9s, l'Eglise s'est crue oblig\u00e9e de lui en ordonner un triomphe. Telle est l'origine de ces processions. Ainsi parloit le s\u00e7avant Pr\u00e9lat dont nous venons de rapporter la pens\u00e9e."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p008", "label": "English", "text": "This is why, Christians, the Church instituted this feast which we solemnly celebrate under the title and in honour of the body of Jesus Christ. She wished to conform to the sentiments and example of Jesus Christ himself. Jesus Christ intended to honour his flesh in the Eucharist, and the Church honours the Eucharist in order to honour that same flesh. You ask me on what this ceremony of carrying the body of the Son of God in splendour is founded? On the soundest and most moving reasons. Listen to them. It is carried, a learned theologian observes, first in remembrance of his carrying himself when he distributed his flesh and blood to his apostles. For then, says Saint Augustine, it is evident that he carried his own body; and what Scripture said figuratively of David, namely that he carried himself in his hands, was fulfilled literally in the person of the Saviour: these are Saint Augustine's express words. But what did this God-man do when he thus carried himself? He made, as it were, a triumphal procession for himself, for he could not be carried more honourably than by himself and in his own hands. This is the mystery the Church represents to us today, having this venerable body carried in the hands of priests, which are as though the very hands of the Son of God. But why carry it outside the church, why into the streets and public squares? It is, replies the author I have cited, in thanksgiving for his once going about the cities and villages himself, travelling through Judaea and Galilee and healing the sick wherever he passed: He went about all the cities and villages. This is why the Church still has him carried throughout Christendom, hoping moreover that he will work among us the same wonders he worked among the Jews. For do not doubt, my dear hearers, that this Saviour, passing before your houses today, has sanctified them by his presence; do not doubt that he has poured special blessings into all the public squares, and that it could be said of him, He went about doing good: he passed, and throughout his passage left effects of his generosity. This is what God seems to have wished to show us in one of the most beautiful figures of the Old Testament. Scripture says that, because Joseph had provided all Egypt with bread in the time of barrenness and famine, King Pharaoh had him mount a chariot and be driven through all the provinces of his kingdom, with orders for everyone to do him homage and prostrate themselves before him. Thus, because the Son of God has given us this heavenly bread which is his Body, the Church displays him as though upon a throne and beneath a canopy; and in this state she conducts him to every part of the Christian world, commanding all the faithful to bend the knee before him and offer him their reverence and adoration. There is more: she carries him, adds the blessed Bishop of Geneva, to make him authentic reparation for all the reproaches he suffered in the streets of Jerusalem, when he was dragged from council to council and from tribunal to tribunal. The Church wishes to make satisfaction to him for this injury; and with this intention she carries him publicly and has all the people follow him with acclamations and songs of gladness. Finally, why does she carry him? Here, Christians, is the principal reason. She carries him, says the great Cardinal du Perron, to honour him, with solemn honour, for all the victories he has won over heresy and unbelief in the Sacrament of his body. Please do not lose sight of this observation: our heretics reproach us that these processions are novelties never practised in the Church's first centuries; and we answer that they must indeed be novelties, since they are held only as a sign of their new errors destroyed and confounded by the truth of the Eucharist. The body of the Son of God was not carried in this way formerly because there had not yet been errors over which he had triumphed. But since heresiarchs arose to fight against him, since men conspired against his real presence in the Sacrament and he struck them down and overthrew them by the force of his word, the Church believed herself obliged to decree him a triumphal procession for it. Such is the origin of these processions. Thus spoke the learned prelate whose thought we have just reported.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p009", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais ajo\u00fbtons un point qui doit encore plus servir \u00e0 notre instruction: disons que par ces processions l'Eglise pr\u00e9tend r\u00e9parer tant d'outrages, qu'ont fait au Sauveur du monde, & que lui font sans cesse les mauvais Chr\u00e9tiens dans l'Eucharistie. Oui, mes chers Auditeurs, c'est pour nous-m\u00eames que l'Eglise a \u00e9tablie cette f\u00eate, en forme d'amende honorable; c'est pour toutes nos profanations, c'est pour tous nos sacril\u00e9ges, c'est pour toutes nos irr\u00e9v\u00e9rences devant les Autels de Jesus-Christ & dans son Sanctuaire; c'est pour tous les scandales que nous y donnons, pour toutes les communions indignes de tant de p\u00e9cheurs hypocrites, pour toutes les Messes c\u00e9l\u00e9br\u00e9es par des Pr\u00eatres vicieux, pour toutes nos froideurs en approchant de la sainte table, pour toutes les n\u00e9gligences m\u00eames qu'y apportent les ames justes; c'est pour les v\u00f4tres, Chr\u00e9tiens, & pour les miennes, depuis tant d'ann\u00e9es que nous fr\u00e9quentons ce myst\u00e8re d'amour; c'est pour vous & pour moi que ces processions sont ordonn\u00e9es, afin que l'honneur qui y est rendu \u00e0 la chair de notre Dieu, la d\u00e9dommage en quelque sorte de toutes les insultes qu'elle a re\u00e7ues jusqu'\u00e0 pr\u00e9sent de nous, & qu'elle en re\u00e7oit tous les jours. Permettez-moi de vous dire une chose qui doit vous confondre, & que vous ne pouvez pleurer trop am\u00e8rement. Que faisons-nous, quand nous manquons de respect envers la sainte Eucharistie? Voici une pens\u00e9e capable, ce me semble, de toucher les c\u0153urs les plus insensibles. Nous faisons que l'Eucharistie institu\u00e9e essentiellement pour honorer la chair du Sauveur, devient pour cette m\u00eame chair un myst\u00e8re d'humiliation, un myst\u00e8re de confusion & de honte, un myst\u00e8re d'ignominie. Pesez bien ce que je dis. Oui, la chair du Sauveur souffre mille fois plus de notre part dans l'Eucharistie, qu'elle n'a jamais souffert des Juifs dans sa passion. Car dans sa passion elle ne souffrit que pour un tems, mais ici elle est expos\u00e9e \u00e0 souffrir jusques \u00e0 la fin des si\u00e9cles: dans sa passion elle ne souffroit qu'autant que Jesus-Christ le vouloit, & que parce qu'il le vouloit; mais ici elle souffre, pour ainsi dire, par force & par violence; si elle souffrit dans sa passion, c'\u00e9toit dans l'\u00e9tat d'une nature passible & mortelle; mais ici elle souffre dans l'\u00e9tat m\u00eame de l'impassibilit\u00e9: ce qu'elle souffrit dans sa passion, \u00e9toit glorieux \u00e0 Dieu & salutaire aux hommes; mais ici ce qu'elle souffre, est pernicieux aux hommes & injurieux \u00e0 Dieu. Ah! Chr\u00e9tiens, les puissans motifs pour r\u00e9veiller & pour exciter toute votre religion \u00e0 l'\u00e9gard de ce grand myst\u00e8re!"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p009", "label": "English", "text": "But let us add a point that should serve our instruction still more: let us say that through these processions the Church intends to repair the many outrages which bad Christians have committed against the Saviour of the world, and continually commit against him in the Eucharist. Yes, my dear hearers, it is for ourselves that the Church established this feast as a solemn act of reparation: for all our profanations, all our sacrileges, all our irreverence before the altars of Jesus Christ and in his sanctuary; for all the scandals we cause there, for all the unworthy communions of so many hypocritical sinners, for all the Masses celebrated by vicious priests, for all our coldness in approaching the holy table, even for all the negligence with which just souls approach it. It is for yours, Christians, and for mine, throughout so many years in which we have frequented this mystery of love; it is for you and for me that these processions are ordained, so that the honour rendered there to the flesh of our God may in some way compensate it for all the insults it has received from us until now and receives every day. Allow me to tell you something that should put you to shame, and which you cannot weep over too bitterly. What do we do when we fail in reverence toward the holy Eucharist? Here is a thought capable, it seems to me, of touching the most insensitive hearts. We cause the Eucharist, instituted essentially to honour the Saviour's flesh, to become for that same flesh a mystery of humiliation, of confusion and shame, of ignominy. Weigh my words well. Yes, the Saviour's flesh suffers a thousand times more at our hands in the Eucharist than it ever suffered from the Jews in his Passion. For in his Passion it suffered only for a time, whereas here it is exposed to suffering until the end of the ages. In his Passion it suffered only so far as Jesus Christ willed it, and because he willed it; but here it suffers, so to speak, by force and violence. If it suffered in his Passion, it was in the condition of a passible and mortal nature; but here it suffers in the very condition of impassibility. What it suffered in his Passion was glorious to God and salutary for men; but here what it suffers is harmful to men and insulting to God. Ah, Christians, what powerful motives to awaken and stir all your devotion toward this great mystery!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p010", "label": "French original", "text": "Quelle doit donc \u00eatre l'occupation d'une ame chr\u00e9tienne pendant les saints jours de cette octave? Ecoutez, Mesdames; \u00e9coutez tous, mes Freres, voici de quoi entretenir votre pi\u00e9t\u00e9. L'occupation d'une ame chr\u00e9tienne en ce saint tems, doit \u00eatre d'entrer dans les sentimens de l'Eglise, & d'honorer avec elle la chair du R\u00e9dempteur. Voil\u00e0 \u00e0 quoi elle doit s'employer. Qu'est-ce \u00e0 dire honorer la chair du R\u00e9dempteur? c'est-\u00e0-dire, lui rendre tout le culte qu'elle peut recevoir de nous, dans le Sacrement de l'Autel; imiter Magdeleine, qui eut un z\u00e9le tout particulier pour cette sainte chair, l'arrosant de ses larmes, l'essuyant de ses cheveux, & r\u00e9pandant sur elle des parfums. Exercice, dit saint Thomas, dont le Fils de Dieu la loua, tout \u00e9loign\u00e9 qu'il \u00e9toit des d\u00e9lices de la vie: pourquoi? parce qu'il aimoit \u00e0 voir que sa chair f\u00fbt honor\u00e9e. De m\u00eame, nous prosterner souvent en la pr\u00e9sence de ce sacr\u00e9 corps, & l\u00e0 lui offrir mille sacrifices de lo\u00fcanges, mille adorations int\u00e9rieures, mille hommages & mille actions de graces. Lui dire quelquefois, mais avec une foi vive, mais avec une d\u00e9votion ardente: Corps divin, corps bienheureux, vous avez \u00e9t\u00e9 le prix de mon salut; que ne dois-je donc pas faire pour vous glorifier? mais puisque vous vous \u00eates mis dans le Sacrement pour y recevoir le tribut de gloire qui vous appartient, comment y a-t-il des Chr\u00e9tiens assez impies pour venir vous y profaner? Du moins j'irai moi, vous pr\u00e9senter mon encens; & je voudrois y conduire avec moi tout ce qu'il y a d'hommes sur la terre. Tels sont, dis-je, les sentimens que nous devons prendre: & parce que le corps de Jesus-Christ doit \u00eatre aujourd'hui port\u00e9 en c\u00e9r\u00e9monie & avec appareil, notre devoir est de contribuer \u00e0 cet appareil & \u00e0 cette c\u00e9r\u00e9monie dans toute l'\u00e9tendue de notre pouvoir. Vous surtout, Mesdames, si curieuses de mille superfluit\u00e9s qui ne servent qu'\u00e0 votre luxe & \u00e0 votre vanit\u00e9, c'est-l\u00e0 que vous le pouvez sanctifier, les consacrant au corps de votre Dieu, les employant \u00e0 enrichir les vases qui le contiennent, \u00e0 embellir les tabernacles o\u00f9 il est renferm\u00e9, \u00e0 parer les oratoires o\u00f9 il doit reposer. Vous \u00eates si soigneuses d'orner vos corps, vous usez pour cela de tant d'artifices, vous faites pour cela tant de d\u00e9penses, vous prenez pour cela tant de mesures & tant de soins: mais vos corps, ces corps infect\u00e9s de la corruption du p\u00e9ch\u00e9, ces corps sujets \u00e0 la pourriture & qui bien-t\u00f4t ne seront que poussiere & cendre, vous doivent-ils \u00eatre plus chers que le corps de Jesus-Christ? Enfin, parce que le corps du Fils de Dieu est enlev\u00e9 hors de ses temples, & port\u00e9 en triomphe, que fait l'ame chr\u00e9tienne? elle le suit dans ce triomphe; c'est-\u00e0-dire, elle l'accompagne dans ces processions & lui fait escorte de sa propre personne. Et c'est, mes chers Auditeurs, ce que l'esprit de Dieu nous a divinement exprim\u00e9 dans l'Epouse des Cantiques: ce passage convient admirablement \u00e0 mon sujet, & l'application que j'en fais vous paro\u00eetra bien naturelle. L'Epouse dit qu'elle a cherch\u00e9 son bien aim\u00e9 dans le lieu ordinaire o\u00f9 il a accoutum\u00e9 de prendre son repos, mais qu'elle ne l'a pas trouv\u00e9: Qu\u00e6sivi quem diligit anima mea, qu\u00e6sivi illum, & non inveni: que l\u00e0-dessus elle a pris r\u00e9solution de sortir, de faire le tour de la ville, d'aller dans les rues & dans les places chercher celui qu'elle aime: Surgam & circuibo civitatem: per vicos & plateas qu\u00e6ram quem diligit anima mea. Elle ajo\u00fbte, que les gardes & les officiers de la ville l'ont rencontr\u00e9e: Invenerunt me vigiles qui custodiunt civitatem; qu'elle leur a demand\u00e9 s'ils n'avoient point v\u00fb son \u00e9poux, & qu'imm\u00e9diatement apr\u00e8s elle l'a apper\u00e7u au milieu d'eux: Paulul\u00f9m c\u00f9m pertransissem eos, inveni quem diligit anima mea; qu'elle a couru \u00e0 lui, qu'elle ne l'a point quitt\u00e9 jusqu'\u00e0 ce qu'elle l'e\u00fbt conduit dans la maison de sa mere: Tenui illum, nec dimittam donec introducam illum in domum matris me\u00e6. Que veut dire tout cela, Chr\u00e9tiens? vous pr\u00e9venez d\u00e9j\u00e0 ma pens\u00e9e. Cette \u00e9pouse est l'ame fid\u00e8le; elle cherche aujourd'hui le Sauveur du monde dans le sanctuaire de l'Eucharistie, qui est comme son lit myst\u00e9rieux, & elle ne l'y trouve pas; elle s'en va donc par les rues, & dans les places publiques, pour voir s'il y sera. C'est l\u00e0 en effet qu'elle le rencontre, environn\u00e9 de gardes, entour\u00e9 de ses ministres qui le portent avec honneur, & de tout le peuple qui lui fait une cour nombreuse; elle se jette \u00e0 ses pieds, elle l'adore, elle le suit des yeux, & elle ne l'abandonne point qu'il ne soit rentr\u00e9 dans le temple d'o\u00f9 il \u00e9toit parti, & qui est proprement la maison de notre mere, puisque c'est la maison de l'Eglise: y a-t-il rien de plus juste que cette figure? Mais reprenons. La gloire du corps de Jesus-Christ est d'avoir \u00e9t\u00e9 donn\u00e9 \u00e0 l'Eglise dans le sacrement de l'Autel: & la gloire aussi de l'Eglise est d'avoir re\u00e7u & de poss\u00e9der le corps de Jesus-Christ dans ce sacrement. Renouvellez, s'il vous pla\u00eet, votre attention pour cette seconde partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p010", "label": "English", "text": "What, then, should occupy a Christian soul during the holy days of this octave? Listen, ladies; listen, all my brethren: here is something to sustain your piety. The occupation of a Christian soul in this holy season should be to enter into the sentiments of the Church and, with her, honour the Redeemer's flesh. This is what it should apply itself to. What does honouring the Redeemer's flesh mean? It means rendering it all the worship it can receive from us in the Sacrament of the Altar; imitating Magdalene, who showed particular zeal for this holy flesh, watering it with her tears, wiping it with her hair and pouring perfumes upon it. An exercise, says Saint Thomas, for which the Son of God praised her, distant though he was from life's delights. Why? Because he loved to see his flesh honoured. Likewise, we should prostrate ourselves often in the presence of this sacred body, and there offer it a thousand sacrifices of praise, a thousand inward adorations, a thousand acts of homage and thanksgiving. We should sometimes say to it, but with living faith and ardent devotion: Divine body, blessed body, you were the price of my salvation; what, then, should I not do to glorify you? Yet, since you have placed yourself in the Sacrament to receive the tribute of glory that belongs to you, how can there be Christians so impious as to come and profane you there? At least I will go and offer you my incense; and I would wish to bring with me every man on earth. Such, I say, are the sentiments we must adopt; and because the body of Jesus Christ is to be carried today with ceremony and splendour, our duty is to contribute to that splendour and ceremony to the full extent of our power. You especially, ladies, so eager for a thousand superfluities serving only your luxury and vanity: here you can sanctify it, consecrating them to the body of your God, employing them to enrich the vessels that contain him, embellish the tabernacles in which he is enclosed, and adorn the oratories where he is to rest. You are so careful to adorn your bodies; you use so many artifices, incur so much expense, take so many measures and so much care for this. But your bodies, these bodies infected with the corruption of sin, subject to decay and soon to be nothing but dust and ashes: should they be dearer to you than the body of Jesus Christ? Finally, because the body of the Son of God is taken out of his churches and carried in triumph, what does the Christian soul do? It follows him in this triumph; that is, it accompanies him in these processions and escorts him in person. And this, my dear hearers, is what the Spirit of God has divinely expressed to us in the Bride of the Canticle. This passage fits my subject admirably, and my application of it will seem quite natural to you. The Bride says that she sought her beloved in the usual place where he is accustomed to rest, but did not find him: I sought him whom my soul loves; I sought him and did not find him. Thereupon she resolved to go out, to make the circuit of the city, to seek him whom she loves in the streets and squares: I will arise and go about the city; through the streets and squares I will seek him whom my soul loves. She adds that the guards and officers of the city met her: The watchmen who guard the city found me; that she asked them whether they had seen her spouse, and that immediately afterwards she perceived him in their midst: When I had passed a little beyond them, I found him whom my soul loves; that she ran to him and did not leave him until she had brought him into her mother's house: I held him, and will not let him go until I bring him into my mother's house. What does all this mean, Christians? You already anticipate my thought. This bride is the faithful soul; today she seeks the Saviour of the world in the sanctuary of the Eucharist, which is as though his mysterious bed, and does not find him there. She therefore goes through the streets and public squares to see whether he is there. There indeed she encounters him, surrounded by guards and his ministers who carry him with honour, and by all the people who form a numerous court around him. She throws herself at his feet, adores him, follows him with her eyes, and does not abandon him until he has returned to the church from which he set out, which is properly our mother's house, since it is the house of the Church. Could anything be more apt than this figure? But let us return to our argument. The glory of the body of Jesus Christ is to have been given to the Church in the Sacrament of the Altar; and the glory of the Church is also to have received and to possess the body of Jesus Christ in this Sacrament. Please renew your attention for this second part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-h004", "label": "French original", "text": "II. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-h004", "label": "English", "text": "PART II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p011", "label": "French original", "text": "Si le Fils de Dieu \u00e9toit int\u00e9ress\u00e9 \u00e0 honorer sa chair, il ne l'\u00e9toit pas moins \u00e0 honorer son corps mystique qui est l'Eglise. Nous ne faisons tous qu'un m\u00eame corps avec Jesus-Christ, dit saint Paul: Vos estis corpus Christi, & membra de membro. En qualit\u00e9 de Sauveur Jesus-Christ est notre chef, & en qualit\u00e9 de justes nous sommes ses membres; & comme il est de l'honneur des membres d'avoir un chef couronn\u00e9 de gloire, aussi est-il de l'honneur du chef de r\u00e9pandre sur ses membres toute la gloire dont ils sont capables. Or c'est ce que Jesus-Christ a fait dans l'institution de la divine Eucharistie, que nous pouvons proprement encore appeller la f\u00eate de l'Eglise, ou la f\u00eate du corps mystique de Jesus-Christ, Festum corporis Christi; pourquoi cela? parce que ce myst\u00e8re est celui dont l'Eglise se tient plus honor\u00e9e, & qui la rend plus glorieuse devant Dieu."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p011", "label": "English", "text": "If the Son of God had an interest in honouring his flesh, he had no less an interest in honouring his mystical body, which is the Church. We all form one and the same body with Jesus Christ, says Saint Paul: You are the body of Christ, and members from a member. As Saviour, Jesus Christ is our head, and as just men we are his members; and just as it is to the members' honour to have a head crowned with glory, so it is to the head's honour to pour upon his members all the glory of which they are capable. This is what Jesus Christ did in instituting the divine Eucharist, which we can also properly call the feast of the Church, or the feast of the mystical body of Jesus Christ: The feast of the body of Christ. Why? Because this mystery is the one by which the Church considers herself most honoured, and which makes her most glorious before God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p012", "label": "French original", "text": "Non, Chr\u00e9tiens, le Sauveur du monde avec toute sa magnificence, ne pouvoit rien faire de plus honorable pour son Eglise ni de plus grand, que de lui laisser le Sacrement de son corps: c'\u00e9toit le comble de toute la gloire qu'il lui pouvoit procurer; & l'on peut bien dire apr\u00e8s cela que cet homme-Dieu avoit pleinement accompli le dessein qu'il s'\u00e9toit form\u00e9, d'avoir, comme parle l'Ap\u00f4tre, une Eglise illustre, \u00e9clatante, enrichie des plus beaux ornemens du ciel: Ut exhiberet ipse sibi gloriosam Ecclesiam; parce qu'en effet la possession du corps & du sang de Jesus-Christ, donne \u00e0 l'Eglise tous ces avantages & toutes ces qualit\u00e9s. Vous voulez s\u00e7avoir comment? Ah! mes chers Auditeurs, la riche matiere \u00e0 vos r\u00e9flexions! Autrefois les Juifs se pr\u00e9f\u00e9roient \u00e0 toutes les nations du monde, & se glorifioient d'avoir un Dieu qui ne d\u00e9daignoit pas de demeurer au milieu d'eux & de marcher avec eux. Non, disoit Moyse, il n'y a point de peuple qui ait des Dieux si proches de soi, & par cons\u00e9quent il n'y a point de peuple sur la terre si honor\u00e9 que nous: Nec est alia natio tam grandis, qu\u00e6 habeat Deos appropinquantes sibi. Mais de quelle mani\u00e8re Dieu demeuroit-il avec les Juifs? par cette Arche d'alliance o\u00f9 il rendoit des oracles, & \u00e0 laquelle il avoit attach\u00e9 sa protection. Cette Arche \u00e9toit-elle le vrai Dieu d'Isra\u00ebl? elle n'en \u00e9toit que la figure, que le tabernacle: & cependant parce qu'elle \u00e9toit plac\u00e9e au milieu des douze Tribus, qu'elle les accompagnoit dans toutes leurs marches, & qu'ils la portoient dans leurs camps & dans leurs arm\u00e9es, ils se vantoient que leur Dieu les suivoit par-tout, & que par-tout il leur \u00e9toit pr\u00e9sent. Mais qu'est-ce que cela, Chr\u00e9tiens, si nous le comparons avec l'honneur que l'Eglise re\u00e7oit, & que nous recevons comme elle dans l'Eucharistie? Un Dieu lui-m\u00eame dans sa propre substance & avec toute la pl\u00e9nitude de sa Divinit\u00e9, demeure corporellement & r\u00e9ellement parmi nous; il r\u00e9side dans nos temples, il vient jusques dans nos maisons; il se laisse non-seulement approcher, mais toucher, mais manger; & c'est bien \u00e0 nous d\u00e9sormais de dire: Nec est alia natio tam grandis, qu\u00e6 habeat deos appropinquantes sibi. Ez\u00e9chiel nous parle d'une cit\u00e9 myst\u00e9rieuse dont il d\u00e9crit les richesses & la grandeur, & qui n'avoit point d'autre nom que celui-ci, c'est le s\u00e9jour de Dieu & Dieu y est: Et nomen civitatis, Dominus ibidem. Mais cette cit\u00e9 ne pouvoit \u00eatre que l'Eglise Chr\u00e9tienne, dont Dieu repr\u00e9sentoit d\u00e9j\u00e0 l'excellence \u00e0 ce Proph\u00e8te. Car quel nom plus propre peut-on donner \u00e0 l'Eglise? Dominus ibidem: c'est-l\u00e0 que Dieu habite, c'est l\u00e0 que par un engagement irr\u00e9vocable il s'est oblig\u00e9 de demeurer jusqu'\u00e0 la consommation des si\u00e9cles; & par quel engagement? par l'Eucharistie qui le tient comme attach\u00e9 \u00e0 son Eglise, sans qu'il puisse jamais s'en s\u00e9parer: Et nomen civitatis, Dominus ibidem."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p012", "label": "English", "text": "No, Christians, with all his magnificence the Saviour of the world could do nothing more honourable or greater for his Church than to leave her the Sacrament of his body. This was the summit of all the glory he could obtain for her; and after this we may well say that this God-man had fully accomplished his purpose of having, as the Apostle says, an illustrious and radiant Church, enriched with heaven's most beautiful ornaments: That he might present to himself a glorious Church. For possession of the body and blood of Jesus Christ does indeed give the Church all these advantages and qualities. You wish to know how? Ah, my dear hearers, what rich material for your reflections! Formerly the Jews preferred themselves to all the nations of the world and gloried in having a God who did not disdain to dwell among them and walk with them. No, said Moses, there is no people whose gods are so near to it, and consequently no people on earth so honoured as we: There is no other nation so great that has gods drawing near to it. But how did God dwell with the Jews? Through that Ark of the Covenant where he gave oracles and to which he had attached his protection. Was this Ark the true God of Israel? It was only his figure, only his tabernacle. Yet because it was placed among the twelve tribes, accompanied them on all their journeys and was carried in their camps and armies, they boasted that their God followed them everywhere and was everywhere present to them. But what is that, Christians, if we compare it with the honour the Church receives, and which we receive with her, in the Eucharist? God himself, in his own substance and with all the fullness of his Divinity, dwells bodily and really among us. He resides in our churches and comes even into our houses; he allows himself not only to be approached but touched and eaten. It is truly for us henceforth to say: There is no other nation so great that has gods drawing near to it. Ezekiel speaks to us of a mysterious city whose riches and greatness he describes, and which had no other name than this: it is God's dwelling, and God is there: And the name of the city: The Lord is there. But this city could only be the Christian Church, whose excellence God was already representing to this Prophet. For what more fitting name can be given to the Church? The Lord is there. There God dwells; there, by an irrevocable engagement, he has bound himself to remain until the consummation of the ages. And by what engagement? By the Eucharist, which holds him, as it were, attached to his Church, so that he can never separate himself from her: And the name of the city: The Lord is there.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p013", "label": "French original", "text": "Cependant est-ce en cela seul que consiste tout l'honneur qui revient \u00e0 l'Eglise, de ce Sacrement? Non, Chr\u00e9tiens, il y a quelque chose de plus important: Ecoutez-le. Etre honor\u00e9 de la pr\u00e9sence d'un Dieu, cela est grand; mais \u00eatre honor\u00e9 de ses entretiens, mais \u00eatre honor\u00e9 de la familiarit\u00e9 la plus intime, c'est bien encore une autre gloire. Or tel est l'avantage de l'Eglise dans le Sacrement du corps de Jesus-Christ. Que fait Jesus-Christ dans ce myst\u00e8re, demande l'Abb\u00e9 Rupert? il y converse avec les hommes, il y visite les hommes, & il y est visit\u00e9 des hommes; il y \u00e9coute les plaintes des hommes, il y re\u00e7oit les requ\u00eates que lui pr\u00e9sentent les hommes, il y accorde les diff\u00e9rends des hommes, il y instruit, il y console les hommes. Parce que les hommes sont les membres de son Eglise, c'est \u00e0 son Eglise qu'il d\u00e9f\u00e9re tout cet honneur. Surquoi, mes Freres, il me souvient d'une remarque qu'a fait Guillaume de Paris, expliquant la proph\u00e9tie de Daniel. Quand le Roi de Babylone consulta les devins sur la vision qu'il avoit eue, & qu'il les obligea de lui dire le songe qui l'avoit occup\u00e9 pendant son sommeil, ils lui r\u00e9pondirent qu'il n'y avoit point d'homme mortel qui le p\u00fbt faire; que cela n'appartenoit qu'aux Dieux, parce que les Dieux n'avoient point de commerce avec les hommes: Nec reperitur quisquam qui indicet illum, exceptis diis, quorum non est cum hominibus conversatio. Cette parole, dit l'Ecriture, l'irrita; & il reconnut que toute la sagesse des devins n'\u00e9toit qu'erreur & que mensonge: pourquoi? ah! r\u00e9pond Guillaume de Paris, il y eut en ceci du myst\u00e8re. Ils pr\u00e9supposoient que les Dieux du ciel ne s'abaissoient pas jusqu'\u00e0 s'entretenir avec les hommes; & en cela ils firent paro\u00eetre, sans y penser, leur ignorance, parce qu'il y avoit un Dieu, le Dieu des Chr\u00e9tiens, qui devoit honorer un jour les hommes de sa conversation & qui mettroit l\u00e0 ses plus cheres d\u00e9lices: Delici\u00e6 me\u00e6 esse cum filiis hominum. Voil\u00e0, dis-je, la pr\u00e9rogative de l'Eglise de Jesus-Christ, de pouvoir traiter famili\u00e9rement avec son Dieu; & par-l\u00e0, reprend saint Chrysost\u00f4me, nous avons en quelque sorte sur la terre le m\u00eame avantage que les bienheureux dans le ciel. Car le bonheur du ciel est de poss\u00e9der Dieu, & ne le poss\u00e9dons-nous pas tout entier dans la divine Eucharistie? Jesus-Christ, ajo\u00fbte saint Chrysost\u00f4me, se trouvoit partag\u00e9 entre l'Eglise triomphante & l'Eglise militante; elles disputoient \u00e0 qui auroit son corps adorable, & l'une & l'autre y pr\u00e9tendoient: mais ce nouveau Salomon a fait ce que le premier avec toute sa sagesse ne put faire. Sans diviser son corps, il l'a donn\u00e9 \u00e0 l'une & \u00e0 l'autre: \u00e0 l'Eglise triomphante, il l'a donn\u00e9 sans voile & \u00e0 d\u00e9couvert; \u00e0 la militante, il l'a donn\u00e9 sous les esp\u00e9ces de son Sacrement."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p013", "label": "English", "text": "Yet does all the honour that comes to the Church from this Sacrament consist in this alone? No, Christians, there is something more important: listen to it. To be honoured by the presence of one who is God is great; but to be honoured by his conversation, by the most intimate familiarity, is yet another glory. Such is the Church's advantage in the Sacrament of the body of Jesus Christ. What does Jesus Christ do in this mystery, asks Abbot Rupert? He converses with men there, visits men and is visited by men; he listens to men's complaints, receives the petitions men present to him, reconciles their disputes, instructs and consoles them. Because men are members of his Church, it is to his Church that he accords all this honour. On this subject, my brethren, I recall an observation made by William of Paris in explaining Daniel's prophecy. When the king of Babylon consulted the diviners about the vision he had seen and required them to tell him the dream that had occupied him in his sleep, they answered that no mortal man could do so; that this belonged only to the gods, because the gods had no dealings with men: Nor is anyone found who can disclose it, except the gods, whose conversation is not with men. This statement, Scripture says, angered him; and he recognised that all the diviners' wisdom was only error and falsehood. Why? Ah, William of Paris replies, there was mystery in this. They presupposed that the gods of heaven did not stoop to converse with men; and in this, without thinking, they displayed their ignorance, because there was a God, the God of Christians, who would one day honour men with his conversation and place his dearest delights in it: My delights are to be with the sons of men. This, I say, is the prerogative of the Church of Jesus Christ: to be able to deal familiarly with her God. And thereby, Saint Chrysostom continues, we have in a certain way on earth the same advantage as the blessed in heaven. For heaven's happiness is to possess God, and do we not possess him wholly in the divine Eucharist? Jesus Christ, Saint Chrysostom adds, found himself divided between the triumphant Church and the militant Church; they disputed which should have his adorable body, and both laid claim to it. But this new Solomon did what the first, with all his wisdom, could not do. Without dividing his body, he gave it to both: to the triumphant Church he gave it unveiled and openly; to the militant Church he gave it under the appearances of his Sacrament.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p014", "label": "French original", "text": "Peut-on, Chr\u00e9tiens, ench\u00e9rir sur ces pens\u00e9es? oui, on le peut; & voici des avantages encore mille fois plus grands; & quoi? souffrez que je les ramasse en abr\u00e9g\u00e9, & que je vous en propose seulement l'id\u00e9e, capables de ravir d'admiration les Anges & les hommes. C'est que le Sacrement de l'Eucharistie est pour nous & pour tous les fid\u00e9les qui le re\u00e7oivent, une extension continuelle & perp\u00e9tuelle du myst\u00e8re de l'incarnation. Ainsi parlent les Peres. Vous s\u00e7avez \u00e0 quel point d'honneur fut \u00e9lev\u00e9e l'humanit\u00e9 de Jesus-Christ, dans ce bienheureux moment qui l'unit au Verbe divin. Or je dis que Jesus-Christ se donnant \u00e0 nous par le Sacrement de l'Autel, a fait entrer tous les membres de son Eglise en communication de la m\u00eame gloire, puisqu'il vient en nous, qu'il s'unit \u00e0 nous, qu'il ne fait, pour ainsi-dire, qu'un avec nous. Et c'est de-l\u00e0, selon la doctrine de saint Cyrille, fond\u00e9e sur la parole du Fils de Dieu, que ce Sacrement s'appelle communion; Qui manducat meam carnem & bibit meum sanguinem, in me manet, & ego in eo. D'o\u00f9 il s'ensuit m\u00eame encore, que dans une certaine propri\u00e9t\u00e9 de termes, le Sauveur du monde est \u00e0 tous momens comme incarn\u00e9 de nouveau, entre les mains des Pr\u00eatres qui sont ses ministres. O veneranda sacerdotum dignitas, in quorum manibus Filius Dei perpetu\u00f2 incarnatur, s'\u00e9crie saint Augustin! O v\u00e9n\u00e9rable & sacr\u00e9 caract\u00e8re des Pr\u00eatres, puisque Jesus-Christ, puisque le Fils du Pere \u00e9ternel, puisque notre Dieu, qui ne s'est incarn\u00e9 qu'une fois dans le sein de Marie, s'incarne sans cesse dans leurs mains! Jugez, Chr\u00e9tiens, de cet honneur, par celui que Dieu fit \u00e0 Marie, quand il la choisit pour sa mere. Nous rendons \u00e0 cette Vierge un culte singulier de religion, parce que c'est en elle que le Verbe s'est fait chair; & que devons-nous penser des Pr\u00eatres qui ont le pouvoir de le former dans leurs propres mains, de le produire dans l'efficace de leur parole, de le faire reposer dans leur sein, non pas une fois, mais autant de fois qu'ils c\u00e9l\u00e9brent les saints myst\u00e8res?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p014", "label": "English", "text": "Can one, Christians, go beyond these thoughts? Yes, one can; and here are advantages a thousand times greater still. What are they? Allow me to gather them briefly and offer you only their idea, capable of carrying angels and men away in admiration. It is that the Sacrament of the Eucharist is, for us and all the faithful who receive it, a continual and perpetual extension of the mystery of the Incarnation. Thus speak the Fathers. You know to what a height of honour the humanity of Jesus Christ was raised at that blessed moment which united it to the divine Word. I say that Jesus Christ, giving himself to us through the Sacrament of the Altar, has brought all the members of his Church into participation in the same glory, since he comes into us, unites himself to us, and becomes, so to speak, one with us. Hence, according to Saint Cyril's teaching founded on the word of the Son of God, this Sacrament is called communion: He who eats my flesh and drinks my blood remains in me, and I in him. From this it follows further that, in a certain proper sense of the terms, the Saviour of the world is at every moment as though incarnate anew in the hands of priests who are his ministers. O venerable dignity of priests, in whose hands the Son of God is perpetually incarnate, cries Saint Augustine! O venerable and sacred character of priests, since Jesus Christ, the Son of the eternal Father, our God, who became incarnate only once in Mary's womb, becomes incarnate without ceasing in their hands! Judge this honour, Christians, by that which God did to Mary when he chose her for his mother. We render this Virgin a special religious veneration because it was in her that the Word became flesh; and what must we think of priests who have the power to form him in their own hands, to bring him forth through the efficacy of their word, to make him rest in their bosom, not once, but as often as they celebrate the holy mysteries?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p015", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais pourquoi entrer dans les secrets de la divine Eucharistie, pour conno\u00eetre les privil\u00e9ges de gloire que l'Eglise y trouve? Arr\u00eatons-nous \u00e0 ce qui se pr\u00e9sente d'abord dans ce myst\u00e8re, \u00e0 ce qui en fait toute la substance, \u00e0 ce que nous voyons, \u00e0 ce qui frappe nos sens. Car c'est-l\u00e0 que Jesus-Christ pour honorer son Eglise, la repa\u00eet de son corps, lui donne son sang pour breuvage & sa chair pour aliment; c'est-\u00e0-dire, la chair d'un Dieu, le sang d'un Dieu, le corps d'un Dieu. Ah! Chr\u00e9tiens, que dirons nous apr\u00e8s cela? Pouvons-nous jamais exprimer ce qui est au-dessus de toute expression, au-dessus de toutes nos pens\u00e9es, & m\u00eame de tous les souhaits de notre c\u0153ur? Etre nourri de la chair d'un Dieu, c'\u00e9toit \u00e0 l'Eglise comme \u00e0 la fille de Sion, comme \u00e0 l'Epouse du Roi de gloire, & particuli\u00e9rement comme au corps mystique de Jesus-Christ, qu'un tel honneur \u00e9toit r\u00e9serv\u00e9. Car il faut que l'\u00e9pouse soit nourrie conform\u00e9ment \u00e0 la grandeur de son \u00e9poux, la fille par rapport \u00e0 la noblesse de son pere, & les membres du corps selon la dignit\u00e9 du chef. Or pour l'\u00e9pouse d'un Dieu, pour la fille d'un Dieu, pour le corps mystique d'un Dieu, il n'y avoit que la chair d'un Dieu qui p\u00fbt \u00eatre une viande sortable. Pour les Juifs, qui furent les esclaves de Dieu, c'\u00e9toit assez, dit saint J\u00e9r\u00f4me, de manger la manne, appell\u00e9e dans l'Ecriture le pain des Anges: mais \u00e0 nous que Dieu a annoblis jusqu'\u00e0 nous faire ses enfans d'adoption, mais \u00e0 l'Eglise qui a \u00e9t\u00e9 engendr\u00e9e du sang de Jesus-Christ, le pain des Anges ne suffit pas; il faut le pain de Dieu, & c'est pour cela que Jesus-Christ nous le donne dans l'Eucharistie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p015", "label": "English", "text": "But why enter into the secrets of the divine Eucharist to know the privileges of glory that the Church finds there? Let us stop at what first presents itself in this mystery, what constitutes its whole substance, what we see and what strikes our senses. For there Jesus Christ, to honour his Church, feeds her with his body, gives her his blood to drink and his flesh for food: that is, the flesh of one who is God, the blood of one who is God, the body of one who is God. Ah, Christians, what shall we say after this? Can we ever express what is above all expression, above all our thoughts and even all our heart's desires? To be nourished with the flesh of one who is God: such an honour was reserved for the Church as the daughter of Sion, as the Bride of the King of glory, and particularly as the mystical body of Jesus Christ. For the bride must be nourished in accordance with her spouse's greatness, the daughter according to her father's nobility, and the body's members according to the dignity of their head. Now, for the bride of one who is God, for his daughter, for his mystical body, only the flesh of one who is God could be fitting food. For the Jews, who were God's slaves, it was enough, says Saint Jerome, to eat manna, called in Scripture the bread of angels. But for us, whom God has ennobled to the point of making us his adopted children, for the Church which was brought forth from the blood of Jesus Christ, the bread of angels is not enough; we need the bread of God, and that is why Jesus Christ gives it to us in the Eucharist.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p016", "label": "French original", "text": "De tout ce que j'ai dit, Chr\u00e9tiens, remportons deux sentimens, qui sont les cons\u00e9quences naturelles de ce discours; l'un de respect & de v\u00e9n\u00e9ration pour l'Eglise, & l'autre de z\u00e9le pour l'innocence & la puret\u00e9 de nos corps. Respect & v\u00e9n\u00e9ration pour l'Eglise, qui est le corps mystique de Jesus-Christ. Car pouvons-nous l'honorer assez, apr\u00e8s que Jesus-Christ lui-m\u00eame l'a tant honor\u00e9e? C'est par elle qu'il nous donne sa chair & son sang: c'est \u00e0 elle qu'il veut que nous en soyons redevables, puisqu'il l'en a fait la d\u00e9positaire; & si nous recevions ce sang & cette chair divine par d'autres mains que par les siennes, la chair & le sang de Jesus-Christ, non-seulement ne nous seroient plus salutaires, mais deviendroient pour nous le poison le plus mortel. Il est vrai, c'est Marie mere de Jesus qui d'abord nous l'a donn\u00e9, ce sacr\u00e9 corps: mais Marie apr\u00e8s tout ne nous l'a donn\u00e9 qu'une fois, & l'Eglise nous le donne tous les jours; mais Marie nous l'a donn\u00e9 \u00e0 tous en g\u00e9n\u00e9ral, & l'Eglise nous le donne \u00e0 chacun en particulier; mais Marie nous l'a donn\u00e9 comme un Sauveur qui devoit r\u00e9gner sur nous, & l'Eglise nous le donne comme une viande qui s'unit \u00e0 nous. D'o\u00f9 il nous est toujours ais\u00e9 de conclure ce que nous devons \u00e0 cette \u00e9pouse du Fils de Dieu, avec quelle fid\u00e9lit\u00e9 nous devons lui demeurer attach\u00e9s, avec quelle ardeur nous devons d\u00e9fendre ses int\u00e9r\u00eats, avec quelle docilit\u00e9 nous devons recevoir ses ordres, avec quelle pi\u00e9t\u00e9 & quelle soumission nous devons les ex\u00e9cuter. Cependant \u00e0 quels combats & \u00e0 quelles insultes ne s'est-elle pas vue expos\u00e9e, en nous faisant le don le plus pr\u00e9cieux, & m\u00eame parce qu'elle nous le faisoit & nous le conservoit? Car vous s\u00e7avez combien de fois les h\u00e9r\u00e9tiques sont entr\u00e9s dans ses Temples pour le lui arracher: vous s\u00e7avez quels exc\u00e8s ils y ont commis; comment ils ont souill\u00e9 son Sanctuaire, renvers\u00e9 ses Autels, bris\u00e9 ses Tabernacles, enlev\u00e9 ses vases sacr\u00e9s; comment ils ont port\u00e9 leurs mains sacril\u00e9ges & parricides jusques sur ses Enfans, jusques sur ses Ministres, jusques sur son Epoux & son redoutable Sacrement. Attentats dont le souvenir nous saisit encore d'horreur. Mais, Chr\u00e9tiens, ce qu'il y a de plus d\u00e9plorable, c'est que cette mere des fid\u00e9les, ainsi outrag\u00e9e par ses ennemis, re\u00e7oit de nous tous les jours les m\u00eames outrages; & n'est-ce pas pour cela qu'elle peut bien dire dans l'amertume de la douleur: Filios enutrivi, & exaltavi; ipsi autem spreverunt me; j'ai form\u00e9 des enfans, je les ai \u00e9lev\u00e9s dans mon sein, je les ai nourris du lait de la plus saine doctrine, je leur ai donn\u00e9 un aliment tout divin, & ils m'ont m\u00e9pris\u00e9e. Car prenez garde, mes chers Auditeurs, & du moins faisons-y quelques r\u00e9flexions; les h\u00e9r\u00e9tiques l'ont m\u00e9pris\u00e9e en profanant ses Temples; & par tant de scandaleuses irr\u00e9v\u00e9rences, n'en sommes-nous pas les profanateurs? Les h\u00e9r\u00e9tiques l'ont m\u00e9pris\u00e9e en souillant son Sanctuaire, en renversant ses Autels, en brisant ses Tabernacles; & combien peut-\u00eatre dans ce m\u00eame Sanctuaire, \u00e0 la face de ces m\u00eames Autels, devant ces m\u00eames Tabernacles, tout sanctifi\u00e9s qu'ils sont par la pr\u00e9sence r\u00e9elle de Jesus-Christ, avez-vous form\u00e9 de criminels desseins, & entretenu de honteuses passions? Les h\u00e9r\u00e9tiques l'ont m\u00e9pris\u00e9e en se jouant de ses myst\u00e8res, & en deshonorant son adorable Sacrement; & n'est-ce pas le deshonorer ce m\u00eame Sacrement, que de le recevoir dans l'\u00e9tat de votre p\u00e9ch\u00e9? n'est-ce pas vous jouer de ces divins myst\u00e8res, que d'y assister avec aussi peu d'attention, avec aussi peu de respect & de retenue, qu'aux assembl\u00e9es les plus mondaines? Quand les h\u00e9r\u00e9tiques l'ont m\u00e9pris\u00e9e; c'\u00e9toient les ennemis d\u00e9clar\u00e9s & les pers\u00e9cuteurs; & d\u00e8s-l\u00e0 leurs m\u00e9pris lui devenoient beaucoup moins sensibles: mais les n\u00f4tres la doivent toucher d'autant plus que nous sommes son troupeau, que nous sommes ses Disciples, que nous sommes ses enfans: Filios enutrivi, & exaltavi; ipsi autem spreverunt me."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p016", "label": "English", "text": "From all I have said, Christians, let us carry away two sentiments which are the natural consequences of this discourse: one of respect and veneration for the Church, the other of zeal for the innocence and purity of our bodies. Respect and veneration for the Church, which is the mystical body of Jesus Christ. For can we honour her enough after Jesus Christ himself has honoured her so greatly? It is through her that he gives us his flesh and blood; it is to her that he wishes us to be indebted for them, since he has made her their custodian. If we received this blood and divine flesh through hands other than hers, the flesh and blood of Jesus Christ would not only cease to be salutary for us, but would become the deadliest poison. It is true that Mary, the mother of Jesus, first gave us this sacred body. Yet Mary, after all, gave him to us only once, and the Church gives him to us every day; Mary gave him to us all in general, and the Church gives him to each of us in particular; Mary gave him to us as a Saviour who was to reign over us, and the Church gives him to us as food that unites itself to us. Hence it is always easy for us to conclude what we owe to this spouse of the Son of God: with what fidelity we must remain attached to her, with what ardour defend her interests, with what docility receive her commands, and with what piety and submission carry them out. Yet to what attacks and insults has she not seen herself exposed in giving us the most precious gift, and even because she gave it to us and preserved it for us? For you know how often heretics entered her churches to tear it away from her; you know the excesses they committed there: how they defiled her sanctuary, overturned her altars, shattered her tabernacles, carried off her sacred vessels; how they laid their sacrilegious and parricidal hands upon her children, her ministers, even her Spouse and his awe-inspiring Sacrament. Attacks whose memory still fills us with horror. But, Christians, what is most deplorable is that this mother of the faithful, thus outraged by her enemies, receives the same outrages from us every day. Is this not why she may well say in the bitterness of sorrow: I nourished children and raised them up, but they despised me; I formed children, raised them in my bosom, nourished them with the milk of the soundest doctrine, gave them wholly divine food, and they despised me. Take heed, my dear hearers, and at least let us reflect a little upon it. The heretics despised her by profaning her churches; and through so much scandalous irreverence, are we not their profaners? The heretics despised her by defiling her sanctuary, overturning her altars and breaking her tabernacles; and how often, perhaps, in that very sanctuary, in the presence of those same altars and before those same tabernacles, wholly sanctified though they are by the real presence of Jesus Christ, have you formed criminal designs and entertained shameful passions? The heretics despised her by mocking her mysteries and dishonouring her adorable Sacrament; and is it not dishonouring that same Sacrament to receive it in the state of your sin? Is it not mocking these divine mysteries to attend them with as little attention, as little respect and restraint, as the most worldly gatherings? When heretics despised her, they were her declared enemies and persecutors, and for that very reason their contempt became much less painful to her. But ours must affect her all the more because we are her flock, her disciples, her children: I nourished children and raised them up, but they despised me.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p017", "label": "French original", "text": "Je dis de plus, que nous devons remporter un sentiment de z\u00e9le pour l'innocence & la puret\u00e9 de nos corps. Oui, mes chers Auditeurs, tout m\u00e9prisables d'ailleurs que nous pouvons \u00eatre, nous devons, si je l'ose dire, nous honorer nous-m\u00eames, puisque nous participons tous \u00e0 cette glorieuse qualit\u00e9 de corps mystique du R\u00e9dempteur, & que c'est de nous comme de l'Eglise que saint Paul a dit: Vos estis corpus Christi; vous \u00eates le corps de Jesus-Christ. Quelque vils que soient nos corps par eux-m\u00eames, nous devons n\u00e9anmoins avoir pour eux un certain respect, que la foi de l'Eucharistie nous doit inspirer & que la pi\u00e9t\u00e9 doit entretenir: pourquoi? non plus seulement parce que nos corps sont les temples du Saint-Esprit, selon l'Ecriture; cela dit beaucoup, mais cela ne dit pas encore assez: non plus seulement parce qu'ils sont les sanctuaires vivans o\u00f9 le corps de Jesus-Christ repose; c'est encore trop peu; mais parce qu'en vertu de la communion, ils deviennent les membres de Jesus-Christ m\u00eame, ainsi que l'Ap\u00f4tre nous l'enseigne: Nescitis quoniam corpora vestra membra sunt Christi? Ne s\u00e7avez-vous pas, disoit-il aux Corinthiens, que vos corps sont les membres de Jesus-Christ; & par cons\u00e9quent que vous n'\u00eates plus ma\u00eetres d'en disposer, mais qu'ils appartiennent \u00e0 Jesus-Christ, qu'ils sont affect\u00e9s \u00e0 Jesus-Christ, qu'ils sont du corps de Jesus-Christ: Et non estis vestri. Ah! Chr\u00e9tiens, la grande v\u00e9rit\u00e9, & le grand motif pour conserver vos corps innocens & purs! Voil\u00e0 l'importante morale, sur laquelle insistoit continuellement saint Paul dans les instructions qu'il faisoit aux Chr\u00e9tiens: il avoit du z\u00e9le pour la sanctification de leurs ames; mais il avoit encore un z\u00e9le sp\u00e9cial pour la sanctification de leurs corps, parce qu'il les consid\u00e9roit comme les membres de Jesus-Christ. Voil\u00e0 surquoi il s'expliquoit dans les termes les plus \u00e9nergiques & les plus forts. Quelle indignit\u00e9, mes Freres, & quelle horreur! ces membres de Jesus-Christ, les profaner, les souiller, les livrer aux sales d\u00e9sirs d'une prostitu\u00e9e! Pl\u00fbt au ciel, mon cher Auditeur, que je n'eusse pas plus lieu que l'Ap\u00f4tre de vous faire le m\u00eame reproche! mais \u00e0 quoi ne vous a pas port\u00e9 la corruption du si\u00e9cle, \u00e0 quels d\u00e9bordemens & \u00e0 quelles profanations? Je dis \u00e0 quelles profanations: car ne vous croyez pas seulement profanateurs du corps de Jesus-Christ, quand vous le recevez dans l'\u00e9tat de votre p\u00e9ch\u00e9; mais vous l'\u00eates encore, comment? par ces volupt\u00e9s brutales & ces plaisirs infames o\u00f9 vous plonge la passion, & qui d\u00e9shonorent le corps du Sauveur en d\u00e9shonorant le v\u00f4tre. Tellement que je puis alors prononcer contre vous le m\u00eame anath\u00eame que saint Paul a prononc\u00e9 contre les Chr\u00e9tiens sacril\u00e9ges: Reus erit corporis & sanguinis Domini, non dijudicans corpus Domini. Parce que vous n'avez pas fait dans vous-m\u00eame le juste discernement qu'il falloit faire du corps du Seigneur, vous \u00eates coupables devant Dieu de ce corps & de ce sang pr\u00e9cieux. N'attirons pas sur nous, mes chers Auditeurs, ce terrible arr\u00eat. Ne renversons pas les favorables desseins de Jesus-Christ. Honorons sur la terre par la saintet\u00e9 de nos corps, la saintet\u00e9 du corps de cet Homme-Dieu, afin d'avoir part \u00e0 sa gloire dans le ciel, o\u00f9 nous conduise, &c."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-p017", "label": "English", "text": "I say further that we must carry away a sentiment of zeal for the innocence and purity of our bodies. Yes, my dear hearers, contemptible as we may otherwise be, we must, if I dare say so, honour ourselves, since we all share in this glorious quality of being the Redeemer's mystical body, and it is of us as of the Church that Saint Paul said: You are the body of Christ; you are the body of Jesus Christ. However lowly our bodies may be in themselves, we must nevertheless have a certain respect for them, which faith in the Eucharist must inspire and piety must sustain. Why? No longer merely because our bodies are the temples of the Holy Spirit, according to Scripture; that says much, but still not enough. No longer merely because they are the living sanctuaries where the body of Jesus Christ rests; that is still too little. But because, by virtue of communion, they become the members of Jesus Christ himself, as the Apostle teaches us: Do you not know that your bodies are members of Christ? Do you not know, he said to the Corinthians, that your bodies are the members of Jesus Christ, and consequently that you are no longer masters to dispose of them, but that they belong to Jesus Christ, that they are assigned to Jesus Christ, that they are of the body of Jesus Christ: And you are not your own. Ah, Christians, what a great truth and a great motive to preserve your bodies innocent and pure! This is the important moral teaching on which Saint Paul continually insisted in the instructions he gave to Christians. He had zeal for the sanctification of their souls, but he also had a special zeal for the sanctification of their bodies, because he considered them members of Jesus Christ. On this he expressed himself in the most vigorous and forceful terms. What indignity, my brethren, and what horror: to profane these members of Jesus Christ, defile them, surrender them to the filthy desires of a prostitute! Would to heaven, my dear hearer, that I had no more cause than the Apostle to make the same reproach against you! But to what has the corruption of the age not driven you, to what excesses and profanations? I say, to what profanations: for do not consider yourselves profaners of the body of Jesus Christ only when you receive him in the state of your sin. You are so in another way as well. How? Through those brutal sensual pleasures and infamous delights into which passion plunges you, which dishonour the Saviour's body by dishonouring your own. So that I can then pronounce against you the same anathema that Saint Paul pronounced against sacrilegious Christians: He will be guilty of the body and blood of the Lord, not discerning the body of the Lord. Because you have not made within yourself the proper discernment you should have made of the Lord's body, you are guilty before God of this body and this precious blood. Let us not draw this terrible sentence upon ourselves, my dear hearers. Let us not overturn the favourable designs of Jesus Christ. Let us honour on earth, through the holiness of our bodies, the holiness of the body of this God-man, so that we may share in his glory in heaven, where may he lead us, &c.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n001", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan. c. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n001", "label": "English", "text": "John, chapter 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n002", "label": "French original", "text": "Psalmo. 57."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n002", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 57.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n022", "label": "French original", "text": "Ambro."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n022", "label": "English", "text": "Ambrose.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n003", "label": "French original", "text": "Matth. c. 9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n003", "label": "English", "text": "Matthew, chapter 9.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n004", "label": "French original", "text": "Act. c. 10."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n004", "label": "English", "text": "Acts, chapter 10.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n005", "label": "French original", "text": "Cant. c. 3."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n005", "label": "English", "text": "Song of Songs, chapter 3.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n006", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n006", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n007", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n007", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n008", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n008", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n009", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n009", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n010", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 12."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n010", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, chapter 12.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n011", "label": "French original", "text": "Ephes. c. 5."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n011", "label": "English", "text": "Ephesians, chapter 5.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n012", "label": "French original", "text": "Deut. c. 4."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n012", "label": "English", "text": "Deuteronomy, chapter 4.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n013", "label": "French original", "text": "Ezech. c. 48."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n013", "label": "English", "text": "Ezekiel, chapter 48.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n014", "label": "French original", "text": "Daniel. c. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n014", "label": "English", "text": "Daniel, chapter 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n015", "label": "French original", "text": "Prov. c. 8."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n015", "label": "English", "text": "Proverbs, chapter 8.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n016", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan. c. 6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n016", "label": "English", "text": "John, chapter 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n017", "label": "French original", "text": "August."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n017", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n018", "label": "French original", "text": "Isai. c. 2."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n018", "label": "English", "text": "Isaiah, chapter 2.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n019", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n019", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, chapter 9.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n020", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n020", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n021", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-n021", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, chapter 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-ed001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The printed margin gives Psalm 57, but the quotation is from Psalm 98:5 in historical Vulgate numbering (99:5 in most modern Bibles). The printed reference is preserved."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-ed002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "This copy prints Isaiah 2 beside a quotation from Isaiah 1:2. Another copy prints chapter 1. This reading retains the reference in the copy used here."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-ed003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The printed margin gives 1 Corinthians 9 and then \u201cIbid.\u201d beside quotations from 1 Corinthians 6:15 and 6:19. The opening \u201cYou are the body of Christ\u201d is from 1 Corinthians 12:27; that reference was added by the editors. The printed references are preserved."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-ed004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The printed margin gives 1 Corinthians 1 beside a quotation combining 1 Corinthians 11:27 and 11:29. Another copy prints chapter 11. This reading retains the reference in the copy used here."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement-ed005", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The sermon attributes this explanation to Augustine, but the margin names Ambrose. Both attributions are preserved; the discrepancy remains unresolved."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "On the Blessed Sacrament: Christ\u2019s Body and the Church", "topics": ["Eucharist", "Corpus Christi", "Church", "adoration", "holiness"], "url": "sermons/bourdaloue-mysteres-sacrement.html"}, {"author": "Louis Bourdaloue", "calendar": "dayKey:trinity", "calendar_label": "Trinity Sunday", "centuries": [17, 18], "composition": "Approximately late seventeenth century\u20131704; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite", "language": "fr", "language_label": "French", "metadata": ["Louis Bourdaloue, Sermons du P\u00e8re Bourdalou\u00eb, de la Compagnie de Jesus, sur les myst\u00e8res, tome premier, quatri\u00e8me \u00e9dition, edited by Fran\u00e7ois de Paule Bretonneau (Paris: chez Rigaud, 1726), Sermon sur la tr\u00e8s-Sainte Trinit\u00e9, pp. 476\u2013511. Kelly Library copy.", "Internet Archive; John M. Kelly Library Rare Books, University of Toronto; sponsored by the University of Ottawa.", "Trinity", "Feast of the Most Holy Trinity", "Matthew 28", "John 4", "Genesis 22", "Ephesians 4", "1 Corinthians 1", "John 13", "Exodus 14 (printed locator; see note)", "Exodus 25:40 (added by editors; see note)", "John 17", "Psalm 132 (source numbering)", "1 Corinthians 6"], "occasion": "Tr\u00e8s-sainte Trinit\u00e9", "occasion_detail": "Feast of the Most Holy Trinity", "occasion_label": "Trinity", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Bourdaloue's sermon on the Most Holy Trinity applies belief in one God in three persons to Christian conduct. Its three parts present faith as homage to God, the Trinity as the foundation of hope, and the unity of the divine persons as the model and bond of charity among the faithful. It closes with a prayer for unity and peace."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-h001", "label": "French original", "text": "SERMON SUR LA TR\u00c8S-SAINTE TRINIT\u00c9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON ON THE MOST HOLY TRINITY.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-e001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "In nomine Patris, & Filii, & Spirit\u00fbs sancti."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-e001", "label": "English", "text": "In the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-e002", "label": "French original", "text": "Au nom du Pere, & du Fils, & du Saint-Esprit: En saint Matthieu, ch. 28."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-e002", "label": "English", "text": "In the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit: In Saint Matthew, chapter 28.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p001", "label": "French original", "text": "Voil\u00e0, Chr\u00e9tiens, en trois paroles le sommaire de notre foi, le fondement de notre Religion, le caract\u00e8re de notre profession, le plus auguste de nos myst\u00e8res. Le Sauveur du monde en a fait une partie essentielle du premier de tous les Sacremens; il a voulu qu'il entr\u00e2t presque dans la composition de tous les autres; la primitive Eglise s'en servoit comme d'un sceau public & universel, pour distinguer les Fid\u00e8les; & c'est pour nous conformer \u00e0 ses sentimens, que nous le mettons \u00e0 la t\u00eate de toutes nos actions, voulant qu'elles soient autant de t\u00e9moignages du culte que nous rendons \u00e0 l'adorable & tr\u00e8s-sainte Trinit\u00e9. Aussi est-ce cette foi, dit saint Augustin, que nous regardons comme le plus pr\u00e9cieux tr\u00e9sor de l'Eglise; cette foi qui justifie les p\u00e9cheurs, qui sanctifie les justes, qui baptise les Cat\u00e9chum\u00e8nes, qui couronne les Martyrs, qui consacre les Pr\u00eatres, qui sauve tout le monde. Cependant, mes chers Auditeurs, \u00e0 quoi m'engage la f\u00eate & la solemnit\u00e9 de ce jour? Le Proph\u00e8te J\u00e9r\u00e9mie disoit \u00e0 Dieu: Seigneur, je suis un enfant qui ne sait encore que b\u00e9gayer, & qui ne s\u00e7ait pas expliquer ses pens\u00e9es; comment voulez-vous que je parle \u00e0 votre peuple, & que je lui annonce votre loi? Mais, lui r\u00e9pondit le Dieu d'Isra\u00ebl, ne crains point, c'est moi qui t'envoie; & puisque je t'envoie, je te soutiendrai dans l'exercice de ton minist\u00e8re: je te mettrai dans la bouche ce que tu auras \u00e0 dire; & je serai en m\u00eame-tems dans les c\u0153urs de ceux qui t'\u00e9couteront, pour les disposer \u00e0 te donner une attention favorable. Voil\u00e0, mes Freres, ce qui fait aujourd'hui toute ma confiance. J'ai \u00e0 vous entretenir du plus profond & du plus imp\u00e9n\u00e9trable myst\u00e8re: mais deux choses me rass\u00fbrent, l'ordre de Dieu, & votre disposition: l'ordre de Dieu, qui me commande de vous parler; & la disposition o\u00f9 vous \u00eates de recevoir avec une r\u00e9flexion toute particuliere sa sainte parole. Implorons n\u00e9anmoins, pour traiter ce grand sujet, le secours du ciel, par l'intercession de Marie. Ave, Maria."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p001", "label": "English", "text": "Christians, here in three words is the summary of our faith, the foundation of our religion, the mark of our profession, the most august of our mysteries. The Saviour of the world made it an essential part of the first of all the sacraments; he willed that it should enter into the composition of almost all the others. The early Church used it as a public and universal seal to distinguish the faithful; and it is to conform ourselves to her sentiments that we place it at the beginning of all our actions, intending them to be so many testimonies of the worship we render to the adorable and most holy Trinity. It is therefore this faith, says Saint Augustine, that we regard as the Church's most precious treasure: this faith that justifies sinners, sanctifies the just, baptizes catechumens, crowns martyrs, consecrates priests, and saves everyone. Yet, my dear hearers, what obligation does this day's feast and solemnity lay upon me? The prophet Jeremiah said to God: Lord, I am a child who can only stammer, who does not know how to express his thoughts; how do you expect me to speak to your people and proclaim your law to them? But the God of Israel answered him: Do not fear; it is I who send you. Since I send you, I shall sustain you in the exercise of your ministry. I shall put into your mouth what you must say; and at the same time I shall be in the hearts of those who listen to you, to dispose them to give you a favourable hearing. This, my brothers, is the whole ground of my confidence today. I must speak to you of the deepest and most impenetrable mystery; but two things reassure me: God's command and your disposition. God's command requires me to speak to you, and your disposition leads you to receive his holy word with particular reflection. Nevertheless, to treat this great subject, let us implore heaven's help through Mary's intercession. Hail, Mary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-h002", "label": "French original", "text": "DIVISION."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-h002", "label": "English", "text": "DIVISION.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p002", "label": "French original", "text": "Pour parler utilement, Chr\u00e9tiens, du myst\u00e8re de la tr\u00e8s-sainte Trinit\u00e9, & pour le rapporter, autant qu'il est possible, \u00e0 l'\u00e9dification de nos m\u0153urs, voici trois propositions que j'avance d'abord, & qui seront le sujet & le partage de ce discours. Je dis que la profession que nous faisons dans le Christianisme, de croire en un seul Dieu une Trinit\u00e9 de personnes, est l'acte le plus glorieux \u00e0 Dieu que notre foi soit capable de produire, premi\u00e8re proposition. Je dis que c'est le fondement le plus essentiel & le plus solide de toute notre esp\u00e9rance, seconde proposition. Et enfin je dis que c'est le lien de la charit\u00e9 qui doit r\u00e9gner entre les hommes, mais particuli\u00e8rement entre les fid\u00e8les, troisi\u00e8me proposition. La premi\u00e8re vous montrera ce que nous faisons pour Dieu, en confessant le myst\u00e8re de la Trinit\u00e9; la seconde, ce que nous faisons pour nous-m\u00eames; & la troisi\u00e8me ce que nous devons faire les uns pour les autres. Croire un Dieu en trois personnes, c'est le plus grand hommage de foi que la cr\u00e9ature puisse rendre \u00e0 Dieu; ce sera la premi\u00e8re partie. Croire un Dieu en trois personnes, c'est le plus grand sujet de confiance que la cr\u00e9ature puisse avoir en son Dieu: ce sera la seconde. Croire un Dieu en trois personnes; c'est avoir devant les yeux le plus puissant motif & le plus excellent mod\u00e8le de la charit\u00e9, qui doit tous nous unir en Dieu & selon Dieu; ce sera la derni\u00e8re. Tout ceci est moral, & m\u00e9rite toute votre attention."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p002", "label": "English", "text": "To speak profitably, Christians, of the mystery of the most holy Trinity, and to apply it as far as possible to the improvement of our conduct, here are three propositions I put forward at the outset, which will form the subject and divisions of this discourse. I say that the profession we make as Christians of believing a Trinity of persons in one God is the act most glorious to God that our faith can produce: the first proposition. I say that it is the most essential and solid foundation of all our hope: the second proposition. Finally, I say that it is the bond of the charity that must reign among people, and particularly among the faithful: the third proposition. The first will show you what we do for God in confessing the mystery of the Trinity; the second, what we do for ourselves; and the third, what we ought to do for one another. To believe in one God in three persons is the greatest homage of faith that a creature can render to God: this will be the first part. To believe in one God in three persons is the greatest ground for confidence that a creature can have in his God: this will be the second. To believe in one God in three persons is to have before our eyes the most powerful motive and most excellent model of the charity that must unite us all in God and according to God: this will be the last. All this concerns our conduct and deserves your full attention.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-h003", "label": "French original", "text": "I. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-h003", "label": "English", "text": "PART I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p003", "label": "French original", "text": "De tous les myst\u00e8res de notre Religion, il n'y en a pas un, o\u00f9 Dieu soit plus incompr\u00e9hensible \u00e0 l'homme que le myst\u00e8re de la Trinit\u00e9; d'o\u00f9 je conclus qu'il n'y en a aucun dont la cr\u00e9ance & la profession soit plus honorable & plus glorieuse \u00e0 Dieu. Car il est certain que nous ne nous formons jamais d'id\u00e9e plus haute, ni plus digne de la grandeur de Dieu, que quand nous avouons qu'il est incompr\u00e9hensible; & la plus excellente protestation que je lui puisse faire, & que vous puissiez tous lui faire avec moi, c'est sans doute, celle-ci: Non, mon Dieu, je ne vous comprends pas, & je ne suis pas capable de vous comprendre. Quand j'\u00e9puiserois toutes les forces & toutes les puissances de mon ame, quand j'y employerois toutes celles des Anges, quand tous les dons de la gr\u00e2ce & de la gloire me seroient communiqu\u00e9s, quand je vous verrois aussi parfaitement que les bienheureux & que l'humanit\u00e9 de Jesus-Christ m\u00eame; non, Seigneur, je ne vous comprendrois jamais, & ma connoissance sera toujours autant \u00e9loign\u00e9e de vous, que le fini l'est de l'infini. Si je vous comprenois, mon Dieu, vous ne seriez plus ce que vous \u00eates, ou bien je ne serois plus ce que je suis; mais en ne vous comprenant pas, je reconnois que vous \u00eates mon Dieu, & que je suis votre cr\u00e9ature: car comment pourrois-je mieux exprimer l'un & l'autre, & d'une mani\u00e8re plus avantageuse \u00e0 votre divinit\u00e9, qu'en disant que vous \u00eates ce que je ne puis comprendre, & ce qui ne peut jamais \u00eatre compris? Bien plus, dit saint Augustin, (\u00e9coutez, Chr\u00e9tiens, une belle remarque de ce Pere) \u00e0 proprement parler, l'unique chose que nous pouvons conno\u00eetre de Dieu & que nous pouvons lui attribuer, c'est cette qualit\u00e9 d'incompr\u00e9hensible: Tunc ver\u00e8 aliquid de Deo cognoscimus, c\u00f9m ipsum comprehendere non possimus. Dans tout le reste nos esprits se perdent, dans tout le reste nous nous \u00e9garons souvent, sur tout le reste nous sommes en danger de tomber dans l'erreur. Quand nous disons: Dieu est puissant, Dieu est juste, Dieu est saint, Dieu est mis\u00e9ricordieux; dans la rigueur des termes, toutes ces propositions ne seroient pas convenables, si nous n'ajoutions ou si nous ne supposions l'incompr\u00e9hensibilit\u00e9 de Dieu pour les modifier. Afin qu'elles soient exactement vraies, il faut dire ou du moins sous-entendre: Dieu est puissant, mais d'une puissance que je ne comprends pas; Dieu est juste, mais d'une justice toute autre que je ne la connois; Dieu est saint, mais d'une saintet\u00e9 qui passe toutes les v\u00fbes de mon esprit. Il en faut donc toujours revenir \u00e0 son incompr\u00e9hensibilit\u00e9, & se r\u00e9duire au sentiment de saint Augustin, que l\u00e0 o\u00f9 Dieu nous paro\u00eet plus incompr\u00e9hensible, c'est-l\u00e0 que nous le connoissons mieux, l\u00e0 que nous sommes plus en \u00e9tat de le glorifier, l\u00e0 que notre foi lui rend un t\u00e9moignage plus parfait. Or je vous demande, dans quel myst\u00e8re de la religion chr\u00e9tienne Dieu est-il plus incompr\u00e9hensible \u00e0 l'homme? n'est-ce pas dans la Trinit\u00e9? Que concevons-nous dans ce myst\u00e8re, sinon que nous n'y concevons rien? Et c'est pourquoi les Proph\u00e8tes, qui en ont e\u00fb les premi\u00e8res r\u00e9v\u00e9lations, lui ont toujours donn\u00e9 ce caract\u00e8re; nous le repr\u00e9sentant, tant\u00f4t comme une lumi\u00e8re inaccessible, tant\u00f4t comme une obscurit\u00e9 imp\u00e9n\u00e9trable, tant\u00f4t comme un abysme sans fond, pour nous signifier que la Trinit\u00e9 des personnes divines est le grand myst\u00e8re de l'incompr\u00e9hensibilit\u00e9 de Dieu. D'o\u00f9 il s'ensuit que je ne puis plus exalter de ma part, ni plus relever le souverain \u00eatre de Dieu, que par la cr\u00e9ance de cette ineffable Trinit\u00e9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p003", "label": "English", "text": "Of all the mysteries of our religion, there is none in which God is more incomprehensible to man than the mystery of the Trinity. From this I conclude that none is more honourable and glorious to God in its belief and profession. For it is certain that we never form an idea higher or more worthy of God's greatness than when we acknowledge that he is incomprehensible; and the most excellent declaration I can make to him, and that you can all make with me, is surely this: No, my God, I do not comprehend you, and I am incapable of comprehending you. Even if I exhausted all the strength and powers of my soul, employed all those of the angels, received every gift of grace and glory, and saw you as perfectly as the blessed and even as the humanity of Jesus Christ: no, Lord, I should never comprehend you, and my knowledge will always be as far removed from you as the finite is from the infinite. If I comprehended you, my God, you would no longer be what you are, or I should no longer be what I am. But in not comprehending you, I acknowledge that you are my God and that I am your creature. How could I express both truths better, or in a manner more favourable to your divinity, than by saying that you are what I cannot comprehend and what can never be comprehended? Moreover, says Saint Augustine\u2014listen, Christians, to a fine observation of this Father\u2014strictly speaking, the only thing we can know of God and attribute to him is this quality of being incomprehensible: Then we truly know something of God, when we cannot comprehend him. In everything else our minds lose themselves; in everything else we often go astray; in everything else we are in danger of falling into error. When we say, God is powerful, God is just, God is holy, God is merciful, strictly speaking all these propositions would be inappropriate unless we added or presupposed God's incomprehensibility to qualify them. To make them exactly true, we must say, or at least imply: God is powerful, but with a power I do not comprehend; God is just, but with a justice quite other than I know it to be; God is holy, but with a holiness that exceeds every conception of my mind. We must therefore always return to his incomprehensibility and confine ourselves to Saint Augustine's thought: where God appears more incomprehensible to us, there we know him better, there we are better able to glorify him, there our faith bears more perfect witness to him. Now I ask you: in what mystery of the Christian religion is God more incomprehensible to man? Is it not in the Trinity? What do we conceive in this mystery except that we conceive nothing in it? This is why the prophets, who received its first revelations, always gave it this character, representing it to us now as an inaccessible light, now as an impenetrable darkness, now as a bottomless abyss, to signify that the Trinity of the divine persons is the great mystery of God's incomprehensibility. It follows that I can do nothing more to exalt and magnify God's sovereign being than believe in this ineffable Trinity.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p004", "label": "French original", "text": "N'en demeurons pas l\u00e0. Que fais-je, Chr\u00e9tiens, quand je crois un Dieu en trois personnes? je lui fais un sacrifice, & de quoi? de la plus noble partie de moi-m\u00eame, qui est ma raison; & comment le fais-je? de la mani\u00e8re la plus excellente & la plus h\u00e9ro\u00efque, & en quoi consiste-t-il? le voici. Je crois un myst\u00e8re dont je n'ai nulle exp\u00e9rience, & dont il m'est impossible d'avoir la moindre id\u00e9e, avant que Dieu me l'ait r\u00e9v\u00e9l\u00e9; & quand Dieu me l'a r\u00e9v\u00e9l\u00e9, je le crois de telle sorte, que ma raison ne peut s'en faire juge, ni l'examiner; enfin, ce qui fait la perfection de mon sacrifice, je crois ce myst\u00e8re, quoiqu'il semble r\u00e9pugner positivement \u00e0 ma raison. N'est-ce pas l\u00e0 tout l'effort que la raison humaine peut faire pour Dieu? ne sont-ce pas tous les droits auxquels elle peut renoncer? & n'est-ce pas sur-tout dans ce myst\u00e8re qu'elle y renonce pleinement, & qu'elle se sacrifie toute entiere? Car il n'en est pas de m\u00eame des autres: je connois mille choses de Dieu, ind\u00e9pendamment des r\u00e9v\u00e9lations de Dieu. Quand Dieu ne m'auroit jamais parl\u00e9, je s\u00e7ai qu'il est sage, je s\u00e7ai qu'il a une providence, je s\u00e7ai que le monde est gouvern\u00e9 par lui: toutes les cr\u00e9atures me le disent; je n'ai qu'\u00e0 ouvrir les yeux, j'en ai des preuves sensibles. Et en cela la foi ne marche point devant la raison, mais elle la suit; elle ne lui apprend rien de nouveau, quoiqu'elle le lui apprenne mieux: elle augmente ses lumi\u00e8res & les perfectionne; mais elle les suppose en les perfectionnant: je crois ce que je s\u00e7avois d\u00e9ja en partie. Mais qu'en Dieu il y ait trois diff\u00e9rentes personnes; que la premi\u00e8re s'appelle Pere, la seconde Verbe, & la troisi\u00e8me Saint-Esprit; que le Fils soit engendr\u00e9 par la connoissance f\u00e9conde que Dieu a de soi-m\u00eame, & que le Saint-Esprit proc\u00e8de du Pere & du Fils par voie d'amour; ce sont des secrets dont je ne d\u00e9couvre aucun vestige dans l'univers, & dont tous les hommes n'auroient p\u00fb m\u00eame se former de conjectures, si Dieu ne les en avoit instruits. On dit qu'un Philosophe pa\u00efen en a e\u00fb autrefois quelque connoissance: mais si cela est, saint Augustin r\u00e9pond qu'elle lui \u00e9toit venue du commerce avec les Juifs. C'est donc \u00e0 la foi seule que je suis oblig\u00e9 de m'en rapporter touchant ce myst\u00e8re. Mais quand ce myst\u00e8re m'est r\u00e9v\u00e9l\u00e9 de Dieu par la foi, puis-je raisonner, puis-je discourir, puis-je occuper mon esprit \u00e0 le conno\u00eetre & \u00e0 en chercher les principes? Non, Chr\u00e9tiens, cela n'est point du ressort de ma raison. Dans le myst\u00e8re de l'incarnation je le puis faire: suppos\u00e9 la foi que le Verbe se soit fait chair, mon esprit y trouve je ne s\u00e7ai combien de convenances admirables. Je dis qu'il n'y avoit qu'un Dieu qui p\u00fbt satisfaire \u00e0 Dieu pour le p\u00e9ch\u00e9; or ce Dieu ne pouvoit satisfaire, sans se faire homme: ainsi je raisonne alors sur la foi. Quoique la foi pr\u00e9c\u00e8de mon raisonnement, mon raisonnement ne laisse pas de venir ensuite au secours de la foi. Mais quand il s'agit de l'auguste myst\u00e8re de la Trinit\u00e9, d'une essence indivisible en plusieurs personnes, du Pere qui n'est pas plus que le Fils, du Fils qui n'a nulle d\u00e9pendance de son Pere, du Saint-Esprit qui est l'amour substantiel de l'un & de l'autre; c'est l\u00e0 que notre raison demeure, qu'elle s'humilie, qu'elle se couvre de ses ailes, comme ces Anges que vit le Proph\u00e8te; qu'elle s'interdit tout examen, toute r\u00e9flexion, toute curiosit\u00e9. Tout ce qu'elle fait, c'est de reconno\u00eetre son ignorance; & cet aveu, dans la pens\u00e9e d'un Pere, est la seule confession v\u00e9ritable de la Trinit\u00e9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p004", "label": "English", "text": "Let us not stop there. What do I do, Christians, when I believe in one God in three persons? I offer him a sacrifice. Of what? Of the noblest part of myself, my reason. And how do I make it? In the most excellent and heroic manner. What does it consist in? Here it is. I believe a mystery of which I have no experience, and of which I cannot have the slightest idea before God reveals it to me. Once God has revealed it, I believe it in such a way that my reason cannot make itself its judge or examine it. Finally, what perfects my sacrifice is that I believe this mystery even though it seems positively repugnant to my reason. Is this not the utmost effort human reason can make for God? Are these not all the rights it can renounce? And is it not above all in this mystery that it renounces them fully and sacrifices itself entirely? The other mysteries are different. I know a thousand things about God independently of God's revelations. Even if God had never spoken to me, I know he is wise, I know he exercises providence, I know the world is governed by him. All creatures tell me so; I need only open my eyes to have sensible proofs of it. In this, faith does not walk ahead of reason but follows it. It teaches it nothing new, although it teaches it better; it increases and perfects its light, but presupposes that light in perfecting it. I believe what I already knew in part. But that in God there are three distinct persons; that the first is called Father, the second Word, the third Holy Spirit; that the Son is begotten through God's fruitful knowledge of himself, and that the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son by way of love: these are secrets of which I discover no trace in the universe, and about which all mankind could not even have formed conjectures had God not taught them. A pagan philosopher is said to have had some knowledge of them long ago; but if that is so, Saint Augustine replies, it came to him through contact with the Jews. In this mystery, then, I must rely on faith alone. But when God reveals this mystery to me through faith, may I reason about it, discuss it, apply my mind to knowing it and searching for its principles? No, Christians; this is outside my reason's province. I can do so in the mystery of the Incarnation: once faith that the Word became flesh is presupposed, my mind finds countless admirable harmonies in it. I say that only one who is God could make satisfaction to God for sin; yet this God could not make satisfaction without becoming man. Thus I then reason upon faith. Though faith precedes my reasoning, my reasoning nonetheless comes afterwards to faith's aid. But when it concerns the august mystery of the Trinity, an indivisible essence in several persons, the Father who is no greater than the Son, the Son who has no dependence on his Father, the Holy Spirit who is the substantial love of both: there our reason stops, humbles itself, covers itself with its wings like those angels the prophet saw, and forbids itself all examination, all reflection, all curiosity. All it does is acknowledge its ignorance; and this acknowledgement, in the thought of one of the Fathers, is the only true confession of the Trinity.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p005", "label": "French original", "text": "Ce qui met le comble au sacrifice que je fais \u00e0 Dieu, en croyant la Trinit\u00e9, c'est que je me soumets \u00e0 croire un myst\u00e8re qui paro\u00eet choquer la raison m\u00eame, & contredire toutes ses lumi\u00e8res. Car il faut que je croie que trois personnes divines, celle du Pere, celle du Fils, & celle du Saint-Esprit, n'\u00e9tant qu'une m\u00eame chose avec l'essence de Dieu, je dis une m\u00eame chose indivisible, sans composition, sans partie, sont n\u00e9anmoins distingu\u00e9es entre elles. Voil\u00e0, si j'ose parler ainsi, la pierre de scandale pour l'homme. Voil\u00e0 la plus apparente contradiction qui se rencontre dans tous nos myst\u00e8res. Mais c'est de-l\u00e0 m\u00eame aussi que notre foi tire sa perfection, quand nous disons \u00e0 Dieu: Oui, Seigneur, je crois tout ce que vous m'avez r\u00e9v\u00e9l\u00e9 de cet incompr\u00e9hensible myst\u00e8re; ma raison semble d'abord s'y opposer; mais je la d\u00e9savoue, mais je la renonce, mais je vous l'immole aux pieds de vos autels. Je crois, mon Dieu, votre unit\u00e9 & votre trinit\u00e9 tout ensemble, & je crois l'un & l'autre dans la m\u00eame disposition de c\u0153ur que s'il falloit mourir. En vertu de cette foi, dont je fais ici profession, je voudrois pour la d\u00e9fendre donner ma vie & verser mon sang: & comme vous \u00eates trois dans le ciel dont je re\u00e7ois aujourd'hui le t\u00e9moignage, le Pere, le Verbe & le Saint-Esprit; aussi voudrois-je, Seigneur, \u00eatre en \u00e9tat de vous rendre sur la terre les trois t\u00e9moignages dont parle le bien-aim\u00e9 disciple, le t\u00e9moignage de l'esprit, le t\u00e9moignage de l'eau, & le t\u00e9moignage du sang. Voil\u00e0 ce que nous disons, Chr\u00e9tiens: mais s\u00e7avez-vous ce que Dieu nous r\u00e9pond? il est important que je vous le fasse entendre. Non, non, nous dit-il, il ne s'agit plus de mourir, ni de perdre la vie: je voulois des martyrs autrefois pour fonder ma religion; mais maintenant les choses ont chang\u00e9: ce n'est plus dans la pers\u00e9cution, mais dans la paix, qu'il faut prouver votre foi; ce n'est plus sur des \u00e9chafauds, ni sur des roues, mais dans les pratiques d'une vie commune & ordinaire, qu'il faut faire paro\u00eetre ce que vous \u00eates: ce n'est plus devant les juges & les tyrans qu'il faut me confesser; mais au milieu de vos proches & de vos amis; ce n'est plus le t\u00e9moignage du sang que je vous demande, mais le t\u00e9moignage de l'esprit. Ne pensez donc point \u00e0 ce que vous feriez, s'il y avoit encore des pers\u00e9cuteurs dans le monde: il n'y en a plus, il est permis de se d\u00e9clarer, & commencez \u00e0 le faire par la saintet\u00e9 de votre vie, par l'innocence & la puret\u00e9 de vos m\u0153urs. En effet, Chr\u00e9tiens, nous nous flattons, en formant ces r\u00e9solutions imaginaires, de confesser notre foi \u00e0 quelque prix que ce f\u00fbt; & en disant comme nous disons quelquefois, je souffrirois plut\u00f4t mille morts, que de la trahir, cette foi. Car nous la trahissons \u00e0 toute heure; & ce qui est plus d\u00e9plorable, nous la trahissons pour un vil int\u00e9r\u00eat, pour un moment de plaisir, pour contenter un d\u00e9sir, une passion honteuse; & tout ce grand z\u00e8le n'est qu'en sp\u00e9culation & en id\u00e9e, n'est que sous des conditions chim\u00e9riques, n'est que pour des occasions & des conjonctures o\u00f9 nous ne nous trouverons jamais; rien de r\u00e9el, ni rien de pr\u00e9sent."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p005", "label": "English", "text": "What brings to its height the sacrifice I make to God in believing the Trinity is that I submit to believing a mystery that seems to offend reason itself and contradict all its light. For I must believe that three divine persons\u2014the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit\u2014though they are one and the same thing with God's essence, one and the same indivisible thing, I say, without composition or parts, are nevertheless distinct from one another. Here, if I dare speak thus, is man's stumbling block. Here is the most apparent contradiction to be found in all our mysteries. Yet it is from this very thing that our faith draws its perfection, when we say to God: Yes, Lord, I believe everything you have revealed to me of this incomprehensible mystery. My reason seems at first to oppose it; but I disavow it, I renounce it, I sacrifice it to you at the foot of your altars. My God, I believe your unity and your trinity together, and I believe both with the same disposition of heart as if I had to die for them. By virtue of this faith, which I here profess, I would give my life and shed my blood to defend it. And as you are three in heaven whose testimony I receive today\u2014the Father, the Word and the Holy Spirit\u2014so, Lord, I would wish to be able to render you on earth the three testimonies of which the beloved disciple speaks: the testimony of the spirit, the testimony of the water, and the testimony of the blood. This is what we say, Christians; but do you know what God answers us? It is important that I make you hear it. No, no, he tells us, it is no longer a question of dying or losing your life. Formerly I wanted martyrs to establish my religion; but now things have changed. You must prove your faith in peace rather than persecution; you must show what you are through the practices of a common and ordinary life rather than on scaffolds or torture wheels. You must confess me among your relatives and friends rather than before judges and tyrants. I ask you for the testimony of the spirit rather than of blood. Do not think, then, of what you would do if there were still persecutors in the world. There are no longer any; you are free to declare yourselves. Begin doing so through the holiness of your lives, the innocence and purity of your conduct. Indeed, Christians, we flatter ourselves by forming these imaginary resolutions to confess our faith at any cost, and by saying, as we sometimes do, I would suffer a thousand deaths rather than betray this faith. For we betray it every hour; and what is more deplorable, we betray it for a sordid advantage, a moment's pleasure, to satisfy a desire, a shameful passion. All this great zeal exists only in speculation and in imagination, only under fanciful conditions, only for occasions and circumstances in which we shall never find ourselves: nothing real, nothing present.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p006", "label": "French original", "text": "Ah! Chr\u00e9tiens, la belle parole que celle d'un saint Ev\u00eaque, en parlant des premiers martyrs: Ils ne s\u00e7avoient pas disputer des choses de la foi, disoit Pacien Ev\u00eaque de Barcelone; mais ils s\u00e7avoient bien souffrir & mourir pour la foi: Sciebant mori, & non sciebant disputare. Mais de nous, on peut dire \u00e0 notre confusion tout le contraire: nous s\u00e7avons disputer des choses de la foi, mais nous ne s\u00e7avons ni mourir ni vivre pour la foi. Jamais tant de raffinemens, jamais tant de contestations ni tant de disputes, jamais tant de libert\u00e9 qu'il y en a aujourd'hui \u00e0 s'expliquer sur les myst\u00e8res de la foi & de la religion, & n\u00e9anmoins jamais si peu de foi & de religion: pourquoi? parce qu'il n'y a rien qui soit plus capable de d\u00e9truire la religion & la foi, que cette vanit\u00e9 dont on se pique, & ce pr\u00e9tendu m\u00e9rite qu'on se fait, d'en s\u00e7avoir raisonner. Ceux dont parle Pacien, se contentoient de s\u00e7avoir deux choses, qui \u00e9toient de croire & de mourir. Ils bornoient l\u00e0 toute leur science: & nous, nous s\u00e7avons toutes choses hors ces deux-l\u00e0, parce que nous ne voulons croire que ce qui nous pla\u00eet; & que nous ne voulons pas d'ailleurs nous faire la moindre violence, pour pratiquer ce que nous croyons. Ceux-l\u00e0 s\u00e7avoient mourir pour la foi: Sciebant mori; & nous avec toute notre subtilit\u00e9, nous n'avons pas encore appris \u00e0 vivre selon la foi. Car nous nous disons chr\u00e9tiens, & nous vivons en pa\u00efens; & par cette alliance que nous faisons dans nous-m\u00eames, d'un certain paganisme d'action & de vie avec le christianisme de profession & de cr\u00e9ance, nous formons un monstre pire que le paganisme m\u00eame, puisqu'il ajoute \u00e0 tous les d\u00e9sordres de celui-ci la profanation de l'autre."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p006", "label": "English", "text": "Ah, Christians, what a fine saying that of a holy bishop speaking of the first martyrs! They did not know how to dispute about matters of faith, said Pacian, Bishop of Barcelona; but they knew well how to suffer and die for the faith: They knew how to die, and did not know how to dispute. Of us, however, the very opposite may be said to our shame: we know how to dispute about matters of faith, but we know neither how to die nor how to live for the faith. Never have there been so many refinements, so many controversies and disputes, so much freedom as there is today to express oneself about the mysteries of faith and religion; and yet never so little faith and religion. Why? Because nothing is more capable of destroying religion and faith than this vanity we pride ourselves on, this supposed merit we claim, of knowing how to reason about them. Those of whom Pacian speaks were content to know two things: how to believe and how to die. They confined all their knowledge to that. We know everything except those two things, because we will believe only what pleases us, and moreover will not make the slightest effort against ourselves to practise what we believe. They knew how to die for the faith: They knew how to die. We, with all our subtlety, have not yet learned to live according to the faith. For we call ourselves Christians and live as pagans; and by joining within ourselves a certain paganism of action and life to Christianity of profession and belief, we form a monster worse than paganism itself, since it adds to all the disorders of the one the profanation of the other.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p007", "label": "French original", "text": "Voil\u00e0, mes chers Auditeurs, la r\u00e9flexion que je vous prie de faire en la pr\u00e9sence de Dieu. Souvenez-vous que vous adorez une Trinit\u00e9 dont le caract\u00e8re propre & essentiel est la saintet\u00e9; & qu'il n'y a point de saintet\u00e9, quelque \u00e9minente qu'elle puisse \u00eatre, \u00e0 laquelle nous ne devions aspirer, pour nous rendre de dignes adorateurs de cette auguste Trinit\u00e9. Pour l'adorer en esprit & en v\u00e9rit\u00e9, il faut par proportion \u00eatre saint comme elle; car ce sont-l\u00e0 les adorateurs que le Pere demande: Nam & Pater tales qu\u00e6rit qui adorent eum. Voil\u00e0 ceux qu'il cherche, & il ne se tiendra jamais vraiment ador\u00e9 par d'autres: Nam & Pater tales qu\u00e6rit. C'est un Dieu Saint, & il veut \u00eatre servi par des saints. Le premier Ange ne le fut pas; & ce Dieu de saintet\u00e9 n'a p\u00fb souffrir qu'il f\u00fbt du nombre de ceux qui l'adorent, & il aime mieux en \u00eatre blasph\u00e9m\u00e9 dans l'enfer, que d'en \u00eatre lou\u00e9 dans le ciel: or il n'est pas probable qu'il en doive user autrement \u00e0 l'\u00e9gard des hommes. Avan\u00e7ons, & apr\u00e8s avoir v\u00fb comment la confession de la Trinit\u00e9 est le plus grand hommage de foi que la cr\u00e9ature rende \u00e0 son Dieu; voyons encore comment c'est le plus grand sujet de confiance qu'une cr\u00e9ature puisse avoir en ce m\u00eame Dieu: c'est la seconde partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p007", "label": "English", "text": "This, my dear hearers, is the reflection I ask you to make in God's presence. Remember that you adore a Trinity whose proper and essential character is holiness; and that there is no holiness, however eminent, to which we ought not aspire in order to become worthy adorers of this august Trinity. To adore it in spirit and in truth, we must be holy like it in due proportion; for these are the adorers the Father seeks: For the Father too seeks such people to adore him. These are the ones he seeks, and he will never regard himself as truly adored by others: For the Father too seeks such people. He is a holy God, and he wills to be served by saints. The first angel was not holy; and this God of holiness could not permit him to be numbered among those who adore him. He would rather be blasphemed by him in hell than praised by him in heaven. It is unlikely that he should act otherwise towards men. Let us proceed: having seen how confessing the Trinity is the greatest homage of faith that a creature can render to his God, let us also see how it is the greatest ground for confidence a creature can have in that same God. This is the second part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-h004", "label": "French original", "text": "II. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-h004", "label": "English", "text": "PART II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p008", "label": "French original", "text": "Il y a, Chr\u00e9tiens, dans notre religion une chose bien particuliere, & que vous n'avez peut-\u00eatre jamais remarqu\u00e9e. Quand on nous instruit au christianisme, & qu'on nous donne les premiers \u00e9l\u00e9mens de la foi, par o\u00f9 commence-t-on? par ce qu'il y a de plus relev\u00e9, & de plus difficile \u00e0 croire, qui est le myst\u00e8re de la Trinit\u00e9. Dans les sciences humaines, on enseigne d'abord les choses les plus communes & les plus ais\u00e9es, & puis on \u00e9l\u00e8ve peu-\u00e0-peu l'esprit aux plus obscures & aux plus sublimes. Mais quand il s'agit de la science d'un chr\u00e9tien, la premi\u00e8re le\u00e7on c'est le pr\u00e9cis de toutes les obscurit\u00e9s qui s'y rencontrent; il faut, pour ainsi dire, que la foi fasse son apprentissage par son chef-d'\u0153uvre, s\u00e7avoir, par la confession d'un Dieu en trois personnes. Vous voulez apprendre \u00e0 un enfant les principes de la doctrine chr\u00e9tienne; c'est un enfant, il ne s\u00e7ait pas encore raisonner, \u00e0 peine a-t-il l'usage de la parole; cependant que lui dites-vous? Trois personnes & un seul Dieu, voil\u00e0 l'instruction que vous lui faites. Mais c'est l'instruction la moins proportionn\u00e9e \u00e0 son esprit; mais c'est celle dont il est moins capable; mais c'est celle par o\u00f9 finissent les plus s\u00e7avans Th\u00e9ologiens: il n'importe, c'est \u00e0 cela qu'il faut s'attacher avant tout le reste, & pourquoi? ah! Chr\u00e9tiens, en voici la raison: parce que la foi des trois personnes divines est le fondement de toute notre esp\u00e9rance, la source de tous nos m\u00e9rites, le principe de toute saintet\u00e9, & pour m'expliquer dans les termes du Concile de Trente, le commencement & la racine de toute la justification des hommes; Initium & radix totius justificationis nostr\u00e6. Peut-on \u00eatre sauv\u00e9 sans la foi? non. Mais quelle est la foi essentielle & n\u00e9cessaire? celle de la Trinit\u00e9. Tous les autres myst\u00e8res de la cr\u00e9ance catholique, hors l'incarnation du Verbe, n'ont pas le m\u00eame avantage. Je pourrois absolument les ignorer, & me sauver: pour celui qui comprend un Dieu en trois personnes, si je l'ignore, je n'ai rien \u00e0 attendre de Dieu, & si je le crois, j'en esp\u00e8re tout. J'avoue, & je l'ai dit, que ce premier acte de religion par lequel nous confessons que trois ne font qu'un, est le plus grand effort de la foi; mais c'est pour cela m\u00eame que Dieu en a fait d\u00e9pendre tout notre bonheur. Il voyoit bien la violence qu'il y auroit \u00e0 se faire pour assujettir nos esprits \u00e0 ce myst\u00e8re; & voil\u00e0 pourquoi il a arr\u00eat\u00e9 dans le conseil de sa sagesse, que la foi de ce myst\u00e8re seroit le principe de tous nos m\u00e9rites devant lui & de notre \u00e9ternelle pr\u00e9destination."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p008", "label": "English", "text": "Christians, there is something very remarkable in our religion that you may never have noticed. When we are instructed in Christianity and taught the first elements of faith, where does the instruction begin? With what is most exalted and most difficult to believe: the mystery of the Trinity. In human sciences, the most common and easiest things are taught first, and then the mind is gradually raised to the most obscure and sublime. But in a Christian's knowledge, the first lesson is a summary of all the obscurities found within it. Faith must, so to speak, begin its apprenticeship with its masterpiece: the confession of one God in three persons. You wish to teach a child the principles of Christian doctrine. He is a child; he cannot yet reason and can barely speak. Yet what do you tell him? Three persons and one God: that is the instruction you give him. But it is the instruction least suited to his mind, the one he is less capable of grasping, the one with which the most learned theologians end. No matter: this must be attended to before everything else. Why? Ah, Christians, here is the reason: because faith in the three divine persons is the foundation of all our hope, the source of all our merits, the principle of all holiness; and, to use the Council of Trent's terms, the beginning and root of all men's justification: The beginning and root of all our justification. Can we be saved without faith? No. But what is the essential and necessary faith? Faith in the Trinity. All the other mysteries of Catholic belief, except the Incarnation of the Word, do not have the same prerogative. I could, strictly speaking, be ignorant of them and be saved. But as for the mystery that comprises one God in three persons, if I am ignorant of it, I have nothing to expect from God; if I believe it, I hope for everything from him. I acknowledge, and have already said, that this first act of religion by which we confess that three are only one is faith's greatest effort. Yet it is for this very reason that God has made all our happiness depend on it. He saw well what effort we would have to make against ourselves to subject our minds to this mystery. This is why, in the counsel of his wisdom, he determined that faith in this mystery would be the principle of all our merits before him and of our eternal predestination.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p009", "label": "French original", "text": "Et en cela, dit saint Chrysostome, Dieu nous a trait\u00e9s avec la m\u00eame bont\u00e9, dont il usa autrefois envers son serviteur Abraham. Ce patriarche, vous le s\u00e7avez, s'\u00e9toit mis en devoir de sacrifier son propre fils, malgr\u00e9 les r\u00e9pugnances que la nature formoit dans son c\u0153ur. Il \u00e9toit pr\u00eat \u00e0 frapper le coup; mais Dieu en fut touch\u00e9, & ne voulut pas avoir moins de lib\u00e9ralit\u00e9 pour Abraham, qu'Abraham n'avoit eu pour lui de fid\u00e9lit\u00e9. Quia fecisti hanc rem, & non pepercisti unigenito tuo propter me, multiplicabo semen tuum: Parce que tu as fait cela, lui dit le Seigneur, & que tu n'as pas \u00e9pargn\u00e9 ton unique pour moi, je multiplierai ta post\u00e9rit\u00e9, je te comblerai de b\u00e9n\u00e9dictions, je te ferai le plus riche & le plus puissant de la terre: & cette ob\u00e9issance que tu m'as rendue sera suivie de toute sorte de prosp\u00e9rit\u00e9s. C'est ainsi que Dieu dit aujourd'hui \u00e0 un Chr\u00e9tien, Parce que tu as cru un myst\u00e8re si fort audessus de toi & de toutes les id\u00e9es humaines: Quia fecisti hanc rem; & que tu as sacrifi\u00e9 ton unique, c'est-\u00e0-dire, ton esprit & ta raison, & non pepercisti unigenito tuo, c'est pour cela que je te remplirai de graces, que je multiplierai le m\u00e9rite de tes actions, que je t'adopterai parmi mes enfans, que je t'enrichirai de vertus, que je te sanctifierai & que je te glorifierai. Car cette foi que tu as profess\u00e9e, est le petit grain de l'Evangile, lequel ayant pris racine dans ton c\u0153ur, poussera ses branches jusqu'\u00e0 la hauteur du ciel, & produira tous les fruits de gloire que tu dois recueillir dans l'\u00e9ternit\u00e9. Et voil\u00e0, Chr\u00e9tiens, pourquoi la formule de foi que nous pronon\u00e7ons en confessant la Trinit\u00e9, & qui est con\u00e7ue en ces termes: Au nom du Pere, & du Fils & du Saint-Esprit, est si sainte, si auguste, si v\u00e9n\u00e9rable dans notre religion. Voil\u00e0 pourquoi selon l'institution de Jesus-Christ, elle entre dans presque tous les Sacremens de la loi de grace. Car si nous sommes r\u00e9g\u00e9n\u00e9r\u00e9s dans le bapt\u00eame, c'est au nom du Pere, & du Fils & du Saint-Esprit; si nous sommes fortifi\u00e9s par la grace de la Confirmation, c'est au nom du Pere, & du Fils & du Saint-Esprit; si nos p\u00e9ch\u00e9s nous sont remis par la P\u00e9nitence, c'est au nom du Pere, & du Fils & du Saint-Esprit; si nous sommes consacr\u00e9s par le caract\u00e8re de l'Ordre, c'est au nom du Pere, & du Fils & du Saint-Esprit; si nous recevons la b\u00e9n\u00e9diction des Pr\u00eatres, des Pasteurs, des Pr\u00e9lats, c'est au nom du Pere, & du Fils & du Saint-Esprit: pour nous apprendre, dit saint Augustin, que dans le Christianisme il n'y a point de grace, point de salut, point de justification, que par la foi de la Trinit\u00e9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p009", "label": "English", "text": "In this, says Saint Chrysostom, God has treated us with the same goodness he once showed his servant Abraham. This patriarch, as you know, had set himself to sacrifice his own son despite the resistance that nature stirred in his heart. He was ready to strike the blow; but God was moved by it, and would not be less generous to Abraham than Abraham had been faithful to him. Because you have done this thing, and have not spared your only-begotten son for my sake, I shall multiply your seed. Because you have done this, the Lord said to him, and have not spared your only son for me, I shall multiply your posterity, heap blessings upon you, and make you the richest and most powerful man on earth. This obedience you have rendered me will be followed by every kind of prosperity. Thus God says to a Christian today: Because you have believed a mystery so far above yourself and all human conceptions\u2014Because you have done this thing\u2014and have sacrificed your only one, that is, your mind and reason\u2014and have not spared your only-begotten son\u2014for this reason I shall fill you with graces, multiply the merit of your actions, adopt you among my children, enrich you with virtues, sanctify and glorify you. For this faith you have professed is the little seed of the Gospel which, having taken root in your heart, will send its branches up to heaven's height and produce all the fruits of glory that you are to gather in eternity. This, Christians, is why the formula of faith we pronounce in confessing the Trinity, expressed in these words, In the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit, is so holy, so august, so venerable in our religion. This is why, according to Jesus Christ's institution, it enters into almost all the sacraments of the law of grace. If we are regenerated in baptism, it is in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. If we are strengthened by the grace of Confirmation, it is in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. If our sins are forgiven through Penance, it is in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. If we are consecrated by the character of Holy Orders, it is in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. If we receive the blessing of priests, pastors and prelates, it is in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit: to teach us, says Saint Augustine, that in Christianity there is no grace, no salvation, no justification except through faith in the Trinity.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p010", "label": "French original", "text": "De-l\u00e0 vient aussi, que, suivant la sainte & religieuse coutume, nous mettons \u00e0 la t\u00eate de toutes nos actions cette profession de foi; n'entreprenant rien, n'ex\u00e9cutant rien, que nous n'ayons auparavant marqu\u00e9 sur nous le signe de la croix, avec ces paroles: Au nom du Pere, & du Fils & du Saint-Esprit: reconnoissant que le m\u00e9rite de notre action d\u00e9pend de-l\u00e0, & que sans cette foi, tout ce que nous allons faire seroit inutile, rejett\u00e9 de Dieu, & perdu pour le ciel. Pratique qui nous est venue des Ap\u00f4tres, dont la tradition est constante, que les Fid\u00e8les ont toujours gard\u00e9e, & que nos H\u00e9r\u00e9tiques n'ont pu condamner, sans faire paro\u00eetre qu'ils \u00e9toient d\u00e9termin\u00e9s \u00e0 condamner tout. Car enfin, qu'y a-t-il de plus conforme \u00e0 l'esprit chr\u00e9tien, que ce saint exercice d'invoquer la Trinit\u00e9, & de nous imprimer nous m\u00eames sur le front le signe de notre salut au commencement de chaque action? Cela n\u00e9anmoins leur d\u00e9pla\u00eet, & un des articles de leur pr\u00e9tendue r\u00e9forme a \u00e9t\u00e9 d'en abolir l'usage: mais c'est pour cela m\u00eame que l'Eglise a t\u00e9moign\u00e9 encore plus de z\u00e8le \u00e0 le retenir & \u00e0 l'observer. C'est pour cela qu'elle commence ses divins Offices par la foi du Pere, & du Fils & du Saint-Esprit, que toutes les pri\u00e8res qu'elle adresse \u00e0 Dieu par forme de demande, expriment toujours ces trois divines personnes; qu'elle ne chante pas un Pseaume, une Hymne, un Cantique, sans les conclure par-l\u00e0: que plus de cent fois le jour elle nous oblige, nous qui sommes les Ministres de ses Autels, \u00e0 r\u00e9p\u00e9ter ce sacr\u00e9 verset: Gloire au Pere, au Fils & au saint-Esprit; parce qu'elle s\u00e7ait bien que nous ne pouvons rien dire \u00e0 Dieu de plus agr\u00e9able, ni qui soit plus propre \u00e0 lui gagner le c\u0153ur; & que cette pri\u00e8re seule a plus de vertu & plus de force que toutes les autres, pour nous sanctifier. Ainsi elle voudroit que nous pussions la faire continuellement, & que jour & nuit notre bouche f\u00fbt occup\u00e9e \u00e0 dire: Gloire au Pere, gloire au Fils, gloire au Saint-Esprit, \u00e0 l'exemple de ce saint Solitaire, qui s'\u00e9tant plac\u00e9 sur une haute colonne, o\u00f9 il demeura plusieurs ann\u00e9es, n'avoit point d'autre exercice que celui-l\u00e0."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p010", "label": "English", "text": "Hence also, following the holy and religious custom, we place this profession of faith at the beginning of all our actions, undertaking and carrying out nothing without first making the sign of the cross upon ourselves with these words: In the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. We acknowledge that the merit of our action depends on this, and that without this faith everything we are about to do would be useless, rejected by God and lost to heaven. This practice came to us from the apostles; its tradition is constant; the faithful have always kept it; and our heretics could not condemn it without showing that they were determined to condemn everything. For what is more in keeping with the Christian spirit than this holy exercise of invoking the Trinity and ourselves tracing upon our foreheads the sign of our salvation at the beginning of every action? Nevertheless it displeases them, and one article of their pretended reform was to abolish its use. Yet for that very reason the Church has shown still greater zeal in retaining and observing it. This is why she begins her divine offices with faith in the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit; why all the prayers she addresses to God by way of petition always express these three divine persons; why she sings no psalm, hymn or canticle without concluding them in this way; why more than a hundred times a day she obliges us, the ministers of her altars, to repeat this sacred verse: Glory to the Father, to the Son and to the Holy Spirit. For she knows well that we can say nothing to God more pleasing or better suited to win his heart; and that this prayer alone has more virtue and power than all the others to sanctify us. Thus she would wish us to be able to pray it continually, and our mouths to be occupied day and night in saying: Glory to the Father, glory to the Son, glory to the Holy Spirit, after the example of that holy solitary who placed himself on a high column, remained there several years, and had no other exercise than this.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p011", "label": "French original", "text": "Ah! Chr\u00e9tiens, permettez-moi de prendre ici occasion de vous instruire sur un point d'une grande utilit\u00e9, quoique peut-\u00eatre vous ne l'estimiez pas tel. Si toutes les fois que vous & moi, nous avons prononc\u00e9 ces v\u00e9n\u00e9rables paroles: Gloire au Pere, au Fils, au Saint-Esprit, ou celles-ci: Au nom du Pere, du Fils & du Saint-Esprit; nous l'avions fait avec le m\u00eame respect & la m\u00eame affection que ce saint anachor\u00e8te, combien de m\u00e9rites aurions-nous acquis devant Dieu? si nous \u00e9tions bien remplis de cette pens\u00e9e, moi qui vous parle & vous qui m'\u00e9coutez, nous les dirions sans cesse par une solide d\u00e9votion, & comptez quels fonds de richesses spirituelles elles nous produiroient. Car ces courtes paroles renferment les actes les plus m\u00e9ritoires de toute la Religion. Mais parce que si nous les disons, c'est sans r\u00e9flexion & avec une imagination \u00e9gar\u00e9e, pensant \u00e0 toute autre chose ou ne pensant \u00e0 rien, nous avons beau les dire, & confesser ainsi la Trinit\u00e9, peut-\u00eatre ne nous ont-elles pas procur\u00e9 un seul d\u00e9gr\u00e9 de grace. Ce qui doit encore plus nous toucher, c'est qu'en pronon\u00e7ant ces paroles sans attention, nous faisons injure aux trois personnes \u00e0 qui elles s'adressent. Non-seulement nous ne louons pas la Trinit\u00e9, mais nous la deshonorons; non-seulement nous perdons ce tr\u00e9sor de grace que nous pouvions acqu\u00e9rir, mais nous amassons contre nous un tr\u00e9sor de colere. Car ces noms de Pere, de Fils, & de Saint-Esprit, sont des noms divins, des noms de gloire & de majest\u00e9, des noms terribles \u00e0 l'enfer, des noms souverainement respectables pour nous, & par cons\u00e9quent qui ne doivent jamais passer par notre bouche, sans que notre esprit & notre c\u0153ur les accompagnent. Que dis-je? ce sont des noms encore plus aimables que redoutables, des noms de salut, & par-l\u00e0 m\u00eame plus dignes de l'attention de nos esprits & des sentimens affectueux de nos c\u0153urs. Appliquez-vous, Chr\u00e9tiens, \u00e0 ma pens\u00e9e. Quand nous nous trouverons au lit de la mort, & que le Pr\u00eatre dans les derniers momens de notre vie, viendra soutenir notre ame pr\u00eate \u00e0 paro\u00eetre devant Dieu, & former des v\u0153ux pour elle; quels noms employera-t-il pour rendre ses v\u0153ux plus efficaces? les noms du Pere, & du Fils & du Saint-Esprit. Proficiscere, anima Christiana: Partez, ame chr\u00e9tienne, dira le Ministre de l'Eglise, partez au nom du Pere qui vous a cr\u00e9\u00e9e, au nom du Fils qui vous a rachet\u00e9e, au nom du Saint-Esprit qui vous a sanctifi\u00e9e. Noms tout-puissans pour mettre en fuite les l\u00e9gions infernales, pour rendre inutiles tous leurs efforts, & pour attirer sur nous dans ce passage si dangereux, les graces & les secours du ciel. Il y a plus encore: car quand ensuite le m\u00eame Ministre s'adressant \u00e0 Dieu, lui recommandera l'ame du mourant, de quelle raison se servira-t-il pour toucher en sa faveur la divine mis\u00e9ricorde? Peut-\u00eatre, mes chers Auditeurs, n'y avez-vous jamais fait r\u00e9flexion, peut-\u00eatre ne l'avez-vous jamais entendue: mais elle est capable de r\u00e9veiller toute votre confiance, & de vous inspirer un z\u00e8le tout nouveau pour l'honneur de l'adorable Trinit\u00e9. Ecoutez-la. Lic\u00e8t enim peccaverit, tamen Patrem, & Filium, & Spiritum Sanctum non negavit, sed credidit. Ah! Seigneur, s'\u00e9criera le Pr\u00eatre du Dieu vivant, il est vrai, c'est pour un p\u00e9cheur que j'implore votre cl\u00e9mence. Il n'a pas \u00e9t\u00e9 exempt des foiblesses humaines, & le poids de la fragilit\u00e9 l'a fait tomber. Mais du reste vous s\u00e7avez, mon Dieu, que tout p\u00e9cheur qu'il est, il a confess\u00e9 votre auguste Trinit\u00e9; qu'il a reconnu le Pere, le Fils & le Saint-Esprit: Tamen Patrem, & Filium & Spiritum sanctum non negavit, sed credidit. Vous s\u00e7avez qu'il s'est int\u00e9ress\u00e9 \u00e0 la gloire de ces trois divines personnes; & qu'en vous adorant, \u00f4 souverain auteur du monde, il les a fid\u00e8lement & religieusement ador\u00e9es. Et zelum Dei in se habuit, & Deum qui fecit omnia, fideliter adoravit. Voyez-vous, Chr\u00e9tiens, comment la confession de la Trinit\u00e9, mais une confession respectueuse, une confession religieuse, est un des plus grands sujets de confiance que la cr\u00e9ature puisse avoir en son cr\u00e9ateur? Finissons, & pour derni\u00e8re le\u00e7on, apprenons encore comment la confession de cette m\u00eame Trinit\u00e9 est le motif le plus puissant & le plus excellent mod\u00e8le de la charit\u00e9 chr\u00e9tienne: c'est la troisi\u00e8me partie."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p011", "label": "English", "text": "Ah, Christians, allow me to take this occasion to instruct you on a very useful point, though you may not think it so. If every time you and I have pronounced these venerable words, Glory to the Father, to the Son, to the Holy Spirit, or these, In the name of the Father, of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, we had done so with the same reverence and affection as that holy anchorite, how much merit would we have acquired before God? If we were deeply filled with this thought\u2014I who speak and you who listen\u2014we would say them unceasingly with solid devotion; and consider what stores of spiritual riches they would produce for us. For these short words contain the most meritorious acts of the whole of religion. But because, when we say them, we do so without reflection and with a wandering imagination, thinking of something else entirely or thinking of nothing, we say them in vain: though thus confessing the Trinity, perhaps they have not gained us a single degree of grace. What should move us still more is that by pronouncing these words without attention we insult the three persons to whom they are addressed. We not only fail to praise the Trinity but dishonour it; we not only lose the treasure of grace we could acquire but heap up against ourselves a treasure of wrath. For these names of Father, Son and Holy Spirit are divine names, names of glory and majesty, names terrible to hell, names supremely worthy of our reverence. Consequently they should never pass through our mouths without our minds and hearts accompanying them. What am I saying? They are names still more lovable than fearsome, names of salvation, and therefore still more worthy of our minds' attention and our hearts' affectionate sentiments. Christians, attend to my thought. When we are on our deathbeds and the priest comes in the last moments of our lives to sustain our souls, ready to appear before God, and to offer prayers for them, what names will he employ to make those prayers more efficacious? The names of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. Depart, Christian soul: Depart, Christian soul, the Church's minister will say; depart in the name of the Father who created you, in the name of the Son who redeemed you, in the name of the Holy Spirit who sanctified you. Names all-powerful to put the infernal legions to flight, to frustrate all their efforts, and to draw heaven's graces and help upon us in this perilous passage. There is still more. When the same minister then addresses God and commends the dying person's soul to him, what reason will he use to move divine mercy in its favour? Perhaps, my dear hearers, you have never reflected on it; perhaps you have never heard it. Yet it can awaken all your confidence and inspire you with fresh zeal for the honour of the adorable Trinity. Listen to it: For although he has sinned, he has not denied the Father, and the Son, and the Holy Spirit, but has believed. Ah, Lord, the priest of the living God will cry, it is true that I implore your clemency for a sinner. He has not been exempt from human weaknesses, and the weight of frailty has made him fall. Yet you know, my God, that sinner though he is, he has confessed your august Trinity; that he has acknowledged the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit: Yet he has not denied the Father, and the Son and the Holy Spirit, but has believed. You know that he has taken an interest in the glory of these three divine persons, and that in adoring you, sovereign author of the world, he has faithfully and religiously adored them. And he had zeal for God within him, and faithfully adored God who made all things. Do you see, Christians, how confessing the Trinity\u2014but confessing it reverently and religiously\u2014is one of the greatest grounds for confidence that a creature can have in his Creator? Let us conclude, and as our final lesson let us also learn how confessing this same Trinity is the most powerful motive and most excellent model of Christian charity: this is the third part.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-h005", "label": "French original", "text": "III. PARTIE."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-h005", "label": "English", "text": "PART III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p012", "label": "French original", "text": "Toutes choses, Chr\u00e9tiens, nous pr\u00eachent la charit\u00e9 que nous nous devons les uns aux autres; mais rien ne nous la pr\u00eache plus hautement que la Trinit\u00e9 des personnes Divines. Vous me demandez pourquoi? pour deux raisons qui nous sont marqu\u00e9es dans l'Ecriture, & qui toutes deux portent un certain caract\u00e8re de l'Esprit de Dieu. La premi\u00e8re, parce que la foi de la Trinit\u00e9 est le motif & comme le lien substantiel de la charit\u00e9, qui doit \u00eatre entre nous; & la seconde, parce que le myst\u00e8re de la Trinit\u00e9 en est encore le grand mod\u00e8le que Jesus-Christ nous a donn\u00e9 dans son Evangile. Deux raisons, mes chers Auditeurs, dignes de toutes vos r\u00e9flexions, & infiniment capables de vous exciter \u00e0 la pratique de cette vertu."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p012", "label": "English", "text": "Everything, Christians, preaches to us the charity we owe one another; but nothing preaches it more loudly than the Trinity of the divine persons. You ask me why? For two reasons indicated to us in Scripture, both bearing a certain mark of God's Spirit. The first is that faith in the Trinity is the motive and, as it were, the substantial bond of the charity that should exist among us; the second, that the mystery of the Trinity is also the great model of it that Jesus Christ gave us in his Gospel. Two reasons, my dear hearers, worthy of all your reflection and supremely capable of stirring you to practise this virtue.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p013", "label": "French original", "text": "Je dis que la cr\u00e9ance de la Trinit\u00e9 doit \u00eatre le lien de notre charit\u00e9 mutuelle; c'est saint Paul qui nous l'enseigne. Car, dit-il, c'est la foi de ce Myst\u00e8re qui nous unit tous dans un m\u00eame corps de religion. Ecoutez-le, Chr\u00e9tiens, parler lui-m\u00eame, ce Docteur des Nations. Ah! mes Freres, disoit-il aux Eph\u00e9siens, je vous conjure, moi qui suis captif pour Jesus-Christ: Obsecro vos ego vinctus in Domino: & de quoi? de vous aimer les uns les autres, de vous supporter les uns les autres: Supportantes invicem in charitate. Ayez du z\u00e8le pour conserver parmi vous cette unit\u00e9 d'esprit qui est le principe de la v\u00e9ritable paix: Solliciti servare unitatem Spirit\u00fbs in vinculo pacis. Et quel motif leur en donnoit-il? sur quoi fondoit-il cette obligation? Le voici. Car enfin, mes Freres, ajoute l'Ap\u00f4tre, vous n'avez tous qu'un m\u00eame Dieu, vous n'avez tous qu'une m\u00eame foi, vous n'avez tous qu'un m\u00eame bapt\u00eame; vous ne faites tous qu'un m\u00eame corps, qui est l'Eglise: n'est-il donc pas juste que vous ayez tous le m\u00eame esprit? Unum corpus & unus Spiritus, unus Dominus, una fides, unum baptisma. C'est-\u00e0-dire, quelle indignit\u00e9, que nous unissant tous, comme nous faisons, pour honorer le m\u00eame Dieu, nous ne soyons pas unis sur tout le reste? Dans ce m\u00eame Dieu, dans ce m\u00eame Seigneur, nous reconnoissons un Pere dont nous sommes tous les enfans; un Fils dont nous sommes tous les freres, un Saint-Esprit dont nous sommes tous anim\u00e9s; Unus Dominus. Or quel monstre qu'\u00e9tant tous enfans d'un m\u00eame pere, nous vivions ensemble comme des \u00e9trangers; qu'\u00e9tant tous freres du m\u00eame Fils de Dieu, on ne voie parmi nous nulle marque de fraternit\u00e9; que voulant tous avoir le m\u00eame Saint-Esprit, nous fassions paro\u00eetre des sentimens si oppos\u00e9s? Mais ce que j'admire, poursuivoit saint Paul, selon la paraphrase de saint Chrysostome, expliquant ce passage, c'est qu'ayant bien pu nous accorder tous sur un point aussi difficile que la foi de ces trois adorables personnes, Pere, Fils & Saint-Esprit, nous contestions tous les jours sur des bagatelles qui font le sujet de nos inimiti\u00e9s. S'il y avoit quelque chose o\u00f9 nous dussions avoir de la peine \u00e0 convenir, & o\u00f9 l'on p\u00fbt craindre que les esprits ne fussent divis\u00e9s, c'\u00e9toit la cr\u00e9ance d'un Dieu en trois personnes. Cependant nous le croyons, nous en faisons tous la m\u00eame profession, nous renon\u00e7ons \u00e0 tous les doutes & \u00e0 toutes les difficult\u00e9s que notre esprit pourroit former; & cela, disons-nous, pour ne pas troubler l'unit\u00e9 de la foi, Una fides? h\u00e9! Chr\u00e9tiens, n'est-il donc pas \u00e9trange que nous rompions celle de la charit\u00e9 sur des sujets de nulle cons\u00e9quence, & que nous entretenions des animosit\u00e9s & des haines, qui d\u00e9truisent absolument une des vertus fondamentales du Christianisme?"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p013", "label": "English", "text": "I say that belief in the Trinity must be the bond of our mutual charity; Saint Paul teaches us this. For, he says, faith in this mystery unites us all in one body of religion. Christians, listen to this Doctor of the Nations speak for himself. Ah, my brothers, he said to the Ephesians, I entreat you, I who am a prisoner for Jesus Christ: I, a prisoner in the Lord, entreat you. To do what? To love one another, to bear with one another: Bearing with one another in charity. Be zealous to preserve among yourselves that unity of spirit which is the principle of true peace: Taking care to preserve the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace. And what motive did he give them? On what did he ground this obligation? Here it is. After all, my brothers, the Apostle adds, you all have only one God, you all have only one faith, you all have only one baptism; you all form only one body, the Church. Is it not therefore just that you should all have the same spirit? One body and one Spirit, one Lord, one faith, one baptism. That is to say: what an indignity that, uniting as we do to honour the same God, we should not be united in everything else! In this same God, in this same Lord, we acknowledge a Father whose children we all are; a Son whose brothers we all are; a Holy Spirit by whom we are all animated: One Lord. What a monstrous thing, then, that being all children of the same Father we live together as strangers; that being all brothers of the same Son of God there should be no sign of brotherhood among us; that wishing all to have the same Holy Spirit we should display such opposed sentiments! But what amazes me, Saint Paul continued, according to Saint Chrysostom's paraphrase explaining this passage, is that, although we have all been able to agree on a point as difficult as faith in these three adorable persons, Father, Son and Holy Spirit, we quarrel every day over trifles that become the grounds of our enmities. If there were anything on which we should have difficulty agreeing, anything about which one might fear that minds would be divided, it was belief in one God in three persons. Yet we believe it; we all make the same profession of it; we renounce all the doubts and difficulties our minds might raise; and we do so, we say, to avoid disturbing the unity of faith: One faith? Ah, Christians, is it not strange, then, that we break the unity of charity over matters of no consequence, and cherish animosities and hatreds that utterly destroy one of Christianity's fundamental virtues?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p014", "label": "French original", "text": "Tel \u00e9toit le raisonnement de l'Ap\u00f4tre saint Paul, pour convaincre les Eph\u00e9siens: Unus Dominus, una fides: Raisonnement qu'il fait encore tant valoir dans une autre de ses Ep\u00eetres, o\u00f9 s'adressant aux Chr\u00e9tiens de Corinthe, il leur dit: Qu'est-ce que j'entends, mes Freres? on me rapporte qu'il y a des cabales parmi vous, qu'il y a des schismes & des factions, l'un tient le parti de Paul, l'autre d'Apollo, celui-ci de Pierre: mais quoi? est-ce au nom de Pierre, est-ce au nom d'Apollo, est-ce au nom de Paul que vous avez \u00e9t\u00e9 baptis\u00e9s? Numquid in nomine Pauli baptizati estis? Je remercie Dieu de ce que je n'ai baptis\u00e9 personne chez vous, de peur qu'on ne dise que vous \u00eates baptis\u00e9s en mon nom: Gratias ago Deo, qu\u00f2d neminem vestr\u00fbm baptizavi, ne quis dicat qu\u00f2d in nomine meo baptizati estis. C'est au nom du Pere, du Fils, & du Saint-Esprit, que vous avez re\u00e7u le bapt\u00eame; tous dans la m\u00eame forme, tous avec le m\u00eame caract\u00e8re, tous par l'efficace & la vertu de la m\u00eame Trinit\u00e9. Or, cela \u00e9tant, vous avez tous un engagement indispensable \u00e0 vivre dans le m\u00eame esprit: & vous oubliez ce que vous \u00eates, quand vous laissez na\u00eetre parmi vous des discordes. Remarquez-vous, Chr\u00e9tiens, comment saint Paul fondoit le devoir de la charit\u00e9 sur la foi de la Trinit\u00e9? Una fides, unum baptisma. En effet, s'il y a un motif qui doive nous engager \u00e0 nous aimer fraternellement, c'est cette unit\u00e9 de cr\u00e9ance & de foi. Comme la diff\u00e9rence de religion a toujours \u00e9t\u00e9, pour ainsi dire, le glaive de division parmi les hommes, jusqu'\u00e0 rompre enti\u00e8rement les liens les plus inviolables de la nature; aussi de tout tems a-t-on consid\u00e9r\u00e9 l'unit\u00e9 de religion comme le plus sacr\u00e9 n\u0153ud de l'amiti\u00e9. Il n'est pas jusqu'\u00e0 nos H\u00e9r\u00e9tiques qui ne le pensent de la sorte. D\u00e8s-l\u00e0 qu'ils font secte, & qu'ils composent une Eglise pr\u00e9tendue, ils commencent \u00e0 s'entraider. Vous en \u00eates t\u00e9moins, mes chers Auditeurs, & vous s\u00e7avez comment ils sont unis ensemble, comment ils prennent les int\u00e9r\u00eats les uns des autres, comment ils se pr\u00eatent secours dans leurs besoins, comment leurs pauvres sont assist\u00e9s, comment ils visitent leurs malades. Qui fait cela? ce n'est pas l'unit\u00e9 de la foi, puisque hors de l'Eglise ils ne peuvent avoir la foi; quoi donc? l'unit\u00e9 d'erreur, l'unit\u00e9 de mensonge, l'unit\u00e9 de schisme. Ce petit troupeau o\u00f9 ils sont tous ramass\u00e9s, voil\u00e0 ce qui les lie, voil\u00e0 ce qui arr\u00eate toutes leurs querelles, voil\u00e0 ce qui termine tous leurs diff\u00e9rens, voil\u00e0 pourquoi ils s'appellent freres & se comportent en freres. Quelle honte, que l'unit\u00e9 de la foi o\u00f9 nous vivons, fasse moins sur nous, que ne fait sur eux l'unit\u00e9 d'une fausse r\u00e9forme! il en va n\u00e9anmoins ainsi: ils s'unissent, & nous nous divisons; ils se rendent des offices de freres, & nous nous traitons souvent en ennemis; ils le voient, ils s'en \u00e9tonnent ils en sont scandalis\u00e9s: ils nous le reprochent m\u00eame. Or \u00e0 qui est-ce de faire cesser ce reproche, qu'\u00e0 nous-m\u00eames? & il cessera d\u00e8s que la charit\u00e9 entrera dans nos c\u0153urs. Car toutes ces haines, toutes ces envies, tous ces d\u00e9sirs de vengeance, tous ces m\u00e9pris que nous faisons du prochain; toutes ces paroles aigres & piquantes, qui nous \u00e9chappent; tout cela s'\u00e9vanouiroit bient\u00f4t, si nous avions la vraie charit\u00e9. La foi d'un Dieu en trois personnes en doit \u00eatre le motif, & j'ajoute qu'elle nous en pr\u00e9sente encore le plus parfait mod\u00e8le."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p014", "label": "English", "text": "Such was the Apostle Saint Paul's reasoning to convince the Ephesians: One Lord, one faith. He also presses this reasoning so strongly in another of his epistles, where, addressing the Christians of Corinth, he says: What is this I hear, my brothers? I am told that there are intrigues among you, schisms and factions: one takes Paul's side, another Apollo's, another Peter's. But what? Was it in Peter's name, in Apollo's name, in Paul's name that you were baptized? Were you baptized in Paul's name? I thank God that I baptized no one among you, lest it be said that you were baptized in my name: I give thanks to God that I baptized none of you, lest anyone say that you were baptized in my name. It was in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit that you received baptism, all in the same form, all with the same character, all through the efficacy and power of the same Trinity. Since this is so, you all have an indispensable obligation to live in the same spirit; and you forget what you are when you allow discord to arise among you. Do you notice, Christians, how Saint Paul grounded the duty of charity on faith in the Trinity? One faith, one baptism. Indeed, if any motive ought to bind us to love one another as brothers, it is this unity of belief and faith. Just as difference of religion has always been, so to speak, a sword of division among men, even breaking entirely the most inviolable bonds of nature, so unity of religion has always been regarded as friendship's most sacred bond. Even our heretics think this way. Once they form a sect and constitute a pretended Church, they begin helping one another. You witness it, my dear hearers, and you know how they are united, how they take up one another's interests, help one another in their needs, assist their poor and visit their sick. What brings this about? It is not unity of faith, since outside the Church they cannot have faith. What, then? Unity of error, unity of falsehood, unity of schism. The little flock in which they are all gathered is what binds them, stops all their quarrels and settles all their differences; this is why they call one another brothers and act as brothers. What a shame that the unity of faith in which we live does less for us than the unity of a false reform does for them! Yet so it is: they unite and we divide; they perform brotherly services for one another, and we often treat one another as enemies. They see it, are astonished and scandalized by it; they even reproach us for it. Who but ourselves can put an end to this reproach? It will cease as soon as charity enters our hearts. All these hatreds, envies, desires for revenge, all our contempt for our neighbour, all the bitter and stinging words that escape us: all this would soon vanish if we had true charity. Faith in one God in three persons should be its motive; and I add that it also presents us with its most perfect model.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p015", "label": "French original", "text": "Quand je vous ai dit, mes Freres, en d'autres discours, que le Fils de Dieu nous avoit oblig\u00e9 \u00e0 nous aimer les uns les autres comme il nous a aim\u00e9s: Mandatum novum do vobis, ut diligatis invicem, sicut dilexi vos; vous ne croyez pas que la charit\u00e9 p\u00fbt \u00eatre port\u00e9e plus haut. Cet amour d'un Dieu sacrifi\u00e9 pour le salut des hommes, vous paroissoit le dernier terme, o\u00f9 l'amour du prochain p\u00fbt s'\u00e9lever. Mais voici quelque chose encore de plus grand: car il faut nous aimer comme les trois personnes de la Trinit\u00e9 s'aiment; comme le Pere aime le Fils, comme le Fils aime le Pere, comme le Pere & le Fils s'aiment dans le Saint-Esprit. Tel est l'exemplaire qui nous est aujourd'hui propos\u00e9: Inspice & fac secundum exemplar. Et par qui nous est-il propos\u00e9? par Jesus-Christ m\u00eame, l'oracle & la sagesse de Dieu. Pater sancte, disoit-il, parlant \u00e0 son Pere, serva eos in nomine tuo quos dedisti mihi, ut sint unum sicut & nos. Mon Pere, je vous offre tous mes \u00e9lus, tous mes fid\u00e8les, tous ceux que vous m'avez donn\u00e9s \u00e0 instruire: conservez-les par votre grace, afin qu'ils soient un comme vous & moi. Que veut-il dire, & comment arriverons-nous \u00e0 cette perfection? le Pere & le Fils ne sont qu'un m\u00eame Dieu dans la Trinit\u00e9; le Fils est consubstantiel au Pere, le Pere est la m\u00eame substance que le Fils: quelle charit\u00e9 nous peut unir de la sorte? Ah! r\u00e9pond saint Augustin, ce que le Sauveur du monde a voulu nous faire entendre, c'est que nous devons \u00eatre parfaitement unis de c\u0153ur & de volont\u00e9; que nous devons \u00eatre par grace & par imitation, ce que les trois divines personnes sont par la n\u00e9cessit\u00e9 de leur \u00eatre; que comme il n'y a rien qui ne soit commun entre elles; aussi la charit\u00e9 du Christianisme doit nous faire renoncer \u00e0 tous nos int\u00e9r\u00eats propres; que de m\u00eame que le Fils de Dieu disoit \u00e0 son Pere: Pater, omnia mea tua sunt, & tua mea sunt; Tout ce qui est \u00e0 moi est \u00e0 vous, & tout ce qui est \u00e0 vous est \u00e0 moi; de m\u00eame il faut que nous soyons pr\u00eats de dire \u00e0 nos freres, ces biens que Dieu m'a donn\u00e9s, sont pour vous aussi bien que pour moi; & ces mis\u00e8res que vous souffrez, sont les miennes aussi-bien que les v\u00f4tres. Que seroit-ce que le Christianisme, si cette charit\u00e9 y r\u00e9gnoit? Que seroit-ce que tant de familles, si les peres & les enfans, si les ma\u00eetres & les domestiques, si le mari & la femme, si les freres & les s\u0153urs gardoient entre eux ce parfait accord? Au lieu de ces troubles qui y mettent la confusion, au lieu de ces proc\u00e8s qui les d\u00e9solent, au lieu de ces \u00e9tats scandaleux qui les d\u00e9crient, elles se soutiendroient, & dans un repos inalt\u00e9rable, elles go\u00fbteroient toutes les douceurs d'une paix chr\u00e9tienne. Alors plein de consolation, j'aurois de quoi vous f\u00e9liciter, & je m'\u00e9crierois avec le Proph\u00e8te: Quam bonum & quam jucundum habitare fratres in unum! Quel bonheur pour ces Chr\u00e9tiens, quel bonheur pour ces maisons, de vivre dans une concorde qui y entretient le calme, & qui y fait fleurir la pi\u00e9t\u00e9."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p015", "label": "English", "text": "When I told you, my brothers, in other discourses, that the Son of God had obliged us to love one another as he loved us\u2014A new commandment I give you, that you love one another as I have loved you\u2014you did not believe charity could be raised any higher. This love of a God sacrificed for men's salvation seemed to you the highest point to which love of our neighbour could rise. Yet here is something still greater: we must love one another as the three persons of the Trinity love one another; as the Father loves the Son, as the Son loves the Father, as the Father and Son love one another in the Holy Spirit. Such is the pattern proposed to us today: Look, and make according to the pattern. And who proposes it to us? Jesus Christ himself, God's oracle and wisdom. Holy Father, he said, speaking to his Father, keep in your name those whom you have given me, that they may be one as we are. My Father, I offer you all my elect, all my faithful, all those you have given me to instruct. Preserve them by your grace, that they may be one as you and I are. What does he mean, and how shall we reach this perfection? The Father and Son are one and the same God in the Trinity; the Son is consubstantial with the Father, the Father is the same substance as the Son. What charity can unite us in that way? Ah, Saint Augustine replies, what the Saviour of the world meant us to understand is that we must be perfectly united in heart and will; that we must be by grace and imitation what the three divine persons are by the necessity of their being. Just as there is nothing that is not common among them, so Christian charity must make us renounce all our private interests. Just as the Son of God said to his Father, Father, all that is mine is yours, and all that is yours is mine\u2014Everything that belongs to me belongs to you, and everything that belongs to you belongs to me\u2014so we must be ready to say to our brothers: These goods God has given me are for you as well as for me; and these miseries you suffer are mine as well as yours. What would Christianity be if this charity reigned within it? What would so many families be if fathers and children, masters and servants, husband and wife, brothers and sisters maintained this perfect harmony among themselves? In place of the troubles that bring confusion, the lawsuits that desolate them, the scandalous conditions that discredit them, they would sustain one another and, in undisturbed repose, enjoy all the sweetness of Christian peace. Then, full of consolation, I would have reason to congratulate you and would cry out with the prophet: How good and how pleasant it is for brothers to dwell together! What happiness for these Christians, what happiness for these households, to live in a harmony that maintains tranquillity and makes piety flourish among them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p016", "label": "French original", "text": "Mais que voyons-nous? tout le contraire, & c'est ce que nous ne pouvons assez d\u00e9plorer. Point d'union dans le christianisme, & m\u00eame entre ceux que les loix de la nature les plus inviolables & les plus sacr\u00e9es devroient tenir \u00e9troitement li\u00e9s les uns aux autres; je veux dire, point d'union, entre qui? souvent entre des proches; souvent entre des freres & des s\u0153urs, souvent entre des peres & des enfans. Je dis plus: point d'union, souvent entre des ministres de Jesus-Christ, qui par \u00e9tat n\u00e9anmoins & par profession doivent \u00eatre des ministres de paix; souvent entre des personnes consacr\u00e9es au Dieu de la paix par les v\u0153ux les plus solemnels, portant le m\u00eame habit & vivant sous la m\u00eame r\u00e8gle. Voil\u00e0 ce que nous voyons: & pourquoi? parce que nous ne s\u00e7avons pas, ou plut\u00f4t, parce que nous ne voulons pas nous former sur le grand mod\u00e8le que la foi nous met devant les yeux. Prenez garde: dans l'adorable Trinit\u00e9, point de sentimens oppos\u00e9s; ce que veut une personne divine, les autres le veulent: mais parmi nous, ce sont des contradictions \u00e9ternelles: soit bizarrerie d'humeur, soit malignit\u00e9 de naturel, soit hauteur d'esprit & fausse gloire qu'on se fait de ne c\u00e9der jamais, quelque puisse \u00eatre le principe du mal, on a ses id\u00e9es particulieres, & l'on veut qu'elles pr\u00e9valent \u00e0 tout; on a ses caprices, & l'on veut qu'ils soient suivis en tout. Et parce que nous ne trouvons pas toujours des gens assez dociles pour s'asservir \u00e0 nos caprices & \u00e0 nos id\u00e9es; parce que chacun au contraire pr\u00e9tend dominer & se faire \u00e9couter, l'emporter: de-l\u00e0 les contestations & les disputes, de-l\u00e0 les guerres qui commencent par l'esprit & qui finissent par le c\u0153ur, de-l\u00e0 les aigreurs & une maligne d\u00e9termination \u00e0 se butter toujours les uns contre les autres. C'est assez qu'un tel ait parl\u00e9 de telle maniere, pour engager un tel \u00e0 tenir un langage tout diff\u00e9rent. C'est assez que celui-ci estime telle chose, pour porter celui-l\u00e0 \u00e0 la condamner: comme si l'on n'avoit point d'autre r\u00e8gle, ou pour penser, ou pour agir, qu'une aveugle obstination \u00e0 ne s'accommoder au gr\u00e9 de personne, & \u00e0 ne convenir avec personne. Dans l'adorable Trinit\u00e9 point d'int\u00e9r\u00eats s\u00e9par\u00e9s: mais parmi nous mille int\u00e9r\u00eats qui nous divisent. On ne pense qu'\u00e0 soi-m\u00eame, on n'a \u00e9gard qu'\u00e0 soi-m\u00eame, on rapporte tout \u00e0 soi-m\u00eame. Et comme cet int\u00e9r\u00eat propre \u00e0 quoi l'on est r\u00e9solu de ne rien refuser, ne peut souvent s'accorder avec l'int\u00e9r\u00eat du prochain, il n'y a point d'injustice & de violence \u00e0 quoi l'on ne se porte, pour \u00e9carter ou pour d\u00e9truire tout ce qui pourroit faire obstacle & arr\u00eater les desseins qu'on a form\u00e9s. De-l\u00e0 les mauvais tours, les trahisons, les faux rapports, les m\u00e9disances, les calomnies, les chicanes, les proc\u00e8s, toutes les vexations qu'inspire la cupidit\u00e9 & qui ruinent la charit\u00e9. C'est sur quoi l'Ap\u00f4tre s'expliquoit encore avec tant d'\u00e9loquence & tant de z\u00e8le en parlant aux Corinthiens. Il avoit appris qu'ils s'appelloient les uns les autres devant les tribunaux de la justice pour terminer leurs diff\u00e9rends, & l\u00e0-dessus que leur disoit-il? Ah! mes Freres, que ne souffrez vous plut\u00f4t l'injure qu'on vous fait? Quare non magis injuriam accipitis? Que ne souffrez-vous plut\u00f4t le dommage que vous recevez? Quare non magis fraudem patimini? Mais bien loin, poursuivoit le saint Ap\u00f4tre, d'\u00eatre ainsi dispos\u00e9s \u00e0 pardonner & \u00e0 souffrir, vous vous outragez mutuellement & vous travaillez \u00e0 vous entred\u00e9truire: Sed vos injuriam facitis & fraudatis. Ce qui le touchoit davantage, & ce qu'il leur reprochoit plus vivement, c'est que des freres, que des Chr\u00e9tiens, se traitassent de la sorte: Et hoc fratribus. Comme s'il leur e\u00fbt dit: Que des pa\u00efens ayent ensemble des d\u00e9m\u00eal\u00e9s, je n'en suis point surpris; ils ont des Dieux qui leur en donnent l'exemple: mais nous, qui dans le Dieu que nous adorons, avons le mod\u00e8le de la plus parfaite unit\u00e9, d'une unit\u00e9 constante, d'une unit\u00e9 indivisible, d'une unit\u00e9 \u00e9ternelle, qu'on nous voye former entre nous des partis, des intrigues, des cabales, que pour les moindres int\u00e9r\u00eats, & pour de viles pr\u00e9tentions dont nous ne voulons rien rel\u00e2cher, on voye des fid\u00e8les s'\u00e9lever contre des fid\u00e8les, parler contre des fid\u00e8les, agir contre des fid\u00e8les: Et hoc fratribus: c'est ce qui m'\u00e9tonne, & ce que je n'accorde pas avec le caract\u00e8re de leur religion."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p016", "label": "English", "text": "But what do we see? The exact opposite, which we cannot deplore enough. No unity in Christianity, even among those whom nature's most inviolable and sacred laws should bind closely together. I mean, no unity among whom? Often among relatives; often among brothers and sisters; often among fathers and children. I say more: often no unity among ministers of Jesus Christ who, by their state and profession, ought nevertheless to be ministers of peace; often among persons consecrated to the God of peace by the most solemn vows, wearing the same habit and living under the same rule. This is what we see. Why? Because we do not know how\u2014or rather, because we refuse\u2014to form ourselves upon the great model faith places before our eyes. Take heed: in the adorable Trinity there are no opposed sentiments; what one divine person wills, the others will. Among us, however, there are endless contradictions. Whether through caprice of temper, malice of disposition, pride of mind or the false glory of never yielding, whatever the source of the evil, we have our private ideas and insist that they prevail over everything; we have our whims and insist that they be followed in everything. Because we do not always find people docile enough to submit to our whims and ideas, because on the contrary everyone claims to dominate, to be heard, to carry the day, there arise controversies and disputes, wars that begin in the mind and end in the heart, bitterness and a malicious determination always to set ourselves against one another. It is enough that one person has spoken in a particular way to induce another to speak quite differently. It is enough that this person esteems something to make that person condemn it, as though we had no other rule for thinking or acting than a blind obstinacy in accommodating no one and agreeing with no one. In the adorable Trinity there are no separate interests; among us there are a thousand interests that divide us. We think only of ourselves, consider only ourselves, refer everything to ourselves. Since this self-interest, to which we are resolved to refuse nothing, often cannot agree with our neighbour's interest, there is no injustice or violence we will not commit to remove or destroy everything that might obstruct and frustrate the designs we have formed. Hence come malicious tricks, betrayals, false reports, detraction, calumnies, quibbles, lawsuits, all the harassments that covetousness inspires and that ruin charity. This is what the Apostle also expressed with such eloquence and zeal when speaking to the Corinthians. He had learned that they summoned one another before courts of justice to settle their disputes. What did he say to them? Ah, my brothers, why do you not rather suffer the wrong done to you? Why do you not rather accept injury? Why do you not rather suffer the loss you receive? Why do you not rather endure fraud? But far from being thus disposed to forgive and suffer, the holy Apostle continued, you wrong one another and labour to destroy one another: But you yourselves do wrong and defraud. What moved him still more, and what he reproached them for more sharply, was that brothers, Christians, should treat one another in this way: And this to brothers. As if he had said to them: I am not surprised that pagans have disputes with one another; they have gods who set them the example. But we, who have in the God we adore the model of the most perfect unity, a constant, indivisible, eternal unity: that we should be seen forming parties, intrigues and factions among ourselves; that for the slightest interests and sordid claims of which we will yield nothing, the faithful should be seen rising against the faithful, speaking against the faithful, acting against the faithful\u2014And this to brothers\u2014this is what astonishes me, and what I cannot reconcile with the character of their religion.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p017", "label": "French original", "text": "Appliquons-nous \u00e0 nous-m\u00eames ces reproches, Chr\u00e9tiens Auditeurs; car ils ne nous conviennent que trop: & en quels termes se f\u00fbt exprim\u00e9 saint Paul, s'il e\u00fbt \u00e9t\u00e9 t\u00e9moin de notre conduite, je veux dire de nos animosit\u00e9s, de nos envies, de nos ressentimens, de nos vengeances, de tant d'\u00e9clats scandaleux qui font le sujet des entretiens du monde, & que le monde lui-m\u00eame est le premier \u00e0 condamner? C'est \u00e0 vous, \u00f4 Dieu de la charit\u00e9 & de la paix, c'est \u00e0 vous \u00e0 maintenir parmi nous l'une & l'autre, ou plut\u00f4t \u00e0 les y \u00e9tablir: car elles ne sont que trop alt\u00e9r\u00e9es. Pere tout-puissant, vous avez form\u00e9 nos c\u0153urs, & vous \u00eates toujours ma\u00eetre de les tourner comme il vous pla\u00eet. Fils \u00e9gal \u00e0 votre Pere, & \u00e9ternel comme lui, mais fait chair pour nous, vous nous avez rassembl\u00e9s sous une m\u00eame loi, & c'est une loi d'amour. Esprit Saint, vous \u00eates l'amour substantiel du Pere & du Fils, & c'est par vous que la charit\u00e9 est r\u00e9pandue dans les ames. Trinit\u00e9 souverainement adorable & aimable, c'est de votre sein que nous sommes tous sortis, & c'est dans votre sein que vous voulez tous nous rappeler. Unissez-nous sur la terre, comme nous devons l'\u00eatre dans l'\u00e9ternit\u00e9 bienheureuse, o\u00f9 nous conduise, &c."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-p017", "label": "English", "text": "Let us apply these reproaches to ourselves, Christian hearers, for they fit us only too well. In what terms would Saint Paul have expressed himself had he witnessed our conduct\u2014I mean our animosities, envies, resentments, acts of revenge, all those scandalous outbursts that are the subject of the world's conversation and that the world itself is the first to condemn? It is for you, God of charity and peace, it is for you to maintain both among us, or rather to establish them here, for they are only too impaired. Almighty Father, you formed our hearts, and you always have the power to turn them as you please. Son, equal to your Father and eternal like him, yet made flesh for us, you have gathered us under one law, a law of love. Holy Spirit, you are the substantial love of the Father and the Son, and through you charity is poured into our souls. Trinity, supremely adorable and lovable, it is from your bosom that we have all come forth, and into your bosom that you will to call us all back. Unite us on earth as we are to be united in blessed eternity, to which may he lead us, &c.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n001", "label": "French original", "text": "Agust."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n001", "label": "English", "text": "Augustine.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n002", "label": "French original", "text": "Pacian."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n002", "label": "English", "text": "Pacian.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n003", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan. c. 4."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n003", "label": "English", "text": "John, chapter 4.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n004", "label": "French original", "text": "Concil: Triden."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n004", "label": "English", "text": "Council of Trent.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n005", "label": "French original", "text": "Genes. c. 22."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n005", "label": "English", "text": "Genesis, chapter 22.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n006", "label": "French original", "text": "Ex Ord: comm. anim."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n006", "label": "English", "text": "From the Order for the Commendation of the Soul.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n007", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n007", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n008", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n008", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n009", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n009", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n010", "label": "French original", "text": "Ephes. c. 4."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n010", "label": "English", "text": "Ephesians, chapter 4.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n011", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n011", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n012", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n012", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n013", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n013", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n014", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 1."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n014", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, chapter 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n015", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n015", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n016", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan. c. 13."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n016", "label": "English", "text": "John, chapter 13.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n017", "label": "French original", "text": "Exod. c. 14."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n017", "label": "English", "text": "Exodus, chapter 14.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n018", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan. c. 17."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n018", "label": "English", "text": "John, chapter 17.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n019", "label": "French original", "text": "Joan. c. 17."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n019", "label": "English", "text": "John, chapter 17.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n020", "label": "French original", "text": "Psalm. 132."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n020", "label": "English", "text": "Psalm 132.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n021", "label": "French original", "text": "1. Cor. c. 6."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n021", "label": "English", "text": "1 Corinthians, chapter 6.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n022", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n022", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n023", "label": "French original", "text": "Ibid."}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-n023", "label": "English", "text": "Ibid.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite-ed001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The printed margin gives Exodus 14, but the quotation is from Exodus 25:40; Hebrews 8:5 also recalls the pattern shown to Moses. The printed reference is preserved."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "On the Most Holy Trinity: Faith, Hope and Charity", "topics": ["Trinity", "faith", "hope", "charity", "holiness"], "url": "sermons/bourdaloue-mysteres-trinite.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-advent", "calendar_label": "Advent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-advent-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De adventu Domini, sermo I, De divinorum promissorum exspectatione, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 11\u201314.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Advent", "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "Titus 2:13", "Matthew 6:21", "Philippians 3:20", "Lamentations 3:24\u201326", "Habakkuk 2:3", "Romans 8:12 (printed locator; see note)", "Micah 4:9 (printed locator; see note)"], "occasion": "De adventu Domini", "occasion_detail": "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "occasion_label": "Advent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This first Advent sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Guerric moves from the fulfilment of God's promises in Christ's first coming to patient hope for his return. The final prayer develops an image of remaining with Christ on the Cross. The source names Advent without assigning a particular Sunday."}, {"anchor": "g-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "DE ADVENTU DOMINI"}, {"anchor": "g-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE ADVENT OF THE LORD", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "g-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De divinorum promissorum exspectatione."}, {"anchor": "g-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On Waiting for God's Promises", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Salvatorem exspectamus (Tit. ii, 13). Vere exspectatio justorum laetitia, exspectantium beatam spem, et adventum gloriae magni Dei et salvatoris nostri Jesu Christi. Et nunc quae est exspectatio mea, inquit justus, nonne Dominus? (Psal. xxxviii, 8.) Et conversus ad Dominum: Scio, inquit, quia non confundes me ab exspectatione mea (Psal. cxviii, 116); quia jam substantia mea apud te est (Psal. xxxviii, 8), quia natura nostra de nobis sumpta, et pro nobis oblata, apud te glorificata est: spem nobis tribuens, quia ad te omnis caro veniet (Psal. lxiv, 3); caputque membra sequentur, ut holocaustum compleatur. Pleniore tamen fiducia, quia tutiore conscientia, Dominum exspectare potest, cui dicere datum est: Substantia possessiunculae meae, o Domine, apud te est; quia facultates meas, vel tibi erogando, vel pro te contemnendo, in coelis thesaurizavi, et ad pedes tuos omnia mea deposui, sciens quia potens es depositum meum non modo servare, sed etiam in centuplum restituere, et vitam aeternam adjicere. Quam beati vos, pauperes spiritu, quia juxta consilium admirabilis Consiliarii thesaurizastis vobis thesauros in coelo; ne si in terra thesauri vestri remanerent, corda vestra pariter putrescerent! Ubi enim, inquit, thesaurus tuus, ibi et cor tuum (Matth. vi, 21). Eant igitur, eant corda post thesauros suos; sursum affixa sit cogitatio, et ad Dominum suspensa exspectatio, ut et vobis competat dicere cum Apostolo: Nostra autem conversatio in coelis est, unde etiam Salvatorem exspectamus (Philipp. iii, 20). O exspectatio gentium! universi qui te exspectant non confundentur. Te expectaverunt patres nostri, omnes justi ab origine mundi in te speraverunt, et non sunt confusi (Psal. xxi, 5, 6). Jam enim suscepta misericordia tua in medio templi tui, chorus laetantium et laudantium concinit: Benedictus qui venit in nomine Domini (Matth. xxi, 9). Exspectans exspectavi Dominum, et intendit mihi (Psal. xxxix, 2). Et in humilitate carnis agnoscentes majestatem divinitatis: Ecce, inquiunt, Dominus noster iste; exspectavimus eum, et salvabit nos: iste Dominus, sustinuimus eum; exsultabimus et laetabimur in salutari ejus (Isa. xxv, 9)."}, {"anchor": "g-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. We await the Saviour (Tit. ii, 13). Truly, the waiting of the righteous is joy, as they await the blessed hope and the coming of the glory of our great God and Saviour Jesus Christ. \u2018And now, what am I waiting for,\u2019 says the righteous person, \u2018if not the Lord?\u2019 (Psal. xxxviii, 8.) Turning to the Lord, he says: \u2018I know that you will not disappoint my hope\u2019 (Psal. cxviii, 116), \u2018because my very being is already with you\u2019 (Psal. xxxviii, 8): for our nature, taken from us and offered for us, has been glorified with you. This gives us hope that all flesh will come to you (Psal. lxiv, 3), and that the members will follow the Head, so that the whole burnt offering may be complete. Yet the person who has been granted these words can await the Lord with fuller confidence, because his conscience is more secure: \u2018O Lord, the substance of my little property is with you. By spending my resources for you, or giving them up for your sake, I have stored them in heaven; I have laid all I possess at your feet, knowing that you can not only keep what I have entrusted to you, but return it a hundredfold and add eternal life.\u2019 How blessed you are, poor in spirit! Following the advice of the wonderful Counsellor, you have stored up treasures for yourselves in heaven, lest your hearts decay along with treasures left on earth. \u2018For where your treasure is,\u2019 he says, \u2018there is your heart also\u2019 (Matth. vi, 21). Let our hearts go, then, let them follow their treasures. Let our thoughts be fixed above and our expectation hang upon the Lord, so that you too may say with the Apostle: \u2018Our citizenship is in heaven, from which we also await the Saviour\u2019 (Philipp. iii, 20). O expectation of the nations! All who await you will not be put to shame. Our fathers awaited you; all the righteous from the beginning of the world hoped in you and were not put to shame (Psal. xxi, 5, 6). For now that your mercy has been received in the midst of your temple, the choir of those rejoicing and praising sings together: \u2018Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord\u2019 (Matth. xxi, 9). \u2018I waited and waited for the Lord, and he attended to me\u2019 (Psal. xxxix, 2). And, recognizing the majesty of divinity in the lowliness of flesh, they say: \u2018Look, this is our Lord; we have waited for him, and he will save us. This is the Lord; we have waited for him. We shall rejoice and be glad in his salvation\u2019 (Isa. xxv, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sicut autem Ecclesia in antiquis justis exspectavit adventum primum; sic in novis reexspectat secundum: sicut in primo certa de pretio redemptionis; sic in secundo, de praemio remunerationis: atque hujus exspectatione spei suspensa a terrenis, tam feliciter quam ardenter inhiat aeternis. Cum igitur alii festinantes praesentibus beari, nec sustinentes consilium Domini, currunt ad diripiendam praedam hujus mundi; beatus vir cujus est nomen Domini spes ejus, et non respexit in vanitates et insanias falsas (Psal. xxxix, 5). Qui abstinet se a vitiis eorum, tanquam ab immunditiis, sciens melius esse humiliari cum mitibus, quam dividere spolia cum superbis (Prov. xvi, 19). Loquensque secum consolatur se, et ait: Pars mea Dominus, dixit anima mea: propterea exspectabo eum. Bonus est Dominus sperantibus in eum, animae quaerenti illum. Bonum est praestolari cum silentio salutare Dei (Thren. iii, 24\u201326). Defecit quidem anima mea in salutare tuum, o Domine, sed in verbum tuum supersperavi (Psal. cxviii, 81). Spes quidem quae differtur, scriptum est, affligit animam (Prov. xiii, 12): sed, licet lassa sit ex desiderii dilatione, secura est tamen ex promissione. In ipsum solum sperans, sed et supersperans adjiciam spem: sicut assidue adjicitur tribulatio super tribulationem, dilatio super dilationem. Certus sum enim quia apparebit in finem, et non mentietur. Ideoque, et si moram fecerit, exspectabo eum, quia veniens veniet, et non tardabit (Habac. ii, 3), ultra praestitutum tempus vel opportunum. Quod opportunum? Cum impletus fuerit numerus fratrum nostrorum, et consummatum fuerit tempus misericordiae ad poenitentiam indultum. Audi Isaiam ad superna saepius admissum consilia, quo videlicet consilio Dominus judicium differat. Propterea, inquit, Dominus exspectat ut misereatur vestri; et ideo exaltabitur parcens vobis, quia Deus judicii Dominus. Beati omnes qui exspectant eum (Isai. xxx, 18)."}, {"anchor": "g-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Just as the Church awaited the first coming in the righteous of old, so she awaits the second in the righteous of today: as sure of the price of redemption at the first as of the reward to be bestowed at the second. Lifted away from earthly things by this hopeful expectation, she longs for eternal things as happily as she does eagerly. While others hurry to find happiness in present things and, refusing to wait for the Lord's counsel, rush to seize this world's spoils, blessed is the man whose hope is the name of the Lord, who has not turned to vanities and lying follies (Psal. xxxix, 5). He keeps away from their vices as from filth, knowing that it is better to be humbled with the meek than to divide spoils with the proud (Prov. xvi, 19). Speaking within himself, he takes comfort and says: \u2018The Lord is my portion, says my soul; therefore I shall wait for him. The Lord is good to those who hope in him, to the soul that seeks him. It is good to wait in silence for God's salvation\u2019 (Thren. iii, 24\u201326). \u2018My soul has indeed grown faint for your salvation, O Lord, but I have hoped all the more in your word\u2019 (Psal. cxviii, 81). It is written that hope deferred afflicts the soul (Prov. xiii, 12); yet, though wearied by the postponement of its desire, it remains secure because of the promise. Hoping in him alone, and hoping beyond hope, I shall add hope, just as tribulation is continually added to tribulation and delay to delay. For I am certain that he will appear at the end and will not lie. Therefore, even if he delays, I shall wait for him, because he who is coming will come and will not be late (Habac. ii, 3), beyond the appointed or fitting time. What is the fitting time? When the number of our brethren has been completed, and the time mercifully granted for repentance has run its course. Listen to Isaiah, so often admitted to heavenly counsels, explaining why the Lord postpones judgment: \u2018Therefore the Lord waits to have mercy on you; therefore he will be exalted in sparing you, because the Lord is a God of judgment. Blessed are all who await him\u2019 (Isai. xxx, 18).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Propterea, si sapis, prospicies tibi quomodo dilationis hujus induciis utaris: utique si peccator es, ad poenitentiam, non ad negligentiam; si sanctus, ut proficias in sanctitate, non ut deficias a fide. Si autem dixerit malus servus ille in corde suo: Moram facit Dominus meus venire; et coeperit percutere conservos suos, manducet autem et bibat cum ebriosis: veniet Dominus servi illius in die qua non sperat, et hora qua ignorat, et dividet eum, partemque ejus ponet cum hypocritis: illic erit fletus et stridor dentium (Matth. xxiv, 48\u201351). Hujus servi nequam et infidelis videtur esse vox illa taedii et desperationis, quae apud Isaiam legitur: quia videlicet moram faciente Domino, frequentibus nuntiis ejus increduli et illusores insultare videntur: Manda, remanda, manda, remanda; exspecta, reexspecta; modicum ibi, modicum ibi. Sed quae poena sequatur miseros, propheta non tacuit, cum post pauca subintulit: Et erit eis, inquit, verbum Domini, quod videlicet subsannant, dicentes prophetis: Manda, remanda; manda, remanda; exspecta, reexspecta; modicum ibi, modicum ibi: ut vadant et cadant retrorsum, et conterantur, et illaqueentur, et capiantur (Isai. xxviii, 13). Vadunt retrorsum per apostasiam, cadunt in criminalia, illaqueantur mortifera delectatione, vel quadam inextricabili peccandi necessitate, ut nec velint, nec possint poenitere: capiuntur improvisa morte, conteruntur aeterna damnatione. Nescit enim finem suum homo: sed sicut pisces capiuntur hamo, et aves laqueo; sic homines, inquit Salomon, capiuntur tempore malo, cum eis extemplo supervenerit (Eccle. ix, 12). Propterea videlicet, ne pro dilatione spei fides nostra tepescat, aut aestuet patientia, et incipiamus esse de illis qui ad tempus credunt, et in tempore tentationis recedunt (Luc. viii, 13): clamat nobis de coelo qui fidem donat, donatam probat, probatam coronat, inquiens: Quis crediderit, non festinet (Isai. xxviii, 16), scilicet videre quod credit. Si enim quod non videmus, speramus: per patientiam exspectamus. Sponsae quoque suae, quam sibi desponsavit in fide, mandat Deus per Osee, dicens: Dies multos exspectabis me: non fornicaberis, et non eris viro (Osee iii, 3). Hoc nempe est Dominum veraciter exspectare, si fidem ei servemus: ut licet careamus consolatione praesentiae ejus, adulterum tamen non sequamur; sed pendeamus ad reditum ejus. Sic namque Dominus ait in eodem propheta: Pendebit populus meus ad reditum meum (Id. xi, 7). Pulchre omnino et proprie, pendebit, tanquam inter coelum et terram: licet adhuc apprehendere coelestia non valeat, non velit tamen tangere terrena. Et si aliquando tangit, nonnisi summis articulis, id est, extremis partibus animae; propter necessitatem scilicet corruptionis nostrae, cui cogimur servire, quamdiu creatura vanitati subjecta est, non volens (Rom. viii, 12). Vulgo dicitur: Male exspectat, qui pendet. Ego autem dico: Feliciter exspectat, qui sic pendet. Quamobrem elegit suspendium anima mea, et in ipso suspendio mortem ossa mea: ut perseveranter in cruce ista pendere merear, donec immoriar."}, {"anchor": "g-p003", "label": "English", "text": "4. Therefore, if you are wise, consider how to use the respite of this delay: if you are a sinner, for repentance rather than negligence; if you are holy, to advance in holiness rather than fall away from faith. But if that wicked servant says in his heart, \u2018My Lord is slow in coming,\u2019 and begins to beat his fellow servants and eat and drink with drunkards, that servant's Lord will come on a day he does not expect and at an hour he does not know, and will cut him apart and assign him a place with the hypocrites: there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth (Matth. xxiv, 48\u201351). The voice of weariness and despair that we read in Isaiah seems to be that of this wicked and faithless servant. Because the Lord delays, unbelievers and scoffers seem to jeer at his frequent messengers: \u2018Command, command again, command, command again; wait, wait again; a little there, a little there.\u2019 But the prophet did not keep silent about the punishment that would follow these wretched people, for a little later he added: \u2018And the word of the Lord will be to them\u2019\u2014the word they mock when they say to the prophets, \u2018Command, command again; command, command again; wait, wait again; a little there, a little there\u2019\u2014\u2018so that they may go and fall backward, and be broken, and ensnared, and captured\u2019 (Isai. xxviii, 13). They go backward through apostasy, fall into grave sins, and are ensnared by deadly pleasure or by a kind of inescapable compulsion to sin, so that they neither wish nor are able to repent. They are captured by sudden death and broken by eternal condemnation. For man does not know his end: \u2018As fish are caught by a hook and birds by a snare, so men,\u2019 says Solomon, \u2018are caught in an evil time when it suddenly falls upon them\u2019 (Eccle. ix, 12). Therefore, lest the delay of hope make our faith grow lukewarm or our patience become agitated, and we begin to be among those \u2018who believe for a time and fall away in the time of temptation\u2019 (Luc. viii, 13), he who gives faith, tests the faith he has given, and crowns the faith he has tested, cries to us from heaven: \u2018Let the one who believes not hurry\u2019 (Isai. xxviii, 16)\u2014that is, to see what he believes. For if we hope for what we do not see, we await it patiently. God also commands his bride, whom he has betrothed to himself in faith, through Hosea: \u2018You will wait for me many days; you will not play the harlot or belong to a man\u2019 (Osee iii, 3). This is truly waiting for the Lord: keeping faith with him, so that although we lack the comfort of his presence we do not follow an adulterer, but hang upon his return. For the Lord says in the same prophet: \u2018My people will hang upon my return\u2019 (Id. xi, 7). How beautifully and aptly he says \u2018hang,\u2019 as though between heaven and earth: though they cannot yet grasp heavenly things, they are unwilling to touch earthly things. And if they sometimes touch them, it is only with their fingertips, that is, the outermost parts of the soul, because of the necessities of our corruptible condition, which we are compelled to serve as long as creation is subjected to vanity against its will (Rom. viii, 12). People commonly say, \u2018He who hangs waits badly.\u2019 But I say, \u2018He who hangs in this way waits happily.\u2019 Therefore my soul has chosen hanging, and my bones death in that hanging, so that I may be found worthy to hang perseveringly upon this cross until I die upon it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. O Domine Jesu, cum animam potestate positurus esses, et quo genere mortis id facere velles, tuo subesset arbitrio; suspendium elegit anima tua, ut exaltatus a terra, nos quoque ad te traheres, et a terrenis suspenderes. Sed nec ante mortem deponi passus es, et nos usque ad mortem in cruce perseveremus, et de ipsa tanquam de sublimi gradu facilior sit in coelum conscensus. Gratias tibi, Domine Jesu! ibi sumus, ibi te exspectamus, non Eliam, qui ad deponendum nos veniat; sed Eli, id est, Dominum nostrum, qui nos suscipiat. Modicum ibi, modicum ibi, si mandas atque remandas. Ego quidem mandatis tuis semel credidi; sed adjuva incredulitatem meam, ut immobilis ibi te exspectem, reexspectem, donec quod credo videam: credo enim videre bona Domini in terra viventium. Credis et tu: igitur exspecta Dominum, viriliter age, confortetur cor tuum, et sustine Dominum! (Psal. xxvi, 13, 14). Vae his qui perdiderunt sustinentiam, et diverterunt in vias pravas. Et quid facient, cum inspicere coeperit Dominus? Qui tamen longanimiter se mandat exspectari, alibi se cito venturum esse promittit: in altero nimirum sustinentiam informans; in altero pusillanimes confortans, imparatos terrens, et pigros excitans. Ecce, inquit, venio cito, et merces mea mecum est, reddere unicuique secundum opera sua (Apoc. xxii, 12): et ad Jerusalem: Cito veniet salus tua: quare moerore consumeris? (Mich. iv, 9). Et revera tempus breve est, maxime uniuscujusque nostrum; licet longum videatur aestuandi, seu prae labore, seu prae amore. Ideoque utrumque necesse est: ut timeamus Judicem qui prope est, et forsitan mihi, forsitan tibi in januis est; et longanimiter exspectemus, si moram fecerit. Veniens veniet ille Dominus, timor et desiderium nostrum, requies et merces laborantium, dulcedo et amplexus amantium, beatitudo omnium, salvator noster Jesus Christus, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum."}, {"anchor": "g-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. O Lord Jesus, when you were about to lay down your life by your own power, and the kind of death you would choose lay within your own decision, your soul chose hanging, so that, lifted up from the earth, you might draw us too to yourself and suspend us above earthly things. You did not allow yourself to be taken down before death: may we too persevere upon the cross until death, and may ascent into heaven be easier from it, as from a lofty step. Thank you, Lord Jesus! There we are; there we await you\u2014not Elijah, coming to take us down, but Eli, that is, our Lord, to receive us. \u2018A little there, a little there,\u2019 if you command and command again. I have indeed believed your commands once for all; but help my unbelief, so that I may remain there unmoved, waiting and waiting again for you, until I see what I believe: for I believe that I shall see the Lord's goodness in the land of the living. You believe too: then wait for the Lord; act courageously, let your heart be strengthened, and wait for the Lord! (Psal. xxvi, 13, 14). Woe to those who have lost their endurance and turned aside into crooked ways. What will they do when the Lord begins his inspection? Yet he who commands us to await him with patience promises elsewhere that he will come quickly: in the one case teaching endurance, in the other comforting the fainthearted, terrifying the unprepared, and rousing the sluggish. \u2018Look,\u2019 he says, \u2018I am coming quickly, and my reward is with me, to repay each according to his works\u2019 (Apoc. xxii, 12); and to Jerusalem: \u2018Your salvation will come quickly; why are you consumed by grief?\u2019 (Mich. iv, 9). Truly the time is short, especially for each one of us, although it may seem long in our anguish, whether from toil or from love. Therefore both are necessary: to fear the Judge who is near, perhaps at my door, perhaps at yours, and to wait patiently if he delays. He who is coming will come: the Lord, our fear and our desire, the rest and reward of those who labour, the sweetness and embrace of those who love, the blessedness of all, our Saviour Jesus Christ, who lives and reigns for ever and ever.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints Romans 8:12 beside words from Romans 8:20. The printed reference is retained."}, {"anchor": "g-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The hanging/death language is Guerric's sustained image of perseverance on Christ's cross, with a Scriptural allusion to Job 7:15. The proverbial contrast and bodily imagery are retained rather than paraphrased away."}, {"anchor": "g-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites Micah 4:9, although the whole sentence follows liturgical wording rather than that verse verbatim. Eli and Elias are Guerric\u2019s wordplay on the Passion narratives."}, {"anchor": "g-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition numbers both final paragraphs 4; that numbering is retained. In the first paragraph, the reading ne si (\u201clest\u201d) corrects the printed ne se, and a missing opening parenthesis before Psalm 64:3 has been supplied."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Advent Sermon I: Waiting for God's Promises", "topics": ["Advent", "hope", "patience", "repentance", "Second Coming", "Cross"], "url": "sermons/guerric-advent-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-advent", "calendar_label": "Advent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-advent-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De adventu Domini, sermo II, Quomodo Christo advenienti occurrendum, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 14-18.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Advent", "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "Proverbs 25:25", "Isaiah 52:7", "Ezekiel 39:8", "Isaiah 39:3", "Luke 1:34, 43", "Sirach 22:31", "Psalm 117:25\u201326 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 55:11", "Psalm 1:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 107:13 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 3:9 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 17:14", "Psalm 79:4, 8, 20 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 33:2", "Luke 10:23\u201324", "1 Thessalonians 4:16", "Psalm 49:23 (historical numbering)", "John 14:18", "Psalm 83:12 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 24:16", "Job 9:11", "Psalm 34:10 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 146:6 (historical numbering)", "James 4:6", "Isaiah 53:2\u20133", "Malachi 3:2", "2 Corinthians 3:18", "2 Timothy 1:12"], "occasion": "De adventu Domini", "occasion_detail": "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "occasion_label": "Advent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This second Advent sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Guerric urges his hearers to run out with joy to meet the coming Christ, as the prophets and the just longed to see him. He then describes a hidden, spiritual coming between the first and the last, in which the soul is transformed into Christ's image. Until that is granted, faith and a pure conscience console the patient. The source names Advent without assigning a particular Sunday."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quomodo Christo advenienti occurrendum"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "How to Go Out to Meet the Coming Christ", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Ecce venit Rex, occurramus obviam Salvatori nostro. Salomon pulchre ait: Aqua frigida animae sitienti, et nuntius bonus de terra longinqua (Prov. XXV, 25). Bonus itaque nuntius, qui adventum Salvatoris nuntiat, reconciliationem mundi, bona superventuri saeculi. Quam pulchri pedes annuntiantium pacem, annuntiantium bona! (Isa, LII, 7.) Multi siquidem, non unus. Multi inquam, sed in uno spiritu longa serie ab initio saeculi nobis supervenere nuntii, et omnium vox una, simul una sententia: Venit ecce venit (Ezech. XXXIX, 8). Et unde, inquis, isti venere nuntii? Ut scriptum est, de terra longinqua (Isa. XXXIX, 3); quia de terra viventium, quae magno interstitio separata est ab hac terra morientium: nam inter nos et illos adhuc chaos magnum firmatum est. Inde tamen prophetae nobis, sicut et angeli missi sunt; quia si corpore hic versabantur, spiritu illuc assumebantur, quando mittendi erant; ut illic audirent et viderent, quod hic nuntiarent. Hujusmodi nuntii aqua refrigerii, potusque sapientiae sunt salutaris animae Deum sitienti: cui nimirum qui nuntiat adventum vel alia mysteria Salvatoris, haurit ei, et propinat aquas in gaudio de fontibus Salvatoris, ut etiam nuntianti sibi, sive Isaiae, sive cuilibet ex prophetis, eadem anima respondere videatur verbis Elizabeth, quia eodem potata spiritu quo Elizabeth: Et unde mihi hoc, ut veniat Dominus meus ad me? (Luc. I, 34, 43.) Ecce enim, ut facta est vox annuntiationis tuae in auribus meis, exsultavit in gaudio spiritus in corde meo, gestiens occurrere Deo Salvatori suo."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Behold, the King is coming; let us go out to meet our Savior. Solomon says beautifully: \u201cCold water to a thirsty soul, and good news from a distant land\u201d (Prov. XXV, 25). Good, then, is the messenger who announces the Savior\u2019s coming, the reconciliation of the world, and the blessings of the age that is to come. \u201cHow beautiful are the feet of those who announce peace, who announce good things!\u201d (Isa, LII, 7.) There are many of them, not just one. Many, I say, but in one spirit, in a long succession from the beginning of the world, messengers have come to us, and the voice of them all is one, their judgment together one: \u201cBehold, he comes, yes, he comes\u201d (Ezech. XXXIX, 8). And where, you ask, did these messengers come from? As it is written, \u201cfrom a distant land\u201d (Isa. XXXIX, 3); for they came from the land of the living, which is separated by a great interval from this land of the dying\u2014for between us and them a great chasm is still fixed. Yet from there the prophets, like the angels, were sent to us; for although they were living here in body, in spirit they were taken there whenever they were to be sent, so that there they might hear and see what they were to announce here. Such messengers are water of refreshment and a drink of saving wisdom for the soul thirsting for God. Whoever announces to it the Savior\u2019s coming or any of his other mysteries draws and offers it water with joy from the fountains of the Savior, so that the same soul seems to answer the one announcing it\u2014whether Isaiah or any other of the prophets\u2014with Elizabeth\u2019s words, because it has been given to drink of the same Spirit as Elizabeth: \u201cAnd how has this happened to me, that my Lord should come to me?\u201d (Luc. I, 34, 43.) For behold, as the voice of your announcement sounded in my ears, the spirit in my heart leaped for joy, eager to go out to meet God its Savior.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Et revera, fratres, exsultatione spiritus Christo venienti occurrendum est. Et jam nunc a longe salutandus, vel certe resalutandus, qui mandat salutes Jacob. Amicum salutare non confundar (Eccli. XXII, 31), inquit Sapiens; quanto magis resalutare? O salutare vultus mei, et Deus meus! quanta dignatio fuit quod servos salutasti? sed quanto major, quod salvasti? Nec enim integra nobis salus esset, si salutes mandares, et non dares. Dedisti autem, non modo, quos salutaveras verbis pacificis, postmodum salutando in osculo pacis, videlicet per unionem carnis; sed etiam salutem operando per mortem crucis. Exsurgat igitur spiritus noster alacri gaudio, curratque obviam Salvatori suo, et jam a longe venientem adoret et salutet, acclamans ei et dicens: O Domine, salvum me fac; o Domine, bene prosperare! Benedictus qui venturus es in nomine Domini (Psal. CXVII, 25, 26). Salve, qui venis salvare nos; benedictus, qui venis benedicere nos. Igitur, o Domine, bene prosperare, qui tam prosper et salutaris advenis generi humano. Intende, prospere procede et regna. Prosperum iter faciet tibi Pater, Deus salutarium nostrorum. Prosperabitur, inquit Pater, in his ad quae misi illum (Isa. LV, 11): non pro votis carnalium, non pro voluntate Petri, qui abhorrebat illum pati. Et omnia quaecunque faciet prosperabuntur (Psal. I, 3): non ad praeproperam hominum voluntatem, sed ad veram eorum salutem. Vana quidem salus hominis (Psal. CVII, 13): sed Domini est salus (Psal. III, 9), qui operatus est salutem de sanguine suo, fundens illum in pretium, et propinans in potum. Veni ergo, o Domine, salvum me fac, et salvus ero (Jerem. XVII, 14). Veni et ostende faciem tuam, et salvi erimus (Psal. LXXIX. 4, 8, 20). Te enim exspectavimus: esto brachium nostrum, salus nostra in tempore tribulationis (Isai. XXXIII, 2). Sic Prophetae et justi desiderio et affectu tanto ante Christo venturo occurrebant, desiderantes, si fieri posset, oculis videre quod spiritu praevidebant. Unde Dominus discipulis dicebat: Beati oculi qui vident quae vos videtis. Dico enim vobis, quod multi prophetae, et reges voluerunt videre quae vos videtis, et non viderunt (Luc. X, 23, 24). Abraham quoque pater noster exsultavit, ut videret diem Christi: vidit, sed apud inferos, et gavisus est. In quo utique tepor et duritia cordis nostri suggillatur, si non videt eum gaudio spirituali."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. And truly, brothers, we must go out to meet the coming Christ with the rejoicing of the spirit. Even now, from afar, he must be greeted\u2014or certainly greeted in return\u2014he who sends greetings of salvation to Jacob. \u201cI will not be ashamed to greet a friend,\u201d says the Wise Man (Eccli. XXII, 31); how much more to greet him in return? O salvation of my face, and my God! How great was your condescension, that you greeted your servants; but how much greater was it that you saved them! For our salvation would not have been complete if you had sent greetings of salvation but had not given salvation. Yet you did give it\u2014not only by greeting those whom you had greeted with words of peace, later greeting them with the kiss of peace, that is, through the union of flesh, but also by accomplishing salvation through the death of the cross. Let our spirit, then, rise up in joyful gladness, and run out to meet its Savior; let it already adore and greet him as he comes from afar, acclaiming him and saying: \u201cO Lord, save me; O Lord, prosper!\u201d \u201cBlessed are you who are to come in the name of the Lord\u201d (Psal. CXVII, 25, 26). Hail, you who come to save us; blessed are you who come to bless us. Therefore, O Lord, prosper, since you come to the human race so prosperously and as its Savior. Attend, proceed prosperously, and reign. The Father, the God of our salvation, will make your journey prosperous. \u201cIt shall prosper,\u201d says the Father, \u201cin the things for which I sent him\u201d (Isa. LV, 11)\u2014not according to the desires of the carnal, nor according to the will of Peter, who shrank from his suffering. \u201cAnd all that he does shall prosper\u201d (Psal. I, 3)\u2014not according to the hasty will of human beings, but for their true salvation. \u201cVain indeed is the salvation of man\u201d (Psal. CVII, 13); \u201cbut salvation belongs to the Lord\u201d (Psal. III, 9), who accomplished salvation by his blood, pouring it out as the price and offering it as a drink. Come, then, O Lord, \u201csave me, and I shall be saved\u201d (Jerem. XVII, 14). \u201cCome and show your face, and we shall be saved\u201d (Psal. LXXIX. 4, 8, 20). For we have waited for you: \u201cbe our arm, our salvation in the time of tribulation\u201d (Isai. XXXIII, 2). In this way the prophets and the righteous, with such great desire and longing, went out so long beforehand to meet the Christ who was to come, wishing, if it were possible, to see with their eyes what they foresaw in spirit. Hence the Lord said to his disciples: \u201cBlessed are the eyes that see what you see. For I tell you that many prophets and kings wished to see what you see, and did not see it\u201d (Luc. X, 23, 24). Our father Abraham, too, rejoiced that he might see Christ\u2019s day; he saw it, but in the netherworld, and rejoiced. In this, surely, the lukewarmness and hardness of our heart are reproved, if it does not see him with spiritual joy.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Christi nascentis diem anniversarium exspectamus; qui nobis in proximo videndus, Domino annuente, promittitur. Hoc sane gaudium nostrum tale videtur exigere Scriptura, ut spiritus noster levans se super se, Christo venienti quodammodo occurrere gestiat: et desiderio se extendens in anteriora, impatiensque morarum, jam videre contendat futura. Ego namque non solum ad secundum adventum, sed etiam ad primum arbitror pertinere, quod tot locis Scripturarum ei monemur occurrere. Quomodo, inquis? Quia videlicet sicut secundo adventui occurremus motu et exsultatione corporis: sic et primo occurrendum est affectu et exsultatione cordis. Scitis enim quia resumptis in resurrectione corporibus novis, secundum doctrinam Apostoli, rapiemur in nubibus obviam Christo in aera, et sic semper cum Domino erimus (I Thess. IV, 16). Sic nec modo desunt nubes, quae spiritus nostros, si nimis non fuerint pigri terraeque affixi, sublevabunt ad altiora; et sic cum Domino erimus, vel hora dimidia. Agnoscit, ni fallor, experientia vestra quod loquor, cum aliquando vocem dederunt nubes, id est, sonuerunt in Ecclesia prophetarum, vel apostolorum voces, ad quam sublimia sensus vestri, quasi vehiculo nubis, fuerint subvecti; et eo usque nonnunquam excesserint, ut gloriam Domini quantulumcunque mererentur speculari. Tunc, ni fallor, vobis innotuit veritas illius sermonis, quem Dominus de illa nube pluit, quam quotidie ponit ascensum vobis: Sacrificium laudis honorificabit me: et illic iter, quo ostendam illi salutare Dei (Psal. XLIX, 23). Ita ergo sit, ut ante adventum suum Dominus veniat ad vos, et antequam mundo generaliter adveniat, familiariter invisat vos. Non vos, inquit, relinquam orphanos: vado, et veniam ad vos (Joan. XIV, 18). Et quidem pro merito cujusque, vel studio, creber est ad unumquemque iste Domini adventus, hoc tempore medio inter adventum primum et novissimum, conformans nos adventui primo, et praeparans novissimo. Ad hoc nempe venit modo in nos, ne primo adventu frustra venerit ad nos, vel ne in novissimo veniat iratus adversus nos. Hoc siquidem adventu satagit reformare sensum superbiae nostrae, configuratum sensui humilitatis suae, quam primo veniens exhibuit; ut perinde reformet corpus humilitatis nostrae, configuratum corpori claritatis suae, quam denuo rediens exhibebit. Prorsus optandus omnibus votis, et expetendus studiis adventus iste familiaris, qui nobis gratiam impertiat adventus primi, et gloriam promittat novissimi. Quia enim misericordiam et veritatem diligit Deus, gratiam et gloriam dabit Dominus (Psal. LXXXIII, 12); per misericordiam praerogans gratiam, per veritatem reddens gloriam."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. We await the anniversary of Christ\u2019s birth, which, with the Lord\u2019s favor, is promised to us as something we shall soon see. Scripture certainly seems to require that our joy be such that our spirit, lifting itself above itself, eagerly desire in some way to go out to meet the coming Christ; stretching forward in its longing and impatient of delay, it strives already to see what is still to come. For I believe that it applies not only to the second coming but also to the first, that in so many places of Scripture we are urged to go out to meet him. \u201cHow so?\u201d you ask. Because, just as we shall go out to meet his second coming through the movement and exultation of the body, so we must go out to meet his first through the affection and exultation of the heart. You know, in fact, that when our bodies have been taken up again in the resurrection, renewed, then, according to the Apostle\u2019s teaching, \u201cwe shall be caught up in the clouds to meet Christ in the air, and so we shall always be with the Lord\u201d (I Thess. IV, 16). Even now, then, clouds are not lacking\u2014clouds that will lift our spirits to higher things, if they are not too sluggish or fixed to the earth\u2014and so we shall be with the Lord, even if only for half an hour. Your own experience, unless I am mistaken, recognizes what I am saying: sometimes the clouds have given voice\u2014that is, the voices of prophets or apostles have sounded in the Church\u2014and your understanding has been carried up to sublime heights, as though in a cloud\u2019s chariot; sometimes it has risen so far that it merited, however slightly, to contemplate the glory of the Lord. Then, unless I am mistaken, you came to know the truth of that saying which the Lord rained down upon you from that cloud, which he sets before you every day as a means of ascent: \u201cThe sacrifice of praise will honor me, and there is the way by which I will show him the salvation of God\u201d (Psal. XLIX, 23). Let it be so, then: before his coming, may the Lord come to you, and before he comes to the world as a whole, may he visit you personally. \u201cI will not leave you orphans,\u201d he says; \u201cI am going away, and I will come to you\u201d (Joan. XIV, 18). And indeed, according to each person\u2019s merit or devotion, this coming of the Lord to each one is frequent during this time between his first and final coming: it conforms us to the first coming and prepares us for the final one. For this is why he comes into us now: so that he may not have come to us in vain in his first coming, and so that at the last he may not come against us in anger. By this coming he earnestly strives to reform our proud mind, conforming it to the mind of his humility, which he displayed when he first came; and thereby to reform our body of humility, conforming it to the body of his glory, which he will display when he returns. This personal coming is wholly to be desired with every prayer and sought with every effort: it grants us the grace of his first coming and promises us the glory of the last. For because God loves mercy and truth, \u201cthe Lord will give grace and glory\u201d (Psal. LXXXIII, 12), granting grace beforehand through mercy and repaying glory through truth.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Porro sicut dispositione temporis, sic etiam proportione similitudinis medius est adventus iste spiritualis inter adventum utrumque corporalem, et velut quidam mediator, participat cum utroque. Primus siquidem adventus, occultus et humilis; ultimus, manifestus et admirabilis: iste autem occultus quidem, sed mirabilis. Occultum sane dixerim, non quod ignoretur ab eo cui venit, sed quia secretus ei advenit. Unde glorians illa gloriosa anima secum cum dixit: Secretum meum mihi, secretum meum mihi (Isa. XXIV, 16). Sed nec ab ipso cui advenit, potest ante videri, quam teneri, juxta illud beati Job de se confitentis: Si venerit ad me, non videbo eum; si abierit, non intelligam (Job IX, 11). Nec veniens quippe videtur, nec recedens intelligitur, qui solummodo, dum praesens est, lumen est animae et intellectus, quo invisibilis videtur, et incogitabilis intelligitur. Caeterum quam admirabilis sit, licet occultus sit iste adventus Domini, quam suavi et felici stupore in admirationem sui suspendat et expendat animam contemplantis, quomodo omnia ossa interioris hominis acclament ei, Domine, quis similis tui (Psal. XXXIV, 10)? norunt quidem experti; sed utinam satis desiderent experiri non experti: ita duntaxat, ut non temeraria curiositas faciat scrutari majestatem opprimendos a gloria, sed pia charitas suspirare dilectum suscipiendos a gratia. Suscipiens enim mansuetos Dominus, humilians autem peccatores usque ad terram (Psal. CXLVI, 6), superbis resistit, humilibus autem dat gratiam (Jac. IV, 6). Cum igitur primus adventus sit gratiae; novissimus, gloriae: iste nimirum, gratiae pariter et gloriae; in quo videlicet per consolantem gratiam futuram nobis utcunque datur praelibare gloriam. Cum igitur in primo Deus majestatis visus sit contemptibilis, in novissimo autem videndus sit terribilis, in isto medio videtur mirabilis et amabilis: ut nec propter dignationem gratiae, qua se praebet amabilem, possit esse contemptui, sed admirationi; nec propter magnificentiam gloriae, qua mirabilis apparet, sit terrori, sed potius consolationi. De primo dicebat Videns: Vidimus eum, et non erat ei species, neque decor: unde nec reputavimus eum (Isa. LIII, 2, 3). De ultimo expavescit etiam justus, dicens: Et quis stabit ad videndum eum? (Malach. III, 2.) De isto autem dicit Apostolus: Gloriam Domini speculantes, in eamdem imaginem transformamur a gloria in gloriam, tanquam a Domini Spiritu (II Cor. III, 18). Mirabile prorsus, et amabile, cum Deus amor amantis sensibus illabitur: cum sponsam Sponsus in unitate Spiritus amplectitur, et in eamdem cum illa imaginem transformatur; per quam, velut per speculum, Domini gloriam speculatur. Quam beati, quorum ardens charitas hanc praerogativam jam meruit obtinere! sed et illi beati, quorum sancta simplicitas idem quandoque potest sperare! Et illi quidem jam de fructu amoris, sui capiunt solatium laboris: isti autem tanto fortasse majore merito, quanto interim minore solatio, portantes pondus diei et aestus, exspectant adventum remunerationis. Nos igitur, fratres, quos nondum consolatur tam sublimis experientia, ut patientes simus usque ad adventum Domini, consoletur interim certa fides et pura conscientia, quae tam feliciter quam fideliter cum Paulo dicat: Scio cui credidi, et certus sum quia potens est depositum meum servare in illum diem (II Tim. I, 12): in adventum scilicet gloriae magni Dei, et Salvatoris nostri Jesu Christi, cui est gloria in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Moreover, just as it is intermediate in the order of time, so also in the likeness it bears, this spiritual advent stands between the two bodily advents and, as a kind of mediator, shares in both. The first advent was hidden and humble; the last will be manifest and admirable; this one, however, is hidden yet wondrous. I call it hidden\u2014not because it is unknown to the one to whom he comes, but because he comes to that person in secret. Hence that glorious soul, glorying within itself, said: \u201cMy secret is for me, my secret is for me\u201d (Isa. XXIV, 16). Yet even by the one to whom he comes, he cannot be seen before he is possessed, in accordance with the blessed Job\u2019s confession about himself: \u201cIf he comes to me, I shall not see him; if he goes away, I shall not understand\u201d (Job IX, 11). For he is neither seen as he comes nor understood as he departs; only while he is present is he the light of the soul and of the intellect, by which the invisible is seen and the unthinkable is understood. But how wondrous this advent of the Lord is, even though hidden; how, with what sweet and joyful astonishment, it suspends and absorbs the soul that contemplates him in wonder at himself, so that all the bones of the inner person cry out to him, \u201cLord, who is like you?\u201d (Psal. XXXIV, 10)\u2014those who have experienced it know; but would that those who have not experienced it desired enough to experience it. Let it be only in such a way that reckless curiosity does not lead one to scrutinize the majesty and be overwhelmed by the glory, but devout love makes one sigh for the Beloved and be received by grace. For the Lord receives the meek, but humbles sinners to the ground (Psal. CXLVI, 6); he resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble (Jac. IV, 6). Since, then, the first advent is one of grace and the last one of glory, this advent is plainly of both grace and glory: in it, through the grace that consoles us, we are in some measure given a foretaste of the glory that is to come. Since, then, in the first advent the God of majesty appeared contemptible, while in the last he will be seen as terrifying, in this middle advent he is seen as wondrous and lovable: so that, because of the condescension of grace by which he makes himself lovable, he cannot be despised but rather admired; and because of the magnificence of the glory in which he appears wondrous, he is not a cause of terror but rather of consolation. Of the first advent the Seer said: \u201cWe saw him, and there was in him neither appearance nor beauty; therefore we did not regard him\u201d (Isa. LIII, 2, 3). Of the last even the just man is afraid, saying: \u201cAnd who will stand to see him?\u201d (Malach. III, 2.) But of this advent the Apostle says: \u201cBeholding the glory of the Lord, we are transformed into the same image, from glory to glory, as by the Spirit of the Lord\u201d (II Cor. III, 18). Truly wondrous and lovable it is when God, who is love, glides into the senses of the one who loves; when the Bridegroom embraces the bride in the unity of the Spirit and is transformed with her into the same image, through which, as through a mirror, the glory of the Lord is beheld. How blessed are those whose ardent love has already merited this privilege! Yet blessed also are those whose holy simplicity may one day hope for the same. The former already draw comfort from the fruit of love for their labor; the latter, perhaps with all the greater merit because meanwhile they have less consolation, bear the burden of the day and the heat while awaiting the advent of their reward. Therefore, brothers, we whom so sublime an experience does not yet console: that we may be patient until the coming of the Lord, may certain faith and a pure conscience console us meanwhile, saying with Paul, as happily as faithfully: \u201cI know whom I have believed, and I am certain that he is able to guard my deposit until that day\u201d (II Tim. I, 12)\u2014that is, until the advent of the glory of the great God and our Savior Jesus Christ, to whom be glory forever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Advent Sermon II: How to Go Out to Meet the Coming Christ", "topics": ["Advent", "joy", "prophets", "messengers of good news", "Christ the King"], "url": "sermons/guerric-advent-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-advent", "calendar_label": "Advent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-advent-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De adventu Domini, sermo III, Quod homo debeat semper esse paratus Domino advenienti, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 18-22.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Advent", "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "Amos 4:12", "Luke 12:40", "Acts 1:7", "Psalm 17:24 (historical numbering)", "Wisdom 5:7 (printed locator; the words are Wisdom 4:7)", "Psalm 56:4\u20135 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 4:9 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 18:19", "Psalm 56:8\u20139 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 118:6 (historical numbering)", "2 Kings 15\u201318 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Samuel)", "Psalm 58:5\u20136 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 138:24 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 8:20", "Sirach 15:1\u20132", "Wisdom 6:17", "Song of Songs 5:2", "Psalm 118:131 (historical numbering)", "Luke 24:28\u201329", "Genesis 19:2\u20133", "Ecclesiastes 7:24", "Song of Songs 5:5\u20136", "Matthew 15:28", "Isaiah 58:2", "Psalm 88:15 (historical numbering)", "Luke 2:14", "Proverbs 18:17", "Psalm 31:5 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 13:19", "Psalm 64:8 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 66:5 (printed locator; see note)", "Psalm 100:1\u20132 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 79:3 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "De adventu Domini", "occasion_detail": "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "occasion_label": "Advent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This third Advent sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Guerric begins from the uncertainty of the hour of Christ's coming and of one's own death: the only security is never to be secure. David is the model of a soul kept ready through persecution, and justice and judgment prepare a dwelling for the Lord. The source names Advent without assigning a particular Sunday."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quod homo debeat semper esse paratus Domino advenienti."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "That a man must always be ready for the coming Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Paratus est Israel in occursum Domini, quoniam venit (Amos IV, 12). Et vos, fratres, estote parati; quia, qua hora non putatis, Filius hominis veniet (Luc. XII, 40). Nihil certius quam quod veniat, sed nihil incertius quam quando veniat. Usque adeo siquidem non est nostrum scire tempora, vel momenta, quae Pater posuit in sua potestate (Act. I, 7), ut nec angelis qui assistunt ei, de die illa vel hora scire datum sit. Dies quoque noster ultimus, certissimus nobis quod veniat: incertissimus est quando, vel ubi, vel unde nobis adveniat; nisi quod, ut ante nos dictum est, senibus est in januis, qui juvenibus est in insidiis. Et utinam vel illi se observarent, qui mortem tam paratam intrare, imo jam intrantem vident. An non ex parte jam intravit, quae aliquas partes corporis jam permortuas occupavit? In pluribus tamen semimortuis videre est adhuc viventem concupiscentiam mundi: frigescunt membra, et fervet avaritia: vita finitur, et ambitio protrahitur. Quandoquidem nobis quoque quibus fortassis aetas, aut salus aliquid longius promittere videtur, quanto minus mors in conspectu est, tanto magis utique, si sapimus, suspecta est: ne videlicet dies illa, sicut fur in nocte, incautos et imparatos nos comprehendat; quae in insidiis posita, quominus videri aut caveri potest, eo magis timenda est. Una igitur securitas est, nunquam esse securum: quatenus timor se observans reddat semper paratum; donec securitas succedat timori, non timor securitati. Observabo me, inquit sapiens, ab iniquitate mea (Psal. XVII, 24), quandoquidem non possum a morte mea; sciens quia justus, si morte praeoccupatus fuerit, in refrigerio erit (Sap. V, 7), imo in morte triumphat, cui non dominatur iniquitas in vita. Quam pulchrum, fratres, quam beatum, in morte non modo securum, sed et gloriosum triumphare; ex auctoritate conscientiae, spiritu et voce Martini cruentam bestiam, si astare praesumpserit, increpare; Judici venienti et pulsanti gratanter aperire! Tum vero videas, heu, similes mei trepidare, inducias petere, nec accipere: oleum conscientiae lamentis poenitentiae velle emere, nec tempus sufficere: larvales illas facies declinare velle, nec posse: a facie irae tonantis velle in corpore delitescere, et cogi exire! Exibit spiritus ejus, et revertetur peccator in terram suam, unde sumptus est. In illa die peribunt omnes cogitationes eorum. Scio quidem, quia conditionis humanae est, sub discrimine exitus conturbari, cum etiam perfecti nolunt exspoliari, sed supervestiri: et qui nihil sibi sunt conscii, quia tamen non in hoc sunt justificati, necesse habent judicium, quod ignorant, vereri. Sed, sive conditionis affectu, sive sanctitatis defectu, sive judicii metu conturbata fuerit anima mea, inquit justus: Tu, Domine, misericordiae memor eris, mittesque misericordiam tuam et veritatem tuam, et eripies animam meam de medio catulorum leonum (Psal. LVI, 4, 5): et qui prius conturbatus eram, postmodum in pace in idipsum dormiam et requiescam (Psal. IV, 9). Vocavit Deus terram, et audivit eum in tremore. Cum enim auditum fecit de coelo judicium, auditu solo terra tremuit, et terrore purgata quievit. Beati qui nunc eatenus excoquunt in se omnem scoriam peccati, ut tunc solo sufficiat timore purgari. Mecum siquidem servo inutili et negligenti bene agitur, si vel per ignem salvus fiam; si, crematis lignis, feno, stipula, quae mihi coarcervavi, vel semiustus evadam. Et quidem comparatione mali, bonum vel per ignem salvari; sed indubitanter longe melius, sola purgatione metus consummari: optimum autem et felicissimum, nec metu turbari."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Israel is prepared to meet the Lord, for he comes\" (Amos IV, 12). \"And you, brothers, be ready; for the Son of Man will come at an hour you do not expect\" (Luc. XII, 40). Nothing is more certain than that he will come, but nothing is more uncertain than when he will come. Indeed, it is so far beyond our power to know the times or seasons, which the Father has placed in his own power (Act. I, 7), that it has not even been granted to the angels who stand by him to know of that day or hour. Our last day too is most certain to us in that it will come, but it is most uncertain when, or where, or from what quarter it will come upon us \u2014 except that, as has been said before our time, it stands at the door for the old, while it lies in ambush for the young. And would that even those would watch themselves who see death so ready to enter \u2014 indeed already entering! For has it not in part already entered, seeing that it has already taken possession of some parts of the body already wholly dead? Yet in many who are half-dead one may still see the desire of the world alive: the limbs grow cold, yet avarice burns hot; life comes to its end, yet ambition is drawn out still further. Since even for us, to whom perhaps our age or our health seems to promise a somewhat longer life, the less death is in view, the more, certainly, if we are wise, it ought to be suspected: lest that day, like a thief in the night, seize us unwary and unprepared; for being set in ambush, the less it can be seen or guarded against, the more it is to be feared. There is therefore but one security: never to be secure \u2014 so that fear, keeping watch over itself, may render us always ready, until security succeed to fear, and not fear to security. \"I will keep myself,\" says the wise man, \"from my iniquity\" (Psal. XVII, 24), since I cannot keep myself from my death; knowing that the just man, if he be overtaken by death, shall be in refreshment (Sap. V, 7) \u2014 indeed he triumphs in death, over whom iniquity has not had dominion in life. How beautiful, brothers, how blessed, to triumph in death not merely secure but even glorious; with the authority of conscience, in the spirit and voice of Martin, to rebuke the bloody beast should it presume to stand near; to open gladly to the Judge as he comes and knocks! Then indeed you would see, alas, those like me trembling, asking for a delay and not obtaining it; wishing to buy the oil of conscience with the laments of penitence, and time not sufficing; wishing to turn away from those ghastly faces, and not being able; wishing to hide in the body from the face of the thundering wrath, and being forced to go forth! \"His spirit shall go forth, and the sinner shall return to his own earth, from which he was taken. In that day all their thoughts shall perish.\" I know indeed that it belongs to the human condition to be troubled at the crisis of one's departure, since even the perfect do not wish to be stripped, but rather to be clothed over; and those who are conscious of nothing against themselves \u2014 because they are not thereby justified \u2014 must still fear the judgment which they do not know. But whether my soul be troubled by the feeling natural to this condition, or by a lack of holiness, or by fear of judgment, says the just man: \"You, Lord, will be mindful of your mercy, and you will send your mercy and your truth, and you will deliver my soul from the midst of the young lions\" (Psal. LVI, 4, 5); and I, who was troubled before, will afterward sleep and rest in peace, in the very same (Psal. IV, 9). \"God called the earth, and it heard him in trembling.\" For when he made judgment heard from heaven, the earth trembled at the mere hearing, and, purged by terror, grew quiet. Blessed are those who now so thoroughly burn away in themselves all the dross of sin, that then it may suffice for them to be purged by fear alone. For with me, a useless and negligent servant, it goes well if I am saved even by fire; if, when the wood, hay, and stubble which I have heaped up for myself are burned away, I escape even half-burned. And indeed, by comparison with evil, it is good to be saved even through fire; but without doubt it is far better to be made perfect by the purification of fear alone; but best and most blessed of all is not even to be troubled by fear.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Idcirco paratus esto, o verus Israel, in occursum Domini: non solum, ut cum venerit et pulsaverit, aperias ei; sed etiam adhuc illo longe agente, alacer et gratulabundus occurras ei; et tanquam fiduciam habens in die judicii, regnum ejus advenire pleno preceris affectu. Si ergo vis tunc paratus inveniri: Ante judicium para justitiam tibi (Eccli. XVIII, 19), juxta consilium Sapientis; paratus sis ad omne opus bonum faciendum; paratus sis nihilominus ad omne malum patiendum: ut sine reprehensione cordis cantet os tuum, Paratum cor meum, Deus, paratum cor meum. Paratum quidem, ut te cooperante faciam digna; paratum, ut patiar indigna. Et ideo paratum ad utrumque, quod cantabo et psallam in gloria mea: id est, laudabo et gloriabor pro utroque. Et statim in hoc ipsum se justus excitat, Exsurge, inquiens, psalterium et cithara (Psal. LVI, 8, 9); id est, cor meum et caro mea, ad exsultandum in Deum vivum: cor scilicet, propter speciales actiones; caro, propter suas passiones. Cor namque sapiens quae sursum sunt, psalterium est sonans de superiori: caro patiens quae deorsum sunt, cithara sonans ab inferiori. Sic et alibi devotionem suam David offerens Deo, Paratus sum, inquit, ut custodiam mandata tua: et tam paratus, quod non sum turbatus (Psal. CXVIII, 6), cum tentatio irrueret, et persecutio grassaretur. Cum persequeretur aemulus, malediceret servus, animam quaereret filius (II Reg. XV-XVIII); non sum tamen turbatus, quominus custodirem mandata perfectionis evangelicae: retribuentibus mala pro bonis, retribuens bona pro malis; sollicitus de persecutorum salute, moestus de morte; sustinens a servo exprobrari, non sustinens ab amico vindicari. Ecce ante Evangelium evangelica perfectio, charitas scilicet patiens et benigna, etiam erga malignos, quos tolerat. Quia igitur tam paratus erat, fiducialiter Domino nimirum occurrebat: Sine iniquitate, inquit, cucurri, et direxi distortos, quantum in me fuit. Tu exsurge igitur in occursum meum, occurrentis tibi: et quia nec sic pertingere possum ad celsitudinem tuam, nisi tu condescendens operi manuum tuarum porrigas dexteram: Exsurge in occursum meum (Psal. LVIII, 5, 6), et vide si via iniquitatis in me est: et si inveneris nescio quam viam iniquitatis, amove a me: et de lege tua misertus mei, deduc me in via aeterna (Psal. CXXXVIII, 24), id est, Christo, qui via est per quam itur, et aeternitas ad quam pervenitur; via immaculata, mansio beata."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Therefore be ready, O true Israel, to meet the Lord: not only that, when he comes and knocks, you may open to him; but also, while he is still far away, that you may run to meet him eagerly and with gladness; and, as one having confidence for the day of judgment, pray with full affection that his kingdom may come. If, then, you wish to be found ready at that time: Before judgment prepare justice for yourself (Eccli. XVIII, 19), according to the counsel of the Wise Man; be ready to do every good work; be ready likewise to suffer every evil: so that your mouth may sing, without reproach from your heart, My heart is ready, O God, my heart is ready. Ready, indeed, that with you cooperating I may do worthy deeds; ready, that I may suffer unworthy things. And therefore ready for both, so that I shall sing and make melody in my glory: that is, I shall praise and glory for both. And immediately for this very purpose the just man rouses himself, saying, Awake, psaltery and harp (Psal. LVI, 8, 9); that is, my heart and my flesh, to exult in the living God: the heart, namely, for its special actions; the flesh, for its sufferings. For the heart, wise toward the things that are above, is a psaltery sounding from above; the flesh, suffering the things that are below, is a harp sounding from below. So also elsewhere David, offering his devotion to God, says: I am ready to keep your commandments; and so ready that I was not troubled (Psal. CXVIII, 6), when temptation rushed in and persecution raged. When a rival persecuted him, a servant cursed him, a son sought his life (II Reg. XV-XVIII); yet I was not troubled, so as to fail to keep the commandments of evangelical perfection: to those repaying evil for good, repaying good for evil; anxious for the salvation of his persecutors, sorrowful over the death; enduring reproach from a servant, not enduring vengeance taken by a friend. Behold, before the Gospel, evangelical perfection\u2014namely, charity that is patient and kind, even toward the malicious, whom it bears with. Because, then, he was so ready, he indeed ran to meet the Lord with confidence: Without iniquity, he says, I have run, and I have set straight the crooked, as far as it was in me. Arise, therefore, to meet me as I run to meet you: and because even so I cannot attain to your height, unless you, condescending to the work of your hands, stretch forth your right hand: Arise to meet me (Psal. LVIII, 5, 6), and see whether there is a way of iniquity in me: and if you find some way of iniquity I know not of, remove it from me: and, having mercy on me according to your law, lead me in the way everlasting (Psal. CXXXVIII, 24), that is, in Christ, who is the way by which one travels, and the eternity to which one arrives; the way without stain, the blessed dwelling.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Puto tamen quia et ante mansionem illam beatam, si paraveris Domino viam immaculatam, ipse dignabitur saepius in via ponere gressus suos, dilatabitque subtus te gressus tuos, ut dilatato corde curras viam mandatorum; cujus angustum forte causabaris initium. Sapientia namque, ut ipsa perhibet, in viis justitiae ambulat (Prov. VIII, 20): et: Qui continens est justitiae apprehendet illam, et obviabit illi quasi mater honorificata. Dignos se ipsa circuit quaerens, et in viis suis ostendit se illis (Eccli. XV, 1, 2, et Sap. VI, 17). Si minus aut nunquam tibi eam occurrere conquereris, vide ne forte viam tuam corruperis. Sic enim scriptum habes: Insipientia viri corrumpit viam suam; Deus autem causabitur in corde suo. Ipse siquidem stat ad ostium et pulsat: ut si quis aperuerit ei, coenet cum eo coelestis mensae delicias. Loquitur sponsa: Vox dilecti mei pulsantis; Aperi mihi, soror mea sponsa (Cantic. V, 2). Aperi mihi cor tuum, et cibabo illud. Aperi mihi os tuum, et implebo illud. Os meum, inquit David, aperui, et attraxi spiritum (Psal. CXVIII, 131). Spiritus enim ante faciem nostram Christus Dominus, non modo invitandus, sed et attrahendus quadam violentia precis, vehementiaque fervoris in hospitium cordis: sicut de duobus discipulis in typum hujus rei historia refert Evangelii (Luc. XXIV, 28, 29). Et ipse siquidem non ob aliud, nisi ut probet devotionem charitatis tuae, aliquando se fingit longius ire: sicut et duo angeli, Loth adorante et obsecrante ut intrarent, dissimulabant tamen dicentes, Minime, sed manebimus in platea. Sed quid dicit Scriptura? Compulit illos oppido, ut diverterent ad eum (Gen. XIX, 2, 3). Pia violentia, qua regnum coeleste rapitur: laudabilis importunitas, quae Christum hospitem, aut angelos promeretur. Sed quid est, inquis, quod Jesus fingit se longius ire? Quid, nisi illud quod Ecclesiastes confitetur de se? Dixi, sapiens efficiar; et ipsa longius recessit a me (Eccle. VII, 24). Hoc autem sponsa manifestius exponit, nostram utique quotidianam exprimens querimoniam, ubi ait: Surrexi aperire dilecto meo: at ille declinaverat, atque transierat. Quaesivi illum, et non inveni (Cantic. V, 5, 6): vocavi, et non respondit mihi, sicut nec mulieri Chananeae. Spiritum sapientiae invocabas, spiritum gratiae quaerebas in oratione: si tibi videtur longius recedere, ne desperes, sed importunus insiste; donec respondeatur tibi, Magna est fides tua, fiat tibi sicut petisti (Matth. XV, 28)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. I think, however, that even before that blessed dwelling, if you prepare for the Lord an unstained way, he himself will deign often to set his steps upon the way, and will enlarge your steps beneath you, so that with enlarged heart you may run the way of the commandments, whose beginning you perhaps complained was narrow. For Wisdom, as she herself attests, walks in the ways of justice (Prov. VIII, 20): and: He who holds fast to justice shall lay hold of her, and she shall meet him like a mother held in honor. She goes about seeking out those worthy of herself, and shows herself to them in their ways (Eccli. XV, 1, 2, et Sap. VI, 17). If you complain that she meets you little or never, see whether perhaps you have corrupted your way. For thus it is written: The folly of a man corrupts his way, but he will accuse God in his own heart. For he himself stands at the door and knocks, so that if anyone opens to him, he may dine with him on the delights of the heavenly table. The bride speaks: The voice of my beloved knocking; Open to me, my sister, my spouse (Cantic. V, 2). Open to me your heart, and I will feed it. Open to me your mouth, and I will fill it. I opened my mouth, says David, and drew in the breath (Psal. CXVIII, 131). For the breath before our face is Christ the Lord, who is not only to be invited but also to be drawn in, by a certain violence of prayer and by the vehemence of fervor, into the lodging of the heart: just as the Gospel story relates of the two disciples, as a figure of this matter (Luc. XXIV, 28, 29). And he himself, indeed, for no other reason than to test the devotion of your charity, sometimes pretends to go farther: just as the two angels also, when Lot worshipped them and begged that they would come in, nevertheless feigned, saying, No, but we will stay in the street. But what does Scripture say? He pressed them greatly, so that they turned aside to him (Gen. XIX, 2, 3). A pious violence, by which the kingdom of heaven is seized; a praiseworthy importunity, which wins Christ as guest, or the angels. But what is it, you ask, that Jesus pretends to go farther? What, but that which Ecclesiastes confesses of himself? I said, I shall become wise; and she herself withdrew farther from me (Eccle. VII, 24). But the bride explains this more plainly, expressing indeed our daily complaint, where she says: I rose up to open to my beloved; but he had turned aside and passed on. I sought him, and I did not find him (Cantic. V, 5, 6): I called him, and he did not answer me, just as he did not answer the Canaanite woman either. You were calling upon the spirit of wisdom, you were seeking the spirit of grace in prayer: if he seems to you to withdraw farther off, do not despair, but press on importunately, until it is answered to you, Great is your faith; be it done to you as you have asked (Matth. XV, 28).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Cum tamen invitas Jesum, vide ne Deum majestatis ad sordidum et indignum invites hospitium; ubi nec te ipsum patiatur habitare quietum uxor litigiosa, seu fumus, aut stillicidium. Non enim nisi in pace fit locus ejus: nec aliud quam justitia et judicium praeparatio sedis ejus. Nunc autem me, inquit, de die in diem quaerunt, et scire vias meas volunt, quia gens quae justitiam fecerit, et judicium Dei sui non dereliquerit (Isa. LVIII, 2). Justitia, inquit, et judicium praeparatio sedis ejus (Psal. LXXXVIII, 15). Noli causari quasi sumptuosum [fort. praesumptuosum] sit, viresque tuae excedat paupertatis, tam magnifico, tamque potenti praeparare domum hospiti: habes ad manum unde id possis. Humanum dico propter infirmitatem carnis tuae, vel magis angustiam mentis tuae. Confitere perfecte de praeterito: bonam voluntatem habe de caetero; pax enim hominibus bonae voluntatis (Luc. II, 14), et hoc judicio et justitia sedem Altissimo praeparasti. Vis manifestius audire de confessione, quod eam oporteat fieri in praeparatione adventus Domini? Justus, ait Scriptura, in initio sermonis accusator est sui. Et quid sequitur? Venit amicus ejus (Prov. XVIII, 17), qui modo ante confessionem inimicus assistebat longius. Cum enim dixit, Confitebor adversum me injustitiam meam Domino (Psal. XXXI, 5); et ipse remisit: Venit, inquam, et investigabit eum. Prorsus investigabit, tanquam potio valida, scrutans corda et renes; pertingens usque ad divisionem animae et spiritus, exhauriens noxia de medullis animae, mentisque visceribus, purgans affectus ut fructum plus afferat: de cujus primitivo fructu confessionis jam gaudet pater agricola. Ipse tamen qui post confessionem nonnunquam et antequam invocetur, adest, nonnunquam exspectat ut tu prior eum invites: et ut meritum tibi augeat, saepe diutius dissimulat; ut tu attentius psallendo, instantius orando, dulciter violentus intrare compellas: alioquin planget propheta quod civitates austri clausae sint, nullo introeunte (Jerem. XIII, 19). Cum ergo dicere potueris, Paratum cor meum, Deus, quia vacuatum a malis; paratum cor meum, quia sanctis plenum desideriis: tunc sedulus fac quod sequitur, Cantabo et psalmum dicam (Psal. LXIV, 8). Et quicunque sit sonus cantantis vel psallentis, haec sit intentio mentis: Exsurge, gloria mea, exsurge in occursum mei; quia, quantum potui, occurri tibi. O benigne Jesu, quam alacer et promptus, quam laetus et festivus occurris hujusmodi devotioni! quam hilarem te ostendis in viis istis! Occurristi, inquit Isaias, laetanti et facienti justitiam: in viis tuis recordabuntur tui (Isa. LXVI, 5). Si enim psallas sapienter, in via immaculata veniet; veniet qui et illuminabit abscondita tua, ut quae nescis intelligas mysteria Scripturarum, eritque ut dicas: Psallam et intelligam in via immaculata, quando veniet ad me (Psal. C, 1, 2). Excita, Domine, potentiam tuam, quae excitet pigritiam nostram in occursum tui: et veni, ut salvos nos facias (Psal. LXXIX, 3), Salvator mundi; qui vivis et regnas per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Yet when you invite Jesus, see that you do not invite the God of majesty into a sordid and unworthy lodging\u2014one in which not even you yourself could be permitted to dwell in peace, on account of a quarrelsome wife, or smoke, or a leaking roof. For his place is made only in peace, and the preparation of his throne is nothing other than justice and judgment. 'But now,' he says, 'they seek me day after day, and desire to know my ways, because they are a nation that has done righteousness and has not forsaken the judgment of its God' (Isa. LVIII, 2). 'Justice and judgment,' he says, 'are the preparation of his throne' (Psal. LXXXVIII, 15). Do not object, as though it were a costly thing [perhaps read: presumptuous], and beyond the strength of your poverty, to prepare a house for so magnificent and so powerful a guest: you have at hand the means by which you can do this. I speak in human terms, on account of the weakness of your flesh, or rather the narrowness of your mind. Make full confession concerning the past; have good will concerning what remains; for 'peace is to men of good will' (Luc. II, 14), and by this judgment and justice you will have prepared a seat for the Most High. Do you wish to hear more plainly about confession, that it must be made in preparing for the Lord's coming? 'The just man,' Scripture says, 'at the beginning of his speech is his own accuser. And what follows? His friend comes' (Prov. XVIII, 17)\u2014he who, just before, before the confession, stood far off as an enemy. For when he said, 'I will confess my injustice against myself to the Lord' (Psal. XXXI, 5), he himself forgave it; 'he comes,' I say, 'and will search him out.' He will indeed search him out thoroughly, like a strong medicine, examining hearts and loins, reaching even to the division of soul and spirit, draining out the harmful matter from the marrow of the soul and the inward parts of the mind, purging the affections so that it may bear more fruit; and in the first-fruit of this confession the Father, the husbandman, already rejoices. Yet he himself, who after confession is sometimes present even before he is invoked, at other times waits for you to invite him first; and in order to increase your merit, he often pretends for a longer time not to notice, so that you, by singing more attentively, by praying more insistently, may compel him, sweetly violent, to enter\u2014otherwise the prophet will lament that the cities of the south are shut up, with no one entering (Jerem. XIII, 19). Therefore, when you are able to say, 'My heart is ready, O God,' because it is emptied of evils; 'my heart is ready,' because it is filled with holy desires\u2014then diligently do what follows: 'I will sing and recite a psalm' (Psal. LXIV, 8). And whatever be the sound of the one singing or playing, let this be the intention of the mind: 'Arise, my glory, arise to meet me; for, as much as I was able, I have come to meet you.' O kind Jesus, how eager and ready, how joyful and festive you come to meet such devotion! How cheerful you show yourself along these paths! 'You met,' says Isaiah, 'the one who rejoices and does justice: in your ways they will remember you' (Isa. LXVI, 5). For if you sing wisely, he will come in the undefiled way; he will come who will also illumine your hidden things, so that you may understand the mysteries of the Scriptures which you did not know, and it will come to be that you say: 'I will sing and understand in the undefiled way, when he comes to me' (Psal. C, 1, 2). Stir up, O Lord, your power, that it may stir up our sluggishness to meet you; and come, that you may save us (Psal. LXXIX, 3), O Savior of the world, who live and reign through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints quia gens quae justitiam fecerit; the Vulgate of Isaiah 58:2 reads quasi gens. The edition's reading is kept. The following reference is printed Isa. LXVI, 5 (the last numeral is damaged in the scan); the words in viis tuis recordabuntur tui are Isaiah 64:5."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Advent Sermon III: That One Should Always Be Ready for the Coming Lord", "topics": ["Advent", "readiness", "confession", "psalmody", "hospitality to Christ"], "url": "sermons/guerric-advent-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-advent", "calendar_label": "Advent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-advent-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De adventu Domini, sermo IV, De vita solitaria, et de via Domino paranda, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 21-26.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Advent", "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "Isaiah 40:3", "Jeremiah 2:2", "Isaiah 35:2", "Psalm 64:13 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 5:17", "Psalm 106:8\u20139 (historical numbering)", "Ecclesiastes 4:10", "Ecclesiastes 9:17", "John 14:6", "Psalm 118:5 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 3:17", "Proverbs 16:7", "Psalm 80:14\u201315 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 13:3 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 17:11", "Psalm 36:7 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 21:11", "Job 21:13", "Psalm 17:31, 33 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 34:6 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 4:19", "Isaiah 40:4", "Luke 3:5", "Ezekiel 18:29", "Wisdom 1:4", "Psalm 141:4 (historical numbering)", "John 12:35", "Proverbs 6:23", "Proverbs 5:12, 14", "Psalm 118:110\u2013111, 94\u201395 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 36:31 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 11:9", "Numbers 22:27, 31", "Proverbs 24:16", "Psalm 145:8 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 93:18 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 16:5 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 138:24 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "De adventu Domini", "occasion_detail": "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "occasion_label": "Advent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This fourth Advent sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Addressed to monks in the solitude of the desert, it reads John the Baptist's cry to prepare the way for the Lord as a call to correct one's life. Guerric commends the peaceful ways of wisdom, the spotless way of chastity, the baptism of penance and knowledge of God's law. The source names Advent without assigning a particular Sunday."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De vita solitaria, et de via Domino paranda."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the solitary life, and on preparing the way for the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Vox clamantis in deserto, Parate viam Domini (Isa. XL, 3). Primo omnium considerandam arbitror gratiam deserti, beatitudinem eremi; quae ab initio gratiae, quieti sanctorum meruit consecrari. Consecravit nobis plane habitationem deserti vox clamantis in deserto, Joannes praedicans et tribuens baptismum poenitentiae in deserto; quanquam et ante ipsum quibusque sanctissimis prophetarum, velut auditorium Spiritus, amica semper fuerit solitudo. Longe tamen excellentior atque divinior ipsi loco gratia sanctificationis accessit, cum Jesus Joanni successit: qui etiam, antequam praedicare poenitentibus inciperet, locum poenitentibus praeparandum putavit; atque per dies quadraginta, dum versatur in deserto, velut purificans locum, novumque novae vitae dedicans locum, tyrannum qui incubabat, omnemque malitiam et subtilitatem ejus, non tam sibi, quam illis qui accolae eremi futuri erant, superavit. Si ergo elongasti fugiens et manens in solitudine, ibi permane, ibi exspecta eum qui te salvum faciet a pusillanimitate spiritus, et tempestate. Quantalibet ingruat tibi tempestas bellorum, quantacunque patiaris in deserto, penuriam etiam victualium, noli pusillanimitate spiritus redire mente in Aegyptum. Felicius te pascet eremus manna, id est pane angelorum, quam Aegyptus ollis carnium. Ipse Jesus quidem in eremo jejunavit; sed multitudinem sequentium se in deserto, saepe et mirabiliter pavit. Saepius autem et mirabilius te satiabit, qui tanto gratiore merito, quanto sanctiore proposito secutus es eum in deserto. Cum enim diutius eum tui oblitum putaveris, ipse suae non immemor bonitatis, consolabitur te, et dicet tibi: Recordatus sum tui, miserans adolescentiam tuam, et charitatem desponsationis tuae, quando secuta es me in deserto (Jerem. II, 2). Tunc plane ponet desertum tuum quasi delicias paradisi: et ipse confiteberis quia gloria Libani data est ei, decor Carmeli et Saron (Isa. XXXV, 2). Sicut enim in multis hodie locis, etiam juxta litteram illud propheticum videmus impleri, Pinguescent speciosa deserti (Psal. LXIV, 13); et, Deserta in ubertatem versa advenae comedent (Isa. V, 17): sic quilibet Scripturae locus, qui ante tibi sterilis videbatur et aridus, repente ad benedictionem Dei mira replebitur ubertate, ac pinguedine spiritus; ut de saturitate mentis eructes hymnum laudis, Confiteantur, inquiens, Domino, misericordiae ejus, et mirabilia ejus filiis hominum: quia satiavit animam inanem, et animam esurientem satiavit bonis (Psal. CVI, 8, 9)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord (Isa. XL, 3). First of all, I think we must consider the grace of the desert, the blessedness of the wilderness; which from the very beginning of grace deserved to be consecrated to the rest of the saints. The voice crying in the desert clearly consecrated for us the dwelling-place of the desert\u2014John, preaching and bestowing the baptism of repentance in the desert; although even before him solitude had always been a friend to the holiest of the prophets, as a hearing-chamber of the Spirit. Yet a far more excellent and more divine grace of sanctification came upon that very place when Jesus succeeded John, who, even before he began to preach to penitents, thought it right that the place be prepared for penitents; and for forty days, while he dwelt in the desert, as though purifying the place and dedicating this new place to a new life, he overcame the tyrant who lay brooding there, and all his malice and cunning\u2014not so much for his own sake as for the sake of those who were to be future dwellers of the wilderness. If, then, you have fled far off and remain in solitude, stay there; wait there for him who will save you from faintness of spirit and from the storm. However great a storm of battles may rush upon you, whatever you may suffer in the desert, even a lack of provisions, do not, through faintness of spirit, turn back in your mind to Egypt. The wilderness will feed you more happily with manna, that is, with the bread of angels, than Egypt with its pots of meat. Jesus himself indeed fasted in the wilderness; but the multitude that followed him in the desert he often and wonderfully fed. But he will satisfy you still more often and still more wonderfully, since you have followed him into the desert with a purpose all the holier, and thus with a merit all the more pleasing. For when you have thought for a long time that he has forgotten you, he himself, not forgetful of his own goodness, will console you and say to you: I have remembered you, having pity on your youth and on the love of your betrothal, when you followed me into the desert (Jerem. II, 2). Then he will surely make your desert like the delights of paradise, and you yourself will confess that the glory of Lebanon has been given to it, the beauty of Carmel and Sharon (Isa. XXXV, 2). For just as today, in many places, we see that prophetic word fulfilled even according to the letter\u2014The beautiful places of the desert shall grow fat (Psal. LXIV, 13); and, The deserts turned into abundance shall strangers eat (Isa. V, 17)\u2014so too any passage of Scripture which before seemed to you barren and dry will suddenly, by God's blessing, be filled with wondrous abundance and with fatness of spirit, so that from the fullness of your mind you will belch forth a hymn of praise: Let them give praise to the Lord, he says, for his mercies, and his wonderful works to the children of men: for he has satisfied the empty soul, and filled the hungry soul with good things (Psal. CVI, 8, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Illud sane mirabili gratia provisum est divinae dispensationis, ut in his desertis nostris quietem habeamus solitudinis, nec tamen consolatione careamus gratiae et sanctae societatis. Licet cuique sedere solitarium, et tacere, eo quod neminem patiatur interpellantem: nec tamen vae soli, eo quod non habeat confoventem: aut, si ceciderit, sublevantem (Eccle. IV, 10). In frequentia hominum sumus, et sine turba sumus; velut in urbe versamur, nullum tamen tumultum patimur, quominus vox clamantis in deserto a nobis audiatur: si modo interius, sicut exterius silentium habeamus. Verba namque sapientium, ait Salomon, audiuntur in silentio, plus quam clamor principis inter stultos (Eccle. IX, 17). Et nunc quoque si medium silentium teneant omnia interiora tua, omnipotens sermo tibi secretus illabetur de sede paterna. Felix igitur, qui sic elongavit fugiens tumultum mundi: qui tam procul recessit in secretum et solitudinem quietae mentis, ut non modo vocem Verbi, sed et Verbum ipsum; non Joannem, sed Jesum mereatur audire! Interim tamen quid vox Verbi nobis clamet audiamus, ut quandoque de voce ad Verbum proficiamus. Parate, inquit, viam Domino: rectas facite semitas ejus (Isa. XL, 3). Parat viam, qui corrigit vitam: rectam facit semitam, qui per arctiorem se dirigit viam. Plane vita correcta, via est recta, per quam Dominus veniat ad nos, qui in hoc ipsum praevenit nos. A Domino siquidem gressus hominis dirigentur, et viam sic ejus volet, ut gratanter per eam veniens ad hominem, cum eo jugiter ambulet. Nisi enim suum ad nos adventum ipse praeveniat, qui est via, veritas et vita (Joan. XIV, 6); non potest via nostra corrigi secundum regulam veritatis, ac per hoc nec dirigi ad vitam aeternitatis. In quo vero corrigit adolescentior viam suam, nisi in custodiendo sermones ejus, nisi in sequendo vestigia ejus, qui viam fecit se ipsum, qua veniamus ad ipsum? Utinam dirigantur viae meae, ad custodiendas, o Domine, vias tuas (Psal. CXVIII, 5); ut propter verba labiorum tuorum custodiam etiam vias duras: quae etsi durae videantur carni, quia infirma est, suaves et pulchrae videbuntur spiritui, si promptus est! Viae, inquit, ejus viae pulchrae, et omnes semitae ejus pacificae (Prov. III, 17). Non solum pacatae, sed et pacificae sunt viae sapientiae: quia, cum placuerint Domino viae hominis, etiam inimicos ejus convertet ad pacem (Id. XVI, 7). Israel, si in viis meis, inquit, ambulasset, pro nihilo forsitan inimicos eorum humiliassem, et super tribulantes eos misissem manum meam (Psal. LXXX, 14, 15). Quare enim contritio et infelicitas in viis eorum, nisi quia viam pacis non cognoverunt (Psal. XIII, 3)? Eapropter, si quando vidi hominem inquietum, contumacem, litigiosum, gravissima tentatione vexari, vel tribulatione flagellari, recordabar illius proverbii: Semper jurgia quaerit malus: angelus autem crudelis mittetur contra eum (Prov. XVII, 11), cui traditur forsitan in afflictionem carnis, ut spiritus salvus sit in die Domini. Si tamen aliquando prosperatur via impiorum, noli aemulari in eo qui prosperatur in via sua, in homine faciente injustitias (Psal. XXXVI, 7): quia haec via eorum scandalum ipsis; ut tanto liberius in mortem currant, quanto minus sepitur eis via qua ambulant. De istis Sapiens loquitur: Via peccantium complanata lapidibus: sed in fine illorum, inferi, tenebrae et poenae (Eccli. XXI, 11): et, Ducunt in bonis dies suos, et in puncto descendunt ad inferos (Job XXI, 13). Beati igitur immaculati in via, qui ambulant in via Domini! quia, etsi de torrente in via bibunt, propterea exaltabunt caput; quia caput suum, quod sequuntur per viam passionis, consequentur in fine mansionis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. It was surely provided by a wonderful grace of divine dispensation that in these deserts of ours we should have the quiet of solitude, and yet not lack the consolation of grace and holy fellowship. Each one may sit solitary and be silent, in that he suffers no one interrupting him; and yet it is not \"woe to him alone,\" in that he has no one to comfort him, or, if he should fall, to lift him up (Eccle. IV, 10). We are amid a throng of men, and yet we are without a crowd; we dwell as it were in a city, yet we suffer no tumult that would keep us from hearing the voice of one crying in the desert\u2014provided only that we keep interior silence as we keep exterior silence. For, as Solomon says, the words of the wise are heard in silence more than the shouting of a prince among fools (Eccle. IX, 17). And even now, if all your inward parts keep deep silence, the omnipotent Word will steal into you secretly from the Father's throne. Happy, then, is he who has so withdrawn, fleeing the tumult of the world, who has retreated so far into the secrecy and solitude of a quiet mind, that he deserves to hear not only the voice of the Word, but the Word itself\u2014not John, but Jesus! Meanwhile, however, let us listen to what the voice of the Word cries to us, so that one day we may advance from the voice to the Word. \"Prepare,\" he says, \"the way for the Lord; make straight his paths\" (Isa. XL, 3). He prepares the way who corrects his life; he makes the path straight who directs himself along the narrower way. Clearly a corrected life is a straight way, by which the Lord may come to us\u2014he who has gone before us for this very purpose. For indeed the steps of man are directed by the Lord, and he so wills his way that, gladly coming by it to man, he may walk continually with him. For unless he himself, who is the way, the truth, and the life (Joan. XIV, 6), should go before us in his own coming to us, our way cannot be corrected according to the rule of truth, and therefore cannot be directed toward the life of eternity. But in what does the young man correct his way, except in keeping his words, except in following the footsteps of him who made himself the way by which we come to him? \"Would that my ways were directed, O Lord, to the keeping of your ways\" (Psal. CXVIII, 5); that for the sake of the words of your lips I might keep even the hard ways\u2014which, though they seem hard to the flesh, because it is weak, will seem sweet and beautiful to the spirit, if it is willing! \"Her ways,\" it says, \"are beautiful ways, and all her paths are peaceful\" (Prov. III, 17). The ways of wisdom are not only peaceable but peace-making: because, when the ways of a man please the Lord, he will turn even his enemies to peace (Id. XVI, 7). \"If Israel,\" he says, \"had walked in my ways, I might perhaps have humbled their enemies with ease, and would have set my hand against those who troubled them\" (Psal. LXXX, 14, 15). For why is there destruction and unhappiness in their ways, except that they have not known the way of peace (Psal. XIII, 3)? For this reason, whenever I have seen a restless, stubborn, quarrelsome man vexed by some very grave temptation or scourged by tribulation, I would recall that proverb: \"The wicked man always seeks quarrels; but a cruel angel will be sent against him\" (Prov. XVII, 11)\u2014to whom perhaps he is handed over for the affliction of the flesh, that his spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord. Yet if sometimes the way of the impious prospers, do not envy the man who prospers in his way, the man who does unjust things (Psal. XXXVI, 7): for this very way of theirs is a snare to them; so that they run the more freely into death, the less their way is hedged in as they walk it. Of such the Wise Man speaks: \"The way of sinners is made smooth with stones; but at their end are hell, darkness, and torments\" (Eccli. XXI, 11); and, \"They spend their days in good things, and in a moment they go down to hell\" (Job XXI, 13). Blessed, then, are the undefiled in the way, who walk in the way of the Lord! For even if they drink of the torrent in the way, they shall therefore lift up the head; because the Head whom they follow through the way of suffering, they shall attain at the end of their dwelling.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Quidquid igitur occurrerit vobis in via Domini, laeto ac dilatato corde currite viam mandatorum Dei: quia etsi pusillanimis arcta videtur via, tamen recta; et si dura videtur, immaculata est. Plane beati immaculati in via! qui sic immaculato calle transeunt viam mundi, ut non inquinent vestimenta sua: aut certe, si inquinaverint, secundum beatitudinis non amittunt locum; sed lavant vestimenta sua baptismo poenitentiae, quo Joannes baptizat in eremo, viam parans Domino. Via utique immaculata, castitas; via grata, per quam Dominus gratiae dignanter veniat: cui propheta cantat, Deus meus, impolluta via ejus. Impolluta castitas Virginis, qua venit in uterum illius: impolluta necesse est sit castitas hominis, qua veniat in animam illius. Felix conscientia, cui convenit vox illa: Deus praecinxit me virtute continentiae, et posuit immaculatam viam meam (Psal. XVII, 31, 33)! Verumtamen, ne de castitate sine virtutibus aliis glorieris, quasi jam viam immaculatam Domino paraveris: scito quia nihilominus necesse est ut via haec etiam recta sit, plana sit, ut etiam tenebrosa vel lubrica non sit. Nam via impiorum tenebrae et lubricum; et angelus Domini persequens eos (Psal. XXXIV, 6). Et via impiorum tenebrosa: nesciunt ubi corruant (Prov. IV, 19). Putamusne, fratres, in aliquo nostrum inveniatur adhuc pravum aliquid in voluntatibus, asperum in moribus, tenebrosum per ignorantiam sensus, lubricum per inconstantiam actus? Quomodo autem scripturam de via paranda implebimus, nisi, sicut scriptum est, faciamus: Ut sint prava, in directa; et aspera, in vias planas (Isa. XL, 4, et Luc. III, 5)?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Therefore, whatever may meet you on the way of the Lord, run the way of God's commandments with a joyful and enlarged heart: for even if the way seems narrow to the faint-hearted, it is nevertheless straight; and if it seems hard, it is spotless. Truly blessed are the spotless in the way! who so pass through the way of the world by a spotless path that they do not stain their garments: or if indeed they have stained them, they do not lose the second place of blessedness; but they wash their garments in the baptism of penance, with which John baptizes in the desert, preparing the way for the Lord. Surely the spotless way is chastity; the pleasing way, by which the Lord of grace may deign to come: to whom the prophet sings, 'My God, his way is undefiled.' Undefiled was the chastity of the Virgin, by which he came into her womb; undefiled must necessarily be the chastity of man, by which he comes into his soul. Happy is the conscience to which that voice applies: 'God has girded me with the strength of continence, and has made my way spotless' (Psal. XVII, 31, 33)! Yet, lest you boast of chastity apart from the other virtues, as though you had already prepared a spotless way for the Lord, know that it is likewise necessary that this way be also straight, be level, and also not be dark or slippery. For the way of the wicked is darkness and slipperiness; and the angel of the Lord pursues them (Psal. XXXIV, 6). And the way of the wicked is dark: they do not know where they will fall (Prov. IV, 19). Do we suppose, brothers, that there may still be found in any of us something crooked in our wills, something rough in our conduct, something darkened through ignorance of understanding, something slippery through inconstancy of action? But how shall we fulfill the Scripture about preparing the way, unless, as it is written, we do this: that the crooked places be made straight, and the rough ways be made smooth (Isa. XL, 4, et Luc. III, 5)?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Primo itaque necesse est ut, si qua est in nobis prava et distorta voluntas, corrigatur, et ad regulam divinae voluntatis dirigatur. Aliter videbimur dicere, sicut impii dicunt: Non est recta via Domini. Sed statim refellit nos irrefragabilis veritas, et increpat terribilis severitas: Nunquid non viae meae rectae sunt, et non magis viae vestrae pravae sunt (Ezech. XVIII, 29)? Qui igitur viam Domino parare festinas primo omnium bona sit voluntas; quoniam in malevolam animam non introibit sapientia (Sap. I, 4). Cum autem hoc modo posueris prava in directa, scito quia non minus necesse est ut aspera sint in vias planas: ut scilicet omnem morum complanes asperitatem, et in quamdam vitae socialis sternas aequalitatem, ne mitis et suavis ille viator offensus asperitate viae resiliat. Quidni resiliret ille mitis et humilis corde, qui non nisi super mansuetum requiescit et humilem; cum etiam humanus omnis sensus refugiat et horreat hominem iracundum, et intractabilem? Sed, et si eatenus profecisti in via Domini, ut et propositum rectitudinis, et disciplinam mansuetudinis assecutus sis; profecisti quidem, sed non perfecisti, nisi lucerna sit pedibus tuis verbum Dei, et lumen semitis tuis. In via enim qua ambulabam, ait, absconderunt laqueum mihi (Psal. CXLI, 4). Et sunt viae quae videntur hominibus rectae, novissimum autem earum ducit in profundum inferni. Via enim impiorum tenebrosa, ut Salomon ait: nesciunt ubi corruant (Prov. IV, 19). Hoc et Dominus ait: Qui ambulat in tenebris, nescit quo vadat (Joan. XII, 35). At vero mandatum lucerna est, et lex lux, et via vitae increpatio disciplinae (Prov. VI, 23). Qui enim increpationes relinquit, errat. Si igitur sapis, in via qua nunquam ambulasti, non ipse tibi doctor aut ductor eris, sed aurem magistris inclinabis, et increpationibus eorum acquiesces, et consiliis; et operam scientiae et lectioni dabis, ne sero poenitens dicas: Cur detestatus sum disciplinam, et increpationibus non acquievit cor meum; nec audivi vocem docentium me, et magistris non inclinavi aurem meam? Pene fui in omni malo, in medio ecclesiae, et synagogae (Prov. V, 12-14). Quod enim scientia legis liberet a laqueis, audi confitentem David: Posuerunt peccatores laqueum mihi, sed de mandatis tuis non erravi. Quomodo id potuisti? Quia haereditate acquisivi testimonia tua. Et in via testimoniorum tuorum delectatus sum, sicut in omnibus divitiis. Me exspectaverunt peccatores ut perderent me: sed per hoc evasi, quia testimonia tua intellexi (Psal. CXVIII, 110, 111, 94, 95). Lex Dei, inquit, in corde justi, et per hoc non supplantabuntur gressus ejus (Psal. XXXVI, 31). Hinc et filius ejus Salomon ait: Simulator ore decipit amicum suum, justi autem liberabuntur scientia (Prov. XI, 9)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. First of all, then, it is necessary that, if there is in us any crooked and twisted will, it be corrected and directed to the rule of the divine will. Otherwise we shall seem to say, as the wicked say: 'The way of the Lord is not straight.' But immediately unanswerable truth refutes us, and terrible severity rebukes us: 'Are not my ways straight, and are not your ways rather crooked?' (Ezech. XVIII, 29). Therefore you who hasten to prepare the way for the Lord, let your will be good above all else; for wisdom will not enter into a malevolent soul (Sap. I, 4). But when you have in this way made the crooked straight, know that it is no less necessary that the rough places be made into smooth ways: namely, that you smooth away every roughness of conduct, and spread a certain evenness in your life together with others, lest that gentle and sweet traveler, offended by the roughness of the way, draw back. And why would he not draw back\u2014he who is gentle and humble of heart, who rests only upon the meek and the humble\u2014since even every human sense flees and shudders at an angry and intractable man? But even if you have progressed thus far on the way of the Lord, so as to have attained both the purpose of rectitude and the discipline of gentleness, you have indeed made progress, but you have not reached perfection, unless the word of God is a lamp to your feet and a light to your paths. For 'in the way in which I walked,' he says, 'they hid a snare for me' (Psal. CXLI, 4). And there are ways that seem right to men, but their end leads into the depths of hell. For the way of the wicked is dark, as Solomon says: they do not know where they will fall (Prov. IV, 19). The Lord also says this: 'He who walks in darkness does not know where he goes' (Joan. XII, 35). But indeed the commandment is a lamp, and the law is light, and the way of life is the rebuke of discipline (Prov. VI, 23). For whoever abandons rebukes goes astray. If, then, you are wise, on a way you have never walked, you will not be your own teacher or guide, but you will incline your ear to teachers, and you will acquiesce to their rebukes and counsels; and you will give diligence to knowledge and to reading, lest, repenting too late, you say: 'Why have I hated discipline, and my heart did not consent to rebukes; and I did not hear the voice of those who taught me, nor did I incline my ear to my teachers? I was almost in every evil, in the midst of the church and the assembly' (Prov. V, 12-14). That knowledge of the law frees from snares, hear David confessing: 'Sinners have laid a snare for me: but I have not strayed from your commandments.' How were you able to do that? 'Because I have acquired your testimonies as an inheritance. And in the way of your testimonies I have delighted, as in all riches. Sinners waited for me, to destroy me: but through this I escaped, because I understood your testimonies' (Psal. CXVIII, 110, 111, 94, 95). 'The law of God,' he says, 'is in the heart of the just, and therefore his steps shall not be overthrown' (Psal. XXXVI, 31). Hence also his son Solomon says: 'He who dissembles with his mouth deceives his friend, but the just shall be delivered by knowledge' (Prov. XI, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sed utinam haec via, qua salutem vel Salvatorem suscipere debemus, sicut jam non est tenebrosa per ignorantiam veri, sic nec lubrica sit per inconstantiam operis. Sed, sicut Balaam cadens apertos habebat oculos (Num. XXII, 27, 31), sic et nos: et videmus apertis oculis per scientiam, et cadimus per negligentiam. Volentes et scientes peccamus, et libentes lubricamus: nec tam lubricum viae causari possumus, quam propositum mentis, id est, pedem cui innitimur. Quis enim non ambulat in lubrico, dum in mundo, dum in corpore vivit luteo? Non igitur tam via quam pes in culpa est, qui in via Dei minus firmus est. Sed nunquid, qui cadit, non adjiciet ut resurgat? Septies cadit justus in die, et septies resurgit (Prov. XXIV, 16). Dominus enim erigit elisos, Dominus dirigit justos (Psal. CXLV, 8). Nam, si dicebam: Motus est pes meus; misericordia tua, Domine, adjuvabat me (Psal. XCIII, 18). Quando igitur aliter non potes cadens, surgens viam ambula, clamans tamen semper ei, quem sequi et consequi desideras, Perfice gressus meos in semitis tuis ut non moveantur vestigia mea (Psal. XVI, 5); et, si via iniquitatis in me est (ut est humana fragilitas), deduc me in via aeterna (Psal. CXXXVIII, 24); quatenus per te, qui es via et veritas, perveniam ad te, qui es veritas, et vita aeterna: tibi gloria per aeterna saecula. Amen"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But would that this way, by which we must receive salvation or the Savior, just as it is now no longer dark through ignorance of the truth, so also might not be slippery through inconstancy of action. But, just as Balaam, when falling, had his eyes open (Num. XXII, 27, 31), so also do we: we see with open eyes through knowledge, and yet we fall through negligence. We sin willingly and knowingly, and we slip gladly: nor can we blame the slipperiness of the way so much as the disposition of the mind, that is, the foot on which we lean. For who does not walk on slippery ground, while living in the world, while living in a body of clay? It is not, then, so much the way that is at fault as the foot, which is less firm on the way of God. But shall he who falls not add to his falling a rising up again? The just man falls seven times in a day, and seven times rises again (Prov. XXIV, 16). For the Lord raises up those who are cast down, the Lord sets the just upright (Psal. CXLV, 8). For, if I said, 'My foot is moved'; your mercy, O Lord, helped me (Psal. XCIII, 18). Therefore, when you cannot do otherwise, walk the way falling and rising, yet always crying out to him whom you desire to follow and to attain: 'Perfect my steps in your paths, that my footsteps be not moved' (Psal. XVI, 5); and, 'if the way of iniquity be in me' (as is human frailty), 'lead me in the way everlasting' (Psal. CXXXVIII, 24); so that through you, who are the way and the truth, I may come to you, who are the truth and life everlasting: to you be glory through everlasting ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Advent Sermon IV: On the Solitary Life and on Preparing the Way for the Lord", "topics": ["Advent", "solitude", "desert", "silence", "way of the Lord", "John the Baptist"], "url": "sermons/guerric-advent-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-advent", "calendar_label": "Advent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-advent-05", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De adventu Domini, sermo V, Quomodo proficiendum in paranda via Domino, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 26-30.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Advent", "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "Isaiah 40:3", "Psalm 118:33 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 76:11 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 7:14", "Psalm 54:20 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 110:10 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 118:59 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 146:7 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 3:2", "Proverbs 16:5", "Psalm 84:14 (historical numbering)", "Genesis 3, Septuagint (printed locator; the words are Genesis 4:7 in the Septuagint)", "Matthew 3:3", "Isaiah 30:21", "Isaiah 35:8", "Isaiah 5:21", "1 Corinthians 3:18", "James 3:17", "Isaiah 35:9", "Proverbs 10:29", "Psalm 118:96 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 35:9\u201310"], "occasion": "De adventu Domini", "occasion_detail": "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "occasion_label": "Advent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This fifth Advent sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. The way for the Lord is prepared by walking it and has no end, so progress must always begin again. Fear of the Lord leads to penance, which goes before grace. Through Isaiah's holy way and the serpent shedding its skin in a narrow passage, Guerric keeps his monks on the royal road toward its promised end. The source names Advent without assigning a particular Sunday."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO V."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON V.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quomodo proficiendum in paranda via Domino."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-h002", "label": "English", "text": "How to Make Progress in Preparing the Way for the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Parate viam Domino (Isa. XL, 3). Via Domini, fratres, quam parare jubemur, ambulando paratur, parando ambulatur. Et licet multum profeceritis in ea, semper tamen vobis restat paranda: ut de his in quae pervenistis, tendatis et extendatis vos in ulteriora; quatenus per singulos profectus Dominus, cujus adventui via paratur, quasi novus, ac seipso semper major occurrat. Ideo bene justus orat: Legem pone mihi, Domine, viam justificationum tuarum, et exquiram eam semper (Psal. CXVIII, 33). Ideo forsitan via aeterna dicta est; quia licet providentia semitam cujusque investigaverit, terminumque constituerit quousque proficiat; terminum tamen non habet illius, in quam proficitis, bonitatis natura. Itaque sapiens impigerque viator cum consummatus fuerit, tunc incipiet: ita scilicet obliviscens quae retro sunt, ut quotidie dicat sibi, Nunc coepi (Psal. LXXVI, 11). Exsultat ut gigas, quem nil terreat ad currendam viam mandatorum Dei: Idithumque factus, pigros qui in via resident, facile transilit in impetu cursus sui. Et licet novissima diei hora venerit, consummatus in brevi explet tempora multa; ut repente de novissimo primus, inter primos coronam accipiat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Prepare the way for the Lord\" (Isa. XL, 3). The way of the Lord, brothers, which we are commanded to prepare, is prepared by walking, and is walked by preparing. And however much progress you have made in it, it always remains for you to prepare further: so that from the point you have reached, you may stretch and extend yourselves into what lies beyond; so that through each advance the Lord, for whose coming the way is prepared, may meet you as it were new, and ever greater than himself. For this reason the just man rightly prays: \"Set before me a law, O Lord, the way of your justifications, and I will always seek it\" (Psal. CXVIII, 33). For this reason, perhaps, it is called the eternal way; because although providence has traced out the path for each person and set a limit up to which he may progress, the nature of that goodness toward which you are progressing has no limit. And so the wise and tireless traveler, when he has been made complete, then begins; forgetting, that is, the things that are behind, so that he may say to himself every day, \"Now I have begun\" (Psal. LXXVI, 11). He exults like a giant, whom nothing may frighten from running the way of God's commandments: having become like Idithun, he easily leaps over those slothful ones who sit by the roadside, in the very impetus of his course. And even though he comes at the last hour of the day, being made complete, he fulfills much time in a short span; so that from being last he suddenly becomes first, and receives the crown among the first.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sed nos qui de profectu viae ejus loquimur, utinam vel initium ejus apprehenderimus! Meo namque sensu atque judicio non parum in ea proficit qui coepit; si tamen vere coepit, si viam civitatis habitaculi invenit. Quam pauci sunt, ait Veritas, qui inveniunt eam! (Matth. VII, 14.) Quam multi sunt qui in solitudine errant! prorsus omnes solitarii, id est omnes superbi, qui se solos reputant. Nullus eorum adhuc dicere potest: Nunc coepi, haec mutatio dexterae Excelsi (Psal. LXXVI, 11). Non enim est illis commutatio, ut desinant esse quod erant, quia non timuerunt Deum (Psal. LIV, 20), cum initium sapientiae sit timor Domini (Psal. CX, 10). Si initium sapientiae, nimirum et viae bonae. Ipse nempe timor Domini, ipse est qui statim illud sapiens consilium generat in corde viri, ut dicere possit: Cogitavi vias meas, et converti pedes meos in testimonia tua (Psal. CXVIII, 59). Ipse confessionem persuadet, quam Psalmista initium boni operis esse docet: Incipite, inquit, domino in confessione (Psal. CXLVI, 7). Ipse ad poenitentiam superbum inclinat, ut vocem clamantis in deserto viamque parari jubentis, et unde incipiendum sit ostendentis, audiat: Poenitentiam agite; appropinquabit enim regnum coelorum (Matth. III, 2). Huic sententia consonat Salomonis, qui manifesta definitione rudes instruit, dicens: Initium viae bonae, facere justa (Prov. XVI, 5). Quid est enim justa facere, nisi poenitentiam agere? nisi de nobisipsis quod Deo debemus exigere, et quod rapuimus exsolvere? Haec est justitia quae ante Dominum ambulat, viamque ei placitam parat, sicut scriptum est: Justitia ante eum ambulabit, et ponet in via gressus suos (Psal. LXXXIV, 14). Sicut enim Joannes Jesum, sic poenitentia gratiam praecedit; illam scilicet gratiam, qua post satisfactionem reconciliati, suscipimur in osculo pacis. In hac siquidem poenitentiae via, grato atque hilari occursu obviant sibi, seseque osculantur justitia et pax; justitia scilicet hominis se punientis, et pax Dei ignoscentis: laetumque ac jucundum celebrant in osculo sancto foedus reconciliationis. Denique ne parvi aestimetur meriti haec justitia hominis de semetipso facientis judicium et justitiam, neve oratiunculae, et vota pigrorum se laboribus poenitentium comparare praesumant; quid de ipsa Salomon sentiat; audiant: Initium, inquit, viae bonae, facere justa: accepta autem Deo magis, quam immolare hostias (Prov. XVI, 5). Si ergo videris hominem huic poenitentiae professum, regularibus ac sanctis laboribus, quorum debitor est, singulares oratiunculas praeferentem; ingere ei hanc Sapientis sententiam: Initium viae bonae, facere justa: accepta autem magis Deo quam immolare hostias. Similiter, si videris hominem, adversus quem frater suus aliquid habet, munus seu laudum, seu sacramentorum, ad altare offerentem; repete ei nihilominus eumdem sermonem: Initium viae bonae, facere justa, ut fratri reconcilieris: accepta autem Deo magis quam immolare hostias. Nam et illo etiam spectat haec sententia, ut daturus eleemosynam, prius, si quem defraudavit, vel simplum restituat. Sic et tu quoque voluntaria oris tui beneplacita nullatenus Deo esse credideris, illa scilicet quae in psalmis, vel in orationibus secretis ei obtuleris; si propterea canonem psalmorum ex Regula constitutum negligentius exsolveris. Nonne, si recte offeras, et non recte dividas peccasti? (Gen. III, sec. LXX.) Rectas, inquit, facite semitas ejus (Matth. III, 3, et Isa. XL, 3), vos qui paratis viam Domini."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But we who speak of progress on this way\u2014would that we had even grasped its beginning! For in my own sense and judgment, he who has begun makes no small progress on it; provided, however, that he has truly begun, if he has found the way to the city of habitation. \"How few are they,\" says the Truth, \"who find it!\" (Matth. VII, 14.) How many are there who wander in solitude! Indeed, all the solitary\u2014that is, all the proud, who consider themselves alone. None of them can yet say: \"Now I have begun; this is the change of the right hand of the Most High\" (Psal. LXXVI, 11). For there is no change for them, that they should cease to be what they were, because they have not feared God (Psal. LIV, 20), since the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Psal. CX, 10). If it is the beginning of wisdom, surely it is also the beginning of the good way. For it is indeed the fear of the Lord itself that immediately generates in a man's heart that wise counsel, so that he can say: \"I have thought on my ways, and turned my feet unto your testimonies\" (Psal. CXVIII, 59). It is this fear that persuades to confession, which the Psalmist teaches is the beginning of a good work: \"Begin,\" he says, \"to the Lord with confession\" (Psal. CXLVI, 7). It is this fear that inclines the proud man to penance, so that he hears the voice of the one crying in the desert, commanding that the way be prepared, and showing where one must begin: \"Do penance, for the kingdom of heaven will draw near\" (Matth. III, 2). With this sentence agrees that of Solomon, who instructs the unlearned with a plain definition, saying: \"The beginning of a good way is to do what is right\" (Prov. XVI, 5). For what is it to do what is right, except to do penance? Except to exact from ourselves what we owe to God, and to repay what we have stolen? This is the righteousness that walks before the Lord, and prepares for him a way pleasing to him, as it is written: \"Righteousness shall walk before him, and shall set her steps in the way\" (Psal. LXXXIV, 14). For just as John preceded Jesus, so penance precedes grace; that grace, namely, by which, after making satisfaction, reconciled, we are received with the kiss of peace. In this way of penance, indeed, righteousness and peace meet one another with a welcome and joyful encounter, and kiss one another; namely, the righteousness of the man punishing himself, and the peace of God forgiving; and they celebrate, in a holy kiss, the glad and joyous covenant of reconciliation. Finally, lest this righteousness of a man passing judgment and doing justice upon himself be reckoned of little merit, and lest little prayers and the vows of the slothful presume to compare themselves to the labors of the penitent, let them hear what Solomon thinks of it: \"The beginning,\" he says, \"of a good way is to do what is right; but more acceptable to God than to offer sacrifices\" (Prov. XVI, 5). If, therefore, you see a man professed to this penance, preferring his own private little prayers to the regular and holy labors of which he is a debtor, press upon him this sentence of the Wise Man: \"The beginning of a good way is to do what is right; but it is more acceptable to God than to offer sacrifices.\" Likewise, if you see a man, against whom his brother has some grievance, offering a gift at the altar, whether of praises or of the sacraments, repeat to him nevertheless the same word: \"The beginning of a good way is to do what is right, that you be reconciled to your brother; but it is more acceptable to God than to offer sacrifices.\" For this sentence also looks to this: that one who is about to give alms should first, if he has defrauded anyone, restore at least the simple amount. So also you yourself should by no means believe that the voluntary offerings of your mouth are pleasing to God\u2014those, namely, which you offer him in psalms or in private prayers\u2014if on that account you perform more negligently the canon of psalms established by the Rule. Have you not sinned, if you offer rightly but do not divide rightly? (Gen. III, sec. LXX.) \"Make straight,\" he says, \"his paths\" (Matth. III, 3, et Isa. XL, 3), you who prepare the way of the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. O Domine, tu parasti directiones nobis, si modo nos recte ambulemus in illis. Tu legem posuisti nobis, viam justificationum tuarum, per illum scilicet, quem hujus sanctae institutionis Legislatorem dedisti: Haec via, inquiens, ambulate in ea, neque ad dexteram, neque ad sinistram (Isai. XXX, 21). Haec via plane, sicut propheta promiserat: Via directa nobis, ita ut stulti non errent per eam (Isa. XXXV, 8). Junior fui, etenim senui; et non vidi, si bene memini, stultum errantem per viam: cum vix aliquem sapientem viderim, qui rectitudinem ejus tenere potuerit. Vae, qui sapientes estis in oculis vestris, et coram vobismetipsis prudentes (Isa. V, 21)! quos sapientia vestra devios abducit a via salutis, nec sinit stultitiam sequi Salvatoris. Non ignorabat ille stultus propter Christum, quid haec sapientia terrena, animalis, diabolica mali faciat, qui dicebat: Si quis sapiens esse videtur in hoc saeculo, stultus fiat, ut sit sapiens apud Deum (I Cor. III, 18). Optabilis stultitia, quae divino judicio reputatur sapientia, quae hominem non sinit errare de via. Haec stultitia, nisi fallor, est illa desursum sapientia, pudica, pacifica, modesta, suadibilis, bonis consentiens (Jacob. III, 17): a quibus omnibus quam procul abhorreat sapiens in oculis suis, utinam nunquam experientia ipsa probasset in oculis nostris. Si contristat vos arrogantia, seu pervicacia hujusmodi sapientium, consolatur abundantius nostrorum pax atque modestia stultorum, qui quanto minus sibi credunt, tanto suadibiliores bonis consentiunt. Tales itaque stulti non errant facile de via Dei, sed juxta verbum prophetae semper audiunt vocem post tergum monentis: Haec via, ambulate in ea, neque ad dexteram, neque ad sinistram (Isai. XXX, 21): ne scilicet per fervorem excedentes modum, aut per teporem subsistentes citra modum, legem praescriptam, viamque deseratis regiam. Spiritus est ad dexteram, caro est ad sinistram; et haec sibi colluctantia semper sibi adversantur, ut non quaecunque vult, sive ista, sive ille, faciamus: ne, si plus justo sequimur impetum spiritus, declinemus ad dexteram; aut, si plus justo carni indulgemus, deviemus ad sinistram."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. O Lord, you have prepared straight paths for us, if only we walk rightly in them. You have set before us a law, the way of your righteous judgments, through him whom you gave as the Lawgiver of this holy institution, saying: 'This is the way, walk in it, neither to the right hand nor to the left' (Isai. XXX, 21). This is indeed the way, as the prophet had promised: 'A way made straight for us, so that fools should not err therein' (Isa. XXXV, 8). I have been young, and now am old; and I have not seen, if I remember rightly, a fool going astray in the way \u2014 though I have scarcely seen any wise man who was able to keep to its straight course. Woe to you who are wise in your own eyes, and prudent in your own sight (Isa. V, 21)! whom your wisdom leads astray from the way of salvation, and does not allow to follow the foolishness of the Savior. That man, a fool for Christ's sake, was not ignorant of what evil this earthly, animal, diabolical wisdom works, who said: 'If any man seem to be wise in this world, let him become a fool, that he may be wise with God' (I Cor. III, 18). Desirable is that folly, which by divine judgment is reckoned wisdom, which does not allow a man to stray from the way. This folly, unless I am mistaken, is that wisdom from above, chaste, peaceable, modest, easy to be persuaded, agreeing with the good (Jacob. III, 17): from all of which how far the man who is wise in his own eyes recoils, would that experience itself had never proved it before our eyes! If the arrogance or stubbornness of such wise men grieves you, the peace and modesty of our fools consoles you all the more abundantly, who, the less they trust themselves, the more readily they agree with what is good. Such fools, then, do not easily stray from the way of God, but, according to the word of the prophet, always hear behind them the voice warning: 'This is the way, walk in it, neither to the right hand nor to the left' (Isai. XXX, 21): that is, lest, by exceeding the measure through fervor, or by falling short of the measure through lukewarmness, you abandon the prescribed law and the royal road. The spirit is on the right, the flesh is on the left; and these, warring against each other, are always opposed to one another, so that we do not do whatever either the one or the other wills: lest, if we follow the impulse of the spirit more than is right, we turn aside to the right; or, if we indulge the flesh more than is right, we deviate to the left.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Hanc denique viam justificationum Domini, viam veritatis quam elegistis, quanta laude praedicet Isaias, delectat me cum charitate vestra recordari. Et erit, inquit, ibi, id est, in antiquis cubilibus draconum, in terra utique deserta et invia, semita et via, sicut hodie cernere est: quia in feris et incultis hominibus, qui sine lege vivebant, in ipsis invenitur ordo vitae, et disciplina. Et haec via, inquit, sancta vocabitur: quia nimirum sanctificatio est peccatorum, perditorumque salus. Quanta vero virtute ac reverentia sanctitatis praeemineat, inde probat propheta, quia non transibit, inquit, pollutus per eam (Isa. XXXV, 8). O mi Isaia, ibunt igitur qui polluti sunt per aliam viam? Imo potius huc omnes, huc veniant, hac incedant. Immundis enim praecipue Christus hanc viam stravit, qui venit quaerere et salvum facere quod perierat in viis saeculi. Quid igitur? transibit pollutus per viam sanctam? Absit! Veniat quantumlibet pollutus ad eam, non tamen transibit pollutus per eam: quia cum transierit, jam pollutus non erit. Admittit quidem via sancta pollutum, sed statim abluit admissum, quia diluit omne commissum, tanquam vere baptismus alter poenitentium. Hic plane, hic baptizat, non Joannes, sed Jesus baptismo poenitentiae: hic patet fons domus David in ablutionem peccatoris et menstruatae. Propterea namque via haec pollutum admittit, sed pollutum non transmittit: quia via arcta est, et quasi foramen illud angustum est, quo serpens innovandus cum exuviis suae vetustatis venire potest, sed cum ipsis transire non potest; sed novum suaque nuditate melius vestitum, transitus angustia trajicit, extricans omnem, quem attulerat, squalorem vetustatis. Bene ergo prudentiam serpentis imitari rogamur, qui nec aliter innovari possumus, nisi per angustum coarctemur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Finally, it delights me to recall, together with your charity, with what great praise Isaiah proclaims this way of the Lord's righteous judgments, this way of truth which you have chosen. 'And it shall be there,' he says \u2014 that is, in the ancient lairs of dragons, in a land indeed desert and trackless \u2014 'a path and a way,' as may be seen even today: because among wild and uncultivated men, who lived without law, there is found in them an order of life and discipline. 'And this way,' he says, 'shall be called holy': because it is, assuredly, the sanctification of sinners and the salvation of the lost. But how greatly it excels in the power and reverence of holiness, the prophet proves from this, that, he says, 'the unclean shall not pass through it' (Isa. XXXV, 8). O my Isaiah, shall those who are unclean, then, go by another way? Nay rather, let all come here, let them come here, let them walk by this road. For Christ laid this way especially for the unclean, he who came to seek and to save what had perished in the ways of the world. What then? Shall the unclean pass through the holy way? God forbid! Let him come to it however unclean he may be, yet he shall not pass through it unclean: because when he has passed through, he will no longer be unclean. The holy way indeed admits the unclean man, but immediately washes the one it has admitted, because it washes away every sin committed, being truly, as it were, a second baptism for penitents. Here plainly, here it is Jesus, not John, who baptizes with the baptism of penance: here the fountain of the house of David lies open for the washing of the sinner and of the menstruous woman. For this reason this way admits the unclean man, but does not let the unclean man pass through: because the way is narrow, and is like that narrow opening through which the serpent, needing to be renewed, can come with the worn skin of its old age, but cannot pass through still wearing it; rather, made new and better clothed in its own nakedness, it squeezes through the narrowness of the passage, freeing itself from all the squalor of the old age it had brought with it. Rightly, then, are we urged to imitate the prudence of the serpent, since we cannot be renewed in any other way unless we are compressed through a narrow strait.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sane quod illius antiqui serpentis insidias evadamus, si per hanc arctam viam hujus novi serpentis exemplum sequamur, Isaias promittit, qui de eadem via subdit: Non erit ibi leo, et mala bestia non ascendet per eam, nec invenietur ibi (Isa. XXXV, 9). Securi ergo simus, si de via non exorbitamus. Potest leo ille, qui circuit quaerens quem devoret, juxta iter scandala ponere, laqueos abscondere, viatores rugitu terrere: sed persistentes in via laedere non potest; quippe cui via ipsa terror et supplicium est. Fortitudo nempe simplicis, via Domini, et pavor his qui operantur malum (Prov. X, 29). Si ergo in via es, hoc solum time, ne devies, ne Dominum offendas, qui te per viam ducit, ut dimittat te vagum in via cordis tui: praeter eum ne timeas alienum. Quod si nimis arctam viam causaris, prospice finem, quo te via ducit. Si enim omnis consummationis videris finem, continuo dices: Latum mandatum tuum nimis (Psal. CXVIII, 96). Si minus videre potes, crede videnti Isaiae, qui oculus tui corporis est. Ipse utcunque videbat, cum id quod sequitur dicebat: Et ecce ambulabunt hac via qui liberati fuerint, et redempti a Domino: et venient in Sion cum laude, et laetitia sempiterna super capita eorum Gaudium quoque et laetitiam obtinebunt, fugiet dolor et gemitus (Isai. XXXV, 9, 10). Qui satis hunc finem cogitat, puto quia non solum viam dilatat sibi; sed etiam pennas, ut jam non ambulet, sed volet, assumit sibi. Semper ergo, fratres, cogitate finem praemiorum, et cum omni facultate et alacritate curretis viam mandatorum. Per ipsam vos deducat et perducat ipse, qui est via currentium, et bravium pervenientium, Christus Jesus; cui honor et gloria per omnia saecula saeculorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Isaiah promises that we shall indeed escape the snares of that ancient serpent, if along this narrow way we follow the example of this new serpent; for he adds concerning this same way: \"There shall be no lion there, and the evil beast shall not go up by it, nor be found there\" (Isa. XXXV, 9). Let us therefore be secure, if we do not stray from the way. That lion, who goes about seeking whom he may devour, can set stumbling blocks along the road, hide snares, and terrify travelers with his roaring; but he cannot harm those who remain on the way, since the way itself is his terror and his torment. For \"the way of the Lord is strength to the simple, and fear to them that work evil\" (Prov. X, 29). If, then, you are on the way, fear only this: that you may turn aside, that you may offend the Lord who leads you along the way, lest he abandon you to wander in the way of your own heart; apart from him, fear no other. But if you complain that the way is too narrow, look to the end to which the way leads you. For if you see the end of all perfection, you will at once say: \"Your commandment is exceedingly broad\" (Psal. CXVIII, 96). If you cannot see this well enough, believe Isaiah who sees it, for he is the eye of your body. He himself saw it, after a manner, when he said what follows: \"And behold, they shall walk in this way who have been set free and redeemed by the Lord; and they shall come to Sion with praise, and everlasting joy shall be upon their heads. They shall obtain joy and gladness; sorrow and mourning shall flee away\" (Isai. XXXV, 9, 10). He who meditates enough on this end, I think, not only widens the way for himself, but also takes to himself wings, so that he no longer walks but flies. Therefore, brethren, always think on the end of the rewards, and with all ease and eagerness you will run the way of the commandments. May he himself lead and bring you through it, who is the way of those who run and the prize of those who arrive, Christ Jesus; to whom be honor and glory for ever and ever.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Advent Sermon V: How to Make Progress in Preparing the Way for the Lord", "topics": ["Advent", "way of the Lord", "penance", "spiritual progress", "humility"], "url": "sermons/guerric-advent-05.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:all-saints", "calendar_label": "All Saints", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-all-saints", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN SOLEMNITATE OMNIUM SANCTORUM,, SERMO, De pauperibus spiritu., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 205-210.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "All Saints", "Solemnity of All Saints", "Matthew 5:3", "Isaiah 61:1", "Ezekiel 40:37", "Sirach 44:6", "Song of Songs 1:5", "Psalm 119:7 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 17", "Proverbs 31:29", "Psalm 33:11 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 11:25", "Romans 14:17", "Psalm 15:5\u20136 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 48:21 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "In solemnitate omnium sanctorum", "occasion_detail": "Solemnity of All Saints", "occasion_label": "All Saints", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads the first Beatitude, \"Blessed are the poor in spirit,\" as the opening of the Gospel and the first of eight steps that lead from renunciation of the world to the office of peacemaker and the merit of martyrdom. Those who possess God possess all things, while the miser is possessed by his money. Poverty of spirit lies more in humility than in want, and the poor already hold the kingdom, carrying God in their hearts. The sermon appears under the heading for the Solemnity of All Saints in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN SOLEMNITATE OMNIUM SANCTORUM,"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE SOLEMNITY OF ALL SAINTS,", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De pauperibus spiritu."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On the poor in spirit.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Beati pauperes spiritu (Matth. V, 3). Agnosco notum illud ac nobile prognosticum, quod in testimonium sui, necdum natus in carne Dei Filius protulit; jam vero natus, sed nondum notus, sibi competere docuit: Spiritus, inquit, Domini super me, evangelizare pauperibus misit me (Isai. LXI, 1). Ecce pauperes evangelizantur, ecce pauperibus Evangelium regni nuntiatur: Beati, inquit, pauperes spiritu, quoniam ipsorum est regnum coelorum (Matth. V, 3). Laetum prorsus ac novae plenum gratiae Novi Testamenti principium, etiam quantumlibet infidelem aut pigrum provocans ad audiendum, sed amplius ad faciendum, cum scilicet beatitudo miseris, exsulibus et egenis promittitur regnum coelorum. Gratum, inquam, et auspicatum novae legis initium, quando in ipso statim initio, tot beatitudinum benedictiones dat Legislator, quibus delectati eant de virtute in virtutem: per istos videlicet octo gradus ascensionis, quos in corde nostro disposuit structura Evangelii, secundum exemplar et imaginem coelestium, quod Ezechieli quoque ostensum est in monte visionum Dei. Est enim manifeste quidam ascensus cordium, et profectus meritorum iste per ordinem digestus octonarius virtutum, gradatim de imis ad summa perducens virum evangelicae perfectionis, donec ad videndum Deum deorum in Sion ingrediatur templum, de quo propheta dicit: Et in octo gradibus ascensus ejus (Ezech. XL, 37)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Blessed are the poor in spirit\" (Matth. V, 3). I recognize that well-known and noble sign which the Son of God, not yet born in the flesh, brought forth as testimony of himself; and indeed, now born, but not yet known, he taught that it applied to himself: \"The Spirit of the Lord is upon me,\" he says, \"he has sent me to preach the gospel to the poor\" (Isai. LXI, 1). Behold, the poor are evangelized; behold, to the poor the Gospel of the kingdom is announced: \"Blessed,\" he says, \"are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven\" (Matth. V, 3). A truly joyful beginning of the New Testament, full of new grace, which summons even the most unbelieving or slothful to listen, but still more to act \u2014 since blessedness is promised to the wretched, the exiles, and the needy, namely, the kingdom of heaven. A pleasing, I say, and auspicious beginning of the new law, since right at its very outset the Lawgiver gives so many blessings of beatitudes, by which men, delighted, may go from virtue to virtue: namely, through those eight steps of ascent which the structure of the Gospel has arranged in our heart, according to the pattern and image of heavenly things, which was also shown to Ezekiel on the mountain of the visions of God. For there is manifestly a certain ascent of hearts, and a progress of merits, this eightfold order of virtues arranged in sequence, leading step by step from the lowest things to the highest the man of evangelical perfection, until he enters to see the God of gods in Sion, into the temple of which the prophet says: \"And by eight steps was its ascent\" (Ezech. XL, 37).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Prima siquidem virtus inchoantium est renuntiatio saeculi, qua pauperes efficimur spiritu : secunda, mansuetudo, qua nos obedientiae subdimus, et assuescimus : deinde, luctus, quo peccata plorantur, vel virtutes implorantur. Ibi nimirum gustamus, unde amplius esuriamus, et sitiamus justitiam, tam in nobis quam in aliis, zeloque contra peccantes incipimus commoveri. Sed ne zelus immoderatior in vitium feratur, sequitur misericordia, qua temperetur. His ergo studiis, vel exercitiis, cum quis justus et misericors esse didicerit, idoneus erit fortassis contemplationi vacare, et mundando cordi, quo Deus videatur, operam dare. Exercitatus autem et probatus tam in actione, quam in contemplatione, tum demum dignus erit, qui nomen et officium Filii Dei gerens, pater et minister factus aliorum, tanquam mediator et sequester pacificet inter ipsos et Deum: pacificet ipsos ad invicem, vel etiam ad eos qui foris sunt, sicut scriptum est in laude sanctorum patrum, Pacificantes in domibus suis (Eccli. XLIV, 6). In hoc officio qui fidelis et constans fuerit, saepe virtutem et meritum sibi martyrii comparabit, persecutionem patiens propter justitiam; aliquando et ab his pro quibus pugnabit, ut sit ei dicere, Filii matris meae pugnaverunt contra me (Cantic. I, 5); et, Cum his qui oderunt pacem, eram pacificus; cum loquebar illis, impugnabant me gratis (Psal. CXIX, 7)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. For the first virtue of beginners is renunciation of the world, by which we are made poor in spirit; the second, meekness, by which we submit ourselves to obedience and grow accustomed to it; then, mourning, by which sins are wept over, or virtues are implored. There, surely, we taste that from which we may hunger and thirst all the more for justice, both in ourselves and in others, and we begin to be moved with zeal against sinners. But lest this zeal, growing too immoderate, turn into vice, mercy follows, by which it is tempered. By these pursuits, or exercises, when someone has learned to be just and merciful, he will perhaps be fit to devote himself to contemplation, and to give effort to the cleansing of the heart by which God may be seen. But one trained and proved both in action and in contemplation will then at last be worthy \u2014 bearing the name and office of the Son of God, having become a father and minister to others \u2014 to make peace, as a mediator and go-between, between them and God: to make peace among themselves, or even with those who are outside, as it is written in praise of the holy fathers, \"Making peace in their own houses\" (Eccli. XLIV, 6). Whoever is faithful and steadfast in this office will often win for himself the virtue and merit of martyrdom, suffering persecution for the sake of justice \u2014 sometimes even at the hands of those for whom he fights, so that he may say, \"The sons of my mother have fought against me\" (Cantic. I, 5); and, \"With them that hated peace I was peaceable: when I spoke to them, they fought against me without cause\" (Psal. CXIX, 7).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Quantae autem gloriae, quamque copiose mercedis in coelo sit culmen hujus perfectionis, inde utcumque licet aestimare, quod ipsa etiam abrenuntiantium saeculo primordia tantae Dominus praedicat felicitatis, ut dicat: Beati pauperes spiritu, quoniam ipsorum est regnum coelorum. Plane beati qui, projectis vilibus, sed gravibus sarcinulis hujus mundi, nolunt divites fieri, nisi de solo Creatore mundi, propter ipsum, tanquam nihil habentes, et per ipsum omnia possidentes. An non possident omnia qui possident continentem et disponentem omnia, quorum Deus portio est et haereditas; qui, ut nihil desit, timentibus eum, caetera prout expedire novit, dispensat ad utendum, et se ipsum servat ad fruendum? Cum haeres Dei, cohaeres autem Christi, adultus et emancipatus introducetur in plenariam possessionem exspectatae haereditatis, tunc profecto jus absolutum, ac liberum imperium habebit in creaturis: qui modo quanto tempore parvulus est, nihil differt a servo, cum sit Dominus omnium; sed sub tutoribus et actoribus est usque ad tempus praefinitum a Patre. Tunc mundus agnoscet justum ac legitimum haeredem Dominum suum, propter quem creatus est: agnoscet, inquam, quia jam non conformem sibi, sed reformatum in novitate sensus sui ad imaginem Dei, ad quam factus est. Sed et nunc quoque fideli homini totus mundus divitiarum est (Prov. XVII, sec. LXX) (12); non solum quia ad agnoscendum vel diligendum auctorem mundi, mundanis omnibus utitur tanquam ad hoc sibi datis; et in via testimoniorum Dei, quae videt facturam sicut et Scripturam perhibere factori, delectatur sicut in omnibus divitiis: sed etiam, quia ita sufficiens et gratus esse didicit, ut quasi totius mundi divitias computet illud prope nihil quod habet, sive hoc ipsum quod nihil habet. Ideo jure beatificat Ecclesiam sanctorum Salomon, quae pauper pro Christo, tam dives est in Christo. Multae, inquit, filiae congregaverunt divitias, tu supergressa es universas (Prov. XXXI, 29). Alii siquidem rapiunt non sua, et semper in egestate sunt: sancti dividunt propria, et ditiores fiunt. Divites enim eguerunt et esurierunt; inquirentes autem Dominum non minuentur omni bono (Psal. XXXIII, 11)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. How great a glory, however, and how abundant a reward in heaven belongs to the summit of this perfection may in some measure be estimated from this: that the Lord proclaims even the very beginnings of those who renounce the world to be of such great blessedness that he says, \"Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.\" Truly blessed are they who, having cast off the cheap but heavy burdens of this world, refuse to become rich except from the Creator of the world alone, for his sake \u2014 as having nothing, and through him possessing all things. Do they not possess all things who possess him who contains and disposes all things, whose portion and inheritance is God himself; who, so that nothing may be lacking to those who fear him, dispenses the rest for their use as he knows to be fitting, and keeps himself for their enjoyment? When the heir of God and co-heir of Christ, having come of age and been emancipated, is brought into the full possession of the inheritance he has awaited, then indeed he will have absolute right and free dominion over creatures \u2014 he who, for as long as he is a little child, differs in nothing from a slave, though he is Lord of all, but is under guardians and stewards until the time appointed by the Father. Then the world will acknowledge him as its Lord, the just and lawful heir for whose sake it was created; it will acknowledge him, I say, because he is now no longer conformed to the world, but reformed in the newness of his mind to the image of God, to which he was made. But even now, too, to the faithful man the whole world is a world of riches (Prov. XVII, sec. LXX) (12); not only because he uses all worldly things as given to him for this purpose \u2014 to know and love the author of the world \u2014 and in the way of the testimonies of God, which he sees creation, like Scripture also, bearing witness to its Maker, he delights as in all riches; but also because he has learned to be so content and grateful that he reckons as the riches of the whole world that almost nothing which he has, or even this very fact \u2014 that he has nothing. Therefore Solomon rightly calls blessed the Church of the saints, which, poor for Christ's sake, is so rich in Christ. \"Many daughters,\" he says, \"have gathered together riches; you have surpassed them all\" (Prov. XXXI, 29). For others indeed seize what is not their own, and are always in want; the saints divide what is their own, and become richer. For \"the rich have wanted and suffered hunger; but they that seek the Lord shall not be deprived of any good thing\" (Psal. XXXIII, 11).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Avarus quo plura possidet, pluribus eget. Quanquam nil minus habeat, quam quod habere se putat; cum habeatur, non habeat, servus pecuniae, mancipium avaritiae, cultorque crumenae, idololatra detestandus, cui nummus est deus. Pulchre autem jam nunc quoque vindicat in peccatores justitia divina, res ipsas quas amant convertens eis in tormenta, et vitia faciens esse supplicia. Pecunia enim quae justum amplius justificat, et verius ditat dispensata, vel semel data; avarum cruciat servata, prodigum polluit profligata. Prorsus beati pauperes Christi, quorum fides sic elusit sapientiam mundi, ut sola, quis sit optimus divitiarum usus, invenerit: quod videlicet divitiae, quae pauperem faciunt et miserum, si diliguntur; tunc demum faciunt divitem et beatum, si pro Christo contemnuntur. Confiteor tibi, Pater, Domine coeli et terrae, quia abscondisti haec a sapientibus et prudentibus, et revelasti ea parvulis (Matth. XI, 25), id est, humilibus; qui utique non sunt alii quam pauperes spiritu, quorum hic beatitudo praedicatur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. The miser, the more he possesses, the more he lacks. Yet nothing is less his than what he thinks he has; since he is possessed rather than possessing, he is the slave of money, the chattel of avarice, the worshiper of his purse, a detestable idolater, for whom a coin is god. And now too divine justice fittingly takes vengeance upon sinners, turning the very things they love into torments for them, and making their vices into their punishments. For money, which more fully justifies and more truly enriches the just man when it is distributed or given away, torments the miser when it is kept, and defiles the prodigal when it is squandered. Truly blessed are the poor of Christ, whose faith has so outwitted the wisdom of the world that it alone has discovered what is the best use of riches: namely, that riches, which make a man poor and wretched if they are loved, then at last make him rich and blessed if they are despised for Christ's sake. I confess to you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, that you have hidden these things from the wise and prudent, and have revealed them to little ones (Matth. XI, 25) \u2014 that is, to the humble, who are none other than the poor in spirit, whose blessedness is proclaimed here.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Hoc enim licet scientes tamen commonere vos velim, fratres, quod vera ac beata paupertas spiritus, plus est in humilitate cordis, quam in angustia rei familiaris: plus consistit in abdicatione superbiae, quam in contemptu substantiae. Substantia nonnunquam utiliter habetur; superbia nunquam nisi damnabiliter retinetur. Diabolus nihil in mundo possidet, aut possidere desiderat, quem sola, vel maxime superbia damnat. Parum igitur prodest renuntiare saeculi possessionibus, nisi renuntietur et moribus: imo stultum ac ridiculum est nudari divitiis, et divitum implicari vitiis; pauperem fieri rebus, nec ditari virtutibus; relinquere omnia, nec sequi Christum, sed magis forsitan in castris Christi juvare partes Antichristi. Plane Antichristi partes adjuvat, quisquis superbiae militat, nomenque sanctum, quod verbis vel habitu profitetur, moribus impugnat. Vexillum Christi humilitas; Antichristi, superbia, vel potius capitis ejus diaboli, qui super omnes filios superbiae regnat, et ab initio per superbiam peccat. Gloriemur igitur, fratres, quia pauperes sumus pro Christo; sed operam demus ut humiles simus cum Christo. Paupere superbo sicut nihil detestabilius, ita nihil miserabilius; cum eum et paupertas nunc affligat, et superbia in perpetuum abdicat. Pauper autem humilis, etsi uritur et purgatur in camino paupertatis, exsultat refrigerio conscientiae divitis: consolatur se promissione sanctae spei, sciens et sentiens quoniam ipsius est regnum Dei; quod jam intra se ipsum velut in semine vel radice gerit, primitias scilicet spiritus, et pignus aeternae haereditatis. Annon vestrum est, fratres, unde toties sicut vobis conscii estis, fructus suavissimos et beata gaudia decerpitis, quorum gustus amarescere vobis jam omnem fecit dulcedinem mundi? Gustastis enim, ni fallor, et vidistis quoniam bona est negotiatio vestra, qui pro re gratis contemnenda ac projicienda, summa comparastis bona. Denique non est regnum Dei esca et potus, sed justitia, et pax, et gaudium in Spiritu sancto (Rom. xiv, 17). Si haec ergo sentimus in nobis, cur non confidenter pronuntiemus intra nos esse regnum Dei? Quod autem intra nos est, illud vere nostrum est, quia nobis invitis eripi non potest."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. For though you know this, brothers, I would still like to remind you that true and blessed poverty of spirit consists more in humility of heart than in the straitness of one's household means; it consists more in the renunciation of pride than in the contempt of substance. Substance is sometimes held to good use; pride is never retained except to one's damnation. The devil possesses nothing in the world, nor desires to possess anything, and yet pride alone, or pride above all, condemns him. It profits little, then, to renounce the possessions of the world unless one also renounces its ways; indeed, it is foolish and ridiculous to be stripped of riches and yet entangled in the vices of the rich \u2014 to become poor in things but not enriched in virtues; to leave all things and yet not follow Christ, but rather, perhaps, while serving in the camp of Christ, to aid the cause of Antichrist. Clearly he aids the cause of Antichrist who serves in the army of pride, and who, while professing the holy name in words or in habit, fights against it by his conduct. The banner of Christ is humility; the banner of Antichrist is pride \u2014 or rather of his head, the devil, who reigns over all the sons of pride, and who from the beginning sins through pride. Let us glory, then, brothers, that we are poor for Christ's sake; but let us take care to be humble with Christ. As nothing is more detestable than a proud poor man, so nothing is more pitiable; for poverty afflicts him now, and pride casts him off forever. But the humble poor man, even though he is burned and purified in the furnace of poverty, exults in the refreshment of a rich conscience; he consoles himself with the promise of holy hope, knowing and feeling that the kingdom of God is his \u2014 which he already bears within himself as in a seed or a root, namely, the firstfruits of the Spirit, and the pledge of the eternal inheritance. Is it not yours, brothers, from which, as you yourselves are conscious, you so often pluck the sweetest fruits and blessed joys, the taste of which has already made all the sweetness of the world bitter to you? For you have tasted, unless I am mistaken, and have seen that your business dealing is good, you who for a thing to be freely despised and cast away have purchased the highest goods. For indeed the kingdom of God is not food and drink, but righteousness, and peace, and joy in the Holy Spirit (Rom. xiv, 17). If, then, we feel these things within ourselves, why should we not confidently declare that the kingdom of God is within us? For what is within us is truly ours, because it cannot be snatched away from us against our will.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Recte itaque Dominus beatitudinem praedicans pauperum, non ait, ipsorum erit, sed, est regnum coelorum: non solum propter jus firmissimum, sed etiam propter pignus certissimum usumque felicissimum; non solum quia paratum est eis ab origine mundi, sed etiam quia jam in quamdam ipsius possessionem coeperunt introduci, jam habentes thesaurum coelestem in vasis fictilibus, jam portantes Deum in corporibus suis et cordibus. Quam beata gens, cujus est Dominus Deus ejus! quam vicini sunt Dei regno qui regem ipsum, cui servire regnare est, jam possident, et gestant in corde suo! Funes, inquit, ceciderunt mihi in praeclaris; etenim haereditas mea praeclara est mihi. Litigent alii de dividenda haereditate hujus mundi: Dominus pars haereditatis meae et calicis mei (Psal. xv, 6, 5). Pugnent inter se quis eorum fiat miserior, nihil omnium quae ambiunt, illis invideo: ego enim et anima mea delectabimur in Domino. O praeclara haereditas pauperum! o beata possessio nihil habentium! quoniam non solum omnem sufficientiam nobis subministras, sed etiam ad omnem gloriam abundas, ad omnem laetitiam redundas, tanquam in sinu reposita supereffluens mensura. Prorsus tecum sunt divitiae et gloria, opes superbae et justitia."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Rightly, then, did the Lord, in proclaiming the blessedness of the poor, not say, 'theirs shall be,' but 'theirs is the kingdom of heaven' \u2014 not only because of the most firm right, but also because of the most certain pledge and the most blessed enjoyment; not only because it has been prepared for them from the foundation of the world, but also because they have already begun to be brought into a certain possession of it, already having the heavenly treasure in earthen vessels, already carrying God in their bodies and hearts. How blessed is the nation whose God is the Lord! How near to the kingdom of God are those who already possess the King himself \u2014 whom to serve is to reign \u2014 and bear him in their own heart! 'The lines,' he says, 'have fallen for me among goodly places; yes, my inheritance is goodly to me.' Let others quarrel over the dividing of the inheritance of this world: 'The Lord is the portion of my inheritance and of my cup' (Psal. xv, 6, 5). Let them fight among themselves over which of them shall become more wretched; I envy them nothing of all they covet, for I and my soul shall take delight in the Lord. O goodly inheritance of the poor! O blessed possession of those who have nothing! For you not only supply us with every sufficiency, but you also overflow for every glory, and run over for every gladness, like an overflowing measure laid up in the lap. Truly with you are riches and glory, glorious riches and righteousness.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Superbiat, o pauperes, superbiat, o humiles, anima vestra, glorians in humilitate sua, omnemque altitudinem hujus mundi longe sub pedibus suis jacentem despiciat; indignumque judicet gloriae suae, ad concupiscentiam vilis praedae suam ulterius majestatem inclinare. Quid? jam jam tollenda es in coelum, et nunc te demum mergeres in coenum? parata tibi sunt aeterna, et praeeligeres transitoria, somnoque similia? te praestolatur curia sanctorum, et praeponeres societatem daemoniorum? Quam miser homo, qui cum in honore esset, non intellexit, ac perinde comparatus est jumentis insipientibus, et similis factus est illis! (Psal. xlviii, 21.) Annon manifeste hoc illis contingit, quos cum beata paupertas fecisset honorabiles coelo, mirabiles mundo, et, ut nihil praeteritum sit, etiam formidabiles inferno: postea arbitrantes miseriam, caecato sensu, paupertatem, humilitatem, ignaviam; voluerunt divites fieri, et inciderunt in tentationes et laqueos diaboli; cumque essent domini universorum, vendiderunt se ipsos sine pretio propter ea quae nihil sunt. Vae his qui perdiderunt sustinentiam, et diverterunt in vias pravas! Et quid facient cum inspicere coeperit Dominus? Ipsi viderint quid facturi sint. Vos enim quibus amica est paupertas, et grata spiritus humilitas, securos fecit de possidendo regno coelorum incommutabilis Veritas: vestrum illud esse asserens, vobis illud repositum fideliter custodiens; si tamen et vos ipsi spem istam in sinu vestro repositam firmiter custodiatis usque in finem, cooperante Domino nostro Jesu Christo, cui est honor et gloria per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Let your soul be proud, O poor; let it be proud, O humble, glorying in its own humility, and let it look down from far above upon all the loftiness of this world lying beneath its feet; let it judge it unworthy of its glory to bend its majesty any further toward the desire for vile prey. What? Are you now, even now, about to be lifted up into heaven, and would you at this very moment sink yourself into the mire? Eternal things are prepared for you, and would you choose instead transitory things, things like a passing dream? The court of the saints awaits you, and would you prefer the company of demons? How wretched is man, who, when he was in honor, did not understand, and so was compared to senseless beasts, and became like them! (Psal. xlviii, 21.) Does not this plainly happen to those whom blessed poverty had made honorable before heaven, admirable before the world, and\u2014that nothing be left unsaid\u2014even fearsome to hell: who afterward, their understanding blinded, judged poverty to be misery, and humility to be cowardice; who wished to become rich, and fell into the temptations and snares of the devil; and though they were lords of all things, sold themselves for no price for the sake of things that are nothing. Woe to those who have lost their endurance, and have turned aside into crooked ways! And what will they do when the Lord begins to examine them? Let them see for themselves what they are to do. But as for you, to whom poverty is a friend, and humility of spirit is dear, the unchangeable Truth has made you secure of possessing the kingdom of heaven: asserting that it is yours, faithfully keeping it stored up for you\u2014if only you yourselves also firmly guard this hope stored up in your bosom even to the end, with the cooperation of our Lord Jesus Christ, to whom be honor and glory through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-n012", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(12) Lectio haec Aug., Bernard. aliisque Patribus familiaris."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-n012", "label": "English", "text": "(12) This reading is familiar to Augustine, Bernard, and other Fathers.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "All Saints Sermon", "topics": ["All Saints", "Beatitudes", "poverty of spirit", "humility", "avarice", "kingdom of heaven"], "url": "sermons/guerric-all-saints.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:annunciation", "calendar_label": "The Annunciation of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-annunciation-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In annuntiatione dominica, sermo I, De gaudio hujus diei et de laudibus Virginis matris, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier freres, 1879), cols. 115-119.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Annunciation", "Annunciation of the Lord", "Proverbs 12:25", "Psalm 118:49, 50 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 9:1 (printed doubled parenthesis retained; see note)", "Luke 1:70 (printed locator; quotation identified as Luke 1:32, see note)", "III Regum 10:18 (historical numbering; modern 1 Kings)", "Matthew 17:1, 2", "III Regum 10:18\u201320 (historical numbering; modern 1 Kings)", "Psalm 131:14 (historical numbering)", "Song of Songs 5:10", "III Regum 10:20 (historical numbering; modern 1 Kings)", "Mark 6:56", "Luke 6:49 (printed locator; quotation identified as Luke 6:19, see note)", "Luke 1:48 (printed Id.; see note)", "Song of Songs 5:14", "Psalm 44:9, 10 (historical numbering; stacte explained in note)", "1 Thessalonians 4:4, 5", "Genesis 27:27", "Song of Songs 4:11", "Song of Songs 3:6 (printed Id.)", "Song of Songs 4:6 (printed Id.)"], "occasion": "In annuntiatione dominica", "occasion_detail": "Annunciation of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Annunciation", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric of Igny presents the Annunciation as spiritual joy within Lenten observance: the angel's promise to Mary announces pardon, redemption and life to those humbled by repentance. He contrasts the fullness of salvation with human ingratitude, then contemplates Mary's royal ancestry and Christ's body through the images of Wisdom's house and Solomon's ivory throne. Mary's virginal chastity leads him to urge chastity joined to humility in his hearers. The fragrance of myrrh and incense becomes an image of bodily continence and prayerful devotion, returning at the close to the Word's descent into Mary's womb. The individual sermon is undated; its collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157 and is read here from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The source names the Annunciation of the Lord."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN ANNUNTIATIONE DOMINICA."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE ANNUNCIATION OF THE LORD.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De gaudio hujus diei et de laudibus Virginis matris."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On the joy of this day and the praises of the Virgin Mother.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Opportune nobis in his diebus Quadragesimalis observantiae solemnitas intervenit Dominicae Annuntiationis, ut qui fatigantur afflictione corporali, gaudio recreari valeant spirituali; et quos humiliavit poenitentiae luctus, annuntiatio illius qui tollit peccata mundi, consoletur. Sic quippe scriptum habes: Moeror in corde viri humiliabit eum; et sermone bono laetificabitur (Prov. xii, 25). Prorsus sermo bonus, sermo fidelis et omni acceptione dignus, Evangelium nostrae salutis; quod angelus missus a Deo, hodierna die Mariae evangelizavit, ac laetum de Incarnatione Verbi, dies diei, angelus Virgini verbum eructavit. Sermo ille, dum Filium promittit Virgini, veniam pollicetur reis, redemptionem captivis, adapertionem clausis, vitamque sepultis. Sermo ille, dum Filii regnum praedicat, justorum quoque gloriam annuntiat; terret inferos, laetificat coelos; et, sicut cognitione mysteriorum, sic etiam novitate gaudiorum perfectionem auxisse videtur Angelorum. Quem ergo non laetificet ille sermo bonus in afflictione sua, quem non consoletur verbum illud in humilitate sua? Memor esto, inquit David, verbi tui servo tuo, in quo mihi spem dedisti; haec enim me consolata est in humilitate mea (Psal. cxviii, 49, 50). Verbum tantum promissionis acceperat, sed nullo adhuc indicio sese effectus prodebat. Affligebat eum dilatio voti, sed consolabatur certitudo spei ex fide promittentis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. The solemnity of the Lord's Annunciation comes at a fitting time for us in these days of Lenten observance, so that those wearied by bodily affliction may be refreshed by spiritual joy, and those whom the sorrow of repentance has humbled may be comforted by the announcement of him who takes away the sins of the world. For you find it written: Sorrow in a man's heart will humble him; and by a good word he will be gladdened (Prov. xii, 25). A good word indeed, a faithful word and worthy of all acceptance, is the Gospel of our salvation, which the angel sent by God proclaimed to Mary on this day; and, as day speaks to day, the angel poured forth to the Virgin a joyful word concerning the Incarnation of the Word. That word, in promising a Son to the Virgin, promises pardon to the guilty, redemption to captives, an opening to those shut in, and life to the buried. That word, in proclaiming the Son's kingdom, announces also the glory of the just; it terrifies the underworld and gladdens the heavens; and it seems to have increased the perfection of the Angels by new joys as well as by knowledge of mysteries. Whom, then, should that good word not gladden in his affliction? Whom should that word not comfort in his lowliness? Remember, says David, your word to your servant, in which you gave me hope; for this has comforted me in my lowliness (Psal. cxviii, 49, 50). He had received only a word of promise, and its fulfillment had not yet shown itself by any sign. The delay of his desire afflicted him, but the certainty of hope, arising from the faithfulness of the one who promised, comforted him.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Si ergo David sola spe salutis hujus, quae nobis servabatur, animum pascebat, quod gaudium quae deliciae, rei ipsius exhibitio nobis esse debuerat? O felicitas temporum istorum! o infelicitas temporum istorum! An non felicitas temporum in quibus tanta plenitudo gratiae et omnium bonorum? an non infelicitas temporum in quibus tanta ingratitudo redemptorum? Ecce enim venit plenitudo temporis, in quo mittit Deus Filium suum, ut fiat Filius hominis hominumque Salvator; et ecce magnitudo temporis, ut Salvatorem suum fastidiat homo peccator. Annuntiatur salus perditis, et contemnunt; promittitur vita desperatis, et negligunt; venit Deus ad homines, et non assurgunt. Assurgit, qui aliqua devotione se erigit ut det gloriam gratiae Dei; assurgit, qui saltem cum gaudio suscipit verbum propriae salutis. Scio autem, scio quis est qui laetificatur illo sermone bono. Ille procul dubio quem prius humiliavit pius moeror in corde suo: moeror de peregrinatione et exsilio, moeror de vinculis mortis et periculis inferni; qui moerens quotidie plangit, quod praeoccupaverint eum laquei mortis, et pericula inferni invenerint. Huic nimirum jucundus advenit hodiernus ille nuntius de coelo, hic gratulabundus excipit sermonem de Dei Filio; huic, inquam, lugenti et moerenti quod tantis sit malis praeventus et circumventus, non sine gaudio liberator annuntiatur; qui oleum gaudii daturus est pro luctu, pallium laudis pro spiritu moeroris; qui videlicet finem dabit miseriis, et sine fine beatitudinem dabit miseris. Beati ergo qui lugent, quoniam ipsi consolabuntur; beati duos humiliavit pius moeror in corde suo, quoniam laetificabuntur sermone bono. Bonus plane sermo et consolatorius omnipotens Sermo tuus, Domine, qui hodie a regalibus sedibus venit in uterum Virginis, ubi etiam sedem sibi regalem exstruxit; in qua cum etiam nunc sedeat, quasi Rex circumstante exercitu Angelorum in coelis, est tamen moerentium consolator in terris."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. If, then, David nourished his soul on the mere hope of this salvation, which was being kept for us, what joy, what delight should the manifestation of the thing itself have been to us! O happiness of these times! O unhappiness of these times! Are they not happy times in which there is such fullness of grace and of every good thing? Are they not unhappy times in which there is such ingratitude among the redeemed? For see, the fullness of time has come, in which God sends his Son to become the Son of man and the Savior of men; and see the enormity of the time, that sinful man should disdain his Savior. Salvation is announced to the lost, and they despise it; life is promised to the despairing, and they neglect it; God comes to men, and they do not rise to greet him. He rises who lifts himself up with some devotion to give glory to God's grace; he rises who at least receives with joy the word of his own salvation. But I know, I know who is gladdened by that good word. It is undoubtedly the man whom devout sorrow has first humbled in his heart: sorrow over his pilgrimage and exile, sorrow over the bonds of death and the perils of hell; the man who mourns and laments daily that the snares of death have overtaken him and the perils of hell have found him. To him that welcome messenger from heaven comes today; he welcomes with rejoicing the word concerning the Son of God. To him, I say, grieving and sorrowing that he has been overtaken and surrounded by such great evils, a deliverer is announced, bringing no little joy: one who will give the oil of gladness in place of mourning, a mantle of praise in place of a spirit of sorrow; one who will put an end to miseries and give the miserable blessedness without end. Blessed, then, are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted; blessed are those whom devout sorrow has humbled in their hearts, for they shall be gladdened by a good word. Truly good and comforting is your almighty Word, Lord, who today came from the royal throne into the Virgin's womb, where he also built himself a royal seat. Although even now he sits upon it as King in heaven, surrounded by the host of Angels, he is nevertheless the comforter of the sorrowful on earth.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Regali siquidem ex progenie Virgo electa est, generositatis quidem regiae nobilis proles, sed virtutis regiae nobilior indoles: ut aeterno Regi, Filio Regis, materna quoque nobilitas regium honorem deferret, et venientem a regali sede Patris, regalis etiam thronus in aula virginali Reginae susciperet Matris. In ipsa quippe et ex ipsa, Sapientia aedificavit sibi domum ((Prov. ix, 1), in ipsa et ex ipsa paravit sibi thronum, cum in ea et ex ea corpus aptavit sibi ita ad omnia perfectum et congruum, ut et domus ei sit ad quiescendum, et thronus ad judicandum, quod primo tabernaculum ei fuit ad pugnandum, et cathedra ad docendum. Vide autem ne forte ipsa sit sedes David patris ejus (Luc. i, 70), quam ei dandam promittit angelus; non quod David in ea sederit, sed quod ex semine David assumpta et fabricata sit. Et certe, si sedes David non est, tamen sedes tua, Deus, in saeculum saeculi ipsa est. Si solium David non est, certe solium excelsum et elevatum super omnem creaturam ipsa est. Si quis autem etiam thronum illum grandem, quem rex Salomon sibi fecit de ebore, corpus ipsum interpretetur, quod Pacificus noster hodie sumpsit ex Virgine, non multum a vero videbitur abhorrere; quandoquidem et illud quod sequitur, et vestivit eum auro fulvo nimis (III Reg. x, 18), eidem satis corpori congruit; quod jam Dominus decorem induit fulgoris intolerabilis. Vestivit, inquit, eum auro fulvo. Prorsus, si interroges oculos Apostolorum qui transfiguratum in monte viderunt (Matth. xvii, 1, 2), confitebuntur aurum nimis esse fulvum, id est, fulgorem corporis esse nimium, quem ferre nequiverunt, quamvis nimis pro Valde ponere consuetudinis sit Scripturarum. Caetera namque quae prosequitur Scriptura de illius throni magnificentia (III Reg. x, 18-20), accommodatius nisi fallor, si quis tractare voluerit, accipientur de corpore Christi, quod est Ecclesia."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. For the Virgin was chosen from royal stock, a noble offspring of royal birth, but nobler still in the character of her royal virtue, so that to the eternal King, the Son of the King, his mother's nobility too might render royal honor, and a royal throne in the virginal court of his Queen Mother might receive him as he came from the Father's royal seat. For in her and from her Wisdom built herself a house ((Prov. ix, 1); in her and from her he prepared himself a throne, when in her and from her he fitted himself with a body so perfect and suitable in every respect that it might be both a house for his rest and a throne for his judgment, having first been a tent for his warfare and a chair for his teaching. Consider, however, whether this may be the seat of David his father (Luc. i, 70), which the angel promises will be given to him: not because David sat upon it, but because it was taken and fashioned from David's seed. And certainly, if it is not David's seat, it is nevertheless your seat, O God, for ever and ever. If it is not David's throne, it is certainly a throne high and lifted up above every creature. But if anyone also interprets that great throne which King Solomon made for himself out of ivory as the very body which our Peacemaker took today from the Virgin, he will seem to be not far from the truth. For the words which follow, and he covered it with exceedingly bright gold (III Reg. x, 18), fit that body well, since the Lord has now clothed it in the beauty of unbearable radiance. He covered it, it says, with bright gold. Indeed, if you question the eyes of the Apostles who saw him transfigured on the mountain (Matth. xvii, 1, 2), they will confess that the gold is exceedingly bright, that is, that the body's radiance is excessive, which they could not endure; although Scripture customarily uses 'excessively' to mean 'very.' For the other things Scripture goes on to say about the magnificence of that throne (III Reg. x, 18-20) will, unless I am mistaken, be more fittingly understood of Christ's body which is the Church, should anyone wish to expound them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Mihi sane magis mirari modo libet illud ebur tam pretiosum, imo et impretiabile, virginalis castimoniae, de quo scilicet sedem sibi facere delegit, qui sedet super Cherubim: Haec, inquiens, requies mea in saeculum saeculi; hic sedebo, quoniam elegi eam (Psal. cxxxi, 14). Quam nitidum ebur illud quod oculis tanti tamque divitis Regis placuit, in cujus diebus argentum nullius est pretii! quam frigidum, quod nec a conceptu incaluit, quam solidum, quod nec partus violavit! quam candidum simul et rubicundum, quod candor lucis aeternae ignisque Spiritus sancti, universa sui plenitudine replevit! Maria siquidem et ipsa candidior nive, rubicundior ebore antiquo; cui scilicet incomparabilem et castitas candorem, et charitas seu martyrium rutilantem prae antiquis electis noscitur contulisse ruborem. Nam et suam ipsius animam pertransivit gladius, ut mater summi Virginis et Martyris esset et virgo ipsa et martyr, candida et rubicunda, quemadmodum dilectus ejus candidus et rubicundus (Cantic. v, 10). Denique, sicut Salomon in omnibus thesauris suis et opibus tantis nihil habuit tam pretiosum, quod in opus illud, magnificum thronum scilicet gloriae suae judicaret ebori praeferendum, sic Maria singularem prae omnibus electis Angelorum et hominum invenit gratiam apud Deum, ut videlicet Dei conciperet et pareret Filium atque ex ebore corporis ejus thronum sibi virtus Altissimi sine manibus excideret gloriosum. Gloriosus prorsus ille thronus ac mirabilis, de quo Scriptura perhibet quia non est factum tale opus in universis regnis (III Reg. x, 20), quod facile testimoniis probaretur Angelorum, qui semper et insatiabiliter in gloriam et decorem Dominici corporis prospicere concupiscunt. Quia enim in universis regnis non est tale opus factum, quidquid factum est, flectat ei genu in omnibus regnis, coelestium, terrestrium, et infernorum. Quam beatus igitur ille venter eburneus, unde caro eburnea sumpta est Redemptoris, pretium animarum, miraculum Angelorum, solium summae majestatis, thronusque potestatis, cibus vitae immortalis, medicina peccati, restitutio sanitatis! Quotquot, inquit, tangebant eum, sanabantur a languoribus suis (Marc. vi, 56): nam virtus de illo exibat, et sanabat omnes (Luc. vi, 49)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. For my part, I would rather marvel now at that precious, indeed priceless, ivory of virginal chastity, from which he who sits above the Cherubim chose to make himself a seat, saying: This is my resting place for ever and ever; here I will sit, for I have chosen it (Psal. cxxxi, 14). How lustrous is that ivory which pleased the eyes of so great and wealthy a King, in whose days silver is of no value! How cool, since even conception did not heat it; how firm, since even childbirth did not violate it! How white and ruddy at once, filled with all the fullness of the brightness of eternal light and the fire of the Holy Spirit! For Mary herself is whiter than snow, ruddier than ancient ivory: chastity is known to have bestowed upon her an incomparable whiteness, and charity, or martyrdom, a glowing redness surpassing that of the elect of old. For a sword passed through her own soul too, so that the mother of the supreme Virgin and Martyr should herself be both virgin and martyr, white and ruddy, just as her beloved is white and ruddy (Cantic. v, 10). Finally, just as Solomon, among all his treasures and such great riches, had nothing so precious that he judged it preferable to ivory for that work, the magnificent throne of his glory, so Mary found singular grace before God above all the elect among Angels and men: namely, that she should conceive and bear the Son of God, and that the power of the Most High should carve for himself, without hands, a glorious throne from the ivory of her body. Glorious indeed and wonderful is that throne, of which Scripture declares that no such work was made in all the kingdoms (III Reg. x, 20). This could readily be proved by the testimony of the Angels, who always and insatiably long to look upon the glory and beauty of the Lord's body. For since no such work has been made in all the kingdoms, let everything that has been made bend the knee to him in all the kingdoms, in heaven, on earth and under the earth. How blessed, then, is that ivory womb, from which the Redeemer's ivory flesh was taken: the price of souls, the wonder of Angels, the seat of supreme majesty and throne of power, the food of immortal life, the medicine for sin, the restoration of health! As many as touched him, it says, were healed of their infirmities (Marc. vi, 56); for power went out from him and healed them all (Luc. vi, 49).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Beatus igitur venter, qui te, Domine Jesu, portavit! felix castitas uteri virginalis, quae huic operi materiam ministravit! Felix prorsus, fratres mei, nitor illius eboris, id est, candor castitatis: cui nec aurum mundanae sapientiae, nec argentum eloquentiae, nec gemmam alicujus excellentis gratiae, nostri praefert electio Salomonis; si modo tamen castitas humilitate commendetur, quia humilitatem ancillae suae respexit Dominus (Id. i, 48). Proinde namque Salomon, cum in carmine amoris castitatem praedicaret virginis, seu sponsae, videlicet seu sponsi (quia quorum spiritus unus et caro una, in plerisque etiam laus una), ut castitatem illius adornatam nostramque adornandam etiam aliis virtutibus doceret, ait Venter ejus eburneus distinctus sapphiris (Cantic. v, 14). Ut autem noveris quia castitas aliarum virtutum seu gratiarum sit receptaculum, apothecae aromaticae seu unguentariae nostri Salomonis non nisi domus eburneae sunt, sicut David in illo epithalamio cecinit, quod inscriptio tituli, Canticum pro dilecto, de Sponso Ecclesiae accipiendum ostendit. Myrrha, inquit, et gutta, et casia a vestimentis tuis, a domibus, eburneis ex quibus delectaverunt te filiae regum in honore tuo (Psal. xliv, 9, 10). Corpora siquidem eburnea sanctorum, domus Christi sunt, vestimentum Christi sunt: membra Christi sunt; templum Spiritus sancti sunt. Ab his vestimentis Christi, ab his domibus eburneis spirat ei omnis fragrantia virtutum et charismatum, ex quibus delectant et honorant Regem regum filiae Apostolorum et Prophetarum. Probastis et ipsi, fratres mei, ex quo coepistis possidere unusquisque vas suum in sanctificatione et honore, non passione desiderii, sicut et gentes quae ignorant Deum (I Thess. iv, 4, 5), quid unguentorum, quid odoramentorum divina largitate sit infusum; quo modo corpus, quod erat sentina sordium, nunc Spiritus sancti sit venerabile sacrarium; et quod erat vorago flagitiorum, nunc quaedam sit apotheca gratiarum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Blessed, then, is the womb that bore you, Lord Jesus! Happy the chastity of the virginal womb which supplied the material for this work! Happy indeed, my brothers, is the luster of that ivory, that is, the whiteness of chastity: our Solomon's choice prefers neither the gold of worldly wisdom nor the silver of eloquence nor the jewel of any excellent grace to it, provided, however, that chastity is commended by humility, for the Lord has looked upon the humility of his handmaid (Id. i, 48). Thus Solomon, when in the song of love he praised the chastity of the virgin, whether of the bride or of the bridegroom, since those who are one spirit and one flesh also share one praise in many things, wished to teach that the chastity of the one is adorned, and ours must be adorned, with other virtues too. He says: His belly is of ivory, set with sapphires (Cantic. v, 14). And so that you may know that chastity is a receptacle for other virtues or graces, our Solomon's storehouses of spices or ointments are nothing but houses of ivory. So David sang in that wedding song which the inscription of its title, A Song for the Beloved, showed was to be understood of the Bridegroom of the Church: Myrrh, he says, and stacte and cassia from your garments, from the ivory houses, from which the daughters of kings have delighted you in your honor (Psal. xliv, 9, 10). For the ivory bodies of the saints are Christ's houses, Christ's clothing, Christ's members and the temple of the Holy Spirit. From these garments of Christ, from these ivory houses, there breathes for him every fragrance of virtues and spiritual gifts, by which the daughters of Apostles and Prophets delight and honor the King of kings. You yourselves too have experienced, my brothers, since each of you began to possess his vessel in sanctification and honor, not in the passion of desire like the nations who do not know God (I Thess. iv, 4, 5), what ointments and perfumes have been poured in by divine bounty; how the body which was a cesspool of filth is now a venerable sanctuary of the Holy Spirit, and what was an abyss of shameful deeds is now a kind of storehouse of graces.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Myrrha, inquit, et gutta, et casia a vestimentis tuis, a domibus eburneis. Inhabitamus quidem domos luteas, sed quae materiae qualitate sunt luteae, continentiae virtute fiunt eburneae; ex quibus si myrrha prima spirare coeperit per mortificationem voluptatum, consequentur et aliae species aromaticae, quae multiformem virtutum gratiam spirabunt. Spirabunt autem Christo delectabiliter, tanquam de vestimentis suis; spirabunt etiam longe lateque proximis, tanquam bonus odor Christi in omni loco. Horum vestimentorum Christi fragrantiam statim ut sensit Isaac: Ecce, inquit, odor filii mei sicut odor agri cui benedixit Dominus (Gen. xxvii, 27). Sed et Sponsus ad sponsam, quae etiam corpus et vestimentum ipsius est, loquitur dicens: Odor vestimentorum tuorum, sicut odor thuris (Cant. iv, 11). Quem nimirum odorem thuris Sponso gratissimum, pietas, o fratres, vestrorum spirabit cordium, si ita fragrans et devota fuerit ut dirigatur oratio vestra sicut incensum in conspectu Altissimi, et ascendat coram eo sicut virgula fumi ex aromatibus myrrhae et thuris (Id. iii, 6). Et quidem myrrha fragrat ei de corporibus vestris; fragrat utique et mihi: utinam ita et thus fragret de cordibus vestris, quatenus Sponsus probans vos sicut castos, sic etiam devotos, frequentius vos sua visitatione dignetur, et dicat: Ibo mihi ad montem myrrhae, et ad collem thuris (Id. iv, 6). Videtur tamen sermo iste, prophetia hujus esse diei, per Salomonem olim praedicta, per Jesum hodie impleta. Hodie namque venit in illum montem altissimum montium, montem non modo myrrhae et thuris, sed et omnium aromatum; dico autem Virginem virginum, omnium plenam gratiarum, inter quas tamen praecipue, nisi fallor, redolebat sponso myrrha castitatis, et thus pietatis. Odor, iste, fratres, mei, super omnia aromata; odor iste Dominum majestatis de excelsis attrahit et invitat, ut inclinet coelos et descendat, sicut probat dies haec, cum Altissimus, angelo misso de coelis, ipse quoque descendit in uterum Matris, qui semper manet in sinu Patris, cum quo vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Myrrh, it says, and stacte and cassia from your garments, from the ivory houses. We do indeed inhabit houses of clay, but those which are clay by the quality of their material become ivory through the virtue of continence. If myrrh first begins to breathe from them through the mortification of pleasures, other kinds of spices will follow, breathing forth the manifold grace of virtues. They will breathe pleasantly for Christ, as from his own garments; they will also breathe far and wide for their neighbors, as the good fragrance of Christ in every place. As soon as Isaac perceived the fragrance of these garments of Christ, he said: Behold, the fragrance of my son is like the fragrance of a field which the Lord has blessed (Gen. xxvii, 27). The Bridegroom too speaks to the bride, who is also his body and his clothing, saying: The fragrance of your garments is like the fragrance of incense (Cant. iv, 11). That fragrance of incense, so pleasing to the Bridegroom, will surely be breathed forth, brothers, by the devotion of your hearts, if it is so fragrant and devout that your prayer is directed like incense before the Most High, and rises before him like a little column of smoke from spices of myrrh and incense (Id. iii, 6). Myrrh does indeed breathe forth for him from your bodies; its fragrance reaches me too. Would that incense might likewise breathe forth from your hearts, so that the Bridegroom, finding you devout as well as chaste, may more often deign to visit you and say: I will go to the mountain of myrrh and to the hill of incense (Id. iv, 6). Yet this saying seems to be a prophecy of this day, foretold long ago through Solomon and fulfilled today through Jesus. For today he came to that mountain, the highest of mountains, a mountain not only of myrrh and incense but of every spice: I mean the Virgin of virgins, full of every grace, among which, unless I am mistaken, the myrrh of chastity and the incense of devotion were especially fragrant to the Bridegroom. This fragrance, my brothers, surpasses every spice; this fragrance draws and invites the Lord of majesty from the heights to bow the heavens and descend, as this day proves, when the Most High, having sent an angel from heaven, himself also descended into his Mother's womb, he who always remains in the Father's bosom, with whom he lives and reigns for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The title and six numbered sections follow the printed edition. A second image of the same source resolves damage in the digitized pages."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites Luke 1:70 for the promise of David\u2019s throne; the promise is in Luke 1:32. It also prints a doubled opening parenthesis before Proverbs 9:1. Both are retained."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites Luke 6:49 for \u201cpower went out from him and healed them all\u201d; the saying is in Luke 6:19. The later Id. i, 48 means Luke 1:48."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Historical III Regum corresponds to modern 1 Kings, and Psalm numbering follows the Latin edition. \u201cStacte\u201d is the traditional name of an aromatic substance; no precise botanical identification is intended. The edition prints duos where the context calls for quos (\u201cthose whom\u201d); the English follows that sense. The imagery of ivory, virginity and martyrdom, bodily chastity and fragrance belongs to Guerric\u2019s argument. The repeated myrrh quotation has no new printed Psalm reference."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Annunciation Sermon I: The Joy of This Day and the Praises of the Virgin Mother", "topics": ["Annunciation", "Incarnation", "Virgin Mary", "joy", "humility", "chastity", "prayer"], "url": "sermons/guerric-annunciation-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:annunciation", "calendar_label": "The Annunciation of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-annunciation-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De verbi incarnatione in Maria et in anima fideli., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 120-124.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Annunciation", "Annunciation of the Lord", "Psalm 84:10 (historical numbering)", "Genesis 3:17\u201318", "Isaiah 16:1", "1 Corinthians 10:4", "Isaiah 45:8", "Ezekiel 44:2", "Isaiah 7:14", "Ezekiel 44:2\u20133", "Song of Songs 5:2", "Song of Songs 8:6", "Isaiah 50:5", "Luke 1:38", "1 Corinthians 6:20", "Romans 10:17", "Malachi 2:16\u201317 (printed locator; quotation from Malachi 2:7)", "Isaiah 26:17\u201318", "Matthew 12:50", "Galatians 4:19", "John 16:21", "Luke 1:45"], "occasion": "In Annuntiatione Dominica", "occasion_detail": "Annunciation of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Annunciation", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric considers the Incarnation in Mary and the formation of Christ in the faithful soul. He connects Mary's hearing and faith with love of God and neighbor, humility, chastity and care for the life of grace. The sermon appears under the Annunciation heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De verbi incarnatione in Maria et in anima fideli."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the incarnation of the Word in Mary and in the faithful soul.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Hodie Verbum caro factum est, et habitare coepit in nobis, secundum regulam scilicet sanae fidei, quam ecclesiasticorum dogmatum definitio tradidit nobis (7). Firmissime namque tenet, et nullatenus dubitat Ecclesia, carnem Christi non prius conceptam quam a Verbo susceptam; sed ipsum Verbum Dei carnis acceptione conceptum, carnemque Verbi incarnatione conceptam. Hodie itaque Sapientia aedificare coepit sibi domum corporis nostri in utero Virginis, et ad aedificandam unitatem Ecclesiae, angularem lapidem de monte sine manibus abscidit; dum sine opere humano de corpore virginali carnem sibi nostrae redemptionis separavit. Ab hoc ergo die Dominus virtutum nobiscum, susceptor noster Deus Jacob, quia hodie Domini est assumptio nostra, ut inhabitet gloria in terra nostra (Psal. LXXXIV, 10). Prorsus hodie benedixisti, Domine, terram tuam, illam benedictam in mulieribus. Hodie dedisti benignitatem Spiritus sancti, ut terra nostra daret benedictum fructum ventris sui, et rorantibus coelis desuper uterus virginalis Salvatorem germinaret. Maledicta terra in opere praevaricatoris, quae etiam exercitata spinas et tribulos germinat haeredibus maledictionis (Gen. III, 17, 18). At nunc benedicta terra in opere Redemptoris, quae remissionem peccatorum fructumque vitae parturit universis, et filiis Adae praejudicium originalis dissolvit maledicti. Prorsus benedicta illa terra, quae omnino intacta, nec fossa, nec seminata, de solo rore coeli Salvatorem germinat, et mortalibus panem Angelorum, alimoniam vitae aeternae ministrat. Haec itaque terra, quia inculta erat, videbatur esse deserta, sed erat opimo fructu referta; videbatur esse eremus solitudinis, sed erat paradisus beatitudinis. Plane hortus deliciarum Dei, eremus cujus campi germinaverunt germen odoris, plane desertum refertum, de quo Agnum dominatorem terrae Pater emisit. Emitte, inquit, Agnum, Domine, de petra deserti (Isai. XVI, 1), id est, abscinde Petram de Petra, Sanctum et inviolabilem sancta et inviolata proferat virginitas. Sane in hoc ipso satis decenter Christi occasus ortui, sepultura respondet conceptioni, dum videlicet emittitur Agnus de petra deserti, condendus in petra monumenti; et cujus corpori monumentum excidendum erat in petra, ipse ab initio conceptus sui, et corpus sibi excidit de petra, et corpori locum in petra; sic integritatem petrae, cum de ea emitteretur, non imminuens, sicut nec signatam sepulturae petram, cum de ea egrederetur, aperiens."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Today the Word was made flesh, and began to dwell in us, according, that is, to the rule of sound faith which the definition of the Church's teachings has handed down to us (7). For the Church holds most firmly, and in no way doubts, that the flesh of Christ was not conceived before it was taken up by the Word; but that the Word of God itself was conceived by the taking of flesh, and the flesh was conceived by the incarnation of the Word. Today, then, Wisdom began to build for herself the house of our body in the womb of the Virgin, and in order to build the unity of the Church, cut from the mountain the corner-stone without hands; while, without human labor, from the virginal body he separated for himself the flesh of our redemption. From this day, therefore, the Lord of hosts is with us, our protector, the God of Jacob, because today the assumption of our nature is the Lord's, so that glory may dwell in our land (Psal. LXXXIV, 10). Today, indeed, O Lord, you have blessed your land, that land blessed among women. Today you gave the kindness of the Holy Spirit, that our land might give forth the blessed fruit of its womb, and that, with the heavens raining down dew from above, the virginal womb might bring forth the Savior. Cursed was the earth in the deed of the transgressor, which even when tilled brings forth thorns and thistles for the heirs of the curse (Gen. III, 17, 18). But now the earth is blessed in the work of the Redeemer, which brings forth for all the remission of sins and the fruit of life, and dissolves for the sons of Adam the sentence of the original curse. Truly blessed is that land which, wholly untouched, neither dug nor sown, from the dew of heaven alone brings forth the Savior, and ministers to mortals the bread of angels, the nourishment of eternal life. This land, then, because it was untilled, seemed to be a desert, but it was filled with rich fruit; it seemed to be a wilderness of solitude, but it was a paradise of blessedness. Truly a garden of God's delights, a wilderness whose fields brought forth the bud of fragrance, truly a desert filled full, from which the Father sent forth the Lamb, ruler of the earth. \"Send forth,\" he says, \"O Lord, the Lamb from the rock of the desert\" (Isai. XVI, 1), that is, cut off the Rock from the Rock; let holy and inviolate virginity bring forth the Holy and Inviolable One. Indeed, in this very thing it is quite fitting that Christ's setting should correspond to his rising, his burial to his conception, since the Lamb is sent forth from the rock of the desert, to be laid in the rock of the tomb; and he whose body was to have a tomb hewn for it in rock, himself from the beginning of his conception both hewed for himself a body from a rock, and a place for his body in a rock; thus not diminishing the integrity of the rock when he was sent forth from it, just as he did not open the sealed rock of the tomb when he came forth from it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Si ergo petra Christus (I Cor. X, 4), ut ait Apostolus, non degenerat a Matre Filius, quando et ipsa petrae nomine censetur. An non recte vocatur petra, quae et in amorem integritatis proposito firma, affectu solida, sensu quoque ipso adversus illecebram peccati tota insensibilis erat et lapidea? an non recte petra virginalis integritas, quae et nihil parit per naturam sui; et cum parit, roris virtute divini, nec admittens conceptum, nec emittens partum, novit aperiri? Sed aperietur terra, inquit Isaias, aperiatur, et germinet Salvatorem (Isai. XLV, 8). O sancte Isaia, qui dicis aperiatur, quid ergo est quod Dominus ad Ezechielem testatur: Porta haec clausa erit, et non aperietur (Ezech. XLIV, 2)? An tibi reseratum fuit tantummodo consilium fecundandae Matris, et clausum tibi fuit mysterium clausae perenniter integritatis? Absit! inquit; nemo magis quam ego conscius hujus secreti. Quomodo enim me latuisset perpetuae virginitatis mysterium, qui tanto ante praedixi: Ecce virgo concipiet, et pariet filium (Isai. VII, 14)? Quaeris igitur quomodo dicamus, ego, aperiatur; ille, non aperietur? Non aperietur viro, aperietur Domino; sicut ibidem ad ipsum dicitur Ezechielem: Vir non transibit per eam, quoniam Dominus Deus Israel ingressus est per eam. Aperietur autem Domino non virginalis integritas corporis, quia, sicut Ezechiel post praedicta subdidit: Haec clausa erit ipsi etiam principi (Ezech. XLIV, 2, 3), sed aperietur auris et porta cordis; quia videlicet intravit per aurem Virginis Verbum incarnandum, et exivit per clausam portam corporis incarnatum. Omnipotens namque Verbum Dei, etsi infirmitatem nostram suscepit, nihil tamen de sua sibi omnipotentia minuit, quominus contra naturam carnis, et supra intelligentiam sensus nostri, corpus suum palpabile per clausa posset et integra trajicere, sicut etiam [ad] discipulos ingrediens januis clausis, oculis probavit, quod ratio non capit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. If then Christ is the Rock (I Cor. X, 4), as the Apostle says, the Son does not degenerate from his Mother, since she too is reckoned by the name of rock. Is she not rightly called rock, who was in the resolve of love for integrity firm, solid in affection, and even in sense itself wholly insensible and stony against the enticement of sin? Is not virginal integrity rightly called rock, which brings forth nothing by its own nature and, when it brings forth by the power of the divine dew, knows no opening either to receive conception or to bring forth the child? But Isaiah says, \"The earth shall be opened; let it be opened and bud forth the Savior\" (Isai. XLV, 8). O holy Isaiah, you who say \"let it be opened,\" what then is it that the Lord testifies to Ezekiel: \"This gate shall be shut, and shall not be opened\" (Ezech. XLIV, 2)? Was the plan for making the Mother fruitful revealed to you alone, while the mystery of her perpetually closed integrity was hidden from you? \"Far from it!\" he says; \"no one is more conscious of this secret than I. For how could the mystery of perpetual virginity have escaped me, who so long before foretold: 'Behold, a virgin shall conceive and bear a son' (Isai. VII, 14)?\" You ask, then, how we can say, I, \"let it be opened\"; he, \"it shall not be opened\"? It shall not be opened to man, it shall be opened to the Lord; just as it is said there to Ezekiel himself: \"No man shall pass through it, because the Lord God of Israel has entered through it.\" But it is not the virginal integrity of the body that is opened to the Lord, because, as Ezekiel adds after the foregoing: \"This shall be shut even to the prince himself\" (Ezech. XLIV, 2, 3); rather, it is the ear and the gate of the heart that are opened; because, namely, the Word to be incarnated entered through the ear of the Virgin, and came forth incarnate through the closed gate of the body. For the almighty Word of God, although he took up our weakness, nevertheless diminished nothing of his omnipotence, so that, contrary to the nature of flesh and beyond the understanding of our sense, he could pass his palpable body through closed and intact things, just as also, entering [to] the disciples through closed doors, he proved by sight what reason cannot grasp.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Secura igitur, o Virgo intemerata, o porta sanctuarii perenniter clausa, secura aperi Domino Deo Israel, qui tibi jam dudum clamat: Aperi mihi, soror mea, amica mea (Cantic. V, 2). Non est quod timeas integritati tuae: Deus non novit integra violare, sed violata consolidare. Si Verbo Dei apta es, tum demum non modo clausa, sed et signata es. Pone me, inquit, ut signaculum super cor tuum, ut signaculum super brachium tuum (Id. VIII, 6). Jesus quippe impressus in corde, expressus in opere, sigillum plane et munimentum est inviolabile castitatis sponsae suae, et eo ipso quo se imprimit imitationis formam, imponit etiam incorruptionis custodiam. Sit ergo, o Virgo fidelis, auris tua aperta ad audiendum et mens tua ad credendum: aure percipe verbum angeli, et corde suscipe Verbum Altissimi, et corpore concipe Filium Dei. Dic et tu, o beata, tam humilis quam fidelis, dic Dominus aperuit mihi aurem; ego autem non contradico, retrorsum non abii (Isai. L, 5). Ecce ancilla Domini: praesto sum ejus voluntati; quinimo juvabo votis si possum, fiat mihi secundum verbum tuum (Luc. I, 38). Hoc dicere, sic suam devotionem offerre, istud plane est Domino pectus aperire, istud est etiam os aperire, et spiritum attrahere. Sic profecto aperiebatur terra, ut, quem rorabant coeli desuper, susciperet rorem, et germinaret Salvatorem. Nobile germen, germen justum, germen odoris, germen Domini, quod jam est in magnificentia et gloria, cum idem fructus terrae sub imis est super omnia, id est, exaltatus est super coelos Deus, et super omnem terram gloria ejus. Nihil enim interest sive germen, sive fructum aut florem dixeris, cum haec omnia, imo infinita alia unus Christus sit, res una, sed multiformis gratia, et virtutis unius operatio multifaria; cui si infinita nomina paupertas humani sensus et sermonis pro similitudine commodaverit, rei tamen implere significationem non poterit. Bene autem simul vocatur germen, et flos, et fructus, qui sine gradu profectus, ab initio conceptus sui omni virtute et gratia in se ipso exstitit perfectus. In nobis enim primum ipse germen est, cum fides in confessionem prorumpit vel opus manifestum: postmodum flos, cum super proficientem efflorescit sanctificatio Dei quodam spectabili decore virtutum; demum autem fructus, cum beatitudo satiat hominem consummatum. Pulcherrime sane ac vigilantissime non solum mysteria, sed et mysteriorum praesagia dispensatio praeordinavit divina, ut locus in quo terra germinavit Salvatorem germen justum, et flos de virga et radice Jesse ascendit, Nazareth, id est sanctitas, germen, flos, virgultum vocaretur; ut videlicet negotium loco, locusque negotio consona voce contestaretur, pariterque et nomen loci rem gerendam pronuntiaret, et res gesta causam nominis indicaret."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Therefore, without fear, O undefiled Virgin, O gate of the sanctuary closed forever, without fear open to the Lord God of Israel, who has long been crying out to you: 'Open to me, my sister, my love' (Cantic. V, 2). You have no reason to fear for your integrity: God does not know how to violate what is whole, but to make whole what is violated. If you are fitted to the Word of God, then you are not only closed, but also sealed. 'Set me,' he says, 'as a seal upon your heart, as a seal upon your arm' (Id. VIII, 6). For Jesus, impressed upon the heart, expressed in deed, is plainly the seal and the inviolable safeguard of the chastity of his bride; and by the very fact that he impresses himself as a pattern for imitation, he also imposes upon her the guardianship of incorruption. Let your ear, then, O faithful Virgin, be open to hear, and your mind to believe: with your ear perceive the word of the angel, with your heart receive the Word of the Most High, and in your body conceive the Son of God. Say, too, O blessed one, as humble as you are faithful, say: 'The Lord has opened my ear; but I do not contradict, I have not drawn back' (Isai. L, 5). 'Behold the handmaid of the Lord: I am ready for his will; indeed I will even assist his purposes if I can \u2014 be it done to me according to your word' (Luc. I, 38). To say this, to offer one's devotion in this way, is plainly to open one's breast to the Lord; it is also to open one's mouth and draw in the Spirit. Thus indeed was the earth opened, so that it might receive as dew him whom the heavens rained down from above, and bring forth the Savior as its sprout. A noble sprout, a righteous sprout, a sprout of fragrance, the sprout of the Lord, who is now in magnificence and glory, since that same fruit of the earth, though from the lowest depths, is above all things \u2014 that is, God has been exalted above the heavens, and his glory above all the earth. For it makes no difference whether you call him sprout, or fruit, or flower, since all these things \u2014 indeed, countless others besides \u2014 are one Christ, one reality, but grace of many forms, and the working of one power in many ways; and to him, even if the poverty of human understanding and speech lends countless names by way of likeness, it can never fulfill the full meaning of the reality. Rightly, however, is he called at once sprout, and flower, and fruit, who, without any gradual advance, existed perfect in himself in every virtue and grace from the very beginning of his conception. For in us, he is first a sprout, when faith breaks forth into confession or into a manifest work; afterward a flower, when the sanctification of God blossoms upon the one who is advancing, with a certain visible beauty of virtues; and finally a fruit, when beatitude satisfies the person brought to completion. Most beautifully indeed, and most watchfully, divine providence foreordained not only the mysteries themselves but also the foreshadowings of the mysteries, so that the place in which the earth brought forth the Savior, the righteous sprout, and where the flower arose from the rod and root of Jesse, should be called Nazareth \u2014 that is, 'holiness,' 'sprout,' 'flower,' 'shoot' \u2014 so that the event might bear witness to the place, and the place to the event, with a single harmonious voice, and that likewise the name of the place might proclaim the deed to be accomplished, and the deed accomplished might reveal the reason for the name.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Ignoscite mihi, fratres mei, quia, qui morum vestrorum servire debueram instructioni, plus forsitan quam necesse erat demoratus sum in illius ineffabilis admiratione et praeconio mysterii. Quid enim mirum si miror quod stupent Angeli, si praedicare gestio, quam coeli enarrant, gloriam Dei? quid mirum si me delectat quod beatos spiritus laetificat, quod peccatores justificat, justosque glorificat? Nescio tamen si ulla esse possit efficacior ac suavior morum aedificatio, quam hujus mysterii, id est Verbi incarnati, fidelis et pia consideratio. Quid enim sic hominem excitare potest ad amorem Dei, quam praeveniens hominem amor Dei tam vehemens ad hominem, ut homo propter hominem velit fieri? Quid autem ita nutrit amorem proximi, quam similitudo et natura proximi in humanitate Dei? Nam humilitatis exemplum nec cogitari posse majus aliquod existimo, quam exinanitionem Dei in formam servi, et servitutem plus quam servi. Castitatem porro quid aeque commendat, sicut quod ipsa castitas Salvatorem germinat? Aut fidei virtutem ac meritum quid evidentius ostendit, quam quod Virgo Deum fide concepit, fide omnia sibi promissa divinitus impleri promeruerit? Beata, inquit, quae credidit, quoniam perficientur quae dicta sunt ei a Domino (ibid. 45). Et ut plenius noveris conceptum Virginis non solum esse mysticum, sed et moralem; quod sacramentum est ad redemptionem, exemplum quoque tibi est ad imitationem; ut manifeste evacues in te gratiam sacramenti, si non imiteris virtutem exempli. Quae enim Deum fide concepit, si fidem habeas, idem tibi promittit, quod videlicet si verbum ex ore nuntii coelestis fideliter velis suscipere, Deum quem totus orbis non potest capere, possis et ipse concipere. Concipere autem corde, imo et corpore, licet non corporali opere aut specie, tamen plane corpore tuo; quandoquidem jubemur ab Apostolo glorificare et portare Deum in corpore nostro (I Cor. VI, 20). Attende itaque, ut scriptum est, diligenter auditui tuo, nam fides ex auditu, auditus autem per Verbum Dei (Rom. X, 17); quod tibi absque dubio evangelizat angelus Dei, cum de timore aut amore ejus tecum agit praedicator fidelis, quem angelum Domini exercituum dici et esse non est tibi fas ambigere (Malach. II, 16, 17). Quam beati qui dicere possunt: A timore tuo, Domine, concepimus et parturivimus spiritum salutis (Isa. XXVI, 17, 18)! qui nimirum non est alius quam spiritus Salvatoris, quam veritas Jesu Christi. Vide ineffabilem dignationem Dei, simulque virtutem incomprehensibilis mysterii: qui creavit te, creatur in te; et, quasi parum esset te ipsum habere patrem, vult etiam te sibi fieri matrem. Quicumque, inquit, fecerit voluntatem patris mei, ipse meus et frater, et soror, et mater est (Matth. XII, 50). O fidelis anima, expande sinus, dilata affectus; ne angustieris in visceribus tuis, concipe quem creatura non capit. Aperi Verbo Dei aurem audiendi: haec est ad uterum cordis via spiritus concipiendi, hac ratione compinguntur ossa Christi, id est, virtutes in ventre praegnantis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Forgive me, my brothers, because I, who ought to have served the instruction of your morals, have perhaps lingered longer than was necessary in admiration and praise of that ineffable mystery. For what wonder is it if I marvel at what makes the Angels stand amazed, if I am eager to proclaim the glory of God which the heavens declare? What wonder is it if I am delighted by what gladdens the blessed spirits, what justifies sinners, and glorifies the just? Yet I do not know whether there can be any more effective or sweeter edification of morals than the faithful and pious consideration of this mystery, that is, of the Word incarnate. For what can so arouse man to the love of God as the love of God going before man, so vehement toward man that he willed to become man for man's sake? And what so nourishes love of neighbor as the likeness and nature of a neighbor found in the humanity of God? For I judge that no greater example of humility can even be conceived than the emptying of God into the form of a servant, and a servitude greater than that of a servant. Furthermore, what commends chastity so much as the fact that chastity itself brings forth the Savior as its offspring? Or what shows the power and merit of faith more clearly than that the Virgin conceived God by faith, and by faith merited that all things divinely promised to her should be fulfilled? Blessed, he says, is she who believed, for the things that were spoken to her by the Lord shall be fulfilled (ibid. 45). And that you may know more fully that the Virgin's conception is not only mystical but also moral \u2014 what is a sacrament for redemption is also an example for you to imitate \u2014 so that you plainly render void in yourself the grace of the sacrament, if you fail to imitate the virtue of the example. For she who conceived God by faith promises you the same thing, if you have faith: namely, that if you are willing to receive faithfully the word from the mouth of the heavenly messenger, you too can conceive the God whom the whole world cannot contain. And to conceive \u2014 in heart, indeed also in body, though not by bodily act or bodily form, yet truly in your body \u2014 since we are commanded by the Apostle to glorify and to carry God in our body (I Cor. VI, 20). Attend, therefore, as it is written, diligently to your hearing, for faith comes from hearing, and hearing through the word of God (Rom. X, 17); which without doubt the angel of God announces to you as gospel, when a faithful preacher deals with you concerning fear or love of him \u2014 and you may not rightly doubt that he is rightly called, and truly is, the angel of the Lord of hosts (Malach. II, 16, 17). How blessed are they who can say: From fear of you, O Lord, we have conceived and brought forth the spirit of salvation (Isa. XXVI, 17, 18)! \u2014 which indeed is none other than the spirit of the Savior, the truth of Jesus Christ. Behold the ineffable condescension of God, and at the same time the power of this incomprehensible mystery: he who created you is created in you; and, as though it were not enough for him to have you yourself as a father, he wills also that you become a mother to him. Whoever, he says, does the will of my Father, he is my brother, and sister, and mother (Matth. XII, 50). O faithful soul, open wide your bosom, enlarge your affections; do not be straitened in your inmost being \u2014 conceive him whom no creature can contain. Open to the Word of God the ear of hearing: this is the way to the womb of the heart for conceiving the spirit; by this means the bones of Christ \u2014 that is, the virtues \u2014 are knit together in the womb of her who is pregnant.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Gratias tibi, sancte Spiritus, qui ubi vis spiras. Video munere tuo non unam, sed innumeras fidelium animas illo generoso germine gravidas; custodi opera tua, ne aliqua illarum abortiat, conceptumque divinae sobolis informem aut mortuum excutiat. Vos quoque, o matres beatae tam gloriosae prolis, attendite vobis ipsis, donec formetur Christus in vobis (Galat. IV, 19); attendite, ne qua foris offensa gravior fetum tenerum laedat, ne quid ingeratis ventri, id est menti, quod spiritum quem concepistis exstinguat. Parcite, si non vobis, certe Filio Dei in vobis; parcite, inquam, non solum ab operibus et sermonibus malis, sed etiam a cogitationibus noxiis et delectationibus mortiferis, quae plane suffocant semen Dei. Omni itaque custodia servate corda vestra, quia ex ipsis vita procedet, cum scilicet maturus se partus absolvet, et vita Christi, quae nunc abscondita est in cordibus vestris, manifestabitur in carne vestra mortali. Concepistis spiritum salutis, sed adhuc parturitis, nondum peperistis. Si labor in parturiendo, magna de spe partus consolatio. Mulier cum parit, tristitiam habet (Joan. XVI, 21) laboris; cum autem pepererit puerum, jam non memor erit pressurae propter gaudium, quia natus erit homo Christus in mundum exteriorem corporis nostri, quod et minor mundus solet appellari. Qui enim nunc conceptus est Deus in spiritibus nostris, configurans eos spiritui charitatis suae, tunc velut homo nascetur in corporibus, configurans ea corpori claritatis suae, in qua vivit et gloriatur Deus per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Thanks be to you, Holy Spirit, who breathe where you will. I see, by your gift, not one but countless souls of the faithful made heavy with that noble offspring; guard your works, lest any of them miscarry, and cast out the conceived offspring of divine progeny unformed or dead. You too, O blessed mothers of so glorious a child, attend to yourselves, until Christ be formed in you (Galat. IV, 19); take heed lest some graver offense from without injure the tender offspring, lest you introduce into the womb \u2014 that is, into the mind \u2014 anything that might extinguish the spirit you have conceived. Spare \u2014 if not yourselves, then certainly the Son of God within you; spare, I say, not only from evil deeds and words, but also from harmful thoughts and deadly pleasures, which utterly suffocate the seed of God. Keep your hearts, therefore, with all vigilance, for from them life shall proceed, when, that is, the mature birth is brought to completion, and the life of Christ, which is now hidden in your hearts, shall be made manifest in your mortal flesh. You have conceived the spirit of salvation, but you are still in labor; you have not yet given birth. If there is labor in giving birth, there is great consolation in the hope of the birth. A woman, when she gives birth, has sorrow (Joan. XVI, 21) of her labor; but when she has borne the child, she no longer remembers the anguish, for joy, because the man Christ shall have been born into the outer world of our body, which is also commonly called the lesser world. For he who is now conceived as God in our spirits, conforming them to the spirit of his charity, shall then, as it were, be born as man in our bodies, conforming them to the body of his glory, in which God lives and is glorified through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-n007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(7) Scilicet Gennadius."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-n007", "label": "English", "text": "(7) Namely, Gennadius.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The description of the priest as the messenger of the Lord of hosts comes from Malachi 2:7; the edition prints Malach. II, 16, 17."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition supplies ad in brackets before discipulos, in the comparison with Christ entering through closed doors."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Annunciation Sermon II", "topics": ["Annunciation", "Incarnation", "Virgin Mary", "faith", "charity", "humility", "chastity", "Holy Spirit", "spiritual growth"], "url": "sermons/guerric-annunciation-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:annunciation", "calendar_label": "The Annunciation of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-annunciation-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., De hujus mysterii signis., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 124-128.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Annunciation", "Annunciation of the Lord", "Isaiah 7:13\u201314", "Jeremiah 31:22", "Psalm 66:6 (printed locator; quotation from Psalm 66:6-7, historical numbering)", "Isaiah 7:10\u201312", "Matthew 16:4", "Isaiah 1:4", "Psalm 85:17 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 7:14", "Luke 1:31", "1 Corinthians 1:18", "Ephesians 4:9\u201310", "Luke 1:30\u201331 (printed locator; quotation from Luke 1:37)", "Isaiah 30:15", "Isaiah 32:17", "Ecclesiastes 11:5", "Psalm 138:15 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 25:28", "Ezekiel 44:3", "Psalm 31:14 (printed locator; quotation from Psalm 131:14, historical numbering)", "3 Kings 10:18 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Kings)", "Revelation 3:20", "John 6:64", "Matthew 4:4", "John 5:26", "Luke 22:15, 19", "Genesis 25:22", "Luke 3:8"], "occasion": "In Annuntiatione Dominica", "occasion_detail": "Annunciation of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Annunciation", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads Isaiah's sign of the Virgin through the Incarnation, Passion and exaltation of Christ. His argument includes polemic against Jewish interpretations of the prophecy. He turns from Christ's hidden life in the womb to monastic silence, nourishment by the divine Word and patience within the Church. The sermon appears under the Annunciation heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De hujus mysterii signis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the signs of this mystery.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Audite, domus David: ecce dabit Dominus ipse vobis signum. Ecce virgo concipiet (Isai. VII, 13, 14). Hodie impleta est haec prophetia in auribus vestris. Hodie namque illud ineffabile conceptus virginalis miraculum, sicut audieratis promissum, sic audistis impletum: Virgo hodie concepit. Et hoc signum, prioribus retro saeculis inauditum, nostris saeculis Dominus dedit. Et sicut Jeremias eodem spiritu et eodem sensu praedixit: Creabit Dominus novum super terram, quia femina circumdabit virum (Jerem. XXXI, 22). Quid est enim, creabit Dominus novum super terram, femina circumdabit virum, quod ait Jeremias, nisi quod ait Isaias: Dabit Dominus signum, Virgo concipiet filium (Isai. VII, 13, 14)? Hoc nempe est, circumdabit. Non de consortio virili suscipiet, sed de se sola intra semetipsam concipiet, et solius materni corporis involucro vestiet. Alioquin dicant, si possunt Judaei, quod signum Dominus in hoc dedit, si non virgo, sed adolescentula, ut ipsi falso interpretantur, concepit; aut quid novum Dominus creavit, si femina masculum de masculo susceptum circumdedit. Potest quidem mentiri iniquitas sibi; sed in multitudine virtutis tuae, o Domine, mentientur tibi inimici tui, ut virtutem tuam dum negat pravitas et paucitas Judaeorum, gloriosius et copiosius confiteatur fides omnium populorum. Confiteantur tibi populi, Deus, confiteantur tibi populi omnes, quia terra dedit fructum suum, Virgo germinavit Jesum (Psal. LXVI, 6). Velint, nolint Judaei, creavit Dominus novitatem hujus miraculi, in signum eorum incredulitatis; utique in signum cui contradicunt usque hodie, plus, nisi fallor, pertinacia odibili, quam ignorantia miserabili."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Hear, O house of David: behold, the Lord himself will give you a sign. Behold, a virgin shall conceive (Isai. VII, 13, 14). Today this prophecy has been fulfilled in your hearing. For today that ineffable miracle of virginal conception, just as you had heard it promised, so you have heard it fulfilled: today a Virgin has conceived. And this sign, unheard of in former ages, the Lord has given to our age. And just as Jeremiah foretold in the same spirit and with the same meaning: The Lord will create something new upon the earth, for a woman shall encompass a man (Jerem. XXXI, 22). For what is it that Jeremiah says, \"The Lord will create something new upon the earth, a woman shall encompass a man,\" except what Isaiah says: \"The Lord will give a sign, a Virgin shall conceive a son\" (Isai. VII, 13, 14)? This indeed is what \"encompass\" means: she will not conceive through union with a man, but will conceive within herself alone, from herself alone, and will clothe him solely in the wrapping of a maternal body. Otherwise, let the Jews say, if they can, what sign the Lord gave in this, if it was not a virgin but a young woman who conceived, as they falsely interpret it; or what new thing the Lord created, if a woman merely enclosed within herself a male conceived from a male. Iniquity can indeed lie to itself; but in the multitude of your power, O Lord, your enemies will lie to you, so that while the wickedness and the small number of the Jews deny your power, the faith of all peoples may confess it more gloriously and abundantly. Let the peoples confess to you, O God, let all the peoples confess to you, for the earth has given its fruit, the Virgin has brought forth Jesus (Psal. LXVI, 6). Whether the Jews wish it or not, the Lord has created the novelty of this miracle as a sign of their unbelief; indeed as a sign which they contradict even to this day, more, unless I am mistaken, out of hateful obstinacy than out of pitiable ignorance.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Non alienum tamen ab origine et consuetudine viperei germinis, si modo contradicunt huic signo, postquam factum est, qui ab initio in patre et rege suo impiissimo Achaz contradixerunt ne fieret. Locutus est enim Dominus ad Achaz, dicens: Pete tibi signum. Non petam, inquit, et non tentabo Dominum (Isai. VII, 10-12). O religio profana! o pietas exsecranda! o humilitas dolosa! Ne tentes, ut ais, Dominum, contemnis Dominum. Quomodo enim tentares, si fideliter obedires? Nunc autem quomodo non graviter tentas, quem manifesto contemptu irritas? Novimus utique, novimus dolum et invidiam Judaicae radicis, quae et antequam Christus nasceretur, invidere coepit gloriae illius. Nam et iste Achaz, quantum ex vita et moribus ipsius intelligi datur (nam cultor idolorum erat), non alia religione aut metu signum recusavit petere cum juberetur, nisi ideo scilicet ne Dominus glorificaretur. Sane mira et plane digna ira Dei et hominum, tam dira perversitas Judaeorum, qui, cum jubentur signa quaerere, renuunt, non, ut fingunt, ne tentent, sed ne glorificent Dominum (Matth. XVI, 4); cum non jubentur, tentant et quaerunt. Nam velut natura et usu Judaei signa quaerunt, et, si dantur, calumniantur et inficiari contendunt, ut manifeste confiteantur quia tentantes quaerebant, non ut crederent facienti, sed ut insultarent non facienti. O gens peccatrix; semen nequam, filii scelerati (Isai. I, 4)! ait Isaias, Numquid parum est vobis molestos esse hominibus, mihi et aliis prophetis, imo universo generi hominum, quia molesti estis et Deo meo? Propter hoc dabit Dominus ipse vobis signum (Isa. VII, 13, 14). Quia enim vos in perversum adversum eum inceditis, et ipse, ut ait, perversus incedet vobis. Non vultis signum dari, ne glorificetur auctor miraculi; et ipse propter hoc dabit, ut ipse glorificetur, et vos confundamini. Fac, Domine, fac mecum signum in bonum, ut videant qui me oderunt, et confundantur (Psal. LXXXV, 17), ait de Judaeis ad Patrem Filius."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Yet it is not foreign to the origin and custom of the viperous brood, if they now contradict this sign after it has been accomplished, seeing that from the beginning they contradicted in their father and most impious king Ahaz that it should ever come to pass. For the Lord spoke to Ahaz, saying: Ask for yourself a sign. I will not ask, he said, and I will not tempt the Lord (Isai. VII, 10-12). O profane religion! O execrable piety! O deceitful humility! When you say, \"Lest I tempt the Lord,\" you are despising the Lord. For how would you be tempting him, if you obeyed faithfully? But now, how are you not gravely tempting him, whom you provoke by manifest contempt? We know indeed, we know the deceit and envy of the Jewish root, which even before Christ was born began to envy his glory. For this Ahaz too, as far as can be understood from his life and character (for he was a worshiper of idols), refused to ask for a sign when commanded, not out of any religious scruple or fear, but precisely so that the Lord might not be glorified. Truly wondrous, and plainly deserving the wrath of God and of men, is such dire perversity of the Jews, who, when commanded to seek signs, refuse \u2014 not, as they pretend, lest they tempt God, but lest they glorify the Lord (Matth. XVI, 4); and when not commanded, they tempt and seek. For as if by nature and custom the Jews seek signs, and, if given, they slander them and strive to deny them, so that they plainly confess that in tempting they sought not in order to believe in the one who performed the sign, but in order to insult the one who did not perform it. O sinful nation, wicked seed, corrupt children (Isai. I, 4)! says Isaiah. Is it too little for you to be troublesome to men \u2014 to me and to other prophets, indeed to the whole human race \u2014 that you are troublesome also to my God? Therefore the Lord himself will give you a sign (Isa. VII, 13, 14). For because you walk perversely against him, he too, as he says, will walk perversely toward you. You do not wish a sign to be given, lest the author of the miracle be glorified; and for this very reason he will give it, that he himself may be glorified, and you confounded. Work, O Lord, work with me a sign for good, that those who hate me may see it and be confounded (Psal. LXXXV, 17), says the Son to the Father concerning the Jews.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Primum autem signum quod Pater et Filius operati sunt in confusionem infidelium et gloriam fidelium, in testimonium suae virtutis et opus nostrae salutis, arbitror hodiernum esse Virginis conceptum. Nam de ipso cum praemissum esset, dabit Dominus signum, quasi quaerentibus quod signum, intulit contextus Prophetae, verbis tamen Evangelistae, Ecce virgo concipiet et pariet filium (Isai. VII, 14, et Luc. I, 31); ut plane aut consequentia vel veritate contextus careat, aut (quod est probabilius) mendacium Judaeorum latebram non habeat. Merito proinde cum jam generatio prava et adultera signum quaerit, signum non datur ei, nisi signum Jonae (Matth. XVI, 4); ut videlicet qui ob perversitatem mentis non aedificantur signo virtutis, scandalizentur signo infirmitatis, triduanae scilicet sepulturae et mortis. Nam verbum crucis et mortis Judaeis, qui pereunt, scandalum est; his autem qui salvi fiunt, id est, nobis, virtus Dei est (I Cor. I, 18); nec minor aut infirmior nobis est Filius hominis in corde terrae, quam in consessu paternae dexterae. Signum quippe quod illi recusaverunt, sive in profundum inferni, sive in excelsum supra, nos fide plena ac veneratione devota suscipimus, agnoscentes Filium quem concipit Virgo, in profundo inferni signum nobis esse liberationis et veniae; in excelsum supra, signum et spem exsultationis et gloriae. Qui enim primum descendit in inferiores partes terrae, ut in sanguine testamenti sui extraheret vinctos de lacu in quo non est aqua, ipse est et qui ascendit super omnes coelos, ut adimpleret omnia (Ephes. IV, 9, 10). Jam levavit Dominus signum primum in patibulo crucis, postmodum in throno regni, et exaltavit signum ad populos nationum, quia contradictum est ei a populo Judaeorum, et quotidie a quatuor ventis congregat dispersos veri Israel ad hoc signum. O Radix Jesse, qui stas in signum populorum, super quem jam continent reges os suum, obstruatur etiam os loquentium iniqua, id est, Judaeorum blasphemantium, qui adhuc signo immaculato conceptus tui contradicunt, et nec angelo Gabrieli credunt dicenti quia non est impossibile apud te omne verbum (Luc. I, 30, 31). Beata quae credidit, cui haec ratio satisfecit, et, cum quaesisset quomodo Filium susciperet, quae non cognosceret virum, tam de integritate quam de prole securam deinceps effecit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. The first sign which the Father and the Son worked for the confusion of unbelievers and the glory of the faithful, in testimony of their power and as the work of our salvation, I judge to be the Son's conception in the Virgin today. For concerning it, when it had been foretold, \"The Lord shall give a sign,\" as though to those asking what that sign might be, the continuation of the Prophet added\u2014though in the words of the Evangelist\u2014\"Behold, a virgin shall conceive and bear a son\" (Isai. VII, 14, et Luc. I, 31); so that plainly either the context lacks consequence or truth, or (which is more probable) the lie of the Jews finds no hiding place. Fittingly, then, since this crooked and adulterous generation now seeks a sign, no sign is given it except the sign of Jonah (Matth. XVI, 4); so that those who, on account of the perversity of their mind, are not built up by the sign of power, may be scandalized by the sign of weakness\u2014namely, of the three-day burial and death. For the word of the cross and of death is to the Jews who perish a stumbling block; but to us who are being saved it is the power of God (I Cor. I, 18); nor is the Son of Man any less or weaker for us in the heart of the earth than seated at the Father's right hand. For the sign which they refused, whether in the depth of hell or on high above, we receive with full faith and devout veneration, acknowledging that the Son whom the Virgin conceives is, in the depth of hell, a sign to us of liberation and pardon; and on high above, a sign and hope of exultation and glory. For he who first descended into the lower parts of the earth, that in the blood of his testament he might draw out the bound from the pit in which there is no water, is also he who ascended above all the heavens, that he might fill all things (Ephes. IV, 9, 10). The Lord has already raised up his first sign on the gibbet of the cross, afterward on the throne of the kingdom, and he has exalted the sign to the peoples of the nations, because it was contradicted by the people of the Jews, and daily he gathers from the four winds the scattered of the true Israel to this sign. O Root of Jesse, who stand as a sign of the peoples, before whom kings now keep their mouths shut, let the mouth of those who speak iniquity also be stopped\u2014that is, of the Jews who blaspheme, who still contradict the immaculate sign of your conception, and do not even believe the angel Gabriel saying that with you no word is impossible (Luc. I, 30, 31). Blessed is she who believed, for whom this reasoning was satisfying, and who, when she had asked how she should receive a Son, she who knew not man, was thereafter made secure both as to her integrity and as to her offspring.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Quidquid ergo garriat impietas infidelium, nobis concipiat et pariat Virgo Filium; nos quippe signum habemus in bonum, etiam Matrem et Filium. Nobis plane Mater tota miraculum est, quae singulariter et sine exemplo mater est et virgo; nobis Filius totus miraculum, qui non solum singulariter, sed incomprehensibiliter Deus est et homo. Mater, virgo concipiens et pariens, signum nobis est quia Deus est homo iste qui concipitur et paritur; Filius, divina faciens, et humana patiens, signum nobis est quia in Deum transferet hominem, pro quo concipitur et paritur, etiam et patitur. Omnium tamen humanarum infirmitatum vel injuriarum quas pro nobis pertulit divina dignatio, sicut tempore primam, sic etiam humilitate fere maximam existimo, quod in utero concipi, in utero novem mensium tempore majestas illa incircumscripta passa est contineri. Ubi enim sic se exinanivit? aut quando ita penitus a semetipso defecisse visus fuit? Tanto tempore nihil illa sapientia loquitur, nihil illa virtus manifestum operatur, nullo signo visibili majestas, quae clausa latet, proditur. Non sic in cruce visus est infirmus, ubi quod infirmum ipsius fuit, statim apparuit fortius omnibus hominibus: quando et moriens glorificat latronem, et exspirans inspirat Centurionem; quando horarius dolor passionis ei non solum compati fecit elementa creaturarum, sed etiam contrarias fortitudines subigit aeternorum passioni dolorum. In utero autem sic est quasi non sit, sic omnipotens virtus vacat quasi nihil possit et Verbum aeternum sub silentio se premit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Whatever, then, the impiety of unbelievers may chatter, let the Virgin conceive and bear a Son for us; for we indeed have a sign for good, even the Mother and the Son. For us, the Mother is entirely a miracle, who singularly and without precedent is both mother and virgin; for us, the Son is entirely a miracle, who not only singularly but incomprehensibly is God and man. The Mother, a virgin conceiving and bearing, is a sign to us that this one who is conceived and born is God and man; the Son, doing divine things and suffering human things, is a sign to us that he will transfer into God the man for whom he is conceived and born, and also suffers. Yet of all the human infirmities or injuries which the divine condescension bore for us, I judge that, just as it was first in time, so also it was almost the greatest in humility, that his boundless majesty suffered to be conceived in the womb, and contained within the womb for the space of nine months. For where did he so empty himself? Or when did he seem so utterly to have ceased to be himself? For such a long time that wisdom speaks nothing, that power works nothing manifest, and majesty, lying hidden and enclosed, is betrayed by no visible sign. Not so did he appear weak on the cross, where what was weak in him immediately appeared stronger than all men: when, even while dying, he glorifies the thief, and, while breathing his last, he inspires the Centurion; when the brief pain of the Passion not only made the elements of creation suffer with him, but also subjects the opposing powers to the suffering of eternal pains. But in the womb he is as though he were not; thus the omnipotent power lies idle as though it could do nothing, and the eternal Word presses itself down into silence.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Vobis tamen, fratres, vobis illud silentium verbi loquitur, vobis clamat; vobis utique disciplinam silentii commendat. In silentio enim et spe erit fortitudo vestra (Isai. XXX, 15), sicut Isaias promittit, qui cultum justitiae silentium definivit (Id. XXXII, 17). Sicut namque puer ille conceptus in utero ad maturitatem partus sub alto et diuturno profecit silentio, sic et spiritum hominis disciplina silentii nutrit, format et roborat, et quanto occultius, tanto tutius et salubrius incrementum praestat. Nescit homo animalis, qui non percipit quae sunt spiritus Dei, quae sit via spiritus, et quomodo compingantur ossa in ventre praegnantis (Eccle. XI, 5); sed non occultatum os meum a te, ait sanctus Deo, quod fecisti mihi in occulto (Psal. CXXXVIII, 15) mentis, sub secreto silentii. Sed nec a vobis occultatum est hoc mysterium, fratres mei, quorum experientia et confessio testis mihi est quomodo quietus et modestus spiritus sub silentio roboretur, pinguescat et floreat; quomodo e contrario per verba, velut quadam paralysi, dissolvatur et defluat; quomodo macrescat, et marcescat, et aridus decidat. Denique si in silentio fortitudo non esset, Salomon nequaquam dixisset: Sicut urbs patens et absque murorum ambitu, ita vir qui non potest in loquendo cohibere spiritum suum (Prov. XXV, 28). Caeterum, si quaeris quo negotio sit occupanda mens in silentio, nihil tibi imponimus onerosum, comede panem tuum, sicut et ipse Dominus conceptus suo tibi commonstrat exemplo. Quid enim propheta de eo dixit, cum loqueretur de porta orientali semper clausa in domo Domini, quae tamen Deum Israel admisit et emisit? Princeps ipse, inquit, sedebit in ea, ut comedat panem coram Domino (Ezech. XLIV, 3). Sedebit, inquit, in ea, quoniam requiescet in ea, de qua et ipse loquitur: Haec requies mea (Psal. XXXI, 14); sedebit in ea tanquam in throno grandi, quem, sicut alias dixi, Salomon rex sibi de ebore fecit (III Reg. X, 18). Si attendas angustias uteri, locus prorsus angustus est, si latitudinem cordis, thronus grandis est, propter quam etiam uterus tantae majestatis capax factus est. In ea ergo princeps sedit et panem comedit; quia si quis, inquit, aperuerit mihi, intrabo ad eum, et coenabo cum eo, et ipse mecum (Apoc. III, 20)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Yet to you, brothers, to you that silence of the Word speaks, to you it cries out; to you indeed it commends the discipline of silence. For \"in silence and hope shall your strength be\" (Isai. XXX, 15), as Isaiah promises, who defined the practice of righteousness as silence (Id. XXXII, 17). For just as that child, conceived in the womb, advanced toward the maturity of birth under deep and prolonged silence, so too the discipline of silence nourishes, forms, and strengthens the spirit of man; and the more hidden it is, the safer and more wholesome growth it affords. The natural man, who does not perceive the things of the Spirit of God, does not know what is the way of the spirit, and how the bones are joined together in the womb of her that is with child (Eccle. XI, 5); but \"my bone is not hidden from thee,\" says the saint to God, \"which thou hast made for me in secret\" (Psal. CXXXVIII, 15) \u2014 in secret of mind, under the secrecy of silence. But neither is this mystery hidden from you, my brothers, whose experience and confession is witness to me how a quiet and modest spirit is strengthened, grows rich, and flourishes under silence; how, on the contrary, through words, as by a kind of paralysis, it is dissolved and wastes away; how it grows thin, and withers, and falls away dried up. Finally, if there were no strength in silence, Solomon would by no means have said: \"As a city that lies open and without the enclosure of walls, so is the man who cannot restrain his spirit in speaking\" (Prov. XXV, 28). But if you ask with what business the mind ought to be occupied in silence, we impose on you nothing burdensome: eat your bread, as the Lord himself, conceived in the womb, shows you by his own example. For what did the prophet say of him, when he spoke of the eastern gate, always shut, in the house of the Lord, which nevertheless admitted and sent forth the God of Israel? \"The prince himself,\" he says, \"shall sit in it, to eat bread before the Lord\" (Ezech. XLIV, 3). \"He shall sit,\" he says, \"in it,\" because he will rest in it, of which he himself also says: \"This is my rest\" (Psal. XXXI, 14); he shall sit in it as on a great throne, which, as I have said elsewhere, King Solomon made for himself of ivory (III Reg. X, 18). If you consider the narrowness of the womb, it is altogether a narrow place; if you consider the breadth of the heart, it is a great throne \u2014 on account of which the womb was made capable of so great a majesty. In it, therefore, the prince sat and ate bread; for \"if any man,\" he says, \"shall open to me, I will come in to him, and will sup with him, and he with me\" (Apoc. III, 20).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Non est haec coena sine pane, quando et ipse qui coenat panis est vitae, panis qui hodie descendit de coelo et dat vitam mundo. Sed res mira, si idem est qui coenat, et quod coenatur; et qui comedit, ipse est panis qui ab eo comeditur. Revera res mira, sed res vera; quia Christus non alio pane quam se ipso pascitur. Ipse namque totus est panis, Verbum propter se, caro propter unionem Verbi. Alioquin caro non prodest quidquam, cum spiritus sit qui vivificat (Joan. VI, 64), nec in solo pane vivat homo, sed in omni verbo quod procedit de ore Dei (Matth. IV, 4). Omne verbum procedens de ore Dei unum et unigenitum est Verbum Patris, quod, cum simplex sit, in se tamen continet rationem et formam omnis verbi divini. Verbum itaque pascitur de Verbo, Filius vivit de se ipso; quia sicut Pater habet vitam in semetipso, sic dedit et Filio vitam habere in semetipso (Joan. V, 26). Alia tamen ratione, sed tamen ineffabili beatitudine, Verbo pascebatur anima illa, Verbo ipsi in persona conjuncta; et incomparabili felicitate princeps ille sedens in porta virginalis uteri, panem verbi coram Domino comedebat. Id igitur operis tu quoque, si sapis, in silentio tuo actitabis, ut panem verbi divini comedas coram Domino, conservans, sicut Maria, quae de Christo dicuntur, et conferens in corde tuo. Hunc panem tecum Christus manducare delectabitur; et qui te pascit, ipse in te pascetur (Luc. XXII, 15, 19); et panis ipse quo plus editur, plus abundabit edendus: quia gratia non minuitur usu, sed augetur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. This supper is not without bread, since he himself who sups is the bread of life, the bread which today came down from heaven and gives life to the world. But it is a wondrous thing, if he who sups is the same as that which is supped upon; and he who eats is himself the bread which is eaten by him. Indeed it is a wondrous thing, but a true thing; for Christ is fed by no other bread than himself. For he himself is wholly bread: the Word on his own account, the flesh on account of its union with the Word. Otherwise the flesh profits nothing, since it is the spirit that gives life (Joan. VI, 64); nor does man live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God (Matth. IV, 4). Every word proceeding from the mouth of God is the one and only-begotten Word of the Father, which, although it is simple, nevertheless contains within itself the reason and form of every divine word. The Word, therefore, is fed from the Word; the Son lives from himself; for as the Father has life in himself, so has he given to the Son also to have life in himself (Joan. V, 26). Yet in another respect \u2014 though still by an ineffable blessedness \u2014 that soul was fed by the Word, being joined to the Word itself in person; and with incomparable felicity that prince, sitting in the gate of the virginal womb, ate the bread of the Word before the Lord. This work, therefore, you too, if you are wise, will carry on in your silence: that you may eat the bread of the divine Word before the Lord, keeping, as Mary did, the things that are said of Christ, and pondering them in your heart. Christ will delight to eat this bread with you; and he who feeds you will himself be fed in you (Luc. XXII, 15, 19); and this very bread, the more it is eaten, the more it will abound to be eaten: because grace is not diminished by use, but increased.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. In hoc propterea tibi sit exemplo Jesus conceptus et gestatus in utero, ut, sicut onus illud leve ac suave, etsi gravidavit, non tamen gravavit uterum Mariae, sic te non sentiat gravem aut molestum uterus Ecclesiae. Gravida est, o fratres, Ecclesia, non, sicut Maria, solo Jesu, sed, sicut Rebecca, Jacob et Esau, non solum scilicet bonis et modestis, sed etiam dyscolis et indisciplinatis; quos tamen et ipsos, propter nomen Jesu, vel forsitan aliquod initium substantiae ejus, Ecclesiae viscera suscipiunt et amplectuntur. Sed cum sese colliderent parvuli discordes in utero Rebeccae, quae prius oraverat ut conciperet, dolensque ventrem suum a tribulatione malorum et dolore, pene poenitebat quod concepisset: Si sic, inquit, futurum mihi erat, quid necesse erat concipere? (Gen. XXV, 22.). Si de aliquo nostrum, fratres, sic conqueri contigerit viscera matris nostrae, timeo ne melius fuisset si conceptus non fuisset homo ille; nisi quod nec de talibus sinit nos desperare, qui etiam de lapidibus suscitat filios Abrahae (Luc. III, 8). Ipse in eis, si qui tales sunt, emolliat cor lapideum, ne quatiant viscera matris; ipse viscera matris consoletur, ne fatigetur eos, qualescunque sint, portare, donec formetur in eis Christus, qui perfectus Deus, perfectus homo vivit et regnat, per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Let Jesus, therefore, conceived and carried in the womb, be an example to you in this: that just as that burden, light and sweet, though it filled Mary's womb with pregnancy, did not weigh it down, so may the womb of the Church not feel you to be heavy or troublesome. The Church, O brothers, is pregnant \u2014 not, like Mary, with Jesus alone, but, like Rebecca, with Jacob and Esau \u2014 that is, not only with the good and well-behaved, but also with the unruly and undisciplined; yet even these, for the sake of the name of Jesus, or perhaps some small beginning of his substance in them, the womb of the Church receives and embraces. But when the quarreling little ones struggled against each other in the womb of Rebecca \u2014 she who had earlier prayed that she might conceive \u2014 grieving in her womb over the trouble and pain caused by these evil ones, she almost regretted that she had conceived, saying: 'If it was going to be like this for me, what need was there to conceive?' (Gen. XXV, 22.). If it should happen, brothers, that the womb of our mother thus complains concerning any one of us, I fear it would have been better if that man had not been conceived \u2014 except that he who raises up children of Abraham even from stones (Luc. III, 8) does not allow us to despair even over such as these. May he himself soften the stony heart in them, if any such there be, lest they shake the womb of their mother; may he himself console the womb of the mother, lest she grow weary of carrying them, whatever they may be, until Christ be formed in them \u2014 who, perfect God, perfect man, lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The words on the Lord choosing his resting place come from Psalm 131:14 in historical numbering; the edition prints Psal. XXXI, 14."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Gabriel's words that no word is impossible with God come from Luke 1:37; the edition prints Luc. I, 30, 31."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The call for the peoples to praise God and the words on the earth yielding its fruit come from Psalm 66:6-7 in historical numbering; the edition prints Psal. LXVI, 6."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Annunciation Sermon III", "topics": ["Annunciation", "Incarnation", "Virgin Mary", "prophecy", "Passion", "silence", "Scripture", "Church", "patience"], "url": "sermons/guerric-annunciation-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:viri-galilaei", "calendar_label": "The Ascension of the Lord", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-ascension", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In die ascensionis Domini, Sermo, De amore Christi in nos et de nostro in ipsum, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier freres, 1879), cols. 153-156.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Ascension", "Ascension of the Lord", "John 17:11, 12", "John 13:1 (printed Id.)", "John 17:11-13, 15, 24 (printed scope)", "Psalm 32:9 (historical numbering)", "John 17:24, 20 (printed order)", "Philippians 1:29", "Matthew 24:28 and Luke 17:37 (combined printed locator; closing parenthesis absent)", "Deuteronomy 32:14 (printed locator retained)", "1 Thessalonians 4:16 (printed locator retained)", "Psalm 17:11 (historical numbering)", "2 Corinthians 12:2", "Psalm 72:25 (historical numbering; closing parenthesis present)"], "occasion": "In die ascensionis Domini", "occasion_detail": "Ascension of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Ascension", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric of Igny applies Christ's prayer before the Passion to the Ascension, reading his departure through the love shown to his disciples. The Eucharist offers a new manner of remaining with and in them, while the washing of their feet teaches humility and forgiveness. Christ's prayer promises protection from evil, sanctification in truth and glory with him, without excusing negligence. Guerric urges his brothers to practise the daily struggle against vice: like young eagles learning to fly, they must lift their hearts after the ascended Christ. The sermon ends with contemplation, love and the liturgical exchange Lift up your hearts / We have them lifted to the Lord, directing desire toward Christ as heavenly treasure. The individual sermon is undated; its collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138-1157 and is read here from the 1879 Patrologia Latina edition. The source names the day of the Lord\u2019s Ascension."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN DIE ASCENSIONIS DOMINI."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE DAY OF THE LORD'S ASCENSION.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De amore Christi in nos et de nostro in ipsum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On Christ's love for us and our love for him.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Pater, cum essem cum eis, ego servabam eos in nomine tuo (Joan. xvii, 11, 12). Haec oratio Domini habita est ante diem passionis. Non absurde tamen aptatur diei Ascensionis, quando scilicet supremo discessurus erat a filiolis suis, quos Patri commendabat. Qui enim in coelis creavit, docet et regit multitudinem Angelorum, in terris aggregaverat sibi pusillum gregem discipulorum sub praesentia suae carnis erudiendum, quoadusque grandiusculi sensus effecti, disciplinae Spiritus fierent idonei. Magnus itaque parvulos magna dilectione diligebat, quippe quos ab more mundi abstraxerat, et relicta omni spe saeculi, de se solo pendere videbat. Quandiu tamen cum eis corporaliter conversari voluit, non facile aut multum hunc suum eis affectum prodidit; maturiorem se eis, quam teneriorem exhibens, sicut magistrum decebat et patrem. Cum autem tempus quo ab eis recessurus erat instaret, tunc veluti vinci tenero eorum affectu visus est, ut magnam multitudinem dulcedinis suae, quam eis absconderat, dissimulare non posset. Hinc illud est, quod cum dilexisset suos qui erant in mundo, in finem dilexit eos (Id. xiii, 1). Tunc enim propemodum omnem vim amoris effudit amicis, antequam etiam ipse sicut aqua effunderetur pro inimicis. Tunc eis sacramentum Corporis et Sanguinis sui tradidit, et celebrandum instituit, nescio virtute an charitate mirabiliori, hoc novum genus mansionis adinveniens in consolationem recessus sui, ut si discederet ab eis specie corporis, maneret non solum cum eis, sed etiam in eis virtute Sacramenti. Tunc veluti prorsus suae oblitus majestatis, et velut injuriam faciens sibi, nisi quia gloria est charitatis humiliare se pro amicis, dignatione ineffabili Dominus, et talis Dominus, pedes servorum lavit: uno opere condens eis et humilitatis exemplum, et remissionis sacramentum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Father, when I was with them, I kept them in your name (Joan. xvii, 11, 12). This prayer of the Lord was offered before the day of his Passion. Yet it is fittingly applied to the day of the Ascension, when he was about to leave his little children for the last time and was commending them to the Father. For he who in heaven created, teaches and rules the multitude of Angels had gathered for himself on earth a little flock of disciples, to be instructed in the presence of his flesh until their understanding had grown somewhat and they became fit for the teaching of the Spirit. Thus the great one loved the little ones with great love: he had drawn them away from the ways of the world and saw them depending on him alone, having abandoned every worldly hope. Yet as long as he chose to live bodily among them, he did not readily or fully reveal this affection of his to them, showing himself more reserved than tender, as befitted a teacher and father. But when the time was drawing near for him to depart from them, he seemed overcome by tender affection for them, so that he could no longer conceal the great abundance of his sweetness that he had hidden from them. Hence the saying that, having loved his own who were in the world, he loved them to the end (Id. xiii, 1). For then he poured out almost the whole force of his love upon his friends, before he himself was also poured out like water for his enemies. Then he handed on to them the sacrament of his Body and Blood and instituted its celebration, devising this new manner of remaining to console them for his departure, with power or charity more wonderful, I know not which. Thus, though he departed from them in bodily appearance, he would remain not only with them but also in them through the power of the Sacrament. Then, as though utterly forgetful of his own majesty and as though doing himself an indignity, except that it is the glory of charity to humble oneself for friends, the Lord, and such a Lord, with ineffable condescension washed his servants' feet, establishing for them in a single act both an example of humility and a sacrament of forgiveness.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Tunc denique post exhortationem prolixioris sermonis Patri eos commendans, sublevatis in coelum oculis, inter caetera sic ait, Pater, cum essem cum eis, ego servabam eos in nomine tuo, et nemo ex his periit, nisi filius perditionis. Nunc autem ad te venio: serva eos in nomine tuo quos dedisti mihi. Non rogo ut tollas eos de mundo, sed ut serves eos a malo; et caetera, quae non est hujus temporis retexere, multo minus exponere. Summa tamen orationis, sicut indicat textus lectionis, est in his tribus, in quibus est et summa salutis, imo et perfectionis, ut nihil possit addi, scilicet, ut a malo serventur, ut in veritate sanctificentur, ut cum ipso glorificentur. Pater, inquit, quos dedisti mihi, volo ut ubi sum ego, et ipsi sint mecum, ut videant claritatem meam (Joan. xvii, 11-13, 15, 24). O felices, quorum advocatus ipse judex est; pro quibus orat qui pari honore cum eo quem orat adorandus est! Voluntate labiorum ejus non fraudabit eum Pater, cum quo est ei una voluntas et una potestas, quia unus Deus est. Omnino quod Christus orat, impleri necesse est: cujus sermo virtus est, et voluntas effectus; qui de omnibus quae sunt, dixit, et facta sunt, mandavit, et creata sunt (Psal. xxxii, 9). Volo, inquit, ut ubi ego sum, et ipsi sint mecum. O quanta securitas fidelium! quanta fiducia credentium! tantum non abjiciant gratiam quam acceperunt. Non enim solis Apostolis proposita est haec securitas, vel condiscipulis eorum, sed omnibus qui per eorum verbum credituri sunt in Dei verbum. Non pro eis, inquit, rogo tantum, sed pro eis qui credituri sunt per verbum eorum in me (Joan. xvii, 24, 20)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Then, finally, after the exhortation of a lengthy discourse, commending them to the Father and lifting his eyes to heaven, he said among other things: Father, when I was with them, I kept them in your name, and none of them perished except the son of perdition. But now I come to you: keep in your name those whom you have given me. I do not ask that you take them out of the world, but that you keep them from evil; and the rest, which this is not the time to repeat, much less explain. Yet the substance of the prayer, as the text of the reading shows, lies in these three things, in which also lies the fullness of salvation, indeed of perfection, so that nothing can be added: that they be kept from evil, that they be sanctified in truth, and that they be glorified with him. Father, he says, those whom you have given me, I will that where I am, they too may be with me, so that they may see my glory (Joan. xvii, 11-13, 15, 24). O happy people, whose advocate is himself their judge, for whom he prays who is to be adored with equal honor alongside him to whom he prays! The Father will not deny him the desire of his lips: with the Father he has one will and one power, because they are one God. What Christ prays for must assuredly be fulfilled: his word is power, and his will is accomplishment. Concerning all things that exist, he spoke and they were made; he commanded and they were created (Psal. xxxii, 9). I will, he says, that where I am, they too may be with me. O how great the security of the faithful! How great the confidence of believers! Only let them not cast away the grace they have received. For this security is offered not to the Apostles alone or to their fellow disciples, but to all who through their word will believe in the Word of God. I pray, he says, not for them alone, but for those who through their word will believe in me (Joan. xvii, 24, 20).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Vobis autem fratres, donatum est, ut ait Apostolus, non solum ut in eum credatis, sed etiam ut patiamini pro eo (Philipp. i, 29): quos scilicet fides promissionis Christi, non securitate negligentiores, sed alacritate faciens ferventiores, et quotidiano vitiorum conflictu, martyrio coronat assiduos: assiduo, sed facili; facili, sed sublimi. Facile est, ubi supra virtutem nihil imperatur; sublime, ubi de omni virtute illius fortis armati triumphatur. An non facile suave Christi jugum portare? an non sublime in regno ejus excellere? Quid facilius, rogo, quam portare pennas quae portent portantem se? quid sublimius, quam supra omnem coelorum altitudinem, quo Christus ascendit, evolare? Plane sancti, quorum sicut aquilae juventus renovabitur, assument pennas sicut aquilae, et volabunt. Quo volabunt? Ubicunque, inquit, fuerit corpus, illuc congregabuntur et aquilae (Matth. xxiv, 28, et Luc. xvii, 37."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But to you, brothers, it has been granted, as the Apostle says, not only to believe in him but also to suffer for him (Philipp. i, 29). Faith in Christ's promise makes you more fervent through eagerness rather than more negligent through security, and crowns you, steadfast in the daily struggle against vices, with martyrdom: continual, yet easy; easy, yet sublime. It is easy where nothing beyond one's strength is commanded; sublime where the whole power of that strong armed one is overcome. Is it not easy to bear Christ's sweet yoke? Is it not sublime to excel in his kingdom? What is easier, I ask, than to carry wings that carry their bearer? What is more sublime than to fly above the whole height of the heavens, where Christ has ascended? Surely the saints, whose youth will be renewed like the eagle's, will take wings like eagles and fly. Where will they fly? Wherever the body is, he says, there the eagles too will gather (Matth. xxiv, 28, et Luc. xvii, 37.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Sed quid putamus, fratres, quomodo tunc repente de terris ad coelos evolare poterit, qui nunc exercitio et usu quotidiano volitare non didicerit? Si quaeris quo doctore, quo duce, nunquid non Christus sicut aquila, provocabat hodie ad volandum pullos suos, quando super eos volitabat, cum scilicet videntibus illis elevaretur, diuque sequerentur oculis euntem in coelum? Poterat utique repente in ictu oculi, ex oculis eorum rapi, et ubi vellet, constitui. Sed plane, sicut aquila provocans ad volandum pullos suos, et super eos volitans (Deut. xxxii, 14), et corda sursum levare post se nitebatur amore sui, et corpora similiter posse sublevari pollicebatur exemplo corporis sui: sicut Apostolus ait, aeterni conscius mysterii, quia et nos bajulis nubibus rapiemur obviam Christo (I Thess. iv, 16) redeunti. Ipse quidem ascendit super Cherubim, et volavit super pennas ventorum (Psal. xvii, 11), id est supergressus est virtutes Angelorum: tuae tamen condescendens infirmitati, expandet alas suas, et assumet te, atque portabit in humeris suis, si modo pullus degener non existas, ut a terra levari, et aura puriore perfrui non extimescas."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. But what do we think, brothers? How will someone suddenly be able then to fly from earth to heaven if he has not now learned to fly through daily practice and exercise? If you ask who is the teacher and who the guide, did not Christ, like an eagle, encourage his young to fly today when he flew above them, that is, when he was lifted up before their eyes and they followed him with their gaze for a long time as he went into heaven? He could certainly have been snatched from their sight suddenly, in the blink of an eye, and set wherever he wished. But indeed, like an eagle encouraging its young to fly and flying above them (Deut. xxxii, 14), he strove to lift their hearts up after him through love of himself and promised, by the example of his own body, that their bodies too could be lifted up. As the Apostle says, privy to the eternal mystery, we too, with clouds bearing us, shall be caught up to meet Christ (I Thess. iv, 16) as he returns. He himself indeed ascended upon Cherubim and flew upon the wings of the winds (Psal. xvii, 11), that is, he surpassed the powers of the Angels. Yet, stooping to your weakness, he will spread his wings, take you up and carry you on his shoulders, provided that you are not a degenerate fledgling, afraid to be lifted from the earth and enjoy a purer air.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Alii volant contemplando, tu saltem amando. Paulus mente excedit, et ad tertium usque pervolat coelum (II Cor. xii, 2); Joannes ad id quod id principio erat Verbum: tu saltem humi degenerem non trahas animum, nec patiaris obrutum inertia in terra putrescere cor tuum; sed clamanti, \u00ab Sursum corda, \u00bb Pontifici magno (qui hodie introivit in Sancta, aeterna redemptione inventa, ubi et assistit vultui Dei interpellans pro nobis) responde fideliter, \u00ab Habemus ad Dominum. \u00bb Sed et si aliquando quaesivisti non quae sursum sunt, sed quae super terram; jam te ipsum increpa, et dic Domino cum propheta: Quid enim mihi est in coelo? et a te quid volui super terram? (Psal. lxxii, 25) Heu, quam misere errabam! tam magnum est illud in coelo mihi repositum, quod contemnebam; tam nihil hoc super terram, quod tantopere expetebam! Christus itaque thesaurus tuus ascendit in coelum: ibi sit et cor tuum. Inde ducis originem, ibi habes Patrem et haereditatem, inde exspectas Salvatorem. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Others fly by contemplation; you, at least, by love. Paul passes beyond himself in mind and flies as far as the third heaven (II Cor. xii, 2); John to that Word who was in the beginning. You, at least, must not drag a debased spirit along the ground or let your heart, buried in sloth, rot in the earth. Rather, to the great High Priest crying, 'Lift up your hearts' (who today entered the holy place, having obtained eternal redemption, where he also stands before the face of God, interceding for us), answer faithfully, 'We have them lifted to the Lord.' And if at times you have sought not the things above but the things on earth, now rebuke yourself and say to the Lord with the prophet: For what have I in heaven? And apart from you, what have I desired upon earth? (Psal. lxxii, 25) Alas, how wretchedly I was mistaken! So great is that which was laid up for me in heaven and which I despised; so utterly nothing is this upon earth which I sought so eagerly! Christ, then, your treasure, has ascended into heaven: let your heart be there too. From there you draw your origin; there you have your Father and inheritance; from there you await the Savior. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition titles this simply \u201cSermon,\u201d without a number. Its ending is \u201cthe Savior. Amen.\u201d"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition\u2019s Bible references and historical Psalm numbering are retained. The combined Matthew 24:28 / Luke 17:37 reference has no closing parenthesis in print."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Latin prints id principio where the context calls for in principio (\u201cin the beginning\u201d). The translation follows that sense. \u201cLift up your hearts\u201d and \u201cWe have them lifted to the Lord\u201d are the liturgical call and response."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "\u201cFrom the ways of the world\u201d translates ab more mundi, not \u201cfrom love of the world.\u201d Christ\u2019s bodily departure is contrasted with his continuing sacramental presence in the disciples. The daily martyrdom described here is spiritual combat against vice, not bodily execution."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Ascension Sermon: Christ's Love for Us and Our Love for Him", "topics": ["Ascension", "love", "Eucharist", "humility", "prayer", "faith", "spiritual combat", "contemplation", "hope"], "url": "sermons/guerric-ascension.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:assumption", "calendar_label": "The Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-assumption-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In assumptione B. Mariae, Sermo I, Quod Maria duplici titulo sit mater, quodque recte dicatur thronus Dei, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier freres, 1879), cols. 187-190.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Assumption", "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Psalm 64:5", "Psalm 131:14", "Isaiah 62:4,5", "Genesis 3", "Galatians 4:19", "Isaiah 62:5", "Psalm 86:7", "Matthew 19:28", "Revelation 3:21", "Psalm 44:14,15", "Wisdom 4:1"], "occasion": "In assumptione B. Mariae", "occasion_detail": "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Assumption", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This first sermon for the Assumption of Blessed Mary belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval liturgical collection, associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138-1157 and read here from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The individual delivery year is unknown. Beginning with an invitation from the Office of Virgins, Guerric presents Mary as mother both of Christ and, through him, of Christians, contrasting her with Eve and relating her to the Church. He develops the image of Mary as God's throne and ends with a prayer for her intercession. The Assumption is the source\u2019s historical occasion."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN ASSUMPTIONE B. MARIAE."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE ASSUMPTION OF BLESSED MARY.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quod Maria duplici titulo sit mater, quodque recte dicatur thronus Dei."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "That Mary is mother by a double title, and is rightly called the throne of God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Veni, electa mea, et ponam in te thronum meum (11). Multi vocati, pauci vero electi. Beati quos elegisti, Domine; habitabunt in atriis tuis (Psal. lxiv, 5) : imo tu in eis habitabis, tu in eis regnabis, thronumque regni tui in eis collocabis. Sane omnium beatorum beatissima est Maria, quae de numero omnium electorum singulariter est electa et praeelecta : quoniam elegit eam Dominus, elegit eam in habitationem sibi, dicens, Hic requies mea in saeculum saeculi : hic habitabo quoniam elegi eam (Psal cxxxi, 14). Habitavit in ea novem mensibus, habitavit cum ea et sub ea annis pluribus. Habitans in ea replebat eam singulari gratia charismatum : habitans cum ea pascebat eam incomparabili piorum suavitate morum, et divinorum desiderabili sapientia sermonum. Nunc autem in ea et cum ea, sicut interminabili aevo, sic incomprehensibili modo habitans, satiat eam beatificantium gloria visionum; foris quidem glorificatae carnis formam exhibens, intus autem formam Verbi glorificantis imprimens. O Maria, non vocaberis, ait Dominus, ultra derelicta, et terra tua non vocabitur ultra desolata, ut quia virgo, ideo sis infecunda : sed vocaberis Voluntas mea, id est, dilectus Filius meus in ea, quia complacuit Domino in te, et terra tua inhabitabitur. Habitabit enim juvenis in virgine, et habitabit in te Filius tuus; imo, si magis placet, ut non recedamus a verbis Scripturae, habitabunt in te filii tui (Isa. lxii, 4, 5)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Come, my chosen one, and I will set my throne in you (11). Many are called, but few are chosen. Blessed are those whom you have chosen, Lord; they will dwell in your courts (Psal. lxiv, 5) : rather, you will dwell in them, you will reign in them, and you will establish the throne of your kingdom in them. Certainly Mary is the most blessed of all the blessed, singled out and chosen before all among the whole number of the elect : for the Lord has chosen her, chosen her as a dwelling for himself, saying, This is my rest for ever and ever : here I will dwell, for I have chosen her (Psal cxxxi, 14). He dwelt in her for nine months; he dwelt with her and under her for many years. Dwelling in her, he filled her with a unique grace of spiritual gifts : dwelling with her, he nourished her with the incomparable sweetness of his holy ways and the desirable wisdom of his divine words. But now, dwelling in her and with her both for an endless age and in an incomprehensible manner, he satisfies her with the glory of visions that make her blessed; outwardly displaying the form of his glorified flesh, and inwardly impressing the form of the Word who glorifies her. O Mary, the Lord says, you will no longer be called forsaken, nor will your land any longer be called desolate, as though being a virgin meant that you must be barren : instead you will be called My Will, that is, my beloved Son is in her, because the Lord has taken pleasure in you, and your land will be inhabited. For the young man will dwell in the virgin, and your Son will dwell in you; or rather, if you prefer, so that we do not depart from the words of Scripture, your sons will dwell in you (Isa. lxii, 4, 5).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Quid, haeretice, caput erigis? quid mysterium pietatis ad occasionem perfidiae rapis? Unum quidem genuit : qui sicut unicus est Patri in coelis, ita unicus est Matri in terris. Neque enim sicut tu impie blasphemas, alios postea filios genuit; sed intemeratum manet, sicut signaculum perpetuae virginitatis in matre, ita sacramentum catholicae unitatis in prole. Ipsa tamen unica virgo Mater, quae se Patris Unicum genuisse gloriatur, eumdem unicum suum in omnibus membris ejus amplectitur, omniumque in quibus Christum suum formatum agnoscit, vel formari cognoscit, matrem se vocari non confunditur. Eva vetus illa, non tam mater quam noverca, quae filiis ante propinavit praejudicium mortis, quam initium lucis. Dicta est quidem mater cunctorum viventium : sed inventa est verius interfectrix viventium, seu genitrix morientium; cum suum generare nihil aliud sit, quam mortem ingenerare (Gen. iii). Et quia illa non potuit fideliter interpretari nomen suum, ista implevit mysterium : quae et ipsa, sicut Ecclesia, cujus forma est, mater est omnium ad vitam renascentium. Mater siquidem est Vitae qua vivunt universi; quam dum ex se genuit, nimirum omnes ex ea victuri sunt, quodammodo regeneravit. Unus generabatur, sed nos omnes generabamur : quia videlicet secundum rationem seminis, quo regeneratio fit, jam tunc in illo omnes eramus. Sicut enim in Adam fuimus ab initio propter semen carnalis generationis; sic in Christo ante initium, propter semen spiritualis regenerationis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Why do you raise your head, heretic? Why do you seize the mystery of godliness as an occasion for unbelief? She bore one Son : who, just as he is the Father's only Son in heaven, is also his Mother's only Son on earth. For she did not afterwards bear other sons, as you impiously blaspheme; rather, just as the seal of perpetual virginity remains inviolate in the mother, so does the mystery of Catholic unity in her offspring. Yet this unique virgin Mother, who glories in having borne the Father's Only-begotten, embraces that same only Son of hers in all his members, and is not ashamed to be called the mother of all in whom she recognizes her Christ already formed or knows him to be taking form. That Eve of old was less a mother than a stepmother, who gave her children the sentence of death to drink before they first saw the light. She was indeed called the mother of all the living : but she proved more truly the slayer of the living, or the mother of the dying; for her giving birth means nothing other than bringing death into being (Gen. iii). And because she could not faithfully express the meaning of her name, this other mother fulfilled the mystery : she too, like the Church of which she is the figure, is mother of all who are born again to life. For she is the Mother of the Life by which all live; and in giving birth to that Life from herself, she in a certain manner gave rebirth to all who would live by it. One was being born, but we were all being born : for, in respect of the seed through which rebirth takes place, we were all already in him then. Just as we were in Adam from the beginning through the seed of bodily generation, so we were in Christ before the beginning through the seed of spiritual regeneration.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Porro beata Mater illa Christi, quia se matrem Christianorum cognoscit ratione mysterii; cura quoque se matrem eis praestat et affectu pietatis. Neque enim duratur ad filios, quasi non sint sui, cujus viscera semel quidem foeta, sed nunquam effoeta, nunquam desinunt fructum parturire pietatis. Benedictus siquidem fructus ventris tui, gravidam te, o pia Mater, inexhausta pietate reliquit; ex te quidem semel nascens, sed in te semper manens et affluens, et in horto concluso castitatis fontem signatum charitatis semper abundare faciens; qui licet signatus sit, foras tamen derivatur, et in plateis aquae ejus nobis dividuntur. Licet namque fons charitatis, proprius Ecclesiae, incommunicabilis sit alienis, beneficium tamen ejus impertire gaudet etiam inimicis. Denique si servus Christi filiolos suos iterum atque iterum parturit cura atque desiderio pietatis, donec formetur in eis Christus (Galat. iv, 19); quanto magis ipsa Mater Christi? Et Paulus quidem genuit eos, verbum veritatis quo regenerati sunt praedicando : Maria autem longe divinius atque sanctius, Verbum ipsum generando. Laudo quidem in Paulo praedicationis mysterium, sed plus miror ac veneror in Maria generationis mysterium."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Moreover, because that blessed Mother of Christ knows that she is the mother of Christians by reason of the mystery, she also shows herself their mother by her care and loving devotion. She does not harden herself toward her children as though they were not her own : her womb, fruitful once but never exhausted, never ceases to bring forth the fruit of loving devotion. For the blessed fruit of your womb left you, O loving Mother, pregnant with inexhaustible devotion; born from you once, yet always remaining and overflowing in you, he makes the sealed fountain of charity abound continually within the enclosed garden of chastity. Although sealed, it is nevertheless channeled outward, and its waters are shared with us in the streets. For although the fountain of charity belongs to the Church and cannot be shared with those outside her, it gladly shares its benefit even with enemies. Indeed, if the servant of Christ labors in birth for his little children again and again, with devoted care and longing, until Christ is formed in them (Galat. iv, 19); how much more does the Mother of Christ herself? Paul indeed begot them by preaching the word of truth through which they were born again : but Mary did so in a far more divine and holy manner, by giving birth to the Word himself. I praise in Paul the mystery of preaching, but I admire and venerate still more in Mary the mystery of giving birth.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Vide autem si non et filii Matrem videntur agnoscere, dictante utique ipsis veluti quadam naturali pietate fidei, ut ad invocationem nominis ejus, primo vel maxime refugiant in omnibus necessitatibus et periculis, tanquam parvuli ad sinum matris. Unde nimirum de his filiis puto non absurde intelligi, quod per prophetam promissum est ei. Habitabant in te filii tui (Isai. lxii, 5); salvo duntaxat intellectu, quo de Ecclesia haec prophetia principaliter accipitur. Et nunc siquidem habitamus in adjutorio Matris Altissimi, in protectione ipsius commoramur, tanquam sub umbra alarum ejus : et postmodum in consortio gloriae ipsius, tanquam in sinu ipsius confovebimur. Tunc erit vox una laetantium et aggratulantium Matri : Sicut laetantium omnium nostrum habitatio est in te (Psal. lxxxvi, 7), sancta Dei Genitrix. Nullatenus autem credideris majoris esse felicitatis et gloriae, habitare in sinu Abrahae, quam in sinu Mariae, cum thronum suum in ea posuerit Rex gloriae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. See, too, whether the children do not seem to recognize their Mother, prompted, as it were, by a certain natural devotion of faith to flee first or above all to the invocation of her name in every need and danger, like little children to their mother's bosom. Hence I think it is reasonable to understand of these children what was promised to her through the prophet. Your sons dwelt in you (Isai. lxii, 5); always preserving the interpretation according to which this prophecy is understood principally of the Church. Even now we dwell in the help of the Mother of the Most High; we remain under her protection, as though beneath the shadow of her wings : and afterwards, sharing her glory, we shall be cherished as though in her bosom. Then there will be one voice of those rejoicing with their Mother and congratulating her : The dwelling of all of us who rejoice is in you (Psal. lxxxvi, 7), holy Mother of God. Never believe that dwelling in Abraham's bosom is a greater happiness and glory than dwelling in Mary's bosom, since the King of glory has established his throne in her.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Veni, inquit, electa mea, et ponam in te thronum meum. Non poterat signantius aut elegantius describi praerogativa gloriae ejus, quam ut thronus Dei regnantis esse diceretur. Nulli siquidem animae tanta plenitudine aut familiaritate copiam sui divina majestas videtur indulgere, sicut illi in qua specialiter prae caeteris residere delegerit. Discipulis quidem Dominus loquebatur, qui pauperes facti pauperem sequebantur : In regeneratione, cum sederit Filius hominis in sede majestatis suae, sedebitis et vos super sedes (Matth. xix, 28). Alibi quoque promittit idem Agonotheta noster, de coelo spectans et incitans decertantes : Qui vicerit, inquit, dabo ei sedere mecum in throno meo, sicut et ego vici et sedi in throno Patris mei (Apoc. iii, 21). Matri vero, cujus longe differens est meritum, nihilominus differens promittit praemium. Veni, inquit, electa mea, et ponam in te thronum meum. Parum est, inquit, ut judicanti consedeas, nisi et ipsa mihi sedes fias : ut majestatem regnantis eo felicius quo familiarius in te contineas, et specialius prae caeteris incomprehensibilem comprehendas. Continuisti parvulum in gremio : continebis immensum in animo. Fuisti diversorium peregrinantis; eris palatium regnantis. Fuisti tabernaculum pugnaturi in mundo; eris solium triumphantis in coelo. Fuisti thalamus Sponsi incarnati; eris thronus Regis coronati."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Come, he says, my chosen one, and I will set my throne in you. The prerogative of her glory could not be described more expressively or beautifully than by calling her the throne of God who reigns. For the divine majesty seems to grant no soul such full or intimate possession of himself as the soul in which he has chosen to dwell especially above all others. The Lord indeed spoke to the disciples who had become poor and followed him in his poverty : In the regeneration, when the Son of man sits on the seat of his majesty, you too will sit upon seats (Matth. xix, 28). Elsewhere our same Master of the contest, watching from heaven and urging on those who struggle, promises : To him who conquers, he says, I will grant to sit with me on my throne, just as I too conquered and sat on the throne of my Father (Apoc. iii, 21). But to his Mother, whose merit is far different, he promises a reward no less different. Come, he says, my chosen one, and I will set my throne in you. It is little, he says, for you to sit beside me as I judge, unless you yourself become my seat : so that you may contain within yourself the majesty of him who reigns, the more blessedly for being the more intimately, and may embrace the incomprehensible more particularly than all others. You held the little child in your lap : you will hold the boundless one in your soul. You were the lodging of the traveler; you will be the palace of him who reigns. You were the tent of him who was to fight in the world; you will be the throne of him who triumphs in heaven. You were the bridal chamber of the incarnate Bridegroom; you will be the throne of the crowned King.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. O Fili Dei, nihil tibi, nihil in illo tuo displicuit hospitio quod tam libenter repetit, tamque affluenter remunerat tua dignatio. Nihil utique in illo sordidum offendisti, quia nulla erat libido, sed purissima castitas : nihil ruinosum, quia nulla erat superbia, sed fundatissima humilitas : nihil obscurum, quia exclusa infidelitas : nihil angustum, quia diffusa erat charitas. Adornaverat thalamum Virgo prudentissima, te regem Christum non solum hospitem susceptura; sed et sponsum habitura. Adornaverat, inquam, multiplici decore virtutum et gloria, tanto fortasse locupletius, quanto interius totum erat. Hunc ornatum mirabatur qui dicebat, Omnis gloria ejus filiae regis ab intus, in fimbriis aureis, circumamicta varietate (Psal. xliv, 14, 15) : et alius, O quam pulchra est casta generatio cum charitate ! (Sap. iv, 1.) Domum tuam decet Domine, hujusmodi sanctitudo et decor. Is decor te invitavit ut intrares : illexit, ut redires. Intrans benedictionis gratiam multiplicasti : sed rediens cumulasti. Cum intrasti, homo natus es in ea : cum redisti, Deus glorificatus es in ea. Tunc tibi posuisti in ea sacrarium gratiae; nunc autem, thronum gloriae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. O Son of God, nothing displeased you, nothing in that lodging of yours which your graciousness so gladly revisits and so abundantly rewards. Certainly you found nothing defiled there, for there was no lust, but the purest chastity : nothing ruinous, for there was no pride, but humility most firmly founded : nothing dark, for unbelief was excluded : nothing cramped, for charity was poured abroad. The most prudent Virgin had adorned the bridal chamber, ready to receive you, Christ the King, as her guest, and also to have you as her bridegroom. She had adorned it, I say, with the manifold beauty and glory of virtues, perhaps all the more richly because it was wholly inward. He admired this adornment who said, All the glory of her, the king's daughter, is within, in golden fringes, clothed about with variety (Psal. xliv, 14, 15) : and another said, O how beautiful is a chaste generation with charity ! (Sap. iv, 1.) Such holiness and beauty befit your house, Lord. That beauty invited you to enter : it drew you to return. By entering you multiplied the grace of blessing : but by returning you heaped it up. When you entered, you were born in her as man : when you returned, you were glorified in her as God. Then you established in her a sanctuary of grace for yourself; but now, a throne of glory.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Dicuntur quidem et sunt alii throni, quidam scilicet spiritus divini, prae aliis, ut putamus, subjectis ordinibus, Dei praesidentis majestate pleni : quorum quae sit praerogativa, Scriptura non exprimit, licet innuat ex vocabulo quod nonnulla sit. Sed et anima cujuslibet justi dicitur sedes sapientiae. Quae autem nunc es sedes sapientiae, profecto tunc erit et gloriae. Sit igitur illud coeli palatium plenum sedibus et thronis, sedeatque Deus in omnibus, accommodans se et aptans cuique pro meritis. Non immerito tamen creditur, sine injuria vel invidia caeterarum sedium quoddam esse speciale Regis solium excelsum et elevatum super gloriam omnium : Mariam dico exaltatam super choros Angelorum, ut nihil contempletur supra se Mater nisi Filium solum; nihil miretur supra se Regina nisi Regem solum; nihil veneretur supra se Mediatrix nostra nisi Mediatorem solum : cui nos ipsa precibus suis conciliet, commendet, et repraesentet unico Filio suo Jesu Christo, cui est honor et gloria in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. There are indeed others who are called thrones and truly are thrones : certain divine spirits, filled with the majesty of God who presides, beyond the other subordinate orders, as we suppose. Scripture does not express what their prerogative is, although their name suggests that they have some prerogative. The soul of every just person is also called a seat of wisdom. But you who are now a seat of wisdom will assuredly then be a seat of glory as well. Let that heavenly palace, then, be full of seats and thrones, and let God sit in all, accommodating and adapting himself to each according to their merits. Yet it is reasonably believed, without wrong or envy toward the other seats, that there is one particular royal throne, lofty and raised above the glory of them all : I mean Mary exalted above the choirs of Angels, so that the Mother contemplates above herself only the Son; the Queen admires above herself only the King; our Mediatress venerates above herself only the Mediator. May she by her prayers reconcile us to him, commend us to him, and present us to her only Son Jesus Christ, to whom belong honor and glory for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-n011", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(11) In Offic. de Virginibus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-n011", "label": "English", "text": "(11) In the Office of Virgins.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The damaged source footnote number reads (11); it is retained."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The sermon follows historical Psalm numbering. In Isaiah 62:5 it first reads \u201cyour sons will dwell\u201d and later \u201cyour sons dwelt\u201d; both forms follow the edition. Genesis 3 is cited without a verse."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Mary\u2019s motherhood and throne, the contrast with Eve, and the distinction between Mediatress and Mediator are Guerric\u2019s own imagery. \u201cMystery of Catholic unity\u201d translates sacramentum catholicae unitatis without imposing a later sacramental classification. \u201cMaster of the contest\u201d renders agonotheta. In section 2 the translation follows the sense of a grammatically irregular Latin clause. In section 7 a damaged final letter may be the t of est (\u201cis\u201d); the Latin retains the visible es, while the English gives the contextual sense. The phrase about those rejoicing means \u201cone voice of those rejoicing.\u201d"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Assumption Sermon I: Mary's Motherhood and the Throne of God", "topics": ["Assumption", "Mary", "Church", "motherhood", "virginity", "charity", "glory", "contemplation", "hope"], "url": "sermons/guerric-assumption-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:assumption", "calendar_label": "The Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-assumption-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De mutuo amore Jesu et Mariae., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 190-193.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Assumption", "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Song of Songs 5:8", "Matthew 9:20\u201322", "Mark 5:25\u201334", "Isaiah 53:4", "Matthew 8:17", "Sirach 24:29", "John 6:35", "Luke 8:43\u201348"], "occasion": "In Assumptione B. Mariae", "occasion_detail": "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Assumption", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads the bride's words, \"tell my beloved that I languish with love,\" as Mary's on her deathbed. Using a licence he credits to Jerome, he imagines the angels visiting her: she has languished with fear and with sorrow, and now with love. She declines Gabriel's attendance, content with the disciple Jesus loved, and asks for the kiss of her Son's mouth, trusting that his glory has not changed his gentleness. The sermon appears under the heading for the Assumption of Blessed Mary in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De mutuo amore Jesu et Mariae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the mutual love of Jesus and Mary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Filiae Jerusalem, nuntiate dilecto quia amore langueo (Cantic. V, 8). Verba ista quae a nobis hac nocte cantata sunt, qualiter ad Assumptionem beatae Mariae possint videri pertinere, volumus, si placet, cum vestra charitate tractare. Id autem eo genere sermonis faciendum videtur, quo non modo saecularium, sed etiam ecclesiasticarum auctores litterarum nonnunquam usi sunt, maxime in tractandis Canticis, unde haec sunt sumpta. Hoc namque genus sermonis, salva veritate, quadam ampliori utitur libertate, cum videlicet sumpto themate causae, ut beatus Hieronymus ait, non tam loquitur quae revera facta vel dicta sint, quam ejusmodi fuisse negotium ostendit, ut ea quae dicuntur, etsi facta vel dicta non fuerint, fieri tamen et dici potuisse, aut etiam in affectu facientis, vel dicentis fuisse non absurde credi possint. Maria igitur migratura corpore decumbebat, ut est humana infirmitas. Porro filiae Jerusalem quae sursum est, id est angelicae Virtutes, scientes nimirum quia obsequiis Matris captanda est gratia Filii, visitabant satis officiose Dominam suam, Matrem Domini sui. Et fortasse aliquid hujusmodi primo post salutationis officia locuti sunt ei Angeli; sicut speciem humano vultui, ita sermonem humano nimirum conformantes affectui vel usui:"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Daughters of Jerusalem, tell my beloved that I languish with love\" (Cantic. V, 8). We wish, if it please you, to treat with your charity how these words, which have been sung by us this night, may be seen to pertain to the Assumption of blessed Mary. This must be done, it seems, in that kind of discourse which not only the authors of secular letters but also of ecclesiastical letters have sometimes employed, especially in treating the Canticle, from which these words are taken. For this kind of discourse, while preserving the truth, makes use of a certain ampler liberty, since, having taken up the theme of the matter, as blessed Jerome says, it does not so much speak of what was actually done or said, as show that the business was of such a kind that the things which are said, even if they were not done or said, could nevertheless have been done and said, or could even, not absurdly, be believed to have been in the affection of the one doing or speaking. Mary, therefore, about to depart in body, lay abed, as is human weakness. Moreover the daughters of the Jerusalem that is above, that is, the angelic Virtues, knowing well that favor with the Son is to be sought through attentions paid to the Mother, were visiting their Lady, the Mother of their Lord, with due dutifulness. And perhaps the Angels said something of this sort to her first, after the courtesies of greeting; conforming their appearance to a human countenance, and so their speech to human affection or custom:", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Quid est, obsecro, mi Domina, quod sic aegra videris et languida? quid est quod praeter solitum tristis et pigra, ab heri et nudiustertius, sicut solebas, non sancta revisis loca, quorum contemplatione amorem tuum pascebas? Jam per aliquot dies non vidimus te, aut rupem Calvariae ascendentem, ut ibi locum crucis impleres lacrymis; aut ad sepulcrum Filii adorantem gloriam resurrectionis, aut in monte Oliveti deosculantem extrema vestigia ascendentis. --Ob hoc ipsum siquidem creditur in valle Josaphat commorata (ubi et sepulchrum ejus monstratur, ut ait Beatus Hieronymus, in ecclesia miro lapideo fabricata tabulatu), ne scilicet a sanctis locis longius recederet, sed saepius ea invisens, licet omnia memoria teneret, formam tamen gestorum, velut corporaliter in ipsis effigiatam locis, dulcius amplecteretur, ut vel sic suum aliquatenus amorem solaretur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. \"What is it, I beg, my Lady, that you seem thus sick and languishing? What is it that, contrary to your custom, you have been sad and sluggish since yesterday and the day before, and have not, as you were wont, revisited the holy places, by contemplation of which you used to nourish your love? For some days now we have not seen you either ascending the rock of Calvary, there to fill the place of the cross with your tears; or adoring the glory of the resurrection at the Son's tomb; or on the Mount of Olives kissing the last footprints of him who ascended.\" --For this very reason indeed she is believed to have dwelt in the valley of Josaphat (where also her tomb is shown, as blessed Jerome says, in a church wondrously built with a stone roof), namely so that she might not withdraw too far from the holy places, but by visiting them more often, although she held everything in memory, might the more sweetly embrace the form of the deeds, as it were bodily figured in those very places, so that even thus she might in some measure console her love.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Causam itaque percunctantibus illis, cur ab his modo vacaret, et ex toto decumberet: Langueo, inquit. Quare langues? Languor enim quem locum in corpore tuo reperit, in quo salus mundi tam diu habitavit? De corpore Filii tui virtus exibat et sanabat omnes, adeo ut etiam fimbria vestimenti haemorrhoissam sanaret (Matth. IX, 20-22; Marc V, 25-34 et Luc. VIII.43-48): et tu quae tamdiu ipsum in utero, in sinu, in gremio continuisti, ulli infirmitati, aut languori vel accessibilis esse potuisti? Non est, inquit, quod id miremini, si illud ipsum corpus Filii mei, quale aliquando fuerit recordemini. Et ipsum enim quam infirmum, quantisve defectibus subditum fuerit, voluntate tamen, ego novi quae ipsum in utero alui, uberibus lactavi, sinu fovi: nec solius infantiae, sed et sequentium defectus aetatum vidi, et eis, ut potui, ministravi: ad extremum non sine passione mea, ludibria et supplicia passionis et crucis aspexi, per singula discens quam vere de eo noster Isaias dixerit, Vere languores nostros ipse tulit, et dolores nostros ipse portavit (Isai. LIII, 4; Matth. VIII, 17). Cur ergo doleam non eum meo dedisse corpori, quod suo non dedit? Non sum ita delicata aut superba, ut quod ille dignatus est pati, ego quantulacunque ex parte pati non possim aut nolim. Et ille quidem misericordi voluntate, ego naturali necessitate. Sane aliud est sanitas, aliud est sanctitas. Sanctitatem dedit corpori meo, concepti sui corporis sacramento: sanitatem se daturum promisit, suscitati corporis exemplo. Denique, ut minus miremini languorem meum, amore langueo. Langueo plus impatientia amoris, quam passione doloris: plus vulnerata charitate, quam gravata infirmitate."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. When, therefore, they inquired the cause of why she was now free from these things, and lying abed altogether, she said: \"I languish.\" \"Why do you languish? For what place has languor found in your body, in which the salvation of the world dwelt for so long? Power went forth from the body of your Son and healed all, so much so that even the hem of his garment healed the woman with the flow of blood (Matth. IX, 20-22; Marc V, 25-34 et Luc. VIII.43-48): and you, who for so long held him in your womb, in your bosom, in your lap, could you be accessible to any infirmity or languor?\" \"There is no need,\" she said, \"for you to marvel at this, if you remember what that body of my Son was once like. For how weak it was, and to how many defects it was subject, by his own will, I know, who nourished him in the womb, suckled him at my breasts, cherished him in my lap: and I saw the defects not of infancy alone but of the ages that followed, and I ministered to him as I was able: and at the last, not without my own suffering, I beheld the mockeries and torments of the passion and the cross, learning from each thing how truly our Isaiah said of him, 'Surely he has borne our languors, and carried our sorrows' (Isai. LIII, 4; Matth. VIII, 17). Why then should I grieve that he did not give to my body what he did not give to his own? I am not so delicate or proud that I cannot or will not suffer, to whatever small degree, what he deigned to suffer. He indeed suffered by merciful will, I by natural necessity. Yet health is one thing, holiness another. He gave holiness to my body, by the sacrament of his own body conceived; health he promised he would give, by the example of his body raised up. In short, that you may marvel less at my languor, I languish with love. I languish more from the impatience of love than from the suffering of pain: more wounded by charity than weighed down by infirmity.\"", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Heu! inquiunt illi, quam frequentes, imo continuae causae languoris! Bone Jesu, quomodo haec Mater tua postquam te genuit, nunquam fere nisi in languore fuit? Primo languit timore; postea, dolore; nunc, amore. Timore, a nativitate usque ad passionem, cum semper vitam Filii insidiis appeti videret: dolore, toto tempore passionis, donec redivivum reciperet: nunc amore et desiderio felicius, sed mirabilius cruciatur, quia sedentem in coelo non tenet. Quomodo, Jesu bone, qui summi fructus es gaudii, tam longi factus es illi causa martyrii, ut charissimam tibi ipsius animam, tot et tam acuti sine cessatione pertranseant gladii? Sed obsecramus te, Domina, quid vis faciamus tibi? Vis saltem ut resideat hic iste Gabriel symmystes tuus, ut assideat et ministret tibi, qui ab initio conscius et minister mysterii tui, custos quoque tui cubiculi meruit deputari? Non opus est, inquit Virgo. Sufficit mihi meus novus in carne angelus, discipulum dico quem diligebat Jesus, cujus me dilectionis haeredem reliquit, cum illum mihi, et me illi in cruce commendavit: cujus obsequio nihil mihi gratius, quia conversatione et affectu nihil castius, moribus nihil suavius, nihil sincerius fide, nihil sanctius sermone. Nos ergo, aiunt illi, in quo tibi esse poterimus officiosi? Filiae, inquit, Jerusalem, nuntiate dilecto quia amore langueo. Ipse novit quomodo meo medendum sit languori."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. \"Alas,\" they say, \"how frequent \u2014 nay, how continual \u2014 are the causes of your languishing! Good Jesus, how is it that this Mother of yours, after she bore you, has scarcely ever been free from languor? First she languished with fear; afterward, with sorrow; now, with love. With fear, from the nativity even to the passion, since she always saw the life of her Son beset by snares; with sorrow, throughout the whole time of the passion, until she received him alive again; now she is tormented more happily, but more wondrously, by love and longing, since she does not hold him as he sits in heaven. How is it, good Jesus, that you, who are the fruit of highest joy, became for her the cause of so long a martyrdom, that so many and so sharp swords should pass, without ceasing, through that soul of hers so dear to you?\" \"But we beseech you, Lady, what do you wish us to do for you? Do you wish at least that this Gabriel, your companion in the mystery, remain here, to sit by you and minister to you \u2014 he who from the beginning was made privy to, and minister of, your mystery, and deserved also to be appointed guardian of your chamber?\" \"There is no need,\" says the Virgin. \"My new angel in the flesh suffices me \u2014 I mean the disciple whom Jesus loved, whose heir in love he left me to be, when on the cross he commended him to me, and me to him: nothing is more pleasing to me than his service, since in conversation and affection nothing is more chaste, in manner nothing more gentle, in faith nothing more sincere, in speech nothing more holy.\" \"What, then,\" they say, \"can we do to be of service to you?\" \"Daughters of Jerusalem,\" she says, \"tell the beloved that I languish with love. He himself knows how my languor must be healed.\"", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. At nosti, inquiunt, quia cum sciat omnia, de multis quasi nescius interrogat. Si ergo quaesierit, quid illud sit quod tuo vulneri velis adhiberi, quid respondebimus ei? Sodales, inquit, estis sponsi, Gabriel iste meus paranymphus: non puto vobis esse celandum amoris mysterium. Dicam igitur: tantum ne judicer temeraria, quod majora me quaerere videar. Osculetur me osculo oris sui. Si quid mihi conscia essem, contenta essem cum Maria Magdalene osculo pedum, ubi indulgentia sumitur delictorum. Sed quia in omni vita mea non me reprehendit cor meum, non puto me arroganter flagitare de osculo oris, gratiam gaudiorum. Cur autem arrogans videar, si illud os mihi reposco, quod de me sibi formavit ipse conditus et conditor? Cum parvulum tenebam inter brachia, quoties osculari speciosum forma prae filiis hominum mihi libebat, satis licebat: nunquam faciem avertebat, nunquam matrem repellebat. Et si fortassis impatientia desiderii eram nimia; ipse tamen more suo, morem matri gerebat. Gaudebat eam replere gratia, quae diffusa erat in labiis ejus, et dulcedine qua totus erat plenus, totus concupiscentia et desiderium, castarum scilicet animarum. Unde sicut ipse de se confitetur, Qui edunt me adhuc esurient, et qui bibunt me adhuc sitient (Eccli. XXIV, 29, et Joan. VI, 35): quanto gratiam oris illius gustavi suavius, tanto nimirum repeto nunc ardentius. Crevit quidem gloria et majestate: sed nihil ab illa ingenita mutatus est mansuetudine et bonitate. Nihil ad illum illud superbiae saecularis elogium: \u201cHonores mutant mores.\u201d Sublimior est, sed non superbior; gloriosior, sed non dedignantior. Non fastidiet matrem quam elegit, nec aeternam electionem suam novo judicio reprobabit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. \"But you know,\" they say, \"that although he knows all things, he asks about many things as though ignorant. If, then, he should ask what it is you wish applied to your wound, what shall we answer him?\" \"You are companions of the bridegroom,\" she says, \"and this Gabriel of mine is his paranymph: I do not think the mystery of love should be hidden from you. I will tell it, then \u2014 only let me not be judged presumptuous for seeming to seek things greater than myself. Let him kiss me with the kiss of his mouth. If I were conscious of any guilt in myself, I would be content, like Mary Magdalene, with the kiss of the feet, where pardon of sins is received. But because, throughout my whole life, my heart does not reproach me, I do not think that in demanding the kiss of the mouth I am arrogantly asking for the grace of joys. But why should I seem arrogant, if I ask back for myself that mouth which he, both the one fashioned and the fashioner, formed for himself out of me? When I held him as a little one between my arms, as often as it pleased me to kiss him who was beautiful in form above the sons of men, it was granted me freely enough: he never turned away his face, never repelled his mother. And if perhaps I was too impatient in my longing, he nevertheless, after his own manner, indulged his mother's way. He rejoiced to fill her with the grace that was poured out upon his lips, and with the sweetness of which he was wholly full \u2014 wholly the desire and longing, namely, of chaste souls. Whence, as he himself confesses of himself, 'They who eat me shall still hunger, and they who drink me shall still thirst' (Eccli. XXIV, 29, et Joan. VI, 35): the more sweetly I have tasted the grace of his mouth, the more ardently, surely, do I now seek it again. He has indeed grown in glory and majesty; but he has in no way been changed from that innate gentleness and goodness. To him applies nothing of that maxim of worldly pride: 'Honors change manners.' He is more exalted, but not more proud; more glorious, but not more disdainful. He will not disdain the mother whom he chose, nor by some new judgment reject his eternal election.\"", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6.\n\nNe timeas, Maria, ait Gabriel more suo: invenisti enim gratiam apud Deum. Etiam in caeteris qui longe jacent tuis sub vestigiis, probare consuevit improbitatem hujus affectus et precis; qui nihil nimium, aut injuriosum sibi deputat, quod in eum vera duntaxat charitas praesumat. Et conversus ad sociam multitudinem: Eamus, inquit, eamus ne ipsi Filio facere videamur injuriam, si gloriam Matris differamus. Cum ergo reversi nuntiassent haec Domino suo, quid putamus Jesum nisi tale aliquid locutum? Ego sum qui patrem et matrem filiis honorandos commendavi; ego ut facerem quod docui, et exemplo essem aliis, ut Patrem honorarem, in terram descendi: nihilominus, ut Matrem honorarem, in coelum reascendi. Ascendi et praeparavi ei locum, thronum gloriae; ut a dextris Regis Regina coronata consedeat in vestitu deaurato, circumdata varietate. Neque hoc dico, quod in parte thronus ei collocetur: quin potius ipsa erit thronus meus. Veni igitur, electa mea, et ponam in te thronum meum. In te mihi quamdam regni sedem constituam, de te judicia decernam, per te preces exaudiam. Nullus mihi plus ministravit in humilitate mea. Communicasti mihi praeter alia quod homo sum: communicabo tibi quod Deus sum. Flagitabas osculum oris, quinimo tota de toto osculaberis. Non imprimam labia labiis, sed spiritum spiritui osculo perpetuo et indissolubili: quia concupivi speciem tuam, etiam desiderantius quam tu meam; nec satis glorificatus videbor mihi, donec tu conglorificeris. Gloria tibi, Domine Jesu, subintulit chorus Angelorum. Gloria tibi, Domine Jesu, ingeminet coetus fidelium. Glorificatio Matris tibi proficiat ad gloriam, nobis ad veniam; te praestante cui est honor, et gloria, per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6.\n\n\"Do not fear, Mary,\" says Gabriel, in his accustomed way, \"for you have found grace with God.\" Even in others who lie far beneath your footsteps, he is accustomed to show that this boldness of affection and petition is no fault; for he counts nothing excessive or injurious to himself which true charity alone presumes to ask of him. And turning to the company gathered with him, he said, \"Let us go, let us go, lest we ourselves seem to do injury to the Son, if we delay the Mother's glory.\" When, then, they had returned and reported these things to their Lord, what do we suppose Jesus said but something like this? \"I am he who commended fathers and mothers to be honored by their children; I, in order to do what I taught, and to be an example to others, that I might honor my Father, descended to earth; nonetheless, that I might honor my Mother, I ascended again to heaven. I have ascended and prepared a place for her, a throne of glory, that at the right hand of the King the Queen, crowned, may sit in gilded raiment, surrounded with manifold adornment. Nor do I say this as though a throne were set apart for her in some portion: rather, she herself shall be my throne. Come, then, my chosen one, and I will set my throne in you. In you I will establish for myself a certain seat of my kingdom; from you I will decree judgments; through you I will grant hearing to prayers. No one has ministered more to me in my humility. You shared with me, besides other things, that I am man: I will share with you that I am God. You were begging for the kiss of the mouth; nay rather, you shall be kissed wholly, by the whole of me. I will not press lip to lip, but spirit to spirit, in a perpetual and indissoluble kiss; for I have desired your beauty, even more longingly than you have desired mine; nor shall I seem to myself sufficiently glorified, until you are glorified together with me.\" \"Glory to you, Lord Jesus,\" the choir of Angels added. \"Glory to you, Lord Jesus,\" let the assembly of the faithful repeat again and again. May the glorification of the Mother avail for your glory, and for our pardon; through you granting it, to whom is honor and glory, for all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Assumption Sermon II", "topics": ["Assumption", "Mary", "love of God", "Song of Songs", "angels", "longing for God"], "url": "sermons/guerric-assumption-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:assumption", "calendar_label": "The Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-assumption-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., De quiete spirituali., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 193-197.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Assumption", "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Sirach 24:11", "Song of Songs 2:5", "Sirach 34:19", "Psalm 139:8 (historical numbering)", "Genesis 49:15", "Hebrews 4:3", "Psalm 94:12 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 11:9 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 13:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 26:4, 8 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 114:7 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 14:8\u20139", "Isaiah 28:12", "Psalm 40:2 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 25:35", "Isaiah 66:2", "Psalm 1:4 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 57:20", "1 Thessalonians 4:11", "Proverbs 7:10\u201312", "1 Thessalonians 5:14", "2 Thessalonians 3:11\u201312, 14", "Isaiah 30:15"], "occasion": "In Assumptione B. Mariae", "occasion_detail": "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Assumption", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Preaching on a feast that gives the brothers rest from the harvest, Guerric takes the words \"In all things I sought rest\" as the voice of Wisdom, of the Church and of Mary, who finds rest today. Christ still seeks rest among the humble and quiet, while the restless are driven like a wheel. Guerric urges quiet through humility and manual work, and recalls Paul's rebuke of those who walk unquietly. The sermon appears under the heading for the Assumption of Blessed Mary in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De quiete spirituali."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On spiritual rest.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1.\n\nIn omnibus requiem quaesivi (Eccli. XXIV, 11). Grata quies fessis. Grate igitur et opportune vobis, qui fessi estis, dies iste quietis et feriationis intervenit, ut dum sanctae Dei Genitricis requiem celebramus, non solum corpora recreentur hac diurna quiete a labore messis, sed etiam corda respirent in recordationem et amorem illius aeternae quietis. Et tibi tamen, o fratres, ibi etiam metetis, sed requiem metetis qui nunc laborem hujus messis seminatis. Fructus hujus laboris, illa requies erit: requies a labore, merces pro labore, cujus etiam recordatio fideles vires reparat in labore. Umbra est aestuantibus, cibus esurientibus. Sic enim ait illa, quae memoriam abundantiae suavitatis ejus eructabat: Sub umbra illius quem desideraveram sedi, et fructus ejus dulcis gutturi meo (Cantic. II, 5). O qui laboratis, o qui portatis pondus diei et aestus! sub umbra alarum Jesu invenietis requiem animabus vestris, firmamentum, ut scriptum est, virtutis, tegimen ardoris, umbraculum meridiani (Eccli. XXXIV, 19); ut ex sententia cordis sit illa confessio oris: Domine, Domine, virtus salutis meae, obumbrasti super caput meum in die belli (Psal. CXXXIX, 8), in die aestus et laboris, certaminis et tentationis. Cum enim meditatio aeternae requiei capitibus laborantium obumbrat, non solum ab aestu tentationum refrigerat, sed etiam ad laborem animos innovat, juxta illud quod scriptum est de asino forti Issachar, Vidit requiem quod esset bona, et terram quod optima, et supposuit humerum ad portandum (Gen. XLIX, 15): id est, desiderio illius quietis et haereditatis sponte humiliavit se ad laborandum. Et iste ergo dicere potuit: In omnibus requiem quaesivi, et in haereditate Domini morabor."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1.\n\n\"In all things I sought rest\" (Eccli. XXIV, 11). Rest is pleasant to the weary. Pleasantly, then, and opportunely for you who are weary, this day of rest and holiday has come, so that while we celebrate the rest of the holy Mother of God, not only may our bodies be refreshed by this day's rest from the labor of harvest, but our hearts too may breathe again in remembrance and love of that eternal rest. And yet for you, O brothers, there too you shall reap \u2014 but you who now sow the labor of this harvest shall reap rest. The fruit of this labor will be that rest: rest from labor, wages for labor \u2014 the very remembrance of which restores the faithful's strength in labor. It is shade for those who are scorched, food for those who hunger. For thus she speaks, who poured forth the memory of his abundant sweetness: \"Under the shadow of him whom I desired I sat, and his fruit was sweet to my throat\" (Cantic. II, 5). O you who labor, O you who bear the burden of the day and the heat! Under the shadow of Jesus' wings you will find rest for your souls \u2014 a stronghold of strength, as it is written, a covering from the heat, a shade from the noonday sun (Eccli. XXXIV, 19); so that from the conviction of the heart may come that confession of the mouth: \"Lord, Lord, the strength of my salvation, you have overshadowed my head in the day of battle\" (Psal. CXXXIX, 8), in the day of heat and labor, of struggle and temptation. For when meditation on eternal rest overshadows the heads of those who labor, it not only cools them from the heat of temptations but also renews their minds for labor, according to what is written of the strong ass Issachar: \"He saw rest, that it was good, and the land, that it was excellent, and he bowed his shoulder to bear burdens\" (Gen. XLIX, 15): that is, out of desire for that rest and inheritance, he willingly humbled himself to labor. And so he too could say: In all things I sought rest, and I will abide in the inheritance of the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2.\n\nFelix qui in omnibus laboribus et viis suis requiem quaerit beatam: semper festinans ingredi, ut Apostolus monet, in illam requiem (Hebr. IV, 3), ejus desiderio corpus affligens, animum autem jam illi requiei parans et componens: cum omnibus hominibus, quod ex ipso est, pacem habens; requiem et otium Mariae voluntate praeeligens, laborem autem et negotium Marthae necessitate quidem suscipiens; sed quanta praevalet pace et quiete spiritus adimplens, et semper ad illud unum necessarium de illa multiplici distractione sese recolligens. Homo hujusmodi etiam cum laborat, quiescit: sicut e diverso impius etiam cum quiescit, laborat. Quomodo enim quiescerent, quibus Deus immittit rotam malorum: quibus juravit in ira sua, Si introibunt in requiem meam? (Psal. XCIV, 12.) Extra illam non est nisi afflictio et miseria; et in circuitu ejus tempestas valida: sicut et in natura rerum fit, ut quidquid extra simplicitatem et unitatem puncti fuerit, in motu et agitatione sit; tantoque concitatiore quilibet circulus impetu rotetur, quanto ab immobilitate principii sui, id est, a medietate centri recesserit longius. Sane in circuitu impii ambulant (Psal. XI, 9), quibus Deus immittit rotam malorum: ideoque in illam internam et aeternam requiem intrare non possunt. Ideo contritio et infelicitas in viis eorum, quia viam pacis non cognoverunt (Psal. XIII, 3): quia nec quaesierunt, ut dicere possint: In omnibus requiem quaesivi; ut videlicet in multiplicitate actuum suorum, quibus turbantur, et turbant, unum quod est necessarium, cogitent aut requirant. Justorum potius est vox illa, qui et dicere possunt, Unam petii a Domino, hanc requiram. Vultum tuum, Domine, quaesivi, vultum tuum requiram (Psal. XXVI, 4, 8): qui nonnisi quietis illius amore laborant, qui omnino praeoptant ingredi putredinem in ossibus suis, ut requiescant in die tribulationis, quam in bonis ducere dies suos, et in puncto ad infernum descendere."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2.\n\nHappy is he who in all his labors and ways seeks blessed rest: always hastening, as the Apostle counsels, to enter into that rest (Hebr. IV, 3), afflicting his body with desire for it, yet already preparing and composing his mind for that rest; having peace with all men, so far as it depends on him; preferring by choice the rest and leisure of Mary, yet undertaking, by necessity indeed, the labor and business of Martha \u2014 but carrying it out with as much peace and quiet of spirit as he can, and always gathering himself back, out of that manifold distraction, to that one thing which is necessary. Such a man, even when he labors, rests; just as, on the contrary, the wicked man, even when he rests, labors. For how could they rest, upon whom God sends the wheel of evils, to whom he swore in his wrath, \"If they shall enter into my rest\" (Psal. XCIV, 12.)? Outside that rest there is nothing but affliction and misery; and round about it a violent storm \u2014 just as happens also in the nature of things, that whatever is outside the simplicity and unity of the point is in motion and agitation; and the farther any circle recedes from the immobility of its origin, that is, from the center-point, the more violent is the impetus with which it is driven round. Indeed, the wicked walk round about (Psal. XI, 9), upon whom God sends the wheel of evils: and therefore they cannot enter into that inward and eternal rest. Therefore ruin and unhappiness are in their ways, because they have not known the way of peace (Psal. XIII, 3): because they have not even sought it, so that they might say, In all things I sought rest \u2014 that is, so that amid the multiplicity of their actions, by which they are disturbed and by which they disturb others, they might think of or seek that one thing which is necessary. Rather that voice belongs to the just, who can also say, \"One thing I have asked of the Lord, this will I seek. Your face, Lord, have I sought; your face will I seek\" (Psal. XXVI, 4, 8): who labor only out of love of that rest \u2014 who altogether prefer that rottenness enter into their bones, that they may rest in the day of tribulation, rather than spend their days in good things and in a moment go down to hell.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Si quis tamen curiosius inquirere velit, cujus potissimum vox illa sit, In omnibus requiem quaesivi: vox est utique Sapientiae, vox est Ecclesiae, vox est Mariae, vox est cujuslibet sapientis animae. Sapientia in omnibus requiem quaesivit, sed in solis humilibus invenit. Ecclesia in omnibus mundi nationibus requiem quaesivit, sed in solis credentibus invenit. Maria, sicut et quaelibet fidelis anima, in omnibus actionibus suis requiem quaesivit; sed hodie demum invenit, cum post persecutionem Herodis, et fugam Aegypti, post tot insidias et atrocitates Judaicae impietatis, post tot passiones suas, de passione et morte Filii; postquam tot et tam acerbi ipsius animam pertransierunt gladii, tandem hodie dicere datum est ei: Convertere, anima, in requiem tuam, quia Dominus benefecit tibi (Psal. CXIV, 7). Qui creavit me, creatus ex me, requievit in tabernaculo corporis mei, negare non poterit mihi requiem coeli sui. Qui enim gratiam ultro cumulat aliis, par pari quomodo non referet matri? Perge, Maria, perge secura in bonis Filii tui; fiducialiter age tanquam regina, mater Regis et sponsa. Requiem quaerebas, sed amplioris gloriae est, quod tibi debetur, regnum et potestas. Indivisum habere tecum cupit imperium, cui tecum in carne una, et uno spiritu indivisum fuit pietatis et unitatis mysterium: dum scilicet salvo honore naturae, geminato munere gratiae, juncta est mater in matrimonium. Requiesce igitur, o felix, inter brachia Sponsi. Replicabit tibi, ni fallor, inter amplexus et oscula, quam suaviter requieverit in tabernaculo corporis tui, quam suavis in cubiculo cordis tui. Non est injustus Deus, o fratres, ut obliviscatur operis boni: vivit semper apud eum memoria beneficii semel accepti. Beatus, apud quem Deus vel semel requiem invenerit, in cujus tabernaculo vel hora una requieverit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Yet if anyone wishes to inquire more carefully whose voice above all this is, \"In all things I sought rest\": it is indeed the voice of Wisdom, the voice of the Church, the voice of Mary, the voice of any wise soul. Wisdom sought rest in all things, but found it only in the humble. The Church sought rest among all the nations of the world, but found it only among believers. Mary, like any faithful soul, sought rest in all her actions; but only today has she finally found it, when, after the persecution of Herod, and the flight into Egypt, after so many plots and atrocities of Jewish impiety, after so many sufferings of her own concerning the passion and death of her Son\u2014after so many bitter swords had pierced her soul\u2014it was at last granted to her today to say: \"Return, O my soul, into thy rest, for the Lord hath been bountiful to thee\" (Psal. CXIV, 7). He who created me, having been created from me, rested in the tabernacle of my body; he cannot deny me the rest of his heaven. For he who freely heaps grace upon others\u2014how will he not repay his mother in like measure? Go forward, Mary, go forward secure in the goods of your Son; act with confidence as a queen, mother of the King and his spouse. You were seeking rest, but what is owed to you is of a greater glory\u2014kingdom and power. He desires to have undivided rule with you, he with whom you had, in one flesh and one spirit, an undivided mystery of devotion and unity: since, with the honor of nature preserved, and the gift of grace doubled, the mother was joined to him in marriage. Rest, then, O blessed one, between the arms of the Bridegroom. He will tell you, unless I am mistaken, amid embraces and kisses, how sweetly he rested in the tabernacle of your body, how sweet he was in the chamber of your heart. God is not unjust, O brothers, that he should forget a good work: the memory of a benefit once received lives always with him. Blessed is he with whom God has found rest even once, in whose tabernacle he has rested even for a single hour.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Ecce nunc quoque Sapientia clamitat in plateis: in omnibus requiem quaesivi. Pulsavi, nec fuit qui aperiret: vocavi, nec fuit qui responderet. Filius hominis, factus, secundum prophetam, velut vir vagus, et quasi viator declinans ad manendum (Jerem. XIV, 9, 8), non habet ubi reclinet caput suum: foris stat plenus caput rore, et cincinnos guttis noctium. Quis ille in nobis tam humanus atque hospitalis, ut surgat et aperiat ei, et inducat in cubiculum suum; aut certe ostendat ei coenaculum grande stratum, ubi cum discipulis pascha manducet novum? Hoc enim dico, fratres, nisi requiem quam quaerit, invenerit apud nos, nec nos optatam requiem inveniemus in illo. Ait quidem Dominus per prophetam: Haec est requies mea, reficite lassum, et hoc est meum refrigerium (Isa. XXVIII, 12). Beatus qui intelligit super egenum et pauperem: in die mala (Psal. XL, 2) parabit ei Dominus vicissitudine debita requiem et refrigerium. Si ergo humanitatem membris suis exhibitam Deus imputat sibi: quanto magis quod ipsius fit spiritui, cum gratiarum actione recolet, Hospes, inquiens, fui et suscepistis me? (Matth. XXV, 35.) Nunquid enim multorum paupertas sanctorum, quae vagos colligere, esurientes pascere non sufficit, Domino qui magis apud pauperes hospitari consuevit, inhumana et inhospitalis erit? Super quem autem requiescam, inquit, nisi super humilem et quietum, et trementem verba mea? (Isai. LXVI, 2.) O humilitas angusta tibi, ampla divinitati; pauper et insufficiens tibi, sufficiens ei quem non capit orbis: copiose ac deliciose reficiens illum, qui et Angelos pascit. Super quem, inquit, requiescam nisi super humilem? In omnibus requiem quaesivi: sed apud humilem ancillam inveni. Non est inventa similis illi in gratia humilitatis: ideo in plenitudine humilitatis requievit etiam corporaliter omnis plenitudo divinitatis. Quanquam aliter requieverit in Filio: quia etsi Mater humillima, longe tamen Filius humilior. Idcirco Spiritus septiformis non solum super ipsum requievit, sed etiam omnibus qui mites et humiles ab eo esse didicerunt, diversas in eo felicissimae quietis mansiones praeparavit; imo totum ipsum quoddam aureum fecit reclinatorium ad quiescendum. Hujus nimirum reclinatorii beatissimam requiem visus est quadam specie praelibasse, qui supra pectus ejus meruit in coena recumbere."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Behold, even now Wisdom cries aloud in the streets: \"In all things I sought rest.\" I knocked, and there was none to open; I called, and there was none to answer. The Son of man, made, according to the prophet, like a wandering man, and like a traveler turning aside to lodge (Jerem. XIV, 9, 8), has nowhere to lay his head: he stands outside, his head full of dew, and his locks with the drops of the night. Who among us is so humane and hospitable as to rise and open to him, and bring him into his chamber; or at least show him a large furnished upper room, where he may eat the new Passover with his disciples? For I say this, brothers: unless he finds among us the rest he seeks, neither shall we find in him the rest we desire. The Lord indeed says through the prophet: \"This is my rest, refresh the weary, and this is my refreshment\" (Isa. XXVIII, 12). Blessed is he who understands concerning the needy and the poor: in the evil day (Psal. XL, 2) the Lord will prepare for him, in due return, rest and refreshment. If then God counts as done to himself the kindness shown to his members: how much more will he, giving thanks, recall what was done to his own spirit, saying, \"I was a stranger, and you took me in\" (Matth. XXV, 35.)? For shall the poverty of many saints, which does not suffice to gather in the wandering or feed the hungry, be inhospitable and inhumane to the Lord, who is accustomed rather to lodge among the poor? \"But upon whom shall I rest,\" he says, \"but upon him that is humble and quiet, and trembleth at my words?\" (Isai. LXVI, 2.) O humility, narrow to yourself, but spacious to divinity; poor and insufficient in yourself, yet sufficient for him whom the world cannot contain: abundantly and delightfully refreshing him who feeds even the Angels. \"Upon whom,\" he says, \"shall I rest but upon the humble?\" \"In all things I sought rest\": but I found it with a humble handmaid. None was found like her in the grace of humility: therefore in the fullness of her humility the whole fullness of divinity rested even bodily. Though it rested differently in the Son: for although the Mother was most humble, the Son was far more humble still. For this reason the sevenfold Spirit not only rested upon him, but also prepared various dwellings of most blessed rest in him for all who learned from him to be meek and humble; indeed, he made him wholly a kind of golden resting-place for repose. The most blessed rest of this resting-place he who deserved to recline upon his breast at the supper seemed, in some fashion, to have tasted beforehand.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sed adhuc libet intueri, quam plenum rationis et congruentiae sit quod ait: Super quem requiescam nisi super humilem et quietum? Quomodo namque super inquietum aliquid quiesceret? quomodo super basem, quae nutaret aut vacillaret, columna immobilis staret? Quis autem, nisi humilis, quietus esse potest? quis, nisi humilis in pace quieti et modesti spiritus, seipsum possidere potest? Impium projicit ventus a facie terrae (Psal. I, 4), circumferturque omni vento doctrinae. Impius, inquit propheta, quasi mare fervens, quod quiescere non potest (Isai. LVII, 20). Fervet ira, aestuat avaritia, intumescit superbia, se ipsum jugiter pugna quatit intestina, ac seditione collidit domestica. Ut ergo requiescat in vobis, o fratres mei, amator ille largitorque quietis, date operam, juxta consilium Apostoli, ut quieti sitis. Quonam modo istud erit? Dico, inquit, vobis: Ut vestrum negotium agatis, et operemini manibus vestris (I Thess. IV, 11). Opus est onus, quo veluti pondus navibus, ita quies et gravitas inquietis additur cordibus, sed exterioris hominis fundatur atque componitur status. Magnorum initium malorum est, sicut legistis, mulier vaga, quietis impatiens, nec valens consistere in domo pedibus suis: nunc foris, nunc in plateis, nunc juxta angulos insidians (Prov. VII, 10-12). Nec sine causa est quod Magister gentium hoc inquietudinis malum sic habet suspectum, ut non solum correptione, sed etiam separatione censeat illud persequendum. Rogamus, inquit, vos, fratres, corripite inquietos (I Thess. V, 14). Et item in secunda ad eosdem Thessalonicenses, inter alia quae super inquietos velut e coelis tonabat: Audivimus, inquit, inter vos quosdam ambulantes inquiete, nihil operantes, sed curiose agentes. Hic qui ejusmodi sunt denuntiamus, ut cum silentio operantes, suum panem manducent. Quod si quis non obedit verbo nostro per epistolam, hunc notate: et ne commisceamini cum illo, ut confundatur (II Thess. III, 11, 12, 14)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But it is still worth considering how full of reason and fittingness is what he says: Upon whom shall I rest, but upon him that is humble and quiet? For how could he rest upon something unquiet? How could an immovable column stand upon a base that trembled or wobbled? Who, indeed, except the humble, can be quiet? Who, except the humble man at peace, of a quiet and modest spirit, can possess himself? The wind casts away the wicked from the face of the earth (Psal. I, 4), and he is carried about by every wind of doctrine. The wicked man, says the prophet, is like the raging sea, which cannot rest (Isai. LVII, 20). He burns with anger, seethes with avarice, swells with pride, perpetually shakes himself with inward battle, and collides in domestic sedition. Therefore, my brothers, that he who loves and bestows rest may rest in you, strive, according to the counsel of the Apostle, to be quiet. How shall this be? I say to you, he says: that you do your own business, and work with your own hands (I Thess. IV, 11). Work is a burden, by which, just as weight is added to ships, so quiet and gravity are added to unquiet hearts, and the condition of the outer man is founded and composed. The beginning of great evils is, as you have read, a wandering woman, impatient of rest, unable to abide with her feet in her own house: now abroad, now in the streets, now lying in wait near the corners (Prov. VII, 10-12). It is not without cause that the Teacher of the Gentiles holds this evil of restlessness in such suspicion that he thinks it should be pursued not only by rebuke but even by separation. We beseech you, brothers, he says, rebuke the unquiet (I Thess. V, 14). And again in the second epistle to the same Thessalonians, among other things which he thundered down as from heaven against the unquiet: We have heard, he says, that there are some among you who walk unquietly, doing no work, but being busy about other men's business. Now them that are such, we charge, that working with silence, they eat their own bread. And if any man obey not our word by this epistle, note that man: and do not keep company with him, that he may be ashamed (II Thess. III, 11, 12, 14).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Si quis forte (quod absit!) ejusmodi invenitur inter vos, pulchrius et honestius, antequam tam graviter et publice notetur, ultro confundetur, inde scilicet, quia notabilis est, et dissimulatur; reprehensibilis, et toleratur. Sic autem confundatur, ut confusione corrigatur, correptione laetetur, laetificetque non modo nos, sed Spiritum Dei: qui cum dicat, In omnibus requiem quaesivi, non invenit eam nisi in quietis, nec praestat nisi quietis. Unde et inquietos per prophetam revocat et compescit: Si revertamini, et quiescatis, salvi eritis (Isai. XXX, 15). Omnes ergo pariter sic operam demus, ut quieti simus, ut in quiete nostra semper in meditatione aeternae quietis occupemur, ac desiderio illius ad omnem parati laborem inveniamur. Hoc nobis impetret beata Dei Genitrix, cujus requiem celebramus, ab eo qui requievit in tabernaculo corporis et cordis ejus. Ipse est requies aeterna Christus Jesus; cui est honor, et gloria per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. If perhaps (which God forbid!) someone of this sort is found among you, it would be fairer and more decent that, before he is so gravely and publicly noted, he should be shamed of his own accord beforehand\u2014precisely because he is notable, and yet is overlooked; reprehensible, and yet is tolerated. But let him be so shamed that by shame he may be corrected, that he may rejoice in correction, and may give joy not only to us but to the Spirit of God: who, since he says, In all things I sought rest, finds it only in the quiet, and grants it only to the quiet. Hence through the prophet he recalls and restrains the unquiet: If you return and be quiet, you shall be saved (Isai. XXX, 15). Let us all, then, equally take care to be quiet, so that in our quiet we may always be occupied in meditation on eternal rest, and may be found ready, by desire of that rest, for every labor. May the blessed Mother of God, whose rest we celebrate, obtain this for us from him who rested in the tabernacle of her body and heart. He himself is eternal rest, Christ Jesus; to whom be honor and glory for all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Assumption Sermon III", "topics": ["Assumption", "Mary", "rest", "humility", "manual labor", "monastic life"], "url": "sermons/guerric-assumption-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:assumption", "calendar_label": "The Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-assumption-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO IV, Quod beata Virgo Mariae et Marthae officia implevit., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 197-200.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Assumption", "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Luke 10:42", "Sirach 24:12", "Luke 10:39", "Sirach 2:19, 51 (printed locator; passage from Luke 2:19, 51)", "John 19:25", "John 2:4", "Matthew 12:47\u201348", "Luke 2:49", "Matthew 12:49", "Isaiah 62:4", "Luke 2:19, 51", "Isaiah 53:11", "Genesis 29", "Sirach 32:1\u20133", "Hosea 10:12", "2 Corinthians 9:6", "Psalm 111:2 (historical numbering)", "Luke 1:48", "Song of Songs 6:8"], "occasion": "In Assumptione B. Mariae", "occasion_detail": "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Assumption", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric applies \"Mary has chosen the best part\" to the Mother of the Lord, who served Christ as Martha did and listened as her sister did. He explains why Christ seemed to set his mother aside, and why he entrusted her to the virgin disciple John. Contemplation is the reward of faithful labor, as Leah came before Rachel, and Mary now reaps what she sowed. The sermon appears under the heading for the Assumption of Blessed Mary in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quod beata Virgo Mariae et Marthae officia implevit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "That the Blessed Virgin fulfilled the offices of Mary and of Martha.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Maria optimam partem elegit (Luc. X, 42). Hoc de Maria sorore Marthae scriptum est: sed in Maria matre Domini plenius et sanctius impletum est. Hodie namque beata virgo Maria optimam partem elegit, imo quam dudum elegerat, hodie perpetuo possidendam accepit: ut scilicet inseparabiliter adhaereat Domino, perenniter fruatur Dei Verbo. Nec alienum aut incongruum, si quod de illa Maria dictum est, in istam transumatur; cum ad convenientiam transformationis, similitudo non solum nominis, sed etiam operis suffragetur. Illa siquidem Dominum hospitio tecti, haec thalamo suscepit uteri. Et qui creavit me, inquit, requievit in tabernaculo meo (Eccli. XXIV, 12). Illa sedens secus pedes Domini audiebat verbum illius (Luc. X, 39): haec sedula circa curam humanitatis ejus, conservabat omnia verba quae de illo erant, conferens in corde suo (Eccli. II, 19, 51). Sed et cum evangelizans circumiret Jesus civitates et castella, Maria comes individua vestigiis ejus adhaerebat, pendebatque ex ore docentis: adeo, ut nec procella persecutionis, nec horrore supplicii a consectatu Filii et Magistri potuerit absterreri, Stabat, inquit, juxta crucem Jesu Maria mater ejus (Joan. XIX, 25). Plane Mater, quae nec in terrore mortis filium deserebat. Quomodo enim morte terreri poterat, cujus charitas fortis ut mors, imo fortior quam mors erat? Plane juxta crucem Jesu stabat, cujus mentem dolor crucis simul cruci figebat, suamque ipsius animam tam multiplex pertransibat gladius, quantis confossum corpus Filii cernebat vulneribus. Merito igitur ibi mater agnita est, et cura ipsius idoneo tutori delegata est, ubi maxime probata est et Matris ad Filium sincera charitas, et Filii de Matre vera humanitas. Nam alias velut dissimulasse Matrem visus est: sive cum in nuptiis flagitanti miraculum ait, Quid mihi et tibi, mulier? (Joan. II, 4.) sive cum in mediis sermonibus Evangelii dicenti cuidam, Ecce mater tua et fratres tui stant quaerentes te: Quae est, inquit, mater mea? (Matth. XII, 47, 48.) Sed et Matri, cum miraculum posceret, sic respondendum erat, ut aliunde quam a Matre se miracula habere significaret: et illi qui sermonem Evangelii parentes annuntiando interrumpebat, melius respondere non poterat, ut videlicet spiritualia carnalibus praeferenda monstraret. Ac si more suo diceret parentibus, occupatum in negotio Evangelii quaerentibus: Quid est quod me quaeritis? Nescitis quia in his quae Patris mei sunt oportet me esse? (Luc. II, 49.)"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Mary has chosen the best part (Luc. X, 42). This was written of Mary the sister of Martha; but it was fulfilled more fully and more holily in Mary the Mother of the Lord. For today the blessed Virgin Mary has chosen the best part\u2014or rather, the part which she had long ago chosen, she today received to possess forever: namely, that she might cling inseparably to the Lord, and enjoy the Word of God perpetually. Nor is it foreign or unfitting that what was said of that other Mary should be transferred to this one; since, for the fittingness of the transfer, the likeness supports it not only in name but also in deed. For the one received the Lord under the hospitality of a roof; this one, in the bridal chamber of her womb. And 'He who created me,' she says, 'rested in my tabernacle' (Eccli. XXIV, 12). The one, sitting beside the Lord's feet, was listening to his word (Luc. X, 39); this one, diligently occupied with the care of his humanity, kept all the words concerning him, pondering them in her heart (Eccli. II, 19, 51). But also, when Jesus went about preaching the gospel through the cities and towns, Mary, an inseparable companion, clung to his footsteps, and hung upon the mouth of the teacher: so much so that neither the storm of persecution nor the horror of the punishment could frighten her away from following her Son and Master. 'There stood,' it says, 'by the cross of Jesus, Mary his mother' (Joan. XIX, 25). Truly a Mother, who did not abandon her son even in the terror of death. For how could she be terrified by death, whose love was strong as death\u2014indeed, stronger than death? Truly she stood beside the cross of Jesus, whose mind the sorrow of the cross fixed to the cross at the same time, and through her own soul there passed a sword as manifold as the wounds with which she saw the body of her Son pierced. Deservedly, then, she was there acknowledged as mother, and the care of her was entrusted to a fitting guardian, at the very place where both the Mother's sincere love for her Son and the Son's true humanity toward his Mother were most fully proven. For elsewhere he seemed, as it were, to disregard his Mother: whether when, at the wedding, as she urged him to a miracle, he said, 'Woman, what is that to me and to you?' (Joan. II, 4.); or when, in the midst of the discourses of the Gospel, to one who said, 'Behold, your mother and your brothers stand seeking you,' he said, 'Who is my mother?' (Matth. XII, 47, 48.). But to his Mother, when she was asking for a miracle, he had to answer in such a way as to show that he had his miracles from some source other than his Mother; and to the one who was interrupting the discourse of the Gospel by announcing his kinsfolk, he could not have answered better, in order to show that spiritual things are to be preferred to carnal ones. As if, according to his custom, he were saying to his parents, who sought him while he was occupied in the business of the Gospel: 'Why is it that you seek me? Did you not know that I must be about those things which are my Father's?' (Luc. II, 49.).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Absit enim ut Matrem aspernatus sit, qui tanta cura de parentibus honorandis legem dedit! Absit, inquam, ut in terra fastidierit Matrem, cujus e coelo concupivit decorem! Imo potius ordinabat in nobis charitatem, tam verbis quam exemplis nobiscum agens, ut carnalium affectibus necessitudinum, non solum Dei praeponamus dilectionem, sed etiam eorum qui Dei faciunt voluntatem. Ille siquidem affectus de cordibus exigitur omnium nostrum, quos summi Patris adoptavit dignatio, quo fideliter dicamus cum ipsius Unigenito: Quicunque fecerit voluntatem Patris mei qui in coelis est, ipse meus frater, et soror, et mater est (Matth. XII, 49). Prorsus filiis Dei competit vox ista, nec ullum fidelius testimonium reddit ipse Spiritus spiritui nostro quod simus filii Dei, quam ut sonet de cordibus nostris haec vox Unigeniti Dei. Itaque Mariam, quae secundum carnem mater erat, alia quoque ratione matrem sibi Jesus probat: quandoquidem et ipsa voluntatem Patris in tantum faciebat, ut de ipsa praediceret Pater, Vocaberis voluntas mea in ea (Isai. LXII, 4). Igitur ubi eam visus est ignorasse Filius, ibi amplius honorasse invenitur: cum ei scilicet materni nominis honor duplicatur, pro eo quod eumdem Filium, quem alvo gestaverat incarnatum, etiam nunc animo gestabat inspiratum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Far be it that he should have scorned his Mother, he who gave the law with such care concerning the honoring of parents! Far be it, I say, that he should have disdained on earth the Mother whose beauty he had desired from heaven! Rather, he was ordering love within us, acting with us both by words and by examples, so that we might set the love of God\u2014and also the love of those who do the will of God\u2014above the affections of carnal ties. For that affection is demanded of the hearts of all of us whom the condescension of the most high Father has adopted, so that we may faithfully say with his Only-Begotten: 'Whoever shall do the will of my Father who is in heaven, he is my brother, and sister, and mother' (Matth. XII, 49). This utterance indeed belongs to the children of God, nor does the Spirit himself give any more faithful testimony to our spirit that we are children of God, than when this utterance of the Only-Begotten of God resounds from our hearts. And so Jesus proves Mary, who was his mother according to the flesh, to be his mother also by another title: since she herself did the will of the Father to such a degree that the Father foretold concerning her, 'You shall be called my will in her' (Isai. LXII, 4). Therefore, where the Son seemed to have disregarded her, there he is found to have honored her the more: since for her the honor of the maternal name is doubled, in that the same Son whom she had carried incarnate in her womb, she even now carried in her soul by inspiration.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Caeterum cum dilexisset eam Jesus, in finem dilexit eam, ut non solum propter ipsam finem vivendi, verum et in ipsam finem pene faceret loquendi: dum velut inter ultima verba testamenti, curam Matris, cujus debitorem se cognoscebat, charissimo transcribit haeredi. Sic quippe inter Petrum qui plus diligebat, et Joannem qui plus diligebatur, haereditatem suam Christus divisit, ut Petrus sortiretur Ecclesiam, Joannes Mariam. Huic plane competebat haec portio non solum jure propinquitatis, sed etiam amoris privilegio testimonioque pudoris. Decebat namque ut nonnisi virgo virgini deserviret, quatenus et Virgo beata, divino languens prae amore, floribus fulciretur castitatis, et virginitas adolescentis id interim perciperet remunerationis, ut de contubernio proficeret tantae sanctitatis. Qui etiam, quia fidelis probatus est in obsequio incorruptae matris; mysteria quoque divinitatis, et arcana Verbi incorruptibilis sibi credi promeruit. Decebat, inquam, ut Matri Domini non alius obsequeretur quam dilectus Filii: quatenus et Mater semper suspirans Filium in dilectum Filii suavius respiraret, et discipulus Magistrum sibi conquerens nimis cito subtractum, Magistram totius veritatis se reperisse gauderet. Idque satis commode provisum, ut scripturus Evangelium familiarius de singulis conferret cum ea, quae conscia erat omnium; quippe quae ab initio omnia Filii sui diligentius observaverat, conservans omnia verba quae de illo erant, conferens in corde suo (Luc. II, 19, 51). Sic nempe se Martham exhibuit in cura pueri nutriendi; ut nihilominus Mariae partes exsecuta credatur in studio verbi cognoscendi. Maxime tamen postquam idem Filius ascendit ubi erat prius, Mater absoluta omni sollicitudine temporali, sanctoque Spiritu, quem praeter singulares illas conceptus sui primitias communiter cum Apostolis acceperat, plenius illuminata, gaudebat nimirum vacare, et videre quoniam Jesus est Deus. Visio prorsus ineffabilis gaudii, summaeque delectationis omnibus qui diligunt Jesum; sed prae omnibus illi quae genuit Jesum: cui sicut singulariter excepta est gratia Deum generandi; sic et praerogativa gloriandi in eo quem genuit. Omnino singularis atque incomparabilis gloria Virginis matris, videre Deum regem omnium in diademate carnis quo coronavit eum; ut et Deum agnoscat et adoret in corpore proprio, et corpus proprium glorificatum videat in Deo. His interim, ut opinor, contemplationibus pascebatur Maria, hanc optimam partem elegerat: quae non ablata, sed perfecta est hodie in ea. Quia enim negligens aut segnis non exstitit in opere Marthae, nequaquam vacua derelicta est a fructu Mariae. Labor in actione, fructus seu merces in contemplatione. Propterea, inquit, quod laboravit anima ejus, videbit et saturabitur (Isa. LIII, 11)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Moreover, when Jesus had loved her, he loved her unto the end, so that for her sake he not only made an end of living, but in her regard almost made an end of speaking as well: since, as it were among the last words of his testament, he transferred the care of his Mother\u2014whose debtor he knew himself to be\u2014to his most beloved heir. For thus Christ divided his inheritance between Peter, who loved more, and John, who was loved more: so that Peter obtained the Church as his lot, and John, Mary. This portion plainly belonged to John not only by right of kinship, but also by the privilege of love and the testimony of chastity. For it was fitting that none but a virgin should serve a virgin: so that the Blessed Virgin, languishing for divine love, might be propped up by the flowers of chastity, and that the virginity of the young man might meanwhile receive this as its reward, that he might profit from the companionship of such great holiness. He also, because he was proved faithful in the service of the incorrupt Mother, deserved that the mysteries of the Godhead and the secrets of the incorruptible Word should be entrusted to him. It was fitting, I say, that none other than the beloved of the Son should serve the Mother of the Lord: so that the Mother, ever sighing for her Son, might breathe more sweetly in the beloved of the Son, and the disciple, lamenting that his Master had been taken from him too soon, might rejoice that he had found a Mistress of all truth. And this was quite fittingly provided, that he who was to write the Gospel might confer more intimately about particular matters with her who was privy to all things; since from the beginning she had more diligently observed all things concerning her Son, keeping all the words that were about him, and pondering them in her heart (Luc. II, 19, 51). Thus indeed she showed herself a Martha in the care of nursing the child; yet is nonetheless believed to have carried out the part of Mary in the pursuit of knowing the Word. But most of all, after that same Son ascended to where he was before, the Mother, freed from all temporal care, and more fully illumined by the Holy Spirit\u2014whom, besides those singular first-fruits of her conceiving, she had received in common with the Apostles\u2014rejoiced indeed to be at leisure, and to see that Jesus is God. A vision truly of ineffable joy and of the highest delight to all who love Jesus; but above all to her who bore Jesus: to whom, just as the grace of begetting God was singularly granted, so too was the prerogative of glorying in him whom she bore. Altogether singular and incomparable is the glory of the Virgin Mother, to see God, the King of all, in the diadem of flesh with which she crowned him; so that she both recognizes and adores God in her own body, and sees her own body glorified in God. With such contemplations, I suppose, Mary was being fed meanwhile; this best part she had chosen, which is not taken away but today is perfected in her. For because she was not negligent or slothful in the work of Martha, she was by no means left empty of the fruit of Mary. The labor is in action; the fruit, or reward, is in contemplation. Therefore, he says, because her soul labored, she shall see and be satisfied (Isa. LIII, 11).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Vobis haec dicimus, fratres, ut si quis desiderio tangitur illius optimae partis quae in Maria laudatur, sciat quia haec praemium est illius quae in Martha non improbatur: nec aequum est ut praemium ante meritum requiratur. Jacob prius oportet jungi Liae, quam fruatur amplexibus Rachel (Gen. XXIX): sed et ipsum prius vocari et esse Jacob, quam Israel. Rectorem, inquit, te posuerunt? curam illorum habe, et sic considera, et omni cura tua explicita recumbe, ut laeteris propter illos, et ornamentum gratiae accipias coronam (Eccli. XXXII, 1-3). Labor siquidem operis, seu sollicitudo administrationis, semina sunt justitiae: pro quibus gaudia metenda sunt ex ore consolantis misericordiae. Sic enim ait propheta: Seminate vobis in justitia, metite in ore misericordiae (Osee X, 12). At enim qui parce seminat, parce et metet: et qui seminat in benedictione, de benedictionibus et metet (II Cor. IX, 6). Nullus sane tam profusa benedictione seminavit, sicut illa benedicta in mulieribus; quae benedictum semen de ventre profudit. Sed semen dicam, an fructum? Melius utrumque. Qui enim semen est operantibus justitiam, fructus erit metentibus gloriam. Semen, in passione: fructus, in resurrectione. Potens in terra semen istud, quod cadens in terram mox excitavit potentiam suam, ut multum fructum afferret, et in semine illo benedicerentur omnes gentes. Unde et ibi sequitur: Generatio rectorum benedicetur (Psal. CXI, 2)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. We say these things to you, brethren, so that if anyone is touched by desire for that best part which is praised in Mary, he may know that this is the reward of that part which is not disapproved in Martha: nor is it right that the reward be sought before the merit. Jacob must first be joined to Leah, before he enjoys the embraces of Rachel (Gen. XXIX): and he himself must first be called and be Jacob, before he is Israel. Have they made you a ruler? he says; take care of them, and so consider, and when all your care has been discharged, take your rest, that you may rejoice because of them, and receive the crown as the ornament of grace (Eccli. XXXII, 1-3). For the labor of work, or the solicitude of administration, are the seeds of righteousness: for which joys are to be reaped from the mouth of consoling mercy. For thus says the prophet: Sow for yourselves in righteousness, reap in the mouth of mercy (Osee X, 12). But indeed he who sows sparingly shall also reap sparingly; and he who sows in blessing shall also reap of blessings (II Cor. IX, 6). No one indeed has sown with so abundant a blessing as she who is blessed among women, who poured forth the blessed seed from her womb. But shall I call it seed, or fruit? Better to call it both. For he who is seed to those laboring in righteousness will be fruit to those reaping glory. Seed, in the Passion; fruit, in the Resurrection. Mighty on earth was that seed, which, falling into the earth, soon roused its power, so that it might bring forth much fruit, and in that seed all nations might be blessed. Whence it follows there also: The generation of the upright shall be blessed (Psal. CXI, 2).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. De benedictionibus suis itaque Maria metat: et quae benedictionem omnium gentium seminavit, benedictionem omnium gentium singulariter accipiat. Beatam me dicent, inquit, omnes generationes (Luc. I, 48). Parum est hoc. Beatam te dicent omnes beatorum spirituum ordines. Viderunt hodie ascendentem filiae Sion coelestis et beatam dixerunt, et reginae laudaverunt eam (Cantic. VI, 8). Prorsus de benedictionibus suis hodie Maria metit, quia refusa est spiritualiter in eam omnimoda benedictio illa quam de se profudit. Date ei, inquit Spiritus sanctus, de fructu ventris sui; et saturetur illo quem genuit. O Mater misericordiae, saturare gloria Filii tui; et dimitte reliquias tuas parvulis tuis. Tu jam ad mensam, Domina; nos sub mensa catelli. Sicut oculi ancillae in manibus dominae suae, ita familia haec famelica de te praestolatur alimoniam vitae. Per te fructum vitae communicavimus, in mensa praesentium sacramentorum: per te eumdem fructum vitae communicemus in mensa perennium gaudiorum, Jesum benedictum fructum ventris tui; cui est honor et gloria, per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. So let Mary reap from her own blessings: and let her who sowed the blessing of all nations receive singularly the blessing of all nations. \"All generations shall call me blessed,\" she says (Luc. I, 48). This is too little. All the ranks of the blessed spirits shall call you blessed. Today the daughters of heavenly Sion saw her ascending, and called her blessed; and queens praised her (Cantic. VI, 8). Indeed, today Mary reaps from her own blessings, because that whole blessing which she poured forth from herself has been poured back upon her spiritually. \"Give her,\" says the Holy Spirit, \"of the fruit of her womb; and let her be satisfied with him whom she bore.\" O Mother of mercy, be satisfied with the glory of your Son; and leave your leftovers to your little ones. You are now at the table, O Lady; we are the little dogs beneath the table. As the eyes of the handmaid are on the hands of her mistress, so this famished household awaits from you the nourishment of life. Through you we have shared the fruit of life at the table of the sacraments now present: through you may we share that same fruit of life at the table of everlasting joys\u2014Jesus, the blessed fruit of your womb; to whom be honor and glory, forever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Mary keeping all these words in her heart is Luke 2:19 and 2:51. The edition prints Eccli. ii, 19, 51 here and Luc. ii, 19, 51 in paragraph 3."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Assumption Sermon IV", "topics": ["Assumption", "Mary", "Mary and Martha", "contemplation", "active life", "John the Apostle"], "url": "sermons/guerric-assumption-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-benedict-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN FESTO SANCTI BENEDICTI., SERMO I., De mansione in sapientia., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 99-103.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint Benedict", "Feast of Saint Benedict", "Sirach 14:22", "Proverbs 3:13", "3 Kings 11 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Kings)", "Romans 1:21\u201332", "Luke 8:13\u201314", "Isaiah 18:5", "Hosea 8:7", "Proverbs 27:8", "Sirach 6:22", "John 6:61", "John 6:69", "Psalm 93:20 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 30:20 (historical numbering)", "Ecclesiastes 10:4", "Psalm 64:9 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 13:12", "Isaiah 30:15", "Isaiah 32:17", "Sirach 1:33", "Joshua 5:13", "Wisdom 8:16", "Psalm 70:23 (historical numbering; opening parenthesis absent in print)", "Matthew 25:29"], "occasion": "In festo sancti Benedicti", "occasion_detail": "Feast of Saint Benedict", "occasion_label": "Saint Benedict", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric considers perseverance in wisdom through monastic stability, prayer, Scripture, work and silence. He connects obedience under difficult teaching with sacramental nourishment and the fear of God. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina under the feast of Saint Benedict. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN FESTO SANCTI BENEDICTI."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE FEAST OF SAINT BENEDICT.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De mansione in sapientia."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On abiding in wisdom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Beatus vir qui in sapientia morabitur, et qui in justitia meditabitur, et in sensu cogitabit circumspectionem Dei! (Eccli. XIV, 22.) Quam congrue verba haec in laudem beati Benedicti cantata sint, facile quivis vestrum intelligit, cui tamen vita et doctrina ipsius non ignota sit. Quam commode vero ad vitae nostrae doctrinam trahi possint, ipsa per se verborum facies ostendit, quae scilicet illa quibus nihil est utilius in vita, sapientiam, justitiam, timorem Dei, cum praemio etiam beatitudinis commendat. Beatus, inquit, vir qui in sapientia morabitur. Prorsus haec est beatitudo, haec sapientia, si moreris in sapientia, ut perseveranter eam teneas: non enim statim qui invenerit, sed qui tenuerit eam, beatus. Dicit quidem Scriptura: Beatus qui invenit sapientiam. Sed non ibi quievit, imo addidit, et qui affluit sapientia (Prov. III, 13); ne videlicet solum invenisse statim putaretur ad beatitudinem sufficere, nisi, cum inveneris, cum ea et in ea demoreris, et contubernio atque convictu ipsius delecteris; nec discedas a magisterio ejus, donec meditando justitiam, cogitando circumspectionem Dei, affluere prudentia possit. Nam et Salomon sapientiam invenit; sed, quia prudentia minus affluxit, nec satis prudenter sibi circumspexit, dum videlicet non caute sibi providit a mulieribus alienis, non modo sapientiam perdidit, sed usque ad ultimam idololatriae insipientiam proruit (III Reg. XI). Sapientes quoque hujus mundi, qui invisibilia Dei, a creatura mundi per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta conspexerunt, sapientiam invenisse visi sunt; sed, quia nec ipsi prudentia affluxerunt, et, cum cognovissent Deum, non sicut Deum glorificaverunt, stulti et ipsi facti sunt, et obscuratum est insipiens cor eorum usque ad reprobum sensum, et ignominiam turpitudinum (Rom. I, 21-32)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Blessed is the man who shall abide in wisdom, and who shall meditate in justice, and in understanding shall think upon the circumspection of God!\" (Eccli. XIV, 22.) How fittingly these words are sung in praise of blessed Benedict, anyone of you to whom his life and teaching are not unknown easily understands. But how conveniently they can be drawn into instruction for our own life, the very face of the words themselves shows, since they commend those things than which nothing is more useful in life\u2014wisdom, justice, the fear of God\u2014together with the reward of blessedness as well. Blessed, he says, is the man who shall abide in wisdom. This indeed is blessedness, this is wisdom: if you abide in wisdom, so that you hold it perseveringly; for blessed is not at once the one who has found it, but the one who has held fast to it. Scripture indeed says: \"Blessed is he who finds wisdom.\" But it did not stop there; rather it added, \"and who overflows with wisdom\" (Prov. III, 13); lest, namely, one should think that merely having found it suffices at once for blessedness, unless, when you have found it, you remain with it and in it, and delight in its company and converse; nor do you depart from its teaching, until, by meditating on justice, by thinking on the circumspection of God, you can overflow with prudence. For Solomon too found wisdom; but because he overflowed less with prudence, and did not look about himself prudently enough\u2014since, namely, he did not warily provide for himself against strange women\u2014he not only lost wisdom, but rushed forward even to the final folly of idolatry (III Reg. XI). The wise men of this world too, who gazed upon the invisible things of God, understood from the creation of the world through the things that were made, seemed to have found wisdom; but because they too did not overflow with prudence, and, when they had known God, did not glorify Him as God, they themselves also became fools, and their foolish heart was darkened even unto a reprobate sense, and the disgrace of base things (Rom. I, 21-32).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Alios itaque, sicut istorum exemplo liquet, repellit a sapientia quam invenerant, superbia cordis; alios, sicut in Salomone patet, abstrahit illecebra carnis; alii autem levitate et inconstantia mentis, levi perturbatione offensi eam relinquunt, qui scilicet ad tempus credunt, et in tempore tentationis recedunt. Quare autem recedunt? Quia radices non habent, quibus teneantur? (Luc. VIII, 13, 14.) Quomodo autem radicari poterunt, nisi morentur? Quae unquam planta radicatur, nisi loco cui affixa fuerit immoretur? Sic justus plantatus in domo Domini, nec radicari nec fundari in charitate poterit, nisi mora et stabilitate loci. Si autem radicatus non fuerit, nec florere, nec fructum qui maneat, afferre poterit; et, si florere visus fuerit alicujus spei initio, dicetur de illo: Ante messem totus floruit, et immatura perfectio germinavit (Isai. XVIII, 5); et secundum alium prophetam: Si fecerit fructum, alieni comedent eum (Osee, VIII, 7). Vis autem scire quam sit necessaria stabilitas loci ut in sapientia moreris, ut radicari et fructificare possis mora temporis? Interroga Patrem tuum Benedictum, et annuntiabit tibi quia claustrum monasterii, et stabilitas in congregatione, locus idoneus sit ad ferendum omnium fere fructus virtutum, quarum longum ibidem texit catalogum (Reg. S. Bened. cap. 4). Quid enim de instabili Salomon ait? Sicut avis transmigrans de nido suo, sic vir qui relinquit locum suum (Prov. XXVII, 8). Invenerat turtur nidum ubi poneret pullos suos, coeperat eum calefacere et ab eo calefieri, venerant usque ad partum pulli, et ecce avolat, opusque coeptum imperfectum relinquit. Quare, unde, vel quo avolet, ipse viderit, vel quando tot dispendia, quae interim patitur, compensabit, vel qua ratione primam fidem irritam factam excusabit. Ego enim non putarem esse consilii, pro spe incerta, certa subire dispendia; etsi profectus quorumdam me jubeat interim a praecipitatione cohibere sententiam."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Therefore, as is clear from the example of these men, pride of heart drives some away from the wisdom they had found; others, as is plain in the case of Solomon, the allurement of the flesh drags away; while others still, by lightness and inconstancy of mind, offended by some slight disturbance, abandon it\u2014namely, those who believe for a time, and in the time of temptation fall away. But why do they fall away? Is it not because they have no roots by which they might be held fast? (Luc. VIII, 13, 14.) But how will they be able to be rooted, unless they abide? What plant was ever rooted, unless it remained in the place to which it had been fixed? So the just man, planted in the house of the Lord, will not be able to be rooted or founded in charity, except through continued residence and stability of place. But if he has not been rooted, he will not be able either to flower, or to bring forth fruit that remains; and if he has seemed to flower with some beginning of hope, it will be said of him: \"Before the harvest it flowered entirely, and an unripe perfection sprouted\" (Isai. XVIII, 5); and according to another prophet: \"If it bears fruit, strangers shall eat it\" (Osee, VIII, 7). But do you wish to know how necessary stability of place is, so that you may abide in wisdom, so that you may be able to be rooted and to bear fruit through length of time? Ask your Father Benedict, and he will declare to you that the cloister of the monastery, and stability in the community, is a place fit for bearing almost all the fruits of the virtues, of which he weaves a long catalogue in that very place (Rule of St. Benedict, ch. 4). For what does Solomon say of the unstable man? \"As a bird that wanders from her nest, so is the man who abandons his place\" (Prov. XXVII, 8). The turtledove had found a nest where she might lay her young; she had begun to warm it and to be warmed by it; the young had come even to the point of birth, and behold, she flies away, and leaves the work she had begun unfinished. Why, whence, or whither she flies away, let her see to it herself; or when she will make up for the many losses which she meanwhile suffers; or by what reasoning she will excuse her broken first fidelity. For I would not think it a matter of good counsel, for an uncertain hope, to undergo certain losses; although the progress of certain persons bids me meanwhile restrain my judgment from precipitate haste.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Enimvero multum dissimilis est causa eorum qui amore sapientiae inquieti fiunt, et eorum de quibus loqui coeperam, qui levi et frivola permoti causa a sapientia resiliunt. Sicut enim per patientiam disciplinis sapientiae quis immoratur, ut discat sapientiam; sic qui facile perdunt sustinentiam, non demorabuntur, ut scriptum est, projicere illam. Quid autem eos offendat, praemisit Scriptura, cum ait: Quasi lapidis virtus, probatio erit in illis (Eccli. VI, 22). Offenderunt in lapidem offensionis et petram scandali, cujus probata virtus corripiebat et erudiebat insipientes, probabatque mentes; cum ipsi virtutem sapientiae duritiam lapidis interpretarentur, duraque omnia, disciplinam, vultum, sermonem causarentur. Durus est, inquiunt, hic sermo (Joan. VI, 61). Esto, sermo durus est; numquid non verus est? Lapis durus est; nunquid non pretiosus est? Quare autem veritas dura est tibi, nisi propter duritiam cordis tui? si cor tibi pietate mollesceret, plus tibi soliditas veritatis quam vanitas mendacii vel oleum adulationis placeret. Durus est, aiebant, hic sermo, quia quasi lapidis virtus erat illis probatio sapientiae; ideoque non sunt demorati eam projicere, et retro abire. Nec ob aliud pretiosum et electum a Deo lapidem reprobaverunt, nisi quia durum putaverunt. Et erat quidem petra Christus, sed virtute, non duritia. Erat petra, sed quae converti posset, imo et conversa est in stagna vel fontes aquarum, ubi mollia et humilia, quibus influeret, invenit corda fidelium. Nam et isti, qui tam cito offensi quadam specie duritiae retro abierunt, si permansissent cum Apostolis, bibissent forsitan et ipsi cum eis de consequenti eos Petra, bibissent flumina aquae vivae de petra percussa in cruce fluentis largissime, ita ut hodieque bibat populus et jumenta; imo suxissent etiam mel de petra, oleumque de saxo durissimo."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Indeed, the case of those who become restless out of love of wisdom is very different from that of those I had begun to speak of, who, moved by some light and frivolous cause, spring back from wisdom. For just as a man dwells patiently under the discipline of wisdom in order to learn wisdom, so those who quickly lose their endurance will not delay, as it is written, to cast it away. And Scripture has already told us what offends them, saying: \"As the virtue of a stone, so shall the trial be in them\" (Eccli. VI, 22). They stumbled against the stone of stumbling and the rock of scandal, whose proven virtue was correcting and instructing the foolish, and testing minds; while they themselves interpreted the virtue of wisdom as the hardness of a stone, and complained that everything\u2014the discipline, the countenance, the word\u2014was hard. \"This is a hard saying,\" they said (Joan. VI, 61). Granted, the word is hard; but is it not true? A stone is hard; but is it not precious? Why then is the truth hard to you, unless because of the hardness of your heart? If your heart were softened by devotion, the solidity of truth would please you more than the vanity of falsehood or the oil of flattery. \"This is a hard saying,\" they said, because the trial of wisdom was to them as it were the virtue of a stone; and therefore they did not delay to cast it away and turn back. Nor for any other reason did they reject the precious and chosen stone of God, except that they thought it hard. And indeed Christ was the Rock\u2014but by virtue, not by hardness. He was a Rock, but one that could be turned\u2014indeed, was turned\u2014into pools or fountains of water, where it found the soft and humble hearts of the faithful into which it could flow. For these men too, who so quickly took offense at a certain appearance of hardness and turned back, if they had remained with the Apostles, would perhaps have drunk themselves, with them, of the Rock that followed them; they would have drunk most abundantly of the rivers of living water flowing from the Rock struck upon the Cross, so that even today the people and the cattle drink of it; indeed, they would even have sucked honey from the rock, and oil from the hardest stone.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Prorsus tu beatus es, Simon Bar-Jona, tibi jam revelabat Pater pietatem mysterii, latentem, ut videbatur, sub duritia sermonis, qui, interrogatis duodecim, an et ipsi vellent abire, constanter respondisti: Domine, verba vitae aeternae habes; ad quem ibimus? (Joan. VI, 69.) Plane beatus, qui cum sapientia morari, et cum domesticis ejus ad mensam ipsius pane interim Sacramenti nutriri delegisti; donec de fide proficientem ad intellectum pane vitae et intellectus te cibaret, et aqua sapientiae salutaris potaret. Beati et vos, fratres mei, qui in disciplinam sapientiae, et christianae scholam philosophiae nomina dedistis, sed, si perseveranter in sapientia moremini; ut cum durior sermo ipsius visus fuerit, duriora scilicet praecipientis aut durius corripientis, non sit in aliquo vestrum cor malum incredulitatis discedendi a Deo vivo, sed constanter dicatis cum Apostolo: Verba vitae aeternae habes; ad quem ibimus? Fingis quidem laborem in praecepto (Psal. XCIII, 20), duritiam in sermone; sed scimus quia magna est multitudo dulcedinis tuae, Domine, quam abscondisti timentibus te (Psal. XXX, 20), perficies sperantibus in te. Ego autem semper sperabo, etiamsi occideris me. Imo tunc amplius sperabo, cum flagellaveris, secueris, usseris, occideris quidquid vivebat in me; ut non ego, sed Christus vivat in me. Omnino non discedimus a te, quia vivificabis nos etiam occidens, sanabis percutiens. Vere beatus qui cum sapientia moratur, ac constantia et fide longanimiter patiens et fideliter obediens usque ad mortem, nec deserens locum suum, quotiescunque spiritus potestatem habentis ascenderit super eum, sciens quia curatio disciplinae faciet cessare maxima peccata (Eccle. X, 4)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Truly you are blessed, Simon Bar-Jona; to you already the Father was revealing the graciousness of the mystery hidden, as it seemed, beneath the hardness of the word\u2014you who, when the twelve were asked whether they too wished to go away, answered steadfastly: \"Lord, thou hast the words of eternal life; to whom shall we go?\" (Joan. VI, 69.). Truly blessed are you, who chose to dwell with wisdom, and to be nourished for a time at her table among her household with the bread of the Sacrament; until, as you advanced from faith to understanding, she fed you with the bread of life and understanding, and gave you to drink the water of saving wisdom. Blessed also are you, my brothers, who have given your names to the discipline of wisdom and to the Christian school of philosophy\u2014but only if you dwell perseveringly in wisdom; so that when her word seems harder\u2014that is, when her precepts are harder, or her correction more severe\u2014there may not be in any of you an evil heart of unbelief, departing from the living God, but that you may say steadfastly with the Apostle: \"Thou hast the words of eternal life; to whom shall we go?\" You indeed fashion labor in your precept (Psal. XCIII, 20), hardness in your word; but we know that great is the multitude of your sweetness, O Lord, which you have hidden for those who fear you (Psal. XXX, 20), and which you will perfect for those who hope in you. And I will always hope, even if you should slay me. Indeed, I will hope the more when you have scourged, cut, burned, and slain whatever was living in me; so that it is not I, but Christ who lives in me. We will in no way depart from you, for you will give us life even by killing us, and heal us even by striking us. Truly blessed is the one who dwells with wisdom, and who, with constancy and faith, patiently enduring and faithfully obeying even unto death, does not desert his place, as often as the spirit of the one having power rises up against him, knowing that \"the remedy of discipline shall make the greatest sins to cease\" (Eccle. X, 4).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sane ad hanc sapientiam morandi in sapientia, illud praecipue pertinere arbitror, ut inquietudo vel quaelibet levis molestia non facile quodcunque opus sapientiae nobis excutiat, verbi gratia, solemnem psalmodiam, orationem, lectionem divinam, pensum operis diurni, aut silentii disciplinam. Quia enim sapientia in exitu canitur: Gaudebunt, ait sanctus, labia mea, cum cantavero tibi Psal LXX, 23), dum etiam ad litteram, exitus matutini et vespere delectabis (Psal. LXIV, 9.) De oratione quoque certum et ipsi quotidie capitis experimentum, quod melior est finis orationis quam principium; ut fidele probetis dominicum illud toties inculcatum, tot exemplis commendatum de orationis perseverantia consilium. Jam legere et negligere, vel, antequam ceperis librum, de manibus projicere, quem fructum, putas, tibi poterit parere? Si Scripturae immoratus non fueris, ut assiduitate studii ei fias familiaris, quando, putas, se tibi revelabit? Qui habet, inquit, amorem verbi, dabitur ei intellectus, et abundabit; qui vero non habet, et quod habet per naturam, auferetur ab eo (Matth. XIII, 12; XXV, 29) propter negligentiam. De opere autem manuum, nonne et de isto satis comperistis quia sicut denarius operariis, sic consolatio saepius servatur in fine operis? De silentio quippe nota est promissio prophetica, quia in silentio et spe erit, inquit, fortitudo vestra (Isai. XXX, 15). Si enim colas justitiam in silentio, et, sicut Jeremias bonum esse ait, praestoleris cum silentio salutare Domini; in medio silentio secretus tibi illabetur a regalibus sedibus omnipotens sermo, et aquae Siloe, quae vadunt cum silentio, convallem quieti ac placidi pectoris grato rigabunt profluvio. Hoc non semel, sed saepius experieris, si modo silentium tuum cultus justitiae sit (Isai. XXXII, 17), id est, si in justitia mediteris, ut perseveres in Scriptura quam proposui, et in sensu cogites circumspectionem Dei."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Indeed, I think this belongs especially to that wisdom of abiding in wisdom: that restlessness, or any slight annoyance, should not easily shake us loose from whatever work of wisdom we are engaged in\u2014for example, the solemn chanting of the psalms, prayer, divine reading, the task of the day's work, or the discipline of silence. For wisdom is sung at its conclusion: 'My lips shall rejoice,' says the saint, 'when I shall sing to thee' Psal LXX, 23); as also, according to the letter, 'thou shalt make the outgoings of the morning and the evening to be joyful' (Psal. LXIV, 9.). Concerning prayer also, you yourselves receive daily a sure proof that the end of prayer is better than its beginning; so that you may prove faithful that counsel of the Lord, so often urged, commended by so many examples, concerning perseverance in prayer. Now what fruit, do you think, can it bear you, to read and then neglect what you have read, or to throw the book out of your hands almost before you have taken it up? If you do not linger over Scripture, so that by diligent study you become familiar with it, when, do you suppose, will it reveal itself to you? 'He who has,' it says, 'love of the word, to him shall understanding be given, and he shall have abundance; but he who has not, even what he has by nature shall be taken away from him' (Matth. XIII, 12; XXV, 29), on account of negligence. As for the work of the hands, have you not also learned well enough from this, that just as the wage is kept for the laborers, so consolation is more often reserved for the end of the work? Concerning silence, indeed, the prophetic promise is well known: 'In silence and in hope shall your strength be,' he says (Isai. XXX, 15). For if you cultivate justice in silence, and, as Jeremiah says it is good, wait with silence for the salvation of the Lord, then in the midst of silence the almighty Word will come down secretly to you from the royal throne, and the waters of Siloam, which flow with silence, will water the valley of a quiet and peaceful breast with their pleasant stream. This you will experience not once but often, provided only that your silence be the cultivation of justice (Isai. XXXII, 17); that is, if you meditate in justice, so that you persevere in the Scripture I have set before you, and in your understanding think upon the circumspection of God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Haec meditare, in his esto, ut profectus tuus manifestus fiat. Si enim iniquitatem mediteris in cubili tuo, malitias scilicet quas malignus immittit, aut phantasias quas cor tuum parturit, aut inanes philosophias, seu fallaces theorias, quae sunt velut aegri somnia; nonne magis injustitiae quam justitiae cultus erit silentium tuum? Si ergo vis in sapientia morari, stude in justitia meditari. Concupisti, inquit, sapientiam? conserva justitiam, et Dominus praebebit illam tibi (Eccli. I, 33). Si autem quasi violente irruunt cogitationes noxiae, pone tibi virilem et fidelem custodiam, quae omni custodia servet cor tuum. Dico autem timorem Dei, qui nihil negligit, qui nullum indiscussum intrare permittit, saepius interrogans etiam angelum lucis: Noster es, an adversariorum? (Josue V, 13.) Undique enim sibi circumspicit, tanquam omni hora cogitans circumspectionem Dei, quem sine intermissione intueri ac judicare corda filiorum hominum attendit. Pulchre autem dictum est: In sensu cogitabit (Eccli. XIV, 22); quia prorsus sine sensu est et a corde excessit qui timorem Dei cogitare negligit, qui pondus tantae majestatis ac judicii sibi incumbentis non sentit. Signanter quoque circumspicere Deus dicitur, quia omnia tam praeterita quam futura habet praesentia, ut nec ista respiciat, nec illa prospiciat, sed similiter, quia simpliciter, cuncta circumspiciat; sitque illa aeternitas velut punctum, omnium media temporalium, cujus immobili simplicitati semper aequaliter praesens est circuitus et rota temporum. Hunc oculum aeternum, sine intermissione videntem et judicantem omnia, semper cogitat, suaque se cogitatione sollicitat timor Domini, qui revocat a malis non solum operibus, sed et cogitationibus; erudiens nos, ut justitiam potius meditemur; continens, ut cum sapientia moremur. Ita enim sensim fit ut qui primo coercetur timore judicii et poenae, postea pascatur amore ac meditatione justitiae, demumque requiescat ac delectetur in convictu et amplexu sapientiae. Haec non modo foras mittit timorem, diffundendo charitatem; sed etiam pellit ab animo taedium et langorem, infundendo suavitatem, sicut quidam, contubernium habens ipsius, de ea loquebatur: Intrans in domum meam conquiescam cum illa; non enim habet amaritudinem conversatio illius, nec taedium convictus illius, sed laetitiam et gaudium (Sap. VIII, 16). Horum participes nos faciat, qui particeps nostri fieri dignatus est Dei sapientia Christus Jesus, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Meditate on these things, be occupied in them, that your progress may be manifest. For if you meditate on iniquity in your bed\u2014that is, the malicious thoughts which the evil one injects, or the fancies which your own heart brings forth, or empty philosophies, or deceptive speculations, which are like the dreams of a sick man\u2014will not your silence then be the cultivation of injustice rather than of justice? If, therefore, you wish to abide in wisdom, strive to meditate in justice. 'Have you desired wisdom?' it says. 'Keep justice, and the Lord will give it to you' (Eccli. I, 33). But if harmful thoughts rush upon you as though by force, set for yourself a manly and faithful guard, which may keep your heart with all watchfulness. I mean the fear of God, which neglects nothing, which allows nothing to enter unexamined, often questioning even an angel of light: 'Art thou one of ours, or of the adversaries?' (Josue V, 13.). For it looks about on every side, as though at every hour thinking upon the circumspection of God, whom it attends as beholding without ceasing and judging the hearts of the children of men. It is beautifully said: 'He shall think with understanding' (Eccli. XIV, 22); for he is utterly without sense, and has gone out of his heart, who neglects to think upon the fear of God, who does not feel the weight of so great a majesty and of the judgment pressing upon him. God is also fittingly said to 'look around,' because he holds all things, past as well as future, as present to him, so that he neither looks back upon the one nor looks forward to the other, but looks around upon all things equally, because simply; and that eternity of his is, as it were, a point in the midst of all temporal things, to whose unmoving simplicity the circling wheel of the ages is ever equally present. Upon this eternal eye, seeing and judging all things without ceasing, the fear of the Lord continually thinks, and by its own thought disquiets itself\u2014that fear which calls us back from evils not only in deeds but also in thoughts; teaching us rather to meditate on justice, restraining us, that we may abide with wisdom. For thus it comes about by degrees that he who is first held in check by fear of judgment and punishment is afterward fed by love and meditation of justice, and at last rests and delights in the companionship and embrace of wisdom. This wisdom not only casts out fear, by pouring forth charity, but also drives weariness and listlessness from the soul, by infusing sweetness\u2014just as a certain man, who had companionship with her, spoke of her: 'Entering into my house, I will take my repose with her; for her company has no bitterness, nor her companionship any tediousness, but joy and gladness' (Sap. VIII, 16). May he make us partakers of these things, who deigned to become a partaker of us\u2014the Wisdom of God, Christ Jesus, who lives and reigns for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints violente in its description of harmful thoughts forcing their way in."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition omits the opening parenthesis before Psal LXX, 23, the quotation on rejoicing lips from Psalm 70:23 in historical numbering."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saint Benedict Sermon I", "topics": ["Saint Benedict", "wisdom", "perseverance", "monastic stability", "obedience", "prayer", "Scripture", "work", "silence", "fear of God", "Eucharist"], "url": "sermons/guerric-benedict-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-benedict-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De fiducia in Deum., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 103-107.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint Benedict", "Feast of Saint Benedict", "Jeremiah 17:7", "Proverbs 10:6", "Genesis 27:28", "Hosea 14:6", "Isaiah 26:19", "Genesis 27:29", "Matthew 9:2, 22, 29", "Mark 11:22", "Matthew 8:26", "John 16:33", "1 John 5:4", "Psalm 124:1 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 2:11\u201312", "Matthew 27:46", "Psalm 36:25 (historical numbering)", "Romans 8:31", "1 Peter 3:13", "Jeremiah 17:5", "Psalm 9:11 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 32:17\u201318", "Song of Songs 2:5 (printed locator; quotation from Song of Songs 2:3)", "Proverbs 1:33", "Job 5:17", "Revelation 3:19", "Proverbs 3:12", "Psalm 2:13 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 12:1", "Jeremiah 17:7\u20138", "Ezekiel 47:3\u201312", "Jeremiah 17:6, 8", "Joshua 15:19", "Sirach 24:41\u201342", "Isaiah 66:9", "Sirach 39:17"], "occasion": "In festo sancti Benedicti", "occasion_detail": "Feast of Saint Benedict", "occasion_label": "Saint Benedict", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Trust in God gives steadiness amid tribulation and the loss of spiritual consolation. Guerric develops the image of a tree rooted in living water through Scripture, compunction and the sacraments. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina under the feast of Saint Benedict. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De fiducia in Deum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On Trust in God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Benedictus vir qui confidit in Domino (Jerem. XVII, 7). Sanctus Pater noster gratia Benedictus et nomine, cujus memoria in benedictione est, ipse profecto fuit benedictus vir qui confisus est in Domino. Quem enim Dominus praevenit in benedictionibus dulcedinis, ut in Domino confideret, jam benedixit omni benedictione spirituali in coelestibus in Christo, quia in Domino confisus est. Nec solum in coelestibus benedictionem omnium angelorum, sed etiam in terrestribus benedictionem omnium gentium dedit illi Dominus. Ubi enim gentium non benedicetur hodie benedictus Domini Benedictus? Plane benedictio Domini super caput justi (Prov. X, 6), quem tot benedictionibus coeli et terrae gratia Dei cumulavit. Non tamen, sicut Esau, in pinguedine terrae et in rore coeli benedictio ejus (Gen. XXVII, 28); sed in pinguedine spiritus et in rore coeli, qui per prophetam loquitur: Ero quasi ros (Osee XIV, 6), et cui dicitur: Ros lucis, ros tuus (Isai. XXVI, 19). In Christo utique benedictio Patris, cui Pater imprecatus est: Qui benedixerit tibi, benedictionibus repleatur (Gen. XXVII, 29). Merito proinde benedictus in Domino vir qui confidit in Domino, quia qui in Domino fidit, in eo se ipsum figit. Ubi autem arbor radicem figit, inde succum vitae atque humorem pinguedinis bibit. Plane ad humorem radices misit, qui, ut verbis utar Benedicti magistri, spem suam Deo commisit, ac de ipso fonte summi boni aquas vitae totius benedictionis et gratiae bibit (Reg. S. Bened. cap. 4)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Blessed is the man who trusts in the Lord (Jerem. XVII, 7). Our holy Father, Benedict both in grace and in name, whose memory is in benediction, was indeed himself a blessed man who trusted in the Lord. For him whom the Lord went before with the blessings of sweetness, so that he might trust in the Lord, the Lord had already blessed with every spiritual blessing in the heavenly places in Christ, because he trusted in the Lord. And the Lord gave him not only, in heavenly things, the blessing of all the angels, but also, in earthly things, the blessing of all nations. For in what nation today is Benedict, the Lord's blessed one, not blessed? Truly the blessing of the Lord is upon the head of the just man (Prov. X, 6), whom the grace of God has heaped with so many blessings of heaven and earth. Yet his blessing was not, like Esau's, in the fatness of the earth and the dew of heaven (Gen. XXVII, 28); but in the fatness of the spirit and the dew of heaven, who speaks through the prophet: I will be as the dew (Osee XIV, 6), and to whom it is said: The dew of light is your dew (Isai. XXVI, 19). In Christ indeed is the blessing of the Father, for whom the Father invoked this blessing: May he who blesses you be filled with blessings (Gen. XXVII, 29). Rightly, then, is the man blessed in the Lord who trusts in the Lord, because he who trusts in the Lord fixes himself in him. Now where a tree fixes its root, from there it drinks the sap of life and the moisture of richness. Clearly he sent his roots toward that moisture who, to use the words of our master Benedict, 'committed his hope to God,' and drank from that very fount of the highest good the waters of life of all blessing and grace (Rule of St. Benedict, ch. 4).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Per hanc nempe piam et fidelem fiduciam remittuntur peccata; sanitatum corporalium, sed magis spiritualium, obtinentur remedia; propulsantur pericula; terrores contemnuntur; vincitur mundus; omnia denique possibilia credenti redduntur. Illi quippe qui in peccatis erat: Confide, ait, fili, remittuntur tibi peccata tua. Illis quibus donabatur sanitas corporum vel animarum: Secundum fidem tuam fiat tibi; et: Fides tua te salvum fecit (Matth. IX, 2, 29, 22). Illis, qui periculo terrebantur naufragandi: Habete, inquit, fidem Dei (Marc. XI, 22); et: Quid timidi estis, modicae fidei? (Matth. VIII, 26.) Illis quos armabat adversus saevitiam mundi furoresque diaboli: Confidite, ait; ego vici mundum (Joan. XVI, 33). Et utique haec est victoria quae vincit mundum, fides nostra (I Joan. V, 4), si modo non sit tepida, non trepida; sed sit fiducia, id est fides non ficta, seu spes non infirma. Non solum autem mundus per eam vincitur, sed et coelum possidetur, super aeternitatem homo stabilitur, et in Deo ipso per charitatem radicatur et fundatur. Qui enim confidit in Domino, est sicut mons Sion; non commovebitur in aeternum, qui fundatus est super Aeternum (Psal. CXXIV, 1). Tam enim perire non potest, quam nec ille cui adhaerens unus cum ipso spiritus est. Quis enim speravit in Domino, et confusus est, permansit in mandatis illius, et derelictus est? (Eccli. II, 11, 12.) Si dixerit infidelis, Ille derelictus erat qui in cruce clamabat: Deus, Deus meus, utquid me dereliquisti? (Matth. XXVII, 46.) puto quia non usquequaque dereliquit, in quo erat mundum reconcilians sibi. O quanta consolatio, sic desolari; quanta dilectio, sic derelinqui, ut Unico Patris unice dilecto merearis vel in passionibus consociari!"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. By this devout and faithful trust, indeed, sins are forgiven; remedies are obtained for bodily health, but even more for spiritual health; dangers are driven off; terrors are held in contempt; the world is overcome; and finally, all things become possible to the one who believes. For to the one who was in sins, he said: Have confidence, son, your sins are forgiven you. To those to whom health of body or soul was granted: According to your faith be it done to you; and: Your faith has made you whole (Matth. IX, 2, 29, 22). To those who were terrified by the danger of shipwreck, he said: Have the faith of God (Marc. XI, 22); and: Why are you fearful, O you of little faith? (Matth. VIII, 26.) To those whom he armed against the savagery of the world and the ragings of the devil, he said: Have confidence; I have overcome the world (Joan. XVI, 33). And indeed this is the victory that overcomes the world, our faith (I Joan. V, 4), provided only that it be not lukewarm, not fearful; but be trust\u2014that is, faith not feigned, or hope not weak. Not only is the world overcome through it, but heaven is also possessed, man is made stable upon eternity, and he is rooted and grounded in God himself through charity. For he who trusts in the Lord is like Mount Sion; he shall not be moved forever, who is founded upon the Eternal One (Psal. CXXIV, 1). For he can no more perish than can the one to whom, clinging to him, he is made one spirit with him. For who has hoped in the Lord, and been confounded? Who has remained in his commandments, and been forsaken? (Eccli. II, 11, 12.) Should the unbeliever say: He was forsaken who cried out on the cross: My God, my God, why have you forsaken me? (Matth. XXVII, 46.) \u2014 I think that he did not altogether forsake him, in whom he was reconciling the world to himself. O how great a consolation, thus to be desolated; how great a love, thus to be forsaken, that you might merit to be associated even in his sufferings with the Only-begotten of the Father, the uniquely beloved!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Aperi, Domine, oculos hujus pueri, rudem loquor et novitium, cui, cum tribulatur, putat se derelictum. Novum hoc omnino et insolitum, si justum quempiam viderem derelictum, cum Ecclesia dicat: Junior fui, etenim senui, et non vidi justum derelictum (Psal. XXXVI, 25). Aperi, inquam, o Domine, oculos pueri tui, ut videat quia plures nobiscum sunt quam cum adversariis. Siquidem Dominus virtutum nobiscum, et cum eo omnis virtus et militia coeli, imo favor omnis creaturae, servientis nutui et arbitrio Creatoris sui. Si enim Deus pro nobis, quis contra nos? (Rom. VIII, 31.) et quis est qui nobis noceat, si boni aemulatores fuerimus? (I Petr. III, 13.) Poterit invidus ille saevire; sed hoc erit servire. Poterit urere; tundere, dissecare; sed hoc erit coronas fabricare. Diligam te, Domine, fortitudo mea, per quem mihi vermiculo succumbit omnis fortitudo contraria, et illuditur ab angelis tuis, in ministerium nostrum missis, antiqui draconis astutia, ut, volens nocere, prosit. Diligam te, Domine, propugnator potens, gubernator prudens, consolator clemens, remunerator affluens. Securus in eum projicio omnem sollicitudinem meam, cujus nec potentia vinci, nec sapientia falli, nec benevolentia potest fatigari quominus expleat omnem indigentiam meam. Quam satius, quam securius est ipsum esse sollicitum, quam me ipsum pro me! quam bonum est confidere in Domino, quam in homine! quam jure maledictus qui confidit in homine, carnem ponit brachium suum, et recedit a Domino cor ejus! (Jerem. XVII, 5.) et quidem egenus et pauper ego sum; sed si Dominus sollicitus est mei, dives sum, beatus sum; cui nimirum omnia cooperantur in bonum. Sperent igitur in te qui noverunt nomen tuum, quoniam non derelinquis sperantes in te, Domine (Psal. IX, 11); sedeatque populus tuus, sicut scriptum est, in pulchritudine pacis, in tabernaculis fiduciae, in requie opulenta, in securitate sempiterna (Isai. XXXII, 18, 17). Omnino pulchra pax et securitas sempiterna, habitare in adjutorio Altissimi, in protectione Dei coeli commorari. Prorsus requies opulenta, sedere feriatum sub vite vera, ficu et oliva; et post saturitatem variorum fructuum, se ipsum mulcere carmine amatorio, ac dicere: Sub umbra illius quam desiderabam [Vulg. quem desideraveram] sedi, et fructus illius dulcis gutturi meo? (Cant. II, 5.) Plane dulcis gutturi fructus de cujus gustu tam dulcis erumpit ructus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Open, Lord, the eyes of this child\u2014I speak of one unformed and a novice\u2014who, when he is afflicted, thinks himself abandoned. This would be altogether new and unheard of, were I to see any just man abandoned, since the Church says: \"I was young, and now I am old, and I have not seen the just man abandoned\" (Psal. XXXVI, 25). Open, I say, O Lord, the eyes of your servant, that he may see that there are more with us than with our adversaries. For the Lord of hosts is with us, and with him every power and the army of heaven, indeed the favor of every creature, serving the nod and will of its Creator. For if God is for us, who is against us? (Rom. VIII, 31.) And who is there to harm us, if we are zealous for what is good? (I Petr. III, 13.) That envious one may rage; but this will be to serve us. He may burn, beat, cut apart; but this will be to fashion crowns. I will love you, Lord, my strength, through whom every opposing strength yields to me, a mere worm, and the cunning of the ancient dragon is mocked by your angels, sent for our service, so that, wishing to do harm, it profits us. I will love you, Lord, mighty defender, prudent governor, gentle comforter, abundant rewarder. Secure, I cast all my anxiety upon him, whose power cannot be conquered, whose wisdom cannot be deceived, whose benevolence cannot be wearied from supplying all my need. How much better, how much more secure it is that he himself be anxious for me, than that I should be anxious for myself! How good it is to trust in the Lord, rather than in man! How justly cursed is he who trusts in man, and makes flesh his arm, and whose heart withdraws from the Lord! (Jerem. XVII, 5.) And indeed I am needy and poor; but if the Lord is anxious for me, I am rich, I am blessed; for to such a one, surely, all things work together for good. Let those, then, hope in you who know your name, for you do not abandon those who hope in you, O Lord (Psal. IX, 11); and let your people sit, as it is written, in the beauty of peace, in the tabernacles of confidence, in opulent rest, in everlasting security (Isai. XXXII, 18, 17). Truly it is a beautiful peace and an everlasting security, to dwell in the help of the Most High, to abide under the protection of the God of heaven. Truly it is an opulent rest, to sit at leisure beneath the true vine, the fig tree, and the olive; and, after being filled with manifold fruits, to soothe oneself with a love-song, and to say: \"Under his shadow, which I desired [Vulg.: whom I had desired], I sat, and his fruit was sweet to my throat\" (Cant. II, 5.). Truly sweet to the throat is that fruit, from the tasting of which so sweet an eructation breaks forth.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Haec sunt ergo tabernacula fiduciae, in quibus verus Israel habitat confidenter, pascit et cubat, et non est qui exterreat; et, sicut Sapientia promittit, Requiescet absque terrore, abundantia perfruetur, malorum timore sublato (Prov. I, 33). Et bene timore malorum sublato, non timore Domini; ne fiducia ista et securitate, quam commendamus, negligentiam putes generari, cum non in alio quam in timore Domini sit fiducia fortitudinis. Timor quippe, dum cavet offensam, custodit gratiam; locumque servat fiduciae, quandiu tibi conscius non es Dominum offendisse. Inde fit ut nonnisi caste timeas eum; praeter eum nullatenus timeas alienum. Sola ergo conscientia bona fiduciam istam praesumit, suadet corda nostra esse in conspectu Judicis aeterni. Quis enim bene sibi de illo promittat, a quo se minime diligi intelligat? Qui autem diligit, nunquam diffidit, sciens quia is quem corda non fallunt, diligentes se diligit, etiam cum arguit et corripit. Siquidem eruditis corde in sapientia non aufert, sed auget fiduciam Patris disciplina, scientes scriptum, Beatus qui corripitur a Domino (Job V, 17), et: Ego quos amo arguo et castigo (Apoc. III, 19; Prov. III, 12). Cum igitur exarserit in brevi ira ejus, beati omnes qui confidunt in eo (Psal. II, 13), quos scilicet amoris proprii conscientia consolatur, quia, cum iratus fuerit, misericordiae recordabitur; et, cum dixerint: Confitebor tibi, Domine, quoniam iratus es mihi, statim subsequentur: Conversus est furor tuus, et consolatus es me (Isa. XII, 1), eo quod nonnisi in brevi exarserit ira ejus, quam tam cito placaverit confessio reatus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. These, then, are the tabernacles of confidence, in which the true Israel dwells securely, feeds and lies down, and there is none to terrify him; and, as Wisdom promises, \"He shall rest without terror, and shall enjoy abundance, the fear of evils being removed\" (Prov. I, 33). And rightly is it \"the fear of evils\" that is removed, not the fear of the Lord; lest you should think that this confidence and security which we commend breeds negligence, since the confidence of strength lies in nothing other than the fear of the Lord. For fear, while it guards against giving offense, preserves grace, and keeps a place open for confidence, so long as you are not conscious of having offended the Lord. Hence it follows that you should fear him alone chastely; and besides him you should in no way fear any other. It is a good conscience alone, then, that lays claim to this confidence, persuading our hearts that they stand in the sight of the eternal Judge. For who could rightly promise himself good from one by whom he understands himself to be in no way loved? But he who loves never distrusts, knowing that he whom hearts cannot deceive loves those who love him, even when he rebukes and corrects them. Indeed, for those whose hearts are instructed in wisdom, the Father's discipline does not take away but increases confidence, since they know it is written: \"Blessed is the man whom the Lord corrects\" (Job V, 17), and: \"Those whom I love, I rebuke and chastise\" (Apoc. III, 19; Prov. III, 12). When, therefore, his anger has been kindled but for a short while, blessed are all they who trust in him (Psal. II, 13), namely those whom the awareness of their own love consoles, because, when he has been angry, he will remember mercy; and when they shall have said: \"I will give thanks to you, O Lord, for you were angry with me,\" there shall at once follow: \"Your wrath is turned away, and you have comforted me\" (Isa. XII, 1), because his anger was kindled only for a short while, which the confession of guilt so quickly appeased.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Merito igitur benedictum virum qui confidit in Domino ligno comparat Jeremias quod transplantatur super aquas: quod quia ad humorem amoris mittit radices cordis, non timebit cum venerit aestus irae et tribulationis; sed et in tempore siccitatis, cum multo tempore coelum clausum fuerit, nec ros nec pluvia gratiae super eum descenderit, non erit tamen sollicitum, quasi Deus eum projecerit (Jerem. XVII, 7, 8). Sentit quippe se plantatum fide, radicatum charitate super aquas vitae, quae, secundum Ezechielem, de sanctuario egredientes vivificant omnia, et habent in utraque ripa omne lignum pomiferum, cujus folium non defluit, nec fructus deficit (Ezech. XLVII, 3-12). Cur igitur lignum illud benedictum aut timeret aestum, aut de siccitate esset sollicitum, cui aqua viva, id est Spiritus sancti gratia, vitalem in occulto succum spei et charitatis subministrare non desinit; unde et folium sit viride, id est Verbum plenum gratiae et veritatis; et ipsum non desinat aliquando facere fructum quodcunque opus pietatis? Et grata quidem illa verna temperies, pacis et laetitiae; desiderabilis illa pluvia voluntaria, quam segregavit Deus haereditati suae; sed, si ita necesse est, adurat omnia aestus tribulationis, veniant iterum quaecunque prophetavit Jeremias de sermonibus siccitatis spiritualis; non tamen timebit, quem fiducia Domini radicavit super aquam refectionis (Jerem. XVII, 6, 8), id est gratiam Spiritus sancti; quae etsi manifeste non pluat, ut sensibiliter influat, occulte tamen interius vivificat et fecundat; quandiu videlicet fidem propositi conservat, ad perseverandum confortat, verbumque irreprehensible et opus stabile subministrat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Rightly, then, does Jeremiah compare the blessed man who trusts in the Lord to a tree that is transplanted beside the waters: because it sends the roots of its heart into the moisture of love, it will not fear when the heat of wrath and tribulation comes; but even in time of drought, when the sky has long been shut up, and neither dew nor rain of grace has descended upon it, it will nevertheless not be anxious, as though God had cast it off (Jerem. XVII, 7, 8). For it feels itself planted by faith, rooted by charity, upon the waters of life, which, according to Ezekiel, going forth from the sanctuary, give life to all things, and have on either bank every fruit-bearing tree, whose leaf does not fall nor its fruit fail (Ezech. XLVII, 3-12). Why, then, should that blessed tree either fear the heat, or be anxious about drought, since the living water\u2014that is, the grace of the Holy Spirit\u2014does not cease to supply it, in secret, with the vital sap of hope and charity; whence its leaf also is green, that is, the Word full of grace and truth; and it itself never at any time ceases to bear fruit, whatever work of piety it may be? Pleasant indeed is that springtime mildness of peace and joy; desirable is that bountiful rain which God has set apart for his inheritance; but if it must needs be so, let the heat of tribulation scorch everything, let there come again whatever Jeremiah prophesied concerning words of spiritual drought; still he will not fear, whom trust in the Lord has rooted upon the water of refreshment (Jerem. XVII, 6, 8), that is, the grace of the Holy Spirit; which, even if it does not rain down openly so as to flow in perceptibly, nevertheless secretly gives life and fruitfulness within\u2014so long, that is, as it preserves the faith of one's purpose, strengthens him to persevere, and supplies a blameless word and a stable work.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. In hujusmodi vestra, fratres, sit consolatio, quoties alias consolationes non modo carnales, sed et spirituales subtraxerit vobis non negligentia vestra, sed dispensatio divina. Et fortasse quidem illa, quam diximus, occulta gratia Spiritus irriguum est inferius, quo nimirum Axa non vult esse contenta, nisi adjiciatur ei et irriguum superius (Josue XV, 19); ut scilicet de excelso effundatur Spiritus, rorent coeli desuper, et nubes angelicae pluant justum, justificans Dei verbum, loquens ad cor Jerusalem. Et bene illud irriguum inferius, quod quasi ad radices fluens nutrit humilitatem; hoc autem superius, quod sese desuper effundens, spe et exsultatione attollit mentem. Si ergo et vos, fratres, ad illud suspiratis superius irriguum, laudabile quidem desiderium; si tamen nondum attingitis, mittite radices interim ad humorem, salubre remedium. Qui igitur non praevalet capere laetitiam contemplativae, meditetur justitiam vitae activae. Inde radices affectionum impinguet, dulcoret mores, omnemque vitae statum informet, quatenus in ipso nec marcescat aut defluat folium, id est verbum leviter aut inutiliter prolatum, nec aliquando vita desinat facere fructum. Benedictum lignum, cujus folium ad medicinam, fructus ad vitam; id est, cujus sermo gratiam parit audienti, et opus vitam facienti."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Let your consolation, brothers, be of this kind, whenever other consolations\u2014not only carnal but also spiritual\u2014are withdrawn from you, not through your own negligence, but by divine dispensation. And perhaps indeed that hidden grace of the Spirit of which we spoke is the lower watering, with which Axa surely does not wish to be content, unless the upper watering also be added to her (Josue XV, 19); so that, namely, the Spirit may be poured out from on high, the heavens may drop dew from above, and the clouds of angels may rain down the Just One, the justifying Word of God, speaking to the heart of Jerusalem. And rightly is that called the lower watering, which, flowing as it were to the roots, nourishes humility; but this the upper watering, which, pouring itself out from above, lifts up the mind with hope and exultation. If, then, you too, brothers, sigh after that upper watering, it is indeed a praiseworthy desire; yet if you do not yet attain it, in the meantime send your roots down to the moisture\u2014a wholesome remedy. Let him, then, who cannot grasp the joy of the contemplative life, meditate upon the righteousness of the active life. From this let him fatten the roots of his affections, sweeten his habits, and shape his whole manner of life, so that in him the leaf may neither wither nor fall away\u2014that is, no word spoken lightly or uselessly\u2014nor may his life ever cease to bear fruit. Blessed is the tree whose leaf is for healing and whose fruit is for life; that is, whose speech brings forth grace for the hearer, and whose work brings forth life for the one who does it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Ad hoc profecto vos, fratres mei, quos carnalis generatio vel consuetudo saecularis plantaverat in terra inaquosa, in terra salsuginis, divina generatio vel mutatio dexterae Excelsi transplantavit super aquas refectionis; ut quibus prius propter sterilitatem parabatur securis et ignis, nunc plantati in domo Domini, in atriis domus Dei nostri, floreatis, et fructum faciatis, et fructus vester maneat. An non aquae refectionis Scripturae Spiritus sancti, in quibus meditamur die ac nocte? an non aquae refectionis, lacrymae compunctionis, quae nobis factae sunt panes die ac nocte? an non aquae refectionis, Sacramenta et subsidia nostrae salutis, quibus pascimur et potamur in altari? In his siquidem omnibus, quasi quibusdam rivulis, fons sapientiae qui oritur in medio paradisi, derivatur foris, et in plateis aquas suas dividit. Ego, inquit Sapientia, quasi aquaeductus exivi de paradiso. Dixi: Rigabo hortum meum plantationum, inebriabo partus mei fructum (Eccli. XXIV, 41, 42). Ecce, ut colligere potestis ex ipsius ore Sapientiae parientis et plantantis, hortus plantationum est congregatio filiorum. Numquid enim ego, qui alios parere facio, ipse non pariam, dicit Dominus? (Isai. LXVI, 9.) Ipse parit, cum ingenerat voluntatem bonam; plantat, cum instituit vitam; rigat, cum sensibus infundit gratiam; colit, cum moribus adhibet disciplinam. Audite me, divini fructus, et quasi rosa plantata super rivos aquarum fructificate (Eccli. XXXIX, 17). Mittite radices ad humorem vitae, id est ad amorem terrae viventium, non hujus in qua omnia senescunt et putrescunt. Non potest arbor facere fructum qui permaneat, nisi sursum in coelestibus radicem figat, ut quae sursum sunt, non quae super terram, quaerat et sapiat. Quod enim physici dicunt hominem esse arborem inversam, eo quod nervi corporis radicem et initium in vertice habeant, ego illo potius sensu interpretor, quod radicem amoris et desiderii figere debeat in coelo, in summo vertice rerum capite nostro Jesu Christo. Qui illuc radices miserit, et de illo fonte aeterno succum vitae et gratiae jugiter biberit, non timebit cum venerit aestus judicii; sed afferens et offerens fructum quem fecit plurimum, id remunerationis accipiet ut floreat in aeternum ante Dominum, cui est honor et gloria per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. For this very purpose, my brothers, you whom carnal generation or worldly custom had planted in a waterless land, in a land of saltiness, the divine generation or the change wrought by the right hand of the Most High has transplanted upon the waters of refreshment; so that you, for whom formerly, because of barrenness, the axe and the fire were being prepared, now, planted in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God, may flourish, and bear fruit, and your fruit may remain. Are not the waters of refreshment the Scriptures of the Holy Spirit, on which we meditate day and night? Are not the waters of refreshment the tears of compunction, which have become bread for us day and night? Are not the waters of refreshment the Sacraments and supports of our salvation, by which we are fed and given drink at the altar? In all these, as it were through certain little streams, the fountain of wisdom which rises in the midst of paradise is channeled abroad, and divides its waters in the streets. 'I,' says Wisdom, 'came forth from paradise like a watercourse. I said: I will water my garden of plantings, I will make drunk the fruit of my offspring' (Eccli. XXIV, 41, 42). See, as you can gather from the very mouth of Wisdom bringing forth and planting, the garden of plantings is the congregation of her sons. 'For shall not I, who make others to bring forth, myself also bring forth?' says the Lord (Isai. LXVI, 9.). He himself brings forth, when he begets a good will; he plants, when he establishes a life; he waters, when he pours grace into the senses; he cultivates, when he applies discipline to conduct. 'Hear me, divine fruits, and bear fruit like the rose planted beside streams of water' (Eccli. XXXIX, 17). Send down your roots to the moisture of life, that is, to the love of the land of the living, not of this land in which all things grow old and rot. A tree cannot bear fruit that will endure unless it fixes its root above, in the heavenly places, so that it may seek and savor the things that are above, not the things that are upon the earth. For as the natural philosophers say that man is an inverted tree, because the nerves of the body have their root and beginning in the crown of the head, I interpret this rather in this sense: that he ought to fix the root of love and desire in heaven, in the highest crown of all things, our head, Jesus Christ. Whoever sends his roots there, and continually drinks from that eternal fountain the sap of life and grace, will not fear when the heat of judgment comes; but bringing and offering the fruit that he has produced in abundance, he will receive this as his reward: that he may flourish forever before the Lord, to whom be honor and glory through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The quotation on sitting under the beloved's shadow and tasting his fruit comes from Song of Songs 2:3; the edition prints Cant. II, 5."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saint Benedict Sermon II", "topics": ["Saint Benedict", "trust in God", "faith", "hope", "charity", "tribulation", "consolation", "Holy Spirit", "Scripture", "compunction", "sacraments"], "url": "sermons/guerric-benedict-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-benedict-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., De amicitia Dei prae omnibus quaerenda., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 107-111.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint Benedict", "Feast of Saint Benedict", "Sirach 45:1", "2 Corinthians 12:9", "Sirach 9:1\u20132 (printed locator; quotation from Ecclesiastes 9:1-2)", "1 Kings 2:6\u20137 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Samuel)", "Romans 8:38\u201339", "Galatians 2:20", "1 Corinthians 9:27", "2 Corinthians 12:7", "Isaiah 28:18", "John 15:14", "Psalm 88:23 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 52:6 (historical numbering)", "John 17:1", "Romans 12:17", "Luke 6:22", "Galatians 1:10", "Amos 5:10", "Micah 7:6", "Galatians 4:16", "Song of Songs 1:5", "Romans 12:21", "Wisdom 7:30", "2 Corinthians 7:21 (printed locator; quotation from 2 Corinthians 8:21)"], "occasion": "In festo sancti Benedicti", "occasion_detail": "Feast of Saint Benedict", "occasion_label": "Saint Benedict", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric places friendship with God before human favor. He considers humility, charity, reputation and fraternal correction, including the hostility that truthful correction can provoke. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina under the feast of Saint Benedict. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De amicitia Dei prae omnibus quaerenda."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On seeking the friendship of God above all things.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Dilectus a Deo et hominibus (Eccli. XLV, 1). Paucis expressit bonum ac beatum virum, qualem nimirum constat dilectum Domini fuisse Benedictum. Paucis, inquam, comprehendit summam perfectionis, plenitudinem gratiae et virtutis, simulque beatitudinem vitae perennis, et consolationem praesentis. Quid enim deesse potest ad aeternam felicitatem illi qui a Deo diligitur? quid ad praesentem consolationem, illi qui ab hominibus diligitur? Illi nempe qui a Deo diligitur, etsi aliquid deesse videtur, non ob aliud deest, nisi ut nihil ei desit, et suo, ut putatur, defectu perfectior sit. Nam virtus in infirmitate perficitur. Paule, ait Dominus, sufficit tibi gratia mea (II Cor. XII, 9). Cui gratia Dei sufficit, sine damno gravi, imo cum lucro non mediocri aliquid gratiae deficit, quandoquidem ipse defectus et infirmitas virtutem perficit; et imminutio cujusdam gratiae, summam omnium gratiam Dei ampliorem ei et stabiliorem efficit. Longe fac, Domine, a servis tuis gratiam illam, quaecunque sit, quae adimere aut imminuere gratiam tui possit; unde videlicet aliquis, gloriosior in oculis suis, odiosior fiat in oculis tuis. Non est illa gratia, sed ira, illis plane quibus iratus es dari digna; quibus propter dolos posuisti talia, dejiciens eos dum allevantur, et elidens valide, dum super ventum ponuntur. Ut igitur illa sola gratia, sine qua nullus a te diligitur, salva nobis maneat, omnem aliam gratiam tua nobis gratia subtrahat, aut certe gratiam utendi simul conferat; quatenus habentes gratiam, per quam serviamus placentes cum metu et reverentia, de gratia muneris gratiam promereamur remuneratoris; et quanto quis fuerit gratiosior, tanto tibi fiat gratior."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Beloved of God and men\" (Eccli. XLV, 1). In few words he has expressed the good and blessed man, such as Benedict is known indeed to have been, the beloved of the Lord. In few words, I say, he has comprehended the sum of perfection, the fullness of grace and virtue, and at the same time the blessedness of life everlasting and the consolation of the present life. For what can be lacking to eternal felicity in him who is loved by God? What can be lacking to present consolation in him who is loved by men? Indeed, for him who is loved by God, even if something seems to be lacking, it is lacking for no other reason than that nothing may be lacking to him, and that by his seeming defect he may be made more perfect. For virtue is perfected in weakness. \"Paul,\" says the Lord, \"My grace is sufficient for thee\" (II Cor. XII, 9). To him for whom the grace of God suffices, some grace is lacking without grave loss\u2014indeed with no small gain\u2014since the very defect and weakness perfects virtue; and the diminishing of a certain grace makes God's grace, the highest of all graces, fuller and more stable for him. Keep far, O Lord, from Thy servants that grace, whatever it be, which could take away or diminish Thy grace; whereby, namely, someone, more glorious in his own eyes, becomes more hateful in Thine eyes. That is not grace but wrath, truly worthy to be given to those with whom Thou art angry; to whom, on account of deceits, Thou hast appointed such things, casting them down while they are being raised up, and dashing them violently while they are set upon the wind. Therefore, that that grace alone, without which none is loved by Thee, may remain safe to us, let Thy grace withdraw from us every other grace, or at least grant together with it the grace of using it rightly; so that, having the grace by which we may serve, pleasing with fear and reverence, we may merit from the grace of the gift the grace of the Rewarder; and the more gracious anyone is, the more may he become pleasing to Thee.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sane mirabili arte, sed mirabiliori charitate, prudens clementia clemensque prudentia Dei, saluti consuluit humanae, ut, cum cunctos diligat, non tamen facile de dilectione sua certos aut securos esse sinat; nec ob aliud magnam multitudinem dulcedinis suae timentibus se abscondat, nisi ut, dum semper custodit humiles, semper habeat amabiles. Sunt enim justi et sapientes, et opera eorum in manu Dei, et tamen nescit homo utrum amore an odio dignus sit; sed omnia in futurum servantur incerta (Eccli. IX, 1, 2) Moderator nempe omnium sic eis dispensat gratiam munerum et operum, ut ex quibusdam quae concedit, consolationem quod digni sint amore tribuat; ex quibusdam quae subtrahit, suspicionem ac metum quod odio digni sint injiciat. Consolatur, cum diluculo visitat; terret, cum subito probat. Nunc mortificat, nunc vivificat; nunc deducit ad inferos, nunc reducit. Nunc pauperem facit et ditat, nunc humiliat et sublevat (I Reg. II, 6, 7). Et in tanta vicissitudinum varietate tanto certius salutem nostram operatur, quanto nos incertiores de ea, cum majore metu ac tremore ei cooperamur. Ait quidem Paulus: Certus sum quia nihil me separabit a charitate Dei (Rom. VIII, 38, 39). Sed vere Paulus hic erat, non ego aut tu, quibus omnia in futurum servari expedit incerta. Paulus, inquam, hic erat qui hoc dicebat, cui nimirum et illud dicere competebat: Vivo jam non ego, vivit vero in me Christus (Galat. II, 20), qui scilicet ita totus in quemdam divinum conversus erat affectum, ut adhaerens Domino unus cum ipso esset spiritus. Vide tamen hunc ipsum Paulum, qui ita modo certus erat, alias trepidum ac sollicitum, et castigantem corpus suum, ne forte, cum aliis praedicaverit, ipse reprobus efficiatur (I Cor. IX, 27): vide etiam colaphizari a Satana (II Cor. XII, 7), ne forte extollatur. In ipso itaque Paulo jam tibi est videre quomodo omnis certitudo quae ad horam confortatur et convalescit de spiritus consolatione, item infirmatur et evanescit superveniente tentatione. Ego igitur miser et ipsa indignus vita, qua consolatione, qua fiducia me dignum esse amore praesumam, cui omnia et interiora et exteriora manifestum odium denuntiant; cujus vita, non tentatio, non militia est adversus peccatum, sed ultronea servitus peccati; qui tam mente quam carne servio legi peccati; qui cum prudentia carnis et amicitia mundi, quae ambae inimicae sunt Deo, foedus miserum pepigisse videor, plane foedus cum morte, pactum cum inferno? Scio tamen, scio qui dixit: Delebitur foedus vestrum cum morte, et pactum cum inferno non stabit (Isai. XXVIII, 18); quia nimirum inimicis Dei nil amicum esse poterit, et non mortem aut infernum, sed omnem quoque creaturam ad ultionem inimicorum zelus armabit. Quam beatus in die illo quicunque erit ille dilectus a Deo! quam laudabiles quibus tunc laus erit a Deo, quibus dicet evidenti rerum argumento: Vos amici mei estis, eo quod feceritis quae praecepi vobis! (Joan. XV, 14). Et haec quidem prima virtus, istud optimum gratiae munus, praecipuus vitae fructus: quia beatitudinis certissimum pignus, Dei scilicet amicitia promereri; quo propitio poterit homini aliquis inimicari, sed nihil proficiet inimicus in eo, et filius iniquitatis non apponet nocere ei (Psal. LXXXVIII, 23)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Indeed, by a wonderful art, but by a more wonderful charity, the prudent clemency and the clement prudence of God has taken counsel for human salvation, so that, although He loves all, He does not easily allow them to be certain or secure of His love; nor does He, for any other reason, hide from those who fear Him the great abundance of His sweetness, except that, while He always keeps them humble, He may always have them lovable. For there are just and wise men, and their works are in the hand of God, and yet man does not know whether he is worthy of love or of hatred; but all things are kept uncertain for the future (Eccli. IX, 1, 2). The Ruler of all things indeed so dispenses to them the grace of gifts and works, that from certain things which He grants, He gives the consolation that they are worthy of love; from certain things which He withdraws, He instills the suspicion and fear that they are worthy of hatred. He consoles when He visits at daybreak; He terrifies when He suddenly puts to the test. Now He brings death, now He brings life; now He leads down to the underworld, now He brings back. Now He makes poor and makes rich, now He humbles and lifts up (I Reg. II, 6, 7). And in so great a variety of changes, He works our salvation all the more surely, the more uncertain we are about it, since we cooperate with Him with greater fear and trembling. Paul indeed says: \"I am certain that nothing shall separate me from the charity of God\" (Rom. VIII, 38, 39). But truly this was Paul speaking, not I or you, for whom it is expedient that all things be kept uncertain for the future. Paul, I say, was the one who said this, to whom indeed it was also fitting to say: \"I live, now not I, but Christ liveth in me\" (Galat. II, 20)\u2014he who was so wholly turned into a certain divine affection that, clinging to the Lord, he was one spirit with Him. Yet see this very Paul, who was thus certain at one moment, at other times fearful and anxious, chastising his own body, lest perhaps, when he had preached to others, he himself should become reprobate (I Cor. IX, 27): see him also buffeted by Satan (II Cor. XII, 7), lest perhaps he be exalted. In Paul himself, then, you may now see how all certainty which is strengthened for an hour and grows strong from the consolation of the spirit, likewise grows weak and vanishes when temptation comes upon it. I, therefore, wretched and unworthy even of life itself\u2014with what consolation, with what confidence shall I presume myself worthy of love, I to whom all things, both inward and outward, proclaim manifest hatred; whose life is not a temptation, not a warfare against sin, but a willing servitude to sin; who serve the law of sin as much in mind as in flesh; who seem to have struck a wretched covenant with the prudence of the flesh and the friendship of the world, both of which are enemies to God\u2014plainly a covenant with death, a pact with hell? Yet I know, I know Him who said: \"Your covenant with death shall be done away, and your pact with hell shall not stand\" (Isai. XXVIII, 18); because surely nothing can be friendly to the enemies of God, and zeal will arm not only death and hell but every creature as well for the vengeance upon enemies. How blessed on that day will whoever is beloved of God be! How praiseworthy will those be to whom praise shall then come from God, to whom He will say, with manifest proof of the fact: \"You are my friends, because you have done what I have commanded you!\" (Joan. XV, 14). And this indeed is the first virtue, this the best gift of grace, the chief fruit of life: because it is the most certain pledge of blessedness, namely, to merit the friendship of God; with God favorable to him, someone may indeed be an enemy to a man, but the enemy will profit nothing against him, and the son of iniquity shall not have power to hurt him further (Psal. LXXXVIII, 23).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Jam vero si post hanc et per hanc summam omnium causamque bonorum, gratiam etiam illam promerearis, ut, dilectus a Deo, etiam dilectus sis ab hominibus, quae consolatio est illa in miseriis hujus vitae, quae requies, quod gaudium, quae deliciae; praesertim si didiceris favore gratiae humanae non evanescere, sed exardescere ad diligendum Deum amplius, propter quem diligaris ab hominibus? Alioquin nempe si aliunde oriatur, vel alio referatur, quid est favor humanus, nisi ventus urens, aura corrumpens vel pestilens, latrunculus spolians, homicida insidians, coluber in via, cerastes in semita, mordens ungulas equi, ut cadat ascensor ejus retro? Vir quippe vanus erigitur in superbiam, et vanitate sensus sui, velut equo praecipiti, fertur in ruinam. Hujusmodi nimirum serpens vulnerat et subvertit vestigia, dum, veluti blandiens capiti, tenera illius facit laudari primordia. Haud secus, nisi fallor, contingit homini qui, antequam diligere sciat, diligi nimie affectat, et, antequam vel sibi ipsi amicus esse didicerit, amicos sibi omnes conciliare quaerit; facit divitias non cum judicio, in medio dierum suorum derelinquet eas, et in novissimo suo erit insipiens, cum omnes amici ejus spreverint eum, pro quibus sprevit ipse Deum. Tunc fiet sermo qui scriptus est: Quoniam Deus dissipavit ossa eorum qui hominibus placent: confusi sunt, quoniam Deus sprevit eos (Psal. LII, 6)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Now if, after this and through this love, supreme above all and the cause of good things, you should also merit that grace, that, being loved by God, you might also be loved by men, what consolation is that in the miseries of this life, what rest, what joy, what delight\u2014especially if you have learned not to grow vain through human favor, but rather to be kindled to love God the more, on whose account you are loved by men? Otherwise, indeed, if it arises from elsewhere, or is referred to something else, what is human favor but a scorching wind, a corrupting or pestilent breeze, a thieving brigand, a lurking murderer, a serpent in the way, a horned snake in the path, biting the horse's heels so that its rider falls backward? For the vain man is lifted up into pride, and by the vanity of his own mind, as if by a headlong horse, is carried to ruin. This kind of serpent indeed wounds and overturns the footsteps, while, as if flattering the head, it makes his tender beginnings to be praised. Not otherwise, unless I am mistaken, does it happen to a man who, before he knows how to love, excessively desires to be loved, and, before he has even learned to be a friend to himself, seeks to win all men as friends to himself; he gathers riches without judgment, in the midst of his days he will abandon them, and in his last end he will be a fool, when all his friends have despised him, for whose sake he himself despised God. Then will be fulfilled the saying that is written: 'For God has scattered the bones of those who please men: they are confounded, because God has despised them' (Psal. LII, 6).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Primo itaque omnium illa, quae prima et finis est omnium, quaerenda est dilectio Dei, cujus merito digni simus etiam ab hominibus diligi; et cujus studio docti simus, dilectione hominum qualiter nos oporteat uti. Cum autem rem in tuto locaveris; ita scilicet firmato affectu mentis, ut nonnisi in Deo et propter Deum diligi velis: tunc omnino volo ut dulcedo morum tuorum, humilitas obsequiorum, honestas studiorum, ad omnem te commendet conscientiam hominum, ut ultro rapias affectus omnium, testimonio praediceris omnium; quatenus ipsa religio, quae te commendat, justa vicissitudine per te commendabilis fiat. Sic in te quoque filio adoptivo, Unigeniti complebitur oratio: Pater, clarifica Filium tuum, ut Filius tuus clarificet te (Joan. XVII, 1); dum videntes claritatem operum tuorum glorificabunt Patrem tuum. Neque id dicimus, quod inter dilectionem Dei et dilectionem proximi sit aliquis ordo temporis, quamvis sit ordo affectionis. Statim quippe ab initio et illam oportet observare, et istam non negligere; quando nec Deus sine proximo, nec proximus sine Deo vere potest diligi; quamvis ordinata affectio ignorare non possit quae de duabus dilectionibus praeponderet alteri, quae debeat alteri formam et modum praescribere, finemque praefigere. Sed hoc dicimus, quod aliud est amari, aliud amari studere. Quod profecto studium sicut periculose assumitur ab illis qui plus vanitate quam charitate blandi sunt, sic periculose negligitur ab his qui plus superbia quam sapientia austeri sunt. Quam ob rem beatum dixerunt, qui inter utrumque vitium, illius scilicet vanae amicabilitatis, et superbae severitatis, mediam tenere potuit regalem viam veritatis; id est ut, vera charitate plenus, nec per vanitatem hoc studium sectetur, nec per tumorem dedignetur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Therefore, before all things, that love of God must be sought which is the first and the end of all things, by whose merit we may be worthy to be loved by men as well; and by whose discipline we may be taught how we ought to make use of the love of men. But when you have placed the matter in a safe position\u2014that is, when the affection of your mind has been so fixed that you wish to be loved only in God and for God's sake\u2014then I wholly desire that the sweetness of your character, the humility of your service, the uprightness of your pursuits, should commend you to the conscience of all men, so that you may win the affections of all unsought, and be proclaimed by the testimony of all; so that religion itself, which commends you, may by a just reciprocity become commendable through you. Thus in you too, as an adopted son, the prayer of the Only-begotten will be fulfilled: 'Father, glorify your Son, that your Son may glorify you' (Joan. XVII, 1); while men, seeing the splendor of your works, will glorify your Father. Nor do we say this as though there were some order of time between the love of God and the love of neighbor, although there is an order of affection. For from the very beginning one must both observe the former and not neglect the latter, since neither can God truly be loved without neighbor, nor neighbor without God; although a well-ordered affection cannot fail to know which of the two loves outweighs the other, which ought to prescribe form and measure to the other, and set its end before it. But this we say: that it is one thing to be loved, another to strive to be loved. Which pursuit indeed is dangerously undertaken by those who are winsome more from vanity than from charity, and is just as dangerously neglected by those who are austere more from pride than from wisdom. For this reason they have been called blessed who, between both vices\u2014namely that of vain amiability and that of proud severity\u2014have been able to hold the middle royal road of truth; that is, so that, being full of true charity, they neither pursue this pursuit through vanity, nor disdain it through arrogance.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Illa tamen est vis et natura veri amoris, ut, etiam cum non affectat, tamen efficiat se reamari: quoniam veritas, etiam sine alieno, suffragio facile se ad omnem conscientiam commendat, nisi dira malignae mentis obsistat nequitia, quae parata est sinistre interpretari omnia. Ad hunc tamen sanctum amorem conciliandum quidam proprium donum habent ex Deo; qui exhilarans facies eorum in oleo, perfudit eos quodam placore ac nitore gratioso, omnia tam facta quam dicta ipsorum gratificans in oculis omnium; cum multi qui forsitan non minus, imo et amplius amant, non facile eamdem gratiam inveniant. Ad omnes autem pertinet ut, providentes bona non solum coram Deo sed etiam coram hominibus (Rom. XII, 17 et II Cor. VII, 21), nec conscientiam negligant amore famae, nec famam fiducia conscientiae. Nam et de ipsa conscientia quomodo tibi blandiri potes, nisi sine querela sis inter fratres; imo, nisi te fratrem inter fratres in omni conversatione tua demonstres? Putas enim satis esse, si non scandalizas? Imo scandalizas, si non aedificas; si non, testimonium habens bonum intus et foris, quantum ad gradum tuum attinet, Deum ubique glorificas. Sane malis, quibus non placet nisi malum, quibus justus gravis est etiam ad videndum, si illis placere te non sinit, non tua negligentia (cum omnes etiam inimicos diligas), sed ipsorum nequitia, qui retribuunt mala pro bonis, odium pro dilectione tua; consolatur te qui ait. Beati eritis cum vos oderint homines (Luc. VI, 22), et ille qui ait quia, talibus si placeret, Christi servus non esset (Galat. I, 10)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Yet it is the force and nature of true love that, even when it does not aim at it, it nevertheless brings it about that it is loved in return: because truth, even without another's support, easily commends itself to every conscience, unless the dire wickedness of a malignant mind stands in the way, which is ready to interpret everything in the worst sense. Yet for the winning of this holy love, some have a particular gift from God; who, gladdening their faces with oil, has suffused them with a certain graciousness and gracious brightness, making both their deeds and their words pleasing in the eyes of all; while many who perhaps love no less\u2014indeed even more\u2014do not easily find the same grace. But it belongs to all to take thought for what is good, not only before God but also before men (Rom. XII, 17 et II Cor. VII, 21), neither neglecting conscience out of love of reputation, nor neglecting reputation out of confidence in conscience. For how can you flatter yourself even about your conscience itself, unless you are without reproach among the brethren\u2014indeed, unless you show yourself a brother among brothers in all your conduct? Do you think it enough if you do not give scandal? Nay, you do give scandal if you do not build up; if you do not, having good testimony both within and without, as far as pertains to your rank, glorify God everywhere. Indeed, as for the wicked, whom nothing pleases but evil, to whom the just man is grievous even to look upon\u2014if they do not allow you to please them, it is not through your negligence (since you love even your enemies), but through their own wickedness, who repay evil for good, hatred for your love; he consoles you who says, 'Blessed shall you be when men hate you' (Luc. VI, 22), and he who says that if he pleased such men, he would not be a servant of Christ (Galat. I, 10).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Verum illud miserabile est malum, omnique lacrymarum flumine plangendum, quod quidem saepius experiuntur non modo praelati, sed etiam subditi, qui fratres suos aemulantur Dei aemulatione; quod scilicet et boni, licet non ex ea parte qua sunt boni, bonos inveniuntur odisse. Oderunt corripientem in porta (Amos V, 10), et inimici hominis domestici ejus (Mich. VII, 6), ita ut etiam Paulus ingemiscat quod filiis et amicis inimicus factus sit, vera dicens (Galat. IV, 16). Ergo et ipse expertus est quod comicus ait, \u00ab Veritas odium parit (6); \u00bb imo et illud quod sponsa memorat: Filii matris meae pugnaverunt contra me (Cantic. I, 5), cum ego pugnarem pro eorum salute. O victrix regina, o triumphatrix magnifica, pugna, ne cedas, ne discedas, aut lassescas. Noli vinci a malo, sed vince in bono malum (Rom. XII, 21). Sapientia vincit malitiam (Sap. VII, 30); quanto magis infirmitatem aut imprudentiam? Neque contradictionem carnalium, malitiam interpretari potest charitas spiritualium; sed magis ignorantiam, aut infirmitatem qua praeventi sunt. Quanquam nec ipsis Apostolis contumelia visa sit quod mali a Domino sunt appellati. Quare autem haec ita loquor, fratres mei, dilecti Deo et mihi? Nunquid quia malum hujusmodi suspicer in aliquo vestrum, contra quod mihi vel fratribus meis sit pugnandum? Nunquid enim aliquis invenitur in vobis, non dico rebellis, sed vel durus et intractabilis? Loquor haec non quia ita in vobis sit, sed ne aliquando sit; ne forte aliquando cuipiam aliqua infirmitas subrepat, ut quamcunque correptionem charitatis, nisi cum charitate suscipiat. Sic fiet ut tam is qui corripit, quam ille qui corripitur, dilectus sit a Deo et hominibus, et memoria utriusque in benedictione sit; quod meritis beati Benedicti patris nostri praestare dignetur ille singulariter benedictus Dei Patris Jesus Christus, qui est Deus benedictus in saecula. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. But that is a wretched evil, to be mourned with every flood of tears, which indeed not only superiors but also subjects often experience, who are zealous for their brothers with a godly zeal; namely, that even good men\u2014though not in that respect in which they are good\u2014are found to hate good men. \"They hate him who reproves in the gate\" (Amos V, 10), and \"a man's enemies are those of his own household\" (Mich. VII, 6), so that even Paul groans that he has become an enemy to his sons and friends by telling them the truth (Galat. IV, 16). Therefore he too experienced what the comic poet says, \"Truth begets hatred\"; indeed, he experienced also that which the Bride recalls: \"The sons of my mother have fought against me\" (Cantic. I, 5), while I was fighting for their salvation. O victorious queen, O magnificent triumphant one, fight on; do not yield, do not withdraw, nor grow weary. \"Be not overcome by evil, but overcome evil with good\" (Rom. XII, 21). \"Wisdom overcomes malice\" (Sap. VII, 30); how much more, then, weakness or imprudence? Nor can the charity of spiritual men interpret the opposition of carnal men as malice; but rather as ignorance, or as a weakness by which they have been overtaken. Although it did not even seem an insult to the Apostles themselves that they were called evil by the Lord. But why do I speak this way, my brothers, beloved of God and of me? Is it because I suspect such an evil in any of you, against which I or my brothers must fight? Is anyone found among you who is\u2014I do not say rebellious\u2014but even hard and intractable? I say these things not because it is so among you, but lest it ever be so; lest perhaps at some time some weakness should creep upon someone, so that he would not receive any correction of charity except with charity. Thus it will come about that both he who corrects and he who is corrected will be loved by God and by men, and the memory of each will be in blessing; which may he who is singularly blessed of God the Father, Jesus Christ, who is God blessed forever, deign to grant through the merits of our blessed father Benedict. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-n006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(6) TERENT. Andr. I, 1, 41."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-n006", "label": "English", "text": "(6) TERENCE, Andria, I, 1, 41.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Ecclesiastes 9:1-2 describes the just and wise in God's hand; the edition prints Eccli. IX, 1, 2."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The words on providing good things before God and men come from Romans 12:17 and 2 Corinthians 8:21; the edition prints Rom. XII, 17 et II Cor. VII, 21."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saint Benedict Sermon III", "topics": ["Saint Benedict", "friendship with God", "grace", "humility", "charity", "human favor", "reputation", "fraternal correction", "perseverance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-benedict-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-benedict-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO IV., De lenitate et fide., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 111-116.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint Benedict", "Feast of Saint Benedict", "Sirach 45:4", "Numbers 11:24\u201327", "Hebrews 11:24\u201325", "Numbers 12:13 (printed locator; quotation from Numbers 12:3)", "Numbers 16:15", "1 Kings 2 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Samuel)", "Mark 9:49", "Hebrews 11:26\u201327", "Ephesians 2:6", "Hebrews 11:27", "Romans 8:24", "Psalm 15:8 (historical numbering)", "Habakkuk 2:4", "Romans 1:17", "Hebrews 11:6", "Psalm 52:1\u20132 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 35:2\u20133 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 7:28", "Luke 18:8", "James 2:19", "Hebrews 11:1, 6", "Psalm 33:17 (historical numbering)", "Luke 23:30", "1 Corinthians 16:13", "Jeremiah, verse 3 (printed locator; quotation from Jeremiah 5:3)", "Hebrews 11:1"], "occasion": "In festo sancti Benedicti", "occasion_detail": "Feast of Saint Benedict", "occasion_label": "Saint Benedict", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric compares Benedict with Moses through faith and gentleness. He distinguishes living faith from feigned belief and connects vigilance before God with hope of the promised reward. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina under the feast of Saint Benedict. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De lenitate et fide."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On meekness and faith.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. In fide et lenitate ipsius sanctum fecit illum (Eccli. XLV, 4). Haec quidem de Moyse scripta sunt, sed hodie beato Benedicto non incongrue, quantum arbitror, aptata sunt. Qui, cum omnino plenus fuerit Spiritu sanctorum, multo magis de spiritu Moysi credendus est habuisse non minimum. Si enim Dominus de spiritu Moysi tulit, et posuit super omnem illam suffraganeam turbam seniorum, qui in partem ministerii sunt asciti (Num. XI, 24-27), quanto magis super istum qui plenitudinem totius ministerii tanto verius quanto spiritualius implevit? Ille dux fuit exeuntium de Aegypto; iste, renuntiantium saeculo: legislator ille, legislator et iste: ille tantum minister litterae occidentis; iste, spiritus vivificantis: ille ad duritiam cordis Judaeorum dedit eis justificationes non bonas, praeter pauca moralia; iste solam puritatem evangelicam simplicemque morum tradidit disciplinam: ille tam multa scribit intellectu difficilia, factu impossibilia, vel inutilia; iste rectissimam vitae scribit Regulam, sermone luculentam, discretione praecipuam. Denique dux ille filiorum Israel, quos de Aegypto eduxit, in requiem promissam non induxit; dux noster recta via, orientis tramite, tanquam signifer exercitus monachorum hodie praecessit nos in regnum coelorum. Itaque non erit absurdum si credatur illius adaequasse meritum, cujus invenitur etiam superasse ministerium. Nec incongrue videbitur ad ipsum esse translatum quod de illo scriptum legitur: In fide et lenitate ipsius sanctum illum Dominus; praesertim cum harum praecipue virtutum, fidei scilicet et lenitatis, nobis ipse magister sit, qui nequaquam aliter vixit quam docuit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"In his faith and meekness he made him holy\" (Eccli. XLV, 4). These words indeed were written of Moses, but today, as far as I judge, they have not been unfittingly applied to blessed Benedict. For since he was altogether full of the Spirit of the saints, he is all the more to be believed to have had no small share of the spirit of Moses. For if the Lord took from the spirit of Moses and placed it upon that whole assisting crowd of elders who were taken into a part of his ministry (Num. XI, 24-27), how much more upon him who fulfilled the fullness of that whole ministry all the more truly as he fulfilled it more spiritually? That man was the leader of those going out of Egypt; this one, of those renouncing the world: that man was a lawgiver, and this one also a lawgiver: that man was only a minister of the letter that kills; this one, of the spirit that gives life: that man, on account of the hardness of the Jews' hearts, gave them statutes that were not good, apart from a few moral precepts; this one handed down only the purity of the Gospel and a simple discipline of morals: that man writes so many things difficult to understand, impossible or useless to do; this one writes a most upright Rule of life, brilliant in style, outstanding in discretion. Finally, that leader of the sons of Israel, whom he led out of Egypt, did not bring them into the promised rest; our leader, by the straight way, along the path of the rising sun, has today gone before us like a standard-bearer of an army of monks into the kingdom of heaven. Therefore it will not be absurd if it is believed that his merit equaled that of the one whose ministry he is even found to have surpassed. Nor will it seem unfitting that what is read as written of that man should be transferred to him: \"The Lord made him holy in his faith and meekness\"; especially since he himself is our master chiefly of these virtues, namely faith and meekness, he who never lived otherwise than he taught.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Quid enim praeclarius illius fide, qui in aetate puerili mundum sibi arridentem irridens, tam mundi quam corporis sui florem quasi jam aridum calcavit, magis appetens pro Deo mala mundi perpeti, quam in eo temporaliter prosperari? Quid similius illi fidei Moysi, quam Apostolus commendans ait: Fide Moyses grandis factus negavit se filium filiae Pharaonis, magis eligens affligi cum populo Dei, quam temporalis peccati habere jucunditatem (Hebr. XI, 24, 25)? Quid autem sanctius lenitate Patris nostri, qui nec malitia falsorum fratrum vitae ipsius insidiantium: et pro vino venenum propinantium, potuit exasperari? Moysen quidem Scriptura praedicat mittissimum fuisse super omnes homines qui morabantur in terra (Num. XII, 13); nunquid tamen exacerbatum spiritum ejus negat? nunquid non iratum et valde iratum adversus aemulos memorat (Id. XVI, 15)? Hujus autem magistri nostri mansuetudinem, non solum erga maledicos, sed etiam erga maleficos, mirabilem me legisse memini, iracundiam non memini. Quanquam nec in ipso, sicut nec in Moyse, praeconio sanctae lenitatis praejudicet, si justus zelo contra peccantes fervet, sine quo lenitas tepor aut timiditas est. Quomodo namque lenitas illa sanctum quempiam faceret, quae in Heli, qui alius sanctus erat, damnata est? (I Reg. II). Fratres, habete pacem inter vos, mandat ille Magister pacificus et lenis; prius tamen praemittit: habete salem in vobis (Marc. IX, 49); sciens nimirum pacis lenitatem vitiorum esse nutricem, nisi prius asperitas zeli mordacem eis insperserit salem; sicut et carnes vermescere facit clementia temporis, nisi desiccaverit eas fervor salis. Habete itaque pacem inter vos, sed quae sale condita sit sapientiae; sectamini lenitatem, sed quae ferveat fide."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. For what is more splendid than the faith of him who, in his boyhood, mocking the world that smiled upon him, trampled underfoot the flower both of the world and of his own body as though already withered, desiring rather to suffer the evils of the world for God's sake than to prosper temporally in it? What is more like that faith of Moses which the Apostle commends, saying: \"By faith Moses, when he was grown a man, denied himself to be the son of Pharaoh's daughter, choosing rather to be afflicted with the people of God than to have the pleasure of temporal sin\" (Hebr. XI, 24, 25)? But what is holier than the meekness of our Father, who could not be provoked even by the malice of false brothers plotting against his very life, and offering him poison instead of wine? Scripture indeed proclaims that Moses was the meekest of all men who dwelt on the earth (Num. XII, 13); yet does it not also record his spirit as exasperated? Does it not relate him as angry, and very angry, against his rivals (Id. XVI, 15)? But I recall having read of the wonderful gentleness of this our master, not only toward those who spoke evil of him, but even toward those who did evil to him; I do not recall any anger in him. Although neither in him, nor in Moses, should this prejudice the praise of holy meekness, if a just man burns with zeal against sinners, without which meekness is mere lukewarmness or timidity. For how could that meekness which was condemned in Eli, who was otherwise a holy man, have made anyone holy? (I Reg. II). \"Brothers, have peace among yourselves,\" that peaceful and gentle Master commands; yet he first says beforehand: \"Have salt in yourselves\" (Marc. IX, 49); knowing surely that the meekness of peace is a nurse of vices, unless the sharpness of zeal first sprinkles them with biting salt; just as the mildness of the weather causes flesh to breed worms, unless the heat of salt dries it out. Have peace, then, among yourselves, but peace seasoned with the salt of wisdom; pursue meekness, but meekness that burns with faith.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. In fide et lenitate vos quoque sancti eritis; nec jam lenitas suspecta erit, si fides praecesserit: tantum fides non ficta, sed vera sit; fides non mortua, sed viva et vivida sit. Non modo viva vel vivida, sed et constans et intrepida fides erat Moysi, de quo Paulus scribit: Fide reliquit Aegyptum, non veritus animositatem regis. Animosi sunt reges, sed animosior est fides, quippe quae potentiam eorum nullam esse videt, ac per hoc omnem persequentium insaniam secura, quasi superior, irridet; promptior et fortior ad perferendum, quam illorum furor ad persequendum. Duo sane commendare mihi videtur Apostolus in illa memorabili fide Moysi, cui occasione lectionis hodiernae comparandam diximus fidem sancti patris nostri Benedicti: scilicet quod idem Moyses, qui fidei exemplum factus est, hujus mundi contempsit prospera, nec timuit adversa. Prospera contempsit, majores divitias aestimans thesauris Aegyptiorum improperium Christi: adversa non timuit, non veritus scilicet animositatem regis. Ad utrumque vero causam Apostolus subjunxit, unde scilicet id potuerit, ut discamus unde fides nostra infirmetur in nobis. Majores divitias reputabat thesauris Aegyptiorum improperium Christi: aspiciebat enim, inquit, in remunerationem. Non est veritus animositatem regis: invisibilem enim, inquit, tanquam videns sustinuit (Hebr. XI, 27, 26). Nimirum inde fit ut nihil reputentur temporalia, si prae oculis habeantur aeterna; inde fit ut facile potestas contemnatur humana, si tanquam semper imminens potestas timeatur divina. Utrumque vero sola fides agit, cujus tam vividi, tam perspicaces sunt oculi, ut et in ea quae futura sunt prospectum vivaciter porrigat, et in ea quae, licet praesentia sint, occulta tamen, intuitum perspicaciter figat. Fidei siquidem, quam aeternus Spiritus illuminat, nec mora temporis, nec moles corporis praejudicare potest quin et tempus praecedat, praesumendo quod futurum est, et corpus excedat, intuendo quod spiritus est. Gemina siquidem est fidei virtus, contemplantis non ea quae videntur, sed quae non videntur; sicut gemina est ratio eorum quae non videntur. Aut enim ideo non videntur quia praesentia non sunt, aut ideo quia, etsi praesentia sunt, spiritualia sunt. Praesentia non sunt bona futura quae promittuntur; praesens est, sed occultus est, quia spiritus est, Deus ipse qui promittit vel minatur. Porro fides, quia est substantia sperandarum rerum, bona futura, quae sperat, quasi praesentia praesumit, et jam quasi subsistere in corde credentis facit. Item quia est argumentum non apparentium (Ibid. 1), Deum, etsi non appareat, praesentem tamen esse sibi coarguit, probat et convincit. Illi qui dicebat: Qui conresuscitavit et consedere fecit in coelestibus in Christo (Ephes. II, 6), fides erat substantia rerum sperandarum; illi qui invisibilem tanquam videns sustinebat (Hebr. XI, 27), argumentum erat non apparentium. Qui enim dicebat: Spe salvi facti sumus (Rom. VIII, 24), nonne quod sperabat et per patientiam exspectabat, jam per fidem in corde suo subsistere demonstrabat? Et qui proponebat Dominum in conspectu suo semper, nonne persuasum tenebat, argumento fidei, eum praesentem esse, qui non apparebat? (Psal. XV, 8)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. In faith and gentleness you too will be holy; and gentleness will no longer be suspect, if faith has gone before it\u2014provided only that the faith be not feigned but true, not dead but living and vigorous. Not merely living or vigorous, but also steadfast and fearless was the faith of Moses, of whom Paul writes: \"By faith he left Egypt, not fearing the fierceness of the king.\" Kings are fierce, but faith is fiercer, since it sees that their power is nothing, and therefore, secure as one who is superior, it laughs at all the madness of persecutors; it is more ready and stronger to endure than their fury is to persecute. Two things, surely, the Apostle seems to me to commend in that memorable faith of Moses, which, on the occasion of today's reading, we said should be compared to the faith of our holy father Benedict: namely, that this same Moses, who became an example of faith, both despised the prosperities of this world and did not fear its adversities. He despised prosperity, esteeming the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasures of the Egyptians; he did not fear adversity, that is, he was not afraid of the fierceness of the king. For both the Apostle adds the cause, namely whence he was able to do this, so that we may learn whence our faith grows weak in us. He esteemed the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasures of the Egyptians: \"for he looked,\" it says, \"unto the reward.\" He did not fear the fierceness of the king: \"for he endured, as seeing him who is invisible\" (Hebr. XI, 27, 26). Surely it comes about from this that temporal things are reckoned as nothing, if eternal things are kept before the eyes; it comes about from this that human power is easily held in contempt, if divine power is feared as always imminent. But faith alone does both of these things, whose eyes are so lively, so keen-sighted, that it both stretches its gaze vividly toward things that are future, and fixes its sight keenly upon things which, although present, are nonetheless hidden. For to faith, which the eternal Spirit illumines, neither the delay of time nor the bulk of the body can be an obstacle, so that it both goes before time, presuming what is to come, and goes beyond the body, beholding what is spirit. The power of faith is indeed twofold, contemplating not those things which are seen, but those which are not seen; just as the reason of things not seen is also twofold. For either they are not seen because they are not present, or they are not seen because, although present, they are spiritual. The future goods that are promised are not present; but he who promises or threatens, God himself, is present, yet hidden, because he is spirit. Furthermore, faith, because it is the substance of things to be hoped for, presumes the future goods which it hopes for as if they were present, and makes them, as it were, already subsist in the heart of the believer. Likewise, because it is the evidence of things not appearing (ibid. 1), it proves, demonstrates, and convinces that God, even though he does not appear, is nevertheless present to it. To him who said: \"Who raised us up together, and made us sit together in the heavenly places in Christ\" (Ephes. II, 6), faith was the substance of things to be hoped for; to him who endured, as seeing him who is invisible (Hebr. XI, 27), faith was the evidence of things not appearing. For did not he who said, \"We are saved by hope\" (Rom. VIII, 24), show that what he hoped for and awaited through patience already subsisted in his heart through faith? And did not he who set the Lord always in his sight hold it firmly persuaded, by the evidence of faith, that he who did not appear was present? (Psal. XV, 8).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Huic fidei prorsus aptissime congruit quod Scriptura dicit: Justus ex fide vivit (Habac. II, 4, et Rom. I, 17). Haec enim justum facit, justumque custodit, et, ut vivat in aeternum, interim spei gaudio pascit. Quid enim sic hominem a peccato revocat et servat sicut fides, quae invisibilem tanquam videns sustinet et observat? quid ita spe gaudentem facit sicut fides, quae semper in remunerationem aspicit? Nos enim, fratres, quare negligentes sumus, nisi quia praesentiam Judicis nostri minus vigilanter intendimus? aut quare tristitia pene absorbemur, nisi quia remunerationem promissam minus fideliter cogitamus? Haec siquidem duo mala sunt, negligentia scilicet et tristitia, quibus miserabiliter infidelitas nostra laborat; quia videlicet aut negligimus mandata, aut, si necessitate timoris a negligentia coercemur, nequaquam hilariter operamur, nec, sicut fides postulat, spe remunerationis in labore consolamur. Credere, inquit, oportet accedentes ad Deum quia est, et inquirentibus se remunerator est (Hebr. XI, 6). Si non dissimulemus quia est, habemus custodiam timoris; si non dissimulemus quia inquirentibus se remunerator est, habemus consolationem spei. Ubi custodia timoris, nullus locus negligentiae; ubi consolatio spei, nullus locus tristitiae. Sicut autem de fide, qua Deus esse creditur, timor salutis nascitur, ita cum minime creditur, aut dissimulatur, omnibus malis frena laxantur. Quare enim corrupti et abominabiles facti sunt in iniquitatibus, nisi quia dixit ille generalis insipiens in corde suo: Non est Deus (Psal. LII, 2, 1)? aut certe si nescire Deum non permittitur, non est timor Dei ante oculos ejus, sed dolose agit in conspectu ejus, dissimulans quod novit invitus, ut inveniatur iniquitas ejus ad odium (Psal. XXXV, 2, 3). Merito prorsus dolosus ad odium, qui ignorantiam Dei non habet, sed odium. Quomodo enim non odit Deum, cujus declinat aspectum, contemnit imperium, vellet non esse judicium? Ignorans quidem ignorabitur, odiens autem et odietur. Ille reprobabitur veritate judicii, in istum vindicabitur severitate odii."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. To this faith what Scripture says applies most fittingly: \"The just man lives by faith\" (Habac. II, 4, et Rom. I, 17). For faith makes a man just, and keeps him just, and meanwhile, that he may live forever, feeds him with the joy of hope. For what so recalls a man from sin and keeps him from it as faith, which, as though seeing the Invisible One, endures and keeps watch? What so fills a man with the joy of hope as faith, which always looks toward the reward? For why, brothers, are we negligent, except because we attend less watchfully to the presence of our Judge? Or why are we almost swallowed up by sadness, except because we think less faithfully upon the promised reward? These indeed are two evils\u2014negligence and sadness\u2014under which our unfaithfulness miserably labors; for either we neglect the commandments, or, if we are restrained from negligence by the necessity of fear, we by no means work cheerfully, nor, as faith requires, do we console ourselves in our labor with the hope of reward. \"It is necessary,\" he says, \"that he who comes to God believe that he exists, and that he is a rewarder to those who seek him\" (Hebr. XI, 6). If we do not pretend otherwise but believe that he exists, we have the guard of fear; if we do not pretend otherwise but believe that he is a rewarder to those who seek him, we have the consolation of hope. Where there is the guard of fear, there is no room for negligence; where there is the consolation of hope, there is no room for sadness. But just as from the faith by which God is believed to exist, a saving fear is born, so when he is not believed in at all, or is dissembled, the reins are loosed to every evil. For why are men become corrupt and abominable in their iniquities, except because that universal fool said in his heart, \"There is no God\" (Psal. LII, 2, 1)? Or certainly, if it is not permitted him not to know God, then \"the fear of God is not before his eyes,\" but he deals deceitfully in his sight, dissembling what he unwillingly knows, \"that his iniquity may be found unto hatred\" (Psal. XXXV, 2, 3). Rightly indeed is he deceitful unto hatred, who has not ignorance of God, but hatred. For how does he not hate God, whose gaze he avoids, whose command he despises, whose judgment he would wish not to exist? He who is ignorant will indeed be ignored; but he who hates will also be hated. The former will be condemned by the truth of judgment; upon the latter vengeance will be taken by the severity of hatred.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Adeo, fratres, periculosior est fides ficta, fide dolosa, quae se ipsam dissimulat, quam fides nulla. Fides nulla, per insipientiam dicit in corde suo: Non est Deus; fides ficta per nequitiam dolose agit in conspectu ejus, et se scire Deum vel sciri a Deo dissimulat; dum sciens et prudens, imo imprudens et impudens sub oculis ejus peccat. Nescio tamen quomodo possit dici fides, quae fictos et dolosos potius facit quam fideles. Scio quidem quia confessione fidei plena est terra, sed tamen audio prophetam plangentem, quia periit fides de terra (Jerem. VII, 28). Putas enim, Filius hominis si nunc veniret, inveniret fidem super terram (Luc. XVIII, 8)? Nunquid fidem reputaret hanc fidem fictam negligentium et contemnentium, qua levius damnat caecitatem infidelium, qua meliorem judicat credulitatem daemonum? Daemones credunt et contremiscunt (Jacob. II, 19); homines credunt nec contremiscunt. Daemones quem credunt verentur; homines dum quem credunt, nec timent nec verentur, gravius de contemptu judicantur. Non fallamur ergo, fratres, generali nomine fidei, quasi quaecunque fides ad justitiam debeat reputari; sed meminerimus Doctor gentium in fide et veritate, qualem definierit fidem, qua Deo est placendum: Fides, inquiens, est substantia rerum sperandarum argumentum non apparentium. Haec est fides quae per dilectionem operatur; ut, suadente conscientia meritorum, de fide spes nascatur, fidesque supponatur velut subjectum et fundamentum, cui superaedificentur bona aeterna, quae speranda sunt. Sine ista fide impossibile est placere Deo (Hebr. XI, 1, 6), et cum ista impossibile est displicere Deo. Oculi tui Domine, respiciunt fidem (Jerem. 3 v.), dixit ille qui jugiter in conspectu tuo stabat per fidem. Omnino juste et merito. Prorsus debita vicissitudo, ut oculi tui, Domine, respiciant fidem, quia oculi mei semper ad Dominum fidelem."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. So much, brothers, is a feigned faith, a deceitful faith that dissembles itself, more dangerous than no faith at all. No faith at all, through folly, says in its heart, \"There is no God\"; feigned faith, through wickedness, deals deceitfully in his sight, and pretends either that it does not know God or that it is not known by God; while, knowingly and deliberately\u2014nay, imprudently and shamelessly\u2014it sins under his eyes. Yet I do not know how that can be called faith which makes men feigning and deceitful rather than faithful. I know indeed that the earth is full of the confession of faith, but yet I hear the prophet lamenting, because \"faith has perished from the earth\" (Jerem. VII, 28). Do you suppose that if the Son of Man came now, he would find faith upon the earth? (Luc. XVIII, 8). Would he reckon as faith this feigned faith of the negligent and the contemptuous, compared with which he condemns unbelievers' blindness more lightly and judges demons' belief better? \"The demons believe and tremble\" (Jacob. II, 19); men believe and do not tremble. The demons fear him whom they believe; men, while they believe him whom they fear not and do not revere, are more gravely judged for their contempt. Let us not, then, brothers, be deceived by the general name of faith, as though any faith whatsoever ought to be reckoned unto righteousness; but let us remember how the Doctor of the Gentiles defined, in faith and truth, the kind of faith by which God is to be pleased: \"Faith,\" he says, \"is the substance of things to be hoped for, the evidence of things not appearing.\" This is the faith that works through love; so that, with the consciousness of merits giving assurance, hope may be born from faith, and faith may be set beneath it as subject and foundation, upon which the eternal goods that are to be hoped for are built. \"Without this faith it is impossible to please God\" (Hebr. XI, 1, 6), and with it, it is impossible to displease God. \"Your eyes, Lord, look upon faith\" (Jerem. 3 v.), said he who stood continually in your sight through faith. Altogether justly and rightly. It is indeed a fitting exchange, that your eyes, Lord, should look upon faith, since my eyes are ever toward the faithful Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Nos autem, fratres, si tanquam sine fide simus, Deum post dorsum nostrum ponimus, ut, dissimulantes timorem ejus, in vanitates potius respiciamus, quo merito nos respiciendos putamus? Imo respiciemur, sed quo vultu? Vultus Domini super facientes mala (Psal. XXXIII, 17); sed quam iratus, quam terribilis, quam importabilis, tunc demum scietur, quando fugient qui oderunt eum a facie ejus. Et a facie tua, Domine, quo fugient, nisi in tenebras exteriores, in illud chaos et abyssum ignis et caliginis? Tunc enim dicent montibus, Cadite super nos; et collibus, Operite nos (Luc. XXIII, 30), levius aestimantes absorberi voragine inferni, quam sustinere faciem Dei irati. Enim vero tunc stabunt justi in magna constantia; tunc fides, quae modo sollicita stat in conspectu Domini ad videndam voluntatem ipsius, secura stabit ad videndam gloriam ejus. Vigilate, fratres, state in fide (I Cor. XVI, 13). Quem fides timore sollicitat, non potest dormitare per negligentiam; quem fides in spe radicat, non potest titubare per diffidentiam. Omnia autem vestra in charitate fiant, ut fidei lenitas jungatur, quatenus de unoquoque vestrum dicatur, In fide et lenitate ipsius sanctum fecit illum Dominus (Eccli. XLV, 4); quod ipse praestet Sanctus sanctorum, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. But we, brothers, if we are as though without faith, placing God behind our backs so that, disregarding fear of him, we look instead toward vanities\u2014with what justice do we think we deserve to be looked upon? Indeed we shall be looked upon, but with what face? \"The face of the Lord is against those who do evil\" (Psal. XXXIII, 17); but how angry, how terrible, how unbearable that face will be, will only then be known, when those who hated him flee from before his face. And from your face, Lord, where will they flee, except into outer darkness, into that chaos and abyss of fire and gloom? For then they will say to the mountains, \"Fall on us,\" and to the hills, \"Cover us\" (Luc. XXIII, 30), judging it lighter to be swallowed up in the chasm of hell than to endure the face of an angry God. But then indeed the just will stand in great steadfastness; then faith, which now stands anxiously in the sight of the Lord to see his will, will stand secure to see his glory. Watch, brothers, stand firm in faith (I Cor. XVI, 13). He whom faith troubles with fear cannot slumber through negligence; he whom faith roots in hope cannot waver through distrust. But let all your things be done in charity, so that gentleness may be joined to faith, so that it may be said of each one of you, \"In his faith and gentleness the Lord made him holy\" (Eccli. XLV, 4); which may he grant, the Holy One of holies, who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints mittissimum, where mitissimum (most gentle) is expected."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The description of Moses as the meekest of men comes from Numbers 12:3; the edition prints Num. XII, 13."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The words on the Lord's eyes looking upon faith come from Jeremiah 5:3; the edition prints Jerem. 3 v. without a chapter number."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saint Benedict Sermon IV", "topics": ["Saint Benedict", "Moses", "faith", "gentleness", "zeal", "hope", "fear of God", "judgment", "charity", "vigilance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-benedict-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-canticle", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO, In verba Cantici canticorum: \u00ab Quae habitas in hortis, amici auscultant, fac me audire vocem tuam. \u00bb\n(Cant. VIII, 13.), in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 210-214.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Song of Songs", "No liturgical occasion", "Song of Songs 8:13", "John 11:43\u201344", "Jeremiah 22:19", "Song of Songs 7:13", "Psalm 118:2, 129 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 24:27", "Psalm 118:103 (historical numbering)", "John 1:38\u201339", "Song of Songs 4:12", "Sirach 39:17", "Proverbs 16:23", "Sirach 40:21", "Psalm 103:31 (historical numbering; printed locator; passage from verse 34)", "Sirach 15:9", "Psalm 49:16 (historical numbering)", "Amos 5:25", "Jeremiah 8:6", "John 3:29", "Song of Songs 2:14"], "occasion": "No historical liturgical occasion established", "occasion_detail": "No liturgical occasion", "occasion_label": "Song of Songs", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric takes the Bridegroom's words \"You who dwell in the gardens, the friends are listening; let me hear your voice\" and declines them for himself, as one who dwells among the tombs of the flesh. The brothers dwell in gardens when they meditate on Scripture, and Christ, the true gardener, plants and waters the Church. Paul walked in the gardens of paradise and sang of Christ to the friends who listened. The words call the devout to psalmody and prayer, with the angels listening, and the preacher to speak, with the faithful listening. The sermon follows the All Saints sermon in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina and carries no liturgical heading. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "In verba Cantici canticorum: \u00ab Quae habitas in hortis, amici auscultant, fac me audire vocem tuam. \u00bb\n(Cant. VIII, 13.)"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the Words of the Song of Songs: \"You who dwell in the gardens, the friends are listening; let me hear your voice.\" (Cant. VIII, 13.)", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. In conventu sodalium et amicorum, id est, Ecclesia sanctorum, cujus vocem deceat audiri, sponsus ipse sponsae loquens, innuit cum ait: Quae habitas in hortis, amici auscultant, fac me audire vocem tuam. Non sum ego cui hoc dictum debeat videri: non sum qui habitem in hortis; magis mihi videor esse de illis qui habitant in sepulcris. Corpora enim peccatorum, quid sunt nisi sepulcra mortuorum? Qui ergo dediti sunt corporibus suis, non in hortis habitant, sed in sepulcris: Deumque exasperant, donec qui educit vinctos in fortitudine, forti voce inclamet, Lazari, veni foras; suisque discipulis super eo praecipiat, Solvite eum et sinite abire (Joan. XI, 43, 44). Magna sane differentia est sepulcrorum et hortorum. Haec plena omni spurcitia et ossibus mortuorum; illi, omni suavitate et gratia florum, aut fructuum. Quid enim si aliquando sepulcra videntur in hortis? Nam et Dominus in horto sepultus fuit. Etsi enim sepulcra in hortis, nunquid tamen horti in sepulcris? Ita fortassis, sed in sepulcris justorum. Ibi plane vernabit gratissima quaedam amoenitas hortorum, verno scilicet illo tempore resurrectionis eorum, cum reflorebit caro eorum, et non solum ossa justi sicut herba germinabunt, sed et totus justus germinabit sicut lilium, et florebit in aeternum ante Dominum. Non sic impii, non sic, qui sepultura asini sepeliuntur (Jerem. XXII, 19), et sine omni spe melioris resurrectionis subditi corruptioni, hac praesenti futuram auspicantur. De sepulcris eorum dicere coeperam, quod quantum a spurcitia illorum abhorret florentium species hortorum, tantum, imo incomparabiliter amplius distat oblectatio spiritualium a voluptate gaudiorum carnalium."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. In the assembly of companions and friends, that is, the Church of the saints, whose voice it is fitting should be heard, the Bridegroom himself, speaking to the Bride, hints at this when he says: You who dwell in the gardens, the friends are listening; let me hear your voice. I am not one to whom this should seem to be said: I am not one who dwells in gardens; I seem to myself rather to be among those who dwell in tombs. For what are the bodies of sinners but tombs of the dead? Those, then, who are given over to their bodies do not dwell in gardens but in tombs, and they provoke God to anger, until he who leads forth the bound in strength cries out with a mighty voice, \"Lazarus, come forth,\" and commands his disciples concerning him, \"Loose him and let him go\" (Joan. XI, 43, 44). There is indeed a great difference between tombs and gardens. The one is full of all filth and the bones of the dead; the other, of all the sweetness and grace of flowers or fruits. For what if tombs are sometimes seen in gardens? For even the Lord was buried in a garden. Though tombs may be in gardens, are gardens therefore in tombs? Perhaps so\u2014but in the tombs of the just. There, surely, a most pleasing charm of gardens will bloom, namely in that springtime of their resurrection, when their flesh will flower again, and not only will the bones of the just sprout like grass, but the whole just person will sprout like a lily, and will flourish forever before the Lord. Not so the wicked, not so those who are buried with the burial of an ass (Jerem. XXII, 19), and who, subject to corruption without any hope of a better resurrection, in this present corruption begin the corruption to come. I had begun to speak of their tombs, to say that as much as the appearance of flourishing gardens recoils from their filth, so much\u2014indeed incomparably more\u2014does the delight of spiritual things differ from the pleasure of carnal joys.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Vos igitur, ni fallor, estis qui in hortis habitatis, qui scilicet in lege Domini meditamini die ac nocte, et quot libros legitis, tot hortos perambulatis; quot sententias eligitis, tot poma carpitis. Et beati quibus omnia poma nova et vetera servata sunt: id est, tam prophetarum, quam evangelistarum, vel apostolorum eloquia reposita sunt, ut et unicuique vestrum illud sponsae ad sponsum videatur dictum, Omnia poma, nova et vetera, dilecte mi, servavi tibi (Cantic. VII, 13). Scrutamini igitur Scripturas. Vos enim non falso vitam in ipsis putatis vos habere, qui nihil aliud in illis quaeritis quam Christum, cui testimonium perhibent Scripturae. Beati plane qui scrutantur testimonia ejus; in toto corde exquirunt eum. Mirabilia testimonia tua, Domine; ideo scrutata est ea anima mea (Psal. CXVIII, 2, 129). Scrutinio quidem opus est, non solum ut eruantur mystica, sed etiam ut sugantur moralia. Ideoque vos, qui perambulatis hortos Scripturarum, nolite negligenter et otiose transvolare; sed scrutantes singula, velut apes sedulae mel de floribus, spiritum de sermonibus colligite. Spiritus enim meus, inquit Jesus, super mel dulcis, et haereditas mea super mel et favum (Eccli. XXIV, 27). Ita probantes quid sapiat manna absconditum, eructabitis illud Davidicum: Quam dulcia faucibus meis eloquia tua! super mel et favum ori meo (Psal. CXVIII, 103)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. You, then, unless I am mistaken, are they who dwell in gardens, who meditate on the law of the Lord day and night, and for as many books as you read, so many gardens you walk through; for as many sentences as you choose, so many fruits you pluck. And blessed are those for whom all fruits, new and old, are kept in store: that is, the sayings of the prophets, as well as of the evangelists and apostles, are laid up, so that to each of you that word of the Bride to the Bridegroom may seem said: \"All fruits, new and old, my beloved, I have kept for you\" (Cantic. VII, 13). Search the Scriptures, then. For you do not falsely suppose that you have life in them, you who seek in them nothing other than Christ, to whom the Scriptures bear witness. Blessed indeed are they who search his testimonies; they seek him with their whole heart. \"Wonderful are your testimonies, O Lord; therefore has my soul searched them\" (Psal. CXVIII, 2, 129). There is indeed need for searching, not only that mystical meanings may be dug out, but also that moral lessons may be sucked out. Therefore you who walk through the gardens of the Scriptures, do not fly over them carelessly and idly; but searching out each thing, like busy bees gathering honey from flowers, gather spirit from the words. For \"my spirit,\" says Jesus, \"is sweeter than honey, and my inheritance above honey and the honeycomb\" (Eccli. XXIV, 27). Thus, proving what the hidden manna tastes like, you will belch forth that word of David: \"How sweet are your words to my palate! above honey and the honeycomb to my mouth\" (Psal. CXVIII, 103).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Ab his hortis in alios, ubi requies secretior et voluptas beatior, et species mirabilior, sponsus, ni fallor, vos introducit, cum intentos laudibus suis in voce exsultationis et confessionis, rapit in locum tabernaculi admirabilis usque ad domum Dei, lucem scilicet inaccessibilem, ubi habitat, ubi pascit, ubi cubat in meridie. Si enim devotio psallentium et orantium habet aliquid de illa pia curiositate quaerentium, Rabbi, ubi habitas? puto quia merentur audire, Venite et videte. Venerunt, inquit, et viderunt, et apud eum manserunt die illo (Joan. I, 38, 39). Quandiu apud Patrem luminum sumus, apud quem non est transmutatio, nec vicissitudinis obumbratio; noctem nescimus, tantummodo die beato fruimur. Cum inde labimur, in noctem nostram recidimus. Heu mihi! quam cito defecerunt dies mei, quam cito sicut fenum arui, qui quandiu fui in horto cum illo, sicut paradisus Dei virui et florui! Cum illo hortus voluptatis: sine illo locus horroris et vastae solitudinis. Existimo enim qui in hortum illius intrat, quod et ipse hortus fiat; sitque anima illius velut hortus irriguus, ut et sponsus in laudem ejus dicat: Hortus conclusus, soror mea sponsa (Cant. IV, 12). An non sunt hortus, in quibus fit quod ipse hortulanus loquitur plantationi, quam plantavit Pater suus? Audite me, inquit, divini fructus, et quasi rosa plantata super rivos aquarum fructificate (Eccli. XXXIX, 17). Quasi Libanus, odorem suavitatis habete. Florete flores quasi lilium, date odorem, et frondete in gratiam."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. From these gardens into others, where the rest is more secret and the pleasure more blessed, and the beauty more wonderful, the Bridegroom, unless I am mistaken, leads you in, when, as you are intent upon his praises in a voice of exultation and confession, he snatches you away into the place of the wonderful tabernacle, even to the house of God, that is, the inaccessible light, where he dwells, where he feeds, where he lies down at midday. For if the devotion of those singing psalms and praying has something of that holy curiosity of those who seek, \"Rabbi, where do you dwell?\" I think that they deserve to hear, \"Come and see.\" \"They came,\" it says, \"and saw, and remained with him that day\" (Joan. I, 38, 39). As long as we are with the Father of lights, with whom there is no change nor shadow of alteration, we know no night; we enjoy only the blessed day. When we slip away from there, we fall back into our own night. Woe to me! How quickly my days have failed, how quickly I have withered like grass\u2014I, who, as long as I was in the garden with him, flourished and blossomed like the paradise of God! With him, the garden is a place of delight; without him, a place of horror and vast solitude. For I think that whoever enters into his garden becomes himself a garden; and that his soul becomes like a watered garden, so that the Bridegroom too may say in praise of it: \"A garden enclosed, my sister, my spouse\" (Cant. IV, 12). Are they not a garden, in whom happens what the Gardener himself speaks to the planting which his Father planted? \"Listen to me,\" he says, \"you divine fruits, and bear fruit like the rose planted beside the streams of water\" (Eccli. XXXIX, 17). \"Have a sweet fragrance like frankincense. Bloom like the lily, give forth fragrance, and put forth leaves in grace.\"", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. O Domine Jesu, vere hortulane, operare in nobis quod exigis a nobis; nam sine te nihil possumus facere. Tu enim verus es hortulanus, idem creator qui cultor vel custos horti tui, qui verbo plantas, spiritu rigas, virtute incrementum das. Errabas, Maria, quae eum existimabas hortulanum illius horti pauperis et exigui, in quo sepultus fuit. Hortulanus est totius mundi, hortulanus est coeli, hortulanus est Ecclesiae, quam hic plantat et rigat, donec incremento consummato transplantet eam in terram viventium, secus decursus aquarum viventium, ubi non timebit cum venerit aestus, sed erit folium ejus viride, nec aliquando desinet facere fructum. Beati qui habitant in illis hortis tuis, Domine! in saecula saeculorum laudabunt te. Paulus habitabat in illis, cujus conversatio erat in coelis: qui mente saepius excedens perambulabat paradisum beatae voluptatis, hortos deliciarum Dei, ubi et pertransiens rosas Martyrum, et lilia Virginum, desinens etiam mirari altitudinem cedrorum Dei delectabatur potius carpere de fructu ligni vitae, quod est in medio paradisi, in ipso nimirum gustans plenius et felicius quam suavis est Dominus. Ideoque cum inde redibat, amicis auscultantibus memoriam abundantiae suavitatis ejus profusius eructabat, et ex abundantia cordis os redundabat. Quia enim sicut adipe et pinguedine repleta erat anima ejus, ideo labiis exsultationis laudabat os ejus. Cor quippe sapientis erudiet os ejus, et labiis illius addet gratiam (Prov. XVI, 23). Eructabat itaque cor ejus verbum bonum, ac de bono thesauro bonus bona proferebat, sponsumque ipsum qui in amicis auscultabat, velut jucundo carmine oblectabat. Prorsus carmen jucundum, suavisque melodia, ubi tam canorum Spiritus sancti organum,, tanta morum et sermonum consonantia, tanta dulcedine amoris, tanta gratia oris Christum Jesum jubilum cordis sui personabat. Si enim lingua suavis vim carminis non haberet, scriptum non esset: Tibiae et psalterium faciunt suavem melodiam, et super utraque lingua suavis (Eccli. XL, 21). Si carmine hujusmodi Psalmista Deum oblectari nesciret, nequaquam diceret: Jucundum sit ei eloquium meum (Psal. CIII, 31). Sed nec ipse sponsus diceret: Fac me audire vocem tuam; sonet vox tua in auribus meis. Vox enim tua suavis (Cantic. VIII, 13, et II, 14), quia exaltationes Dei in faucibus tuis, tanquam habitantis in hortis, in deliciis paradisi. E contra vero, Non est speciosa laus in ore peccatoris (Eccli. XV, 9), qui habitat in sepulcris. Cujus enim vita Deum exasperat, lingua non delectat, sed terribiliter increpatur voce divina: Quare tu enarras justitias meas; (Psal. XLIX, 16.) Cantica lyrae tuae non audiam (Amos V, 25)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. O Lord Jesus, true gardener, work in us what you require of us; for without you we can do nothing. For you are the true gardener, being yourself the creator who is also the tiller and keeper of your garden, who plant by the word, water by the Spirit, and give increase by power. You were mistaken, Mary, when you thought him the gardener of that poor and small garden in which he had been buried. He is the gardener of the whole world, the gardener of heaven, the gardener of the Church, which he plants and waters here, until, the growth being completed, he transplants her into the land of the living, beside the courses of living waters, where she shall not fear when the heat comes, but her leaf shall be green, and she shall never cease to bear fruit. Blessed are they who dwell in those gardens of yours, Lord! They shall praise you forever and ever. Paul dwelt in them, whose citizenship was in heaven: who, often going out of himself in mind, walked through the paradise of blessed delight, the gardens of God's delights, where, passing by the roses of the Martyrs and the lilies of the Virgins, and ceasing even to marvel at the height of the cedars of God, he delighted rather to pluck from the fruit of the tree of life, which is in the midst of paradise, in it indeed tasting. And so, when he returned from there, with his friends listening, he poured forth more abundantly the memory of the abundance of its sweetness, and out of the abundance of the heart his mouth overflowed. For because his soul was filled, as it were, with fat and fatness, therefore his mouth praised with lips of exultation. For the heart of the wise shall instruct his mouth, and shall add grace to his lips (Prov. XVI, 23). And so his heart poured forth a good word, and as a good man out of a good treasure brought forth good things, and he delighted, as it were with a pleasant song, the very Bridegroom who was listening in his friends. Truly a pleasant song, and a sweet melody, where so tuneful an instrument of the Holy Spirit, so great a harmony of conduct and of words, with so much sweetness of love, with so much grace of mouth, sounded forth Christ Jesus, the jubilation of his heart. For if a sweet tongue did not have the power of song, it would not be written: 'The pipe and the psaltery make sweet melody, but a pleasant tongue surpasses them both' (Eccli. XL, 21). If the Psalmist did not know that God is delighted by a song of this kind, he would by no means say: 'Let my speech be pleasing to him' (Psal. CIII, 31). Nor would the Bridegroom himself say: 'Make me hear your voice; let your voice sound in my ears. For your voice is sweet' (Cantic. VIII, 13, et II, 14), because 'the exaltations of God are in your throat,' as of one dwelling in the gardens, in the delights of paradise. But on the contrary, 'Praise is not seemly in the mouth of a sinner' (Eccli. XV, 9), who dwells among tombs. For whoever's life provokes God, his tongue does not delight him, but he is terribly rebuked by the divine voice: 'Why do you declare my justices?' (Psal. XLIX, 16.); 'I will not hear the songs of your lyre' (Amos V, 25).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sed quoniam Scriptura quam assumpseram in excusationem sermonis, occasionem et materiam ministravit sermonis; si placet, finiamus quod de illa coeperamus. Dupliciter itaque potest intelligi, quod a sponso dicitur, Quae habitas in hortis, amici auscultant, fac me audire vocem tuam: vel quod devotum amatorem invitet ad psallendum vel orandum; vel quod sanctum praedicatorem excitet ad loquendum. Quod ut magis persuadeat, amicos auscultare memorat, angelos scilicet orantem vel psallentem; fideles praedicantem. Ubi primo intuemur, qua disciplina cordis et corporis nos oporteat in conspectu angelorum psallere vel orare, ne vacui dimissi vacuos nos dimittant, qui venerant ut vota deferrent, et dona referrent; imo recedant inimici qui venerant amici. Quinimo sponsus ipse, qui stat ad ostium et pulsat, si nulla ei digna devotio de intus respondens aperiat, recedet conquerens, et dicens: Attendi et auscultavi, nemo quod bonum est loquitur. Nullus est qui agat poenitentiam super peccato suo, dicens: Quid feci? omnes conversi sunt ad cursum suum, quasi equus impetu vadens in praelium (Jerem. VIII, 6). Si autem praedicatorem exhortatur, fiduciam ei loquendi tribuit de benevolentia et attentione auditorum, quia scilicet non increduli obloquuntur, aut litigant; non aemuli detrectant, aut subsannant; non tepidi dormitant, aut oscitant: sed amici attenti auscultant, quorum amor et meritum possit evangelizanti impetrare verbum et spiritum. Et bene amici auscultant. Est enim velut proprium amici, scilicet quod devotus audiat vocem sponsi, sicut Joannes ait: Amicus sponsi est qui stat, non vagans animo, aut prostratus somno, audit et gaudet gaudio propter vocem sponsi (Joan. III, 29), quam agnoscit etiam in servis. Et nos itaque inde probemus nos amicos, ut sive per os loquentis, sive legentis aut cantantis vox sponsi sonuerit, sic stemus ad audiendum, quatenus auditui nostro det gaudium et laetitiam; et non solum cum gaudio, suscipere verbum, sed et fructum afferre in patientia."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But since the Scripture which I had taken up as an excuse for my sermon has furnished the occasion and material for the sermon, let us, if it please you, finish what we had begun concerning it. What is said by the Bridegroom\u2014'You who dwell in the gardens, the friends are listening; make me hear your voice'\u2014can therefore be understood in two ways: either as inviting the devout lover to sing psalms or to pray; or as rousing the holy preacher to speak. To make this more persuasive, he recalls that friends are listening\u2014namely, the angels listening to one praying or singing psalms, and the faithful listening to one preaching. Here we first consider with what discipline of heart and body we ought to sing psalms or pray in the sight of the angels, lest, being sent away empty, they in turn send us away empty\u2014they who had come to carry away our vows and bring back gifts; nay, lest those who had come as friends depart as enemies. Indeed, the Bridegroom himself, who stands at the door and knocks, if no devotion worthy of him answers from within and opens to him, will withdraw complaining, and say: 'I attended and listened; no one speaks what is good. There is none who does penance for his sin, saying, What have I done? They have all turned aside to their own course, like a horse rushing with impetuosity into battle' (Jerem. VIII, 6). But if he is exhorting the preacher, he grants him confidence to speak on account of the benevolence and attentiveness of his hearers\u2014namely, that the unbelieving do not speak against him or quarrel; that rivals do not disparage or mock; that the lukewarm do not doze or yawn: but that attentive friends listen, whose love and merit can obtain for the one preaching the gospel both the word and the spirit. And rightly do friends listen. For it is, as it were, proper to a friend that the devout one should hear the voice of the Bridegroom, just as John says: 'He that stands is the friend of the bridegroom'\u2014not wandering in mind, nor laid low in sleep\u2014'he hears him, and rejoices with joy because of the bridegroom's voice' (Joan. III, 29), which he recognizes even in his servants. Let us, then, likewise prove ourselves to be friends, so that whether through the mouth of one speaking, or reading, or singing, the voice of the Bridegroom sounds forth, we may so stand ready to listen that it gives to our hearing joy and gladness; and that we may not only receive the word with joy, but also bring forth fruit in patience.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The prayer asking God to make his speech pleasing comes from Psalm 103:34 in Vulgate numbering (104:34 in modern numbering); the edition prints Psal. CIII, 31."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "On the Words of the Song of Songs", "topics": ["Song of Songs", "Scripture", "Christ the gardener", "psalmody", "preaching", "Paul the Apostle"], "url": "sermons/guerric-canticle.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:ecce-advenit", "calendar_label": "Epiphany", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-epiphany-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In epiphania Domini, sermo I, De donis Domino offerendis, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 46\u201350.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Epiphany", "Epiphany of the Lord", "Psalm 28:1\u20132 (source numbering)", "Proverbs 30:15", "Acts 3:6", "Proverbs 8:20\u201321, 18", "1 Corinthians 1:4\u20135, 7 (printed wording; see note)", "Apocalypse 3:17", "Ecclesiasticus 8:6 (printed locator)", "Matthew 13:44", "Jeremiah 41:8", "Job 10:2", "Song of Songs 1:12; 3:6; 4:6, 11 (source numbering)", "Matthew 27:48 (printed locator; see note)", "1 Timothy 4:5 (printed locator; see note)", "Proverbs 25:20; 21:26, 25 (printed locators)", "Isaiah 61:3, 10", "Psalm 29:12\u201313 (source numbering)", "2 Corinthians 6:12\u201313", "Job 37:17", "Lamentations 1:13", "Philippians 3:13, 12", "James 1:5"], "occasion": "In epiphania Domini", "occasion_detail": "Epiphany of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Epiphany", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric's first Epiphany sermon asks what gifts Christ's poor can offer the newborn King. Seven numbered sections interpret myrrh as penitential sorrow and toil, incense as devotion, and gold as wisdom. The address to brothers whose clothing scarcely suffices in winter places the argument in its monastic setting. This reading follows the 1879 Patrologia Latina edition; the approximate 1138\u20131157 composition range is distinct from that printing date. Editorial notes explain retained quotation variants and anomalous Scripture locators. The source explicitly names Epiphany."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN EPIPHANIA DOMINI."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE EPIPHANY OF THE LORD", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De donis Domino offerendis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On the gifts to be offered to the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Afferte Domino, filii Dei, afferte (Psal. xxviii, 1). Satius est ei afferre, quam sanguisugae, id est concupiscentiae, cujus duae filiae, voluptas et vanitas, non dant requiem die ac nocte, dicentes: Affer, affer (Prov. xxx, 15). Quid igitur afferemus Domino? Afferte Domino gloriam auri, et honorem thuris: afferte Domino gloriam nomini ejus, myrrham (Psal. xxviii, 1, 2) sepulturae ejus. Sed dicit mihi discipulus Christi, filius Petri: Argentum et aurum non est mihi (Act. iii, 6). Nec sacculi peregrinarum mercium myrrhae et thuris. Ergone vacuus apparebis in conspectu Domini, et cunabula novi Regis nullis honorabis xeniis? O dives paupertas, o locuples nuditas, si tamen Christiana et voluntaria! Quibus enim divitiis non abundas, non solum auri, sed etiam auri primi, auri igniti; non solum myrrhae et thuris, sed etiam universi pulveris pigmentarii? Imo qui sunt alii qui possint abundare divitiis hujusmodi, nisi pauperes Christi? In viis, inquit, justitiae ambulo, ut ditem diligentes me, et thesauros eorum repleam. Nec deest ei unde id possit: Mecum sunt, inquit, divitiae et gloria, opes superbae, et justitia (Prov. viii, 20, 21, 18). Bonae opes prorsus divitiae salutis, in quibus ita superbitur, ut justitia non deseratur. Fratres, superbia ista est gloria exsultantium in Domino, et insultantium mundo, quod nihil habeat tam pretiosum, ut paupertati valeat comparari sanctorum. O qui pusillo animo estis, cur non superbitis mecum in opibus istis? Superbiam istam non damnat, sed remunerat Magister humilitatis, si videlicet ita magnanimus sis in contemptu mundi, ut omnem gloriam ejus quasi de sublimi despicias, et nihili pendas prae amore et gloria tuae nuditatis. Omnino dives es, si in paupertate gloriaris, et de te quoque gratias aget congratulatio Apostoli: Gratias, inquit, ago Deo meo, quod in omnibus divites facti estis in illo, in omni verbo et sanctitate, ut nihil vobis desit in ulla gloria (I Cor. i, 4, 5, 7). Longe aliter illum alloquitur Dominus, qui sibi applaudebat de affluentia mundi hujus: Tu, inquit, dicis Quia dives sum, et nullius egeo; et tu miser es, et miserabilis, et pauper et caecus, et nudus (Apoc. iii, 17)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Bring to the Lord, children of God, bring (Psalm xxviii, 1). It is better to bring to him than to the leech, that is, concupiscence, whose two daughters, pleasure and vanity, give no rest day or night, saying: Bring, bring (Proverbs xxx, 15). What, then, shall we bring to the Lord? Bring to the Lord the glory of gold and the honour of incense; bring to the Lord the glory due to his name, myrrh (Psalm xxviii, 1, 2) for his burial. But a disciple of Christ, a son of Peter, says to me: Silver and gold I have none (Acts iii, 6). Nor have I bags of foreign wares, of myrrh and incense. Will you then appear empty-handed before the Lord, and honour the new King's cradle with no gifts? O rich poverty, O wealthy nakedness, provided that it is Christian and voluntary! For what riches do you not abound in: not only gold, but the finest gold, gold tried by fire; not only myrrh and incense, but every powder of the perfumer? Indeed, who else can abound in such riches except Christ's poor? I walk, he says, in the ways of justice, to enrich those who love me and fill their treasuries. Nor does he lack the means: With me, he says, are riches and glory, magnificent wealth and justice (Proverbs viii, 20, 21, 18). The riches of salvation are truly good wealth, in which one takes pride without abandoning justice. Brothers, this pride is the glory of those who exult in the Lord and mock the world because it has nothing so precious that it can compare with the poverty of the saints. O faint-hearted ones, why do you not take pride with me in these riches? The Master of humility does not condemn this pride but rewards it, provided that you are so great-hearted in despising the world that you look down, as from on high, upon all its glory and count it as nothing beside the love and glory of your nakedness. You are altogether rich if you glory in poverty, and the Apostle's rejoicing will give thanks for you too: I thank my God, he says, that in all things you have been made rich in him, in every word and in holiness, so that you lack nothing in any glory (1 Corinthians i, 4, 5, 7). Very differently does the Lord address the man who congratulated himself upon this world's abundance: You say, I am rich and need nothing; and you are wretched and miserable and poor and blind and naked (Apocalypse iii, 17).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sane inter divitias hujusmodi, pro quibus Paulus congratulabatur discipulis, non dubitaverim aurum, thus, et myrrham posse reperiri, quae digne et grate Christo nato queant offerri. At ego, inquies, non sum mihi conscius tale quid accepisse, nec aliquid tam pretiosum, ut est aurum, thus et myrrha, penes me posse inveniri. Ego enim, videns paupertatem meam, vix mendicabo stipem quotidianam, pauperem exigens vitam. Putas, inquit, te nihil tale accepisse? vide magis ne acceptam a Patre substantiam dissipaveris vivendo luxuriose. Sed omitto istud; non enim improperandum est, ut Sapiens ait, homini avertenti se a peccato (Eccli. viii, 6). Vellem magis ut non pigeret te tentare et quaerere si forte vel minimum aliquid paternae substantiae resedisset apud te, unde initium recuperandi posses capere. Vellem foderes intra te; solent namque pretiosi latere thesauri in abditis terrae. In agro absconditus erat thesaurus ille qui desiderio sui compulit hominem omnia sua vendere (Matth. xiii, 44). In agro se dicebant thesauros absconditos habere decem viri Israelitae, et per hoc gladium evaserunt homicidae (Jerem. xli, 8). O quanti thesauri bonorum operum, quantae divitiae piorum fructuum absconditi latent in agro humani corporis, et quanto plures in abdito cordis, si modo sit qui exerceat et fodiat! Neque illud Platonicum dico, quod anima ante corpus artes didicerit, quae oblivione et mole corporis obrutae, disciplina et industria refodiendae sint: sed quod ratio hominis et ingenium, juvante gratia, omnium sit virtutum seminarium. Si ergo ad cor redeas, et corpus exerceas, non diffidas te inventurum thesauros desiderabiles; et si non auri statim in initio, aut thuris, certe vel myrrhae non inutilis. Inutilem aut vilem tu ne dixeris, quam Christus acceptat in munere, qua sui corporis sepultura non solum praesignari, cum ei offerretur, sed etiam consummari voluit, cum ex ea perungeretur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Surely among those riches for which Paul congratulated his disciples, I would not doubt that gold, incense and myrrh can be found which may worthily and acceptably be offered to the newborn Christ. But I, you will say, am not aware of having received anything of the kind, nor can anything as precious as gold, incense and myrrh be found in my possession. Seeing my poverty, I can scarcely beg my daily pittance as I lead a poor life. Do you think, he says, that you have received nothing of the kind? Consider rather whether you have squandered the substance received from your Father by living in luxury. But I leave that aside; for, as the Wise Man says, one must not reproach a man who turns away from sin (Ecclesiasticus viii, 6). I would rather that you should not be reluctant to try and search whether perhaps some smallest part of your Father's substance remains with you, from which you might begin to recover. I would have you dig within yourself; for precious treasures often lie hidden in the secret places of the earth. Hidden in a field was that treasure which, through the desire for it, compelled a man to sell all he had (Matthew xiii, 44). Ten men of Israel said that they had treasures hidden in a field, and by this escaped the murderer's sword (Jeremiah xli, 8). O what treasures of good works, what riches of devout fruits lie concealed in the field of the human body, and how many more in the hidden heart, if only someone will cultivate and dig! I am not speaking of that Platonic notion that the soul learned the arts before entering the body, and that, buried by forgetfulness and the body's weight, they must be dug out again by instruction and effort; rather, that human reason and natural ability, with grace assisting, are a seedbed of all the virtues. If, then, you return to your heart and exercise your body, do not doubt that you will find desirable treasures; if not gold or incense immediately at the beginning, then certainly at least useful myrrh. Do not call useless or worthless what Christ accepts as a gift, by which he willed his body's burial not only to be foreshadowed when it was offered to him, but also to be accomplished when he was anointed with it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Si ergo manifestius vis audire, myrrha in corde tuo dolor est, myrrha in corpore tuo labor est, si tamen iste vel ille poenitentialis est. Myrrham siquidem utrumque esse non solum probat etymologia nominis, aut qualitas saporis, sed etiam medicinalis operatio virtutis. Myrrha namque amarissimum gustum sui etiam nomine prodit, effectu autem, praeter alias sui utilitates, corruptioni resistit. Et quid gustu amarius, quid effectu salubrius, quam dolor quo peccator ad poenitentiam contristatur? Recogitat annos suos in amaritudine animae suae, dicens Deo, Noli me condemnare (Job. x, 2); sed omnis haec amaritudo nihil aliud quam myrrha est, vindicans a corruptione tam luxuriarum, in quibus computruit, quam vermium immortalium, quos meruit. Quid vero labor corporis? Non tam myrrha est, quam fasciculus myrrhae, si credere volumus his qui de saeculo nuper venere; quibus regula jejuniorum et vigiliarum, quotidianum opus manuum, asperitas vestium, et omnia pene amara, quia insolita, velut in unum colligata fasciculum, imposita sunt ad ferendum. Et quidem amarissime portarent hunc myrrhae fasciculum, nisi quia, etsi nondum gaudent communicare passionibus Dilecti, ut dicant, Fasciculus myrrhae dilectus meus mihi (Cantic. i, 12), dulce tamen nimium habent amaritudinem cordis amaritudine mitigare exteriori, tanquam choleram stomachi potu absinthii. Sicut enim stomachus, immoderato usu dulcium corruptus, potione purgatur amara, sic eorum qui suaviter vixerunt in amaricata conscientia, nunquam melius quam contrario, id est, vita et consuetudine curantur austera. Maxime autem, si saepius detur eis bibere vinum myrrhatum Dominicae passionis, id est potentur vino compunctionis, quod quanto amarius est ex recordatione peccati, tanto salubrius est peccatori. Ideo Jesus noluit bibere (Matth. xxvii, 48), quia peccatum non fecerat, quod ei crucifixor imputabat, qui de amaritudine vitis alienae ei propinabat qui de genimine vitis verae, vinum novum cum amicis in regno Patris habebat bibere."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. If, then, you wish to hear it more plainly, myrrh in your heart is sorrow; myrrh in your body is toil, provided that the one or the other is penitential. Both are myrrh, as is shown not only by the word's derivation or the nature of its taste, but also by the medicinal working of its power. For myrrh reveals its intensely bitter taste even by its name, while in its effects, besides its other uses, it resists corruption. And what is more bitter to taste, what more wholesome in its effect, than the sorrow by which a sinner is grieved into repentance? He recalls his years in the bitterness of his soul, saying to God: Do not condemn me (Job x, 2). Yet all this bitterness is nothing other than myrrh, rescuing him from the corruption both of the lusts in which he rotted and of the undying worms he has deserved. What, then, of bodily toil? It is not so much myrrh as a bundle of myrrh, if we believe those who have recently come from the world. The rule of fasts and vigils, daily manual work, rough clothing, and almost everything bitter because unfamiliar have been laid upon them to bear, as though bound into one bundle. They would indeed carry this bundle of myrrh with great bitterness, except that, even if they do not yet rejoice to share the sufferings of the Beloved and say, My beloved is a bundle of myrrh to me (Song of Songs i, 12), they nevertheless find it exceedingly sweet to soothe the bitterness of the heart with outward bitterness, like the stomach's bile with a drink of wormwood. For just as a stomach spoiled by excessive consumption of sweets is purged by a bitter draught, so those who have lived pleasantly are never better cured in their embittered conscience than by the opposite, that is, an austere life and habit. Especially is this so if they are often given the myrrhed wine of the Lord's Passion to drink, that is, the wine of compunction: the more bitter it is from remembering sin, the more wholesome it is for the sinner. For this reason Jesus would not drink (Matthew xxvii, 48), because he had committed no sin, which his crucifier imputed to him by offering him the bitterness of a foreign vine, whereas he was to drink new wine from the fruit of the true vine with his friends in his Father's kingdom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Caeterum quanquam perfectis, qualis Timotheus erat, exercitatio corporalis ad modicum utilis sit comparatione pietatis (I Tim. iv, 5); quantum tamen rudibus et imperfectis, quales nos sumus, utilis sit, vos ipsi, fratres, vobis testes estis; quomodo scilicet redimit de corruptione vitam nostram amaritudo parcimoniae et laboris. Ipsi etenim scitis quomodo vermescerent corda, quomodo vermescerent corpora, si non quotidie de manibus laborantium distillaret myrrha. An non vermis luxuria? Nescio si alter nocentior. Ingreditur blandiendo, mordet ridendo, transforat delectando, perimit consensu voluntario. An non vermis acedia et tristitia? Sicut tinea, inquit, vestimento, et vermis ligno, ita tristitia viri nocet cordi (Prov. xxv, 20). Quid omnia desideria mala? an non vermes et ipsa? At in desideriis est omnis otiosus, et desideria occidunt pigrum (Id. xxi, 26, 25). Beatus igitur cui ad mortificandos vermes hujuscemodi, manus propriae distillant myrrham per universum sui, jamque praevenit ungere corpus suum in sepulturam. Sed suum dicam, an Jesu? Imo et Jesu. Corpus enim illius, membrum est Jesu. Quisquis ille est qui sic unctus est, non est quod timeat vermem qui non moritur; quia jam mortuus est ei vermis unde illi moritur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Yet although for the perfect, such as Timothy was, bodily exercise is useful for little in comparison with godliness (1 Timothy iv, 5), you yourselves, brothers, can testify how useful it is for beginners and the imperfect, such as we are: how the bitterness of frugality and toil redeems our life from corruption. For you yourselves know how hearts would breed worms, how bodies would breed worms, if myrrh did not drip daily from the hands of those who labour. Is lust not a worm? I do not know whether another is more harmful. It enters by flattering, bites with a laugh, pierces by giving pleasure, and kills through voluntary consent. Are sloth and sadness not a worm? As the moth, he says, harms clothing, and the worm wood, so a man's sadness harms his heart (Proverbs xxv, 20). What of all evil desires? Are they not worms too? But every idle man is among desires, and desires kill the sluggard (ibid. xxi, 26, 25). Blessed, then, is the man whose own hands drip myrrh over his whole being to mortify worms of this kind, and who thus anoints his body beforehand for burial. But should I say his body, or Jesus'? Indeed, Jesus' too. For his body is a member of Jesus. Whoever is thus anointed has no reason to fear the worm that does not die, because for him the worm through which he dies is already dead.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Myrrham itaque, et myrrham electam atque probatissimam Domino cum regibus rex et ipse obtulisti, si interim, cum non sufficit manus tua ad offerendum aurum sapientiae aut thus devotionis, saltem cor contritum corpusque afflictum cum amaritudine poenitentiae Domino sanctificasti. Existimo enim, sine praejudicio tamen melioris intellectus, myrrham esse primam oblationem incipientium, deinde thus proficientium, demumque aurum perfectorum; atque idcirco cum simul ab Evangelista nominata sunt, pretiosiora quaeque et digniora pro merito sui praelata esse in ordine nominum. Alias namque secundum ordinem profectuum myrrham thuri praepositam invenis, ut est illud, Sicut virgula fumi ex aromatibus myrrhae et thuris (Cantic. iii, 6). Et, Vadam ad montem myrrhae, et ad collem thuris (Id. iv, 6). Non parvus siquidem profectus est, cum de myrrha ad thus proficitur, cum de ista, quae ad usus est infirmitatis humanae, ad illud quod divinis et festivis deputatum est sacrificiis, pervenitur; ut qui sacrificium spiritus contribulati, corporisque humiliati offerebat non sine myrrha amaritudinis, jam sacrificium laudis offerat cum thure devotionis, et, juxta promissionem Domini, tanta consolatio immittatur lugentibus Sion, ut det eis coronam pro cinere, oleum gaudii pro luctu, pallium laudis pro spiritu moeroris (Isa. lxi, 3). Tunc cantat qui lugebat: Convertisti planctum meum in gaudium mihi, conscidisti saccum meum, spiritum moeroris; et circumdedisti me laetitia, pallio scilicet laudis, ut cantet tibi gloria mea, et non compungar (Psal. xxix, 12, 13). Gaudens enim gaudebo in Domino, et exsultabit anima mea in Deo meo, quia induit me vestimento salutis, et indumento laetitiae circumdedit me (Isa. lxi, 10)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. You too, then, have offered myrrh to the Lord with the kings, yourself a king, and myrrh choice and most excellent, if meanwhile, when your hand cannot supply the gold of wisdom or the incense of devotion, you have at least consecrated to the Lord a contrite heart and an afflicted body with the bitterness of penance. For I think, without prejudice to a better understanding, that myrrh is the first offering of beginners, then incense that of those making progress, and finally gold that of the perfect; and that, when the Evangelist named them together, each more precious and worthy gift was therefore placed earlier in the order of names according to its worth. Elsewhere you find myrrh placed before incense according to the order of progress, as in the saying, Like a slender column of smoke from the spices of myrrh and incense (Song of Songs iii, 6); and, I will go to the mountain of myrrh and to the hill of incense (ibid. iv, 6). Indeed, it is no small progress to advance from myrrh to incense, from what serves human infirmity to what is assigned to divine and festal sacrifices; so that he who offered the sacrifice of a troubled spirit and a humbled body, not without the myrrh of bitterness, may now offer a sacrifice of praise with the incense of devotion. And, according to the Lord's promise, such consolation is sent to those mourning in Sion that he gives them a crown for ashes, the oil of joy for mourning, a mantle of praise for the spirit of sorrow (Isaiah lxi, 3). Then he who mourned sings: You have turned my lament into joy for me; you have torn my sackcloth, the spirit of sorrow; and surrounded me with gladness, that is, the mantle of praise, so that my glory may sing to you and I may not be pierced with sorrow (Psalm xxix, 12, 13). For rejoicing I will rejoice in the Lord, and my soul will exult in my God, because he has clothed me in the garment of salvation and surrounded me with the robe of gladness (Isaiah lxi, 10).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. O fratres, quibus minus sufficiunt vestimenta vestrae paupertatis in hac asperitate hiemis, qui dicitis, Ante faciem frigoris hujus quis sustinebit? cur non induimini, quaeso, hoc vestimento laetitiae et salutis, hoc pallio laudis? Si Dominum laudatis, in Domino laudabitur anima vestra, et pallio laudis quo opertus est laudabilis nimis, vos quoque operiet et confovebit, quemadmodum gallina pullos suos sub alis, si modo velitis. Ah quoties voluit, et noluistis! Nunquid enim hoc pallium breve est, ut utrumque operire non possit? Non angustiamini, inquit, in nobis, angustiamini autem in visceribus vestris. Dilatamini et vos (II Cor. vi, 12, 13), et pallium dilatabit se usque ad vos. Non est abbreviata manus ejus, ut salvare nequeat: nec grande est ei ut consoletur nos, nisi pravitas nostra prohibeat. O qui algetis, pallium hoc calidum est; quia calor est, qui non modo interius, sed exterius calefacere potest. Nonne vestimenta tua calida sunt, cum terra tua perflata fuerit austro (Job xxxvii, 17); imo ignis missus in ossibus, ac vehementer accensus (Thren. i, 13)? Hoc igne thus illud adole, quod ubi cum regibus obtuleris, dirigatur oratio tua sicut incensum in conspectu Domini: et cum de sacrificio tuo odoratus fuerit odorem suavitatis, dicat tibi, Odor vestimentorum tuorum sicut odor thuris (Cantic. iv, 11)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. O brothers, whose poor clothing does not suffice in the harshness of this winter, who say, Who can endure before the face of this cold? Why, I ask, do you not put on this garment of gladness and salvation, this mantle of praise? If you praise the Lord, your soul will be praised in the Lord, and with the mantle of praise in which he who is exceedingly praiseworthy is clothed, he will cover and warm you too, as a hen her chicks beneath her wings, if only you will. Ah, how often he has willed it, and you have not! Is this mantle so short that it cannot cover both? You are not straitened in us, he says, but in your own affections. Be enlarged yourselves too (2 Corinthians vi, 12, 13), and the mantle will widen to reach you. His hand is not shortened so that he cannot save; nor is it difficult for him to console us, unless our wickedness prevents it. O you who are cold, this mantle is warm; for it is a warmth that can heat not only within but without. Are not your garments warm when the south wind has blown over your land (Job xxxvii, 17); indeed, is it not fire sent into the bones and fiercely kindled (Lamentations i, 13)? With this fire burn that incense; when you have offered it with the kings, let your prayer be directed like incense before the Lord. And when he has smelled a sweet fragrance from your sacrifice, may he say to you: The fragrance of your garments is like the fragrance of incense (Song of Songs iv, 11).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Jam vero de auro sapientiae (quae oblatio, ut diximus, perfectorum est) loquatur vobis qui illud assecutus est, quia nec novit illud nisi qui accipit. Qui ergo sapientiam didicit, sapientiam loquatur inter perfectos; et de auro quod possidet, murenulas aureas auribus vestris fabricet. Ego enim non arbitror comprehendisse me; sequor autem si quo modo comprehendam (Philipp. iii, 13, 12). Magnus prorsus ac beatus qui invenit sapientiam, et affluit prudentia: sapientiam, in contemplatione aeternorum; prudentiam, in administratione temporalium: vel, ut moderatius sapientem definiam, qui novit se ipsum regere et alium. Pretiosus est hoc aurum cunctis opibus, etiam thure et myrrha; et omnia quae desiderantur in virtutibus vel gratiis, huic non valent comparari. Denique sapiens, tanquam vir virtutum, dives in omnibus, et si praevalet aurum offerre in sublimi contemplatione vel prudenti administratione, non tamen negligit offere thus in devotione divini operis, myrrham in mortificatione sui. Nos igitur, fratres, quod habemus offeramus in gloriam novi Regis: quod minus habemus, ab ipso petamus cui offerre cupimus. Si quis indiget sapientia, postulet a Deo, qui dat omnibus affluenter (Jac. i, 5). Munera mea, data mea, ait Pater luminum, a quo omne donum perfectum, et omne datum optimum; ipsi gloria in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Now let one who has attained it speak to you of the gold of wisdom, which, as we have said, is the offering of the perfect; for no one knows it except the one who receives it. Let him, then, who has learned wisdom speak wisdom among the perfect, and from the gold he possesses fashion little golden ornaments for your ears. For I do not consider that I have attained; but I press on, if somehow I may attain (Philippians iii, 13, 12). Truly great and blessed is the man who finds wisdom and abounds in prudence: wisdom in contemplating eternal things, prudence in administering temporal things; or, to define the wise man more modestly, one who knows how to govern himself and another. This gold is more precious than all wealth, even incense and myrrh; and all that is desired in virtues or graces cannot compare with it. Finally, the wise man, as a man of virtues, rich in all things, even when able to offer gold in lofty contemplation or prudent administration, nevertheless does not neglect to offer incense in devotion to the divine office, and myrrh in the mortification of himself. Let us, then, brothers, offer what we have to the glory of the new King; what we lack, let us ask from him to whom we desire to offer it. If anyone lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives abundantly to all (James i, 5). My gifts, my offerings, says the Father of lights, from whom comes every perfect gift and every bestowal that is best; to him be glory for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition\u2019s quotation of 1 Corinthians 1:5, 7 reads \u201choliness\u201d and \u201cglory,\u201d where the usual Vulgate reads \u201cknowledge\u201d and \u201cgrace.\u201d The translation follows the sermon\u2019s wording."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Latin reads Pretiosus est hoc aurum, an irregular adjective agreement meaning \u201cthis gold is precious.\u201d It also spells offerre as offere. Both forms are retained. The final phrase \u201cMy gifts, my offerings\u201d has no printed Bible reference."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites Matthew 27:48 for the refusal of the drink, usually located at Matthew 27:34 (compare Mark 15:23). It cites 1 Timothy 4:5 for the bodily-exercise saying, usually located at 1 Timothy 4:8. The printed references are retained."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Epiphany Sermon I: Gifts to Offer the Lord", "topics": ["Epiphany", "poverty", "penance", "prayer", "wisdom", "monastic life"], "url": "sermons/guerric-epiphany-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:ecce-advenit", "calendar_label": "Epiphany", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-epiphany-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In Epiphania Domini, sermo II, De variis modis quibus nos Deus illuminat, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 51-54.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Epiphany", "Epiphany of the Lord", "Isaiah 60:1", "Psalm 47:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 33:6 (historical numbering)", "Ephesians 5:8", "John 10:25", "Luke 3:16, 21\u201322", "Zechariah 6:12", "John 3:19", "1 Peter 2:9", "2 Corinthians 4:6", "John 17:11, 23", "Proverbs 4:18", "Daniel 12:3", "Philippians 2:15", "Matthew 5:14, 16", "Sirach 1:33", "Sirach 3:22", "Psalm 18:9 (historical numbering)", "Ecclesiastes 11:7", "Isaiah 26:7", "1 Timothy 4:7\u20139", "Job 36:32\u201333"], "occasion": "In epiphania Domini", "occasion_detail": "Epiphany of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Epiphany", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This second Epiphany sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. From Isaiah's \"Arise, be enlightened, Jerusalem\", Guerric gathers the day's three manifestations: the star to the Magi, the Jordan baptism, and the water made wine. The Magi's faith, which saw only weakness and still adored, shows how faith begins and grows. He bids his hearers look up to the lights of heaven, the saints, before they can bear the Sun itself. An editorial note records a printer's error. The source explicitly names Epiphany."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De variis modis quibus nos Deus illuminat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the Various Ways in Which God Enlightens Us", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Surge, illuminare, Jerusalem, quia venit lumen tuum (Isa. LX, 1). Hodiernum diem luminum illuminavit nobis et consecravit lumen de lumine, quod hodie cum lateret incognitum, ad illuminationem omnium gentium sese dignatum est mundo revelare. Hodie namque se revelavit Chaldaeis novi sideris indicio, cum eorum primitiis omnium fidem gentium dedicavit. Hodie se revelavit Judaeis, non solum Joannis, sed et Patris et Spiritus sancti testimonio, ubi in Jordane baptizatus, baptisma omnium consecravit. Hodie manifestavit gloriam suam coram discipulis suis, quando mutatatione aquae in vinum, illud ineffabile praeostendit mysterium, in quo, verbo ipsius, substantiae mutantur rerum. His omnibus Dei apparitionibus praevidens Spiritus illuminandam Ecclesiae fidem, alloquitur eam sub typo et nomine Jerusalem, dicens: Surge, illuminare, Jerusalem, quia venit lumen tuum. Venerat quidem lumen; in mundo erat, et mundus per ipsum factus erat; sed mundus ipsum non cognoscebat. Erat natus, sed non erat notus, donec declarare eum coepit luminosa dies ista. Ait ergo propheta: O Jerusalem nova, civitas magna novi regis, mons Sion, latera aquilonis (Psal. XLVII, 3), id est, de utroque aedificanda pariete circumcisionis et praeputii; Surge, illuminare, quia venit lumen tuum. Surgite, qui sedetis in tenebris; intendite lumini, quod exortum est in tenebris, sed non comprehenditur a tenebris. Accedite ad eum, et illuminamini (Psal. XXXIII, 6), et in lumine ejus videbitis lumen, diceturque vobis, Fuistis aliquando tenebrae, nunc autem lux in Domino (Ephes. V, 8). Intendite lumini aeterno quod se vestris aspectibus temperavit, ut, qui lucem habitat inaccessibilem, infirmis quoque et lippientibus oculis posset accedi. Advertite lumen in lucerna testae, solem in nube, Deum in homine, in luteo vasculo carnis nostrae splendorem gloriae, candoremque lucis aeternae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Arise, be enlightened, Jerusalem, for your light has come\" (Isa. LX, 1). The Light from Light has illumined and consecrated this day of lights for us: the Light which, though today it lay hidden and unknown, deigned to reveal itself to the world for the enlightenment of all nations. For today he revealed himself to the Chaldeans by the sign of a new star, when in their firstfruits he dedicated the faith of all the nations. Today he revealed himself to the Jews, not only by John's testimony, but by the testimony of the Father and the Holy Spirit, when, baptized in the Jordan, he consecrated the baptism of all. Today he manifested his glory before his disciples, when, by the changing of water into wine, he showed forth beforehand that ineffable mystery in which, by his own word, the substances of things are changed. Foreseeing that the faith of the Church would be enlightened by all these appearings of God, the Spirit addresses her under the type and name of Jerusalem, saying: Arise, be enlightened, Jerusalem, for your light has come. Indeed the light had come; it was in the world, and the world was made through it; but the world did not know it. He had been born, but was not yet known, until this day of light began to declare him. The prophet therefore says: O new Jerusalem, great city of the new King, Mount Sion, on the sides of the north (Psal. XLVII, 3), that is, to be built up from both walls, of circumcision and of the uncircumcision; Arise, be enlightened, for your light has come. Arise, you who sit in darkness; attend to the light which has risen in the darkness, but is not comprehended by the darkness. Come to him, and be enlightened (Psal. XXXIII, 6), and in his light you will see light, and it will be said to you: You were once darkness, but now you are light in the Lord (Ephes. V, 8). Attend to the eternal light which has tempered itself to your sight, so that he who dwells in unapproachable light might also be approached even by weak and bleary eyes. Observe the light in the lamp of clay, the sun in the cloud, God in man, in the little earthen vessel of our flesh the splendor of glory and the brightness of eternal light.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Latet quidem majestas in humanitate, virtus in humilitate; sed signa virtutum erumpentia, unde haec prodeant, non sinunt ambigere. Opera, inquit, quae ego facio, testimonium perhibent de me (Joan. X, 25). Magnum quidem testimonium Joannis, qui venit ut testimonium perhiberet, lucerna de lumine; at enim multo majus illud testimonium coeleste, quod Pater et Spiritus sanctus Filio perhibuere (Luc. III, 16, 21, 22), Pater, in voce, Spiritus, in specie, cum in ore duorum testium stet omne verbum. Sed et hoc ipsum si minus accipitur: Opera, inquit, innumerabilia et irrefragabilia, quae ego facio, testimonium perhibent de me (Joan. X, 25). Plane incorruptus et irrevincibilis testis, cum creatura insensibilis sensum quemdam accipit in confessionem Creatoris, voluntatem ejus exaudiens, ac nutui incunctanter obediens. Quid enim divinius poterat exhiberi sensibus mortalium, quam hodiernae novitatis signum, quod etiam ante signorum suorum praemisit Jesus initium? Vagit puer novus in terris, et sidus novum creat in supernis, ut lumen testetur de lumine, stella de sole, et reges in splendore ortus sui, ad exortum quoque aeternum splendorem, ab oriente ad verum Orientem, id est, virum cui nomen Oriens (Zach. VI, 12), stella perducat; non quasi stella, sed quasi rationale animal in via praecedens, in fine viae consistens, ac velut digito ipsum qui quaerebatur ostendens. Sed esto, calumnietur infidelis hanc stellam non esse opus Christi, dummodo confiteatur opus esse Patris in testimonium Filii. Attamen obruet eum multitudo infinita ac magnitudo mirabilium plane divinorum, quae absque contradictione Jesus noster operatus est in carne, sed non ex carne."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Majesty indeed lies hidden in humanity, power in humility; but the signs of power breaking forth do not allow us to doubt whence they proceed. \"The works,\" he says, \"that I do bear witness of me\" (Joan. X, 25). Great indeed is the testimony of John, who came to bear witness, a lamp testifying of the Light; but far greater is that heavenly testimony which the Father and the Holy Spirit bore to the Son (Luc. III, 16, 21, 22), the Father in the voice, the Spirit in the visible form, since every word stands firm in the mouth of two witnesses. But if even this be deemed insufficient: \"the works,\" he says, \"innumerable and irrefutable, which I do, bear witness of me\" (Joan. X, 25). Clearly an incorruptible and unconquerable witness, when insensible creation receives, as it were, a certain sense in confessing the Creator, hearkening to his will and obeying his command without delay. For what more divine sign could be shown to mortal senses than the sign of today's newness, which Jesus sent forth even before the beginning of his own [other] signs? A new child cries on earth, and creates a new star in the heavens above, so that light might testify of Light, the star of the Sun, and lead kings, by the splendor of its rising, to the eternal Splendor, who likewise has arisen\u2014from the east to the true East, that is, to the man whose name is the Orient (Zach. VI, 12)\u2014the star leading them not as a mere star, but as a rational creature going before on the way, standing still at the end of the way, and pointing out, as it were with a finger, the very one who was being sought. But suppose the unbeliever should slander this, saying that the star is not the work of Christ\u2014so long as he confesses it to be the work of the Father in testimony of the Son. Yet even so he will be overwhelmed by the infinite multitude and the magnitude of the plainly divine wonders which our Jesus worked in the flesh, but not from the flesh, without any possibility of contradiction.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Sed operiant tenebrae terram, et caligo populos, qui, cum lux venit in mundum, dilexerunt magis tenebras quam lucem (Joan. III, 19); dummodo tu illumineris, Jerusalem, civitas coelestis, quae in omni terra et populo, Deo paritura es filios lucis, quos de potestate tenebrarum transferet in regnum claritatis suae lumen verum. Gratias tibi, Pater luminum, qui de tenebris nos vocasti in admirabile lumen tuum (I Petr. II, 9). Gratias tibi, qui dixisti de tenebris lumen splendescere, et illuxisti in cordibus nostris ad illuminationem scientiae in facie Christi Jesu (II Cor. IV, 6). Haec siquidem est lux vera, imo vita aeterna, ut cognoscamus te unum Deum, et quem misisti Jesum Christum. Cognoscimus te, quia cognoscimus Jesum, quoniam Pater et Filius unum (Joan. XVII, 23, 11). Cognoscimus quidem per fidem, ipsam tenentes arrham fidelem, quia cognoscemus te per speciem. Interim tamen auge nobis fidem, deducens nos ex fide in fidem, a claritate in claritatem, tanquam a tuo Spiritu, ut de die in diem thesauros luminis profundius ingrediamur, quo fides amplior, scientia plenior, charitas ferventior fiat et diffusior, donec per fidem perducamur ad faciem, tanquam per stellam praeducem ad nostrum Bethlehemiticum ducem, qui egressus de Bethlehem regit Israelem, et regnat in Jerusalem; non ista quae occidit prophetas, ipsumque Dominum prophetarum, sed illa in qua coronat occisos idem Dominus, causa, virtus, et gloria martyrum"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But let darkness cover the earth, and gloom the peoples, who, when the light came into the world, loved darkness rather than the light (Joan. III, 19); provided only that you be enlightened, O Jerusalem, heavenly city, who in every land and people are to bear sons of light for God\u2014sons whom the true light will transfer from the power of darkness into the kingdom of its own brightness. Thanks be to you, Father of lights, who have called us out of darkness into your wonderful light (I Petr. II, 9). Thanks be to you, who said that light should shine out of darkness, and who have shone in our hearts for the illumination of the knowledge in the face of Christ Jesus (II Cor. IV, 6). For this indeed is the true light, nay rather eternal life, that we should know you the one God, and him whom you sent, Jesus Christ. We know you because we know Jesus, since the Father and the Son are one (Joan. XVII, 23, 11). We know you indeed through faith, holding fast that same faithful pledge, because we shall know you through vision. Meanwhile, however, increase our faith, leading us from faith to faith, from brightness to brightness, as it were by your own Spirit, so that from day to day we may enter more deeply into the treasures of light, whereby faith may become ampler, knowledge fuller, charity more ardent and more widely diffused, until through faith we be brought to the vision of your face, as it were through a guiding star to our Bethlehem-born Leader, who, having gone forth from Bethlehem, rules Israel, and reigns in Jerusalem\u2014not that Jerusalem which killed the prophets, and the very Lord of the prophets, but that one in which the same Lord crowns those who were slain\u2014he who is the cause, the strength, and the glory of the martyrs.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. O quanto gaudio ibi tripudiat fides Magorum, cernentium in illa Jerusalem regnantem, quem in Bethlehem adoraverunt vagientem! Hic visus est in diversorio pauperum, ibi in palatio videtur Angelorum; hic in pannis parvulorum, ibi in splendoribus sanctorum; hic in gremio Matris, ibi in solio Patris. Plane digna beatorum fides Magorum, ut tam felici visione remuneretur; quae cum in eo nihil nisi infirmum et contemptibile videret, non tamen scandalizari potuit, quominus Deum in homine, et hominem in Deo veneraretur. Procul dubio stella ex Jacob illuxerat in cordibus eorum; stella, inquam, matutina, lucifer qui nescit occasum, qui etiam foris stellam, matutini ortus sui indicem, accenderat cenderat in cordibus eorum. De his omnino convenienter intelligi potest quod apud Salomonem scriptum est: Justorum semita, quasi lux splendescens procedit, et crescit usque ad perfectum diem (Prov. IV, 18). Primo namque semitam justitiae ingressi sunt ad lucem splendentis sideris, cujus ductu profecerunt ad videndum novum ortum matutinae, lucis sicque demum pervenerunt ad contemplandam faciem meridiani Solis, in die virtutis suae rutilantis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Oh, with what great joy does the faith of the Magi there leap for joy, beholding reigning in that Jerusalem the one whom they adored crying as an infant in Bethlehem! Here he was seen in the lodging-place of the poor, there he is seen in the palace of the Angels; here in the swaddling clothes of infants, there in the splendors of the saints; here in the lap of his Mother, there on the throne of the Father. Truly worthy was the faith of the blessed Magi, to be rewarded with so happy a vision\u2014they who, though they saw in him nothing but what was weak and contemptible, could not be scandalized so as to be kept from venerating God in man, and man in God. Without doubt the star out of Jacob had shone in their hearts; the star, I say, the morning star, the light-bearer who knows no setting, who had kindled also outwardly the star, the sign of his own morning rising, within their hearts. Of these it can altogether fittingly be understood what is written in Solomon: The path of the just, as a shining light, goes forward, and increases even to perfect day (Prov. IV, 18). For first they entered the path of righteousness by the light of the shining star, under whose guidance they advanced to the seeing of the new rising of the morning light, and so at last they arrived at the contemplation of the face of the noonday Sun, in the day of his glowing power.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Pulchre igitur signanterque in his Gentium primitiis, in his nascentis Ecclesiae primordiis, praeostensus est fidei profectus in singulis: unde scilicet incipiat, quomodo proficiat, et quo demum perveniat; ut facile ipsorum qui patres sunt, in filiis agnoscantur vestigia. Sicut illi coeperunt a visione sideris, profecerunt ad visionem pueri, pervenerunt ad visionem Dei, sic in nobis fides nascitur supernorum praedicatione luminarium; roboratur visione quarumdam imaginum, per speculum et in aenigmate Deum velut incarnatum nobis exhibentium; consummabitur, cum praesens et nuda videbitur veritas rerum facie ad faciem contemplantibus quod vix tenuiter et raptim in aenigmate nunc attingitur; cum et ipsa fides transibit in cognitionem, spes in possessionem, desiderium in fruitionem. Lucent namque et nobis stellae, non una, sed multae; nisi quia multae una, quarum scilicet cor unum est, et anima una, fides eadem, praedicatio consona, similisque vita. Quae sint hae stellae, si dubitas, Danielem interroga: Qui ad justitiam, inquit, erudiunt multos, quasi stellae in perpetuas aeternitates (Dan. XII, 3). Paulus etiam luminaria vocat eos, qui lucent in medio nationis pravae et perversae (Philipp. II, 15), continentque verbum vitae, tanquam splendorem mutuatum ab aeterna luce, quo et noctem hujus mundi jussi sunt illuminare. Ideo Dominus fons et origo lucis, cum ordinaret lunam et stellas in potestatem noctis, dicebat illis, Vos estis lux mundi: et iterum, Sic luceat lux vestra coram hominibus, ut videant opera vestra bona, et glorificent Patrem vestrum, qui in coelis est (Matth. V, 14, 16). Lucent itaque verbo, lucent exemplo, et utroque lucis radio ortum aeternae lucis annuntiant, dum coelestem, quem voce praedicant, etiam coelestis vitae similitudine commendant."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Beautifully then and significantly, in these firstfruits of the Gentiles, in these beginnings of the nascent Church, the progress of faith in each soul was shown beforehand: namely, whence it begins, how it advances, and to what at last it arrives; so that the footsteps of the fathers may easily be recognized in the children. Just as they began from the sight of the star, advanced to the sight of the child, and arrived at the sight of God, so in us faith is born from the preaching of the heavenly luminaries; it is strengthened by the vision of certain images, which, through a mirror and in a riddle, display God to us as it were incarnate; it will be consummated when the present and naked truth of things shall be seen, face to face, by those who contemplate it\u2014that truth which is now attained but faintly and fleetingly, in a riddle\u2014when faith itself shall pass into knowledge, hope into possession, desire into enjoyment. For stars shine for us too\u2014not one, but many; except that many are one, namely those whose heart is one, and soul one, faith the same, preaching harmonious, and life alike. If you doubt what these stars are, ask Daniel: Those who instruct many unto righteousness, he says, shall shine as stars for all eternity (Dan. XII, 3). Paul too calls them luminaries, those who shine in the midst of a crooked and perverse nation (Philipp. II, 15), and who hold fast the word of life, as a brightness borrowed from the eternal light, by which they are also commanded to illumine the night of this world. Therefore the Lord, the fountain and origin of light, when he ordained the moon and the stars for dominion over the night, said to them, You are the light of the world; and again, Let your light so shine before men, that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father who is in heaven (Matth. V, 14, 16). They shine therefore by word, they shine by example, and by both rays of light they announce the rising of the eternal light, while they commend, by the likeness of a heavenly life, that heavenly one whom they preach by voice.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Suspicite, fratres, in coelum; in coelo siquidem est conversatio eorum; suspicite, et intendite mentis oculos, si necdum potestis in rotam solis, saltem in fulgorem siderum; admiramini splendorem sanctorum, imitamini fidem, aemulamini sanctitatem. Stellae istae sicut flamma coruscant, et lumen luminum ortum demonstrant; ducunt ad cunabula novi Regis, ad cubiculum Virginis matris, quod est inviolabile fidei mysterium; imo perducent in templum Regis, in sanctuarium Dei Patris; quod est incogitabile fidei praemium. Interim autem, dum idonei non sumus, sapientiam Dei, quae in mysterio abscondita est, scrutari, vel majestatem quae in praemium reposita est, contemplari; contenti simus sanctorum claritatem mirari, sanctitatem imitari. Fili, inquit, concupisti sapientiam? serva mandata; et Dominus praebebit tibi illam (Eccli. I, 33). Altiora autem te ne quaesieris, et fortiora te ne scrutatus fueris: sed quae praecipit Dominus tibi, ipsa conserva semper (Eccli. III, 22). Praeceptum namque Domini lucidum, illuminans oculos (Psal. XVIII, 9), quibus postmodum ipsum corpus luminis valeas intueri, cum in luce radiorum, id est, in opere mandatorum, versari consueveris. Dulce quidem lumen, ut ait Salomon, et delectabile oculis videre solem (Eccle. XI, 7). Dulce prorsus et delectabile, sed illis quibus tolerabile. Sanos oculos fovet, infirmos torquet. Et quis est tam sano et acuto visu, ut in aspectu illius invisibilis Solis non caecutiat? quis ille scrutator majestatis, qui non opprimatur a gloria? Mirabilis facta est, o Domine, claritas tua, tum ex se, tum etiam ex me; quia confortata est adversum me, dum acies mea infirmata est in me, et jam non potero ad eam, sicut poteram in Adam. Potero forsitan ad claritatem siderum, qui non possum ad solem ipsum, et per hanc quandoque confortabor ad illum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Look up, brothers, to heaven; for their way of life is in heaven. Look up, and fix the eyes of your mind\u2014if you cannot yet gaze upon the wheel of the sun, at least upon the brightness of the stars. Admire the splendor of the saints, imitate their faith, strive to equal their holiness. These stars flash like flame, and show forth the rising of the light of lights; they lead to the cradle of the new King, to the chamber of the Virgin Mother\u2014which is the inviolable mystery of faith; indeed, they will lead us further, into the temple of the King, into the sanctuary of God the Father\u2014which is the inconceivable reward of faith. Meanwhile, however, since we are not fit to search out the wisdom of God which lies hidden in mystery, or to contemplate the majesty laid up as reward, let us be content to admire the brightness of the saints and to imitate their holiness. Son, it says, have you desired wisdom? Keep the commandments, and the Lord will give it to you (Eccli. I, 33). But do not seek things higher than yourself, and do not search into things stronger than yourself; but keep always what the Lord commands you (Eccli. III, 22). For the commandment of the Lord is lightsome, enlightening the eyes (Psal. XVIII, 9), with which you may afterward be able to gaze upon the very body of light itself, once you have grown accustomed to dwell in the light of its rays\u2014that is, in the doing of the commandments. Light is sweet indeed, as Solomon says, and it is delightful to the eyes to see the sun (Eccle. XI, 7). Sweet indeed and delightful, but only to those for whom it is bearable. It soothes sound eyes, it torments weak ones. And who has sight so sound and so keen that he would not be blinded at the sight of that invisible Sun? Who is that searcher of majesty who would not be overwhelmed by its glory? Wonderful, O Lord, has your brightness become, both in itself and also because of me; for it has grown strong against me, since my sight has grown weak within me, and now I can no longer attain to it as I could in Adam. Perhaps I shall be able to attain to the brightness of the stars, I who cannot attain to the sun itself, and through this I shall in time be strengthened toward that.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Eia, surge, illuminare, anima mea, quae sedebas in tenebris suspice luminaria coeli, leva oculos tuos in montes unde veniet auxilium tibi, si veneris ad illum qui habitat in coelis, id est, in montibus ipsis. A montibus, inquam, veniet auxilium tibi, quia lux tibi inaccessibilis illuminat mirabiliter a montibus aeternis. Susceperunt montes lucem populo, a quibus ad valles camposque descendat subjectos. Hoc itaque, dico, fratres, optimum est, nostraeque infirmitati congruum illuminationis initium, si intendamus in eos qui illuminati sunt. Rectissima via est inveniendi Jesum, si praecedentium patrum sequamur lumen praevium. Semita justi recta est, rectus callis justi ad ambulandum (Isa. XXVI, 7). Qui justum sequitur, non ambulat in tenebris; non modo videbit, sed habebit lumen vitae. Videbit ad consolationem praesentis vitae: habebit in possessionem haereditatis aeternae. Pietas nempe promissionem habet vitae quae nunc est, et futurae. Exerceamur ad pietatem, et neutro nos fraudabit qui se ultro nobis fecit debitorem (I Tim. IV, 8, 7, 9). Exerceamur in operibus lucis, et qui abscondit lucem in manibus suis, annuntians de ea amico suo, quod possessio ejus sit, et ad eam possit ascendere (Job XXXVI, 32, 33), nunc aliquando in solatium operis eam nobis demonstrabit, et postmodum in praemium dabit, ipse lux nostra Jesus Christus qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Come now, arise, be enlightened, my soul, you who were sitting in darkness; look up at the lights of heaven; lift up your eyes to the mountains, whence help will come to you, if you come to him who dwells in the heavens\u2014that is, in those very mountains. From the mountains, I say, will help come to you, because the light inaccessible to you shines wonderfully from the eternal mountains. The mountains have received light for the people, that from them it may descend to the valleys and plains lying below. This, then, I say, brothers, is the best and most fitting beginning of enlightenment for our weakness: that we should fix our gaze upon those who have been enlightened. It is the most direct way of finding Jesus, if we follow the guiding light of the fathers who went before us. The path of the just is straight; the way of the just is straight for walking (Isa. XXVI, 7). He who follows the just man does not walk in darkness; he will not only see but will possess the light of life. He will see it for the consolation of the present life; he will possess it as an eternal inheritance. For godliness holds the promise of the life that now is, and of that which is to come. Let us train ourselves unto godliness, and he who has of his own accord made himself our debtor will cheat us of neither (I Tim. IV, 8, 7, 9). Let us exercise ourselves in the works of light; and he who hides light in his hands, declaring to his friend concerning it that it is his possession and that he can ascend to it (Job XXXVI, 32, 33), will now at times show it to us for the comfort of our labor, and afterward will give it as our reward\u2014he himself, our light, Jesus Christ, who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints mutatatione, evidently a printer's doubling of mutatione (the changing of water into wine). The printed form is kept."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Epiphany Sermon II: On the Various Ways God Enlightens Us", "topics": ["Epiphany", "light", "Magi", "faith", "manifestation of Christ"], "url": "sermons/guerric-epiphany-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:ecce-advenit", "calendar_label": "Epiphany", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-epiphany-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In Epiphania Domini, sermo III, Quibus gradibus ad sapientiae lumen perveniatur, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 54-59.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Epiphany", "Epiphany of the Lord", "Isaiah 60:1", "2 Corinthians 4:3", "Psalm 17:29 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 6:22, 34", "Romans 13:12", "Ephesians 5:8", "John 9:41", "Mark 8:22\u201324", "Psalm 39:13 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 18:13 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 35:4 (historical numbering)", "2 Kings 4:7 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Samuel)", "Isaiah 60:5", "Isaiah 2:3", "Proverbs 31:18", "John 3:20", "Sirach 23:26\u201327", "Hosea 10:12", "Colossians 1:10", "Psalm 118:66, 100 (historical numbering)", "Romans 8:30", "Isaiah 11:9"], "occasion": "In epiphania Domini", "occasion_detail": "Epiphany of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Epiphany", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This third Epiphany sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Guerric sets out the steps by which the soul comes to the light of wisdom: faith first, then righteousness, then knowledge, and at last wisdom, the taste of eternal things. Along the way he asks for the light to see one's own sins, and he draws on the healing of the blind man in two stages and on David's understanding gained from the commandments. The source explicitly names Epiphany."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quibus gradibus ad sapientiae lumen perveniatur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "By What Steps One Comes to the Light of Wisdom", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Surge, illuminare, Jerusalem, quia venit lumen tuum (Isa. LX, 1). Benedictum lumen quod venit in nomine Domini, Deus Dominus, et illuxit nobis; de cujus munere etiam dies iste, dies sanctificatus de illuminatione Ecclesiae illuxit nobis. Gratias tibi, lux vera, quae illuminas omnem hominem venientem in hunc mundum, quae ad hoc ipsum venisti homo in hunc mundum. Illuminata est Jerusalem mater nostra, mater omnium qui illuminari meruerunt: adeo ut jam luceat omnibus qui in mundo sunt. Gratias tibi, lux vera, quae lucerna es facta, ut Jerusalem illuminares, et lucerna pedibus meis Verbum Dei fieret. Gratias, inquam, tibi, quoniam ipsa Jerusalem illuminata facta est lucerna, ut luceat omnibus qui sunt in domo magni patris familias. Non solum enim illuminata est, sed exaltata, et super candelabrum illud totum aureum sita est; civitas super montem montium, illa quondam derelicta et odio habita, posita est in superbiam saeculorum, ut tam longe lateque fulgeat ejus Evangelium, quam longe lateque mundi vigebat imperium. Et si opertum est ejus Evangelium, in his qui pereunt opertum est (II Cor. IV, 3), in quibus Deus hujus saeculi excaecavit mentes infidelium, ut non fulgeat eis gloria Ecclesiae Christi. Quibus tamen non fulget ut videant, fulget ut invideant, et Ecclesiae torquentur gloria, qui ipsius illuminari nolunt gratia. Verum Ecclesia nec propter gloriam qua floret, superbiens, nec gratiam qua pollet, invidens, etiam illis quos molestos tolerat, lumen quod accepit implorat, dicens: Quoniam tu illuminas lucernam meam, Domine; Deus meus, illumina tenebras meas (Psal. XVII, 29). Mei forsitan sunt, quia praedestinati; sed adhuc tenebrae sunt, quia nondum vocati aut justificati. Voca et ipsos in admirabile lumen tuum, et praedicabunt mecum admirabile nomen tuum. Quanquam justus quisque ac sanctus in Ecclesia ita se illuminatum gaudeat, ut adhuc se non minima ex parte tenebrosum videat et doleat; et ideo necessario, cum illuminatus sit, adhuc se illuminari petat. Quanto namque amplius lucerna ejus illuminata fuerit, tanto verius ex lucerna ipsa tenebras suas deprehendit. Nec statim contrarium putaveris, quod in Evangelio Veritas ait: Lucerna corporis tui est oculus tuus. Si oculus tuus simplex fuerit, totum corpus tuum lucidum erit (Matth. VI, 22 et Luc. XI, 34). Non enim, quia ex oculo sincerae intentionis totum opus nostrum lucidum fit, ideo consequens est ut statim omnes tenebrae ignorantiarum nostrarum illuminatae sint. Is nempe modus est adhuc illuminationis nostrae, ut in lumen veritatis multum existimetur profecisse, qui defectum suum potuerit nosse, et scire quid desit sibi. Inde est quod et apud sapientes hujus mundi, qui magis sobrie disputaverunt de scientia, primus, numeratur gradus scientiae, scire quod nescias."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Arise, be enlightened, O Jerusalem, for thy light has come (Isa. LX, 1). Blessed is the light that comes in the name of the Lord \u2014 God is the Lord, and he has shone upon us; by whose gift this day too, a day made holy by the enlightenment of the Church, has shone upon us. Thanks be to thee, true light, who dost enlighten every man coming into this world, who for this very purpose didst come as man into this world. Jerusalem our mother has been enlightened, the mother of all who have deserved to be enlightened, so that now she shines for all who are in the world. Thanks be to thee, true light, who hast become a lamp, that thou mightest enlighten Jerusalem, and that the Word of God might become a lamp to my feet. Thanks, I say, to thee, because Jerusalem herself, once enlightened, has become a lamp, so that she might shine for all who are in the house of the great father of the family. For she was not only enlightened but exalted, and set upon that lampstand all of gold; a city upon the mountain of mountains \u2014 she who was once forsaken and held in hatred \u2014 has been set up as the pride of the ages, that her Gospel might shine as far and wide as the empire of the world once flourished far and wide. And if her Gospel is veiled, it is veiled in those who are perishing (II Cor. IV, 3), in whom the god of this world has blinded the minds of unbelievers, so that the glory of the Church of Christ may not shine upon them. Yet for them it does not shine so that they may see, but shines so that they may envy, and they are tormented by the glory of the Church, they who refuse to be enlightened by her grace. But the Church, neither growing proud on account of the glory in which she flourishes, nor begrudging the grace by which she is strong, even for those whom she patiently endures as troublesome, begs for the light she has received, saying: For thou wilt light my candle, O Lord; O my God, enlighten my darkness (Psal. XVII, 29). Perhaps they are mine, because predestined; but they are still darkness, because not yet called or justified. Call them too into thy wonderful light, and they will proclaim thy wonderful name together with me. Although every just and holy person in the Church rejoices that he has been enlightened, yet he sees and grieves that he is still, in no small part, dark; and so, necessarily, even though he has been enlightened, he still asks to be enlightened further. For the more his lamp has been enlightened, the more truly does he discover his own darkness by that very lamp. And you should not at once think this contrary to what the Truth says in the Gospel: The lamp of thy body is thine eye. If thine eye be single, thy whole body shall be full of light (Matth. VI, 22 et Luc. XI, 34). For it is not the case that, because our whole work is made full of light from the eye of sincere intention, all the darkness of our ignorance is therefore at once illumined. For this indeed is still the measure of our enlightenment, that he is reckoned to have made great progress in the light of truth who has been able to know his own defect, and to know what he lacks. Hence it is that even among the wise men of this world, who have discussed knowledge more soberly, the first degree of knowledge is reckoned to be this: to know what you do not know.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Fratres, omnes vos filii lucis estis, et filii Dei. Non sumus noctis, neque tenebrarum. Nox enim praecessit, dies autem appropinquavit (Rom. XIII, 12). Et si fuimus aliquando tenebrae, nunc autem lux in Domino (Ephes. V, 8). Verumtamen si, quoniam non sumus tenebrae nec filii tenebrarum, dixerimus nos nihil pati tenebrarum, nos ipsos seducimus, et tenebras mortis, quae illuminari non mereantur, nobis inducimus. Quid enim, ait Lux mundi, quae in judicium venit in hunc mundum, ut qui non vident videant, et qui vident caeci fiant? Quia, inquit, dicitis, Videmus, peccatum vestrum manet (Joan. IX, 41). Video quantulumcunque, quoniam tu illuminas lucernam meam, o Domine, sed, quia parum est quod video, Deus meus, illumina tenebras meas (Psal. XVII, 29). Videmus ut multum per speculum et in aenigmate; sed multum longe est hoc ipsum a claritate vera claraque veritate, quae videbitur in facie. Ideo forsitan caecus, quem apud Marcum Dominus illuminare coeperat, homines quasi arbores ambulantes videbat (Marc. VIII, 22-24), quia videlicet non nisi per speculum et in aenigmate videbat; sed secundo imponens manum restituit eum, ita ut videret clare omnia; quod in nobis fiet, cum nos reficiet, et sanitatem perficiet impositione manuum secunda, quam coepit prima, restituens nos in claritate perfecta."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Brothers, you are all children of light, and children of God. We are not of the night, nor of darkness. For the night has passed, and the day is at hand (Rom. XIII, 12). And if we were once darkness, now we are light in the Lord (Ephes. V, 8). Yet if, because we are not darkness nor children of darkness, we should say that we suffer nothing of darkness, we deceive ourselves, and bring upon ourselves the darkness of death, which deserves not to be enlightened. For what does the Light of the world say, who came into this world for judgment, that those who do not see might see, and those who see might become blind? Because, he says, you say, We see: your sin remains (Joan. IX, 41). I see some little, however small, since thou, O Lord, dost light my candle; but because what I see is little, O my God, enlighten my darkness (Psal. XVII, 29). At most we see through a glass and in a dark manner; but this is very far indeed from the true clarity and clear truth which shall be seen face to face. Perhaps that is why the blind man, whom the Lord in Mark had begun to enlighten, saw men as though they were trees walking (Marc. VIII, 22-24), evidently because he saw only through a glass and in a dark manner; but by laying his hand on him a second time, he restored him so that he saw all things clearly \u2014 which shall happen in us, when he renews us and perfects our healing by a second laying on of hands, continuing what he began with the first, and restoring us to perfect clarity.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Sed hoc ipsum per speculum et in aenigmate, cui, aut quando, vel quandiu potest contingere? Quam misericorditer mecum ageretur, quam feliciter mihi viderer illuminatus, si peccata mea possem videre, cui quotidie necesse est plangere ac dicere: Circumdederunt me mala quorum non est numerus, et non potui ut viderem! (Psal. XXXIX, 13.) Nam delicta quis intelligit (Psal. XVIII, 13), quando etiam specie virtutis vitium decipiens interficit, quasi vere angelus tenebrarum transfigurans se in angelum lucis? Magnus utique, multumque illuminatus, qui delicta ad liquidum intelligere potuit et voluit; nam neque et dolosus potuit, et noluit intelligere, ut bene ageret (Psal. XXXV, 4). Deus illuminator omnium gentium, de te nobis cantabatur: Ecce veniet Dominus, et illuminabit oculos servorum suorum. Ecce venisti, o lumen meum, illumina oculos meos, ne unquam obdormiam in morte, ne peccatum ad mortem oculos nostros aliqua delectatione claudat, et ad consensum illiciat, aut, velut Isboseth, dormientem inveniens transfodiat (II Reg. IV, 7). Venisti, o lumen fidelium omnium, et ecce hodie de illuminatione verae fidei, id est, lucernae nostrae, dedisti nobis gaudere; da etiam de illuminatione tenebrarum nostrarum, quae residuae sunt, semper in Domino gaudere. Dedisti lumen verae fidei, da et lumen verae justitiae, da lumen divinae scientiae, da etiam et divinae sapientiae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But this very seeing \"through a glass and in a dark manner\" \u2014 to whom, or when, or for how long can it happen? How mercifully would it be dealt with me, how happily would I seem to myself illumined, if I could see my sins \u2014 I for whom it is necessary daily to lament and say: \"Evils without number have surrounded me, and I was not able to see\" (Psal. XXXIX, 13.)! For \"who understands his faults\" (Psal. XVIII, 13), when even vice, deceiving under the appearance of virtue, kills \u2014 as if truly an angel of darkness transforming himself into an angel of light? Great indeed, and much illumined, is he who was able and willing to understand his sins clearly; for the deceitful man could not and would not understand, that he might do well (Psal. XXXV, 4). O God, enlightener of all nations, of you it was sung to us: \"Behold, the Lord will come, and he will enlighten the eyes of his servants.\" Behold, you have come, O my light; enlighten my eyes, lest I ever fall asleep in death, lest sin unto death close our eyes with some delight and entice us into consent, or, like Ishbosheth, find us sleeping and pierce us through (II Reg. IV, 7). You have come, O light of all the faithful, and behold, today you have given us to rejoice over the illumination of the true faith, that is, of our lamp; grant also that we may always rejoice in the Lord over the illumination of the darkness that still remains in us. You have given the light of true faith; give also the light of true righteousness, give the light of divine knowledge, give also the light of divine wisdom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. His profecto gradibus proficiendum, hac via proficiscendum tibi esse puto, o anima fidelis, quo, fallacis mundi hujus tenebris exuta, pervenias ad patriam claritatis aeternae, ubi tenebrae tuae erunt sicut clara meridies, et nox sicut dies illuminabitur. Tunc prorsus, tunc videbis et afflues, et mirabitur, et dilatabitur cor tuum (Isa. LX, 5), quando replebitur divina majestate incircumscripti luminis omnis terra, et gloria ejus in te videbitur. Domus Jacob, venite, ambulemus in lumine Domini (Id. II, 3): ut veri filii lucis ambulemus de claritate in claritatem, tanquam praelucente Domini Spiritu, ut per singulos gradus virtutum regnum claritatis ulterius invadamus. Qui igitur jam per fidem sumus in lumine, ex ea et per eam in amplius et serenius lumen progrediamur, primo justitiae, dein scientiae, demumque sapientiae. Quod enim credimus per fidem, consequenter operandum vel promerendum est per justitiam, post intelligendum per scientiam, ac postremo contemplandum per sapientiam. Prima namque omnium accensa est lucerna fidei, ad quam operemur in nocte hujus saeculi. Inde scriptum est in laudem mulieris fortis, quae de nocte surrexit, et, de nocte ac die operans manibus suis, panem otiosa non comedit, Non exstinguetur, inquit, in nocte lucerna ejus (Prov. XXXI, 18), id est, non deficiet in tentatione fides ejus. Ad hanc lucernam non fiunt opera tenebrarum, sed exstinguitur cum fiunt. Qui enim male agit, odit lucem (Joan. III, 20). Ideoque exstinguit lucernam fidei, postponit memoriam Dei; non est timor Dei ante oculos ejus, et de tenebris quas sibi fecit, arbitrorumque absentia blanditur sibi, dicens: Quis me videt? Tenebrae circumdant me, et parietes cooperiunt me, et nemo circumspicit me. Quem vereor? Delictorum meorum non memorabitur Altissimus. Et non intelligit quoniam omnia videt oculus illius (Eccli. XXIII, 26, 27). Bona vero opera, etsi aliquando propter vanitatem declinandam in abscondito fiunt, ad lucem tamen internam et aeternam, id est sub judicio fidei et testimonio Dei fiunt, quia opera lucis sunt; lucernae utique ardentes in manibus operantium, et exspectantium adventum sponsi, qui educet quasi lumen justitiam tuam ad conspectum hominum et angelorum, et, sicut lux meridiana clara est, clarescere faciet opera tua, quia in Deo sunt facta, ut Deum in te, et te in Deo clarificent omnium judicia."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. By these steps indeed, I think, O faithful soul, you must advance; by this road you must set out, so that, stripped of the darkness of this deceitful world, you may arrive at the homeland of eternal brightness, where your darkness shall be as the clear noonday, and night shall be lit up as day. Then truly, then you shall see and overflow, and your heart shall wonder and be enlarged (Isa. LX, 5), when the whole earth shall be filled with the divine majesty of uncircumscribed light, and his glory shall be seen in you. \"House of Jacob, come, let us walk in the light of the Lord\" (Id. II, 3): that as true children of light we may walk from brightness to brightness, as the Spirit of the Lord shines before us, so that through each degree of the virtues we may press on further into the kingdom of brightness. Therefore we who are already in the light through faith, from faith and through it let us advance into a fuller and more serene light \u2014 first of righteousness, then of knowledge, and finally of wisdom. For what we believe by faith must consequently be worked out, or earned, through righteousness; afterward it must be understood through knowledge; and finally it must be contemplated through wisdom. For of all lamps the first to be lit is the lamp of faith, by which we must work in the night of this present age. Hence it is written in praise of the valiant woman, who rose while it was yet night, and, working with her hands by night and by day, did not eat her bread in idleness: \"Her lamp shall not be put out in the night\" (Prov. XXXI, 18), that is, her faith shall not fail under temptation. By this lamp works of darkness are not done; rather, it is extinguished when such works are done. For \"he who does evil hates the light\" (Joan. III, 20). And so he extinguishes the lamp of faith, he puts away the memory of God; the fear of God is not before his eyes, and from the darkness he has made for himself, and the absence of witnesses, he flatters himself, saying: Who sees me? Darkness surrounds me, and walls cover me, and no one looks around at me. Whom do I fear? The Most High will not remember my sins. And he does not understand that the eye of the Most High sees all things (Eccli. XXIII, 26, 27). But good works, even when sometimes done in secret in order to avoid vanity, are nevertheless done before the inward and eternal light, that is, under the judgment of faith and the witness of God, because they are works of light \u2014 lamps indeed burning in the hands of those who work and who await the coming of the Bridegroom, who will bring forth your righteousness as light before the sight of men and angels, and, as the light of noonday is clear, he will make your works to shine clearly, because they were done in God, so that the judgment of all may glorify God in you, and you in God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Quanta vero claritate justitia resplendeat etiam in praesenti, quanta luce laetificet conscientiam justi, familiarius et suavius conscientiae vestrae testimonium vos docebit. Saepe autem et fides radiat, et justitia coruscat, et adhuc tamen intellectus caligat, ut mysterium fidei, quod velut involutum tenens veneratur, explicare nesciat; signatus sit ei liber Scripturae, quasi litteras nesciat; sed nec ad discretionem boni et mali, veri et falsi sensus exercitatos habeat. Et tales quidem multi in Ecclesia, quorum fides multa, abundans justitia, scientia fere nulla. Quod tamen ad lumen justitiae accedere oporteat lumen scientiae, docet sermo prophetae, qui, cum praemisisset: Seminate vobis ad justitiam, ut ostenderet quas interim primitias colligamus de hoc semine, subjunxit: Illuminate vobis lumen scientiae (Osee. X, 12). Sic et Apostolus ait: Fructificantes in opere bono, et crescentes in scientia Dei (Coloss. I, 10). Tu quoque, David, sedens in cathedra sapientissimus princeps inter tres, quomodo super senes intellexisti? Quia mandata, inquit, quaesivi. A mandatis prorsus intellexi, quia intellectus bonus omnibus facientibus timorem Domini. Nam et unctio docens de omnibus, sciens nimirum quo ordine quidque apud eam discendum sit, prius docet bonitatem et disciplinam, et postmodum scientiam: si tamen qui docendus est, primo omnium crediderit, id est, elementa exordii sermonum justitiae didicerit. Ideo ille discipulus unctionis tam prudenter precem ordinabat, atque allegabat: Bonitatem, inquit, et disciplinam et scientiam doce me, quia mandatis tuis credidi (Psal. CXVIII, 100, 66). Ac si diceret: Didici prima elementa, id est, fidem non fictam; jam, quod sequitur, doce justitiam, id est bonitatem et disciplinam, ut sic demum provehat ad sanctorum scientiam. Vocasti ad gratiam fidei, jam justifica per bonitatem amoris, et disciplinam casti timoris, ut post magnifices munere scientiae spiritualis. Quos autem vocavit, hos et justificavit; et quos justificavit illos et magnificavit (Rom. VIII, 30)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. With how great a brightness righteousness shines even in the present life, with how great a light it gladdens the conscience of the just, the testimony of your own conscience will teach you more intimately and more sweetly. But often it happens that faith shines, and righteousness glitters, and yet understanding remains dark, so that one who holds and venerates the mystery of faith as though wrapped up cannot unfold it; the book of Scripture is sealed to him as if he did not know letters; nor does he have senses trained to discern good from evil, true from false. And indeed there are many such in the Church, in whom faith is great, righteousness abundant, but knowledge almost none. Yet that the light of knowledge ought to be added to the light of righteousness is taught by the word of the prophet, who, having first said: 'Sow for yourselves unto righteousness,' in order to show what first-fruits we are meanwhile to gather from this seed, added: 'Light for yourselves the light of knowledge' (Osee. X, 12). So too the Apostle says: 'Being fruitful in good work, and increasing in the knowledge of God' (Coloss. I, 10). You too, David, sitting in the chair, the wisest prince among the three\u2014how did you understand above the elders? 'Because,' he says, 'I sought the commandments.' Entirely from the commandments I understood, for good understanding belongs to all who do the fear of the Lord. For the anointing which teaches concerning all things, knowing full well in what order each thing is to be learned from it, first teaches goodness and discipline, and afterward knowledge\u2014provided, however, that the one to be taught has first of all believed, that is, has learned the elementary first principles of the words of righteousness. Therefore that disciple of the anointing so prudently ordered his prayer, and pleaded: 'Teach me goodness,' he says, 'and discipline and knowledge, for I have believed your commandments' (Psal. CXVIII, 100, 66). As if he were saying: I have learned the first elements, that is, unfeigned faith; now, what follows, teach me righteousness, that is, goodness and discipline, so that it may at last advance me to the knowledge of the saints. You have called to the grace of faith; now justify through the goodness of love and the discipline of chaste fear, so that afterward you may magnify with the gift of spiritual knowledge. 'Whom he called, these he also justified; and whom he justified, these he also magnified' (Rom. VIII, 30).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Sciendum sane quod, cum sit haec scientia sanctorum, non tamen omnes sancti docentur eam, sed illi duntaxat in quibus nec industria gratiam, nec gratia destituit industriam; sed utrumque mutuo sibi suffragio cooperatur, ut et docibilis sit homo discipulus, et spiritus adsit doctor sedulus. Cum autem varia charismata contineat haec scientia, Spiritus tamen moderator non in unum facile confert omnia, sed dividit singulis prout vult, alii notitiam mysteriorum, alii intellectum Scripturarum, alii interpretationem sermonum, alii discretionem spirituum, alii illam, quae pernecessaria est, velut in gustu dignoscentiam, atque dijudicationem virtutum ac vitiorum, ne fallant vitia specie virtutum. Non nocebunt, inquit, et non occident in universo sancto monte meo; quia repleta est terra scientia Domini (Isai. XI, 9)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. It should indeed be known that, although this is the knowledge of the saints, not all saints are taught it, but only those in whom neither does industry fail grace, nor grace fail industry; rather, each cooperates with the other by mutual support, so that the man is a teachable disciple, and the Spirit is present as a diligent teacher. But since this knowledge contains various charisms, the Spirit who governs does not readily confer all of them on one person, but divides them to each as he wills: to one, knowledge of the mysteries; to another, understanding of the Scriptures; to another, interpretation of discourses; to another, discernment of spirits; to another, that most necessary gift, as it were a tasting discernment, and a judging apart of virtues and vices, lest vices deceive under the appearance of virtues. 'They shall not hurt,' it says, 'nor shall they kill in all my holy mountain; for the earth is filled with the knowledge of the Lord' (Isai. XI, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Jam vero si quis post haec et per haec tria, fidem, justitiam atque scientiam, proficiat ad sapientiam, id est, saporem et gustum aeternorum, ut possit vacare et videre, videndoque gustare quam suavis Dominus; et quod oculus non vidit, et auris non audivit, et in cor hominis non ascendit, ei per Spiritum reveletur: hunc omnino dixerim illuminatum magnifice et gloriose, tanquam qui gloriam Domini revelata facie speculetur, et super quem gloria Domini saepius oriatur. Huic utique dicere habet non propheta, sed prophetarum Spiritus: Surge, illuminare, Jerusalem, quia venit lumen tuum, et gloria Domini super te orta est (Id. LX, 1). Fratres, non omnes capiunt hoc verbum, sed qui potest capere capiat. Non condemnatur qui non capit: sed qui non cupit, utique teporis arguitur. Qui autem cupit, noverit quia hoc lumen sapientiae fervens accendit oratio, sicut et lumen scientiae frequens lectio; si tamen cum legis, lucernam ardentem adhibueris, id est justitiam operum, piorumque experientiam sensuum. Tu autem, Domine, Pater luminum, qui lumen de te lumine unicum tuum, misisti sisti illuminare tenebras mortalium, da nobis per viam luminum pervenire ad lumen aeternum, ut placeamus coram te in lumine viventium, qui vivis et regnas per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Now if anyone, after these and through these three\u2014faith, righteousness, and knowledge\u2014advances to wisdom, that is, to the savor and taste of eternal things, so that he may be free to see, and in seeing to taste how sweet the Lord is; and so that what eye has not seen, nor ear heard, nor has entered into the heart of man, may be revealed to him through the Spirit: this man I would call altogether magnificently and gloriously illumined, as one who beholds the glory of the Lord with unveiled face, and upon whom the glory of the Lord more often arises. To such a one, surely, it is not the prophet but the Spirit of the prophets who must say: 'Arise, be illumined, Jerusalem, for your light has come, and the glory of the Lord has arisen upon you' (Id. LX, 1). Brothers, not all grasp this word, but let him who can grasp it, grasp it. He who does not grasp it is not condemned; but he who does not desire it is surely convicted of lukewarmness. But let him who desires it know that this light of wisdom is kindled by fervent prayer, just as the light of knowledge is kindled by frequent reading\u2014provided, however, that when you read, you bring to it a burning lamp, that is, the righteousness of works and the experience of devout senses. But you, O Lord, Father of lights, who have sent your Only One, Light from your Light, to illumine the darkness of mortals, grant us to arrive by the way of lights at the eternal light, that we may be pleasing before you in the light of the living, you who live and reign through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Epiphany Sermon III: By What Steps One Comes to the Light of Wisdom", "topics": ["Epiphany", "light", "wisdom", "self-knowledge", "spiritual progress"], "url": "sermons/guerric-epiphany-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:ecce-advenit", "calendar_label": "Epiphany", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-epiphany-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In Epiphania Domini, sermo IV, De nova nostra nativitate et vita, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 59-64.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Epiphany", "Epiphany of the Lord", "1 Timothy 3:9", "Titus 1:16", "Ephesians 5:12", "2 Timothy 3:5", "Isaiah 61:9", "Hebrews 10:22", "John 13:10", "Sirach 34:30", "2 Peter 2:4", "4 Kings 5:10, 12, 14 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Kings)", "Job 40:18", "4 Kings 5:10, 13\u201314 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Kings)"], "occasion": "In epiphania Domini", "occasion_detail": "Epiphany of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Epiphany", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This fourth Epiphany sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Christ's baptism today brings a second birth for believers, but Guerric fears that monks keep the appearance of piety while denying its power. Naaman, washed seven times in the Jordan, becomes the figure of a sevenfold humility of Christ, in which those who lost the grace of their first Baptism may be cleansed again. The source explicitly names Epiphany."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De nova nostra nativitate et vita."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On Our New Birth and Life", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Secunda nobis hodie, fratres, nativitas celebratur, quae de illa prima nativitate, tanquam de causa effectus, consequenter nata videtur. Illa siquidem, quam usque hodie celebravimus, nativitas Christi est; ista, quam hodie celebramus, nativitas nostra est. In illa namque Christus natus est, in ista Christianitas nata est. Cum enim tria sint quae nos faciunt christianos, fides, Baptismus, et altaris participatio, hodierna dies fidem nobis initiavit, Baptisma consecravit, ac mensae coelestis miracula praeformavit. Quomodo sane prima gentium illuminatio fidem nobis initiaverit, quomodo Christus baptizatus Baptisma nostrum consecraverit, qualiter illa conversio aquae in vinum, mutationem rerum in convivio mensae Dominicae praeostenderit, nequaquam jam opus est ut doceamus: sed illud arbitror operae pretium, ut nosmetipsos commoneamus, ne fides in nobis a suis degeneret initiis, ne vacuetur in nobis gratia Baptismatis, ne cedat nobis in judicium participatio calicis Christi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Today, brothers, we celebrate a second birth for ourselves, which seems to have been born as a consequence of that first birth, as an effect follows from its cause. For that birth which we have celebrated up until today is the birth of Christ; but this one, which we celebrate today, is our own birth. For in that birth Christ was born, but in this one Christianity was born. Since there are three things that make us Christians\u2014faith, Baptism, and participation at the altar\u2014today's feast has initiated faith in us, consecrated Baptism, and prefigured the miracles of the heavenly table. Indeed, there is no longer any need for us to teach how that first illumination of the Gentiles initiated faith for us, how Christ's being baptized consecrated our Baptism, how that turning of water into wine foreshadowed the change of things in the banquet of the Lord's table: but I think it worthwhile that we remind ourselves, lest faith in us should degenerate from its beginnings, lest the grace of Baptism be made void in us, lest our participation in the cup of Christ turn into judgment against us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Et ipsa quidem fides, qualis ab illis Magis, primis videlicet auctoribus suis, nobis hodie tradita sit; qualiter maximum fidei sacramentum mysticis insinuaverint munerum figuris, satis abundeque disseruere patres nostri, ut ex ea parte nullus jam alius labor quam in labores eorum introire nobis resederit. Sed neque magnopere postulat status, imo ruina temporis hujus, ut de mysterio fidei disputetur sed ut magis id modis omnibus elaboretur ut, sicut Apostolus ait, in conscientia pura idem fidei mysterium habeatur (I Tim. III, 9). Ecce enim hodie si de mysterio fidei interrogas, omnes fere invenies christianissimos; si conscientias discutias, paucos admodum invenies vere christianos. Totus fere mundus confitetur verbis se nosse Deum, factis autem negat (Tit. I, 16); adeo ut et qui speciem pietatis videntur habere, virtutem ejus saepius inveniantur propemodum abnegare. Quid putatis autem, fratres mei, quos in hoc significare voluerim, quos scilicet dicam pietatis habere speciem, sed abnegare virtutem? Si illos qui spatiosam viam saeculi ambulant, intelligitis, quid illi vel de sola praeferunt pietatis specie, qui nomine solo christiani, atque in vacuum assumentes nomen sanctum Christi, tota ac libera professione sectantur quae huic inimica sunt nomini; qui omni specie conversationis et modo vitae, se protestantur inimicos crucis Christi? Haeccine est species pietatis quam in eis videmus, habitus et incessus meretricius, sermo scurrilis, oculus impudicus, venter qui pro Deo colitur, et omnis illa vitae contumelia, quae publice et gloriabunde, velut in faciem pietati irrogatur? Quae enim in occulto ab ipsis fiunt, turpe est et dicere (Ephes. V, 12). Fodiat parietem eorum qui ad ipsos missus est prophetare, ut adhuc majores abominationes videat, quas non erubescunt aut verentur coram illa tremenda majestate perpetrare. Quid enim mihi, qui monachus videor, de his qui extra hoc monasterium sunt judicare? Utinam nec de his ipsis qui hic intus sunt, mihi esset judicare. Ad me ipsum namque conturbata est anima mea, dum scilicet vereor ne haec ipsa species pietatis, quam utcunque vobiscum gero, gravius me incipiat accusare, si pietatem ipsa me convicerit abnegasse."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. As for faith itself\u2014how it was handed down to us today by those Magi, who were indeed its first authors; how they expressed the greatest sacrament of faith through the mystical figures of their gifts\u2014our fathers have discussed this fully and more than abundantly, so that in that regard no other labor remains for us than to enter into their labors. But neither does the condition of the times, or rather the ruin of this present age, greatly demand that we dispute about the mystery of faith, but rather that we strive by every means to hold, as the Apostle says, the same mystery of faith in a pure conscience (I Tim. III, 9). For behold, today, if you ask about the mystery of faith, you will find almost everyone most Christian; but if you examine consciences, you will find very few who are truly Christian. Almost the whole world confesses in words that it knows God, but denies it by its deeds (Tit. I, 16); so much so that even those who seem to have the appearance of piety are often found to deny its power almost entirely. But what do you suppose, my brothers, I meant to signify by this\u2014whom indeed do I mean by those who have the appearance of piety, but deny its power? If you understand those who walk the broad way of the world, what mere appearance of piety do even they put forward, who are Christians in name only, and who, taking up the holy name of Christ in vain, pursue, by their whole and free profession, things that are hostile to this name; who, by every outward show and manner of life, declare themselves enemies of the cross of Christ? Is this the appearance of piety that we see in them\u2014harlot-like bearing and gait, scurrilous speech, shameless eye, the belly that is worshiped in place of God, and all that reproach of life which is publicly and boastfully inflicted, as it were, upon the very face of piety? For the things that are done by them in secret are shameful even to mention (Ephes. V, 12). Let him who was sent to them to prophesy dig through their wall, that he may see even greater abominations, which they do not blush or fear to commit in the presence of that tremendous majesty. But what right have I, who only appear to be a monk, to judge those who are outside this monastery? Would that I had no need to judge even those who are within it. For within myself my soul is troubled, since indeed I fear lest this very appearance of piety, which I somehow bear among you, may begin to accuse me the more gravely, if that appearance itself should convict me of having denied piety.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Omnino, fratres, verendum est mihi, an vero et vobis ipsis, vos videritis, ne ad nos praecipue spectet ille sermo qui per Apostolum prophetatus est de temporibus novissimis; ubi, cum malitiam hominum describeret tunc futurorum, qualis nunc manifeste legi potest in vita et moribus eorum, longum tandem sic conclusit malorum catalogum: Habentes, inquit, speciem pietatis, virtutem autem ejus abnegantes (II Tim. III, 5). Quid enim ita speciem pietatis habet, sicut haec quam praetendimus, humilitas tonsurae et habitus, parcimonia cibi et potus, labor fere continuus, et ordinatissimus per omnia vivendi modus? Caeterum de virtute ipsa pietatis, quam haec species exterior promittit, quam prope nihil in me meique similibus valeat inveniri, pudor mihi esset confiteri, si non omnibus essemus manifesti. Quae est enim virtus pietatis, nisi charitas non ficta, humilitas vera, patientia longanimis, obedientia non segnis? In his virtutibus earumque similibus, quantum vobis liceat gloriari, vestro, fratres, relinquimus judicio; de me siquidem ingenue confiteor, quia nomina quidem earum scio, saporem adhuc ignoro. Merito itaque pudorem mihi pariter et timorem incutit habitus quem gero religionis, cui fere nullum suffragatur testimonium virtutis. Numquid enim tuto mihi possum usurpare monachi nomen et honorem, cujus non habeo meritum et virtutem; cum, sicut ante nos dictum est, simulata sanctitas, duplicata sit iniquitas (AUG. in psal. LXIII); et lupus sub ovina pelle deprehensus graviore sit sententia percellendus? Propterea vos quoque, fratres, ut tuto gloriari possitis, in hac, qua corpora vestra decorata sunt, specie pietatis, ponite corda vestra in virtute ejus; ut species in corporibus, virtus in cordibus, probatos vos exhibeant tam angelis quam hominibus, sitque, sicut scriptum est: Omnes qui viderint eos, cognoscent illos quia isti sunt semen cui benedixit Dominus (Isa. LXI, 9)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Brothers, I must altogether fear\u2014whether you too should, you yourselves must judge\u2014that this saying which was prophesied by the Apostle concerning the last times applies especially to us; where, describing the wickedness of the men who were then to come, such as can now clearly be read in the life and conduct of men, he concluded his long catalogue of evils thus: \"Having,\" he says, \"the appearance of godliness, but denying the power thereof\" (II Tim. III, 5). For what has so much the appearance of godliness as this which we put forward\u2014the humility of the tonsure and habit, the sparingness of food and drink, the almost continuous labor, and the altogether well-ordered manner of life? But as for the very power of godliness which this outward appearance promises, how little it is to be found in me and those like me\u2014it would shame me to confess, were we not already manifest to all. For what is the power of godliness, but unfeigned charity, true humility, long-suffering patience, ready obedience? How far you may boast in these and similar virtues, brothers, I leave to your own judgment; as for myself, I confess frankly that I know the names of these virtues, but I do not yet know their taste. Rightly, then, the habit of religion which I wear strikes me with shame and fear alike, since it is supported by almost no testimony of virtue. For can I safely assume the name and honor of monk, when I do not have the merit and virtue of one? As it has been said before us, \"feigned holiness is doubled iniquity\" (AUG. in psal. LXIII); and a wolf caught under sheep's clothing is to be struck with a graver sentence. Therefore you too, brothers, so that you may boast safely in this outward appearance of godliness with which your bodies are adorned, place your hearts in its power; so that the appearance in your bodies and the power in your hearts may show you approved both to angels and to men, and so that it may be as it is written: \"All that see them shall know them, that these are the seed which the Lord hath blessed\" (Isa. LXI, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Haec est, fratres, christianae fidei pietas vera, hoc est habere mysterium fidei in conscientia pura. Ut enim mysterium fidei, quod hodie nobis mystica commendaverunt Magorum munera, fideliter et digne conservaremus immaculatum in conscientia pura, hodie itidem Christus baptizatus, non sibi sed nobis, Aspersit, sicut Apostolus ait, corda a conscientia mala, et abluit corpora aqua munda (Hebr. X. 22). Quam felix, o fratres, anima illa, quae sic lota in salutem, mox e vestigio secuta est Salvatorem, ut deinceps adhaerens ei cum Apostolis, illam meruit audire vocem: Qui lotus est, non indiget nisi ut pedes lavet, sed est mundus totus! (Joan. XIII, 10.) Caeterum qui baptizatur a mortuo, et iterum tangit eum, quid proficit lavatio illius? (Eccli. XXXIV, 30.) Ego postquam Baptismo lotus fui, non unum mortuum, sed tot mortuos quot opera mortis, tetigi; et, juxta Petrum apostolum, contigit mihi illud veri proverbii, Canis reversus ad vomitum suum, et sus lota in volutabro luti (II Petr. II, 4). Quid igitur faciam? quibus ulterius aquis sordes tantas diluam? Audio quia sicut una fides, sic unum Baptisma est, nec secundo Baptismati locus est, quoniam quicunque baptizamur, in morte Christi baptizamur; qui sicut semel mortuus est, sic et semel baptizatus est, ut et nobis unici modum et formam Baptismatis praescriberet. Diffiderem forsitan ulterius posse mundari, nisi quia desperantem reducit in spem, inter alias Scripturae consolationes, etiam fidele consilium Elisei, etiamsi totus instar Naaman Syri lepra sim perfusus, ac veterno incurabili possessus. Vade, inquit, et lavare septies in Jordane, et mundaberis. Si dixisset, Lavare ter, mundaberis, continuo intellexissem trinam mersionem qua inundantur baptizati, diceremque totum illud miraculum gratiam prophetare Baptismatis, ut jam tunc Jordanis de virtute Dominici Baptismi coepisset ad salutem gentium operari. Nunc autem cum signanter dicat, lavare septies in Jordane, quid sibi vult septenarius iste, aut quis est Jordanis ille? Jordanis, ut aiunt, interpretatur Descensus eorum. Quorum? Illorum utique qui humiliantur ad poenitentiam, qui nimirum quanto profundius descendunt per gradus humilitatis, tanto se penitius mergunt et plenius abluunt spiritualis alveo Jordanis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. This, brothers, is the true godliness of the Christian faith; this is to hold the mystery of faith in a pure conscience. For in order that we might faithfully and worthily keep undefiled in a pure conscience the mystery of faith which the mystical gifts of the Magi commended to us today, Christ, baptized today, not for himself but for us, as the Apostle says, \"sprinkled our hearts from an evil conscience, and washed our bodies with clean water\" (Hebr. X. 22). How happy, O brothers, is that soul which, thus washed unto salvation, straightway followed the Savior in his very footsteps, so that thereafter, clinging to him with the apostles, it deserved to hear that voice: \"He that is washed needeth not but to wash his feet, but is clean wholly!\" (Joan. XIII, 10.). But he who is washed after touching a dead body, and touches it again, what does his washing profit him? (Eccli. XXXIV, 30.) I, after I was washed in Baptism, have touched not one dead body, but as many dead bodies as there are works of death; and, according to the apostle Peter, that true proverb has befallen me: \"The dog returned to his vomit,\" and \"the sow washed, to her wallowing in the mire\" (II Petr. II, 4). What, then, shall I do? With what further waters shall I wash away such great filth? I hear that, just as there is one faith, so there is one Baptism, and there is no place for a second Baptism, since whoever of us are baptized, are baptized into the death of Christ; who, just as he died once, so was baptized once, that he might prescribe for us too the manner and form of a single Baptism. I might perhaps despair of being able to be cleansed any further, were it not that, among the other consolations of Scripture, the faithful counsel of Elisha brings back the despairing soul to hope\u2014even if I were, like Naaman the Syrian, entirely covered with leprosy, and possessed by an incurable lethargy. \"Go,\" he said, \"and wash seven times in the Jordan, and you shall be made clean.\" If he had said, \"Wash three times, and you shall be made clean,\" I would at once have understood the threefold immersion by which the baptized are overwhelmed, and I would say that that whole miracle prophesied the grace of Baptism, so that the Jordan even then began, by the power of the Lord's future Baptism, to work for the salvation of the nations. But now, since he expressly says, \"wash seven times in the Jordan,\" what does this number seven mean, or what is that Jordan? Jordan, as they say, is interpreted \"Their Descent.\" Whose descent? Surely of those who are humbled unto repentance, who indeed, the more deeply they descend by the steps of humility, the more inwardly do they immerse themselves and the more fully do they wash in the channel of the spiritual Jordan.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Ad meipsum, o Deus, conturbata est anima mea, memor peccatorum suorum; propterea memor ero tui de terra Jordanis, quomodo scilicet in descensu humiliationis mundaveris Naaman leprosum. Descendit, inquit, et lavit septies in Jordane, juxta sermonem viri Dei, et mundatus est. Descende et tu, o anima mea, de curru superbiae in salubres aquas Jordanis, qui de fonte domus David, toto jam profluit orbe in ablutionem peccatoris et menstruatae. Haec est nimirum humilitas poenitendi, quae, de domo pariter et exemplo profluens Christi, toto jam praedicatur orbe, totiusque purgat crimina mundi. O vos, Naaman Syri: non enim unus, sed innumeri: o, inquam, divites, sed leprosi; superbi, sed aerumnosi; quare tam vehementer abhorretis lavari his medicinalibus aquis? cur vobis ita viluit noster iste Jordanis prae fluviis Damasci? Dicitis enim, si forte quaerentibus aliquando consilium salutis imitanda praedicetur humilitas et paupertas Christi: Nunquid non meliores sunt fluvii Damasci omnibus aquis Israel, ut laver in eis, et munder? (IV Reg. V, 10, 14, 12.) Nunquid non est melius, et ad purgationem peccatorum efficacius, quotidie benefacere de affluentia mundi, quam semel omnia relinquendo pauperem fieri? Sed enim audi, Naaman. Fluvium Damascenum absorbebit Leviathan, et non mirabitur; nam de Jordane si habeat fiduciam quod influat in os ejus (Job XL, 18), spes eum frustrabitur. Neque enim grande reputat quantiscunque, praeter humilitatem christianam, virtutibus affluas: cum etiam pauperes Christi devorare se posse confidat, sed nihil in eis inimicus proficiat. Damascus secundum nomen suum, Civitas sanguinis est, et aquae ejus sanguine mistae sunt; quia opera etiam bona carnalium et saecularium, vix a quocunque peccato pura sunt. Et quomodo id quod infectum est sanguine, sanguinem mundabit? qua ratione lepram, id quod leprosum est curabit? At Jordanis noster ipse quidem purum profluit, nec te calumniari poterunt superbi, si totus in eo mersus fueris, ac velut consepultus humilitati Christi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Within myself, O God, my soul is troubled, mindful of its sins; therefore I will remember you from the land of the Jordan\u2014namely, how, in the descent of humiliation, you cleansed Naaman the leper. He went down, it says, and washed seven times in the Jordan, according to the word of the man of God, and was cleansed. Go down, you too, O my soul, from the chariot of pride into the healthful waters of the Jordan, which, flowing from the fountain of the house of David, now pours forth through the whole world for the washing of the sinner and of her who is unclean. This indeed is the humility of repentance, which, flowing alike from the household and the example of Christ, is now preached throughout the whole world, and purges the crimes of the whole world. O you Naamans of Syria\u2014for there is not one, but countless many: O, I say, rich, yet leprous; proud, yet wretched\u2014why do you so vehemently shrink from being washed in these healing waters? Why has our Jordan grown so cheap in your eyes compared to the rivers of Damascus? For you say, if perchance to those seeking the counsel of salvation the humility and poverty of Christ is preached as something to be imitated: \"Are not the rivers of Damascus better than all the waters of Israel, that I may wash in them, and be made clean?\" (IV Reg. V, 10, 14, 12.). Is it not better, and more effective for the cleansing of sins, to do good daily out of the abundance of the world, than, by forsaking all at once, to become poor? But hear this, Naaman. Leviathan will swallow up the river of Damascus, and not marvel at it; for if he should have confidence that the Jordan will flow into his mouth (Job XL, 18), his hope will be disappointed. For he does not reckon it a great thing, however much you may abound in virtues apart from Christian humility, since he is confident that he can devour even the poor of Christ\u2014but in them the enemy makes no progress. Damascus, according to its name, is the \"City of Blood,\" and its waters are mingled with blood; for even the good works of carnal and worldly men are scarcely pure from any sin whatsoever. And how shall that which is stained with blood cleanse blood? By what reasoning shall that which is itself leprous cure leprosy? But our Jordan flows pure indeed, nor will the proud be able to slander you, if you have been wholly immersed in it, and as it were buried together with the humility of Christ.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Audi saltem, o Naaman, domesticos tuos, humiles amicos, fideles consiliarios. Naaman recedebat indignans adversum virum Dei, quem consuluerat; at illi, Pater, inquiunt, etsi rem grandem dixisset tibi propheta, certe facere debueras. Fidele prorsus verbum, rationis et sapientiae plenum; quippe suggestio est rationalium hominis sensuum, quos sibi Deus testes retinuit adversus hominem ipsum. Pater, inquiunt, etsi rem grandem dixisset tibi Propheta, certe facere debueras. Non credere namque Prophetae, Dei est injuria: non tentare omnia pro salute, odium est sui ipsius et invidia. Quid enim grande esse potest in praecepto, ubi salus est in promisso? Quanto magis non debet videri grande vel difficile, discere ab Jesu quia mitis est et humilis corde, cum in eo pariter inveniatur et requies animae, et praesens medela spiritualis leprae, pignusque salutis aeternae? Tandem vix aegreque obtinetur a superbo peccatore ut descendat in Jordanem et lavet; si tamen septies lavat, totumque se plenius et saepius mergat, non moratur effectus salutis fidem prophetico facere sermoni. Sed, proh dolor! quanta est hujus nostri miseria temporis! quia non solum divites, sed etiam pauperes leprosi, ita inveniuntur delicati, ut vix usque ad talos salubribus intingi patiantur aquis: aut si semel laverint, mox sibi plenissime videantur mundati."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Listen at least, O Naaman, to your own household, your humble friends, your faithful counselors. Naaman was going away indignant against the man of God whom he had consulted; but they said, \"Father, even if the prophet had told you a great thing, surely you ought to have done it.\" A thoroughly faithful word, full of reason and wisdom; indeed it is the suggestion of a man's rational senses, which God has kept for himself as witnesses against the man himself. \"Father,\" they say, \"even if the Prophet had told you a great thing, surely you ought to have done it.\" For not to believe the Prophet is an injury to God; not to attempt everything for one's salvation is hatred and envy toward oneself. For what great thing can there be in a command, where salvation lies in the promise? How much less ought it to seem a great or difficult thing to learn from Jesus, because he is meek and humble of heart, since in him is found alike both rest for the soul, and a present remedy for spiritual leprosy, and the pledge of eternal salvation? At last, it is only with difficulty and reluctance that a proud sinner is brought to go down into the Jordan and wash; yet if he washes seven times, and plunges himself more fully and more often, the effect of salvation does not delay in proving the prophetic word true. But, alas, how great is the misery of this our present time! For not only the rich, but even the poor who are leprous, are found so delicate that they scarcely allow themselves to be dipped up to the ankles in these healthful waters; or if they wash once, they straightway seem to themselves most fully cleansed.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Et Elisaeus non ita sentit, sed signanter exprimit, Septies lavare, et mundaberis. Sciebat nimirum humilitatem Christi, quae nobis imitanda est, si perfecte volumus mundari, septemplicis esse virtutis. Prima virtus, quod, cum dives esset, pauper factus est. Secunda, quod paupertati extremitatem adjiciens, in praesepi positus est. Tertia, quod Matri subditus. Quarta, quod servi manibus hodie inclinavit caput. Quinta, quod discipulum furem tulit et proditorem. Sexta, quod sic fuit mansuetus ante iniquum judicem. Septima, quod crucifixoribus tam clemens intercessor fuit apud Patrem. His vestigiis, licet satis a longe, gigantem sequeris, si paupertatem diligis, si inter pauperes extremitatem eligis, si monasterii subditus es disciplinae, si minorem tibi pateris praeesse, si falsos fratres aequanimiter tuleris, si mansuetudine vincas cum judicaris, si charitatem rependas illis a quibus injuste pateris. Haec humilitas plane sine injuria unici Baptismi rebaptizat: quia mortem Christi non iterat, sed mortificationem et sepulturam criminum instaurat; et quod in illo Baptismo gestum est specie, hic rursus impletur veritate. Haec siquidem humilitas coelos aperit, spiritum adoptionis restituit: Pater agnoscit filium, quippe in innocentia et puritate pueri regenerati reformatum. Unde bene de Naaman Scriptura meminit quia restituta est caro ejus, sicut caro pueri parvuli (IV Reg. V, 13, 10 et 14): significans ut arbitror, quia eorum, qui sic se abluunt, non solum corda recuperant innocentiam parvulorum; sed etiam corpora secundum quemdam modum puerilem sibi vindicant puritatem mortificatione membrorum. Sic enim de quodam sanctorum legitur, quia in membris defuncti, septennis quodammodo pueri gratia videbatur (De S. Martino dictum apud Sulpicium). Ecce, fratres, qui primi gratiam Baptismatis tantis voluptatum sordibus amisimus, ecce verum Jordanem, descensum scilicet humilium, ubi die rebaptizari licet, invenimus. Hoc tantum est, ut non parcamus nobis de die in diem descendere profundius, et plenius mergi, Christoque penitus consepeliri. Ipsi gratias agamus, cujus humilitas et formam Baptismi hodie dedicavit credentibus, et gratiam non disparem reservavit poenitentibus. Ipsi gloria per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. And Elisha thinks no differently, but expressly states: 'Wash seven times, and you shall be clean.' For he knew that the humility of Christ, which we must imitate if we wish to be perfectly cleansed, is of a sevenfold virtue. The first virtue: that, though he was rich, he became poor. The second: that, adding the extremity of poverty, he was laid in a manger. The third: that he was subject to his Mother. The fourth: that today he bowed his head to the hands of a servant. The fifth: that he bore a disciple who was a thief and a betrayer. The sixth: that he was so meek before an unjust judge. The seventh: that he was so merciful an intercessor with the Father for those who crucified him. In these footprints, though at quite a distance, you follow the giant, if you love poverty, if among the poor you choose the lowest place, if you are subject to the discipline of the monastery, if you allow one lesser than yourself to be set over you, if you bear false brothers with equanimity, if you conquer by meekness when you are judged, if you repay with charity those at whose hands you suffer unjustly. This humility plainly rebaptizes without injury to the one Baptism, because it does not repeat the death of Christ, but renews the mortification and burial of sins; and what was accomplished in that Baptism as a figure is here fulfilled again in reality. Indeed, this humility opens the heavens, restores the spirit of adoption: the Father recognizes the son, inasmuch as he is reshaped into the innocence and purity of a regenerated child. Hence Scripture rightly records of Naaman that his flesh was restored like the flesh of a little child (IV Reg. V, 13, 10 et 14): signifying, as I judge, that the hearts of those who wash themselves in this way not only recover the innocence of little children, but their bodies too, in a certain childlike manner, claim for themselves a purity through the mortification of the members. For thus it is read of a certain one of the saints, that in the limbs of the deceased there seemed to appear, as it were, the grace of a seven-year-old child. (Said of St. Martin, in Sulpicius.) Behold, brothers, we who lost the grace of the first Baptism through such great filth of pleasures\u2014behold, we have found the true Jordan, namely the descent of the humble, where it is permitted to be rebaptized daily. This alone is needed: that we not spare ourselves from descending more deeply day by day, and being more fully submerged, and being wholly buried together with Christ. Let us give thanks to him, whose humility today both consecrated the form of Baptism for believers, and reserved a grace not unlike it for penitents. To him be glory forever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Epiphany Sermon IV: On Our New Birth and Life", "topics": ["Epiphany", "Baptism", "humility", "monastic life", "Naaman"], "url": "sermons/guerric-epiphany-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:john-baptist", "calendar_label": "The Nativity of St. John the Baptist", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN NATIVITATE S. JOANNIS BAPTISTAE., SERMO I., De laudibus sancti., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 163-167.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint John the Baptist", "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "Jeremiah 1:6", "Luke 16:16", "Matthew 3:8", "Romans 6:19", "Jeremiah 1:10", "Isaiah 9:10", "Isaiah 55:13"], "occasion": "In Nativitate S. Joannis Baptistae", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "occasion_label": "Saint John the Baptist", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric, pleading with Jeremiah that he is a child, praises John the Baptist, who prophesied before he could speak and made his parents prophets. Grace flowed principally into Mary, from her into John and from John into his parents. John stood nearer to Christ than any other mortal and prepared a perfect people for the Lord. Guerric urges the brothers to bring forth fruits worthy of repentance and seeks John's intercession. The sermon appears under the heading for the Nativity of Saint John the Baptist in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN NATIVITATE S. JOANNIS BAPTISTAE."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE NATIVITY OF SAINT JOHN THE BAPTIST.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De laudibus sancti."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On the praises of the saint.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Ah! Domine Deus, ecce nescio loqui, quia puer ego sum (Jerem. I, 6). Fratres, si Jeremias, sicut ex lectione audistis hesterna, ne praedicationis susciperet officium, verecundius quam justius minorem causabatur aetatem, quanto rectius ego sensum causarer minorem? Si sanctum ex utero, et quem manifeste divina mittebat electio, aetas deterrebat infirmior, mihi quae frons esse potest ad loquendum, cui nec ad sanctitatem suffragatur conscientia. nec ad sermonis facultatem suppetit scientia? Illi erat timori pueritia sua, mihi non erit puerilitas mea? Verumtamen tu, puer propheta Altissimi, de quo sermonem hodie nostri debet locus officii, puerilitatem nostram non pateris excusatam haberi, quin saltem gestiamus more puerorum de te quoquomodo balbutire; qui etiam in materia leviori, plena necdum didicimus verba formare. Tuum erit, o vox Verbi, vox sapientiae, tibi devotae solvere vinculum linguae, qui necdum loquens, usum loquendi muto potuisti patri reformare. Nec sine fenore magno rediit intercepta facultas loquendi, cui simul accessit gratia prophetandi, ut videlicet uterque parens aliquid sibi gauderet praerogatum de praerogativa filii."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Ah, Lord God, behold, I know not how to speak, for I am a child\" (Jerem. I, 6). Brethren, if Jeremiah, as you heard from yesterday's reading, pleaded his younger age\u2014more out of modesty than out of right\u2014so as not to take up the office of preaching, how much more rightly might I plead my lesser understanding? If a man holy from the womb, and one whom the divine election was manifestly sending, was deterred by his tender age, what boldness can there be for me to speak, for whom conscience lends no support toward holiness, nor knowledge supplies ability of speech? If his boyhood was a source of fear to him, shall not my childishness be one for me? Yet you, O boy-prophet of the Most High, of whom our office today must speak, do not allow our childishness to excuse us from being at least eager, in the manner of children, to stammer something about you however we can\u2014we who, even in lighter matters, have not yet learned to form complete words. It will be yours, O voice of the Word, voice of Wisdom, to loose the bond of the tongue devoted to you\u2014you who, before you could yet speak, were able to restore to your mute father the use of speech. Nor did the recovered faculty of speech return without great profit, since along with it came also the grace of prophesying, so that each parent might rejoice in having received something as a prerogative from the prerogative of the son.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Qui enim propheta, et plus quam propheta futurus erat, imo ante prophetare quam profari, prius Deum quam se ipsum sentire coeperat, parentes quoque suos prophetas faciebat; et in eos qui in se transfuderant carnis substantiam, spiritus sui et gratiae superabundantiam refundebat. Nam et Elisabeth, postquam exsultavit in gaudio infans in utero ejus, tunc et ipsa prophetavit repleta Spiritu sancto; quia necdum loquens filius, matri tamen occultam Domini praesentiam exsultatione manifesta revelavit. Salutabat Salvatorem motu, quo poterat, et in occursum Domini praecursor impiger erumpere gestiebat. Plane incomparabilis gratia, inaestimabilis divinae virtutis potentia. Vox Mariae dum auribus Elisabeth insonat, ad cor Joannis penetrat, qui abstrusus intra materna latebat viscera, spiritum ejus animat, gaudioque salutari vegetat: et cui virtus naturae vix adhuc totam infuderat animam, virtus vocis Mariae pleniorem infudit prophetiam, adeo ut etiam de plenitudine filii copiose videatur in matrem refundi. Vere gratia plena Maria: manifeste Deus totius gratiae in ea erat, de cujus munificentia tam copiose, tam magnifice, principaliter in Matrem, de Matre in Joannem, de Joanne in parentes gratiae largitas profluebat. Flumina prorsus de ventre Mariae fluebant aquae vivae, et fons vitae et gratiae oriebatur de medio paradisi ad irriganda ligna paradisi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. For he who was to be a prophet, and more than a prophet\u2014indeed, who began to perceive God before he perceived himself, and to prophesy before he could speak\u2014made his own parents prophets as well; and upon those who had transfused into him the substance of their flesh, he poured back the overflow of his spirit and grace. For Elizabeth too, after the infant leapt for joy in her womb, then herself prophesied, filled with the Holy Spirit; because the son, not yet able to speak, nevertheless revealed to his mother by manifest exultation the hidden presence of the Lord. He greeted the Savior by the movement he was able to make, and, as forerunner, longed eagerly to burst forth to meet the Lord. Truly incomparable grace, inestimable power of divine virtue! While the voice of Mary sounded in the ears of Elizabeth, it penetrated to the heart of John, who lay hidden within his mother's womb; it animated his spirit and quickened him with saving joy. And to him, into whom the power of nature had scarcely yet infused a complete soul, the power of Mary's voice infused a fuller prophecy, so much so that from the fullness of the son there seems to have overflowed abundantly even into the mother. Truly \"full of grace\" is Mary: manifestly the God of all grace was in her, from whose munificence the bounty of grace flowed so abundantly, so magnificently\u2014principally into the Mother, from the Mother into John, from John into the parents. Truly rivers of living water flowed from the womb of Mary, and the fountain of life and grace arose from the midst of paradise to water the trees of paradise.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Proxima erat fonti cedrus ista nobilis, Joannem loquor, consobrinum et amicum sponsi, praecursorem, Baptistam, et martyrem Domini: ideoque nimirum uberius irrigata in tantum excrevit, ut inter natos mulierum nihil illa sublimius posset inveniri. Omnino Salvatori proximus erat, qui non solum copula sanguinis conjunctus, aut amicitia intimus erat, sed etiam gloria annuntiationis, novitate nativitatis, sanctitate pene originali, praedicatione consimili, auctoritate baptizandi, virtute patiendi, ante mortales omnes ei propius accedebat. Denique si alia cessarent omnia, si cuncta de eo silerent prophetarum praeconia; solius gratia nominis ejus (9), quod vocatum est ab angelo, priusquam in utero conciperetur, satis abundeque singularem in eo futuram Dei gratiam testabatur. Dignum quippe erat ut gratiam Dei, quam gratia plena profudit, plenus gratia praedicaret, et gratia non mediocri emineret, qui gratiae tempus a tempore legis velut medius limes disterminaret. Usque ad Joannem enim Lex et Prophetae prophetaverunt (Luc. XVI, 16); qui videlicet primus ostendit praesentem, quem Lex et Prophetia promiserant venturum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Nearest to the fountain was this noble cedar\u2014I speak of John, cousin and friend of the Bridegroom, forerunner, Baptist, and martyr of the Lord: and therefore, watered more abundantly, he grew so great that among those born of women none more exalted could be found. He was altogether nearest to the Savior\u2014he who was joined not only by the bond of blood or intimate in friendship, but who also approached nearer to him than all other mortals, in the glory of the annunciation, the novelty of his birth, a holiness almost original, a preaching very similar, authority to baptize, and courage in suffering. In short, if all else were silent, if the proclamations of the prophets said nothing of him; the grace of his name alone (9)\u2014which was given by an angel before he was conceived in the womb\u2014would sufficiently and abundantly testify to the singular grace of God that was to be in him. For it was fitting that he who was full of grace should preach the grace of God which she who was full of grace poured forth, and that he who was to mark the dividing line, as it were, between the time of grace and the time of the law, should himself stand out with no small grace. For \"the Law and the Prophets prophesied until John\" (Luc. XVI, 16); he indeed was the first to show as present the one whom the Law and Prophecy had promised as coming.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Merito igitur nativitas hujus pueri multos gaudere tunc fecit, hodieque facit, qui in senectute parentum natus, mundo senescenti gratiam praedicaturus erat renascendi. Merito nativitatem istam, quam mirabiliter operatur gratia, miraturque natura, solemniter veneratur Ecclesia: praesertim cum singularis illius nativitatis, cujus gratia naturam reparavit, per hanc pignora fidelia sibi videat esse praemissa. Non ingratam se probat Ecclesia, non immemorem: fideliter agnoscit qua devotione, qua gratiarum actione suscipere debeat Praecursorem, per quem ipsum agnovit Salvatorem. Mihi plane novo nascitur gaudio lucerna mundi, cujus indicio lucem veram agnovi lucentem in tenebris, sed non comprehensam a tenebris. Mihi prorsus ineffabili nascitur gaudio, cum quo tot et tanta mundo nascuntur bona; qui videlicet Ecclesiam primus catechizat, per poenitentiam initiat, per baptismum praeparat, praeparatam Christo consignat et copulat; et quam ad vivendum temperanter informat, etiam ad moriendum fortiter propria morte confirmat, atque in his omnibus plebem perfectam Domino parat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Rightly, then, did the birth of this boy cause many to rejoice at that time, and it causes rejoicing today as well\u2014he who, born in the old age of his parents, was to preach to an aging world the grace of being born again. Rightly does the Church solemnly venerate this birth, which grace wrought so wondrously and nature marveled at: especially since she sees in it faithful pledges sent before her of that singular nativity whose grace repaired nature. The Church proves herself neither ungrateful nor forgetful: she faithfully recognizes with what devotion, with what thanksgiving, she ought to receive the Forerunner, through whom she came to know the Savior himself. For me indeed the lamp of the world is born with new joy, by whose sign I have recognized the true light shining in the darkness, yet not overcome by the darkness. For me indeed he is born with ineffable joy, with whom so many and such great goods are born to the world\u2014he who first catechizes the Church, initiates her through penance, prepares her through baptism, and, once prepared, consigns and joins her to Christ; and who forms her to live temperately, and also, by his own death, confirms her to die bravely, and in all these things prepares a perfect people for the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. O fratres, quorum propositum est (utinam et ita desiderium) ad perfectionem festinare; quam cito christianam assequeretur perfectionem, qui huic magistro animum accommodaret docibilem: cujus etiam prima elementa justitiae modum supergressa sunt perfectionis humanae; cujus rudimenta primaevae aetatis gravitatem sapientiae vicere senilis! Qui enim, antequam natus, sanctus fuit, quid mirum si in processu conversationis plus quam sanctus fuit? Mirari possumus, imitari non possumus sanctitatem tuam, sanctorum sanctissime: omnino necesse est ut, qui de publicanis et peccatoribus plebem perfectam Domino parare festinas, humanius eis loquaris quam vivas; et modum perfectionis non secundum vitae formulam tuae, sed secundum virtutem mediocritatis temperes humanae. Facite, inquit, dignos fructus poenitentiae (Matth. III, 8). Nos quidem, o fratres, gloriamur perfectius loqui, quam vivere: Joannes autem sublimius vivens quam possint homines vel intelligere, loquitur tamen eis prout possunt audire. Facite, inquit, fructus dignos poenitentiae Humanum, inquit, dico, propter infirmitatem carnis vestrae (Rom. VI, 19) Si nondum esse potest in vobis omnium plenitudo bonorum, sit saltem vera omnium poenitudo malorum. Si nondum facere praevaletis perfectae fructus justitiae, sit interim haec vestra perfectio, ut faciatis dignos fructus poenitentiae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. O brothers, whose purpose it is (would that the desire were likewise!) to hasten toward perfection: how quickly would that man attain Christian perfection who made his mind teachable to this master\u2014whose very first elements of righteousness surpassed the measure of human perfection; whose rudiments, in his earliest age, outdid the gravity of old men's wisdom! For he who was holy before he was born\u2014what wonder if, in the course of his life, he was more than holy? We can marvel at your holiness, O holiest of the holy ones, but we cannot imitate it. It is altogether necessary that you, who hasten to prepare for the Lord a perfect people out of publicans and sinners, speak to them more mildly than you live, and temper the measure of perfection not according to the pattern of your own life, but according to the capacity of human mediocrity. 'Bring forth,' he says, 'fruits worthy of repentance' (Matth. III, 8). We indeed, O brothers, boast of speaking more perfectly than we live; but John, living more sublimely than men can even understand, nevertheless speaks to them as they are able to hear. 'Bring forth,' he says, 'fruits worthy of repentance. I speak in human terms,' he says, 'because of the weakness of your flesh' (Rom. VI, 19). If the fullness of all good things cannot yet be in you, let there at least be true repentance for all evil things. If you cannot yet manage to produce the fruits of perfect righteousness, let this be your perfection for the present: that you bring forth fruits worthy of repentance.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Si recordari volumus, fratres, lectionis hesternae, ad dignos fructus poenitentiae pertinet quod Jeremiae, aut certe Joanni sub typo Jeremiae dictum est, Ut evellas, et destruas, et disperdas, et dissipes: ad fructus justitiae, ut aedifices et plantes (Jerem. I, 10, et XVIII, 7). Beatus qui manus hujus exstirpatoris adjuverit (nam ipse Dei adjutor est), qui videlicet cooperans verbo et gratiae Dei, in hoc studium et operam dederit ut evellat et disperdat de affectionibus et moribus suis omnem plantationem quam non plantavit Pater coelestis, destruat et dissipet omnem aedificationem Babylonicae superbiae et confusionis; ut postmodum melius aedificet et plantet, fiatque sicut scriptum est, Lateres ceciderunt, sed quadris lapidibus aedificabimus. Sycomoros succiderunt, sed cedros immutabimus (Isa. IX, 10). Ascendat abies pro saliuncula, et myrtus pro urtica (Id. LV, 13): omnis scilicet pulchritudo et gratia virtutum, pro situ et horrore vitiorum. Quis, putas, est ille tam perfectus in nobis, ut saltem hoc initium perfectionis habeat, qui scilicet ita digne perfecteque poenitens sit, ac tota severitate damnet mala sua quae fecit, prioribus usquequaque renuntians vitiis, ut omne germen maledictionis radicitus evellat et disperdat de agro cordis, ita ut de radice pessima, nullus amplius germinet fructus amaritudinis; quique semel ita destruat ac dissipet omnem altitudinem extollentem se adversus humilitatem Christi, ut iterum non reaedificet quae destruxit? Quam felix hodie esset Ecclesia sanctorum, quanta pace et gratia florerent congregationes beatorum pauperum, si fructus istos quos facere debuerat, rudium poenitentia, vel eorum qui perfecti et sancti videri volunt, afferret justitia! Denique, qui nec justitiam sanctorum, nec exactam habemus poenitentiam peccatorum, redimamus vel ex parte aliqua teporem nostrum devota veneratione sanctorum: praecipue beati Joannis, cujus tam magnifice sanctitas effloruit supra modum aliorum, ut crederetur Sanctus sanctorum. Magnificentiam gloriae sanctitatis ejus loquamur ac meditemur, fratres mei, ut propitium faciat peccatis nostris eum, cujus singulariter amicus esse meruit, Filium Dei, qui cum Patre et Spiritu sancto vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. If we wish to recall, brothers, yesterday's reading, it belongs to worthy fruits of repentance what was said to Jeremiah\u2014or indeed to John under the type of Jeremiah\u2014'that you may root out, and pull down, and destroy, and throw down'; and to the fruits of righteousness belongs, 'that you may build and plant' (Jerem. I, 10, et XVIII, 7). Blessed is he who helps the hands of this uprooter (for he himself is God's helper)\u2014namely, one who, cooperating with the word and grace of God, devotes study and effort to this: that he may root out and cast away from his affections and habits every planting which the heavenly Father did not plant, and may destroy and throw down every edifice of Babylonian pride and confusion; so that afterward he may build and plant the better, and it may come to pass as it is written: 'The bricks have fallen, but we will build with squared stones; the sycamores have been cut down, but we will change them for cedars' (Isa. IX, 10). 'Let the fir tree rise up in place of the brier, and the myrtle in place of the nettle' (Id. LV, 13)\u2014that is, let all the beauty and grace of the virtues rise up in place of the squalor and horror of the vices. Who, do you suppose, is so perfect among us that he has even this beginning of perfection\u2014namely, one who repents so worthily and so perfectly, and with total severity condemns the evils he has done, utterly renouncing his former vices, that he roots out and casts away, down to the root, every seed of cursing from the field of his heart, so that from that most evil root no fruit of bitterness may ever sprout again; and who, having once destroyed and thrown down every height that exalts itself against the humility of Christ, does not build up again what he has destroyed? How happy would the Church of the saints be today, with what peace and grace would the congregations of the blessed poor flourish, if the repentance of the untaught, or the righteousness of those who wish to appear perfect and holy, bore those fruits which it ought to have produced In short, since we have neither the righteousness of the saints nor the exact repentance of sinners, let us redeem our lukewarmness, at least in some part, by devout veneration of the saints\u2014especially of blessed John, whose holiness blossomed so magnificently beyond the measure of others that he was believed to be the Holy One of the holy ones. Let us speak of and meditate upon the magnificence of the glory of his holiness, my brothers, so that he may render favorable toward our sins the one whose singular friend he merited to be\u2014the Son of God, who with the Father and the Holy Spirit lives and reigns through all the ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-n009", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(9) Joannes enim gratia Dei interpretatur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-n009", "label": "English", "text": "(9) For John is interpreted as \"the grace of God.\"", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist Sermon I", "topics": ["John the Baptist", "Virgin Mary", "grace", "prophecy", "repentance", "perfection", "intercession"], "url": "sermons/guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:john-baptist", "calendar_label": "The Nativity of St. John the Baptist", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De vi necessaria ad rapiendum regnum coelorum., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 167-169.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint John the Baptist", "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "Matthew 11:12", "Genesis 32:26, 29\u201330", "Hosea 12:4", "Wisdom 7:10", "2 Corinthians 12:3", "Genesis 32:25", "Ecclesiastes 7:24", "Isaiah 64:7", "Jeremiah 20:7", "Proverbs 16 (Septuagint; printed Prov. XVI, sec. LXX)", "Matthew 3:2"], "occasion": "In Nativitate S. Joannis Baptistae", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "occasion_label": "Saint John the Baptist", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads the saying that from the days of John the Baptist the kingdom of heaven suffers violence. Repentance is the force by which sinners seize what was not granted to nature, and the brothers must wrestle without relenting, as Jacob wrestled with the angel. Christ, weakened even to death by love, cannot resist such force. Guerric calls the brothers to magnanimity and constancy under the example of John. The sermon appears under the heading for the Nativity of Saint John the Baptist in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De vi necessaria ad rapiendum regnum coelorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the force necessary to seize the kingdom of heaven.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. A diebus Joannis Baptistae regnum coelorum vim patitur, et violenti diripiunt illud (Matth. XI, 12). Merito igitur laeta nobis est ejus nativitas, cujus tam fausta sunt tempora, ut regnum Dei, jam exinde nobis expositum sit ad diripiendum, quibus utique justitia non sufficiebat ad promerendum. Merito multi in nativitate ejus gaudent, sicut promittebat angelus; sub quo tanta felicitate mutatus est temporum status, ut regnum Dei quod ante nulla innocentium obtinebat justitia, nunc poenitentium invadat et possideat violentia. Quid enim aliud debuit dici poenitentia peccatorum, nisi violentia in regnum coelorum? An non violentia est, rapere virtute quod non erat concessum naturae; ut qui natura erant filii irae et gehennae, labore improbo qui omnia vincit, intrudant se in haereditatem sanctorum et consortium gloriae? An non plane violentus Deo strenuus ille luctator patriarcha Jacob, qui, ut scriptum est, contra Deum fortis fuit et invaluit, qui cum eo luctatus usque mane, constanter et obnixe tenebat rogantem dimitti, Non dimittam te, inquiens, nisi benedixeris mihi? Dico quia cum Deo luctatus est: Deus siquidem erat in angelo, cum quo luctatus est. Alioquin nec angelus diceret, Cur quaeris nomen meum, quod est mirabile? nec Jacob diceret, Vidi Dominum facie ad faciem (Gen. XXXII, 26, 29, 30): sed neque Dominus de Jacob diceret per Osee prophetam, in Bethel locutus est nobiscum (Osee XII, 4). Bona igitur violentia, quae benedictionem extorsit: felix lucta, qua Deus homini succubuit, victusque victorem gratia benedictionis et honore sanctioris nominis muneravit! Quid enim, si tetigit nervum femoris ejus, et emarcuit, ac de caetero claudicavit? Facilis jactura corporis damnumque consolabile, quod tanto compensatum est munere: praesertim illi qui potuit dicere, Super salutem et omnem pulchritudinem dilexi sapientiam (Sap. VII, 10). Utinam marcescat in me non solum nervus femoris, sed virtus totius corporis; dummodo vel unam merear benedictionem angeli. Utinam non solo claudicem cum Jacob, sed et moriar cum Paulo, ut gratiam et nomen Israelis obtineam munere perpetuo. Jacob quidem femur gerit marcidum, Paulus vero, corpus emortuum: quia mortificationem membrorum, quam coepit propheticae religionis initium, ad integrum consummavit Evangelium. Jacob claudicans, quia ex parte quae mundi sunt cogitans, alterum pedem a terra suspensum portat. Paulus cogitans solum quae Dei sunt, sive in corpore, sive extra corpus, nescit, Deus scit (II Cor. XII, 3), totus tamen in spiritu liber ad coelum volat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"From the days of John the Baptist the kingdom of heaven suffers violence, and the violent bear it away\" (Matth. XI, 12). Rightly, then, is his birth joyful for us, since its times are so blessed that the kingdom of God is thenceforth set before us to be seized by force\u2014by us, indeed, for whom righteousness alone would not have sufficed to merit it. Rightly do many rejoice at his nativity, as the angel promised; for under him the state of the times was changed to such happiness that the kingdom of God, which before no righteousness of the innocent could obtain, is now invaded and possessed by the violence of the penitent. For what else ought the repentance of sinners to be called, but violence against the kingdom of heaven? Is it not violence, to seize by force what was not granted to nature; so that those who were by nature children of wrath and of hell, by relentless labor that conquers all things, thrust themselves into the inheritance of the saints and the fellowship of glory? Was not that strenuous wrestler, the patriarch Jacob, plainly violent toward God, who, as it is written, was strong against God and prevailed, who, wrestling with him until morning, held fast, steadfastly and insistently, to him who asked to be let go, saying, \"I will not let thee go, except thou bless me\"? I say that he wrestled with God: for God indeed was in the angel with whom he wrestled. Otherwise the angel would not have said, \"Why dost thou ask my name, which is wonderful?\" nor would Jacob have said, \"I have seen the Lord face to face\" (Gen. XXXII, 26, 29, 30): nor would the Lord have said of Jacob through the prophet Hosea, \"In Bethel he spoke with us\" (Osee XII, 4). Good, then, was the violence that extorted a blessing: happy the struggle, in which God yielded to man, and, being conquered, rewarded the victor with the grace of a blessing and the honor of a holier name! For what matter is it, if he touched the sinew of his thigh, and it withered, and he limped thereafter? Easy is the loss of the body, and consolable the damage, which was compensated by so great a gift: especially for him who could say, \"Above health and all beauty have I loved wisdom\" (Sap. VII, 10). Would that not only the sinew of my thigh might wither in me, but the strength of my whole body, provided only I might merit even one blessing of the angel. Would that I might not only limp with Jacob, but also die with Paul, that I might obtain the grace and name of Israel by an everlasting gift. Jacob indeed bears a withered thigh, but Paul a body as it were dead: because the mortification of the members, which the beginning of prophetic religion first undertook, the Gospel brought to full consummation. Jacob limps, because, thinking in part on the things that are of the world, he carries one foot suspended from the earth. Paul, thinking only on the things that are of God, whether in the body or out of the body, he knows not, God knows (II Cor. XII, 3), yet wholly free in spirit flies to heaven.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Vobis itaque dicimus, fratres, qui aggressi estis coelum rapere, qui congressi estis luctari cum angelo qui viam custodit ligni vitae: vobis, inquam, dicimus omnino necesse esse ut constanter et irremisse luctemini; non dico usque ad enervationem femoris, unde generatio propagatur carnis, sed etiam usque ad mortificationem corporis. Hoc tamen ipsum nec sic labor noster poterit assequi, nisi tactu et beneficio divinae virtutis, cum vestram videlicet constantiam invincibilem sibi probaverit. Sic enim scriptum est: Cumque videret quod eum superare non posset, tetigit nervum femoris ejus, et statim emarcuit! (Gen. XXXII, 25). An non tibi cum angelo, imo cum Deo ipso luctari videris, quando quotidie praeproperis tuis resistit votis? Lavaris velut aquis nivis, ut mundus sis corde et corpore, et ipse sordibus intingit te. Dicis, Sapiens efficiar (Eccle. VII, 24), et ipse longius recedit a te. Clamas ad eum, nec exaudit te: vis accedere ad eum, et repellit te. Decernis rem, et in contrarium tibi cedit, et per omnia fere in duritia manus suae adversatur tibi. O dissimulatrix clementia, quae duritiam te simulas; quanta pietate pugnas adversus eos pro quibus pugnas! Licet enim haec celes in corde tuo, scio tamen quia diligis diligentes te, et immensa multitudo dulcedinis tuae, quam abscondis timentibus te. Noli igitur desperare, constanter age, felix anima, quae cum Deo luctari coepisti: amat utique vim abs te pati, desiderat a te superari. Nam et cum iratus est, manumque ad feriendum extendit, quaerit, ut ipse confitetur, virum similem Moysi, qui resistat sibi; et si non invenerit, conqueritur et dicit: Non est qui consurgat, et teneat me (Isai. LXIV, 7). Nam si implacabilis sit ejus ira, inflexibilisque sententia, flebit et dicet Jeremias qui resistere tentaverat: Fortior fuisti, et invaluisti (Jerem. XX, 7)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. To you, then, we speak, brothers, who have set out to seize heaven, who have come to grapple with the angel who guards the way of the tree of life: to you, I say, we declare that it is altogether necessary that you wrestle steadfastly and without relenting; I do not say to the withering of the thigh, whence the generation of flesh is propagated, but even to the mortification of the body. Yet even this our labor will not be able to attain, unless by the touch and favor of divine power, when he has proved your constancy invincible to himself. For thus it is written: \"And when he saw that he could not overcome him, he touched the sinew of his thigh, and immediately it withered\" (Gen. XXXII, 25). Do you not seem to yourself to wrestle with the angel, nay with God himself, when daily he resists your hasty desires? You wash yourself as it were with the waters of snow, that you may be clean in heart and body, and he dips you in filth. You say, \"I shall become wise\" (Eccle. VII, 24), and he withdraws further from you. You cry out to him, and he does not hear you: you wish to approach him, and he repels you. You determine upon a thing, and it turns out contrary to you, and in almost everything, by the hardness of his hand, he opposes you. O dissembling mercy, that feigns hardness; with how great pity do you fight against those for whom you fight! For although you conceal these things in your heart, I know nevertheless that you love those who love you, and the immense abundance of your sweetness, which you hide for those who fear you. Do not despair, then; act steadfastly, happy soul, who have begun to wrestle with God: he indeed loves to suffer violence from you, he desires to be overcome by you. For even when he is angry, and stretches out his hand to strike, he seeks, as he himself confesses, a man like Moses, who may resist him; and if he does not find one, he complains and says: \"There is none that ariseth, and taketh hold of me\" (Isai. LXIV, 7). For if his anger were implacable, and his sentence inflexible, Jeremiah, who had attempted to resist, will weep and say: \"Thou wast stronger than I, and didst prevail\" (Jerem. XX, 7).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Sed absit, fratres, qui placita ei postulatis, absit ut fortis adversum vos sit, qui pro vobis infirmari usque ad mortem voluit! Tot vulneribus confossus est, toto corpore crucifixus est; unde, quaeso, virtus ei esse potest ad resistendum illi charitati, quae veluti victum et captum per omnia genera infirmitatum usque ad mortem, mortem autem crucis perduxit? Jam non fortis ut mors, sed fortior quam mors dilectio: cum virtute dilectionis infirmata sit usque ad mortem Dei fortitudo; cujus tamen infirmitas fortior inventa est omni fortissimo; cujus mors probata est esse mors tua, o mors. Virtute igitur dilectionis armatus sis, quicunque es ille pius invasor, qui rapere contendis regnum coelorum: et securus esto, quia facile vinces regem ipsum coelorum. Si qua enim videtur tibi adversari difficultate, aut duritia; ne sis pusillanimis, sed intellige qua mente id faciat. Nempe ut contrarietate ipse tibi acuat animum, sicut solet esse natura magnanimorum ac fortium; ut vires exercitet, constantiam probet, multiplicet victorias, augeatque coronas."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But far be it, brothers\u2014you who ask of him what is pleasing\u2014far be it that he should be strong against you, he who was willing to be weakened even unto death for you! He was pierced with so many wounds, he was crucified in his whole body; whence, I ask, can he have strength to resist that love which led him, as though conquered and captured, through every kind of weakness even unto death\u2014death, moreover, of the cross? Now love is not strong as death, but stronger than death: since by the strength of love the strength of God was weakened even unto death; yet his weakness was found stronger than every strongest thing; whose death has proved to be your death, O death. Be armed, therefore, with the strength of love, whoever you are, you devout invader who strive to seize the kingdom of heaven: and be assured that you will easily conquer the King of heaven himself. For if some difficulty or hardness seems to oppose you, do not be faint of heart, but understand with what mind he does this. Surely it is so that by this opposition he may himself sharpen your spirit, as is the nature of the magnanimous and strong; so that he may exercise your strength, test your constancy, multiply your victories, and increase your crowns.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Quamobrem accingimini, filii Israel, et estote filii fortitudinis: non enim opus est nisi magnanimitate et constantia, quae nullis possit terreri adversis. Infirmus dicat, Fortis sum: et prae gaudio spei se nesciat infirmum, cui tam facile jam jamque capiendum est coelum. Plane coelum violenter rapit, qui imbecillitati suae vim facit, aut aetati. Imo, ut magis proprie loquar, plane vim facit propriae perditioni, qui intonante tuba mandati, nescit parcere sibi. Homo enim in laboribus, ut Scriptura ait, laborat sibi, et vim facit perditioni suae (Prov. XVI, sec. LXX). Accingimini, inquam, viri virtutis, et sequimini ducem ac magistrum felicis hujus militiae; Joannem Baptistam loquor, a diebus cujus coelum esse coepit expugnabile. Iste siquidem est, qui velut alter David princeps factus latronum, piorumque ductor praedonum, et victoriosum illum exercitum publicanorum et peccatorum per illam laudabilem ac religiosam violentiam post se induxit in regnum coelorum. Quis enim scelestus aut profanus tubam ejus audivit, Poenitentiam agite, appropinquavit enim regnum coelorum (Matth. III, 2); et non se continuo praeparavit ad bellum? Sequimini, inquam, ducem istum, cujus vexilla proprio rutilant sanguine, cujus hodie virtutes ac triumphos debita decantastis veneratione. Ipse, nisi fallor, quos post se traxerit exemplo, juvabit merito, commendabitque suffragio, cum inter natos mulierum nullus sit acceptior regi summo Jesu Christo Domino nostro, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Wherefore gird yourselves, sons of Israel, and be sons of fortitude: for nothing is needed but magnanimity and constancy, which can be terrified by no adversities. Let the weak man say, I am strong; and for the joy of hope let him not know himself weak, for whom heaven is now so easily, at any moment, to be seized. He plainly seizes heaven by violence who does violence to his own weakness, or to his age. Indeed, to speak more properly, he plainly does violence to his own ruin who, when the trumpet of the command is sounding, does not know how to spare himself. For man, as Scripture says, labors for himself in his labors, and does violence to his own ruin (Prov. XVI, sec. LXX). Gird yourselves, I say, men of valor, and follow the leader and master of this happy warfare\u2014I speak of John the Baptist, from whose days heaven began to be stormable. For he it is who, like another David made prince of robbers, and leader of devout plunderers, led after him into the kingdom of heaven that victorious army of publicans and sinners through that praiseworthy and religious violence. For what wicked or profane man heard his trumpet, 'Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand' (Matth. III, 2), and did not immediately prepare himself for war? Follow, I say, this leader, whose banners gleam red with his own blood, whose virtues and triumphs you have today sung with due veneration. He himself, unless I am mistaken, will aid by his merit and commend by his intercession those whom he has drawn after him by example, since among those born of women none is more acceptable to the highest King, Jesus Christ our Lord, who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints non solo rather than the expected non solum (not only)."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist Sermon II", "topics": ["John the Baptist", "kingdom of heaven", "repentance", "spiritual combat", "perseverance", "love of Christ", "constancy"], "url": "sermons/guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:john-baptist", "calendar_label": "The Nativity of St. John the Baptist", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., Iterum de laudibus Sancti., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 169-173.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint John the Baptist", "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "Matthew 11:11", "Proverbs 27:2", "1 Corinthians 4:4", "Ecclesiastes 9:1\u20132", "Genesis 7:1", "Genesis 17", "Job 1:8", "Numbers 12:6\u20138", "3 Kings 11:4 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Kings)", "Matthew 3:2", "Romans 13:12", "Ephesians 5:14", "Psalm 144:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 88:7 (historical numbering)", "Luke 3:16", "John 3:29\u201330", "2 Corinthians 4:5", "2 Corinthians 10:17\u201318", "Psalm 4:3 (historical numbering)", "Ecclesiastes 3:20", "Philippians 2:3"], "occasion": "In Nativitate S. Joannis Baptistae", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "occasion_label": "Saint John the Baptist", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric takes up Christ's praise that no one born of women is greater than John the Baptist, and sets it beside the praise God gave Noah, Moses and David. John's greatness lay in humility: thought to be the greatest of all, he declared himself unworthy to take off Christ's sandals, and so Christ glorified him. Guerric warns that the proud imitate the devil by envy and detraction, while John lessened himself so that another might appear. The sermon appears under the heading for the Nativity of Saint John the Baptist in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Iterum de laudibus Sancti."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "Again, on the praises of the Saint.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Inter natos mulierum non surrexit major Joanne Baptista (Matth. XI, 11). Salomon ait: Laudent te labia proximi tui (Prov. XXVII, 2). Sed quam felicius et gloriosius, cum laudant quempiam labia Dei sui! Deus quippe nec falli potest, nec adulari. Deus neminem facile laudat, quem laude posse inflari videat, vel quem ex fine debere reprobari praevideat. Homini quidem recte dicitur, ne laudet hominem in vita sua; cujus sicut scire non potest intima, si nec praescire novissima; cum de seipso quoque confiteri habeat: Nihil mihi conscius sum, sed non in hoc justificatus sum (I Cor. IV, 4). Sunt enim justi et sapientes, et opera eorum in manu Dei sunt et tamen nemo scit utrum sit dignus amore an odio, sed omnia in futurum servantur incerta (Eccle. IX, 1, 2). Felix igitur, qui testimonio ipsius Judicis dignum se amore scire meruerit; nisi quod testimonium justitiae praesentis, mutabilitati humanae suspicionem ac metum non adimit de futuris. Indubitabile tamen eximiae virtutis et magnae perfectionis est indicium, quoties hominem adhuc corruptioni subditum, suae suffragio laudis illud irrefragabile Dei dignatur judicium. Magnum profecto justitiae praeconium quod summa justitia dicit ad Noe: Te vidi justum coram me (Gen. VII, 1). Magni quidem insigne meriti, quod Abrahae testificatur Deus, propter ipsum implendas esse promissiones, quae ad ipsum factae sunt (Gen. XVII, et XXII, 17, 18). Quantae autem illud est gloriae, quod de beato Job Dominus gloriatur adversus invidum: Numquid considerasti servum meum Job, quod non sit ei similis in terra: homo simplex et rectus, et timens Deum, ac recedens a malo? (Job. I, 8.) Quantae etiam illud erat gratiae, quod pro Moyse aemulabatur et confundebat aemulos ejus? Si quis, inquit, fuerit inter vos propheta Domini, in visione apparebo ei, vel per somnium loquar ad illum. At non talis servus meus Moyses, qui in omni domo mea fidelissimus est. Ore enim ad os loquor ei, et palam, non per aenigmata videt Deum. Quare ergo non timuistis detrahere servo meo Moysi? (Num. XII, 6-8.) Quis vero in omnibus sicut David, de quo gratulatur Dominus, quia invenit virum secundum cor suum? (III Reg. XI, 4.)"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Among those born of women there has not arisen a greater than John the Baptist (Matth. XI, 11). Solomon says: Let the lips of your neighbor praise you (Prov. XXVII, 2). But how much more blessed and glorious it is when the lips of his God praise someone! For God can neither be deceived nor flattered. God does not easily praise anyone whom he sees could be puffed up by praise, or whom he foresees must in the end be rejected. To a man indeed it is rightly said that he should not praise a man in his lifetime; for just as he cannot know his inmost parts, so he cannot foreknow his final end; since he must also confess of himself: I know nothing against myself, yet I am not thereby justified (I Cor. IV, 4). For there are just and wise men, and their works are in the hand of God, and yet no one knows whether he is worthy of love or of hatred, but all things are kept uncertain for the future (Eccle. IX, 1, 2). Happy, therefore, is he who by the testimony of the Judge himself has deserved to know himself worthy of love; except that the testimony of present righteousness does not remove from human mutability the suspicion and fear concerning things to come. Nevertheless it is an undoubted sign of surpassing virtue and great perfection whenever that unshakable judgment of God deigns to support with its praise a man still subject to corruption. Great indeed is the proclamation of righteousness when supreme Righteousness says to Noah: I have seen you just before me (Gen. VII, 1). Great indeed is the mark of merit when God testifies concerning Abraham, that for his sake the promises made to him would be fulfilled (Gen. XVII, et XXII, 17, 18). But how great is that glory, when the Lord boasts of blessed Job against the envious one: Have you considered my servant Job, that there is none like him on the earth: a simple and upright man, and fearing God, and avoiding evil? (Job. I, 8.) How great also was that grace, that he was jealous on Moses' behalf and confounded his rivals? If there be, he says, a prophet of the Lord among you, I will appear to him in a vision, or speak to him in a dream. But not so my servant Moses, who is most faithful in all my house. For I speak to him mouth to mouth, and openly, not in riddles does he see God. Why then did you not fear to speak against my servant Moses? (Num. XII, 6-8.) But who among all men was like David, of whom the Lord rejoices, because he found a man after his own heart? (III Reg. XI, 4.)", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Verumtamen quantumlibet magni fuerint, sive isti, sive alii; nec inter istos, nec inter alios natos mulierum, teste Nato Virginis, surrexit major Joanne Baptista. Nam etsi stella differat a stella in claritate, et in choro sanctorum siderum, quae ante exortum veri Solis illustrarunt noctem hujus saeculi, quadam effulserunt claritate mirabili; nullum tamen in omnibus majus aut splendidius lucifero isto, lucerna scilicet ardenti et lucenti, quam Pater Christo suo paravit: lucifero, inquam, praelucanae lucis, qui praecursor Solis diem instantem mortalibus nuntiavit; dormientibus in tenebris et umbra mortis clamitans et dicens: Poenitentiam agite; appropinquavit enim regnum coelorum (Matth. III, 2). Ac si diceret: Nox praecessit, dies autem appropinquavit: abjicite opera tenebrarum (Rom. XIII, 12). Surge, qui dormis, et exsurge a mortuis, et illuminabit te Christus (Ephes. V, 14)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Nevertheless, however great these men were, whether these or others; neither among these nor among others born of women, by the testimony of the Son born of the Virgin, has there arisen a greater than John the Baptist. For although star differs from star in brightness, and in the choir of holy stars which before the rising of the true Sun illumined the night of this age, shining with a certain wondrous brightness; yet none among them all was greater or more splendid than this day-star, that is, the lamp burning and shining, which the Father prepared for his Christ: the day-star, I say, of the light before dawn, who as forerunner of the Sun announced to mortals the day now at hand; crying out and saying to those sleeping in darkness and the shadow of death: Repent; for the kingdom of heaven is at hand (Matth. III, 2). As if he said: The night has passed, but the day has drawn near: cast off the works of darkness (Rom. XIII, 12). Awake, you who sleep, and rise from the dead, and Christ shall enlighten you (Ephes. V, 14).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Non est autem negligenter praetereundum, sed etiam atque etiam considerandum, quam sublimis meriti, quam excellentis gratiae et virtutis, oculus ille quem nil fallit, eum perviderit, qui post tanta praecedentium praeconia, hanc de illo sententiam tulit: quod inter natos mulierum, nullus illo major surrexit; nec nisi de ordine Angelorum, vel qui pervenisset ad aequalitatem Angelorum, aliquem ei praeferendum judicavit. Nec semel quidem, aut perfunctorie vel breviter laudem ejus attigit, sed quotiescunque se offerebat occasio sermonis, laudibus ejus delectabatur immorari: quod satis probat historia Evangelii. Propterea Marcus quoque, Lucas et Joannes initia librorum praeconiis ejus dedicaverunt: ut videlicet auctoritas tanti nominis prima fronte praelati, totum deinceps commendabilius redderet Evangelium; et lucerna in ipso ingressu ac vestibulo ardens et lucens perduceret ad lucem, quae lucebat in tenebris, sed a tenebris nequibat comprehendi. Matthaeus enim, quia eum nativitas Domini in initiis occupatum tenuit, mox ut puerum nutriendum Nazareth commendavit, ad Praecursorem et Baptistam ejus stylum convertit, arbitrans imperfectum fore quidquid de Sponso diceret, si de individuo amico Sponsi reticeret. Haec namque cura non minima fuit, non solum Evangelistis narrantibus, sed etiam Prophetis et Angelis praenuntiantibus, ut lucerna Christi, testis Domini talis induceretur, cujus splendore et auctoritate inimici facile confunderentur, et ex cujus magnitudine incomparabilis Altissimi magnitudo manifeste monstraretur. Cum enim maximus inter natos mulierum Natum Virginis testabatur tam longe magnitudinem suam vincere, ut indignum se confiteretur ejus calceamenta portare: quid aliud dabatur intelligi, nisi quia ille erat magnus Dominus, cujus magnitudinis non est finis (Psal. CXLIV, 3); et de quo dicitur: Quoniam quis in nubibus aequabitur Domino, similis erit Deo in filiis Dei? (Psal. LXXXVIII, 7.) Et haec utique illa magnitudo Joannis fuit, qua inter magnos tam magnus excrevit, quia videlicet virtutes suas magnas et innumeras, quibus nulli mortalium secundus fuit, maxima omnium virtutum humilitate cumulavit sibi; cum putaretur summus omnium, ultro ac devotissime praeferens humillimum omnium et in tantum praeferens, ut calceamentis ejus detrahendis se testaretur indignum (Luc. III, 16)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But this must not be passed over carelessly; rather it must be weighed again and again, how sublime the merit, how excellent the grace and virtue, which that eye that nothing deceives beheld in him of whom, after such great praises of those who went before, he pronounced this sentence: that among those born of women none greater than he has arisen; nor did he judge anyone fit to be set above him, except one from the order of Angels, or one who had attained equality with the Angels. Nor did he touch on his praise only once, or in passing, or briefly; but as often as an occasion for speaking presented itself, he delighted to linger over his praises \u2014 as the history of the Gospel sufficiently proves. For this reason Mark also, and Luke, and John dedicated the openings of their books to his praises: so that the authority of so great a name, set forth at the very front, might thereafter render the whole Gospel more commendable; and the lamp, burning and shining right at the entrance and in the vestibule, might lead on to the light which was shining in the darkness, but which the darkness could not grasp. For Matthew, since the nativity of the Lord kept him occupied at the outset, as soon as he had committed the child to Nazareth to be raised, turned his pen to the Forerunner and Baptist of the Lord, judging that whatever he said about the Bridegroom would be incomplete if he kept silent about the Bridegroom's inseparable friend. For this concern was no small one, not only for the Evangelists in their narrating, but also for the Prophets and the Angels in their foretelling: that the lamp of Christ, such a witness of the Lord, should be set forth, by whose splendor and authority enemies might easily be confounded, and from whose greatness the incomparable greatness of the Most High might be plainly displayed. For when the greatest among those born of women testified that the Born of the Virgin so far surpassed his own greatness that he confessed himself unworthy to carry his sandals, what else was one given to understand, except that he was that great Lord, of whose greatness there is no end (Psal. CXLIV, 3); and of whom it is said: For who among the clouds shall be equal to the Lord? who shall be like to God among the sons of God? (Psal. LXXXVIII, 7.). And this indeed was that greatness of John, by which he grew so great among the great: namely, that he crowned his great and countless virtues \u2014 in which he was second to no mortal \u2014 with the greatest of all virtues, humility; so that, though he was thought the highest of all, he freely and most devoutly set before himself the lowliest of all, and preferred him so greatly that he testified himself take off his sandals (Luc. III, 16).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Mirentur igitur alii, quod a Prophetis praenuntiatus; quod ab archangelo promissus, et eodem quo Christus; cum tamen Christus in cubiculo, Joannes in oraculo; quod de tam sanctis ac nobilibus parentibus; quod de senibus et sterilibus, contra debitum naturae, dono gratiae; quod ante sanctus quam natus; ante propheta, quam prophetaret; quod plus quam propheta, quia angelus angelicum officium vitamque gerens in terris, in carne praeter carnem vivens; et licet innocentissimus, poenitentiae tamen formam plus exemplo quam verbo tradens; quod in spiritu et virtute Eliae praecurrens adventum Redemptoris, et viam ei parans in eremo; quod corda patrum ad filios, et filiorum ad patres convertit; quod Filium baptizare, Patrem audire, Spiritum sanctum videre meruit; quod denique usque ad mortem pro veritate certavit: ut etiam ad inferos Christum praecurreret; ante passionem Christi, martyr Christi fuit. Mirentur, inquam, haec alii, si tamen est qui digne possit mirari: nobis, fratres, non solum miranda, sed et imitanda proponitur virtus humilitatis ejus, qua major se credi, cum potuit, noluit; imo credentes ne errarent, quantum valuit compescuit. Fidelis enim amicus Sponsi, magis amator Domini sui, quam sui; se minui, ut ille cresceret optabat, et de minoratione sui gloriam illius augere satagebat (Joan. III, 29, 30), ante Apostolum opere et veritate dicens illud apostolicum: Non nosmetipsos praedicamus, sed Dominum Jesum Christum (II Cor. IV, 5). Ideo nimirum magna est gloria ejus in salutari tuo, in Jesu tuo, Domine, cujus justitiae et bonitatis est diligentes se diligere, est glorificantes se glorificare. Magna prorsus gloria ejus in Jesu, magna a Jesu: qui et apud se glorificavit eum consortio gloriae suae, et apud homines testimonio vocis suae. Persuasum sibi habebat Joannes illud fidele Sapientis consilium: Qui gloriatur, in Domino glorietur: non enim qui se ipsum commendat, ille probatus est, sed quem Deus commendat (II Cor. X, 17, 18). Maluit itaque in Domino sane, quam in seipso vane gloriari; quia et maluit veraciter a Domino, quam fallaciter a seipso commendari. Ideo probatus est Deo et hominibus; et gloria ejus veritas apud homines, felicitas apud Deum: qui si seipsum glorificasset, gloria sua nihil esset."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Let others, then, marvel that he was foretold by the Prophets; that he was promised by an archangel, and by the same archangel as Christ, though Christ was announced in a chamber, John in the sanctuary; that he was born of such holy and noble parents; that he was born of parents old and barren, against the due course of nature, by the gift of grace; that he was holy before he was born; a prophet before he prophesied; that he was more than a prophet, because he was an angel, carrying on an angelic office and life on earth, living in the flesh beyond the flesh; and although most innocent, yet delivering the pattern of penance more by example than by word; that, going before the coming of the Redeemer in the spirit and power of Elijah, he prepared his way in the wilderness; that he turned the hearts of the fathers to the children, and of the children to the fathers; that he merited to baptize the Son, to hear the Father, to see the Holy Spirit; and finally, that he strove even unto death for the truth, so that he went before Christ even into the underworld \u2014 a martyr of Christ before the passion of Christ. Let others, I say, marvel at these things, if indeed there is anyone who can worthily marvel at them: to us, brothers, there is set forth not only to be marveled at, but also to be imitated, the virtue of his humility, by which \u2014 though he could have let himself be believed greater \u2014 he was unwilling; indeed, he restrained, as far as he was able, those who so believed, lest they err. For as the faithful friend of the Bridegroom, loving his Lord more than himself, he desired to be made small that the Bridegroom might grow great, and he strove, by his own diminishment, to increase the Bridegroom's glory (Joan. III, 29, 30), saying by deed and in truth, before the Apostle, that apostolic word: We preach not ourselves, but the Lord Jesus Christ (II Cor. IV, 5). Therefore, no wonder, great is his glory in your salvation, in your Jesus, O Lord, whose justice and goodness it is to love those who love him, to glorify those who glorify him. Great indeed is his glory in Jesus, great from Jesus: who glorified him both with himself, by the fellowship of his own glory, and before men, by the testimony of his own voice. John had taken to heart that faithful counsel of the Wise One: He who glories, let him glory in the Lord; for it is not he who commends himself who is approved, but he whom God commends (II Cor. X, 17, 18). He therefore preferred to glory soundly in the Lord, rather than vainly in himself; for he preferred also to be commended truly by the Lord, rather than falsely by himself. Therefore he was approved by God and by men; and his glory is truth before men, happiness before God \u2014 for if he had glorified himself, his glory would have been nothing.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. O filii hominum, usquequo gravi corde? utquid diligitis vanitatem, et quaeritis mendacium? (Psal. IV, 3.) Utquid vanam et mendacem gloriam diligitis, et gloriam quae a Deo solo est, non vultis? Sed quomodo quaeritis? Utinam agendo fortia, non loquendo sublimia! utinam saltem vera loquendo, licet vane, et non mentiendo plane! utinam denique mentiendo tantum de vobis, non etiam detrahendo de aliis! Hoc jam non est gloriam accipere vel quaerere, sed magis furari et rapere. Quaerere siquidem gloriam, non sua, sed contraria via; non illa scilicet cui debetur gloria, sed contumelia, id est, non virtutis titulo, sed mendacii et detractionis latrocinio: quid aliud est hoc, nisi rapere, vel furari nequiter, quod concupieris turpiter? Certe si gloriam ab homine non quaereres, sed oblatam acciperes, jam ex hoc secundum veritatem non ambulares. Nunc autem quid, cum non solum accipis si offertur, sed et quaeris cum non offertur? quid denique, cum alium velut trucidas venenosa et insidiosa lingua, ut gloriam ejus tibi rapias; ut de malo ejus tu melior, de contemptu ejus tu gloriosior appareas? Sit ergo illud, gloriam scilicet accipere vel quaerere ab homine, insipientium vanitas: quid istud, nisi saevientium crudelitas? Sit certe illud, ut mitius loquar, tentatio humana: quid istud, nisi imitatio diabolica? Plane imitantur diabolum qui sunt ex parte illius, id est, omnes superbi. Ipse est enim rex super omnes filios superbiae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. O sons of men, how long will you be dull of heart? Why do you love vanity and seek after a lie? (Psal. IV, 3.) Why do you love vain and lying glory, and refuse the glory that comes from God alone? But how do you seek it? Would that it were by doing brave deeds, not by speaking lofty words! Would that it were at least by speaking the truth, even if vainly, and not by plainly lying! Would that, finally, it were only by lying about yourselves, and not also by detracting from others! This is no longer to receive or seek glory, but rather to steal and to plunder. For to seek glory by a way not its own but contrary to it \u2014 that is, not by the way to which glory is properly due, but by disgrace; not, that is, by the title of virtue, but by the robbery of lying and detraction \u2014 what else is this but wickedly to seize or steal what you have shamefully coveted? Certainly, if you did not seek glory from man but accepted it when offered, even in this you would not be walking according to truth. But what of it, when you not only accept it if offered, but seek it when it is not offered? What, finally, when you all but murder another with a venomous and treacherous tongue, so as to snatch his glory for yourself \u2014 so that from his disgrace you may appear better, from his contempt more glorious? Let it be, then, that to receive or seek glory from man is the vanity of fools: but what is this other thing, if not the cruelty of the savage? Let the former, to speak more gently, be a human temptation: but what is this latter, if not an imitation of the devil? Plainly those who belong to his party \u2014 that is, all the proud \u2014 imitate the devil. For he is king over all the children of pride.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Quomodo, inquis, imitantur? Diabolus quia superbivit, meliori se, id est, Deo invidit: quia invidit, detraxit. Sic filii superbiae ejus, mox ut infecti sunt vitio ejus, id est amore propriae excellentiae, invident alienae. Ut coeperint invidere, incipient, si possunt, detrahere, ut scilicet accrescat eis quod derogaverint aliis. Quanto salubrius imitarentur humilitatem Joannis, qui detrahebat sibi, ut adderet alii: qui contendebat minor credi quam credebatur, ut alius inciperet apparere quod non putabatur? Denique si non placet humilitas ex sui honestate et aequitate, placeat saltem ex utilitate: quia nulla via rectior, aut facilior ad gloriam divinam; sed nec ad gratiam humanam ulla pulchrior aut justior, et saepe nulla compendiosior: Quanto, inquit, magnus es, humilia te in omnibus, et invenies gratiam coram Deo et hominibus (Eccle. III, 20, et Philipp. II, 3). Haec beatum Joannem glorificavit apud Deum et homines, sicut est dies haec, quae de nativitate ejus gloriosa consolationem praestat mundo, gaudium coelo, gloriam Deo: cui honor et gloria in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. How, you ask, do they imitate him? The devil, because he was proud, envied one better than himself, that is, God; and because he envied, he detracted. So the children of his pride, as soon as they are infected with his vice \u2014 that is, the love of their own excellence \u2014 envy another's excellence. Once they have begun to envy, they will begin, if they can, to detract, so that what they have taken away from others may grow to their own credit. How much more wholesomely would they imitate the humility of John, who detracted from himself in order to add to another; who strove to be thought less than he was thought to be, so that another might begin to appear what he was not thought to be! Finally, if humility does not please by reason of its own honesty and fairness, let it at least please by reason of its usefulness: for there is no path straighter or easier to divine glory; nor is there any way more beautiful or more just \u2014 and often none shorter \u2014 to human favor either. As it is said: However great you are, humble yourself in all things, and you will find favor before God and men (Eccle. III, 20, et Philipp. II, 3). This is what glorified blessed John before God and men, as this day does, which by his glorious nativity grants consolation to the world, joy to heaven, and glory to God \u2014 to whom be honor and glory for ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist Sermon III", "topics": ["John the Baptist", "humility", "glory", "praise", "envy", "pride"], "url": "sermons/guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:john-baptist", "calendar_label": "The Nativity of St. John the Baptist", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO IV., De fortitudine et mortificatione., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 173-176.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint John the Baptist", "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "Matthew 11:7", "Song of Songs 2:16", "Proverbs 8:17", "Isaiah 35:2", "John 1:19\u201320", "John 3:26, 28", "Job 31:18", "Acts 14:21", "1 Timothy 5:5\u20136", "Matthew 11:8", "Revelation 18:7", "4 Kings 6:30 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Kings)", "Proverbs 30:20", "Sirach 5:11\u201312", "Job 1:19", "Job 40:10, 16", "Isaiah 36:7", "Isaiah 24:20", "Psalm 67:31 (historical numbering)", "Wisdom 3:7", "Wisdom 2:2"], "occasion": "In Nativitate S. Joannis Baptistae", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "occasion_label": "Saint John the Baptist", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads Christ's questions to the crowds about John: no reed shaken by the wind, no man in soft garments, but a prophet. John's firmness before Herod, his contempt of bodily comfort and his prophetic grace are set against those who seek ease. Those who cannot match John's rough clothing and food should at least not be reeds in the wind. The sermon appears under the heading for the Nativity of Saint John the Baptist in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De fortitudine et mortificatione."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On fortitude and mortification.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Coepit Jesus dicere ad turbas de Joanne: Quid existis videre in deserto? (Matth. XI, 7.) Ecce illud quod sponsa de sponso gloriatur in Cantico amoris sui, Ego dilecto meo, et dilectus meus mihi (Cantic. II, 16): Joannes Jesu, et Jesus Joanni. Joannes Jesum praedicat, Joannem Jesus commendat. Par pari redditur, et tam amica quam justa vicissitudine charitas in invicem provocatur, aut remuneratur. Ego enim, inquit Jesus, diligentes me diligo, et glorificantes me glorificabo (Prov. VIII, 17). Habet pietas promissionem vitae quae nunc est, et futurae; et Dominus dilectum sibi quem glorificaturus est in futuro redditione praemii, etiam nunc incipit glorificare redditione [Al. exhibitione] praeconii, testemque suum etiam apud homines proprii sublimat honore testimonii. Quid existis, inquit, videre in deserto? Vos hominem miramini commorantem in deserto: sed per istum pinguescent speciosa deserti, et florebit solitudo, cum ubique novi cultores eremi de Joannis succrescent exemplo. Tunc erit desertum quasi deliciae paradisi, et solitudo quasi hortus Domini. Tunc gloria Libani dabitur ei, decor Carmeli et Saron (Isai. XXXV, 2). Sed quid, inquam, existis videre in deserto? arundinem vento agitatam? (Matth. XI, 7.) Et si moretur in desertis, non est tamen arundo deserti, sed cedrus paradisi, columna coeli, gloria generis humani, miraculum mundi, virtute et merito ultra mensuram hominum, conditione citra naturam angelorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Jesus began to say to the crowds concerning John: What did you go out into the desert to see? (Matth. XI, 7.) Behold that which the bride boasts of concerning the bridegroom in the Song of her love: I am my beloved's, and my beloved is mine (Cantic. II, 16): John is Jesus', and Jesus is John's. John preaches Jesus, Jesus commends John. Like is repaid with like, and charity, by so friendly as well as just a reciprocity, is either provoked or rewarded in turn. For Jesus says, I love those who love me, and I will glorify those who glorify me (Prov. VIII, 17). Godliness has the promise of the life that now is, and of that which is to come; and the one dear to him, whom the Lord will glorify in the future by the giving of a reward, he even now begins to glorify by the giving [Other reading: showing] of a proclamation, and he exalts his own witness, even among men, by the honor of his testimony. What, he says, did you go out to see in the desert? You marvel at a man dwelling in the desert: but through him the fair places of the desert shall grow fat, and the solitude shall flourish, when everywhere new cultivators of the wilderness shall spring up after John's example. Then the desert shall be as the delights of paradise, and the solitude as the garden of the Lord. Then the glory of Lebanon shall be given to it, the beauty of Carmel and Sharon (Isai. XXXV, 2). But what, I say, did you go out to see in the desert? A reed shaken by the wind? (Matth. XI, 7.) And though he dwells in the deserts, yet he is not a reed of the desert, but a cedar of paradise, a pillar of heaven, the glory of the human race, the wonder of the world, beyond the measure of men in virtue and merit, yet short of the nature of angels in his condition.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Non est arundo vento agitata, sed palma fortior omni procella, quam nullus turbo quatiat: aut certe cypressus in monte Sion aeternae plantata, altius scilicet quam ut ventorum rabiem timeat. Non est obnoxius tempestatibus hujus aeris, quia superior est cunctis cupiditatibus mundi. In coelo radicem fixit, quo nullus spiritus procellarum aspirat, unde minas et bella ventorum, et cuncta in saeculo adversantia securus irrideat. Irascatur Herodes, insidietur Herodias, de interioribus eorum ebulliat tempestas, ac nequam spiritus procellarum, qui in abyssis cordium eorum cubat, omnes vires suas et argumenta malitiae commoveat: nihil illum terrebit, nihil a sententia movebit, quominus libere nuptias eorum incestas arguat. Quomodo enim flecterent adversa, quem mollire non potuerunt prospera? Flaverunt siquidem aliquando et molles, atque blandientes venti, aurae scilicet favoris popularis: sed nec illis a rectitudine sua potuit inclinari. Vos misistis ad Joannem et testimonium perhibuit veritati. Miserunt enim Judaei sacerdotes et levitas, ut interrogarent eum, Tu quis es? Et confessus est, et non negavit: Quia non sum ego Christus (Joan. I, 19, 20): cum tamen ei flaret favor popularis, et blandiretur existimatio communis, non ut Christum praedicaret se esse, sed ut credentibus tantum non contradiceret. Sed et alias cum discipuli ejus dicerent ei, Rabbi, cui tu testimonium perhibuisti, ecce hic baptizat, et omnes veniunt ad eum: numquid hoc vento potuit agitari, aut de sua desertione, et alterius frequentia et gloria, ad dolorem vel invidiam moveri? Imo constantia immobili ventum repellens et frangens, respondit: Ipsi vos mihi testimonium perhibetis, quia dixerim, Non sum ego Christus, sed quia missus sum ante illum (Joan. III, 26, 28)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. He is not a reed shaken by the wind, but a palm stronger than any storm, which no whirlwind can shake: or rather a cypress planted on the eternal Mount Sion, that is, planted too high to fear the raging of the winds. He is not subject to the storms of this air, because he is above all the lusts of the world. He has fixed his root in heaven, where no spirit of storms breathes, whence he may securely mock the threats and wars of the winds, and all things in the world that are opposed to him. Let Herod rage, let Herodias lay snares, let the storm boil up from their inward parts, and let the wicked spirit of the tempests, which lies in the depths of their hearts, stir up all his powers and the devices of his malice: nothing will terrify him, nothing will move him from his resolve to freely rebuke their incestuous marriage. For how could adversities bend him whom prosperity could not soften? For once gentle and flattering winds also blew\u2014namely the breezes of popular favor: but not even by these could he be bent from his uprightness. You sent to John, and he bore witness to the truth. For the Jews sent priests and Levites to ask him, Who are you? And he confessed, and did not deny: I am not the Christ (Joan. I, 19, 20): although the favor of the people was blowing upon him, and common opinion was flattering him, not that he should preach himself to be the Christ, but that he should merely not contradict those who believed it. But also at another time, when his disciples said to him, Rabbi, he to whom you bore witness, behold, he baptizes, and all come to him: could he be shaken by this wind, or be moved to sorrow or envy at his own forsaking and another's increase and glory? Rather, with unshaken constancy repelling and breaking the wind, he answered: You yourselves bear me witness that I said, I am not the Christ, but that I am sent before him (Joan. III, 26, 28).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Sed quid existis videre? hominem mollibus vestitum? Laudaverat eum de constantia animi, et nunc laudat de contemptu corporis sui, ac postremo de superexcellentia gratiae prophetalis, atque dignitate angelici officii et nominis. His etenim duobus gradibus ad spiritualia ac divina nititur humilitas humana; si videlicet adversus irruentium tentationum ventos immobilis persistat, et desiderio spiritualium bonorum corpus affligat. Sicut enim in initiis, ut vincamus tentationes, corpus est castigandum, ne regnet in eo peccatum: sic etiam victis tentationibus in eodem tamen est persistendum, non solum metu recidendi, sed etiam desiderio proficiendi; ut scilicet de mortificatione carnis spiritus vegetior fiat, et quanto levius ac subtilius vinculum gestat, tanto ad spiritualia liberior assurgat. Joannes qui antequam exiret de ventre, sanctificatus erat, cui illud dicere competebat: Ab infantia mea mecum crevit sanctitas, et de utero matris meae egressa est mecum (Job XXXI, 18); quem nullus tentationis ventus agitare poterat: nec mollibus tamen vestiebatur, nec suavibus vescebatur; sciens utique, quia sicut peccatoribus, ut sancti fiant, sic sanctis, ut sanctiores fiant, necessaria est severior vivendi disciplina. Nunc autem Deo gratias, qui dedit nobis, si tamen dedit, sine pugna victoriam, sine poenitentia veniam, sine opere justitiam, sanctimoniam sine labore, deliciis carnalibus simul et spiritualibus affluere. Induimur, si non purpura et bysso, certe mollioribus et calidioribus quam sint purpura et byssus; et epulamur quotidie splendide: et nihilominus distenti et ructantes crapulam, dormiemus cum Lazaro quondam paupere in sinu Abrahae, vel potius in sinu Christi cum Joanne? Plane si ita est, melius nobiscum actum est, quam cum illis, qui tantis emere laboribus quod nos delicati gratuito possidemus. Plane si ita est, ridenda est magis quam praedicanda vita Joannis: et omnem nationem filiorum Dei, qui dura et arcta via secuti sunt Unicum Patris, reprobare magis expedit, quam imitari."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But what did you go out to see? A man clothed in soft garments? He had praised him for steadfastness of soul, and now he praises him for contempt of his own body, and finally for the surpassing excellence of his prophetic grace, and the dignity of his angelic office and name. For by these two steps human humility strains toward spiritual and divine things: namely, if it stands unmoved against the winds of assailing temptations, and afflicts the body out of desire for spiritual goods. For just as at the beginning, in order to conquer temptations, the body must be chastised, lest sin reign in it, so also, once temptations have been conquered, one must nevertheless persist in the same discipline, not only from fear of falling back but also from desire of advancing\u2014so that, by the mortification of the flesh, the spirit may become more vigorous, and the lighter and finer the bond it bears, the freer it may rise toward spiritual things. John, who was sanctified before he came forth from the womb, to whom it was fitting to say, 'From my infancy holiness grew up with me, and it went forth with me from my mother's womb' (Job XXXI, 18); whom no wind of temptation could stir\u2014yet he was not clothed in soft garments, nor did he feed on delicacies; knowing well that, just as for sinners in order to become holy, so also for the holy in order to become holier, a stricter discipline of life is necessary. But now, thanks be to God, who has given to us\u2014if indeed he has given it\u2014victory without struggle, pardon without penance, righteousness without labor, holiness without toil, so that we may abound in carnal and spiritual delights at once! We are clothed, if not in purple and fine linen, certainly in garments softer and warmer than purple and fine linen; and we feast splendidly every day; and nevertheless, bloated and belching from our surfeit, shall we sleep with Lazarus, once a poor man, in the bosom of Abraham\u2014or rather, in the bosom of Christ with John? Clearly, if this is so, it has gone better for us than for those who had to purchase with such great labors what we, in our softness, possess as a free gift. Clearly, if this is so, the life of John is more to be laughed at than preached, and it is more fitting to reject than to imitate the whole nation of the sons of God who followed the Only-begotten of the Father along a hard and narrow way.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Verisimilius autem videtur quod non ita est; sed magis sicut Veritas dicit, quia per multas tribulationes oportet nos intrare in regnum Dei (Act. XIV, 21): et sicut discipulus Veritatis perhibet, quia vidua in deliciis vivens mortua est (I Tim. V, 5, 6). Ideo Dominus cum de asperitate vestis laudasset amicum in omni domo sua fidelissimum, Ecce, inquit, qui mollibus vestiuntur, in domibus regum sunt (Matth. XI, 8): volens nimirum intelligi, quia qui sectantur delicias carnis, regno hujus saeculi militant, non regno Dei. Heu! quam terribilem sententiam coelitus audivi contra mollitiem illius magnae et purpuratae meretricis! Quantum, inquit, glorificavit se et in deliciis fuit, tantum date illi tormentum et luctum (Apoc. XVIII, 7). Utinam tamen his deliciis, tantum mollitie vestium, et suavitate epularum contenta esset tenuitudo delicatorum, nec alias spinas vitiorum germinaret in eis rhamnus voluptatum, ut hoc esset omne peccatum eorum, deliciari, non luxuriari! Ipsi viderint, si ita exstinctus est in eis ingenitus ille pestifer ignis, ut tot fomentis circumpositis nequeat reaccendi; praesertim ventis ex adverso flantibus, id est, verbis et risibus lascivis tentantibus. Soleo tamen audire quia saepe vivit etiam in moriente, viget in sene: suscitatur et exsurgit etiam sine fomite, furit nullo irritante. Ipsi ergo viderint sibi. Nihil enim mihi de aliorum conscientiis judicare, cum sciam multos in omni copia et gloria mundi temperanter et pudice vixisse: et e diverso nonnullos in habitu asperiore et victu parciore nequiter egisse. Sed et scio regem Israel sub purpura sua cilicio ad carnem fuisse indutum (IV Reg. VI, 30); et panem solum nonnunquam regibus appositum: cum hodie delicatissima quaeque ac mollia tegat pulla vestis poenitentium, vel magis poenitentiam profitentium; et fastidiis eorum non satis sint opes mundi, non satisfaciant artes et tormenta coquorum. Verum quia in hujusmodi causis facile quisque sibi invenit patrocinium; alius, infirmitatis, alius, societatis cui morem gerit; alius, honoris quem gerit: esto, vivant ut volunt, comedant ut volunt; tantum ne excedat ulterius hoc malum, ad id scilicet, quod qui perpetrare volunt, confiteri nolunt, quia tueri non possunt; et incipiant notari proverbio mulieris nequam, quae comedit, et tergens os suum dicit: Non sum operata malum (Prov. XXX, 20). Nam et de magna illa Babylone, de cujus medio delicati poenitentes isti videbantur exisse, audeam dicere: Quantum vult purpurata sit, quantum vult delicata sit; tantum meretrix non sit, nec divaricet pedes suos omni transeunti."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. But it seems more likely that this is not so; rather, as the Truth says, that we must enter the kingdom of God through many tribulations (Act. XIV, 21), and as the disciple of the Truth testifies, that the widow who lives in delights is dead (I Tim. V, 5, 6). Therefore, when the Lord had praised his friend\u2014most faithful in all his house\u2014for the roughness of his garment, he said, 'Behold, those who are clothed in soft garments are in the houses of kings' (Matth. XI, 8): wishing it to be understood, no doubt, that those who pursue the delights of the flesh serve the kingdom of this world, not the kingdom of God. Alas! What a terrible sentence I have heard from heaven against the softness of that great harlot clothed in purple! 'As much,' it says, 'as she glorified herself and lived in delights, so much torment and mourning give to her' (Apoc. XVIII, 7). Would that the frailty of the delicate were content with these delights alone\u2014the softness of garments and the sweetness of feasts\u2014and that the bramble of pleasures did not also germinate other thorns of vice in them, so that this alone might be their whole sin: to take delight, not to indulge in lust! Let them see to it themselves, whether that inborn pestilent fire is so extinguished in them that it cannot be rekindled though so much kindling is heaped around it\u2014especially when winds blow against it from the other side, that is, when lascivious words and laughter tempt it. Yet I am accustomed to hear that it often lives even in the dying man, and thrives in the old man; it is stirred up and rises even without kindling, it rages though nothing incites it. Let them, then, see to it themselves. For it is nothing to me to judge the consciences of others, since I know that many have lived temperately and chastely amid all the abundance and glory of the world, while, on the other hand, some have acted wickedly under a rougher habit and a more frugal diet. But I also know that the king of Israel wore, under his purple, a hairshirt next to his flesh (IV Reg. VI, 30), and that sometimes bread alone was set before kings\u2014whereas today the dark garment of penitents, or rather of those who merely profess penitence, covers the most delicate and soft of things, and the riches of the world are not enough to satisfy their fastidiousness, nor do the arts and torments of cooks suffice. But since in such matters everyone easily finds himself an excuse\u2014one, the excuse of infirmity; another, of the company whose custom he follows; another, of the rank he bears\u2014so be it: let them live as they wish, let them eat as they wish; only let this evil go no further, namely, to the point where those who wish to commit it are unwilling to confess it, because they cannot defend it, and begin to be marked by the proverb of the wicked woman, who eats, and wiping her mouth says, 'I have done no evil' (Prov. XXX, 20). For indeed, concerning that great Babylon, out of whose midst these delicate penitents seem to have come forth, I would dare to say: Let her be clothed in purple as much as she wishes, let her be as delicate as she wishes; only let her not be a harlot, nor spread her legs for every passerby.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Tu ergo si non potes imitari Joannem in asperitate vestitus, vel parcimonia victus: vel in hoc imitari contende, ut non sis arundo vento agitata; et, ut Sapiens ait, Non te ventiles in omnem ventum, et non eas in omnem viam. Sic enim peccator probatur in duplici lingua. Sed esto firmus, inquit, in via Domini (Eccli. V, 11, 12), ne te projiciat ventus a facie terrae seu loci, cui professus es; seu regni, cui praeparandus es. Solet repente ventus vehemens irruere a regione deserti, et concutere quatuor angulos domus filiorum Job (Job I, 19), et fit ruina ejus magna, si tamen super arenam fundata sit, non supra petram; si arundinea sit, non lapidea: imo si is qui aedificat eam, et inhabitat, arundo sit; foris per hypocrisim nitidus, jactantia vel promissione cassa sonorus; intus veritate vacuus. In secreto hujus arundinis Behemoth dormire amat, quae et in locis humentibus (Job XL, 10, 16), ubi nulla fluxarum rerum penuria, sed ad luxum superfluunt omnia, saepius et uberius crescit atque luxuriat (Isai. XXXVI, 7). Huic arundini si quis innitatur, manum ejus perforabit: quia si quis in adjutorium operis eum asciverit, vel alicujus ei muneris functionem imposuerit, sperantem in se gravi scandalo vulnerabit. Patientia tamen Domini hujusmodi arundinem etiam quassatam non conterit, sed sustinet, ut in melius convertatur (Isa. XXIV, 20). Nonnunquam etiam increpat feras arundinis (Psal. LXVII, 31), quae cum principe suo in secreto ejus dormiunt: nonnunquam justi tanquam scintillae aeris candentis in arundineto discurrunt (Sap. III, 7), qui tanquam carbones desolatorii, quidquid in eo steriliter silvescit incendunt, novisque fructibus purum praeparant locum. Hoc agebat Joannes, vox clamantis in deserto, non arundo, sed scintilla in arundineto: cujus sermo, ut scriptum est, sermo scintillae ad commovendum cor (Sap. II, 2) pigrum et stolidum, ut pararet viam Domino. Hoc etiam nunc in nobis agat eadem vox, cooperante Verbo ipso, cujus erat vox, Jesu Christo Domino nostro, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. You, then, if you cannot imitate John in roughness of clothing or frugality of food, strive at least to imitate him in this: that you not be a reed shaken by the wind; and, as the Wise man says, \"Do not winnow yourself to every wind, and do not go in every way.\" For in this way a sinner is proved to have a double tongue. But \"be firm,\" he says, \"in the way of the Lord\" (Eccli. V, 11, 12), lest the wind cast you down from the face of the land or place to which you have professed yourself, or of the kingdom for which you are to be made ready. A violent wind is accustomed to rush in suddenly from the region of the desert and strike the four corners of the house of the sons of Job (Job I, 19), and its ruin is great \u2014 if, that is, it was founded upon sand and not upon rock; if it was made of reed and not of stone; or rather, if the one who builds it and dwells in it is himself a reed \u2014 outwardly sleek through hypocrisy, resounding with boasting or with empty promise, but within empty of truth. In the hiding-place of such a reed Behemoth loves to sleep, a reed that also in moist places (Job XL, 10, 16) \u2014 where there is no lack of transient things, but all things overflow unto luxury \u2014 grows and flourishes more often and more abundantly (Isai. XXXVI, 7). If anyone leans upon this reed, it will pierce his hand: for if anyone takes him on to help in some work, or lays upon him the carrying out of some office, he will wound with grievous scandal the one who trusted in him. Yet the patience of the Lord does not crush even a reed of this kind when it is bruised, but sustains it, that it may be turned to something better (Isa. XXIV, 20). Sometimes, too, he rebukes the beasts of the reed (Psal. LXVII, 31), who sleep with their prince in its hiding-place: and sometimes the just run to and fro in the reed-bed like sparks of glowing bronze (Sap. III, 7), who, like desolating coals, burn up whatever grows there wild and barren, and make ready a clean place for new fruits. This is what John did, the voice of one crying in the desert \u2014 not a reed, but a spark within the reed-bed: whose speech, as it is written, was \"the speech of a spark\" (Sap. II, 2) to stir the sluggish and foolish heart, that he might prepare the way of the Lord. May that same voice now also work this in us, with the cooperation of the Word himself, whose voice it was \u2014 Jesus Christ our Lord, who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist Sermon IV", "topics": ["John the Baptist", "fortitude", "mortification", "steadfastness", "prophecy", "poverty"], "url": "sermons/guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-lent", "calendar_label": "Lent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-lent-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN QUADRAGESIMA., SERMO I., De tribulatione et consolatione., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 91-96.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Lent", "Lent", "Psalm 33:20 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 2:14", "Luke 6:24", "Hebrews 12:5", "2 Timothy 2:12", "Psalm 93:17\u201318 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 14:29\u201331", "Psalm 117:13 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 36:24 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 18:17", "Isaiah 43", "Psalm 10:8 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 64:5", "Psalm 138:16 (historical numbering)", "Joel 3:21", "Isaiah 3:4 (printed locator; quotation from Isaiah 4:4)", "1 Corinthians 3:13, 15", "Daniel 3:49\u201350", "Isaiah 9:5", "Luke 7:47", "Habakkuk 3:16", "Job 6:9\u201312", "Psalm 6:2 (historical numbering)", "Revelation 3:19", "Job 5:17", "Jeremiah 10:24", "Psalm 88:45 (historical numbering)", "Ezekiel 24:12\u201313", "Malachi 3:2\u20133", "Micah 7:8\u201310", "Isaiah 12:1", "Ecclesiastes 9:10", "Galatians 6:10", "Hebrews 4:3", "Isaiah 3:12", "2 Corinthians 1:3\u20134"], "occasion": "In Quadragesima", "occasion_detail": "Lent", "occasion_label": "Lent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric distinguishes sorrow over adversity from sorrow over sin. Consolation, confession and penance lead into his account of purification through love, suffering and the judgment to come. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN QUADRAGESIMA."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "FOR LENT.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De tribulatione et consolatione."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On Tribulation and Consolation.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Benedictus Deus, Pater misericordiarum et Deus totius consolationis, qui consolatur nos in omni tribulatione nostra (II Cor. 1, 3, 4). Multae tribulationes justorum, et de omnibus his liberabit eos Dominus (Psal. XXXIII, 20). Duplex autem genus tribulationum patimur, quia non solum duplici natura subsistimus, carne scilicet ac spiritu, sed et duplici qualitate vivimus; partim scilicet carnaliter, partim spiritualiter. In mundo eramus toti carnales; in coelo erimus toti spirituales: nunc autem ex parte carnales, ex parte spirituales. Prout autem proficimus in spiritu, aut magis efficimur spirituales et minus carnales, aut magis remanemus carnales et minus spirituales. Propterea duplex modo consurgit tribulatio; quia, quod in nobis carnale est, contristat adversitas; quod spirituale, iniquitas. Si nihil in nobis esset carnale, nulla nos contristaret, imo nulla esset adversitas: si nihil spirituale, non nos nostra contristaret, sed magis delectaret iniquitas, sicut de quibusdam dicitur: Laetantur cum male fecerint, et exsultant in rebus pessimis (Prov. II, 14). Illa ergo nostra de adversitate tristitia, carnalis et secundum saeculum est: haec autem de iniquitate, spiritualis et secundum Deum. Illa mortem operatur, si desit consolatio: haec salutem operatur, si non desit consolatio. Multum tamen interest in adversitate exteriori, cujusmodi sit consolatio tristium; quia consolationi divitum comminatio divina vae intentat aeternum (Luc. VI, 24). Quia nimirum plerumque qui humiliari poterant ex adversitate, gravius superbiunt de prosperitate, proditque ex adipe iniquitas eorum: imo et audacter provocant Deum, cum ipse dederit omnia in manibus eorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Blessed be God, the Father of mercies and the God of all comfort, who comforts us in all our tribulation (II Cor. 1, 3, 4). Many are the tribulations of the just, and out of all these the Lord will deliver them (Psal. XXXIII, 20). Now we suffer a twofold kind of tribulation, because we not only subsist in a twofold nature\u2014namely flesh and spirit\u2014but also live by a twofold quality; that is, partly carnally, partly spiritually. In the world we were wholly carnal; in heaven we shall be wholly spiritual: but now we are partly carnal, partly spiritual. But insofar as we advance in the spirit, either we become more spiritual and less carnal, or we remain more carnal and less spiritual. Therefore a twofold tribulation now arises; because adversity grieves what is carnal in us, while iniquity grieves what is spiritual. If there were nothing carnal in us, no adversity would grieve us\u2014indeed, there would be no adversity at all; if there were nothing spiritual, our own iniquity would not grieve us, but would rather delight us, as is said of certain men: 'They are glad when they have done evil, and rejoice in most wicked things' (Prov. II, 14). That sorrow of ours, then, which comes from adversity, is carnal and according to the world; but this sorrow, which comes from iniquity, is spiritual and according to God. The former works death, if consolation is lacking; the latter works salvation, if consolation is not lacking. Yet in outward adversity it matters greatly what kind of consolation the sorrowful have; for upon the consolation of the rich the divine threat holds out eternal woe (Luc. VI, 24). For indeed it often happens that those who could have been humbled by adversity grow more gravely proud through prosperity, and their iniquity comes forth from their fatness: nay, they even boldly provoke God, though he himself has given all things into their hands.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Nos autem et cum abundat illa consolatio temporalis, oportet esse gratiores et humiliores: et cum deficit, de aeterna redemptione certiores et laetiores. Cum tamen suppetit sive salus corporum, sive tranquillitas temporum aut copia rerum; ea moderatione utenda ac dispensanda est, ut sit non occasio peccati, sed adjutorium virtutis, ac potius de bonis bona faciamus quam de bonis mali fiamus. Illa namque consolatione, qua se diffundit voluptas, vel extollit vanitas, renuat consolari anima mea (Hebr. XII, 5); magisque eligat cum Moyse et viris Israeliticis affligi, quam temporalis peccati habere jucunditatem. Sive autem consolemur exterius, sive tribulemur, benedictus Deus, qui consolationem internam et aeternam dedit in cordibus nostris, id est, gaudium spei, quae suadet nos etiam gloriari in tribulationibus nostris, pollicens, quia si compatimur, et conregnabimus (II Tim. II, 12). Illam sane tribulationem, quam interius de iniquitate nostra patimur, tanto molestiorem portamus, quanto periculosiorem experimur; quia nimirum quotidie de vita periclitamur, ut veraciter confiteamur: Nisi quia Dominus adjuvit me, paulo minus habitasset in inferno anima mea. Gratias tibi, Domine Jesu, quia si gravia sunt pericula, facilia sunt remedia; quia, si dicebam, Motus est pes meus; misericordia tua, Domine, adjuvabat me (Psal. XCIII, 17, 18). Et quod semel in Petro figurasti corporaliter (Matth. XIV, 29-31), in filiis Petri quotidie adimples spiritualiter. Jussisti nos venire ad te super aquas, id est, hoc mare magnum et spatiosum manibus: ecce virtute tua ambulamus, sed pondere nostro gravamur, et plerumque irruente spiritu procellarum impellimur, ut etiam mergi incipiamus, id est, pene jam jamque tentatori consentiamus. Sed, si celeriter confiteamur pedem motum, id est, titubasse animum, et fideliter invocemus auxilium, tu misericorditer porrigis manum, firmasque et dirigis gressum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But we, when that temporal consolation abounds, ought to be the more grateful and the more humble; and when it fails, the more certain and the more joyful concerning the eternal redemption. Yet when there is at hand either health of body, or tranquility of the times, or abundance of goods, it must be used and administered with such moderation, that it may be not an occasion of sin but a help to virtue, and that we may rather make good things out of goods than become evil men because of goods. For let my soul refuse to be consoled by that consolation in which pleasure spreads itself abroad, or vanity exalts itself (Hebr. XII, 5); and let it rather choose to be afflicted with Moses and the men of Israel, than to have the temporal delight of sin. But whether we be consoled outwardly or be afflicted, blessed be God, who has given an inward and eternal consolation in our hearts, that is, the joy of hope, which persuades us even to glory in our tribulations, promising that if we suffer with him, we shall also reign with him (II Tim. II, 12). That tribulation indeed, which we suffer inwardly from our own iniquity, we bear it as all the more troublesome, the more dangerous we find it to be; for indeed daily we are in peril of our life, so that we may truly confess: 'Unless the Lord had helped me, my soul had almost dwelt in hell.' Thanks be to thee, Lord Jesus, for though the dangers are grievous, the remedies are easy; for, 'if I said: My foot is moved; thy mercy, O Lord, assisted me' (Psal. XCIII, 17, 18). And that which thou didst once figure bodily in Peter (Matth. XIV, 29-31), thou dost daily fulfill spiritually in the sons of Peter. Thou hast bidden us come to thee upon the waters, that is, upon this great and wide sea: behold, by thy power we walk, but we are weighed down by our own weight, and often, when the spirit of storms rushes in, we are driven on so that we even begin to sink\u2014that is, we are almost on the very point of consenting to the tempter. But if we quickly confess that our foot is moved, that is, that our soul has wavered, and faithfully call upon thy help, thou dost mercifully stretch forth thy hand, and dost steady and direct our step.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Super hoc quotidie justus decantat: Impulsus sum, ut caderem, sed non cecidi, quia Dominus suscepit me (Psal. CXVII, 13). Sed, cum ceciderit non collidetur, quia Dominus supponit manum suam (Psal. XXXVI, 24): id est, cum peccaverit, non condemnabitur, quia advocatum habet Jesum, eo quod etsi septies in die cadit, septies resurgit. Non delectatur jacere ubi cadit, et in luto volutari; sed ocius exsurgens excutit se de pulvere, et sordes diluit satisfactione. Per hoc namque justus fit, quod semper in initio sermonis accusator est sui (Prov. XVIII, 17), memor consilii divini, quo dictum est ei: Dic iniquitates tuas, ut justificeris (Isai. XLIII, sec. LXX). Ita fit ut, dum seipsum judicat, et Deum de se vindicat, illum quem judicem metuebat justum, reus advocatum habeat. Justus enim Dominus, et justitias dilexit (Psal. X, 8); nec potest injustas causas suscipere aut tueri, qui, cum acceperit tempus, etiam justitias judicabit. Et tamen qui judicem se minatur futurum superbe de justitia praesumentium, advocatum se pollicetur humiliter peccata confitentium; quoniam apud illum, in cujus conspectu non justificabitur omnis homo, nullatenus causam testificari nostram melius possumus, quam si nosmetipsos accusantes et punientes, justitiam ipsius in nos transferamus, et partes judicis adversus reos exsequamur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Concerning this the just man daily sings: 'I was thrust, that I might fall, but I did not fall, because the Lord upheld me' (Psal. CXVII, 13). 'But when he shall fall, he shall not be bruised, because the Lord puts his hand under him' (Psal. XXXVI, 24): that is, when he has sinned, he will not be condemned, because he has Jesus as his advocate, for although he falls seven times a day, he rises seven times. He does not delight to lie where he falls, and to wallow in the mire; but rising up quickly, he shakes the dust from himself, and washes away his filth by satisfaction. For by this a man becomes just, because at the very outset of his speech he is always his own accuser (Prov. XVIII, 17), remembering the divine counsel by which it was said to him: 'Declare thy iniquities, that thou mayest be justified' (Isai. XLIII, sec. LXX). Thus it comes about that, while he judges himself and vindicates God's justice against himself, the guilty man has as his advocate him whom, as judge, he feared to be just. For 'the Lord is just, and he has loved justice' (Psal. X, 8); nor can he who, when he shall have received the appointed time, will judge even deeds of justice, take up or defend unjust causes. And yet he who threatens that he will be a judge to those who presume proudly upon their own justice, promises that he will be an advocate to those who humbly confess their sins; since before him, in whose sight no man shall be justified, we can in no way better bear witness to our cause than if, accusing and punishing ourselves, we transfer his justice onto ourselves, and carry out the part of a judge against the guilty.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Sed etsi in aliquo veniali vel per ignorantiam fallimur, vel per infirmitatem vincimur, ut Jebusaeum nostrum nequaquam exterminare praevaleamus, sed semper sub oculis nostris in terra nobiscum habitare patiamur; in hac quoque imperfectione nostra consolatur nos sermo propheticus, qui nos in se transfigurans ait: In ipsis fuimus semper et salvabimur (Isai. LXIV, 5). Imperfectum meum, inquit corpus Christi, viderunt misericordes oculi tui, et in libro tuo omnes scribentur (Psal. CXXXVIII, 16); id est, scripti invenientur tam perfecti quam imperfecti. Pollicetur siquidem Dominus se perfecturum imperfectionem nostram, loquens per Joelem prophetam: Et mundabo, inquit, sanguinem eorum quem non mundaverunt (Joel III, 21). Hinc Isaias, cum praemisisset de perfectione futurae coelestis Jerusalem: Et erit, omnis qui relictus fuerit in Sion, et residuus in Jerusalem, sanctus vocabitur omnis qui scriptus est in vita in Jerusalem: quod in infirmis nonnisi purgatione divina perficienda sit sanctitas illa, subjunxit, dicens: Si abluerit Dominus sordem filiarum Sion, et sanguinem laverit de medio Jerusalem. Consolatur quidem me lavatio quae promittitur; sed terret modus qui subditur: Spiritu, inquit, judicii, et spiritu ardoris (Isai. III, 4). Ignis enim opus cujusque probabit. Si cujus opus arserit, detrimentum patietur: ipse autem salvus fiet merito fundamenti; sic tamen quasi per ignem (I Cor. III, 13, 15)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. But even if in some venial matter we are deceived through ignorance, or overcome through weakness, so that we are by no means able to exterminate our Jebusite, but always allow him to dwell with us in the land before our eyes; in this imperfection of ours too the prophetic word consoles us, which, transfiguring us into itself, says: \"In these we have always been, and we shall be saved\" (Isai. LXIV, 5). \"My imperfection,\" says the body of Christ, \"your merciful eyes have seen, and in your book all shall be written\" (Psal. CXXXVIII, 16); that is, both the perfect and the imperfect will be found written there. For the Lord promises that he will perfect our imperfection, speaking through the prophet Joel: \"And I will cleanse,\" he says, \"their blood which they have not cleansed\" (Joel III, 21). Hence Isaiah, having first said concerning the perfection of the future heavenly Jerusalem: \"And it shall come to pass, that everyone who is left in Sion, and remains in Jerusalem, shall be called holy, everyone who is written among the living in Jerusalem\" \u2014 because in the weak that holiness cannot be perfected except by divine purification, he added, saying: \"If the Lord shall wash away the filth of the daughters of Sion, and shall wash the blood from the midst of Jerusalem.\" The washing that is promised indeed consoles me; but the manner that is added terrifies me: \"By the spirit,\" he says, \"of judgment, and the spirit of burning\" (Isai. III, 4). For fire will test the work of each one. If anyone's work shall burn, he shall suffer loss; but he himself shall be saved on account of the foundation, yet so as by fire (I Cor. III, 13, 15).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Quanto melius nunc arderemus delectabili amore, quam tunc illo poenali ardore? quanto suavius nunc igne amoris purgaremur, ut mundanum in nobis non relinqueretur quod mundi incendio obnoxium inveniretur, sed, sicut tribus pueris ventus roris fieret nobis fornax Babylonis? (Dan. III, 49, 50.) Alioquin, si tepide et negligenter viventes, ligna, fenum, stipulam nobis coacervamus, et tantam ignium materiam hinc nobiscum portamus; cum examen illius purgatorii tales intraverimus, quis poterit de nobis habitare cum igne devorante? aut quis habitabit ex nobis cum ardoribus sempiternis, quando omne etiam vestimentum mistum sanguine erit in combustionem et cibus ignis? (Isai. IX, 5.) Scio quidem quia, si satis arderem igne charitatis, de me quoque diceretur: Dimittuntur ei peccata multa, quoniam dilexit multum (Luc. VII, 47). Nunc autem, si purgari non mereor igne dilectionis, utinam vel purgarer alicujus igne passionis, fieretque mihi quod propheta imprecatur sibi: Ingrediatur putredo in ossibus meis, et subter me scateat, ut requiescam in die tribulationis (Habac. III, 16). Utinam qui coepit, ipse me conterat; solvat manum suam, et succidat me: et haec mihi sit consolatio, ut affligens me dolore non parcat. Verumtamen nosti, Domine, infirmitatem figmenti mei. Quae est enim fortitudo mea, ut sustineam? aut quis finis meus, ut patienter agam? Nec fortitudo lapidum fortitudo mea, nec caro mea aenea est (Job VI, 9-12). Hoc primum quidem peto, Domine, ne in furore tuo arguas me, neque in ira tua corripias me (Psal. VI, 2). Sed, quoniam audio abs te: Ego quos amo, arguo et castigo (Apoc. III, 19); et: Beatus homo qui corripitur a Domino (Job V, 17): corripe me, Domine, in misericordia, et non in furore tuo, ne forte ad nihilum redigas me (Jerem. X, 24), et dicatur de me: Detraxisti eum ab emundatione (Psal. LXXXVIII, 45). Intuere infirmitatem meam, et secundum illam manum tuam moderare, ne vehementia verberis frangat patientiam cordis; sed magis tribulatio patientiam operetur, patientia probationem, probatio spem. Sed vae mihi, si quotidie uror, et non emundor, ut et de me dici contingat: Multo labore sudatum est, et non exivit de anima rubigo ejus, neque per ignem. Immunditia tua exsecrabilis, quia mundare te volui, et non es mundata a sordibus tuis; sed nec mundaberis prius, donec quiescere faciam indignationem meam in te (Ezech. XXIV, 12, 13)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. How much better would it be for us to burn now with delightful love, than then with that punishing fire? How much more sweetly would we be purged now by the fire of love, so that nothing worldly would be left in us found liable to the world's burning, but, as for the three youths, the furnace of Babylon would become for us a wind of dew? (Dan. III, 49, 50.) Otherwise, if living tepidly and negligently we heap up for ourselves wood, hay, and straw, and carry with us from here so much material for fires; when we enter that purgatorial trial being such as this, who among us will be able to dwell with the devouring fire? Or who of us will dwell with the everlasting burnings, when every garment too, mingled with blood, shall be for burning and food for the fire? (Isai. IX, 5.) I know indeed that, if I burned sufficiently with the fire of charity, it would also be said of me: \"Many sins are forgiven her, because she loved much\" (Luc. VII, 47). But now, if I do not merit to be purged by the fire of love, would that I might at least be purged by the fire of some suffering, and that there might happen to me what the prophet calls down upon himself: \"Let rottenness enter into my bones, and swarm beneath me, that I may rest in the day of tribulation\" (Habac. III, 16). Would that he who has begun would himself crush me; would that he would loosen his hand and cut me off; and let this be my consolation, that in afflicting me with sorrow he would not spare me. Yet you know, Lord, the weakness of my frame. For what is my strength, that I should endure? Or what is my end, that I should act patiently? Neither is my strength the strength of stones, nor is my flesh of bronze (Job VI, 9-12). This first indeed I ask, Lord: rebuke me not in your fury, neither chastise me in your wrath (Psal. VI, 2). But since I hear from you: \"Those whom I love, I rebuke and chastise\" (Apoc. III, 19); and: \"Blessed is the man whom the Lord corrects\" (Job V, 17): correct me, Lord, in mercy, and not in your fury, lest perhaps you reduce me to nothing (Jerem. X, 24), and it be said of me: \"You have cut him off from cleansing\" (Psal. LXXXVIII, 45). Look upon my weakness, and moderate your hand according to it, lest the violence of the blow break the patience of my heart; but rather let tribulation work patience, patience proof, and proof hope. But woe to me, if I am burned daily and am not cleansed, so that it should happen to be said of me too: \"Much labor has been expended, and its rust has not gone out of the soul, not even by fire. Your filthiness is execrable, because I wished to cleanse you, and you have not been cleansed from your filth; nor shall you be cleansed, until I have caused my indignation to rest upon you\" (Ezech. XXIV, 12, 13).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Quam vereor ne id cadat in nos, qui districtione vitae, velut quodam purgatorio igne, quotidie coquimur, et nunquam ab iniquitate nostra, sicut oportet, mundamur! et forsitan non mundabimur, donec sedeat Judex ipse quasi ignis conflans et emundans argentum, et purgabit filios Levi (Malach. III, 2, 3). Verumtamen, quantulumcunque proficiat nunc iste ignis, malo tamen interim mihi hunc adhiberi, quam ad illum penitus differri. Nam, si ad invicem comparentur, iste quidem medicina est misericordis, ille ultio irati. Ita sentiebat, qui dicebat: Domine, ne in furore tuo arguas me, neque in ira tua corripias me (Psal. VI, 2). Scio quidem quia tunc etiam, cum conturbata fuerit anima mea, Domine, in ira misericordiae memor eris. Scio quia etiam in luctu et poenis consolarer me spe meliori: et inimicae potestati, cui emendandus ad tempus traditus essem, verbis prophetae responderem, dicens: Ne laeteris, inimica mea, super me, quia cecidi: consurgam, cum sedero in tenebris; Dominus lux mea est. Iram Domini portabo, quia peccavi ei, donec judicet causam meam. Educet me in lucem, videbo justitiam ejus: et aspiciet me inimica mea, et operietur confusione (Mich. VII, 8-10). Et dicam in illa die: Confitebor tibi, Domine, quoniam iratus es mihi: conversus est furor tuus, et consolatus es me (Isai. XII, 1). Verumtamen cur timebo in die mala? Cur non magis in die bona bonis fruor, et diem malam praecaveo? Nonne ecce nunc tempus acceptabile, ecce nunc dies salutis? Cur non audiam modo consilium Sapientis: Quodcunque potest manus tua instanter operare: quia non est consilium, nec ratio, nec sapientia apud inferos quo tu properas (Eccle. IX, 10); et illud Apostoli: Dum tempus habemus, operemur bonum ad omnes? (Galat. VI, 10.) Coeperamus quidem a consolationibus, sed ad terrores devenimus; quia tunc nimirum consolatio utilis erit, si terrori mista fuerit; tunc ingrediemur in veritate tua (Hebr. IV, 3), Domine, si ita laetatur cor nostrum, ut timeat nomen tuum. Alioquin, si omnino vos securos faceremus, diceretur et de nobis: Popule meus, qui beatum te dicunt, ipsi te decipiunt, et vestigia gressuum tuorum dissipant (Isai. III, 12). Tunc autem vere beati erimus, si miseriam nostram recte agnoscamus et pie doleamus: tunc securi in morte erimus, si semper pavidi suspectique vivamus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. How I fear that this may fall upon us, who are daily cooked, as it were by a kind of purgatorial fire, through the strictness of our life, and yet are never cleansed from our iniquity as we ought to be! And perhaps we shall not be cleansed until the Judge himself sits like a refiner's fire, purifying silver, and shall purge the sons of Levi (Malach. III, 2, 3). Nevertheless, however little this fire now profits, I would rather in the meantime have this fire applied to me than be put off entirely until that other one. For if the two be compared with one another, this one is the medicine of a merciful God, that one the vengeance of an angry God. So thought he who said: Lord, rebuke me not in thy fury, nor chastise me in thy wrath (Psal. VI, 2). Indeed I know that even then, when my soul shall be troubled, Lord, in wrath thou wilt remember mercy. I know that even in mourning and punishments I would console myself with better hope, and to the hostile power, to which I would be handed over for a time to be corrected, I would answer in the words of the prophet, saying: Rejoice not, my enemy, over me, because I have fallen; I shall rise again when I sit in darkness; the Lord is my light. I will bear the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him, until he judge my cause. He will bring me forth into the light, I shall see his justice; and my enemy shall look upon me, and shall be covered with confusion (Mich. VII, 8-10). And I will say in that day: I will give thanks to thee, O Lord, for thou wast angry with me: thy wrath is turned away, and thou hast comforted me (Isai. XII, 1). But why should I fear in the evil day? Why should I not rather enjoy good things in the good day, and take precaution against the evil day? Is it not now the acceptable time, is it not now the day of salvation? Why should I not now heed the counsel of the Wise Man: Whatsoever thy hand is able to do, do it earnestly: for neither counsel, nor reason, nor wisdom shall be in hell, whither thou art hastening (Eccle. IX, 10); and that saying of the Apostle: While we have time, let us do good to all men (Galat. VI, 10.). We began indeed with consolations, but we have come round to terrors; because surely consolation will be profitable only if it be mingled with terror; then shall we enter into thy truth (Hebr. IV, 3), O Lord, if our heart so rejoices that it fears thy name. Otherwise, if we were to make you altogether secure, it would be said of us too: O my people, they that call thee blessed, the same deceive thee, and destroy the way of thy steps (Isai. III, 12). But then shall we truly be blessed, if we rightly acknowledge our wretchedness and devoutly grieve over it; then shall we be secure in death, if we live always fearful and watchful.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The quoted spirit of judgment and burning comes from Isaiah 4:4; the edition prints Isai. III, 4."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Lent Sermon I", "topics": ["Lent", "tribulation", "consolation", "repentance", "confession", "penance", "purgation", "purgatory", "hope", "judgment"], "url": "sermons/guerric-lent-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-lent", "calendar_label": "Lent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-lent-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., SABBATO HEBDOMADAE QUADRAGESIMAE. De parabola filii prodigi. (Luc. XV, 11-32.), in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 96-100.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Lent", "Saturday in Lent; week unspecified", "Luke 15:11\u201332", "Psalm 31:5 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 5:5", "Isaiah 44:6 (printed locator; quotation from Isaiah 64:6)"], "occasion": "Sabbato hebdomadae Quadragesimae", "occasion_detail": "Saturday in Lent; week unspecified", "occasion_label": "Lent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "The father in the parable of the prodigal son receives the penitent before his confession is complete. Guerric considers the abundance of mercy, union with God and the humility that sustains the virtues. The 1879 edition places the sermon on a Saturday in Lent without specifying the week. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SABBATO HEBDOMADAE QUADRAGESIMAE. De parabola filii prodigi. (Luc. XV, 11-32.)"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "ON SATURDAY OF THE WEEK OF LENT. On the parable of the prodigal son. (Luc. XV, 11-32.)", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. O Felix humilitas poenitentium! o beata spes confitentium! quam potens es apud omnipotentem, quam facile vincis invincibilem, quam cito tremendum judicem convertis in piissimum patrem! Prodigus iste filius, cujus hodie in magnam aedificationem nostram audivimus peregrinationem aerumnosam, poenitentiam lacrymosam, susceptionem gloriosam: prodigus, inquam, iste tam graviter reus, nondum confessus erat, sed tantum confiteri deliberaverat; nondum satisfecerat, sed tantum ad satisfaciendum animum inclinaverat: et de solo fere proposito conceptae humilitatis veniam statim obtinuit, quae tanto tempore tantis expetitur votis, imploratur lacrymis, ambitur obsequiis. Latronem in cruce sola confessio absolvit; istum, sola voluntas confitendi. Dixi, inquit, Confitebor adversum me injustitiam meam Domino: et tu remisisti impietatem peccati mei (Psal. XXXI, 5): ubique misericordia praevenit. Praevenerat voluntatem confessionis, ipsam inspirando: praevenit et vocem confessionis, quod confitendum erat indulgendo. Cum adhuc, inquit, longe esset, vidit illum pater ipsius, et misericordia motus est; et accurrens cecidit super collum ejus, et osculatus est eum. Quantum haec verba sonare videntur, tardius videbatur patri filio veniam dedisse, quam illi accepisse. Sic festinabat absolvere reum a tormento conscientiae suae, quasi plus cruciaret misericordem compassio miseri, quam ipsum miserum passio sui. Neque hoc dicimus quo in natura incommutabili humanos ponamus affectus, sed ut in amorem illius summae bonitatis affectus noster dulcescat; dum ipsam plus nos amare, quam ipsi nos amemus, ex similitudine discimus humana."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. O happy humility of penitents! O blessed hope of those who confess! How powerful you are before the Almighty, how easily you conquer the unconquerable, how swiftly you turn the dreadful Judge into the most loving Father! That prodigal son, whose grievous wandering, tearful repentance, and glorious welcome we heard today to our great edification: that prodigal, I say, so gravely guilty, had not yet confessed, but had only resolved to confess; he had not yet made satisfaction, but had only inclined his mind toward making satisfaction\u2014and from the mere purpose of a humility now conceived, he at once obtained the pardon which is sought for so long with such great prayers, implored with tears, and courted with services. The thief on the cross was absolved by confession alone; this man, by the mere will to confess. \"I said,\" he says, \"I will confess against myself my injustice to the Lord: and thou hast forgiven the wickedness of my sin\" (Psal. XXXI, 5): everywhere mercy goes before. It had gone before the will to confess, by inspiring it itself; it went before the voice of confession too, by pardoning what was to be confessed. \"When he was yet a great way off,\" it says, \"his father saw him, and was moved with compassion; and running to him, fell on his neck, and kissed him.\" As far as these words seem to suggest, it seemed the father was slower to give pardon to the son than the son was to receive it! So he hastened to absolve the guilty one from the torment of his conscience, as though the compassion of the merciful one tormented him more than the suffering of his own wretchedness tormented the wretched one himself. We do not say this so as to place human feelings within the unchangeable nature of God, but so that our affection may grow sweet toward the love of that highest goodness, while from this human likeness we learn that he loves us more than we love ourselves.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Vide autem quomodo ubi abundavit delictum, superabundet gratia. Reus vix poterat sperare veniam; Judex, imo non jam judex, sed advocatus, accumulat gratiam. Cito, inquit, proferte stolam primam, et induite illum, et date annulum in manu ejus, et calceamenta in pedes; et adducite vitulum saginatum, et occidite, et manducemus et epulemur: quia hic filius meus mortuus erat, et revixit. Verum ut haec omnia praetereamus, scilicet stolam primam, id est sanctificationem spiritus, qua baptizatus induitur, et poenitens reinduitur; annulum fidei, quo subarrhatur; calceamenta, quibus ad calcanda serpentum venena munitur, vel ad evangelizandum praeparatur; vitulum saginatum, qui ei in altari immolatur; festiva illa gaudia, quae, pro recepto indicta filio, toto celebrantur coelo: ut haec, inquam, omnia taceamus, et doctioribus tractanda servemus; amplexus ille et osculum paternae pietatis, quid non habet gratiae et dulcedinis? quid non felicissimi gaudii? quid non sanctissimae voluptatis? Cecidit inquit, super collum ejus, et osculatus est eum. Cum sic super eum affectabat, quid agebat amplectens et osculans, nisi illum sibi vel se ipsum illi insinuabat, se illi inspirabat, ut, adhaerendo sibi unus, secum fieret spiritus, sicut adhaerendo meretricibus unum cum eis corpus fuerat affectus? Parum erat illi summae misericordiae viscera suae miserationis non claudere miseris; in ipsa sua eos viscera trahit, suisque inserit membris. Non poterat nos sibi arctius astringere, non poterat non magis intimos habere, quam ut incorporaret sibi, et, sicut charitate, sic virtute ineffabili, non solum assumpto corpori, sed etiam ipsi conniteretur spiritui. Quod si tanta est gratia poenitentium, quae erit gloria regnantium? si ista sunt solatia miserorum, quae erunt gaudia beatorum? et qui hoc praerogat in via, quid servat in patria? Utique quod in cor hominis non ascendit; ut ei similes simus, et sit Deus omnia in omnibus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But see how, where sin abounded, grace abounds the more. The guilty one could scarcely hope for pardon; the Judge\u2014no, no longer a judge, but an advocate\u2014heaps up grace. \"Quickly,\" he says, \"bring forth the first robe, and clothe him, and put a ring on his hand, and shoes on his feet; and bring the fatted calf, and kill it, and let us eat and feast: for this my son was dead, and is come to life again.\" But to pass over all these things\u2014namely, the first robe, that is, the sanctification of the spirit with which the baptized is clothed and the penitent reclothed; the ring of faith, by which he is betrothed; the shoes, with which he is armed to tread upon the venom of serpents, or prepared to preach the Gospel; the fatted calf, which is sacrificed for him upon the altar; those festive joys which, proclaimed for the recovered son, are celebrated throughout all heaven\u2014to pass over all these, I say, and leave them for more learned men to treat: that embrace and kiss of fatherly love\u2014what grace and sweetness does it not contain? What most blessed joy? What most holy delight does it not hold? \"He fell,\" it says, \"on his neck, and kissed him.\" When he showed such feeling toward him, what was he doing in embracing and kissing him, but insinuating him to himself, or himself to him, breathing himself into him, so that, by clinging to him, the two might become one spirit together, just as, by clinging to harlots, he had been made one body with them? It was too little for that highest mercy not to close the inward depths of its compassion to the wretched; it draws them into its very depths, and grafts them into its own members. It could not bind us more closely to itself or hold us in greater intimacy than by incorporating us into itself and, by ineffable power as by charity, uniting us not only to the body it had assumed but also to its very spirit. But if such is the grace of penitents, what will be the glory of those who reign? If these are the comforts of the wretched, what will be the joys of the blessed? And he who gives this in advance on the way, what does he keep in store in the homeland? Surely that which has not entered into the heart of man: that we may be like him, and God may be all in all.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Jam vero tu, beate peccator (sed licet non ideo beatus quia peccator, sed quia poenitens de peccato), quid tibi, rogo, animi erat inter amplexus patris et oscula, cum prope desperatum refovebat, cum cor mundum tibi innovans, laetitiam salutaris sui tibi refundebat? Et quomodo, inquit, sermo explicabit quod mens non capit? Inenarrabiles gemitus, et inexplicabiles sunt affectus, quos de incomprehensibili velut impraegnatus parturit animus. Angustum est eis cor humanum: unde et scissum effundit se ipsum; et ardorem quem concipit, sed non capit, quibus potest modis, lacrymis, gemitibus, suspiriis, evaporat ac digerit. Norunt haec melius, qui gustaverunt saepius et uberius. Nunc quoque, inquam, cum dimissus post illos amplexus et oscula, te et te ipsum recogitas, cum causam tuam qualis fuerit, qualiterve ab eo judicata sit retractas, et illinc abundantiam delicti tui, hinc superabundantiam gratiae pensas: quid tibi, obsecro, parit cogitatio tua? Quidni, inquit, intolerabilis in meditatione mea exardescat ignis, illinc dolore et pudore, hinc gaudio et amore? Non me hominem putarem, sed lapidem, si ita duri cordis existerem, ut me ipsum non dolerem vel erubescerem, vel ita nequam et ingrati, ut non totus in gaudium vel amorem illius Patris liquefierem."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Now you, blessed sinner (though not blessed because a sinner, but because repentant of sin), what, I ask, was in your heart amid the embraces and kisses of the Father, when he revived you who were nearly in despair, when, renewing a clean heart in you, he poured back into you the joy of his salvation? And how, he says, shall speech unfold what the mind cannot grasp? Unspeakable are the groans, and inexplicable the feelings, which the soul brings forth in labor, as if made pregnant by something incomprehensible. The human heart is too narrow for them; hence, being torn, it pours itself forth, and the fervor it conceives but cannot contain, it releases and works off by whatever means it can\u2014tears, groans, sighs. Those who have tasted this more often and more abundantly know it better. Now too, I say, when, released after those embraces and kisses, you reconsider yourself and yourself again, when you turn over in your mind what your case was, and how it was judged by him, and weigh on the one hand the abundance of your sin, on the other the overabundance of grace: what, I ask, does your reflection bring forth in you? Why, he says, would not an intolerable fire blaze up in my meditation, on the one side from grief and shame, on the other from joy and love? I would not think myself a man but a stone, if I were so hard of heart as not to grieve or blush for myself, or so wicked and ungrateful as not to melt entirely into joy and love of that Father.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Custodi igitur, o felix peccator, custodi sollicite ac vigilanter hunc spiritum tuum, hunc justissimum humilitatis ac pietatis affectum; quo semper ita de te sentias in humilitate, de Domino in bonitate. Nihil illo majus in donis Spiritus sancti, nihil pretiosius in thesauris Dei, nihil sanctius inter omnia charismata, nihil salubrius inter omnia sacramenta. Custodi, inquam, si vis ipse custodiri, humilitatem illius sensus et vocis, qua Patri confiteris, et dicis: Pater, jam non sum dignus vocari filius tuus; fac me sicut unum de mercenariis tuis. Nihil ita Patrem promeretur, sicut hujus vocis affectus; nec melius te filium facies dignum, quam si semper confitearis indignum. Haec humilitas non solum peccatores justificat, sed etiam justos consummat, justitiamque eis accumulat, si videlicet se servos fateantur inutiles, etiam cum quae praecepta sunt, fecerint omnia. Peccatum tuum coram te sit semper, et, juxta consilium Sapientis, etiam de propitiato peccato noli esse absque metu (Eccli. V, 5). Incerta et occulta sunt judicia Dei; non temere praesumendum est de illis, cum nil inde certius teneamus, quam quod in conspectu Dei non justificabitur omnis vivens, nisi in quantum se judicat peccatorem. Alioquin quasi pannus menstruatae universae justitiae nostrae (Isai. XLIV, 6). Blande te misericordia suscepit, pie refovit; time judicium, ne gratia, quae data est humili, auferatur superbienti. Elegisti abjectus esse in domo patris tui; contentus eras fieri quasi unus ex mercenariis; sta in sententia, ut, etiamsi promotus es, adhuc ad majora proveharis. Extremum locum semper occupa, aut certe desidera; servitutem tibi mercenarii, non libertatem filii tibi vindica. Devotione quidem filii Patrem cole, conscius quid ipse de te meruerit; sed humilitate et labore mercenarii contentus esto, conscius quid ipse merueris. Nunquam tibi displiceat humilitas, per quam placere coepisti, et sine qua displicere incipies, quantiscunque pollere virtutibus, quantumcunque patri officiosus deservire videaris. Humilitas siquidem omnium virtutum est maxima, cum tamen virtutem se esse nesciat. Ipsa fere omnium radix, et seminarium, et fomes, et incentivum, et ipsa cumulus et fastigium, custodia et disciplina. Ab ipsa incipiunt, per ipsam proficiunt, in ipsa consummantur, per ipsam conservantur. Cumque omnibus ut virtutes sint tribuat, si qua earum defuerit, vel minus perfecta fuerit, ipsa alterius defectu proficiens, dispendium illius de se compensat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Keep, then, O happy sinner, keep carefully and watchfully this spirit of yours, this most just affection of humility and devotion; by which you should always feel thus about yourself\u2014in humility\u2014and about the Lord\u2014in goodness. Nothing is greater than this among the gifts of the Holy Spirit, nothing more precious among the treasures of God, nothing holier among all the charisms, nothing more wholesome among all the sacraments. Keep, I say, if you wish yourself to be kept, the humility of that feeling and of that voice by which you confess to the Father and say: \"Father, I am no longer worthy to be called your son; make me as one of your hired servants.\" Nothing so wins the Father's favor as the affection of this voice; nor will you make yourself more worthy to be a son than if you always confess yourself unworthy. This humility not only justifies sinners, but also perfects the just, and increases righteousness for them, namely if they confess themselves unprofitable servants even when they have done all that was commanded. Let your sin always be before you, and, according to the counsel of the Wise Man, even concerning sin that has been forgiven, do not be without fear (Eccli. V, 5). The judgments of God are uncertain and hidden; one must not presume rashly about them, since we hold nothing more certain from this than that in the sight of God no living person will be justified, except insofar as he judges himself a sinner. Otherwise, all our righteousness is like a menstrual cloth (Isai. XLIV, 6). Mercy has received you gently, has tenderly nursed you back; fear judgment, lest the grace that was given to the humble be taken away from the one who grows proud. You chose to be an outcast in your father's house; you were content to become as one of the hired servants; stand firm in that resolve, so that even if you are promoted, you may still be advanced to greater things. Always occupy the lowest place, or at least desire it; claim for yourself the servitude of a hired servant, not the freedom of a son. Indeed, worship the Father with the devotion of a son, being conscious of what he himself has deserved from you; but be content with the humility and labor of a hired servant, conscious of what you yourself have merited. Never let humility displease you, through which you began to be pleasing, and without which you will begin to be displeasing, however great the virtues you may possess, however dutifully you may seem to serve the Father. For humility is indeed the greatest of all virtues, even though it does not know itself to be a virtue. It is almost the root, and seedbed, and kindling, and spark of all virtues, and it is itself their crown and summit, their guard and discipline. From it they begin, through it they advance, in it they are perfected, through it they are preserved. And although it grants to all of them that they be virtues, if any of them should be lacking, or less than perfect, humility itself, profiting by the defect of another, compensates by itself for that one's loss.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The 1879 edition prints non where the 1576 edition has nos (us), and conniteretur where it has couniret (unite). The English follows the earlier readings. The comparison is Sermones D. Guarrici abbatis Igniacensis, edited by Ioannes Costerius (Antwerp: Philippus Nutius, 1576), signature K v (Google Books PA112)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The menstrual-cloth quotation comes from Isaiah 64:6; the edition prints Isai. XLIV, 6."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Lent Sermon II", "topics": ["Lent", "repentance", "confession", "humility", "mercy", "grace", "hope", "Eucharist"], "url": "sermons/guerric-lent-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:nativity-mary", "calendar_label": "The Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-mary-nativity-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN NATIVITATE B. MARIAE, SERMO I., Comparatio partus Virginis cum vite quae incorrupta dat odorem suum., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 199-203.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Nativity of Mary", "Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Sirach 24:23", "2 Peter 1:19", "Genesis 27:27", "Song of Songs 1:13", "Psalm 33:9 (historical numbering)", "Romans 8:30", "Song of Songs 1:15", "Song of Songs 2:5"], "occasion": "In Nativitate B. Mariae", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Nativity of Mary", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "For Mary's birthday Guerric takes the words \"Like the vine I brought forth the sweetness of fragrance.\" As the vine's flower gives its scent without losing its bloom, the Virgin brought forth the Savior without losing her virginity. Christ is the sweetness that draws, the honorableness that sanctifies and the honor that glorifies, and Guerric asks the brothers for a chastity that flowers in gaze, speech and dress. The sermon appears under the heading for the Nativity of Blessed Mary in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN NATIVITATE B. MARIAE"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE NATIVITY OF BLESSED MARY", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Comparatio partus Virginis cum vite quae incorrupta dat odorem suum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "A comparison of the Virgin's childbearing with a vine that, uncorrupted, gives its fragrance.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Ego quasi vitis fructificavi suavitatem odoris (Eccli. XXIV, 23). Natalem beatissimae Virginis matris celebramus, de qua Vita omnium accepit natalem. Nata est hodie Virgo, de qua Salus omnium voluit nasci, ut natis ad mortem, daret ad vitam posse renasci. Nata est hodie Mater nova, quae primae matris maledictionem dissolvit; ut per istam benedictionem haereditate possideant, qui per illam sub praejudicio maledicti aeterni fuerant nati. Prorsus nova mater, quae novitatem attulit filiis inveteratis, vitiumque sanavit tam ingenitae, quam superadditae vetustatis. Prorsus nova mater, quae tam novo miraculo parit, ut pariat, et virgo sit; ipsumque pariat, qui omnia matremque ipsam inter omnia creavit. Mirabilis quidem novitas fecundae virginitatis, sed longe mirabilior novitas editae prolis. Nulli enim incredibile jam erit Virginem permansisse quae peperit, qui Deum agnoverit esse qui natus fuit. Nullatenus siquidem cum injuria maternae nasceretur integritatis, qui etiam corrupta redintegrare consuevit: nec veritas assumpti corporis potentiae praejudicavit Creatoris, quominus servaret sibi, quod pluribus dedit creaturis. Multas nempe creaturas invenies nasci sine corruptione gignentium, et quadam voce sua Creatori super inviolabili partu suo perhibere testimonium."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"I, like the vine, have brought forth the sweetness of fragrance\" (Eccli. XXIV, 23). We celebrate the birthday of the most blessed Virgin Mother, from whom the Life of all received his birth. Today was born the Virgin from whom the Salvation of all willed to be born, so that to those born unto death he might give the power to be reborn unto life. Today was born the new Mother who dissolved the curse of the first mother, so that through this blessing those might possess as an inheritance who, through that first mother, had been born under the judgment of the eternal curse. Truly a new mother, who brought newness to aged children, and healed the fault both of inherited and of added old age. Truly a new mother, who gives birth by so new a miracle that she both gives birth and remains a virgin; and brings forth him who created all things, and the mother herself among all things. Marvelous indeed is the novelty of fruitful virginity, but far more marvelous is the novelty of the offspring brought forth. For to no one will it now be incredible that she who bore remained a virgin, once he has acknowledged that he who was born is God. For in no way would he be born to the injury of his mother's integrity, he who was accustomed even to restore wholeness to corrupted things; nor did the truth of the body he assumed prejudice the power of the Creator, so that he could not keep for himself what he gave to many creatures. For indeed you will find many creatures born without the corruption of those who bear them, and bearing witness, as it were, by their own voice to the Creator concerning their inviolate birth.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sed loquatur Mater ipsa, quae sui conscia est mysterii, et nos edoceat quomodo vel quid ipsa genuerit. Loquatur autem non novae assertionis argumento, sed antiquo prophetiae oraculo; quia, ut ait Petrus apostolus, etiam miraculis firmior testis est propheticus sermo (II Petr. I, 19). Quid enim minus patet calumniae, aut suspicionem admittit falsitatis, quam testimonium divinitus de nondum natis prolatum? Longe igitur antequam nasceretur Maria, vocem ipsius assumebat Spiritus, qui in ea futurus erat, et tam divinitatem Filii, quam integritatem Matris, opus videlicet suum, contra blasphemias impiorum defendebat, et si persona ipsius, in modo communem intellectum sequimur, quod nunc audistis dicebat: Ego quasi vitis fructificavi suavitatem odoris (Eccli. XXIV, 23). Quod enim personae Sapientiae, id est ipsius Filii, tribuit haec verba contextus lectionis, sicut ipsi scitis, qui regulas nostis Scripturarum, nihil huic praejudicat intellectui, quominus, sicut alia multa, possint aptari personae quoque Virginis matris. Sed neque illud ignoratis quod satis superque suppetant alia testimonia, familiarius ac manifestius huic negotio servientia: sed non erit fraudanda exspectatio vestra de his quae lectio proposuit hodierna."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But let the Mother herself speak, who is conscious of her own mystery, and let her teach us how, or what, she herself brought forth. Let her speak, however, not by the argument of some new assertion, but by the ancient oracle of prophecy; for, as the apostle Peter says, even more firm than miracles is the prophetic word as witness (II Petr. I, 19). For what is less open to calumny, or admits less suspicion of falsehood, than a testimony uttered by God concerning those not yet born? Long, then, before Mary was born, the Spirit who was to be in her took up her voice, and defended both the divinity of the Son and the integrity of the Mother\u2014his own work\u2014against the blasphemies of the impious; and, speaking in her person, if we follow the common understanding of the manner, he was saying what you have just heard: \"I, like the vine, have brought forth the sweetness of fragrance\" (Eccli. XXIV, 23). For the fact that the context of the reading attributes these words to the person of Wisdom, that is, to the Son himself, as you yourselves know, who know the rules of Scripture, in no way prejudices this understanding, so that, like many other things, they may also be fitted to the person of the Virgin Mother. But you are also not unaware that there are more than enough other testimonies, more closely and manifestly serving this matter; yet your expectation shall not be cheated as regards what today's reading has set before us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Respondeat ergo Maria blasphemis tam pro se, quam pro Filio; cunctasque haereses solo interimat verbo, et dicat: Ego quasi vitis fructificavi suavitatem odoris. Ac si aperte dicat: Partus quidem meus non habet exemplum in sexu mulierum, sed habet similitudinem in naturis rerum. Quaeris quomodo virginitas genuit Salvatorem? Sicut flos vitis odorem. Si corruptum inveneris florem, pro eo quod dedit odorem, violatum crede pudorem, quia edidit Salvatorem. Quid potes calumniari in proprietate similitudinis? Quid enim aliud est virginitas, quam flos inviolati corporis? Quid aliud est filius virginitatis, quam suavitas odoris? Tu tamen cave ne bono moriaris odore. Bonus enim aliis odor vitae in vitam, his qui scilicet salvi fiunt: aliis odor mortis in mortem, his scilicet qui pereunt, nimirum tanquam odor vineae florentis animalibus venenatis. Sane suavitate hujus odoris refocillabatur spiritus patriarchae senis, qui filium tangens, et fragrantiam Christi sentiens, de memoria abundantiae suavitatis illius eructabat, dicens: Ecce odor filii mei, sicut odor agri pleni, cui benedixit Dominus (Gen. XXVII, 27). Suavitatem hujus odoris odoratus est Deus Pater, et delectatus repropitiatus est humano generi, cum Filius offerret semetipsum oblationem et hostiam Deo in odorem suavitatis. Hoc suavi odore trahimur, cum per conversionem currimus ad ipsum: hoc trahuntur adolescentulae, cum per devotionem currunt post ipsum. Quanquam alius sit odor ille qui de fama praedicationis percipitur, alius odor iste qui de vestimentis vel unguentis, vel de ipso fortassis corpore ejus quodammodo crassior aspergitur. Qui utique non est aliud quam virtus quae de illo exit, quae pigros excitat, et fervorem amoris innovat, ut ad currendam viam mandatorum exsultare faciat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Let Mary, then, answer the blasphemers both on her own behalf and on behalf of her Son, and let her destroy every heresy with a single word, saying: \"I, like the vine, have brought forth the sweetness of fragrance.\" As if she were saying openly: My childbearing indeed has no example among the sex of women, but it has a likeness among the natures of things. You ask how virginity brought forth the Savior? As the flower of the vine brings forth fragrance. If you find the flower corrupted because it gave forth fragrance, then believe that modesty was violated because it brought forth the Savior. What fault can you find with the fitness of the comparison? For what else is virginity but the flower of an inviolate body? What else is the son of virginity but the sweetness of fragrance? Yet take care that you do not die by a good fragrance. For to some it is a good fragrance of life unto life\u2014namely, to those who are saved; to others it is an odor of death unto death\u2014namely, to those who perish\u2014just as the fragrance of a flowering vineyard is to venomous animals. Indeed, by the sweetness of this fragrance was the spirit of the aged patriarch refreshed, who, touching his son and perceiving the fragrance of Christ, breathed out from the memory of that abundant sweetness, saying: \"Behold, the smell of my son is as the smell of a plentiful field, which the Lord hath blessed\" (Gen. XXVII, 27). The sweetness of this fragrance God the Father smelled, and, being delighted, became favorable again to the human race, when the Son offered himself as an offering and a sacrifice to God in an odor of sweetness. By this sweet fragrance we are drawn, when through conversion we run toward him; by this the young maidens are drawn, when through devotion they run after him. Although the fragrance that is perceived from the report of his preaching is one thing, and that fragrance, in a way more tangible, which is shed from his garments or ointments, or perhaps from his very body, is another. Yet this is nothing other than the power that goes forth from him, which rouses the slothful and renews the fervor of love, so that it makes them exult in running the way of the commandments.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Quia ergo tam odoriferum fructum edidit, glorietur Maria et dicat: Ego quasi vitis fructificavi suavitatem odoris. Pulchre quasi vitis: nam botrus Cypri dilectus suus illi (Cantic. I, 13), de quo non solum torcular passionis rubens mustum expressit pretiosi sanguinis, quo calix praeclarus inebriat; sed et quotidie devotio sancta sibi vinum exprimit, quod laetificat cor hominis, et inebriat voluptate gaudii et amoris. Verum non prius inebriat gustus saporis, quam trahat suavitas odoris; nec laetificat gaudium visionis, nisi prius alliciat pietas opinionis: quia nisi crediderimus non intelligemus, nec gustabimus quoniam suavis est Dominus (Psal. XXXIII, 9). Fides siquidem est quae odoratur, experientia quae gustat et fruitur. Ideo fortassis Maria Jesum suum describens per virtutes ipsius et efficientias, primo suavitatem odoris eum nominat; quia videlicet hoc initio Jesus in nobis habet subsistere, si redolentia sanctae opinionis suae ad se nos trahat. Quis sit autem fructus unde iste odor prodit, et quo nos trahat, aperit cum subdit: Et flores mei fructus honoris et honestatis (Eccli. XXIV, 23). Prorsus iste est Jesus suavitas odoris invitans, honestas sanctificans, honor glorificans. Suavitas odoris, qua velut ad viam adducimur: honestas, per quam deducimur: honor, ad quem perducimur. Pulchre autem honestas dicitur, quasi honoris status. Neque enim honor ille summae dignitatis et gloriae statum tunc in nobis habere posset, nisi nunc honestas vitae et morum sedem ei praepararet. Nullus tunc, sicut nunc, passim cernitur honoratus sine honestate, sicut nec ullus honestus sine honore. Est ergo Jesus primo suavitas odoris his quos vocat: deinde, honestas his quos justificat: demum, honor illis quos magnificat. Nam quos praedestinavit, hos et vocavit: et quos vocavit, hos et justificavit: quos autem justificavit, illos et magnificavit (Rom. VIII, 30)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Since, then, she brought forth so fragrant a fruit, let Mary glory and say: I, like a vine, have borne the sweetness of fragrance. Beautifully does she say \"like a vine\": for her Beloved is to her a cluster of henna (Cantic. I, 13), from which not only did the winepress of the passion squeeze forth the reddening must of precious blood, by which the splendid cup inebriates; but also daily holy devotion presses out for itself a wine which gladdens the heart of man, and inebriates with the delight of joy and love. Yet the taste of flavor does not inebriate before the sweetness of fragrance draws us; nor does the joy of vision gladden unless the devotion of belief first entices us: for unless we have believed we shall not understand, nor shall we taste that the Lord is sweet (Psal. XXXIII, 9). Faith indeed is what smells the fragrance; experience is what tastes and enjoys it. Perhaps for this reason Mary, in describing her Jesus by his virtues and effects, first names him the sweetness of fragrance; because indeed this is the beginning by which Jesus must take root in us, if the fragrance of his holy reputation draws us to himself. But what the fruit is from which this fragrance proceeds, and whither it draws us, she reveals when she adds: And my flowers are the fruit of honor and honorableness (Eccli. XXIV, 23). This indeed is Jesus: the sweetness of fragrance inviting, the honorableness sanctifying, the honor glorifying. The sweetness of fragrance, by which we are drawn, as it were, onto the way; the honorableness, through which we are led along; the honor, to which we are brought. And \"honorableness\" is beautifully so named, as though it were the settled state (status) of honor. For that honor of highest dignity and glory could not then have its state in us, unless now the honorableness of life and conduct prepares a seat for it. No one then is seen honored without honorableness, as many are everywhere now, just as no one will be honorable without honor. Jesus, therefore, is first the sweetness of fragrance to those whom he calls; then, honorableness to those whom he justifies; finally, honor to those whom he magnifies. For whom he predestined, these he also called; and whom he called, these he also justified; and whom he justified, these he also magnified (Rom. VIII, 30).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Talis est igitur Dilectus meus, ait Maria: et iste est filius meus, o filiae Jerusalem. Iste est benedictus fructus ventris mei, hunc fructificaverunt flores mei. Non ait. Flos; sed, flores: quia cum virgo sancta est, flos virginitatis in ea multiformis est. In Maria tamen gratia singulari prae omnibus multipliciter floruit, quae tota pulchra intus et foris, tota vernabat quadam florulentia et venustate pudoris. Nam et in te quoque si castimonia perfecta sit, non solum reflorebit caro tua, sed et super te totum efflorebit sanctificatio quaedam divina. Non erit aspectus petulans, aut vagus, sed pudicitia floridus: non sermo lascivus aut ineptus, sed verecundia gratus, aut sapientia conditus. Non prurient aures libidine audiendi nova vel turpia, nec palatum desiderio sumendi dulcia. Non erit incessus inordinatus, sed modestus: non ipse habitus non dicam meretricius, sed neque superstitiosus, imo religiosus; totusque hominis tui status sanctimoniae gratia florulentus, ut recte sponso dicere possis, cum eum ad secretum tuum invitaveris, Lectulus noster floridus (Cantic. I, 15). Quinimo ipse totus eris quidam flos pulcherrimus, qualibus sponsa languorem amoris sui fulciri ac recreari desiderat, cum ait: Fulcite me floribus, stipate me malis, quia amore langueo (Cantic. II, 5). Hujusmodi justus, etiam cum senuerit in terra radix ejus, et in pulvere emortuus fuerit truncus illius, ad odorem aquae vivae in resurrectione, id est in refloritione justorum, germinabit sicut lilium: et florebit in aeternum ante Dominum, florem de flore, natum virginem, virginis filium, Sponsum et coronam virginum; florem, inquam, quo coronanda est non solum integritas virginum, sed et castitas continentium: ipsi gloria per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Such, then, is my Beloved, says Mary: and this is my son, O daughters of Jerusalem. This is the blessed fruit of my womb, which my flowers have borne as fruit. She does not say \"flower,\" but \"flowers\": because, since she is a holy virgin, the flower of virginity in her is manifold. Yet in Mary, by singular grace above all others, it flowered in manifold ways, for she, wholly beautiful within and without, blossomed entirely with a certain flowering and loveliness of modesty. For in you too, if chastity be perfect, not only will your flesh reflower, but also upon you a certain divine sanctification will flower forth entirely. Your gaze will not be wanton or wandering, but blooming with modesty; your speech not lascivious or foolish, but gracious with reserve, or seasoned with wisdom. Your ears will not itch with lust to hear new or shameful things, nor your palate with desire to taste sweet things. Your walk will not be disorderly, but modest; your very dress will be not, I will not say meretricious, but not even superstitious, but rather religious; and the whole condition of your person will be flowering with the grace of holiness, so that you may rightly say to the Bridegroom, when you have invited him into your secret chamber: Our bed is flowery (Cantic. I, 15). Indeed, you yourself will be wholly a most beautiful flower, of the kind with which the Bride desires the languor of her love to be propped up and refreshed, when she says: Prop me up with flowers, surround me with apples, for I languish with love (Cantic. II, 5). Such a just one, even when his root has grown old in the earth, and his trunk has died away into dust, will sprout like a lily at the scent of living water in the resurrection, that is, in the reflowering of the just: and he will flourish forever before the Lord \u2014 a flower born of a flower, a virgin born, the son of a virgin, the Bridegroom and crown of virgins; a flower, I say, by which is to be crowned not only the integrity of virgins, but also the chastity of the continent: to him be glory through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary Sermon I", "topics": ["Nativity of Mary", "Mary", "virgin birth", "virginity", "chastity", "resurrection"], "url": "sermons/guerric-mary-nativity-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:nativity-mary", "calendar_label": "The Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-mary-nativity-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De pulchritudine sanctae dilectionis., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 203-206.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Nativity of Mary", "Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Sirach 24:24", "2 Corinthians 5:16", "Isaiah 53:2", "Wisdom 7:26", "James 1:17", "Proverbs 25:27", "Psalm 44:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 44:5 (historical numbering)", "Micah 4:2", "Isaiah 66:7", "Sirach 2:10", "Psalm 9:11 (historical numbering)", "1 John 3:3"], "occasion": "In Nativitate B. Mariae", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Nativity of Mary", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Returning to the feast's reading from a year before, Guerric takes Mary's words \"I am the mother of fair love.\" Christ has a bodily, a moral and a divine form, and Christians love him now in the beauty of his life and teaching. Mary labors to form her Son in all the adopted children, and fair love must rule over base, natural and worldly loves. The sermon appears under the heading for the Nativity of Blessed Mary in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De pulchritudine sanctae dilectionis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the beauty of holy love.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Ego mater pulchrae dilectionis, et timoris, et agnitionis, et sanctae spei (Eccli. XXIV, 24). Meminisse potestis cum praeterito anno loqueremur de principio lectionis hodiernae, non inconvenienter, ut arbitror, beatae Dei Genitrici nos illud assignasse, salvo tamen intellectu illo, quo tota lectio Filio ejusdem proprie competit, id est, Dei sapientiae. Multo autem manifestius in eo quod modo de eadem lectione proposuimus, ejusdem Virginis se demonstrat vox et persona, si tamen advertamus, in eo quod ait, Ego mater pulchrae dilectionis, etc., quam pulchre et commode Filium his virtutum nominibus describat. Notus erat Matri Filius, nec minus utique quam illi qui dicebat: Etsi cognovimus Christum secundum carnem, sed nunc jam non novimus (II Cor. V, 16). Primo novit eum secundum formam carnis, in qua ipsa eum genuit: sed longe hoc a cognitione illius formae, in qua Pater eum genuit. In illa visus est aliquando, et non erat ei species neque decor (Isa. LIII, 2): in ista splendor est gloriae et candor lucis aeternae (Sap. VII, 26), apud quem non est transmutatio, nec vicissitudinis obumbratio (Jac. I, 17). Visio illius formae incredulis auxit peccatum; visio istius justis servatur in praemium. Igitur inter formam carnis et formam Verbi, quasi medius de ista ad illam gradus est, quaedam alia forma Christi, spiritualis quidem, sed quam in carne palam exhibuit; forma scilicet vitae quam in corpore gessit ad informationem eorum qui erant credituri. Si enim secundum exemplar vitae et morum, quod in eo monstratum est, formatus fuerit in nobis Christus; tunc demum idonei erimus videre non formam solum, quae formata est propter nos, sed etiam illam quae formavit nos."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"I am the mother of fair love, and of fear, and of knowledge, and of holy hope\" (Eccli. XXIV, 24). You may remember that last year, when we were speaking about the beginning of today's reading, we assigned it, not unfittingly I think, to the Blessed Mother of God\u2014while still preserving that understanding by which the whole reading properly belongs to her Son, that is, to the Wisdom of God. But much more clearly, in what we have now proposed from this same reading, the voice and person of this same Virgin shows itself\u2014if indeed we notice how beautifully and fittingly, in saying, \"I am the mother of fair love,\" etc., she describes her Son by these names of virtues. The Son was known to the Mother, and indeed no less than to him who said: \"Though we have known Christ according to the flesh, yet now we know him so no longer\" (II Cor. V, 16). First she knew him according to the form of flesh, in which she herself bore him; but this is far removed from the knowledge of that form in which the Father begot him. In the former he was once seen, and \"he had no beauty nor comeliness\" (Isa. LIII, 2); in the latter he is \"the brightness of glory and the whiteness of eternal light\" (Sap. VII, 26), with whom there is \"no change, nor shadow of alteration\" (Jac. I, 17). The sight of that first form increased the sin of unbelievers; the sight of this second form is kept as a reward for the just. Therefore, between the form of flesh and the form of the Word, as a kind of middle step from the one to the other, there is a certain other form of Christ\u2014spiritual indeed, but one which he openly displayed in the flesh: namely, the form of life which he led in the body for the instruction of those who were to believe. For if Christ shall have been formed in us according to the pattern of life and conduct which was shown in him, then at last we shall be fit to see not only the form which was formed for our sake, but also that form which formed us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Est utique in Christo alia forma corporalis, alia moralis, alia intellectualis. In corporali frater est; in morali magister est; in intellectuali Deus noster est. Corporalem suscepit, ut impleret sacramentum: moralem exhibuit, ut praeberet exemplum: intellectualem seu divinam revelabit ad praemium. Quanquam etiam corporalem tunc cernere, in quam angeli concupiscunt prospicere, non minima sit portio gloriae. Beatus autem qui nunc amator factus est formae hujus, quae in exemplum est proposita: nam qui scrutator est illius quae in praemium est reposita, opprimetur a gloria (Prov. XXV, 27). Hujus formae mirator et amator factus erat, qui dicebat: Speciosus forma prae filiis hominum (Psal. XLIV, 3). Vis scire quia non formam corporis, sed cordis, non pulchritudinem membrorum, sed morum praedicabat? audi sequentia: Specie tua, inquit, et pulchritudine tua, intende, prospere procede, et regna. Adhuc fortassis dubium est, nisi addat: Propter veritatem, inquit, et mansuetudinem et justitiam (Psal. XLIV, 5). Prorsus haec est species tua et pulchritudo tua, qua regnum acquisivisti, pulcherrime regum; veritas utique sermonum, mansuetudo morum, justitia judiciorum. Hac pulchritudine facile illexisti et subjecisti tibi etiam corda inimicorum, quippe totus concupiscentia et desiderium. Mirabilis triumphus gratiae, omnino novum ac pulcherrimum genus victoriae, hostem non perdere ad mortem fortitudine, sed convertere ad amorem pulchritudine. Ecce mundus totus post eum abiit concupiscens speciem decoris ejus, non quia faciem ejus viderit, sed quia tam multa amabilia de mansuetudine, veritate et justitia ejus audivit. Ex Sion species decoris ejus, quia de Sion exivit lex, et verbum Domini de Jerusalem (Mich. IV, 2): unde scilicet Evangelium ad nos missum est, in quo quaedam pulchrior Christi facies ostensa est; forma scilicet vitae et doctrinae, quam et tradidit verbo, et in se expressit exemplo."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. There is indeed in Christ one form that is bodily, another moral, another intellectual. In the bodily form he is our brother; in the moral form he is our teacher; in the intellectual form he is our God. He took on the bodily form in order to fulfill the sacrament; he displayed the moral form in order to provide an example; he will reveal the intellectual or divine form as a reward. Although even to behold the bodily form then, into which the angels desire to look, is no small portion of glory. But blessed is he who has now become a lover of this form which has been set forth as an example; for he who is a searcher into that form which is reserved as a reward \"shall be overwhelmed by its glory\" (Prov. XXV, 27). A marveler and lover of this form was he who said: \"Thou art beautiful in form above the sons of men\" (Psal. XLIV, 3). Do you wish to know that he was praising not the form of the body but of the heart, not the beauty of the limbs but of conduct? Listen to what follows: \"With thy comeliness,\" he says, \"and thy beauty, set out, proceed prosperously, and reign.\" Perhaps this is still doubtful, unless he adds: \"Because of truth,\" he says, \"and meekness, and justice\" (Psal. XLIV, 5). This indeed is your comeliness and your beauty, by which you acquired your kingdom, O most beautiful of kings: truth of speech, meekness of conduct, justice of judgments. By this beauty you easily enticed and subjected to yourself even the hearts of your enemies, being wholly desire and longing. A wondrous triumph of grace, altogether a new and most beautiful kind of victory\u2014not to destroy the enemy unto death by strength, but to convert him to love by beauty. Behold, the whole world has gone after him, desiring the beauty of his comeliness, not because it saw his face, but because it heard so many lovable things about his meekness, truth, and justice. \"Out of Sion is the loveliness of his beauty\", because \"out of Sion went forth the law, and the word of the Lord out of Jerusalem\" (Mich. IV, 2)\u2014whence, namely, the Gospel was sent to us, in which a certain more beautiful face of Christ was shown: namely, the form of life and doctrine, which he both delivered in word and expressed in himself by example.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. In hac forma nosse Christum, interim pietas est Christianorum; cum nosse in forma carnis, scandalum fuerit Judaeorum: nam nosse in forma divinitatis felicitas est, et gaudium angelorum. Ideo et Paulus sciens quia caro non prodest quidquam, sine spiritu videlicet qui vivificat, nosse jam Christum secundum carnem repudiat (II Cor. V, 16): nimirum ut spiritui vivificanti studiosius totus intendat. Hoc ipsum et Maria sapere videtur, quae dilectum uteri sui, dilectum votorum suorum insinuare cupiens affectibus omnium, describit eum non secundum carnem, sed secundum spiritum: quasi diceret et ipsa: Etsi cognovi Christum secundum carnem, sed nunc jam non novi. Cupit namque et ipsa formare Unigenitum suum in omnibus filiis adoptionis, qui etsi geniti sunt verbo veritatis, nihilominus tamen parturit eos quotidie desiderio et cura pietatis, donec occurrant in virum perfectum, in mensuram plenitudinis aetatis Filii sui, quem semel parturivit et peperit; imo, ut Isaias ait, Antequam parturiret peperit (Isai. LXVI, 7): quia peperit sine dolore, nec experta est difficultatem et molestiam parturitionis, cum pareret gaudii fructum aeterni."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. To know Christ in this form is, for the time being, the piety of Christians; whereas to know him in the form of flesh was the stumbling-block of the Jews; for to know him in the form of divinity is the happiness and joy of angels. Therefore Paul also, knowing that the flesh profits nothing without the spirit which gives life, refuses now to know Christ according to the flesh (II Cor. V, 16)\u2014surely so that he might more earnestly direct his whole attention to the life-giving spirit. This same wisdom Mary too seems to show, who, desiring to instill into the affections of all the beloved of her womb, the beloved of her prayers, describes him not according to the flesh but according to the spirit\u2014as if she too were saying: \"Though I have known Christ according to the flesh, yet now I know him so no longer.\" For she too desires to form her Only-begotten in all the sons of adoption, who, although they have been begotten by the word of truth, nevertheless she labors to bring them forth daily by the desire and care of piety, until they attain to a perfect man, to the measure of the fulness of the age of her Son, whom once she labored with and brought forth\u2014or rather, as Isaiah says, \"Before she was in labor, she brought forth\" (Isai. LXVI, 7); because she brought forth without pain, nor did she experience the difficulty and distress of labor, since she was bringing forth the fruit of eternal joy.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Hunc igitur commendans nobis sic ait: Ego mater pulchrae dilectionis, et timoris, et agnitionis, et sanctae spei (Eccli. XXIV, 24). Ergone est iste Filius tuus, o Virgo virginum? ergone talis est dilectus tuus, o pulcherrima mulierum? Plane talis est dilectus meus, et ipse est Filius meus, o filiae Jerusalem. Dilectus meus est pulchra dilectio in se ipso: dilectus meus est pulchra dilectio, timor, et spes, et agnitio in illo qui natus est ex ipso. Ipse enim est non solum quem diligimus, timemus, agnoscimus, et in quem speramus; sed haec etiam omnia in nobis operatur, atque his virtutibus, veluti quibusdam membris et partibus, in nobis perficitur atque formatur. Tunc enim perfecte, pro modulo hujus vitae, Christus in te formatus est, tunc veritas ipsius in te expressa est, si veritatem quae ipse est agnovisti, et agnitam glorificasti, timore videlicet et spe; et ne spes confundat, charitas diffusa sit in corde. Si huic sensui judicas incongruum, quod alius sit istarum ordo virtutum vel perfectuum, et alius ordo positionis nominum: non enim ait: Ego mater agnitionis, et timoris, et spei, et dilectionis; sed ait: Ego mater pulchrae dilectionis, et timoris, et agnitionis, et sanctae spei: fortassis et in hoc ordine ipso quem ponit, inveniri poterit aliquid congruentiae et rationis. Sicut enim de agnitione timor nascitur, quem, ne in desperationem concidat, spes adjuncta consolatur, ipsique spei, ne confundat, dilectio suffragatur: ita versa vice dilectio castum parit timorem, timorque cum dilectione illuminat agnitionem, secundum illud Sapientis, Qui timetis Dominum, diligite illum, et illuminabuntur corda vestra (Eccli. II, 10). Quanto autem verius illuminato corde Deus agnoscitur, tanto fiducialius in eum speratur. Unde est illud: Sperent in te omnes qui noverunt nomen tuum, Domine (Psal. IX, 11). Spes vero sancta est, sicut Joannes de spe videndi Deum loquens ait: Omnis qui habet hanc spem in illum sanctificat se ipsum, sicut et ille sanctus est (I Joan. III, 3)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Commending him to us, therefore, she speaks thus: I am the mother of fair love, and of fear, and of knowledge, and of holy hope (Eccli. XXIV, 24). Is this, then, your Son, O Virgin of virgins? Is this, then, your beloved, O most beautiful of women? Truly such is my beloved, and he is my Son, O daughters of Jerusalem. My beloved is fair love in himself: my beloved is fair love, fear, and hope, and knowledge in him who was born of him. For he is not only the one whom we love, fear, acknowledge, and in whom we hope; but he also works all these things in us, and by these virtues, as by certain members and parts, is perfected and formed in us. For then, perfectly\u2014so far as this life allows\u2014is Christ formed in you, then is his truth expressed in you, if you have acknowledged the truth which he himself is, and having acknowledged it, have glorified it, namely with fear and hope; and lest hope confound, let charity be poured out in the heart. If you judge it inconsistent with this meaning that the order of these virtues or perfections should be one thing, and the order of the naming of them another\u2014for she does not say: I am the mother of knowledge, and of fear, and of hope, and of love; but she says: I am the mother of fair love, and of fear, and of knowledge, and of holy hope\u2014perhaps even in this very order which she sets forth, something of fittingness and reason may be found. For just as fear is born of knowledge, which, lest it fall into despair, is consoled by hope joined to it, and hope itself, lest it confound, is supported by love: so, conversely, love brings forth chaste fear, and fear together with love illumines knowledge, according to that word of the Wise Man: You who fear the Lord, love him, and your hearts shall be enlightened (Eccli. II, 10). But the more truly God is known by an enlightened heart, the more confidently is he hoped for. Hence that word: Let all who know your name, O Lord, hope in you (Psal. IX, 11). And holy is that hope, as John says, speaking of the hope of seeing God: Every one that has this hope in him sanctifies himself, even as he is holy (I Joan. III, 3).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Pulchre autem et proprie dicta est pulchra dilectio, cum charitas Deus sit, ac per hoc summa pulchritudo; sitque virtus ista fere tota pulchritudo Ecclesiae, quam ipse Sponsus ejus in Cantico amoris invenitur tantum ac toties in ea mirari et extollere. Prorsus dilectio pulchra, quae de corde puro est, et conscientia bona, et fide non ficta. Ubi enim cor purum est, nulla est ruga; ubi conscientia bona, nulla est macula: ubi fides non ficta, non est aliquid ejusmodi quod oculis Sponsi displiceat, quominus sibi Ecclesiam nunc gratiosam, tunc gloriosam exhibeat. Distinguenda etiam erat hoc nomine dilectio sanctorum ab amore hominum, sive turpi, qui nec nominandus est inter fideles; sive naturali, quo amantur parentes; sive saeculari, quo se invicem amant propter saeculi necessitates vel cupiditates. Procul ergo, procul; imo nusquam prorsus sub oculis Dei amor turpitudinis: seorsum autem interim cedat amor necessitudinis, solusque regnet amor internae et aeternae pulchritudinis, quo solum Deum, vel propter Deum amant vere pulchri. Tu vero, bone Jesu, pulchritudo Sanctorum, speciosus forma, non modo prae filiis hominum, sed et prae virtutibus coelorum; specie tua et pulchritudine tua, intende, prospere procede, et regna: tamque late regnet pulchra dilectio tua, ut turpem de finibus rerum perturbet et eliminet, saecularem ad se convertat et inclinet, naturalem sub se regat et ordinet; ut illa pulchra et vera dilectione te diligat mundus, qua dilexisti mundum, Salvator mundi, qui vivis et regnas Deus, per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Beautifully and fittingly is it called fair love, since charity is God, and by this is the highest beauty; and this virtue is almost the whole beauty of the Church, which her very Bridegroom in the Song of love is found so greatly and so often to admire and extol in her. Truly it is fair love, which comes from a pure heart, and a good conscience, and unfeigned faith. For where the heart is pure, there is no wrinkle; where the conscience is good, there is no stain; where faith is unfeigned, there is nothing of the sort that could displease the eyes of the Bridegroom, so as to prevent him from presenting the Church to himself now as gracious, and then as glorious. It was also necessary that the love of the saints be distinguished by this name from the love of men\u2014whether the base love, which must not even be named among the faithful; or the natural love, by which parents are loved; or the worldly love, by which people love one another on account of the necessities or desires of this world. Far, then, far away\u2014nay, altogether absent from the eyes of God\u2014be the love of baseness: let the love of natural ties withdraw aside for the time being, and let the love of inward and eternal beauty alone reign, by which the truly beautiful love God alone, or love others for God's sake. But you, good Jesus, beauty of the Saints, fair in form not only above the sons of men, but even above the virtues of the heavens; with your beauty and your comeliness, set out, proceed prosperously, and reign; and may your fair love reign so widely that it may disturb and banish base love from the bounds of all things, turn worldly love toward itself and incline it, and rule and order natural love beneath itself; so that the world may love you with that fair and true love with which you loved the world, O Savior of the world, who live and reign, God, through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary Sermon II", "topics": ["Nativity of Mary", "Mary", "charity", "beauty", "Christ", "spiritual motherhood"], "url": "sermons/guerric-mary-nativity-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-christmas", "calendar_label": "Christmastide", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-nativity-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De nativitate Domini, sermo I, Qualia nos doceat Deus puer, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 29\u201332.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Christmas", "Nativity of the Lord", "Isaiah 9:6", "Psalm 84:8 (source numbering)", "Matthew 18:4 (printed locator; see note)", "John 10:9", "Psalm 111:6 (printed locator; see note)", "Galatians 6:3", "Ecclesiasticus 3:20 (source numbering)", "James 4:6", "1 Peter 5:5"], "occasion": "De nativitate Domini", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Christmas", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric's first Nativity sermon contemplates the eternal Word as a vulnerable child. Four numbered paragraphs move from the renewal brought by Christ's birth to humility, confidence in mercy, and the gentleness that should shape Christian conduct. This reading uses the 1879 Patrologia Latina edition; the approximate 1138\u20131157 composition range is distinct from that publication date. The Nativity heading does not specify a Christmas Mass. Two editorial notes explain inaccurate Scripture locators printed in the edition without changing the sermon."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "DE NATIVITATE DOMINI."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE NATIVITY OF THE LORD", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Qualia nos doceat Deus puer."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "What God as a child teaches us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Puer natus est nobis (Isai. ix, 6). Puer antiquus dierum. Puer forma corporis et aetate; antiquus dierum, incomprehensibili Verbi aeternitate. Quanquam et in ipsa sua dierum antiquitate et si puer non sit, novus tamen semper sit; imo non tam novus sit, quam ipsa novitas in se semper manens et innovans omnia, a quo quaeque res prout recedit, inveteratur; prout reaccedit, renovatur. Et novo quodam modo unde antiquus, inde novus: quoniam quidem aeternitas ipsius sine initio nascendi, sine defectu veterascendi, ipsa est et antiqua novitas, et nova antiquitas ipsius. Alia tamen est novitas hujus temporalis nativitatis, quia puer natus est nobis innovandis, qui ab aeterno Deus natus est angelis beatificandis. Illa quidem nativitas amplioris gloriae, sed ista profusioris misericordiae. Ista siquidem propter me, qui misericordia indigebam, quoniam miseria circumdatus eram, et miseria quam expiare non poteram. Ostende nobis misericordiam tuam (Psal. lxxxiv, 8), qui nondum idonei sumus videre gloriam tuam: appareat benignitas et humanitas Salvatoris nostri Dei, ut per hanc digni efficiamur et idonei, quibus appareat majestas et divinitas Creatoris nostri Dei. Ostende nobis, Domine, misericordiam tuam, nostra indutam miseria, et novo miserendi genere de ipsa miseria miserorum operantem remedia. Ad hoc siquidem ars misericordiae beatitudinem Dei, et miseriam hominis in unitate Mediatoris temperavit; ut operante sacramento unitatis, per virtutem resurrectionis beatitudo miseriam absorbeat, mortemque vita deglutiat; et totus homo glorificatus in divinae consortium naturae transeat. Usque adeo autem universitas [sic] infirmitates carnis, quibus ob culpam humana subjacet natura, absque culpa videlicet, dignatio suscepit divina; ut nec infantiae recusaret injurias, nec alia, quam communis habet conditio, sortiri vellet exordia: excepto sane, quod per Spiritum sanctum immaculatus nascens de immaculata, vitia nostrae originis emundabat, atque secundae nativitatis nobis sacramenta dedicabat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. A child is born to us (Isaiah ix, 6). A child, the Ancient of Days. A child in bodily form and age; the Ancient of Days in the incomprehensible eternity of the Word. Yet even in that ancientness of his days, although he is not a child, he is always new; indeed, he is not so much new as newness itself, always abiding in itself and making all things new. Every thing grows old as it withdraws from him, and is renewed as it draws near again. And in a certain new way, the very reason he is ancient is the reason he is new: for his eternity, without a beginning through birth or a decline through growing old, is itself both his ancient newness and his new ancientness. Yet the newness of this birth in time is different: he who from eternity was born as God to give the angels blessedness has been born as a child to make us new. That birth indeed has greater glory, but this one has more abundant mercy. This birth is for me, who needed mercy because I was surrounded by misery, and by misery which I could not expiate. Show us your mercy (Psalm lxxxiv, 8), for we are not yet fit to see your glory. Let the kindness and humanity of God our Saviour appear, so that through this we may become worthy and fit for the majesty and divinity of God our Creator to appear to us. Show us, Lord, your mercy, clothed in our misery and, by a new manner of showing mercy, working remedies from the very misery of the miserable. For this purpose the art of mercy has blended God's blessedness and man's misery in the unity of the Mediator, so that, as the sacrament of unity works, blessedness may through the power of the resurrection swallow up misery and life devour death, and the whole man, glorified, may pass into fellowship with the divine nature. Indeed, the divine condescension took upon itself, without sin, all the infirmities of the flesh to which human nature is subject because of sin, to such an extent that it did not refuse the hardships of infancy, nor did it wish to have a beginning different from that of our common condition; except, of course, that, born immaculate of the immaculate Virgin through the Holy Spirit, he cleansed the faults of our origin and consecrated for us the sacraments of a second birth.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Itaque parvulus natus est nobis (Isa. ix, 6), et Deus majestatis exinaniens semetipsum, non solum terreno mortalium corpori, sed etiam infirmae ac pusillae infantium se configuravit aetati. O beata infantia, cujus infirmum et stultum fortius et sapientius est omnibus hominibus; quia nimirum Dei virtus, et Dei sapientia sua in nostris, divina operatur in humanis! Hujus siquidem infantis infirmitas principem mundi triumphat, fortem armatum ligat, crudelem tyrannum captivat, et captivitatem nostram absolvit et liberat. Hujus infantis muta, ut videtur, et elinguis simplicitas linguas infantium facit disertas; linguis hominum loqui facit et angelorum, linguas dividens igneas: inscius iste, ut videtur, ipse est qui docet hominem scientiam et angelum, tanquam vere Deus scientiarum, Sapientia Dei, et Verbum. O sacra et dulcis infantia, quae veram humanis innocentiam refudisti, per quam omnis aetas in beatam redeat infantiam; tibique conformis fiat, non pusillitate membrorum, sed humilitate sensus, et pietate morum. Prorsus vos filii Adae, qui nimis grandes estis in oculis vestris, et in giganteam enormitatem per superbiam excrevistis; nisi conversi fueritis, et effecti sicut parvulus iste, non intrabitis in regnum coelorum (Matth. xviii, 4). Ego sum ostium (Joan. x, 9) regni, ait parvulus iste: nisi incurvetur altitudo virorum, non eos admittet humilitatis janua. Ideo nimirum conquassabit capita in terra multorum (Psal. cxi, 6); et qui elati accedunt, repulsi capite illiso cadent retrorsum. Quid enim? adhuc tu superbis, terra et cinis, postquam Deus factus est humilis? adhuc magnus es in oculis tuis, postquam Deus parvulus factus est sub oculis tuis? Ille semetipsum exinanivit ut pene videretur esse nihil, sine quo factum est nihil: et tu in immensum inflaris et extolleris, existimans te aliquid esse, cum vere sis nihil! ipse te seducis (Galat. vi, 3), clamat tibi Apostolus; cum, etsi aliquid esses, si magnum aliquid esses, tanto magis humiliari te oporteret. Quanto, inquit Sapiens, magnus es, humilia te in omnibus: et invenies gratiam coram Deo (Eccli. iii, 20), qui superbis resistit, humilibus autem dat gratiam (Jacobi iv, 6 et I Petr. v, 5); qui et ipse, ut tibi fieret exemplum, cum esset maximus omnium, factus est humillimus omnium et minimus. Parum erat ei, ut fieret minor angelis conditione mortalis naturae, nisi et hominibus minor fieret aetate imbecillis infantiae. Videat igitur pius et humilis, et glorietur, videat impius et superbus, et confundatur. Videat, inquam, Deum immensum, parvulum factum, et infantulum adorandum, stupendum mysterium, redemptionem piorum, gloriam humilium, judicium impiorum, ruinam superborum. O venerandum ac tremendum mysterium! Quam sanctum et terribile est nomen tuum, fons misericordiarum, et abyssus judiciorum! Quis de hoc fonte bibit, et non amavit? quis hanc abyssum consideravit, et non expavit? Qui non amavit, nequam et impius est: sicut qui non expavit, vecors et insensatus est. Nihil tamen est cur judicium expavescas, si non adversus misericordiam insolescas. Et ipse, siquidem mallet amari, quam serviliter timeri: magisque probat quod liber ac voluntarius offert affectus filiorum, quam quod coactitius extorquet metus servulorum. Ideo et nunc cum primo se manifestavit mortalibus, puerum se maluit exhibere, magisque amabilis quam terribilis apparere: ut, quia salvare veniebat, et non judicare; praeferret interim unde provocaret amorem, differret unde incutere posset timorem."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Thus a little child is born to us (Isaiah ix, 6), and the God of majesty, emptying himself, conformed himself not only to the earthly body of mortals, but also to the weak and tiny age of infants. O blessed infancy, whose weakness and foolishness are stronger and wiser than all men, because God's power and God's wisdom work what is their own in what is ours, divine things in human things! For this infant's weakness triumphs over the prince of the world, binds the strong armed man, takes the cruel tyrant captive, and releases and frees our captivity. This infant's simplicity, seemingly mute and without speech, makes the tongues of infants eloquent; dividing fiery tongues, it makes them speak with the tongues of men and angels. This seemingly unknowing child is himself the one who teaches knowledge to man and angel, as truly the God of knowledge, the Wisdom of God, and the Word. O holy and sweet infancy, which has poured true innocence back into mankind, through which every age may return to blessed infancy and be conformed to you, not in smallness of limbs, but in humility of mind and holiness of conduct. Truly, you children of Adam, who are too great in your own eyes and have grown through pride to a monstrous gigantic size: unless you are converted and become like this little child, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven (Matthew xviii, 4). I am the door (John x, 9) of the kingdom, says this little child: unless the tall stature of men bends low, the gate of humility will not admit them. For this reason he will crush the heads of many upon the earth (Psalm cxi, 6); and those who approach with heads raised will be repulsed, their heads struck, and fall backwards. What then? Do you still boast, dust and ashes, after God has become humble? Are you still great in your own eyes, after God has become little before your eyes? He emptied himself so as almost to seem nothing, he without whom nothing was made; and you swell and exalt yourself beyond measure, thinking yourself something when you truly are nothing! You deceive yourself (Galatians vi, 3), the Apostle cries to you; whereas, even if you were something, even something great, you ought all the more to humble yourself. The greater you are, says the Wise Man, humble yourself in all things, and you will find grace before God (Ecclesiasticus iii, 20), who resists the proud but gives grace to the humble (James iv, 6 and I Peter v, 5). He himself, to become your example, though greatest of all, became humblest and least of all. It was not enough for him to become less than the angels through the condition of mortal nature, unless he also became less than men through the age of helpless infancy. Let the devout and humble see and rejoice; let the impious and proud see and be confounded. Let them see, I say, the immeasurable God made little, an infant to be adored: an astounding mystery, the redemption of the devout, the glory of the humble, the judgment of the impious, the downfall of the proud. O mystery worthy of reverence and trembling! How holy and terrible is your name, fountain of mercies and abyss of judgments! Who has drunk from this fountain and not loved? Who has contemplated this abyss and not trembled? Whoever has not loved is wicked and impious; just as whoever has not trembled is senseless and foolish. Yet there is no reason to dread judgment if you do not rise insolently against mercy. He himself would rather be loved than feared as a slave fears; and he approves more highly what the free and willing affection of children offers than what the forced terror of servants wrings out. Therefore, when he first manifested himself to mortals, he chose to show himself as a child and to appear lovable rather than terrible: since he was coming to save and not to judge, he would for the time display what would awaken love, and defer what could strike fear.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Cum fiducia igitur accedamus ad thronum gratiae ejus, qui nec cogitare sine tremore poteramus de throno gloriae ejus. Nihil hic terroris, nihil severitatis quam metuas; sed omnimoda benignitas atque mansuetudo, de qua praesumas. Et si potestas et terror apud ipsum est, totum tamen interim dissimulat, donec poenitenti parcat, confitentem recipiat. Ne sit tibi scrupulo, quod graviter deliquisti: nescit puer laesus irasci; aut, si irascitur, facile potest placari. Vere nihil placabilius animo hujus pueri, qui in hoc ipsum pacem tuam satisfactionemque praevenit, priorque legatos de pace mittit: ut qui reus es, velis reconciliari. Tantummodo velis, et vere perfecteque velis: non modo veniam indulgebit, sed et gratiam cumulabit: quinimo lucrum non mediocre deputans, ovem recuperasse perditam, diem festum aget cum angelis. Congruebat ergo cum divina mansuetudine aetas innocentis ac facilis infantiae, satisque convenienter et commode salutem peccatorum ab hac exorditus est aetate; ut quos reatus propriae terrebat conscientiae, spes consolaretur non difficilis indulgentiae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Let us therefore approach with confidence the throne of his grace, we who could not even think of the throne of his glory without trembling. Here there is no terror, no severity for you to fear, but every kind of goodness and gentleness in which to place your trust. Even if power and terror are with him, for the time he conceals it all, until he spares the penitent and receives the one who confesses. Do not let the fact that you have sinned grievously trouble you: a child who has been hurt does not know how to be angry; or, if he is angry, he is easily appeased. Truly nothing is more ready to be appeased than the heart of this child, who anticipates your peace-making and satisfaction for this very purpose, and sends envoys of peace first, so that you, the guilty one, may be willing to be reconciled. Only be willing, truly and fully willing: he will not merely grant pardon, but heap grace upon you; indeed, reckoning the recovery of the lost sheep no small gain, he will celebrate a feast with the angels. The innocent and yielding age of infancy therefore accorded with divine gentleness, and it was fitting and advantageous that he began the salvation of sinners at this age, so that those whom the guilt of their own conscience terrified might be comforted by the hope of pardon readily obtained.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. O puer dulcissime, Jesu bone, quam magna est multitudo dulcedinis tuae, quam abscondisti timentibus te, et perficies sperantibus in te, qui tantam exhibuisti necdum scientibus te! Prorsus dulcedo incomparabilis, et pietas ineffabilis; ut Deum qui creavit me, videam puerum creatum propter me: Deusque majestatis et gloriae non solum similis mihi fiat corporis veritate, sed etiam miserabilis, et velut humanae indigens opis appareat aetatis infirmitate. Vere tu puer Deus, salutare vultus mei, et Deus meus, cum totus sis dulcedo, et desiderium, dulciorem tamen te mihi facit teneritudo membrorum. Haec nimirum te capabilem facit sensibus et affectibus parvulorum, qui nondum sunt te solidum idonei capere cibum. Dulce interim, dulce prorsus et sapidum, cogitare puerum Deum: quin etiam efficax et operatorium ad medendum, ac dulcorandum, si quis in nobis est, rancorem animorum, sermonum amaritudinem, morum austeritatem. Neque enim crediderim, ubi sensus et memoria fuerit hujus divinae dulcedinis, locum irae vel tristitiae inveniri: sed omnis indignatio, et amaritudo cum omni malitia tolletur a nobis. Ita fiet ut quasi modo geniti infantes, modo genitum infantem Dominum digne laudemus, et in consonantia morum et vocum, ex ore infantium et lactentium perficiatur laus infantis, et lactentis Domini Jesu Christi, cui cum Patre et Spiritu sancto laus et jubilatio, per aeterna saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. O sweetest child, good Jesus, how great is the abundance of your sweetness, which you have hidden for those who fear you and will bring to fulfilment for those who hope in you, you who have shown so much to those who do not yet know you! Truly, incomparable sweetness and ineffable loving-kindness: that I should see God who created me as a child created for me; and that the God of majesty and glory should not only become like me in the reality of his body, but even appear pitiable and as if needing human assistance through the weakness of his age. Truly, you, the child who is God, the salvation of my countenance and my God, although you are wholly sweetness and desire, the tenderness of your limbs makes you sweeter still to me. For this makes you comprehensible to the senses and affections of little children, who are not yet fit to receive you as solid food. Meanwhile it is sweet, altogether sweet and savoury, to contemplate God as a child; indeed it is effective and powerful to heal and sweeten whatever rancour of spirit, bitterness of speech, or harshness of conduct is in us. For I could not believe that where there is a sense and remembrance of this divine sweetness, room could be found for anger or sadness; rather, all indignation and bitterness, with all malice, will be taken away from us. Thus, like newborn infants, we shall worthily praise the infant Lord newly born, and in the harmony of our conduct and our voices, from the mouths of infants and nursing children shall be perfected the praise of the infant and nursing Lord Jesus Christ, to whom with the Father and the Holy Spirit be praise and jubilation, through the eternal ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-n001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints Matth. xviii, 4. The sentence beginning nisi conversi fueritis corresponds to Matthew 18:3; verse 4 continues the teaching on becoming humble like a child. The historical locator remains unchanged in the sermon."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-n002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints Psal. cxi, 6. The quotation conquassabit capita in terra multorum corresponds to Psalm 109:6 in Vulgate numbering (Psalm 110:6 in common modern numbering). The apparent reference error is retained and explained rather than silently corrected."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity Sermon I: What God as a Child Teaches Us", "topics": ["Nativity", "Incarnation", "humility", "mercy", "repentance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-nativity-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-christmas", "calendar_label": "Christmastide", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-nativity-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De nativitate Domini, sermo II, Quale donum, contemnente synagoga, suscepit Ecclesia, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 32-34.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Christmas", "Nativity of the Lord", "Isaiah 9:6", "John 1:11", "Isaiah 1:2", "Acts 13:46", "Isaiah 54:1", "Isaiah 60:5", "Isaiah 49:21", "Isaiah 54:2\u20133", "Psalm 2:8 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 33:11 (historical numbering)", "Romans 8:32", "Wisdom 7:11"], "occasion": "De nativitate Domini", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Christmas", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This second Nativity sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Taking Isaiah's \"a child is born to us, a son is given to us\", Guerric reads the gift as the barren Church's joy and the rejected Synagogue's loss: the dowry of the old covenant passes to the new Bride. He then counts what is given with the Son, and asks for right faith, a pure conscience and ready devotion to receive it. The heading names the Nativity without a particular Christmas Mass."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quale donum, contemnente synagoga, suscepit Ecclesia."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "What Gift the Church Received When the Synagogue Spurned It", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Puer natus est nobis, et filius datus est nobis (Isai. IX, 6). Inde probatur quod natus est nobis, quia scilicet datus est nobis. Illis enim recte dicitur natus, quibus videtur datus. Et omnibus quidem gratia nativitatis hujus oblata est, sed non ab omnibus suscepta est. Non enim omnium est fides. In propria venit, et sui eum non receperunt (Joan. I, 11). Inutiliter autem natus esset nisi et datus esset: frustraque factus esset filius hominis, si non recepissent eum filii hominum, quibus daret potestatem filios Dei fieri. Vae igitur, Judaea incredula, ingrata et impia: ablatum est a te regnum Dei, et datum est genti facienti fructus ejus. Ejectus Jesus est de Synagoga, sed susceptus est ab Ecclesia. Ejectus est de contradictionibus Judaeorum, constitutus est in caput Gentium. Ecce populus, quem non cognovit, servivit illi, auditu auris obedivit ei; cum de vobis filiis alienis, filiis sceleratis et parricidis, sit pii patris illa querela terribilis: Filios enutrivi et exaltavi, ipsi autem spreverunt me (Isai. I, 2). Sed, qui spernit, nunquid non et ipse spernetur? Quia repulistis verbum Dei, inquiunt apostoli, et indignos vos judicastis vitae aeternae, ecce convertimur ad gentes (Act. XIII, 46). O felix gentilitas! ecce affertur tibi Jesus: curre obviis manibus, extende manus, expande sinus, devotionem tuam probet tam actus quam effectus; filium qui tibi offertur, suscipe fide, amplexare affectu; semperque inter ubera tua commoretur. Et ecce videor mihi audire vocem exsultationis et confessionis de cunctis partibus orbis; a finibus terrae laudes audio, gloriam justi. Est autem sonus ut multitudinis, ut sonus castrorum Dei, vox una laudantium et dicentium: Puer natus est nobis, et filius datus est nobis (Isa. IX, 6). Ipsa est, ni fallor, Ecclesia laetabunda, et laudans, toto orbe terrarum; pro eo quod sibi natum, sibi datum amplexatur filium, quem oblatum, sed ablatum merito videt perfidiae Judaeorum. Lauda, sterilis, quae non pariebas, decanta laudem et jubila, quae non parturiebas: quia per hunc filium qui datus est tibi, multi erunt filii desertae, magis quam ejus, quae vinculo legis Deo maritata (Isa. LIV, 1), ei videbatur adhaerere. Tunc mirabitur et dilatabitur cor tuum (Isa. LX, 5). Tunc dices in corde tuo: Quis genuit mihi istos? Ego sterilis et non pariens, transmigrata et captiva: et istos quis enutrivit? Ego destituta et sola, et isti ubi hic erant? (Isa. XLIX, 21.) O mater incorrupta, virgo fecunda, filius, qui datus est tibi, hos tibi dedit. Est enim Filius Altissimi, cui Pater istos adoptavit, ut sint omnes conformes imaginis ejus, et sit ipse primogenitus in multis fratribus. Dilata, inquit, itaque locum tentorii tui ab ortu ad occasum, et a mari ad mare, quia ad dexteram et ad laevam penetrabis, et semen tuum gentes haereditabit (Isa. LIV, 2, 3). Iste namque unigenitus Mariae, primogenitus est omni creaturae, cui dicitur a Patre: Postula a me, et dabo tibi gentes haereditatem tuam, et possessionem tuam terminos terrae (Psal. II, 8)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. A child is born to us, and a son is given to us (Isai. IX, 6). From this it is proved that he was born to us, namely because he was given to us. For he is rightly said to be born to those to whom he appears to be given. And indeed the grace of this birth was offered to all, but it was not received by all. For not all have faith. He came into his own, and his own did not receive him (Joan. I, 11). But he would have been born in vain unless he had also been given; and the Son of man would have been made in vain, if the sons of men had not received him, to whom he might give the power to become sons of God. Woe, then, unbelieving Judea, ungrateful and impious: the kingdom of God has been taken away from you, and given to a nation that produces its fruits. Jesus was cast out of the Synagogue, but received by the Church. He was cast out amid the contradictions of the Jews; he was set up as the head of the Gentiles. Behold, a people whom he did not know served him; at the hearing of the ear they obeyed him; while concerning you, strange children, wicked and parricidal children, there stands that terrible complaint of the loving Father: 'I have nourished and raised up children, but they have despised me' (Isai. I, 2). But he who despises\u2014shall he not also himself be despised? Because you have rejected the word of God, say the apostles, and have judged yourselves unworthy of eternal life, behold, we turn to the Gentiles (Act. XIII, 46). O happy Gentile people! Behold, Jesus is brought to you: run to meet him with open hands, stretch forth your hands, open wide your bosom; let both deed and effect prove your devotion; the son who is offered to you, receive with faith, embrace with affection; and let him abide always between your breasts. And behold, I seem to hear a voice of exultation and confession from all parts of the world; from the ends of the earth I hear praises, the glory of the just one. And the sound is as of a multitude, as the sound of the camps of God, one voice of those praising and saying: 'A child is born to us, and a son is given to us' (Isa. IX, 6). This is, unless I am mistaken, the Church, rejoicing and praising throughout the whole world; because she embraces as born to her, given to her, the son whom she sees offered to the faithlessness of the Jews, but deservedly taken away from it. Praise, O barren one who did not bear; sing out praise and shout for joy, you who did not travail: because through this son who is given to you, there will be more children of the deserted one than of her who, bound by the law in marriage to God (Isa. LIV, 1), seemed to cling to him. Then your heart shall marvel and be enlarged (Isa. LX, 5). Then you will say in your heart: Who has borne these for me? I was barren and not bearing, exiled and captive: and who has reared these? I was destitute and alone, and where were these? (Isa. XLIX, 21.) O undefiled mother, fruitful virgin, the son who was given to you has given these to you. For he is the Son of the Most High, for whom the Father has adopted these, that all might be conformed to his image, and that he himself might be the firstborn among many brethren. Enlarge, he says, therefore, the place of your tent from the rising to the setting, and from sea to sea, for you shall spread abroad to the right hand and to the left, and your seed shall possess the nations as an inheritance (Isa. LIV, 2, 3). For this only-begotten of Mary is the firstborn of all creation, to whom it is said by the Father: Ask of me, and I will give you the nations for your inheritance, and the ends of the earth for your possession (Psal. II, 8).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sicut autem, suscepto Dei Filio, Ecclesia sterilis peperit plurimos, et roborata est: ita ipso ejecto, aemula ejus Synagoga, quae multos filios habebat, infirmata est. Ideo enim hodie Ecclesia gratulabunda de dato sibi filio gratiarum actione et voce laudis replet coelos: illa muta sedet in tenebris, aut certe ejulatu fatigat inferos. Misera et caeca, cur non advertit quam manifeste Deus ejus ad nos transierit; quam evidenter ipsa repudiata, solumque retinente libellum repudii, litteram dico legis, quae adulteria ipsius, coarguit, universam dotem ipsius ad novam Sponsam nuptam transtulerit? Ecce in manibus nostris tabulae matrimonii, in duobus Testamentis; ecce sacramenta unctionum, dignitas regum, ordo sacerdotum, ministeria levitarum, cultus templi et vasorum, sacrificiorum veritas, omnis denique ipsorum gloria, translata ab ipsis, collata est nobis, quia causa et veritas omnium, Filius datus est nobis. Ipse calicem legis, quae in manu est Mediatoris, ex hoc Judaeo in hunc Christianum inclinavit. Et omne quod in ea merum ac verum fuit, eliquatum nobis transfudit: faex sola, quae non est exinanita, apud Judaeos remansit; ipsam bibunt, ipsa pars calicis eorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But just as, having received the Son of God, the barren Church has borne very many children and has been strengthened, so, with him cast out, her rival the Synagogue, who had many children, has been weakened. For this reason, today the rejoicing Church fills the heavens with thanksgiving and the voice of praise for the son given to her; that other one sits mute in darkness, or indeed wearies the depths below with wailing. Wretched and blind one, why does she not notice how plainly her God has passed over to us; how evidently, having repudiated her and left her only the bill of divorce\u2014I mean the letter of the law, which convicts her of her adulteries\u2014he has transferred her whole dowry to the new Bride, now wedded? Behold, in our hands are the marriage-tablets, in the two Testaments; behold the sacraments of anointings, the dignity of kings, the order of priests, the ministries of Levites, the service of the temple and its vessels, the truth of the sacrifices, and in short, all their glory, taken away from them, has been bestowed upon us, because the cause and the truth of all these things, the Son, has been given to us. He himself has tilted the cup of the law\u2014the law which is in the hand of the Mediator\u2014away from this Jew and toward this Christian. And all that was unmixed and true in the law, he has strained out and poured over to us: only the dregs, which were not emptied, remained among the Jews; that is what they drink, that is their portion of the cup.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Sed quid verbis moror? Numquid enim potest enarrari, quae et quanta sunt data nobis, cum iste Filius datus est nobis? Nonne omne datum optimum, et omne donum perfectum ab ipso, et cum ipso, pariter ad nos descendit? nonne universas opes coeli, omnes thesauros Dei, secum nobis advexit? qui licet adhuc in ipso contineantur absconditi, multa tamen jam et magna, et quasi donativum pugnaturis erogavit; sed nihil omnia ad ea quae victoribus reservavit. Quis enumeret tot munera charismatum, tot genera virtutum, nostrorum scilicet varietatem armorum, subsidia Sacramentorum, fercula Scripturarum, gradus et ordines ministrorum; in omni aetate, sexu, et conditione, natione et lingua, tot martyrum trophaea, confessorum insignia, virginum coronas? si miraris, inquit Ecclesia, unde repente mihi pauperculae haec multitudo divitiarum, et magnitudo gloriae: inde nimirum, quia Filius Dei datus est nobis, Deus gratiae, Dominus virtutum, et Rex gloriae. Imo magis mirum esset, si non sequerentur Dominum bona sua, cum etiam ipse sit omnia bona. Inquirentes, inquit, Dominum, non minuentur omni bono (Psal. XXXIII, 11); quanto magis suscipientes? Si enim miraris ista nobis per Filium data, ego (ut plus mireris) adjiciam omnia omnino nobis cum illo esse donata. Cum enim Filium, per quem et in quo omnia subsistunt, Pater nobis donavit, quomodo non omnia nobis cum illo donavit? (Rom. VIII, 32.) Omnia nostra sunt, ait Apostolus, quia noster est auctor omnium Dominus; ut, etsi videamur tanquam nihil habentes, inveniamur tamen omnia possidentes. Vides igitur quantum, et quale datum sit Filius; quamque probabile sit quod de Sapientia, quae ipse est, legitur: Venerunt mihi omnia bona cum illa, et innumerabilis honestas per manus illius (Sap. VII, 11). Quare autem hoc ita inculco, fratres mei? Quare, nisi ut discatis gloriari, quia filius datus est nobis; et quidquid mundus potest dare, aut promittere vobis, non modo vile et indignum ad illum, sed etiam detrimentum propter illum deputetis? Non quomodo mundus dat, dat ille vobis: tantum grati estote, quia datus est vobis, et satagite ut amplius et perfectius detur vobis. Qui enim semel datus est mundo in forma carnis, et certis diebus aut horis datur fidelibus in specie panis, scilicet in esu Sacramenti sui, saepius et incertis horis datur devotis, in gustu spiritus sui. Primum, ad redemptionem; secundum, ad sanctificationem; tertium etiam ad consolationem. Primum exigit ut fides sit recta; secundum, ut conscientia pura; tertium, ut devotio prompta. Haec mentem elevat, ut gratiae occurrat; cor aperit, ut excipiat; affectum dilatat, ut plurimum ipsius capiat. Non solum autem istam, sed et omnem gratiam abundare faciat in vobis, Deus omnis gratiae, Filius, qui datus est vobis, qui est Deus benedictus in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But why do I linger over words? For can it be told, how many and how great are the things given to us, when this Son was given to us? Does not every best gift and every perfect gift come down to us from him, and with him equally? Did he not bring with him to us all the riches of heaven, all the treasures of God? For although these still remain hidden, contained within him, yet already he has paid out many and great things, as it were a donative to those about to fight; but all these are nothing compared to what he has reserved for the victors. Who could count so many gifts of graces, so many kinds of virtues\u2014that is, the variety of our weapons, the aids of the Sacraments, the courses of the Scriptures, the ranks and orders of ministers; in every age, sex, and condition, nation and tongue, so many trophies of martyrs, insignia of confessors, crowns of virgins? If you wonder, says the Church, whence suddenly came to me, poor little one, this multitude of riches and this greatness of glory: it is, of course, because the Son of God was given to us, the God of grace, the Lord of powers, and the King of glory. Indeed it would be far more wonderful if his own goods did not follow the Lord, since he himself is all good things. Those who seek the Lord, it says, shall not be diminished in any good thing (Psal. XXXIII, 11); how much more those who receive him? For if you wonder at these things given to us through the Son, I will add (that you may wonder the more) that absolutely all things have been given to us together with him. For when the Father gave us the Son, through whom and in whom all things subsist, how did he not give us all things together with him? (Rom. VIII, 32.) All things are ours, says the Apostle, because the Lord, the author of all things, is ours; so that, although we seem as having nothing, yet we are found possessing all things. You see, then, how great and of what kind a gift the Son is; and how fitting it is that what is read of Wisdom, which he himself is, applies here: All good things came to me together with her, and innumerable riches through her hands (Sap. VII, 11). Why, then, do I press this point so insistently, my brothers? Why, except that you may learn to glory in this, that the Son has been given to us; and that whatever the world can give or promise you, you should reckon not only as vile and unworthy compared to him, but even as a loss on account of him. He does not give to you as the world gives: only be grateful, because he has been given to you, and strive that he may be given to you more fully and more perfectly. For he who was given once to the world in the form of flesh, and is given to the faithful on fixed days or hours under the appearance of bread\u2014that is, in the eating of his Sacrament\u2014is given more often, and at uncertain hours, to the devout, in the tasting of his Spirit. The first was for redemption; the second, for sanctification; the third, also for consolation. The first requires right faith; the second, a pure conscience; the third, ready devotion. This last lifts up the mind, that it may go out to meet grace; opens the heart, that it may receive it; enlarges the affection, that it may grasp as much of it as possible. May God of all grace, the Son who has been given to you, who is God blessed forever and ever, cause not only this grace but every grace to abound in you. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity Sermon II: The Gift the Church Received When the Synagogue Spurned It", "topics": ["Nativity", "Incarnation", "Church and Synagogue", "faith", "grace"], "url": "sermons/guerric-nativity-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-christmas", "calendar_label": "Christmastide", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-nativity-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De nativitate Domini, sermo III, De admirabili Dei cum homine commercio, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 35-38.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Christmas", "Nativity of the Lord", "Isaiah 9:6", "Hebrews 2:16", "John 1:12", "Romans 5:2", "Psalm 84:11 (historical numbering)", "Song of Songs 1:2", "Philippians 2:7", "Isaiah 25:9", "Jeremiah 23:24", "Baruch 3:36", "Psalm 47:15 (historical numbering)", "Judges 6:39\u201340", "Jonah 3\u20134", "Isaiah 40:17", "Isaiah 45:14\u201315", "Isaiah 53:4\u20135", "Psalm 97:1\u20133 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 40:6", "Psalm 97:2 (historical numbering)", "3 Kings 3:26\u201327 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Kings)", "Matthew 11:50 (printed locator; the words are Matthew 12:50)", "Luke 1:38", "3 Kings 3:26 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Kings)"], "occasion": "De nativitate Domini", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Christmas", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This third Nativity sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. The child is born for us, not for himself or the angels, and his birth annuls the ancient condemnation of ours. Guerric dwells on God poured out in the manger, the scandal of hidden strength, and Solomon's judgment between the two mothers. Every soul that does the Lord's will is called to be a mother of Christ in others. The heading names the Nativity without a particular Christmas Mass."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De admirabili Dei cum homine commercio."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the Wonderful Exchange Between God and Man", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Puer natus nobis (Isa. IX, 6). Nobis prorsus; non enim sibi, non angelis. Sibi, inquam, natus non est, ut scilicet hac nativitate esse, aut melius esse inciperet, qui antequam temporaliter nasceretur, aeternaliter erat, et perfecta beatitudo sibi erat, quia de Deo perfecto perfectus Deus natus erat. Angelis natus non est; quia nec angelus ille qui in veritate stabat, reparatione indigebat; nec illius qui ceciderat, casus reparabilis erat. Ideo nusquam angelos apprehendit, sed semen apprehendit Abrahae (Hebr. II, 16); et qui Deus natus erat sibi, puer natus est nobis: se quodammodo relinquens, et angelos transiliens, ut ad nos usque deveniens unus fieret e nobis; et exinanitus a semetipso, minoratur ab angelis, ut aequalis fieret nobis. Qui igitur natus aeternaliter sibi et angelis erat beatitudo, natus temporaliter nobis, factus est nobis redemptio; quia nos solos laborare videbat antiquo nostrae nativitatis praejudicio. Quam felix, quam amabilis nativitas tua, puer Jesu, quae nativitatem omnium emendat, conditionem reformat, praejudicium solvit, chirographum damnatae naturae rescindit: ut si quem piget damnabiliter esse natum, possit felicissime renasci! Quotquot autem receperunt eum, dedisti eis potestatem filios Dei fieri (Joan. I, 12). Gratias agimus gratuitae atque gratiosae nativitati tuae, Deus filii hominis, per quem accessum habemus in gratiam istam in qua stamus, et gloriamur in spe gloriae filiorum Dei (Rom. V, 2). Prorsus admirabile commercium, ut sumens carnem, largiaris deitatem! commercium, inquam, charitate, non cupiditate contractum; indulgentiae quidem tuae gloriosum, sed indigentiae meae omnino lucrosum. Vere tu puer misericors, quem sola misericordia puerum fecit: licet pariter misericordia et veritas in te obviaverunt sibi (Psal. LXXXIV, 11). Vere, inquam, tu misericors puer, natus es nobis, non tibi: lucra nostra, non augmenta tua nascens de nobis quaesisti, quia ad hoc solum nasci dignatus es, ut nos minoratione tui proveheres, humiliatione glorificares. Tu exinanitus nos replesti: tu enim omnem tuae plenitudinem divinitatis in hominem transfudisti. Transfudisti, sed non confudisti. Transfusum tamen Deum in hominem non dicerem, si ad mensuram datum huic homini spiritum audirem: si de omni sua plenitudine penes Deum aliquid recedisset, quod non in hominem, cui unitus est, effudisset."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"A child is born to us\" (Isa. IX, 6). To us indeed; for he was not born for himself, nor for the angels. He was not born, I say, for himself, as though by this birth he should begin to exist, or begin to exist better\u2014he who, before he was born in time, existed eternally, and was perfect blessedness to himself, because he who was born was perfect God from perfect God. Nor was he born for the angels; for neither did that angel who stood firm in truth need restoration, nor was the fall of the one who had fallen reparable. Therefore he nowhere took hold of angels, but took hold of the seed of Abraham (Hebr. II, 16); and he who was born God for himself was born a child for us: in a certain manner leaving himself behind, and passing over the angels, so that, coming all the way to us, he might become one of us; and having emptied himself of himself, he was made lower than the angels, that he might become equal to us. He, therefore, who born eternally was blessedness to himself and to the angels, born in time for us, was made redemption for us; because he saw that we alone were laboring under the ancient condemnation attached to our birth. How happy, how lovable is your birth, child Jesus, which amends the birth of all, reforms their condition, annuls the sentence, cancels the bond of condemned nature: so that if anyone is grieved to have been born under condemnation, he may be reborn most happily! \"But as many as received him, to them you gave power to become sons of God\" (Joan. I, 12). We give thanks to your free and gracious birth, God, Son of man, through whom we have access into this grace in which we stand, and we glory in the hope of the glory of the sons of God (Rom. V, 2). Truly a wonderful exchange, that taking on flesh you should bestow deity! An exchange, I say, contracted by charity, not by greed; glorious indeed for your generosity, but wholly profitable for my need. Truly you are a merciful child, whom mercy alone made a child: although in you mercy and truth have met together equally (Psal. LXXXIV, 11). Truly, I say, you, merciful child, were born for us, not for yourself: being born of us, you sought our gain, not any increase for yourself, because you deigned to be born for this alone, that by your diminishment you might advance us, by your humiliation you might glorify us. You, emptied, filled us: for you poured out the whole fullness of your divinity into man. You poured it out, but you did not confound it. Yet I would not say that God was poured into man, if I heard that the Spirit was given to this man by measure: as if something had remained with God from his whole fullness which he had not poured out into the man to whom he was united.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Merito igitur oleum effusum nomen (Cantic. I, 2) Dei hominis, vel certe unguentum exinanitum; cum ita funditus Dei in hominem effusus sit, ut eum semetipsum exinanisse (Phil. II, 7) confiteatur fides Apostoli. Quamquam sic utique se exinanivit, ut in se ipso nihil prorsus minuerit, aut mutaverit; nec alia esse potuit naturae immutabilis exinanitio, quam naturae nostrae, quae terra inanis et vacua est, assumptio. Magna prorsus exinanitio ista in oculis videntium, ut splendor gloriae et figura paternae substantiae, nonnisi formam praeferat servilem, et in ipsa non habens speciem neque decorem. Quasi enim parum exinanitus esset, si homo solum fieret, etiam humanae carnis gloriam ita funditus in se vacuavit, sapientiam infatuavit, infirmavit virtutem, minuit magnitudinem; ut et in nativitate minimum, et in passione se exhiberet novissimum virorum: unde nec reputaverunt eum. Vis autem videre Deum exinanitum a semetipso? vide jacentem in praesepio. Ecce Deus noster (Isa. XXV, 9), Isaias ait, qui de tam longinquo vidit, et cognovit praesepe Domini sui, imo Deum in praesepi. Ecce, inquit, Deus noster. Ubi, quaeso? In praesepio, inquit. Infantulum quidem ibi invenio. Hunccine tu dicis illum esse, qui dicit, Coelum et terram ego impleo (Jerem. XXIII, 24): imo cujus majestati angusta est omnis coeli latitudo? Pannis involutum video. Hunccine tu dicis illum esse, qui gloriam et decorem induit lucis inaccessibilis, amictus incircumscripto lumine sicut vestimento? Vagientem audio. Hiccine est qui in coelis tonat, sub cujus vocis tonitruo potestates angelicae submittunt alas suas? Sic est, inquit alius propheta satisfaciens pro Isaia, omnino sic est: Hic est Deus noster, sed exinanitus est, ut te repleat, et a se quodammodo deficere voluit, ut te reficiat. Conclamat omnis chorus prophetarum uno spiritu, eademque sententia, etsi aliqui voce diversa, sic est: Hic est Deus noster, et non aestimabitur alius praeter eum (Baruch. III, 36); neque enim est alius praeter eum. Sed scio, inquit David, scio, quia non credet signo huic, sed contradicet generatio prava et adultera Judaeorum; enarrate vos, apostoli, in progenie altera Gentium. Quid? Quoniam hic quem Judaeus jacentem in praesepi nec respicit, pendentem in cruce despicit; cum miro modo nascitur, non videt; cum mira operatur, invidet; cum dira patitur, irridet: Hic, inquam, hic est Deus noster in aeternum et in saeculum saeculi, ipse reget nos in saecula (Psal. XLVII, 15)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Rightly, then, is the name of God-made-man called \"oil poured out\" (Cantic. I, 2), or indeed \"ointment emptied out\"; since God is so utterly poured out into man that the faith of the Apostle confesses that he \"emptied himself\" (Phil. II, 7). Although he indeed so emptied himself that he diminished or changed nothing at all in himself; nor could the emptying of an unchangeable nature be anything other than the assumption of our nature, which is earth empty and void. Truly this is a great emptying in the eyes of those who behold it, that the splendor of glory and the figure of the Father's substance should show forth nothing but the form of a servant, and in it have neither comeliness nor beauty. For as though it were too little an emptying to become man only, he so utterly emptied in himself even the glory of human flesh, made foolish wisdom, weakened power, diminished greatness; that both in his birth he showed himself the least, and in his passion the last of men: wherefore they did not even esteem him. Do you wish to see God emptied of himself? See him lying in the manger. \"Behold our God\" (Isa. XXV, 9), says Isaiah, who from so far off saw and recognized the manger of his Lord, indeed God in the manger. \"Behold,\" he says, \"our God.\" Where, I ask? \"In the manger,\" he says. I find there indeed a little infant. Do you say that this is he who says, \"I fill heaven and earth\" (Jerem. XXIII, 24); indeed, for whose majesty the whole breadth of heaven is too narrow? I see him wrapped in swaddling cloths. Do you say that this is he who is clothed with glory and beauty of inaccessible light, wrapped in unbounded light as with a garment? I hear him crying. Is this he who thunders in the heavens, under the thunder of whose voice the angelic powers lower their wings? \"So it is,\" says another prophet, answering for Isaiah, \"entirely so it is: This is our God, but he has emptied himself, that he might fill you, and he willed in a certain manner to fail from himself, that he might restore you.\" The whole choir of the prophets cries out together with one spirit and the same judgment, though with different voices: \"So it is: This is our God, and none other shall be accounted beside him\" (Baruch. III, 36); for indeed there is no other besides him. \"But I know,\" says David, \"I know that this perverse and adulterous generation of the Jews will not believe this sign, but will contradict it; you, apostles, declare it among another people, the Gentiles.\" What? That he whom the Jew does not even look upon lying in the manger, and despises hanging on the cross; when he is born in a wondrous manner, the Jew does not see it; when he performs wondrous works, he envies; when he suffers dreadful things, he mocks: This, I say, this is our God forever and world without end; he himself shall rule us forever (Psal. XLVII, 15).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Et ecce jam praedicantibus apostolis, testificantibus prophetis, etsi vellus siccatum est, sed areae latitudo infusa est (Judic. VI, 39, 40): etsi cucurbita aruit, Ninive salvata est (Jonae III et IV); et amissio Judaeorum, divitiae facta est mundi, et salus gentium. Adorant omnes reges terrae parvulum qui natus est nobis, omnes gentes serviunt ei: quia si qui sunt qui non serviant, vel non sint servituri, quasi non sint, sic sunt coram eo (Isa. XL, 17); gens etiam et regnum, quod non servierit ei, peribit. Jam tamen videmus et gaudemus impletum, quod per Isaiam fuerat a Patre promissum: Viri sublimes ad te transibunt, tui erunt, post te ambulabunt, te adorabunt, teque deprecabuntur. Et merito, inquit, quia in te tantum est Deus, et non est absque te Deus. Vere tu es Deus absconditus, Deus Israel Salvator (Isa. XLV, 14, 15). Si unquam manifestius, si expressius potuit dici Jesus Deus, nunquam credat Judaeus: aut certe si derogat haec esse dicta de Jesu, ostendat alium in quo sit Deus, et qui sit Deus, nec praeter eum Deus; quando quidem nec personarum Trinitatem fatetur, ut assignare possit Deum, in quo sit Deus, ita ut alius Deus praeter eum non sit. Sed te, perfide, scandalizat, quod ad pietatem aedificare debuerat; quod scilicet Deus absconditus, et homo oculis adhibitus est, tanquam lutum ex sputo, quod ad videndum Deum caecum illuminaret. Hoc, inquam, scandalo tibi est, quod in infirmitate carnis abscondita est fortitudo Dei; et in infirmitate crucis abscondita est fortitudo hominis Dei; quod inglorius inter viros aspectus ejus, et forma ejus inter filios hominum: unde nec reputasti eum, sed putasti quasi leprosum, et percussum a Deo, et humiliatum; cum Deus posuerit in eo iniquitates omnium nostrum (Isa. LIII, 4, 5). Nam et tuam quoque suscepisset, si tu eam vel ad extremum deponere voluisses. At tu, miser, non deponis, sed apponis iniquitatem super iniquitatem; super sanguinem prophetarum, sanguinem Filii Dei et Apostolorum. Impone igitur, quandoquidem ita vis, impone jugum iniquitatum tuarum, tibi et filiis tuis: mihi enim regnum Christi portare magis decet, ut meas ipse portet iniquitates. Tu porta colligationes impietatis, fasciculos deprimentes: ego portem fasciculum myrrhae, quem Maria mihi colligavit, pannisque involutum in praesepi reposuit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. And behold, now that the apostles are preaching and the prophets bearing witness, even though the fleece was dried up, yet the breadth of the threshing floor was soaked (Judic. VI, 39, 40); even though the gourd withered, Nineveh was saved (Jonae III et IV); and the loss of the Jews has become the riches of the world, and the salvation of the Gentiles. All the kings of the earth adore the little child who is born for us, all nations serve him; for if there are any who do not serve him, or who will not serve him, they are before him as though they were not (Isa. XL, 17); indeed, the nation and the kingdom that does not serve him shall perish. Now, however, we see and rejoice that what was promised by the Father through Isaiah has been fulfilled: 'Lofty men shall pass over to you, they shall be yours, they shall walk after you, they shall worship you, and shall make supplication to you.' And rightly so, he says, because God is in you alone, and there is no God apart from you. Truly you are the hidden God, the God of Israel, the Savior (Isa. XLV, 14, 15). If Jesus could ever have been called God more plainly, more expressly \u2014 let the Jew never believe it. Or indeed, if he denies that these things were said of Jesus, let him show another in whom God dwells, and who is God, and besides whom there is no other God \u2014 since he does not even confess the Trinity of Persons, so that he might be able to point out some God in whom God is, such that there is no other God besides him. But you, faithless one, are scandalized by what ought to have built up your piety: namely, that God was hidden, and the man was applied to the eyes like the clay made from spittle, to enlighten the blind so that he might see God. This, I say, is a scandal to you: that in the weakness of the flesh the strength of God was hidden; and in the weakness of the cross was hidden the strength of the God-man; that his appearance was without glory among men, and his form among the sons of men \u2014 hence you did not esteem him, but thought him as it were a leper, struck by God, and humbled; whereas God had placed upon him the iniquities of us all (Isa. LIII, 4, 5). For he would have taken up your iniquity too, if you had only been willing at last to lay it down. But you, wretched man, do not lay it down, but heap iniquity upon iniquity \u2014 upon the blood of the prophets, the blood of the Son of God, and of the Apostles. Impose, then, since you so wish, impose the yoke of your iniquities upon yourself and upon your children: for it is more fitting for me to bear the kingdom of Christ, so that he himself may bear my iniquities. You carry the bundles of impiety bound together, weights that press down; I will carry the bundle of myrrh, which Mary bound up for me, and wrapped in swaddling clothes and laid in the manger.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Vos igitur, fratres mei, qui cognovistis praesepe Domini vestri, Dominumque in praesepi, quem Israel non cognovit: vos, inquam, quibus Salvator ideo non viluit, quia misericors esse voluit; sed eo vobis tenerior in affectu, quo minor est in aspectu: cantate, et exsultate et psallite, Puer natus est nobis, filius datus est nobis. De Judaeis quidem est natus, sed nobis est natus; quia illis ablatus, nobis est datus. Cantate Domino canticum novum, quia mirabilia fecit. Notum fecit Dominus salutare suum (Psal. XCVII, 1-3): adeo ut et asinus de Gentibus cognoscat in praesepi Dominum suum, fenum sibi factum, quia omnis caro fenum (Isa. XL, 6). In conspectu namque Gentium revelavit justitiam suam (Psal. XCVII, 2): quam Judaeus ignorat, velum adhuc habens super faciem suam. Velum habet, quia zelum tenet: unde nec velum videt, sed invidet quod puer natus est nobis, filius datus est nobis. Nec invidet, quod cupiat habere sibi, sed quod cupiat perire et mihi et sibi. Mallet aemula et mala meretrix infantem necari, quam vivum mihi dari. Sed judicium nostri Salomonis, cujus sermo penetrabilior omni gladio ancipiti, scrutans corda et renes in invenienda matre non erravit: Date, inquit, Ecclesiae infantem vivum: haec est enim mater ejus (III Reg. III, 26, 27). Quicunque enim fecerit voluntatem ejus, ipse mater, et frater, et soror ejus est (Matth. XI, 50). O Domine Salomon, tu matrem dicis, ego ancillam profiteor. Ancilla Christi sum: fiat mihi secundum verbum tuum (Luc. I, 38). Et quidem matrem amore et sollicitudine me praestabo, quantum potero; sed conditionis meae semper memor ero."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. You therefore, my brothers, who have known the manger of your Lord, and the Lord in the manger, whom Israel did not know: you, I say, to whom the Savior has not thereby become worthless, because he wished to be merciful; but is all the dearer to your affection, the less he is in outward appearance \u2014 sing, and exult, and make music: 'A child is born to us, a son is given to us.' He was indeed born of the Jews, but he was born for us; because, taken away from them, he was given to us. Sing to the Lord a new song, for he has done wonderful things. The Lord has made known his salvation (Psal. XCVII, 1-3): so much so that even the ass from among the Gentiles recognizes in the manger his Lord, who has been made hay for him, since all flesh is hay (Isa. XL, 6). For in the sight of the Gentiles he has revealed his righteousness (Psal. XCVII, 2), which the Jew does not know, still having a veil over his face. He has a veil, because he clings to jealousy; hence he does not even see the veil, but envies that a child is born to us, a son is given to us. Nor does he envy in the sense of wishing to have him for himself, but in that he wishes him to perish both for me and for himself. The rival and wicked harlot would rather the infant be killed than be given alive to me. But the judgment of our Solomon, whose word is sharper than any two-edged sword, searching hearts and inmost parts, did not err in finding the mother: 'Give,' he said, 'the living child to the Church, for she is his mother' (III Reg. III, 26, 27). For whoever does his will, he is his brother, and sister, and mother (Matth. XI, 50). O Lord Solomon, you call me mother; I profess myself a handmaid. I am the handmaid of Christ: be it done to me according to your word (Luc. I, 38). And indeed I will show myself a mother in love and care, as far as I am able; but I will always remain mindful of my condition.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. O fratres, hoc nomen matris non est singulare praelatis, quamvis specialis eis incumbat cura maternae sollicitudinis et pietatis: commune est et vobis, qui facitis voluntatem Domini. Utique et vos matres estis pueri qui datus est in vobis, et in vobis, ex quo videlicet a timore Domini concepistis, et parturistis spiritum salutis. Vigila igitur, o mater sancta, vigila super curam recens nati, donec formetur in te Christus, qui natus est tibi: quia quo tenerior est, eo facilius potest perire tibi, qui nunquam perit sibi. Spiritus siquidem qui in te est, si tibi exstinctus fuerit, redit ad Deum qui dedit illum. Vigila, inquam, super curam recens nati, memor quoniam aemula tua dormiens oppressit filium, qui datus erat ei. Quae est enim illa, nisi carnalis anima, quae spiritum exstinguit negligentia et inertia. Hujusmodi homines, cum religionis amiserint fervorem, ipsius tamen sibi arrogant gloriam et nomen. Inde contentiones carnalium adversus spirituales, etiam in capitulis, ubi verus Salomon invisibiliter judex praesidet. Filius meus, inquiunt carnales, vivit, et tuus mortuus est: ego spiritum Dei habeo, tu non habes; in me vivit amor Dei, in te mortuus est: dum scilicet aemulantur sibi religionis auctoritatem, cujus spirituales habent veritatem; ut subducta eis auctoritate, consuetudines inducant pro sui sensus libidine. Et revera quidem mater vult aemulae dari vivum et integrum infantem, non invidens ei gloriam, dummodo habeat et virtutem, sed illa, Nec mihi, inquit, nec tibi sit, sed dividatur (III Reg. III, 26); quia nimirum sanctitatis sibi cupit retinere honorem, et aliis relinquere laborem. Sed non errat qui judicat, etsi aliquandiu dissimulat. Gladius Salomonis matrem invenit, cui adjudicat indivisum, sicut affectum charitatis, sic effectum potestatis; sicut fervorem operandi, sic favorem imperandi. Vos itaque, fratres, in quibus fides per dilectionem operans nata est de Spiritu sancto, custodite eam, pascite, nutrite, tanquam parvulum Jesum, donec formetur in vobis puer qui natus est nobis; qui non solum nascendo, sed vivendo et moriendo, formam cui conformemur tradidit nobis: memor quippe semper, quod non nisi nobis natus fuit, nec nisi nobis vivere voluit, nec mori nisi pro nobis, qui pro se non habuit; ut et nos per ipsum renasceremur, secundum ipsum viveremus; in ipso moreremur, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. O brothers, this name of mother is not reserved to prelates alone, although a special care of maternal solicitude and devotion falls upon them: it is common also to you, who do the will of the Lord. Indeed you too are mothers of the child who is given in you, and in you, from the moment, that is, when you conceived from the fear of the Lord and brought forth the spirit of salvation. Watch, therefore, O holy mother, watch over the care of the newly born, until Christ, who was born for you, be formed in you: for the more tender he is, the more easily he can perish for you, though he never perishes for himself. For the Spirit who is in you, if he be extinguished in you, returns to God who gave him. Watch, I say, over the care of the newly born, remembering that your rival, while sleeping, overlay the son who had been given to her. For who is that rival but the carnal soul, which extinguishes the spirit through negligence and sloth? Such men, when they have lost the fervor of religion, nonetheless claim for themselves its glory and name. Hence arise contentions of carnal men against spiritual men, even in chapters, where the true Solomon invisibly presides as judge. 'My son,' say the carnal, 'lives, and yours is dead: I have the Spirit of God, you do not; in me the love of God lives, in you it is dead'\u2014while, that is, they jealously claim for themselves the authority of religion, of which the spiritual possess the truth; so that, authority having been wrested from them, they may introduce customs according to the lust of their own judgment. And indeed the true mother wishes the living and whole infant to be given to her rival, not envying her the glory, provided she also have the virtue; but that other says, 'Let it be neither mine nor yours, but let it be divided' (III Reg. III, 26); because, of course, she desires to keep for herself the honor of holiness, and leave to others the labor. But he who judges does not err, even though for a while he dissembles. The sword of Solomon finds out the mother, to whom he awards the child undivided, both the affection of charity and the effect of power; both the fervor of working and the favor of commanding. You therefore, brothers, in whom faith working through love has been born of the Holy Spirit, guard it, feed it, nourish it, as the little Jesus, until the child who is born to us be formed in you; who delivered to us not only by being born, but by living and dying, a form to which we should be conformed: ever mindful that he was born for us alone, and willed to live for us alone, and to die for none but us\u2014he who had no need to die for himself\u2014so that through him we might be reborn, according to him we might live, and in him we might die, who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity Sermon III: On the Wonderful Exchange Between God and Man", "topics": ["Nativity", "Incarnation", "self-emptying", "humility", "spiritual motherhood"], "url": "sermons/guerric-nativity-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-christmas", "calendar_label": "Christmastide", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-nativity-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De nativitate Domini, sermo IV, De plenitudine temporis a Christo allata, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 38-43.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Christmas", "Nativity of the Lord", "Galatians 4:4", "Colossians 2:9", "Psalm 84:2, 13 (historical numbering)", "Romans 14:17", "Psalm 118:64 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 64:12 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 35:7\u20138 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 106:15 (historical numbering)", "Job 6:5", "Proverbs 20:14", "Romans 8:18", "Psalm 70:15\u201316 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 142:1\u20132 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 70:16 (historical numbering)", "2 Corinthians 12:10", "2 Corinthians 6:2", "Ephesians 5:16", "Song of Songs 8:7", "John 3:17", "Galatians 6:1\u20139 (printed locator; see note)", "Wisdom 4:13", "Ecclesiastes 7:11", "Sirach 14:14", "Isaiah 9:6", "Sirach 14:8, 10", "Psalm 22:1\u20132 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "De nativitate Domini", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Christmas", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This fourth Nativity sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Beginning from Paul's fullness of time, Guerric reads it as an abundance of grace, the fulfillment of prophecy and the mature age of faith. Salvation is bought at a trifling price, yet the present age meets it with complaint and ingratitude. He closes with thanks for the Word made hay in the manger for beasts as well as men. An editorial note explains an inaccurate printed locator. The heading names the Nativity without a particular Christmas Mass."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De plenitudine temporis a Christo allata."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the Fullness of Time Brought by Christ", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Ecce jam plenitudo temporis (Galat. IV, 4). Plenitudo ista temporis apud Paulum accipitur, seu propter abundantiam gratiae, seu propter adimpletionem praecedentis prophetiae, seu propter pleniorem aetatem fidei adultae. De singulis videamus. Omnium plenitudo bonorum Christus Dominus est: quippe qui omnibus thesauris sapientiae et scientiae Dei, totiusque gratiae plenus est; imo in quo omnis plenitudo divinitatis habitat corporaliter (Coloss. II, 9). Viderunt apostoli plenum gratiae et veritatis, et de plenitudine ejus omnes acceperunt: adeo ut novissimus eorum et abortivus Paulus, vas electionis, de plenitudine quam acceperat, disseminaret ubique plenitudinem gratiae et veritatis, clamans et dicens: Ecce jam venit plenitudo temporis (Galat. IV, 4). Cum enim plenus omnibus bonis natus esset temporaliter auctor temporis, quidni plenitudinem attulisset temporis? Cum coeli rorarent desuper, et nubes pluerent Justum, Salvatorem terra germinaret, tanta supernae benedictionis ubertas quidni fecunditatem terris omnibus praestaret? Benedixisti, Domine, terram tuam, dedisti benignitatem, et terra nostra dedit fructum suum (Psal. LXXXIV, 2, 13); videlicet de uno grano frumenti, quod germinavit alvus Virginis, ubique terrarum pullulante copiosa messe fidelium. Nolo enim hanc plenitudinem temporis requiras in copia bonorum temporalium, sed aeternorum; non in proventu agrorum, sed coelorum. Si coeli rorant desuper, et nubes pluunt Justum; si terra germinat Salvatorem, et justitia oritur simul; si denique oritur in diebus Domini non solum justitia, sed et abundantia pacis: feliciora tempora ne quaesieris, quando et regnum Dei nihil aliud est quam justitia, et pax, et gaudium in Spiritu sancto (Rom. XIV, 17), quod de illis procedit. Nam et nostrorum temporum ille status judicatur optimus, atque pulcherrimus, cum et justitia moribus imponit disciplinam, et abundantia cum pace quietem vitae praestat et laetitiam. Denique misericordia Domini plena est terra (Psal. CXVIII, 64): benedixit Dominus coronae anni benignitatis suae, et campi ejus repleti sunt ubertate (Psal. LXIV, 12) totius gratiae spiritualis. Et quis, nisi ingratus, plenitudinem neget esse temporis? Quae unquam aurea aetas simile aliquid habuit hujus plenitudini temporis, quando panis angelorum, omnem habens saporem suavitatis et omne delectamentum, apponitur etiam jumentis; et non solum homines, sed et jumenta, cibo pascuntur coelesti? Homines enim et jumenta salvabis, Domine, quemadmodum multiplicasti misericordiam tuam, Deus (Psal. XXXV, 7, 8). Infinite multiplex haec tua, Deus, misericordia, ut qui panis angelorum, non solum hominum dites ac beatifices mensas, sed etiam fenum factus, jumentorum repleas praesepia. Confiteris, o Domine sapientia misericors, quia sapientibus et insipientibus debitor es, ut qui utrosque fecisti, utrisque necessariam alimoniam procures; et tam homines quam jumenta, tam spirituales quam animales, suo quemque gradu et ordine salves. Confiteantur igitur Domino misericordiae ejus, et mirabilia ejus filiis hominum (Psal. CVI, 15): quia misit Verbum suum carnem factum, medicinam et escam omnium; ut etiam incapaces Verbi, sanentur tamen et satientur carne Verbi. Edant itaque pauperes et saturentur, et confiteantur venisse beatam temporis plenitudinem; quando et in praesepibus suis paratum sine sudore vultus sui panem inveniunt coelestem. Numquid ergo mugiet bos, cum ante praesepe sic plenum steterit? (Job VI, 5.) Mugiat, sed prae gaudio spiritus in jubilo cordis; quia praesepe Domini sui, imo Dominum in praesepi suo cognovit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Behold, now is the fullness of time (Galat. IV, 4). This \"fullness of time\" is taken by Paul in reference either to the abundance of grace, or to the fulfillment of earlier prophecy, or to the fuller age of mature faith. Let us look at each in turn. Christ the Lord is the fullness of all good things: for he is full of all the treasures of the wisdom and knowledge of God, and of all grace; indeed, in him dwells all the fullness of the Godhead bodily (Coloss. II, 9). The apostles saw him full of grace and truth, and from his fullness all received: so much so that the last of them and the one born as it were out of due time, Paul, the chosen vessel, from the fullness he had received, scattered everywhere the fullness of grace and truth, crying out and saying: Behold, now the fullness of time has come (Galat. IV, 4). For since the author of time, full of all good things, was born in time, why should he not have brought the fullness of time? When the heavens dropped dew from above, and the clouds rained down the Just One, and the earth brought forth the Savior as its growth, why should such abundance of heavenly blessing not bring fruitfulness to all the earth? You have blessed, O Lord, your land; you have given your bounty, and our land has yielded its fruit (Psal. LXXXIV, 2, 13); namely, from the one grain of wheat which sprouted from the Virgin's womb, a plentiful harvest of the faithful has sprung up throughout all the earth. For I do not want you to seek this fullness of time in an abundance of temporal goods, but of eternal ones; not in the yield of fields, but of heavens. If the heavens drop dew from above, and the clouds rain down the Just One; if the earth brings forth the Savior as its sprout, and righteousness arises at the same time; and if, finally, in the days of the Lord there arises not only righteousness but also abundance of peace: do not seek for happier times, since the kingdom of God is nothing other than righteousness, and peace, and joy in the Holy Spirit (Rom. XIV, 17), which proceeds from these things. For indeed that condition of our times is judged best and most beautiful, when righteousness imposes discipline on morals, and abundance together with peace provides quiet of life and gladness. In short, the earth is full of the mercy of the Lord (Psal. CXVIII, 64): the Lord has blessed the crown of the year with his bounty, and his fields are filled with the abundance (Psal. LXIV, 12) of all spiritual grace. And who, unless he is ungrateful, would deny that this is the fullness of time? What golden age ever possessed anything like this fullness of time, when the bread of angels, having every taste of sweetness and every delight, is set before even the beasts of burden; and not only men, but even beasts of burden, are fed with heavenly food? For you will save men and beasts, O Lord; O how you have multiplied your mercy, O God (Psal. XXXV, 7, 8). Infinitely manifold is this your mercy, O God, that you who are the bread of angels not only enrich and make blessed the tables of men, but, having become hay, fill the mangers of beasts of burden. You confess, O Lord, merciful Wisdom, that you are debtor both to the wise and to the foolish, since, having made both, you provide necessary nourishment for both; and you save men as well as beasts, the spiritual as well as the animal, each in its own degree and order. Let his mercies therefore give praise to the Lord, and his wonders to the children of men (Psal. CVI, 15): for he sent his Word made flesh, the medicine and food of all; so that even those incapable of the Word might nevertheless be healed and satisfied by the flesh of the Word. Let the poor therefore eat and be filled, and confess that the blessed fullness of time has come; since even in their mangers they find heavenly bread prepared without the sweat of their brow. Will the ox then bellow, when he has stood before a manger so full? (Job VI, 5.) Let him bellow\u2014but let it be for the joy of spirit, in the jubilation of the heart; because he has recognized the manger of his Lord, indeed, the Lord himself in his manger.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Libet autem adhuc manifestius videre, Christus veniens e coelis quantam plenitudinem temporis advexerit, quam facili et exiguo pretio omne pretiosum comparari posse decreverit. Duobus minutis, seu calice aquae frigidae, aut certe sola bona voluntate, jam a diebus ejus emitur regnum coelorum, quanquam etiam nunc vix inveniat emptorem in tanta multitudine divitum. Proh pudor! etiam nos ipsi, qui jam emere coepimus, jam tabulas emptionis conscripsimus, jam pignus haereditatis accepimus; etiam nos, inquam, toties a pacto resilimus, ac pene complaciti poenitemus, querimur, murmuramus, quasi in negotio simus circumventi; quasi magnum sit illud prope nihil, quod exigitur a nobis. Bene de nobis prophetavit Scriptura: Malum est; malum est, dicit omnis emptor, et cum recesserit, gloriabitur (Prov. XX, 14). An non merito poterit gloriari, qui immensum illud et aeternum pondus gloriae, hoc levi et momentaneo tribulationis suae comparaverit, qui sic pro nihilo salvus factus fuerit? At nunc quotidianas hinc inde querelas et molesta murmura audias: Malum hoc, malum illud; grave hoc, importabile illud; quis tanta ac talia ferat? Ita nemo fere est qui digne pro merito sui rem aestimans dicat: Non sunt condignae passiones hujus temporis ad futuram gloriam quae revelabitur in nobis (Rom. VIII, 18), nisi tunc demum cum coeperit revelari. Tunc plane gloriabitur, qui modo detrahit, ac de pretio se gravari gemit; cum scilicet de foro, id est, de mundo hoc, ubi contractus celebratur hujus commercii, recesserit et in domum aeternitatis suae, secum ferens rem tantam tantillo emptam, redierit. Homo plane secundum cor Dei erat ille, homo simplex sine querela, qui ab hac arte, imo inertia et infidelitate emptorum alienus erat; Davidem dico, qui dicebat: Quoniam non cognovi litteraturam, Domine, memorabor justitiae tuae solius (Psal. LXX, 15, 16). Justitiae meae nullatenus memorabor, ut labores meos exaggerem, merita magnificem; potius memorabor justitiae tuae solius, qui gratis obligasti te mihi promissorem. In veritate tua exaudi me, in justitia tua, et non intres in judicium cum servo tuo (Psal. CXLII, 1, 2): qui, si voluero me justificare, os meum condemnabit me. Per hoc enim, inquit, introibo in potentias Domini (Psal. LXX, 16); quia scilicet justitias meas non statuam: per hoc potentem me faciet, et nunc in praelio, et tunc in regno; quia semper infirmitates meas confitebor. Cum enim infirmor, tunc potens sum (II Cor. XII, 10). Sicut ergo prudenter apud Deum causam agit, qui justitiae suae immemor totum misericordiae committit; sic prudenter emit, qui omnem fictionem, atque avaritiam negotiationis scire renuit; qui inventa illa pretiosa margarita, non solum omnia sua, sed et seipsum ultro ac desideranter inpendit. At nos tepidi, ficti, ingrati, indevoti, voluptatum amatores magis quam Dei, vix ab illa voce infidelis et nequissimi murmuris, Malum est, malum est (Prov. XX, 14), valemus compesci; etiam postquam gustavimus, et vidimus, sicut de muliere forti scriptum est, quoniam bona est negotiatio nostra."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But I wish to see still more clearly how great a fullness of time Christ brought when he came from the heavens, and with how easy and small a price he determined that everything precious could be purchased. With two mites, or a cup of cold water, or indeed with good will alone, the kingdom of heaven has been bought ever since his days \u2014 though even now it scarcely finds a buyer amid so great a multitude of the rich. For shame! Even we ourselves, who have already begun to buy, who have already drawn up the tablets of purchase, who have already received the pledge of inheritance \u2014 even we, I say, so often draw back from the bargain, and, having almost agreed, repent, complain, murmur, as though we had been cheated in the transaction; as though that were a great thing \u2014 that next-to-nothing which is required of us. Scripture prophesied well concerning us: \"It is bad, it is bad,\" says every buyer, and when he has gone away, he will boast (Prov. XX, 14). And will he not rightly be able to boast, who has purchased that immense and eternal weight of glory with this light and momentary tribulation of his, who has thus been saved for next to nothing? But now you may hear on every side daily complaints and troublesome murmurs: \"This is bad, that is bad; this is heavy, that is unbearable; who can bear such great and such things?\" Thus there is scarcely anyone who, rightly valuing the matter according to its worth, says: \"The sufferings of this time are not worthy to be compared with the future glory that shall be revealed in us\" (Rom. VIII, 18) \u2014 except only then, when it has begun to be revealed. Then clearly he will boast, who now disparages it and groans that he is burdened by the price; that is, when he has departed from the marketplace \u2014 that is, from this world, where the contract of this trade is transacted \u2014 and has returned to the house of his eternity, carrying with him so great a thing bought for so small a price. That man was truly a man after God's own heart, a simple man without complaint, who was a stranger to this art \u2014 or rather, to this sluggishness and faithlessness of buyers; I mean David, who said: \"Because I have not known letters, O Lord, I will be mindful of your justice alone\" (Psal. LXX, 15, 16). \"I will by no means be mindful of my own justice, so as to exaggerate my labors or magnify my merits; rather I will be mindful of your justice alone, who freely bound yourself to me as one who promises.\" \"Hear me in your truth, in your justice, and do not enter into judgment with your servant\" (Psal. CXLII, 1, 2): for if I wished to justify myself, my own mouth would condemn me. \"For by this,\" he says, \"I will enter into the powers of the Lord\" (Psal. LXX, 16); that is, because I will not establish my own righteousness: by this he will make me powerful, both now in the battle and then in the kingdom; because I will always confess my weaknesses. \"For when I am weak, then I am powerful\" (II Cor. XII, 10). Just as, therefore, he pleads his case wisely before God who, forgetful of his own righteousness, commits everything to mercy, so he buys wisely who refuses to know any pretense or greed in the trading; who, having found that precious pearl, spends not only all that he has, but also himself, freely and eagerly. But we, lukewarm, false, ungrateful, undevout, lovers of pleasures more than of God, can scarcely restrain ourselves from that voice of faithless and most wicked murmuring, \"It is bad, it is bad\" (Prov. XX, 14) \u2014 even after we have tasted and seen, as it is written of the valiant woman, that our trading is good.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Adhuc ferebat me quidam implacabilis zelus adversus ingratitudinem et infidelitatem hujus temporis nostri; sed revocat me, de qua sermonem institui, sancta ac beata plenitudo temporis Christi, qua etiam hodie plenius pascendi estis. Conjunctim tamen currunt, pariterque feruntur haec duo tempora tam diversa, tamque adversa, tempus scilicet gratiae, et tempus malitiae; cum tamen nonnisi unum tempus inveniatur utriusque. Si enim modo non esset tempus gratiae, non diceret Apostolus: Ecce nunc tempus acceptabile, ecce nunc dies salutis (II Cor. VI, 2). Et iterum, si modo non esset tempus malitiae, non diceret idem apostolus: Redimentes tempus, quoniam dies mali sunt (Ephes. V, 16). Confligunt igitur in tempore uno gratia et ingratitudo, velut in stadio uno. Jam olim siquidem sapientia pugnat adversus malitiam, propter quod et nunc in hujus mundi descendit aream. Pugnat, inquam, nolens vinci a malo, sed volens vincere in bono malum. Abundabat iniquitas, et licet humana congelata esset, divina tamen non frigescebat charitas. Magnus ille ardor charitatis, et revera, sicut scriptum est, aquis multis inexstinguibilis (Cantic. VIII, 7); quod cum extremum jam judicium postularet multitudo peccatorum, Deus tamen Filium suum misit, non ut judicaret mundum, sed ut salvaretur per ipsum (Joan. III, 17). Cum igitur jam pene consummata mundi malitia finem indicaret temporis, Redemptoris adventus novam et inopinatam plenitudinem temporis rebus infudit humanis. Cum enim mundus consenuisset, atque prope interitum esset temporis aetate, subito ad adventum Conditoris sui, nova et insperata renovatus est virtutis juventute, fideique quodam juvenili calore. Fides siquidem, cujus primaevum tempus, velut quaedam pueritia, fuit in Patriarchis, qui exorti sunt primo mane Ecclesiae nascentis; adolescentia vero in Prophetis; ad plenitudinem demum juvenilis excrevit roboris in Apostolis, quando et calorem suae virtutis mundo spectabilem praebuit in tam praeclaris, atque fortissimis innumerabilium martyrum triumphis. Hanc adultam ac plenam aetatem fidei vocat Apostolus plenitudinem temporis, quando scilicet hi qui sub paedagogo legis erant, nihilque a servo differebant, dum erant parvuli, jam adulti libertatem filiorum receperunt per Unigenitum Patris (Galat. VI, 1-9). Qui nimirum, ut nulla plenitudo suo deesset tempori, plenus tam gratia quam veritate venit, quatenus et per gratiam impleri faceret legis mandata, et per veritatem impleret ipse promissa; adeo ut quidquid etiam universis retro saeculis mystice gestum aut dictum fuit, totum verius ac plenius impletum sit in hac plenitudine temporis. Nam et hic ipse dies tot sacramenta, tot oracula, quasi recapitulata in brevi hodie exhibuit, ut de eo vere dici possit: Consummatus in brevi, explevit tempora multa (Sap. IV, 13)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. A certain relentless zeal against the ingratitude and unfaithfulness of our present time was still carrying me along; but the holy and blessed fullness of the time of Christ, on which I began this sermon, and with which you are to be fed more fully even today, calls me back. Yet these two times, so different and so opposed to each other\u2014namely, the time of grace and the time of malice\u2014run together and are carried along jointly, even though there is found to be but one single time for both. For if the present were not the time of grace, the Apostle would not say: 'Behold, now is the acceptable time; behold, now is the day of salvation' (II Cor. VI, 2). And again, if the present were not the time of malice, the same Apostle would not say: 'Redeeming the time, because the days are evil' (Ephes. V, 16). Grace and ingratitude, therefore, clash together in one single time, as if in one single arena. For wisdom has long since been fighting against malice, and for this reason it has now also come down into the arena of this world. It fights, I say, unwilling to be conquered by evil, but willing to conquer evil with good. Iniquity was abounding, and although human charity had grown cold, divine charity nevertheless did not grow cold. That great ardor of charity is indeed, as it is written, unquenchable by many waters (Cantic. VIII, 7); for though the multitude of sins now demanded the final judgment, yet God sent his Son, not to judge the world, but that the world might be saved through him (Joan. III, 17). Therefore, when the malice of the world, now almost complete, was signaling the end of time, the coming of the Redeemer poured out upon human affairs a new and unexpected fullness of time. For when the world had grown old, and was near destruction through the age of time, suddenly, at the coming of its Founder, it was renewed with a new and unhoped-for youthfulness of virtue, and with a certain youthful warmth of faith. For faith, whose earliest age\u2014a kind of childhood, as it were\u2014was in the Patriarchs, who arose in the first morning of the Church then being born; whose adolescence was in the Prophets; at last grew up into the fullness of youthful strength in the Apostles, when it also displayed to the world the warmth of its vigor, made visible in the splendid and most valiant triumphs of countless martyrs. This mature and full age of faith the Apostle calls the fullness of time\u2014namely, when those who were under the schoolmaster of the law, and who, while they were children, differed in nothing from a slave, now grown up, received the freedom of sons through the Only-begotten of the Father (Galat. VI, 1-9). He indeed, so that no fullness might be lacking to his own time, came full both of grace and of truth, so that through grace he might cause the commands of the law to be fulfilled, and through truth he himself might fulfill the promises; to such a degree that whatever had been done or said mystically throughout all the ages gone before was fulfilled more truly and more fully in this fullness of time. For even this very day has displayed, as it were recapitulated in brief, so many mysteries, so many oracles, that of it may truly be said: 'Being perfected in a short time, he fulfilled many years' (Sap. IV, 13).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Cum ergo tot modis constet quia venit beata plenitudo temporis, recte querelas insipientium Salomon compescuit, dicens: Ne dicas, Quid, putas, causae est quod meliora fuere tempora priora quam nunc sunt? Stulta est, inquit, hujusmodi interrogatio (Eccle. VII, 11), cum manifeste felicissima tempora fecerit hominibus gratia Dei; ingratitudo autem hominum pessima fecerit ea sibi. O tempus acceptabile, cui tam beatum, tam auspicatum initium dedit dies iste, quo natus conditor aeternus temporis, aeternitatem mortalibus nuntiavit! O vere dies salutis dies iste, quo nata salus mundi se ipsam poculum salutare miscuit aegrotis! O fratres, de die isto loquebatur, ni fallor, Sapiens filio suo: Non defrauderis a die bono. Et exponens quid sit non defraudari: Particula, inquit, boni doni non te praetereat (Eccli. XIV, 14). Diem quippe bonum pro eo dicit, quia datum est hodie donum bonum, prorsus datum optimum, donumque perfectum, descendens a Patre luminum; quod quale sit nemo scit, nisi qui accepit. At vos, fratres, spiritum qui ex Deo est accepistis, ut sciatis quae a Deo donata sunt vobis, qui ex sententia cordium cantatis: Filius datus est nobis (Isai. IX, 6). Filius iste, panis est filiorum, qui de seipso hodie beatum ac solemne universae familiae Patris exhibet epulum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Since, then, it is established in so many ways that the blessed fullness of time has come, Solomon rightly checked the complaints of fools, saying: 'Do not say, What do you suppose is the reason that former times were better than now are?' 'Such a question,' he says, 'is foolish' (Eccle. VII, 11), since plainly the grace of God has made the times most happy for mankind, but the ingratitude of men has made those same times most wretched for themselves. O acceptable time, to which this day gave so blessed, so auspicious a beginning\u2014the day on which the eternal Founder of time was born and announced eternity to mortals! O truly this day is the day of salvation, on which the salvation of the world, being born, mixed itself as a saving cup for the sick! O brothers, it was of this day, unless I am mistaken, that the Wise Man spoke to his son: 'Do not defraud yourself of a good day.' And explaining what it means not to be defrauded, he says: 'Let not the portion of a good gift pass you by' (Eccli. XIV, 14). He calls it a good day precisely because today a good gift was given\u2014indeed the very best gift given, and a perfect gift, descending from the Father of lights\u2014a gift of such a kind that no one knows it except the one who has received it. But you, brothers, have received the Spirit who is from God, that you may know the things that have been given to you by God, you who sing from the conviction of your hearts: 'A Son is given to us' (Isai. IX, 6). This Son is the bread of sons, who today, from himself, provides a blessed and solemn banquet for the whole family of the Father.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Gratias tibi, Pater misericordiarum, qui panem nostrum quotidianum das nobis hodie, tantaque largitate aperuisti manum tuam ad implendum omne animal benedictione, ut ipsa etiam benedictione plenum inveniatur eorum praesepe. Quam miserum, quam brutum ac stolidum, imo quam sibi nequam et invidum illud animal, quod sese isto die bono defraudat, ut eum particula doni boni praetereat, ut scilicet expers gratiae coelestis, quae ei proposita est, fiat, diemque totius refectionis et gaudii tristi ac jejuno corde praetereat; quasi necdum venerit copiosissima plenitudo temporis, nec praesepia simplicium et humilium panis impleverit coelestis! Hujusmodi hominem sibi nequam et invidum, Deo ingratum et injuriosum, notat Sapientia, cum ait: Oculus malus ad mala opera, et non saturabitur pane, indigens et in tristitia erit super mensam suam. Ideo, inquit, non saturabitur bonis anima ipsius, quia oculus ejus ad mala, nec revertetur oculus ejus, ut videat bona, ut cum pietate et fide consideret quae ei apposita sunt in magna divitis mensa. Nequam est enim, inquit, oculus lividi, avertens faciem suam et despiciens animam suam (Eccli. XIV, 10, 8). Procul dubio, fratres, si faciem non avertimus a consideratione illius qui jacet in praesepi, ipso intuitu possumus felicissime pasci, dicemusque: Dominus pascit me, et nihil mihi deerit; in loco pascuae, ibi me collocavit (Psal. XXII, 1, 2). Tunc plane sciemus quia venit desideratissima plenitudo temporis, in quo misit Deus Filium suum, per quem tanta jam replemur plenitudine bonorum: ipsi benedictio et gratiarum actio, et nunc, et per infinita saecula saeculorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Thanks be to you, Father of mercies, who give us our daily bread today, and who with such great generosity have opened your hand to fill every living creature with blessing, that even their manger is found full of that same blessing. How wretched, how brutish and foolish\u2014indeed how wicked and grudging toward itself\u2014is that creature which cheats itself on this good day, so that the portion of the good gift passes it by; so that, lacking the heavenly grace set before it, it lets the day of all refreshment and joy pass by with a sad and fasting heart, as though the most abundant fullness of time had not yet come, and the heavenly bread had not filled the mangers of the simple and the humble! Wisdom marks out such a man, wicked and grudging toward himself, ungrateful and offensive toward God, when it says: 'An evil eye is bent toward evil deeds, and he shall not be satisfied with bread; he shall be needy and sorrowful at his own table.' 'Therefore,' it says, 'his soul shall not be filled with good things, because his eye is bent toward evil things, nor will his eye turn back to see good things,' so that he might with devotion and faith consider what has been set before him at the great table of the rich host. 'For wicked,' it says, 'is the eye of the envious man, turning away his face and despising his own soul' (Eccli. XIV, 10, 8). Without doubt, brothers, if we do not turn our face away from gazing on him who lies in the manger, by that very gaze we can be most happily fed, and we shall say: 'The Lord feeds me, and I shall want nothing; in a place of pasture, there he has set me' (Psal. XXII, 1, 2). Then we shall clearly know that the most longed-for fullness of time has come, in which God sent his Son, through whom we are now filled with so great a fullness of good things: to him be blessing and thanksgiving, both now and through the endless ages of ages.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints the reference as Galat. VI, 1-9; the passage on the heir under tutors and the fullness of time is Galatians 4:1-9. The printed reference is kept."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity Sermon IV: On the Fullness of Time Brought by Christ", "topics": ["Nativity", "fullness of time", "grace", "ingratitude", "Christmas joy"], "url": "sermons/guerric-nativity-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-christmas", "calendar_label": "Christmastide", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-nativity-05", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De nativitate Domini, sermo V, De mira Dei providentia in nativitate Christi, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 43-46.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Christmas", "Nativity of the Lord", "Luke 2:15", "John 20:29", "John 1:1 (printed locator; the words are 1 John 1:1)", "John 1:14", "Psalm 47:9 (historical numbering)", "Wisdom 18:15", "Psalm 11:5 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 10:23", "Matthew 11:25\u201326", "Isaiah 9:6", "Isaiah 5:18", "Matthew 19:14", "Psalm 83:11 (historical numbering)", "1 Peter 3:15", "James 1:21"], "occasion": "De nativitate Domini", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Christmas", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This fifth Nativity sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. The brothers came to hear the Word of God, but at Bethlehem they may also see it. Guerric draws lessons from the infant Word's silence, poverty and humility, and from the shepherds' simple faith. He ends at the altar, where the same body lies hidden under humble forms as the infant was wrapped in cloths. The heading names the Nativity without a particular Christmas Mass."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO V."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON V.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De mira Dei providentia in nativitate Christi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On God's Wonderful Providence in the Birth of Christ", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Convenistis, fratres, ad audiendum verbum Dei. Deus autem aliquid melius providit nobis, ut non solum audire, sed etiam videre datum nobis hodie sit Verbum Dei, si modo transeamus usque Bethlehem, et videamus hoc verbum quod fecit Dominus, et ostendit nobis (Luc. II, 15). Noverat hominum sensus invisibilium incapaces, coelestium indocibiles, ad fidem quoque difficiles, nisi res ipsa de qua fides suadetur, visibiliter ipsis sensibus convincendis ingeratur. Nam etsi fides est ex auditu, multo tamen proclivius ac promptius ex visu: sicut illius exemplo docemur, cui dicitur, Quia vidisti me, credidisti (Joan. XX, 29); qui scilicet dum audiebas incredulus fuisti. Quia enim difficilius audita, quam visa creduntur, ideo merito fides illorum qui non viderunt, a Domino beata praedicatur; quippe qui plus detulerint auctoritati sermonis, quam experientiae proprii sensus, aut rationis. Deus tamen per omnia volens satisfacere tarditati nostrae, Verbum suum quod prius fecerat audibile, hodie fecit nobis etiam visibile, imo et tractabile; ut et quidam ex nobis potuerint dicere: Quod fuit ab initio, quod audivimus, quod vidimus oculis nostris, quod perspeximus, et manus nostrae tractaverunt de Verbo vitae (Joan. I, 1). Fuit ab initio illius, quae sine initio est, aeternitatis; audivimus promissum ab initio temporis; vidimus et contrectavimus exhibitum in fine temporis. Alias autem invenies quod non solum visibile et tractabile, sed etiam gustabile et odorabile nobis Verbum Dei factum sit, quippe quod per omnes vias sensuum, aditum sibi ad animam quaesierit, quatenus sicut per sensus mors intraverat, sic per eosdem rediret et vita. Quod ergo Verbum caro factum est (Joan. I, 14), nobis qui toti sumus caro, factum est; ut qui antea Verbum Dei tantum poteramus audire, jam carnem factum et videre possimus et gustare, omnesque sensus in testimonium auditus convocare; quatenus omnes nostri sensus consensu uno, et voce una confiteantur, Sicut audivimus, sic vidimus (Psal. XLVII, 9). Incomparabiliter tamen amplius tunc concessum est visui, quam unquam fuerat auditui, cum modo videatur Verbum, quod Deus est, et antea magnum haberetur audire verbum quodcunque ex Deo. Et verbum quidem quod ex Deo est, vidi aliquando, fratres, cum taedio audiri; sed Verbum quod Deus est, nunquid poterit nisi cum gaudio videri? In me primum sententiam feram: quia Verbum, quod Deus est, cum se hodie mihi videndum praebeat in eo quod sum, si me non laetificat, impius sum; si non aedificat, reprobus sum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. You have come together, brothers, to hear the word of God. But God has provided something better for us, so that today it is given to us not only to hear but also to see the Word of God \u2014 if only we go over as far as Bethlehem, and see this word which the Lord has made, and shown to us (Luc. II, 15). He knew that the senses of men are incapable of invisible things, unteachable in heavenly things, and also slow to believe, unless the very thing of which faith is being persuaded is impressed visibly upon the senses themselves so as to convince them. For although faith is from hearing, yet it comes much more readily and easily from sight: as we are taught by the example of him to whom it was said, \"Because you have seen me, you have believed\" (Joan. XX, 29) \u2014 that is, you who, while you were only hearing, were unbelieving. For since things heard are believed with more difficulty than things seen, therefore the faith of those who have not seen is rightly proclaimed blessed by the Lord; since they gave more credit to the authority of the word than to the experience of their own sense or reason. Yet God, wishing in every way to satisfy our slowness, has today made for us even visible \u2014 indeed even touchable \u2014 his Word, which before he had made only audible; so that some of us have even been able to say: \"That which was from the beginning, which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have looked upon, and our hands have handled, concerning the Word of life\" (Joan. I, 1). He was from the beginning of that eternity which has no beginning; we heard him promised from the beginning of time; we saw and touched him shown forth at the end of time. Elsewhere, too, you will find that the Word of God was made for us not only visible and touchable, but even tastable and smellable, since through every avenue of the senses it sought an entrance to the soul, so that just as death had entered through the senses, so also life might return through the same senses. Therefore that \"the Word was made flesh\" (Joan. I, 14) was done for us, who are wholly flesh; so that we, who before could only hear the Word of God, might now both see and taste it made flesh, and might summon all our senses as witness to our hearing; so that all our senses, with one consent and one voice, might confess, \"As we have heard, so have we seen\" (Psal. XLVII, 9). Yet incomparably more was then granted to sight than had ever been granted to hearing, since now the Word which is God is seen, whereas before it was reckoned a great thing merely to hear any word whatsoever from God. And indeed, brothers, I have sometimes seen a word which is from God heard with weariness; but can the Word which is God possibly be seen except with joy? Let me pass judgment first upon myself: since the Word, which is God, offers itself today to be seen by me in that which I am \u2014 if it does not make me glad, I am impious; if it does not build me up, I am reprobate.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Si quis ergo, fratres, acediosus invenitur inter nos, nolo diutius ut fatigentur aures ejus sermone nostro contemptibili; transeat usque Bethlehem, et illud in quod desiderant Angeli prospicere, prospiciat ibi, Verbum scilicet Dei, quod Dominus ostendit nobis; imaginetur animo, Sermo Dei vivus et efficax qualis ibi jaceat [ al. qualiter ibi taceat] in praesepi. Si tantum pietas oculum illuminet intuentis, quid tam delectabiliter potest videri, tam salubriter cogitari? Quid aeque mores aedificat, spem roborat, charitatem inflammat? Omnino fidelis sermo, et omni acceptione dignus, omnipotens Sermo tuus, Domine; qui tam alto silentio a regalibus Patris sedibus animalium illapsus praesepibus (Sap. XVIII, 15), melius interim suo nobis silentio sermocinatur. Qui habet aures audiendi, audiat quid nobis hoc pium et mysticum aeterni Verbi loquatur silentium; quoniam, nisi me fallit auditus, inter alia quae loquitur, loquitur pacem in plebem sanctorum, quibus reverentia ipsius et exemplum religiosum imposuit silentium, et rectissime quidem imposuit. Quid enim tanto pondere, tantaque auctoritate disciplinam commendat silentii, quid tanto terrore compescit inquietum malum linguarum procellasque verborum, sicut Sermo Dei silens in medio hominum? Non est sermo in lingua mea, confiteri videtur omnipotens Sermo, dum Matri subditus est; et nos qua dementia dicemus, Linguam nostram magnificabimus, labia nostra a nobis sunt; quis noster Dominus est? (Psal. XI, 5.) Liberet mihi, si liceret, obmutescere et humiliari, et silere etiam a bonis, ut attentius et diligentius aurem possem adhibere vocibus arcanis, sacrisque sensibus hujus divini silentii, et vel tanto tempore sub silentio discere in schola Verbi, quanto Verbum ipsum sub disciplina siluit Matris."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. If then, brothers, anyone among us is found weighed down with listlessness, I do not wish his ears to be wearied any longer by our contemptible discourse; let him go over to Bethlehem, and there look upon that into which the angels desire to look, namely the Word of God, which the Lord has shown us; let him picture in his mind how the living and effective Word of God lies there [alternate reading: how it keeps silence there] in the manger. If only piety would illumine the eye of the one beholding, what could be seen so delightfully, thought upon so wholesomely? What equally builds up conduct, strengthens hope, inflames love? Altogether faithful is the saying, and worthy of all acceptance \u2014 your almighty Word, O Lord; who, gliding down in so deep a silence from the Father's royal throne into the mangers of animals (Sap. XVIII, 15), meanwhile speaks to us the better by his very silence. Let him who has ears to hear, hear what this pious and mystical silence of the eternal Word speaks to us; for, unless my hearing deceives me, among the other things it speaks, it speaks peace to the people of the saints, upon whom reverence for it and its devout example have imposed silence \u2014 and most rightly has it imposed it. For what commends the discipline of silence with such weight and such authority, what restrains with such terror the restless evil of tongues and the storms of words, as the Word of God, silent in the midst of men? \"There is no word in my tongue\" \u2014 the almighty Word seems to confess this, while he is subject to his Mother; and with what madness shall we say, \"We will magnify our tongue, our lips are our own; who is our Lord?\" (Psal. XI, 5.). It would please me, if it were permitted, to become mute and be humbled, and to keep silence even from good things, so that I might the more attentively and diligently apply my ear to the secret voices and the sacred meanings of this divine silence, and might learn in silence in the school of the Word for at least as long a time as the Word himself kept silence under the discipline of his Mother.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. O fratres, si pie ac diligenter intendamus huic Verbo quod Dominus fecit hodie, et ostendit nobis, quanta, quamque facile possemus ab ipso perdoceri? Verbum siquidem breviatum est, ita tamen ut in eo consummatum sit omne verbum quod ad salutem est, quia nimirum verbum consummans et abbrevians in aequitate ipsum est. Et haec consummatio abbreviata inundavit justitiam (Isa. X, 23), sicut promissum meministis per Isaiam; quia de sua plenitudine in participes suos redundavit justitiam, ut abundet justitia ipsorum, plus quam et Pharisaeorum et Scribarum; licet illi per tam multiplices se exercitent justificationes legis, isti autem brevi ac simplici contenti sint verbo fidei. Sed quid mirum si omnia verba sua nobis Verbum Dei breviavit, quando et se ipsum breviari, et quodammodo minui voluit, adeo ut de incomprehensibili immensitate sui ad augustias uteri quodammodo se contraxerit, et, continens mundum, in praesepi se passum sit contineri? In coelo Verbum istud horribili altitudine sua virtutes angelicas stupefacit: in praesepi simplices et hebetes pascit: ibi subtilissimis Angelorum intellectibus inscrutabile, hic etiam obtusis hominum sensibus palpabile. Quia enim Deus non poterat loqui nobis quasi spiritualibus, sed quasi carnalibus, Verbum ejus factum est caro, ut non solum audire, sed et videre posset omnis caro quod os Domini locutum est. Et quia in sapientia sua non cognovit mundus sapientiam Dei, dignatione ineffabili eadem Sapientia Dei se stultitiam fecit, ut quamlibet idiotis vel hebetibus se discibilem praeberet, et per stultitiam praedicationis salvos faceret credentes. Confiteor tibi, Pater, Domine coeli et terrae, quia sapientiam tuam abscondisti a sapientibus et prudentibus, et revelasti eam parvulis. Ita, Pater, sic fuit placitum ante te (Matth. XI, 25, 26), ut parvulis daretur parvulus qui natus est nobis, quandoquidem altitudo superborum nimis abhorret ab hujus humilitate parvuli, et quod altum est apud homines, abominabile est apud Deum, qui, cum veraciter altus sit, parvulus factus est pro nobis. Cum solis nimirum parvulis concordat iste parvulus, in solis requiescit humilibus et quietis. Sicut igitur parvuli gloriantes de eo, cantant, Parvulus natus est nobis (Isa. IX, 6), sic et ipse gloriatur de eis: Ecce ego, inquiens, et pueri mei, quos mihi donavit Dominus (Id. V, 18). Ut enim parvulo Filio suo Pater sodales daret de coaetaneis suis, statim primo omnium ab innocentia parvulorum coepit gloria martyrii; hoc significante Spiritu sancto, quia nonnisi talium esset regnum coelorum (Matth. XIX, 14)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. O brothers, if we attend to this Word which the Lord has made today, and shown to us, piously and diligently, how much\u2014and how easily\u2014might we be thoroughly taught by it? For indeed the Word has been shortened, yet in such a way that in it every word that pertains to salvation is fulfilled, because it is indeed the word that completes and cuts short in equity. And this shortened completion has overflowed with righteousness (Isa. X, 23), as you recall was promised through Isaiah; for from his own fullness he has poured forth righteousness upon those who share in him, so that their righteousness abounds more than that of the Pharisees and Scribes\u2014though those exercise themselves through so many manifold justifications of the law, while these are content with the brief and simple word of faith. But what wonder is it if the Word of God has shortened all his words for us, when he himself willed to be shortened, and in a certain manner diminished, so much so that from the incomprehensible immensity of himself he contracted himself, as it were, to the narrow confines of a womb, and he who contains the world suffered himself to be contained in a manger? In heaven this Word, by its awesome height, strikes the angelic powers with astonishment; in the manger it feeds the simple and the dull: there inscrutable even to the most subtle intellects of the Angels, here even palpable to the dull senses of men. For since God could not speak to us as to spiritual beings, but as to carnal ones, his Word was made flesh, so that all flesh might not only hear, but also see, what the mouth of the Lord has spoken. And because the world, in its own wisdom, did not know the wisdom of God, by ineffable condescension this same Wisdom of God made itself foolishness, so that it might offer itself as teachable even to the unlearned and the dull, and through the foolishness of preaching might save those who believe. I confess to you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, that you have hidden your wisdom from the wise and prudent, and have revealed it to little ones. Thus, Father, so it was pleasing before you (Matth. XI, 25, 26), that the little one who was born for us should be given to little ones, since the haughtiness of the proud shrinks utterly from the humility of this little one, and what is high among men is abominable before God, who, though he is truly high, was made a little one for us. For this little one agrees indeed only with little ones; he rests only in the humble and the quiet. Therefore, just as little ones, glorying in him, sing, 'A little one is born to us' (Isa. IX, 6), so also he himself glories in them, saying, 'Behold, I and my children, whom the Lord has given me' (Id. V, 18). For in order that the Father might give companions to his little Son from among his own contemporaries, the glory of martyrdom began first of all from the innocence of little children; the Holy Spirit signifying by this that the kingdom of heaven belongs only to such as these (Matth. XIX, 14).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Si tales effici volumus, fratres, iterum atque iterum transeamus usque Bethlehem, et diligentius intueamur hoc Verbum quod caro factum est per Deum immensum, qui parvulus factus est, ut in hoc visibili et breviato Verbo discamus sapientiam Dei, quae tota humilitas facta est. In hanc quippe virtutem illa omnimoda virtus se interim totam contulit. Nihil aliud illa summa Sapientia interim scire voluit, nisi illam scilicet humilitatem, cujus se postmodum magistrum voluit profiteri. Et iste quidem, quod nimirum in suggillationem mei ipsius loquor, iste, inquam, digne et juste magister docendae humilitatis effectus est; qui, licet eam sibi non ignoraret et originalem a Matre, et naturalem a Patre, nihilominus tamen ab ipso Matris utero didicit eam ex his quae passus est. In diversorio viatorum natus est, ut hospites et peregrinos super terram, ipsius exemplo edocti, nos esse confiteremur. Ibi quoque novissimum eligens locum, in praesepio positus est, ut et nos illud Davidicum: Elegi abjectus esse in domo Dei, magis quam habitare in tabernaculis peccatorum (Psal. LXXXIII, 11), opere disceremus. Pannis involutus est, ut habentes tantum quibus tegamur, his contenti simus; per omnia paupertate materna contentus, Matrique per omnia subditus, ut totius religionis forma jam in ejus nativitate nata videretur. Beata sane simplicium fides pastorum, quae, cum invenisset infantem pannis involutum, nequaquam in illis scandalizata est ad incredulitatem, ut minora de eo sentiret, sed aedificata potius ad pietatem, ut tantae dignationi gratior fieret! Quanto namque se pro ipsis profundius humiliatam ac funditus exinanitam illa exhibuit majestas, tanto facilius et plenius, si digne de eis sentire volumus, totos eorum affectus sibi illius rapuit et vindicavit charitas."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. If we wish to become such as these, brothers, let us again and again go over to Bethlehem, and look more diligently upon this Word made flesh through the immense God, who became a little one, so that in this visible and shortened Word we may learn the wisdom of God, which has become wholly humility. For indeed that all-encompassing power has for the time being wholly given itself over to this one virtue. For the time being that highest Wisdom willed to know nothing else but that very humility, of which it afterward willed to profess itself the teacher. And he indeed\u2014which I say to my own reproach\u2014he, I say, became worthily and justly the teacher appointed to teach humility; who, although he was not ignorant of it, having it as original from his Mother and natural from his Father, nevertheless learned it even from his Mother's womb, from the things which he suffered. He was born in a wayfarers' inn, so that, taught by his example, we might confess ourselves to be guests and pilgrims upon the earth. There also, choosing the lowest place, he was laid in a manger, so that we too might learn by deed that word of David: 'I have chosen to be an outcast in the house of God, rather than to dwell in the tabernacles of sinners' (Psal. LXXXIII, 11). He was wrapped in swaddling clothes, so that we, having only enough to cover us, might be content with that; content in all things with his Mother's poverty, and subject to his Mother in all things, so that the whole pattern of religion might be seen to be already born in his very birth. Blessed indeed was the simple faith of the shepherds, which, when it found the infant wrapped in swaddling clothes, was by no means scandalized by them into unbelief, so as to think less of him, but was rather built up into piety, so as to become more pleasing to so great a condescension! For the more deeply that majesty showed itself humbled and utterly emptied out for their sake, the more easily and fully\u2014if we wish to think worthily of them\u2014did his love seize and claim for itself the whole of their affections.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Fratres, et vos invenietis hodie infantem pannis involutum, et positum in praesepio altaris; videte vilitas tegminis ne offendat aut turbet obtutum vestrae fidei, veritatem reverendi corporis sub aliis rerum formis intuentis. Sicut enim mater Maria quibusdam assumentis pannorum involvit infantem, sic mater gratia dispensatoriis speciebus rerum ejusdem sacri corporis nobis obtegit veritatem; sic etiam mater sapientia aenigmatibus et figuris arcanam divini Verbi contegit majestatem; quatenus et ibi simplicitas fidei, et hic exercitium studii, meritum sibi cumulet ad salutem. Nam ego, fratres, cum vobis veritatem, quae Christus est, verbis meis enuntio, quid aliud quam Christum pannis satis vilibus involvo? Beatus tamen, cui nec in pannis istis Christus est vilior, sicut nec merces pretiosae prudenti vilescunt propter veteres quibus amiciuntur sacculos. Christus plane est, quem vobis tradere cupio, qualibuscunque sermonibus nostris, ut, juxta sermonem Petri apostoli, sanctificetis eum in cordibus vestris (I Petr. III, 15.) Suscipite in mansuetudine insitum verbum, quod potest salvare animas vestras (Jacobi I, 21). Et verbum Christi abundanter habitet in vobis, amor scilicet et memoria Verbi incarnati, quatenus tam feliciter quam fideliter decantetis: Verbum caro factum est, et habitavit in nobis (Joan. I, 14). Tota igitur pietate cogitemus Christum in pannis, quibus Mater eum operuit, ut aeterna felicitate videamus gloriam et decorem quem ei Pater induit, gloriam quasi Unigeniti a Patre, cum quo et cum Spiritu sancto honor et gloria, per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Brothers, you too will find today an infant wrapped in swaddling clothes, and laid in the manger of the altar; see that the cheapness of the covering does not offend or disturb the gaze of your faith, which beholds the truth of that venerable body under other forms of things. For just as Mother Mary wrapped the infant in some scraps of cloth, so Mother Grace covers for us the truth of that same sacred body with outward forms of things appointed for the dispensation; and so too Mother Wisdom covers the hidden majesty of the divine Word with enigmas and figures; so that in the one case the simplicity of faith, and in the other the exercise of study, may each heap up its own merit unto salvation. For I, brothers, when with my words I announce to you the truth which is Christ, what else am I doing but wrapping Christ in rather cheap swaddling-cloths? Yet blessed is he to whom Christ is not thereby made cheaper, even in such cloths \u2014 just as precious wares do not become worthless in the eyes of a prudent man because of the old sacks in which they are wrapped. It is truly Christ whom I desire to hand over to you, by whatever kind of words of ours, so that, according to the saying of the apostle Peter, you may sanctify him in your hearts (I Petr. III, 15.). Receive with meekness the implanted word, which is able to save your souls (Jacobi I, 21). And let the word of Christ dwell abundantly in you \u2014 that is, the love and remembrance of the incarnate Word \u2014 so that you may sing, as happily as faithfully: The Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us (Joan. I, 14). Let us therefore, with all devotion, think upon Christ in the swaddling-clothes with which his Mother covered him, so that in eternal blessedness we may see the glory and beauty with which the Father has clothed him \u2014 the glory as it were of the Only-Begotten from the Father \u2014 with whom, and with the Holy Spirit, be honor and glory, through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity Sermon V: On God's Wonderful Providence in the Birth of Christ", "topics": ["Nativity", "Word made visible", "silence", "humility", "Eucharist"], "url": "sermons/guerric-nativity-05.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:palm", "calendar_label": "Palm Sunday", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-palms-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, Dominica in ramis palmarum, sermo I, Quomodo Christus omnia homini factus, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 127\u2013130.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Palm Sunday", "Palm Sunday", "Philippians 2:5\u20136, 8", "Galatians 6:2", "Psalm 23:6 (printed locator; see note)", "Luke 12:47\u201348 (printed locator; see note)", "Mark 7:37", "Isaiah 43:23\u201324", "Luke 22:44", "Isaiah 63:3", "Philippians 2:5"], "occasion": "Dominica in ramis palmarum", "occasion_detail": "Palm Sunday", "occasion_label": "Palm Sunday", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric of Igny\u2019s first Palm Sunday sermon presents Christ\u2019s service as the remedy for human pride. Christ is imagined addressing the disobedient servant, offering to bear his burdens, feed him and redeem him; the meditation then turns to the Passion and a prayer for grace to imitate humility, patience and obedience in the common life. The collection belongs approximately to Guerric\u2019s abbacy in 1138\u20131157, but no individual delivery date is established. This reading follows the 1879 Patrologia Latina edition. The source explicitly names Palm Sunday. Historical polemic is retained and identified in an editorial note."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "DOMINICA IN RAMIS PALMARUM."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "PALM SUNDAY", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quomodo Christus omnia homini factus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "How Christ Became All Things for Man", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Hoc sentite in vobis, quod et in Christo Jesu, qui, cum in forma Dei esset (Philipp. ii, 5, 6), etc. Audiat servus nequam et fugitivus, hominem dico, qui, cum esset in natura et conditione servili et necessitate serviendi, servire detrectans, arripere sibi conatus est libertatem et aequalitatem Domini sui. Christus cum in forma Dei esset, non rapina, sed natura aequalis, quia coomnipotens, coaeternus et consubstantialis, exinaniens semetipsum, non solum formam servi accepit, in similitudinem hominum factus; sed etiam ministerium servi implevit, humilians semetipsum, et obediens factus Patri usque ad mortem, mortem autem crucis (Ibid. 8). Sed parum tibi videatur quod, cum esset Filius et coaequalis, tanquam servus servivit Patri, si non et servo suo plus quam servus servivit. Factus quidem erat homo, ut serviret Creatori. Et quid justius quam ut servias illi a quo creatus es, sine quo nec esse potes? et quid beatius aut sublimius quam servire illi cui servire regnare est? Non serviam, inquit homo Creatori. Ego igitur serviam tibi, inquit Creator, homini. Tu recumbe; ego ministrabo, ego tibi pedes lavabo. Tu quiesce; ego languores tuos feram, infirmitates portabo. Utere me, ut libet, in omni necessitate tua, non modo ut servo tuo, sed etiam ut jumento tuo, ut peculio tuo. Si fatigatus aut oneratus es, ego te et onus tuum feram, ut legem meam primus impleam. Alter, inquit, alterius onera portate, et sic adimplebitis legem Christi (Galat. vi, 2). Si esuris, aut sitis, et ad manum forte nihil melius habes, nec alter tibi vitulus aeque saginatus praesto est, ecce ego paratus immolari, ut carnes meas comedas, et sanguinem bibas. Nec verearis ex morte servi damna servitii ejus pati; etiam comestus et bibitus, manebo tibi integer et vivus, serviamque ut prius. Si in captivitatem ductus aut venumdatus es, ecce vende me, teque pretio mei, vel me ipso pretio redime. Vile quidem mancipium videor; sed etsi noctu et clanculo distrahar quasi res furtiva, etsi ab avarissimis Judaeorum sacerdotibus emar; attamen saltem triginta argenteos appretiari potero; hoc pretio mei poterit emi sepultura peregrinorum, me pretio vita sepultorum. Si infirmaris, et mori times, ego moriar pro te, ut de sanguine meo tibi conficias medicamenta vitae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Have this mind in you which was also in Christ Jesus, who, being in the form of God (Philipp. ii, 5, 6), etc. Let the wicked runaway servant hear this\u2014I mean man, who, although by nature and condition a servant and under the necessity of serving, refused to serve and tried to seize for himself the freedom and equality of his Lord. Christ, being in the form of God, equal by nature rather than by robbery, because he is equally almighty, coeternal and consubstantial, emptied himself. He not only took the form of a servant, being made in the likeness of men, but also fulfilled a servant\u2019s ministry, humbling himself and becoming obedient to the Father unto death, even death on a cross (Ibid. 8). Yet think it little that, although the Son and coequal, he served the Father as a servant, unless he also served his own servant more than a servant would. Man had indeed been made to serve the Creator. What could be more just than to serve him by whom you were created, without whom you cannot even exist? And what could be happier or more exalted than to serve him whom to serve is to reign? \u201cI will not serve,\u201d man says to the Creator. \u201cThen I will serve you,\u201d the Creator says to man. \u201cRecline at table; I will minister to you, I will wash your feet. Rest; I will bear your ailments and carry your infirmities. Use me as you please in every need, not only as your servant but also as your beast of burden, as your property. If you are tired or burdened, I will carry you and your burden, so that I may be the first to fulfil my own law. Bear one another\u2019s burdens, he says, and so you will fulfil the law of Christ (Galat. vi, 2). If you are hungry or thirsty and happen to have nothing better at hand, and no other calf as well fattened for you is at hand, here I am, ready to be sacrificed, so that you may eat my flesh and drink my blood. Do not fear that the servant\u2019s death will cost you his service: even when eaten and drunk, I shall remain whole and alive for you, and I shall serve as before. If you have been taken captive or sold, sell me, and redeem yourself with my price, or with myself as the price. I seem a worthless slave; yet even if I am sold by night and in secret as stolen property, even if I am bought by the most avaricious priests of the Jews, I can still be valued at least at thirty pieces of silver. With this price paid for me a burial place for strangers can be bought; with me as the price, life for the buried. If you are sick and afraid to die, I shall die for you, so that from my blood you may prepare medicines of life for yourself.\u201d", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Euge, serve bone et fidelis, servisti revera, servisti in omni fide et veritate, servisti in omni patientia et longanimitate. Non tepide, qui exsultasti ut gigas ad currendam viam (Psal. xxiii, 6) obedientiae non ficte, qui etiam animam post tot et tantos labores superimpendisti; non murmurose, qui, innocens flagellatus, nec os aperuisti. Scriptum est, et justum est: Servus sciens voluntatem domini sui, et non faciens digna, plagis vapulabit multis (Luc. xii, 47, 48). Sed iste servus, obsecro, quid non dignum fecit? quid debuit facere et non fecit? Bene omnia fecit, clamant qui facta ejus observabant, et surdos fecit audire et mutos loqui (Marc. vii, 37). Omnia fecit digna, et quomodo sic omnia passus est indigna? Dorsum posuit ad flagella, plagis vapulavit non paucis nec parvis; indicio sunt sanguinis rivuli qui de tot partibus profluunt corporis. Contumelia et tormento interrogatus fuit, veluti servus aut latro subditus quaestioni, qua confessio extorqueatur criminis. O detestabilem superbiam hominis servire contemnentis, quae alio exemplo non poterat humiliari, nisi servitute et tali servitute Domini sui! et utinam vel sic possit! utinam vel nunc habeat et referat gratiam tantae humilitati ac bonitati! Sed, ut mihi videtur, adhuc audio eumdem Dominum apud Isaiam conquerentem de ingratitudine nequam servi, ubi scilicet ait: Non te servire feci in oblatione, nec laborem tibi praebui in thure. Verum tamen me servire fecisti in peccatis tuis, laborem praebuisti mihi in iniquitatibus tuis (Isai. xliii, 23, 24). Et quem laborem? Usque ad defatigationem, esuriem et sitim; sed usque ad sudorem, sudorem autem sanguinis decurrentis in terram (Luc. xxii, 44); sed usque ad mortem, mortem autem crucis: ne omnia modo replicem, quod alapis caesus, sputis illitus, coronatus spinis, confixus clavis, lancea perforatus, aceto et felle potatus. Hoc torcular, inquit, calcavi solus, et de gentibus non est vir mecum (Isai. lxiii, 3). Vos ergo, qui statis tota die otiosi, attendite et videte si est labor sicut labor meus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Well done, good and faithful servant! You have truly served; you have served with all faithfulness and truth, with all patience and forbearance. Not lukewarmly, for you rejoiced like a giant to run the course (Psal. xxiii, 6) of obedience; not insincerely, for after so many and such great labours you spent your very life besides; not with murmuring, for, though innocent and scourged, you did not even open your mouth. It is written, and it is just: \u201cThe servant who knows his lord\u2019s will and does not act worthily will be beaten with many blows\u201d (Luc. xii, 47, 48). But this servant, I ask you, what did he do that was unworthy? What ought he to have done that he did not do? \u201cHe has done all things well,\u201d cry those who observed his deeds, \u201cand has made the deaf hear and the mute speak\u201d (Marc. vii, 37). He did everything worthily; how, then, did he suffer all these unworthy things? He offered his back to the scourges; he was beaten with blows neither few nor small, as the streams of blood flowing from so many parts of his body bear witness. He was questioned with insult and torture, like a slave or robber subjected to torment to wring out a confession of crime. O detestable pride of man, who disdains to serve! No other example could humble it except the service, and such service, of his own Lord. If only even this could do so! If only even now he would feel and show gratitude for such humility and goodness! Yet I seem still to hear the same Lord complaining in Isaiah about the wicked servant\u2019s ingratitude, where he says: \u201cI did not make you serve me with offerings, nor impose labour on you with incense. Yet you have made me serve with your sins; you have imposed labour on me with your iniquities\u201d (Isai. xliii, 23, 24). And what labour? To the point of exhaustion, hunger and thirst; even to sweat, sweat of blood running down to the ground (Luc. xxii, 44); even to death, death on a cross. I need not now recount everything: how he was struck with blows, smeared with spittle, crowned with thorns, fastened with nails, pierced with a lance, given vinegar and gall to drink. \u201cThis winepress,\u201d he says, \u201cI have trodden alone, and of the nations there is no man with me\u201d (Isai. lxiii, 3). You, then, who stand idle all day, attend and see whether there is any labour like my labour.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Utique valde laborasti, Domine mi, in serviendo mihi; justum profecto et aequum esset ut saltem de caetero tu quiesceres, tuusque tibi servus vel ordine vicis suae, quia ad ipsum ventum est, serviret. Quam magno, Domine mi, inutile tibi meum redemisti servitium, qui nec ministeriis indiges Angelorum! Quam suavi et benigna arte pietatis recuperasti tibi et subdidisti contumacem servum, vincens in bono malum, humilitate confundens superbum, beneficiis obruens ingratum! Sic nimirum, sic sapientia vincit malitiam; sic carbones ignis congessisti super caput contumacis, quibus accenderetur ad poenitentiam. Vicisti ergo, Domine, vicisti rebellem: ecce do manus in vincula tua, jugoque tuo suppono cervicem. Dignare tantum ut tibi serviam, patere ut tibi laborem. Accipe me servum sempiternum, licet inutilem, nisi nunc quoque mecum sit et mecum laboret gratia tua, semper et praeveniens et subsequens. Praeveniat nos, prior ostendens humilitatis et patientiae exempla, subsequatur nos, adjuvans ut imitemur ostensa. Quam felices nos, o fratres mei, si super hoc audiamus consilium Apostoli: Hoc sentite, inquit, in vobis quod et in Christo Jesu (Philipp. ii, 5) praecessisse cognoscitis! hoc est, nullus extollatur supra se, sed magis humilietur infra se; qui major est, aliis serviat; si quis laeditur, prior satisfaciat; in commune usque ad mortem quisque obediat. His vestigiis, fratres, sequemur Christum in forma Dei, in qua vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. You have indeed laboured greatly, my Lord, in serving me. Surely it would be just and right that at least from now on you should rest, and that your servant should serve you, if only because his turn has now come. At what a price, my Lord, you have redeemed my service, useless to you who do not even need the ministries of angels! With what a gentle and kindly art of loving mercy you have won back and subjected your stubborn servant, overcoming evil with good, confounding the proud by humility, overwhelming the ungrateful with benefits! In this way, truly, wisdom conquers malice; in this way you have heaped coals of fire upon the stubborn man\u2019s head, to kindle him to repentance. You have conquered, then, Lord; you have conquered the rebel. Here I offer my hands for your bonds and bow my neck beneath your yoke. Only deign to let me serve you; allow me to labour for you. Receive me as your servant for ever, though a useless one, unless even now your grace is with me and labours with me, always both going before and following after. May it go before us, first showing examples of humility and patience; may it follow us, helping us to imitate what has been shown. How happy we shall be, my brothers, if in this we heed the Apostle\u2019s counsel: \u201cHave this mind in you,\u201d he says, \u201cwhich was also in Christ Jesus\u201d (Philipp. ii, 5), as you know it was in him before you. That is, let no one exalt himself above himself, but rather humble himself beneath himself; let the greater serve the others; if anyone is injured, let him be the first to make amends; let each obey in the common life unto death. In these footsteps, brothers, we shall follow Christ in the form of God, in which he lives and reigns for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The passage about the Jewish priests preserves the sermon\u2019s historical anti-Jewish polemic. It is source rhetoric, not an editorial endorsement. The sacrifice, flesh, blood and continuing life imagery is retained in the translation."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites Psalm 23:6 for the running giant. The image is in Psalm 18:6 in historical Vulgate numbering (Psalm 19 in modern numbering). The following Luke 12:47\u201348 reference is also retained; the saying about many blows occurs in verse 47."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The sermon has three numbered sections; the final prayer continues within the third."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "\u201cGoing before and following after\u201d describes grace that initiates and assists our imitation of Christ. Guerric asks the injured person\u2014not the person causing injury\u2014to be first to make amends."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Palm Sunday Sermon I: Christ Becomes All Things for Us", "topics": ["Palm Sunday", "Passion", "humility", "service", "obedience", "Eucharist", "grace", "repentance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-palms-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:palm", "calendar_label": "Palm Sunday", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-palms-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De crucis scientia et virtute., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 130-134.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Palm Sunday", "Palm Sunday", "1 Corinthians 2:2", "1 Corinthians 1:30", "John 14:30", "Galatians 6:14", "Job 40:18", "Song of Songs 7:8", "4 Kings 2:23 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Kings)", "Psalm 106:2 (historical numbering)", "Ezekiel 9:4\u20137", "Galatians 5:24", "2 Corinthians 7:1", "Psalm 118:120 (historical numbering)", "Numbers 25:4\u20139", "Joshua 10:26", "Numbers 23:10"], "occasion": "Dominica in ramis palmarum", "occasion_detail": "Palm Sunday", "occasion_label": "Palm Sunday", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric preaches Christ crucified in the days of the Passion. He presents the cross as hidden strength that overcame the devil, and Christ's ascent of the cross as free, triumphant and fruitful for the redeemed. He warns those who bear the sign of the cross falsely and urges the brothers to crucify their vices through the fear of God and to persevere on the cross until evening. His argument includes polemic against Jews. The sermon appears under the Palm Sunday heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De crucis scientia et virtute."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the knowledge and power of the cross.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Paulus, doctor noster in fide et veritate, si hodie veniret ad nos, puto quia non judicaret se aliquid scire inter nos, nisi Jesum Christum, et hunc crucifixum (I Cor. II, 2). In his siquidem diebus in quibus anniversaria dominicae passionis et crucis memoria solemniter celebratur, nihil, ut arbitror, convenientius praedicatur, quam Jesus Christus, et hic crucifixus. Nam et aliis quibuslibet diebus quid unquam potest praedicari fidelius? quid audiri salubrius? quid cogitari fructuosius? Quid enim tam pium fidelium affectibus, tam medicinale moribus; quid sic interficit peccata, crucifigit vitia, virtutes nutrit et roborat, sicut crucifixi memoria? Loquatur ergo Paulus inter perfectos sapientiam in mysterio absconditam; mihi, cujus imperfectum vident etiam oculi hominum, loquatur Christum crucifixum, his quidem qui pereunt stultitiam, mihi autem et his qui salvi fiunt, plane Dei virtutem Deique sapientiam; mihi prorsus altissimam atque nobilissimam philosophiam, per quam infatuatam irrideo tam mundi quam carnis sapientiam. Quam perfectum me putarem, quam provectum in sapientia, si Crucifixi vel idoneus invenirer auditor, qui factus est nobis a Deo non solum sapientia, sed et justitia, et sanctificatio, et redemptio! (Id. I, 30.) Omnino si Christo confixus es cruci, sapiens es, justus es, sanctus es, liber es. An non sapiens, qui, cum Christo exaltatus a terra, sapit et quaerit quae sursum sunt? an non justus, in quo destructum est corpus peccati, ut ultra non serviat peccato? an non sanctus, qui semetipsum exhibuit hostiam vivam, sanctam, placentem Deo? an non vere liber, quem Filius liberavit, qui de libertate conscientiae illam liberam vocem Filii sibi assumere confidit: Venit princeps mundi hujus, et in me non habet quidquam? (Joan. XIV, 30.) Vere apud Crucifixum misericordia, et copiosa apud eum redemptio, qui sic redimit Israel ex omnibus iniquitatibus ejus, ut principis hujus mundi calumnias liber evadere mereatur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Paul, our teacher in faith and truth, if he came to us today, I think he would judge that he knew nothing among us except Jesus Christ, and him crucified (I Cor. II, 2). Indeed, in these days in which the yearly memory of the Lord's passion and cross is solemnly celebrated, nothing, I think, is more fittingly preached than Jesus Christ, and him crucified. For on any other days as well, what can ever be preached more faithfully? What heard more wholesomely? What thought upon more fruitfully? For what is so pious for the affections of the faithful, so medicinal for morals; what so kills sins, crucifies vices, nourishes and strengthens virtues, as the memory of the Crucified One? Let Paul, then, speak wisdom hidden in mystery among the perfect; to me, whose imperfection even the eyes of men can see, let him speak of Christ crucified \u2014 to those indeed who perish, foolishness, but to me and to those who are being saved, plainly the power of God and the wisdom of God; to me indeed the loftiest and most noble philosophy, by which, having made foolish the wisdom of both world and flesh, I laugh it to scorn. How perfect I would think myself, how advanced in wisdom, if I were found a fit hearer of the Crucified, who was made for us by God not only wisdom, but also righteousness, and sanctification, and redemption! (Id. I, 30.) Altogether, if you have been nailed with Christ to the cross, you are wise, you are righteous, you are holy, you are free. Is he not wise who, lifted up from the earth with Christ, savors and seeks the things that are above? Is he not righteous in whom the body of sin has been destroyed, so that he no longer serves sin? Is he not holy who has presented himself a living sacrifice, holy, pleasing to God? Is he not truly free whom the Son has set free, who from the freedom of conscience dares to take up for himself that free utterance of the Son: The prince of this world is coming, and he has nothing in me? (Joan. XIV, 30.) Truly with the Crucified is mercy, and with him abundant redemption, who so redeems Israel from all his iniquities that he deserves to escape free from the slanders of the prince of this world.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sciat tamen, sciat quicunque est ille beatus et verus Israel, non esse hoc suae meritum perfectionis, sed divinae gratiam redemptionis; non scilicet quia peccatum non fecerit, nec inventus sit dolus in ore ejus, sed quia ille cujus haec laus propria est, Christus scilicet, purgavit peccata illius, qui per sanguinem crucis suae purgationem peccatorum faciens, ibi maxime triumphavit principatus et potestates, ubi abscondita erat fortitudo ejus. Erat abscondita, sed non perdita; quia crucifixus ex infirmitate, vivebat ex virtute Dei. Erat occulta, sed non erat otiosa; quia crucifixus veterem hominem in omnibus electis crucifigebat. Crucifigebat mundum Paulo, et Paulum mundo (Galat. VI, 14). Crucifigebat denique tyrannum hujus mundi, omnesque suae antiquae ministros tyrannidis. Hamum plane sub esca recondebat, qui sub infirmitate fortitudinem abscondebat. Ac perinde ille homicida ab initio sitiens humanum sanguinem, dum irruit in infirmitatem, incidit in virtutem: morsus dum momordit, confixus dum Crucifixum appetivit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Yet let him know, let whoever is that blessed and true Israel know, that this is not the merit of his own perfection, but the grace of divine redemption; not indeed because he has committed no sin, nor has guile been found in his mouth, but because he to whom this praise properly belongs, namely Christ, has purged his sins \u2014 he who, by making purgation of sins through the blood of his cross, triumphed over principalities and powers precisely there where his strength was hidden. It was hidden, but not lost; for, crucified out of weakness, he lived by the power of God. It was concealed, but not idle; for the Crucified One was crucifying the old man in all the elect. He was crucifying the world to Paul, and Paul to the world (Galat. VI, 14). He was crucifying, in short, the tyrant of this world, and all the ministers of his ancient tyranny. He was plainly hiding the hook beneath the bait, he who was hiding strength beneath weakness. And accordingly that murderer from the beginning, thirsting for human blood, while he rushed upon weakness, fell into power: biting, he was bitten; and while he sought to seize the Crucified, he was himself pierced.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Gratias cruci et clavis tuis, Domine Jesu: video perforatas fauces illius draconis, ut transeant liberati, qui etiam fuerant absorpti. Et qui fiduciam habebat quod influeret Jordanis in os ejus (Job. XL, 18), fluvium, quem absorbuerat, se non minima ex parte perdidisse frendens irascitur. De illis quippe faucibus nobis isti venerunt qui hodie nobiscum nobilem ac magnificum crucis decantant triumphum. Plane de ore leonis liberati sunt, imo de ventre inferi regressi sunt. Irascatur ergo, fremat et tabescat de cujus dentibus praeda erepta est: nam Christus gratulabitur quod non gratis crucifixus est. Lugeat infernus et mors; ille morsus, ista mortua: nam coeli laetantur, et exsultat Ecclesia, quod Christus infernum spoliat, mortemque triumphat. In conversione nempe istorum triumphum suae renovavit passionis, crucisque miracula suscitavit. In istis crux refloruit, hunc fructum pretiosum nunc etiam arbor vitae tulit. Quomodo namque maneret sterilis, quae non modo rigata, sed etiam vivifica facta est sanguine Salvatoris? jam non poenitebit eum quod in palmam ascendit, cum tantum tamque pretiosum de ea fructum collegerit. Hunc quippe fructum inter alios praevidebat, cum voluntarius ad crucem properabat. Dixi, inquit, ascendam in palmam, et apprehendam fructus ejus (Cantic. VII, 8). Paucis absolvit quod voluntate passus sit, quod passione exaltatus sit, quod non sine fructu nostro passus sit. In dicto siquidem proponentis, arbitrii libertas; in ascensu, triumphi sublimitas; in apprehensione fructuum, redemptionis monstratur utilitas. Vos, insensati Judaei, clamabatis quidem, Ascende, calve; ascende, calve (IV Reg. II, 23); sed ordinationi ejus liberrimae, qui sponte decreverat ascendere, non potuit furor vester nisi servire. Ascendit crucem voluntate, triumphavit in ea potestate, fructum ex ea collegit pietate. Uno igitur opere et Judaeum irrisit, et diabolum peremit, atque christianum redemit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Thanks be to your cross and your nails, Lord Jesus: I see the jaws of that dragon pierced through, so that they who had even been swallowed up might pass through, now set free. And he who had confidence that the Jordan would flow into his mouth (Job. XL, 18) now, gnashing his teeth, is enraged that he has lost no small part of the river he had swallowed. For from those jaws came to us these who today with us sing the noble and magnificent triumph of the cross. Plainly they have been freed from the mouth of the lion, indeed they have returned from the belly of hell. Let him then be enraged, let him gnash and waste away, from whose teeth the prey has been snatched away: for Christ will rejoice that he was not crucified in vain. Let hell and death mourn; the one bitten, the other dead: for the heavens rejoice, and the Church exults, that Christ despoils hell and triumphs over death. In the conversion of these, indeed, he has renewed the triumph of his passion, and raised up again the miracles of the cross. In these the cross has flowered again; this precious fruit the tree of life has borne even now. For how could it remain barren, which has been not only watered, but even made life-giving by the blood of the Savior? Now he will not regret that he climbed up into the palm tree, since he has gathered from it so great and so precious a fruit. For indeed he foresaw this fruit among others when, of his own will, he hastened to the cross. I said, he says, I will climb up into the palm tree, and I will take hold of its fruits (Cantic. VII, 8). In a few words he sums up that he suffered willingly, that he was exalted by his passion, that he did not suffer without fruit for us. For in the saying of one proposing, the freedom of will is shown; in the climbing, the sublimity of triumph; in the taking hold of the fruits, the usefulness of redemption is displayed. You, senseless Jews, cried out indeed, Go up, you baldhead; go up, you baldhead (IV Reg. II, 23); but your rage could do nothing but serve the most free ordinance of him who of his own accord had decreed to go up. He climbed the cross by his will, he triumphed on it by his power, he gathered fruit from it by his loving-kindness. By one and the same work, then, he mocked the Jew, destroyed the devil, and redeemed the Christian.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Dicant igitur qui redempti sunt a Domino, quos redemit de manu inimici, et de regionibus congregavit eos (Psal. CVI, 2); dicant, inquam, voce et mente magistri sui: Mihi autem absit gloriari, nisi in cruce Domini nostri Jesu Cristi (Galat. VI, 14)! in qua sapientia Dei nequam infatuavit consilium, justitia destruxit eum qui habebat mortis imperium, misericordia liberavit captivum. Merito prorsus, o sapiens gloriator, gloriaberis in cruce Domini tui, cujus liberatus es triumpho, vivificatus mysterio, justificatus exemplo, munitus signo. Sic enim justae rationis consequentia postulare videtur, ut exemplum Crucifixi ad justificationem suam imprimant moribus, qui signum crucis ad munimen sui imprimunt frontibus, et illius vivant lege, cujus armantur fide. Alioquin fallaciter miles characterem regis gestat, cujus praescriptum non observat; nec recte se tuetur illius signo, cujus non paret imperio. Vide autem quanta perversitas et abusio sit, ut inimici crucis Christi tueri se velint signo crucis Christi: dum securi sunt, luxuriantes contra pietatem crucis; cum in periculo deprehensi sunt, volentes esse defensi virtute crucis. Plane inimici crucis Christi sunt, amici ventris sui; quorum deus venter est, quorum idolum nummus est. Sciat tamen, sciat quicunque nunc fallaciter sibi Crucifixi signum usurpat; nequaquam se illo tueri poterit in illa necessitate suprema, quando jam non arbitrio hominum, sed judicio et ministerio Angelorum signabitur Thau super frontes virorum gementium et dolentium, ut discernantur salvandi a multitudine perditorum (Ezech. IX, 4-7)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Let those therefore say who have been redeemed by the Lord, whom he redeemed from the hand of the enemy, and gathered out of the regions (Psal. CVI, 2); let them say, I say, with the voice and mind of their teacher: But far be it from me to glory, save in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ (Galat. VI, 14)! In which the wisdom of God made foolish the counsel of the wicked one, justice destroyed him who had the empire of death, mercy freed the captive. Most deservedly indeed, O wise boaster, will you glory in the cross of your Lord, by whose triumph you were set free, by whose mystery you were given life, by whose example you were justified, by whose sign you were fortified. For thus the sequence of right reason seems to require: that those who imprint the sign of the cross on their foreheads for their protection should also imprint the example of the Crucified on their character for their justification, and should live by the law of him by whose faith they are armed. Otherwise a soldier falsely bears the mark of a king whose decree he does not observe; nor does he rightly protect himself by the sign of him whose command he does not obey. But see what great perversity and abuse it is, that enemies of the cross of Christ should wish to protect themselves by the sign of the cross of Christ: while they are secure, living wantonly against the piety of the cross; when they are caught in danger, wishing to be defended by the power of the cross. Plainly they are enemies of the cross of Christ, friends of their own belly; whose god is the belly, whose idol is money. Let him know, however, let whoever now falsely usurps to himself the sign of the Crucified know this: he will by no means be able to protect himself by it in that supreme necessity, when no longer at the discretion of men, but by the judgment and ministry of Angels, the Thau will be marked upon the foreheads of men who groan and grieve, so that those to be saved may be distinguished from the multitude of the lost (Ezech. IX, 4-7).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sed et Paulus, dux ille strenuus militiae christianae, fidelis signifer, qui stigmata Crucifixi in corpore suo portabat, nunc quoque in hac verorum ac falsorum militum confusione, signo utique notabili istos ab illis discern[e]bat, cum dicebat: Qui autem sunt Christi, carnem crucifixerunt cum vitiis et concupiscentiis (Galat. V, 24). Cauta prorsus et circumspecta definitio, ac velut formam referens de ipsius veritatis exemplari impresso. Nimirum quod impressum retinet vita, signantius exprimit lingua. Qui ergo Christo confixus erat cruci, de propriae conscientiae exemplari formam istam protulit: Qui Christi sunt, carnem crucifixerunt cum vitiis et concupiscentiis. Noverat vir multae scientiae et experientiae, multos esse aut fore qui crucifigerent concupiscentias carnis, et sinerent regnare vitia cordis; multos e contrario qui, fiducia pacati cordis, negligerent mortificationem corporis. Sed sicut nonnunquam divina justitia spiritum sibi non subditum, contumeliis carnis licet afflictae flagellat, sic plerumque corpus incrassatum recalcitrat, et nova bella spiritui jam quieto resuscitat. Eapropter vult Apostolus ut, crucifixis tam vitiis interioribus quam concupiscentiis exterioribus, mundemus nos ab omni inquinamento carnis et spiritus, perficientes sanctimoniam in timore Dei (II Cor. VII, 1). Timor nempe Dei, velut quidam clavi in altum defixi, cruci nos configit, et velut affixus justitiae continere nos facit, ut non exhibeamus membra nostra arma iniquitatis, sed justitiae; et, licet sit, non tamen regnet peccatum in nostro mortali corpore. Sane quod timor Dei comparetur configentibus clavis, sententia est David, qui ait: Confige, quasi a clavis, timore tuo carnes meas; a judiciis enim tuis timui (Psal. CXVIII, 120). Si ergo nondum praevales ut vitia exstinguas, in hoc vult te esse sollicitum Apostolus, ut crucifigas. Non ait: Qui Christi sunt, exstinxerunt vitia, quae virtus paucorum est; sed, crucifixerunt, sine quo salus nulla est, sicut, praeter crucem Christi, nulla redemptio est. Ideo namque Redemptor ad operandam et informandam salutem nostram hunc passionis modum elegit, ut redemptionis sacramentum, justificationis fieret exemplum; ut quomodo videlicet ipse similitudinem carnis peccati crucifixit, de peccato damnans peccatum, sic nos, imo multo magis nos, carnem peccatricem cruciemus, crucifigentes, etsi nondum exstinguentes in ea peccatum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But Paul too, that vigorous leader of the Christian army, that faithful standard-bearer, who bore the marks of the Crucified in his own body, now also, in this confusion of true and false soldiers, distinguished the one from the other by a quite notable sign, when he said: But those who are Christ's have crucified the flesh with its vices and desires (Galat. V, 24). A truly cautious and circumspect definition, and one that, as it were, reproduces the form impressed from the very model of truth itself. For indeed, what life retains as impressed, the tongue expresses the more pointedly. He, then, who had been fastened with Christ to the cross, brought forth this form from the model of his own conscience: Those who are Christ's have crucified the flesh with its vices and desires. This man of great knowledge and experience knew that there are, or will be, many who crucify the desires of the flesh and yet allow the vices of the heart to reign; and many others, on the contrary, who, trusting in a heart at peace, neglect the mortification of the body. But just as divine justice sometimes scourges a spirit not subject to itself with the reproaches of the flesh, even though the flesh be already afflicted, so too the body, once fattened, often grows restive, and stirs up new wars against a spirit already at rest. For this reason the Apostle wishes that, with both the inner vices and the outer desires crucified, we should cleanse ourselves from all defilement of flesh and spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of God (II Cor. VII, 1). For indeed the fear of God, like certain nails driven deep, fastens us to the cross, as though fixed to righteousness, makes us hold ourselves in check, so that we do not present our members as weapons of iniquity, but of righteousness; and, though sin be present, it does not reign in our mortal body. Indeed, that the fear of God should be compared to nails that fasten us is the saying of David, who says: Pierce my flesh, as with nails, with your fear; for I have feared your judgments (Psal. CXVIII, 120). If, then, you are not yet able to extinguish your vices, the Apostle wishes you to be anxious about this at least: that you crucify them. He does not say: Those who are Christ's have extinguished their vices \u2014 which is the virtue of few \u2014 but, have crucified them, without which there is no salvation, just as, apart from the cross of Christ, there is no redemption. For this is why the Redeemer chose this manner of suffering for working out and shaping our salvation: that the sacrament of redemption might become the pattern of justification; so that, just as he himself crucified the likeness of sinful flesh, for sin condemning sin, so we \u2014 indeed, all the more we \u2014 should torment our sinful flesh, crucifying it, even if we do not yet extinguish sin within it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Hic recordari potes quia Moyses ad placandam iram Domini crucifixit principes Israelitarum (Num. XXV, 4-9); Josue autem, qui et Jesus, quinque reges Amorrhaeorum (Josue X, 26). Si enim iram Domini, quam meruimus, placare volumus, necesse est ut per continentiam nos cruciemus. Jesus autem noster, qui nos in terram promissionis introducturus est, vitia quinque sensuum in nobis crucifiget, imo et exstinguet; si tamen, sicut est justum, pendeamus in patibulis usque ad vesperum. Hujus enim perseverantiae in cruce Salvator de se ipso tibi curavit exemplum praebere, nolens consummari nisi in cruce, aut deponi de cruce ante vesperum tam diei ipsius quam vitae suae. Balaam dicebat, Moriatur anima mea morte justorum (Num. XXIII, 10); tu vero dic, Moriatur anima mea morte Domini mei Jesu Christi, et fiant novissima mea illius similia; ut videlicet in voluntaria cruce poenitentiae pendere merear usque ad terminum vitae. Quanta fiducia de cruce Filii, in manus Patris spiritum commendabis! imo quanta clementia Pater suscipiet quem Filius commendabit (8)! Filius namque qui causam animae tuae semel in cruce suscepit agendam, nunquam eam agere desinit, apud Patrem semper interpellans. Vade securus, vade gratulabundus, ubi judex tuus est advocatus tuus; tantummodo signum crucis, mortificationem Jesu, quam circumfers in corpore tuo, secum deferat spiritus tuus. Dominus autem gloriae, qui pro vobis, fratres, passus, in vobis est glorificatus, dignetur vos habere socios passionis et gloriae, et gloriantes in cruce glorificet in ea claritate quam habuit apud Patrem ante initium saeculorum, et habiturus est per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Here you may recall that Moses, to appease the wrath of the Lord, crucified the princes of the Israelites (Num. XXV, 4-9); and Joshua\u2014who is also called Jesus\u2014crucified the five kings of the Amorites (Josue X, 26). For if we wish to appease the wrath of the Lord, which we have deserved, it is necessary that we crucify ourselves through self-restraint. But our Jesus, who is to lead us into the land of promise, will crucify in us the vices of the five senses\u2014indeed, will even extinguish them\u2014provided only that, as is right, we hang upon the gibbets until evening. For of this perseverance on the cross the Savior took care to offer you an example in his own person, unwilling to be consummated except on the cross, or to be taken down from the cross before the evening both of that very day and of his life. Balaam said, 'Let my soul die the death of the righteous' (Num. XXIII, 10); but you should say, 'Let my soul die the death of my Lord Jesus Christ, and let my last end be like his'\u2014that is, that I may merit to hang upon the voluntary cross of penance even to the end of life. With what confidence, from the cross of the Son, will you commend your spirit into the hands of the Father! Indeed, with what tenderness will the Father receive him whom the Son commends! (8) For the Son, who once on the cross undertook to plead the cause of your soul, never ceases to plead it, interceding always before the Father. Go in security, go rejoicing, where your judge is your advocate; only let your spirit carry with it the sign of the cross, the mortification of Jesus, which you bear about in your body. And may the Lord of glory, who suffered for you, brothers, and was glorified in you, deign to count you companions of his passion and his glory, and, as you glory in the cross, glorify you in that splendor which he had with the Father before the beginning of the ages, and which he will have through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-n008", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(8) Vide similia sup. serm. 1 de Adventu, circ. fin., item apud S. Bernard., serm. 3 pasch., n. 3 et 8."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-n008", "label": "English", "text": "(8) See similar passages above in Sermon 1 for Advent, near the end; likewise in St. Bernard, Sermon 3 for Easter, nn. 3 and 8.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The supplied e in discern[e]bat represents damaged print."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Palm Sunday Sermon II", "topics": ["Palm Sunday", "Passion", "cross", "redemption", "devil", "fear of God", "mortification", "perseverance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-palms-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:palm", "calendar_label": "Palm Sunday", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-palms-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., De duplici specie qua Christus proponitur spectandus., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 134-137.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Palm Sunday", "Palm Sunday", "Psalm 68:21 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 52:14", "Matthew 21:10", "Psalm 87:16 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 68:8 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 50:7", "Psalm 30:2 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 17:18", "Matthew 21:9", "Matthew 26:66", "James 5:11", "Job 16:18", "Matthew 27:46", "Luke 16:25", "Matthew 24:30", "John 6:15", "Matthew 21:12\u201313", "Sirach 27:12", "Ecclesiastes 8:1", "Proverbs 16:15", "Genesis 1:27", "Luke 22:61\u201362", "Job 25:3", "2 Corinthians 6:14", "Psalm 118:74 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "Dominica in ramis palmarum", "occasion_detail": "Palm Sunday", "occasion_label": "Palm Sunday", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric sets the procession and the Passion side by side: Christ honored as king and Christ condemned as a thief. He hears the Son's cry of abandonment and answers it with the future glory that the procession prefigured. Christ's meekness when honored and his zeal in cleansing the temple form a pattern for those in authority; his unchanged mind amid changed fortunes is set before the brothers. His argument includes polemic against Christ's Jewish opponents. The sermon appears under the Palm Sunday heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De duplici specie qua Christus proponitur spectandus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the twofold appearance under which Christ is set before us to be beheld.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Desiderium animae nostrae, speciosum forma prae filiis hominum, hodierna dies alia et alia specie repraesentat filiis hominum: utraque quidem specie spectabilem, in utraque desiderabilem et amabilem, quia in utraque salvatorem hominum; licet in una sublimem, in altera humilem; in ista gloriosum, in illa aerumnosum; in ista venerabilem, in illa miserabilem: si tamen dici decet miserabilis, qui miseriam suscepit dignatione miserationis, ut de miseria sua misericordiam praestaret miseris; non ut qui sibi beatitudo est, misericordiam a miseris flagitaret. Ubi ergo miserandus videri voluit, ibi et amplius venerandus fuit. Sed sustinui, inquit, qui simul contristaretur, et non fuit; qui consolaretur, et non inveni (Psal. LXVIII, 21). Ita qui miseratione omnium miser ultro fieri voluit, apud nullum fere miserationem invenit. Sed ubi, inquies, spectatur hodie sublimis et gloriosus, ubi humilis et aerumnosus? Vide processionem; audi passionem. In his evidenter agnoscere poteris quod Isaias ait: Sicut obstupuerunt super eum multi, sic inglorius erit inter viros vultus ejus, et forma ejus inter filios hominum (Isai. LII, 14). Obstupuerunt multi de gloria quasi victoris triumphantis, cum Jerusalem ingrederetur; sed nihilominus paulo post vultus ejus inglorius et despectus, cum pateretur. Cum intrasset Jerosolymam, ait Matthaeus, commota est universa civitas, dicens: Quis est hic? (Matth. XXI, 10). Cum pateretur, confusio faciem ejus operuit, ut vere confiteatur: Exaltatus, humiliatus sum et confusus (Psal. LXXXVII, 16). Sane quod hic se confusum dicit, intelligendum est secundum hoc quod alibi dicit de facie corporis: Operuit confusio faciem meam (Psal. LXVIII, 8); quia non pepercerunt in faciem meam spuere, velare, percutere et illudere. Nam facies mentis quae semper et immobiliter habitabat cum vultu Dei, nec turbari poterat nec confundi. Dominus Deus, inquit, auxiliator meus; ideo non sum confusus; ideo posui faciem meam ut petram durissimam, et scio quoniam non confundar (Isai. L, 7). In te enim, Domine, speravi, non confundar in aeternum (Psal. XXX, 2). Confundantur potius illi, et non confundar ego; paveant, et non paveam ego (Jerem. XVII, 18)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. The desire of our soul, beautiful in form beyond the sons of men, today's feast presents to the sons of men under one appearance and another: in each appearance indeed worthy of beholding, in each desirable and lovable, because in each he is the Savior of men; although in one sublime, in the other humble; in this one glorious, in that one full of hardship; in this one venerable, in that one pitiable\u2014if indeed it is fitting to call pitiable him who took on wretchedness by the condescension of his compassion, so that out of his own misery he might show mercy to the wretched; not as one who, being blessedness itself, should beg for mercy from the wretched. Therefore where he wished to appear pitiable, there he was all the more to be venerated. But I waited, he says, for one who would grieve with me, and there was none; for one who would console me, and I found none (Psal. LXVIII, 21). Thus he who willingly chose to become wretched out of compassion for all found compassion from almost no one. But where, you will ask, is he beheld today as sublime and glorious, and where as humble and full of hardship? Look at the procession; listen to the Passion. In these you will be able clearly to recognize what Isaiah says: As many were astonished at him, so his countenance shall be without glory among men, and his appearance among the sons of men (Isai. LII, 14). Many were astonished at the glory, as of a triumphant victor, when he entered Jerusalem; but nonetheless, a little afterward his countenance was without glory and despised, when he suffered. When he had entered Jerusalem, says Matthew, the whole city was stirred, saying: Who is this? (Matth. XXI, 10). When he suffered, confusion covered his face, so that he might truly confess: I was exalted, I have been humbled and confounded (Psal. LXXXVII, 16). Indeed, when he here says he was confounded, it must be understood according to what he says elsewhere about the face of his body: Confusion has covered my face (Psal. LXVIII, 8); because they did not spare to spit upon my face, to cover it, to strike it, and to mock it. For the face of his mind, which dwelt always and unchangeably with the countenance of God, could neither be disturbed nor confounded. The Lord God, he says, is my helper; therefore I am not confounded; therefore I have set my face like the hardest rock, and I know that I shall not be confounded (Isai. L, 7). For in thee, O Lord, have I hoped; let me never be confounded (Psal. XXX, 2). Let them rather be confounded, and let me not be confounded; let them fear, and let me not fear (Jerem. XVII, 18).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Si igitur, ut dicere coeperam, hodierna processio simul et passio considerentur, ibi plane videtur Jesus sublimis et gloriosus, hic humilis et aerumnosus. In processione namque cogitatur in honore regis; in passione spectatur in poena latronis. Ibi circumdant eum gloria et honor; hic non est ei species neque decor. Ibi gaudium hominum et gloria plebis; hic opprobrium hominum et abjectio plebis. Ibi acclamatur ei: Hosanna filio David, benedictus qui venit rex Israel (Matth. XXI, 9); hic inclamatur, reus mortis (Id. XXVI, 66); subsannaturque quod se fecerit regem Israel. Ibi cum ramis palmarum ei occurritur; hic alapis palmarum in faciem caeditur, et arundine percutitur caput. Ibi praeconiis sublimatur; hic saturatur opprobriis. Ibi certatim ei via sternitur vestimentis alienis; hic exuitur propriis. Ibi tanquam rex justus et salvator suscipitur in Jerusalem; hic tanquam reus et seductor damnatus ejicitur de Jerusalem. Ibi sedet in asino stipatus obsequiis; hic pendet in ligno crucis caesus flagellis, confossus plagis, et desertus a suis. Ecce plus quam Job hic, cui tam repente, tam vehementer omnia semel in contrarium Deus vertit. Sufferentiam Job audistis, finem Domini vidistis (Jacobi V, 11), ait Jacobus apostolus. Ac si diceret: Sufferentia Job fuit usque ad reditum substantiae; sufferentia Domini usque ad exitum vitae. Job quidem patienter damna sustinuit, sed mox in terra sua duplicia suscepit; Christus ut erat repletus miseriis, et inebriatus amaritudinibus, e mundo migravit. Itaque plus quam Job hic, qui de summa, ut putabatur, repente semelque dejectus felicitate, in extrema atque gravissima finem sortitus est calamitate. Et haec, inquit, passus sum absque iniquitate manus meae, cum haberem mundas ad Deum preces (Job XVI, 18), etiam pro crucifixoribus, ut eis ignosceret."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. If, then, as I began to say, today's procession and Passion are considered together, there Jesus is plainly seen as sublime and glorious, here as humble and full of hardship. For in the procession he is regarded with the honor of a king; in the Passion he is beheld with the punishment of a thief. There glory and honor surround him; here there is in him no comeliness nor beauty. There is the joy of men and the glory of the people; here the reproach of men and the outcast of the people. There he is acclaimed: Hosanna to the son of David, blessed is he who comes, the king of Israel (Matth. XXI, 9); here it is cried out against him that he is guilty of death (Id. XXVI, 66); and he is mocked for having made himself king of Israel. There he is met with branches of palms; here he is struck in the face with blows of the palm of the hand, and his head is beaten with a reed. There he is exalted with acclamations; here he is filled with reproaches. There the way is strewn before him eagerly with others' garments; here he is stripped of his own. There he is received into Jerusalem as a just king and savior; here he is cast out of Jerusalem as a condemned criminal and deceiver. There he sits upon an ass surrounded by homage; here he hangs on the wood of the cross, scourged with lashes, pierced through with wounds, and abandoned by his own. Behold, here is one greater than Job, for whom God so suddenly, so violently, turned all things at once into their opposite. You have heard of the patience of Job, you have seen the end of the Lord (Jacobi V, 11), says the apostle James. As if he said: The patience of Job lasted until the return of his possessions; the patience of the Lord lasted until the end of his life. Job indeed patiently bore his losses, but soon received double in his own land; Christ, filled as he was with miseries and made drunk with bitterness, departed from the world. Therefore here is one greater than Job, who, cast down suddenly and once for all, as it seemed, from the summit of happiness, met his end in the utmost and gravest calamity. And these things, he says, I have suffered without iniquity of my hand, since I had pure prayers to offer to God (Job XVI, 18), even on behalf of those who crucified me, that he might forgive them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. An non igitur recte videbitur velut turbatus tanta tamque subita rerum mutatione Filius Patrem interpellare illa voce lacrymabili: Deus, Deus meus, utquid dereliquisti me? (Matth. XXVII, 46). Certe manus tuae fecerunt me! in voluntate tua deduxisti me, et paulo ante cum gloria suscepisti me; et sic repente praecipitas me? Elevans allisisti me, quasi super ventum ponens me, et elidens valde. Exaltatus, humiliatus sum et confusus; et eo confusus amplius, quo tunc exaltatus sublimius, et nunc humiliatus profundius; quantoque tunc altius elevatus, tanto nunc gravius allisus. Justum quidem est, o Pater, ut qui se exaltat humilietur, et elatio indigni digna dejectione puniatur; sed nunquid similiter justum erat ut, quem tu exaltaveras, ita confusibiliter humiliaretur, et gloriam quam humilitas meruerat, tanta sequeretur contumelia? Nunquid mihi, Pater, iratus fuisti, quod vel ad horam hic me passus sum honorari; et illud etiam perexiguum quod susceperam de bonis hujus mundi, exsolvendum erat ante mortem in poenis et contumeliis, ne postea mihi posset objici: Fili, suscepisti bona in vita tua? (Luc. XVI, 25). At qui tuus erat honor, o Pater, honor erat Filii; quia qui non honorificat Filium, non honorificat Patrem qui misit illum. Ecce exprobrant inimici tui, Domine, exprobrant commutationem Christi tui; ecce gloriantur in contumelia mea et tormento, qui paulo ante cruciabantur gloria et honore meo. Nunquid non respondebo exprobrantibus mihi verbum, ut sciant aliquando qua dispensatione commutaveris Christum tuum, quo consilio sic exaltaveris mox humiliandum, vel humiliaveris exaltatum?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Will it not, then, rightly seem that the Son, as though troubled by so great and so sudden a change of circumstances, cries out to the Father with that tearful voice: \"God, my God, why have you forsaken me?\" (Matth. XXVII, 46). Surely your hands made me! By your will you led me, and but a little while ago you received me with glory; and do you now so suddenly cast me down? Lifting me up, you have dashed me down, setting me as it were upon the wind, and shattering me exceedingly. Exalted, I have been humbled and confounded; and the more confounded, the more sublimely I was then exalted, and the more deeply I am now humbled; and the higher I was then lifted up, the more grievously am I now dashed down. It is indeed just, O Father, that whoever exalts himself should be humbled, and that the pride of the unworthy should be punished with a fitting downfall; but was it likewise just that he whom you had exalted should be so shamefully humbled, and that such great contempt should follow the glory which humility had merited? Were you, Father, angry with me, because I allowed myself to be honored here even for an hour; and was even that very small portion which I had received of the goods of this world to be paid back before death in sufferings and insults, lest afterward it might be objected to me: \"Son, you received good things in your life\" (Luc. XVI, 25)? But the honor that was yours, O Father, was the honor of the Son; for he who does not honor the Son does not honor the Father who sent him. Behold, your enemies, Lord, reproach\u2014they reproach the changed state of your Christ; behold, they glory in my contempt and torment, who but a little while ago were tormented by my glory and honor. Shall I not then answer those who reproach me with a word, that they may know at last by what dispensation you have changed your Christ, by what counsel you have thus exalted one soon to be humbled, or humbled one who was exalted?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Respondebis, o Domine Jesu, respondebis exprobrantibus tibi verbum, et verbum asperum, quando scilicet respondebit eis in facie arrogantia eorum, et arguet eos malitia eorum, videntes in quem transfixerunt. Cum enim videbunt Filium hominis venientem in nubibus cum potestate magna et majestate (Matth. XXIV, 30), tunc scient quod modo nolunt credere, quia illius posterioris gloriae, prior hodiernae processionis gloria, mysterium fuerit et figura; sicut passionis contumelia, meritum et causa. Tunc intelligent quia Christus nunc honoratus, nunc ludibrio habitus, his qui pereunt positus est in offensionem et ruinam, sicut his qui salvi fiunt, in resurrectionem et doctrinam; quia quod sacramentum est redemptionis, simul est etiam documentum aedificationis. Sic enim postulabant merita superborum, ut eorum superbiae et honor Christi fieret scandalum, et mors praecipitium; atque invidiam juste pereuntium gloria triumphantis excitaret, morientis poena damnaret. Caeterum his qui salvi fiunt, id est nobis, omnino erat necessarium ut Christus, transiens viam hujus mundi, sequentibus se viam sterneret, sicut per adversa, sic etiam per prospera; atque prius exaltatus, post humiliatus, suo traderet exemplo qualiter modestia servaretur in honore, patientia teneretur in contumelia vel dolore. Ipse siquidem honorari potuit, inflari non potuit; voluit despici, sed nescivit aut pusillanimitate dejici, aut ira morderi. Sicut enim alias cum vellent eum rapere et facere regem, ipse refugiens, et in solitudinem ad orandum aufugiens (Joan. VI, 15), docuit exemplo quod dixit et verbo, ne scilicet velimus in sublime tolli; sic modo quadam alia dispensatione sese ad horam honorari patiens, et nihilominus solitam, quia inolitam, mansuetudinem in honore retinens, formam dabat his qui in potestate sunt constituti. Qui tamen ut discerent ea discretione per humilitatem esse mansueti, ut, cum res postularet, per zelum essent erecti, Dominus statim in templum regressus, flagello facto de funiculis vindicat injurias Patris, magisque eligit furorem sacerdotum in necem suam provocare, quam templi profanationem dissimulare (Matth. XXI, 12, 13)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. You will answer, O Lord Jesus, you will answer those who reproach you with a word\u2014and a harsh word\u2014namely, when their own arrogance shall answer them to their face, and their own malice shall accuse them, as they see him whom they pierced. For when they shall see the Son of man coming in the clouds with great power and majesty (Matth. XXIV, 30), then they will know what they now refuse to believe: that the earlier glory of today's procession was the mystery and figure of that later glory, just as the contempt of the Passion was its merit and cause. Then they will understand that Christ, now honored, now held up to mockery, has been set, for those who perish, as a stumbling block and a ruin, just as for those who are being saved he has been set for resurrection and instruction; because that which is a sacrament of redemption is at the same time also a lesson for edification. For thus the deserts of the proud demanded: that for their pride even the honor of Christ should become a scandal, and his death a stumbling-block to ruin; and that the glory of the triumphant one should arouse the envy of those who are justly perishing, while the punishment of the dying one should condemn that envy. But for those who are being saved, that is, for us, it was altogether necessary that Christ, passing along the way of this world, should pave the way for those who follow him, as much through adversity as through prosperity; and that, first exalted and afterward humbled, he should hand down by his own example how modesty is to be kept in honor, and patience held fast in contempt or sorrow. For he indeed could be honored, but could not be puffed up; he chose to be despised, but did not know how to be cast down by faintheartedness, or stung by anger. For just as on another occasion, when they wished to seize him and make him king, he fled, withdrawing into solitude to pray (Joan. VI, 15), teaching by example what he had also said in word\u2014namely, that we should not desire to be raised to the heights\u2014so now, by a certain other dispensation, allowing himself to be honored for an hour, and nevertheless retaining in that honor his accustomed meekness (accustomed, because ingrained), he gave a pattern to those who are set in positions of power. Yet, that they might learn to be meek through humility with such discretion that, when the matter demanded it, they should be roused through zeal, the Lord, returning at once into the temple, with a whip made of cords avenges the injuries done to his Father, and chooses rather to provoke the fury of the priests to his own death than to overlook the profanation of the temple (Matth. XXI, 12, 13).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Ut igitur, fratres, inoffenso pede tam per prospera, quam per adversa sequamur ducem nostrum, in processione consideremus eum in honore positum, in passione contumeliis et doloribus subditum; nunquam tamen in tanta mutatione rerum mente mutatum, etsi vultum immutaverit coram Abimelech, id est regno [al. rege] Judaeorum, quam commutationem Christi caecitas exprobrat perfidorum. De immutabili mentis illius statu dicit Scriptura: Homo sanctus in sapientia permanet sicut sol; nam stultus ut luna mutatur (Eccli. XXVII, 12). De mutatione faciei illius dicit alia Scriptura: Sapientia hominis lucet in vultu ejus, et potentissimus faciem illius immutabit (Eccle. VIII, 1). Prorsus in vultu tuo, Domine Jesu, qualitercunque videatur immutatus, sive gloriosus sive inglorius appareat, lucet sapientia, de vultu tuo candor lucis aeternae radiat; utinam super nos lumen vultus tui, Domine, effulgeat! Vultus tuus sicut in tristibus, sic in laetis sibi modestus, serenus, ac totus arcano lumine cordis floridus; justis hilaris et jucundus, poenitentibus clemens et pius. Intendite, fratres, in vultum serenissimi Regis. In hilaritate vultus regis vita, dicit Scriptura, et clementia ejus quasi imber serotinus (Prov. XVI, 15). Aspexit in Protoplastum, et mox animatus spiravit in vitam (Gen. I, 27); respexit Petrum, et mox compunctus respiravit ad veniam. Mox enim ut Dominus respexit Petrum, Petrus de clementia piissimi vultus suscepit imbrem serotinum, lacrymas post peccatum (Luc. XXII, 61, 62). Lux vultus tui, o lumen aeternum, teste Job, non cadit in terram (Job. XXV, 3). Quae enim communicatio luci ad tenebras (II Cor. VI, 14)? Animae potius fidelium radios illius excipiant, aspiretque bene consciis laetitiam, sauciis medicinam. Plane vultus triumphantis Jesu, qualis spectandus est in processione, laetitia et jubilus; vultus morientis, qualis cogitandus est in passione, medela et salus. Qui timent te, videbunt me, inquit, et laetabuntur (Psal. CXVIII, 74); qui dolent se, videbunt me, et sanabuntur, sicut aspicientes in serpentem ligno suspensum post venena serpentium. Te igitur, gaudium et salus omnium, seu videant sedentem in asino, seu pendentem in ligno, vota benedicant omnium; ut, cum viderint regnantem in throno, laudent in saecula saeculorum, cui laus et honor per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Therefore, brothers, so that we may follow our leader with unstumbling foot both through prosperity and through adversity, let us consider him in the procession set in honor, and in the passion subjected to insults and sorrows; yet never changed in mind amid so great a change of circumstances, even though he changed his countenance before Abimelech, that is, before the kingdom [or, in another reading, the king] of the Jews\u2014a change in Christ which the blindness of the faithless throws back at him as a reproach. Concerning the unchangeable state of his mind, Scripture says: The holy man abides in wisdom like the sun; but the fool is changed like the moon (Eccli. XXVII, 12). Concerning the change of his countenance, another Scripture says: The wisdom of a man shines in his face, and the most powerful will change his countenance (Eccle. VIII, 1). Indeed, in your countenance, Lord Jesus, however it may seem changed, whether it appears glorious or inglorious, wisdom shines; from your countenance the brightness of eternal light radiates. Would that the light of your countenance might shine upon us, Lord! Your countenance, whether in sorrow or in joy, is modest in itself, serene, and altogether flourishing with the hidden light of the heart; to the just, cheerful and pleasant; to the penitent, merciful and kind. Attend, brothers, to the countenance of the most serene King. In the cheerfulness of the king's countenance is life, says Scripture, and his clemency is like the late rain (Prov. XVI, 15). He looked upon the first-formed man, and at once the man, given life, breathed into life (Gen. I, 27); he looked back upon Peter, and at once, struck with compunction, Peter breathed again unto pardon. For as soon as the Lord looked back upon Peter, Peter received from the clemency of that most merciful countenance the late rain, tears after sin (Luc. XXII, 61, 62). The light of your countenance, O eternal light, as Job bears witness, does not fall to the ground (Job. XXV, 3). For what fellowship has light with darkness (II Cor. VI, 14)? Rather let the souls of the faithful receive its rays, and let it breathe joy upon those of good conscience, and healing upon the wounded. Clearly the countenance of Jesus triumphant, such as is to be beheld in the procession, is joy and jubilation; the countenance of the dying one, such as is to be contemplated in the passion, is remedy and salvation. Those who fear you, he says, will see me and will rejoice (Psal. CXVIII, 74); those who grieve over themselves will see me and will be healed, just as those who looked upon the serpent raised up on wood were healed after the poison of serpents. You, therefore, joy and salvation of all\u2014whether they see you seated upon the ass, or hanging upon the wood\u2014let the prayers of all bless you; so that when they shall see you reigning upon the throne, they may praise you forever and ever, to whom be praise and honor through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Palm Sunday Sermon III", "topics": ["Palm Sunday", "Passion", "humility", "glory", "meekness", "zeal", "constancy", "repentance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-palms-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:palm", "calendar_label": "Palm Sunday", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-palms-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO IV., Qualiter Christus benedicendus., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 137-142.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Palm Sunday", "Palm Sunday", "Matthew 21:9", "Psalm 12:1\u20132 (printed locator; quotation from Psalm 112:1\u20132, historical numbering)", "Job 1:21", "Psalm 8:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 7:17 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 108:2 (historical numbering)", "Zechariah 9:9", "Psalm 88:16 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 15:10\u201311", "John 8:48, 52\u201353", "Psalm 108:28 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 15:10", "Proverbs 19:17", "2 Timothy 1:12", "Luke 10:35", "Luke 19:39\u201340", "Matthew 27:51\u201352", "Hosea 4:5", "Isaiah 42:11", "Isaiah 2:10", "Psalm 21:17 (historical numbering)", "Colossians 3:3", "Matthew 21:5"], "occasion": "Dominica in ramis palmarum", "occasion_detail": "Palm Sunday", "occasion_label": "Palm Sunday", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric hears the children's Hosanna as praise prompted by the Holy Spirit. The poverty of the king riding on an ass is the sign by which the humble recognize Christ, and he warns against a lukewarm response to grace. He contrasts the Church that received Christ with the Synagogue that rejected him, then invites the brothers to nest like doves in the clefts of the rock by meditating on Christ's wounds. His argument includes polemic against the Synagogue. The sermon appears under the Palm Sunday heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Qualiter Christus benedicendus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "How Christ Is to Be Blessed.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Hosanna filio David (Matth. XXI, 9). Vox exsultationis et salutis, vox gaudii et pietatis, vox fidei et amoris, adgratulans adventui Redemptoris, atque prophetico protestans gaudio desideratae laetitiam redemptionis. Hosanna filio David, ait haec familia David; salus est illi qui factus est ex semine David, ut salvet eos qui sunt ex fide David. Laudate, laudate, pueri, Dominum, laudate nomen Domini (Psal. XII, 1, 2); dicite: Sit nomen Domini benedictum (Job. I, 21), sit benedictus, qui venit in nomine Domini. Ex ore istorum perfecisti, o Pater, laudem Filii tui, ut incorrupto simplicis innocentiae testimonio destruas inimicum et ultorem (Psal. VIII, 3), pharisaeum et pontificem; non ultorem divinae legis, ut mentita est iniquitas sibi, sed invidiae propriae et furoris. Sed convertetur dolor ejus in caput ejus, et in verticem ipsius iniquitas ejus descendet (Psal. VII, 17). Deus laudem meam ne tacueris, ait Filius Patri; quia os peccatoris et os dolosi super me apertum est (Psal. CVIII, 2). Nihil potest negare Pater Filio; non tacuit laudem Filii vox paterna saepius audita de coelo, non tacuerunt creaturae, in tot signis et prodigiis confitentes eum auctorem naturae. Angeli testati sunt, daemones confessi sunt, ordo propheticus voce consona sibi invicem respondentes cecinerunt. Sed ista demum perfecta laus est, quam aetas illa non tacet, quae nescit adulari, et quod Spiritus suggerit, dissimulare non potest. Quid enim manifestius quam quod illa aetas a semetipsa, tam nova, tam insolita nec dicebat nec faciebat; sed Spiritus sanctus more suo per ora simplicium loquens, testimonium Filio perhibebat?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Hosanna to the Son of David (Matth. XXI, 9). A voice of exultation and salvation, a voice of joy and devotion, a voice of faith and love, rejoicing at the coming of the Redeemer, and with prophetic gladness proclaiming the joy of the long-desired redemption. Hosanna to the Son of David, says this household of David; salvation belongs to him who was made of the seed of David, that he might save those who are of the faith of David. Praise, praise, O children, the Lord; praise the name of the Lord (Psal. XII, 1, 2); say: Blessed be the name of the Lord (Job. I, 21); blessed be he who comes in the name of the Lord. Out of the mouths of these, O Father, you have perfected the praise of your Son, that by the incorrupt testimony of simple innocence you might destroy the enemy and the avenger (Psal. VIII, 3) \u2014 the Pharisee and the high priest; not an avenger of the divine law, as iniquity falsely claims of itself, but an avenger of his own envy and fury. But his sorrow shall be turned back upon his own head, and his iniquity shall come down upon his own crown (Psal. VII, 17). O God, do not be silent concerning my praise, says the Son to the Father; for the mouth of the sinner and the mouth of the deceitful has been opened against me (Psal. CVIII, 2). The Father can deny nothing to the Son; the Father's voice, heard many times from heaven, did not keep silent the praise of the Son; the creatures did not keep silent, confessing him, in so many signs and wonders, to be the author of nature. The angels bore witness, the demons confessed, the order of the prophets sang in harmonious voice, answering one another. But this, after all, is the perfect praise, which that age does not keep silent \u2014 an age that does not know how to flatter, and cannot hide what the Spirit suggests. For what could be plainer than that that age, of itself, neither said nor did things so new, so unheard of; but the Holy Spirit, speaking in his own manner through the mouths of the simple, was bearing witness to the Son?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Plane Spiritus sanctus conscius operum Christi, quid ille scilicet Christi pareret adventus, quid gaudii, quid salutis humano generi passio parturiret illius, praesaga in cordibus innocentium gaudia suscitabat, atque ad prophetandam mundo redemptionis laetitiam, simplicium sibi ministeria sensuum assumebat. Istos nempe Spiritus alloquebatur, vocans ea quae non sunt, tanquam ea quae sunt, cum per prophetam suum dicebat quae hodie per ipsum facta sunt, mandabat tunc, et nunc creata sunt. Exsulta, inquit, satis, filia Sion; jubila, filia Jerusalem: ecce Rex tuus veniet tibi justus et Salvator, ipse pauper et ascendens super asinum (Zach. IX, 9 et Matth. XXI, 5). Jam, inquit, exsulta satis, quae hactenus in tristitia fuisti; jam te satia, si tamen satiari potes, gaudio ineffabili, quod sic desiderium satiat ut amplius et felicius esurire faciat. Repleatur gaudio os tuum, et lingua tua exsultatione; cui si nec os nec lingua possunt sufficere, supereffundat jubilus quod non capit affectus. Jubila, inquit, filia Jerusalem. Beatus enim populus qui scit jubilationem (Psal. LXXXVIII, 16). Plane beatus populus qui scit et intelligit hodie sibi ineffabiliter gaudendum, quando ei Salvator advenit promissus et exspectatus ab initio saeculorum. Beatus populus qui tota alacritate devotionis hodie ei occurrit, corde simul et voce acclamans ei: Benedictus qui venit in nomine Domini (Matth. XXI, 9). In hoc siquidem benedictus est a Patre Filius, ut qui benedixerit ei, benedictionibus repleatur, non una scilicet, sed pluribus; quia nimirum benedictio quam Domino quia feneratur, cum multiplici fenore redit in caput ejus. Vae genti peccatrici, semini nequam, filiis sceleratis! ad quos spectat illa Domini querela terribilis: Non feneravi, nec feneravit mihi quisquam; omnes maledicunt mihi, dicit Dominus (Jerem. XV, 10, 11). Judaicus populus hic erat, qui in ratione dati et accepti communicare contempsit Domino, nolens benedicere Benedictum Dei Patris, ut benediceretur ab eo; sed Samaritanum, daemoniacum vocans, insanis atque blasphemis Deum insectabatur maledictis (Joan. VIII, 48, 52, 53). Maledicent illi, inquit, et tu benedices (Psal. CVIII, 28); nam ab eis benedictio quam noluerunt, elongabitur ad gentes. Benedicite, gentes, Deum nostrum, quoniam qui prior feneravit vobis, praeveniens vos in benedictionibus dulcedinis, fenerantibus sibi multiplices restituet usuras in benedictionibus beatitudinis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Clearly the Holy Spirit, aware of the works of Christ \u2014 of what the coming of Christ would bring forth, what joy, what salvation his passion would bring forth for the human race \u2014 was stirring up prophetic joys in the hearts of the innocent, and was taking to himself the service of simple senses in order to prophesy to the world the gladness of redemption. Indeed the Spirit was speaking to these, calling the things that are not as though they were, when through his prophet he spoke of what is today being accomplished through him; he commanded it then, and now it has been brought into being. Rejoice greatly, he says, O daughter of Sion; shout for joy, O daughter of Jerusalem: behold, your King shall come to you, just and a Savior, himself poor and riding upon an ass (Zach. IX, 9 et Matth. XXI, 5). Now, he says, rejoice greatly, you who until now have been in sorrow; now sate yourself \u2014 if indeed you can be sated \u2014 with ineffable joy, which so satisfies desire that it makes it hunger more abundantly and more happily. Let your mouth be filled with joy, and your tongue with exultation; and if neither mouth nor tongue can suffice for this, let the shout of joy overflow with what feeling cannot contain. Shout for joy, he says, O daughter of Jerusalem. For blessed is the people that knows jubilation (Psal. LXXXVIII, 16). Truly blessed is the people that knows and understands that today it must rejoice ineffably, since the Savior has come to it, promised and awaited from the beginning of the ages. Blessed is the people that today, with the whole eagerness of devotion, goes out to meet him, acclaiming him with heart and voice together: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord (Matth. XXI, 9). For in this the Son is blessed by the Father, so that whoever blesses him may be filled with blessings \u2014 not one only, but many; because indeed the blessing that is lent to the Lord returns upon the lender's own head with manifold interest. Woe to the sinful nation, the wicked seed, the children of wickedness! It is to them that the Lord's terrible complaint applies: I have not lent, nor has anyone lent to me; all curse me, says the Lord (Jerem. XV, 10, 11). This was the Jewish people, who scorned to share with the Lord in the account of giving and receiving, refusing to bless the Blessed One of God the Father, so that they might be blessed by him; but instead, calling him a Samaritan and possessed by a demon, they pursued God with mad and blasphemous curses (Joan. VIII, 48, 52, 53). They will curse, he says, but you will bless (Psal. CVIII, 28); for from them the blessing which they refused will be carried far off to the nations. Bless, O nations, our God, for he who first lent to you, going before you in blessings of sweetness, will restore to those who lend to him manifold interest in blessings of beatitude.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Ego tamen vereor, fratres mei, ne forte haec tempora teporis et infidelitatis tangat illa querela Domini: Non feneravi, nec feneravit mihi quisquam (Jerem. XV, 10); quia videlicet gratia offertur, nec suscipitur; promittitur merces operi, et vix aliquis spe illius operatur. Dominus siquidem fenerat, cum talenta servis partiens, scientiam verbi vel gratiam cujuslibet muneris ad lucrum praerogat: Domino autem fenerat jucundus homo qui miseretur et commodat, dicente Scriptura: Feneratur Domino, qui misericordiam facit proximo (Prov. XIX, 17). Imo Domino feneratur quicunque spe divinae retributionis aliquid operatur, ut dicere possit: Scio cui credidi (II Tim. I, 12), scio qui dixit: Si quid erogaveris, cum rediero reddam tibi (Luc. X, 35). Nos autem aut nihil aut ita timide gelideque feneramus ei, quasi debitor infidelis sit, aut non habeat unde reddere possit. Magnae prorsus fidei illa plebs Hebraea, quae, cum eum pauperem sedentem super asellum, et ipsum non suum, cerneret, omni tamen securitate ac devotione fenerabat ei, non solum vestimenta sua in via sternens, sed et se totam, quantum poterat, honori ejus impendens. Sed nimirum intelligebat super egenum et pauperem; quippe cui propheta signum dederat intelligendi Salvatorem, ipsam qua superbis viluit, paupertatem. Ipse, inquit, pauper, et ascendens super asinum (Zach. IX, 9). Hoc, inquit, signo poteris venientem agnoscere Regem tuum, cujus regnum non est de hoc mundo, quia scilicet, ad debellandam superbiam quae regnat in mundo, paupertatem seu humilitatem evangelizabit sicut verbo sic etiam exemplo."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Yet I fear, my brothers, lest perhaps these times of lukewarmness and unbelief be touched by that complaint of the Lord: \"I have not lent, nor has anyone lent to me\" (Jerem. XV, 10); because, that is, grace is offered and not received, a reward is promised for the work, and scarcely anyone works in hope of it. For the Lord lends when, distributing talents to his servants, he grants beforehand knowledge of the word or the grace of any gift, for profit; but a man who is kind, who shows mercy and lends, lends to the Lord, as Scripture says: \"He who shows mercy to his neighbor lends to the Lord\" (Prov. XIX, 17). Indeed, whoever does anything in hope of divine repayment lends to the Lord, so that he can say: \"I know whom I have believed\" (II Tim. I, 12), \"I know him who said: If you spend anything, when I return I will repay you\" (Luc. X, 35). But we either lend nothing to him, or lend so timidly and coldly, as if he were an unfaithful debtor, or had nothing with which to repay. Truly great was the faith of that Hebrew people, who, although they saw him sitting poor upon a young ass, and not even his own, nevertheless lent to him with complete confidence and devotion, not only spreading their garments in the way, but spending their whole selves, as much as they could, in his honor. For they understood, no doubt, concerning the needy and the poor one; since the prophet had given them a sign by which to recognize the Savior\u2014namely, that very poverty for which the proud held him cheap. \"He himself,\" it says, \"poor, and riding upon an ass\" (Zach. IX, 9). By this sign, it says, you will be able to recognize your King as he comes, whose kingdom is not of this world, because, to overthrow the pride that reigns in the world, he would preach poverty or humility as much by example as by word.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Beata itaque filia Sion, quae humilitatem tanquam armaturam coelestem, tanquam insigne regium didicit venerari. Misera mater ejus infidelis illa Sion, quae cum humilem vidit, fastidivit, cum honoratum vidit, invidit. Beata, inquam, Ecclesia primitivorum, quae tam fideliter agnovit, tamque gratanter suscepit venientem in nomine Domini; infelix Synagoga perfidorum, quae, venturum in suo nomine parata suscipere, illius qui Patris gloriam quaerebat cruciabatur honore. Increpa, inquiunt, discipulos tuos; quasi scilicet aut illorum simplicitas posset adulari, aut ipsius puritas vanis posset laudibus delectari. Dico, inquit, vobis, quia si hi tacuerint, lapides clamabunt (Luc. XIX, 39, 40); quia Deus laudem meam non tacebit. Prorsus ita est: si tacuerint, lapides clamabunt; quia tempore passionis isti tacuerunt, sed lapides clamaverunt, dum in testimonium et laudem morientis, petrae scissae sunt, et monumenta aperta sunt (Matth. XXVII, 51, 52). Prorsus ita est, quia modo tacente Synagoga, juxta illud: Nocte tacere feci matrem tuam (Ose. IV, 5), clamat vivis ex lapidibus Gentium Ecclesia, clamant plane lapides, de quibus suscitavit filios Abrahae qui potens est. Laudate, inquit propheta, habitatores petrae, de vertice montium clamabunt (Isai. XLII, 11). Ecce hodie in foraminibus petrae, in cavernis maceriae vox columbae resonat, clamantis et dicentis: Hosanna filio David; benedictus qui venit in nomine Domini (Matth. XXI, 9)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Blessed, then, is the daughter of Sion, who has learned to venerate humility as a heavenly armor, as a royal insignia. Wretched is her unbelieving mother, that Sion, who, when she saw him humble, disdained him, and when she saw him honored, envied him. Blessed, I say, is the Church of the firstborn, which so faithfully recognized and so gratefully received him who came in the name of the Lord; unhappy is the Synagogue of the faithless, which, prepared to receive one coming in his own name, was tormented by the honor paid to him who sought the glory of the Father. \"Rebuke,\" they say, \"your disciples\"\u2014as though either their simplicity could flatter, or his purity could be delighted by empty praises. \"I tell you,\" he says, \"that if these are silent, the stones will cry out\" (Luc. XIX, 39, 40); because God will not be silent about my praise. And indeed it is so: if they are silent, the stones will cry out; for at the time of the Passion these men were silent, but the stones cried out, when, in testimony and praise of the dying one, the rocks were split and the tombs were opened (Matth. XXVII, 51, 52). And indeed it is so, because now, while the Synagogue is silent\u2014according to that word: \"I have made your mother be silent at night\" (Ose. IV, 5)\u2014the Church of the Gentiles cries out from living stones; truly the stones cry out, from which he who is able raised up sons of Abraham. \"Praise,\" says the prophet, \"you inhabitants of the rock, they will cry out from the top of the mountains\" (Isai. XLII, 11). Behold, today in the clefts of the rock, in the hollows of the wall, the voice of the dove resounds, crying out and saying: \"Hosanna to the Son of David; blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord\" (Matth. XXI, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Benedictus qui, ut nidificare possem in foraminibus petrae, manus, pedes et latus perforari sibi tulit, et se mihi totum aperuit, ut ingrediar in locum tabernaculi admirabilis, et protegar in abscondito tabernaculi sui. Opportunum quidem petra refugium erinaciis, sed et habitatio grata columbis, cujus foramina tot vulneribus toto fere corpore patentia, et veniam offerunt reis, et gratiam conferunt justis. Imo vero tuta habitatio, fratres mei, turrisque fortitudinis a facie inimici, pia ac sedula meditatione Christi Domini vulneribus immorari, fideque et amore Crucifixi, animam ab aestu carnis, a turbine saeculi, ab impetu diaboli contutari. Super omnem gloriam mundi protectio hujus tabernaculi, videlicet in umbraculum diei ab aestu, in securitatem et absconsionem a turbine et a pluvia, ut per diem sol non urat te prosperitate, nec turbo moveat in tempestate. Ingredere igitur in petram, o homo, abscondere in fossa humo (Isai. II, 10), pone tibi latibulum in Crucifixo. Ipse petra, ipse humus, quia Deus et homo; ipse petra forata, humus fossa, quia foderunt manus meas, inquit, et pedes meos (Psal. XXI, 17). Abscondere, inquit, in fossa humo a facie timoris Domini (Isai. II, 10): hoc est, ab ipso fuge ad ipsum, a judice ad redemptorem, a tribunali ad crucem, a justo ad misericordem; ab eo qui percutiet terram virga oris sui, ad eum qui inebriat terram stillicidiis cruoris sui; ab eo qui spiritu labiorum suorum interficiet impium, ad eum qui sanguine vulnerum suorum vivificat exstinctum. Quinimo non ad ipsum tantum, sed in ipsum fuge, in foramina petrae ingredere, in fossa humo abscondere, in ipsis manibus foratis, in fosso latere te ipsum reconde. Vulnus enim in latere Christi, quid nisi ostium in latere Arcae salvandis a facie diluvii? Illud tamen figura, istud autem veritas, ubi non modo servatur vita mortalis, sed recuperatur immortalis. Ideo quippe latus suum pius et misericors aperuit, ut cruor te vulneris vivificet, calor corporis refocillet, spiritus cordis quasi patenti et libero meatu aspiret. Ibi tuto latebis, donec traneat iniquitas; ibi nequaquam algebis, eo quod in visceribus Christi non frigescat charitas; ibi deliciis afflues, ibi gaudiis superefflues; vel tunc demum cum a vita capitis tua et omnium membrorum corporis ejus absorpta fuerit mortalitas."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Blessed is he who, so that I might build a nest in the clefts of the rock, allowed his hands, feet, and side to be pierced, and opened himself wholly to me, that I might enter into the place of the wonderful tabernacle, and be sheltered in the hidden place of his tabernacle. The rock is indeed a fitting refuge for hedgehogs, but it is also a pleasing dwelling for doves, and its openings\u2014laid open by so many wounds over almost the whole body\u2014both offer pardon to the guilty and bestow grace upon the just. Indeed, my brothers, it is a safe dwelling, a tower of strength against the face of the enemy, to dwell with pious and diligent meditation upon the wounds of the Lord Christ, and by faith and love of the Crucified, to keep the soul safe from the heat of the flesh, from the whirlwind of the world, from the assault of the devil. Above all the glory of the world is the protection of this tabernacle, namely, a shelter by day from the heat, a security and hiding place from whirlwind and rain, so that the sun of prosperity may not burn you by day, nor the whirlwind disturb you in time of storm. Enter, therefore, into the rock, O man; hide yourself in the dug earth (Isai. II, 10); make for yourself a hiding place in the Crucified One. He himself is the rock, he himself the earth, because he is God and man; he himself is the pierced rock, the dug earth, for he says, \"they have dug through my hands and my feet\" (Psal. XXI, 17). \"Hide yourself,\" he says, \"in the dug earth from before the fear of the Lord\" (Isai. II, 10): that is, flee from him to him, from the judge to the redeemer, from the tribunal to the cross, from the just one to the merciful one; from him who shall strike the earth with the rod of his mouth, to him who makes the earth drunk with the drippings of his blood; from him who shall slay the wicked with the breath of his lips, to him who gives life to the dead by the blood of his wounds. Nay, flee not only to him, but into him; enter into the clefts of the rock, hide yourself in the dug earth; conceal yourself in his very pierced hands, in his opened side. For what is the wound in the side of Christ but the door in the side of the Ark for those to be saved from the face of the flood? That, however, was a figure, but this is the truth, where not only is mortal life preserved, but immortal life is recovered. For this reason indeed the pious and merciful one opened his side, so that the blood of the wound might give you life, the warmth of the body might refresh you, and the breath of the heart might breathe upon you, as it were, through an open and free passage. There you will lie hidden in safety until iniquity passes away; there you will never grow cold, because love does not grow cold in the heart of Christ; there you will abound in delights, there you will overflow with joys\u2014or rather, then at last, when your mortality, and that of all the members of his body, shall have been swallowed up by the life of the Head.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Merito itaque columba Christi, formosa Christi, cui ipsius vulnera tam tuta quam grata ad nidificandum paravere foramina, laudes ipsius utique hodie laetabunda decantat, ac de passionis memoria vel imitatione, de vulnerum meditatione, tanquam de foraminibus petrae vox suavis in auribus sponsi resonat. Caeterum vobis, fratres mei, qui tanto interius nidificastis in foraminibus petrae, quanto secretius vivitis in Christo, et vita vestra abscondita est cum Christo in Deo (Coloss. III, 3); vobis incumbit omnino ut quorum est conversatio quietior et tutior, sit etiam devotio dulcior; praesertim hodie, cum et recursus temporis et repraesentatio operis velut interesse nos facit illi gaudio solemni quo susceptus est in Jerusalem. Benedictus qui venit in nomine Domini; ipsi benedictio, regnum et imperium, qui est super omnia Deus benedictus in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Rightly, then, does the dove of Christ, the fair one of Christ\u2014for whom his very wounds have prepared clefts as safe as they are pleasant for nesting\u2014today joyfully sing forth his praises; and from the memory or imitation of the passion, from meditation on the wounds, as it were from the clefts of the rock, a sweet voice resounds in the ears of the Bridegroom. But as for you, my brothers, who have nested the more inwardly in the clefts of the rock the more secretly you live in Christ, and whose life is hidden with Christ in God (Coloss. III, 3): it falls upon you entirely that, since your way of life is quieter and safer, your devotion should also be sweeter\u2014especially today, when both the return of the season and the re-enactment of the deed make us, as it were, present at that solemn joy with which he was received into Jerusalem. Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord; to him be blessing, kingdom, and dominion, who is over all things, God blessed for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The quoted call to praise the name of the Lord comes from Psalm 112:1\u20132 in Vulgate numbering (113:1\u20132 in modern numbering); the edition prints Psal. XII, 1, 2."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Palm Sunday Sermon IV", "topics": ["Palm Sunday", "praise", "Holy Spirit", "poverty", "humility", "Church", "wounds of Christ", "contemplation"], "url": "sermons/guerric-palms-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:spiritus-domini", "calendar_label": "Pentecost Sunday", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-pentecost-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo Pentecostes, sermo I, De Dei charitate et hominum ingratitudine, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier freres, 1879), cols. 157-160.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Pentecost", "Feast of Pentecost", "Romans 8:32", "Luke 5 (printed locator; Luke 15 narrative, see note)", "Romans 5:15, 20", "John 1:9", "Matthew 5:45", "Psalm 100:1 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 30:16", "Ecclesiastes 2:2", "Proverbs 14:13", "Acts 2:2", "Isaiah 66:12 (printed punctuation retained; see note)", "Acts 6:10", "John 7:37", "Isaiah 55:1", "Hebrews 6:5 (printed locator; quotation includes 6:4, see note)", "Psalm 101:5 (historical numbering; printed punctuation retained, see note)", "1 Corinthians 10:20\u201321 (printed locator; see note)"], "occasion": "In festo Pentecostes", "occasion_detail": "Feast of Pentecost", "occasion_label": "Pentecost", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric of Igny presents the Father's gift of the Son for redemption and of the Holy Spirit for adoption as signs of divine generosity. He contrasts that generosity with human ingratitude, urging his hearers to hope for others' repentance while avoiding both despair about others and presumption about themselves. Fear made chaste by love guards spiritual joy. The Spirit's outpouring at Pentecost becomes an invitation to thirst for God's gifts; Guerric then warns against spiritual loss of appetite, slander and the passions that conflict with sharing Christ's table. The individual sermon is undated; its collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157 and is read here from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The historical heading names the feast of Pentecost, and Guerric recalls the Spirit given to the Apostles on this day."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN FESTO PENTECOSTES."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE FEAST OF PENTECOST", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De Dei charitate et hominum ingratitudine."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On God's Charity and Human Ingratitude", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Ineffabilis Deus, ineffabilis misericordia ejus. Secundum nomen ejus, ita et opus ejus. Plane ineffabilis divinae circa nos dignatio charitatis. Parum erat Patri, tradidisse Filium, ut redimeret servum; nisi daret et Spiritum sanctum, quo servum adoptaret in Filium. Dedit filium in pretium redemptionis; dedit Spiritum in privilegium adoptationis: se denique totum servat haereditatem adoptatis. O Deum, si fas est dici, prodigum sui, prae desiderio hominis! An non prodigum, qui non solum sua, sed et se ipsum impendit, ut hominem recuperaret, non tam sibi, quam homini ipsi? an non prodigum, qui, sicut proprio Filio suo non pepercit, sed pro nobis omnibus tradidit illum (Rom. viii, 32), sic nec Spiritui sancto pepercit, ut ita loquar, sed nova et mira largitate super omnem carnem effudit illum? Multum quidem effusus sui prodigus ille filius, qui tam patrimonium, quam se ipsum donavit meretricibus; sed multo effusior pater in recuperationem perditi filii, quam ille in perditionem sui (Luc. v); si tamen ulla debet esse comparatio gratiae et pecuniae, spiritus et carnis, Dei et hominis: Vide enim quanta largitate toto orbe gratia Spiritus effusa sit, non solum justis confirmandis, sed et peccatoribus justificandis: quomodo ubique gentium, Spiritu creante novum genus hominum, renovata sit facies terrae: imo quanta quotidie mutatio fiat per dexteram Excelsi, ut repente perditissimi hominum publicani et meretrices, justos quam plurimos praecedant in regno Dei, et novissimi fiant primi. Prorsus non sicut delictum, ita et donum: quia ubi abundavit delictum, ibi superabundans gratia (Rom. v, 15, 20): non solum donat delicta, sed etiam virtutum accumulat merita; et sublimius restituit lapsos redemptio, quam constituerat prima creatio. Sane in his omnibus quanto mirabilior Dei gratia praedicatur, tanto damnabilior hominis duritia convincitur, si gratiam vel oblatam recuset, vel acceptam non servet. Cui enim non offertur? super quem non effulget lumen illius? quis se abscondit a calore ejus? Neque enim reliquit se Deus sine testimonio apud conscientias hominum, illustrans eos splendore veritatis, et fovens calore bonitatis; cum et lux vera illuminet omnem hominem venientem in hunc mundum (Joan. i, 9), et Deus faciat oriri solem suum super bonos et malos, et pluat super justos et injustos (Matth. v, 45). Sed vae illis qui rebelles sunt lumini, et resistunt Spiritui sancto, nec acquiescunt veritati quam intelligunt; et sicut lutum ex calore solis, ita ex bonitate et beneficiis Dei duriores fiunt, audacter etiam provocantes eum, cum ipse dederit omnia in manibus eorum!"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. God is beyond all telling; his mercy is beyond all telling. As is his name, so also is his work. Beyond all telling indeed is the condescension of divine charity towards us. It was too little for the Father to have handed over the Son to redeem the servant, unless he also gave the Holy Spirit, through whom he might adopt the servant as a son. He gave the Son as the price of redemption; he gave the Spirit as the privilege of adoption; finally, he keeps his whole self as the inheritance of those adopted. O God, prodigal of himself, if one may say so, in his longing for man! Is he not prodigal, who spends not only what is his but his very self, to recover man, not so much for himself as for man's own sake? Is he not prodigal, who, just as he did not spare his own Son but handed him over for us all (Rom. viii, 32), likewise did not spare the Holy Spirit, so to speak, but poured him out upon all flesh with a new and wondrous generosity? That prodigal son indeed poured himself out lavishly, giving both his patrimony and himself to harlots; but the father was far more lavish in recovering his lost son than the son had been in losing himself (Luc. v), if indeed grace and money, spirit and flesh, God and man ought to be compared at all. For see how generously the grace of the Spirit has been poured out throughout the world, not only to strengthen the just but also to justify sinners; how everywhere among the nations the face of the earth has been renewed, as the Spirit creates a new race of men; indeed, how great a change is wrought daily by the right hand of the Most High, so that suddenly the most abandoned of men, publicans and harlots, go before very many of the just into the kingdom of God, and the last become first. Truly, the gift is not like the trespass, for where the trespass abounded, grace is superabundant (Rom. v, 15, 20): it not only forgives trespasses but heaps up the merits of virtues; and redemption restores the fallen to a higher state than the first creation had established. In all this, surely, the more wondrous God's grace is proclaimed to be, the more deserving of condemnation man's hardness is proved to be, if he either refuses grace when offered or fails to keep it once received. For to whom is it not offered? Upon whom does its light not shine? Who hides himself from its warmth? For God has not left himself without witness in men's consciences, enlightening them with the splendour of truth and warming them with the heat of goodness, since the true light enlightens every man coming into this world (Joan. i, 9), and God makes his sun rise upon the good and the evil, and rains upon the just and the unjust (Matth. v, 45). But woe to those who rebel against the light and resist the Holy Spirit, who will not assent to the truth they understand; and who, like clay under the sun's heat, grow harder through God's goodness and benefits, even boldly provoking him, although he himself has put all things into their hands!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Verum quid ad nos de his qui foris sunt? Ad vos potius nobis est sermo, qui spiritum adoptionis filiorum accepistis, habentes eumdem spiritum in signum adoptionis et pignus haereditatis; qui velut quidam character insignis, vasa misericordiae a vasis irae discernit. Quanquam de salute nostra, vel magis de spe salutis, ea nobis modestia gaudendum sit, ut quod in illis dolemus, adhuc de nobis pro nostra mutabilitate timeamus. Sicut enim de illis desperare impium, ita de nobis praesumere est temerarium, cum tam de nobis quam de illis, qualiscunque sit scientia praesentium, nulla possit esse praescientia futurorum; nec levis sit injuria summae potestati, penes quam est arbitrium vitae et mortis, si vel de illis tam crudeliter, vel de nobis tam audacter praejudicetur ejus judiciis. Denique de massa eorum sumus assumpti, et qui paulo ante eramus, sicut et illi, natura et vita filii irae, repente facti sumus filii gratiae. Nos ergo simus illis exemplum spei ad poenitentiam; ipsi nobis, timoris ad perseverantiam. In nobis se misericordia commendat, ut ametur; in illis se judicium exserit ut timeatur. Misericordiam et judicium cantabo tibi, Domine (Psal. c, 1): pro ista gratias agens, pro illo preces fundens, pro ista gratus et laetus, pro illo timoratus et cautus: de utroque eruditus exsultabo tibi cum tremore, quia tunc ingrediar in veritate tua, si ita laetetur cor meum ut timeat nomen tuum. Neque enim timor iste, quem amor castum facit, gaudium tollit, sed custodit; non destruit, sed instruit; non inamaricat, sed condit: ut tanto sit durabilius, quanto modestius; tanto verius, quanto severius; tanto dulcius, quanto sanctius. O castum et fidele gaudium! quam fidelis est illa de te sententia Sapientis: Non est census super salutem corporis, et non est oblectatio super gaudium cordis! (Eccli. xxx, 16) Hoc utique gaudio non est gaudere impiis, quorum fatuos risus et frivola gaudia Sapiens detestatur et increpat. Risum, inquit, reputavi errorem, et gaudio dixi: Quid frustra deciperis? (Eccle. ii, 2) Risus dolore miscebitur, et extrema gaudii luctus occupat (Prov. xiv, 13)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But what have we to do with those who are outside? Our discourse is addressed rather to you, who have received the spirit of adoption as sons, possessing that same spirit as the sign of adoption and pledge of the inheritance; like a distinctive seal, he distinguishes vessels of mercy from vessels of wrath. Yet we must rejoice in our salvation, or rather in the hope of salvation, with such restraint that, because of our own changeableness, we still fear for ourselves what we lament in them. For just as it is impious to despair of them, so it is rash to presume concerning ourselves, since, whatever knowledge there may be of present things, there can be no foreknowledge of things to come, either concerning ourselves or concerning them; nor is it a slight affront to the supreme power, in whose hands lies the determination of life and death, if his judgments are prejudged so cruelly concerning them or so boldly concerning ourselves. After all, we have been taken from their same mass, and we who a little while ago, like them, were children of wrath by nature and manner of life have suddenly become children of grace. Let us therefore be for them an example of hope leading to repentance; let them be for us an example of fear leading to perseverance. In us mercy commends itself, that it may be loved; in them judgment shows itself, that it may be feared. I will sing to you of mercy and judgment, O Lord (Psal. c, 1): giving thanks for the one, pouring out prayers concerning the other; grateful and glad for the one, fearful and watchful concerning the other. Instructed by both, I will exult before you with trembling, for I shall walk in your truth when my heart rejoices in such a way as to fear your name. For this fear, which love makes chaste, does not take joy away but guards it; does not destroy it but builds it up; does not embitter it but seasons it, so that it is the more lasting for being more restrained, the truer for being more serious, the sweeter for being holier. O chaste and faithful joy! How true is the Wise Man's saying about you: There is no wealth above bodily health, and no delight above joy of heart! (Eccli. xxx, 16) It is not for the impious to rejoice with this joy: the Wise Man detests and rebukes their foolish laughter and frivolous joys. Laughter, he says, I reckoned an error, and to joy I said: Why are you vainly deceived? (Eccle. ii, 2) Laughter will be mingled with sorrow, and mourning takes possession of the end of joy (Prov. xiv, 13).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. O bone Jesu, quam dissimile est illud gaudium tuum, quo interim consolaris renuntiantes illi falso et fallaci gaudio! quam melior est misericordia tua super vitas! quam melior est dies una in atriis tuis super millia! quanto beatiores facis pauperes tuos ipsa paupertate tua, quam mundus facere possit sua quantalibet affluentia, ubi quidquid affluit, defluit, sibique inhaerentem pereffluere facit! Aliis affluebat deliciis illa pauper familia Christi, quam fluminis impetus inundabat laetificans civitatem Dei; cum scilicet hodierna die Spiritus, tanquam torrens, replevit totam domum ubi erant sedentes (Act. ii, 2.) Apostoli. Exhibebat quippe divina Veritas, quod per prophetam promiserat: Ecce ego declino in vos, ut flumen pacis, et ut torrens inundans gloriae gentium (Isai. lxvi, 12. Quantum illis affluebat, quibus tantus influebat! quantumque de illis profluebat, de quorum ventre fluebant flumina aquae vivae! Nec solum de corde benevolentia charitatis, sed etiam ex ore vehementia profluebat torrentis eloquii, cui non poterant resistere et contradicere omnes eorum adversarii: sicut de Stephano dicitur, quia non poterant resistere sapientiae et Spiritui qui loquebatur (Act. vi, 10)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. O good Jesus, how different is that joy of yours, with which you meanwhile console those who renounce that false and deceitful joy! How much better is your mercy than lives! How much better is one day in your courts than thousands! How much happier you make your poor through your very poverty than the world could make them with any abundance of its own, where whatever flows in flows away, and makes whoever clings to it flow away with it! That poor household of Christ abounded in other delights, flooded by the rushing river that gladdens the city of God, when on this very day the Spirit, like a torrent, filled the whole house where the Apostles were sitting (Act. ii, 2.). For divine Truth was fulfilling what he had promised through the prophet: Behold, I turn towards you as a river of peace, and as an overflowing torrent of the glory of the nations (Isai. lxvi, 12. How abundantly he flowed upon them, into whom so great a One flowed! And how much flowed forth from those out of whose innermost being flowed rivers of living water! Not only did the benevolence of charity flow from their hearts, but from their mouths also flowed the force of a torrent of speech, which none of their adversaries could resist or contradict, as it is said of Stephen that they could not resist the wisdom and the Spirit who spoke (Act. vi, 10).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Ad hujusmodi gaudia vos interim, fratres, consolator vester invitat. Hoc torrente voluptatis suae sitibundas amantium mentes potare desiderat. Si quis, inquit, sitit, veniat et bibat (Joan. vii, 37). O affluens liberalitas Dei! o indeficiens largitas divinae bonitatis! Spiritum, cujus hodie primitias dedit Apostolis, offert universis. Thesaurum suum, fontem aquae vivae aperit tam hominibus quam jumentis; tanquam et ipse omnibus sapientibus et insipientibus debitor sit. Omnes, inquit, sitientes, venite ad aquas (Isai. lv, 1). Ecce personas non accipit, conditiones non discernit, merita non requirit: tantum sitire quis noverit, venire velit. Gratia quippe non admittit fastidientes; sed sicut esurientes implet bonis, sic divites dimittit inanes. O fastidium tinea cordium, rubigo mentium languor pessimus animarum, quod abominari facis bonum Dei verbum, contemnere coeleste donum, fastidire manna propter ollas carnium! Quae enim lues tam pestifera, quis morbus tam lethalis, qui sic faciat hominem immemorem suae salutis, ridentem ac male securum appropinquare usque ad portas mortis? Sed unde, rogo tam late lues ista Christi ovilia infecit, greges invasit, ut quam pluribus de grege Domini videatur etiam locus horroris et vastae solitudinis, locus pascuae ubi sunt collocati, et in pascuis uberrimis, in herbis virentibus misere pereant fastidio languidi? An non, quaeso gustaverunt donum coeleste, et participes facti sunt Spiritus sancti; gustaverunt nihilominus bonum Dei verbum, et virtutes saeculi venturi? (Hebr. vi, 5.) Si enim non gustaverunt bonum Dei verbum, unde toties eructavit cor eorum verbum bonum, cum scilicet de memoria abundantiae suavitatis ejus eructabant labia eorum hymnum? Nunc autem divinis intersunt laudibus, et dormitant; aut otiosa vel etiam perniciosa mente pertractant: sedent ad librum, et oscitant: verbum exhortationis audiunt, et ipso auditu laborant: de pascuis mutantur in pascua, et tam ista fastidiunt quam illa: jugiter versantur inter fercula vitae, et moriuntur fame. Unde ergo post illam felicem experientiam, gustumque suavem supernae dulcedinis, tanta haec oblivio, et incuria boni, languorque mentium subintravit? Sola relicta est eis vox illa plangentis, si tamen plangere velint: Percussus sum ut fenum, et aruit cor meum, quia oblitus sum comedere panem meum (Psal. ci, 5. Currebant utique bene: quis eos fascinavit retrorsum converti? Spiritu coeperunt: quomodo nunc carne consummantur? Voluptuose vescebantur: quomodo nunc intereunt in viis?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Meanwhile, brothers, your Comforter invites you to joys of this kind. With this torrent of his delight he longs to give drink to the thirsty minds of those who love him. If anyone thirsts, he says, let him come and drink (Joan. vii, 37). O overflowing generosity of God! O unfailing bounty of divine goodness! The Spirit whose firstfruits he gave to the Apostles today he offers to everyone. He opens his treasury, the fountain of living water, to men and beasts alike, as though he too were debtor to all, wise and unwise. All you who thirst, he says, come to the waters (Isai. lv, 1). See, he shows no partiality, distinguishes no ranks, requires no merits: let a man only know how to thirst and be willing to come. For grace does not admit those who have lost their appetite; but as it fills the hungry with good things, so it sends the rich away empty. O loss of appetite, moth of hearts, rust of minds, most grievous sickness of souls, you who make them loathe the good word of God, despise the heavenly gift, and tire of manna for the sake of the flesh pots! For what plague is so deadly, what sickness so lethal, as to make a man so forgetful of his salvation that he approaches the very gates of death laughing and falsely secure? But how, I ask, has this plague infected Christ's sheepfolds so widely and invaded his flocks, that to so many of the Lord's flock even the pasture where they have been placed seems a place of horror and vast solitude, and in the richest pastures, amid green grass, they miserably perish, sick with loss of appetite? Have they not, I ask, tasted the heavenly gift and become sharers in the Holy Spirit; have they not also tasted the good word of God and the powers of the age to come? (Hebr. vi, 5.) For if they have not tasted the good word of God, how did their hearts so often pour forth a good word, when their lips poured forth a hymn from the remembrance of his abundant sweetness? But now they attend the divine praises and doze; or turn over idle or even harmful thoughts in their minds. They sit at their books and yawn; they hear a word of exhortation and find even listening a burden; they move from pasture to pasture and tire of the one as much as the other; they are continually surrounded by dishes of life, and die of hunger. How then, after that happy experience and sweet taste of heavenly sweetness, have such forgetfulness, such neglect of the good and such sickness of mind crept in? All that remains to them is that cry of lament, if indeed they are willing to lament: I have been struck down like grass, and my heart has withered, because I have forgotten to eat my bread (Psal. ci, 5. They were indeed running well: who has bewitched them into turning back? They began in the Spirit: how are they now being brought to completion in the flesh? They used to feed in delight: how are they now perishing on the roads?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Videant, quaeso, ipsi, ne forte inimicus homo, qui super bonum semen patrisfamilias lolium suum superseminare consuevit, post illam suavem escam Christi, felle suo fauces eorum tinxerit, ac per hoc non modo desiderium, sed et memoriam prioris gustus aboleverit. Enimvero statim denuntiat Apostolus his qui ejusmodi sunt: Non potestis calicem Domini bibere, et calicem daemoniorum. Non potestis mensae Domini participes esse, et mensae daemoniorum (I Cor. x, 20, 21). An non tibi videtur non modo potatus sed et inebriatus calice daemoniorum, qui libidinis, irae, impatientiae, aut similium raptatur furore passionum? Ego, ut loquar de his quae jam magis in usu quam in crimine sunt, puto quod et ille mensae daemoniorum particeps sit, et pascens eos pascatur ab eis, cujus os abundat malitia, lingua concinnat dolos, sedens adversus fratrem suum loquitur et adversus filium matris suae ponit scandalum: qui etiamsi non detrahit, detrahentem libenter audit; qui scurrilitate stultiloquii cachinnantes dissolvit. Videat ergo qui ejusmodi est, an dignum sit ut, volutatus velut in cruore immolatio et sordibus daemonum, mox admittatur ad mensam Christi et Angelorum. Absit autem, fratres mei, ut, haec dicens, innocentiam vestram, qua plurimum delector, inscribam sceleri; sed haec dico ut salvi sitis, et aliorum exemplo cautiores facti, manusque lavantes in sanguine peccatoris, gratiam Spiritus sancti quam alii negligentia perdunt, vos omni vigilantia servare curetis; ad laudem et gloriam ipsius largitoris Jesu Christi Domini nostri, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Let them themselves consider, I beg, whether perhaps the enemy, who is accustomed to sow his tares over the householder's good seed, has, after that sweet food of Christ, stained their throats with his gall, and thereby blotted out not only the desire but even the memory of their former taste. Indeed, the Apostle at once warns those who are like this: You cannot drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of demons. You cannot share in the table of the Lord and the table of demons (I Cor. x, 20, 21). Does he not seem to you to have drunk from the cup of demons and even to be drunk with it, who is swept away by the fury of lust, anger, impatience or similar passions? To speak of things which are now more often treated as customary than as sinful, I think that he too shares in the table of demons, feeding them and being fed by them, whose mouth abounds in malice, whose tongue contrives deceit, who sits and speaks against his brother and sets a stumbling block against his mother's son; who, even if he does not slander, gladly listens to a slanderer; who with the buffoonery of foolish talk makes his hearers dissolve in loud laughter. Let such a man consider, then, whether it is fitting that, after wallowing as though in sacrificial blood and the filth of demons, he should at once be admitted to the table of Christ and the Angels. But far be it from me, my brothers, in saying this to charge with wickedness your innocence, in which I take very great delight. I say these things so that you may be saved and, made more watchful by the example of others and washing your hands in the blood of the sinner, may take care with all vigilance to preserve the grace of the Holy Spirit which others lose through negligence, to the praise and glory of its giver himself, Jesus Christ our Lord, who lives and reigns for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The sermon contains five numbered sections. The titles follow the printed edition."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites Luke 5 for the prodigal son; the narrative is in Luke 15. The printed reference is retained."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition leaves the parenthesis after Isaiah 66:12 unclosed. The quotation is complete; the words that follow begin Guerric\u2019s comment."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Latin prints cruore immolatio, an irregular blood-and-sacrifice phrase. \u201cSacrificial blood\u201d conveys its meaning in this warning about the table of demons; the uncertain Latin wording is retained."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-e005", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Hebrews quotation includes Hebrews 6:4 as well as the printed 6:5. The edition also cites 1 Corinthians 10:20\u201321 and leaves the parenthesis after Psalm 101:5 unclosed. The final image of washing hands in a sinner\u2019s blood is Guerric\u2019s biblical language."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Pentecost Sermon I: God's Charity and Human Ingratitude", "topics": ["Pentecost", "Holy Spirit", "charity", "grace", "hope", "presumption", "despair", "Eucharist"], "url": "sermons/guerric-pentecost-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:spiritus-domini", "calendar_label": "Pentecost Sunday", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-pentecost-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De linguarum mysterio et dono lacrymarum., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 160-164.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Pentecost", "Feast of Pentecost", "Acts 2:11", "Psalm 62:6 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 118:12 (historical numbering)", "John 6:45", "Psalm 93:12 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 18:8 (historical numbering)", "Lamentations 1:13", "Sirach 51:30", "Isaiah 50:4", "Acts 11:9", "Job 6:7", "Luke 15:7", "Psalm 118:103 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 118:24 (historical numbering)", "Amos 5:10", "Romans 12:9", "Psalm 49:16 (historical numbering)", "Job 16:7", "Proverbs 16:26", "Psalm 6:7 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 31:19", "Matthew 5:5", "John 16:7", "Proverbs 31:6\u20137", "2 Corinthians 6:10", "2 Corinthians 5:16", "Psalm 68:30 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 7:3"], "occasion": "In festo Pentecostes", "occasion_detail": "Feast of Pentecost", "occasion_label": "Pentecost", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads the fiery tongues of the apostles at Pentecost. He wishes for a tongue to praise God, or at least one to heal the wounds of those who confess, and calls blessed those who draw grace from licking the sores of sinners. He asks for the unutterable groanings of the Spirit in place of the manual labor his office excuses, and urges the brothers to seek the gift of tears, through which the Spirit consoles those who mourn. The sermon appears under the Pentecost heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De linguarum mysterio et dono lacrymarum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the mystery of tongues and the gift of tears.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Loquebantur variis linguis Apostoli magnalia Dei (Act. II, 11.). Nimirum ex abundantia cordis linguae loquebantur in eis. Exsultationes Dei in gutture eorum, quia charitas Dei diffusa erat in cordibus eorum. O Domine Deus meus, et ego utique similiter laudarem, si similiter potatus essem. Quia vero arida est anima mea, ideo est et torpida lingua. Sed sicut adipe et pinguedine repleatur anima mea, et labiis exsultationis laudabit os meum (Psal. LXII, 6). Eructabunt labia mea hymnum; sed cum docueris me justificationes tuas: id est cum dederis gustare quam suavis es, ut discam te diligere toto corde, tota anima, tota virtute. Bonus es tu, et in bonitate tua doce me justificationes tuas (Psal. CXVIII, 12). Bonitas siquidem tua, unctio tua est, qua doces eos de quibus praedictum est: Erunt omnes docibiles Dei (Joan. VI, 45). Beatus homo quem tu erudieris, Domine, et de lege tua docueris eum (Psal. XCIII, 12). Lex Domini immaculata convertens animas (Psal. XVIII, 8), charitas est: lex utique ignea, quae est in dextera ejus, quae cum digito Dei scribitur super latitudinem cordis, et ipsum cor amoris incendio, et eos ignito fervere facit eloquio. De excelso, inquit, misit ignem in ossibus meis, et erudivit me (Thren. I, 13). O quanta facilitate et celeritate, quanta copia et facultate, ignis ille quem Dominus Jesus misit in terram, non solum erudivit imperitos, sed etiam expedivit impeditos! Prorsus igneae linguae, quas ex se dispertivit ignis iste, quae sic ignescere fecerunt non solum mentes, sed et linguas Apostolorum, ut etiam nunc pius auditor ad sermones ignescat eorum. Prorsus ignea lingua Petri, ignea lingua Pauli, in quorum verbis nunc quoque vivit ignis perpetuus, qui et super corda nostra scintillat, si accedamus, si aurum vel animum ab eorum sermonibus non avertamus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"The Apostles spoke in various tongues the great works of God\" (Act. II, 11.). Surely it was out of the abundance of the heart that their tongues spoke in them. The exultations of God were in their throat, because the charity of God had been poured out in their hearts. O Lord my God, I too would indeed praise you in like manner, if I had been given to drink in like manner. But because my soul is parched, my tongue too is sluggish. But as my soul shall be filled with marrow and fatness, my mouth shall praise you with lips of exultation (Psal. LXII, 6). My lips shall pour forth a hymn; but only when you have taught me your justifications \u2014 that is, when you have given me to taste how sweet you are, so that I may learn to love you with my whole heart, my whole soul, my whole strength. You are good, and in your goodness teach me your justifications (Psal. CXVIII, 12). For your goodness is your anointing, by which you teach those of whom it was foretold: \"They shall all be taught of God\" (Joan. VI, 45). Blessed is the man whom you, O Lord, shall instruct, and whom you shall teach out of your law (Psal. XCIII, 12). The law of the Lord, unspotted, converting souls (Psal. XVIII, 8), is charity \u2014 indeed, a fiery law, which is in his right hand, which is written with the finger of God upon the breadth of the heart, and sets the heart itself ablaze with the fire of love, and makes them burn with fiery speech. \"From on high,\" he says, \"he sent fire into my bones, and taught me\" (Thren. I, 13). O with what ease and swiftness, with what abundance and power, did that fire which the Lord Jesus sent upon the earth not only instruct the untaught, but also set free the hindered! Truly fiery were the tongues which this fire distributed from itself, which so set ablaze not only the minds but also the tongues of the Apostles, that even now the devout hearer catches fire at their sermons. Truly fiery is the tongue of Peter, fiery the tongue of Paul, in whose words even now a perpetual fire lives, which also sparks upon our hearts, if we draw near, if we do not turn our ear or our mind away from their sermons.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Si meruissem aliquam accipere de linguis hujusmodi, dicerem utique et ego, Dominus dedit mihi linguam mercedem meam, et in ipsa laudabo eum (Eccli. LI, 30); sicut de illis scriptum est, Loquebantur variis linguis Apostoli magnalia Dei. Dicerem et illud: Dominus dedit mihi linguam eruditam ut sciam sustentare eum qui lapsus est, verbo (Isai. L, 4). Apostoli et eorum similes linguis sibi datis magnalia Dei praedicant, tyrannos verberant, daemonia flagellant, terram compluunt, coelos aperiunt; quia linguae eorum claves coeli factae sunt, quippe quibus de ipsis coelis linguae missae sunt. Mihi autem utinam vel lingua canis data sit, qua possim primum quidem propria, deinde et aliena ulcera lingere, si qui forte sunt qui ad hoc ipsum sibi me dignentur admittere. Beati quidem, quibus amor et dilectio divinae laudis cor replet gaudio et os jubilo. Sed et illos utique beatos dixerim, qui, cum de vulneribus animarum virus ablingunt et purulentiam, trahunt in se, quo anima eorum saginetur, spiritum et gratiam. Esuriunt siquidem et sitiunt justitiam, et famem patiuntur ut canes: ideoque nihil fastidiunt quod in corpus suum possint trajicere; neminem peccatorem abominantur, quem ad justitiam possint convertere. Quod Deus mundavit, tu ne immundum dixeris (Act. XI, 9), dictum est Principi Apostolorum, et in ipso aliis: et ob hoc mactat et manducat omne genus reptilium et volatilium, et ait, Quae prius tangere nolebat anima mea, nunc prae angustia impatientis desiderii cibi mei sunt (Job. VI, 7). Quinimo mirum in modum quanto quisque peccator ante conversionem amarior, tanto postmodum conversio ejus dulcior; quantoque desperatior, tanto gratior nobis ejus salus: quia et salvantis gratiam plus miramur, qui ovem perditam reportans in humeris, gaudium facit Angelis super uno peccatore poenitentiam agente, quam super nonaginta novem justis (Luc. XV, 7)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. If I had merited to receive any of such tongues, I too would certainly say: \"The Lord has given me a tongue as my reward, and with it I will praise him\" (Eccli. LI, 30); just as it is written of them, \"The Apostles spoke in various tongues the great works of God.\" I would also say this: \"The Lord has given me a learned tongue, that I may know how to sustain with a word him who has fallen\" (Isai. L, 4). The Apostles, and those like them, with the tongues given them, preach the great works of God, scourge tyrants, flog demons, rain down upon the earth, open the heavens; for their tongues have become the keys of heaven, since it was indeed from heaven itself that tongues were sent to them. But as for me \u2014 would that at least the tongue of a dog were given to me, with which I might lick, first my own wounds, and then those of others, if perhaps there are any who would deign to admit me to this very task on their behalf. Blessed indeed are those whose heart the love and affection of divine praise fills with joy, and whose mouth with jubilation. But I would also surely call blessed those who, in licking away the venom and pus from the wounds of souls, draw into themselves the spirit and grace by which their own soul is fattened. For they hunger and thirst after justice, and suffer hunger like dogs; and therefore they disdain nothing that they can take into their own body; they abominate no sinner whom they might be able to convert to justice. \"What God has cleansed, do not call unclean\" (Act. XI, 9), was said to the Prince of the Apostles, and in him to others; and for this reason he slaughters and eats every kind of creeping thing and bird, and says: \"The things my soul formerly refused to touch are now, through the anguish of impatient desire, my food\" (Job. VI, 7). Indeed, in a wondrous way, the more bitter any sinner was before his conversion, the sweeter afterward is his conversion; and the more he was despaired of, the more welcome to us is his salvation \u2014 because we marvel all the more at the grace of him who saves, who, carrying the lost sheep back on his shoulders, causes more joy to the Angels over one sinner doing penance, than over ninety-nine just men (Luc. XV, 7).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Dicant igitur alii quibus datum est: Quam dulcia faucibus meis eloquia tua Domine (Psal. CXVIII, 103)! anima autem mea esuriens et famem patiens ut canis, etiam amarum sumet pro dulci, et abominabile pro desiderabili. Delectentur alii lambere mella Scripturarum; ego vero delectabor ulcera lingere peccatorum, mei scilicet mihique similium. Foedum quidem ulcus peccati, visuque horribile; sed tamen ipsum lingere, cujus sit saporis et gratiae, non sapit, non capit, nisi qui salutem periclitantium esurit, et famem patitur ut canis: de qualibus dictum est, Lingua canum tuorum ex inimicis (Psal. CXVIII, 24). Sed vae miseris qui tanto studio pereunt, tantoque desiderio mortem sibi consciscunt, ut vulnera sua tegant, curamque canum refugiant, et asperitatem linguae medicinalis arbitrentur morsum odii lethalis! Oderunt corripientem in porta, et perfecte loquentem abominati sunt (Amos. V, 10). Perfecte loquentem dico, non tam de perfectione disserentem, quam perfecta dilectione corripientem. Sed dicam, Perfecta dilectione, an perfecto odio? Imo utrumque, et perfecta dilectione, et perfecto odio: quia perfectum odium nihil aliud est, quam perfecta dilectio; et una est utriusque perfectio, quam Apostolus determinat, dicens, Odientes malum, adhaerentes autem bono (Rom. XII, 9)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Let others, then, to whom it has been given, say: \"How sweet are your words to my palate, O Lord!\" (Psal. CXVIII, 103). But my soul, hungering and suffering famine like a dog, will take even the bitter for sweet, and the abominable for the desirable. Let others delight to lick the honey of the Scriptures; I, however, will delight to lick the sores of sinners\u2014namely, my own, and those of men like me. Foul indeed is the sore of sin, and horrible to see; yet what savor and grace there is in licking it, no one tastes, no one grasps, except him who hungers for the salvation of those in peril, and suffers famine like a dog: of such it is said, \"The tongue of your dogs shall be red from the enemies\" (Psal. CXVIII, 24). But woe to the wretched who perish with such zeal, and bring death upon themselves with such desire, that they cover their wounds and flee the care of dogs, judging the roughness of the healing tongue to be the bite of deadly hatred! \"They hated him that reproved in the gate, and abhorred him that spoke perfectly\" (Amos. V, 10). I say 'spoke perfectly'\u2014not so much one discoursing about perfection, as one reproving with perfect love. But shall I say 'with perfect love,' or 'with perfect hatred'? Rather both: with perfect love and with perfect hatred; for perfect hatred is nothing other than perfect love, and the perfection of both is one, which the Apostle defines, saying, \"Hating that which is evil, cleaving to that which is good\" (Rom. XII, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Sed revertar ad id unde digressus eram. Optabam mihi linguam qua laudarem Deum, vel certe linguam qua confitentium ulcera curarem: in altero quidem mihi quaerens divinae devotionis fructum; in altero, fraternae salutis lucrum: vobis quoque in altero dulcis, in altero fieri desiderans utilis. Nam et de saecularium arte et officio poetarum, ita sumptum est apud quemdam eorum: \nAut prodesse volunt, aut delectare poetae.\nOmne tulit punctum qui miscuit utile dulci.\n(HORAT., Art. poet.)\n Optabam, inquam, mihi gratiam oris, qua Deo et vobis officiosus deservirem; ut aliquatenus compensarem verbo, quod minus prosum exemplo. Quanquam satis suspecta mihi sit haec consolatio, si dicam et non faciam; si suppetant suffragia linguae, cum desint merita vitae; imo magis fortasse timendum sit, ne peccanti dicat Deus, Quare tu enarras justitias meas, et assumis testamentum meum per os tuum? (Psal. XLIX, 16). Sed quid agam? Si locutus fuero, non quiescet dolor meus; et si tacuero, non recedet a me (Job. XVI, 7). Pavor est utrobique molestus, et coarctor e duobus; quia locutionem et officii locus exigit, et vita contradicit. Sed recordor verbi quod apud Sapientem inveni: Anima laborantis laborat sibi, quia compulit eum os suum (Prov. XVI, 26). Loquar igitur, non prout locus expetit, sed prout sensus suppetit, imo prout Dominus dederit, in cujus manu sumus et nos, et sermones nostri. Loquar, inquam, et me ipsum lingua obligabo propria, ut vel prae confusione aliquando laborare compellar: et si corpus a labore manuum excusaverit, certe anima laborantis laboret sibi, ut dicat cum David, Laboravi in gemitu meo (Psal. VI, 7). O si mihi dentur illi gemitus inenarrabiles, quibus Spiritus postulat pro sanctis, ut laborem in eis! Procul dubio labor hujusmodi gemituum satis digne pensaret mihi quotidianum laborem manuum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. But I will return to the point from which I digressed. I was wishing for myself a tongue with which to praise God, or at least a tongue with which to heal the sores of those who confess: seeking in the one case for myself the fruit of divine devotion, in the other the gain of my brothers' salvation; desiring to become, for you also, sweet in the one case, useful in the other. For concerning the art and office of the secular poets, this is taken from one of them: 'Poets wish either to profit or to please. He has carried every point who has mixed the useful with the sweet.' (Horace, Art of Poetry). I was wishing, I say, for the grace of speech, by which I might dutifully serve both God and you, so that I might in some measure make up by word for my being of less use by example. Though this consolation is quite suspect to me, if I should speak and not do; if the tongue's resources should be at hand while the merits of life are lacking\u2014indeed it is rather to be feared that God might say to the sinner, \"Why do you declare my justices, and take my covenant into your mouth?\" (Psal. XLIX, 16). But what shall I do? \"If I speak, my sorrow will not rest; and if I am silent, it will not depart from me\" (Job. XVI, 7). The dread is troublesome on both sides, and I am constrained between the two; for the place of duty demands speech, and my life contradicts it. But I recall a saying I found in the Wise Man: \"The soul of him who labors, labors for himself, because his own mouth has compelled him\" (Prov. XVI, 26). I will speak, then, not as the occasion demands, but as understanding supplies\u2014rather, as the Lord shall grant, in whose hand are both we ourselves and our words. I will speak, I say, and I will bind myself by my own tongue, so that I may at least be compelled by shame to labor at some point: and if the body be excused from the labor of the hands, let the soul of him who labors certainly labor for itself, that it may say with David, \"I have labored in my groaning\" (Psal. VI, 7). O, if only those unutterable groanings might be given to me, with which the Spirit intercedes for the saints, that I might labor in them! Without doubt, labor of such groanings would quite worthily make up for my daily labor of hands.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sed et vos ipsi, fratres, si aemulari meliora charismata didicistis, optate gemitus hujusmodi a Spiritu vobis infundi. Nescio si sit in donis Spiritus aliud convenientius aut commodius his qui sunt infirmitate et miseria circumdati: nescio si Spiritui sancto qui in columba apparuit, alia vox familiarior gemitu, aut gratior sit. Hoc autem scio, quia in nullo opere tanta tamque parata nobis suppetat materia, sicut in gemitu et planctu nostri: nisi miseriam suam nostra dissimulet superbia, aut sensus obduretur stupore vel amentia. Hoc autem primum circa infirmos, quorum curam suscipit, operatur medicina sancti Spiritus, qui est illuminatio nostra et salus nostra, ut videlicet se ipsum phreneticus sentiat et sciat, et conversus ad cor dicat Domino cum propheta: Postquam convertisti me, egi poenitentiam; et postquam ostendisti mihi, percussi femur meum (Jerem. XXXI, 19). Nisi enim quis apponat scientiam, non apponet dolorem: nisi doluerit, non meretur consolationem. Beati enim qui lugent, quoniam ipsi consolabuntur (Matth. V, 5). Puto quia nec in Apostolis hodie locum invenisset Paracleti consolatio, nisi quia lugebant se desolatos, quia non poterant non lugere filii sponsi, cum sponsus ablatus esset ab eis. Inde erat quod eis dicebat: Nisi ego abiero, Paracletus non veniet ad vos (Joan. XVI, 7). Nisi desolati fueritis praesentia mea corporali, non vos consolabitur visitatio spiritualis. Date, inquit, siceram moerentibus, et vinum his qui amaro animo sunt, non scilicet illis qui ebrii sunt gaudio et luxu saeculi. Quae enim participatio justitiae cum iniquitate? nunquid bibere possunt calicem Domini, et calicem daemoniorum? Bibant, inquit, potius pauperes Apostoli, et obliviscantur egestatis suae (Prov. XXXI, 6, 7), ut dicant: Sicut egentes multos autem, locupletantes (II Cor. VI. 10). Bibant moerentes de absentia sponsi, et dolorum non recordentur amplius, sed dicant: Etsi cognovimus Christum secundum carnem, sed nunc jam non novimus (Id. V, 16). Nam et te ipsum, si pio affectu Psalmistae dicere potueris, Ego sum pauper et dolens (Psal. LXVIII, 30), sic ditabit, sic, laetificabit sobria ebrietas hujus praeclari calicis, ut, etsi pauper sis, paupertas animum non urat; et si dolebas quia peccasti, jam peccati reatus conscientiam non mordeat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But you yourselves also, brothers, if you have learned to be zealous for the better gifts, desire that such groanings be poured into you by the Spirit. I do not know whether there is among the gifts of the Spirit anything more fitting or more suitable for those who are surrounded by weakness and misery; I do not know whether to the Holy Spirit, who appeared in the form of a dove, any voice is more familiar or more pleasing than a groan. But this I know: that in no work is there so great and so ready a store of material for us as in groaning and lamenting over ourselves\u2014unless our pride disguises our own misery, or our perception is hardened by stupor or madness. Now this is the first thing that the medicine of the Holy Spirit, who is our illumination and our salvation, works upon the sick whose care it undertakes: that the one who is deranged should feel and know himself to be so, and, turning to his heart, say to the Lord with the prophet: 'After you converted me, I did penance; and after you showed me, I struck my thigh' (Jerem. XXXI, 19). For unless one adds knowledge, he will not add sorrow; unless he has grieved, he does not merit consolation. For blessed are they that mourn, for they shall be comforted (Matth. V, 5). I think that the consolation of the Paraclete would not have found a place among the Apostles today, had they not been mourning themselves as desolate\u2014for the sons of the bridegroom could not but mourn when the bridegroom had been taken from them. Hence it was that he said to them: 'Unless I go away, the Paraclete will not come to you' (Joan. XVI, 7). Unless you have been left desolate of my bodily presence, a spiritual visitation will not console you. 'Give,' he says, 'strong drink to those that mourn, and wine to those who are of bitter mind'\u2014not, that is, to those who are drunk with the joy and excess of this world. For what fellowship has justice with iniquity? Can they drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of devils? Let the poor Apostles rather drink,' he says, 'and forget their poverty' (Prov. XXXI, 6, 7), so that they may say: 'As needy, yet enriching many' (II Cor. VI. 10). Let those who mourn over the absence of the bridegroom drink, and let them remember their sorrows no more, but say: 'And if we have known Christ according to the flesh, yet now we know him so no longer' (Id. V, 16). For you yourself too, if with the pious feeling of the Psalmist you can say, 'I am poor and sorrowful' (Psal. LXVIII, 30), the sober inebriation of this glorious cup will so enrich you, will so gladden you, that although you are poor, poverty will not burn your soul; and if you were sorrowful because you had sinned, the guilt of sin will no longer gnaw at your conscience.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Vide autem, si non et ipse Spiritus sanctus in judicium venit in hunc mundum, ut qui dolent non doleant, et qui rident, aeterno et inconsolabili se luctui tradant. Melius est igitur ire ad domum luctus quam ad domum convivii (Eccli. VII, 3). Et revera sapiens, etsi aliquando consolationem meretur, ut dolorum non recordetur amplius, eorum scilicet de quibus consolationem accipit, semper tamen, ut locum paret consolationibus novis, novas exquirit de se ipso causas doloris; non statim blandiens sibi, quasi jam per omnia justus sit; sed eo subtilior, quo jam illuminari coepit; eo districtior, quo justificari coepit, fit accusator et judex sui. Ei ergo qui ejusmodi est, crebro, nisi fallor, Spiritus consolator advenit, quoniam et adventum jam ipse suum antevenit; adveniens scilicet ut consolationem tribuat, sed anteveniens, ut lugere doceat. Luctus siquidem pius atque religiosus, in doctrina Spiritus et ordine primus, et utilitate invenitur praecipuus; quippe cum sit summa sapientia sanctorum, custodia justorum, sobrietas modestorum, prima incipientium virtus, proficientium stimulus, perfectorum cumulus, pereuntium salus, periclitantium portus, promissionem denique habens consolationum quae nunc sunt, et gaudiorum quae futura sunt; ad quae nos perducat qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. But consider whether the Holy Spirit himself does not come into this world for judgment, so that those who grieve may grieve no more, and those who laugh may give themselves over to eternal and inconsolable mourning. It is better, therefore, to go to the house of mourning than to the house of feasting (Eccli. VII, 3). And indeed the wise man, even though he sometimes merits consolation so that he no longer remembers those sorrows for which he receives comfort, nevertheless always seeks out new causes of sorrow from himself, so as to make room for new consolations; not at once flattering himself as though he were already righteous in every respect, but becoming his own accuser and judge\u2014the more discerning, the more he has begun to be enlightened; the more strict, the more he has begun to be justified. To one, then, who is of this kind, the comforting Spirit, unless I am mistaken, comes often, since he himself anticipates his own coming\u2014coming, that is, to grant consolation, but anticipating it in order to teach mourning. For pious and devout mourning is found, in the teaching of the Spirit, to be first in order and chief in usefulness; since it is the highest wisdom of the saints, the guardianship of the just, the sobriety of the modest, the first virtue of beginners, the spur of those advancing, the crown of the perfect, the salvation of those perishing, the harbor of those in peril\u2014having, finally, the promise both of the consolations that now are, and of the joys that are to come; to which may he bring us who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints aurum (gold), where aurem (ear) is expected alongside animum (mind)."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Pentecost Sermon II", "topics": ["Pentecost", "Holy Spirit", "tongues", "preaching", "confession", "conversion", "tears", "compunction"], "url": "sermons/guerric-pentecost-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:peter-paul", "calendar_label": "Sts. Peter and Paul, Apostles", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-peter-paul-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN NATALI APOSTOLORUM PETRI ET PAULI, SERMO I., De duabus olivis ad dexteram candelabri., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 177-180.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saints Peter and Paul", "Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles", "Zechariah 4:4, 11, 14", "John 8:12", "Zechariah 4:14", "Psalm 127:3 (historical numbering)", "Habakkuk 3:17", "Jeremiah 11:16", "Lamentations 1:13", "Psalm 108:24 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 118:66 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 122:2 (historical numbering)", "4 Kings 5:16 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Kings)"], "occasion": "In natali Apostolorum Petri et Pauli", "occasion_detail": "Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles", "occasion_label": "Saints Peter and Paul", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads Zechariah's vision of two olive trees beside the golden lampstand as Peter and Paul, sons of the oil of splendor who stand before the Lord. Christ is the lampstand, golden by substance, while believers are gilded by grace. Guerric reminds the brothers, who share one property and one house, to keep the fervor of their first profession, and reads the oil of splendor as the Holy Spirit, who anoints and enlightens. The sermon appears under the heading for the Apostles Peter and Paul in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN NATALI APOSTOLORUM PETRI ET PAULI"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE BIRTHDAY (FEAST) OF THE APOSTLES PETER AND PAUL", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De duabus olivis ad dexteram candelabri."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On the two olive trees at the right hand of the lampstand.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Et respondi, inquit Zacharias, et dixi ad angelum qui loquebatur in me: Quid sunt duae olivae istae ad dexteram candelabri et ad sinistram ejus? Isti sunt, inquit, duo filii olei splendoris, qui assistunt Dominatori universae terrae (Zach. IV, 4, 11, 14). De candelabro quod viderat totum aureum, et septem lucernas ejus super illud intellexerat Propheta, sed nec nobis dubitare jam licet, cum in lumine ejus videamus lumen. Hoc lumen cum alia demonstret, seipsum ignorari non patitur, sed testimonium perhibens de seipso loquitur: Ego sum lux mundi (Joan. VIII, 12). Lux ex divinitate, candelabrum ex humanitate. Et ipsum totum aureum, non auratum; quia sive divinitas, sive sapientia, sive charitas auro significetur, haec omnia Christus non per participationem, sed per substantiam. Nos cum simus natura plumbei, vel magis lutei, gratia et participatione ipsius, non aurum vel aurei, sed ut multum aurati. Septem lucernae super ipsum, septem spiritus, septem oculi Domini, qui discurrunt in universam terram. De his ergo Propheta certus, de duabus olivis quidnam sint percunctatur, auditque ab angelo qui in eo loquebatur: Isti sunt duo filii olei splendoris, qui assistunt Dominatori universae terrae (Zach. IV, 14). Interpretentur alii ut volunt aut possunt, unusquisque pro sensu suo: sive Enoch et Eliam, qui apud Deum in secreto vivunt; sive Moysen et Eliam, qui cum eo in monte visi sunt; sive duos ordines praedicatorum, veteris scilicet ac novi Testamenti: ut qui Veteris, ad sinistram sint propter promissa temporalia; qui Novi, ad dexteram, propter aeterna: ego quidquid a regula fidei non discrepat, aut a contextu lectionis non nimie dissonat, suscipio, veneror, amplector."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"And I answered,\" says Zechariah, \"and I said to the angel who spoke in me: What are these two olive trees at the right hand of the lampstand, and at the left thereof? These,\" he says, \"are the two sons of the oil of splendor, who stand before the Ruler of the whole earth\" (Zach. IV, 4, 11, 14). The Prophet had understood the lampstand which he had seen, all of gold, and its seven lamps upon it; but we are no longer permitted to doubt, since in his light we see light. This light, though it shows other things, does not allow itself to go unknown, but bears witness of itself and says: \"I am the light of the world\" (Joan. VIII, 12). Light from his divinity, lampstand from his humanity. And it itself is wholly gold, not gilded; because whether divinity, or wisdom, or charity is signified by gold, Christ is all these, not by participation, but by substance. We, since by nature we are leaden, or rather of clay, by grace and participation in him are not gold or golden, but at most gilded. The seven lamps upon him are the seven spirits, the seven eyes of the Lord, which run through the whole earth. Concerning these, then, the Prophet, being certain, inquires what the two olive trees might be, and hears from the angel who was speaking in him: \"These are the two sons of the oil of splendor, who stand before the Ruler of the whole earth\" (Zach. IV, 14). Let others interpret as they will or can, each according to his own understanding: whether Enoch and Elijah, who live with God in secret; or Moses and Elijah, who were seen with him on the mountain; or the two orders of preachers, namely of the Old and the New Testament\u2014so that those of the Old are on the left on account of temporal promises, and those of the New are on the right on account of eternal ones: I accept, venerate, and embrace whatever does not disagree with the rule of faith, nor conflict excessively with the context of the reading.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Puto tamen, sine praejudicio melioris intellectus, Petrum et Paulum non absurde posse intelligi: imo absurdum videri, qui hodie aliud interpretari velit, cum hodie nihil aliud cantet aut tractet Ecclesia ubique terrarum, quam praeconia Patrum doctorumque suorum. Nam etsi aliis possit aptari, puto quia istis specialius congruit, seu illud quod tanquam duae olivae circa candelabrum a Propheta videntur, seu quod duo filii esse olei splendoris ab angelo memorantur. Et alterum quidem altero exponitur, visio scilicet prophetica interpretatione angelica: ut inde intelligantur olivae, quia filii olei splendoris; inde circa candelabrum, quia Domino assistunt. Quia enim ex oleo Spiritus sancti sunt regenerati, hodieque florent et fructificant sicut oliva fructifera in domo Domini, etiam oleastro gentili, qui in ipsos insertus est, impertientes pinguedinis suae succum ac dulcedinis, gratias illis agat sterilis dudum et amara gentilitas; et si non ab ipsis, tamen per ipsos est, quod nunc tam copioso, tam pretioso fructu per orbem abundat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. I think, however, without prejudice to a better understanding, that Peter and Paul can not unreasonably be understood here; indeed it would seem unreasonable for anyone today to wish to interpret it otherwise, since today the Church throughout the whole world sings or treats of nothing else than the praises of her Fathers and teachers. For even though it could be fitted to others, I think that it applies more specially to these two, whether as regards what is seen by the Prophet as two olive trees around the lampstand, or what is called by the angel the two sons of the oil of splendor. And indeed the one is explained by the other\u2014the prophetic vision, that is, by the angelic interpretation\u2014so that from it we understand them to be olive trees because they are sons of the oil of splendor, and around the lampstand, because they stand before the Lord. For since they were regenerated from the oil of the Holy Spirit, and today they flourish and bear fruit like a fruitful olive tree in the house of the Lord, even the wild olive of the Gentiles, which has been grafted into them, receiving from them the sap of their richness and sweetness\u2014let the Gentile world, once barren and bitter, give thanks to them; and even if it is not from them, yet it is through them that there now abounds throughout the world so abundant, so precious a fruit.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Vobis tamen, fratres, quibus sicut substantia et domus una, ita cor unum et anima una: vobis, inquam, specialius in ipsis censeo gloriandum, qui sicut novella olivarum de eorum radice traduxistis non solum fidei succum, sed et formam vitae atque ordinis exemplum. Quod si novella olivarum vos, utique et filii olei vos, filii uncti, qui est Christus Dei; diceturque et de vobis, cum hodie ad altare accedetis: Filii tui sicut novellae olivarum, in circuitu mensae tuae (Psal. CXXVII, 3). O novella olivarum nobilium! o generosum germen tam pinguium, tamque feracium arborum! semper, obsecro, recordare de qua pullulaveris stirpe, de qua succreveris radice, ne unquam sterilitas operum, aut amaritudo morum te degenerem arguat, aut, quod absit, inutilem ignibus addicat. Cave, quaeso, notam infelicium temporum, de quibus meministi prophetatum: Mentietur opus olivae, et arva non afferent cibum (Habac. III, 17). Duo tamen praecipue tibi existimo cavenda: ne aut per teporem fias sicut oliva projiciens florem suum, sicut legis in Propheta, ut videlicet fructum quem in initiis novitii fervoris promittebas non facias; aut cum feceris, ne per superbiam perdas, et dicat tibi Jeremias: Olivam uberem, pulchram, fructiferam, speciosam vocavit Dominus nomen tuum: sed heu! ad vocem loquelae grandis exarsit ignis in ea, et combusta sunt omnia fruteta ejus (Jerem. XI, 16). Confidimus autem de vobis meliora, dilectissimi, tametsi ita loquamur. Confidimus quia oleum splendoris, vel magis olei splendor, cujus estis filii, sicut et patres nostri Apostoli, unctione sua docebit vos de omnibus: nec solum deterget torporem, quia oleum est; sed et discutiet errorem, quia splendor est."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Yet to you, brothers, for whom, just as there is one property and one house, so there is one heart and one soul: to you, I say, I judge it belongs more especially to glory in them, you who, like young shoots of the olive, have derived from their root not only the sap of faith, but also the pattern of life and the example of your order. But if you are young shoots of the olive trees, then surely you too are sons of the oil, sons of the Anointed, who is the Christ of God; and it shall be said of you also, when today you approach the altar: \"Your children are like young olive plants round about your table\" (Psal. CXXVII, 3). O noble young shoots of olive trees! O generous offspring of trees so rich and so fruitful! Always, I beg you, remember from what stock you have sprung, from what root you have grown, lest ever the barrenness of works, or the bitterness of conduct, convict you of degeneracy, or\u2014God forbid\u2014condemn you as useless to the fires. Beware, I beg, of the mark of unhappy times, of which you remember it was prophesied: \"The labor of the olive shall fail, and the fields shall yield no food\" (Habac. III, 17). Yet I think there are two things especially you must guard against: either that through lukewarmness you become like an olive tree casting off its flower, as you read in the Prophet\u2014that is, that you fail to produce the fruit which in the beginnings of your novice fervor you promised; or, having produced it, that through pride you lose it, and Jeremiah say to you: \"The Lord called your name a fruitful olive tree, fair, fruitful, beautiful: but alas! at the voice of a great utterance fire has blazed up in it, and its branches are all burned\" (Jerem. XI, 16). We trust, however, better things of you, most beloved, even though we speak thus. We trust that the oil of splendor\u2014or rather, the splendor of the oil\u2014whose sons you are, as were our fathers the Apostles, will by its anointing teach you all things: not only will it wipe away sluggishness, because it is oil, but it will also dispel error, because it is splendor.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Scio posse intelligi olei splendoris, id est, quod de splendore Filio procedit, vel quod splendorem illuminationis diffundit: sed puto quia non inconvenienter etiam per intransitionem accipi potest oleum, quod splendor est. Utrumque siquidem Spiritus est in natura sui, et unctio scilicet spiritualis, et splendor invisibilis: utrumque in nobis operatur munere sui. Ungit affectus ex eo quod oleum est: illuminat intellectus ex eo quod splendor est. Qui tamen non ex alio atque alio, sed ex uno ipso et oleum, et splendor est; quia nonnisi unum ipsum est. Ungit affectus, quia charitas est: illuminat intellectus, quia veritas est. Ungit affectus, cum devotionem donat: illuminat intellectus, cum mysteria revelat. Cum bonitatem docet, ut simus simplices sicut columbae, ungit affectus: cum scientiam docet, ut simus prudentes sicut serpentes, illuminat intellectus. Illuminat, ut habeamus in nobis salem: ungit, ut habeamus inter nos pacem. In hoc igitur te nobis probabis filium olei splendoris, si suavem et unctum per benevolentiam, discretum et maturum per sapientiam te nobis exhibueris; ut suavitas veritate sit condita, et aequitate charitas ordinata. Puto autem quod hanc germinam virtutem sui, vel affectum muneris sui, propter quam splendor voluit dici, etiam in illo igne Spiritus sanctus significaverit, in quo ipse invisibilis invisibiliter Apostolis apparuit. Qui enim splendor fuit in aspectu oculorum, oleum, nisi fallor, fuit in sensu corporum: ut veluti in quodam lavacro olei viderentur sibi baptizati, cum super eos effunderetur ignis iste coelestis. Verumtamen qualiscunque fuerit in eis corporaliter sensus illius ignis (parum enim interest qualis sentiatur, sed qualiter operetur), illud, ut opinor, et probabilius dicitur, et religiosius creditur, quod idem ignis qui inflammavit et illuminavit corda, refrigeravit corpora: ut per charitatem mentes arderent, et per castitatem membra frigerent, et utrumque congrueret singulis, sive illud Jeremiae, De excelso misit ignem in ossibus meis (Thren. I, 13); sive illud Psalmistae, Caro mea immutata est propter oleum (Psal. CVIII, 24). Nisi forte aliquid de lege membrorum contra legem mentis relinquere voluit Spiritus, ut virtus in infirmitate perficeretur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. I know that 'oil of splendor' can be understood as meaning that which proceeds from Splendor, the Son, or that which pours forth the splendor of illumination. But I think that, not unfittingly, it can also be taken appositionally, as 'oil, which is splendor.' For indeed the Spirit is both of these in his very nature\u2014spiritual anointing, and invisible splendor\u2014and he works both of these in us by the gift of himself. He anoints the affections insofar as he is oil; he illuminates the intellect insofar as he is splendor. Yet he is not oil from one source and splendor from another, but from one and the same self he is both oil and splendor, because he is nothing other than one and the same. He anoints the affections, because he is charity; he illuminates the intellect, because he is truth. He anoints the affections when he grants devotion; he illuminates the intellect when he reveals mysteries. When he teaches goodness, that we may be simple as doves, he anoints the affections; when he teaches knowledge, that we may be prudent as serpents, he illuminates the intellect. He illuminates, that we may have salt within us; he anoints, that we may have peace among ourselves. In this, then, you will prove yourself to us a son of the oil of splendor, if you show yourself to us sweet and anointed through benevolence, and discreet and mature through wisdom; so that sweetness may be seasoned with truth, and charity ordered by equity. I think, moreover, that the Holy Spirit signified this twofold power of his\u2014or this affective working of his gift\u2014on account of which he wished to be called splendor, even in that fire in which he, invisible, appeared invisibly to the Apostles. For what was splendor to the sight of their eyes was, unless I am mistaken, oil to the sense of their bodies: so that they seemed to themselves baptized as in a kind of bath of oil, when that heavenly fire was poured out upon them. Yet whatever the bodily sensation of that fire may have been in them (for it matters little how it was felt, but how it worked), this, I think, is both the more probable and the more devout belief: that the same fire which kindled and illumined their hearts also cooled their bodies, so that through charity their minds burned, and through chastity their members grew cool; and each saying fit the case\u2014whether that of Jeremiah, 'He sent fire from on high into my bones' (Thren. I, 13), or that of the Psalmist, 'My flesh is changed because of oil' (Psal. CVIII, 24). Unless perhaps the Spirit wished to leave something of the law of the members at war with the law of the mind, so that power might be perfected in weakness.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sed prosequamur quod proposuimus. Isti sunt, inquit, duo filii olei splendoris, qui assistunt Dominatori universae terrae (Zach. IV, 14). Ecce habes bonitatem in oleo, scientiam in splendore: accipe disciplinam in eo quod ait, qui assistunt Dominatori universae terrae; ut agnoscas eos vere discipulos illius fuisse cui cantamus, Bonitatem et disciplinam et scientiam doce me (Psal. CXVIII, 66). Haec est namque perfectio disciplinae, tam in mente, quam in corpore; ut proponentes Dominum in conspectu nostro semper, semper et ubique majestati aeternae, quae nos inspicit et judicat sine intermissione, cum metu et reverentia assistamus, pervigili fide ac jugi devotione. Sicut oculi, inquit, servorum in manibus dominorum suorum, ita oculi nostri ad Dominum Deum nostrum (Psal. CXXII, 2), intentissime et indeclinabiliter observantes nutus ejus, quid velit, quid jubeat, ut quam celerrime devotio nostra obediat. Sic Moyses invisibilem tanquam videns sustinebat, nec unquam imminentis metum judicis dissimulare poterat. Sic Elias et alii Prophetae qui dicebant: Vivit Dominus, in cujus conspectu sto (IV Reg. V, 16). Inde nimirum et gratiam, et nomen assecuti sunt Videntium; quoniam quae semper intuebantur per fidem non fictam, merebantur saepius intueri per speciem, licet factam. Haec fides vigil, non ficta, non dissimulatrix, ipsa est, ut arbitror, quae sola vel maxime facili compendio ad perfectionem perducit; quae gravitatem disciplinae, sobrietatemque modestiae, tam interiori quam exteriori nostro tribuit, et velut in disciplina famulantium Domino semper assistere facit. Hac virtute meruerunt Apostoli dici filii olei splendoris: ut sicut Domino astiterunt in terris contemplando per fidem, ita nunc ei assistant in coelis contemplando per speciem; Regemque in decore suo videant oculi eorum, cujus timorem hic meditabatur cor eorum. Hac via, fratres, sequimini Patres fidei vestrae, et auctores Ordinis, quatenus eorum provocati exemplis et adjuti suffragiis, quo ipsi hodie praecesserunt, pervenire valeatis, adjuvante Domino nostro Jesu Christo, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But let us continue what we have proposed. 'These,' he says, 'are the two sons of the oil of splendor, who stand before the Lord of all the earth' (Zach. IV, 14). Behold, you have goodness in the oil, knowledge in the splendor: receive discipline in what he says, 'who stand before the Lord of all the earth'; that you may recognize that they were truly disciples of him to whom we sing, 'Teach me goodness, and discipline, and knowledge' (Psal. CXVIII, 66). For this is the perfection of discipline, both in mind and in body: that, setting the Lord ever before our sight, we should always and everywhere stand before the eternal majesty\u2014which beholds and judges us without ceasing\u2014with fear and reverence, with ever-watchful faith and unfailing devotion. 'As the eyes of servants are on the hands of their masters, so are our eyes toward the Lord our God' (Psal. CXXII, 2), watching most intently and unswervingly for his least sign\u2014what he wills, what he commands\u2014so that our devotion may obey as swiftly as possible. Thus Moses endured, as one who saw him who is invisible, and could never put out of mind the fear of the Judge who was at hand. Thus Elijah and the other Prophets, who said: 'The Lord lives, in whose sight I stand' (IV Reg. V, 16). From this, surely, they obtained both the grace and the name of 'Seers'; because that which they always beheld through unfeigned faith, they often merited to behold through a vision\u2014though a created one. This watchful faith, not feigned, not dissembling, is itself, I think, that which alone, or at least most readily and by the easiest path, leads to perfection; which bestows the gravity of discipline and the sobriety of modesty on both our inward and our outward self, and causes us, as it were in the discipline of servants, to stand always before the Lord. By this virtue the Apostles deserved to be called sons of the oil of splendor: so that just as they stood before the Lord on earth, contemplating through faith, so now they stand before him in heaven, contemplating through vision; and their eyes behold the King in his beauty, whose fear their heart meditated here below. By this way, brothers, follow the Fathers of your faith and the founders of your Order, so that, roused by their examples and helped by their intercessions, you may be able to arrive where they themselves have gone before this day, with the help of our Lord Jesus Christ, who lives and reigns for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saints Peter and Paul Sermon I", "topics": ["Saints Peter and Paul", "Holy Spirit", "grace", "Christ", "monastic life", "perseverance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-peter-paul-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:peter-paul", "calendar_label": "Sts. Peter and Paul, Apostles", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-peter-paul-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., Quomodo fidelium animos alant apostoli., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 180-183.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saints Peter and Paul", "Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles", "Song of Songs 4:5\u20136", "Isaiah 49:23", "Isaiah 60:16", "1 Peter 2:2", "1 Corinthians 3:2", "1 Thessalonians 2:7", "Song of Songs 8:8", "Song of Songs 1:1", "John 21:15\u201316", "Song of Songs 8:10", "Song of Songs 4:5", "Song of Songs 2:16\u201317", "Song of Songs 7:2", "Acts 20:34", "1 Corinthians 4:12", "1 Thessalonians 2:9", "2 Thessalonians 3:8", "1 Thessalonians 3:8", "1 Thessalonians 2:19", "Philippians 4:1", "2 Corinthians 12:3", "Matthew 16:2\u20136 (printed locator; passage from Matthew 16:17)", "Acts 10:10\u201316"], "occasion": "In natali Apostolorum Petri et Pauli", "occasion_detail": "Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles", "occasion_label": "Saints Peter and Paul", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads the bride's two breasts in the Song of Songs as Peter and Paul, who nourish the Church with the milk of doctrine. The heavenly Church grieved that its little sister had no breasts until the Spirit filled the apostles at Pentecost. Paul fed others with his words and was fed by their works; Peter received the revelation of the Son and the keys of heaven. The sermon appears under the heading for the Apostles Peter and Paul in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quomodo fidelium animos alant apostoli."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "How the apostles nourish the souls of the faithful.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Duo ubera tua sicut duo hinnuli capreae gemelli, qui pascuntur in liliis, donec aspiret dies, et inclinentur umbrae (Cant. IV, 5, 6). Videte, fratres, ne degeneretis. Ut taceam de nobilitate patris vestri, nobilis est mater quae vos genuit, generosa sunt ubera quibus lactati estis. Mater vestra est sponsa cui haec dicuntur, cujus ubera voce sponsi laudantur. Quae nimirum duo ubera Ecclesiae Petrum et Paulum esse, non solum interpretatur nobis dies ista, in qua potamur ab uberibus consolationis eorum satis jucunde et abunde; sed etiam evidentia operis ipsorum approbant argumenta ac monumenta doctrinae. Quibus enim aliis uberibus nutriti sunt filii Ecclesiae, sive ex praeputio, sive ex circumcisione? Istos utique ego reor esse, de quibus olim parvulae et pauperculae consolatio divina promittebat Ecclesiae: Reges nutritii tui erunt (Isai. XLIX, 23), et mammilla regum lactaberis (Isa. LX, 16). Nisi enim tam generoso lacte nutrita esset, nunquam ad hoc virtutis ac gloriae culmen excrevisset. Ad ubera parvulos Petrus invitabat, quibus dicebat: Sicut modo geniti infantes, lac concupiscite (I Petr. II, 2). Ubera Paulus praebuerat quibus dicebat: Lac vobis potum dedi (I Cor. III, 2); et illud; Factus sum in medio vestri sicut nutrix sedens et nutriens filios suos (I Thess. II, 7). Quam plenus autem lacte redundaverit spirituali, pulchre significatum est unda illius lactis visibilis, quod de corpore ipsius fertur exisse loco sanguinis, cum decollatus hodierna die pro illis, quibus dabat mammillam, dedit et animam. Plane nihil in eo sanguineum, sed totum lacteum; qui nihil carnale, nihil suum cogitabat, sed tantum quod aliis utile erat. Nec tam habebat ubera, quam ipse totus uber erat; qui ita misericordia uberi affluebat, ut non modo spiritum suum desideraret totum filiis immulgere, sed et corpus impendere."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Your two breasts are like two twin fawns of a roe, which feed among the lilies, until the day break and the shadows retire (Cant. IV, 5, 6). Take care, brothers, that you do not degenerate. To say nothing of the nobility of your father, noble is the mother who bore you, and generous are the breasts at which you were nursed. Your mother is the bride to whom these words are spoken, whose breasts are praised by the voice of the bridegroom. That these two breasts of the Church are, without doubt, Peter and Paul, is interpreted for us not only by this present day, on which we drink from the breasts of their consolation most pleasantly and abundantly, but also the clear evidence of their work and the monuments of their teaching confirm it. For by what other breasts have the children of the Church been nourished, whether from the uncircumcised or from the circumcision? These, surely, I judge to be they of whom long ago the divine consolation promised to the little and poor Church: Kings shall be your nursing fathers (Isai. XLIX, 23), and you shall be suckled with the breast of kings (Isa. LX, 16). For unless she had been nourished with milk so generous, she would never have grown to this summit of virtue and glory. Peter invited little children to the breasts, to whom he said: As newborn babes, desire the milk (I Petr. II, 2). Paul had offered breasts to those to whom he said: I gave you milk to drink (I Cor. III, 2); and also this: I became in the midst of you as a nurse sitting and nourishing her children (I Thess. II, 7). How full he overflowed with spiritual milk is beautifully signified by that wave of visible milk which is reported to have flowed from his body in place of blood, when, beheaded on this very day for those to whom he gave the breast, he gave also his soul. Clearly nothing bloody was in him, but all was milky; for he thought nothing carnal, nothing of his own, but only what was useful to others. Nor did he so much have breasts, as he himself was wholly a breast; for he so abounded in overflowing mercy that he desired not only to pour out his whole spirit for his children, but also to spend his very body.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Antequam haec duo ubera, Petrum et Paulum, accepisset primitiva Ecclesia sanctorum in terris, conquerebatur et dicebat Ecclesia beatorum spirituum in coelis: Soror nostra parva est, et ubera non habet (Cantic. VIII, 8). Cum enim Christus rediens in coelum reliquisset pusillum gregem discipulorum, ac nondum misisset Spiritum suum, quo erant fetanda viscera, et implenda ubera sanctorum: erat, ut opinor, illa Ecclesia in coelis sollicita pro filiolis sponsi, sive jam natis, sive nascituris, cui videlicet committerentur alendi; eo quod Ecclesiam istam viderent parvam numero, virtute et auctoritate, et sine uberibus doctrinae. Nam et ipse Sponsus in diebus carnis suae genuerat aliquos verbo veritatis, et quandiu fuerat cum eis, lactaverat eos uberibus aedificationis et consolationis. Et ipse siquidem Sponsus habet ubera meliora vino (Cant. I, 1), id est, doctrina legali, vel gaudio saeculari. Habet, inquam, Sponsus ubera, ne ullum de omnibus officiis et nominibus pietatis eum praetereat: ut qui pater est creatione naturae, vel regeneratione gratiae, seu etiam auctoritate disciplinae, sit etiam mater affectu clementiae, sit et nutrix sedulitate officii curae. Erant igitur parvuli, quos alebat initium aliquod creaturae ejus, sed nonnisi initium: multumque supererat curae et laboris, donec adducerentur ad perfectum, et formaretur Christus in eis. Cum autem et ipse reliquisset eos; superni spiritus, etsi laeti de reditu Unigeniti, de nova tamen adoptionis progenie solliciti, quodammodo affectu suo videbantur apud eum conqueri: Quis illos nutriet? Lactasti eos, sed ante tempus ablactasti. Non enutristi juvenes, nec ad incrementum perduxisti virgines: quis illos nutriet? Soror nostra parva est, et ubera non habet. Dixisti Petro: Pasce agnos meos (Joan. XXI, 15, 16): sed nec ipse adhuc sufficientiam lactis in uberibus habet. Cito pietas ejus aresceret, qui plus adhuc cuti suae quam animabus parvulorum timet. Facile relinqueret agnos in tentatione, qui Pastorem tam suum quam illorum negavit in interrogatione. Ecce autem repente Spiritu misso de coelo, tanquam lacte de propriis Christi uberibus effuso, Petrus impletus est lacte plurimo: nec multo post de Saulo fit Paulus, praedicator de persecutore, mater de tortore, nutrix de carnifice; ut plane intelligeres totum ejus sanguinem mutatum in lactis dulcedinem, saevitiam in pietatem."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Before the primitive Church of the saints on earth had received these two breasts, Peter and Paul, the Church of the blessed spirits in heaven was complaining and saying: Our sister is little, and has no breasts (Cantic. VIII, 8). For when Christ, returning to heaven, had left behind a tiny flock of disciples, and had not yet sent his Spirit, by which the wombs of the saints were to conceive and their breasts to be filled, that Church in heaven was, I suppose, anxious for the little children of the bridegroom, whether already born or yet to be born, to whom they were to be entrusted for nourishing\u2014because they saw this Church to be small in number, in power and in authority, and without the breasts of doctrine. For the Bridegroom himself, in the days of his flesh, had begotten some by the word of truth, and as long as he was with them had suckled them with the breasts of edification and consolation. For indeed the Bridegroom himself has breasts better than wine (Cant. I, 1), that is, better than the teaching of the law, or than worldly joy. The Bridegroom has breasts, I say, lest any of all the offices and titles of piety should pass him by: so that he who is father by the creation of nature, or by the regeneration of grace, or even by the authority of discipline, might also be mother by the affection of his clemency, and nurse by the diligence of his caring office. There were, then, little children, whom he was nourishing, a kind of beginning of his creation, but only a beginning: and much of care and labor still remained, until they should be brought to perfection, and Christ be formed in them. But when he too had left them, the spirits above, though rejoicing at the return of the Only-begotten, yet anxious about the new offspring of adoption, seemed in a way, by their own affection, to complain before him: Who will nourish them? You have nursed them, but you have weaned them before their time. You have not nourished the young men, nor brought virgins to full growth: who will nourish them? Our sister is little, and has no breasts. You said to Peter: Feed my lambs (Joan. XXI, 15, 16); but even he does not yet have sufficiency of milk in his breasts. Quickly would his devotion wither, who still fears more for his own skin than for the souls of the little ones. Easily would he abandon the lambs in temptation, who denied the Shepherd, both his own and theirs, under questioning. But behold, suddenly, the Spirit having been sent from heaven, as if milk had been poured out from Christ's own breasts, Peter was filled with abundant milk: and not long after, Saul became Paul\u2014a preacher from a persecutor, a mother from a torturer, a nurse from an executioner; so that you might clearly understand that all his blood had been changed into the sweetness of milk, and his savagery into tenderness.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. His igitur uberibus geminis pectori suo affixis gloriatur Ecclesia, non modo matrem se esse fecundam, sed et civitatem munitam. Ego, inquit, murus, et ubera mea turris (Cantic. VIII, 10). Mille clypei pendent jam de turribus istis, omnis armatura fortium. Ipse quoque sponsus de eisdem uberibus in laudem sponsae in carmine amoris, mystico sermone sic ait: Duo ubera tua sicut duo hinnuli capreae gemelli (Cant. IV, 5). Capream hoc loco accipimus Ecclesiam; acutam visu ad penetranda mysteria Christi, agilem saltu ad transilienda spinosa hujus saeculi, potentem ad exsuperanda venena serpentis antiqui. Cujus duo gemelli non inconvenienter, ut arbitror, intelliguntur hi duo Apostoli; germani fide, consimiles devotione, pares merito et virtute, unanimes charitate, conjuncti in ipsa etiam passione et morte. Quomodo enim in vita sua dilexerunt se, ita et in morte non sunt separati. Quia vero desideria summorum pascuntur in profectibus minorum, donec scilicet aspirante gratia tollant se ad contemplandum sublimia, bene de his hinnulis subditur: Qui pascuntur in liliis, donec aspiret dies, et inclinentur umbrae (Cant. II, 16, 17). Animalia cum declinant aestum diei, captant umbrosa pascua convallium, ubi et liliorum solet esse proventus uberior, donec spirante tepescentis aura vesperi, egrediantur ad aperta camporum, vel ardua montium. Sic et hinnuli nostri quandiu non aspirat eis ad contemplandum clementior aura diei aeterni, pascuntur in liliis convallium; id est, delectantur in virtutibus humilium, aut etiam occupantur in actionibus eorum. Cum autem aspirat dies ille mentibus eorum, egrediuntur et saliunt ad uberiora et feliciora pascua montium aeternorum. Per me, inquit Pastor et ostium, si quis introierit salvabitur, et ingredietur Ecclesiam, et egredietur saepius contemplando, semel migrando ad coelestem patriam; et pascua inveniet, et hic, et ibi. Hic, inter lilia agri; ibi, inter ligna paradisi: hic, in floribus; ibi, in fructibus: hic, in virtutibus sanctorum; ibi, in gaudiis Angelorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. With these two breasts, then, fastened to her bosom, the Church glories not only that she is a fruitful mother, but also a fortified city. 'I,' she says, 'am a wall, and my breasts are a tower' (Cantic. VIII, 10). A thousand shields now hang from these towers, the whole armory of the mighty. The Bridegroom himself, too, speaking of these same breasts in praise of the Bride, in the song of love, says in mystical language: 'Your two breasts are like two twin fawns of a roe' (Cant. IV, 5). By 'roe' here we understand the Church: sharp of sight to penetrate the mysteries of Christ, nimble in leaping to spring over the thorns of this world, strong to overcome the poisons of the ancient serpent. Her two twin fawns, I think, are not unfittingly understood to be these two Apostles: brothers in faith, alike in devotion, equal in merit and virtue, of one mind in charity, joined even in their very passion and death. For as they loved one another in life, so in death they were not separated. Now because the desires of the highest are fed on the progress of the lesser, until, that is, with grace breathing upon them they lift themselves up to contemplate things sublime, it is rightly added of these fawns: 'Who feed among the lilies till the day break, and the shadows retire' (Cant. II, 16, 17). Animals, when they avoid the heat of the day, seek out the shaded pastures of the valleys, where too the growth of lilies is usually more abundant, until, as the cooling breeze of evening breathes, they go out to the open fields or the steep mountains. So too our fawns, so long as the gentler breeze of the eternal day does not breathe upon them for contemplation, feed among the lilies of the valleys; that is, they delight in the virtues of the humble, or are even occupied in their actions. But when that day breathes upon their minds, they go out and leap to the richer and happier pastures of the eternal mountains. 'By me,' says the Shepherd and the door, 'if any man enter in, he shall be saved'; and he shall enter the Church, and shall go out\u2014often by contemplating, once by passing over to the heavenly homeland\u2014and he shall find pastures, both here and there. Here, among the lilies of the field; there, among the trees of paradise: here, in flowers; there, in fruits: here, in the virtues of the saints; there, in the joys of the Angels.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Prorsus in loco pascuae ibi me Dominus collocavit, cum ecclesiae sanctorum associavit, cujus venter sicut acervus tritici vallatus liliis (Cant. VII, 2), ut pascat simul et tritici gustu, et liliorum aspectu. Quid enim acervus tritici, nisi copia divini sermonis in tot libris undique congestis? Quid lilia, nisi justi qui germinant sicut lilium, et florent immarcessibiliter ante Dominum; quorum tantus candor in sanctimonia corporis et cordis puritate, tanta fragrantia in opinione, tanta medicinalis virtus in opere et sermone? Non mediocre pabulum istud fideli animae, videre circa se tot lilia tanta venustate et gratia florentia, ubi omnium virtutum ex eis, licet diversarum ex diversis, capere possit exempla. Iste fundatior humilitate, ille amplior charitate. Alius robustior ad patientiam, alius velocior ad obedientiam. Iste frugalitate parcior, ille labore utilior: iste devotior in oratione, ille in lectione studiosior: iste in administratione prudentior, ille in quiete sanctior. Sed licet aliquam insignius florentem in unoquoque gratiam mireris, non una tamen, sed multae virtutes in singulis, sicut flores in liliis. Quot enim justi, tot lilia: quot virtutes eorum, tot flores liliorum. Qui igitur haec videns gaudet in illis, aut etiam proficit ex illis, quid, nisi pascitur in liliis?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Indeed, it was there, in that place of pasture, that the Lord set me, when he joined me to the church of the saints, whose belly is like a heap of wheat set about with lilies (Cant. VII, 2), so that it may feed at once by the taste of the wheat and the sight of the lilies. For what is the heap of wheat but the abundance of the divine word, gathered together from so many books on every side? And what are the lilies but the just, who spring up like the lily and flower unfadingly before the Lord; whose whiteness is so great in the holiness of body and purity of heart, whose fragrance so great in reputation, whose healing power so great in deed and word? It is no small pasture for a faithful soul to see around it so many lilies flowering with such beauty and grace, where it may gather examples of all the virtues from them, though these be diverse virtues from diverse persons. This one is more firmly grounded in humility, that one more abundant in charity. One is stronger for patience, another swifter for obedience. This one is more sparing in frugality, that one more useful in labor; this one more devout in prayer, that one more studious in reading; this one more prudent in administration, that one more holy in quiet. But although in each you may admire some one grace flowering more conspicuously, yet there is not one virtue alone, but many, in each, as there are many flowers in lilies. For as many as the just, so many the lilies; as many as their virtues, so many the flowers of the lilies. He, then, who, seeing these things, rejoices in them, or even profits by them\u2014what is he doing, but feeding among the lilies?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Pascebat Paulus: sed nihilominus in illis quos pascebat. Pascebat eos sermonibus, pascebatur eorum operibus. Ventrem quidem suum, imo et alienum pascebat manibus propriis (Act. XX, 34; I Cor. IV, 12; I Thess. II, 9, et II Thess. III, 8): mentem vero non tam suis pascebat bonis quam alienis, qui non quaerebat quod sibi utile, sed quod aliis. Nunc vivimus, inquit, si vos statis in Domino (I Thess. III, 8). Quae enim gloria mea, aut gaudium, aut corona? Nonne vos ante Deum? (I Thess. II, 19; Philipp. IV, 1.) Ideo saepius ei spirabat dies aeterni luminis, atque afflabat eum lenior aura Spiritus sancti, cujus vi rapiebatur ad interna pascua, aliquando paradisi, aliquando tertii coeli, sive in corpore, sive extra corpus, nescio, inquit, Deus scit (II Cor. XII, 3). Dignum quippe erat, ut qui fideliter pascebat, fideliter pasceretur: et qui foris gaudebat in bonis dominicae familiae, in gaudium Domini sui juberetur intrare, atque repleretur in bonis domus Dei, non solum istius visibilis, pro qua sudabat, sed etiam illius invisibilis, ad quam spirabat, unde et dies ei aspirabat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Paul fed others, but nonetheless was himself fed in those whom he fed. He fed them with his words, and was fed by their works. His own belly indeed\u2014nay, even that of others\u2014he fed with his own hands (Act. XX, 34; I Cor. IV, 12; I Thess. II, 9, et II Thess. III, 8); but his mind he fed not so much with his own goods as with those of others, for he sought not what was profitable to himself, but what was profitable to others. 'Now we live,' he says, 'if you stand fast in the Lord' (I Thess. III, 8). 'For what is my glory, or my joy, or my crown? Are you not it, before God?' (I Thess. II, 19; Philipp. IV, 1.). Therefore the day of eternal light breathed upon him the more often, and the gentler breeze of the Holy Spirit blew upon him, by whose power he was caught up to the inward pastures, now of paradise, now of the third heaven\u2014'whether in the body, or out of the body, I know not,' he says, 'God knows' (II Cor. XII, 3). It was indeed fitting that he who fed others faithfully should himself be faithfully fed, and that he who outwardly rejoiced in the goods of the Lord's household should be bidden to enter into the joy of his Lord, and be filled with the good things of the house of God\u2014not only of that visible house, for which he toiled, but also of that invisible one, toward which he breathed, whence also the day breathed upon him.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Nec minora de beato Petro sentienda puto, cum maxima sit gloria Pauli, illius coaequari meritis, quem judicium Veritatis potestate et primatu praetulit universis. Cum enim Pater qui est in coelis veritatem Unigeniti Petro revelabat (Matth. XVI, 2-6), quid aliud quam dies ei aeternus aspirabat, et dies diei verbum eructabat? Cui autem magis pervium fuisse coelum credendum est, quam ipsi Janitori, cujus lingua clavis coeli facta est? Qui potestatem habebat claudere coelum nubibus, et aperire portas ejus, quomodo ipse non saepius intrasse credetur? Esuriit aliquando Petrus, et coelum ei apertum est, cum adhuc in terra consisteret; tantaque ei inde ciborum copia missa est, ut adhuc multa supersint, licet plurima mactaverit, et manducaverit, immunda et virulenta animalia in sancta et florulenta convertens lilia (Act. X, 10-16): ut videlicet deliciose pasceretur in liliis, donec aspirante die, gloriose in ipsis pasceretur coelis. Sed ecce dum commendamus vobis duo haec ubera sponsae, hora jam praeteriit, qua de ipsis uberibus lac vobis cupiebamus immulgere: quanquam et aliis diebus hoc ipsum facere possimus et soleamus. De uberibus siquidem Apostolorum lac vobis trahimus, quoties de verbis eorum ad aedificationem vestram tractamus. Nunc igitur pro tempore satis sit admonere, si tamen moneri indigetis, ut sicut ubera ista diligitis, ita lac eorum semper concupiscatis; ut in eo crescatis in salutem, donec formatum in vobis exhibeatis Salvatorem Jesum Christum Dominum nostrum, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. I think we should judge no less highly of blessed Peter, since it is Paul's greatest glory to be made equal in merits to him whom the judgment of Truth preferred above all others in power and primacy. For when the Father who is in heaven revealed to Peter the truth concerning the Only-Begotten (Matth. XVI, 2-6), what else was this but that the eternal day breathed upon him, and day uttered speech unto day? And to whom must heaven be believed to have been more open than to that very Doorkeeper, whose tongue was made the key of heaven? He who had the power to shut heaven with clouds, and to open its gates \u2014 how shall he not be believed to have entered it the more often himself? Once Peter hungered, and heaven was opened to him while he still stood on earth; and so great an abundance of food was sent to him from there, that much still remained over, although he had already slaughtered and eaten many unclean and venomous creatures, turning them into holy and flowering lilies (Act. X, 10-16): so that, to be sure, he might be fed delightfully among lilies, until, when the day breaks,, he might be fed gloriously in the very heavens themselves. But behold, while we commend to you these two breasts of the Bride, the hour has already passed in which we desired to draw out for you the milk from those very breasts \u2014 although on other days we are able, and are accustomed, to do this very thing. For indeed we draw milk for you from the breasts of the Apostles, as often as we treat of their words for your edification. For now, then, let it suffice for the time to admonish you, if indeed you need admonishing, that just as you love these breasts, so you may always long for their milk; that in it you may grow unto salvation, until you show forth, formed in you, the Saviour Jesus Christ our Lord, who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Father's revelation to Peter is Matthew 16:17. The edition prints Matth. xvi, 2-6."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saints Peter and Paul Sermon II", "topics": ["Saints Peter and Paul", "Church", "preaching", "Pentecost", "Song of Songs", "spiritual nourishment"], "url": "sermons/guerric-peter-paul-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:peter-paul", "calendar_label": "Sts. Peter and Paul, Apostles", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-peter-paul-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., De eo quod scriptum est: \u00ab Donec aspiret dies, et inclinentur umbrae. \u00bb (Cant. II, 17)., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 183-188.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saints Peter and Paul", "Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles", "Song of Songs 2:17", "1 Corinthians 13:12", "Exodus 19:20", "Psalm 58:6 (historical numbering)", "2 Corinthians 3:18", "Isaiah 18:11 (printed locator; passage from Isaiah 58:11)", "Psalm 101:12 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 83:11 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 58:11", "James 1:17", "Isaiah 27:8", "Matthew 11:28", "Job 7:2", "Song of Songs 2:3", "Hosea 14:8", "Lamentations 4:20", "Job 40:17, 20"], "occasion": "In natali Apostolorum Petri et Pauli", "occasion_detail": "Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles", "occasion_label": "Saints Peter and Paul", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Pressed by the brothers, Guerric finishes the verse he began the day before: \"Until the day breathe, and the shadows be inclined.\" He distinguishes shadows that chill and darken the soul from those that cool and enlighten it, and holds that even the clearest vision of this life is a bright shadow in a mirror. He invites the laboring brothers to rest in the shade of Christ, and warns against the heavy shade of worldly cares in which Leviathan sleeps. The sermon appears under the heading for the Apostles Peter and Paul in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De eo quod scriptum est: \u00ab Donec aspiret dies, et inclinentur umbrae. \u00bb (Cant. II, 17)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On what is written: \"Until the day breathe, and the shadows be inclined.\" (Cant. II, 17).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Erratis, fratres, in me; sed amore magis, ut arbitror, aut humilitate, quam temeritate. Putatis me Scripturarum scientiam habere, qui vix unquam attigi vel primum limen scientiae. Displicet enim vobis, ut mihi videtur, quod capitulum Scripturae, de quo heri sum locutus, non sum ad finem usque persecutus: quasi illa mihi sit facultas, ut valeam Scripturas explanare, aut etiam explanationes aliorum digne et congrue ad memoriam revocare. Sed neque hoc mihi solet propositum esse, ut Scripturam, unde sermonem ordior, exponam; sed ut de ipsa, et secundum ipsam sermonem debitum suo diei persolvam. Huc accedit quod magister noster (10) ille interpres Spiritus sancti, de toto illo carmine nuptiali loqui instituit, spemque nobis dedit ex eis quae jam edidit, quia si pervenerit ad locum de quo quaeritis, donec aspiret dies, et inclinentur umbrae, umbras ipsas ponat in lucem intelligentiae: quod dictum est vel erit in tenebris, nobis dicet in lumine. Vos tamen dicitis, et recte dicitis, quia vetera novis supervenientibus projicietis; quantoque magis incondita seu rancida fuerint vetera nostra, tanto nova illius sapidiora erunt et gratiora. Nam et ipse Jesus servat vivum bonum usque ad extremum, sciens utique quomodo nostro sit fastidio medendum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. You are mistaken about me, brothers; but out of love, I think, or out of humility, rather than out of rashness. You suppose me to have knowledge of the Scriptures\u2014I who have scarcely ever touched even the first threshold of knowledge. For it displeases you, as it seems to me, that I did not pursue to its end the chapter of Scripture of which I spoke yesterday: as though I had the capacity to explain the Scriptures, or even to recall to memory, worthily and fittingly, the explanations of others. But it has never been my custom to set out to expound the Scripture from which I begin my sermon; rather, my aim is to pay to each day the sermon owed to it, drawn from and in accordance with that Scripture. To this is added the fact that our master (10), that interpreter of the Holy Spirit, has undertaken to speak on that whole nuptial song, and has given us hope, from what he has already set forth, that if he should come to the passage you are asking about\u2014\"until the day breathe, and the shadows be inclined\"\u2014he will set those very shadows into the light of understanding: what has been said, or will be said, in darkness, he will tell us in the light. Yet you say, and you say rightly, that you will cast aside the old things when the new ones come; and the more unrefined or stale our old things have been, the more savory and pleasing the new ones from him will be. For Jesus himself keeps the living good even to the last, knowing well how our distaste is to be remedied.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Quia igitur cogitis me (nam impatientes dilationis vos video), nec spes incerta futuri praesenti satisfacit desiderio; ecce vobis morem gero, finemque versiculi, de quo heri loquebamur, suo, prout potero, reddam principio. Illud enim quaerere videmini, quid sit aspirante die spirituali umbras inclinari; cum magis consequens videretur, ut non crescere, sed minui tunc et deficere, dicerentur. Nam si sequamur proprietatem verbi, hoc est umbris crescere, quod inclinari: cum umbrarum ea natura sit, ut quanto inclinantur, tanto procumbant longiores; quantoque surgunt, tanto stent minores. Unde et poeta vesperum significans ait: Majoresque cadunt altis de montibus umbrae. (VIRG. Buc. eclog. 1.) Sed sciendum, quia cum haec proprietas umbrarum sit corporalium, longe alia est spiritualium, de quibus non dubitamus hic loqui Spiritum sanctum. Nam sicut corporalibus umbris inclinari est crescere, sic spiritualibus hoc est inclinari, quod deponi, minui, vel deficere. Umbrae autem spirituales sunt umbrosi spiritus et tetri, seu daemonum, seu hominum: umbrae sunt tenebrae errorum: umbrae sunt obscurae significationes veterum sacramentorum. Et umbrae quidem significationum jam inclinatae sunt ad occasum et finem: umbrae autem errorum de die in diem inclinantur ad diminutionem: umbrae vero tenebrosorum spirituum tandem inclinabuntur in infernum et mortem. Primum quidem factum est, cum dies aeternus aspiravit, apparens in carne: secundum quotidie fit, cum magis ac magis aspirat illustrans veritate: tertium novissime fiet, cum aspirabit rutilans in majestate."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Since, then, you compel me (for your impatience at delay is plain to me), and an uncertain hope of the future does not satisfy a present desire; behold, I will indulge you, and will render, as best I can, the end of the little verse we were discussing yesterday, together with its own beginning. For you seem to be asking what it means for the shadows to be inclined when the spiritual day breathes forth; whereas it would seem more fitting to say that then they are not increasing but diminishing and failing. For if we follow the proper sense of the word, for shadows \"to be inclined\" is the same as \"to grow\": since it is the nature of shadows that the more they are inclined, the longer they stretch out; and the more they rise, the shorter they stand. Whence the poet, signifying evening, says: \"And longer shadows fall from the high mountains.\" But it must be understood that, while this is the property of bodily shadows, the case is far different with spiritual shadows, of which we do not doubt the Holy Spirit is speaking here. For just as for bodily shadows to be inclined is to grow, so for spiritual shadows to be inclined is to be laid down, diminished, or to fail. Now the spiritual shadows are the shadowy and gloomy spirits, whether of demons or of men: shadows are the darknesses of errors: shadows are the obscure significations of the old sacraments. And indeed the shadows of significations have already been inclined toward their setting and their end: but the shadows of errors are inclined, day by day, toward diminishment: while the shadows of the dark spirits will at last be inclined into hell and death. The first indeed came to pass when the eternal Day breathed forth, appearing in the flesh: the second happens daily, as he breathes forth more and more, enlightening with truth: the third will happen at the very last, when he will breathe forth glowing in majesty.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Videre autem mihi videor et alias umbras intra nos, quae velut in quodam speculo de rebus aliis nascuntur in sensu nostro, et obumbrant nobis, vel obumbrant nos. Obumbrant nos quae refrigidant et obscurant nos: obumbrant nobis quae refrigerant et illuminant nos. Istae quidem noxiae et graves, illae delectabiles et salubres, istae namque de inferioribus sunt et mundanis; illae, de superioribus et divinis. Anima nempe humana in quodam medio condita et posita est, ut sub ipsa esset mundus, supra ipsam Deus. Supra ipsam, a quo et ad quem et propter quem facta est: sub ipsa, quod propter ipsam factum est. Nam sicut propter animam corpus, sic propter corpus domus ejus, id est, mundus. Cum igitur incurvatur ad corporalia vel mundana, ascendunt in eam umbrae de interioribus: cum erigitur ad divina, inclinantur super eam umbrae de superioribus. Illius enim rei quam cogitat, umbram sibi format. Quanquam de divinis satis dubia umbra est, quantumcunque cogitationis evacuatur acies: aut certe non umbra ipsius rei, sed aliud pro illa est, nisi cum aspirat dies ille. Nam tunc quoque non videtur, ut arbitror, nisi umbra, quae etsi luminosa, etsi gloriosa est, tamen umbra est; sicut cum in facie speculi tersa et lucida, splendidissimae rei splendens imago resultat. Caeterum videre faciem veritatis ipsius, vel veritatem faciei ejus, sicut nunquam erit hujus corporis: ita nec modo est hujus temporis, nec aliquando erit, donec corpus dissolvet mortalitas, aut absolvet immortalitas, et tempus absorbebit aeternitas, et spiritum qui nunc gravatur corpore et mutatur tempore, sublevabit ad se, et firmabit in se immortalis et immutabilis divinitas."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But it seems to me that I can see other shadows within us as well, which, as it were in a kind of mirror, arise in our senses from other things, and either cast shade for us, or overshadow us. Those overshadow us which chill and darken us: those cast shade for us which cool and illuminate us. The former indeed are harmful and heavy, the latter delightful and wholesome; for the former come from lower, worldly things, the latter from higher, divine things. For the human soul has been established and placed in a kind of middle position, so that the world should be beneath it, and God above it. Above it is he from whom, and to whom, and for whose sake it was made; beneath it is what was made for its sake. For just as the body exists for the sake of the soul, so the soul's house\u2014that is, the world\u2014exists for the sake of the body. When, therefore, the soul bends down toward bodily or worldly things, shadows from the lower things rise up into it; but when it is lifted up toward divine things, shadows from the higher things are inclined over it. For of whatever thing it thinks upon, it forms for itself a shadow. Although, concerning divine things, the shadow is quite uncertain, however much the keenness of thought is emptied out: or rather, it is not the shadow of the thing itself, but something else in its place, except when that day breathes forth. For then too, I think, nothing is seen except a shadow\u2014which, even though luminous, even though glorious, is nevertheless a shadow; just as when, on the face of a mirror polished and bright, the shining image of a most splendid thing is reflected back. But to see the face of truth itself, or the truth of its face, will never belong to this body\u2014just as it is not now the lot of this present time, nor ever will be, until mortality dissolves the body, or immortality releases it, and eternity swallows up time, and the immortal and unchangeable divinity lifts up to itself, and establishes firmly in itself, the spirit which is now weighed down by the body and changed by time.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Initium hujus gratiae, et perfectionem illius gloriae Paulus satis proprie distinguebat, cum dicebat: Videmus nunc per speculum in aenigmate; tunc autem facie ad faciem (I Cor. XIII, 12). Ut autem et hoc ipsum sit, scilicet ut videamus per speculum et in aenigmate, opus est non solum ut facies speculi nostri ab omni phantasmate et umbra corporalium tersa sit: sed etiam ut ille sublimis, habitans lucem inaccessibilem, dignetur ad nos inclinari, et vel per umbram suae imaginis manifestari. Quantumcunque enim contendamus a visibilibus ad invisibilia, nihil nostra apprehendet humilitas, nisi condescendat illa majestas. Moyses ascendit in montem, et Dominus inclinavit coelos et descendit (Exod. XIX, 20). Et alius: Exsurge inquit in occursum meum, et vide (Psal. LVIII, 6). Ideo nec umbra videtur, nisi inclinetur; nec inclinari dicitur, nisi aspirante die: quia neque per speculum et in aenigmate possumus Deum videre, nisi inclinante se majestate, et aspirante favore gratiae. Sane hoc ipsum quod umbra vocatur comparatione manifestae veritatis, ineffabilis plerumque gloriae est et splendoris, in illis scilicet quorum est perlucidum speculum mentis. Cujus felicis experientiae Paulus sibi conscius, de se sibique similibus loquebatur: Nos vero omnes gloriam Domini speculantes, in eamdem imaginem transformamur a claritate in claritatem, tanquam a Domini Spiritu (II Cor. III, 18). Sic enim in promissione dictum est: Implebit splendoribus animam tuam (Isai. XVIII, 11.). Ut autem claritates istas umbras esse noveris, audi David: Dies mei, inquit, sicut umbra declinaverunt. Transibat quidem et ipse David de claritate in claritatem, tanquam de die in diem, Sed heu! tam breves et obscuros dies, tam longas et laboriosas noctes habet hiems ista, ut et propheta laborans in gemitu suo, lavansque per singulas noctes lectum lacrymis, merito plangat: Dies mei sicut umbra declinaverunt! (Psal. CI, 12.) Prorsus melior est dies una in atriis tuis super millia (Psal. LXXXIII, 11) dierum meorum; quia dies mei sicut umbra quamvis lucida: dies illa una, lux vera et mera, sicut sine umbra, sic sine vespera; cum dies meos faciat plurimos et brevissimos assidua longarum noctium interpolatio."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Paul distinguished quite properly the beginning of this grace and the perfection of that glory, when he said: \"We see now through a glass in a dark manner; but then face to face\" (I Cor. XIII, 12). But in order for this very thing to come about\u2014namely, that we see through a glass and in a dark manner\u2014it is necessary not only that the face of our mirror be wiped clean of every phantasm and shadow of corporeal things, but also that he who is exalted on high, dwelling in inaccessible light, deign to incline himself toward us, and be made manifest to us at least through the shadow of his image. For however much we strive to pass from the visible to the invisible, our lowliness will apprehend nothing unless that majesty condescends. Moses went up into the mountain, and the Lord bowed down the heavens and came down (Exod. XIX, 20). And another says: \"Arise to meet me, and behold\" (Psal. LVIII, 6). Therefore the shadow is not seen unless it is inclined; nor is it said to incline except when the day breathes forth: because we cannot see God through a glass and in a dark manner unless majesty inclines itself and the favor of grace breathes upon us. Indeed, this very thing which is called a shadow, in comparison with manifest truth, is for the most part of ineffable glory and splendor\u2014namely, in those whose mirror of the mind is perfectly clear. Conscious of this happy experience, Paul spoke of himself and those like him: \"But we all, beholding the glory of the Lord, are transformed into the same image from glory to glory, as by the Spirit of the Lord\" (II Cor. III, 18). For thus it was said in the promise: \"He will fill your soul with splendors\" (Isai. XVIII, 11.). But that you may know that these very splendors are shadows, listen to David: \"My days,\" he says, \"have declined like a shadow.\" David too was indeed passing from glory to glory, as it were from day to day. But alas! this winter has days so short and dark, and nights so long and laborious, that the prophet himself, laboring in his groaning and washing his bed every night with tears, justly laments: \"My days have declined like a shadow!\" (Psal. CI, 12.). Truly \"better is one day in your courts\" (Psal. LXXXIII, 11) than a thousand of my days; because my days are like a shadow, however bright; but that one day is true and pure light\u2014as it is without shadow, so it is without evening\u2014whereas my days are made very many and very brief by the constant interruption of long nights.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Caeterum, si quaeratur cur umbrae pluraliter dictae sint, cum res ipsa, cujus umbrae sunt, una sit: ideo nimirum, quia nunc minus, nunc amplius nobis Altissimi virtus obumbrat; nunc obscurius, nunc manifestius veritas sese nobis innotescens adumbrat: et sicut non aequaliter semper spirat, ita nec similiter semper nobis imagines format. Hoc et Apostolus innuit, qui transire se confitetur a claritate in claritatem (II Cor. III, 18), a minore scilicet in majorem: et propheta, qui non splendore sed splendoribus implendam promittit fidelem animam (Isai. LVIII, 11). Sic Jacobus Deum memorat patrem luminum (Jacobi I, 17), cum lumen, cujus pater est, et unum sit et unicum. Quanquam sic possit intelligi pater luminum, quomodo pater misericordiarum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Furthermore, if it be asked why the shadows are spoken of in the plural, when the thing itself, of which they are shadows, is one: it is doubtless because now the power of the Most High overshadows us less, now more; now the truth, making itself known to us, casts its shadow more obscurely, now more manifestly: and just as he does not always breathe upon us equally, so neither does he always form images for us in like manner. The Apostle hints at this too, confessing that he passes \"from glory to glory\" (II Cor. III, 18), that is, from a lesser to a greater glory; and the prophet, who promises that the faithful soul will be filled not with a splendor but with splendors (Isai. LVIII, 11). Thus James calls God \"the Father of lights\" (Jacobi I, 17), though the light of which he is Father is one and unique. Although it can be understood that he is called \"Father of lights\" in the same way he is called \"Father of mercies.\"", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Jam vero vos fratres, qui laboratis portantes pondus diei et aestus, vel secundum Isaiam, in spiritu vestro duro meditamini per diem aestus (Isa. XXVII, 8), ad umbras istas placet invitare quas ille dies serenus aspirans rorem misericordiae cupit aestuantibus inclinare. Audite ipsum diem, ad umbrarum refrigeria clementer vos invitantem: Venite ad me, omnes qui laboratis et onerati estis, et ego reficiam vos (Matth. XI, 28). Servus enim sub sole laborans, ut ait Job, desiderat umbram (Job VII, 2), in qua scilicet a labore recreetur ad laborem, vel per horam dimidiam, qua silentium fit in coelo: quam cum obtinuerit indulgentia Prioris sui, gloriatur et dicit, Sub umbra illius, quem desiderabam, sedi. In qua nimirum umbra, quia non sola sessio est ad quietem, sed etiam refectio ad satietatem, congrue subdit: Et fructus ejus dulcis gutturi meo (Cantic. II, 3). Sic enim dictum est per prophetam: Convertentur sedentes in umbra ejus, vivent tritico et germinabunt sicut vitis (Osee XIV, 8). Servus seu mercenarius, quem saepius haec umbra recreaverit, longanimiter praestolabitur finem operis sui. Sic enim habes: Servus desiderat umbram, et mercenarius praestolatur finem operis sui (Job VII, 2). In umbra quidem non finis, sed pausatio. Finis enim erit operis remuneratio, cum evacuatis omnibus umbris, videbimus eum sicuti est: cui modo dicimus, In umbra tua vivemus inter gentes (Thren. IV, 20); inter angelos nempe, non in umbra, sed in luce manifesta."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Now, brothers, you who labor, bearing the burden of the day and the heat, or who, according to Isaiah, meditate in your hard spirit through the day of heat (Isa. XXVII, 8), it is fitting to invite you to these shadows which that serene day, breathing forth the dew of mercy, desires to incline toward those who burn with heat. Hear the Day himself, mercifully inviting you to the refreshments of the shadows: \"Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened, and I will refresh you\" (Matth. XI, 28). For the servant laboring under the sun, as Job says, desires the shadow (Job VII, 2), namely, that in it he may be refreshed from labor for labor, or at least for the half hour in which silence is made in heaven; and when he has obtained this by the indulgence of his Prior, he glories and says, \"Under the shadow of him whom I desired, I sat.\" And because in this shadow there is not only sitting for rest, but also feasting to satisfaction, he fittingly adds: \"And his fruit was sweet to my throat\" (Cantic. II, 3). For so it is said through the prophet: \"They that dwell under his shadow shall be turned, they shall live by the wheat and shall blossom as the vine\" (Osee XIV, 8). The servant or hireling, whom this shadow has often refreshed, will patiently await the end of his work. For thus you have it: \"The servant desires the shadow, and the hireling awaits the end of his work\" (Job VII, 2). In the shadow, indeed, there is not the end, but a pause. For the end will be the reward of the work, when, all shadows having been emptied away, we shall see him as he is\u2014to whom we now say, \"Under your shadow we shall live among the nations\" (Thren. IV, 20)\u2014that is, among the angels, not in shadow, but in manifest light.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Hoc autem notandum, quod cum umbrae illae de superioribus inclinantur supra nos, umbrae istae de inferioribus inclinantur infra nos. Cum enim divinis propinquamus, mundana superamus: et quatenus vel ad umbram lucis accedimus, eatenus mortis umbram evadimus. Lux enim et vita in coelo, mors in inferno: umbra autem mortis in hoc terreno et tenebroso loco. Cum igitur spiritus altius nos elevat inter terram et coelum, non tangimur umbra terrenorum: et licet nondum plena luce fruamur coelorum, tamen umbris illuminamur montium aeternorum, vel certe montis illius umbrosi et condensi, de quo venit Sanctus sanctorum. Mox tamen pondere nostro in regionem umbrae mortis revolvimur, ubi et diutissime sedere cogimur, donec visitatione orientis ex alto consolemur. Cogimur, inquam, et utinam cogamur, et non delectemur! utinam quod patimur, necessitatis tantum sit, non etiam voluntatis aut voluptatis! Sed heu! tam facile, tam libenter projicimus nos, et obdormiscimus sub gravi umbra juniperi spinosae et infructuosae arboris, tam negligentes et securi torpescimus et immorimur curis et desideriis hujus mundi! Propterea Leviathan, qui sub umbra dormit, cubile sibi, nisi praecaveamus, ponit in nobis; quia videlicet amicas sibi umbras hujus saeculi invenit in sensibus et affectibus nostris. Istae siquidem umbrae, teste Job, protegunt umbra ejus (Job. XL, 20, 17); quia nimirum curae et desideria saecularia, quae sunt in nobis, umbram nobis abscondunt imminentis damnationis, ut eam non praevideamus, donec in ipsam impingamus. Aspira, o dies dierum, obumbra supra caput nostrum in die belli, inclina super nos umbras salutis et refrigerii, et inclinentur subtus nos umbrae torporis et taedii, imo caecitatis et mortis. Et licet contingant nos umbrae de cogitatione terrenorum, non nos contegant tenebrae, id est, umbrae crassiores de amore eorum; sed semper respiremus in luminosum amorem tuum, o Pater luminum, qui vivis et regnas per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Now this must be noted: that while those shadows from the higher things incline over us, these shadows from the lower things incline beneath us. For when we draw near to things divine, we rise above worldly things; and to the extent that we approach even the shadow of the light, to that extent we escape the shadow of death. For light and life are in heaven, death is in hell; but the shadow of death is in this earthly and darksome place. When, therefore, the spirit lifts us higher, between earth and heaven, we are not touched by the shadow of earthly things; and although we do not yet enjoy the full light of the heavens, nevertheless we are illumined by the shadows of the eternal mountains \u2014 or at least of that shadowy and dense mountain from which the Holy One of holy ones came. Yet soon, by our own weight, we are rolled back down into the region of the shadow of death, where we are forced to sit for a very long time, until we are consoled by the visitation of the Dayspring from on high. We are forced, I say \u2014 and would that we were only forced, and did not take delight in it! Would that what we suffer were a matter of necessity alone, and not also of will or of pleasure! But alas, how easily, how willingly we fling ourselves down and fall asleep under the heavy shadow of the juniper, a thorny and fruitless tree; so negligent and so unconcerned do we grow sluggish and lose ourselves in the cares and desires of this world! Therefore Leviathan, who sleeps beneath the shadow, makes his lair in us, unless we take care \u2014 because, indeed, he finds shadows of this age that are friendly to him in our senses and affections. For these shadows, as Job testifies, protect his shadow (Job. XL, 20, 17); because, truly, the worldly cares and desires that are in us hide from us the shadow of the damnation that hangs over us, so that we do not foresee it until we strike against it. Breathe upon us, O Day of days; overshadow our heads in the day of battle; incline over us the shadows of salvation and refreshment, and let the shadows of torpor and weariness \u2014 nay, of blindness and death \u2014 be inclined beneath us. And though the shadows that come from the thought of earthly things may touch us, let not the darkness \u2014 that is, the thicker shadows that come from the love of them \u2014 cover us over; but let us always breathe toward your luminous love, O Father of lights, who live and reign through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-n010", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(10) S. Bernardum intelligit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-n010", "label": "English", "text": "(10) He means St. Bernard.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The quoted promise of rest and brightness comes from Isaiah 58:11; the edition prints Isai. XVIII, 11 here and LVIII, 11 later."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints vivum bonum; the allusion is to the good wine kept until last in John 2:10."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saints Peter and Paul Sermon III", "topics": ["Saints Peter and Paul", "Song of Songs", "contemplation", "vision of God", "rest", "worldly cares"], "url": "sermons/guerric-peter-paul-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:candlemas", "calendar_label": "The Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-purification-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis, sermo I, De caeremonia luminum hoc festo usitata, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier freres, 1879), cols. 63-67.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Candlemas", "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "Luke 22:35 (printed locator; quotation identified as Luke 12:35, see note)", "Job 14:4", "Zechariah 13:1", "James 4:17", "Luke 12:47", "Deuteronomy 4:24", "III Regum 1:17 (printed locator; Abishag episode identified as modern 1 Kings 1:1\u20134, see note)", "Song of Songs 1:12", "Luke 12:35 (lamps in the hands)", "Proverbs 31:18", "Song of Songs 8:6, 7", "Luke 12:35 (good works as a lamp)", "Matthew 5:16", "Psalm 118:105 (historical numbering; word as a lamp)", "2 Peter 1:19", "Psalm 118:130 (historical numbering)", "1 Peter 4:11", "Psalm 118:105 (historical numbering; repeated in counsel on speech)", "Proverbs 20:27", "Job 29:2, 3", "Zephaniah 1:12 (divine examination contrasted with illuminating grace)", "Matthew 6:22 and Luke 11:34 (single printed locator group)", "Zephaniah 1:12 (Jerusalem searched with lamps)", "Proverbs 20:20", "Job 11:17 and Isaiah 58:10 (single printed locator group)"], "occasion": "In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis", "occasion_detail": "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "occasion_label": "Candlemas", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric of Igny reads the Purification's ceremony of lights through Simeon receiving Christ in the temple. Mary's obedience prompts a call to humility, bodily chastity and purity of heart; carrying a candle should express devotion and good deeds. Simeon cherishes the Word in flesh like light in wax, and Guerric invites his hearers to become lamps themselves. He develops the image through faith, charitable works, speech that builds up others, prayer and a sincere intention before God. The sermon closes by contrasting divine examination with the enduring light promised beyond death. The individual sermon is undated; its collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157 and is read here from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The source names the Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary, traditionally known as Candlemas."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN FESTO PURIFICATIONIS BEATAE MARIAE VIRGINIS."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE FEAST OF THE PURIFICATION OF THE BLESSED VIRGIN MARY.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De caeremonia luminum hoc festo usitata."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On the ceremony of lights customary at this feast.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Sint lumbi vestri praecincti, et lucernae ardentes in manibus vestris (Luc. xxii, 35). Sint lumbi nostri praecincti, ut Purificationem Mariae aemulemur; sint lucernae ardentes in manibus, ut gaudium Simeonis, lumen in manibus portantis, etiam visibili signo in nobis repraesentemus. Simus videlicet casti corpore, et mundi corde; et Purificationem Mariae expressimus: simus ardentes devotione, et lucentes opere; et cum Simeone Christum portamus in manibus. Maria tamen non tam purificata fuit, quam mysterium Purificationis commendavit, implendo legalem, significando spiritualem. Quid enim in ea purificandum esse potuit, quae virgo concepit, virgo peperit, virgoque permansit, imo quae ipso conceptu plane purificata fuit, si quid forte minus ante puritatis habuisset [alias habuit)? Quid, inquam, mundandum habuit conceptus ille, qui solus potest facere mundum de immundo conceptum semine (Job. xiv, 4), qui fontem quo immundus purificetur edidit, fontem domus David, qui hodieque patet indefesseque scatet in ablutionem peccatoris, et menstruatae? (Zach.. xiii, 1.) Suscepit tamen Mater totius puritatis, purgationis legitimae speciem, ut simul et obedientissimae humilitatis virtutem, et evangelicae purificationis insinuaret veritatem. Ubi nunc ille, qui tam fallaciter quam contumaciter praesumens de sanctitate, detrectat purgatoria poenitentium remedia subire? Esto quod sanctus sit; nunquid sicut sanctarum Sanctissima, quae Sanctum sanctorum peperit, Maria? Utinam, fratres mei, utinam nos illam haberemus humilitatem in peccatis nostris quam vere habuerunt sancti in virtutibus suis. Sed omittimus nunc istud, ut quod coepimus prosequamur. Ac primo quidem de illa spirituali Purificatione, quam Maria, offerens pro se et pro Filio par turturum aut duos pullos columbarum, nobis commendavit, prosequeremur, nisi quod patres nostri, sicut vobis notissimum est, omnia ad liquidum prosecuti sunt; et quomodo turtur ad castitatem carnis, columba ad simplicitatem mentis pertineat, diligenter discurrerunt; nec illud quidem praetereuntes, quid sit sacrificium de uno turture vel pullo columbino, quid holocaustum de altero. Hoc unum tamen adjicimus, quod quanto notiora nobis sunt mysteria, tanto districtius et justius eorum a nobis exiguntur opera; quia scienti bonum et non facienti, peccatum est illi (Jac. iv, 17); et servus sciens voluntatem domini sui, et non faciens digna, plagis vapulabit multis (Luc. xii, 47)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Let your loins be girded, and lamps burning in your hands (Luc. xxii, 35). Let our loins be girded, so that we may imitate Mary's Purification; let lamps be burning in our hands, so that by a visible sign we too may represent the joy of Simeon carrying the light in his hands. Let us be chaste in body and clean in heart, and we have expressed Mary's Purification; let us burn with devotion and shine in our deeds, and with Simeon we carry Christ in our hands. Yet Mary did not so much undergo purification as commend the mystery of Purification, by fulfilling the legal purification and signifying the spiritual one. For what could there have been in her needing purification, when she conceived as a virgin, gave birth as a virgin, and remained a virgin; indeed, when in the very act of conceiving she was fully purified, if perhaps she had previously possessed any lesser degree of purity [variant: possessed)? What, I ask, did that conceived child have that needed cleansing, he who alone can make clean one conceived of unclean seed (Job. xiv, 4), who brought forth the fountain by which the unclean may be purified, the fountain of the house of David, which even today stands open and flows unceasingly for the washing of the sinner and the menstruating woman? (Zach.. xiii, 1.) Nevertheless, the Mother of all purity accepted the outward form of purification prescribed by the law, to convey both the virtue of most obedient humility and the reality of evangelical purification. Where now is the man who, presuming on his holiness as deceitfully as obstinately, refuses to undergo the cleansing remedies of penitents? Granted that he is holy: is he as holy as Mary, the Holiest of holy women, who bore the Holy of holies? Would that, my brothers, would that we had in our sins the humility that the saints truly had in their virtues! But we leave this aside now, to continue what we began. First, indeed, we would pursue that spiritual Purification which Mary commended to us by offering for herself and her Son a pair of turtledoves or two young pigeons, were it not that our fathers, as you well know, have treated everything with complete clarity. They have carefully explained how the turtledove pertains to chastity of the flesh and the dove to simplicity of mind, without passing over what is meant by the sacrifice of one turtledove or young pigeon and the whole burnt offering of the other. Yet we add this one point: the better known the mysteries are to us, the more strictly and justly are the deeds they require demanded of us; for to him who knows the good and does not do it, it is sin (Jac. iv, 17); and the servant who knows his master's will and does not act worthily will be beaten with many blows (Luc. xii, 47).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Relinquentes igitur quod tractatum est sufficienter, illud potius tractemus, si placet, quid haec pulcherrima Ecclesiae consuetudo luminarium hodie gestandorum, aut factum repraesentet, aut faciendum demonstret. Quanquam ne id puto quidem vobis incognitum, etiamsi nunquam sit auditum. Quis enim hodie, cereum ardentem gestans in manibus, non statim illius senis recordetur, qui, hodie Jesum accipiens in ulnas suas, Verbum in carne tanquam lumen in cera, ipsum esse lumen ad illuminationem gentium perhibebat? Erat profecto et ipse lucerna ardens et lucens, testimonium perhibens de lumine, qui ad hoc ipsum in Spiritu quo plenus erat, venit in templum, ut, suscipiens misericordiam tuam, Deus, in medio templi tui praedicaret ipsam esse misericordiam, et lumen populi tui. Vere tu, senex serene, lumen portabas non modo manibus, sed et sensibus; qui sic eras illuminatus, ut, cum tanto ante futuram illuminationem gentium tam clare videres, etiam nunc inter tenebras Judaicae infidelitatis, hodierna claritas fidei nostrae tibi coruscaret. Laetare jam, senior juste, vide jam quod praevidebas: discussae sunt mundi tenebrae, ambulant gentes in lumine tuo; plena est omnis terra gloria luminis illius quod occultum in sinu fovebas, imo quo sensus tuos refovebas. O fomes amoris, ipse amor suavis et dulcis ad refocillandam animam! Prorsus suavis et dulcis, etiam cum crucias et affligis; cruciatus feliciter delectans, sicut ignis salubriter refrigerans. Ignis iste, fratres, si in sinu absconditus fuerit, vestimenta non comburit; imo illum ignem exstinguit, qui, in sinu celatus, non modo vestimentum adurit, sed et quod vestitum est, depascit, id est, corpus et animam pariter, consumit. Deus, inquit, noster ignis consumens est (Deut. iv, 24), sed ignem consumentem; nam animam refovet, demulcet mentem, spiritum recreat, reparatque pereuntem. Hujus ignis vim Simeon non ignorabat, qui in sinu suo eum portare gaudebat, pectusque senile fotu illius calefaciebat. Quanto salubrius et suavius ignis iste senem nostrum calefecit, quam regem David Abisag Sunamitis? (III Reg. i, 17) nisi forte Abisag hic ignis idem fuit, sapientia scilicet, cujus amplexu non solum recalescunt frigidi, sed etiam reviviscunt mortui."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Leaving aside, then, what has been sufficiently treated, let us consider instead, if you please, what past event this most beautiful custom of the Church, carrying lights today, represents, or what action it shows us we should perform. Yet I do not think even this is unknown to you, though you may never have heard it explained. For who today, carrying a burning candle in his hands, would not immediately remember that old man who today took Jesus in his arms, the Word in flesh like light in wax, and testified that he was the light to enlighten the nations? He himself, indeed, was also a burning and shining lamp, bearing witness to the light. In the Spirit who filled him he came into the temple for this very purpose: receiving your mercy, O God, in the midst of your temple, to proclaim it to be the mercy and light of your people. Truly, serene old man, you carried the light in your understanding as well as in your hands. You were so enlightened that, while seeing so clearly so far in advance the future enlightenment of the nations, even then, amid the darkness of Jewish unbelief, the present brightness of our faith flashed before you. Rejoice now, righteous elder; see now what you foresaw: the world's darkness has been scattered, the nations walk in your light; the whole earth is full of the glory of that light which you cherished hidden in your bosom, or rather, by which you warmed your understanding. O kindling of love, love itself, pleasant and sweet for reviving the soul! Truly pleasant and sweet, even when you torment and afflict: a torment that brings happy delight, like a fire that gives wholesome coolness. This fire, brothers, if it is hidden in the bosom, does not burn the garments; rather, it extinguishes that fire which, concealed in the bosom, not only scorches the garment but devours what is clothed, that is, consumes body and soul together. Our God, it says, is a consuming fire (Deut. iv, 24), but one that consumes the consuming fire; for it warms the soul, soothes the mind, refreshes the spirit, and restores the one who is perishing. Simeon knew the power of this fire: he rejoiced to carry it in his bosom and warmed his aged breast with its heat. How much more wholesomely and pleasantly this fire warmed our old man than Abishag the Shunammite warmed King David! (III Reg. i, 17) Unless perhaps Abishag was this very same fire, namely wisdom, in whose embrace not only do the cold grow warm again, but even the dead return to life.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Amplectere igitur, o beate senex, sapientiam Dei, et recalescant atque recanescant quasi de novo sensus tui; astringe uberibus tuis misericordiam Dei, et erit senectus tua in misericordia uberi. Inter ubera, inquit, mea commorabitur (Cantic. i, 12), etiam cum reddam illum Matri suae, manebit apud me. Et cum inter materna ubera continebitur, nihilominus inter ubera mea commorabitur, et ubera quoque mea inebriabit misericordia uberi; etsi non quantum ubera Matris. Illa quippe sicut singulariter mater est summae misericordiae, sic excellenter habet ubera uberis misericordiae. Gratulor et gratificor tibi, gratia plena, quae misericordiam genuisti quam suscepi; cereum praeparasti, quem accepi. Tu ceram ministrasti suscepto lumini, Virgo virginum Virgini; dum incorrupta mater incorruptibile Verbum, incorrupta carne vestisti. Eia, fratres mei, ecce ardet cereus in manibus Simeonis, accendite et vos cereos vestros de illius mutuatione luminis, lucernas dico quas Dominus jubet esse in manibus vestris (Luc. xii, 35). Accedite ad eum, et illuminamini, ut non tam lucernas feratis quam ipsi lucernae sitis, lucentes intus et foris, vobis et proximis. Sit ergo lucerna in corde, sit in manu, sit in ore. Lucerna in corde luceat vobis: lucerna in manu vel ore luceat proximis Lucerna in corde, est pietas fidei; lucerna in manu, exemplum operis; lucerna in ore, sermo aedificationis. Non solum autem coram hominibus lucere nos necesse est per operationem et sermonem, sed et coram angelis, per orationem, coram Deo per intentionem. Lucerna nostra coram angelis est pura devotio, cum in conspectu angelorum psallimus sapienter vel oramus ardenter; lucerna coram Deo, simplex intentio, ut ei soli placeamus, cui nos probavimus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "4. Embrace, then, blessed old man, the wisdom of God, and let your faculties grow warm and gray again, as though anew; clasp God's mercy to your breasts, and your old age will abound in mercy. Between my breasts, he says, he will dwell (Cantic. i, 12); even when I give him back to his Mother, he will remain with me. And when he is held between his Mother's breasts, he will nevertheless dwell between my breasts, and will also fill my breasts to overflowing with abundant mercy, though not as much as his Mother's breasts. For just as she is uniquely the mother of supreme mercy, so her breasts possess abundant mercy in an outstanding way. I rejoice with you and give you thanks, full of grace, who bore the mercy I have received; you prepared the candle I have taken. You supplied wax for the light that was received, O Virgin of virgins, for the Virgin, when as an uncorrupted mother you clothed the incorruptible Word in uncorrupted flesh. Come, my brothers: see, the candle burns in Simeon's hands. Light your own candles too by borrowing from his light, I mean the lamps the Lord commands to be in your hands (Luc. xii, 35). Come to him and be enlightened, so that you may not so much carry lamps as be lamps yourselves, shining within and without, for yourselves and your neighbors. Let there be a lamp, then, in the heart, in the hand, and in the mouth. Let the lamp in the heart shine for you; let the lamp in the hand or mouth shine for your neighbors. The lamp in the heart is the devotion of faith; the lamp in the hand, the example of deeds; the lamp in the mouth, speech that builds up. We must shine not only before men through deeds and speech, but also before angels through prayer, and before God through intention. Our lamp before angels is pure devotion, when in the sight of angels we sing psalms with understanding or pray fervently; the lamp before God is a single intention, to please him alone, to whom we have approved ourselves.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Et ne putemur, in his quae dicimus, sensus nostri somnia secuti fuisse; probemus nos potius in omnibus adhaesisse testimoniis Scripturae. Quod enim fides lucerna sit, testatur Salomon loquens de muliere forti, Non exstinguetur in nocte lucerna ejus (Prov. xxxi, 18); quod utique intelligimus, Non deficiet in tentatione fides ejus. Nam et de charitate, quae nihil aliud est quam fides operans per dilectionem, apud eumdem legimus Salomonem: Lampades ejus, lampades ignis atque flammarum. Aquae multae non poterunt exstinguere charitatem, nec flumina obruent illam (Cantic. viii, 6, 7). Quod opus bonum lucerna sit, lux ipsa veritatis indicat, cum ait, Sint lumbi vestri praecincti, et lucernae ardentes in manibus vestris (Luc. xii, 35): et, Luceat lux vestra coram hominibus, ut videant opera vestra bona (Matth. v, 16). Quod sermo aedificationis lucerna sit, David dicit: Lucerna pedibus meis verbum tuum, et lumen semitis meis (Psal. cxviii, 105). Et Petrus apostolus de sermone prophetico, cui benefacitis, inquit, intendentes, tanquam lucernae lucenti in obscuro loco (II Petr. i, 19). Declaratio quoque sermonum Dei illuminat, et intellectum dat parvulis (Psal. cxviii, 130), sive cum sermones evangelici declarant umbram legis, sive cum ipsi sermones tam Evangelii quam legis declarantur expositionibus dilucidis, qui non intelligentibus parvulis videbantur obscuri; et in lumine dicimus quod dictum erat nobis in tenebris. Ideo Philippus in figura cujuslibet ecclesiastici doctoris dictus est os lampadis, eo quod de ore ipsius ad illuminationem audientium coruscaret ignitum eloquium Domini. Sermonem autem Dei intelligo, fratres mei, quidquid spiritus ejus dignatione sua loquitur in vobis; omnem utique sermonem qui bonus est ad aedificationem fidei, in provocationem charitatis. Si quis ergo vestrum loquitur, quasi sermones Dei (I Petr. iv, 11); ut etiam in privatis colloquiis omnis sermo malus non procedat de ore vestro, sed si quis est ad aedificationem fidei, ut gratiam det audienti; ut qui audierit, dicat gratias agens tibi, Benedictus sermo oris tui, quia lucerna pedibus meis verbum tuum, et lumen semitis meis (Psal. cxviii, 105). De oratione autem quod et ipsa lucerna sit, maxime si divinitus sit illuminata, ex eo colligimus quod Salomon ait: Lucerna Domini spiraculum hominis, quae investigat omnia secreta ventris (Prov xx, 27). Lumen namque quod nobis orantibus vel psallentibus desuper aperitur, spiraculum vitae est, in quod suaviter respiramus. Hujus lucernae videtur Job recordari, cum in moerore tentationis recolens cum suspirio laetitiam praeteritae consolationis, ait: Quis mihi tribuat ut sim juxta menses pristinos, secundum dies quibus Deus custodiebat me, quando splendebat lucerna ejus super caput meum, et ad lumen ejus ambulabam in tenebris? (Job, xxix, 2, 3.) Caeterum quod lucerna haec dicitur investigare omnia secreta ventris, id est mentis; non illud esse putandum est, quod Dominus comminatur se scrutaturum in lucernis (Sophon. i, 12): cum istud gratiae sit illuminantis, illud judicii exquirentis. Nam et aliter investigat secreta ventris potio medici; aliter gladius carnificis. Jam vero de intentione, quod et ipsa dicatur lucerna, nemo est qui ambigat, si tamen evangelicum illud intelligat: Lucerna corporis tui est oculus tuus (Matth. vi, 22, et Luc. xi, 34)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. And lest we be thought, in what we say, to have followed the dreams of our own understanding, let us show instead that in everything we have kept to the testimonies of Scripture. For Solomon testifies that faith is a lamp when he says of the valiant woman, Her lamp will not be extinguished in the night (Prov. xxxi, 18); which we understand to mean, Her faith will not fail in temptation. Concerning charity too, which is nothing other than faith working through love, we read in the same Solomon: Its lamps are lamps of fire and flame. Many waters will not be able to extinguish charity, nor will rivers overwhelm it (Cantic. viii, 6, 7). That a good deed is a lamp is shown by the light of truth itself when it says, Let your loins be girded, and lamps burning in your hands (Luc. xii, 35); and, Let your light shine before men, that they may see your good deeds (Matth. v, 16). That speech which builds up is a lamp, David declares: Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my paths (Psal. cxviii, 105). And the Apostle Peter says of the prophetic word, to which you do well to attend as to a lamp shining in a dark place (II Petr. i, 19). The unfolding of God's words also gives light and understanding to little ones (Psal. cxviii, 130), whether the words of the Gospel make plain the shadow of the law, or the words of both Gospel and law themselves are made plain by clear explanations, having seemed obscure to little ones who did not understand; and we speak in the light what was said to us in darkness. Thus Philip, as a figure of every teacher in the Church, was called the mouth of a lamp, because the Lord's fiery utterance flashed from his mouth to enlighten his hearers. By God's word, my brothers, I mean whatever his Spirit graciously speaks in you: every word, indeed, that is good for building up faith and stirring up charity. If any of you speaks, let him speak as the words of God (I Petr. iv, 11), so that even in private conversations no evil word may proceed from your mouth, but only such as serves to build up faith and give grace to the hearer; so that whoever hears may thank you and say, Blessed is the word of your mouth, for your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my paths (Psal. cxviii, 105). That prayer too is a lamp, especially when divinely illuminated, we gather from Solomon's saying: The breath of man is the Lord's lamp, which searches all the hidden places of the belly (Prov xx, 27). For the light opened to us from above as we pray or sing psalms is the breath of life, into which we breathe with delight. Job seems to remember this lamp when, in the sorrow of trial, recalling with a sigh the joy of past consolation, he says: Who will grant me to be as in former months, in the days when God guarded me, when his lamp shone above my head and by its light I walked in darkness? (Job, xxix, 2, 3.) Yet when this lamp is said to search all the hidden places of the belly, that is, of the mind, we must not suppose it to be the same as the Lord's threat to search with lamps (Sophon. i, 12): this belongs to illuminating grace, that to searching judgment. For a physician's medicine searches the hidden places of the belly in one way, an executioner's sword in another. As for intention, no one doubts that it too is called a lamp, provided he understands the Gospel saying: The lamp of your body is your eye (Matth. vi, 22, et Luc. xi, 34).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Ut igitur tot lucernas vobis, fratres, illuminetis, accedite ad fontem luminis, et illuminamini, Jesum dico, qui lucet in manibus Simeonis, ut scilicet fidem illuminet, opus illustret, sermonem homini subministret, orationem inflammet, intentionem purget; ut sive opere, sive sermone vel oratione quaeratis placere coram eo in lumine viventium, qui scrutari habet Jerusalem in lucernis suis (Sophon. i, 12), et examinare etiam lumen nostrum. His omnibus accensis, o fili lucis, jam non ambulabis in tenebris, nec timenda tibi erit sententia maledicti, Qui maledixerit patri, vel matri, exstinguetur lucerna ejus in tenebris (Prov. xx, 20), id est, deficiet consolatio hujus lucis, irruentibus undique hinc exterioribus, inde interioribus tenebris. Tibi enim, cui tot interius ardent lucernae, cum exstincta fuerit lucerna hujus vitae, orietur lux inexstinguibilis vitae, et quasi fulgor meridianus consurget tibi ad vesperum: et cum te consumptum putaveris, orieris ut lucifer, et tenebrae tuae erunt sicut meridies (Job. xi, 17, et Isai. lviii, 10). Non tamen erit tibi sol amplius ad lucendum per diem, aut splendor lunae illuminabit te; sed Dominus erit tibi in lucem sempiternam; quia Agnus lucerna est novae Jerusalem cui benedictio et claritas in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Therefore, brothers, to light so many lamps for yourselves, come to the fountain of light and be enlightened: I mean Jesus, who shines in Simeon's hands, so that he may enlighten faith, make deeds radiant, supply man with speech, inflame prayer, and purify intention. Thus, whether by deed, speech, or prayer, you may seek to please him in the light of the living, before him who is to search Jerusalem with his lamps (Sophon. i, 12) and examine even our light. With all these kindled, O child of light, you will no longer walk in darkness, nor will you have to fear the sentence of the curse: Whoever curses father or mother, his lamp will be extinguished in darkness (Prov. xx, 20); that is, the consolation of this light will fail, as darkness rushes in from every side, outward darkness on one side and inward darkness on the other. For you, within whom so many lamps burn, when the lamp of this life is extinguished, the inextinguishable light of life will arise, and at evening a brightness like noonday will rise for you. When you think yourself consumed, you will rise like the morning star, and your darkness will be as midday (Job. xi, 17, et Isai. lviii, 10). Yet the sun will no longer serve to give you light by day, nor will the brightness of the moon illuminate you; rather, the Lord will be your everlasting light, for the Lamb is the lamp of the new Jerusalem, to whom be blessing and glory for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The source numbers the sections 1, 2, 4, 4, 5; the repeated 4 is retained."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition includes an alternative Latin form after habuisset: [alias habuit). The English translates alias as \u201cvariant\u201d and retains the mismatched brackets. Guerric\u2019s conditional wording about Mary\u2019s purity, and his distinction between Mary and the child conceived, are retained. The language about the menstruating woman comes from the sermon\u2019s biblical quotation."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The opening quotation about lamps is printed with Luke 22:35, but comes from Luke 12:35. The reference to Abishag warming David is printed as III Regum 1:17; the episode is in modern 1 Kings 1:1\u20134. Both source references are retained."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The divine fire consumes the destructive fire while renewing the person. \u201cGrow warm and gray again\u201d preserves Guerric\u2019s wordplay about age and warmth. He applies \u201cVirgin\u201d to Christ as well as to Mary, and develops the imagery of breasts, wax and light. His explanation of Philip as \u201cmouth of a lamp\u201d is his own interpretation. The contrast between a physician\u2019s medicine and an executioner\u2019s sword belongs to the sermon."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Purification Sermon I: The Ceremony of Lights", "topics": ["Purification", "Virgin Mary", "Simeon", "light", "humility", "faith", "charity", "prayer", "good works"], "url": "sermons/guerric-purification-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:candlemas", "calendar_label": "The Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-purification-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis, sermo II, Quanto sit affectu occurrendum Jesu advenienti, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 67-71.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Purification", "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Psalm 117:26\u201328 (historical numbering)", "Luke 2:38", "Psalm 117:27 (historical numbering)", "Luke 2:27", "Lamentations 1:4", "Malachi 3:1", "Isaiah 21:12", "Daniel 4:14", "Proverbs 25:5", "Psalm 88:15 (historical numbering)", "John 7:34\u201336", "1 John 5:6", "Song of Songs 1:2", "Isaiah 25:1", "Luke 2:32", "Psalm 118:82 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 117:28 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 42:4 (historical numbering)", "John 4:23", "Acts 7:48", "Isaiah 69:16 (printed locator; no such chapter)", "Isaiah 66:10"], "occasion": "In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis", "occasion_detail": "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Purification", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Simeon and Anna receive Christ with longing, joy and praise. Guerric describes the encounter as an anointing of the heart, then reads the four figures in the temple as examples of prayer, devotion, humility and charity. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quanto sit affectu occurrendum Jesu advenienti."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "With What Love We Should Go Out to Meet the Coming Jesus", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Sicut ad diem nativitatis, id est adventus Dominici, pertinuit illa gratiarum actio et vox laudis, Benedictus qui venit in nomine Domini; et ad diem apparitionis, id est, nostrae illuminationis, illud quod sequitur: Deus Dominus et illuxit nobis, sic etiam quod subsequitur, Constituite diem solemnem in confrequentationibus [Vulg. in condensis] usque ad cornu altaris; non absurde, ut arbitror, pertinere dicetur ad hunc diem, nobis utique celebrem, frequentia solemni, diem scilicet Dominicae Praesentationis, quando a Matre solemniter ad altare praesentatus est Patri. Nam et in eo quod adjungitur, Deus meus es tu, et confitebor tibi; Deus meus es tu, et exaltabo te; confitebor tibi quoniam exaudisti me, et factus es mihi in salutem (Psal. CXVII, 26-28), quid tam convenienter accipimus quam fidem et confessionem Simeonis et Annae, quae et ipsa hora superveniens confitebatur Domino, et loquebatur de illo omnibus qui exspectabant consolationem Israel. (Luc. II, 38)? Bene ergo citharista noster monebat laetari Jerusalem et conventum facere in die tam solemni: Constituite, inquiens, diem solemnem in confrequentationibus usque ad cornu altaris (Psal. CXVII, 27). Non mediocris aut parum solemnis illa frequentia fuit, cum inde venit Jesus et Maria cum turba parentum inducentium puerum, ut facerent secundum consuetudinem legis pro eo (Luc. II, 27); inde occurrit Simeon et Anna cum turba exspectantium consolationem Israel, quibus testificati sunt de eo."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Just as that thanksgiving and voice of praise, \"Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord,\" belonged to the day of the Nativity, that is, of the Lord's Advent; and just as what follows, \"The Lord is God, and he has shone upon us,\" belonged to the day of the Appearing, that is, of our illumination; so too, I think, it may not unfittingly be said that what comes next, \"Appoint a solemn day in the gatherings [Vulg. amid the thick boughs] even to the horn of the altar,\" belongs to this day \u2014 a day certainly made festive for us by a solemn gathering \u2014 namely, the day of the Lord's Presentation, when he was solemnly presented by his Mother to the Father at the altar. For in what follows, \"You are my God, and I will give thanks to you; you are my God, and I will exalt you; I will give thanks to you, for you have heard me, and you have become my salvation\" (Psal. CXVII, 26-28), what do we more fittingly understand than the faith and confession of Simeon and Anna, who, coming in at that very hour, gave thanks to the Lord and spoke of him to all who were awaiting the consolation of Israel (Luc. II, 38)? Well, then, did our harpist urge Jerusalem to rejoice and to hold assembly on so solemn a day, saying: \"Appoint a solemn day in the gatherings, even to the horn of the altar\" (Psal. CXVII, 27). That gathering was no mean or unimpressive one, when from one side came Jesus and Mary with the crowd of relatives bringing in the child, that they might do for him according to the custom of the law (Luc. II, 27); and from the other side Simeon and Anna came to meet them with the crowd of those awaiting the consolation of Israel, to whom they bore witness concerning him.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Non crediderim ego de hac solemnitate quae tam pium, tam laetum conventum habuit, illam fuisse prophetae querelam lacrymabilem, Viae Sion lugent, quia non est qui veniat ad solemnitatem (Thren. I, 4): nisi forte arguat paucos venisse de tanto populo qui erat in omni Judaea et Jerusalem. Ad comparationem siquidem tantae multitudinis pauci venerunt, et de ipsis paucis paucissimi, ut arbitror, eum receperunt, cum praesentem viderent diu exspectatum, sicut voce Patris par Malachiam fuerat repromissum: Ecce, inquit, veniet ad templum suum sanctum Dominator quem vos quaeritis, et Angelus testamenti quem vos vultis (Malach. III, 1). O Judaei, adest, ecce, Dominator quem vos quaeritis? cur offerentem se vobis non recipitis? Quaeritis et non quaeritis. Sed si quaeritis; quaerite: convertimini et venite (Isai. XXI, 12). Quaeritis qui redimat de manu hominum, non quaeritis qui redimat de manu daemonum. Quaeritis qui eruat de servitute Romanorum, non quaeritis qui eruat de captivitate vitiorum. Si vere utique liberatorem quaeritis, quaerite liberatorem animarum vestrarum, qui salvum faciat populum suum a peccatis eorum. Si quaeritis sublimem, quaerite prius humilem, quoniam, ut ait Daniel: Humillimum hominem constituet Deus super omne regnum (Dan. IV, 14). Si quaeritis dominatorem potentiae, quaerite prius doctorem justitiae, quia justitia firmabitur thronus ejus (Prov. XXV, 5), et justitia et judicium praeparatio sedis ejus (Psal. LXXXVIII, 15). Quia enim perfecte non quaeritis, ideo nec praesentem invenitis, sicut de vobis ipse praedixit: Quaerent me mali, et non invenient me, quia oderunt me (Joan. VII, 34-36). Quaerent absentem, nec invenient etiam praesentem, quia oderunt lucem mala eorum opera arguentem, cum sapientia non nisi per dilectionem possit quaeri, nedum inveniri. Simeon namque, quia pio et fideli quaerebat desiderio, quaesitum invenit, et inventum agnovit sine alterius indicio, sine humano scilicet testimonio. Nam Spiritus est qui testificatur quoniam Christus est veritas (I Joan. V, 6); virtus utique quae de ipso procedit, unctio docens de omnibus, quae de illo, singulariter secundum nomen suum uncto, multipliciter profluit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. I would not believe that of this solemnity, which had so devout and so joyful a gathering, there held true that tearful complaint of the prophet: \"The ways of Sion mourn, because there is none that comes to the solemnity\" (Thren. I, 4) \u2014 unless perhaps it be charging that few came, out of so great a people as was then in all Judea and Jerusalem. For in comparison with so great a multitude, few came; and of those few, I think, very few indeed received him, though they saw present before them him who had long been awaited, just as had been promised by the voice of the Father through Malachi: \"Behold,\" he says, \"the Ruler whom you seek shall come to his holy temple, and the Angel of the covenant whom you desire\" (Malach. III, 1). O Jews, behold \u2014 is the Ruler whom you seek present here? Why do you not receive him who offers himself to you? You seek, and you do not seek. But if you seek, then seek: turn and come (Isai. XXI, 12). You seek one who may redeem you from the hand of men; you do not seek one who may redeem you from the hand of demons. You seek one who may rescue you from servitude to the Romans; you do not seek one who may rescue you from captivity to vices. If indeed you truly seek a liberator, seek the liberator of your souls, who will save his people from their sins. If you seek one who is exalted, seek first one who is humble, since, as Daniel says: \"God will set the most humble of men over every kingdom\" (Dan. IV, 14). If you seek a ruler of power, seek first a teacher of justice, for \"his throne shall be established by justice\" (Prov. XXV, 5), and \"justice and judgment are the preparation of his seat\" (Psal. LXXXVIII, 15). For because you do not seek perfectly, therefore you do not find him even when he is present, just as he himself foretold concerning you: \"The wicked will seek me, and will not find me, because they have hated me\" (Joan. VII, 34-36). They will seek him absent, and will not find him even when present, because they hated the light that exposes their evil works, since wisdom cannot be sought \u2014 much less found \u2014 except through love. For Simeon, because he sought with pious and faithful desire, found what he sought, and recognized what he had found without any other indication, that is, without human testimony. For it is the Spirit who testifies that Christ is the truth (I Joan. V, 6) \u2014 namely, the power that proceeds from him, the anointing that teaches concerning all things, which flows forth in many ways from him who, uniquely according to his own name, is the Anointed One.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Quid autem putamus, fratres mei, unctus iste, qui ungit etiam quos non tangit, quomodo candidum et sanctum pectus senis nostri perunxerit, cum suscipiens eum in ulnas suas, astringeret eum uberibus suis, et gestiret includere, si posset, in intimis cordis sui? Quid, inquam, putamus ille suavis et mitis, qualem insinuabat seipsum castissimo sinui pii senis, qualis illabebatur medullis, quam delectabilem et salubrem, imo quam omnino ineffabilem se implicabat ossibus, inspirabat sensibus? Prorsus liquescebat unctus in unctionem, quasi ad ignem resolutus ad amplectentis amorem; ut nimirum doceret unctio quod dicit lectio, sed non discit nisi dilectio. Quomodo enim legis? Unguentum, inquit, exinanitum [Vulg. effusum] nomen tuum (Cantic. I, 2). Hoc siquidem unguentum exinanitur, cum a quadam duritia sui resolvitur, non ut pereffluat substantia illius, sed ut fluant aromata illius, ut quasi nihil habens solidi sentiri possit, teneri non possit. Aut certe magis anima senis liquescebat in amplexu hujus uncti, vel unguenti, ut diceret: Anima mea liquefacta est, ut dilectus meus infusus est mihi. Infusio itaque unctionis, testimonium est uncti. Haec seni testabatur de puero, Simeoni de Christo, tanquam spiritus veritatis et charitatis, veritate docens, charitate ungens et accendens, et omnia interiora gelidi senis ponens, quasi cum unguenta bulliunt. Motus bullientium unguentorum, aestus desideriorum: sonus eorum, jubilus affectuum pariter dicentium (si tamen aliquid exprimere valentium), Deus meus es tu, et confitebor tibi; Deus meus es tu, et exaltabo te (Isa. XXV, 1). Lumen ad revelationem gentium, et gloriam plebis tuae Israel (Luc. II, 32), annuntians te. Confitebor, inquam, tibi, quoniam exaudisti me, quotidie cum lacrymis dicentem, Quando consolaberis me? (Psal. CXVIII, 82.) Exaudisti me, et nunc demum Simeonem, id est, exauditum fecisti me; quia factus es mihi in salutem (Psal. CXVII, 28). Jesus mihi factus ex semine David secundum carnem, qui David et omnia fecisti secundum deitatem, omnibus quidem generaliter factus es in Salvatorem; sed mihi specialiter factus es in salutem, pectus morbidum perungens, vitalemque resuscitans calorem, animam refocillans, et oculos illuminans. Defecerat enim anima mea in salutare tuum, o Domine, Pater Domini mei Jesu Christi, defecerunt oculi mei in eloquium tuum, dicentes: Quando consolaberis me? (Psal. CXVIII, 82.) Factus eram sicut uter in pruina, frigidus corpore, aridus corde, spiritu marcidus, et totus desiderio languidus. Sed quia in verbum tuae promissionis supersperavi, non visurum scilicet me mortem, nisi viderem Christum tuum, quod speravi jam video, quod desideravi teneo, quod concupivi complector. Video Deum Salvatorem meum in carne mea, et salva facta est anima mea. Viderunt oculi mei salutare Dei, et illuminati sunt interiores oculi mei, qui languebant prae inopia. Omnino ad tactum hujus pueri, hujus hominis novi, renovata est ut aquilae juventus mea, sicut paulo ante mihi loquens pollicebar. Introibo ad altare Dei, ubi Maria Jesum offert Patri, ad Deum qui laetificat senectutem meam, imo renovabit juventutem meam (Psal. XLII, 4)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But what do we think, my brothers, as to how this Anointed One\u2014who anoints even those he does not touch\u2014anointed the white and holy breast of our old man, when, taking him up into his arms, he pressed him to his breast and longed, if he could, to enclose him in the innermost depths of his heart? What, I say, do we think\u2014how sweet and gentle he was, in the way he insinuated himself into the most chaste bosom of the devout old man, how he glided into his inmost marrow, how delightful and health-giving\u2014nay, how utterly ineffable\u2014was the way he entwined himself in his bones and breathed himself into his senses? Truly the Anointed was melting into anointing, as though dissolved by fire into the love of the one embracing him; so that the anointing itself might teach what the reading tells, but which only love can learn. For how do you read? 'Your name,' it says, 'is an ointment emptied out [Vulg. poured out]' (Cantic. I, 2). This ointment indeed is emptied out when it is dissolved from a certain hardness of itself, not so that its substance flows entirely away, but so that its fragrant spices may flow forth, so that, having as it were nothing solid, it may be perceptible but not graspable. Or rather, the old man's soul was melting in the embrace of this Anointed One, or of this ointment, so that he might say: 'My soul has melted, as my beloved has been poured into me.' The pouring-in of the anointing, then, is the testimony of the Anointed One. This it testified to the old man concerning the child, to Simeon concerning Christ, as the Spirit of truth and of love, teaching by truth, anointing and kindling by love, and setting all the inward parts of the chilled old man aglow, as it were, when ointments come to a boil. The stirring of boiling ointments is the ardor of desires; their sound is the jubilation of affections speaking together (if indeed anything can be expressed), 'You are my God, and I will give thanks to you; you are my God, and I will exalt you' (Isa. XXV, 1). 'A light for the revelation of the Gentiles, and the glory of your people Israel' (Luc. II, 32), announcing you. I will give thanks to you, I say, because you have heard me, day after day saying with tears, 'When will you console me?' (Psal. CXVIII, 82.). You have heard me, and now at last you have made Simeon\u2014that is, you have made me\u2014one who is heard; because you have become my salvation (Psal. CXVII, 28). Jesus, made for me of the seed of David according to the flesh, you who made David and all things according to your Godhead, have become Savior of all generally; but to me specially you have become salvation, anointing my sick breast, reviving my vital warmth, refreshing my soul, and enlightening my eyes. For my soul had fainted for your salvation, O Lord, Father of my Lord Jesus Christ, my eyes had failed for your word, saying, 'When will you console me?' (Psal. CXVIII, 82.). I had become like a wineskin in the frost, cold in body, dry in heart, withered in spirit, and altogether languishing with desire. But because I had hoped beyond hope in the word of your promise, namely that I should not see death unless I had seen your Christ, what I hoped for I now see, what I desired I now hold, what I longed for I now embrace. I see God my Savior in my flesh, and my soul has been saved. My eyes have seen the salvation of God, and my inward eyes, which were failing for want, have been enlightened. Altogether, at the touch of this child, this new man, my youth has been renewed like the eagle's, just as a little while ago I was promising myself, speaking to myself. 'I will go in to the altar of God, where Mary offers Jesus to the Father, to God who gives joy to my old age\u2014nay, who will renew my youth' (Psal. XLII, 4).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Nec minore fortassis gaudio exsultabat cor Annae in Domino; quae, ut arbitror tanto majoris fuit meriti, quanto altioris propositi et sanctioris desiderii, quam illa Anna mater Samuelis; cum illa cogitaret quae mundi sunt, quomodo placeret viro; haec quomodo Deo. illa de thoro viri quaereret sobolem; haec de sinu Patris peteret Salvatorem. Haec igitur, quodam presagio gratiae excellentis quae in vita ejus effloruit, qua etiam gratiam Redemptoris agnoscere et praedicare meruit, gratiae nomen accepit; exemplar verae viduitatis, speculum sanctitatis, et vivum in emortuo corpore simulacrum totius virtutis. Haec finem Legis et initium Evangelii sentiens propinquare, ritus Judaicos christiana jam coeperat pietate mutare, jejuniis potius et orationibus serviens nocte ac die, quam cibis et potibus et variis baptismatibus, justitiis carnis, non animae. Inde erat quod benedictione filiorum, quae in Lege magni habebatur, contempta, nomen melius a filiis et filiabus elegerat, et jam per annos octoginta quatuor, lignum esse aridum lignoque insidere arido, tanquam turtur amissa semel conjuge, gaudebat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Nor was the heart of Anna perhaps exulting in the Lord with any less joy; who, I think, was of so much the greater merit as she was of a higher purpose and a holier desire than that other Anna, the mother of Samuel; since that Anna thought on the things of the world, how she might please her husband, but this one thought how she might please God; that Anna sought offspring from her husband's bed, but this one sought the Savior from the bosom of the Father. This woman, therefore, by a kind of presaging of the excellent grace which flowered in her life\u2014by which she also deserved to recognize and proclaim the grace of the Redeemer\u2014received the name of grace; an example of true widowhood, a mirror of holiness, and a living image, in a body already as good as dead, of every virtue. Sensing that the end of the Law and the beginning of the Gospel were drawing near, she had already, with Christian devotion, begun to exchange the Jewish rites, serving night and day rather in fastings and prayers than in foods and drinks and various washings\u2014righteousnesses of the flesh, not of the soul. Hence it was that, despising the blessing of children, which was held in great esteem under the Law, she had chosen a better name than sons and daughters, and now, for eighty-four years, rejoiced to be a dry stick and to sit upon a dry stick, like a turtledove that has lost its mate once for all.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Ecce habes par turturum, senem justum, et viduam anum, utrumque castum, utrumque desiderio Redemptoris gemebundum; qui utique semetipsos hostiam vivam, sanctam, Deo placentem, Domino et pro Domino obtulerunt. Par turturum sane dico, non conjugum, sed conjunctorum, non copula matrimonii, sed sacratioris mysterii; pares nimirum fide, aequales castitate, similes devotione, consortes praedicatione gratiae, ambos provectae aetatis, ambos perfectae sanctitatis, qui etiam ante Evangelium, evangelicae puritatis ac pietatis in utroque sexu primitias dedicaverunt. Hi duo vario decore virtutem adornaverunt thalamum tuum, Sion, ad suscipiendum Christum Regem; non parietes templi tui, sed penetralia cordis sui secreta cubiculi, ubi adorare Patrem in spiritu et veritate jubemur (Joan. IV, 23), ubi et suscipitur qui in manufactis se nequaquam habitare testatur (Act. VII, 48). In istis itaque suscepit Christum fidelis Sion, in istis gaudens et laetabunda occurrit Deo suo. Alias vix habet ubi reclinet caput suum, et secundum Isaiam, Aporiatus est, et non est qui occurrat (Isa. LXIX, 16). Sufficit tamen istorum occursus ad omnem solemnitatem et laetitiam, eo quod consummatio abbreviata inundet justitiam, et consummata atque abundans justitia paucorum facile compenset infidelitatem multorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Behold, you have a pair of turtledoves: the righteous old man, and the aged widow, both chaste, both groaning with desire for the Redeemer; who indeed offered themselves as a living sacrifice, holy and pleasing to God, to the Lord and for the Lord. I call them, indeed, a pair of turtledoves \u2014 not of spouses, but of those joined together; not by the bond of matrimony, but by a more sacred mystery; equal, surely, in faith, equal in chastity, alike in devotion, companions in the preaching of grace, both of advanced age, both of perfect holiness, who, even before the Gospel, dedicated in both sexes the firstfruits of evangelical purity and piety. These two, with varied beauty, adorned your bridal chamber, O Zion, with virtue, for the receiving of Christ the King \u2014 not the walls of your temple, but the inner recesses of their hearts, the secret chamber, where we are bidden to worship the Father in spirit and in truth (Joan. IV, 23), where also he is received who testifies that he dwells in no way in things made by hands (Act. VII, 48). In these, then, faithful Zion received Christ; in these, rejoicing and glad, she went out to meet her God. Apart from these, he scarcely has anywhere to lay his head, and, according to Isaiah, 'He was dismayed, and there was none to meet him' (Isa. LXIX, 16). Yet the meeting of these two suffices for all solemnity and gladness, because a shortened consummation overflows with righteousness, and the consummated and abundant righteousness of a few easily compensates for the unfaithfulness of many.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Mihi omnino celebris est iste conventus, mihi solemnis ista processio, omnique festiva gaudio, ubi hinc advenit puer et puerpera, Jesus et Maria; inde occurrit senex et vidua, Simeon et Anna; hinc Dominus et Domina, inde servus et ancilla; hinc Mediator et Mediatrix, inde tam fideles tamque devoti ipsorum testes et ministri. In hoc denique conventu misericordia et veritas obviaverunt sibi, misericordia scilicet redemptionis in Jesu, veritas confessionis in senibus. In hoc occursu, justitia et pax osculatae sunt; dum justitia piorum senum, et pax reconciliantis mundum invicem in osculo affectuum et gaudio Spiritus consociatae sunt. Recte ergo quod hac nocte cantavimus, ad laetitiam hujus diei pertinere credimus, salvo duntaxat alio profundiore intellectu: Laetare, Jerusalem; et diem festum agite, omnes qui diligitis eam (Isai. LXVI, 10). Ac si diceretur: Laetata est Nazareth de annuntiatione, Bethlehem de nativitate: laetare et tu, Jerusalem, de purificatione, quoniam qui in Nazareth conceptus, et in Bethlehem natus est, in Jerusalem est susceptus et praedicatus. Et diem, inquit, festum agite, sive conventum facite, omnes qui diligitis eam, scilicet Jerusalem, id est, Festiva devotione convenite in templum, ad suscipiendum Redemptorem, omnes qui exspectatis redemptionem Israel."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "7. To me this gathering is altogether festive, to me this procession is solemn and full of festive joy, where on this side comes the child and the woman who bore him, Jesus and Mary; on that side comes to meet them the old man and the widow, Simeon and Anna; here the Lord and the Lady, there the servant and the handmaid; here the Mediator and the Mediatrix, there such faithful and devoted witnesses and ministers of them. In this gathering, finally, mercy and truth have met one another \u2014 namely, the mercy of redemption in Jesus, the truth of confession in the elders. In this meeting, righteousness and peace have kissed, while the righteousness of the devout elders and the peace of him who reconciles the world have been joined together in the kiss of affections and the joy of the Spirit. Rightly, then, do we believe that what we sang this night pertains to the joy of this day \u2014 without prejudice to another, deeper interpretation: 'Rejoice, Jerusalem, and keep festival, all you who love her' (Isai. LXVI, 10). As if it were said: Nazareth rejoiced at the annunciation, Bethlehem at the nativity; rejoice also, you, Jerusalem, at the purification, since he who was conceived in Nazareth and born in Bethlehem is received and proclaimed in Jerusalem. 'And keep festival,' it says, or 'make assembly, all you who love her' \u2014 namely, Jerusalem \u2014 that is: Gather with festive devotion into the temple, to receive the Redeemer, all you who await the redemption of Israel.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Jam vero, fratres, si moraliter, sicut mos vester est, aedificari vestra quaerit Sanctitas, quatuor illas illustres in hac processione consideret personas; quarum vita, non solum ecclesias illustrat, sed et coelos adornat: Jesum loquor et Mariam, Simeonem et Annam. Et, ut ab inferioribus ad superiora progrediatur sermo, in Anna, quae jejuniis et obsecrationibus serviebat nocte ac die, jejunium nobis commendatur et oratio; in Simeone, qui tanto Jesum amplectitur gaudio, pietas et devotio; in Maria, quae cum nihil deberet legi, in hac scilicet parte purificationis legem nihilominus implevit, humilitas et obedientia; in Domino autem Jesu, qui factus ex muliere, factus est etiam sub lege, ut eos qui sub lege erant redimeret, charitas et misericordia. Nam castitatis sanctimonia, cujus laus, ut comperi, vestram semper puritatem delectat, in omnibus effulsit communiter, licet satis dissimiliter; cum in senibus fuerit labore continentiae; in puella, munere gratiae; in puero, jure et proprietate naturae, non pueritiae recens natae, sed puritatis innatae. In ipso non solum hujus virtutis, sed etiam omnium origo et perfectio; ab ipso singularum distributio; per ipsum earum conservatio; apud ipsum remuneratio. Apud ipsum igitur ambiamus, tam virginis Mariae, quam Simeonis et Annae meritis suffragantibus, ut quod minus habemus virtutis largiatur, quod largitus fuerit tueatur; ut depositum ipsius, per tutelam ipsius integrum ei reconsignantes, remunerationem consequamur ipsius, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula seculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Now indeed, brothers, if your Holiness seeks to be built up morally, as is your custom, let it consider those four illustrious persons in this procession, whose life not only illumines churches but also adorns the heavens: I mean Jesus and Mary, Simeon and Anna. And, that the sermon may proceed from lower things to higher, in Anna, who served night and day in fastings and prayers, fasting and prayer are commended to us; in Simeon, who embraces Jesus with such great joy, piety and devotion; in Mary, who, though she owed nothing to the Law, nevertheless in this matter of the purification fulfilled the Law all the same, humility and obedience; and in the Lord Jesus, who, made of a woman, was also made under the Law, that he might redeem those who were under the Law, charity and mercy. For the holiness of chastity \u2014 the praise of which, as I have learned, always delights your own purity \u2014 shone forth in all of them alike, though in quite dissimilar ways: in the elders it was through the labor of continence; in the maiden, through the gift of grace; in the child, by the right and property of nature \u2014 not of a childhood recently born, but of purity that is innate. In him is the origin and perfection not only of this virtue but of all virtues; from him comes the distribution of each; through him their preservation; with him their reward. Let us therefore petition him, with the merits of the Virgin Mary, and of Simeon and Anna, interceding for us, that he may grant what virtue we lack, and may guard what he has granted; so that, handing back to him intact, under his own protection, his deposit entrusted to us, we may obtain his reward \u2014 who lives and reigns through all the ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints par Malachiam, evidently a misprint for per Malachiam. The printed form is kept."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition numbers two consecutive sections 7 (Mihi omnino and Jam vero) and prints no section 6. The printed numbers are kept."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Purification Sermon II: With What Love We Should Go Out to Meet the Coming Jesus", "topics": ["Purification", "Simeon and Anna", "meeting Christ", "spiritual anointing", "joy"], "url": "sermons/guerric-purification-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:candlemas", "calendar_label": "The Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-purification-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis, sermo III, Quomodo Simeon imitandus, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 71-75.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Purification", "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Isaiah 65:20", "Ecclesiastes 11:8", "Wisdom 4:8\u20139", "Proverbs 16:31", "Psalm 5:8 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 47:10 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 75:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 88:15 (historical numbering)", "Luke 2:25", "Psalm 124:1\u20132 (historical numbering)", "Luke 2:29", "Psalm 4:9 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 57:2", "1 Corinthians 4:5", "1 Corinthians 11:31\u201332", "1 Corinthians 4:4", "Sirach 1:28", "Malachi 3:1", "Isaiah 52:1", "Psalm 47:10\u201311 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis", "occasion_detail": "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Purification", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Simeon carries the child who is the Ancient of Days. Guerric considers the virtues of old age, the reception of the Word in hearing and prayer, and the fear of God that accompanies justice. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quomodo Simeon imitandus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "How Simeon Is to Be Imitated", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Senex puerum portabat, senex plenus dierum, puerum antiquum dierum. Longitudine dierum adimpleverat Deus senem istum, ut ostenderet ei salutare suum in vultu nostro; sed adhuc aliorum longitudine dierum adimpleturus erat, ut ostenderet ei salutare suum in vultu suo. Plenus ergo dierum temporalium portabat antiquum dierum antiquorum, atque spei aeternae pignus fidelissimum tenebat in sinu repositum; quod videlicet dies suos super dies regis adjiceret; puer ille, juventutemque ejus renovaret ut aquilae. Quanquam senem quoque istum, sicut Abraham, cum dicitur plenus dierum, libentius accipiam plenum virtutum quam temporum: cum tempora statum nullum habeant, suoque accessu decedentia, hominem potius exhauriant quam adimpleant. Sed neque magnum est, imo magis malum est, multis vixisse diebus, si non senueris virtutibus; cum puer centum annorum moriturus sit, et peccator centum annorum maledicendus (Isai. LXV, 20). Ideo, Si annis multis vixerit homo, ut ait Salomon, et in omnibus laetatus fuerit, meminisse debet tenebrosi temporis et dierum multorum, qui cum venerint, vanitatis arguentur praeterita (Eccle. XI, 8). Caeterum e contrario, Senectus venerabilis est, non diuturna, neque numero annorum computata. Cani sunt enim sensus hominis, et aetas senectutis vita immaculata (Sap. IV, 8, 9). Si hac utique senectute Simeon non consenuisset, nunquam coronam senum, sapientiam Dei Christum portare meruisset. Sic namque scriptum est: Corona dignitatis senectus, quae in viis justitiae reperitur (Prov. XVI, 31). Et quae est illa senectus, quae est in viis justitiae reperitur, quae reperienti in coronam aptatur, nisi sapientia Dei Christus? ipse namque merito justorum operum tribuit canos sensus sapientiae, quam postmodum formabit justis in coronam gloriae. Sanctus itaque senex Simeon, ut sanctius senesceret adhuc, senectutem sapientium, sive sapientiam senum factam puerum, in viis justitiae, cum scilicet in spiritu venit in templum, reperit, et comperit in veritate; quia senectus erat in lactente, sapientia in infante, virtus in infirmitate, Verbum in carne. O infantia, o senectus! quam bene vobis pariter convenit in hujus pueri sensibus et moribus; quo utique nihil innocentius, sed nihil sapientius, nihil jucundius, sed nihil maturius, nihil mitius, sed nihil justius!"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. An old man was carrying a boy\u2014an old man full of days, carrying the Boy who was ancient of days. God had fulfilled this old man with length of days, that He might show him His salvation in our face; but He was still going to fulfill him with the length of other days, that He might show him His salvation in His own face. Full, then, of temporal days, he was carrying the Ancient of ancient days, and he held, laid up in his bosom, the most faithful pledge of eternal hope: namely, that that Boy would add His days above the days of the king, and renew his youth like the eagle's. Although, when it is said of this old man too, as of Abraham, that he was \"full of days,\" I would rather understand it as full of virtues than of time; since times have no permanence, but by their very passing rather drain a man than fill him up. Nor indeed is it a great thing\u2014rather it is a worse thing\u2014to have lived many days, if you have not grown old in virtues; since the boy of a hundred years shall die, and the sinner of a hundred years shall be accursed (Isai. LXV, 20). Therefore, \"If a man has lived many years,\" as Solomon says, \"and has rejoiced in them all, he ought to remember the dark time and the many days, which when they come, the past will be proved vain\" (Eccle. XI, 8). But on the other hand, \"Venerable old age is not that of long duration, nor counted by the number of years. For the understanding of a man is gray hairs, and a spotless life is the age of old age\" (Sap. IV, 8, 9). If Simeon had not grown old with this very old age, he would never have deserved to carry the crown of old men, the Wisdom of God, Christ. For thus it is written: \"Old age is a crown of dignity, when it is found in the ways of justice\" (Prov. XVI, 31). And what is that old age which is found in the ways of justice, which fits the one who finds it as a crown, except the Wisdom of God, Christ? For He Himself, in reward for the deeds of the just, bestows the gray hairs of wisdom, which afterward He will fashion for the just into a crown of glory. And so holy old Simeon, that he might grow old yet more holily, found and truly discovered, in the ways of justice\u2014that is, when he came in the Spirit into the temple\u2014the old age of the wise, or the wisdom of old men become a child; because old age was in the nursing infant, wisdom in the babe, strength in weakness, the Word in flesh. O infancy, O old age! how well you agree together in the senses and manners of this Boy, than whom indeed nothing is more innocent, yet nothing more wise; nothing more delightful, yet nothing more mature; nothing more gentle, yet nothing more just!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Vobis, o filii hominum, vobis jam tunc ipso suo silentio loquebatur Dei Sermo, ut videlicet malitia sitis parvuli, sensibus autem perfecti. Alii siquidem ex vobis columba seducta non habens cor, simplicitatem non salientes prudentia, relinquuntur fatui; alii sapientes sunt ut faciant mala, bene autem facere nesciunt, et, prudentiam non dulcorantes bonitate, efficiuntur acerbi. Prorsus congruum et gratum utrisque temperamentum atque condimentum puer iste antiquus dierum, in pueritia praeferens ingenitam sibi innocentiam, et de antiquitate dierum referens primogenitam omnium sapientiam. Si quis se imperfectum sensibus, aut inconditum intelligit moribus, veniat in templum cum Simeone, et puerum quem mater affert Maria, accipiat in manibus, id est Verbum Dei quod mater offert Ecclesia, complectatur affectibus Ipsum utique repositum in sinu sensus aedificabit, mores dulcorabit, totum statum mentis et ordinem vitae grata et salubri suavitate temperabit. Non solum autem mater Ecclesia in audiendo, sed multo magis mater gratia in orando, puerum tibi dabit amplectendum, si modo frequens et devotus orator venias in templum, ut dicas quotidie Deo: Adorabo ad templum sanctum tuum in timore tuo (Psal. V, 8). Quem enim Ecclesia praedicans offert auribus, gratia illuminans infert cordibus, eo utique praesentiorem et suaviorem, quo nudam veritatem puris tradidit sensibus. Veritatem quippe, quae est Christus, Maria vestitam carne, Ecclesia vestitam sermone, gratia nudam tradit amplectendam Spiritus infusione: quanquam hoc ipsum diverso modo fiat, pro captu scilicet suscipientis animae, sive pro arbitratu dispensantis misericordiae. Licet enim ipsam summae veritatis faciem non facile videamus, magnusque habeatur, cui vel per speculum et in aenigmate eam intueri datur: quiddam tamen ipsius, cum Spiritus nobis illabitur, veluti nudum sentimus, sensusque noster in amorem ipsius veluti ad nudum confricatur. Tunc prorsus ex sententia dicere possumus, et cantare laetabundi: Suscepimus, Deus, misericordiam tuam, in medio templi tui (Psal. XLVII, 10). Beatus qui ut dignius et crebrius eam suscipiat, congruum ei locum in cordis penetralibus parat. Beata Sion illa, quae thalamum suum diligenter ac decenter adornat, ut cum digno ac placito Christum regem honore suscipiat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. To you, O sons of men, even then by His very silence the Word of God was speaking, namely, that you should be children in malice, but mature in understanding. For some of you, like a dove that has been deceived, having no heart, not tempering simplicity with prudence, are left foolish; others are wise to do evil, but know not how to do good, and, not sweetening prudence with goodness, become harsh. A thoroughly fitting and pleasing blend and seasoning for both is this Boy who is ancient of days, who in His childhood displays the innocence native to Him, and from the antiquity of His days brings forth the wisdom that is firstborn of all things. If anyone perceives himself imperfect in understanding, or unformed in manners, let him come into the temple with Simeon, and let him receive into his hands the Boy whom His mother Mary brings\u2014that is, the Word of God which the Church, His mother, offers\u2014and let him embrace Him in his affections. He, once laid up in the bosom of the mind, will build up the understanding, sweeten the manners, and temper the whole condition of the soul and order of life with a pleasing and wholesome sweetness. But not only will Mother Church give you the Boy to embrace in hearing, but much more will Mother Grace give Him to you in prayer, if only you come as a frequent and devout worshiper into the temple, so as to say daily to God: \"I will worship toward thy holy temple in fear of thee\" (Psal. V, 8). For the One whom the Church, preaching, offers to the ears, grace, illuminating, brings into the hearts\u2014all the more present and sweet, the more it has delivered the naked truth to pure senses. For the Truth, which is Christ, Mary delivers clothed in flesh, the Church delivers clothed in speech, but grace delivers Him naked to be embraced, by the infusion of the Spirit: although this itself happens in different ways, according to the capacity of the soul that receives, or according to the judgment of the mercy that dispenses it. For although we do not easily see the very face of the highest Truth, and he is accounted great to whom it is given even to behold it through a mirror and in an enigma; yet something of it, when the Spirit flows into us, we feel as it were bare, and our spiritual sense is, as it were, rubbed bare so as to love Him. Then indeed we can say with full conviction, and sing joyfully: \"We have received thy mercy, O God, in the midst of thy temple\" (Psal. XLVII, 10). Blessed is he who, that he may receive it more worthily and more often, prepares a fitting place for it in the inner chambers of his heart. Blessed is that Sion which diligently and fittingly adorns her bridal chamber, that she may receive Christ the King with due and pleasing honor.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. O sancta Sion, anima speculans aeterna, considera quae sit illa majestas suscipienda; cogita qua cura ac diligentia te illi praeparare locum oporteat. Si quis dixerit: Et ad haec quis idoneus? quis esse potest magnificentiae et gloriae tanti Regis congruus ornatus? Frustra, inquit David, paupertatem quispiam causetur: In pace factus est locus ejus (Psal. LXXV, 3); et: Justitia et judicium praeparatio sedis ejus (Psal. LXXXVIII, 15). Simeon utique quia hujusmodi palliis thalamum adornavit, ideo Christum cum multa suscipere gratia dignus fuit. Vis videre quia in pace invenerat locum Domino, quia sedem praeparaverat ei in justitia et judicio? Homo, inquit, erat in Jerusalem, cui nomen Simeon; et homo iste justus et timoratus (Luc. II, 25). In pace erat domus Simeonis, qui habitabat in Jerusalem, id est, in visione et amore pacis; de qua scilicet Jerusalem audierat David promittentem: Non commovebitur in aeternum qui habitat in Jerusalem (Psal. CXXIV, 1, 2). Et, ut noveris quia pacis habitator et amator erat, pacemque imperturbabilem illius stabilis et aeternae Jerusalem prae omnibus requirebat; cum pacem dei et hominum Jesum, qui pacem super pacem dare veniebat, sinu recepit, haec prima vox de pectore senis jamdudum pacem meditantis erupit: Nunc dimittis servum tuum, Domine, secundum verbum tuum, in pace (Luc. II, 29). Postquam viderunt oculi mei salutare tuum, qui est pax nostra faciens utraque unum, non solum Judaeum et Gentilem, sed etiam Deum et hominem, et in homine spiritum et carnem: jam hoc solum superest desideriis servi tui, ut in pace in idipsum dormiam et requiescam (Psal. IV, 9); et pax Dei quae exsuperat omnem sensum, me totum in illius summae et simplicis unitatis absorbeat consensum. Venit pax quam sustinui: requiescat in pace qui pacem quaesivit, et quae pacis sunt sectari semper studuit. Sic quippe Isaias ait: Veniat pax, requiescat in cubili suo, quia ambulavit in directione sua (Isai. LVII, 2). In pace utique Simeon straverat cubile suum, qui omnibus aliis curis ejectis solum in corde meditabatur Jesum, ubi et reclinavit susceptum. In directione quoque sua ambulaverat, qui justus et timoratus erat, exspectans consolationem Israel, adventanti quotidie desideriis occurrebat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. O holy Zion, soul that gazes on things eternal, consider what majesty it is you must receive; think with what care and diligence you must prepare a place for him. If someone should say: \"And who is fit for this? What adornment can be worthy of the magnificence and glory of so great a King?\" \u2014 in vain, says David, would anyone plead poverty as an excuse: \"His place is made in peace\" (Psal. LXXV, 3); and: \"Justice and judgment are the preparation of his throne\" (Psal. LXXXVIII, 15). Simeon indeed, because he adorned his chamber with coverings of this kind, was therefore worthy to receive Christ with great grace. Do you wish to see that he had found a place for the Lord in peace, that he had prepared a seat for him in justice and judgment? \"There was,\" it says, \"a man in Jerusalem whose name was Simeon; and this man was just and God-fearing\" (Luc. II, 25). Simeon's house was in peace, for he dwelt in Jerusalem, that is, in the vision and love of peace; concerning which Jerusalem he had heard David promising: \"He who dwells in Jerusalem shall never be moved\" (Psal. CXXIV, 1, 2). And, that you may know he was a dweller in and lover of peace, and sought above all things the unshakeable peace of that stable and eternal Jerusalem: when he received into his bosom Jesus, the peace of God and of men, who came to give peace upon peace, this first word burst forth from the breast of the old man who had long been meditating on peace: \"Now, Lord, you dismiss your servant, according to your word, in peace\" (Luc. II, 29). After my eyes have seen your salvation, who is our peace, making both one \u2014 not only Jew and Gentile, but also God and man, and in man spirit and flesh \u2014 now this alone remains for the desires of your servant, that \"in peace I may sleep and rest in the very same\" (Psal. IV, 9); and that the peace of God which surpasses all understanding may absorb me wholly into agreement with that highest and simple unity. The peace I have awaited has come: let him rest in peace who sought peace, and who always strove to pursue what belongs to peace. For thus indeed Isaiah says: \"Let peace come, let him rest in his bed, because he walked in his straight way\" (Isai. LVII, 2). Simeon indeed had made his bed in peace, since, casting out all other cares, he meditated on Jesus alone in his heart, and there also he laid him down once received. He had likewise walked in his straight way, he who was just and God-fearing, awaiting the consolation of Israel, meeting its coming each day with his desires.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. In eo igitur quod justus dicitur et timoratus, justitiam et judicium intellige, quo sedes Dei praeparatur. Et justitia quidem suo nomine demonstratur: judicium vero timoris nomine, vel timor nomine judicii ideo recte accipi posse putatur, quia scilicet timor non solum nascitur de futuri consideratione judicii, sed etiam operatur nunc in nobis quamdam illius formam et exsecutionem judicii. Qui enim prohibemur judicare ante tempus de proximis (I Cor. IV, 5), jubemur de nobis ut, dum nosmetipsos dijudicamus, a Domino non judicemur (I Cor. XI, 31, 32). Porro timor iste sanctior est, cum justitiam sequitur, quam cum praecedit: quia qui praecedens ad justitiam initiat, sequens eadem consummat et conservat; dum scilicet de justitia praesumere non sinit, sed omnia opera sua hominem vereri facit, ut post judicium sui adhuc de subtiliore judicio suspectus sit, dicatque sibi: Qui judicat me Dominus est (I Cor. IV, 4), qui cum acceperit tempus, etiam justitias judicabit. Qui sine timore isto est, non poterit justificari (Eccli. I, 28), ut Scriptura testatur; quia quantumcunque se justis exercitet operibus, alieni comedunt robur ejus, dum suam volens justitiam constituere, justitiae Dei non est subjectus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. In the fact, then, that he is called just and God-fearing, understand justice and judgment, by which the seat of God is prepared. Justice indeed is shown by its own name; but judgment, it is thought, may rightly be understood under the name of fear, or fear under the name of judgment, because fear not only arises from the consideration of the judgment to come, but also now works in us a certain form and execution of that judgment. For we who are forbidden to judge our neighbors before the time (I Cor. IV, 5) are commanded, as regards ourselves, that while we judge ourselves we may not be judged by the Lord (I Cor. XI, 31, 32). Moreover, this fear is holier when it follows justice than when it precedes it: for the fear that precedes initiates one into justice, while the fear that follows completes and preserves it \u2014 namely, by not allowing a man to presume upon his justice, but making him regard all his own works with fear, so that even after judging himself he remains apprehensive of a more searching judgment, and says to himself: \"He who judges me is the Lord\" (I Cor. IV, 4), who, when he has taken the appointed time, will judge even acts of justice. He who is without this fear cannot be justified (Eccli. I, 28), as Scripture testifies; for however much he exercises himself in just works, strangers devour his strength, since, wishing to establish his own justice, he is not subject to the justice of God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Vigilanter itaque Evangelium perfectam commendans Simeonis justitiam, non ait: Timoratus et justus, ne timorem, qui est initium justitiae, intelligeremus: sed ait, justus et timoratus, ut timorem, qui justitiae per omnes gradus comes individuus ipsam promovet et tuetur, ipsius exemplo disceremus. Nam neque illud putaverim otiosum, quod non timentem dixit, sed timoratum: non novum, non horarium, aut perfunctorium volens intelligi timorem illum; sed qui utique versus esset in habitum, qui altius hominis imbibisset affectum, possideret sensum, modestia et gravitate sermonem ornaret et vultum, circumspectione moderaretur actum, totum denique interioris et exterioris hominis componeret statum. Neque enim vanis animum consolationibus resolvebat, qui consolationem Israel exspectabat. Quod et ipsum ad suae cumulum adjecerat justitiae, ut sollicitus non pro sua tantum, sed etiam pro populi consolatione, longa suspensus maneret exspectatione."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Watchfully, then, the Gospel, commending Simeon's perfect justice, does not say \"fearing and just,\" lest we should understand thereby the fear that is the beginning of justice; but says \"just and fearing,\" so that by his example we might learn of that fear which, as an inseparable companion of justice through all its degrees, advances and guards it. Nor would I think it without purpose that it said not \"fearing\" but \"God-fearing\": not wishing that fear to be understood as something new, momentary, or superficial; but one that had truly become a settled habit, that had penetrated more deeply into the man's affections, possessed his mind, adorned his speech and countenance with modesty and gravity, moderated his action with circumspection, and finally composed the whole state of the inner and outer man. For he who awaited the consolation of Israel did not relax his mind with vain consolations. And this very thing he had added to the sum of his justice: that, anxious not only for his own consolation but also for that of the people, he remained in suspense through a long expectation.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Denique, ut manifestius audias quam pretioso ac vero nitore virtutum, non fuco colorum adornaverat thalamum suum illa Sion, id est Simeon, Spiritus sanctus, inquit, erat in eo. Quid huic ornatui Sion illa terrena comparare poterat, in omni illa multifaria thalami, id est templi sui, ambitione et gloria? Si faciem templi coronis ornasset aureis, quid aurum nisi lutum etiam sine comparatione virtutis? Puto quia nec Salomon in omni gloria sua ornatus est sicut Simeon: cum ille sapientiam, qua florebat, in senectute perdiderit, iste susceperit. Te tamen, o infidelis Sion, thalamus tuus arguit, quod cum satis tibi Praedictum esset: Ecce veniet ad templum suum Dominator quem vos quaeritis (Malach. III, 1); adorna thalamum tuum, Sion, induere vestimentis gloriae tuae, civitas Sancti (Isai. LII, 1): tu tamen nihil prorsus ad quotidianum cultum templi in adventu ipsius addidisti, non vela, non coronas, non lucernas plures, aut victimas, non alios psalmos, aut cantica; omnino inhonoratum, insalutatum remittis, qui te salvare venerat. Ideo utique tuus iste thalamus, domus gloriae tuae deserta tibi relinquetur; imo non relinquetur lapis super lapidem, qui non destruatur. Tibi enim, Domine, templum augustius, amplius et perfectius, scilicet Ecclesiam magnam a solis ortu usque ad occasum fides aedificabit gentium, et secundum nomen tuum, Deus, ita et laus tua in fines terrae; unde etiam hodie laudes audivimus, gloriam justi, voces scilicet laudantium et dicentium: Suscepimus, Deus, misericordiam tuam in medio templi tui (Psal. XLVII, 11, 10), id est, in communione sancti populi tui. O fratres, suscepistis misericordiam Dei et vos, imo abundantius vos: videte ne in vacuum susceperitis, ne gratiam ingratitudine vacuetis. Gaudeat ergo vestra devotio: quem Simeon hodie portavit in sinu suo, vos omni die glorificate et portate in corpore vestro Jesum Christum Dominum nostrum, cui honor et gloria in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Finally, that you may hear more clearly with how precious and true a splendor of virtues\u2014not with the paint of colors\u2014that Sion, that is, Simeon, had adorned his bridal chamber, the Gospel says: The Holy Spirit was in him. What could that earthly Sion compare to this adornment, in all the manifold pomp and glory of her bridal chamber, that is, of her temple? If she had crowned the face of her temple with golden crowns, what is gold but mud, even without comparison to virtue? I think that not even Solomon in all his glory was adorned as Simeon was: since Solomon lost in old age the wisdom in which he had flourished, while Simeon received it. Yet your own bridal chamber convicts you, O unfaithful Sion, since it had been foretold to you plainly enough: \"Behold, the Lord whom you seek shall come to his temple\" (Malach. III, 1); \"Adorn your bridal chamber, O Sion; put on the garments of your glory, O city of the Holy One\" (Isai. LII, 1): yet you added absolutely nothing to the daily service of the temple at his coming\u2014no veils, no crowns, no additional lamps or victims, no other psalms or songs; you sent away wholly unhonored and unsaluted the one who had come to save you. Therefore that very bridal chamber of yours, the house of your glory, will be left deserted to you; indeed, not one stone upon another will be left that will not be thrown down. For you, O Lord, a more august, more ample, and more perfect temple\u2014namely, the great Church\u2014the faith of the nations will build, from the rising of the sun to its setting, and according to your name, O God, so also your praise unto the ends of the earth; whence also today we have heard praises, the glory of the just one, namely, the voices of those praising and saying: \"We have received, O God, your mercy in the midst of your temple\" (Psal. XLVII, 11, 10), that is, in the communion of your holy people. O brothers, you too have received the mercy of God, indeed more abundantly; see that you have not received it in vain, lest you empty grace through ingratitude. Let your devotion therefore rejoice: him whom Simeon today carried in his bosom, do you every day glorify and carry in your body\u2014Jesus Christ our Lord, to whom be honor and glory for ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Purification Sermon III: How Simeon Is to Be Imitated", "topics": ["Purification", "Simeon", "justice and fear of God", "peace", "wisdom of the aged"], "url": "sermons/guerric-purification-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:candlemas", "calendar_label": "The Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-purification-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis, sermo IV, De quatuor purificationis nostrae generibus, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 75-79.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Purification", "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Luke 2:21", "Matthew 3:15", "Malachi 3:4\u20135", "Psalm 17:19 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 6:5\u20137", "Luke 12:49", "Luke 1:35", "Psalm 6:2 (historical numbering)", "1 John 5:6, 8", "Psalm 65:12 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 15:27", "Daniel 4:24", "Luke 11:41", "Acts 15:9", "Mark 10:52", "Proverbs 16:6", "Proverbs 20:6"], "occasion": "In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis", "occasion_detail": "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Purification", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Mary's purification introduces an account of contrition, bodily penance, works of mercy and faith, and patience in tribulation. Guerric closes by asking how faithfulness and mercy govern the use of goods shared with the poor. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De quatuor purificationis nostrae generibus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the Four Kinds of Our Purification", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Postquam impleti sunt dies purgationis Mariae (Luc. II, 21). Scriptura redemptionis nostrae narrans mysteria, sic refert quae pro nobis historialiter sunt gesta, ut significet quae moraliter a nobis sunt gerenda. Cum enim hodie beatae Purificatio Mariae recensetur, manifeste de nostra ipsorum purificatione admonemur. Quem vero tanti non moveat auctoritas exempli, cum videamus scilicet, quia illa sanctarum sanctissima, quae nihil purgandum habuit, purgationis tamen legalis implere mandatum non renuit? O mater immaculata, mater intacta, nonne tuae tibi conscia es puritatis; quia scilicet integritatem tuam nec conceptus, nec partus violavit, sed sacravit? Quare ergo, tanquam muliebre aliquid in concipiendo vel pariendo sis passa, sic mundationis quae muliebri provisa est infirmitati, quaeris remedia? Sic, inquit, decet nos implere omnem justitiam (Matth. III, 15), ut quae summae mater electa sum justitiae, speculum quoque sim totius et exemplar justitiae. Novi ego superbiam filiorum Evae, quae promptior est ad excusandum, quam ad expurgandum commissa. Necessarium arbitror, ut vitiis originis antiquae statim in initiis omnia novae generationis occurrant exempla. Mater praevaricationis peccavit et excusavit procaciter: mater redemptionis non peccet, et satisfaciat humiliter; ut filii hominum qui de matre vetustatis traducunt necessitatem peccandi, de matre saltem novitatis trahant humilitatem purgandi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"After the days of Mary's purification were fulfilled\" (Luc. II, 21). Scripture, in narrating the mysteries of our redemption, relates the things that were historically done for us in such a way as to signify what must morally be done by us. For when today the Purification of the Blessed Mary is commemorated, we are plainly admonished concerning our own purification. For who would not be moved by the authority of so great an example, seeing that she, the holiest of holy women, who had nothing needing to be purged, nevertheless did not refuse to fulfill the mandate of the legal purification? O immaculate Mother, O inviolate Mother, are you not conscious of your own purity\u2014since neither conception nor childbirth violated your integrity, but rather consecrated it? Why then do you seek the remedies of that cleansing which was provided for feminine infirmity, as though you had suffered something womanly in conceiving or giving birth? \"Thus,\" she says, \"it becomes us to fulfill all justice\" (Matth. III, 15), so that I, who have been chosen as the mother of the highest Justice, may also be the mirror and exemplar of all justice. I know the pride of the children of Eve, which is readier to make excuses than to purge away what has been committed. I judge it necessary that, right from the beginning, all the examples of the new generation should at once confront the vices of the ancient origin. The mother of transgression sinned and insolently made excuses: let the mother of redemption not sin, and let her humbly make satisfaction; so that the sons of men, who inherit from the mother of the old order the necessity of sinning, may at least draw from the mother of the new order the humility of being purged.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. O filii hominum, tempus purgationis advenit, quando Mater summae puritatis, de cujus purgatione dies festus est nobis, pariter et fontem edidit, et exemplum dedit nobis quod debeamus purgari. Satius est, o fratres, et suavius fonte purgari quam igne. Prorsus qui fonte non fuerint modo purgati, igne habent purgari, si tamen purgari meruerint: quando scilicet Judex ipse, quasi ignis conflans, sedebit conflans et emundans argentum, et purgabit filios Levi (Malach. III, 4, 5). Nunc Christus aqua diluens; tunc ignis consumens. Modo fons patens in ablutionem peccatoris et menstruatae; tunc flamma saeviens et ignis vorans usque ad medullas animae. De ardore siquidem irati fontis ille purgatorius ignis exardescet in conspectu ejus, et inflammabit in circuitu inimicos ejus. Ascendit, inquit, fumus in ira ejus, et ignis a facie ejus exarsit (Psal. XVII, 19). Inde cadent super eos carbones poenaliter purgatorii, quibus modo non adhibentur carbones salubriter desolatorii: qualem illum fuisse reor carbonem sublatum de altari, qui prophetae labia purgavit (Isai. VI, 5-7). An vero et illi carbones hujusmodi queant intelligi, de quibus cuidam dicitur: Ecce dati sunt tibi carbones, et sedebis super eos; hi erunt tibi in adjutorium: vobis relinquo dijudicandum. Illud autem non dubius assero, quia ignis ille, quem Dominus Jesus misit in terram (Luc. XII, 49), si vehementer (sicut est voluntas mittentis) in nobis arserit, ignis ille purgatorius qui in judicio purgabit filios Levi, non ligna, non fenum, non stipulam consumenda reperiet in nobis. Uterque quidem ignis purgatorius, sed satis differenti modo. Iste siquidem purificat ungendo, ille urendo. Hic refrigerium roris, ibi spiritus judicii et spiritus ardoris, quo abluet Dominus sordes filiarum Sion, et sanguinem Jerusalem lavabit de medio ejus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. O sons of men, the time of purification has come, when the Mother of the highest purity\u2014whose purification is for us a feast day\u2014has both brought forth the fountain and given us the example that we ought to be purified. It is better, brothers, and sweeter, to be purified by the fountain than by fire. Indeed, those who are not purified now by the fountain will have to be purified by fire, if indeed they are found worthy to be purified at all: namely when the Judge himself, like a refining fire, shall sit refining and cleansing silver, and shall purge the sons of Levi (Malach. III, 4, 5). Now Christ washes with water; then he will consume with fire. Now the fountain stands open for the washing of the sinner and the menstruous woman; then the raging flame and the devouring fire will reach to the very marrow of the soul. For indeed, from the heat of his wrathful fountain that purgatorial fire will blaze forth in his sight, and will inflame his enemies round about. \"Smoke went up,\" he says, \"in his wrath, and fire was kindled from his face\" (Psal. XVII, 19). From it the coals of punitive purgation shall fall upon those to whom the healthfully desolating coals are not now applied\u2014such a coal, I think, as that one taken from the altar, which purged the lips of the prophet (Isai. VI, 5-7). Or indeed, might those coals also be understood to be of this kind, of which it is said to a certain one: \"Behold, coals have been given to you, and you shall sit upon them; these shall be for your help\"\u2014I leave it to you to judge. But this I assert without doubt: that the fire which the Lord Jesus sent upon the earth (Luc. XII, 49), if it burns fiercely in us (as is the will of the one who sends it), that purgatorial fire which in judgment will purge the sons of Levi will find in us no wood, no hay, no stubble to be consumed. Both fires are indeed purgatorial, but in a quite different manner. For the one purifies by anointing, the other by burning. In the one there is the refreshment of dew; in the other, the spirit of judgment and the spirit of burning, by which the Lord will wash away the filth of the daughters of Sion, and shall wash the blood of Jerusalem from the midst of her.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Est quidem et in hoc ipso multa Domini clementia erga filias Sion: sed longe clementius et mitius purificata est illa, quam beatam praedicant filiae Sion, cui scilicet dictum est: Spiritus sanctus superveniet in te, et virtus Altissimi obumbrabit tibi. Haec nempe supernae virtutis obumbratio, vera fuit purificatio Mariae; non ista, quae quadam mystica dispensatione tantum specie tenus hodie celebrata est. Haec plane fuit tota ac vera sanctificatio Matris et Filii, sicut et angelus exposuit ei. Ideo, inquit, et quod nascetur ex te Sanctum, vocabitur Filius Dei (Luc. I, 35). Antea namque purificanda erat natura mortalis, ut Deum conciperet, non posteaquam concepisset: cum concepisse Sanctum sanctorum summa sanctificatio sit, et ea quae Mater ipsius sanctitatis effecta sit, nihil sanctius esse possit. Cum ergo filiae Sion quae mollius et dissolutius vivunt, et ardorem forsitan experiuntur carnis, merito purificandae sint spiritu judicii et spiritu ardoris; Virgo perpetua ac singularis, quae sola Spiritus obumbratione Deum concepit, solo purificata est spiritu gratiae et refrigerio roris. Omnium sane purgationum, quibus conditio reparatur mortalis, haec beatae purificatio Virginis mitissima atque felicissima fuit: nam illa filiarum Sion quae purgatorio fiet igne judicii, sicut novissima, sic et severissima omnium erit. Nec ista itaque potest esse nobis nisi admirationi, nec illa nisi formidini: quippe qui nec ad istam possimus aspirare, et in illam, si sapimus, semper formidamus incidere. Omnino si sapimus, semper clamabimus, plus emendatione morum, quam strepitu verborum: Domine, ne in furore tuo arguas me, neque in ira tua corripias me (Psal. VI, 2)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. There is indeed in this very thing much clemency of the Lord toward the daughters of Sion: but far more clement and gentle was the purification of her whom the daughters of Sion proclaim blessed, to whom it was said: \"The Holy Spirit shall come upon you, and the power of the Most High shall overshadow you.\" This overshadowing of supernal power was truly the purification of Mary\u2014not that one which, by a certain mystical dispensation, was today celebrated only in outward appearance. That overshadowing was plainly the whole and true sanctification of both Mother and Son, as the angel also explained to her. \"Therefore,\" he says, \"that which shall be born of you, holy, shall be called the Son of God\" (Luc. I, 35). For mortal nature had to be purified beforehand, so that it might conceive God\u2014not afterward, once it had conceived; since to have conceived the Holy of Holies is the highest sanctification, and nothing can be holier than she who was made the Mother of that very holiness. Therefore, since the daughters of Sion, who live more softly and dissolutely, and perhaps experience the burning of the flesh, are rightly to be purified by the spirit of judgment and the spirit of burning; the perpetual and singular Virgin, who alone conceived God by the overshadowing of the Spirit, was purified by the spirit of grace alone and by the refreshment of dew. Of all the purifications by which mortal condition is restored, this purification of the Blessed Virgin was the gentlest and most blessed; for that purification of the daughters of Sion, which will be accomplished by the purgatorial fire of judgment, will be, as the last of all, so also the severest of all. Neither, therefore, can the one be anything to us but a matter for admiration, nor the other anything but a matter for dread: since we can neither aspire to that former one, and, if we are wise, we must always fear falling into that latter one. Altogether, if we are wise, we shall always cry out\u2014more by the amendment of our conduct than by the noise of words\u2014\"Lord, rebuke me not in your fury, nor chastise me in your wrath\" (Psal. VI, 2).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Necessarie igitur nobis et misericorditer inter utrumque genus purgationis, illud summum ad quod non attingimus, et illud ultimum quod metuimus, alia purgationum genera pietas providit divina, sicut tempus et locum poenitentiae, sic plurima salutis indulgens remedia. Quae licet multa ac varia sint, sub quatuor tamen generibus videntur omnia fere posse comprehendi; ne scilicet indigesta multitudine rerum memoriam confundamus auditoris. Haec igitur quatuor esse arbitror quibus opportune et commode his diebus purgationis nostrae purgari possumus: scilicet contritionem cordis, afflictionem corporis, opera pietatis et fidei, patientiam tribulationis. Ad hunc siquidem modum quatuor invenies, quibus mundum generaliter purificat Christus, purgationem peccatorum faciens: aquam scilicet et sanguinem, spiritum et ignem. Hic est, inquit Joannes, qui venit per aquam et sanguinem. Hic est qui baptizat in Spiritu sancto et igni (I Joan. V, 6, 8). Aqua baptizavit, pedesque discipulorum lavit, dedicans baptismum quoque lacrymarum, quae de contritione profluunt cordis. Sanguinem fudit; ut et nos mortificatione corporis participantes ejus passioni, lavemus stolas nostras in sanguine Agni. Spiritum dedit; ut charitas Dei ac proximi diffusa in cordibus nostris per Spiritum, multitudinem in nobis operiat peccatorum. Quae, si minus perfecta est, ut tot et tanta non sufficiat operire, admovet ignem conflator ille, qui purgat filios Levi; et quidquid residuum est rubiginis, ignis excoquit, seu praesentis, seu futurae tribulationis, ut tandem decantare possint: Transivimus per ignem et aquam, et eduxisti nos in refrigerium (Psal. LXV, 12). Sic nempe et mundus iste prius baptizatus aqua diluvii, postea purgatus igne judicii, in novum transibit statum incorruptionis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Necessarily, then, and mercifully, between these two kinds of purgation\u2014the highest, which we do not attain, and the last, which we fear\u2014divine kindness has provided for us other kinds of purgation, granting, as it grants a time and place for penance, many remedies of salvation as well. Though these are many and varied, they can nevertheless almost all be comprehended under four kinds, lest we confuse the hearer's memory with an undigested multitude of things. These, then, I judge to be the four by which we can be fittingly and conveniently purified in these days of our purification: namely, contrition of heart, affliction of the body, works of piety and faith, and patience in tribulation. In this manner you will find four things by which Christ generally purifies the world, effecting a purgation of sins: namely, water and blood, spirit and fire. 'This is he,' says John, 'who came by water and blood. This is he who baptizes in the Holy Spirit and in fire' (I Joan. V, 6, 8). He baptized with water, and washed the feet of the disciples, consecrating also the baptism of tears, which flow from contrition of heart. He shed blood, that we too, sharing in his passion through mortification of the body, might wash our robes in the blood of the Lamb. He gave the Spirit, that the love of God and of neighbor, poured into our hearts through the Spirit, might cover a multitude of sins in us. If this love is too imperfect to suffice to cover so many and so great sins, that refiner applies the fire, who purges the sons of Levi; and whatever rust remains, the fire\u2014whether of present or future tribulation\u2014smelts away, so that at last they may be able to sing: 'We have passed through fire and water, and you have brought us out into a place of refreshment' (Psal. LXV, 12). Thus, indeed, this world too, first baptized with the water of the flood, and afterward purged by the fire of judgment, will pass into a new state of incorruption.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Denique, ut teneris atque delicatis geramus aliquatenus morem, si lacrymas non habent, si labores horrent, si nesciunt ferre tribulationem: nunquid et illud excusabunt, quod Salomon commendat: Per misericordiam, inquiens, et fidem purgantur peccata? (Prov. XV, 27; XVI, 6.) Quid misericordia suavius? quid fide jucundius? Haec oleum est membris, illa lumen oculis. Haec ungit affectus, illa sensus illuminat, dirigitque gressus: ut ad lumen ejus ambules in tenebris, ut invisibilia contempleris, et futuram tuam beatitudinem jam animo praesumptam mediteris. Bene autem dicitur, per misericordiam et fidem purgari peccata: quia misericordia de proprio impendens, redimit debita; fides autem etiam gratis sine operibus indulgentiam impetrat. De misericordia nempe dicit propheta: Iniquitates tuas redime misericordiis pauperum (Dan. IV, 24); et Dominus prophetarum: Date eleemosynam, et omnia munda sunt vobis (Luc. XI, 41). De fide autem ait Apostolus: Fide mundans corda eorum (Act. XV, 9); et Dominus apostolorum: Vade, inquit, fides tua te salvum fecit (Marc. X, 52). Quanquam in hac Salomonis sententia, possit intelligi non fides qua credimus, sed qua credibiles nos exhibemus, dum fidem Deo et hominibus servamus; ut quod hic dicitur: Per misericordiam et fidem purgantur peccata (Prov. XV, 27), et paulo post: Misericordia et veritate redimitur iniquitas (Prov. XVI, 6), eadem sit in diversitate verborum repetita sententia. Recte igitur misericordia et veritas seu fides sociata sunt: cum in omnibus viis nostris nisi misericordia et veritas obvient sibi, plus augeri quam purgari peccata sit verendum. Ut enim de aliis actionibus interim taceam, et de misericordiis pauperum loquar, quas abundantius nunc fieri temporis hujus postulat necessitas: quid in ipsis etiam, quibus peccata purganda sunt, peccati committitur, si misericordia fide, vel fides misericordia destituatur?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Finally, that we may to some extent make allowance for the tender and delicate\u2014if they have no tears, if they shrink from labors, if they do not know how to bear tribulation\u2014will they also excuse themselves from what Solomon commends, saying: 'By mercy and faith sins are purged'? (Prov. XV, 27; XVI, 6.). What is sweeter than mercy? What more delightful than faith? The one is oil for the limbs, the other light for the eyes. The one anoints the affections, the other illumines the senses and directs the steps, so that by its light you may walk in darkness, that you may contemplate invisible things, and meditate upon your future blessedness already anticipated in the soul. And it is well said that sins are purged by mercy and faith: because mercy, spending from one's own goods, redeems debts, while faith, even freely, without works, obtains pardon. Of mercy indeed the prophet says: 'Redeem your iniquities by mercies shown to the poor' (Dan. IV, 24); and the Lord of the prophets: 'Give alms, and all things are clean to you' (Luc. XI, 41). Of faith the Apostle says: 'cleansing their hearts by faith' (Act. XV, 9); and the Lord of the apostles says: 'Go, your faith has made you whole' (Marc. X, 52). Although in this saying of Solomon one might understand not the faith by which we believe, but that by which we show ourselves trustworthy, in that we keep faith with God and with men; so that what is said here, 'By mercy and faith sins are purged' (Prov. XV, 27), and a little afterward, 'By mercy and truth iniquity is redeemed' (Prov. XVI, 6), is the same statement repeated in different words. Rightly, then, are mercy and truth, or faith, joined together: since unless mercy and truth meet one another in all our ways, it is to be feared that sins are increased rather than purged. For, to say nothing for now of other actions, and to speak of mercies shown to the poor, which the necessity of this present time demands be done more abundantly: what sin is committed even in those very acts by which sins are to be purged, if mercy is deprived of faith, or faith of mercy?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Audiant ergo quibus dispensatio credita est; audiant quibus credita non est: isti scilicet, ne sic velint esse fideles, ut misericordiam deserant; illi, ne sic velint esse misericordes, ut fidem laedant. Sunt enim aliqui qui plus volunt esse fideles quam oportet; et sunt aliqui qui plus volunt esse misericordes quam eis liceat. Qui ergo contra fidem indebitam usurpant misericordiam, audiant Salomonem dicentem: Multi homines misericordes vocantur; fidelem autem virum quis inveniet? (Proverb. XX, 6.) Qui autem velut propter fidem negligunt debitam misericordiam, audiant plus quam Salomonem dicentem: Quod superest date eleemosynam (Luc. XI, 41); ut saltem abundantia vestra aliorum suppleat inopiam. Multum prorsus pepercit infirmae et pusillae nostrae fidei, qui bona communia non ex aequo fratribus partiri, sed ex eo quod superest propriae necessitati, jussit eleemosynam eis impertiri. Ut enim taceamus de jure naturae, quae quidquid parens omnium terra generat, omnibus commune terrigenis adjudicat: illis saltem quibus indifferens est germanitas adoptionis, et communis sors haereditatis quomodo communis non erit usus panis quem Pater administrat coelestis? Esto tamen, sit istud jus ordinatae charitatis, quod moderatio Evangelii suas cuique necessitates primum excepit: quis ille est qui recte metiatur necessitates proprias qui inter necessarium et superfluum aequo judicio discernat? Attendamus nobis, fratres, ne de mortibus pauperum fratrum nostrorum incipiamus judicari, si superflue retinemus aut insumimus nobis unde vita eorum valeat sustentari. Et quoniam hodierna purificatio purissimae ac pauperrimae Virginis de nostra nos purificatione loqui commonuit, in hoc maxime nostram constare puritatem noverimus, si quidquid superfluum est nobis amputemus: ut non solum sanctitate castitatis, sed etiam simplicitate paupertatis aliquatenus aemulemur pauperem matrem pauperis Christi, cui regnum et imperium, et nunc et per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Let them hear, then, to whom the stewardship has been entrusted; and let them hear to whom it has not been entrusted: those without the stewardship, lest they wish to be so faithful that they abandon mercy; those entrusted with it, lest they wish to be so merciful that they injure faith. For there are some who wish to be more faithful than they ought, and there are some who wish to be more merciful than is lawful for them. Those, therefore, who contrary to faith, arrogate to themselves an undue mercy, let them hear Solomon saying: \"Many men are called merciful; but who shall find a faithful man?\" (Proverb. XX, 6.) But those who, as it were for the sake of faith, neglect due mercy, let them hear one greater than Solomon saying: \"That which remains over, give as alms\" (Luc. XI, 41), that at least your abundance may supply the want of others. He has indeed greatly spared our weak and feeble faith, who did not command that common goods be divided equally among the brethren, but that alms be given to them out of what remains over and above one's own necessity. For to say nothing of the law of nature, which assigns whatever the earth, the parent of all, brings forth, as common to all her children\u2014for those at least to whom the brotherhood of adoption is undivided, and the lot of inheritance common, how shall the use of the bread which the heavenly Father dispenses not also be common? Yet granted that this is the right of ordered charity, that the moderation of the Gospel has first reserved to each his own necessities: who is he that rightly measures his own necessities, who with fair judgment distinguishes between the necessary and the superfluous? Let us take heed, brothers, lest we begin to be judged for the deaths of our poor brothers, if we superfluously keep back or spend upon ourselves that by which their life might be sustained. And since today's purification of the most pure and most poor Virgin has reminded us to speak of our own purification, let us know that our purity consists chiefly in this: that we cut away whatever is superfluous to us; so that not only in holiness of chastity, but also in simplicity of poverty, we may in some measure imitate the poor mother of the poor Christ, to whom belong kingdom and dominion, both now and for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Purification Sermon IV: On the Four Kinds of Our Purification", "topics": ["Purification", "purgation", "contrition and penance", "mercy and faith", "almsgiving"], "url": "sermons/guerric-purification-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:candlemas", "calendar_label": "The Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-purification-05", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis, sermo V, De mundanda et adornanda Christo anima, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 79-89.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Purification", "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Sirach 10:9", "Isaiah 40:6\u20137", "Sirach 14:18", "Galatians 6:14", "2 Corinthians 1:12", "1 Corinthians 1:31", "Jeremiah 9:24", "2 Corinthians 10:17", "Jeremiah 17:6", "Matthew 25:11\u201312", "Wisdom 2:24\u201325", "Job 5:2", "Matthew 5:22", "Psalm 6:8 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 4:5 (historical numbering)", "Ephesians 4:26", "Ecclesiastes 7:4", "Psalm 119:5\u20136 (historical numbering)", "2 Corinthians 6:10", "John 16:20", "Colossians 3:5", "Psalm 118:36 (historical numbering)", "Exodus 16:3", "Numbers 11:5\u20136", "Isaiah 22:13", "2 Corinthians 15:32", "1 Corinthians 6:13", "Exodus 32:6", "1 Corinthians 10:7", "Matthew 12:45", "Hosea 7:4", "Psalm 20:10 (historical numbering)", "Luke 13:27", "Psalm 6:9 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 7:23", "Proverbs 28:14", "Psalm 2:11 (historical numbering)", "Luke 1:48", "Matthew 5:3", "Luke 6:20", "Sirach 30:24", "Isaiah 66:2", "Matthew 5:4", "Psalm 36:11 (historical numbering)", "Song of Songs 1:7", "Mark 9:22", "Matthew 5:6", "Isaiah 61:2", "Psalm 119:2 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 5:4, 7", "Exodus 3:5", "Exodus 33:20", "Matthew 5:8", "Philippians 1:21, 23\u201324", "Matthew 5:9", "Leviticus 12:2, 4, 6, 8", "Luke 2:29\u201330, 32", "Isaiah 9:2", "Romans 11:25\u201326", "Genesis 22:18", "Psalm 131:11 (historical numbering)", "1 Corinthians 11:29", "Deuteronomy 4:24", "Deuteronomy 23:10", "1 Corinthians 10:16"], "occasion": "In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis", "occasion_detail": "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Purification", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Cleansing the bridal chamber of the soul begins with expelling vices and cultivating virtues. The sermon then turns to Mary and Simeon in the temple and to reverence for the Eucharist. The 1879 edition includes it under Guerric, but its footnote records Horstius's doubt about that attribution and its earlier printing among Bernard's works. The individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO V (2)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON V (2).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De mundanda et adornanda Christo anima."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On Cleansing and Adorning the Soul for Christ", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. \u00ab Adorna thalamum tuum, Sion, et suscipe regem Christum, quem virgo concepit, virgo peperit, virgo post partum quem genuit adoravit (3). \u00bb Ad nos, fratres, ad nos loquitur Spiritus sanctus, qui sumus Sion, qui sumus speculantes, qui die ac nocte levamus oculos nostros ad Dominum Deum nostrum, donec misereatur nostri. Adornemus igitur thalamum nostrum, et suscipiamus regem Christum. Ecce, fratres, Christus in propria veniens, et a suis repulsus, non habet ubi caput reclinet. Prae foribus nostris inter materna brachia vagiens, per Spiritum sanctum quaerit hospitium, dicens: \u00ab Adorna thalamum tuum, Sion. \u00bb Emundemus igitur thalamum nostrum, prius expellendo quae oculos Christi intrando offendere possunt; et sic demum adornemus ea inferendo quibus delectatur et gaudet. Quae sunt, ait forsitan aliquis vestrum, quae oculos Christi offendere possunt? Cui dicimus: Nihil ita oculos ejus offendit, sicut superbia, quae angelum de coelo, et hominem expulit de paradiso: quae est initium omnis peccati: quam hortatur nos Spiritus sanctus vitare, dicens: Quid superbis, terra et cinis? (Eccli. X, 9); et alibi: Omnis caro fenum; exsiccatum est fenum, et flos ejus decidit (Isai. XL, 6, 7; Eccli. XIV, 18). Videamus nunc quid superbia fecit. Angelum de coelo, et hominem expulit de paradiso. Merito haec oculos Christi offendit quae pulcherrimum opus ejus in coelo et in paradiso foedavit; quae fecit de angelo diabolum, et de homine nuper creato justo perfidum. Quae sicut est peccatorum primum et maximum, sic est omnium regina et mater vitiorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Adorn your bridal chamber, Sion, and welcome Christ the king, whom a virgin conceived, a virgin bore, and after bearing him a virgin adored him (3).\" It is to us, brothers, to us that the Holy Spirit speaks, we who are Sion, we who are watchmen, who day and night lift up our eyes to the Lord our God until he have mercy on us. Let us, then, adorn our bridal chamber and welcome Christ the king. Behold, brothers, Christ coming into his own, and rejected by his own, has nowhere to lay his head. Crying before our doors amid his mother's arms, he seeks lodging through the Holy Spirit, saying: \"Adorn your bridal chamber, Sion.\" Let us therefore cleanse our bridal chamber, first by expelling whatever might offend the eyes of Christ upon his entering; and only then let us adorn it by bringing in those things in which he delights and rejoices. What things, someone among you may perhaps ask, can offend the eyes of Christ? To him we say: Nothing offends his eyes so much as pride, which cast an angel out of heaven and man out of paradise; which is the beginning of all sin; which the Holy Spirit urges us to avoid, saying: Why are you proud, earth and ashes? (Eccli. X, 9); and elsewhere: All flesh is grass; the grass withers and its flower falls (Isai. XL, 6, 7; Eccli. XIV, 18). Let us now see what pride has done. It cast an angel out of heaven and man out of paradise. Rightly does this offend the eyes of Christ, which defiled his most beautiful work in heaven and in paradise; which made of an angel a devil, and of man, newly created righteous, a faithless man. As it is the first and greatest of sins, so it is the queen and mother of all vices.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Videndum est igitur et sollicite considerandum, ut eam mens nostra cum tota prole sua excludat, ut thalamus noster emundatus et ornatus Christum regem qui foris [al. fores] pulsat, suscipiat. Cum tota, inquam, prole sua, septem scilicet principalibus vitiis. Quae sunt haec? Inanis gloria, invidia, ira, tristitia, avaritia, ventris ingluvies, luxuria. Hae sunt filiae superbiae, quae matrem suam sequuntur: quae hospitium quodcunque intraverint, fetidum reddunt. Sed quae nobis cum istis communio? Quis consensus templo Dei cum idolis? Nulla nobis (Deo agimus gratias) cum eis societas, quas, exuentes veterem hominem cum actibus suis, gladio verae confessionis et poenitentiae enecavimus. Nihil igitur in nobis ultra possit superbia, quae de archangelo diabolum, et de primo homine fecit impium et Creatori suo rebellem. Nihil habeat in nobis vana gloria, qui a die conversionis nostrae, in cruce Domini nostri Jesu Christi coepimus gloriari cum Apostolo dicentes: Mihi autem absit gloriari, nisi in cruce Domini nostri Jesu Christi, per quem mihi mundus crucifixus est, et ego mundo! (Galat. VI, 14). Gloria itaque nostra sit crux Christi quam portamus, et testimonium conscientiae per crucem Christi purgatae, de qua Apostolus ait: Gloria nostra haec est, testimonium conscientiae nostrae (II Cor. I, 12). In qua conscientia gloriari, de donis Dei est Deo gratias agere. Ad quod nos hortatur Apostolus, dicens: Qui gloriatur, in Domino glorietur (I Cor. I, 31; Jer. IX, 24; II Cor. X, 17): id est, in Domino de puritate conscientiae suae glorietur. Non enim qui se ipsum commendat, etc. Caeterum qui in se gloriatur, et qui sibi placet, stulto homini placet. Qui in alio homine gloriatur, aut laudem in mente ab ore alterius exspectat, mercedem operum recipit, ac oleo peccatoris ungit caput suum, auditque prophetam sibi dicentem: Maledictus homo qui ponit spem suam in homine (Jerem. XVII, 6). Et in die judicii cum dixerint: Domine, Domine, aperi nobis, ait: Amen dico vobis, nescio vos (Matth. XXV, 11, 12). Haec vana gloria filia est superbiae; quae in quocunque fuerit, inobedientem facit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. We must therefore watch and carefully consider that our mind exclude pride together with its whole offspring, so that our chamber, cleansed and adorned, may welcome Christ the king who knocks outside [or: at the doors]. With its whole offspring, I say, namely the seven principal vices. What are these? Vainglory, envy, anger, sadness, avarice, gluttony of the belly, lust. These are the daughters of pride, who follow their mother: whatever lodging they enter, they make foul. But what fellowship have we with these? What agreement has the temple of God with idols? We have no partnership with them (thanks be to God), for, stripping off the old man with his deeds, we have slain them with the sword of true confession and penance. Let pride, then, have no further power in us \u2014 pride which made of an archangel a devil, and of the first man a wicked man rebellious against his Creator. Let vainglory have nothing in us, we who from the day of our conversion have begun to glory in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, saying with the Apostle: But God forbid that I should glory, save in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, through whom the world is crucified to me, and I to the world! (Galat. VI, 14). Let our glory, then, be the cross of Christ which we bear, and the testimony of a conscience purged through the cross of Christ, of which the Apostle says: Our glory is this, the testimony of our conscience (II Cor. I, 12). To glory in such a conscience is to give thanks to God for God's gifts. To this the Apostle exhorts us, saying: He who glories, let him glory in the Lord (I Cor. I, 31; Jer. IX, 24; II Cor. X, 17): that is, let him glory in the Lord concerning the purity of his conscience. For it is not he who commends himself, etc. But he who glories in himself, and who pleases himself, pleases a foolish man. He who glories in another man, or expects praise in his mind from another's mouth, receives the reward of his works, and anoints his own head with the oil of the sinner, and hears the prophet saying to him: Cursed is the man who puts his hope in man (Jerem. XVII, 6). And on the day of judgment, when they shall say: Lord, Lord, open to us, he says: Amen I say to you, I know you not (Matth. XXV, 11, 12). This vainglory is a daughter of pride; whoever harbors it, it makes disobedient.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Rursum, post conversionem, nihil ultra in nobis habeat invidia, per quam mors intravit in orbem terrarum. Diabolus enim invidit homini ascensum unde corruerat, et propter hoc tentavit, et in anima occidit. Haec est illa, quae non magnos, sed parvos semper torquet (Sap. II, 24, 25), dicente sancto Job: Parvulum occidit invidia (Job V, 2). In eo enim quod aliquis alicui invidet, minorem se ostendit ei cui invidet. Sic diabolus invidit primo homini, sic Esau Jacob, et Cain Abel. Haec invidia parvulum occidit; quia nisi inferior esset, de bono alterius non doleret. Hac peste nullus moritur, nisi qui terrena haec appetit, quae tota non veniunt pluribus. Unde si qua desiderat, illi invidet qui penitus ea accepit, vel illi partem habendo quantitatem ei restringit. Hunc parvulum occidit invidia, quia dum non potest adipisci quod alii invidet, in odium, invidiae filiam, cadit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Again, after conversion, let envy have no further place in us, through which death entered into the world. For the devil envied man the ascent from which he himself had fallen, and for this reason tempted him, and slew him in the soul. This is that vice which torments not the great, but always the small (Sap. II, 24, 25), as holy Job says: Envy slays the little one (Job V, 2). For in that someone envies another, he shows himself lesser than the one he envies. Thus the devil envied the first man, thus Esau envied Jacob, and Cain envied Abel. This envy slays the little one; because unless he were inferior, he would not grieve over another's good. No one dies of this plague except one who craves these earthly things, which do not come whole to many. Hence if anyone desires such things, he envies the one who has received them entirely, or the one who, by having a part, restricts their quantity for him. Envy slays this little one, because, since he cannot obtain what he envies in another, he falls into hatred, the daughter of envy.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Rursum, fratres, post conversionem nostram ira non habeat in nobis locum, quia, ut ait Dominus in Evangelio, gradatim ducit ad gehennae puteum, dicens: Qui irascitur fratri suo, reus erit judicio. Qui dixerit, Racha, hoc est, vocem indignationis per iram emiserit, reus erit concilio, in quo tractatur qua poena sit mulctandus. Qui dixerit, Fatue, hoc est, tale quid fecerit aut verbo aut verbere, ut inter fratres despiciatur, reus erit gehennae ignis (Matth. v, 22), nisi resipuerit. Haec est ira, quae stultorum oculos exstinguit, sapientium oculos nonnunquam turbat, dicente Psalmista: Turbatus est prae ira oculus meus (Psal. vi, 8). Et de ira stulti Job ait: Virum stultum interficit iracundia (Job. v, 2). Ecce duas iras videmus; quarum altera nonnunquam turbat oculum, altera semper exstinguit. Altera venit ex impatientia; altera, ex zelo rectitudinis. Altera venit ex vitio; altera, ex virtute. De altera quae venit ex virtute Psalmista ait: Irascimini, et nolite peccare (Psal. iv, 5; Ephes. iv, 26). Quasi dicat: Crudeles estote in vitium, mites in fratrem, considerantes vosmetipsos, ne et vos tentemini. De altera, quae venit ex vitio, ait Apostolus: Sol non occidat super iracundiam vestram (Ephes. iv, 26): id est, Christus, qui est sol justitiae, mentem vestram irascentem non deserat, qui cum ira nunquam habitat. Ira, quae venit ex zelo rectitudinis, debemus irasci prius nostris erratibus; et post, erratibus proximorum: quae melior est risu, quia per tristitiam vultus corrigitur proximus (Eccle. vii, 4). Quae debet tamen subesse rationi, ne sit immoderata, sed in ultione peccati tempus et modum consideret. Haec sapientes interdum turbat: ira per vitium stultos occidit, dum in rixas et in contumeliam linguam relaxat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Again, brothers, after our conversion let anger have no place in us, because, as the Lord says in the Gospel, it leads step by step down to the pit of hell, saying: Whoever is angry with his brother shall be liable to judgment. Whoever says, Raca\u2014that is, utters a cry of indignation through anger\u2014shall be liable to the council, in which it is determined with what penalty he is to be punished. Whoever says, Fool\u2014that is, does something, either by word or by blow, such that his brother is held in contempt among the brothers\u2014shall be liable to the fire of hell (Matth. v, 22), unless he comes to his senses. This is the anger that puts out the eyes of fools, and sometimes troubles the eyes of the wise, as the Psalmist says: My eye is troubled because of anger (Psal. vi, 8). And of the fool's anger Job says: Anger kills the foolish man (Job. v, 2). Behold, we see two angers; of which the one sometimes troubles the eye, the other always puts it out. The one comes from impatience; the other, from zeal for righteousness. The one comes from vice; the other, from virtue. Of the one that comes from virtue the Psalmist says: Be angry, and do not sin (Psal. iv, 5; Ephes. iv, 26). As if he were saying: Be fierce against vice, gentle toward your brother, considering yourselves, lest you too be tempted. Of the other, which comes from vice, the Apostle says: Let not the sun go down upon your anger (Ephes. iv, 26): that is, let Christ, who is the sun of righteousness, not abandon your angry mind, for he never dwells together with anger. As for the anger that comes from zeal for righteousness, we ought to be angry first at our own faults, and afterward at the faults of our neighbors; and this is better than laughter, because through the sadness of the countenance the neighbor is corrected (Eccle. vii, 4). Yet it must remain subject to reason, lest it be immoderate, but should consider the time and measure in punishing sin. This anger sometimes troubles the wise; but anger through vice kills fools, as it lets the tongue loose into quarrels and insult.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Rursum, fratres, post conversionem nostram muniendus est thalamus cordis nostri, ne tristitia rediens introeat: illam dico quae mortem operatur, quae de amissione rerum temporalium venit. Haec est illa quae pravas cogitationes generat, murmurosum monachum reddit, quae fastidium parit, et tenentem aratrum Dei cum uxore Loth retro respicere compellit. Sine dolore namque non potest amittere, quod cum inordinato amore possedit. Hanc itaque tristitiam quae mortem operatur excludamus: et intromittamus eam quae venit de memoria peccatorum, et de peregrinatione nostra, dicamusque cum Psalmista: Heu mihi, quia incolatus meus prolongatus est! habitavi cum habitantibus Cedar, multum incola fuit anima mea (Psal. cxix, 5, 6). Haec est tristitia, quae vitam operatur, de qua ait Apostolus: Quasi tristes, semper autem gaudentes (II Cor. vi, 10). Haec est illa de qua Dominus ad discipulos dicit: Tristitia vestra vertetur in gaudium (Joan. xvi, 20). Altera parit mortem, parit animi rancorem."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Again, brothers, after our conversion the bedchamber of our heart must be fortified, lest sadness return and enter in: I mean that sadness which works death, which comes from the loss of temporal things. This is the sadness that breeds wicked thoughts, makes the monk a grumbler, gives rise to disgust, and compels the one holding the plow of God to look back, like the wife of Lot. For one cannot lose without sorrow what one possessed with disordered love. Let us therefore shut out this sadness which works death, and let us admit that sadness which comes from the memory of our sins and from our condition as pilgrims, and let us say with the Psalmist: Woe is me, for my sojourning is prolonged! I have dwelt with the inhabitants of Kedar, my soul has been long a sojourner (Psal. cxix, 5, 6). This is the sadness that works life, of which the Apostle says: As sorrowful, yet always rejoicing (II Cor. vi, 10). This is that of which the Lord says to his disciples: Your sorrow shall be turned into joy (Joan. xvi, 20). The other sadness brings forth death, brings forth rancor of soul.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Rursum, fratres, post conversionem muniamus thalamum nostrum, ne avaritia, quae est radix omnium malorum, rediens introeat, nosque ab unius Dei cultura abstrahat, idolorum cultores faciat. De qua inter caetera sic ait Apostolus ad discipulos suos: Mortificate membra vestra quae sunt super terram: fornicationem, immunditiam, libidinem, concupiscentiam malam, et avaritiam, quae est idolorum servitus (Coloss. iii, 5). Ecce avaritiam idololatriae comparat, et merito: quia sicut uni Deo idololatra nititur auferre gloriam, ne solus habeat nomen Deitatis; ita avarus in Dei res se extendit, ut solus usurpet quae Deus omnipotens in usum plurimorum fecit. Utraque Deo inimica, quia negat Deo quae sunt ejus. Est et avaritia non solum amor congregandae pecuniae aut conservandae, sed ambitio praelationis, cum nititur homo homini praeesse. Haec, ut verum dicam, etiam pauperes (qui semel pro Christo reliquerunt sua) in tantum vexat, ut contra jus et fas, et propositum suum, non tantum velint, sed etiam studeant fratribus et conservis praeesse. Quod si abbas aut prior eorum voto non consenserit, irascuntur, obloquuntur, eosque aut invidos, aut sibi malevolos (qui se non provehunt) clamant. Haec est avaritia quae memoriam mandatorum Dei turbat, dicente Psalmista Domino: Inclina cor meum in testimonia tua, et non in avaritiam (Psal. cxviii, 36): quae proditionis est mater, quae Judam Dei discipulum primum ad mandatorum Dei oblivionem, de oblivione ad proditionem, de proditione traxit ad laqueum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Again, brothers, after our conversion let us fortify our bedchamber, lest avarice, which is the root of all evils, return and enter in, and draw us away from the worship of the one God, making us worshippers of idols. Concerning this, among other things, the Apostle says thus to his disciples: Put to death your members which are upon the earth: fornication, uncleanness, lust, evil desire, and avarice, which is the service of idols (Coloss. iii, 5). See how he compares avarice to idolatry, and rightly so: for just as the idolater strives to take glory away from the one God, so that he alone may not bear the name of Deity, so the avaricious man extends himself into the things of God, so that he alone may seize for himself what almighty God made for the use of many. Both are enemies of God, because each denies to God what belongs to him. And avarice is not only the love of gathering or keeping money, but also the ambition for precedence, when a man strives to set himself over another man. This, to speak truly, torments even the poor (who have once left their own goods for Christ's sake) to such a degree that, against right and justice and against their own purpose, they not only desire but even strive to be set over their brothers and fellow-servants. And if the abbot or prior does not consent to their wish, they grow angry, they murmur against him, and cry out that those who do not advance them are either envious or ill-disposed toward them. This is the avarice that disturbs the memory of God's commandments, as the Psalmist says to the Lord: Incline my heart to your testimonies, and not to avarice (Psal. cxviii, 36): which is the mother of betrayal, which drew Judas, God's disciple, first to forgetfulness of God's commandments, then from forgetfulness into betrayal, and from betrayal to the noose.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Rursum, fratres, post conversionem nostram muniamus thalamum cordis, ne gula rediens introeat, quae et Adam innocentiae veste, et Esau privavit primogeniti dignitate. Haec est illa pestis quae patres nostros in deserto ingratos beneficiis Dei fecit, quae compulit eos murmurare contra Deum, et dicere: Utinam mortui essemus in Aegypto, quando sedebamus super ollas carnium, et comedebamus allia, cepe, et pepones (Exod. xvi, 3; Num. xi, 5, 6; xiv, 3.) Haec est illa funesta pestis quae eos ad blasphemiam duxit, quasi non esset alia vita post istam, cum dicebant: Comedamus et bibamus; cras enim moriemur (Isa. xxii, 13; II Cor. xv, 32), id est, in futuro. Quasi dicerent: Hoc solum habemus quod comedimus; nihil in futuro post hanc vitam nos habituros speramus. Hanc pestem cum homine perituram pronuntiat Apostolus, dicens: Esca ventri, et venter escis. Deus autem et hunc et hanc destruet (I Cor. vi, 13). Haec pestis tripartita est: quia aut ante horam canonicam, aut aliud quam licet, aut plus quam necesse est facit comedere, et carnis curam in concupiscentiis nititur facere. De qua nascitur inepta laetitia: sicut de populo Israel dicitur: Sedit populus manducare et bibere, et surrexerunt ludere (Exod. XXXII, 6; I Cor. X, 7)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Again, brothers, after our conversion let us fortify the chamber of our heart, lest gluttony, returning, enter in \u2014 the vice which deprived Adam of the garment of innocence, and Esau of the dignity of the firstborn. This is that plague which made our fathers in the desert ungrateful for the benefits of God, which drove them to murmur against God and say: \"Would that we had died in Egypt, when we sat by the pots of flesh and ate garlic, onions, and melons\" (Exod. xvi, 3; Num. xi, 5, 6; xiv, 3.). This is that deadly plague which led them to blasphemy, as though there were no other life after this one, when they said: \"Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall die\" (Isa. xxii, 13; II Cor. xv, 32) \u2014 that is, in the life to come. As if they said: We have only what we eat; we hope to have nothing in the future after this life. The Apostle declares that this plague will perish along with man, saying: \"Food for the belly, and the belly for food; but God will destroy both the one and the other\" (I Cor. vi, 13). This plague is threefold: for it makes a man eat either before the canonical hour, or something other than is permitted, or more than is necessary, and it strives to make provision for the flesh in its lusts. From it is born foolish merriment, just as it is said of the people of Israel: \"The people sat down to eat and drink, and rose up to play\" (Exod. XXXII, 6; I Cor. X, 7).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p008", "label": "Latin original", "text": "8. Rursum, fratres, post conversionem nostram muniamus thalamum cordis nostri, ne luxuria rediens introitum inveniat: quae pestilentia rediens est pejor quam ante fuerat. Nunquam enim sola redit; sed sicut Dominus ait in Evangelio: Assumit septem spiritus nequiores se, et ingressi habitant ibi: et fiunt novissima hominis illius pejora prioribus (Matth. XII, 45). Haec est pestis illa quae corpus Christi dimembrat, quae de membris Christi facit membra diaboli. Nam qui adhaeret alicui personae causa libidinis explendae, unum corpus fit cum illa persona, et fit membrum diaboli: cujus et illa membrum est per conjunctionem malorum operum. Haec est illa pestis quae ignem infernalem in corpus humanum accendit, ut vegetum sit et robustum ad libidinem explendam: et quae igni illi inexstinguibili materiam semper apponit, et vires ministrat, ut de suo semper habeat unde sine fine ardeat. De his duobus ignibus luxuriae et gehennae habemus auctoritatem Scripturae. De igne luxuriae ait: Omnes adulterantes quasi clibanus succensus a coquente (Osee VII, 4). De igne gehennae ait: Pones eos ut clibanum ignis in tempore vultus tui, hoc est, judicii, ut intus ardeant (sicut clibanus accensus) in conscientia. Dominus in ira sua conturbabit eos (Psal. XX, 10), dicens: Discedite a me, operarii iniquitatis (Luc. XIII, 27; Psal. VI, 9; Matth. VII, 23). Et devorabit eos ignis extremus infernalis, quem accenderunt propter luxuriae foeda opera. Sit ergo, fratres, summa cura et summum studium his qui in talibus sunt, de talibus evadere et fugere."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p008", "label": "English", "text": "8. Again, brothers, after our conversion let us fortify the chamber of our heart, lest lust, returning, find an entrance \u2014 a plague which, returning, is worse than it was before. For it never returns alone; but, as the Lord says in the Gospel: \"It takes with it seven spirits more wicked than itself, and they enter in and dwell there; and the last state of that man becomes worse than the first\" (Matth. XII, 45). This is that plague which dismembers the body of Christ, which turns members of Christ into members of the devil. For whoever clings to another person for the sake of satisfying lust becomes one body with that person, and becomes a member of the devil \u2014 of whom that other person is likewise a member, through the joining together of their evil deeds. This is that plague which kindles the infernal fire within the human body, so that it may be lively and vigorous for the satisfying of lust; and which continually supplies fuel to that unquenchable fire, and furnishes it strength, so that it may always have from its own store that by which it burns without end. Concerning these two fires, that of lust and that of hell, we have the authority of Scripture. Of the fire of lust it says: \"They are all adulterers, like an oven heated by the baker\" (Osee VII, 4). Of the fire of hell it says: \"Thou shalt make them as an oven of fire in the time of thy anger\" (that is, of judgment), that they may burn within (as in a heated oven) in their conscience. \"The Lord in his wrath shall trouble them\" (Psal. XX, 10), saying: \"Depart from me, you workers of iniquity\" (Luc. XIII, 27; Psal. VI, 9; Matth. VII, 23). And the fire of hell at the last shall devour them, which they kindled on account of the foul works of lust. Let it be, therefore, brothers, the greatest care and the greatest zeal of those who are caught in such things, to escape and flee from such things.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p009", "label": "Latin original", "text": "9. Nobis vero qui per gratiam Dei evasimus, et veterem exuimus hominem, qui sumus Sion, sit summum studium thalamum nostrum munire et adornare, et Christum regem suscipere, sicut haec scriptura praecipit quam recitavimus: \u201cAdorna thalamum tuum, Sion.\u201d Quid est adornare thalamum tuum? Virtutes congregare, quibus et pavimentum et parietes quasi cortinis, et floribus tota domus adornetur. Primus ornatus thalami nostri, qui sumus Sion, sit timor, qui est initium sapientiae, id est, primus gradus per quem ascenditur ad sapientiam. De quo timore ait Scriptura: Beatus homo qui semper pavidus (Prov. XXVIII, 14). Et Psalmista ait: Servite Domino in timore, et exsultate ei cum tremore (Psal. II, 11): ut timor auferat securitatem noxiam, et exultatio mortiferam tristitiam. Hic timor generat humilitatem, quae est pretiosissimus ornatus thalami Sion, quae magis placuit Christo cum in thalamum virgineum ingrederetur, dicente beata Maria: Quia respexit humilitatem ancillae suae, ecce enim ex hoc beatam me dicent omnes generationes (Luc. I, 48). Haec, inquam, humilitas est ornatus Sion, id est, pauperum Christi, de quibus ipse dicit: Beati pauperes spiritu, quoniam ipsorum est regnum coelorum (Matth. V, 3; Luc. VI, 20). Ecce quomodo timor Domini primus gradus ascensionis, et primus ornatus thalami Sion, alios etiam ornatus generat, humilitatem scilicet, et voluntariam paupertatem. Adorna ergo thalamum tuum, Sion, timore Domini."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p009", "label": "English", "text": "9. But as for us, who by the grace of God have escaped and have put off the old man, who are Sion, let it be our greatest zeal to fortify and adorn our chamber, and to receive Christ the King, just as this Scripture which we have recited commands: \"Adorn thy chamber, O Sion.\" What does it mean to adorn your chamber? To gather together virtues, by which the whole house \u2014 both floor and walls \u2014 may be adorned as though with hangings and flowers. Let the first adornment of our chamber, we who are Sion, be fear, which is the beginning of wisdom, that is, the first step by which one ascends to wisdom. Of this fear Scripture says: \"Blessed is the man that is always fearful\" (Prov. XXVIII, 14). And the Psalmist says: \"Serve the Lord with fear, and rejoice unto him with trembling\" (Psal. II, 11): so that fear may remove harmful security, and rejoicing remove deadly sorrow. This fear begets humility, which is the most precious adornment of the chamber of Sion, which pleased Christ most when he entered the virginal chamber, as blessed Mary says: \"Because he has regarded the humility of his handmaid; for behold, from henceforth all generations shall call me blessed\" (Luc. I, 48). This humility, I say, is the adornment of Sion, that is, of the poor of Christ, of whom he himself says: \"Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven\" (Matth. V, 3; Luc. VI, 20). Behold how the fear of the Lord, the first step of ascent and the first adornment of the chamber of Sion, also begets other adornments, namely humility and voluntary poverty. Adorn, therefore, your chamber, O Sion, with the fear of the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p010", "label": "Latin original", "text": "10. Secundus ornatus thalami Sion, et secundus gradus ad sapientiam, est pietas, de qua dicit Scriptura: Miserere animae tuae placens Deo (Eccli. XXX, 24); et alibi: Super quem requiescet spiritus meus, nisi super humilem et quietum? (Isa. LXVI, 2.) Haec miseretur in se peccantibus, fraterna onera portat; magis eligit quaelibet temporalia bona perdere, quam cum fratre lite contendere. Quae profecto pietas aliud nihil est nisi Deum colere, et per cultum Dei Deo placere. Haec in nobis generat mansuetudinem, optimum ornatum thalami Sion, quae filios Dei nos facit, dicente Domino: Beati mites, quoniam filii Dei vocabuntur (Matth. V, 4; Psal. XXXVI, 11). Ecce quomodo pietas secundus ornatus thalami Sion et secundus gradus ascensionis, alium ornatum de se generat mansuetudinem, quae filios Dei efficit. Adorna igitur thalamum tuum, Sion, pietate."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p010", "label": "English", "text": "10. The second adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion, and the second step toward wisdom, is piety, of which Scripture says: \"Have mercy on your own soul, pleasing God\" (Eccli. XXX, 24); and elsewhere: \"Upon whom shall my spirit rest, but upon the humble and quiet?\" (Isa. LXVI, 2.). This piety has mercy on those who sin against it, bears the burdens of brothers; it chooses rather to lose any temporal goods whatsoever than to contend with a brother in a lawsuit. This piety, indeed, is nothing other than to worship God, and through the worship of God to please God. This generates in us meekness, the best adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion, which makes us sons of God, the Lord saying: \"Blessed are the meek, for they shall be called sons of God\" (Matth. V, 4; Psal. XXXVI, 11). Behold how piety, the second adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion and the second step of ascent, generates from itself another adornment, meekness, which makes us sons of God. Adorn, therefore, your bridal chamber, O Zion, with piety.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p011", "label": "Latin original", "text": "11. Tertius ornatus thalami Sion et tertius gradus ascensionis est scientia, quae homini sui cognitionem praebet, ut scilicet homo, neque minus neque plus quam debet de se praesumat. Quorum alterum, scilicet de se nimium praesumere, in mala facit praecipitem; alterum, scilicet se non cognoscere, facit ad quaelibet bona torpentem. Ad hanc scientiam Spiritus sanctus invitat Ecclesiam in Canticis: Si ignoras te, o pulchra inter mulieres, abi post vestigia gregum tuorum, scilicet haereticorum: et pasce haedos tuos (Cantic. I, 7), hoc est, carnales concupiscentias. Haec scientia praebet nobis fiduciam vincendi omnes tentationes per veram fidem, dicente Domino in Evangelio: Si potes credere, omnia possibilia sunt credenti (Marc. IX, 22), per Dei scilicet gratiam, et liberum arbitrium. Videmus jam quomodo scientia tertius ornatus thalami Sion, et tertius gradus ascensionis, alium ornatum in nobis generat, religiosum videlicet luctum, qui consolationem a Domino in futuro meretur. Haec scientia parat in nobis dolorem et luctum peregrinationis, ut videndo mala exsilii quae patimur, cum luctu desideremus patriam a qua peregrinamur, audiamusque a Domino nobis dici: Beati qui lugent, quoniam ipsi consolabuntur (Matth. V, 6; Isa. LXI, 2)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p011", "label": "English", "text": "11. The third adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion and the third step of ascent is knowledge, which grants a man knowledge of himself, so that a man presumes of himself neither less nor more than he ought. Of these two faults, the one\u2014presuming too much of oneself\u2014makes one headlong into evils; the other\u2014not knowing oneself\u2014makes one sluggish toward whatever is good. To this knowledge the Holy Spirit invites the Church in the Canticle: \"If you know not yourself, O fairest among women, go forth after the footsteps of your flocks\"\u2014that is, of the heretics\u2014\"and feed your kids\" (Cantic. I, 7), that is, carnal desires. This knowledge affords us confidence to overcome all temptations through true faith, the Lord saying in the Gospel: \"If you can believe, all things are possible to him that believes\" (Marc. IX, 22), namely through the grace of God and free will. We see now how knowledge, the third adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion and the third step of ascent, generates in us another adornment, namely religious mourning, which merits consolation from the Lord in the future. This knowledge prepares in us the sorrow and grief of pilgrimage, so that, seeing the evils of exile which we suffer, we may with grief desire the homeland from which we are pilgrims, and may hear it said to us by the Lord: \"Blessed are they that mourn, for they shall be comforted\" (Matth. V, 6; Isa. LXI, 2).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p012", "label": "Latin original", "text": "12. Quartus ornatus thalami Sion, est fortitudo, quae convenit laborantibus pro veris bonis acquirendis. Haec est quae munit thalamum Sion, ut non extollatur prosperis, non frangatur adversis: quae linguam increpantem et blandientem his verbis confundit, dicens: Domine, libera animam meam a labiis iniquis, et a lingua dolosa (Psal. CXIX, 2). Haec est illa quae semper de die in diem robustior, esuriem et sitim justitiae in thalamo Sion accendit, facitque eam audire a Domino: Beati qui esuriunt et sitiunt justitiam, quoniam ipsi saturabuntur. Adorna igitur thalamum tuum, Sion, fortitudine. Quintus ornatus thalami Sion est consilium, quo homo infirmitates suas requirit, suum posse et suum non posse investigat: et ex sua infirmitate alios considerat, aliis parcit et miseretur, sicut et vult Deum sibi misereri; aliis dimittit debita, sicut sibi vult dimitti a Deo: mereturque audire a Domino: Beati misericordes, quoniam ipsi misericordiam consequentur (Matth. V. 4, 7). Adorna igitur thalamum tuum, Sion, consilio. Sextus ornatus thalami Sion, est intellectus; qui mentem hominis super haec infirma rapit, omnesque corporeas substantias intellectuali intuitu transiens, non corporeis substantiis figitur, volensque ultra progredi, et propius accedere audit a Domino: Ne appropinques huc (Exod. III, 5): non enim videbit me homo, et vivet (Exod. XXXIII, 20). Hic intellectus convenit iis qui a Deo purgatum oculum habent, de quibus dicitur: Beati mundo corde, quoniam ipsi Deum videbunt (Matth. V, 8). Adorna igitur thalamum tuum, Sion, intellectu."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p012", "label": "English", "text": "12. The fourth adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion is fortitude, which belongs to those laboring to acquire true goods. This is what fortifies the bridal chamber of Zion, so that it is not lifted up by prosperity, nor broken by adversity; which confounds the tongue that rebukes and the tongue that flatters with these words, saying: \"Lord, deliver my soul from wicked lips, and from a deceitful tongue\" (Psal. CXIX, 2). This is that virtue which, growing ever stronger from day to day, kindles in the bridal chamber of Zion a hunger and thirst for justice, and makes it hear from the Lord: \"Blessed are they that hunger and thirst after justice, for they shall be filled.\" Adorn, therefore, your bridal chamber, O Zion, with fortitude. The fifth adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion is counsel, by which a man examines his own weaknesses, searches out what he can and cannot do; and from his own weakness considers others, spares and shows mercy to others, just as he wishes God to show him mercy; forgives others their debts, as he wishes to be forgiven by God; and merits to hear from the Lord: \"Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy\" (Matth. V. 4, 7). Adorn, therefore, your bridal chamber, O Zion, with counsel. The sixth adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion is understanding, which snatches the mind of man above these weak things, and, passing by intellectual gaze beyond all bodily substances, is not fixed upon bodily substances; and wishing to go further and draw nearer, hears from the Lord: \"Come not near hither\" (Exod. III, 5): \"for man shall not see me, and live\" (Exod. XXXIII, 20). This understanding belongs to those who have had their eye purged by God, of whom it is said: \"Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God\" (Matth. V, 8). Adorn, therefore, your bridal chamber, O Zion, with understanding.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p013", "label": "Latin original", "text": "13. Septimus ornatus thalami Sion, est sapientia, quae convenit iis qui intellectualibus oculis aeterna contemplantur; quorum conversatio in coelis est, qui cupiunt dissolvi, et esse cum Christo: in quorum persona dicebat Apostolus: Mihi vivere Christus est, et mori lucrum. Quasi diceret: Ut Christum magnificem, Christo vivere volo tantum, et Christus est praemium mortis. Et post pauca: Coarctor autem e duobus, desiderium habens dissolvi et esse cum Christo, multo melius: manere autem in carne, necessarium propter vos (Philipp. I, 21, 23, 24). Ecce quid facit vera sapientia, quae omnes rebelles motus corporis ita subjugat, ut nullam ultra pugnam in carne sentiat: pro infirmis fratribus (ut eis Christum nuntiet) exsilium libenter patitur; probra non audit, damna non sentit, auferenti tunicam dimittit pallium, percutienti unam maxillam praebet et alteram, auditque a Domino: Beati pacifici, quoniam filii Dei vocabuntur (Matth. V, 9). Hi ornatus thalami Sion: istis nos adornemus qui sumus Sion, et regem Christum suscipiamus, quem Virgo concepit, Virgo peperit, Virgo post partum quadragesima, hoc est hodierna die, in templo Dei praesentavit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p013", "label": "English", "text": "13. The seventh adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion is wisdom, which belongs to those who with intellectual eyes contemplate eternal things; whose conversation is in heaven, who desire to be dissolved and to be with Christ: in whose person the Apostle said: \"To me to live is Christ, and to die is gain.\" As if he said: \"That I may magnify Christ, I wish only to live for Christ, and Christ is the reward of death.\" And shortly after: \"But I am straitened between the two, having a desire to be dissolved and to be with Christ, a thing by far the better: yet to abide in the flesh is necessary for your sake\" (Philipp. I, 21, 23, 24). Behold what true wisdom does, which so subjugates all the rebellious movements of the body that it no longer feels any battle in the flesh: for the sake of weak brothers (that it may announce Christ to them) it willingly endures exile; it does not hear reproaches, it does not feel losses, to him that takes away its coat it gives up its cloak also, to him that strikes one cheek it offers the other also, and hears from the Lord: \"Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called the children of God\" (Matth. V, 9). These are the adornments of the bridal chamber of Zion: with these let us, who are Zion, adorn ourselves, and let us receive Christ the king, whom the Virgin conceived, the Virgin bore, and the Virgin, on the fortieth day after the birth\u2014that is, on this very day\u2014presented in the temple of God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p014", "label": "Latin original", "text": "14. In lege namque scriptum erat, ut mulier quae suscepto semine peperisset masculum, quadragesimo die purgata templum cum filio intraret, deferens hostias secum pro peccato, agnum anniculum immaculatum. Si autem non haberet agnum, par turturum, sive duos pullos columbae offerret (Levit. XII, 2, 4, 6, 8). Haec omnia beata Virgo non ex necessitate, sed ex humilitate implevit. Abstinuit ab introitu templi quadraginta diebus, non necesse habens purgari, quae sine omni delectatione, salvo virginitatis sigillo, de Spiritu sancto concepit. Obtulit hostiam non pro peccato, sicut aliae mulieres, sed pro humilitate: ostendens etiam in hoc quod Filius Dei Jesus Christus non solvere venerat legem, sed adimplere. Et ecce Simeon grandaevus, qui acceperat responsum non visurum se mortem nisi videret Christum Domini, et eum susciperet quem exspectabat, accepit eum in ulnas suas, hoc est, inter brachia sua amplexatus est; illum qui appendit tribus digitis molem terrae, qui omnia implet, omnia complectitur, inter brachia portat, dicens: Nunc dimittis, servum tuum, Domine, secundum verbum tuum, in pace. Cupio dissolvi, et ejus adventum cum patriarchis et prophetis in inferno exspectare, qui inter te et humanum genus facturus est pacem. Quia viderunt oculi mei salutare tuum, Filium tuum, quem desideravi, de quo responsum a Spiritu sancto accepi. Lumen ad revelationem gentium, et gloriam plebis tuae Israel (Luc. II, 29, 30, 32). De quo ait Isaias: Populus qui ambulabat in tenebris, vidit lucem magnam (Isa. IX, 2): quia, cum plenitudo Gentium intraverit, tunc omnis Israel salvus fiet (Rom. XI, 25, 26)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p014", "label": "English", "text": "14. For it was written in the Law that a woman who, having conceived, had borne a male child, should on the fortieth day, once purified, enter the temple with her son, bringing with her sacrifices for sin, a lamb of one year without blemish. But if she had no lamb, she was to offer a pair of turtledoves, or two young pigeons (Levit. XII, 2, 4, 6, 8). All these things the Blessed Virgin fulfilled not out of necessity, but out of humility. She abstained from entering the temple for forty days, though she had no need of purification, she who, without any delight, with the seal of her virginity unbroken, had conceived of the Holy Spirit. She offered the sacrifice not for sin, as other women did, but out of humility, showing also by this that the Son of God, Jesus Christ, had not come to abolish the Law, but to fulfill it. And behold, Simeon, now advanced in years, who had received the answer that he would not see death until he had seen the Christ of the Lord, and had received him whom he awaited, took him into his arms\u2014that is, embraced him between his arms\u2014him who weighs the mass of the earth with three fingers, who fills all things and embraces all things. Simeon carries him in his arms, saying: Now, Lord, you dismiss your servant, according to your word, in peace. I desire to be dissolved, and to await with the patriarchs and prophets in hell his coming, who is to make peace between you and the human race. For my eyes have seen your salvation, your Son, whom I desired, concerning whom I received an answer from the Holy Spirit. A light for the revelation of the Gentiles, and the glory of your people Israel (Luc. II, 29, 30, 32). Of whom Isaiah says: The people who walked in darkness have seen a great light (Isa. IX, 2): because, when the fullness of the Gentiles has entered in, then all Israel shall be saved (Rom. XI, 25, 26).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p015", "label": "Latin original", "text": "15. Audite, fratres, audite quid offert Maria, quid Simeon suscipit: Unigenitum Dei, Deo Patri consubstantialem et coaeterum, Verbum in principio. Et, ut utilitatem et consolationem nostram audiamus, illum offert Virgo, quem Deus Abrahae promisit, dicens: In semine tuo, hoc est, in Christo, benedicentur omnes gentes (Gen. XXII, 18; XII, 3, et XVIII, 18); et quem David promisit, dicens: De fructu ventris tui ponam super sedem tuam (Psal. CXXXI, 11). Hic est quem Virgo offert, patriarcharum gaudium, desiderium prophetarum, Gentium exspectatio, Judaeorum qui eum receperunt salvatio, ruinae angelorum reparatio. Talis est quem Virgo offert Deo, imo multo major quam lingua, aut hominum, aut angelorum possit dicere. Et cum sit tantus ac talis, qualem aut quantum ipse se novit, non sufficit Virgini ipsum solum offerre; sed hostiam, quam lex praeceperat, pro eo obtulit: ob humilitatis scilicet exemplum, ut nos doceret quomodo et nos eum debeamus offerre. Duos turtures, aut duos pullos columbae pro eo obtulit. Columba, fratres, simplex est animal, felle caret, rostro non laedit, in cavernis petrarum nidificat, alienos pullos nutrit, juxta fluenta manet, ut visa umbra accipitris fugiat, meliora grana eligit, gemitum pro cantu reddit, gregatim volat, alis se defendit, visum recuperat. Et nos, fratres, cum ante altare steterimus, ut Dei Filium Deo offeramus, cum eo et pro eo par turturum, castitatem corporis et animae offeramus: et duos pullos columbarum, pro cantu gementes, compunctionem geminam pro desiderio patriae coelestis, et de consideratione nostrae miseriae habentes. Columba felle caret: et nos ira careamus, quae peccare nos facit. Rostro non laedamus, sed percussi in unam maxillam, alium praebeamus. In cavernis petrarum nidificemus; in fide plagarum Christi refugium habeamus. Alienos pullos nutriamus; de filiis diaboli facientes verbo praedicationis filios Dei. Juxta flumina maneamus: id est, juxta hunc mortalitatis fluxum sedentes, umbram accipitris, hoc est, peccata quae sunt umbra diaboli, malorum ruinas considerantes, ab ejus ungulis fugiamus. Meliora grana eligamus id est, meliores sententias in Scripturis sanctis. Gregatim volemus. unum consensum in bono in congregatione sub pastore habentes. Alis Novi et Veteris Testamenti contra inimicos Dei nos defendamus; vel duobus praeceptis charitatis, Dei videlicet amore et proximi. Visum recuperemus, de bono actionis ad bonum semper intendentes contemplationis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p015", "label": "English", "text": "15. Hear, brothers, hear what Mary offers, what Simeon receives: the Only-begotten of God, consubstantial and coeternal with God the Father, the Word in the beginning. And, that we may hear of our profit and consolation, the Virgin offers him whom God promised to Abraham, saying: In your seed, that is, in Christ, all nations shall be blessed (Gen. XXII, 18; XII, 3, et XVIII, 18); and whom David promised, saying: Of the fruit of your womb I will set upon your throne (Psal. CXXXI, 11). This is he whom the Virgin offers: the joy of the patriarchs, the desire of the prophets, the expectation of the Gentiles, the salvation of the Jews who received him, the restoration of the ruin of the angels. Such is he whom the Virgin offers to God\u2014indeed far greater than any tongue, whether of men or of angels, can tell. And although he is so great and of such a kind as he alone knows himself to be, it was not enough for the Virgin to offer him alone; but she offered for him the sacrifice which the Law had commanded\u2014namely, as an example of humility, that she might teach us how we too ought to offer him. She offered for him two turtledoves, or two young pigeons. The dove, brothers, is a simple creature; it has no gall, does not wound with its beak, builds its nest in the clefts of the rocks, nourishes the young of others, dwells beside the streams so that, seeing the shadow of the hawk, it may flee; it chooses the better grains, utters a moan instead of a song, flies in flocks, defends itself with its wings, and recovers its sight. And we, brothers, when we stand before the altar to offer the Son of God to God, let us offer with him and for him a pair of turtledoves\u2014the chastity of body and soul; and two young pigeons, groaning instead of singing, having a twofold compunction: one from desire for our heavenly homeland, and one from the consideration of our own misery. The dove has no gall: let us likewise be without anger, which makes us sin. Let us not wound with our beak, but, struck on one cheek, let us offer the other. Let us nest in the clefts of the rocks; let us have our refuge in the faith of the wounds of Christ. Let us nourish the young of others, making, by the word of preaching, sons of God out of the children of the devil. Let us dwell beside the rivers: that is, sitting beside this flow of mortality, considering the shadow of the hawk\u2014that is, sins, which are the shadow of the devil\u2014and the ruin of the wicked, let us flee from his claws. Let us choose the better grains, that is, the better teachings in the Holy Scriptures. Let us fly in flocks, having one accord in good within the congregation under the shepherd. Let us defend ourselves against the enemies of God with the wings of the New and Old Testament; or with the two precepts of charity, namely, the love of God and of neighbor. Let us recover our sight, always directing ourselves from the good of action toward the good of contemplation.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p016", "label": "Latin original", "text": "16. Fratres charissimi, tales oportet nos esse cum corpus Christi consecramus, cum consecratum manducantes sacrificamus, cum vobis idem corpus in salutem corporis et animae porrigimus. Tales etiam vos oportet esse, cum sacrum sacramentum de manibus nostris accipitis, scientes, quod qui corpus Christi indigne accipit, et sanguinem ejus indigne bibit, judicium sibi manducat et bibit (I Cor. XI, 29). Verum, quia raro invenitur talis qui in se haec omnia habeat, diligat illum qui bonum habet, quod in se non habet: et in illo habeat, quod in se non videt. Neque enim credere debemus quod soli sacerdoti supradictae virtutes sint necessariae, quasi solus consecret et sacrificet corpus Christi. Non solus sacrificat, non solus consecrat, sed totus conventus fidelium qui astat, cum illo consecrat, cum illo sacrificat (4). Nec solus ligni faber facit domum, sed alius virgas, alius ligna, alius trabes, etc., comportat. Debent itaque astantes habere de suo, sicut et sacerdos, fidem firmam, orationem puram, devotionem piam. Sciendumque quod sine firma fide nemo astantium esse debet. Caeteras vero virtutes, quas supra diximus, etsi omnes non habent, diligant illum qui habet quod in se non inveniunt, et in illo habeant quod in se nondum vident. Sicut Petrus in Joanne virginitatis habet meritum: sic Joannes in Petro martyrii habet praemium."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p016", "label": "English", "text": "16. Dearest brothers, such as these ought we to be when we consecrate the body of Christ, when, by eating what has been consecrated, we offer sacrifice, when we hold out that same body to you for the salvation of body and soul. Such also ought you to be when you receive the sacred sacrament from our hands, knowing that whoever receives the body of Christ unworthily and drinks his blood unworthily eats and drinks judgment to himself (I Cor. XI, 29). But because one is rarely found who has all these things in himself, let him love the one who has the good which he himself does not have, and let him possess in that other what he does not see in himself. For we ought not to believe that the aforesaid virtues are necessary to the priest alone, as though he alone consecrates and sacrifices the body of Christ. He does not sacrifice alone, he does not consecrate alone, but the whole assembly of the faithful who stand present consecrates with him, sacrifices with him (4). Nor does the carpenter alone build the house, but one brings rods, another timber, another beams, and so on. Therefore those standing present ought to have of their own, just as the priest does, firm faith, pure prayer, pious devotion. And it should be known that no one of those standing present ought to be without firm faith. As for the other virtues we have mentioned above, even if not all possess them, let them love the one who has what they themselves do not find in themselves, and let them possess in him what they do not yet see in themselves. Just as Peter has in John the merit of virginity, so John has in Peter the reward of martyrdom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p017", "label": "Latin original", "text": "17. Sciendum quoque, quod corpori Christi, quem Maria hodie praesentavit in templo, quem Simeon in ulnis suis suscepit, tria debemus: dignitatem, reverentiam et fructum. Dignitatem, ut cum Dei Filium, Deum de Deo, omnipotentem, creatorem omnium, judicem vivorum et mortuorum credamus. Dignitatem, ut credamus nos statim postquam de corpore exierimus, ab eo, sicut a judice, percepturos sicut gessimus, sive bonum, sive malum. Ille namque quem Maria in templo praesentavit, quem Simeon suscepit, quem in manibus consecrantes ante Deum levamus, benedicimus, manducamus, vobis porrigimus manducandum; ille familiaris noster, judex erit noster. Caveamus ergo ab eo separari per judicium post mortem, cui ita sumus familiares in vita. Dicamus itaque ei in corde, cum eum tractamus; dicamus cum eum consecramus: Domine, ad te veniemus post mortem, ante tuum tribunal judicandi astabimus. Qui nobis ita familiaris esse dignaris in vita, quod a nobis tractaris, consecraris, manducaris, noli nobis alienus esse in morte, sed parce nobis in die mala. Confundantur daemones qui nos tunc persequentur, destrue fortitudinem illorum in virtute tua; quia non est qui pugnet pro nobis, nisi tu Deus et familiaris noster. Haec est dignitas quam ei debemus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p017", "label": "English", "text": "17. We should also know that to the body of Christ, which Mary presented today in the temple, which Simeon received into his arms, we owe three things: dignity, reverence, and fruit. Dignity, that we believe him to be the Son of God, God of God, omnipotent, creator of all things, judge of the living and the dead. Dignity, that we believe that immediately after we leave the body, we shall receive from him, as from a judge, according as we have done, whether good or evil. For he whom Mary presented in the temple, whom Simeon received, whom we, consecrating, lift up in our hands before God, whom we bless, whom we eat, whom we hold out to you to be eaten \u2014 he who is so familiar to us will be our judge. Let us take care, then, not to be separated from him at the judgment after death, from him with whom we are so intimate in this life. Let us therefore say to him in our heart, when we handle him; let us say when we consecrate him: Lord, we shall come to you after death; we shall stand before your tribunal to be judged. You who deign to be so familiar with us in this life, that you are handled by us, consecrated by us, eaten by us \u2014 do not be a stranger to us in death, but spare us on the evil day. Let the demons who will then pursue us be confounded; destroy their strength by your power; for there is no one to fight for us except you, God and our familiar one. This is the dignity we owe him.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p018", "label": "Latin original", "text": "18. Debemus ei et reverentiam, investigare scilicet nosmetipsos: et si invenimus cor pollutum foedis cogitationibus aut mala voluntate, ab ejus contactu, ut ab igne, et nos et alios arceamus. Ignis enim consumens est (Deut. IV, 24.), ad quem indignus non debet accedere, ne interius consumatur, et in reprobum sensum tradatur. Exeat ergo extra castra, sicut Moyses praecepit, qui nocturno pollutus est somno (Deut. XXIII, 10), hoc est, qui per negligentiam istius obscurae vitae (quae tota ut somnus pertransit) pervenit usque ad pollutas cogitationes. Exeat extra castra, hoc est, dum in illo statu est, judicet se indignum societate fidelium communicantium hoc sacro corpore, lavetque vestimenta sua per sanctam confessionem; et post solis occasum, hoc est, post tentationis calorem et ardorem jam mortuum, laetus redeat, et cum aliis fidelibus communicet. Haec est reverentia quam debemus corpori Christi, quem virgo Maria hodierna die in templo praesentavit, quem Simeon laetus inter brachia suscepit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p018", "label": "English", "text": "18. We also owe him reverence, namely, to search ourselves, and if we find a heart defiled by foul thoughts or evil will, to keep both ourselves and others from contact with him, as from fire. For he is a consuming fire (Deut. IV, 24.), whom an unworthy person ought not approach, lest he be consumed within and be given over to a depraved mind. Let him therefore go outside the camp, as Moses commanded, who has been defiled by nocturnal sleep (Deut. XXIII, 10) \u2014 that is, let him who through the negligence of this dark life (which passes away wholly like sleep) has come even to defiled thoughts. Let him go outside the camp, that is, while he is in that state, let him judge himself unworthy of the fellowship of the faithful who partake of this sacred body, and let him wash his garments through holy confession; and after the setting of the sun \u2014 that is, after the heat and burning of temptation has now died down \u2014 let him return joyfully, and commune with the other faithful. This is the reverence we owe to the body of Christ, whom the Virgin Mary presented in the temple on this day, whom Simeon joyfully received into his arms.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p019", "label": "Latin original", "text": "19. Debemus etiam fructum scire, intelligere scilicet quem fructum accipimus, si eum digne comedimus. Audite, fratres, non me, sed Apostolum dicentem: Calix benedictionis cui benedicimus, nonne communicatio sanguinis Christi est? Calix, id est communicatio calicis, facit nos habere quamdam communionem cum Christo. Et panis, quem frangimus, nonne participatio corporis est Domini? (I Cor. X, 16.) Ac si dicat: Panis, quem frangimus, facit nos unum corpus sub Christo capite: quoniam, sicut unus panis ex multis granis conficitur, qui postea in corpus Christi et per fidem, et per sancta verba, quae Christus suos docuit, convertitur: sic diversi participantes hoc corpore in unitate fidei, spei et charitatis, unum corpus cum Christo sunt, ad Christum statim post mortem venturi, et cum Christo sicut cum capite suo sine fine regnaturi: quem Virgo concepit, Virgo peperit. Virgo in templo hodierna die praesentavit, qui cum Patre et Spiritu sancto, vivit et regnat Deus, per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p019", "label": "English", "text": "19. We ought also to know the fruit, that is, to understand what fruit we receive if we eat him worthily. Hear, brothers, not me, but the Apostle saying: The cup of blessing which we bless, is it not a communion of the blood of Christ? The cup, that is, the sharing of the cup, makes us have a certain communion with Christ. And the bread, which we break, is it not a participation in the body of the Lord? (I Cor. X, 16.). As if he were saying: The bread which we break makes us one body under Christ as head: for just as one bread is made from many grains, which is afterward converted into the body of Christ both by faith and by the holy words which Christ taught his own, so the various people who partake of this body in the unity of faith, hope, and charity are one body with Christ, destined to come to Christ immediately after death, and to reign without end with Christ as with their head \u2014 whom the Virgin conceived, the Virgin bore. The Virgin presented him in the temple on this day \u2014 he who with the Father and the Holy Spirit lives and reigns as God, through all the ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-n002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(2) De hoc sermone dubitat Horstius an sit Guerrici: tum quod desideretur in manuscripto Coloniensi, qui ipsius sermones continet. tum quod Guerrici stylus pressior et nervosior videatur. Porro licet hactenus Bernardi operibus insertus sit, minus tanto dignus est auctore."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-n002", "label": "English", "text": "(2) Horstius doubts whether this sermon is Guerric's: both because it is missing from the Cologne manuscript, which contains his sermons, and because Guerric's style seems more compressed and vigorous. Moreover, although it has hitherto been inserted among the works of Bernard, it is less worthy of so great an author.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-n003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(3) Ita canit Ecclesia in officio hujus diei."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-n003", "label": "English", "text": "(3) So the Church sings in the Office of this day.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-n004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(4) Solum sacerdotem esse proprium et sufficientem ministrum Eucharistiae consecrandae de fide est, ut patet ex can. 4 conc. Nic. ubi Patres dum diaconos reprehendunt, quod quibusdam in locis administrent Eucharistiam presbyteris, idque de caetero fieri prohibent, hanc ejusce decreti reddunt rationem: Hoc inquiunt, neque regula neque consuetudo tradidit ut qui offerendi sacrificii non habent potestatem, his qui offerunt corpus Christi porrigant. Idem statuit conc. Lateran. sub Innoc. III, et refertur cap. Firmiter extra de sum. Trin. Nam veritate sacramenti explicata, ita subjungit: Et hoc utique sacramentum nemo potest conficere nisi sacerdos, qui rite fuerit ordinatus, secundum claves Ecclesiae, quas concessit apostolis eorumque successoribus Jesus Christus. Ita fere Isambertus, in 3 q. 82, art. 1. Quanquam dici potest, astantes etiam suo modo sacrificium offerre et conficere per sacerdotem et cum sacerdote qui populi mediator et minister est. Unde in canone missae olim ita legebatur: Et omnium circumstantium, qui tibi offerunt hoc sacrificium laudis, quibus verbis haec inserta sunt: Pro quibus tibi offerimus, vel qui tibi offerunt. etc."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-n004", "label": "English", "text": "(4) That the priest alone is the proper and sufficient minister for consecrating the Eucharist is a matter of faith, as is clear from canon 4 of the Council of Nicaea, where the Fathers, in rebuking the deacons because in certain places they were administering the Eucharist to the presbyters, and forbidding that this be done henceforth, give this reason for the decree: \"Neither rule nor custom,\" they say, \"has handed down that those who have no power of offering the sacrifice should give the Body of Christ to those who offer it.\" The Lateran Council under Innocent III established the same thing, and it is referred to in the chapter \"Firmiter\" in the Decretals, On the Most High Trinity; for having explained the truth of the sacrament, it adds: \"And indeed no one can consecrate this sacrament except a priest who has been duly ordained, according to the keys of the Church which Jesus Christ granted to the apostles and their successors.\" So, more or less, Isambert, in part 3, question 82, article 1. Although it may be said that those standing by also, in their own way, offer and accomplish the sacrifice through the priest and with the priest, who is the mediator and minister of the people. Whence in the canon of the Mass it used to be read: \"And of all those standing round, who offer to Thee this sacrifice of praise,\" words into which these were inserted: \"For whom we offer to Thee,\" or \"who offer to Thee,\" etc.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Authorship uncertain. The edition presents this sermon as Guerric's, but its footnote (2) reports that the earlier editor Horstius doubted it: the sermon is missing from a Cologne manuscript of Guerric's sermons, and its style is less terse and vigorous than his. It had also been printed among the works of Bernard of Clairvaux."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints alium, where aliam (agreeing with maxillam) is expected. The printed form is kept."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints cum, evidently a misprint for eum (him). The printed form is kept."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Purification Sermon V: On Cleansing and Adorning the Soul for Christ", "topics": ["Purification", "adorning the soul", "vices and virtues", "Eucharist", "Mary and Simeon"], "url": "sermons/guerric-purification-05.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:candlemas", "calendar_label": "The Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-purification-06", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis, sermo VI, De perficienda nostri purificatione, ut Domino sisti possimus, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 89-92.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Purification", "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Luke 2:22", "Proverbs 20:9", "Sirach 7:34", "Psalm 54:19 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 6:8\u201311", "Leviticus 12:4", "Isaiah 1:15", "Ezekiel 16:6", "1 Corinthians 15:50", "Jeremiah 48:10", "Psalm 50:16 (historical numbering)", "Luke 2:34", "Job 9:30\u201331", "Isaiah 9:5", "Luke 2:23", "Psalm 83:4 (historical numbering)", "2 Corinthians 3:18"], "occasion": "In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis", "occasion_detail": "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Purification", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Simeon's completed purification prompts an examination of negligence and the use of time. Guerric considers purification through prayer, watchful faith and the final vision of God. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO VI."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON VI.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De perficienda nostri purificatione, ut Domino sisti possimus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On Perfecting Our Purification So That We May Be Presented to the Lord", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Postquam impleti sunt dies purgationis, tulerunt Jesum in Jerusalem. O quam felix est, de quo dici potest: Impleti sunt dies purgationis ejus: ut jam nihil aliud ei supersit, nisi ut ferant eum in Jerusalem supernam, et sistant eum Domino (Luc. II, 22). Talis nimirum erat Simeon ille noster senex, tam desiderabilis quam plenus desideriis, cujus cum jam olim, ut arbitror, essent impleti dies purgationis, hodie impleti sunt etiam dies exspectationis: ut jam, secundum verbum Domini, nihil aliud ei restaret, postquam vidisset Christum Domini, Christum pacem Dei et hominum, nisi ut dimitteretur in pace, et dormiret in pace in idipsum; hoc est, ut ferrent eum in Jerusalem pacis aeternae, et sisterent eum ad contemplandam pacem quae exsuperat omnem sensum. O Simeon vir desideriorum, impletum est in bonis desiderium tuum. O beate senex, renovata est ut aquilae juventus tua. Introisti jam ad altare Dei illud supernum, aeternum, totum aureum, ad Deum qui laetificat juventutem tuam aeterna visione sui, cujus laetificavit senectutem hodierna visione Christi sui. Ad altare illud invisibile jam ipse praesentatus es Patri; cui ad altare hoc visibile Filium hodie praesentasti. Ibi Filium ipsum, quem hodie tuo sinu gestasti, aeterno jam et indissolubili tibi conseris amplexu."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"After the days of purification were fulfilled, they carried Jesus up to Jerusalem.\" O how happy is he of whom it can be said: \"The days of his purgation were fulfilled,\" so that nothing else remains for him but that they carry him up to the heavenly Jerusalem and present him to the Lord (Luc. II, 22). Such, surely, was our Simeon of old, that elder as desirable as he was full of desires, whose days of purgation had, I think, long since been fulfilled; and today his days of expectation also were fulfilled, so that, according to the word of the Lord, nothing else remained for him, once he had seen the Christ of the Lord, the Christ who is the peace of God and of men, but to be dismissed in peace and to sleep in peace in that very peace \u2014 that is, that they should carry him into the Jerusalem of eternal peace, and present him to behold the peace which surpasses all understanding. O Simeon, man of desires, your desire has been fulfilled with good things! O blessed elder, your youth has been renewed like the eagle's! You have already come to that heavenly altar, eternal, wholly golden, to God who gladdens your youth with the eternal vision of himself, who gladdened your old age today with the vision of his own Christ. At that invisible altar you yourself have already been presented to the Father, to whom at this visible altar you presented the Son today. There you now embrace in an eternal and indissoluble embrace that very Son whom today you bore in your own arms.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Repletum est igitur in bonis beati senis desiderium, cujus tota erat exspectatio et desiderium, Exspectatio gentium et Desideratus earum. Renovata est ut aquilae juventus ejus, quoniam in senectute bona impleti sunt dies purgationis ejus. Alioquin nequaquam illius capax novitatis esse posset, nisi sedulo vetustatem his diebus expurgasset. Fratres, expurgate vetus fermentum, dum tempus habetis ad expurgandum; ut cum impleti fuerint dies vestrae purgationis, inveniamini et vos capaces illius gaudii, quo plena est jam anima Simeonis. Et quidem dies qui ad purgandum nobis dati sunt, velimus, nolimus, implemus: sed vae nobis si dies implentur, et purgatio minime impletur; ut postea necesse sit illo nos igne repurgari, quo nihil poenalius, nihil acrius, aut vehementius in hac vita excogitari potest. Sed quis ille tam perfectus, tam sanctus, qui cum hinc exierit, nihil illi debeat igni: qui sic ad purum omnem in se scoriam excoxerit peccati, ut gloriari possit castum se habere cor, ut dicere possit: Mundum est cor meum, purus sum a peccato? (Prov. XX, 9.) Pauci quidem sunt electi; sed inter illos paucos paucissimi sunt, ut arbitror, ita perfecti, ut purgationem illam impleverint, de qua Sapiens dicit: De negligentia purga te cum paucis (Eccli. VII, 34). Omnino, si de negligentia nos purgaremus, de paucis essemus. Nunc autem quoniam non modo minima, sed et maxima negligimus, longe a paucis de terra divisi sumus, et de nobis dicit ille, qui paucitatem sanctorum multitudini praefert negligentium: Inter multos erant mecum (Psal. LIV, 19)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. The desire of the blessed old man, therefore, was fulfilled with good things: his whole expectation and desire was the Expectation of the nations and their Desired One. His youth was renewed like the eagle's, because in a good old age the days of his purgation were fulfilled. Otherwise he could by no means have been capable of that newness, had he not diligently purged away his oldness in those days. Brethren, purge out the old leaven while you have time to purge it, so that when the days of your purgation have been fulfilled, you too may be found capable of that joy with which the soul of Simeon is now filled. And indeed the days given us for purging, whether we will or not, we do fulfill; but woe to us if the days are fulfilled and the purgation is by no means fulfilled, so that afterward we must needs be purged again by that fire than which nothing more penal, nothing more bitter or more violent can be conceived in this life. But who is so perfect, so holy, that when he departs from here he owes nothing to that fire \u2014 who has so thoroughly burned out every dross of sin in himself that he can boast of having a chaste heart, that he can say: \"My heart is clean, I am pure from sin\"? (Prov. XX, 9.) Few indeed are the elect; but among those few, fewest of all, I think, are so perfect as to have fulfilled that purgation of which the Wise Man says: \"Purge yourself from negligence with the few\" (Eccli. VII, 34). Certainly, if we purged ourselves of negligence, we would be among the few. But now, since we neglect not only the smallest things but even the greatest, we are far removed from the few on earth, and of us he says, who prefers the fewness of the saints to the multitude of the negligent: \"They were with me among the many\" (Psal. LIV, 19).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Et quid hodie tam proprium simul et commune his quos religionis fovet umbra, sicut negligentia? et, ut de meipso loquar, quoties derident hostes Sabbata mea? quoties dormitat anima mea prae taedio, diemque fere totum, ac si tempus revocabile esset, inertia consumo? Et utinam sic dormitaret anima mea, ut apertis et vigilantibus oculis, ipse mihi misero non fingerem somnia vana et illusoria! At nunc in perversum mutata sententia, ego vigilo, et cor meum dormit, somnoque tam gravi, ut vix aliquando valeat expergisci ad tonitruum supernae illius increpationis: Usquequo, piger, dormis? quando consurges e somno tuo? Paululum dormies, paululum dormitabis, paululum conseres manus ut dormias; et veniet tibi quasi viator egestas, et pauperies quasi vir armatus (Prov. VI, 8-11; XXIV, 33, 34). Illud sane miserrimum est, quod hodie non putatur jactura, sed lucrum, si dies qui nobis ad purgationem dati sunt, elabantur nobis dormiendo, praetereant et pereant negligendo. Gentilis, aiunt, cum sederet ad mensam, venissetque in mentem nihil strenuum, aut memorabile se egisse die illo; cum gemitu gravi et dolore mensam repulit, exclamans: \u00ab O amici, diem perdidi (5) \u00bb Nos hodie dicere videmur, diem lucrifeci, si sine opere totus per inertiam possit elabi. Sic nullus fere est qui tempori pretium ponat, qui cogitet apud se quantum valeat ad comparandam aeternitatem dies una. Putasne est aliquis in nobis, qui quotidianam dierum suorum a semetipso rationem exigat; qui cujusque diei dispendia vel emolumenta diligenter sibi describat: ut si deprehenderit se hodie negligenter egisse, vindicet in seipsum, ac de caetero cautius agat? Beatus ille servus, quem, cum venerit dies extremus, invenerit ita facientem."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. And what today is at once so proper and so common to those whom the shadow of religion shelters, as negligence? And, to speak of myself, how often do enemies mock my Sabbaths? How often does my soul drowse from weariness, and I consume almost the whole day in idleness, as though time could be recalled! And would that my soul drowsed in such a way that, with eyes open and watchful, I did not fashion for myself, wretched man, vain and illusory dreams! But now, in a perverse reversal, I am awake, and my heart sleeps \u2014 and sleeps so heavily that it can scarcely ever be roused by the thunder of that heavenly rebuke: \"How long will you sleep, O sluggard? When will you rise from your sleep? You will sleep a little, you will slumber a little, you will fold your hands a little to sleep; and your want shall come upon you like a traveler, and your poverty like an armed man\" (Prov. VI, 8-11; XXIV, 33, 34). This indeed is most wretched, that today it is not thought a loss but a gain, if the days given us for purgation slip away from us in sleep, and pass by and perish through negligence. The pagan, they say, sitting at table, when it came to his mind that he had done nothing vigorous or memorable that day, pushed back the table with heavy groaning and grief, exclaiming: \"O friends, I have lost a day (5)!\" We today seem to say, \"I have gained a day,\" if it can slip away entirely through idleness, without any work. Thus there is almost no one who sets a price on time, who considers within himself how much a single day is worth toward securing eternity. Do you think there is anyone among us who demands of himself a daily account of his days, who diligently sets down for himself the losses or gains of each day, so that if he finds he has acted negligently today, he punishes himself for it, and acts more cautiously thereafter? Blessed is that servant whom, when the last day comes, he shall find so doing.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Hoc nempe esset de negligentia cum paucis purgari, si recte et utiliter implerentur dies purgationis, ut aliquando posset accessus ad sancta salubriter obtineri. Alioquin, quandiu manserit quis in sanguine purificationis suae, sancivit aeterna justitia (sicut scriptum est in lege Moysi) ut omne sanctum non tangat, nec ingrediatur sanctuarium, donec impleantur dies purgationis ejus (Levit. XII, 4). Viderint ergo qua fronte ingrediantur sanctuarium, qua conscientia non solum tangant sancta, sed etiam tractent et sumant Sanctum sanctorum; qui scilicet nondum liberati de sanguinibus, nullum tamen suae purificationis studium gerunt. Annon audiunt protestantem et dicentem: Cum extenderitis manus vestras, avertam oculos meos; manus enim vestrae sanguine plenae sunt? (Isai. I, 15.) Quid enim, si mundus sum a sanguine alieno, et conculcari me Dominus videt in sanguine proprio (Ezech. XVI, 6), sicut de Jerusalem loquitur per Ezechielem? Quid, inquam, mihi proderit, si veloces non sunt pedes mei ad effundendum sanguinem proximorum; et veloces sunt affectus mei ad acquiescendum carni et sanguini, de quibus dicit Apostolus: Quia caro et sanguis regnum Dei non possidebunt? (I Cor. XV, 50.) Maledictus, inquit Jeremias, qui prohibet gladium suum a sanguine (Jerem. XLVIII, 10): id est, qui vitia carnis et sanguinis sibi parcit abscindere. Libera me de sanguinibus, Deus, Deus salutis meae (Psal. L, 16), ne cum sanguine purificationis meae sanctuarium ingrediens, aut sancta contingens, reus inveniar corporis et sanguinis tui, indigne manducans et bibens. Verumtamen gratias tibi, Deus, Deus salutis meae, qui in hoc ipsum pretiosum tuum fudisti sanguinem, ut immunditiam nostri purificares sanguinis, tactumque peccatricis sanguinem fluentis, sed salutem desiderantis non abhorreres: sciens nimirum quod nullus potest facere mundum de immundo conceptum semine, nisi tu, qui solus es; nec sanis, sed male habentibus, melius tamen habere cupientibus, medicus opus est. Tangere itaque Sanctum sanctorum, sicut illis quos delectat in sanguine suo versari, judicium est mortis: sic illis qui desiderant purificari, remedium est salutis, juxta prophetiam Simeonis, Quia hic positus est in ruinam et in resurrectionem multorum (Luc II, 34)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. This indeed would be to be purified from negligence along with the few, if the days of purification were rightly and profitably fulfilled, so that access to holy things might at some point be beneficially obtained. Otherwise, as long as anyone remains in the blood of his purification, eternal justice has decreed (as it is written in the law of Moses) that he shall not touch anything holy, nor enter the sanctuary, until the days of his purification are fulfilled (Levit. XII, 4). Let them consider, then, with what effrontery they enter the sanctuary, with what conscience they not only touch holy things but even handle and consume the Holy of Holies \u2014 those, namely, who are not yet freed from blood, and yet make no effort at all toward their own purification. Do they not hear him protesting and saying: 'When you stretch out your hands, I will turn away my eyes; for your hands are full of blood'? (Isai. I, 15.). For what does it profit me if I am clean of another's blood, while the Lord sees me trampled underfoot in my own blood (Ezech. XVI, 6), as he speaks of Jerusalem through Ezekiel? What, I say, will it profit me if my feet are not swift to shed the blood of my neighbors, while my affections are swift to acquiesce to flesh and blood, of which the Apostle says: 'For flesh and blood shall not possess the kingdom of God' (I Cor. XV, 50.)? 'Cursed,' says Jeremiah, 'is he who withholds his sword from blood' (Jerem. XLVIII, 10): that is, he who spares himself from cutting away the vices of flesh and blood. Deliver me from blood, O God, God of my salvation (Psal. L, 16), lest, entering the sanctuary with the blood of my purification, or touching holy things, I be found guilty of your body and blood, eating and drinking unworthily. Yet thanks be to you, God, God of my salvation, who for this very purpose poured out your precious blood, that you might purify the uncleanness of our blood, and did not abhor the touch of a sinful woman flowing with blood, yet desiring salvation: knowing well that no one can make clean what was conceived of unclean seed, except you, who alone can; nor is the physician needed for the healthy, but for those who are ill, yet desire to be better. Thus to touch the Holy of Holies is, for those who delight in wallowing in their own blood, a sentence of death; but for those who desire to be purified, it is a remedy of salvation, according to the prophecy of Simeon: 'For this one is set for the fall and for the resurrection of many' (Luc II, 34).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Fratres, et vos quidem in sanguine fuistis, sed abluti estis, sed purificati estis: nisi quia, sicut justus confitetur: Si loti fueritis velut aquis nivis, sordibus vos statim intingit (Job IX, 30, 31), non solum conditio corrupta, sed etiam voluntas infirma; ut semper novis sordibus opus sit purificatione nova. Illa vero mirabilis et miserabilis est in hominibus sui negligentia, ut, cum nemo sit qui se purificatione non indigere praesumat, vix tamen est aliquis qui tempus ad hoc indultum, quasi purgatissimus sit, non negligat. Fratres, breves dies hominis sunt (ibid., 25), sed tamen valde pretiosi sunt; imo quo breviores, eo pretiosiores; quia dies sunt purificationis, cum statim postmodum futuri sint dies retributionis. Beati qui in diebus istis lavant stolas suas, et candidas eas faciunt in sanguine Agni; ne postea vestimentum eorum, si mistum fuerit sanguine, sit in combustionem et cibus ignis (Isai. IX, 5). Nec solum timor urget supplicii, sed etiam spes invitat praemii, ut omni cura et instantia nostrae modo studeamus purificationi; ut quod hodie figurare voluit in seipso Filius Dei quadam dispensatoria specie, completum quandoque gaudeamus in nobis ipsa rerum veritate. Quid illud? Postquam, inquit, impleti sunt dies purgationis, tulerunt Jesum in Jerusalem, ut sisterent eum Domino (Luc. II, 23). Et nos utique, si tempora purgationis legitime fuerint a nobis impleta, tollent in supernam Jerusalem ministeria angelorum, ut illic assistentes vultui Dei, oblatio ejus simus accepta et immaculata. Ibi demum purificabimur ex toto; sicut a peccato, sic etiam a poena peccati. Ibi nostrae purificationis ipsa erit consummatio, quae et remuneratio; cum scilicet divinus ignis totos nos absumet in holocaustum Domino."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Brothers, you too were indeed in blood, but you have been washed, you have been purified \u2014 except that, as the just man confesses: 'If you have been washed as it were with waters of snow, it immediately plunges you into filth' (Job IX, 30, 31), it is not only our corrupted condition but also our weak will that does this, so that there is always need of new purification for new filth. But this is a marvelous and wretched thing in men \u2014 this negligence of themselves \u2014 that, though there is no one who presumes himself not to need purification, there is scarcely anyone who does not neglect the time granted for this, as though he were already most thoroughly purified. Brothers, the days of man are few (ibid., 25), yet they are very precious; indeed, the shorter they are, the more precious \u2014 because they are days of purification, since immediately afterward will come the days of retribution. Blessed are those who in these days wash their robes, and make them white in the blood of the Lamb; lest afterward their garment, if it be mingled with blood, be for burning and fuel for fire (Isai. IX, 5). Not only does the fear of punishment press us, but the hope of reward also invites us, so that we should now strive toward our purification with all care and urgency; so that what the Son of God today willed to prefigure in himself by a certain outward dispensation, we may one day rejoice to see fulfilled in us in the very truth of the reality. What is that? 'After,' it says, 'the days of purification were fulfilled, they brought Jesus to Jerusalem, to present him to the Lord' (Luc. II, 23). And we too, surely, if the times of our purification have been rightly fulfilled by us, the ministries of angels will carry us up into the heavenly Jerusalem, so that, standing there before the face of God, we may be his acceptable and unblemished offering. There at last we shall be purified wholly \u2014 as from sin, so also from the punishment of sin. There the very consummation of our purification will take place, which will also be our reward; when, that is, the divine fire shall consume us wholly as a holocaust to the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Porro hunc ipsum beatissimae purificationis inexplicabilem modum nec modo quidem cessat aemulari devotio sanctorum, quantum tamen corruptela corporum, et sollicitudo terrenae inhabitationis permittunt: dum scilicet mente excedentes in illam Jerusalem, verum orationis locum, illic in conspectu Domini quasi turturem et columbam pro seipsis ac de seipsis offerunt; corde videlicet et carne exsultantes in Deum vivum, quia scilicet columba invenit sibi domum, et turtur nidum, altaria tua, Domine virtutum (Psal. LXXXIII, 4). Ego tamen arbitror quod ad meritum non sit inferius, et fortassis ad purgationem efficacius, si illud quod per speculum et in aenigmate raro et raris experiri datur, id est, ut in Jerusalem sistantur Domino, nos per fidem jugiter actitemus: proponentes scilicet Dominum in conspectu nostro semper, et oculos ejus et judicia super nos pervigili fide, irremissoque semper timore cogitantes. Fides ista, fratres, sit in vobis, et eritis de paucis: timor iste sit in vobis, et de negligentia purgabitis vos cum paucis; quia timor iste nihil facile negligit. Denique ab initio hujus pertransibitis, sicut sponsus sponsae pollicetur, euntes de virtute in virtutem, a claritate in claritatem, tanquam a Domini Spiritu (II Cor. III, 18): proficientes de visione quae est per fidem, ad illam quae est per speculum et imaginem, ac postremo, de illa, quae est in imagine speciei, ad eam quae erit in ipsa veritate faciei, seu facie veritatis. Si enim jugiter praesentiam Domini, licet velatam, intendatis per fidem; aliquando etiam revelata facie gloriam ejus speculabimini, licet per speculum et imaginem. Cum autem impletis diebus purgationis venerit quod perfectum est, assistetis Domino in Jerusalem, habitantes cum vultu ejus, ac sine fine contuentes eum facie ad faciem, cui benedictio, et claritas in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Moreover, the devotion of the saints does not even now cease to imitate this inexpressible manner of most blessed purification, so far as the corruption of our bodies and the anxious care of our earthly dwelling permit: that is, when, rising above in mind into that Jerusalem which is the true place of prayer, they there offer in the sight of the Lord, as it were, a turtledove and a dove for themselves and of themselves; rejoicing, namely, in heart and flesh unto the living God, because indeed the dove has found herself a house, and the turtledove a nest, even your altars, O Lord of hosts (Psal. LXXXIII, 4). Yet I reckon that it is no less meritorious, and perhaps more effective for purgation, if that which is granted to be experienced rarely and by the few through a mirror and in a riddle \u2014 namely, to be presented to the Lord in Jerusalem \u2014 we continually practice through faith: setting the Lord always before our sight, and pondering, with ever-watchful faith and unrelaxing fear, his eyes and his judgments upon us. Let this faith, brothers, be in you, and you will be among the few; let this fear be in you, and you will purify yourselves from negligence along with the few, since this fear neglects nothing easily. Finally, from this beginning you will pass on, as the Bridegroom promises to his bride, going from strength to strength, from glory to glory, as from the Spirit of the Lord (II Cor. III, 18): advancing from the vision that comes through faith, to that which comes through a mirror and image, and at last, from that which is in the image of a likeness, to that which will be in the very truth of the face, or the face of truth. For if you continually fix your attention, through faith, upon the presence of the Lord, veiled though it be, you will one day also behold his glory with face unveiled, though still as through a mirror and image. But when, the days of purification being fulfilled, that which is perfect shall come, you will stand before the Lord in Jerusalem, dwelling with his countenance, and gazing upon him face to face without end \u2014 to whom be blessing and glory for ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-n005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(5) Titus imp., apud Sueton. Iisdem pene verbis queritur Seneca, epist. 1."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-n005", "label": "English", "text": "(5) The Emperor Titus, in Suetonius. Seneca complains in almost the same words, Epistle 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Purification Sermon VI: On Perfecting Our Purification So That We May Be Presented to the Lord", "topics": ["Purification", "negligence", "spiritual progress", "purgatorial fire", "Simeon"], "url": "sermons/guerric-purification-06.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:resurrexi", "calendar_label": "Easter Sunday of the Resurrection", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-resurrection-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De ressurrectione Domini, sermo I, Pulchre verba Jacob de Joseph applicantur ad resurrectionem Christi, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 141\u2013144.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Easter", "Resurrection of the Lord", "Genesis 45:26, 28", "Matthew 5:6", "Proverbs 9:9", "Genesis 39:6", "Genesis 41:45", "Hosea 12:10", "Genesis 45:26", "Mark 16:10\u201313 (historical wording; see note)", "Luke 24:9, 11", "Genesis 45:27\u201328", "Luke 24:37", "John 20:22", "John 6:40; 11:26 (printed locators; see note)", "Matthew 28:18", "Matthew 11:28 (adapted quotation; see note)", "Matthew 25:34 (adapted quotation; see note)"], "occasion": "De resurrectione Domini", "occasion_detail": "Resurrection of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Easter", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric of Igny reads the news that Joseph is alive as a figure of the announcement that Jesus has risen. Jacob\u2019s grief and renewed spirit become a pattern for the apostles\u2019 passage from doubt to joy, and for the hearers\u2019 growth in love. The Holy Spirit\u2019s witness, love that seeks Christ\u2019s life rather than its own advantage, and Christ\u2019s flesh as provision for the journey shape the concluding exhortation. The individual sermon is not dated; the collection is associated approximately with Guerric\u2019s abbacy in 1138\u20131157 and is read here from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its historical heading names the Resurrection of the Lord, and the sermon explicitly celebrates Easter."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "DE RESSURRECTIONE DOMINI."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "THE RESURRECTION OF THE LORD", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Pulchre verba Jacob de Joseph applicantur ad resurrectionem Christi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "Jacob\u2019s Words about Joseph Are Aptly Applied to Christ\u2019s Resurrection", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Nuntiaverunt Jacob, dicentes: Joseph vivit. Quo audito, revixit spiritus ejus, et ait: Sufficit mihi, si Joseph filius meus vivit. Vadam et videbo eum antequam moriar. (Gen. xlv, 26, 28). Dicetis forsitan, mihi, Bene; sed quid ad rem? quid de Joseph ad gaudium hujus diei, ad gloriam resurrectionis Christi? Pascha est, et tu iterum apponis quadragesimalia? Agnum paschalem esurit anima nostra, cui se per tam longa praeparavit jejunia. Ardens est cor nostrum in nobis de Jesu, Jesum desideramus; sed nondum meremur ipsum videre vel de ipso audire. Jesum, non Joseph esurimus; Salvatorem, non somniatorem; dominatorem coeli, non Aegypti; non qui pavit ventres, sed qui pascit mentes, sed esurientes. In hoc saltem nos adjuvet sermo tuus, ut, quem esurimus, amplius esuriamus. Legimus enim: Beati qui esuriunt, quoniam ipsi saturabuntur. (Matth. v, 6). Cum audimus, magis esurimus. Nam et qui epulas commendat, famem irritat. Si de Jesu audierimus, auditui nostro dabitur gaudium et laetitia, et exsultabunt ossa humiliata. Humiliata sunt ossa nostra quadragesimali afflictione et luctu, magis autem dolore passionis ejus; sed exsultabunt nuntio resurrectionis ejus. Cur ergo tuum Joseph nobis ingeris, cum praeter Jesum, quidquid loquaris non sapiat nobis: praesertim hodie, cum paschalis agnus comeditur, cum Pascha nostrum immolatus est Christus?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. They brought news to Jacob, saying: Joseph is alive. On hearing this, his spirit revived, and he said: It is enough for me if Joseph my son is alive. I will go and see him before I die. (Gen. xlv, 26, 28). Perhaps you will say to me: Very well; but what has this to do with the matter? What has Joseph to do with the joy of this day, with the glory of Christ\u2019s resurrection? It is Easter, and you are serving Lenten fare again? Our soul hungers for the Paschal Lamb, for whom it has prepared itself through such long fasts. Our heart burns within us for Jesus; we long for Jesus, but we do not yet deserve to see him or hear about him. We hunger for Jesus, not Joseph; for the Saviour, not the dreamer; for the ruler of heaven, not Egypt; for him who feeds minds, and hungry minds at that, rather than him who fed bellies. At least let your sermon help us in this: to hunger still more for him for whom we hunger. For we read: Blessed are those who hunger, for they shall be filled. (Matth. v, 6). As we hear, we hunger more. For one who praises a feast also whets the appetite. If we hear about Jesus, joy and gladness will be granted to our hearing, and the bones brought low will exult. Our bones have been brought low by Lenten affliction and mourning, and still more by grief for his Passion; but they will exult at the news of his resurrection. Why, then, do you thrust your Joseph upon us, when whatever you say apart from Jesus has no savour for us, especially today, when the Paschal Lamb is eaten, when Christ our Passover has been sacrificed?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Ovum sive nucem apposui vobis, fratres; frangite testam, et invenietis escam. Joseph discutiatur, et Jesus invenietur agnus paschalis, quem esuritis; qui tanto dulcius comeditur, quanto latens abstrusius, et studiosius quaeritur, et difficilius invenitur Dicitis mihi: Quid Joseph ad Christum? quid historia quam proposui ad diem istum? Multum per omnem modum. Historiam recordamini, et ultro sese vobis revelabit pietas mysterii, si modo Jesum interpretem habeatis, qui, hodieque resurgens, de occidente littera loquitur suis in via, et aperit Scripturas. Quis enim in omnibus Patriarchis et Prophetis manifestius aut signantius quam Joseph figuram exprimit Salvatoris? Et, ut breviter summam perstringam, secundum illud: Da occasionem sapienti, et addetur ei sapientia (Prov. ix, 9), si cum fide ac pietate cogitemus interpretationem nominis ejus, deinde quod prae caeteris fratribus pulchra facie et decorus aspectu (Gen. xxxix, 6), quod innocens actu, prudens intellectu, quod venditus a suis, suos a morte redemit, quod prius humiliatus usque ad ergastulum, et sic exaltatus usque ad solium, quod denique pro merito operis sui novo nomine appellatus est inter gentes Salvator mundi (Gen. xli, 45); si haec omnia, inquam, pie ac fideliter cogitemus, nonne incunctanter agnoscimus quam vere sit a Domino dictum: In manibus Prophetarum assimilatus sum? (Ose. xii, 10)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. I have served you an egg or a nut, brothers; break the shell, and you will find food. Let Joseph be examined, and Jesus will be found, the Paschal Lamb for whom you hunger. He is eaten all the more sweetly when, hidden more deeply, he is sought more earnestly and found with greater difficulty. You say to me: What has Joseph to do with Christ? What has the story I have set before you to do with this day? Much in every way. Recall the story, and the loving mercy of the mystery will reveal itself to you of its own accord, provided you have Jesus as your interpreter, who, rising even today from the letter that kills, speaks to his own on the road and opens the Scriptures. For who among all the patriarchs and prophets expresses the figure of the Saviour more clearly or distinctly than Joseph? And, to touch briefly on the main points, according to the saying, Give the wise man an opportunity, and wisdom will be added to him (Prov. ix, 9): if we consider with faith and devotion the meaning of his name; then how, beyond his other brothers, he was beautiful in face and comely in appearance (Gen. xxxix, 6); how he was innocent in deed and prudent in understanding; how, sold by his own, he redeemed his own from death; how he was first humbled as far as the prison and then raised as high as the throne; and finally how, in recognition of his work, he was given a new name among the nations, Saviour of the world (Gen. xli, 45)\u2014if, I say, we consider all these things devoutly and faithfully, do we not acknowledge without hesitation how truly the Lord said: Through the hands of the prophets I have been represented in likeness? (Ose. xii, 10).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Jam vero si veniamus ad verba illa quae de historia proposui, puto quia non tam expositione indigebunt, quam admirationem et gaudium movebunt; quod videlicet tam evidenter resurrectio Christi testificata sit a Lege et Prophetis, et historia vetus tam proprie nova sacramenta loquatur, ut, cum prophetia legitur, pene Evangelium mutatis duntaxat nominibus audiri videatur. Nuntiaverunt, inquit, Jacob, dicentes: Joseph vivit. Quid aliud hic intelligere possum, nisi, Nuntiaverunt Apostolis dicentes: Jesus vivit? Jacob namque nihil aliud quam Apostolorum chorum intelligo; nec absurde, ut arbitror: non solum quia de Jacob orti sunt, non solum quia de Jacob Israel facti sunt, cum de lucta activae ad visionem et requiem contemplativae transierunt; sed etiam quia Patres sunt multitudinis credentium, id est verorum Israelitarum, sicut ille carnalium. Sicut ille, sic isti, suum Joseph cum putarent periisse, inconsolabiliter doluerunt; cum audissent vivere, difficulter et tarde crediderunt; cum cognovissent, ineffabiliter gavisi sunt. Nuntiaverunt, inquit, Jacob, dicentes: Joseph vivit. Quo audito Jacob, quasi de gravi somno evigilans, tamen non credebat eis (Gen. xlv, 26). Quasi aliis verbis mihi videtur dictum, quod in Evangelio legitur scriptum: Illa vadens, haud dubium quin Maria Magdalene, nuntiavit his qui cum ea fuerant lugentibus et flentibus; et illi audientes quia viveret et visus esset ab ea, non crediderunt. Post haec ostensus est duobus in via, et illi exeuntes nuntiaverunt caeteris, nec illis crediderunt (Marc. xvi, 10-13). Item apud Lucam. Et regressae a monumento, nuntiaverunt haec omnia illis undecim, et caeteris omnibus; et visa sunt ante illos sicut deliramentum verba ista, et non credebant illis (Luc. xxiv, 9, 11); nimirum quia evigilabant de gravi somno taedii et desperationis. Cum autem, inquit, vidisset Jacob universa quae Joseph miserat ei, revixit spiritus ejus, et ait: Sufficit mihi si Joseph filius meus vivit. Vadam et videbo eum, antequam moriar. (Gen. xlv, 27, 28). Sic utique in Apostolis parum profecerunt verba, donec acceperunt munera. Nam et ipse Jesus cum se eis praesentem exhibuit, non tam persuasit ostensione corporis, quam aspiratione muneris."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Now, if we turn to those words from the story which I set before you, I think they will need explanation less than they will stir wonder and joy. For Christ\u2019s resurrection is so plainly attested by the Law and the Prophets, and the ancient story speaks so aptly of the new mysteries, that when the prophecy is read, it seems almost as though we were hearing the Gospel with only the names changed. They brought news to Jacob, it says, saying: Joseph is alive. What else can I understand here but: They brought news to the apostles, saying: Jesus is alive? By Jacob I understand none other than the company of the apostles, and not unreasonably, I think. This is not only because they descended from Jacob, nor only because from Jacob they became Israel when they passed from the struggle of the active life to the vision and rest of the contemplative life, but also because they are fathers of the multitude of believers, that is, of the true Israelites, just as he was of the Israelites according to the flesh. Like him, when they thought their Joseph had perished, they grieved inconsolably; when they heard he was alive, they believed with difficulty and slowly; when they knew it, they rejoiced beyond words. They brought news to Jacob, it says, saying: Joseph is alive. On hearing this, Jacob, as though waking from a deep sleep, nevertheless did not believe them (Gen. xlv, 26). It seems to me to say in other words what we read written in the Gospel: She went\u2014undoubtedly Mary Magdalene\u2014and brought the news to those who had been with her, mourning and weeping; and when they heard that he was alive and had been seen by her, they did not believe. After this he appeared to two on the road, and they went out and brought the news to the others, but they did not believe them either (Marc. xvi, 10-13). Again, in Luke: Returning from the tomb, they told all these things to the eleven and to all the others; and these words seemed to them like nonsense, and they did not believe them (Luc. xxiv, 9, 11). For they were indeed waking from the deep sleep of weariness and despair. But when Jacob saw all that Joseph had sent him, it says, his spirit revived, and he said: It is enough for me if Joseph my son is alive. I will go and see him before I die. (Gen. xlv, 27, 28). So too, words made little headway with the apostles until they received gifts. For when Jesus himself showed himself present to them, he persuaded them less by displaying his body than by breathing his gift upon them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Scitis quia cum venit ad eos januis clausis, et stetit in medio eorum, illi conturbati et conterriti existimabant se spiritum videre (Luc. xxiv, 37). Cum autem insufflavit in eos, dicens: Accipite Spiritum sanctum (Joan. xx, 22), vel cum coelitus misit eumdem Spiritum, sed aliud donum, haec utique dona, resurrectionis et vitae indubitata testimonia fuerunt et argumenta. Spiritus enim est qui testificatur apud sanctorum corda et per eorum ora, quoniam Christus est veritas, vera resurrectio et vita. Ideo et Apostoli, qui prius dubitabant etiam post intuitum corporis viventis, post gustum spiritus vivificantis, virtute magna reddebant testimonium resurrectionis. Adeo plus est corde Jesum concipere, quam oculis videre, vel auribus de ipso audire; tantoque potentior est operatio Spiritus apud sensus hominis interioris, quam corporalium apud sensus exterioris. Quis enim dubietati relinquatur locus, ubi qui testificatur, et cui testificatur, unus fuerit Spiritus? Si unus Spiritus et unus sensus, parque consensus. Tunc ergo vere, sicut de Jacob legitur, revixit eorum spiritus, qui jam propemodum luctu erat exstinctus, imo desperatione sepultus. Tunc, nisi fallor, quisque eorum dicebat sibi: Sufficit mihi quod Joseph meus vivit; quia vivere mihi Christus est, et mori lucrum. Vadam igitur in Galilaeam, in montem ubi constituit nobis Jesus, et videbo eum, et adorabo antequam moriar, ut postea nunquam moriar; quia omnis qui videt Filium et credit in eum, habet vitam aeternam, ut, etiamsi mortuus fuerit, vivat (Joan. vi, 40, et xi, 26)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. You know that when he came to them with the doors closed and stood in their midst, they were troubled and terrified and thought they were seeing a spirit (Luc. xxiv, 37). But when he breathed upon them, saying: Receive the Holy Spirit (Joan. xx, 22), or when he sent from heaven the same Spirit but a different gift, these gifts were indeed unquestionable testimonies and proofs of resurrection and life. For it is the Spirit who bears witness in the hearts of the saints and through their mouths that Christ is the truth, the true resurrection and life. Thus the apostles, who had doubted even after looking upon his living body, after tasting the life-giving Spirit bore witness to the resurrection with great power. So much greater is it to receive Jesus in the heart than to see him with the eyes or hear about him with the ears; and so much more powerful is the Spirit\u2019s action upon the senses of the inward man than that of bodily things upon the senses of the outward man. For what room can remain for doubt when the one who bears witness and the one to whom witness is borne are one Spirit? If there is one Spirit, there is also one perception and equal agreement. Then truly, as we read of Jacob, their spirit revived, which had already been almost extinguished by grief, indeed buried by despair. Then, unless I am mistaken, each of them said to himself: It is enough for me that my Joseph is alive, for to me to live is Christ and to die is gain. I will therefore go to Galilee, to the mountain where Jesus has appointed to meet us, and I will see him and worship before I die, so that thereafter I may never die; for everyone who sees the Son and believes in him has eternal life, so that even if he has died, he may live (Joan. vi, 40, et xi, 26).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Nunc ergo, fratres mei, quid vobis testatur gaudium cordis vestri de amore Christi? Puto equidem (an recte, vos videritis) quoniam si unquam amastis Jesum, seu vivum, seu mortuum, sive redivivum, hodie, cum tam crebro in Ecclesia personant et consonant nuntii Resurrectionis, cor vestrum in vobis gloriatur sibi, et dicit: Nuntiaverunt mihi, dicentes: Jesus Deus meus vivit. Quo audito revixit spiritus meus, qui dormitabat prae taedio, vel languebat tepore, aut nimia deficiebat pusillanimitate. Nam et de morte suscitat criminosos jucunda vox hujus felicis nuntii. Alioquin certe desperandus est et oblivione sepeliendus, quem Jesus, regrediens ab inferis, in intimo reliquit inferni. In hoc sane noveris quod spiritus tuus plene in Christo revixerit. si quod sequitur ex sententia dixerit: Sufficit mihi, si Jesus vivit. O vocem fidelem et plane dignam amicis Jesu! o castissimum affectum, qui sic loquitur: Sufficit mihi si Jesus vivit! Si vivit, vivo, cum de ipso pendeat anima mea, imo ipse sit vita mea, ipse sufficientia mea. Quid enim mihi deesse poterit, si Jesus vivit? Imo desint omnia alia, nihil interest mea, dummodo Jesus vivat. Ipse ergo, si placet ei, desit mihi; sufficit mihi, dummodo vivat ipse vel sibi. Cum sic amor Christi totum absorbuerit affectum hominis, ut, negligens et immemor sui nonnisi Jesum Christum et ea quae sunt Jesu Christi sentiat, tunc demum, ut arbitror, perfecta est in eo charitas. Huic utique qui sic affectus est, non est onerosa paupertas: iste non sentit injurias, ridet opprobria, contemnit damna, mortem lucrum deputat; imo nec mori se putat, cum magis de morte ad vitam transire se sciat, et fidenter dicat; Vadam et videbo eum, antequam moriar."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Now then, my brothers, what does the joy of your heart testify to you about love for Christ? I think indeed\u2014you must judge whether rightly\u2014that if you have ever loved Jesus, whether living, dead, or restored to life, today, when the messengers of the Resurrection sound out so often and in harmony in the Church, your heart within you takes its delight and says: They brought me news, saying: Jesus my God is alive. On hearing this, my spirit revived, which had been dozing from weariness, languishing in lukewarmness, or failing through excessive faintheartedness. For the joyful voice of this happy news raises even sinners from death. Otherwise, surely, that man must be given up for lost and buried in oblivion whom Jesus, returning from the underworld, has left in its deepest depths. By this you may know for certain that your spirit has fully come to life again in Christ: if it sincerely says what follows, It is enough for me if Jesus is alive. O faithful utterance, entirely worthy of Jesus\u2019 friends! O most chaste love, which speaks in this way: It is enough for me if Jesus is alive! If he lives, I live, since my soul depends upon him; indeed, he himself is my life, he himself all that suffices me. For what could I lack if Jesus is alive? Indeed, let everything else be lacking: it matters nothing to me, provided Jesus lives. Let even he himself, then, be absent from me, if that pleases him; it is enough for me, provided he himself lives, even for himself alone. When love for Christ has so absorbed all a person\u2019s affection that, neglectful and forgetful of self, that person is mindful only of Jesus Christ and the things of Jesus Christ, then at last, I think, charity is perfected in that person. For one so disposed, poverty is no burden; he does not feel injuries, laughs at reproaches, despises losses, and counts death as gain. Indeed, he does not even think he is dying, knowing rather that he is passing from death to life, and confidently saying: I will go and see him before I die.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Nos tamen, fratres mei, licet non simus tantae nobis puritatis conscii, eamus nihilominus, eamus videre Jesum in montem Galilaeae coelestis, ubi constituit nobis. Eundo crescet affectus, et saltem perveniendo perficietur. Eundo, via prius arcta et difficilis dilatatur, et virtus infirmis augetur. Ut enim nec Jacob, nec aliquis de domo Jacob se excusaret a via praeter alia munera, missa sunt pauperi seni sumptus et vehicula, ne quis videlicet aut paupertatem causaretur, aut infirmitatem. Caro Christi est viaticum, spiritus vehiculum. Ipse est cibus, ipse currus Israel et auriga ejus. Cum perveneris, tua sunt optima quaeque, non Aegypti, sed coeli: in optimo loco regni tuus Joseph tibi requiem providit. Qui prius misit Angelos, mulieres, et Apostolos resurrectionis suae testes et nuntios, nunc ipse clamat e coelo: Ecce ego quem mortuum lugebatis hoc triduo, mortuus quidem fui propter vos, sed ecce vivo, et data est mihi omnis potestas in coelo et in terra (Matth. xxviii, 18). Venite ad me, qui laboratis fame: et ego reficiam vos. (Id. xi, 28), Venite, benedicti Patris mei, percipite regnum quod vobis paravi (Id, xxv, 34). Qui vos eo vocat, ipse perducat, ubi cum Patre et Spiritu sancto vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Yet, my brothers, although we are not conscious of such purity in ourselves, let us go nonetheless; let us go to see Jesus on the mountain of heavenly Galilee, where he has appointed to meet us. As we go, love will grow, and at least on arriving it will be perfected. As we go, the road, at first narrow and difficult, grows wider, and strength increases for the weak. For, so that neither Jacob nor anyone of Jacob\u2019s household might excuse himself from the journey, besides the other gifts, provisions and vehicles were sent to the poor old man, so that no one might plead either poverty or weakness. Christ\u2019s flesh is provision for the journey; the spirit is the conveyance. He himself is the food, he himself the chariot of Israel and its driver. When you arrive, all the best things are yours, of heaven rather than Egypt: in the best place in the kingdom your Joseph has provided rest for you. He who first sent angels, women, and apostles as witnesses and messengers of his resurrection now himself cries from heaven: Behold, I whom you mourned as dead these three days was indeed dead for your sake, but behold, I live, and all power in heaven and on earth has been given to me (Matth. xxviii, 18). Come to me, you who labour with hunger, and I will refresh you. (Id. xi, 28), Come, blessed of my Father, receive the kingdom which I have prepared for you (Id, xxv, 34). May he who calls you there himself lead you to where, with the Father and the Holy Spirit, he lives and reigns for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints the Latin title RESSURRECTIONE. The opening quotation from Genesis belongs to the first paragraph."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Mark quotation reads \u201cthose who had been with her,\u201d rather than the familiar Gospel wording \u201cwith him.\u201d The Latin and English follow the sermon. Guerric\u2019s comparison between Jacob and Israel and the Apostles is his own typological interpretation."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites John 6:40 and 11:26. Its combined quotation also draws on John 11:25 (\u201ceven if he has died\u201d)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The sermon adapts Christ\u2019s invitations: \u201cyou who labour with hunger,\u201d \u201cI will refresh you,\u201d and \u201cthe kingdom which I have prepared for you.\u201d The abbreviation Id. refers back to Matthew."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Resurrection Sermon I: Joseph Lives, Jesus Lives", "topics": ["Resurrection", "Easter", "hope", "Holy Spirit", "charity", "Eucharist", "Scripture"], "url": "sermons/guerric-resurrection-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:resurrexi", "calendar_label": "Easter Sunday of the Resurrection", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-resurrection-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De duplici nostra resurrectione., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 144-148.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Easter", "Resurrection of the Lord", "Revelation 20:5 (printed locator; quotation from Revelation 20:6)", "John 11:25", "Philippians 3:21", "Romans 8:11", "Revelation 20:6", "Romans 5:14", "Romans 6:9", "Hosea 13:14", "1 Corinthians 15:55\u201356", "Romans 5:12", "Romans 4:25", "Philippians 3:7\u201311", "Philippians 1:21", "Isaiah 55:2", "Sirach 10:10", "Acts 20:24", "Isaiah 55:1", "Song of Songs 5:1", "Hebrews 3:14", "Job 20:15, 21, 23", "Hebrews 6:7\u20138"], "occasion": "De Resurrectione Domini", "occasion_detail": "Resurrection of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Easter", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads the blessing on those who share in the first resurrection. Christ is both resurrections: by the sacrament of his rising he works the first, of souls, and by its example he will work the second, of bodies. Death reigned from Adam until Christ, free of sin and of the debt of death, died and rose for us. Guerric calls the poor to buy and eat the paschal bread, and warns those who squander in idle talk the sweetness received in devotion. The sermon appears under the Resurrection heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De duplici nostra resurrectione."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On our twofold resurrection.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Beatus et sanctus qui habet partem in resurrectione prima (Apoc. XX, 5). Ego sum, inquit Jesus, resurrectio et vita (Joan. XI, 25). Ipse utique resurrectio prima, ipse et resurrectio secunda. Resurgens quippe a mortuis primiti\u00e6 dormientium Christus, et sacramento su\u00e6 resurrectionis operatus est nobis resurrectionem primam, et exemplo ejusdem su\u00e6 resurrectionis operabitur nobis secundam. Prima enim est animarum, cum eas sibi conresuscitat in novitatem vitae: secunda erit corporum cum reformabit corpus humilitatis nostrae, configuratum corpori claritatis suae (Philipp. iii, 21). Recte igitur resurrectionem et vitam Christus se profitetur, quandoquidem per ipsum et in ipsum resurgimus, ut secundum ipsum et apud ipsum vivamus: nunc quidem secundum ipsum, in sanctitate et justitia; postmodum apud ipsum, in beatitudine et gloria. Porro sicut resurrectio prima capitis nostri Domini Jesu Christi causa est et argumentum secundae resurrectionis, quae erit totius ipsius corporis, sic unicuique nostrum prima resurrectio animae, qua reviviscit a morte peccati, argumentum est et causa secundae suae resurrectionis, qua corpus liberabitur non modo a corruptione mortis, sed etiam ab omni corruptibilitate mortalitatis. Quod enim haec illius argumentum et causa sit, Apostolus evidenter ostendit, ubi ait: Si spiritus Christi habitat in vobis, qui suscitavit Christum a mortuis vivificabit et mortalia corpora vestra, propter inhabitantem spiritum ejus in vobis (Rom. viii, 11)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Blessed and holy is he who has a part in the first resurrection (Apoc. XX, 5). I am, says Jesus, the resurrection and the life (Joan. XI, 25). He himself is indeed the first resurrection, and he himself the second resurrection. For Christ, rising from the dead, the firstfruits of those who sleep, has by the sacrament of his own resurrection wrought for us the first resurrection, and by the example of that same resurrection of his will accomplish for us the second. For the first is of souls, when he raises them together with himself into newness of life; the second will be of bodies, when he will refashion the body of our lowliness, conformed to the body of his glory (Philipp. iii, 21). Rightly, therefore, does Christ declare himself to be resurrection and life, since it is through him and in him that we rise, that according to him and with him we may live: now indeed according to him, in holiness and justice; afterward with him, in blessedness and glory. Moreover, just as the first resurrection of our Head, the Lord Jesus Christ, is the cause and proof of the second resurrection, which will be that of his whole body, so for each one of us the first resurrection of the soul, by which it revives from the death of sin, is the proof and cause of his second resurrection, by which the body will be freed not only from the corruption of death but also from all corruptibility of mortality. That the one is indeed the proof and cause of the other, the Apostle clearly shows where he says: If the Spirit of Christ dwells in you, he who raised up Christ from the dead will also give life to your mortal bodies, because of his Spirit dwelling in you (Rom. viii, 11).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3 Bene igitur dicitur. Beatus et sanctus qui habet partem in prima resurrectione (Apoc. xx, 6). Sanctus videlicet propter primam, quam jam adeptus est per innovationem animae; beatus propter secundam, quam feliciter exspectat in corporis restitutione. Denique unde beatus sit, eadem aperit Scriptura, cum subdit: In his scilicet qui partem habent in prima resurrectione, mors secunda non habet potestatem, etiamsi prima mors visa est ad horam in eis exercuisse dominationem. Regnavit quippe mors ab Adam usque ad Moysen, etiam in eos qui peccaverunt in similitudinem praevaricationis Adae (Rom. v, 14). Sed sicut Christus, sic et christianus resurgens a mortuis, jam non moritur, mors illi ultra non dominabitur (Id. vi, 9). Itaque in beatis illic nec mors secunda potestatem habebit, nec prima potestatem quam ad tempus habuit, retinebit; quoniam mors una Christi utramque nostram triumphavit, ab ista liberans jam captos, ab illa, capiendos; ne videlicet in illam incidamus, nec in ista remaneamus. Quam vera, quam pia simul et magnifica illa morientis comminatio: Ero mors tua, o mors (Osee. xiii, 14)! Quam pulchre et mirifice triumphavit, qui, mortem pro omnibus gustans, tam suam quam omnium omnimodam mortem absorbuit! Prorsus absorpta est mors in victoria. Securus insultet ei quicunque beatus ille est, qui partem habet in resurrectione prima: Ubi est, mors, victoria tua? ubi est, mors, stimulus tuus? Victa es, quae omnia vincebas; sed et arma perdidisti, in quibus confidebas. Ubi enim est stimulus tuus? Stimulus mortis peccatum (I Cor. xv, 55, 56), quod semel pungens humani radicem generis, in totam propaginem immedicabile mortis venenum diffudit, dicente Apostolo quia per unum hominem peccatum, et per peccatum mors, et ita in omnes homines pertransivit (Rom. v, 12). Victrix ergo regnabat mors ab Adam primo etiam usque ad secundum; quoniam sicut originali lege tenebatur universum genus humanum vinculo peccati, sic etiam debito moriendi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "3. Rightly, then, is it said: Blessed and holy is he who has a part in the first resurrection (Apoc. xx, 6). He is holy, namely, on account of the first resurrection, which he has already attained through the renewal of the soul; he is blessed on account of the second, which he happily awaits in the restoration of the body. Finally, Scripture itself reveals the reason for this blessedness when it adds: namely, that in those who have a part in the first resurrection, the second death has no power, even though the first death seemed for a time to have exercised dominion over them. For death reigned from Adam to Moses, even over those who sinned after the likeness of Adam's transgression (Rom. v, 14). But just as Christ, so too the Christian, rising from the dead, no longer dies; death will no longer have dominion over him (Id. vi, 9). And so among the blessed there, neither will the second death have power, nor will the first retain the power it held for a time; since the one death of Christ triumphed over both our deaths, freeing from the one those already captive, and from the other those who were to be captured\u2014namely, that we might not fall into that second death, nor remain in this first one. How true, how pious and at the same time how magnificent is that threat of the dying Christ: I will be your death, O death! (Osee. xiii, 14). How beautifully and wonderfully he triumphed, who, tasting death for all, swallowed up death utterly, both his own and that of all! Death is indeed swallowed up in victory. Let whoever is that blessed one who has a part in the first resurrection boldly taunt death: Where, O death, is your victory? Where, O death, is your sting? You who conquered all things are conquered; and you have lost also the weapons in which you trusted. For where is your sting? The sting of death is sin (I Cor. xv, 55, 56), which, once piercing the root of the human race, spread an incurable poison of death through the whole lineage, as the Apostle says, that through one man sin, and through sin death, entered, and so passed into all men (Rom. v, 12). Death, then, reigned as victor from the first Adam even to the second, since, just as the whole human race was held bound by the original law in the bond of sin, so also it was held under the debt of dying.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Deo autem gratias, qui dedit nobis victoriam tam peccati quam mortis, utique per Dominum Jesum Christum, qui prorsus a peccato immunis, ac per hoc liber a debito mortis, hanc tamen ultro pro nobis exsolvit moriens, et peccato nos absolvit resurgens. Christus enim, ait Apostolus, mortuus est propter delicta nostra, et resurrexit propter justificationem nostram (Rom. iv, 25) Moriens namque delictorum nostrorum pendit poenam, et resurgens justificationis perpetuae nobis condidit formam et causam, ut, quomodo videlicet Christus resurgens a mortuis jam non moritur, mors illi ultra non dominabitur (Rom. vi, 9), sic christianus conresurgens Christo jam non peccet ad mortem, nec ei peccatum ultra dominetur. Hic est ille beatus et sanctus, qui partem habet in resurrectione prima, in quem nec potestatem habebit mors secunda, sed absorbebitur in victoriam resurrectionis Christi etiam mors prima. Hic est qui non solum agnovit, sed etiam apprehendit virtutem resurrectionis Christi, et societatem passionis illius; configuratus morti ejus, ut occurrat ad resurrectionem ex mortuis. Non errabat Apostolus, qui, ob lucrum illud, omnia quae ei lucra fuerant non solum detrimenta reputabat, sed etiam velut stercora arbitrabatur, tantum ut Christo inveniretur, configuratus sicut morti, sic et resurrectioni ipsius (Philipp. iii, 7-11). Lucrosum omnino commercium, contemnere ea quae te deterunt et polluunt, ut lucrifacias Christum; imo, si opus sit, non solum tua, sed et te ipsum superimpendere, ut cum tanto fenore immortalitatis et gloriae te ipsum merearis recipere! Quis enim ambigat lucrosum esse commercium, seminare corpus mortale, animale, ignobile, ut surgat immortale, spirituale, gloriosum; mori mundo, ut possis dicere, Mihi vivere Christus est, et mori lucrum? (Id. i, 21.) O cupidi, quid immoramini studio lucrandi? cur non discitis artem lucrandi? cur non vilia, imo detrimenta et stercora contemnitis, ut Christum lucrifaciatis? Quare appenditis argentum non in panibus, et laborem vestrum non in saturitate (Isai. lv, 2)? Ut video, vilior est vobis argento vestro panis ille qui de coelo descendit, et dat vitam mundo. Sed non potest scire quanti [ alias quantus. ] sit, qui non vult gustare qualis sit. Utinam avarus vel ipse sibi pretiosior esset pecunia sua, nec amore illius venalem exponeret animam suam, projiceretque in vita sua intima sua (Eccli. x, 10). Prudens ille negotiator dignusque rerum aestimator, Paulum nimirum loquor, qui nec animam suam, id est animalem sensualemque vitam, pretiosiorem fecit se ipso (Act. xx, 24), id est spiritu suo, quo videlicet connexus inhaerebat Christo, paratus animam perdere, ut in vitam aeternam posset eam custodire."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Thanks be to God, who gave us victory over both sin and death, namely through our Lord Jesus Christ, who, being entirely free from sin, and therefore free from the debt of death, nevertheless willingly paid that death for us by dying, and absolved us from sin by rising again. For Christ, says the Apostle, died for our sins, and rose again for our justification (Rom. iv, 25). For by dying he paid the penalty of our sins, and by rising he established for us the pattern and cause of perpetual justification, so that just as Christ, rising from the dead, dies no more, and death shall no longer have dominion over him (Rom. vi, 9), so the Christian, rising together with Christ, may no longer sin unto death, nor may sin have dominion over him any longer. This is that blessed and holy one who has a part in the first resurrection, over whom the second death shall have no power, but even the first death shall be swallowed up in the victory of Christ's resurrection. This is he who has not only recognized but also laid hold of the power of Christ's resurrection, and the fellowship of his passion; being conformed to his death, so that he may attain to the resurrection from the dead. The Apostle was not mistaken, who, for the sake of that gain, counted all the things that had been gains to him not only as losses, but even reckoned them as dung, provided only that he might be found in Christ, conformed both to his death and to his resurrection (Philipp. iii, 7-11). It is altogether a profitable exchange to despise the things that wear you down and defile you, so that you may gain Christ; indeed, if need be, to spend not only your goods but even yourself, so that with such great interest of immortality and glory you may deserve to receive yourself back! For who could doubt that it is a profitable exchange, to sow a mortal, natural, ignoble body, that it may rise immortal, spiritual, glorious; to die to the world, so that you may say, For to me to live is Christ, and to die is gain? (Id. i, 21.) O you who are greedy, why do you linger in your zeal for gaining? Why do you not learn the art of gaining? Why do you not despise cheap things \u2014 indeed losses and dung \u2014 so that you may gain Christ? Why do you weigh out your silver for that which is not bread, and your labor for that which does not satisfy (Isai. lv, 2)? As I see it, that bread which came down from heaven and gives life to the world is of less worth to you than your silver. But one who refuses to taste what it is like cannot know how great it is. Would that the miser were at least more precious to himself than his money, and would not, for love of it, offer his own soul up for sale, and cast away his inmost self while yet alive (Eccli. x, 10). That prudent merchant and worthy appraiser of things \u2014 I mean, of course, Paul \u2014 who did not reckon his own soul, that is, his natural and sensual life, more precious than himself (Act. xx, 24), that is, than his spirit, by which, closely joined, he clung to Christ, ready to lose his soul so that he might keep it unto eternal life.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Quia igitur qui divitias habent, difficile intrant in regnum coelorum; et qui thesaurizant argentum, citius appendunt illud in inanibus, quam in panibus, in azymis dico sinceritatis et veritatis, cum quibus hodie comedendus est Agnus paschalis; vos beati pauperes, filii pauperis Crucifixi, vos inquam, qui non habetis argentum, properate, emite et comedite. Promptius omnino et facilius emunt bonum illud nihil habentes, quam multa possidentes. Cum deest facultas, sufficit ad emendum bona voluntas, qua plerumque sunt ditiores, qui rebus sunt pauperiores. Hos nimirum recte invitat Scriptura: Venite, emite absque argento, et absque ulla commutatione, vinum et lac (Isai. LV, 1). Vides, o felix pauper, solam a te voluntatem bonam exigi, in illa sola rationem tanti constare commercii? Ne recuses ingratus quod tam gratis offertur, imo ne perdas ingrata voluntate, quod jam merueras beata paupertate. Agnosce quanti lucri sit, non habere partem in ruina mundi, ut partem habeas in resurrectione Christi. Intellige quantae felicitatis sit, non inebriari luxu et furore saeculi, ut cum Christo novum bibas vinum in regno Patris sui. Invitat ipse Agnus paschalis amicos ad delicias corporis et sanguinis sui: Comedite, inquiens, amici, bibite, et inebriamini, charissimi (Cantic. V, 1). Cibus iste et potus, vitae est mysterium, immortalitatis medicamentum, causa resurrectionis primae, pignusque secundae, quia divinae plane in nobis initium substantiae. Participes, inquit Apostolus, Christi effecti sumus, si tamen initium substantiae ejus usque ad finem firmum retineamus (Hebr. III, 14). Qui enim post acceptam gratiam ad vomitum suum revertitur, Divitias quas devoravit evomet, et de ventre illius extrahet eas Deus. Vel certe panis ejus in utero illius vertetur in fel aspidum intrinsecus: quia nimirum gratia percepta in poenam conscientiae vertitur, cum sanguis Testamenti, quo sanctificatus quis fuerat, pollutus ducitur, et Spiritui gratiae contumelia irrogatur. Hoc contemptu et nausea divitiae devoratae evomuntur, ita ut de eo qui ejusmodi est, dicatur: Non remansit quidquam de cibo ejus, et propterea nihil permanebit de bonis illius."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Therefore, since those who have riches enter the kingdom of heaven with difficulty, and those who hoard up silver sooner weigh it out for empty things than for bread \u2014 I mean the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth, with which the paschal Lamb is to be eaten today \u2014 you blessed poor, children of the poor Crucified One, you, I say, who have no silver, hasten, buy and eat. Those who have nothing buy that good thing far more readily and easily than those who possess much. When means are lacking, good will suffices for the purchase \u2014 and those who are poorer in goods are often the richer in that very will. It is indeed such as these whom Scripture rightly invites: Come, buy wine and milk without silver, and without any price (Isai. LV, 1). Do you see, O happy poor man, that only good will is demanded of you, and that the whole worth of so great a transaction consists in that alone? Do not ungratefully refuse what is offered so freely; rather, do not lose, by an ungrateful will, what you had already merited by blessed poverty. Recognize how great a gain it is to have no part in the ruin of the world, so that you may have a part in the resurrection of Christ. Understand how great a happiness it is not to be made drunk with the luxury and fury of this age, so that you may drink new wine with Christ in the kingdom of his Father. The paschal Lamb himself invites his friends to the delights of his body and blood: Eat, he says, O friends, drink, and be inebriated, O dearly beloved (Cantic. V, 1). This food and drink is the mystery of life, the medicine of immortality, the cause of the first resurrection, and the pledge of the second, because it is plainly in us the beginning of divine substance. We have been made partakers, says the Apostle, of Christ, if indeed we hold the beginning of his substance firm unto the end (Hebr. III, 14). For he who, after receiving grace, returns to his own vomit, shall vomit up the riches he had devoured, and God shall draw them out of his belly. Or certainly, within him, his bread shall be turned to the gall of asps in his belly \u2014 because indeed the grace received is turned into punishment of conscience, when the blood of the Testament, by which one had been sanctified, is accounted polluted, and insult is done to the Spirit of grace. By this contempt and loathing, the riches that had been devoured are vomited up, so that of such a one it is said: Nothing remained of his food, and therefore nothing of his goods shall remain.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Videat autem ne forte et ipsum terribilis ista perstringat sententia, qui, cum per gratiam devotionis repletus fuerit in bonis domus Dei, nihil prorsus ex illis in memoria retinet, ut memoriam abundantiae suavitatis divinae nobis eructet; non saporem in sermonibus, tanquam gustum in faucibus; non virtutem in moribus, tanquam succum in visceribus; sed dum statim totum per inania vel scurrilia revomit, gratiam sibi in iram convertit. Prorsus in iram, et iram terribilem, si in ipsum cadit quod Scriptura contexuit dicens: Cum satiatus fuerit, arctabitur, et irruet in eum omnis dolor. Utinam impleatur venter ejus, ut immittat in eum iram furoris sui, et pluat super eum bellum suum (Job XX, 15, 21, 23). Justum quippe sentiebat atque consentiebat Propheta ut pluat super peccatores bellum, qui ad pluviam voluntariam, fructum pacis non retulerunt; sitque ignis, sulphur et spiritus procellarum pars calicis eorum, qui calicem Domini indigne biberunt. Terra enim saepe venientem bibens imbrem, et generans herbam opportunam, accipit benedictionem; proferens autem spinas et tribulos, reproba est et maledictio proxima; cujus consummatio in combustionem (Hebr. VI, 7, 8). Confidimus autem de vobis, fratres, meliora et viciniora saluti. Tantum gratiae Dei grati sitis, et sicut in novam creaturam translati estis paschalibus mysteriis, sic in novitate vitae semper ambuletis. Qui ergo participes Christi effecti estis societate fidei, participatione sacramenti, communione Spiritus sancti, initium substantiae ejus non solum usque ad finem firmum retinere, sed etiam sedulo augere nitimini: ut, qui per tot munerum praerogativas partem habere coepistis in resurrectione prima, tanti fiducia pignoris in die agnitionis consignati, jus vobis perpetuum vindicetis in resurrectione secunda, praestante Domino nostro Jesu Christo, resurrectione et vita nostra, qui pro nobis mortuus per triduum, jam vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But let him take care lest this terrible sentence strike him as well who, when he has been filled through the grace of devotion with the good things of the house of God, retains nothing at all of them in memory from which to belch forth\u2014no savor in his speech, as a taste lingering in the mouth; no virtue in his conduct, as sap in the inward parts\u2014but who, straightway vomiting back the whole through empty or scurrilous things, turns grace into wrath for himself. Into wrath indeed, and terrible wrath, if upon him falls what Scripture has woven together, saying: 'When he shall be filled, he shall be straitened, and every sorrow shall rush upon him. Would that his belly might be filled, that God may send upon him the wrath of His fury, and rain down His war upon him' (Job XX, 15, 21, 23). For the Prophet rightly judged, and rightly consented, that war should rain down upon sinners who, in return for the willing rain, did not bring forth the fruit of peace; and that fire, brimstone, and a spirit of storms should be the portion of the cup of those who drank the cup of the Lord unworthily. For the earth, which often drinks in the rain that comes upon it, and brings forth herbage fit for use, receives a blessing; but that which brings forth thorns and briers is worthless and near to a curse, whose end is to be burned (Hebr. VI, 7, 8). But we are confident of better things concerning you, brothers, and things nearer to salvation. Only be grateful for the grace of God, and just as you have been transformed into a new creature by the paschal mysteries, so may you always walk in newness of life. You, then, who have been made partakers of Christ through fellowship of faith, participation in the sacrament, and communion of the Holy Spirit, strive not only to hold firm the beginning of his substance even to the end, but also diligently to increase it: so that you, who through so many prerogatives of gifts have begun to have part in the first resurrection, sealed by the assurance of so great a pledge on the day of recognition, may claim for yourselves a perpetual right in the second resurrection, through the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, our resurrection and our life, having been dead for us for three days, now lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition numbers the second section 3, so two sections carry that number. The reading keeps the printed numeral."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The blessing on those who share in the first resurrection is Revelation 20:6. The edition prints Apoc. XX, 5 here and Apoc. xx, 6 when the verse returns in the second section."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Resurrection Sermon II", "topics": ["Resurrection", "Easter", "first resurrection", "sin", "death", "Eucharist", "poverty", "devotion"], "url": "sermons/guerric-resurrection-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:resurrexi", "calendar_label": "Easter Sunday of the Resurrection", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-resurrection-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., De vigilantia necessaria ut partem habeamus in resurrectione Christi., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 148-152.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Easter", "Resurrection of the Lord", "Revelation 20:6", "Psalm 83:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 3:6 (historical numbering)", "Ephesians 5:14", "Psalm 117:24 (historical numbering)", "John 8:56", "Matthew 27:61", "Luke 24:1\u20138", "Wisdom 6:13\u201315", "Proverbs 8:17", "Isaiah 26:9", "Psalm 62:2 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 89:14 (historical numbering)", "Romans 13:11\u201312", "Psalm 117:24, 27 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 6:9\u201310", "Wisdom 5:6", "Malachi 4:2", "Isaiah 33:15, 17", "Wisdom 6:17", "Luke 24:3\u20134, 13\u201327", "4 Kings 4:34\u201335 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Kings)", "Psalm 38:4 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 118:131 (historical numbering)", "Philippians 3:11"], "occasion": "De Resurrectione Domini", "occasion_detail": "Resurrection of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Easter", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric calls the brothers to watchfulness, so that they may share in the resurrection of Christ, the morning of a day without evening. Like Abraham and the women at the tomb, those who keep vigil at the doors of Wisdom see the risen Christ, who meets those who pray and those who work. Elisha's raising of the Shunammite's son marks the stages of the soul's return to life: a warmed heart, sighs that enlarge desire, and eyes opened to contemplation. The sermon appears under the Resurrection heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De vigilantia necessaria ut partem habeamus in resurrectione Christi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the vigilance necessary that we may have a part in the resurrection of Christ.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Beatus et sanctus qui habet partem in resurrectione prima (Apoc. XX, 6). Primitiae dormientium Christus, primogenitus ex mortuis, quia sua resurrectione, quae prima est omnium, et primam nobis resurrectionem animarum, et secundam corporum dedicavit; dum in corpore suo, quod suscitavit a mortuis, et animabus sacramentum, et corporibus exemplum resurgendi nuntiavit. Sed et animabus ipsis duplicem gratiam resurrectionis simplex Christi resurrectio praeparavit, dum et quotidie reviviscunt a morte peccati per operationem mysterii, et hodie maxime resurgunt a somno torporis per devotionem gaudii. Quis enim ille tam piger aut tepidus, qui hodie audiens vocem illam omni gaudio plenam, Resurrexit Dominus, non totus in exsultationem suscitetur, non totus reviviscat et recalescat spiritu? Imo et cor meum et caro mea exsultaverunt, inquit, in Deum vivum (Psal. LXXXIII, 3), qui totus concideram in moerorem ac desperationem, Jesum aspiciens mortuum. Non mediocri lucro fidei, nec parvo fenore gaudii, Jesus mihi de sepulchro redit, dum et Deus vivus agnoscitur, qui paulo ante ut homo mortuus lugebatur, et quem occisum cor meum dolebat, jam in eum vivum non solum cor meum, sed et caro mea exsultat, de sua scilicet per eum resurrectione et immortalitate secura. O anima mea, Ego dormivi et exsurrexi (Psal. III, 6), ait Christus; surge et tu, quae dormis, et exsurge a mortuis, et illuminabit te Christus (Ephes. V, 14). An non, fratres, mortuo similis, qui adhuc stetit orto jam sole, qui adhuc negligentia et socordia premitur, quasi desperato sepultus torpore, jam ubique gratia resurrectionis rutilante? Ferit oculos Sol novus emergens ab inferis, his qui de mane vigilant ad eum, diem reserans aeternitatis. Dies iste vesperam nescit, quia non occidet ultra sol ejus, qui semel occidens, semel ascendit super occasum, mortem sibi subjiciens."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Blessed and holy is he who has a part in the first resurrection (Apoc. XX, 6). Christ is the firstfruits of those who sleep, the firstborn from the dead, because by his resurrection, which is first of all, he consecrated for us both the first resurrection, that of souls, and the second, that of bodies; since in his body, which he raised from the dead, he announced to souls a sacrament, and to bodies an example, of rising again. But Christ's single resurrection also prepared a twofold grace of resurrection for souls themselves, since they daily come back to life from the death of sin through the working of the mystery, and today especially they rise from the sleep of sluggishness through the devotion of joy. For who is so sluggish or lukewarm that, hearing today that word so full of all joy, 'The Lord has risen,' is not wholly roused to exultation, does not wholly come back to life and grow warm again in spirit? Indeed, 'my heart and my flesh have exulted,' he says, 'in the living God' (Psal. LXXXIII, 3) \u2014 I who had wholly collapsed into grief and despair, beholding Jesus dead. With no small gain of faith, nor with slight interest of joy, Jesus returns to me from the tomb, since he who a little before, as man, was mourned as dead, is now recognized as the living God; and he whom, slain, my heart grieved over, now \u2014 living \u2014 not only my heart but my flesh as well exults in him, being made secure, through him, of its own resurrection and immortality. O my soul, 'I have slept and risen up' (Psal. III, 6), says Christ; rise up, you too who sleep, and arise from the dead, and Christ will enlighten you (Ephes. V, 14). Is he not, brothers, like one dead, who still stands motionless when the sun has already risen, who is still weighed down by negligence and sloth, as if buried in hopeless torpor, now that the grace of the resurrection glows red everywhere? The new Sun, emerging from the world below, strikes the eyes of those who watch for him early in the morning, unlocking for them the day of eternity. This day knows no evening, because its sun will set no more \u2014 he who, setting once, rose once above his setting, subjecting death to himself.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. O fratres, haec dies quam fecit Dominus, exsultemus et laetemur in ea (Psal. CXVII, 24). Exsultemus in spe ejus, ut videamus et gaudeamus in luce ejus. Exsultavit Abraham ut videret diem Christi, et hoc merito vidit, et gavisus est (Joan. VIII, 56). Nam et tu quoque si vigiles quotidie ad fores Sapientiae, et observes ad postes ejus, pervigilque excubes cum illa Magdalena ad ostium monumenti ejus (Matth. XXVII. 61), probabis, ni fallor, et ipse cum eadem Maria (Luc. XXIV, 1-8), quam verum sit quod de ipsa Sapientia, quae Christus est, legitur: Facile videtur ab his qui diligunt eam, et invenitur ab his qui quaerunt illam. Praeoccupat qui se concupiscunt, ut illis se prior ostendat. Qui de luce vigilaverit ad illam, non laborabit assidentem enim illam foribus suis inveniet (Sap. VI, 13-15). Sic enim et ipse promisit dicens: Ego diligentes me diligo, et qui mane vigilaverint ad me, invenient me (Prov. VIII, 17). Invenit quidem Maria corporaliter Jesum ad quem vigilabat, ad cujus monumentum, cum adhuc essent tenebrae, excubare venerat. Tu vero qui jam non debes nosse Jesum secundum carnem, sed secundum spiritum, spiritualiter nimirum eum invenire poteris, si simili eum desiderio quaesieris, si te similiter pervigilem in oratione adverterit. Dic ergo Domino Jesu desiderio et affectu Mariae: Anima mea desideravit te in nocte, sed et spiritus meus in praecordiis meis; de mane vigilabo ad te (Isai. XXVI, 9). Dic voce et mente Psalmistae: Deus, Deus meus, ad te de luce vigilo: sitivit in te anima mea (Psal. LXII, 2). Et vide si non cum eis tibi cantare congruerit: Repleti sumus mane misericordia tua et exsultavimus, et delectati sumus (Psal. LXXXIX, 14)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. O brothers, 'this is the day which the Lord made; let us exult and rejoice in it' (Psal. CXVII, 24). Let us exult in hope of it, that we may see and rejoice in its light. Abraham exulted that he might see the day of Christ, and this he deservedly saw, and rejoiced (Joan. VIII, 56). For you too, if you watch daily at the doors of Wisdom, and keep watch at her posts, and keep vigil, like that Magdalene, at the door of his tomb (Matth. XXVII. 61), will prove, unless I am mistaken, together with that same Mary (Luc. XXIV, 1-8), how true is what is read of Wisdom herself, who is Christ: 'She is easily seen by those who love her, and is found by those who seek her. She anticipates those who desire her, that she may show herself to them first. He who watches for her from early light shall not labor, for he shall find her sitting at his doors' (Sap. VI, 13-15). For he himself promised thus, saying: 'I love those who love me, and those who watch for me early shall find me' (Prov. VIII, 17). Mary indeed found, bodily, the Jesus for whom she watched, having come to keep vigil at his tomb while it was still dark. But you, who ought now to know Jesus no longer according to the flesh but according to the spirit, will surely be able to find him spiritually, if you seek him with a like desire, if he finds you likewise keeping vigil in prayer. Say therefore to the Lord Jesus, with the desire and affection of Mary: 'My soul has desired you in the night, and my spirit also within me; from the morning I will watch for you' (Isai. XXVI, 9). Say with the voice and mind of the Psalmist: 'O God, my God, from early light I watch for you; my soul has thirsted for you' (Psal. LXII, 2). And see whether it will not be fitting for you to sing with them: 'We have been filled in the morning with your mercy, and we have exulted and been delighted' (Psal. LXXXIX, 14).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Vigilate ergo, fratres, in orationibus intenti, vigilate in actionibus circumspecti; praesertim quia jam mane illius diei indeclinabilis inclaruit, postquam videlicet lux aeterna serenior nobis et gratior ab inferis rediit, solemque novum susceptio matutina reparavit. Prorsus hora est jam nos de somno surgere, postquam nox praecessit, dies autem appropinquavit (Rom. XIII, 11. 12). Vigilate, inquam, ut oriatur vobis matutina lux, Christus scilicet, cujus quasi diluculum praeparatus est egressus, paratus utique vigilantibus ad se, mysterium suae matutinae resurrectionis saepius innovare. Tunc utique cantabis in jubilo cordis, Deus Dominus illuxit nobis. Haec dies quam fecit Dominus, exsultemus et laetemur in ea (Psal. CXVII, 27. 24), cum videlicet lucem quam in manibus abscondit interlucere tibi permiserit, annuntians de ea amico quod possessio ejus sit, et ad eam possit ascendere. Usquequo, piger, dormis? usquequo dormitas? Paululum dormies, paululum dormitabis, paululum conseres manus ut dormias (Prov. VI, 9, 10, et XXIV, 33); et cum te dormiente, te nesciente Christus de sepulcro surrexerit, et gloria ejus transeunte, nec posteriora ejus videre merueris; tunc sera poenitentia planges, et dices cum impiis, Ergo erravimus a via veritatis, et justitiae lumen non luxit nobis, et sol intelligentiae non est ortus nobis (Sap. V, 6). Vobis autem, inquit, timentibus nomen meum, orietur sol justitiae (Malach. IV, 2); et, qui ambulat in justitiis, Regem in decore suo videbunt oculi ejus (Isai. XXXIII, 15, 17). Et id quidem beatitudo est vitae sequentis, sed juxta quemdam modum indulgetur etiam ad consolationem praesentis, sicut evidenter probat resurrectio Christi. In multis siquidem argumentis per dies quadraginta Sapientia nobis probavit quoniam dignos se ipsa circuit quaerens, et in viis suis ostendit se illis hilariter, et in omni prudentia occurrit illis (Sap. VI, 17.) Ut enim Jesus se illam esse sapientiam, de qua haec scripta sunt, ostenderet; et, quod quotidie spiritualiter actitare non desinit, id est, in viis justitiae se ostendere hilariter, hodie corporalis etiam operis specie figuraret, in via hodie occurrit mulieribus a monumento redeuntibus, in via se ostendit duobus euntibus in Emmaus (Luc. XXIV, 3, 4, 13-27)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Watch, then, brothers, intent on your prayers; watch, circumspect in your actions\u2014especially since now the morning of that unending day has shone forth, since indeed the eternal light, more serene and more welcome to us, has returned from the underworld, and the morning's rising has restored to us a new sun. Truly it is now the hour for us to rise from sleep, since the night is past and the day is at hand (Rom. XIII, 11. 12). Watch, I say, that the morning light may rise for you\u2014namely Christ, whose going forth is prepared like the dawn, ready indeed, for those who watch for him, to renew again and again the mystery of his morning resurrection. Then truly you will sing with the jubilation of your heart: 'The Lord God has shone upon us. This is the day which the Lord has made; let us exult and be glad in it' (Psal. CXVII, 27. 24)\u2014when, that is, he allows the light which he hides in his hands to shine through to you, declaring of it to his friend that it is his possession, and that he can ascend to it. How long, O sluggard, will you sleep? How long will you slumber? A little you will sleep, a little you will slumber, a little you will fold your hands to sleep (Prov. VI, 9, 10, et XXIV, 33); and when, while you are sleeping, while you know nothing of it, Christ has risen from the tomb, and his glory has passed by, and you have not merited even to see his back as he goes; then with late repentance you will weep, and say with the wicked: 'So we have strayed from the way of truth, and the light of righteousness has not shined on us, and the sun of understanding has not risen for us' (Sap. V, 6). But to you, he says, who fear my name, the sun of righteousness shall rise (Malach. IV, 2); and he who walks in righteousness\u2014his eyes shall see the King in his beauty (Isai. XXXIII, 15, 17). And this indeed is the blessedness of the life to come, but in a certain measure it is granted even for the consolation of the present life, as the resurrection of Christ plainly proves. For by many proofs, over forty days, Wisdom showed us that she herself goes about seeking out those worthy of her, and shows herself to them cheerfully in their ways, and meets them with all prudence (Sap. VI, 17.). For in order that Jesus might show himself to be that very Wisdom of whom these things are written, and might today figure, even under the appearance of a bodily deed, what he never ceases to do daily in a spiritual way\u2014that is, to show himself cheerfully in the ways of righteousness\u2014today he met the women returning from the tomb on the road, and on the road he showed himself to the two going to Emmaus (Luc. XXIV, 3, 4, 13-27).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Audiant et gaudeant qui in viis justitiae ambulant; audiant, inquam, quia non solum inhaerentes studio contemplationis, sed etiam ambulantes juste ac pie vias actionis, Jesus dignatur et occursu et manifestatione sui. Agnoscit, ni fallor, experientia quorumdam vestrum, quia saepe Jesus quem quaesierunt velut ad monumentum, ad memorias altarium, nec invenerunt, insperatus occurrit eis in viis laborum. Tunc nimirum accesserunt, et tenuerunt pedes ejus, quorum scilicet pedes non tenuerat pigritia, prae desiderio ejus. Noli ergo tu, frater, nimis parcere pedibus tuis a viis obedientiae et discursibus operum, quandoquidem Jesus pedibus suis non perpercit propter te etiam a dolore clavorum; et adhuc eorumdem amplexu et osculo pedum non gravatur laborem remunerare aut relevare pedum tuorum. Nam et illud quantae consolationis erit, si se tibi viae comitem adjunxerit, miraque delectatione suae sermocinationis, etiam sensum tibi laboris ademerit, aperiens tibi sensum, ut intelligas Scripturas, quas fortasse domi sedens legebas et non intelligebas? Obsecro vos, fratres mei, quibus hoc aliquando dedit experiri dignatio divina, nonne cor vestrum ardens erat in vobis de Jesu, cum loqueretur vobis in via, et aperiret Scripturas? Recordentur igitur illud experti, et cantent in viis Domini, quoniam magna est gloria Domini; credant, et experiri satagant inexperti, ut aliquando et ipsis cantabiles fiant justificationes Dei, in loco peregrinationis suae et afflictionis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Let those who walk in the ways of righteousness hear and rejoice; let them hear, I say, that Jesus deigns to grant both his meeting and the manifestation of himself not only to those who cling to the pursuit of contemplation, but also to those who walk justly and devoutly in the ways of action. The experience of some of you recognizes, unless I am mistaken, that often Jesus, whom they sought as it were at the tomb, at the memorials of the altars, and did not find, meets them unexpectedly in the ways of their labors. Then indeed they approached and held his feet\u2014those, namely, whose feet laziness had not held back, because of their desire for him. Do not, then, brother, spare your feet too much from the ways of obedience and the busy comings and goings of works, since Jesus did not spare his own feet for your sake, even from the pain of the nails; and even now he is not burdened, by the embrace and kiss of those same feet, to reward or relieve the labor of your feet. For how great a consolation it will be, if he joins himself to you as a companion on the road, and by the wondrous delight of his conversation even takes from you the very feeling of your labor, opening your understanding, that you may understand the Scriptures which perhaps, sitting at home, you were reading and not understanding? I beseech you, my brothers, to whom the divine condescension has at some time granted to experience this\u2014was not your heart burning within you concerning Jesus, when he spoke to you on the road and opened the Scriptures? Let those, then, who have experienced this remember it, and sing in the ways of the Lord, for great is the glory of the Lord; and let those who have not experienced it believe, and strive earnestly to experience it, that at some time the justifications of God may become songs even for them, in the place of their pilgrimage and affliction.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Resurgat itaque ac reviviscat spiritus omnium nostrum, sive ad vigilantiam orandi, sive ad instantiam operandi, ut quadam rediviva ac vivida alacritate probet se de novo portionem accepisse in resurrectione Christi. Et primum quidem vitae in hominem redeuntis indicium est, si strenuus et impiger sit ad actionem; nam perfecta resurrectio ejus est in hoc duntaxat moribundo corpore, si oculos aperiat ad contemplationem. Quod tamen non ante promeretur intellectus, quam crebris suspiriis et vehementibus desideriis dilatetur affectus, ut tantae nimirum capax majestatis reddatur. Quod videlicet velut per quosdam gradus incrementum resurgendi signanter figuratum arbitror in mortuo quem Elisaeus suscitavit, qui cum ad vitam redire inciperet, primo dicitur quia calefacta est caro pueri; deinde, quia septies oscitavit, et sic demum oculos aperuit (IV Reg. IV, 34, 35). Caro pueri, cor carneum est in Christo parvuli, cui prima spes vitae est, si dicere possit: Concaluit cor meum intra me, et in meditatione mea exardescet ignis (Psal. XXXVIII, 4). Quanquam et vestimenta ejus calida sint, cum terra ejus perflata austro, Spiritu utique sancto, quem verus Elisaeus praeveniendo aspirat redivivo. Amplius autem et evidentius ad resurrectionem proficit, cum desiderio et esurie quadam justitiae oscitare frequentius incipit, illo scilicet modo quo oscitabat qui dicebat: Os meum aperui et attraxi spiritum, quia mandata tua desiderabam (Psal. CXVIII, 131). Oscitatio quippe ista, distensio est affectus, ut capacior fiat spiritus vitae, quatenus post alia charismata septiformis gratiae, infusus etiam spiritus intellectus et sapientiae, oculos tandem ad contemplandum Deum faciat aperire. Primus ergo calor vitae redeuntis est, cum bonus exercetur actus: secundus resurrectionis profectus, cum per orationem dilatatur affectus: perfectio autem, cum ad contemplandum illuminatur intellectus. His gradibus virtutum, his sanctioris vitae incrementis, fratres mei, resurgere magis ac magis enitimini, si quo modo occurratis, ut Apostolus ait, ad resurrectionem Christi ex mortuis (Philipp. III, 11), qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Let the spirit of us all, therefore, rise again and come back to life, whether for watchfulness in prayer or for eagerness in working, so that by a certain revived and lively readiness it may prove that it has newly received a share in the resurrection of Christ. And the first sign indeed of life returning into a man is if he be vigorous and unwearied for action; for his resurrection is perfect in this mortal body only to this extent, that he open his eyes to contemplation. Yet the intellect does not merit this before the affection is first enlarged by frequent sighs and ardent desires, so that it may be made capable of so great a majesty. This increase of rising again I think is signified, as it were by certain steps, in the dead man whom Elisha raised to life; who, when he began to return to life, is said first to have had the flesh of the boy grow warm; then, that he yawned seven times, and only then at last opened his eyes (IV Reg. IV, 34, 35). The flesh of the boy is the heart of flesh in a little one in Christ, for whom the first hope of life is if he can say: My heart grew warm within me, and in my meditation a fire shall flame out (Psal. XXXVIII, 4). And indeed his garments too are warm, when his earth is fanned by the south wind \u2014 that is, by the Holy Spirit, whom the true Elisha, going before, breathes into the one being revived. But he progresses further and more plainly toward resurrection when he begins to yawn more frequently with a certain desire and hunger for righteousness, in that manner in which he yawned who said: I opened my mouth and drew in breath, because I longed for your commandments (Psal. CXVIII, 131). For that yawning is a stretching of the affection, so that the affection may become more capable of receiving the spirit of life, until, after the other gifts of the sevenfold grace, the spirit of understanding and wisdom also being poured in, it at last makes the eyes open to the contemplation of God. The first warmth, then, of returning life is when a good act is exercised; the second advance of resurrection, when the affection is enlarged through prayer; but the perfection, when the understanding is illumined for contemplation. By these steps of virtues, by these increases of a holier life, my brothers, strive to rise more and more, that you may somehow attain, as the Apostle says, to the resurrection of Christ from the dead (Philipp. III, 11), who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Resurrection Sermon III", "topics": ["Resurrection", "Easter", "vigilance", "prayer", "contemplation", "action", "spiritual growth"], "url": "sermons/guerric-resurrection-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-rogations", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN DIEBUS ROGATIONUM., SERMO, De pane verbi petendo et apponendo, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 151-154.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Rogation Days", "Rogation Days", "Isaiah 3:7", "Sirach 32:1", "Psalm 80:11 (historical numbering)", "Romans 10:12", "Psalm 144:16 (historical numbering)", "James 1:5", "Lamentations 4:5", "Sirach 3:21", "Hebrews 5:12", "1 Corinthians 14:3", "Luke 11:5"], "occasion": "In diebus Rogationum", "occasion_detail": "Rogation Days", "occasion_label": "Rogation Days", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric takes the friend who asks for three loaves at midnight as the figure of one who must feed his brothers with the word. He confesses that he presides by office without bread of his own, asks God to make him humble and useful, and pleads the merit of those to be fed. He leaves the loaves of speculation on the Trinity to the angels, since God is to be believed rather than discussed, and sets out other threefold loaves from Scripture. The sermon appears under the Rogation Days heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN DIEBUS ROGATIONUM."}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE DAYS OF ROGATION.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De pane verbi petendo et apponendo"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On asking for and setting forth the bread of the word", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Commoda mihi tres panes Luc. XI, 5). Amici venerunt ad nos de via, sed non habeo quod ponam ante illos. Non sum medicus, et in domo mea non est panis: ideo dicebam ab initio, Nolite me constituere principem (Isai. III, 7). Non enim debet praeesse, qui non potest prodesse. Quomodo autem prodesse potest, qui nec medicus est, nec panem in domo habet, id est, nec artem novit qua sciat curare, nec doctrinam habet qua sufficiat pascere? Haec quidem dicebam; sed heu! non audistis me: principem enim constituistis me. Supererat igitur ut, quia non potui effugere periculum, confugerem ad remedium, et audirem super hoc illud Sapientis consilium: Principem te constituerunt; esto inter illos quasi unus ex ipsis (Eccli. XXXII, 1). Sed vae mihi! neque hoc ipsum relictum est mihi. Sicut enim imperitia prohibet esse super alios, sic imbecillitas non patitur inter alios esse; et sicut spiritu non sufficio ad ministrandum verbum, sic corpore deficio ad praebendum exemplum. Qui igitur idoneus non sum nec praeesse, nec coesse: ubi potero esse, nisi ultimum et tutissimum eligam locum, scilicet omnibus subesse? Et hoc quidem valeo, humilia scilicet, imo vera de me sentiendo; nihilque me prohibet, imo plurimum veritas ipsa monet, subesse cunctis animo, licet praeesse cogar officio."}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Lend me three loaves\" Luc. XI, 5). Friends have come to us from the road, but I have nothing to set before them. I am not a physician, and in my house there is no bread: therefore I said from the beginning, \"Do not make me a ruler\" (Isai. III, 7). For he who cannot be of use ought not to be set over others. But how can he be of use who is neither a physician nor has bread in his house\u2014that is, who neither knows the skill by which he might know how to heal, nor has the teaching by which he might suffice to feed? This indeed I said; but alas! you did not listen to me: for you made me a ruler. It remained, then, since I could not escape the danger, that I should take refuge in the remedy, and hear on this matter that counsel of the Wise Man: \"They have made you a ruler; be among them as one of themselves\" (Eccli. XXXII, 1). But woe is me! not even this was left to me. For just as inexperience forbids being over others, so weakness does not allow one to be among others; and just as in spirit I am not sufficient to minister the word, so in body I fail to provide example. Since, then, I am fit neither to preside nor to stand as a companion among others, where can I be, unless I choose the last and safest place\u2014namely, to be beneath all? And this indeed I am able to do, that is, by thinking humbly\u2014or rather truly\u2014of myself; nor does anything prevent me, indeed the truth itself most strongly urges me, to be beneath all in spirit, though I am compelled by office to preside.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Tu, Domine Deus, ipse es qui et subesse mones, et nihilominus praeesse jubes; a te expeto, a te exspecto ut humilem simul et utilem in injuncto me facias ministerio: humilem, vera de me sentiendo; utilem, recta de te loquendo. Illud inspira cordi, hoc ministra ori. Da sermonem rectum et bene sonantem in apertione oris mei, qui dixisti, Aperi os tuum et implebo illud (Psal. LXXX, 11), ut omnis familia tua benedictionibus impleatur. Ecce venerunt amici, mei quidem amici, sed amplius tui. Non habeo quod ponam ante illos, nisi commodatum fuerit ab alio. Et quis alius aeque dives aut liberalis ad praestandum, sicut Dominus omnium, dives in omnes qui invocant illum (Rom. X, 12); qui aperit manum suam, et implet omne animal benedictione (Psal. CXLIV, 16), qui dat omnibus affluenter, et non improperat (Jacobi I, 5), nisi forte illi qui vel pigre petit, vel ingrate detinet gratiam quam accepit? Quanti mercenarii in domo Patrisfamilias istius abundant panibus, qui, quoniam Christum annuntiant, licet non sincere, propter alios tamen eis doctrinae gratia non negatur? Et ubi abundant mercenarii, nunquid egebunt filii? Tu ergo, Domine (amicum enim dicere vereor, sed Dominum confiteor), commoda mihi tres panes, unde reficiantur amici, ne, si dimisero eos jejunos, deficiant in via, et tunc vocer in causam pro eis, et dicatur mihi: Parvuli petierunt panem, et non fuit qui frangeret eis (Thren. IV, 5). Commoda Domine, quod in lucrum tuum proficiat, recepturus utique cum tibi placuerit, quod tuum est, cum usura. Commoda, inquam, tres panes, si placet; aut certe quodcunque placet, quantulumque fuerit, etiamsi buccella panis sufficiet in quantalibet millia, si tantum benedicas. Scio equidem quia vis nos esse tibi importunos, ut, licet dissimules, licet excuses, eo quod jam in coelum receptus sis, et Apostoli tui tecum sint in cubili, nos tamen nihilominus perseveremus in petendo, quaerendo, pulsando, sciente quia doctrinam non sola vitae meretur innocentia, qua amici efficimur, sine assiduitate studii et instantia precis, qua importuni videamur. Mihi tamen neutrum suffragatur: solum eorum qui pascendi sunt meritum obtendo; illi merentur quod ego non mereor."}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. You, Lord God, are the very one who both counsel me to be beneath, and nonetheless command me to preside; from you I ask, from you I expect, that you may make me at once humble and useful in the ministry laid upon me: humble, by thinking truly of myself; useful, by speaking rightly of you. Breathe the one into my heart; supply the other to my mouth. Give a right word, well-sounding, at the opening of my mouth, you who said, \"Open your mouth, and I will fill it\" (Psal. LXXX, 11), that all your household may be filled with blessings. Behold, friends have come\u2014my friends indeed, but yours even more. I have nothing to set before them unless it be lent me by another. And who else is equally rich or generous in granting, as the Lord of all, rich toward all who call upon him (Rom. X, 12); who opens his hand and fills every living thing with blessing (Psal. CXLIV, 16); who gives to all abundantly and does not reproach (Jacobi I, 5)\u2014unless perhaps to him who either asks sluggishly, or ungratefully withholds the grace he has received? How many hired men abound with loaves in the house of this Father of the family, who, because they proclaim Christ\u2014though not sincerely\u2014yet for the sake of others the grace of teaching is not denied them? And where hired men abound, shall the sons be in want? You, therefore, Lord\u2014for I fear to call you friend, yet I confess you Lord\u2014lend me three loaves, from which my friends may be refreshed, lest, if I send them away hungry, they faint along the way, and I then be called to account for them, and it be said to me: \"The little ones asked for bread, and there was none to break it for them\" (Thren. IV, 5). Lend, Lord, what will turn to your profit, since you will certainly receive back, when it pleases you, what is yours, with interest. Lend, I say, three loaves, if it please you; or indeed whatever pleases you, however small it be\u2014even if a single morsel of bread will suffice for however many thousands, if only you bless it. I know well that you wish us to be importunate with you, so that, although you pretend otherwise, although you make excuse on the ground that you have already been received into heaven, and your Apostles are with you in your chamber, we should nevertheless persevere in asking, seeking, knocking\u2014knowing that teaching is not merited by innocence of life alone, by which we become friends, without diligence of study and the insistence of prayer, by which we may seem importunate. Yet neither of these avails for me: I plead only the merit of those who are to be fed; they deserve what I do not deserve.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Vos autem, fratres mei, usibus quorum, et pro meritis quorum peto panes istos, putatis, sufficimus, sive ego ad frangendum, sive vos ad edendum? Timeo ne dicatur mihi: Altiora te ne quaesieris, et fortiora te ne scrutatus fueris (Eccli. III, 21). Timeo ne dicatur et vobis: Facti estis quibus opus est lacte, non pane (Hebr. V, 12). Scio revera panes esse apud Patrem familias, quos si nostra praesumat infantia, citius frangentur dentes, quam impleantur ventres, id est aedificentur mentes. Quis enim capiat, quis explicare aut cogitare digne sufficiat illud Trinitatis mysterium ineffabile: quomodo Pater a se, Filius a Patre, Spiritus sanctus ab utroque, et tres personae in unitate substantiae? Mulier insipiens, audax haereticorum vanitas, prurientes auribus ad talia discutienda sollicitat, cum Deus credendus sit, non discutiendus. Panes, inquit, occultos libenter attingite. Quasi vero tu insipiens attigeris, quod sublimius est Angelis. Et ad quid mihi panes occultos attingere, quos non licet sine periculo frangere vel edere? Sufficit mihi scire quia sunt, quia tres panes sunt; non dico Trinitatem personarum, sed trinitatem sermonum, vel magis intellectuum, qui de personis habendi sunt: tres, inquam, panes unius magnitudinis et ponderis, unius formae et saporis. Quanta enim et qualia de Patre, tanta et talia de Filio seu de Spiritu sancto sentienda sunt, excepto duntaxat, quod proprietatem discretio numerum facit personarum, sicut in Trinitate, sic intelligentiarum in earum distinctione."}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But you, my brothers, for whose use, and for whose merits, I ask these loaves \u2014 do you think we are sufficient, either I for the breaking, or you for the eating? I fear lest it be said to me: Seek not the things that are too high for thee, and search not into things above thy ability (Eccli. III, 21). I fear lest it also be said to you: You are become such as have need of milk, not of bread (Hebr. V, 12). I know indeed that there are loaves with the Father of the household which, if our infancy should presume upon them, our teeth would be broken sooner than our stomachs filled \u2014 that is, our minds built up. For who could grasp, who could suffice to explain or worthily consider that ineffable mystery of the Trinity: how the Father is from himself, the Son from the Father, the Holy Spirit from both, and yet three persons in unity of substance? A foolish woman, the bold vanity of heretics, stirs up those whose ears itch to discuss such things, though God is to be believed, not discussed. 'Gladly touch hidden loaves,' she says. As though you, foolish one, had touched what is loftier than the Angels! And to what purpose should I touch hidden loaves, which it is not lawful to break or eat without danger? It is enough for me to know that they exist, that there are three loaves \u2014 I do not say a Trinity of persons, but a trinity of words, or rather of understandings, which are to be held concerning the persons: three loaves, I say, of one magnitude and weight, of one form and savor. For whatever and however great things are to be thought of the Father, the same and equally great things are to be thought of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, except only that the distinction of properties makes a number of persons, just as in the Trinity, so in the distinction of our understandings concerning them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Hos ergo panes relinquamus frangendos angelicae sublimitati, donec ad eorum crescentes aequalitatem, mensae eorum simus idonei. Multifariae siquidem aliorum trinitates panum de Scriptura nobis possunt proponi, qui nostrae magis congruant infirmitati, verbi gratia, ut nondum longius ab illa summa Trinitate recedamus, quod ex ipso, et per ipsum, et in ipso sunt omnia; quod a Patre creati, per Filium redempti, in Spiritu sancto sumus sanctificati. In hunc modum tam multa dici possunt, ut quamlibet famelicus venerit amicus de via, si vel dimidium ei apponas, non minus fortasse incipiat periclitari fastidio, quam prius inedia: ut copia obruat, quem inopia prius angebat. Non solum enim ipsum qui fecit nos, et ea quae facta sunt propter nos, sed etiam quae scripta sunt propter nos, describere tripliciter poteris, ut copiosa refectio sit in tribus panibus, historiae, allegoriae, et moralitatis. Universa quoque Scripturae continentia in tres partes divisa, velut in tres panes digesta est, dum aut de naturali justitia disserit, aut de litterali, aut de spirituali; id est, ante legem, sub lege, et post legem, quod est sub gratia. Natura rectum dedit intellectum; lex, etiam actum; gratia autem, affectum. Sed et Doctor et pastor Gentium in fide et veritate, pascendam docet Ecclesiam quadam panum trinitate; ut qui Ecclesiam aedificat, loquatur ad aedificationem, ad exhortationem, ad consolationem (I Cor. XIV, 3). Ad aedificationem, ut quid agere debeas, noris; ad exhortationem, ut quod noris, velis; ad consolationem, ut etiam in adversis possis quod noveris et volueris. Denique non solum in materia rerum, sensibus et partibus Scripturarum, generibus et modis locutionum, sed etiam in omni fine earum, quamdam panum invenies trinitatem, et ipsam quidem sapidam satis et salubrem: fidem scilicet, spem, et charitatem. Eo quippe omnis Scripturarum, vel sermonum intentio spectare videtur, ut credamus, speremus, diligamus. Nam et charitas sola finis praecepti definita est, quae et ipsa quodammodo triplex est, dum ex toto corde, tota anima, tota virtute habenda est. Sed quoniam vitio ducitur longior et varia epulatio, nos finem dabimus lectioni: vestrum erit fragmenta quae superaverunt, id est subtiliora, quae manus nostras effugerunt, colligere; simul autem et vestrum et nostrum, ei qui pascit nos cantare, Benedictus Deus in donis suis, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Let us therefore leave these loaves to be broken by the sublimity of angels, until, growing to equality with them, we become fit for their table. For indeed many other trinities of loaves can be set before us from Scripture, which are better suited to our weakness \u2014 for example, that we may not yet withdraw too far from that highest Trinity: that of him, and through him, and in him are all things; that we were created by the Father, redeemed through the Son, sanctified in the Holy Spirit. In this way so many things can be said that, however hungry a friend may come from the road, if you set before him even half of it, by surfeit \u2014 so that abundance may overwhelm him whom want formerly distressed. For not only him who made us, and the things that were made for our sake, but also the things that were written for our sake, you may describe in a threefold way, so that there be an abundant refreshment in three loaves: of history, of allegory, and of moral teaching. The whole content of Scripture too, divided into three parts, is as it were arranged into three loaves, in that it treats either of natural justice, or of the literal, or of the spiritual \u2014 that is, before the law, under the law, and after the law, which is under grace. Nature gave a right understanding; the law gave also right action; but grace gave right affection. Moreover the Teacher and Pastor of the Gentiles in faith and truth teaches that the Church is to be fed with a certain trinity of loaves: that he who builds up the Church should speak unto edification, unto exhortation, unto consolation (I Cor. XIV, 3). Unto edification, that you may know what you ought to do; unto exhortation, that you may will what you know; unto consolation, that even in adversities you may be able to do what you have known and willed. Finally, not only in the matter of things, in the senses and parts of the Scriptures, in the kinds and modes of speech, but also in their whole end and purpose, you will find a certain trinity of loaves, and one indeed quite savory and wholesome: namely, faith, hope, and charity. For to this end the whole intention of the Scriptures, or of discourses, seems to look: that we may believe, hope, and love. For charity alone has been defined as the end of the commandment, and it too is in a certain way threefold, since it must be held with the whole heart, the whole soul, and the whole strength. But since a feast too long and too varied is accounted a fault, we will put an end to our reading: it will be yours to gather up the fragments that remain \u2014 that is, the finer points that have escaped our hands \u2014 and it will be both yours and ours together to sing to him who feeds us: Blessed be God in his gifts, who lives and reigns through all the ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints Luc. xi, 5) without an opening parenthesis. The request for three loaves is Luke 11:5."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Rogations Sermon", "topics": ["Rogation Days", "preaching", "prayer", "humility", "Scripture", "Trinity", "faith"], "url": "sermons/guerric-rogations.html"}, {"author": "John Henry Newman", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [19], "composition": "By 1849; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "newman-mixed-05", "language": "en", "language_label": "English", "metadata": ["John Henry Newman, Discourses Addressed to Mixed Congregations, new impression (London, New York, and Bombay: Longmans, Green, and Co., 1906), discourse V, Saintliness the Standard of Christian Principle, pp. 83\u2013103.", "Internet Archive; Kelly, University of Toronto.", "Unspecified", "No particular liturgical occasion is established", "Matthew 6:23 (added by editors; adapted quotation)", "Job 10:22 (added by editors)", "Isaiah 50:11 (added by editors)", "Exodus 32:4; 1 Kings 12:28 parallel (added by editors)", "Matthew 17:2, 4 (added by editors; adapted quotations)", "Hebrews 12:22\u201324 (added by editors)", "1 John 5:18 (added by editors)", "Romans 12:1 (added by editors)", "Sirach 51:18, 26\u201328 (added by editors; Douay numbering, adapted quotation)", "Matthew 5:39\u201340; Luke 6:29 parallel (paraphrase; reference added by editors)"], "occasion": "No specific liturgical occasion established", "occasion_detail": "No particular liturgical occasion is established", "occasion_label": "Unspecified", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This fifth discourse belongs to Newman's Catholic collection first published in 1849, here read in its 1906 impression. Its individual delivery date and liturgical occasion are unestablished. Newman argues that conscience needs teaching and examples, and contrasts reverence for wealth and newspaper fame with the Catholic vision of holiness. Through images of darkness and light, the Transfiguration, and accounts of saints, he presents saintliness as a standard for judging what is good. His criticism of the national religion and Protestant society is retained as historical polemic. The holiness theme does not establish an All Saints occasion."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-h001", "label": "English original", "text": "DISCOURSE V."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-h002", "label": "English original", "text": "SAINTLINESS THE STANDARD OF CHRISTIAN PRINCIPLE."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p001", "label": "English original", "text": "You know very well, my brethren, and there are few persons anywhere who deny it, that in the breast of every one there dwells a feeling or perception, which tells him the difference between right and wrong, and is the standard by which to measure thoughts and actions. It is called conscience; and even though it be not at all times powerful enough to rule us, still it is distinct and decisive enough to influence our views and form our judgments in the various matters which come before us. Yet even this office it cannot perform adequately without external assistance; it needs to be regulated and sustained. Left to itself, though it tells truly at first, it soon becomes wavering, ambiguous, and false; it needs good teachers and good examples to keep it up to the mark and line of duty; and the misery is, that these external helps, teachers, and examples are in many instances wanting."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p002", "label": "English original", "text": "Nay, to the great multitude of men they are so far wanting, that conscience loses its way and guides the soul in its journey heavenward but indirectly and circuitously. Even in countries called Christian, the natural inward light grows dim, because the Light, which lightens every one born into the world, is removed out of sight. I say, it is a most miserable and frightful thought, that, in this country, among this people which boasts that it is so Christian and so enlightened, the sun in the heavens is so eclipsed that the mirror of conscience can catch and reflect few rays, and serves but poorly and scantily to preserve the foot from error. That inward light, given as it is by God, is powerless to illuminate the horizon, to mark out for us our direction, and to comfort us with the certainty that we are making for our Eternal Home. That light was intended to set up within us a standard of right and of truth; to tell us our duty on every emergency, to instruct us in detail what sin is, to judge between all things which come before us, to discriminate the precious from the vile, to hinder us from being seduced by what is pleasant and agreeable, and to dissipate the sophisms of our reason. But alas! what ideas of truth, what ideas of holiness, what ideas of heroism, what ideas of the good and great, have the multitude of men? I am not asking whether they act up to any ideas, or are swayed by any ideas, of these high objects; that is a further point; I only ask, have they any ideas of them at all? or, if they cannot altogether blot out from their souls their ideas of greatness and goodness, I ask still, whether their mode of conceiving of them, and the things and persons in which they embody them, be not such, that we may truly say of the bulk of mankind, that \u201cthe light that is in them is darkness\u201d."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p003", "label": "English original", "text": "Attend to me, my dear brethren, I am saying nothing very abstruse, nothing very difficult to understand, nothing unimportant; but something intelligible, undeniable, and of very general concern. You know there are persons who never see the light of day; they live in pits and mines, and there they work, there they take their pleasure, and there perhaps they die. Do you think they have any right idea, though they have eyes, of the sun\u2019s radiance, of the sun\u2019s warmth? any idea of the beautiful arching heavens, the blue sky, the soft clouds, and the moon and stars by night? any idea of the high mountain, and the green smiling earth? O what an hour it is for him who is suddenly brought from such a pit or cave, from the dull red glow and the flickering glare of torches, and that monotony of an artificial twilight, in which day and night are lost,\u2014is suddenly, I say, brought thence, and for the first time sees the bright sun moving majestically from East to West, and witnesses the gradual graceful changes of the air and sky from morn till fragrant evening! And oh! what a sight for one born blind to begin to see,\u2014a sense altogether foreign to all his previous conceptions! What a marvellous new state of being, which, though he ever had the senses of hearing and of touch, never had he been able, by the words of others, or any means of information he possessed, to bring home to himself in the faintest measure! Would he not find himself, as it is said, in a \u201cnew world\u201d? What a revolution would take place in his modes of thought, in his habits, in his ways, and in his doings hour by hour! He would no longer direct himself with his hands and his hearing, he would no longer grope about; he would see;\u2014he would at a glance take in ten thousand objects, and, what is more, their relations and their positions the one towards the other. He would know what was great and what was little, what was near, what was distant, what things converged together and what things were separate\u2014in a word, he would see all things as a whole, and in subjection to himself as a centre."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p004", "label": "English original", "text": "But further, he would gain knowledge of something closer to himself and more personal than all these various objects; of something very different from the forms and groups in which light dwelt as in a tabernacle, and which excited his admiration and love. He would discover lying upon him, spreading over him, penetrating him, the festering seeds of unhealthiness and disease in their primary and minutest forms. The air around us is charged with a subtle powder or dust, which falls down softly on everything, silently sheds itself on everything, soils and stains everything, and, if suffered to remain undisturbed, induces sickness and engenders pestilence. It is like those ashes of the furnace which Moses was instructed to take up and scatter in the face of heaven, that they might become ulcers and blisters upon the flesh of the Egyptians. This subtle plague is felt in its ultimate consequences by all, the blind as well as those who see; but it is by the eyesight that we discern it in its origin and in its progress; it is by the sun\u2019s light that we discern our own defilement, and the need we have of continual cleansing to rid ourselves of it."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p005", "label": "English original", "text": "Now what is this dust and dirt, my brethren, but a figure of sin? so subtle in its approach, so multitudinous in its array, so incessant in its solicitations, so insignificant in its appearance, so odious, so poisonous in its effects. It falls on the soul gently and imperceptibly; but it gradually breeds wounds and sores, and ends in everlasting death. And as we cannot see the atoms of dust that have settled on us without the light, and as that same light, which enables us to see them, teaches us withal, by their very contrast with itself, their unseemliness and dishonour, so the light of the invisible world, the teachings and examples of revealed truth, bring home to us both the existence and also the deformity of sin, of which we should be unmindful or forgetful without them. And as there are men who live in caverns and mines, and never see the face of day, and do their work as best they can by torch-light, so there are multitudes, nay, whole races of men, who, though possessed of eyes by nature, cannot use them duly, because they live in the spiritual pit, in the region of darkness, \u201cin the land of wretchedness and gloom, where there is the shadow of death, and where order is not\u201d."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p006", "label": "English original", "text": "There they are born, there they live, there they die; and instead of the bright, broad, and all-revealing luminousness of the sun, they grope their way from place to place with torches, as best they may, or fix up lamps at certain points, and \u201cwalk in the light of their fire, and in the flames which they have kindled;\u201d because they have nothing clearer, nothing purer, to serve the needs of the day and the year. Light of some kind they must secure, and, when they can do no better, they make it for themselves. Man, a being endued with reason, cannot on that very account live altogether at random; he is obliged in some sense to live on principle, to live by rule, to profess a view of life, to have an aim, to set up a standard, and to take to him such examples as seem to him to fulfil it. His reason does not make him independent (as men sometimes speak); it forces on him a dependency on definite principles and laws, in order to satisfy its own demands. He must, by the necessity of his nature, look up to something; and he creates, if he cannot discover, an object for his veneration. He teaches himself, or is taught by his neighbour, falsehoods, if he is not taught truth from above; he makes to himself idols, if he knows not of the Eternal God and His Saints."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p007", "label": "English original", "text": "Now, of which of the two, think you, my brethren, are our own countrymen in possession? have they possession of the true Object of worship, or have they a false one? have they created what is not, or discovered what is? do they walk by the luminaries of heaven, or are they as those who are born and live in caverns, and who strike their light as best they may, by means of the stones and metals of the earth?"}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p008", "label": "English original", "text": "Look around, my brethren, and answer for yourselves. Contemplate the objects of this people\u2019s praise, survey their standards, ponder their ideas and judgments, and then tell me whether it is not most evident, from their very notion of the desirable and the excellent, that greatness, and goodness, and sanctity, and sublimity, and truth are unknown to them; and that they not only do not pursue, but do not even admire, those high attributes of the Divine Nature. This is what I am insisting on, not what they actually do or what they are, but what they revere, what they adore, what their gods are. Their god is mammon; I do not mean to say that all seek to be wealthy, but that all bow down before wealth. Wealth is that to which the multitude of men pay an instinctive homage. They measure happiness by wealth; and by wealth they measure respectability. Numbers, I say, there are who never dream that they shall ever be rich themselves, but who still at the sight of wealth feel an involuntary reverence and awe, just as if a rich man must be a good man. They like to be noticed by some particular rich man; they like on some occasion to have spoken with him; they like to know those who know him, to be intimate with his dependants, to have entered his house, nay, to know him by sight. Not, I repeat, that it ever comes into their mind that the like wealth will one day be theirs; not that they see the wealth, for the man who has it may dress, and live, and look like other men; not that they expect to gain some benefit from it: no, theirs is a disinterested homage, it is a homage resulting from an honest, genuine, hearty admiration of wealth for its own sake, such as that pure love which holy men feel for the Maker of all; it is a homage resulting from a profound faith in wealth, from the intimate sentiment of their hearts, that, however a man may look,\u2014poor, mean, starved, decrepit, vulgar; or again, though he may be ignorant, or diseased, or feeble-minded, though he have the character of being a tyrant or a profligate, yet, if he be rich, he differs from all others; if he be rich, he has a gift, a spell, an omnipotence;\u2014that with wealth he may do all things."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p009", "label": "English original", "text": "Wealth is one idol of the day, and notoriety is a second. I am not speaking, I repeat, of what men actually pursue, but of what they look up to, what they revere. Men may not have the opportunity of pursuing what they admire still. Never could notoriety exist as it does now, in any former age of the world; now that the news of the hour from all parts of the world, private news as well as public, is brought day by day to every individual, as I may say, of the community, to the poorest artisan and the most secluded peasant, by processes so uniform, so unvarying, so spontaneous, that they almost bear the semblance of a natural law. And hence notoriety, or the making a noise in the world, has come to be considered a great good in itself, and a ground of veneration. Time was when men could only make a display by means of expenditure; and the world used to gaze with wonder on those who had large establishments, many servants, many horses, richly-furnished houses, gardens, and parks: it does so still, that is, when it has the opportunity of doing so: for such magnificence is the fortune of the few, and comparatively few are its witnesses."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p010", "label": "English original", "text": "Notoriety, or, as it may be called, newspaper fame, is to the many what style and fashion, to use the language of the world, are to those who are within or belong to the higher circles; it becomes to them a sort of idol, worshipped for its own sake, and without any reference to the shape in which it comes before them. It may be an evil fame or a good fame; it may be the notoriety of a great statesman, or of a great preacher, or of a great speculator, or of a great experimentalist, or of a great criminal; of one who has laboured in the improvement of our schools, or hospitals, or prisons, or workhouses, or of one who has robbed his neighbour of his wife. It matters not; so that a man is talked much of, and read much of, he is thought much of; nay, let him even have died justly under the hands of the law, still he will be made a sort of martyr of. His clothes, his handwriting, the circumstances of his guilt, the instruments of his deed of blood, will be shown about, gazed on, treasured up as so many relics; for the question with men is, not whether he is great, or good, or wise, or holy; not whether he is base, and vile, and odious, but whether he is in the mouths of men, whether he has centred on himself the attention of many, whether he has done something out of the way, whether he has been (as it were) canonised in the publications of the hour. All men cannot be notorious: the multitudes who thus honour notoriety, do not seek it themselves; nor am I speaking of what men do, but how they judge; yet instances do occur from time to time of wretched men, so smitten with passion for notoriety, as even to dare in fact some detestable and wanton act, not from love of it, not from liking or dislike of the person against whom it is directed, but simply in order thereby to gratify this impure desire of being talked about, and gazed upon. \u201cThese are thy gods, O Israel!\u201d Alas! alas! this great and noble people, born to aspire, born for reverence, behold them walking to and fro by the torch-light of the cavern, or pursuing the wild-fires of the marsh, not understanding themselves, their destinies, their defilements, their needs, because they have not the glorious luminaries of heaven to see, to consult, and to admire!"}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p011", "label": "English original", "text": "But oh! what a change, my brethren, when the good hand of God brings them by some marvellous providence to the pit\u2019s mouth, and then out into the blessed light of day! what a change for them when they first begin to see with the eyes of the soul, with the intuition which grace gives, Jesus, the Sun of Justice; and the heaven of Angels and Archangels in which He dwells; and the bright Morning Star, which is His Blessed Mother; and the continual floods of light falling and striking against the earth, and transformed, as they fall, into an infinity of hues, which are His Saints; and the boundless sea, which is the image of His divine immensity; and then again the calm, placid Moon by night, which images His Church; and the silent stars, like good and holy men, travelling on in lonely pilgrimage to their eternal rest! Such was the surprise, such the transport, which came upon the favoured disciples, whom on one occasion our Lord took up with Him to the mountain\u2019s top. He left the sick world, the tormented, restless multitude, at its foot, and He took them up, and was transfigured before them. \u201cHis face did shine as the sun, and His raiment was white as the light;\u201d and they lifted their eyes, and saw on either side of Him a bright form;\u2014these were two Saints of the elder covenant, Moses and Elias, who were conversing with Him. How truly was this a glimpse of Heaven! the holy Apostles were introduced into a new range of ideas, into a new sphere of contemplation, till St. Peter, overcome by the vision, cried out, \u201cLord, it is good to be here; and let us make three tabernacles\u201d. He would fain have kept those heavenly glories always with him; everything on earth, the brightest, the fairest, the noblest, paled and dwindled away, and turned to corruption before them; its most substantial good was vanity, its richest gain was dross, its keenest joy a weariness, and its sin a loathsomeness and abomination. And such as this in its measure is the contrast, to which the awakened soul is witness, between the objects of its admiration and pursuit in its natural state, and those which burst upon it when it has entered into communion with the Church Invisible, when it has come \u201cto Mount Sion, and to the city of the Living God, the heavenly Jerusalem, and to a company of many thousand Angels, and to the Church of the first-born, who are enrolled in heaven, and to God the Judge of all, and to the spirits of the just now perfected, and to Jesus the Mediator of the New Testament\u201d. From that day it has begun a new life: I am not speaking of any moral conversion which takes place in it; whether or not it is moved (as surely we believe it will be) to act upon the sights which it sees, still consider only what a change there will be in its views and estimation of things, as soon as it has heard and has faith in the word of God, as soon as it understands that wealth, and notoriety, and influence, and high place, are not the first of blessings and the real standard of good; but that saintliness and all its attendants,\u2014saintly purity, saintly poverty, heroic fortitude and patience, self-sacrifice for the sake of others, renouncement of the world, the favour of Heaven, the protection of Angels, the smile of the Blessed Virgin, the gifts of grace, the interpositions of miracle, the intercommunion of merits,\u2014that these are the high and precious things, the things to be looked up to, the things to be reverently spoken of. Hence worldly-minded men, however rich, if they are Catholics, cannot, till they utterly lose their faith, be the same as those who are external to the Church; they have an instinctive veneration for those who have the traces of heaven upon them, and they praise what they do not imitate."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p012", "label": "English original", "text": "Such men have an idea before them which a Protestant nation has not; they have the idea of a Saint; they believe, they realise the existence of those rare servants of God, who rise up from time to time in the Catholic Church like Angels in disguise, and shed around them a light, as they walk on their way heavenward. Such Catholics may not in practice do what is right and good, but they know what is true; they know what to think and how to judge. They have a standard for their principles of conduct, and it is the image of Saints which forms it for them. A Saint is born like another man; by nature a child of wrath, and needing God\u2019s grace to regenerate him. He is baptised like another, he lies helpless and senseless like another, and like another child he comes to years of reason. But soon his parents and their neighbours begin to say, \u201cThis is a strange child, he is unlike any other child;\u201d his brothers and his playmates feel an awe of him, they do not know why; they both like him and dislike him, perhaps love him much in spite of his strangeness, perhaps respect him more than they love him. But if there were any holy Priest there, or others who had long served God in prayer and obedience, these would say, \u201cThis truly is a wonderful child; this child bids fair to be a Saint\u201d. And so he grows up, whether at first he is duly prized by his parents or not; for so it is with all greatness, that, because it is great, it cannot be comprehended by ordinary minds at once; but time, and distance, and contemplation are necessary for its being recognised by beholders, and, therefore, this special heir of glory of whom I am speaking, for a time at least excites no very definite observation, unless indeed (as sometimes happens) any thing of miracle occurs from time to time to mark him out. He has come to the age of reason, and, wonderful to say, he has never fallen away into sin. Other children begin to use the gift of reason by abusing it; they understand what is right, only to go counter to it; it is otherwise with him,\u2014not that he may not sin in many things, when we place him in the awful ray of divine Sanctity, but that he does not sin wilfully and grievously,\u2014he is preserved from mortal sin, he is never separated from God by sin, nay, perhaps, he is betrayed only at intervals, or never at all, into any deliberate sin, be it ever so slight, and he is ever avoiding the occasions of sin and resisting temptation. He ever lives in the presence of God, and is thereby preserved from evil, for \u201cthe wicked one toucheth him not\u201d. Nor, again, as if in other and ordinary matters he necessarily differed from other boys; he may be ignorant, thoughtless, improvident of the future, rash, impetuous; he is a child, and has the infirmities, failings, fears, and hopes of a child. He may be moved to anger, he may say a harsh word, he may offend his parents, he may be volatile and capricious, he may have no fixed view of things, such as a man has. This is not much to allow; such things are accidents, and are compatible with the presence of a determinate influence of grace, uniting his heart to God. O that the multitude of men were as religious in their best seasons, as the Saints are in their worst! though there have been Saints who seem to have been preserved even from the imperfections I have been mentioning. There have been Saints whose reason the all-powerful grace of God seems wonderfully to have opened from the very time of their baptism, so that they have offered to their Lord and Saviour, \u201ca living, holy, acceptable sacrifice,\u201d \u201ca rational service,\u201d even while they have been infants. And, anyhow, whatever are the acts of infirmity and sin in the child I am imagining, still they are the exception in his day\u2019s course; the course of each day is religious: while other children are light-minded, and cannot fix their thoughts in prayer, prayer and praise and meditation are his meat and drink. He frequents the Churches, and places himself before the Blessed Sacrament: or he is found before some holy image; or he sees visions of the Blessed Virgin, or of the Saints to whom he is devoted. He lives in intimate converse with his guardian Angel, and he shrinks from the very shadow of profaneness or impurity. And thus he is a special witness of the world unseen, and he fulfils the vague ideas and the dreams of the supernatural, which one reads of in poems or romances, with which young people are so much taken, and after which they cannot help sighing, before the world corrupts them."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p013", "label": "English original", "text": "He grows up, and he has just the same temptations as others, perhaps more violent ones. Men of this world, carnal men, unbelieving men, do not believe that the temptations which they themselves experience and to which they yield, can be overcome. They reason themselves into the notion that to sin is their very nature, and, therefore, is no fault of theirs; that is, they deny the existence of sin. And accordingly, when they read about the Saints or about holy men generally, they conclude either that these have not had the temptations which they experienced themselves, or that they have not overcome them. They either consider such an one to be a hypocrite, who practises in private the sins which he denounces in public; or, if they have decency enough to abstain from these calumnies, then they consider that he never felt the temptation, and they regard him as a cold and simple person, who has never outgrown his childhood, who has a contracted mind, who does not know the world and life, who is despicable while he is without influence, and dangerous and detestable from his very ignorance when he is in power. But no, my brethren; read the lives of the Saints, you will see how false and narrow a view this is; these men, who think, forsooth, they know the world so well, and the nature of man so deeply, they know nothing of one great far-spreading phenomenon in man,\u2014and that is, his nature under the operation of grace; they know nothing of the second nature, of the supernatural gift, induced by the Almighty Spirit upon our first and fallen nature; they have never met, they have never read of, and they have formed no conception of, a Saint."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p014", "label": "English original", "text": "He has, I say, the same temptations as another; perhaps greater, because he is to be tried as in a furnace, because he is to become rich in merits, because there is a bright crown reserved for him in Heaven; still temptation he has, and he differs from others, not in being shielded from it, but in being armed against it. Grace overcomes nature; it overcomes indeed in all who shall be saved: none will see God\u2019s face hereafter who do not, while here, put away from them mortal sin of every kind; but the Saints overcome with a determination and a vigour, a promptitude and a success, beyond any one else. You read, my brethren, in the lives of Saints, the wonderful account of their conflicts, and their triumphs over the enemy. They are, as I was saying, like heroes of romance, so gracefully, so nobly, so royally do they bear themselves. Their actions are as beautiful as fiction, yet as real as fact. There was St. Benedict, who, when a boy, left Rome, and betook himself to the Apennines in the neighbourhood. Three years did he live in prayer, fasting, and solitude, while the Evil One assaulted him with temptation. One day, when it grew so fierce that he feared for his perseverance, he suddenly flung himself, in his scanty hermit\u2019s garb, among the thorns and nettles near him, thus turning the current of his thoughts, and chastising the waywardness of the flesh, by sensible stings and smarts. There was St. Thomas, too, the Angelical Doctor, as he is called, as holy as he was profound, or rather the more profound in theological science, because he was so holy. \u201cEven from a youth\u201d he had \u201csought wisdom, he had stretched out his hands on high, and directed his soul to her, and possessed his heart with her from the beginning;\u201d and so, when the minister of Satan came into his very room, and no other defence was at hand, he seized a burning brand from the hearth, and drove that wicked one, scared and baffled, out of his presence. And there was that poor youth in the early persecutions, whom the impious heathen bound down with cords, and then brought in upon him a vision of evil; and he in his agony bit off his tongue, and spit it out into the face of the temptress, that so the intenseness of the pain might preserve him from the seduction."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p015", "label": "English original", "text": "Such acts as these, my brethren, are an opening of the heavens, a sudden gleam of supernatural brightness across a dark sky. They enlarge the mind with ideas it had not before, and they show to the multitude what God can do, and what man can be. Not that all Saints have been such in youth: for there are those on the contrary, who, not till after a youth of sin, have been brought by the sovereign grace of God to repentance, still, when once converted, they differed in nothing from those who had ever served Him,\u2014not in supernatural gifts, not in acceptableness, not in detachment from the world, nor in union with Christ, nor in exactness of obedience,\u2014in nought save in the severity of their penance. Others have been called, not from vice and ungodliness, but from a life of mere ordinary blamelessness, or from a state of lukewarmness, or from thoughtlessness, to heroical greatness; and these have often given up lands, and property, and honours, and station, and repute, for Christ\u2019s sake. Kings have descended from their thrones, bishops have given up their rank and influence, the learned have given up their pride of intellect, to become poor monks, to live on coarse fare, to be clad in humble weeds, to rise and pray while others slept, to mortify the tongue with silence and the limbs with toil, and to avow an unconditional obedience to another. In early times were the Martyrs, many of them girls and even children, who bore the most cruel, the most prolonged, the most diversified tortures, rather than deny the faith of Christ. Then came the Missionaries among the heathen, who, for the love of souls, threw themselves into the midst of savages, risking and perhaps losing their lives in the attempt to extend the empire of their Lord and Saviour, and who, whether living or dying, have by their lives or by their deaths succeeded in bringing over whole nations into the Church. Others have devoted themselves in the time of war or captivity, to the redemption of Christian slaves from pagan or Mahometan masters or conquerors; others to the care of the sick in pestilences, or in hospitals; others to the instruction of the poor; others to the education of children; others to incessant preaching and the duties of the confessional; others to devout study and meditation; others to a life of intercession and prayer. Very various are the Saints, their very variety is a token of God\u2019s workmanship; but however various, and whatever was their special line of duty, they have been heroes in it; they have attained such noble self-command, they have so crucified the flesh, they have so renounced the world; they are so meek, so gentle, so tender-hearted, so merciful, so sweet, so cheerful, so full of prayer, so diligent, so forgetful of injuries; they have sustained such great and continued pains, they have persevered in such vast labours, they have made such valiant confessions, they have wrought such abundant miracles, they have been blessed with such strange successes, that they have been the means of setting up a standard before us of truth, of magnanimity, of holiness, of love. They are not always our examples, we are not always bound to follow them; not more than we are bound to obey literally some of our Lord\u2019s precepts, such as turning the cheek or giving away the coat; not more than we can follow the course of the sun, moon, or stars in the heavens; but, though not always our examples, they are always our standard of right and good; they are raised up to be monuments and lessons, they remind us of God, they introduce us into the unseen world, they teach us what Christ loves, they track out for us the way which leads heavenward. They are to us who see them, what wealth, notoriety, rank, and name are to the multitude of men who live in darkness,\u2014objects of our veneration and of our homage."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-p016", "label": "English original", "text": "O who can doubt between the two? The national religion has many attractions; it leads to decency and order, propriety of conduct, justness of thought, beautiful domestic tastes; but it has not power to lead the multitude upward, or to delineate for them the Heavenly City. It comes of mere nature, and its teaching is of nature. It uses religious words, of course, else it could not be called a religion; but it does not impress on the imagination, it does not engrave upon the heart, it does not inflict upon the conscience, the supernatural; it does not introduce into the popular mind any great ideas, such as are to be recognised by one and all, as common property, and first principles or dogmas from which to start, to be taken for granted on all hands, and handed down as forms and specimens of eternal truth from age to age. It in no true sense inculcates the Unseen; and by consequence, sights of this world, material tangible objects, become the idols and the ruin of its children, of souls which were made for God and Heaven. It is powerless to resist the world and the world\u2019s teaching: it cannot supplant error by truth; it follows when it should lead. There is but one real Antagonist of the world, and that is the faith of Catholics;\u2014Christ set that faith up, and it will do its work on earth, as it ever has done, till He comes again."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-scripture-01", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Matthew 6:23 (added by editors; adapted quotation)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-scripture-02", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Job 10:22 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-scripture-03", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Isaiah 50:11 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-scripture-04", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Exodus 32:4; 1 Kings 12:28 parallel (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-scripture-05", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Matthew 17:2, 4 (added by editors; adapted quotations)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-scripture-06", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Hebrews 12:22\u201324 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-scripture-07", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "1 John 5:18 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-scripture-08", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Romans 12:1 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-scripture-09", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Sirach 51:18, 26\u201328 (added by editors; Douay numbering, adapted quotation)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-05-scripture-10", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Matthew 5:39\u201340; Luke 6:29 parallel (paraphrase; reference added by editors)."}], "status": "English original", "text_kind": "english-original", "title": "Saintliness the Standard of Christian Principle", "topics": ["conscience", "holiness", "saints", "grace", "temptation", "wealth", "fame", "Church"], "url": "sermons/newman-mixed-05.html"}, {"author": "John Henry Newman", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [19], "composition": "By 1849; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "newman-mixed-14", "language": "en", "language_label": "English", "metadata": ["John Henry Newman, Discourses Addressed to Mixed Congregations, discourse XIV, The Mystery of Divine Condescension (London: Longmans, Green, and Co., 1906), pp. 284\u2013304.", "Internet Archive; Kelly, University of Toronto.", "Unspecified", "No historical occasion is established", "2 Corinthians 8:9 (added by editors)", "John 8:57\u201358 (added by editors)", "Exodus 33:18\u201320 (added by editors)", "Song of Songs 5:10\u201316 (quotation and devotional expansion; added by editors)", "1 John 4:19 (added by editors)"], "occasion": "No historical occasion established", "occasion_detail": "No historical occasion is established", "occasion_label": "Unspecified", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "The fourteenth discourse in Newman's Catholic-period Discourses Addressed to Mixed Congregations considers how the infinite God can meet a person's need for love. It moves from divine attributes through the beauty of creation to the Incarnation and the suffering Christ, ending in prayer. The collection was published by 1849; this reading follows the 1906 edition, and the individual delivery date is unestablished. No liturgical occasion is printed for this discourse. Its English is original, and its themes do not establish a Christmas or Passiontide assignment."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-h001", "label": "English original", "text": "DISCOURSE XIV."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-h002", "label": "English original", "text": "THE MYSTERY OF DIVINE CONDESCENSION."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p001", "label": "English original", "text": "THE Eternal Word, the Only-begotten Son of the Father, put off His glory, and came down upon earth, to raise us to heaven. Though He was God, He became man; though He was Lord of all, He became as a servant; \"though He was rich, yet for our sakes He became poor, that we, through His poverty, might be rich\". He came from heaven in so humble an exterior, that the self-satisfied Pharisees despised Him, and treated Him as a madman or an impostor. When He spoke of His father Abraham, and implied His knowledge of him, who was in truth but the creature of His hands, they said in derision, \"Thou art not yet fifty years old, and hast Thou seen Abraham?\" He made answer, \"Amen, amen, I say unto you, Before Abraham was made, I am.\" He had seen Abraham, who lived two thousand years before; yet in truth He was not two thousand years old, more than He was fifty. He was not two thousand years old, because He had no years; He was the Ancient of Days, who never had beginning, and who never will have an end; who is above and beyond time; who is ever young, and ever is beginning, yet never has not been, and is as old as He is young, and was as old and as young when Abraham lived as when He came on earth in our flesh to atone for our sins. And hence He says, \"Before Abraham was, I am,\" and not \"I was\"; because with Him there is no past or future. It cannot be properly said of Him, that He was, or that He will be, but that He is; He is always; always the same, not older because He has lived two thousand years in addition, not younger because He has not lived them."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p002", "label": "English original", "text": "My brethren, if we could get ourselves to enter into this high and sacred thought, if we really comtemplated the Almighty in Himself, then we should understand better what His incarnation is to us, and what it is in Him. I do not mean, if we worthily contemplated Him as He is; but, even if we contemplated Him in such a way as is really possible to us, if we did but fix our thoughts on Him, and make use of the reason which He has given us, we should understand enough of His greatness to feel the awfulness of His voluntary self-abasement. Attend, then, while I recall to your mind the doctrines which reason and revelation combine to teach you about the Most High, and, next, when you have fixed your mind upon His infinity, then go on to view, in the light of that infinity, the meaning of His incarnation."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p003", "label": "English original", "text": "Now first consider that reason teaches you there must be a God; else how was this all-wonderful universe made? It could not make itself; man could not make it, he is but a part of it; each man has a beginning, there must have been a first man, and who made him? To the thought of God then we are forced from the nature of the case; we must admit the idea of an Almighty Creator, and that Creator must have been from everlasting. He must have had no beginning, else how came He to be? Else, we should be in our original difficulty, and must begin our argument over again. The Creator, I say, had no beginning; for, if He was brought into being by another before Him, then how came that other to be? And so we shall proceed in an unprofitable series or catalogue of creators, which is as difficult to conceive as an endless line of men. Besides, if it was not the Creator Himself who was from everlasting, then there would be one being who was from everlasting, and another who was Creator; which is all one with saying there are two Gods. It is least trial then to our reason, it is simplest and most natural, to pronounce, that the Creator of the world had no beginning;\u2014and if so, He is self-existing; and if so, He can undergo no change. What is self-existing and everlasting has no growth or decay; It is what It ever was, and ever shall be the same. As It originated in nothing else, nothing else can interfere with It or affect It. Besides, everything that is has originated in It; everything therefore is dependent on It, and It is independently of everything."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p004", "label": "English original", "text": "Contemplate then the Supreme Being, the Being of beings, even so far as I have yet described Him; fix the idea of Him in your minds. He is one; He has no rival; He has no equal; He is unlike anything else; He is sovereign; He can do what He will. He is unchangeable from first to last; He is all-perfect; He is infinite in His power and in His wisdom, or He could not have made this immense world which we see by day and by night."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p005", "label": "English original", "text": "Next, this follows from what I have said;\u2014that, since He is from everlasting, and has created all things from a certain beginning, He has lived in an eternity before He began to create anything. What a wonderful thought is this! there was a state of things in which God was by Himself, and nothing else but He. There was no earth, no sky, no sun, no stars, no space, no time, no beings of any kind; no men, no Angels, no Seraphim. His throne was without ministers; He was not waited on by any; all was silence, all was repose, there was nothing but God; and this state continued, not for a while only, but for a measureless duration; it was a state which had ever been; it was the rule of things, and creation has been an innovation upon it. Creation is, comparatively speaking, but of yesterday; it has lasted a poor six thousand years, say sixty thousand, if you will, or six million, or six million million; what is this to eternity? nothing at all; not so much as a drop compared to the whole ocean, or a grain of sand to the whole earth. I say, through a whole eternity God was by Himself, with no other being but Himself; with nothing external to Himself, not working, but at rest, not speaking, not receiving homage from any, not glorified in creatures, but blessed in Himself and by Himself, and wanting nothing."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p006", "label": "English original", "text": "What an idea this gives us of the Almighty! He is above us, my brethren, we feel He is; how little can we understand Him! We fall in even with men upon earth, whose ways are so different from our own, that we cannot understand them; we marvel at them; they pursue courses so unlike ours, they take recreations so peculiar to themselves, that we despair of finding anything in common between them and us; we cannot make conversation when we are with them. Thus stirring and ambitious men wonder at those who live among books; sinners wonder at those who attend the Sacraments and mortify their passions; thrifty persons wonder at those who are lavish of their money; men who love society wonder at those who live in solitude and are happy in it. We cannot enter even into our fellows; we call them strange and incomprehensible; but what are they, compared with the all-marvellousness of the Everlasting God? He alone indeed is incomprehensible, who has not only lived an eternity without beginning, but who has lived through a whole eternity by Himself, and has not wearied of the solitude. Which of us, or how few of us, could live a week in comfort by ourselves? You have heard, my brethren, of solitary confinement as a punishment assigned to criminals, and at length it becomes more severe than any other punishment: it is said at length to drive men mad. We cannot live without objects, without aims, without employments, without companions. We cannot live simply in ourselves; the mind preys upon itself, if left to itself. This is the case with us mortal men; now raise your minds to God. Oh, the vast contrast! He lived a whole eternity in that state, of which a few poor years to us is madness. He lived a whole eternity without change of any kind. Day and night, sleep and meal-time, at least are changes, unavoidable changes, in the life of the most solitary upon earth. A prison, if it has nothing else to relieve its dreariness and its hopelessness, has at least this, that the poor prisoner sleeps; he sleeps, and suspends his misery; he sleeps and recruits his power of bearing it; but the Eternal is the Sleepless, He pauses not, He suspends not His powers, He is never tired of Himself; He is never wearied of His own infinity. He was from eternity ever in action, though ever at rest; ever surely in rest and peace profound and ineffable, yet with a living, present mind, self-possessed, and all-conscious, comprehending Himself and sustaining the comprehension. He rested ever, but He rested in Himself; His own resource, His own end, His own contemplation, His own blessedness."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p007", "label": "English original", "text": "Yes, so it was; and if it is incomprehensible that He should have existed solitary through an eternity, is it not incomprehensible too, that He should have ever given up that solitariness, and have willed to surround Himself with creatures? Why was He not content to be as He had been? Why did He bring into existence those who could not add to His blessedness and were not secure of their own? Why did He give them that gift which we see they possess, of doing right or wrong as they please, and of working out their ruin as well as their salvation? Why did He create a world like that which is before our eyes, which at best so dimly shows forth His glory, and at worst is a scene of sin and sorrow? He might have made a far more excellent world than this; He might have excluded sin; but, oh, wonderful mystery, He has surrounded Himself with the cries of fallen souls, and has created and opened the great pit. He has willed, after an eternity of peace, to allow of everlasting anarchy, of pride, and blasphemy, and guilt and hatred of Himself, and the worm that dieth not. Thus He is simply incomprehensible to us, mortal men. Well might the ancient heathen shrink from answering, when a king, his patron, asked him what God was! He begged for a day to consider his reply; at the end of it, for two more; and, when the two were ended, for four besides; for in truth he found that meditation, instead of bringing him towards the solution of the problem, did but drive him back; the more he questioned, the vaster grew the theme, and where he drew one conclusion, thence issued forth a hundred fresh difficulties to confound his reason. For in truth the being and attributes of God are a subject, not for reason simply, but for faith also; and we must accept His own word about Himself."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p008", "label": "English original", "text": "And now proceed to another thought, my brethren, which I have partly implied and partly expressed already. If the Almighty Creator be such as I have described Him, He in no wise depends on His creatures. They sin, they perish, they are saved, they praise Him eternally; but, though He loves all the creatures of His hand, though He visits all of them without exception with influences of His grace, so numerous and so urgent, that not till the disclosures of the last day shall we rightly conceive of them; though He deigns to be glorified in His Saints, though He is their all in all, their continued life, and power, and blessedness, still they are nothing to Him. They do not increase His happiness if they are saved, or diminish it if they are lost. I do not mean that He is at a distance from them; He does not so live in Himself as to abandon His creation to the operation of laws which He has stamped upon it. No; He is everywhere a vigilant and active Providence; He is in every one of His creatures, and in every one of their actions; if He were not in them, they would fall back into nothing. He is everywhere on earth, and sees every crime committed, whether under the sun or in the gloom of night; He is even the sustaining power of those who sin; He is most close to every, the most polluted soul; He is in the midst of the eternal prison; but what I mean to say is, that nothing touches Him, though He touches all things. The sun's rays penetrate into the most hideous recesses, yet keep their brightness and their perfection; and so the Almighty witnesses and suffers evil, yet is not touched or tried by the creature's wilfulness, pride, uncleanness, or unbelief. The lusts of earth and the blasphemies of hell neither sully His purity nor impair His majesty. If the whole world were to plunge wilfully into the eternal gulf, the loss would be theirs, not His. In the dread contest between good and evil, whether the Church conquers at once, or is oppressed for the time, and labours, whether she is in persecution, or in triumph, or in peace, whether His enemies hold out or are routed, when the innocent sin, when the just are falling, when good Angels weep, when souls are hardened, He is one and the same. He is in His blessedness still, and not even the surface is ruffled of His everlasting rest. He neither hopes nor fears, nor desires, nor sorrows, nor repents. All around Him seems full of agitation and confusion, but in His eternal decrees and infallible foreknowledge there is nothing contingent, nothing uncertain, nothing which is not part of one vast plan, as fixed in its issue, and as unchangeable, as His own Essence."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p009", "label": "English original", "text": "Such is the great God, so all-sufficient, so all-blessed, so separate from creatures, so inscrutable, so unapproachable. Who can see Him? who can fathom Him? who can move Him? who can change Him? who can even speak of Him? He is all-holy, all-patient, all-peaceful, and all-true. He says and He does; He delays and He executes; He warns and He punishes; He punishes, He rewards, He forbears, He pardons, according to an eternal decree, without imperfection, without vacillation, without inconsistency."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p010", "label": "English original", "text": "And now that I have set before you, my brethren, in human language, some of the attributes of the Adorable God, perhaps you are tempted to complain that, instead of winning you to the All-glorious and All-good, I have but repelled you from Him. You are tempted to exclaim,\u2014He is so far above us that the thought of Him does but frighten me; I cannot believe that He cares for me. I believe firmly that He is infinite perfection; and I love that perfection, not so much indeed as I could wish, still in my measure I love it for its own sake, and I wish to love it above all things, and I well understand that there is no creature but must love it in his measure, unless he has fallen from grace. But there are two feelings, which, alas, I have a difficulty in entertaining; I believe and I love, but without fervour, without keenness, because my heart is not kindled by hope, nor subdued and melted with gratitude. Hope and gratitude I wish to have, and have not; I know that He is loving towards all His works, but how am I to believe that He gives to me personally a thought, and cares for me for my own sake? I am beneath His love; He looks on me as an atom in a vast universe. He acts by general laws, and if He is kind to me it is, not for my sake, but because it is according to His nature to be kind. And hence it is that I am drawn over to sinful man with an intenser affection than to my glorious Maker. Kings and great men upon earth, when they appear in public, are not content with a mere display of their splendour, they show themselves as well as their glories; they look around them; they notice individuals; they have a kind eye, or a courteous gesture, or an open hand, for all who come near them. They scatter among the crowd the largess of their smiles and of their words. And then men go home, and tell their friends, and treasure up to their latest day, how that so great a personage took notice of them, or of a child of theirs, or accepted a present at their hand, or gave expression to some sentiment, without point in itself, but precious as addressed to them. Thus does my fellow-man engage and win me; but there is a gulf between me and my great God. I shall fall back on myself, and grovel in my nothingness, till He looks down from heaven, till He calls me, till He takes interest in me. It is a want in my nature to have one who can weep with me, and rejoice with me, and in a way minister to me; and this would be presumption in me, and worse, to hope to find in the Infinite and Eternal God."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p011", "label": "English original", "text": "This is what you may be tempted to say, my brethren, not without impatience, while you contemplate the Almighty God, as conscience portrays Him, and as reason concludes about Him, and as creation witnesses of Him; and I have dwelt on it, in order, by way of contrast, to set before you, as I proposed when I began, how your complaint is answered in the great mystery of the Incarnation. Never suppose that you are left by God; never suppose that He does not know you, your minds and your powers, better than you do yourselves. Ought you not to trust Him, that, if your complaint be true, He has thought of it before you? \"Before they call, I will attend,\" says He, \"and while they speak, I will hear.\" Add this to your general notion of His incomprehensibility, viz., that though He is infinite, He can bow Himself to the finite; have faith in the mystery of His condescension; confess that, though He \"inhabiteth eternity,\" He \"dwelleth with a contrite and humble spirit,\" and \"looketh down upon the lowly\". Give up this fretfulness, quit these self-consuming thoughts, go out of yourselves, lift up your eyes, look around, and see if you can discern nothing more hopeful, more gracious in this wide world, than these perplexities over which you have been brooding. No, my brethren, we are so constituted by our Maker, as to be able to love Him ardently, and He has given us means of doing so. He has not founded our worship of Him in hope, nor made self-interest the measure of our veneration. And we have eyes to see much more than the difficulties of His Essence; and the great consolatory disclosures of Him, which Nature begins, Revelation brings to perfection. Lift up your eyes, I say, and look out even upon the material world, and there you will see one attribute above others on its very face which will reverse your sad meditations on Him who made it. He has traced out many of His attributes upon it, His immensity, His wisdom, His power, His loving-kindness, and His skill; but more than all, its very face is illuminated with the glory and beauty of His eternal excellence. This is that attribute in which all His attributes coalesce, which is the perfection, or (as I may say) the flower and bloom of their combination. As among men, youth, and health, and vigour, have their finish in that grace of outline, and lustre of complexion, and eloquence of expression, which we call beauty, so in the Almighty God, though we cannot comprehend His holy attributes, and shrink from their unfathomable profound, yet we can, as creatures recognise and rejoice in the brightness, harmony, and serenity, which is their resulting excellence. This is that quality which, by the law of our nature, is ever able to draw us off ourselves in admiration, which moves our affections, which wins from us a disinterested homage; and it is shed in profusion, in token of its Creator, over the visible world."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p012", "label": "English original", "text": "Leave, then, the prison of your own reasonings, leave the town, the work of man, the haunt of sin; go forth, my brethren, far from the tents of Cedar and the slime of Babylon: with the patriarch go forth to meditate in the field, and from the splendours of the work imagine the unimaginable glory of the Architect. Mount some bold eminence, and look back, when the sun is high and full upon the earth, when mountains, cliffs, and sea rise up before you like a brilliant pageant, with outlines noble and graceful, and tints and shadows soft, clear, and harmonious, giving depth, and unity to the whole; and then go through the forest, or fruitful field, or along meadow and stream, and listen to the distant country sounds, and drink in the fragrant air which is poured around you in spring or summer; or go among the gardens, and delight your senses with the grace and splendour, and the various sweetness of the flowers you find there; then think of the almost mysterious influence upon the mind of particular scents, or the emotion which some gentle, peaceful strain excites in us, or how soul and body are rapt and carried away captive by the concord of musical sounds, when the ear is open to their power; and then, when you have ranged through sights, and sounds, and odours, and your heart kindles, and your voice is full of praise and worship, reflect\u2014not that they tell you nothing of their Maker,\u2014but that they are the poorest and dimmest glimmerings of His glory, and the very refuse of His exuberant riches, and but the dusky smoke which precedes the flame, compared with Him who made them. Such is the Creator in His Eternal Uncreated Beauty, that, were it given to us to behold it, we should die of very rapture at the sight. Moses, unable to forget the token of it he had once seen in the Bush, asked to see it fully, and on this very account was refused. \"He said, Show me Thy glory; and He said, Thou canst not see My Face; for man shall not see Me and live.\" When saints have been favoured with glimpses of it, it has thrown them into ecstasy, broken their poor frames of dust and ashes, and pierced them through with such keen distress, that they have cried out to God, in the very midst of their transports, that He would hold His hand, and, in tenderness to them, check the abundance of His consolations. What saints partake in fact, we enjoy in thought and imagination; and even that mere reflection of God's glory is sufficient to sweep away the gloomy, envious thoughts of Him, which circle round us, and to lead us to forget ourselves in the contemplation of the All-beautiful. He is so bright, so majestic, so serene, so harmonious, so pure; He so surpasses, as being its archetype and fulness, all that is graceful, gentle, sweet, and fair on earth; His voice is so touching, and His smile so winning while so awful, that we need nothing more than to gaze and listen, and be happy. Say not this is not enough for love and joy; even in sights of this earth, the pomp and ceremonial of royalty is sufficient for the beholder; he needs nothing more than to be allowed to see; and were we but admitted to the courts of heaven, the sight of Him, ever transporting, ever new, though He addressed us not, would be our meat and drink to all eternity."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p013", "label": "English original", "text": "And if He has so constituted us, that, in spite of the abyss which lies between Him and us, in spite of the mystery of His attributes and the feebleness of our reason, the very vision of Him dispels all doubt, allures our shrinking souls, and is our everlasting joy, what shall we say, my brethren, when we are told that He has also condescended to take possession of us and to rule us by means of hope and gratitude, those \"cords of Adam,\" by which one man is bound to another? You say that God and man never can be one, that man cannot bear the sight and touch of his Creator, nor the Creator condescend to the feebleness of the creature; but blush and be confounded to hear, O peevish, restless hearts, that He has come down from His high throne and humbled Himself to the creature, in order that the creature might be inspired and strengthened to rise to Him. It was not enough to give man grace; it was little to impart to him a celestial light, and a sanctity such as Angels had received; little to create Adam in original justice, with a heavenly nature superadded to his own, with an intellect which could know God and a soul which could love Him; He purposed even in man's first state of innocence a higher mercy, which in the fulness of time was to be accomplished in his behalf. It became the Wisdom of God, who is the eternally glorious and beautiful, to impress these attributes upon men by His very presence and personal indwelling in their flesh, that, as He was by nature the Only-begotten Image of the Father, so He might also become \"the First-born of every creature\". It became Him who is higher than the highest, to act as if even humility, if this dare be said, was in the number of His attributes, by taking Adam's nature upon Himself, and manifesting Himself to men and Angels in it. It became Him, of whom are all things, and who is in all things, not to create new natures, which had not been before, inconstant spirit and corruptible matter, without taking them to Himself and absorbing them into a personal union with God. And see, my brethren, when you complain that we men are cut off from God, see that He has done more for you than He has done for those \"who are greater in strength and power\". The Angels surpass us in their original nature; they are immortal spirits, and we are subject to death; they have been visited by larger measures of God's grace, and they serve in His heaven, and are blessed by the vision of His face; yet \"nowhere doth He take hold of the Angels\"; He turned aside from the eldest-born of creation, He chose the younger. He chose him in whom an immortal spirit was united to a frail and perishable body. He turned aside to him whom an irritable, wayward, dim-sighted, and passionate nature rendered less worthy of His love; to him He turned; He made \"the first last, and the last first\"; \"He raised the needy from the earth, and lifted the poor out of the mire,\" and bade Angels bow down in adoration to a material form, for it was His own."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p014", "label": "English original", "text": "Well, my brethren, your God has taken on Him your nature, and now prepare yourself to see in human flesh that glory and that beauty on which the Angels gaze. Since you are to see Emmanuel, since \"the brilliancy of the Eternal Light and the unspotted mirror of God's majesty, and the Image of His goodness,\" is to walk the earth, since the Son of the Highest is to be born of woman, since the manifold attributes of the Infinite are to be poured out before your eyes through material channels and the operations of a human soul, since He, whose contemplation did but trouble you in Nature, is coming to take you captive by a manifestation, which is both intelligible to you and a pledge that He loves you one by one, raise high your expectations, for surely they cannot suffer disappointment. Doubtless, you will say, He will take a form such as \"eye hath not seen, nor ear heard of\" before. It will be a body framed in the heavens, and only committed to the custody of Mary; a form of light and glory, worthy of Him, who is \"blessed for evermore,\" and comes to bless us with His presence. Pomp and pride of men He may indeed despise; we do not look for Him in kings' courts, or in the array of war, or in the philosophic school; but doubtless He will choose some calm and holy spot, and men will go out thither and find their Incarnate God. He will be tenant of some paradise, like Adam or Elias, or He will dwell in the mystic garden of the Canticles, where nature ministers its best and purest to its Creator. \"The fig-tree will put forth her green figs, the vines in flower yield their sweet smell;\" \"spikenard and saffron\" will be there; \"the sweet cane and cinnamon, myrrh and aloes, with all the chief perfumes;\" \"the glory of Libanus, the beauty of Carmel,\" before \"the glory of the Lord and the beauty of our God\". There will He show Himself at stated times, with Angels for His choristers and saints for His doorkeepers, to the poor and needy, to the humble and devout, to those who have kept their innocence undefiled, or have purged their sins away by long penance and masterful contrition."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p015", "label": "English original", "text": "Such would be the conjecture of man, at fault when he speculated on the height of God, and now again at fault when he tries to sound the depth. He thinks that a royal glory is the note of His presence upon earth; lift up your eyes, my brethren, and answer whether he has guessed aright. Oh, incomprehensible in eternity and in time! solitary in heaven, and solitary upon earth! \"Who is this, that cometh from Edom, with dyed garments from Bozra? Why is Thy apparel red, and Thy garments like theirs that tread in the wine press?\" It is because the Maker of man, the Wisdom of God, has come, not in strength, but in weakness. He has come, not to assert a claim, but to pay a debt. Instead of wealth, He has come poor; instead of honour, He has come in ignominy; instead of blessedness, He has come to suffer. He has been delivered over from His birth to pain and contempt; His delicate frame is worn down by cold and heat, by hunger and sleeplessness; His hands are rough and bruised with a mechanic's toil; His eyes are dimmed with weeping; His Name is cast out as evil. He is flung amid the throng of men; He wanders from place to place; He is the companion of sinners. He is followed by a mixed multitude, who care more for meat and drink than for His teaching, or by a city's populace which deserts Him in the day of trial. And at length \"the Brightness of God's Glory and the Image of His Substance\" is fettered, haled to and fro, buffeted, spit upon, mocked, cursed, scourged, and tortured. \"He hath no beauty nor comeliness; He is despised and the most abject of men, a Man of sorrows and acquainted with infirmity;\" nay, He is a \"leper, and smitten of God, and afflicted\". And so His clothes are torn off, and He is lifted up upon the bitter Cross, and there He hangs, a spectacle for profane, impure, and savage eyes, and a mockery for the evil spirit whom He had cast down into hell."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p016", "label": "English original", "text": "Oh, wayward man! discontented first that thy God is far from thee, discontented again when He has drawn near,\u2014complaining first that He is high, complaining next that He is low!\u2014unhumbled being, when wilt thou cease to make thyself thine own centre, and learn that God is infinite in all He does, infinite when He reigns in heaven, infinite when He serves on earth, exacting our homage in the midst of His Angels, and winning homage from us in the midst of sinners? Adorable He is in His eternal rest, adorable in the glory of His court, adorable in the beauty of His works, most adorable of all, most royal, most persuasive in His deformity. Think you not, my brethren, that to Mary, when she held Him in her maternal arms, when she gazed on the pale countenance and the dislocated limbs of her God, when she traced the wandering lines of blood, when she counted the weals, the bruises, and the wounds, which dishonoured that virginal flesh, think you not that to her eyes it was more beautiful than when she first worshipped it, pure, radiant, and fragrant, on the night of His nativity? Dilectus meus candidus et rubicundus, as the Church sings; \"My beloved is white and ruddy; His whole form doth breathe of love, and doth provoke to love in turn; His drooping head, His open palms, and His breast all bare. My beloved is white and ruddy, choice out of thousands; His head is of the finest gold; His locks are branches of palm-trees, black as a raven. His eyes as doves upon brooks of waters, which are washed with milk, and sit beside the plentiful streams. His cheeks are as beds of aromatical spices set by the perfumers; His lips are lilies dropping choice myrrh. His hands are turned and golden, full of jacinths; His throat is most sweet, and He is all lovely. Such is my beloved, and He is my friend, O ye daughters of Jerusalem.\""}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-p017", "label": "English original", "text": "So is it, O dear and gracious Lord, \"the day of death is better than the day of birth, and better is the house of mourning than the house of feasting\". Better for me that Thou shouldst come thus abject and dishonourable, than hadst Thou put on a body fair as Adam's when he came out of Thy Hand. Thy glory sullied, Thy beauty marred, those five wounds welling out blood, those temples torn and raw, that broken heart, that crushed and livid frame, they teach me more, than wert Thou Solomon \"in the diadem wherewith his mother crowned him in the day of his heart's joy\". The gentle and tender expression of that Countenance is no new beauty, or created grace; it is but the manifestation, in a human form, of Attributes which have been from everlasting. Thou canst not change, O Jesus; and, as Thou art still Mystery, so wast Thou always Love. I cannot comprehend Thee more than I did, before I saw Thee on the Cross; but I have gained my lesson. I have before me the proof, that in spite of Thy awful nature, and the clouds and darkness which surround it, Thou canst think of me with a personal affection. Thou hast died, that I might live. \"Let us love God,\" says Thy Apostle, \"because He first hath loved us.\" I can love Thee now from first to last, though from first to last I cannot understand Thee. As I adore Thee, O Lover of souls, in Thy humiliation, so will I admire Thee and embrace Thee in Thy infinite and everlasting power."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-scripture-01", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "2 Corinthians 8:9 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-scripture-02", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "John 8:57\u201358 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-scripture-03", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Exodus 33:18\u201320 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-scripture-04", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Song of Songs 5:10\u201316 (quotation and devotional expansion; added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-scripture-05", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "1 John 4:19 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-14-reading-spelling", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The 1906 edition prints \u201ccomtemplated\u201d here; the spelling is retained."}], "status": "English original", "text_kind": "english-original", "title": "The Mystery of Divine Condescension", "topics": ["Incarnation", "God's attributes", "divine condescension", "Cross", "love", "hope"], "url": "sermons/newman-mixed-14.html"}, {"author": "John Henry Newman", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [19], "composition": "By 1849; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "newman-mixed-16", "language": "en", "language_label": "English", "metadata": ["John Henry Newman, Discourses Addressed to Mixed Congregations, new impression (London, New York, and Bombay: Longmans, Green, and Co., 1906), discourse XVI, Mental Sufferings of Our Lord in His Passion, pp. 323\u2013341.", "Internet Archive; Kelly, University of Toronto.", "Passion-tide", "Passion-tide", "Matthew 26:38\u201341 / Mark 14:34\u201338 (parallel quotations; added by editors)", "John 18:11, 23 (added by editors)", "Hebrews 12:2 (added by editors)"], "occasion": "Passion-tide", "occasion_detail": "Passion-tide", "occasion_label": "Passion-tide", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Newman's sixteenth discourse in Discourses Addressed to Mixed Congregations turns from bodily suffering to Christ's conscious bearing of the Passion and the weight of sin. The 1906 edition prints a Passion-tide footnote at its opening; this establishes the historical season but not a particular Sunday. The collection was published by 1849, while the individual delivery date is unestablished. The discourse includes the source\u2019s concluding prayer."}, {"anchor": "n-h001", "label": "English original", "text": "DISCOURSE XVI."}, {"anchor": "n-h002", "label": "English original", "text": "MENTAL SUFFERINGS OF OUR LORD IN HIS PASSION."}, {"anchor": "n-p001", "label": "English original", "text": "EVERY passage in the history of our Lord and Saviour is of unfathomable depth, and affords inexhaustible matter of contemplation. All that concerns Him is infinite, and what we first discern is but the surface of that which begins and ends in eternity. It would be presumptuous for any one short of saints and doctors to attempt to comment on His words and deeds, except in the way of meditation; but meditation and mental prayer are so much a duty in all who wish to cherish true faith and love towards Him, that it may be allowed us, my brethren, under the guidance of holy men who have gone before us, to dwell and enlarge upon what otherwise would more fitly be adored than scrutinised. And certain times of the year, this especially,* call upon us to consider, as closely and minutely as we can, even the more sacred portions of the Gospel history. I would rather be thought feeble or officious in my treatment of them, than wanting to the Season; and so I now proceed because the religious usage of the Church requires it, and though any individual preacher may well shrink from it, to direct your thoughts to a subject, especially suitable now, and about which many of us perhaps think very little, the sufferings which our Lord endured in His innocent and sinless soul."}, {"anchor": "n-p002", "label": "English original", "text": "You know, my brethren, that our Lord and Saviour, though He was God, was also perfect man; and hence He had not only a body, but a soul likewise, such as ours, though pure from all stain of evil. He did not take a body without a soul, God forbid! for that would not have been to become man. How would He have sanctified our nature by taking a nature which was not ours? Man without a soul is on a level with the beasts of the field; but our Lord came to save a race capable of praising and obeying Him, possessed of immortality, though that immortality had lost its promised blessedness. Man was created in the image of God, and that image is in his soul; when then his Maker, by an unspeakable condescension, came in his nature, He took on Himself a soul in order to take on Him a body; He took on Him a soul as the means of His union with a body; He took on Him in the first place the soul, then the body of man, both at once, but in this order, the soul and the body; He Himself created the soul which He took on Himself, while He took His body from the flesh of the Blessed Virgin, His Mother. Thus He became perfect man with body and soul; and as He took on Him a body of flesh and nerves, which admitted of wounds and death, and was capable of suffering, so did He take a soul, too, which was susceptible of that suffering, and moreover was susceptible of the pain and sorrow which are proper to a human soul; and, as His atoning passion was undergone in the body, so it was undergone in the soul also."}, {"anchor": "n-p003", "label": "English original", "text": "As the solemn days proceed, we shall be especially called on, my brethren, to consider His sufferings in the body, His seizure, His forced journeyings to and fro, His blows and wounds, His scourging, the crown of thorns, the nails, the Cross. They are all summed up in the Crucifix itself, as it meets our eyes; they are represented all at once on His sacred flesh, as it hangs up before us\u2014and meditation is made easy by the spectacle. It is otherwise with the sufferings of His soul; they cannot be painted for us, nor can they even be duly investigated: they are beyond both sense and thought; and yet they anticipated His bodily sufferings. The agony, a pain of the soul, not of the body, was the first act of His tremendous sacrifice; \u201cMy soul is sorrowful even unto death,\u201d He said; nay; if He suffered in the body, it really was in the soul, for the body did but convey the infliction on to that which was the true recipient and seat of the suffering."}, {"anchor": "n-p004", "label": "English original", "text": "This it is very much to the purpose to insist upon; I say, it was not the body that suffered, but the soul in the body; it was the soul and not the body which was the seat of the suffering of the Eternal Word. Consider, then, there is no real pain, though there may be apparent suffering, when there is no kind of inward sensibility or spirit to be the seat of it. A tree, for instance, has life, organs, growth, and decay; it may be wounded and injured; it droops, and is killed; but it does not suffer, because it has no mind or sensible principle within it. But wherever this gift of an immaterial principle is found, there pain is possible, and greater pain according to the quality of the gift. Had we no spirit of any kind, we should feel as little as a tree feels; had we no soul, we should not feel pain more acutely than a brute feels it; but, being men, we feel pain in a way in which none but those who have souls can feel it."}, {"anchor": "n-p005", "label": "English original", "text": "Living beings, I say, feel more or less according to the spirit which is in them; brutes feel far less than man, because they cannot reflect on what they feel; they have no advertence or direct consciousness of their sufferings. This it is that makes pain so trying, viz., that we cannot help thinking of it, while we suffer it. It is before us, it possesses the mind, it keeps our thoughts fixed upon it. Whatever draws the mind off the thought of it lessens it; hence friends try to amuse us when we are in pain, for amusement is a diversion. If the pain is slight, they sometimes succeed with us; and then we are, so to say, without pain, even while we suffer. And hence it continually happens that in violent exercise or labour, men meet with blows or cuts, so considerable and so durable in their effect, as to bear witness to the suffering which must have attended their infliction, of which nevertheless they recollect nothing. And in quarrels and in battles wounds are received which, from the excitement of the moment, are brought home to the consciousness of the combatant, not by the pain at the time of receiving them, but by the loss of blood that follows."}, {"anchor": "n-p006", "label": "English original", "text": "I will show you presently, my brethren, how I mean to apply what I have said to the consideration of our Lord's sufferings; first I will make another remark. Consider, then, that hardly any one stroke of pain is intolerable; it is intolerable when it continues. You cry out perhaps that you cannot bear more; patients feel as if they could stop the surgeon's hand, simply because he continues to pain them. Their feeling is that they have borne as much as they can bear; as if the continuance and not the intenseness was what made it too much for them. What does this mean, but that the memory of the foregoing moments of pain acts upon and (as it were) edges the pain that succeeds? If the third or fourth or twentieth moment of pain could be taken by itself, if the succession of the moments that preceded it could be forgotten, it would be no more than the first moment, as bearable as the first (taking away the shock which accompanies the first); but what makes it unbearable is, that it is the twentieth; that the first, the second, the third, on to the nineteenth moment of pain, are all concentrated in the twentieth; so that every additional moment of pain has all the force, the ever-increasing force, of all that has preceded it. Hence, I repeat, it is that brute animals would seem to feel so little pain, because, that is, they have not the power of reflection or of consciousness. They do not know they exist; they do not contemplate themselves; they do not look backwards or forwards; every moment as it succeeds is their all; they wander over the face of the earth, and see this thing and that, and feel pleasure and pain, but still they take everything as it comes, and then let it go again, as men do in dreams. They have memory, but not the memory of an intellectual being; they put together nothing, they make nothing properly one and individual to themselves out of the particular sensations which they receive; nothing is to them a reality, or has a substance, beyond those sensations; they are but sensible of a number of successive impressions. And hence, as their other feelings, so their feeling of pain is but faint and dull, in spite of their outward manifestations of it. It is the intellectual comprehension of pain, as a whole diffused through successive moments, which gives it its special power and keenness, and it is the soul only, which a brute has not, which is capable of that comprehension."}, {"anchor": "n-p007", "label": "English original", "text": "Now apply this to the sufferings of our Lord;\u2014do you recollect their offering Him wine mingled with myrrh, when He was on the point of being crucified? He would not drink of it; why? because such a portion would have stupefied His mind, and He was bent on bearing the pain in all its bitterness. You see from this, my brethren, the character of His sufferings; He would have fain escaped them, had that been His Father's will; \u201cIf it be possible,\u201d He said, \u201clet this chalice pass from Me;\u201d but since it was not possible, He says calmly and decidedly to the Apostle, who would have rescued Him from suffering, \u201cThe chalice which My Father hath given Me, shall I not drink it?\u201d If He was to suffer, He gave Himself to suffering; He did not come to suffer as little as He could; He did not turn away His face from the suffering; He confronted it, or, as I may say, He breasted it, that every particular portion of it might make its due impression on Him. And as men are superior to brute animals, and are affected by pain more than they, by reason of the mind within them, which gives a substance to pain, such as it cannot have in the instance of brutes; so, in like manner, our Lord felt pain of the body, with an advertence and a consciousness, and therefore with a keenness and intensity, and with a unity of perception, which none of us can possibly fathom or compass, because His soul was so absolutely in His power, so simply free from the influence of distractions, so fully directed upon the pain, so utterly surrendered, so simply subjected to the suffering. And thus He may truly be said to have suffered the whole of His passion in every moment of it."}, {"anchor": "n-p008", "label": "English original", "text": "Recollect that our Blessed Lord was in this respect different from us, that, though He was perfect man, yet there was a power in Him greater than His soul, which ruled His soul, for He was God. The soul of other men is subjected to its own wishes, feelings, impulses, passions, perturbations; His soul was subjected simply to His Eternal and Divine Personality. Nothing happened to His soul by chance, or on a sudden; He never was taken by surprise; nothing affected Him without His willing beforehand that it should affect Him. Never did He sorrow, or fear, or desire, or rejoice in spirit, but He first willed to be sorrowful, or afraid, or desirous, or joyful. When we suffer, it is because outward agents and the uncontrollable emotions of our minds bring suffering upon us. We are brought under the discipline of pain involuntarily, we suffer from it more or less acutely according to accidental circumstances, we find our patience more or less tried by it according to our state of mind, and we do our best to provide alleviations or remedies of it. We cannot anticipate beforehand how much of it will come upon us, or how far we shall be able to sustain it; nor can we say afterwards why we have felt just what we have felt, or why we did not bear the suffering better. It was otherwise with our Lord. His Divine Person was not subject, could not be exposed, to the influence of His own human affections and feelings, except so far as He chose. I repeat, when He chose to fear, He feared; when He chose to be angry, He was angry; when He chose to grieve, He was grieved. He was not open to emotion, but He opened upon Himself voluntarily the impulse by which He was moved. Consequently, when He determined to suffer the pain of His vicarious passion, whatever He did, He did, as the Wise Man says, instanter, \u201cearnestly,\u201d with His might; He did not do it by halves; He did not turn away His mind from the suffering as we do\u2014(how should He, who came to suffer, who could not have suffered but of His own act?) no, He did not say and unsay, do and undo; He said and He did; He said, \u201cLo, I come to do Thy will, O God; sacrifice and offering Thou wouldest not, but a body hast Thou fitted to Me\u201d. He took a body in order that He might suffer; He became man, that He might suffer as man; and when His hour was come, that hour of Satan and of darkness, the hour when sin was to pour its full malignity upon Him, it followed that He offered Himself wholly, a holocaust, a whole burnt-offering;\u2014as the whole of His body, stretched out upon the Cross, so the whole of His soul, His whole advertence, His whole consciousness, a mind awake, a sense acute, a living co-operation, a present, absolute intention, not a virtual permission, not a heartless submission, this did He present to His tormentors. His passion was an action; He lived most energetically, while He lay languishing, fainting, and dying. Nor did He die, except by an act of the will; for He bowed His head, in command as well as in resignation, and said, \u201cFather, into Thy hands I commend My Spirit;\u201d He gave the word, He surrendered His soul, He did not lose it."}, {"anchor": "n-p009", "label": "English original", "text": "Thus you see, my brethren, had our Lord only suffered in the body, and in it not so much as other men, still as regards the pain, He would have really suffered indefinitely more, because pain is to be measured by the power of realising it. God was the sufferer; God suffered in His human nature; the sufferings belonged to God, and were drunk up, were drained out to the bottom of the chalice, because God drank them; not tasted or sipped, not flavoured, disguised by human medicaments, as man disposes of the cup of anguish. And what I have been saying will further serve to answer an objection, which I shall proceed to notice, and which perhaps exists latently in the minds of many, and leads them to overlook the part which our Lord's soul had in His gracious satisfaction for sin."}, {"anchor": "n-p010", "label": "English original", "text": "Our Lord said, when His agony was commencing, \u201cMy soul is sorrowful unto death\u201d; now you may ask, my brethren, whether He had not certain consolations peculiar to Himself, impossible in any other; which diminished or impeded the distress of His soul, and caused Him to feel, not more, but less than an ordinary man. For instance, He had a sense of innocence which no other sufferer could have; even His persecutors, even the false apostle who betrayed Him, the judge who sentenced Him, and the soldiers who conducted the execution, testified His innocence. \u201cI have condemned the innocent blood,\u201d said Judas; \u201cI am clear from the blood of this just Person,\u201d said Pilate; \u201cTruly this was a just Man,\u201d cried the centurion. And if even they, sinners, bore witness to His sinlessness, how much more did His own soul! And we know well that even in our own case, sinners as we are, on the consciousness of innocence or of guilt mainly turns our power of enduring opposition and calumny; how much more, you will say, in the case of our Lord, did the sense of inward sanctity compensate for the suffering and annihilate the shame! Again, you may say that He knew that His sufferings would be short, and that their issue would be joyful, whereas uncertainty of the future is the keenest element of human distress; but He could not have anxiety, for He was not in suspense; nor despondency or despair, for He never was deserted. And in confirmation you may refer to St. Paul, who expressly tells us that, \u201cfor the joy set before Him,\u201d our Lord \u201cdespised the shame\u201d. And certainly there is a marvellous calm and self-possession in all He does: consider His warning to the Apostles, \u201cWatch and pray, lest ye enter into temptation; the spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak\u201d; or His words to Judas, \u201cFriend, wherefore art thou come?\u201d and, \u201cJudas, betrayest thou the Son of Man with a kiss?\u201d or to Peter, \u201cAll that take the sword shall perish with the sword\u201d; or to the man who struck Him, \u201cIf I have spoken evil, bear witness of the evil; but if well, why smitest thou Me?\u201d or to His Mother, \u201cWoman, behold thy Son\u201d."}, {"anchor": "n-p011", "label": "English original", "text": "All this is true and much to be insisted on; but it quite agrees with, or rather illustrates, what I have been observing. My brethren, you have only said (to use a human phrase) that He was always Himself. His mind was its own centre, and was never in the slightest degree thrown off its heavenly and most perfect balance. What He suffered, He suffered because He put Himself under suffering, and that deliberately and calmly. As He said to the leper, \u201cI will, be thou clean\u201d; and to the paralytic, \u201cThy sins be forgiven thee\u201d; and to the centurion, \u201cI will come and heal him\u201d; and of Lazarus, \u201cI go to wake him out of sleep\u201d; so He said, \u201cNow I will begin to suffer,\u201d and He did begin. His composure is but the proof how entirely He governed His own mind. He drew back, at the proper moment, the bolts and fastenings, and opened the gates, and the floods fell right upon His soul in all their fulness. That is what St. Mark tells us of Him; and he is said to have written his Gospels from the very mouth of St. Peter, who was one of three witnesses present at the time. \u201cThey came,\u201d he says, \u201cto the place which is called Gethsemani; and He saith to His disciples, Sit you here while I pray. And He taketh with Him Peter and James and John, and He began to be frightened and to be very heavy.\u201d You see how deliberately He acts; He comes to a certain spot; and then, giving the word of command, and withdrawing the support of the Godhead from His soul, distress, terror, and dejection at once rush in upon it. Thus He walks forth into a mental agony with as definite an action as if it were some bodily torture, the fire or the wheel."}, {"anchor": "n-p012", "label": "English original", "text": "This being the case, you will see at once, my brethren, that it is nothing to the purpose to say that He would be supported under His trial by the consciousness of innocence and the anticipation of triumph; for His trial consisted in the withdrawal, as of other causes of consolation, so of that very consciousness and anticipation. The same act of the will which admitted the influence upon His soul of any distress at all, admitted all distresses at once. It was not the contest between antagonist impulses and views, coming from without, but the operation of an inward resolution. As men of self-command can turn from one thought to another at their will, so much more did He deliberately deny Himself the comfort, and satiate Himself with the woe. In that moment His soul thought not of the future, He thought only of the present burden which was upon Him, and which He had come upon earth to sustain."}, {"anchor": "n-p013", "label": "English original", "text": "And now, my brethren, what was it He had to bear, when He thus opened upon His soul the torrent of this predestinated pain? Alas! He had to bear what is well known to us, what is familiar to us, but what to Him was woe unutterable. He had to bear that which is so easy a thing to us, so natural, so welcome, that we cannot conceive of it as of a great endurance, but which to Him had the scent and the poison of death\u2014He had, my dear brethren, to bear the weight of sin; He had to bear your sins; He had to bear the sins of the whole world. Sin is an easy thing to us; we think little of it; we do not understand how the Creator can think much of it; we cannot bring our imagination to believe that it deserves retribution, and, when even in this world punishments follow upon it, we explain them away or turn our minds from them. But consider what sin is in itself; it is rebellion against God; it is a traitor's act who aims at the overthrow and death of His sovereign; it is that, if I may use a strong expression, which, could the Divine Governor of the world cease to be, would be sufficient to bring it about. Sin is the mortal enemy of the All-holy, so that He and it cannot be together; and as the All-holy drives it from His presence into the outer darkness, so, if God could be less than God, it is sin that would have power to make Him less. And here observe, my brethren, that when once Almighty Love, by taking flesh, entered this created system, and submitted Himself to its laws, then forthwith this antagonist of good and truth, taking advantage of the opportunity, flew at that flesh which He had taken, and fixed on it, and was its death. The envy of the Pharisees, the treachery of Judas, and the madness of the people, were but the instrument or the expression of the enmity which sin felt towards Eternal Purity as soon as, in infinite mercy towards men, He put Himself within its reach. Sin could not touch His Divine Majesty; but it could assail Him in that way in which He allowed Himself to be assailed, that is, through the medium of His humanity. And in the issue, in the death of God incarnate, you are but taught, my brethren, what sin is in itself, and what it was which then was falling, in its hour and in its strength, upon His human nature, when He allowed that nature to be so filled with horror and dismay at the very anticipation."}, {"anchor": "n-p014", "label": "English original", "text": "There, then, in that most awful hour, knelt the Saviour of the world, putting off the defences of His divinity, dismissing His reluctant Angels, who in myriads were ready at His call, and opening His arms, baring His breast, sinless as He was, to the assault of His foe,\u2014of a foe whose breath was a pestilence, and whose embrace was an agony. There He knelt, motionless and still, while the vile and horrible fiend clad His spirit in a robe steeped in all that is hateful and heinous in human crime, which clung close round His heart, and filled His conscience, and found its way into every sense and pore of His mind, and spread over Him a moral leprosy, till He almost felt Himself to be that which He never could be, and which His foe would fain have made Him. Oh, the horror, when He looked, and did not know Himself, and felt as a foul and loathsome sinner, from His vivid perception of that mass of corruption which poured over His head and ran down even to the skirts of His garments! Oh, the distraction, when He found His eyes, and hands, and feet, and lips, and heart, as if the members of the Evil One, and not of God! Are these the hands of the Immaculate Lamb of God, once innocent, but now red with ten thousand barbarous deeds of blood? are these His lips, not uttering prayer, and praise, and holy blessings, but as if defiled with oaths, and blasphemies, and doctrines of devils? or His eyes, profaned as they are by all the evil visions and idolatrous fascinations for which men have abandoned their adorable Creator? And His ears, they ring with sounds of revelry and of strife; and His heart is frozen with avarice, and cruelty, and unbelief; and His very memory is laden with every sin which has been committed since the fall, in all regions of the earth, with the pride of the old giants, and the lusts of the five cities, and the obduracy of Egypt, and the ambition of Babel, and the unthankfulness and scorn of Israel. Oh, who does not know the misery of a haunting thought which comes again and again, in spite of rejection, to annoy, if it cannot seduce? or of some odious and sickening imagination, in no sense one's own, but forced upon the mind from without? or of evil knowledge, gained with or without a man's fault, but which he would give a great price to be rid of at once and for ever? And adversaries such as these gather around Thee, Blessed Lord, in millions now; they come in troops more numerous than the locust or the palmer-worm, or the plagues of hail, and flies, and frogs, which were sent against Pharaoh. Of the living and of the dead and of the as yet unborn, of the lost and of the saved, of Thy people and of strangers, of sinners and of saints, all sins are there. Thy dearest are there, Thy saints and Thy chosen are upon Thee; Thy three Apostles, Peter, James, and John; but not as comforters, but as accusers, like the friends of Job, \u201csprinkling dust towards heaven,\u201d and heaping curses on Thy head. All are there but one; one only is not there, one only; for she who had no part in sin, she only could console Thee, and therefore she is not nigh. She will be near Thee on the Cross, she is separated from Thee in the garden. She has been Thy companion and Thy confidant through Thy life, she interchanged with Thee the pure thoughts and holy meditations of thirty years; but her virgin ear may not take in, nor may her immaculate heart conceive, what now is in vision before Thee. None was equal to the weight but God; sometimes before Thy saints Thou hast brought the image of a single sin, as it appears in the light of Thy countenance, or of venial sins, not mortal; and they have told us that the sight did all but kill them, nay, would have killed them, had it not been instantly withdrawn. The Mother of God, for all her sanctity, nay by reason of it, could not have borne even one brood of that innumerable progeny of Satan which now compasses Thee about. It is the long history of a world, and God alone can bear the load of it. Hopes blighted, vows broken, lights quenched, warnings scorned, opportunities lost; the innocent betrayed, the young hardened, the penitent relapsing, the just overcome, the aged failing; the sophistry of misbelief, the wilfulness of passion, the obduracy of pride, the tyranny of habit, the canker of remorse, the wasting fever of care, the anguish of shame, the pining of disappointment, the sickness of despair; such cruel, such pitiable spectacles, such heartrending, revolting, detestable, maddening scenes; nay, the haggard faces, the convulsed lips, the flushed cheek, the dark brow of the willing slaves of evil, they are all before Him now; they are upon Him and in Him. They are with Him instead of that ineffable peace which has inhabited His soul since the moment of His conception. They are upon Him, they are all but His own; He cries to His Father as if He were the criminal, not the victim; His agony takes the form of guilt and compunction. He is doing penance, He is making confession, He is exercising contrition, with a reality and a virtue infinitely greater than that of all saints and penitents together; for He is the One Victim for us all, the sole Satisfaction, the real Penitent, all but the real sinner."}, {"anchor": "n-p015", "label": "English original", "text": "He rises languidly from the earth, and turns around to meet the traitor and his band, now quickly nearing the deep shade. He turns, and lo! there is blood upon His garment and in His footprints. Whence come these first-fruits of the passion of the Lamb? no soldier's scourge has touched His shoulders, nor the hangman's nails His hands and feet. My brethren, He has bled before His time; He has shed blood; yes, and it is His agonising soul which has broken up His framework of flesh and poured it forth. His passion has begun from within. That tormented Heart, the seat of tenderness and love, began at length to labour and to beat with vehemence beyond its nature; \u201cthe foundations of the great deep were broken up;\u201d the red streams rushed forth so copious and fierce as to overflow the veins, and bursting through the pores, they stood in a thick dew over His whole skin; then forming into drops, they rolled down full and heavy, and drenched the ground."}, {"anchor": "n-p016", "label": "English original", "text": "\u201cMy soul is sorrowful even unto death,\u201d He said. It has been said of that dreadful pestilence which now is upon us, that it begins with death; by which is meant that it has no stage or crisis, that hope is over when it comes, and that what looks like its course is but the death agony and the process of dissolution; and thus our Atoning Sacrifice, in a much higher sense, began with this passion of woe, and only did not die, because at His Omnipotent will His Heart did not break, nor Soul separate from Body, till He had suffered on the Cross."}, {"anchor": "n-p017", "label": "English original", "text": "No; He has not yet exhausted that full chalice, from which at first His natural infirmity shrank. The seizure and the arraignment, and the buffeting, and the prison, and the trial, and the mocking, and the passing to and fro, and the scourging, and the crown of thorns, and the slow march to Calvary, and the crucifixion, these are all to come. A night and a day, hour after hour, is slowly to run out before the end comes, and the satisfaction is completed."}, {"anchor": "n-p018", "label": "English original", "text": "And then, when the appointed moment arrived, and He gave the word, as His passion had begun with His soul, with the soul did it end. He did not die of bodily exhaustion, or of bodily pain; at His will His tormented Heart broke, and He commended His Spirit to the Father."}, {"anchor": "n-s001", "label": "English original", "text": "* * * * * *"}, {"anchor": "n-p019", "label": "English original", "text": "\u201cO Heart of Jesus, all Love, I offer Thee these humble prayers for myself, and for all those who unite themselves with me in Spirit to adore Thee. O holiest Heart of Jesus most lovely, I intend to renew and to offer to Thee these acts of adoration and these prayers, for myself a wretched sinner, and for all those who are associated with me in Thy adoration, through all moments while I breathe, even to the end of my life. I recommend to Thee, O my Jesus, Holy Church, Thy dear spouse and our true Mother, all just souls and all poor sinners, the afflicted, the dying, and all mankind. Let not Thy Blood be shed for them in vain. Finally, deign to apply it in relief of the souls in Purgatory, of those in particular who have practised in the course of their life this holy devotion of adoring Thee.\u201d"}, {"anchor": "n-n001", "label": "English original", "text": "* Passion-tide."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-16-scripture-01", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Matthew 26:38\u201341 / Mark 14:34\u201338 (parallel quotations; added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-16-scripture-02", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "John 18:11, 23 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-16-scripture-03", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Hebrews 12:2 (added by editors)."}], "status": "English original", "text_kind": "english-original", "title": "Mental Sufferings of Our Lord in His Passion", "topics": ["Passion", "Christ's humanity", "suffering", "Gethsemane", "sin", "redemption"], "url": "sermons/newman-mixed-16.html"}, {"author": "John Henry Newman", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [19], "composition": "By 1849; individual delivery date unestablished", "id": "newman-mixed-17", "language": "en", "language_label": "English", "metadata": ["John Henry Newman, Discourses Addressed to Mixed Congregations, new impression (London, New York, and Bombay: Longmans, Green, and Co., 1906), discourse XVII, The Glories of Mary for the Sake of Her Son, pp. 342\u2013359.", "Internet Archive; Kelly, University of Toronto.", "Unspecified", "No particular liturgical occasion is established", "Ecclesiasticus 42 (added by editors)", "1 Corinthians 2:7, 10 (added by editors)", "John 1:1, 14 (added by editors)", "Hebrews 10:5 (added by editors)", "1 John 1:1, 3 (added by editors)", "Song of Songs 4:4 (added by editors)", "Luke 11:27\u201328 (added by editors)", "Luke 1:28, 42, 45 (added by editors; traditional wording)", "John 9:31 (added by editors)", "James 5:16 (added by editors)"], "occasion": "No specific liturgical occasion established", "occasion_detail": "No particular liturgical occasion is established", "occasion_label": "Unspecified", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Newman's seventeenth discourse in Discourses Addressed to Mixed Congregations argues that Mary's honours serve belief in her Son's Incarnation. It considers her divine maternity, purity and intercessory power, then turns to the Church's presentation of her honours and a concluding prayer. The collection was published by 1849; this text follows the 1906 impression, retaining its expectation of a future proclamation of Mary's immaculate conception. The printing date is not the composition date. This is an English original, including its closing Latin hymn. No particular Marian feast is established."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-h001", "label": "English original", "text": "DISCOURSE XVII."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-h002", "label": "English original", "text": "THE GLORIES OF MARY FOR THE SAKE OF HER SON."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p001", "label": "English original", "text": "We know, my brethren, that in the natural world nothing is superfluous, nothing incomplete, nothing independent; but part answers to part, and all details combine to form one mighty whole. Order and harmony are among the first perfections which we discern in this visible creation; and the more we examine into it, the more widely and minutely they are found to belong to it. \u201cAll things are double,\u201d says the Wise Man, \u201cone against another; and He hath made nothing defective.\u201d It is the very character and definition of \u201cthe heavens and the earth,\u201d as contrasted with the void or chaos which preceded them, that everything is now subjected to fixed laws; and every motion, and influence, and effect can be accounted for, and, were our knowledge sufficient, could be anticipated. Moreover, it is plain, on the other hand, that it is only in proportion to our observation and our research that this truth becomes apparent; for though a number of things even at first sight are seen to proceed according to an established and beautiful order, yet in other instances the law to which they are conformed is with difficulty discovered; and the words \u201cchance,\u201d and \u201chazard,\u201d and \u201cfortune,\u201d have come into use as expressions of our ignorance. Accordingly, you may fancy rash and irreligious minds who are engaged day after day in the business of the world, suddenly looking out into the heavens or upon the earth, and criticising the great Architect, arguing that there are creatures in existence which are rude or defective in their constitution, and asking questions which would but evidence their want of scientific education."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p002", "label": "English original", "text": "The case is the same as regards the supernatural world. The great truths of Revelation are all connected together and form a whole. Every one can see this in a measure even at a glance, but to understand the full consistency and harmony of Catholic teaching requires study and meditation. Hence, as philosophers of this world bury themselves in museums and laboratories, descend into mines, or wander among woods or on the sea-shore, so the inquirer into heavenly truths dwells in the cell and the oratory, pouring forth his heart in prayer, collecting his thoughts in meditation, dwelling on the idea of Jesus, or of Mary, or of grace, or of eternity, and pondering the words of holy men who have gone before him, till before his mental sight arises the hidden wisdom of the perfect, \u201cwhich God predestined before the world unto our glory,\u201d and which He \u201creveals unto them by His Spirit\u201d. And, as ignorant men may dispute the beauty and harmony of the visible creation, so men, who for six days in the week are absorbed in worldly toil, who live for wealth, or name, or self-indulgence, or profane knowledge, and do but give their leisure moments to the thought of religion, never raising their souls to God, never asking for His enlightening grace, never chastening their hearts and bodies, never steadily contemplating the objects of faith, but judging hastily and peremptorily according to their private views or the humour of the hour; such men, I say, in like manner, may easily, or will for certain, be surprised and shocked at portions of revealed truth, as if strange, or harsh, or extreme, or inconsistent, and will in whole or in part reject it."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p003", "label": "English original", "text": "I am going to apply this remark to the subject of the prerogatives with which the Church invests the Blessed Mother of God. They are startling and difficult to those whose imagination is not accustomed to them, and whose reason has not reflected on them; but the more carefully and religiously they are dwelt on, the more, I am sure, will they be found essential to the Catholic faith, and integral to the worship of Christ. This simply is the point which I shall insist on\u2014disputable indeed by aliens from the Church, but most clear to her children\u2014that the glories of Mary are for the sake of Jesus; and that we praise and bless her as the first of creatures, that we may duly confess Him as our sole Creator."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p004", "label": "English original", "text": "When the Eternal Word decreed to come on earth, He did not purpose, He did not work, by halves; but He came to be a man like any of us, to take a human soul and body, and to make them His own. He did not come in a mere apparent or accidental form, as Angels appear to men; nor did He merely overshadow an existing man, as He overshadows His saints, and call Him by the name of God; but He \u201cwas made flesh\u201d. He attached to Himself a manhood, and became as really and truly man as He was God, so that henceforth He was both God and man, or, in other words, He was One Person in two natures, divine and human. This is a mystery so marvellous, so difficult, that faith alone firmly receives it; the natural man may receive it for a while, may think he receives it, but never really receives it; begins, as soon as he has professed it, secretly to rebel against it, evades it, or revolts from it. This he has done from the first; even in the lifetime of the beloved disciple men arose who said that our Lord had no body at all, or a body framed in the heavens, or that He did not suffer, but another suffered in His stead, or that He was but for a time possessed of the human form which was born and which suffered, coming into it at its baptism, and leaving it before its crucifixion, or, again, that He was a mere man. That \u201cin the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God, and the Word was made flesh and dwelt among us,\u201d was too hard a thing for the unregenerate reason."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p005", "label": "English original", "text": "The case is the same at this day; mere Protestants have seldom any real perception of the doctrine of God and man in one Person. They speak in a dreamy, shadowy way of Christ\u2019s divinity; but, when their meaning is sifted, you will find them very slow to commit themselves to any statement sufficient to express the Catholic dogma. They will tell you at once, that the subject is not to be inquired into, for that it is impossible to inquire into it at all without being technical and subtile. Then, when they comment on the Gospels, they will speak of Christ, not simply and consistently as God, but as a being made up of God and man, partly one and partly the other, or between both, or as a man inhabited by a special Divine presence. Sometimes they even go on to deny that He was in heaven the Son of God, saying that He became the Son when He was conceived of the Holy Ghost; and they are shocked, and think it a mark both of reverence and good sense to be shocked, when they hear the Man spoken of simply and plainly as God. They cannot bear to have it said, except as a figure or mode of speaking, that God had a human body, or that God suffered; they think that the \u201cAtonement,\u201d and \u201cSanctification through the Spirit,\u201d as they speak, is the sum and substance of the Gospel, and they are shy of any dogmatic expression which goes beyond them. Such, I believe, is the ordinary character of the Protestant notions among us as to the divinity of Christ, whether among members of the Anglican communion, or dissenters from it, excepting a small remnant of them."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p006", "label": "English original", "text": "Now, if you would witness against these unchristian opinions, if you would bring out distinctly and beyond mistake and evasion, the simple idea of the Catholic Church that God is man, could you do it better than by laying down in St. John\u2019s words that \u201cGod became man\u201d? and again could you express this more emphatically and unequivocally than by declaring that He was born a man, or that He had a Mother? The world allows that God is man; the admission costs it little, for God is everywhere, and (as it may say) is everything; but it shrinks from confessing that God is the Son of Mary. It shrinks, for it is at once confronted with a severe fact, which violates and shatters its own unbelieving view of things; the revealed doctrine forthwith takes its true shape, and receives an historical reality; and the Almighty is introduced into His own world at a certain time and in a definite way. Dreams are broken and shadows depart; the Divine truth is no longer a poetical expression, or a devotional exaggeration, or a mystical economy, or a mythical representation. \u201cSacrifice and offering,\u201d the shadows of the Law, \u201cThou wouldest not, but a body hast Thou fitted to me. That which was from the beginning, which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have diligently looked upon, and our hands have handled,\u201d \u201cThat which we have seen and have heard, declare we unto you\u201d;\u2014such is the record of the Apostle, in opposition to those \u201cspirits\u201d which denied that \u201cJesus Christ had appeared in the flesh,\u201d and which \u201cdissolved\u201d Him by denying either His human nature or His divine. And the confession that Mary is Deipara, or the Mother of God, is that safeguard wherewith we seal up and secure the doctrine of the Apostle from all evasion, and that test whereby we detect all the pretences of those bad spirits of \u201cAntichrist which have gone out into the world\u201d. It declares that He is God; it implies that He is man; it suggests to us that He is God still, though He has become man, and that He is true man though He is God. By witnessing to the process of the union, it secures the reality of the two subjects of the union, of the divinity and of the manhood. If Mary is the Mother of God, Christ must be literally Emmanuel, God with us. And hence it was, that, when time went on, and the bad spirits and false prophets grew stronger and bolder, and found a way into the Catholic body itself, then the Church, guided by God, could find no more effectual and sure way of expelling them than that of using this word Deipara against them; and, on the other hand, when they came up again from the realms of darkness, and plotted the utter overthrow of Christian faith in the sixteenth century, then they could find no more certain expedient for their hateful purpose than that of reviling and blaspheming the prerogatives of Mary, for they knew full well that, if they could once get the world to dishonour the Mother, the dishonour of the Son would follow close. The Church and Satan agreed together in this, that Son and Mother went together; and the experience of three centuries has confirmed their testimony, for Catholics who have honoured the Mother, still worship the Son, while Protestants, who now have ceased to confess the Son, began then by scoffing at the Mother."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p007", "label": "English original", "text": "You see, then, my brethren, in this particular, the harmonious consistency of the revealed system, and the bearing of one doctrine upon another; Mary is exalted for the sake of Jesus. It was fitting that she, as being a creature, though the first of creatures, should have an office of ministration. She, as others, came into the world to do a work, she had a mission to fulfil; her grace and her glory are not for her own sake, but for her Maker\u2019s; and to her is committed the custody of the Incarnation; this is her appointed office,\u2014\u201cA Virgin shall conceive, and bear a Son, and they shall call His Name Emmanuel\u201d. As she was once on earth, and was personally the guardian of her Divine Child, as she carried Him in her womb, folded Him in her embrace, and suckled Him at her breast, so now, and to the latest hour of the Church, do her glories and the devotion paid her proclaim and define the right faith concerning Him as God and man. Every church which is dedicated to her, every altar which is raised under her invocation, every image which represents her, every litany in her praise, every Hail Mary for her continual memory, does but remind us that there was One who, though He was all-blessed from all eternity, yet for the sake of sinners, \u201cdid not shrink from the Virgin\u2019s womb\u201d. Thus she is the Turris Davidica, as the Church calls her, \u201cthe Tower of David\u201d; the high and strong defence of the King of the true Israel; and hence the Church also addresses her in the Antiphon, as having \u201calone destroyed all heresies in the whole world\u201d."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p008", "label": "English original", "text": "And here, my brethren, a fresh thought opens upon us, which is naturally implied in what has been said. If the Deipara is to witness of Emmanuel, she must be necessarily more than the Deipara. For consider; a defence must be strong in order to be a defence; a tower must be, like that Tower of David, \u201cbuilt with bulwarks\u201d; \u201ca thousand bucklers hang upon it, all the armour of valiant men\u201d. It would not have sufficed, in order to bring out and impress on us the idea that God is man, had His Mother been an ordinary person. A mother without a home in the Church, without dignity, without gifts, would have been, as far as the defence of the Incarnation goes, no mother at all. She would not have remained in the memory, or the imagination of men. If she is to witness and remind the world that God became man, she must be on a high and eminent station for the purpose. She must be made to fill the mind, in order to suggest the lesson. When she once attracts our attention, then, and not till then, she begins to preach Jesus. \u201cWhy should she have such prerogatives,\u201d we ask, \u201cunless He be God? and what must He be by nature, when she is so high by grace?\u201d This is why she has other prerogatives besides, namely, the gifts of personal purity and intercessory power, distinct from her maternity; she is personally endowed that she may perform her office well; she is exalted in herself that she may minister to Christ."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p009", "label": "English original", "text": "For this reason, she has been made more glorious in her person than in her office; her purity is a higher gift than her relationship to God. This is what is implied in Christ\u2019s answer to the woman in the crowd, who cried out, when He was preaching, \u201cBlessed is the womb that bare Thee, and the breasts which Thou hast sucked\u201d. He replied by pointing out to His disciples a higher blessedness; \u201cYea, rather, blessed,\u201d He said, \u201care they who hear the word of God and keep it\u201d. You know, my brethren, that Protestants take these words in disparagement of our Lady\u2019s greatness, but they really tell the other way. For consider them; He lays down a principle, that it is more blessed to keep His commandments than to be His Mother; but who, even of Protestants, will say that she did not keep His commandments? She kept them surely, and our Lord does but say that such obedience was in a higher line of privilege than her being His Mother; she was more blessed in her detachment from creatures, in her devotion to God, in her virginal purity, in her fulness of grace, than in her maternity. This is the constant teaching of the Holy Fathers: \u201cMore blessed was Mary,\u201d says St. Augustine, \u201cin receiving Christ\u2019s faith, than in conceiving Christ\u2019s flesh;\u201d and St. Chrysostom declares, that she would not have been blessed, though she had borne Him in the body, had she not heard the word of God and kept it. This, of course, is an impossible case; for she was made holy, that she might be made His Mother, and the two blessednesses cannot be divided. She who was chosen to supply flesh and blood to the Eternal Word, was first filled with grace in soul and body; still, she had a double blessedness, of office and of qualification for it, and the latter was the greater."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p010", "label": "English original", "text": "And it is on this account that the Angel calls her blessed; \u201cFull of grace,\u201d he says, \u201cBlessed among women\u201d; and St. Elizabeth also, when she cries out, \u201cBlessed thou that hast believed\u201d. Nay, she herself bears a like testimony, when the Angel announced to her the high favour which was coming on her. Though all Jewish women in each successive age had been hoping to be Mother of the Christ, so that marriage was honourable among them, childlessness a reproach, she alone had put aside the desire and the thought of so great a dignity. She, who was to bear the Christ, gave no welcome to the great announcement that she was to bear Him; and why did she thus act towards it? because she had been inspired, the first of womankind, to dedicate her virginity to God, and she did not welcome a privilege which seemed to involve a forfeiture of her vow. How shall this be, she asked, seeing I am to live separate from man? Nor, till the Angel told her that the conception would be miraculous and from the Holy Ghost, did she put aside her \u201ctrouble\u201d of mind, recognise him securely as God\u2019s messenger, and bow her head in awe and thankfulness to God\u2019s condescension."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p011", "label": "English original", "text": "Mary then is a specimen, and more than a specimen, in the purity of her soul and body, of what man was before his fall, and what he would have been, had he risen to his full perfection. It had been hard, it had been a victory for the Evil One, had the whole race passed away, nor any one instance in it occurred to show what the Creator had intended it to be in its original state. Adam, you know, was created in the image and after the likeness of God; his frail and imperfect nature, stamped with a Divine seal, was supported and exalted by an indwelling of Divine grace. Impetuous passion did not exist in him, except as a latent element and a possible evil; ignorance was dissipated by the clear light of the Spirit; and reason, sovereign over every motion of his soul, was simply subjected to the will of God. Nay, even his body was preserved from every wayward appetite and affection, and was promised immortality instead of dissolution. Thus he was in a supernatural state; and, had he not sinned, year after year would he have advanced in merit and grace, and in God\u2019s favour, till he passed from paradise to heaven. But he fell; and his descendants were born in his likeness; and the world grew worse instead of better, and judgment after judgment cut off generations of sinners in vain, and improvement was hopeless; \u201cbecause man was flesh,\u201d and, \u201cthe thoughts of his heart were bent upon evil at all times\u201d."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p012", "label": "English original", "text": "However, a remedy had been determined in heaven; a Redeemer was at hand; God was about to do a great work, and He purposed to do it suitably; \u201cwhere sin abounded, grace was to abound more\u201d. Kings of the earth, when they have sons born to them, forthwith scatter some large bounty, or raise some high memorial; they honour the day, or the place, or the heralds of the auspicious event, with some corresponding mark of favour; nor did the coming of Emmanuel innovate on the world\u2019s established custom. It was a season of grace and prodigy, and these were to be exhibited in a special manner in the person of His Mother. The course of ages was to be reversed; the tradition of evil was to be broken; a gate of light was to be opened amid the darkness, for the coming of the Just;\u2014a Virgin conceived and bore Him. It was fitting, for His honour and glory, that she, who was the instrument of His bodily presence, should first be a miracle of His grace; it was fitting that she should triumph, where Eve had failed, and should \u201cbruise the serpent\u2019s head\u201d by the spotlessness of her sanctity. In some respects, indeed, the curse was not reversed; Mary came into a fallen world, and resigned herself to its laws; she, as also the Son she bore, was exposed to pain of soul and body, she was subjected to death; but she was not put under the power of sin. As grace was infused into Adam from the first moment of his creation, so that he never had experience of his natural poverty, till sin reduced him to it; so was grace given from the first in still ampler measure to Mary, and she never incurred, in fact, Adam\u2019s deprivation. She began where others end, whether in knowledge or in love. She was from the first clothed in sanctity, destined for perseverance, luminous and glorious in God\u2019s sight, and incessantly employed in meritorious acts, which continued till her last breath. Hers was emphatically \u201cthe path of the just, which, as the shining light, goeth forward and increaseth even to the perfect day\u201d; and sinlessness in thought, word, and deed, in small things as well as great, in venial matters as well as grievous, is surely but the natural and obvious sequel of such a beginning. If Adam might have kept himself from sin in his first state, much more shall we expect immaculate perfection in Mary."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p013", "label": "English original", "text": "Such is her prerogative of sinless perfection, and it is, as her maternity, for the sake of Emmanuel; hence she answered the Angel\u2019s salutation, Gratia plena, with the humble acknowledgment, Ecce ancilla Domini, \u201cBehold the handmaid of the Lord\u201d. And like to this is her third prerogative, which follows both from her maternity and from her purity, and which I will mention as completing the enumeration of her glories. I mean her intercessory power. For, if \u201cGod heareth not sinners, but if a man be a worshipper of Him, and do His will, him He heareth\u201d; if \u201cthe continual prayer of a just man availeth much\u201d; if faithful Abraham was required to pray for Abimelech, \u201cfor he was a prophet\u201d; if patient Job was to \u201cpray for his friends,\u201d for he had \u201cspoken right things before God\u201d; if meek Moses, by lifting up his hands, turned the battle in favour of Israel against Amalec; why should we wonder at hearing that Mary, the only spotless child of Adam\u2019s seed, has a transcendent influence with the God of grace? And if the Gentiles at Jerusalem sought Philip, because he was an Apostle, when they desired access to Jesus, and Philip spoke to Andrew, as still more closely in our Lord\u2019s confidence, and then both came to Him, is it strange that the Mother should have power with the Son, distinct in kind from that of the purest angel and the most triumphant saint? If we have faith to admit the Incarnation itself, we must admit it in its fulness; why then should we start at the gracious appointments which arise out of it, or are necessary to it, or are included in it? If the Creator comes on earth in the form of a servant and a creature, why may not His Mother, on the other hand, rise to be the Queen of heaven, and be clothed with the sun, and have the moon under her feet?"}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p014", "label": "English original", "text": "I am not proving these doctrines to you, my brethren; the evidence of them lies in the declaration of the Church. The Church is the oracle of religious truth, and dispenses what the apostles committed to her in every time and place. We must take her word, then, without proof, because she is sent to us from God to teach us how to please Him; and that we do so is the test whether we be really Catholics or no. I am not proving then what you already receive, but I am showing you the beauty and the harmony, in one out of many instances, of the Church\u2019s teaching; which are so well adapted, as they are divinely intended, to recommend that teaching to the inquirer and to endear it to her children. One word more, and I have done; I have shown you how full of meaning are the truths themselves which the Church teaches concerning the Most Blessed Virgin, and now consider how full of meaning also has been the Church\u2019s dispensation of them."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p015", "label": "English original", "text": "You will find, that, in this respect, as in Mary\u2019s prerogatives themselves, there is the same careful reference to the glory of Him who gave them to her. You know, when first He went out to preach, she kept apart from Him; she interfered not with His work; and, even when He was gone up on high, yet she, a woman, went not out to preach or teach, she seated not herself in the Apostolic chair, she took no part in the priest\u2019s office; she did but humbly seek her Son in the daily Mass of those, who, though her ministers in heaven, were her superiors in the Church on earth. Nor, when she and they had left this lower scene, and she was a Queen upon her Son\u2019s right hand, not even then did she ask of Him to publish her name to the ends of the world, or to hold her up to the world\u2019s gaze, but she remained waiting for the time, when her own glory should be necessary for His. He indeed had been from the very first proclaimed by Holy Church, and enthroned in His temple, for He was God; ill had it beseemed the living Oracle of Truth to have withholden from the faithful the very object of their adoration; but it was otherwise with Mary. It became her, as a creature, a mother, and a woman, to stand aside and make way for the Creator, to minister to her Son, and to win her way into the world\u2019s homage by sweet and gracious persuasion. So when His name was dishonoured, then it was that she did Him service; when Emmanuel was denied, then the Mother of God (as it were) came forward; when heretics said that God was not incarnate, then was the time for her own honours. And then, when as much as this had been accomplished, she had done with strife; she fought not for herself. No fierce controversy, no persecuted confessors, no heresiarch, no anathema, were necessary for her gradual manifestation; as she had increased day by day in grace and merit at Nazareth, while the world knew not of her, so has she raised herself aloft silently, and has grown into her place in the Church by a tranquil influence and a natural process. She was as some fair tree, stretching forth her fruitful branches and her fragrant leaves, and overshadowing the territory of the saints. And thus the Antiphon speaks of her: \u201cLet thy dwelling be in Jacob, and thine inheritance in Israel, and strike thy roots in My elect\u201d. Again, \u201cAnd so in Sion was I established, and in the holy city I likewise rested, and in Jerusalem was my power. And I took root in an honourable people, and in the glorious company of the saints was I detained. I was exalted like a cedar in Lebanus, and as a cypress in Mount Sion; I have stretched out my branches as the terebinth, and my branches are of honour and grace.\u201d Thus was she reared without hands, and gained a modest victory, and exerts a gentle sway, which she has not claimed. When dispute arose about her among her children, she hushed it; when objections were urged against her, she waived her claims and waited; till now, in this very day, should God so will, she will win at length her most radiant crown, and, without opposing voice, and amid the jubilation of the whole Church, she will be hailed as immaculate in her conception."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p016", "label": "English original", "text": "Such art thou, Holy Mother, in the creed and in the worship of the Church, the defence of many truths, the grace and smiling light of every devotion. In thee, O Mary, is fulfilled, as we can bear it, an original purpose of the Most High. He once had meant to come on earth in heavenly glory, but we sinned; and then He could not safely visit us, except with a shrouded radiance and a bedimmed Majesty, for He was God. So He came Himself in weakness, not in power; and He sent thee, a creature, in His stead, with a creature\u2019s comeliness and lustre suited to our state. And now thy very face and form, dear Mother, speak to us of the Eternal; not like earthly beauty, dangerous to look upon, but like the morning star, which is thy emblem, bright and musical, breathing purity, telling of heaven, and infusing peace. O harbinger of day! O hope of the pilgrim! lead us still as thou hast led; in the dark night, across the bleak wilderness, guide us on to our Lord Jesus, guide us home."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-p017", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Maria, mater gratiae,\nDulcis parens clementiae,\nTu nos ab hoste protege\nEt mortis hor\u00e2 suscipe."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-scripture-01", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Ecclesiasticus 42 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-scripture-02", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "1 Corinthians 2:7, 10 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-scripture-03", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "John 1:1, 14 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-scripture-04", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Hebrews 10:5 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-scripture-05", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "1 John 1:1, 3 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-scripture-06", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Song of Songs 4:4 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-scripture-07", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Luke 11:27\u201328 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-scripture-08", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Luke 1:28, 42, 45 (added by editors; traditional wording)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-scripture-09", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "John 9:31 (added by editors)."}, {"anchor": "newman-mixed-17-scripture-10", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "James 5:16 (added by editors)."}], "status": "English original", "text_kind": "english-original", "title": "The Glories of Mary for the Sake of Her Son", "topics": ["Mary", "Incarnation", "Mother of God", "holiness", "intercession", "faith"], "url": "sermons/newman-mixed-17.html"}]};
